if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-370"]="Who was Born in Newgate, and during a Life of continu’d Variety for Threescore Years, besides her Childhood, was Twelve Year a Whore, five times a Wife (whereof once to her own Brother), Twelve Year a Thief, Eight Year a Transported Felon in Virginia, at last grew Rich, liv’d Honest, and dies a Penitent. Written from her own Memorandums . . .\n\nby Daniel Defoe\n\n# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE\n\nThe world is so taken up of late with novels and romances, that it will be hard for a private history to be taken for genuine, where the names and other circumstances of the person are concealed, and on this account we must be content to leave the reader to pass his own opinion upon the ensuing sheet, and take it just as he pleases.\n\nThe author is here supposed to be writing her own history, and in the very beginning of her account she gives the reasons why she thinks fit to conceal her true name, after which there is no occasion to say any more about that.\n\nIt is true that the original of this story is put into new words, and the style of the famous lady we here speak of is a little altered; particularly she is made to tell her own tale in modester words that she told it at first, the copy which came first to hand having been written in language more like one still in Newgate than one grown penitent and humble, as she afterwards pretends to be.\n\nThe pen employed in finishing her story, and making it what you now see it to be, has had no little difficulty to put it into a dress fit to be seen, and to make it speak language fit to be read. When a woman debauched from her youth, nay, even being the offspring of debauchery and vice, comes to give an account of all her vicious practices, and even to descend to the particular occasions and circumstances by which she ran through in threescore years, an author must be hard put to it wrap it up so clean as not to give room, especially for vicious readers, to turn it to his disadvantage.\n\nAll possible care, however, has been taken to give no lewd ideas, no immodest turns in the new dressing up of this story; no, not to the worst parts of her expressions. To this purpose some of the vicious part of her life, which could not be modestly told, is quite left out, and several other parts are very much shortened. What is left ’tis hoped will not offend the chastest reader or the modest hearer; and as the best use is made even of the worst story, the moral ’tis hoped will keep the reader serious, even where the story might incline him to be otherwise. To give the history of a wicked life repented of, necessarily requires that the wicked part should be make as wicked as the real history of it will bear, to illustrate and give a beauty to the penitent part, which is certainly the best and brightest, if related with equal spirit and life.\n\nIt is suggested there cannot be the same life, the same brightness and beauty, in relating the penitent part as is in the criminal part. If there is any truth in that suggestion, I must be allowed to say ’tis because there is not the same taste and relish in the reading, and indeed it is too true that the difference lies not in the real worth of the subject so much as in the gust and palate of the reader.\n\nBut as this work is chiefly recommended to those who know how to read it, and how to make the good uses of it which the story all along recommends to them, so it is to be hoped that such readers will be more pleased with the moral than the fable, with the application than with the relation, and with the end of the writer than with the life of the person written of.\n\nThere is in this story abundance of delightful incidents, and all of them usefully applied. There is an agreeable turn artfully given them in the relating, that naturally instructs the reader, either one way or other. The first part of her lewd life with the young gentleman at Colchester has so many happy turns given it to expose the crime, and warn all whose circumstances are adapted to it, of the ruinous end of such things, and the foolish, thoughtless, and abhorred conduct of both the parties, that it abundantly atones for all the lively description she gives of her folly and wickedness.\n\nThe repentance of her lover at the Bath, and how brought by the just alarm of his fit of sickness to abandon her; the just caution given there against even the lawful intimacies of the dearest friends, and how unable they are to preserve the most solemn resolutions of virtue without divine assistance; these are parts which, to a just discernment, will appear to have more real beauty in them, than all the amorous chain of story which introduces it.\n\nIn a word, as the whole relation is carefully garbled of all the levity and looseness that was in it, so it all applied, and with the utmost care, to virtuous and religious uses. None can, without being guilty of manifest injustice, cast any reproach upon it, or upon our design in publishing it.\n\nThe advocates for the stage have, in all ages, made this the great argument to persuade people that their plays are useful, and that they ought to be allowed in the most civilised and in the most religious government; namely, that they are applied to virtuous purposes, and that by the most lively representations, they fail not to recommend virtue and generous principles, and to discourage and expose all sorts of vice and corruption of manners; and were it true that they did so, and that they constantly adhered to that rule, as the test of their acting on the theatre, much might be said in their favour.\n\nThroughout the infinite variety of this book, this fundamental is most strictly adhered to; there is not a wicked action in any part of it, but is first and last rendered unhappy and unfortunate; there is not a superlative villain brought upon the stage, but either he is brought to an unhappy end, or brought to be a penitent; there is not an ill thing mentioned but it is condemned, even in the relation, nor a virtuous, just thing but it carries its praise along with it. What can more exactly answer the rule laid down, to recommend even those representations of things which have so many other just objections leaving against them? namely, of example, of bad company, obscene language, and the like.\n\nUpon this foundation this book is recommended to the reader as a work from every part of which something may be learned, and some just and religious inference is drawn, by which the reader will have something of instruction, if he pleases to make use of it.\n\nAll the exploits of this lady of fame, in her depredations upon mankind, stand as so many warnings to honest people to beware of them, intimating to them by what methods innocent people are drawn in, plundered and robbed, and by consequence how to avoid them. Her robbing a little innocent child, dressed fine by the vanity of the mother, to go to the dancing-school, is a good memento to such people hereafter, as is likewise her picking the gold watch from the young lady’s side in the Park.\n\nHer getting a parcel from a hare-brained wench at the coaches in St. John Street; her booty made at the fire, and again at Harwich, all give us excellent warnings in such cases to be more present to ourselves in sudden surprises of every sort.\n\nHer application to a sober life and industrious management at last in Virginia, with her transported spouse, is a story fruitful of instruction to all the unfortunate creatures who are obliged to seek their re-establishment abroad, whether by the misery of transportation or other disaster; letting them know that diligence and application have their due encouragement, even in the remotest parts of the world, and that no case can be so low, so despicable, or so empty of prospect, but that an unwearied industry will go a great way to deliver us from it, will in time raise the meanest creature to appear again in the world, and give him a new case for his life.\n\nThere are a few of the serious inferences which we are led by the hand to in this book, and these are fully sufficient to justify any man in recommending it to the world, and much more to justify the publication of it.\n\nThere are two of the most beautiful parts still behind, which this story gives some idea of, and lets us into the parts of them, but they are either of them too long to be brought into the same volume, and indeed are, as I may call them, whole volumes of themselves, viz.: 1. The life of her governess, as she calls her, who had run through, it seems, in a few years, all the eminent degrees of a gentlewoman, a whore, and a bawd; a midwife and a midwife-keeper, as they are called; a pawnbroker, a childtaker, a receiver of thieves, and of thieves’ purchase, that is to say, of stolen goods; and in a word, herself a thief, a breeder up of thieves and the like, and yet at last a penitent.\n\nThe second is the life of her transported husband, a highwayman, who it seems, lived a twelve years’ life of successful villainy upon the road, and even at last came off so well as to be a volunteer transport, not a convict; and in whose life there is an incredible variety.\n\nBut, as I have said, these are things too long to bring in here, so neither can I make a promise of the coming out by themselves.\n\nWe cannot say, indeed, that this history is carried on quite to the end of the life of this famous Moll Flanders, as she calls herself, for nobody can write their own life to the full end of it, unless they can write it after they are dead. But her husband’s life, being written by a third hand, gives a full account of them both, how long they lived together in that country, and how they both came to England again, after about eight years, in which time they were grown very rich, and where she lived, it seems, to be very old, but was not so extraordinary a penitent as she was at first; it seems only that indeed she always spoke with abhorrence of her former life, and of every part of it.\n\nIn her last scene, at Maryland and Virginia, many pleasant things happened, which makes that part of her life very agreeable, but they are not told with the same elegancy as those accounted for by herself; so it is still to the more advantage that we break off here.\n\n# MOLL FLANDERS\n\nMy true name is so well known in the records or registers at Newgate, and in the Old Bailey, and there are some things of such consequence still depending there, relating to my particular conduct, that it is not be expected I should set my name or the account of my family to this work; perhaps, after my death, it may be better known; at present it would not be proper, no not though a general pardon should be issued, even without exceptions and reserve of persons or crimes.\n\nIt is enough to tell you, that as some of my worst comrades, who are out of the way of doing me harm (having gone out of the world by the steps and the string, as I often expected to go), knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, so you may give me leave to speak of myself under that name till I dare own who I have been, as well as who I am.\n\nI have been told that in one of our neighbour nations, whether it be in France or where else I know not, they have an order from the king, that when any criminal is condemned, either to die, or to the galleys, or to be transported, if they leave any children, as such are generally unprovided for, by the poverty or forfeiture of their parents, so they are immediately taken into the care of the Government, and put into a hospital called the House of Orphans, where they are bred up, clothed, fed, taught, and when fit to go out, are placed out to trades or to services, so as to be well able to provide for themselves by an honest, industrious behaviour.\n\nHad this been the custom in our country, I had not been left a poor desolate girl without friends, without clothes, without help or helper in the world, as was my fate; and by which I was not only exposed to very great distresses, even before I was capable either of understanding my case or how to amend it, but brought into a course of life which was not only scandalous in itself, but which in its ordinary course tended to the swift destruction both of soul and body.\n\nBut the case was otherwise here. My mother was convicted of felony for a certain petty theft scarce worth naming, viz. having an opportunity of borrowing three pieces of fine holland of a certain draper in Cheapside. The circumstances are too long to repeat, and I have heard them related so many ways, that I can scarce be certain which is the right account.\n\nHowever it was, this they all agree in, that my mother pleaded her belly, and being found quick with child, she was respited for about seven months; in which time having brought me into the world, and being about again, she was called down, as they term it, to her former judgment, but obtained the favour of being transported to the plantations, and left me about half a year old; and in bad hands, you may be sure.\n\nThis is too near the first hours of my life for me to relate anything of myself but by hearsay; it is enough to mention, that as I was born in such an unhappy place, I had no parish to have recourse to for my nourishment in my infancy; nor can I give the least account how I was kept alive, other than that, as I have been told, some relation of my mother’s took me away for a while as a nurse, but at whose expense, or by whose direction, I know nothing at all of it.\n\nThe first account that I can recollect, or could ever learn of myself, was that I had wandered among a crew of those people they call gypsies, or Egyptians; but I believe it was but a very little while that I had been among them, for I had not had my skin discoloured or blackened, as they do very young to all the children they carry about with them; nor can I tell how I came among them, or how I got from them.\n\nIt was at Colchester, in Essex, that those people left me; and I have a notion in my head that I left them there (that is, that I hid myself and would not go any farther with them), but I am not able to be particular in that account; only this I remember, that being taken up by some of the parish officers of Colchester, I gave an account that I came into the town with the gypsies, but that I would not go any farther with them, and that so they had left me, but whither they were gone that I knew not, nor could they expect it of me; for though they send round the country to inquire after them, it seems they could not be found.\n\nI was now in a way to be provided for; for though I was not a parish charge upon this or that part of the town by law, yet as my case came to be known, and that I was too young to do any work, being not above three years old, compassion moved the magistrates of the town to order some care to be taken of me, and I became one of their own as much as if I had been born in the place.\n\nIn the provision they made for me, it was my good hap to be put to nurse, as they call it, to a woman who was indeed poor but had been in better circumstances, and who got a little livelihood by taking such as I was supposed to be, and keeping them with all necessaries, till they were at a certain age, in which it might be supposed they might go to service or get their own bread.\n\nThis woman had also had a little school, which she kept to teach children to read and to work; and having, as I have said, lived before that in good fashion, she bred up the children she took with a great deal of art, as well as with a great deal of care.\n\nBut that which was worth all the rest, she bred them up very religiously, being herself a very sober, pious woman, very house-wifely and clean, and very mannerly, and with good behaviour. So that in a word, expecting a plain diet, coarse lodging, and mean clothes, we were brought up as mannerly and as genteelly as if we had been at the dancing-school.\n\nI was continued here till I was eight years old, when I was terrified with news that the magistrates (as I think they called them) had ordered that I should go to service. I was able to do but very little service wherever I was to go, except it was to run of errands and be a drudge to some cookmaid, and this they told me of often, which put me into a great fright; for I had a thorough aversion to going to service, as they called it (that is, to be a servant), though I was so young; and I told my nurse, as we called her, that I believed I could get my living without going to service, if she pleased to let me; for she had taught me to work with my needle, and spin worsted, which is the chief trade of that city, and I told her that if she would keep me, I would work for her, and I would work very hard.\n\nI talked to her almost every day of working hard; and, in short, I did nothing but work and cry all day, which grieved the good, kind woman so much, that at last she began to be concerned for me, for she loved me very well.\n\nOne day after this, as she came into the room where all we poor children were at work, she sat down just over against me, not in her usual place as mistress, but as if she set herself on purpose to observe me and see me work. I was doing something she had set me to; as I remember, it was marking some shirts which she had taken to make, and after a while she began to talk to me. “Thou foolish child,” says she, “thou art always crying” (for I was crying then); “prithee, what dost cry for?” “Because they will take me away,” says I, “and put me to service, and I can’t work housework.” “Well, child,” says she, “but though you can’t work housework, as you call it, you will learn it in time, and they won’t put you to hard things at first.” “Yes, they will,” says I, “and if I can’t do it they will beat me, and the maids will beat me to make me do great work, and I am but a little girl and I can’t do it”; and then I cried again, till I could not speak any more to her.\n\nThis moved my good motherly nurse, so that she from that time resolved I should not go to service yet; so she bid me not cry, and she would speak to Mr. Mayor, and I should not go to service till I was bigger.\nWell, this did not satisfy me, for to think of going to service was such a frightful thing to me, that if she had assured me I should not have gone till I was twenty years old, it would have been the same to me; I should have cried, I believe, all the time, with the very apprehension of its being to be so at last.\n\nWhen she saw that I was not pacified yet, she began to be angry with me. “And what would you have?” says she; “don’t I tell you that you shall not go to service till your are bigger?” “Ay,” said I, “but then I must go at last.” “Why, what?” said she; “is the girl mad? What would you be—a gentlewoman?” “Yes,” says I, and cried heartily till I roared out again.\n\nThis set the old gentlewoman a-laughing at me, as you may be sure it would. “Well, madam, forsooth,” says she, gibing at me, “you would be a gentlewoman; and pray how will you come to be a gentlewoman? What! will you do it by your fingers’ end?”\n\n“Yes,” says I again, very innocently.\n\n“Why, what can you earn?” says she; “what can you get at your work?”\n\n“Threepence,” said I, “when I spin, and fourpence when I work plain work.”\n\n“Alas! poor gentlewoman,” said she again, laughing, “what will that do for thee?”\n\n“It will keep me,” says I, “if you will let me live with you.” And this I said in such a poor petitioning tone, that it made the poor woman’s heart yearn to me, as she told me afterwards.\n\n“But,” says she, “that will not keep you and buy you clothes too; and who must buy the little gentlewoman clothes?” says she, and smiled all the while at me.\n\n“I will work harder, then,” says I, “and you shall have it all.”\n\n“Poor child! it won’t keep you,” says she; “it will hardly keep you in victuals.”\n\n“Then I will have no victuals,” says I, again very innocently; “let me but live with you.”\n\n“Why, can you live without victuals?” says she.\n\n“Yes,” again says I, very much like a child, you may be sure, and still I cried heartily.\n\nI had no policy in all this; you may easily see it was all nature; but it was joined with so much innocence and so much passion that, in short, it set the good motherly creature a-weeping too, and she cried at last as fast as I did, and then took me and led me out of the teaching-room. “Come,” says she, “you shan’t go to service; you shall live with me”; and this pacified me for the present.\n\nSome time after this, she going to wait on the Mayor, and talking of such things as belonged to her business, at last my story came up, and my good nurse told Mr. Mayor the whole tale. He was so pleased with it, that he would call his lady and his two daughters to hear it, and it made mirth enough among them, you may be sure.\n\nHowever, not a week had passed over, but on a sudden comes Mrs. Mayoress and her two daughters to the house to see my old nurse, and to see her school and the children. When they had looked about them a little, “Well, Mrs. ——,” says the Mayoress to my nurse, “and pray which is the little lass that intends to be a gentlewoman?” I heard her, and I was terribly frighted at first, though I did not know why neither; but Mrs. Mayoress comes up to me. “Well, miss,” says she, “and what are you at work upon?” The word miss was a language that had hardly been heard of in our school, and I wondered what sad name it was she called me. However, I stood up, made a curtsy, and she took my work out of my hand, looked on it, and said it was very well; then she took up one of the hands. “Nay,” says she, “the child may come to be a gentlewoman for aught anybody knows; she has a gentlewoman’s hand,” says she. This pleased me mightily, you may be sure; but Mrs. Mayoress did not stop there, but giving me my work again, she put her hand in her pocket, gave me a shilling, and bid me mind my work, and learn to work well, and I might be a gentlewoman for aught she knew.\n\nNow all this while my good old nurse, Mrs. Mayoress, and all the rest of them did not understand me at all, for they meant one sort of thing by the word gentlewoman, and I meant quite another; for alas! all I understood by being a gentlewoman was to be able to work for myself, and get enough to keep me without that terrible bugbear going to service, whereas they meant to live great, rich and high, and I know not what.\n\nWell, after Mrs. Mayoress was gone, her two daughters came in, and they called for the gentlewoman too, and they talked a long while to me, and I answered them in my innocent way; but always, if they asked me whether I resolved to be a gentlewoman, I answered Yes. At last one of them asked me what a gentlewoman was? That puzzled me much; but, however, I explained myself negatively, that it was one that did not go to service, to do housework. They were pleased to be familiar with me, and like my little prattle to them, which, it seems, was agreeable enough to them, and they gave me money too.\n\nAs for my money, I gave it all to my mistress-nurse, as I called her, and told her she should have all I got for myself when I was a gentlewoman, as well as now. By this and some other of my talk, my old tutoress began to understand me about what I meant by being a gentlewoman, and that I understood by it no more than to be able to get my bread by my own work; and at last she asked me whether it was not so.\n\nI told her, yes, and insisted on it, that to do so was to be a gentlewoman; “for,” says I, “there is such a one,” naming a woman that mended lace and washed the ladies’ laced-heads; “she,” says I, “is a gentlewoman, and they call her madam.”\n\n“Poor child,” says my good old nurse, “you may soon be such a gentlewoman as that, for she is a person of ill fame, and has had two or three bastards.”\n\nI did not understand anything of that; but I answered, “I am sure they call her madam, and she does not go to service nor do housework”; and therefore I insisted that she was a gentlewoman, and I would be such a gentlewoman as that.\n\nThe ladies were told all this again, to be sure, and they made themselves merry with it, and every now and then the young ladies, Mr. Mayor’s daughters, would come and see me, and ask where the little gentlewoman was, which made me not a little proud of myself.\n\nThis held a great while, and I was often visited by these young ladies, and sometimes they brought others with them; so that I was known by it almost all over the town.\n\nI was now about ten years old, and began to look a little womanish, for I was mighty grave and humble, very mannerly, and as I had often heard the ladies say I was pretty, and would be a very handsome woman, so you may be sure that hearing them say so made me not a little proud. However, that pride had no ill effect upon me yet; only, as they often gave me money, and I gave it to my old nurse, she, honest woman, was so just to me as to lay it all out again for me, and gave me head-dresses, and linen, and gloves, and ribbons, and I went very neat, and always clean; for that I would do, and if I had rags on, I would always be clean, or else I would dabble them in water myself; but, I say, my good nurse, when I had money given me, very honestly laid it out for me, and would always tell the ladies this or that was bought with their money; and this made them oftentimes give me more, till at last I was indeed called upon by the magistrates, as I understood it, to go out to service; but then I was come to be so good a workwoman myself, and the ladies were so kind to me, that it was plain I could maintain myself—that is to say, I could earn as much for my nurse as she was able by it to keep me—so she told them that if they would give her leave, she would keep the gentlewoman, as she called me, to be her assistant and teach the children, which I was very well able to do; for I was very nimble at my work, and had a good hand with my needle, though I was yet very young.\n\nBut the kindness of the ladies of the town did not end here, for when they came to understand that I was no more maintained by the public allowance as before, they gave me money oftener than formerly; and as I grew up they brought me work to do for them, such as linen to make, and laces to mend, and heads to dress up, and not only paid me for doing them, but even taught me how to do them; so that now I was a gentlewoman indeed, as I understood that word, I not only found myself clothes and paid my nurse for my keeping, but got money in my pocket too beforehand.\n\nThe ladies also gave me clothes frequently of their own or their children’s; some stockings, some petticoats, some gowns, some one thing, some another, and these my old woman managed for me like a mere mother, and kept them for me, obliged me to mend them, and turn them and twist them to the best advantage, for she was a rare housewife.\n\nAt last one of the ladies took so much fancy to me that she would have me home to her house, for a month, she said, to be among her daughters.\nNow, though this was exceeding kind in her, yet, as my old good woman said to her, unless she resolved to keep me for good and all, she would do the little gentlewoman more harm than good. “Well,” says the lady, “that’s true; and therefore I’ll only take her home for a week, then, that I may see how my daughters and she agree together, and how I like her temper, and then I’ll tell you more; and in the meantime, if anybody comes to see her as they used to do, you may only tell them you have sent her out to my house.”\n\nThis was prudently managed enough, and I went to the lady’s house; but I was so pleased there with the young ladies, and they so pleased with me, that I had enough to do to come away, and they were as unwilling to part with me.\n\nHowever, I did come away, and lived almost a year more with my honest old woman, and began now to be very helpful to her; for I was almost fourteen years old, was tall of my age, and looked a little womanish; but I had such a taste of genteel living at the lady’s house that I was not so easy in my old quarters as I used to be, and I thought it was fine to be a gentlewoman indeed, for I had quite other notions of a gentlewoman now than I had before; and as I thought, I say, that it was fine to be a gentlewoman, so I loved to be among gentlewomen, and therefore I longed to be there again.\n\nAbout the time that I was fourteen years and a quarter old, my good nurse, mother I rather to call her, fell sick and died. I was then in a sad condition indeed, for as there is no great bustle in putting an end to a poor body’s family when once they are carried to the grave, so the poor good woman being buried, the parish children she kept were immediately removed by the church-wardens; the school was at an end, and the children of it had no more to do but just stay at home till they were sent somewhere else; and as for what she left, her daughter, a married woman with six or seven children, came and swept it all away at once, and removing the goods, they had no more to say to me than to jest with me, and tell me that the little gentlewoman might set up for herself if she pleased.\n\nI was frighted out of my wits almost, and knew not what to do, for I was, as it were, turned out of doors to the wide world, and that which was still worse, the old honest woman had two-and-twenty shillings of mine in her hand, which was all the estate the little gentlewoman had in the world; and when I asked the daughter for it, she huffed me and laughed at me, and told me she had nothing to do with it.\n\nIt was true the good, poor woman had told her daughter of it, and that it lay in such a place, that it was the child’s money, and had called once or twice for me to give it me, but I was, unhappily, out of the way somewhere or other, and when I came back she was past being in a condition to speak of it. However, the daughter was so honest afterwards as to give it me, though at first she used me cruelly about it.\n\nNow was I a poor gentlewoman indeed, and I was just that very night to be turned into the wide world; for the daughter removed all the goods, and I had not so much as a lodging to go to, or a bit of bread to eat. But it seems some of the neighbours, who had known my circumstances, took so much compassion of me as to acquaint the lady in whose family I had been a week, as I mentioned above; and immediately she sent her maid to fetch me away, and two of her daughters came with the maid though unsent. So I went with them, bag and baggage, and with a glad heart, you may be sure. The fright of my condition had made such an impression upon me, that I did not want now to be a gentlewoman, but was very willing to be a servant, and that any kind of servant they thought fit to have me be.\n\nBut my new generous mistress, for she exceeded the good woman I was with before, in everything, as well as in the matter of estate; I say, in everything except honesty; and for that, though this was a lady most exactly just, yet I must not forget to say on all occasions, that the first, though poor, was as uprightly honest as it was possible for any one to be.\n\nI was no sooner carried away, as I have said, by this good gentlewoman, but the first lady, that is to say, the Mayoress that was, sent her two daughters to take care of me; and another family which had taken notice of me when I was the little gentlewoman, and had given me work to do, sent for me after her, so that I was mightily made of, as we say; nay, and they were not a little angry, especially madam the Mayoress, that her friend had taken me away from her, as she called it; for, as she said, I was hers by right, she having been the first that took any notice of me. But they that had me would not part with me; and as for me, though I should have been very well treated with any of the others, yet I could not be better than where I was.\n\nHere I continued till I was between seventeen and eighteen years old, and here I had all the advantages for my education that could be imagined; the lady had masters home to the house to teach her daughters to dance, and to speak French, and to write, and other to teach them music; and I was always with them, I learned as fast as they; and though the masters were not appointed to teach me, yet I learned by imitation and inquiry all that they learned by instruction and direction; so that, in short, I learned to dance and speak French as well as any of them, and to sing much better, for I had a better voice than any of them. I could not so readily come at playing on the harpsichord or spinet, because I had no instrument of my own to practice on, and could only come at theirs in the intervals when they left it, which was uncertain; but yet I learned tolerably well too, and the young ladies at length got two instruments, that is to say, a harpsichord and a spinet too, and then they taught me themselves. But as to dancing, they could hardly help my learning country-dances, because they always wanted me to make up even number; and, on the other hand, they were as heartily willing to learn me everything that they had been taught themselves, as I could be to take the learning.\n\nBy this means I had, as I have said above, all the advantages of education that I could have had if I had been as much a gentlewoman as they were with whom I lived; and in some things I had the advantage of my ladies, though they were my superiors; but they were all the gifts of nature, and which all their fortunes could not furnish. First, I was apparently handsomer than any of them; secondly, I was better shaped; and, thirdly, I sang better, by which I mean I had a better voice; in all which you will, I hope, allow me to say, I do not speak my own conceit of myself, but the opinion of all that knew the family.\n\nI had with all these the common vanity of my sex, viz. that being really taken for very handsome, or, if you please, for a great beauty, I very well knew it, and had as good an opinion of myself as anybody else could have of me; and particularly I loved to hear anybody speak of it, which could not but happen to me sometimes, and was a great satisfaction to me.\n\nThus far I have had a smooth story to tell of myself, and in all this part of my life I not only had the reputation of living in a very good family, and a family noted and respected everywhere for virtue and sobriety, and for every valuable thing; but I had the character too of a very sober, modest, and virtuous young woman, and such I had always been; neither had I yet any occasion to think of anything else, or to know what a temptation to wickedness meant.\n\nBut that which I was too vain of was my ruin, or rather my vanity was the cause of it. The lady in the house where I was had two sons, young gentlemen of very promising parts and of extraordinary behaviour, and it was my misfortune to be very well with them both, but they managed themselves with me in a quite different manner.\n\nThe eldest, a gay gentleman that knew the town as well as the country, and though he had levity enough to do an ill-natured thing, yet had too much judgment of things to pay too dear for his pleasures; he began with the unhappy snare to all women, viz. taking notice upon all occasions how pretty I was, as he called it, how agreeable, how well-carriaged, and the like; and this he contrived so subtly, as if he had known as well how to catch a woman in his net as a partridge when he went a-setting; for he would contrive to be talking this to his sisters when, though I was not by, yet when he knew I was not far off but that I should be sure to hear him. His sisters would return softly to him, “Hush, brother, she will hear you; she is but in the next room.” Then he would put it off and talk softlier, as if he had not known it, and begin to acknowledge he was wrong; and then, as if he had forgot himself, he would speak aloud again, and I, that was so well pleased to hear it, was sure to listen for it upon all occasions.\n\nAfter he had thus baited his hook, and found easily enough the method how to lay it in my way, he played an opener game; and one day, going by his sister’s chamber when I was there, doing something about dressing her, he comes in with an air of gaiety. “Oh, Mrs. Betty,” said he to me, “how do you do, Mrs. Betty? Don’t your cheeks burn, Mrs. Betty?” I made a curtsy and blushed, but said nothing. “What makes you talk so, brother?” says the lady. “Why,” says he, “we have been talking of her below-stairs this half-hour.” “Well,” says his sister, “you can say no harm of her, that I am sure, so ’tis no matter what you have been talking about.” “Nay,” says he, “’tis so far from talking harm of her, that we have been talking a great deal of good, and a great many fine things have been said of Mrs. Betty, I assure you; and particularly, that she is the handsomest young woman in Colchester; and, in short, they begin to toast her health in the town.”\n\n“I wonder at you, brother,” says the sister. “Betty wants but one thing, but she had as good want everything, for the market is against our sex just now; and if a young woman have beauty, birth, breeding, wit, sense, manners, modesty, and all these to an extreme, yet if she have not money, she’s nobody, she had as good want them all for nothing but money now recommends a woman; the men play the game all into their own hands.”\n\nHer younger brother, who was by, cried, “Hold, sister, you run too fast; I am an exception to your rule. I assure you, if I find a woman so accomplished as you talk of, I say, I assure you, I would not trouble myself about the money.”\n\n“Oh,” says the sister, “but you will take care not to fancy one, then, without the money.”\n\n“You don’t know that neither,” says the brother.\n\n“But why, sister,” says the elder brother, “why do you exclaim so at the men for aiming so much at the fortune? You are none of them that want a fortune, whatever else you want.”\n\n“I understand you, brother,” replies the lady very smartly; “you suppose I have the money, and want the beauty; but as times go now, the first will do without the last, so I have the better of my neighbours.”\n\n“Well,” says the younger brother, “but your neighbours, as you call them, may be even with you, for beauty will steal a husband sometimes in spite of money, and when the maid chances to be handsomer than the mistress, she oftentimes makes as good a market, and rides in a coach before her.”\n\nI thought it was time for me to withdraw and leave them, and I did so, but not so far but that I heard all their discourse, in which I heard abundance of the fine things said of myself, which served to prompt my vanity, but, as I soon found, was not the way to increase my interest in the family, for the sister and the younger brother fell grievously out about it; and as he said some very disobliging things to her upon my account, so I could easily see that she resented them by her future conduct to me, which indeed was very unjust to me, for I had never had the least thought of what she suspected as to her younger brother; indeed, the elder brother, in his distant, remote way, had said a great many things as in jest, which I had the folly to believe were in earnest, or to flatter myself with the hopes of what I ought to have supposed he never intended, and perhaps never thought of.\n\nIt happened one day that he came running upstairs, towards the room where his sisters used to sit and work, as he often used to do; and calling to them before he came in, as was his way too, I, being there alone, stepped to the door, and said, “Sir, the ladies are not here, they are walked down the garden.” As I stepped forward to say this, towards the door, he was just got to the door, and clasping me in his arms, as if it had been by chance, “Oh, Mrs. Betty,” says he, “are you here? That’s better still; I want to speak with you more than I do with them”; and then, having me in his arms, he kissed me three or four times.\n\nI struggled to get away, and yet did it but faintly neither, and he held me fast, and still kissed me, till he was almost out of breath, and then, sitting down, says, “Dear Betty, I am in love with you.”\n\nHis words, I must confess, fired my blood; all my spirits flew about my heart and put me into disorder enough, which he might easily have seen in my face. He repeated it afterwards several times, that he was in love with me, and my heart spoke as plain as a voice, that I liked it; nay, whenever he said, “I am in love with you,” my blushes plainly replied, “Would you were, sir.”\n\nHowever, nothing else passed at that time; it was but a surprise, and when he was gone I soon recovered myself again. He had stayed longer with me, but he happened to look out at the window and see his sisters coming up the garden, so he took his leave, kissed me again, told me he was very serious, and I should hear more of him very quickly, and away he went, leaving me infinitely pleased, though surprised; and had there not been one misfortune in it, I had been in the right, but the mistake lay here, that Mrs. Betty was in earnest and the gentleman was not.\n\nFrom this time my head ran upon strange things, and I may truly say I was not myself; to have such a gentleman talk to me of being in love with me, and of my being such a charming creature, as he told me I was; these were things I knew not how to bear, my vanity was elevated to the last degree. It is true I had my head full of pride, but, knowing nothing of the wickedness of the times, I had not one thought of my own safety or of my virtue about me; and had my young master offered it at first sight, he might have taken any liberty he thought fit with me; but he did not see his advantage, which was my happiness for that time.\n\nAfter this attack it was not long but he found an opportunity to catch me again, and almost in the same posture; indeed, it had more of design in it on his part, though not on my part. It was thus: the young ladies were all gone a-visiting with their mother; his brother was out of town; and as for his father, he had been in London for a week before. He had so well watched me that he knew where I was, though I did not so much as know that he was in the house; and he briskly comes up the stairs and, seeing me at work, comes into the room to me directly, and began just as he did before, with taking me in his arms, and kissing me for almost a quarter of an hour together.\n\nIt was his younger sister’s chamber that I was in, and as there was nobody in the house but the maids below-stairs, he was, it may be, the ruder; in short, he began to be in earnest with me indeed. Perhaps he found me a little too easy, for God knows I made no resistance to him while he only held me in his arms and kissed me; indeed, I was too well pleased with it to resist him much.\n\nHowever, as it were, tired with that kind of work, we sat down, and there he talked with me a great while; he said he was charmed with me, and that he could not rest night or day till he had told me how he was in love with me, and, if I was able to love him again, and would make him happy, I should be the saving of his life, and many such fine things. I said little to him again, but easily discovered that I was a fool, and that I did not in the least perceive what he meant.\n\nThen he walked about the room, and taking me by the hand, I walked with him; and by and by, taking his advantage, he threw me down upon the bed, and kissed me there most violently; but, to give him his due, offered no manner of rudeness to me, only kissed a great while. After this he thought he had heard somebody come upstairs, so got off from the bed, lifted me up, professing a great deal of love for me, but told me it was all an honest affection, and that he meant no ill to me; and with that he put five guineas into my hand, and went away downstairs.\n\nI was more confounded with the money than I was before with the love, and began to be so elevated that I scarce knew the ground I stood on. I am the more particular in this part, that if my story comes to be read by any innocent young body, they may learn from it to guard themselves against the mischiefs which attend an early knowledge of their own beauty. If a young woman once thinks herself handsome, she never doubts the truth of any man that tells her he is in love with her; for if she believes herself charming enough to captivate him, ’tis natural to expect the effects of it.\n\nThis young gentleman had fired his inclination as much as he had my vanity, and, as if he had found that he had an opportunity and was sorry he did not take hold of it, he comes up again in half an hour or thereabouts, and falls to work with me again as before, only with a little less introduction.\n\nAnd first, when he entered the room, he turned about and shut the door. “Mrs. Betty,” said he, “I fancied before somebody was coming upstairs, but it was not so; however,” adds he, “if they find me in the room with you, they shan’t catch me a-kissing of you.” I told him I did not know who should be coming upstairs, for I believed there was nobody in the house but the cook and the other maid, and they never came up those stairs. “Well, my dear,” says he, “’tis good to be sure, however”; and so he sits down, and we began to talk. And now, though I was still all on fire with his first visit, and said little, he did as it were put words in my mouth, telling me how passionately he loved me, and that though he could not mention such a thing till he came to this estate, yet he was resolved to make me happy then, and himself too; that is to say, to marry me, and abundance of such fine things, which I, poor fool, did not understand the drift of, but acted as if there was no such thing as any kind of love but that which tended to matrimony; and if he had spoke of that, I had no room, as well as no power, to have said no; but we were not come that length yet.\n\nWe had not sat long, but he got up, and, stopping my very breath with kisses, threw me upon the bed again; but then being both well warmed, he went farther with me than decency permits me to mention, nor had it been in my power to have denied him at that moment, had he offered much more than he did.\n\nHowever, though he took these freedoms with me, it did not go to that which they call the last favour, which, to do him justice, he did not attempt; and he made that self-denial of his a plea for all his freedoms with me upon other occasions after this. When this was over, he stayed but a little while, but he put almost a handful of gold in my hand, and left me, making a thousand protestations of his passion for me, and of his loving me above all the women in the world.\n\nIt will not be strange if I now began to think, but alas! it was but with very little solid reflection. I had a most unbounded stock of vanity and pride, and but a very little stock of virtue. I did indeed case sometimes with myself what young master aimed at, but thought of nothing but the fine words and the gold; whether he intended to marry me, or not to marry me, seemed a matter of no great consequence to me; nor did my thoughts so much as suggest to me the necessity of making any capitulation for myself, till he came to make a kind of formal proposal to me, as you shall hear presently.\n\nThus I gave up myself to a readiness of being ruined without the least concern and am a fair memento to all young women whose vanity prevails over their virtue. Nothing was ever so stupid on both sides. Had I acted as became me, and resisted as virtue and honour require, this gentleman had either desisted his attacks, finding no room to expect the accomplishment of his design, or had made fair and honourable proposals of marriage; in which case, whoever had blamed him, nobody could have blamed me. In short, if he had known me, and how easy the trifle he aimed at was to be had, he would have troubled his head no farther, but have given me four or five guineas, and have lain with me the next time he had come at me. And if I had known his thoughts, and how hard he thought I would be to be gained, I might have made my own terms with him; and if I had not capitulated for an immediate marriage, I might for a maintenance till marriage, and might have had what I would; for he was already rich to excess, besides what he had in expectation; but I seemed wholly to have abandoned all such thoughts as these, and was taken up only with the pride of my beauty, and of being beloved by such a gentleman. As for the gold, I spent whole hours in looking upon it; I told the guineas over and over a thousand times a day. Never a poor vain creature was so wrapt up with every part of the story as I was, not considering what was before me, and how near my ruin was at the door; indeed, I think I rather wished for that ruin than studied to avoid it.\n\nIn the meantime, however, I was cunning enough not to give the least room to any in the family to suspect me, or to imagine that I had the least correspondence with this young gentleman. I scarce ever looked towards him in public, or answered if he spoke to me when anybody was near us; but for all that, we had every now and then a little encounter, where we had room for a word or two, and now and then a kiss, but no fair opportunity for the mischief intended; and especially considering that he made more circumlocution than, if he had known my thoughts, he had occasion for; and the work appearing difficult to him, he really made it so.\n\nBut as the devil is an unwearied tempter, so he never fails to find opportunity for that wickedness he invites to. It was one evening that I was in the garden, with his two younger sisters and himself, and all very innocently merry, when he found means to convey a note into my hand, by which he directed me to understand that he would to-morrow desire me publicly to go of an errand for him into the town, and that I should see him somewhere by the way.\n\nAccordingly, after dinner, he very gravely says to me, his sisters being all by, “Mrs. Betty, I must ask a favour of you.” “What’s that?” says his second sister. “Nay, sister,” says he very gravely, “if you can’t spare Mrs. Betty to-day, any other time will do.” Yes, they said, they could spare her well enough, and the sister begged pardon for asking, which they did but of mere course, without any meaning. “Well, but, brother,” says the eldest sister, “you must tell Mrs. Betty what it is; if it be any private business that we must not hear, you may call her out. There she is.” “Why, sister,” says the gentleman very gravely, “what do you mean? I only desire her to go into the High Street” (and then he pulls out a turnover), “to such a shop”; and then he tells them a long story of two fine neckcloths he had bid money for, and he wanted to have me go and make an errand to buy a neck to the turnover that he showed, to see if they would take my money for the neckcloths; to bid a shilling more, and haggle with them; and then he made more errands, and so continued to have such petty business to do, that I should be sure to stay a good while.\n\nWhen he had given me my errands, he told them a long story of a visit he was going to make to a family they all knew, and where was to be such-and-such gentlemen, and how merry they were to be, and very formally asks his sisters to go with him, and they as formally excused themselves, because of company that they had notice was to come and visit them that afternoon; which, by the way, he had contrived on purpose.\n\nHe had scarce done speaking to them, and giving me my errand, but his man came up to tell him that Sir W—— H——’s coach stopped at the door; so he runs down, and comes up again immediately. “Alas!” says he aloud, “there’s all my mirth spoiled at once; sir W—— has sent his coach for me, and desires to speak with me upon some earnest business.” It seems this Sir W—— was a gentleman who lived about three miles out of town, to whom he had spoken on purpose the day before, to lend him his chariot for a particular occasion, and had appointed it to call for him, as it did, about three o’clock.\n\nImmediately he calls for his best wig, hat, and sword, and ordering his man to go to the other place to make his excuse— that was to say, he made an excuse to send his man away—he prepares to go into the coach. As he was going, he stopped a while, and speaks mighty earnestly to me about his business, and finds an opportunity to say very softly to me, “Come away, my dear, as soon as ever you can.” I said nothing, but made a curtsy, as if I had done so to what he said in public. In about a quarter of an hour I went out too; I had no dress other than before, except that I had a hood, a mask, a fan, and a pair of gloves in my pocket; so that there was not the least suspicion in the house. He waited for me in the coach in a back-lane, which he knew I must pass by, and had directed the coachman whither to go, which was to a certain place, called Mile End, where lived a confidant of his, where we went in, and where was all the convenience in the world to be as wicked as we pleased.\n\nWhen we were together he began to talk very gravely to me, and to tell me he did not bring me there to betray me; that his passion for me would not suffer him to abuse me; that he resolved to marry me as soon as he came to his estate; that in the meantime, if I would grant his request, he would maintain me very honourably; and made me a thousand protestations of his sincerity and of his affection to me; and that he would never abandon me, and as I may say, made a thousand more preambles than he need to have done.\n\nHowever, as he pressed me to speak, I told him I had no reason to question the sincerity of his love to me after so many protestations, but—and there I stopped, as if I left him to guess the rest. “But what, my dear?” says he. “I guess what you mean: what if you should be with child? Is not that it? Why, then,” says he, “I’ll take care of you and provide for you, and the child too; and that you may see I am not in jest,” says he, “here’s an earnest for you,” and with that he pulls out a silk purse, with an hundred guineas in it, and gave it me. “And I’ll give you such another,” says he, “every year till I marry you.”\n\nMy colour came and went, at the sight of the purse and with the fire of his proposal together, so that I could not say a word, and he easily perceived it; so putting the purse into my bosom, I made no more resistance to him, but let him do just what he pleased, and as often as he pleased; and thus I finished my own destruction at once, for from this day, being forsaken of my virtue and my modesty, I had nothing of value left to recommend me, either to God’s blessing or man’s assistance.\n\nBut things did not end here. I went back to the town, did the business he publicly directed me to, and was at home before anybody thought me long. As for my gentleman, he stayed out, as he told me he would, till late at night, and there was not the least suspicion in the family either on his account or on mine.\n\nWe had, after this, frequent opportunities to repeat our crime—chiefly by his contrivance—especially at home, when his mother and the young ladies went abroad a-visiting, which he watched so narrowly as never to miss; knowing always beforehand when they went out, and then failed not to catch me all alone, and securely enough; so that we took our fill of our wicked pleasure for near half a year; and yet, which was the most to my satisfaction, I was not with child.\n\nBut before this half-year was expired, his younger brother, of whom I have made some mention in the beginning of the story, falls to work with me; and he, finding me alone in the garden one evening, begins a story of the same kind to me, made good honest professions of being in love with me, and in short, proposes fairly and honourably to marry me, and that before he made any other offer to me at all.\n\nI was now confounded, and driven to such an extremity as the like was never known; at least not to me. I resisted the proposal with obstinacy; and now I began to arm myself with arguments. I laid before him the inequality of the match; the treatment I should meet with in the family; the ingratitude it would be to his good father and mother, who had taken me into their house upon such generous principles, and when I was in such a low condition; and, in short, I said everything to dissuade him from his design that I could imagine, except telling him the truth, which would indeed have put an end to it all, but that I durst not think of mentioning.\n\nBut here happened a circumstance that I did not expect indeed, which put me to my shifts; for this young gentleman, as he was plain and honest, so he pretended to nothing with me but what was so too; and, knowing his own innocence, he was not so careful to make his having a kindness for Mrs. Betty a secret in the house, as his brother was. And though he did not let them know that he had talked to me about it, yet he said enough to let his sisters perceive he loved me, and his mother saw it too, which, though they took no notice of it to me, yet they did to him, an immediately I found their carriage to me altered, more than ever before.\n\nI saw the cloud, though I did not foresee the storm. It was easy, I say, to see that their carriage to me was altered, and that it grew worse and worse every day; till at last I got information among the servants that I should, in a very little while, be desired to remove.\n\nI was not alarmed at the news, having a full satisfaction that I should be otherwise provided for; and especially considering that I had reason every day to expect I should be with child, and that then I should be obliged to remove without any pretences for it.\n\nAfter some time the younger gentleman took an opportunity to tell me that the kindness he had for me had got vent in the family. He did not charge me with it, he said, for he know well enough which way it came out. He told me his plain way of talking had been the occasion of it, for that he did not make his respect for me so much a secret as he might have done, and the reason was, that he was at a point, that if I would consent to have him, he would tell them all openly that he loved me, and that he intended to marry me; that it was true his father and mother might resent it, and be unkind, but that he was now in a way to live, being bred to the law, and he did not fear maintaining me agreeable to what I should expect; and that, in short, as he believed I would not be ashamed of him, so he was resolved not to be ashamed of me, and that he scorned to be afraid to own me now, whom he resolved to own after I was his wife, and therefore I had nothing to do but to give him my hand, and he would answer for all the rest.\n\nI was now in a dreadful condition indeed, and now I repented heartily my easiness with the eldest brother; not from any reflection of conscience, but from a view of the happiness I might have enjoyed, and had now made impossible; for though I had no great scruples of conscience, as I have said, to struggle with, yet I could not think of being a whore to one brother and a wife to the other. But then it came into my thoughts that the first brother had promised to made me his wife when he came to his estate; but I presently remembered what I had often thought of, that he had never spoken a word of having me for a wife after he had conquered me for a mistress; and indeed, till now, though I said I thought of it often, yet it gave me no disturbance at all, for as he did not seem in the least to lessen his affection to me, so neither did he lessen his bounty, though he had the discretion himself to desire me not to lay out a penny of what he gave me in clothes, or to make the least show extraordinary, because it would necessarily give jealousy in the family, since everybody know I could come at such things no manner of ordinary way, but by some private friendship, which they would presently have suspected.\n\nBut I was now in a great strait, and knew not what to do. The main difficulty was this: the younger brother not only laid close siege to me, but suffered it to be seen. He would come into his sister’s room, and his mother’s room, and sit down, and talk a thousand kind things of me, and to me, even before their faces, and when they were all there. This grew so public that the whole house talked of it, and his mother reproved him for it, and their carriage to me appeared quite altered. In short, his mother had let fall some speeches, as if she intended to put me out of the family; that is, in English, to turn me out of doors. Now I was sure this could not be a secret to his brother, only that he might not think, as indeed nobody else yet did, that the youngest brother had made any proposal to me about it; but as I easily could see that it would go farther, so I saw likewise there was an absolute necessity to speak of it to him, or that he would speak of it to me, and which to do first I knew not; that is, whether I should break it to him or let it alone till he should break it to me.\n\nUpon serious consideration, for indeed now I began to consider things very seriously, and never till now; I say, upon serious consideration, I resolved to tell him of it first; and it was not long before I had an opportunity, for the very next day his brother went to London upon some business, and the family being out a-visiting, just as it had happened before, and as indeed was often the case, he came according to his custom, to spend an hour or two with Mrs. Betty.\n\nWhen he came and had sat down a while, he easily perceived there was an alteration in my countenance, that I was not so free and pleasant with him as I used to be, and particularly, that I had been a-crying; he was not long before he took notice of it, and asked me in very kind terms what was the matter, and if anything troubled me. I would have put it off if I could, but it was not to be concealed; so after suffering many importunities to draw that out of me which I longed as much as possible to disclose, I told him that it was true something did trouble me, and something of such a nature that I could not conceal from him, and yet that I could not tell how to tell him of it neither; that it was a thing that not only surprised me, but greatly perplexed me, and that I knew not what course to take, unless he would direct me. He told me with great tenderness, that let it be what it would, I should not let it trouble me, for he would protect me from all the world.\n\nI then began at a distance, and told him I was afraid the ladies had got some secret information of our correspondence; for that it was easy to see that their conduct was very much changed towards me for a great while, and that now it was come to that pass that they frequently found fault with me, and sometimes fell quite out with me, though I never gave them the least occasion; that whereas I used always to lie with the eldest sister, I was lately put to lie by myself, or with one of the maids; and that I had overheard them several times talking very unkindly about me; but that which confirmed it all was, that one of the servants had told me that she had heard I was to be turned out, and that it was not safe for the family that I should be any longer in the house.\n\nHe smiled when he heard all this, and I asked him how he could make so light of it, when he must needs know that if there was any discovery I was undone for ever, and that even it would hurt him, though not ruin him as it would me. I upbraided him, that he was like all the rest of the sex, that, when they had the character and honour of a woman at their mercy, oftentimes made it their jest, and at least looked upon it as a trifle, and counted the ruin of those they had had their will of as a thing of no value.\n\nHe saw me warm and serious, and he changed his style immediately; he told me he was sorry I should have such a thought of him; that he had never given me the least occasion for it, but had been as tender of my reputation as he could be of his own; that he was sure our correspondence had been managed with so much address, that not one creature in the family had so much as a suspicion of it; that if he smiled when I told him my thoughts, it was at the assurance he lately received, that our understanding one another was not so much as known or guessed at; and that when he had told me how much reason he had to be easy, I should smile as he did, for he was very certain it would give me a full satisfaction.\n\n“This is a mystery I cannot understand,” says I, “or how it should be to my satisfaction that I am to be turned out of doors; for if our correspondence is not discovered, I know not what else I have done to change the countenances of the whole family to me, or to have them treat me as they do now, who formerly used me with so much tenderness, as if I had been one of their own children.”\n\n“Why, look you, child,” says he, “that they are uneasy about you, that is true; but that they have the least suspicion of the case as it is, and as it respects you and I, is so far from being true, that they suspect my brother Robin; and, in short, they are fully persuaded he makes love to you; nay, the fool has put it into their heads too himself, for he is continually bantering them about it, and making a jest of himself. I confess I think he is wrong to do so, because he cannot but see it vexes them, and makes them unkind to you; but ’tis a satisfaction to me, because of the assurance it gives me, that they do not suspect me in the least, and I hope this will be to your satisfaction too.”\n\n“So it is,” says I, “one way; but this does not reach my case at all, nor is this the chief thing that troubles me, though I have been concerned about that too.” “What is it, then?” says he. With which I fell to tears, and could say nothing to him at all. He strove to pacify me all he could, but began at last to be very pressing upon me to tell what it was. At last I answered that I thought I ought to tell him too, and that he had some right to know it; besides, that I wanted his direction in the case, for I was in such perplexity that I knew not what course to take, and then I related the whole affair to him. I told him how imprudently his brother had managed himself, in making himself so public; for that if he had kept it a secret, as such a thing ought to have been, I could but have denied him positively, without giving any reason for it, and he would in time have ceased his solicitations; but that he had the vanity, first, to depend upon it that I would not deny him, and then had taken the freedom to tell his resolution of having me to the whole house.\n\nI told him how far I had resisted him, and told him how sincere and honourable his offers were. “But,” says I, “my case will be doubly hard; for as they carry it ill to me now, because he desires to have me, they’ll carry it worse when they shall find I have denied him; and they will presently say, there’s something else in it, and then out it comes that I am married already to somebody else, or that I would never refuse a match so much above me as this was.”\n\nThis discourse surprised him indeed very much. He told me that it was a critical point indeed for me to manage, and he did not see which way I should get out of it; but he would consider it, and let me know next time we met, what resolution he was come to about it; and in the meantime desired I would not give my consent to his brother, nor yet give him a flat denial, but that I would hold him in suspense a while.\n\nI seemed to start at his saying I should not give him my consent. I told him he knew very well I had no consent to give; that he had engaged himself to marry me, and that my consent was the same time engaged to him; that he had all along told me I was his wife, and I looked upon myself as effectually so as if the ceremony had passed; and that it was from his own mouth that I did so, he having all along persuaded me to call myself his wife.\n\n“Well, my dear,” says he, “don’t be concerned at that now; if I am not your husband, I’ll be as good as a husband to you; and do not let those things trouble you now, but let me look a little farther into this affair, and I shall be able to say more next time we meet.”\n\nHe pacified me as well as he could with this, but I found he was very thoughtful, and that though he was very kind to me and kissed me a thousand times, and more I believe, and gave me money too, yet he offered no more all the while we were together, which was above two hours, and which I much wondered at indeed at that time, considering how it used to be, and what opportunity we had.\n\nHis brother did not come from London for five or six days, and it was two days more before he got an opportunity to talk with him; but then getting him by himself he began to talk very close to him about it, and the same evening got an opportunity (for we had a long conference together) to repeat all their discourse to me, which, as near as I can remember, was to the purpose following. He told him he heard strange news of him since he went, viz. that he made love to Mrs. Betty. “Well,” says his brother a little angrily, “and so I do. And what then? What has anybody to do with that?” “Nay,” says his brother, “don’t be angry, Robin; I don’t pretend to have anything to do with it; nor do I pretend to be angry with you about it. But I find they do concern themselves about it, and that they have used the poor girl ill about it, which I should take as done to myself.” “Whom do you mean by _they_?” says Robin. “I mean my mother and the girls,” says the elder brother. “But hark ye,” says his brother, “are you in earnest? Do you really love this girl? You may be free with me, you know.” “Why, then,” says Robin, “I will be free with you; I do love her above all the women in the world, and I will have her, let them say and do what they will. I believe the girl will not deny me.”\n\nIt struck me to the heart when he told me this, for though it was most rational to think I would not deny him, yet I knew in my own conscience I must deny him, and I saw my ruin in my being obliged to do so; but I knew it was my business to talk otherwise then, so I interrupted him in his story thus.\n\n“Ay!” said I, “does he think I cannot deny him? But he shall find I can deny him, for all that.”\n\n“Well, my dear,” says he, “but let me give you the whole story as it went on between us, and then say what you will.”\n\nThen he went on and told me that he replied thus: “But, brother, you know she has nothing, and you may have several ladies with good fortunes.”\n\n“’Tis no matter for that,” said Robin; “I love the girl, and I will never please my pocket in marrying, and not please my fancy.” “And so, my dear,” adds he, “there is no opposing him.”\n\n“Yes, yes,” says I, “you shall see I can oppose him; I have learnt to say No, now though I had not learnt it before; if the best lord in the land offered me marriage now, I could very cheerfully say No to him.”\n\n“Well, but, my dear,” says he, “what can you say to him? You know, as you said when we talked of it before, he will ask you many questions about it, and all the house will wonder what the meaning of it should be.”\n\n“Why,” says I, smiling, “I can stop all their mouths at one clap by telling him, and them too, that I am married already to his elder brother.”\n\nHe smiled a little too at the word, but I could see it startled him, and he could not hide the disorder it put him into. However, he returned, “Why, though that may be true in some sense, yet I suppose you are but in jest when you talk of giving such an answer as that; it may not be convenient on many accounts.”\n\n“No, no,” says I pleasantly, “I am not so fond of letting the secret come out without your consent.”\n\n“But what, then, can you say to him, or to them,” says he, “when they find you positive against a match which would be apparently so much to your advantage?”\n\n“Why,” says I, “should I be at a loss? First of all, I am not obliged to give me any reason at all; on the other hand, I may tell them I am married already, and stop there, and that will be a full stop too to him, for he can have no reason to ask one question after it.”\n\n“Ay,” says he; “but the whole house will tease you about that, even to father and mother, and if you deny them positively, they will be disobliged at you, and suspicious besides.”\n\n“Why,” says I, “what can I do? What would you have me do? I was in straight enough before, and as I told you, I was in perplexity before, and acquainted you with the circumstances, that I might have your advice.”\n\n“My dear,” says he, “I have been considering very much upon it, you may be sure, and though it is a piece of advice that has a great many mortifications in it to me, and may at first seem strange to you, yet, all things considered, I see no better way for you than to let him go on; and if you find him hearty and in earnest, marry him.”\n\nI gave him a look full of horror at those words, and, turning pale as death, was at the very point of sinking down out of the chair I sat in; when, giving a start, “My dear,” says he aloud, “what’s the matter with you? Where are you a-going?” and a great many such things; and with jogging and called to me, fetched me a little to myself, though it was a good while before I fully recovered my senses, and was not able to speak for several minutes more.\n\nWhen I was fully recovered he began again. “My dear,” says he, “what made you so surprised at what I said? I would have you consider seriously of it? You may see plainly how the family stand in this case, and they would be stark mad if it was my case, as it is my brother’s; and for aught I see, it would be my ruin and yours too.”\n\n“Ay!” says I, still speaking angrily; “are all your protestations and vows to be shaken by the dislike of the family? Did I not always object that to you, and you made light thing of it, as what you were above, and would value; and is it come to this now?” said I. “Is this your faith and honour, your love, and the solidity of your promises?”\n\nHe continued perfectly calm, notwithstanding all my reproaches, and I was not sparing of them at all; but he replied at last, “My dear, I have not broken one promise with you yet; I did tell you I would marry you when I was come to my estate; but you see my father is a hale, healthy man, and may live these thirty years still, and not be older than several are round us in town; and you never proposed my marrying you sooner, because you knew it might be my ruin; and as to all the rest, I have not failed you in anything, you have wanted for nothing.”\n\nI could not deny a word of this, and had nothing to say to it in general. “But why, then,” says I, “can you persuade me to such a horrid step as leaving you, since you have not left me? Will you allow no affection, no love on my side, where there has been so much on your side? Have I made you no returns? Have I given no testimony of my sincerity and of my passion? Are the sacrifices I have made of honour and modesty to you no proof of my being tied to you in bonds too strong to be broken?”\n\n“But here, my dear,” says he, “you may come into a safe station, and appear with honour and with splendour at once, and the remembrance of what we have done may be wrapt up in an eternal silence, as if it had never happened; you shall always have my respect, and my sincere affection, only then it shall be honest, and perfectly just to my brother; you shall be my dear sister, as now you are my dear——” and there he stopped.\n\n“Your dear whore,” says I, “you would have said if you had gone on, and you might as well have said it; but I understand you. However, I desire you to remember the long discourses you have had with me, and the many hours’ pains you have taken to persuade me to believe myself an honest woman; that I was your wife intentionally, though not in the eyes of the world, and that it was as effectual a marriage that had passed between us as if we had been publicly wedded by the parson of the parish. You know and cannot but remember that these have been your own words to me.”\n\nI found this was a little too close upon him, but I made it up in what follows. He stood stock-still for a while and said nothing, and I went on thus: “You cannot,” says I, “without the highest injustice, believe that I yielded upon all these persuasions without a love not to be questioned, not to be shaken again by anything that could happen afterward. If you have such dishonourable thoughts of me, I must ask you what foundation in any of my behaviour have I given for such a suggestion?\n\n“If, then, I have yielded to the importunities of my affection, and if I have been persuaded to believe that I am really, and in the essence of the thing, your wife, shall I now give the lie to all those arguments and call myself your whore, or mistress, which is the same thing? And will you transfer me to your brother? Can you transfer my affection? Can you bid me cease loving you, and bid me love him? It is in my power, think you, to make such a change at demand? No, sir,” said I, “depend upon it ’tis impossible, and whatever the change of your side may be, I will ever be true; and I had much rather, since it is come that unhappy length, be your whore than your brother’s wife.”\n\nHe appeared pleased and touched with the impression of this last discourse, and told me that he stood where he did before; that he had not been unfaithful to me in any one promise he had ever made yet, but that there were so many terrible things presented themselves to his view in the affair before me, and that on my account in particular, that he had thought of the other as a remedy so effectual as nothing could come up to it. That he thought this would not be entire parting us, but we might love as friends all our days, and perhaps with more satisfaction than we should in the station we were now in, as things might happen; that he durst say, I could not apprehend anything from him as to betraying a secret, which could not but be the destruction of us both, if it came out; that he had but one question to ask of me that could lie in the way of it, and if that question was answered in the negative, he could not but think still it was the only step I could take.\n\nI guessed at his question presently, namely, whether I was sure I was not with child? As to that, I told him he need not be concerned about it, for I was not with child. “Why, then, my dear,” says he, “we have no time to talk further now. Consider of it, and think closely about it; I cannot but be of the opinion still, that it will be the best course you can take.” And with this he took his leave, and the more hastily too, his mother and sisters ringing at the gate, just at the moment that he had risen up to go.\n\nHe left me in the utmost confusion of thought; and he easily perceived it the next day, and all the rest of the week, for it was but Tuesday evening when we talked; but he had no opportunity to come at me all that week, till the Sunday after, when I, being indisposed, did not go to church, and he, making some excuse for the like, stayed at home.\n\nAnd now he had me an hour and a half again by myself, and we fell into the same arguments all over again, or at least so near the same, as it would be to no purpose to repeat them. At last I asked him warmly, what opinion he must have of my modesty, that he could suppose I should so much as entertain a thought of lying with two brothers, and assured him it could never be. I added, if he was to tell me that he would never see me more, than which nothing but death could be more terrible, yet I could never entertain a thought so dishonourable to myself, and so base to him; and therefore, I entreated him, if he had one grain of respect or affection left for me, that he would speak no more of it to me, or that he would pull his sword out and kill me. He appeared surprised at my obstinacy, as he called it; told me I was unkind to myself, and unkind to him in it; that it was a crisis unlooked for upon us both, and impossible for either of us to foresee, but that he did not see any other way to save us both from ruin, and therefore he thought it the more unkind; but that if he must say no more of it to me, he added with an unusual coldness, that he did not know anything else we had to talk of; and so he rose up to take his leave. I rose up too, as if with the same indifference; but when he came to give me as it were a parting kiss, I burst out into such a passion of crying, that though I would have spoke, I could not, and only pressing his hand, seemed to give him the adieu, but cried vehemently.\n\nHe was sensibly moved with this; so he sat down again, and said a great many kind things to me, to abate the excess of my passion, but still urged the necessity of what he had proposed; all the while insisting, that if I did refuse, he would notwithstanding provide for me; but letting me plainly see that he would decline me in the main point—nay, even as a mistress; making it a point of honour not to lie with the woman that, for aught he knew, might come to be his brother’s wife.\n\nThe bare loss of him as a gallant was not so much my affliction as the loss of his person, whom indeed I loved to distraction; and the loss of all the expectations I had, and which I always had built my hopes upon, of having him one day for my husband. These things oppressed my mind so much, that, in short, I fell very ill; the agonies of my mind, in a word, threw me into a high fever, and long it was, that none in the family expected my life.\n\nI was reduced very low indeed, and was often delirious and light-headed; but nothing lay so near me as the fear that, when I was light-headed, I should say something or other to his prejudice. I was distressed in my mind also to see him, and so he was to see me, for he really loved me most passionately; but it could not be; there was not the least room to desire it on one side or other, or so much as to make it decent.\n\nIt was near five weeks that I kept my bed and though the violence of my fever abated in three weeks, yet it several times returned; and the physicians said two or three times, they could do no more for me, but that they must leave nature and the distemper to fight it out, only strengthening the first with cordials to maintain the struggle. After the end of five weeks I grew better, but was so weak, so altered, so melancholy, and recovered so slowly, that the physicians apprehended I should go into a consumption; and which vexed me most, they gave it as their opinion that my mind was oppressed, that something troubled me, and, in short, that I was in love. Upon this, the whole house was set upon me to examine me, and to press me to tell whether I was in love or not, and with whom; but as I well might, I denied my being in love at all.\n\nThey had on this occasion a squabble one day about me at table, that had like to have put the whole family in an uproar, and for some time did so. They happened to be all at table but the father; as for me, I was ill, and in my chamber. At the beginning of the talk, which was just as they had finished their dinner, the old gentlewoman, who had sent me somewhat to eat, called her maid to go up and ask me if I would have any more; but the maid brought down word I had not eaten half what she had sent me already.\n\n“Alas,” says the old lady, “that poor girl! I am afraid she will never be well.”\n\n“Well!” says the elder brother, “how should Mrs. Betty be well? They say she is in love.”\n\n“I believe nothing of it,” says the old gentlewoman.\n\n“I don’t know,” says the eldest sister, “what to say to it; they have made such a rout about her being so handsome, and so charming, and I know not what, and that in her hearing too, that has turned the creature’s head, I believe, and who knows what possessions may follow such doings? For my part, I don’t know what to make of it.”\n\n“Why, sister, you must acknowledge she is very handsome,” says the elder brother.\n\n“Ay, and a great deal handsomer than you, sister,” says Robin, “and that’s your mortification.”\n\n“Well, well, that is not the question,” says his sister; “that girl is well enough, and she knows it well enough; she need not be told of it to make her vain.”\n\n“We are not talking of her being vain,” says the elder brother, “but of her being in love; it may be she is in love with herself; it seems my sisters think so.”\n\n“I would she was in love with me,” says Robin; “I’d quickly put her out of her pain.”\n\n“What d’ye mean by that, son,” says the old lady; “how can you talk so?”\n\n“Why, madam,” says Robin, again, very honestly, “do you think I’d let the poor girl die for love, and of one that is near at hand to be had, too?”\n\n“Fie, brother!”, says the second sister, “how can you talk so? Would you take a creature that has not a groat in the world?”\n\n“Prithee, child,” says Robin, “beauty’s a portion, and good-humour with it is a double portion; I wish thou hadst half her stock of both for thy portion.” So there was her mouth stopped.\n\n“I find,” says the eldest sister, “if Betty is not in love, my brother is. I wonder he has not broke his mind to Betty; I warrant she won’t say No.”\n\n“They that yield when they’re asked,” says Robin, “are one step before them that were never asked to yield, sister, and two steps before them that yield before they are asked; and that’s an answer to you, sister.”\n\nThis fired the sister, and she flew into a passion, and said, things were come to that pass that it was time the wench, meaning me, was out of the family; and but that she was not fit to be turned out, she hoped her father and mother would consider of it as soon as she could be removed.\n\nRobin replied, that was business for the master and mistress of the family, who where not to be taught by one that had so little judgment as his eldest sister.\n\nIt ran up a great deal farther; the sister scolded, Robin rallied and bantered, but poor Betty lost ground by it extremely in the family. I heard of it, and I cried heartily, and the old lady came up to me, somebody having told her that I was so much concerned about it. I complained to her, that it was very hard the doctors should pass such a censure upon me, for which they had no ground; and that it was still harder, considering the circumstances I was under in the family; that I hoped I had done nothing to lessen her esteem for me, or given any occasion for the bickering between her sons and daughters, and I had more need to think of a coffin than of being in love, and begged she would not let me suffer in her opinion for anybody’s mistakes but my own.\n\nShe was sensible of the justice of what I said, but told me, since there had been such a clamour among them, and that her younger son talked after such a rattling way as he did, she desired I would be so faithful to her as to answer her but one question sincerely. I told her I would, with all my heart, and with the utmost plainness and sincerity. Why, then, the question was, whether there was anything between her son Robert and me. I told her with all the protestations of sincerity that I was able to make, and as I might well, do, that there was not, nor ever had been; I told her that Mr. Robert had rattled and jested, as she knew it was his way, and that I took it always, as I supposed he meant it, to be a wild airy way of discourse that had no signification in it; and again assured her, that there was not the least tittle of what she understood by it between us; and that those who had suggested it had done me a great deal of wrong, and Mr. Robert no service at all.\n\nThe old lady was fully satisfied, and kissed me, spoke cheerfully to me, and bid me take care of my health and want for nothing, and so took her leave. But when she came down she found the brother and all his sisters together by the ears; they were angry, even to passion, at his upbraiding them with their being homely, and having never had any sweethearts, never having been asked the question, and their being so forward as almost to ask first. He rallied them upon the subject of Mrs. Betty; how pretty, how good-humoured, how she sung better than they did, and danced better, and how much handsomer she was; and in doing this he omitted no ill-natured thing that could vex them, and indeed, pushed too hard upon them. The old lady came down in the height of it, and to put a stop it to, told them all the discourse she had had with me, and how I answered, that there was nothing between Mr. Robert and I.\n\n“She’s wrong there,” says Robin, “for if there was not a great deal between us, we should be closer together than we are. I told her I loved her hugely,” says he, “but I could never make the jade believe I was in earnest.” “I do not know how you should,” says his mother; “nobody in their senses could believe you were in earnest, to talk so to a poor girl, whose circumstances you know so well.\n\n“But prithee, son,” adds she, “since you tell me that you could not make her believe you were in earnest, what must we believe about it? For you ramble so in your discourse, that nobody knows whether you are in earnest or in jest; but as I find the girl, by your own confession, has answered truly, I wish you would do so too, and tell me seriously, so that I may depend upon it. Is there anything in it or no? Are you in earnest or no? Are you distracted, indeed, or are you not? ’Tis a weighty question, and I wish you would make us easy about it.”\n\n“By my faith, madam,” says Robin, “’tis in vain to mince the matter or tell any more lies about it; I am in earnest, as much as a man is that’s going to be hanged. If Mrs. Betty would say she loved me, and that she would marry me, I’d have her tomorrow morning fasting, and say, ‘To have and to hold,’ instead of eating my breakfast.”\n\n“Well,” says the mother, “then there’s one son lost”; and she said it in a very mournful tone, as one greatly concerned at it.\n\n“I hope not, madam,” says Robin; “no man is lost when a good wife has found him.”\n\n“Why, but, child,” says the old lady, “she is a beggar.”\n\n“Why, then, madam, she has the more need of charity,” says Robin; “I’ll take her off the hands of the parish, and she and I’ll beg together.”\n\n“It’s bad jesting with such things,” says the mother.\n\n“I don’t jest, madam,” says Robin. “We’ll come and beg your pardon, madam; and your blessing, madam, and my father’s.”\n\n“This is all out of the way, son,” says the mother. “If you are in earnest you are undone.”\n\n“I am afraid not,” says he, “for I am really afraid she won’t have me; after all my sister’s huffing and blustering, I believe I shall never be able to persuade her to it.”\n\n“That’s a fine tale, indeed; she is not so far out of her senses neither. Mrs. Betty is no fool,” says the younger sister. “Do you think she has learnt to say No, any more than other people?”\n\n“No, Mrs. Mirth-wit,” says Robin, “Mrs. Betty’s no fool; but Mrs. Betty may be engaged some other way, and what then?”\n\n“Nay,” says the eldest sister, “we can say nothing to that. Who must it be to, then? She is never out of the doors; it must be between you.”\n\n“I have nothing to say to that,” says Robin. “I have been examined enough; there’s my brother. If it must be between us, go to work with him.”\n\nThis stung the elder brother to the quick, and he concluded that Robin had discovered something. However, he kept himself from appearing disturbed. “Prithee,” says he, “don’t go to shame your stories off upon me; I tell you, I deal in no such ware; I have nothing to say to Mrs. Betty, nor to any of the Mrs. Bettys in the parish”; and with that he rose up and brushed off.\n\n“No,” says the eldest sister, “I dare answer for my brother; he knows the world better.”\n\nThus the discourse ended, but it left the elder brother quite confounded. He concluded his brother had made a full discovery, and he began to doubt whether I had been concerned in it or not; but with all his management he could not bring it about to get at me. At last he was so perplexed that he was quite desperate, and resolved he would come into my chamber and see me, whatever came of it. In order to do this, he contrived it so, that one day after dinner, watching his eldest sister till he could see her go upstairs, he runs after her. “Hark ye, sister,” says he, “where is this sick woman? May not a body see her?” “Yes,” says the sister, “I believe you may; but let me go first a little, and I’ll tell you.” So she ran up to the door and gave me notice, and presently called to him again. “Brother,” says she, “you may come if you please.” So in he came, just in the same kind of rant. “Well,” says he at the door as he came in, “where is this sick body that’s in love? How do ye do, Mrs. Betty?” I would have got up out of my chair, but was so weak I could not for a good while; and he saw it, and his sister too, and she said, “Come, do not strive to stand up; my brother desires no ceremony, especially now you are so weak.” “No, no, Mrs. Betty, pray sit still,” says he, and so sits himself down in a chair over against me, and appeared as if he was mighty merry.\n\nHe talked a lot of rambling stuff to his sister and to me, sometimes of one thing, sometimes of another, on purpose to amuse his sister, and every now and then would turn it upon the old story, directing it to me. “Poor Mrs. Betty,” says he, “it is a sad thing to be in love; why, it has reduced you sadly.” At last I spoke a little. “I am glad to see you so merry, sir,” says I; “but I think the doctor might have found something better to do than to make his game at his patients. If I had been ill of no other distemper, I know the proverb too well to have let him come to me.” “What proverb?” says he, “Oh! I remember it now. What—\n\n“Where love is the case, The doctor’s an ass.”\n\nIs not that it, Mrs. Betty?” I smiled and said nothing. “Nay,” says he, “I think the effect has proved it to be love, for it seems the doctor has been able to do you but little service; you mend very slowly, they say. I doubt there’s somewhat in it, Mrs. Betty; I doubt you are sick of the incurables, and that is love.” I smiled and said, “No, indeed, sir, that’s none of my distemper.”\n\nWe had a deal of such discourse, and sometimes others that signified as little. By and by he asked me to sing them a song, at which I smiled, and said my singing days were over. At last he asked me if he should play upon his flute to me; his sister said she believe it would hurt me, and that my head could not bear it. I bowed, and said, No, it would not hurt me. “And, pray, madam,” said I, “do not hinder it; I love the music of the flute very much.” Then his sister said, “Well, do, then, brother.” With that he pulled out the key of his closet. “Dear sister,” says he, “I am very lazy; do step to my closet and fetch my flute; it lies in such a drawer,” naming a place where he was sure it was not, that she might be a little while a-looking for it.\n\nAs soon as she was gone, he related the whole story to me of the discourse his brother had about me, and of his pushing it at him, and his concern about it, which was the reason of his contriving this visit to me. I assured him I had never opened my mouth either to his brother or to anybody else. I told him the dreadful exigence I was in; that my love to him, and his offering to have me forget that affection and remove it to another, had thrown me down; and that I had a thousand times wished I might die rather than recover, and to have the same circumstances to struggle with as I had before, and that his backwardness to life had been the great reason of the slowness of my recovering. I added that I foresaw that as soon as I was well, I must quit the family, and that as for marrying his brother, I abhorred the thoughts of it after what had been my case with him, and that he might depend upon it I would never see his brother again upon that subject; that if he would break all his vows and oaths and engagements with me, be that between his conscience and his honour and himself; but he should never be able to say that I, whom he had persuaded to call myself his wife, and who had given him the liberty to use me as a wife, was not as faithful to him as a wife ought to be, whatever he might be to me.\n\nHe was going to reply, and had said that he was sorry I could not be persuaded, and was a-going to say more, but he heard his sister a-coming, and so did I; and yet I forced out these few words as a reply, that I could never be persuaded to love one brother and marry another. He shook his head and said, “Then I am ruined,” meaning himself; and that moment his sister entered the room and told him she could not find the flute. “Well,” says he merrily, “this laziness won’t do”; so he gets up and goes himself to go to look for it, but comes back without it too; not but that he could have found it, but because his mind was a little disturbed, and he had no mind to play; and, besides, the errand he sent his sister on was answered another way; for he only wanted an opportunity to speak to me, which he gained, though not much to his satisfaction.\n\nI had, however, a great deal of satisfaction in having spoken my mind to him with freedom, and with such an honest plainness, as I have related; and though it did not at all work the way I desired, that is to say, to oblige the person to me the more, yet it took from him all possibility of quitting me but by a downright breach of honour, and giving up all the faith of a gentleman to me, which he had so often engaged by, never to abandon me, but to make me his wife as soon as he came to his estate.\n\nIt was not many weeks after this before I was about the house again, and began to grow well; but I continued melancholy, silent, dull, and retired, which amazed the whole family, except he that knew the reason of it; yet it was a great while before he took any notice of it, and I, as backward to speak as he, carried respectfully to him, but never offered to speak a word to him that was particular of any kind whatsoever; and this continued for sixteen or seventeen weeks; so that, as I expected every day to be dismissed the family, on account of what distaste they had taken another way, in which I had no guilt, so I expected to hear no more of this gentleman, after all his solemn vows and protestations, but to be ruined and abandoned.\n\nAt last I broke the way myself in the family for my removing; for being talking seriously with the old lady one day, about my own circumstances in the world, and how my distemper had left a heaviness upon my spirits, that I was not the same thing I was before, the old lady said, “I am afraid, Betty, what I have said to you about my son has had some influence upon you, and that you are melancholy on his account; pray, will you let me know how the matter stands with you both, if it may not be improper? For, as for Robin, he does nothing but rally and banter when I speak of it to him.” “Why, truly, madam,” said I, “that matter stands as I wish it did not, and I shall be very sincere with you in it, whatever befalls me for it. Mr. Robert has several times proposed marriage to me, which is what I had no reason to expect, my poor circumstances considered; but I have always resisted him, and that perhaps in terms more positive than became me, considering the regard that I ought to have for every branch of your family; but,” said I, “madam, I could never so far forget my obligation to you and all your house, to offer to consent to a thing which I know must needs be disobliging to you, and this I have made my argument to him, and have positively told him that I would never entertain a thought of that kind unless I had your consent, and his father’s also, to whom I was bound by so many invincible obligations.”\n\n“And is this possible, Mrs. Betty?” says the old lady. “Then you have been much juster to us than we have been to you; for we have all looked upon you as a kind of snare to my son, and I had a proposal to make to you for your removing, for fear of it; but I had not yet mentioned it to you, because I thought you were not thorough well, and I was afraid of grieving you too much, lest it should throw you down again; for we have all a respect for you still, though not so much as to have it be the ruin of my son; but if it be as you say, we have all wronged you very much.”\n\n“As to the truth of what I say, madam,” said I, “refer you to your son himself; if he will do me any justice, he must tell you the story just as I have told it.”\n\nAway goes the old lady to her daughters and tells them the whole story, just as I had told it her; and they were surprised at it, you may be sure, as I believed they would be. One said she could never have thought it; another said Robin was a fool; a third said she would not believe a word of it, and she would warrant that Robin would tell the story another way. But the old gentlewoman, who was resolved to go to the bottom of it before I could have the least opportunity of acquainting her son with what had passed, resolved too that she would talk with her son immediately, and to that purpose sent for him, for he was gone but to a lawyer’s house in the town, upon some petty business of his own, and upon her sending he returned immediately.\n\nUpon his coming up to them, for they were all still together, “Sit down, Robin,” says the old lady, “I must have some talk with you.” “With all my heart, madam,” says Robin, looking very merry. “I hope it is about a good wife, for I am at a great loss in that affair.” “How can that be?” says his mother; “did not you say you resolved to have Mrs. Betty?” “Ay, madam,” says Robin, “but there is one has forbid the banns.” “Forbid, the banns!” says his mother; “who can that be?” “Even Mrs. Betty herself,” says Robin. “How so?” says his mother. “Have you asked her the question, then?” “Yes, indeed, madam,” says Robin. “I have attacked her in form five times since she was sick, and am beaten off; the jade is so stout she won’t capitulate nor yield upon any terms, except such as I cannot effectually grant.” “Explain yourself,” says the mother, “for I am surprised; I do not understand you. I hope you are not in earnest.”\n\n“Why, madam,” says he, “the case is plain enough upon me, it explains itself; she won’t have me, she says; is not that plain enough? I think ’tis plain, and pretty rough too.” “Well, but,” says the mother, “you talk of conditions that you cannot grant; what does she want—a settlement? Her jointure ought to be according to her portion; but what fortune does she bring you?” “Nay, as to fortune,” says Robin, “she is rich enough; I am satisfied in that point; but ’tis I that am not able to come up to her terms, and she is positive she will not have me without.”\n\nHere the sisters put in. “Madam,” says the second sister, “’tis impossible to be serious with him; he will never give a direct answer to anything; you had better let him alone, and talk no more of it to him; you know how to dispose of her out of his way if you thought there was anything in it.” Robin was a little warmed with his sister’s rudeness, but he was even with her, and yet with good manners too. “There are two sorts of people, madam,” says he, turning to his mother, “that there is no contending with; that is, a wise body and a fool; ’tis a little hard I should engage with both of them together.”\n\nThe younger sister then put in. “We must be fools indeed,” says she, “in my brother’s opinion, that he should think we can believe he has seriously asked Mrs. Betty to marry him, and that she has refused him.”\n“Answer, and answer not, say Solomon,” replied her brother. “When your brother had said to your mother that he had asked her no less than five times, and that it was so, that she positively denied him, methinks a younger sister need not question the truth of it when her mother did not.” “My mother, you see, did not understand it,” says the second sister. “There’s some difference,” says Robin, “between desiring me to explain it, and telling me she did not believe it.”\n\n“Well, but, son,” says the old lady, “if you are disposed to let us into the mystery of it, what were these hard conditions?” “Yes, madam,” says Robin, “I had done it before now, if the teasers here had not worried me by way of interruption. The conditions are, that I bring my father and you to consent to it, and without that she protests she will never see me more upon that head; and to these conditions, as I said, I suppose I shall never be able to grant. I hope my warm sisters will be answered now, and blush a little; if not, I have no more to say till I hear further.”\n\nThis answer was surprising to them all, though less to the mother, because of what I had said to her. As to the daughters, they stood mute a great while; but the mother said with some passion, “Well, I had heard this before, but I could not believe it; but if it is so, then we have all done Betty wrong, and she has behaved better than I ever expected.” “Nay,” says the eldest sister, “if it be so, she has acted handsomely indeed.” “I confess,” says the mother, “it was none of her fault, if he was fool enough to take a fancy to her; but to give such an answer to him, shows more respect to your father and me than I can tell how to express; I shall value the girl the better for it as long as I know her.” “But I shall not,” says Robin, “unless you will give your consent.” “I’ll consider of that a while,” says the mother; “I assure you, if there were not some other objections in the way, this conduct of hers would go a great way to bring me to consent.” “I wish it would go quite through it,” says Robin; “if you had as much thought about making me easy as you have about making me rich, you would soon consent to it.”\n\n“Why, Robin,” says the mother again, “are you really in earnest? Would you so fain have her as you pretend?” “Really, madam,” says Robin, “I think ’tis hard you should question me upon that head after all I have said. I won’t say that I will have her; how can I resolve that point, when you see I cannot have her without your consent? Besides, I am not bound to marry at all. But this I will say, I am in earnest in, that I will never have anybody else if I can help it; so you may determine for me. Betty or nobody is the word, and the question which of the two shall be in your breast to decide, madam, provided only, that my good-humoured sisters here may have no vote in it.”\n\nAll this was dreadful to me, for the mother began to yield, and Robin pressed her home on it. On the other hand, she advised with the eldest son, and he used all the arguments in the world to persuade her to consent; alleging his brother’s passionate love for me, and my generous regard to the family, in refusing my own advantages upon such a nice point of honour, and a thousand such things. And as to the father, he was a man in a hurry of public affairs and getting money, seldom at home, thoughtful of the main chance, but left all those things to his wife.\n\nYou may easily believe, that when the plot was thus, as they thought, broke out, and that every one thought they knew how things were carried, it was not so difficult or so dangerous for the elder brother, whom nobody suspected of anything, to have a freer access to me than before; nay, the mother, which was just as he wished, proposed it to him to talk with Mrs. Betty. “For it may be, son,” said she, “you may see farther into the thing than I, and see if you think she has been so positive as Robin says she has been, or no.” This was as well as he could wish, and he, as it were, yielding to talk with me at his mother’s request, she brought me to him into her own chamber, told me her son had some business with me at her request, and desired me to be very sincere with him, and then she left us together, and he went and shut the door after her.\n\nHe came back to me and took me in his arms, and kissed me very tenderly; but told me he had a long discourse to hold with me, and it was not come to that crisis, that I should make myself happy or miserable as long as I lived; that the thing was now gone so far, that if I could not comply with his desire, we would both be ruined. Then he told the whole story between Robin, as he called him, and his mother and sisters and himself, as it is above. “And now, dear child,” says he, “consider what it will be to marry a gentleman of a good family, in good circumstances, and with the consent of the whole house, and to enjoy all that the world can give you; and what, on the other hand, to be sunk into the dark circumstances of a woman that has lost her reputation; and that though I shall be a private friend to you while I live, yet as I shall be suspected always, so you will be afraid to see me, and I shall be afraid to own you.”\n\nHe gave me no time to reply, but went on with me thus: “What has happened between us, child, so long as we both agree to do so, may be buried and forgotten. I shall always be your sincere friend, without any inclination to nearer intimacy, when you become my sister; and we shall have all the honest part of conversation without any reproaches between us of having done amiss. I beg of you to consider it, and to not stand in the way of your own safety and prosperity; and to satisfy you that I am sincere,” added he, “I here offer you £500 in money, to make you some amends for the freedoms I have taken with you, which we shall look upon as some of the follies of our lives, which ’tis hoped we may repent of.”\n\nHe spoke this in so much more moving terms than it is possible for me to express, and with so much greater force of argument than I can repeat, that I only recommend it to those who read the story, to suppose, that as he held me above an hour and a half in that discourse, so he answered all my objections, and fortified his discourse with all the arguments that human wit and art could devise.\n\nI cannot say, however, that anything he said made impression enough upon me so as to give me any thought of the matter, till he told me at last very plainly, that if I refused, he was sorry to add that he could never go on with me in that station as we stood before; that though he loved me as well as ever, and that I was as agreeable to him as ever, yet sense of virtue had not so far forsaken him as to suffer him to lie with a woman that his brother courted to make his wife; and if he took his leave of me, with a denial in this affair, whatever he might do for me in the point of support, grounded on his first engagement of maintaining me, yet he would not have me be surprised that he was obliged to tell me he could not allow himself to see me any more; and that, indeed, I could not expect it of him.\n\nI received this last part with some token of surprise and disorder, and had much ado to avoid sinking down, for indeed I loved him to an extravagance not easy to imagine; but he perceived my disorder. He entreated me to consider seriously of it; assured me that it was the only way to preserve our mutual affection; that in this station we might love as friends, with the utmost passion, and with a love of relation untainted, free from our just reproaches, and free from other people’s suspicions; that he should ever acknowledge his happiness owing to me; that he would be debtor to me as long as he lived, and would be paying that debt as long as he had breath. Thus he wrought me up, in short, to a kind of hesitation in the matter; having the dangers on one side represented in lively figures, and indeed, heightened by my imagination of being turned out to the wide world a mere cast-off whore, for it was no less, and perhaps exposed as such, with little to provide for myself, with no friend, no acquaintance in the whole world, out of that town, and there I could not pretend to stay. All this terrified me to the last degree, and he took care upon all occasions to lay it home to me in the worst colours that it could be possible to be drawn in. On the other hand, he failed not to set forth the easy, prosperous life which I was going to live.\n\nHe answered all that I could object from affection, and from former engagements, with telling me the necessity that was before us of taking other measures now; and as to his promises of marriage, the nature of things, he said, had put an end to that, by the probability of my being his brother’s wife, before the time to which his promises all referred.\nThus, in a word, I may say, he reasoned me out of my reason; he conquered all my arguments, and I began to see a danger that I was in, which I had not considered of before, and that was, of being dropped by both of them and left alone in the world to shift for myself.\n\nThis, and his persuasion, at length prevailed with me to consent, though with so much reluctance, that it was easy to see I should go to church like a bear to the stake. I had some little apprehensions about me, too, lest my new spouse, who, by the way, I had not the least affection for, should be skillful enough to challenge me on another account, upon our first coming to bed together. But whether he did it with design or not, I know not, but his elder brother took care to make him very much fuddled before he went to bed, so that I had the satisfaction of a drunken bedfellow the first night. How he did it I know not, but I concluded that he certainly contrived it, that his brother might be able to make no judgment of the difference between a maid and a married woman; nor did he ever entertain any notions of it, or disturb his thoughts about it.\n\nI should go back a little here to where I left off. The elder brother having thus managed me, his next business was to manage his mother, and he never left till he had brought her to acquiesce and be passive in the thing, even without acquainting the father, other than by post letters; so that she consented to our marrying privately, and leaving her to manage the father afterwards.\n\nThen he cajoled with his brother, and persuaded him what service he had done him, and how he had brought his mother to consent, which, though true, was not indeed done to serve him, but to serve himself; but thus diligently did he cheat him, and had the thanks of a faithful friend for shifting off his whore into his brother’s arms for a wife. So certainly does interest banish all manner of affection, and so naturally do men give up honour and justice, humanity, and even Christianity, to secure themselves.\n\nI must now come back to brother Robin, as we always called him, who having got his mother’s consent, as above, came big with the news to me, and told me the whole story of it, with a sincerity so visible, that I must confess it grieved me that I must be the instrument to abuse so honest a gentleman. But there was no remedy; he would have me, and I was not obliged to tell him that I was his brother’s whore, though I had no other way to put him off; so I came gradually into it, to his satisfaction, and behold we were married.\n\nModesty forbids me to reveal the secrets of the marriage-bed, but nothing could have happened more suitable to my circumstances than that, as above, my husband was so fuddled when he came to bed, that he could not remember in the morning whether he had had any conversation with me or no, and I was obliged to tell him he had, though in reality he had not, that I might be sure he could make to inquiry about anything else.\n\nIt concerns the story in hand very little to enter into the further particulars of the family, or of myself, for the five years that I lived with this husband, only to observe that I had two children by him, and that at the end of five years he died. He had been really a very good husband to me, and we lived very agreeably together; but as he had not received much from them, and had in the little time he lived acquired no great matters, so my circumstances were not great, nor was I much mended by the match. Indeed, I had preserved the elder brother’s bonds to me, to pay £500, which he offered me for my consent to marry his brother; and this, with what I had saved of the money he formerly gave me, about as much more by my husband, left me a widow with about £1200 in my pocket.\n\nMy two children were, indeed, taken happily off my hands by my husband’s father and mother, and that, by the way, was all they got by Mrs. Betty.\n\nI confess I was not suitably affected with the loss of my husband, nor indeed can I say that I ever loved him as I ought to have done, or as was proportionable to the good usage I had from him, for he was a tender, kind, good-humoured man as any woman could desire; but his brother being so always in my sight, at least while we were in the country, was a continual snare to me, and I never was in bed with my husband but I wished myself in the arms of his brother; and though his brother never offered me the least kindness that way after our marriage, but carried it just as a brother ought to do, yet it was impossible for me to do so to him; in short, I committed adultery and incest with him every day in my desires, which, without doubt, was as effectually criminal in the nature of the guilt as if I had actually done it.\n\nBefore my husband died his elder brother was married, and we, being then removed to London, were written to by the old lady to come and be at the wedding. My husband went, but I pretended indisposition, and that I could not possibly travel, so I stayed behind; for, in short, I could not bear the sight of his being given to another woman, though I knew I was never to have him myself.\n\nI was now, as above, left loose to the world, and being still young and handsome, as everybody said of me, and I assure you I thought myself so, and with a tolerable fortune in my pocket, I put no small value upon myself. I was courted by several very considerable tradesmen, and particularly very warmly by one, a linen-draper, at whose house, after my husband’s death, I took a lodging, his sister being my acquaintance. Here I had all the liberty and all the opportunity to be gay and appear in company that I could desire, my landlord’s sister being one of the maddest, gayest things alive, and not so much mistress of her virtue as I thought at first she had been. She brought me into a world of wild company, and even brought home several persons, such as she liked well enough to gratify, to see her pretty widow, so she was pleased to call me, and that name I got in a little time in public. Now, as fame and fools make an assembly, I was here wonderfully caressed, had abundance of admirers, and such as called themselves lovers; but I found not one fair proposal among them all. As for their common design, that I understood too well to be drawn into any more snares of that kind. The case was altered with me: I had money in my pocket, and had nothing to say to them. I had been tricked once by that cheat called love, but the game was over; I was resolved now to be married or nothing, and to be well married or not at all.\n\nI loved the company, indeed, of men of mirth and wit, men of gallantry and figure, and was often entertained with such, as I was also with others; but I found by just observation, that the brightest men came upon the dullest errand—that is to say, the dullest as to what I aimed at. On the other hand, those who came with the best proposals were the dullest and most disagreeable part of the world. I was not averse to a tradesman, but then I would have a tradesman, forsooth, that was something of a gentleman too; that when my husband had a mind to carry me to the court, or to the play, he might become a sword, and look as like a gentleman as another man; and not be one that had the mark of his apron-strings upon his coat, or the mark of his hat upon his periwig; that should look as if he was set on to his sword, when his sword was put on to him, and that carried his trade in his countenance.\n\nWell, at last I found this amphibious creature, this land-water thing called a gentleman-tradesman; and as a just plague upon my folly, I was catched in the very snare which, as I might say, I laid for myself. I said for myself, for I was not trepanned, I confess, but I betrayed myself.\n\nThis was a draper, too, for though my comrade would have brought me to a bargain with her brother, yet when it came to the point, it was, it seems, for a mistress, not a wife; and I kept true to this notion, that a woman should never be kept for a mistress that had money to keep herself.\n\nThus my pride, not my principle, my money, not my virtue, kept me honest; though, as it proved, I found I had much better have been sold by my she-comrade to her brother, than have sold myself as I did to a tradesman that was rake, gentleman, shopkeeper, and beggar, all together.\n\nBut I was hurried on (by my fancy to a gentleman) to ruin myself in the grossest manner that every woman did; for my new husband coming to a lump of money at once, fell into such a profusion of expense, that all I had, and all he had before, if he had anything worth mentioning, would not have held it out above one year.\n\nHe was very fond of me for about a quarter of a year, and what I got by that was, that I had the pleasure of seeing a great deal of my money spent upon myself, and, as I may say, had some of the spending it too. “Come, my dear,” says he to me one day, “shall we go and take a turn into the country for about a week?” “Ay, my dear,” says I, “whither would you go?” “I care not whither,” says he, “but I have a mind to look like quality for a week. We’ll go to Oxford,” says he. “How,” says I, “shall we go? I am no horsewoman, and ’tis too far for a coach.” “Too far!” says he; “no place is too far for a coach-and-six. If I carry you out, you shall travel like a duchess.” “Hum,” says I, “my dear, ’tis a frolic; but if you have a mind to it, I don’t care.” Well, the time was appointed, we had a rich coach, very good horses, a coachman, postillion, and two footmen in very good liveries; a gentleman on horseback, and a page with a feather in his hat upon another horse. The servants all called him my lord, and the inn-keepers, you may be sure, did the like, and I was her honour the Countess, and thus we traveled to Oxford, and a very pleasant journey we had; for, give him his due, not a beggar alive knew better how to be a lord than my husband. We saw all the rarities at Oxford, talked with two or three Fellows of colleges about putting out a young nephew, that was left to his lordship’s care, to the University, and of their being his tutors. We diverted ourselves with bantering several other poor scholars, with hopes of being at least his lordship’s chaplains and putting on a scarf; and thus having lived like quality indeed, as to expense, we went away for Northampton, and, in a word, in about twelve days’ ramble came home again, to the tune of about £93 expense.\n\nVanity is the perfection of a fop. My husband had this excellence, that he valued nothing of expense; and as his history, you may be sure, has very little weight in it, ’tis enough to tell you that in about two years and a quarter he broke, and was not so happy to get over into the Mint, but got into a sponging-house, being arrested in an action too heavy from him to give bail to, so he sent for me to come to him.\n\nIt was no surprise to me, for I had foreseen some time that all was going to wreck, and had been taking care to reserve something if I could, though it was not much, for myself. But when he sent for me, he behaved much better than I expected, and told me plainly he had played the fool, and suffered himself to be surprised, which he might have prevented; that now he foresaw he could not stand it, and therefore he would have me go home, and in the night take away everything I had in the house of any value, and secure it; and after that, he told me that if I could get away one hundred or two hundred pounds in goods out of the shop, I should do it; “only,” says he, “let me know nothing of it, neither what you take nor whither you carry it; for as for me,” says he, “I am resolved to get out of this house and be gone; and if you never hear of me more, my dear,” says he, “I wish you well; I am only sorry for the injury I have done you.” He said some very handsome things to me indeed at parting; for I told you he was a gentleman, and that was all the benefit I had of his being so; that he used me very handsomely and with good manners upon all occasions, even to the last, only spent all I had, and left me to rob the creditors for something to subsist on.\n\nHowever, I did as he bade me, that you may be sure; and having thus taken my leave of him, I never saw him more, for he found means to break out of the bailiff’s house that night or the next, and go over into France, and for the rest of the creditors scrambled for it as well as they could. How, I knew not, for I could come at no knowledge of anything, more than this, that he came home about three o’clock in the morning, caused the rest of his goods to be removed into the Mint, and the shop to be shut up; and having raised what money he could get together, he got over, as I said, to France, from whence I had one or two letters from him, and no more. I did not see him when he came home, for he having given me such instructions as above, and I having made the best of my time, I had no more business back again at the house, not knowing but I might have been stopped there by the creditors; for a commission of bankrupt being soon after issued, they might have stopped me by orders from the commissioners. But my husband, having so dexterously got out of the bailiff’s house by letting himself down in a most desperate manner from almost the top of the house to the top of another building, and leaping from thence, which was almost two storeys, and which was enough indeed to have broken his neck, he came home and got away his goods before the creditors could come to seize; that is to say, before they could get out the commission, and be ready to send their officers to take possession.\n\nMy husband was so civil to me, for still I say he was much of a gentleman, that in the first letter he wrote me from France, he let me know where he had pawned twenty pieces of fine holland for £30, which were really worth £90, and enclosed me the token and an order for the taking them up, paying the money, which I did, and made in time above £100 of them, having leisure to cut them and sell them, some and some, to private families, as opportunity offered.\n\nHowever, with all this, and all that I had secured before, I found, upon casting things up, my case was very much altered, any my fortune much lessened; for, including the hollands and a parcel of fine muslins, which I carried off before, and some plate, and other things, I found I could hardly muster up £500; and my condition was very odd, for though I had no child (I had had one by my gentleman draper, but it was buried), yet I was a widow bewitched; I had a husband and no husband, and I could not pretend to marry again, though I knew well enough my husband would never see England any more, if he lived fifty years. Thus, I say, I was limited from marriage, what offer might soever be made me; and I had not one friend to advise with in the condition I was in, least not one I durst trust the secret of my circumstances to, for if the commissioners were to have been informed where I was, I should have been fetched up and examined upon oath, and all I have saved be taken away from me.\n\nUpon these apprehensions, the first thing I did was to go quite out of my knowledge, and go by another name. This I did effectually, for I went into the Mint too, took lodgings in a very private place, dressed up in the habit of a widow, and called myself Mrs. Flanders.\n\nHere, however, I concealed myself, and though my new acquaintances knew nothing of me, yet I soon got a great deal of company about me; and whether it be that women are scarce among the sorts of people that generally are to be found there, or that some consolations in the miseries of the place are more requisite than on other occasions, I soon found an agreeable woman was exceedingly valuable among the sons of affliction there, and that those that wanted money to pay half a crown on the pound to their creditors, and that run in debt at the sign of the Bull for their dinners, would yet find money for a supper, if they liked the woman.\n\nHowever, I kept myself safe yet, though I began, like my Lord Rochester’s mistress, that loved his company, but would not admit him farther, to have the scandal of a whore, without the joy; and upon this score, tired with the place, and indeed with the company too, I began to think of removing.\n\nIt was indeed a subject of strange reflection to me to see men who were overwhelmed in perplexed circumstances, who were reduced some degrees below being ruined, whose families were objects of their own terror and other people’s charity, yet while a penny lasted, nay, even beyond it, endeavouring to drown themselves, labouring to forget former things, which now it was the proper time to remember, making more work for repentance, and sinning on, as a remedy for sin past.\n\nBut it is none of my talent to preach; these men were too wicked, even for me. There was something horrid and absurd in their way of sinning, for it was all a force even upon themselves; they did not only act against conscience, but against nature; they put a rape upon their temper to drown the reflections, which their circumstances continually gave them; and nothing was more easy than to see how sighs would interrupt their songs, and paleness and anguish sit upon their brows, in spite of the forced smiles they put on; nay, sometimes it would break out at their very mouths when they had parted with their money for a lewd treat or a wicked embrace. I have heard them, turning about, fetch a deep sigh, and cry, “What a dog am I! Well, Betty, my dear, I’ll drink thy health, though”; meaning the honest wife, that perhaps had not a half-crown for herself and three or four children. The next morning they are at their penitentials again; and perhaps the poor weeping wife comes over to him, either brings him some account of what his creditors are doing, and how she and the children are turned out of doors, or some other dreadful news; and this adds to his self-reproaches; but when he has thought and pored on it till he is almost mad, having no principles to support him, nothing within him or above him to comfort him, but finding it all darkness on every side, he flies to the same relief again, viz. to drink it away, debauch it away, and falling into company of men in just the same condition with himself, he repeats the crime, and thus he goes every day one step onward of his way to destruction.\n\nI was not wicked enough for such fellows as these yet. On the contrary, I began to consider here very seriously what I had to do; how things stood with me, and what course I ought to take. I knew I had no friends, no, not one friend or relation in the world; and that little I had left apparently wasted, which when it was gone, I saw nothing but misery and starving was before me. Upon these considerations, I say, and filled with horror at the place I was in, and the dreadful objects which I had always before me, I resolved to be gone.\n\nI had made an acquaintance with a very sober, good sort of a woman, who was a widow too, like me, but in better circumstances. Her husband had been a captain of a merchant ship, and having had the misfortune to be cast away coming home on a voyage from the West Indies, which would have been very profitable if he had come safe, was so reduced by the loss, that though he had saved his life then, it broke his heart, and killed him afterwards; and his widow, being pursued by the creditors, was forced to take shelter in the Mint. She soon made things up with the help of friends, and was at liberty again; and finding that I rather was there to be concealed, than by any particular prosecutions and finding also that I agreed with her, or rather she with me, in a just abhorrence of the place and of the company, she invited to go home with her till I could put myself in some posture of settling in the world to my mind; withal telling me, that it was ten to one but some good captain of a ship might take a fancy to me, and court me, in that part of the town where she lived.\n\nI accepted her offer, and was with her half a year, and should have been longer, but in that interval what she proposed to me happened to herself, and she married very much to her advantage. But whose fortune soever was upon the increase, mine seemed to be upon the wane, and I found nothing present, except two or three boatswains, or such fellows, but as for the commanders, they were generally of two sorts: 1. Such as, having good business, that is to say, a good ship, resolved not to marry but with advantage, that is, with a good fortune; 2. Such as, being out of employ, wanted a wife to help them to a ship; I mean (1) a wife who, having some money, could enable them to hold, as they call it, a good part of a ship themselves, so to encourage owners to come in; or (2) a wife who, if she had not money, had friends who were concerned in shipping, and so could help to put the young man into a good ship, which to them is as good as a portion; and neither of these was my case, so I looked like one that was to lie on hand.\n\nThis knowledge I soon learned by experience, viz. that the state of things was altered as to matrimony, and that I was not to expect at London what I had found in the country: that marriages were here the consequences of politic schemes for forming interests, and carrying on business, and that Love had no share, or but very little, in the matter.\n\nThat as my sister-in-law at Colchester had said, beauty, wit, manners, sense, good humour, good behaviour, education, virtue, piety, or any other qualification, whether of body or mind, had no power to recommend; that money only made a woman agreeable; that men chose mistresses indeed by the gust of their affection, and it was requisite to a whore to be handsome, well-shaped, have a good mien and a graceful behaviour; but that for a wife, no deformity would shock the fancy, no ill qualities the judgment; the money was the thing; the portion was neither crooked nor monstrous, but the money was always agreeable, whatever the wife was.\n\nOn the other hand, as the market ran very unhappily on the men’s side, I found the women had lost the privilege of saying No; that it was a favour now for a woman to have the Question asked, and if any young lady had so much arrogance as to counterfeit a negative, she never had the opportunity given her of denying twice, much less of recovering that false step, and accepting what she had but seemed to decline. The men had such choice everywhere, that the case of the women was very unhappy; for they seemed to ply at every door, and if the man was by great chance refused at one house, he was sure to be received at the next.\n\nBesides this, I observed that the men made no scruple to set themselves out, and to go a-fortunehunting, as they call it, when they had really no fortune themselves to demand it, or merit to deserve it; and that they carried it so high, that a woman was scarce allowed to inquire after the character or estate of the person that pretended to her. This I had an example of, in a young lady in the next house to me, and with whom I had contracted an intimacy; she was courted by a young captain, and though she had near £2000 to her fortune, she did but inquire of some of his neighbours about his character, his morals, or substance, and he took occasion at the next visit to let her know, truly, that he took it very ill, and that he should not give her the trouble of his visits any more. I heard of it, and I had begun my acquaintance with her, I went to see her upon it. She entered into a close conversation with me about it, and unbosomed herself very freely. I perceived presently that though she thought herself very ill used, yet she had no power to resent it, and was exceedingly piqued that she had lost him, and particularly that another of less fortune had gained him.\n\nI fortified her mind against such a meanness, as I called it; I told her, that as low as I was in the world, I would have despised a man that should think I ought to take him upon his own recommendation only, without having the liberty to inform myself of his fortune and of his character; also I told her, that as she had a good fortune, she had no need to stoop to the disaster of the time; that it was enough that the men could insult us that had but little money to recommend us, but if she suffered such an affront to pass upon her without resenting it, she would be rendered low-prized upon all occasions, and would be the contempt of all the women in that part of the town; that a woman can never want an opportunity to be revenged of a man that has used her ill, and that there were ways enough to humble such a fellow as that, or else certainly women were the most unhappy creatures in the world.\n\nI found she was very well pleased with the discourse, and she told me seriously that she would be very glad to make him sensible of her just resentment, and either to bring him on again, or have the satisfaction of her revenge being as public as possible.\n\nI told her, that if she would take my advice, I would tell her how she should obtain her wishes in both these things; and that I would engage to bring the man to her door again, and make him beg to be let in. She smiled at that, and plainly let me see, that if he came to her door, her resentment was not so great as to give her leave to let him stand long there.\n\nHowever, she listened very willingly to my offer of advice; so I told her that the first thing she ought to do was a piece of justice to herself, namely, that whereas she had been told by several people that he had reported among the ladies that he had left her, and pretended to give the advantage of the negative to himself, she should take care to have it well spread among the women—which she could not fail of an opportunity to do in a neighbourhood so addicted to family news as that she live in was—that she had inquired into his circumstances, and found he was not the man as to estate he pretended to be. “Let them be told, madam,” said I, “that you had been well informed that he was not the man that you expected, and that you thought it was not safe to meddle with him; that you heard he was of an ill temper, and that he boasted how he had used the women ill upon many occasions, and that particularly he was debauched in his morals”, etc. The last of which, indeed, had some truth in it; but at the same time I did not find that she seemed to like him much the worse for that part.\n\nAs I had put this into her head, she came most readily into it. Immediately she went to work to find instruments, and she had very little difficulty in the search, for telling her story in general to a couple of gossips in the neighbourhood, it was the chat of the tea-table all over that part of the town, and I met with it wherever I visited; also, as it was known that I was acquainted with the young lady herself, my opinion was asked very often, and I confirmed it with all the necessary aggravations, and set out his character in the blackest colours; but then as a piece of secret intelligence, I added, as what the other gossips knew nothing of, viz. that I had heard he was in very bad circumstances; that he was under a necessity of a fortune to support his interest with the owners of the ship he commanded; that his own part was not paid for, and if it was not paid quickly, his owners would put him out of the ship, and his chief mate was likely to command it, who offered to buy that part which the captain had promised to take.\n\nI added, for I confess I was heartily piqued at the rogue, as I called him, that I had heard a rumour, too, that he had a wife alive at Plymouth, and another in the West Indies, a thing which they all knew was not very uncommon for such kind of gentlemen.\n\nThis worked as we both desire it, for presently the young lady next door, who had a father and mother that governed both her and her fortune, was shut up, and her father forbid him the house. Also in one place more where he went, the woman had the courage, however strange it was, to say No; and he could try nowhere but he was reproached with his pride, and that he pretended not to give the women leave to inquire into his character, and the like.\n\nWell, by this time he began to be sensible of his mistake; and having alarmed all the women on that side of the water, he went over to Ratcliff, and got access to some of the ladies there; but though the young women there too were, according to the fate of the day, pretty willing to be asked, yet such was his ill-luck, that his character followed him over the water and his good name was much the same there as it was on our side; so that though he might have had wives enough, yet it did not happen among the women that had good fortunes, which was what he wanted.\n\nBut this was not all; she very ingeniously managed another thing herself, for she got a young gentleman, who as a relation, and was indeed a married man, to come and visit her two or three times a week in a very fine chariot and good liveries, and her two agents, and I also, presently spread a report all over, that this gentleman came to court her; that he was a gentleman of a £1000 a year, and that he was fallen in love with her, and that she was going to her aunt’s in the city, because it was inconvenient for the gentleman to come to her with his coach in Redriff, the streets being so narrow and difficult.\n\nThis took immediately. The captain was laughed at in all companies, and was ready to hang himself. He tried all the ways possible to come at her again, and wrote the most passionate letters to her in the world, excusing his former rashness; and in short, by great application, obtained leave to wait on her again, as he said, to clear his reputation.\n\nAt this meeting she had her full revenge of him; for she told him she wondered what he took her to be, that she should admit any man to a treaty of so much consequence as that to marriage, without inquiring very well into his circumstances; that if he thought she was to be huffed into wedlock, and that she was in the same circumstances which her neighbours might be in, viz. to take up with the first good Christian that came, he was mistaken; that, in a word, his character was really bad, or he was very ill beholden to his neighbours; and that unless he could clear up some points, in which she had justly been prejudiced, she had no more to say to him, but to do herself justice, and give him the satisfaction of knowing that she was not afraid to say No, either to him or any man else.\n\nWith that she told him what she had heard, or rather raised herself by my means, of his character; his not having paid for the part he pretended to own of the ship he commanded; of the resolution of his owners to put him out of the command, and to put his mate in his stead; and of the scandal raised on his morals; his having been reproached with such-and-such women, and having a wife at Plymouth and in the West Indies, and the like; and she asked him whether he could deny that she had good reason, if these things were not cleared up, to refuse him, and in the meantime to insist upon having satisfaction in points to significant as they were.\n\nHe was so confounded at her discourse that he could not answer a word, and she almost began to believe that all was true, by his disorder, though at the same time she knew that she had been the raiser of all those reports herself.\n\nAfter some time he recovered himself a little, and from that time became the most humble, the most modest, and most importunate man alive in his courtship.\n\nShe carried her jest on a great way. She asked him, if he thought she was so at her last shift that she could or ought to bear such treatment, and if he did not see that she did not want those who thought it worth their while to come farther to her than he did; meaning the gentleman whom she had brought to visit her by way of sham.\nShe brought him by these tricks to submit to all possible measures to satisfy her, as well of his circumstances as of his behaviour. He brought her undeniable evidence of his having paid for his part of the ship; he brought her certificates from his owners, that the report of their intending to remove him from the command of the ship and put his chief mate in was false and groundless; in short, he was quite the reverse of what he was before.\n\nThus I convinced her, that if the men made their advantage of our sex in the affair of marriage, upon the supposition of there being such choice to be had, and of the women being so easy, it was only owing to this, that the women wanted courage to maintain their ground and to play their part; and that, according to my Lord Rochester,\n\n“A woman’s ne’er so ruined but she can Revenge herself on her undoer, Man.”\n\nAfter these things this young lady played her part so well, that though she resolved to have him, and that indeed having him was the main bent of her design, yet she made his obtaining her be to him the most difficult thing in the world; and this she did, not by a haughty reserved carriage, but by a just policy, turning the tables upon him, and playing back upon him his own game; for as he pretended, by a kind of lofty carriage, to place himself above the occasion of a character, and to make inquiring into his character a kind of an affront to him, she broke with him upon that subject, and at the same time that she make him submit to all possible inquiry after his affairs, she apparently shut the door against his looking into her own.\n\nIt was enough to him to obtain her for a wife. As to what she had, she told him plainly, that as he knew her circumstances, it was but just she should know his; and though at the same time he had only known her circumstances by common fame, yet he had made so many protestations of his passion for her, that he could ask no more but her hand to his grand request, and the like ramble according to the custom of lovers. In short, he left himself no room to ask any more questions about her estate, and she took the advantage of it like a prudent woman, for she placed part of her fortune so in trustees, without letting him know anything of it, that it was quite out of his reach, and made him be very well content with the rest.\n\nIt is true she was pretty well besides, that is to say, she had about £1400 in money, which she gave him; and the other, after some time, she brought to light as a perquisite to herself, which he was to accept as a mighty favour, seeing though it was not to be his, it might ease him in the article of her particular expenses; and I must add, that by this conduct the gentleman himself became not only the more humble in his applications to her to obtain her, but also was much the more an obliging husband to her when he had her. I cannot but remind the ladies here how much they place themselves below the common station of a wife, which, if I may be allowed not to be partial, is low enough already; I say, they place themselves below their common station, and prepare their own mortifications, by their submitting so to be insulted by the men beforehand, which I confess I see no necessity of.\n\nThis relation may serve, therefore, to let the ladies see that the advantage is not so much on the other side as the men think it is; and though it may be true that the men have but too much choice among us, and that some women may be found who will dishonour themselves, be cheap, and easy to come at, and will scarce wait to be asked, yet if they will have women, as I may say, worth having, they may find them as uncomeatable as ever and that those that are otherwise are a sort of people that have such deficiencies, when had, as rather recommend the ladies that are difficult than encourage the men to go on with their easy courtship, and expect wives equally valuable that will come at first call.\n\nNothing is more certain than that the ladies always gain of the men by keeping their ground, and letting their pretended lovers see they can resent being slighted, and that they are not afraid of saying No. They, I observe, insult us mightily with telling us of the number of women; that the wars, and the sea, and trade, and other incidents have carried the men so much away, that there is no proportion between the numbers of the sexes, and therefore the women have the disadvantage; but I am far from granting that the number of women is so great, or the number of men so small; but if they will have me tell the truth, the disadvantage of the women is a terrible scandal upon the men, and it lies here, and here only; namely, that the age is so wicked, and the sex so debauched, that, in short, the number of such men as an honest woman ought to meddle with is small indeed, and it is but here and there that a man is to be found who is fit for a woman to venture upon.\nBut the consequence even of that too amounts to no more than this, that women ought to be the more nice; for how do we know the just character of the man that makes the offer? To say that the woman should be the more easy on this occasion, is to say we should be the forwarder to venture because of the greatness of the danger, which, in my way of reasoning, is very absurd.\n\nOn the contrary, the women have ten thousand times the more reason to be wary and backward, by how much the hazard of being betrayed is the greater; and would the ladies consider this, and act the wary part, they would discover every cheat that offered; for, in short, the lives of very few men nowadays will bear a character; and if the ladies do but make a little inquiry, they will soon be able to distinguish the men and deliver themselves. As for women that do not think their own safety worth their thought, that, impatient of their perfect state, resolve, as they call it, to take the first good Christian that comes, that run into matrimony as a horse rushes into the battle, I can say nothing to them but this, that they are a sort of ladies that are to be prayed for among the rest of distempered people, and to me they look like people that venture their whole estates in a lottery where there is a hundred thousand blanks to one prize.\n\nNo man of common-sense will value a woman the less for not giving up herself at the first attack, or for accepting his proposal without inquiring into his person or character; on the contrary, he must think her the weakest of all creatures in the world, as the rate of men now goes. In short, he must have a very contemptible opinion of her capacities, nay, every of her understanding, that, having but one case of her life, shall call that life away at once, and make matrimony, like death, be a leap in the dark.\n\nI would fain have the conduct of my sex a little regulated in this particular, which is the thing in which, of all the parts of life, I think at this time we suffer most in; ’tis nothing but lack of courage, the fear of not being married at all, and of that frightful state of life called an old maid, of which I have a story to tell by itself. This, I say, is the woman’s snare; but would the ladies once but get above that fear and manage rightly, they would more certainly avoid it by standing their ground, in a case so absolutely necessary to their felicity, that by exposing themselves as they do; and if they did not marry so soon as they may do otherwise, they would make themselves amends by marrying safer. She is always married too soon who gets a bad husband, and she is never married too late who gets a good one; in a word, there is no woman, deformity or lost reputation excepted, but if she manages well, may be married safely one time or other; but if she precipitates herself, it is ten thousand to one but she is undone.\n\nBut I come now to my own case, in which there was at this time no little nicety. The circumstances I was in made the offer of a good husband the most necessary thing in the world to me, but I found soon that to be made cheap and easy was not the way. It soon began to be found that the widow had no fortune, and to say this was to say all that was ill of me, for I began to be dropped in all the discourses of matrimony. Being well-bred, handsome, witty, modest, and agreeable; all which I had allowed to my character—whether justly or no is not the purpose—I say, all these would not do without the dross, which way now become more valuable than virtue itself. In short, the widow, they said, had no money.\n\nI resolved, therefore, as to the state of my present circumstances, that it was absolutely necessary to change my station, and make a new appearance in some other place where I was not known, and even to pass by another name if I found occasion.\n\nI communicated my thoughts to my intimate friend, the captain’s lady, whom I had so faithfully served in her case with the captain, and who was as ready to serve me in the same kind as I could desire. I made no scruple to lay my circumstances open to her; my stock was but low, for I had made but about £540 at the close of my last affair, and I had wasted some of that; however, I had about £460 left, a great many very rich clothes, a gold watch, and some jewels, though of no extraordinary value, and about £30 or £40 left in linen not disposed of.\n\nMy dear and faithful friend, the captain’s wife, was so sensible of the service I had done her in the affair above, that she was not only a steady friend to me, but, knowing my circumstances, she frequently made me presents as money came into her hands, such as fully amounted to a maintenance, so that I spent none of my own; and at last she made this unhappy proposal to me, viz. that as we had observed, as above, how the men made no scruple to set themselves out as persons meriting a woman of fortune, when they had really no fortune of their own, it was but just to deal with them in their own way and, if it was possible, to deceive the deceiver.\n\nThe captain’s lady, in short, put this project into my head, and told me if I would be ruled by her I should certainly get a husband of fortune, without leaving him any room to reproach me with want of my own. I told her, as I had reason to do, that I would give up myself wholly to her directions, and that I would have neither tongue to speak nor feet to step in that affair but as she should direct me, depending that she would extricate me out of every difficulty she brought me into, which she said she would answer for.\n\nThe first step she put me upon was to call her cousin, and go to a relation’s house of hers in the country, where she directed me, and where she brought her husband to visit me; and calling me cousin, she worked matters so about, that her husband and she together invited me most passionately to come to town and be with them, for they now live in a quite different place from where they were before. In the next place, she tells her husband that I had at least £1500 fortune, and that after some of my relations I was like to have a great deal more.\n\nIt was enough to tell her husband this; there needed nothing on my side. I was but to sit still and wait the event, for it presently went all over the neighbourhood that the young widow at Captain ——’s was a fortune, that she had at least £1500, and perhaps a great deal more, and that the captain said so; and if the captain was asked at any time about me, he made no scruple to affirm it, though he knew not one word of the matter, other than that his wife had told him so; and in this he thought no harm, for he really believed it to be so, because he had it from his wife: so slender a foundation will those fellows build upon, if they do but think there is a fortune in the game. With the reputation of this fortune, I presently found myself blessed with admirers enough, and that I had my choice of men, as scarce as they said they were, which, by the way, confirms what I was saying before. This being my case, I, who had a subtle game to play, had nothing now to do but to single out from them all the properest man that might be for my purpose; that is to say, the man who was most likely to depend upon the hearsay of a fortune, and not inquire too far into the particulars; and unless I did this I did nothing, for my case would not bear much inquiry.\n\nI picked out my man without much difficulty, by the judgment I made of his way of courting me. I had let him run on with his protestations and oaths that he loved me above all the world; that if I would make him happy, that was enough; all which I knew was upon supposition, nay, it was upon a full satisfaction, that I was very rich, though I never told him a word of it myself.\n\nThis was my man; but I was to try him to the bottom, and indeed in that consisted my safety; for if he baulked, I knew I was undone, as surely as he was undone if he took me; and if I did not make some scruple about his fortune, it was the way to lead him to raise some about mine; and first, therefore, I pretended on all occasions to doubt his sincerity, and told him, perhaps he only courted me for my fortune. He stopped my mouth in that part with the thunder of his protestations, as above, but still I pretended to doubt.\n\nOne morning he pulls off his diamond ring, and writes upon the glass of the sash in my chamber this line—\n\n“You I love, and you alone.”\n\nI read it, and asked him to lend me his ring, with which I wrote under it, thus—\n\n“And so in love says every one.”\n\nHe takes his ring again, and writes another line thus—\n\n“Virtue alone is an estate.”\n\nI borrowed it again, and I wrote under it—\n\n“But money’s virtue, gold is fate.”\n\nHe coloured as red as fire to see me turn so quick upon him, and in a kind of a rage told me he would conquer me, and writes again thus—\n\n“I scorn your gold, and yet I love.”\n\nI ventured all upon the last cast of poetry, as you’ll see, for I wrote boldly under his last—\n\n“I’m poor: let’s see how kind you’ll prove.”\n\nThis was a sad truth to me; whether he believed me or no, I could not tell; I supposed then that he did not. However, he flew to me, took me in his arms, and, kissing me very eagerly, and with the greatest passion imaginable, he held me fast till he called for a pen and ink, and then told me he could not wait the tedious writing on the glass, but, pulling out a piece of paper, he began and wrote again—\n\n“Be mine, with all your poverty.”\n\nI took his pen, and followed him immediately, thus—\n\n“Yet secretly you hope I lie.”\n\nHe told me that was unkind, because it was not just, and that I put him upon contradicting me, which did not consist with good manners, any more than with his affection; and therefore, since I had insensibly drawn him into this poetical scribble, he begged I would not oblige him to break it off; so he writes again—\n\n“Let love alone be our debate.”\n\nI wrote again—\n\n“She loves enough that does not hate.”\n\nThis he took for a favour, and so laid down the cudgels, that is to say, the pen; I say, he took if for a favour, and a mighty one it was, if he had known all. However, he took it as I meant it, that is, to let him think I was inclined to go on with him, as indeed I had all the reason in the world to do, for he was the best-humoured, merry sort of a fellow that I ever met with, and I often reflected on myself how doubly criminal it was to deceive such a man; but that necessity, which pressed me to a settlement suitable to my condition, was my authority for it; and certainly his affection to me, and the goodness of his temper, however they might argue against using him ill, yet they strongly argued to me that he would better take the disappointment than some fiery-tempered wretch, who might have nothing to recommend him but those passions which would serve only to make a woman miserable all her days.\n\nBesides, though I jested with him (as he supposed it) so often about my poverty, yet, when he found it to be true, he had foreclosed all manner of objection, seeing, whether he was in jest or in earnest, he had declared he took me without any regard to my portion, and, whether I was in jest or in earnest, I had declared myself to be very poor; so that, in a word, I had him fast both ways; and though he might say afterwards he was cheated, yet he could never say that I had cheated him.\n\nHe pursued me close after this, and as I saw there was no need to fear losing him, I played the indifferent part with him longer than prudence might otherwise have dictated to me. But I considered how much this caution and indifference would give me the advantage over him, when I should come to be under the necessity of owning my own circumstances to him; and I managed it the more warily, because I found he inferred from thence, as indeed he ought to do, that I either had the more money or the more judgment, and would not venture at all.\n\nI took the freedom one day, after we had talked pretty close to the subject, to tell him that it was true I had received the compliment of a lover from him, namely, that he would take me without inquiring into my fortune, and I would make him a suitable return in this, viz. that I would make as little inquiry into his as consisted with reason, but I hoped he would allow me to ask a few questions, which he would answer or not as he thought fit; and that I would not be offended if he did not answer me at all; one of these questions related to our manner of living, and the place where, because I had heard he had a great plantation in Virginia, and that he had talked of going to live there, and I told him I did not care to be transported.\n\nHe began from this discourse to let me voluntarily into all his affairs, and to tell me in a frank, open way all his circumstances, by which I found he was very well to pass in the world; but that great part of his estate consisted of three plantations, which he had in Virginia, which brought him in a very good income, generally speaking, to the tune of £300, a year, but that if he was to live upon them, would bring him in four times as much. “Very well,” thought I; “you shall carry me thither as soon as you please, though I won’t tell you so beforehand.”\n\nI jested with him extremely about the figure he would make in Virginia; but I found he would do anything I desired, though he did not seem glad to have me undervalue his plantations, so I turned my tale. I told him I had good reason not to go there to live, because if his plantations were worth so much there, I had not a fortune suitable to a gentleman of £1200 a year, as he said his estate would be.\n\nHe replied generously, he did not ask what my fortune was; he had told me from the beginning he would not, and he would be as good as his word; but whatever it was, he assured me he would never desire me to go to Virginia with him, or go thither himself without me, unless I was perfectly willing, and made it my choice.\n\nAll this, you may be sure, was as I wished, and indeed nothing could have happened more perfectly agreeable. I carried it on as far as this with a sort of indifferency that he often wondered at, more than at first, but which was the only support of his courtship; and I mention it the rather to intimate again to the ladies that nothing but want of courage for such an indifferency makes our sex so cheap, and prepares them to be ill-used as they are; would they venture the loss of a pretending fop now and then, who carries it high upon the point of his own merit, they would certainly be less slighted, and courted more. Had I discovered really and truly what my great fortune was, and that in all I had not full £500 when he expected £1500, yet I had hooked him so fast, and played him so long, that I was satisfied he would have had me in my worst circumstances; and indeed it was less a surprise to him when he learned the truth than it would have been, because having not the least blame to lay on me, who had carried it with an air of indifference to the last, he would not say one word, except that indeed he thought it had been more, but that if it had been less he did not repent his bargain; only that he should not be able to maintain me so well as he intended.\n\nIn short, we were married, and very happily married on my side, I assure you, as to the man; for he was the best-humoured man that every woman had, but his circumstances were not so good as I imagined, as, on the other hand, he had not bettered himself by marrying so much as he expected.\n\nWhen we were married, I was shrewdly put to it to bring him that little stock I had, and to let him see it was no more; but there was a necessity for it, so I took my opportunity one day when we were alone, to enter into a short dialogue with him about it. “My dear,” said I, “we have been married a fortnight; is it not time to let you know whether you have got a wife with something or with nothing?” “Your own time for that, my dear,” says he; “I am satisfied that I have got the wife I love; I have not troubled you much,” says he, “with my inquiry after it.”\n\n“That’s true,” says I, “but I have a great difficulty upon me about it, which I scarce know how to manage.”\n\n“What’s that, my dear?” says he.\n\n“Why,” says I, “’tis a little hard upon me, and ’tis harder upon you. I am told that Captain ——” (meaning my friend’s husband) “has told you I had a great deal more money than I ever pretended to have, and I am sure I never employed him to do so.”\n\n“Well,” says he, “Captain —— may have told me so, but what then? If you have not so much, that may lie at his door, but you never told me what you had, so I have no reason to blame you if you have nothing at all.”\n\n“That’s is so just,” said I, “and so generous, that it makes my having but a little a double affliction to me.”\n\n“The less you have, my dear,” says he, “the worse for us both; but I hope your affliction you speak of is not caused for fear I should be unkind to you, for want of a portion. No, no, if you have nothing, tell me plainly, and at once; I may perhaps tell the captain he has cheated me, but I can never say you have cheated me, for did you not give it under your hand that you were poor? and so I ought to expect you to be.”\n\n“Well,” said I, “my dear, I am glad I have not been concerned in deceiving you before marriage. If I deceive you since, ’tis ne’er the worse; that I am poor is too true, but not so poor as to have nothing neither”; so I pulled out some bank bills, and gave him about £160. “There’s something, my dear,” said I, “and not quite all neither.”\n\nI had brought him so near to expecting nothing, by what I had said before, that the money, though the sum was small in itself, was doubly welcome to him; he owned it was more than he looked for, and that he did not question by my discourse to him, but that my fine clothes, gold watch, and a diamond ring or two, had been all my fortune.\n\nI let him please himself with that £160 two or three days, and then, having been abroad that day, and as if I had been to fetch it, I brought him £100 more home in gold, and told him there was a little more portion for him; and, in short, in about a week more I brought him £180 more, and about £60 in linen, which I made him believe I had been obliged to take with the £100 which I gave him in gold, as a composition for a debt of £600, being little more than five shillings in the pound, and overvalued too.\n\n“And now, my dear,” says I to him, “I am very sorry to tell you, that there is all, and that I have given you my whole fortune.” I added, that if the person who had my £600 had not abused me, I had been worth £1000 to him, but that as it was, I had been faithful to him, and reserved nothing to myself, but if it had been more he should have had it.\n\nHe was so obliged by the manner, and so pleased with the sum, for he had been in a terrible fright lest it had been nothing at all, that he accepted it very thankfully. And thus I got over the fraud of passing for a fortune without money, and cheating a man into marrying me on pretence of a fortune; which, by the way, I take to be one of the most dangerous steps a woman can take, and in which she runs the most hazard of being ill-used afterwards.\n\nMy husband, to give him his due, was a man of infinite good nature, but he was no fool; and finding his income not suited to the manner of living which he had intended, if I had brought him what he expected, and being under a disappointment in his return of his plantations in Virginia, he discovered many times his inclination of going over to Virginia, to live upon his own; and often would be magnifying the way of living there, how cheap, how plentiful, how pleasant, and the like.\n\nI began presently to understand this meaning, and I took him up very plainly one morning, and told him that I did so; that I found his estate turned to no account at this distance, compared to what it would do if he lived upon the spot, and that I found he had a mind to go and live there; and I added, that I was sensible he had been disappointed in a wife, and that finding his expectations not answered that way, I could do no less, to make him amends, than tell him that I was very willing to go over to Virginia with him and live there.\n\nHe said a thousand kind things to me upon the subject of my making such a proposal to him. He told me, that however he was disappointed in his expectations of a fortune, he was not disappointed in a wife, and that I was all to him that a wife could be, and he was more than satisfied on the whole when the particulars were put together, but that this offer was so kind, that it was more than he could express.\n\nTo bring the story short, we agreed to go. He told me that he had a very good house there, that it was well furnished, that his mother was alive and lived in it, and one sister, which was all the relations he had; that as soon as he came there, his mother would remove to another house, which was her own for life, and his after her decease; so that I should have all the house to myself; and I found all this to be exactly as he had said.\n\nTo make this part of the story short, we put on board the ship which we went in, a large quantity of good furniture for our house, with stores of linen and other necessaries, and a good cargo for sale, and away we went.\n\nTo give an account of the manner of our voyage, which was long and full of dangers, is out of my way; I kept no journal, neither did my husband. All that I can say is, that after a terrible passage, frighted twice with dreadful storms, and once with what was still more terrible, I mean a pirate who came on board and took away almost all our provisions; and which would have been beyond all to me, they had once taken my husband to go along with them, but by entreaties were prevailed with to leave him;—I say, after all these terrible things, we arrived in York River in Virginia, and coming to our plantation, we were received with all the demonstrations of tenderness and affection, by my husband’s mother, that were possible to be expressed.\n\nWe lived here all together, my mother-in-law, at my entreaty, continuing in the house, for she was too kind a mother to be parted with; my husband likewise continued the same as at first, and I thought myself the happiest creature alive, when an odd and surprising event put an end to all that felicity in a moment, and rendered my condition the most uncomfortable, if not the most miserable, in the world.\n\nMy mother was a mighty cheerful, good-humoured old woman—I may call her old woman, for her son was above thirty; I say she was very pleasant, good company, and used to entertain me, in particular, with abundance of stories to divert me, as well of the country we were in as of the people.\n\nAmong the rest, she often told me how the greatest part of the inhabitants of the colony came thither in very indifferent circumstances from England; that, generally speaking, they were of two sorts; either, first, such as were brought over by masters of ships to be sold as servants. “Such as we call them, my dear,” says she, “but they are more properly called slaves.” Or, secondly, such as are transported from Newgate and other prisons, after having been found guilty of felony and other crimes punishable with death.\n\n“When they come here,” says she, “we make no difference; the planters buy them, and they work together in the field till their time is out. When ’tis expired,” said she, “they have encouragement given them to plant for themselves; for they have a certain number of acres of land allotted them by the country, and they go to work to clear and cure the land, and then to plant it with tobacco and corn for their own use; and as the tradesmen and merchants will trust them with tools and clothes and other necessaries, upon the credit of their crop before it is grown, so they again plant every year a little more than the year before, and so buy whatever they want with the crop that is before them.\n\n“Hence, child,” says she, “man a Newgate-bird becomes a great man, and we have,” continued she, “several justices of the peace, officers of the trained bands, and magistrates of the towns they live in, that have been burnt in the hand.”\n\nShe was going on with that part of the story, when her own part in it interrupted her, and with a great deal of good-humoured confidence she told me she was one of the second sort of inhabitants herself; that she came away openly, having ventured too far in a particular case, so that she was become a criminal. “And here’s the mark of it, child,” says she; and, pulling off her glove, “look ye here,” says she, turning up the palm of her hand, and showed me a very fine white arm and hand, but branded in the inside of the hand, as in such cases it must be.\n\nThis story was very moving to me, but my mother, smiling, said, “You need not think a thing strange, daughter, for as I told you, some of the best men in this country are burnt in the hand, and they are not ashamed to own it. There’s Major ——,” says she, “he was an eminent pickpocket; there’s Justice Ba——r, was a shoplifter, and both of them were burnt in the hand; and I could name you several such as they are.”\n\nWe had frequent discourses of this kind, and abundance of instances she gave me of the like. After some time, as she was telling some stories of one that was transported but a few weeks ago, I began in an intimate kind of way to ask her to tell me something of her own story, which she did with the utmost plainness and sincerity; how she had fallen into very ill company in London in her young days, occasioned by her mother sending her frequently to carry victuals and other relief to a kinswoman of hers who was a prisoner in Newgate, and who lay in a miserable starving condition, was afterwards condemned to be hanged, but having got respite by pleading her belly, dies afterwards in the prison.\n\nHere my mother-in-law ran out in a long account of the wicked practices in that dreadful place, and how it ruined more young people than all the town besides. “And child,” says my mother, “perhaps you may know little of it, or, it may be, have heard nothing about it; but depend upon it,” says she, “we all know here that there are more thieves and rogues made by that one prison of Newgate than by all the clubs and societies of villains in the nation; ’tis that cursed place,” says my mother, “that half peopled this colony.”\n\nHere she went on with her own story so long, and in so particular a manner, that I began to be very uneasy; but coming to one particular that required telling her name, I thought I should have sunk down in the place. She perceived I was out of order, and asked me if I was not well, and what ailed me. I told her I was so affected with the melancholy story she had told, and the terrible things she had gone through, that it had overcome me, and I begged of her to talk no more of it. “Why, my dear,” says she very kindly, “what need these things trouble you? These passages were long before your time, and they give me no trouble at all now; nay, I look back on them with a particular satisfaction, as they have been a means to bring me to this place.” Then she went on to tell me how she very luckily fell into a good family, where, behaving herself well, and her mistress dying, her master married her, by whom she had my husband and his sister, and that by her diligence and good management after her husband’s death, she had improved the plantations to such a degree as they then were, so that most of the estate was of her getting, not her husband’s, for she had been a widow upwards of sixteen years.\n\nI heard this part of the story with very little attention, because I wanted much to retire and give vent to my passions, which I did soon after; and let any one judge what must be the anguish of my mind, when I came to reflect that this was certainly no more or less than my own mother, and I had now had two children, and was big with another by my own brother, and lay with him still every night.\n\nI was now the most unhappy of all women in the world. Oh! had the story never been told me, all had been well; it had been no crime to have lain with my husband, since as to his being my relation I had known nothing of it.\n\nI had now such a load on my mind that it kept me perpetually waking; to reveal it, which would have been some ease to me, I could not find would be to any purpose, and yet to conceal it would be next to impossible; nay, I did not doubt but I should talk of it in my sleep, and tell my husband of it whether I would or no. If I discovered it, the least thing I could expect was to lose my husband, for he was too nice and too honest a man to have continued my husband after he had known I had been his sister; so that I was perplexed to the last degree.\n\nI leave it to any man to judge what difficulties presented to my view. I was away from my native country, at a distance prodigious, and the return to me unpassable. I lived very well, but in a circumstance insufferable in itself. If I had discovered myself to my mother, it might be difficult to convince her of the particulars, and I had no way to prove them. On the other hand, if she had questioned or doubted me, I had been undone, for the bare suggestion would have immediately separated me from my husband, without gaining my mother or him, who would have been neither a husband nor a brother; so that between the surprise on one hand, and the uncertainty on the other, I had been sure to be undone.\n\nIn the meantime, as I was but too sure of the fact, I lived therefore in open avowed incest and whoredom, and all under the appearance of an honest wife; and though I was not much touched with the crime of it, yet the action had something in it shocking to nature, and made my husband, as he thought himself, even nauseous to me.\n\nHowever, upon the most sedate consideration, I resolved that it was absolutely necessary to conceal it all and not make the least discovery of it either to mother or husband; and thus I lived with the greatest pressure imaginable for three years more, but had no more children.\n\nDuring this time my mother used to be frequently telling me old stories of her former adventures, which, however, were no ways pleasant to me; for by it, though she did not tell it me in plain terms, yet I could easily understand, joined with what I had heard myself, of my first tutors, that in her younger days she had been both whore and thief; but I verily believed she had lived to repent sincerely of both, and that she was then a very pious, sober, and religious woman.\n\nWell, let her life have been what it would then, it was certain that my life was very uneasy to me; for I lived, as I have said, but in the worst sort of whoredom, and as I could expect no good of it, so really no good issue came of it, and all my seeming prosperity wore off, and ended in misery and destruction. It was some time, indeed, before it came to this, for, but I know not by what ill fate guided, everything went wrong with us afterwards, and that which was worse, my husband grew strangely altered, forward, jealous, and unkind, and I was as impatient of bearing his carriage, as the carriage was unreasonable and unjust. These things proceeded so far, that we came at last to be in such ill terms with one another, that I claimed a promise of him, which he entered willingly into with me when I consented to come from England with him, viz. that if I found the country not to agree with me, or that I did not like to live there, I should come away to England again when I pleased, giving him a year’s warning to settle his affairs.\n\nI say, I now claimed this promise of him, and I must confess I did it not in the most obliging terms that could be in the world neither; but I insisted that he treated me ill, that I was remote from my friends, and could do myself no justice, and that he was jealous without cause, my conversation having been unblamable, and he having no pretense for it, and that to remove to England would take away all occasion from him.\n\nI insisted so peremptorily upon it, that he could not avoid coming to a point, either to keep his word with me or to break it; and this, notwithstanding he used all the skill he was master of, and employed his mother and other agents to prevail with me to alter my resolutions; indeed, the bottom of the thing lay at my heart, and that made all his endeavours fruitless, for my heart was alienated from him as a husband. I loathed the thoughts of bedding with him, and used a thousand pretenses of illness and humour to prevent his touching me, fearing nothing more than to be with child by him, which to be sure would have prevented, or at least delayed, my going over to England.\n\nHowever, at last I put him so out of humour, that he took up a rash and fatal resolution; in short, I should not go to England; and though he had promised me, yet it was an unreasonable thing for me to desire it; that it would be ruinous to his affairs, would unhinge his whole family, and be next to an undoing him in the world; that therefore I ought not to desire it of him, and that no wife in the world that valued her family and her husband’s prosperity would insist upon such a thing.\n\nThis plunged me again, for when I considered the thing calmly, and took my husband as he really was, a diligent, careful man in the main work of laying up an estate for his children, and that he knew nothing of the dreadful circumstances that he was in, I could not but confess to myself that my proposal was very unreasonable, and what no wife that had the good of her family at heart would have desired.\n\nBut my discontents were of another nature; I looked upon him no longer as a husband, but as a near relation, the son of my own mother, and I resolved somehow or other to be clear of him, but which way I did not know, nor did it seem possible.\n\nIt is said by the ill-natured world, of our sex, that if we are set on a thing, it is impossible to turn us from our resolutions; in short, I never ceased poring upon the means to bring to pass my voyage, and came that length with my husband at last, as to propose going without him. This provoked him to the last degree, and he called me not only an unkind wife, but an unnatural mother, and asked me how I could entertain such a thought without horror, as that of leaving my two children (for one was dead) without a mother, and to be brought up by strangers, and never to see them more. It was true, had things been right, I should not have done it, but now it was my real desire never to see them, or him either, any more; and as to the charge of unnatural, I could easily answer it to myself, while I knew that the whole relation was unnatural in the highest degree in the world.\n\nHowever, it was plain there was no bringing my husband to anything; he would neither go with me nor let me go without him, and it was quite out of my power to stir without his consent, as any one that knows the constitution of the country I was in, knows very well.\n\nWe had many family quarrels about it, and they began in time to grow up to a dangerous height; for as I was quite estranged from my husband (as he was called) in affection, so I took no heed to my words, but sometimes gave him language that was provoking; and, in short, strove all I could to bring him to a parting with me, which was what above all things in the world I desired most.\n\nHe took my carriage very ill, and indeed he might well do so, for at last I refused to bed with him, and carrying on the breach upon all occasions to extremity, he told me once he thought I was mad, and if I did not alter my conduct, he would put me under cure; that is to say, into a madhouse. I told him he should find I was far enough from mad, and that it was not in his power, or any other villain’s, to murder me. I confess at the same time I was heartily frighted at his thoughts of putting me into a madhouse, which would at once have destroyed all the possibility of breaking the truth out, whatever the occasion might be; for that then no one would have given credit to a word of it.\n\nThis therefore brought me to a resolution, whatever came of it, to lay open my whole case; but which way to do it, or to whom, was an inextricable difficulty, and took me many months to resolve. In the meantime, another quarrel with my husband happened, which came up to such a mad extreme as almost pushed me on to tell it him all to his face; but though I kept it in so as not to come to the particulars, I spoke so much as put him into the utmost confusion, and in the end brought out the whole story.\n\nHe began with a calm expostulation upon my being so resolute to go to England; I defended it, and one hard word bringing on another, as is usual in all family strife, he told me I did not treat him as if he was my husband, or talk of my children as if I was a mother; and, in short, that I did not deserve to be used as a wife; that he had used all the fair means possible with me; that he had argued with all the kindness and calmness that a husband or a Christian ought to do, and that I made him such a vile return, that I treated him rather like a dog than a man, and rather like the most contemptible stranger than a husband; that he was very loth to use violence with me, but that, in short, he saw a necessity of it now, and that for the future he should be obliged to take such measures as should reduce me to my duty.\n\nMy blood was now fired to the utmost, though I knew what he had said was very true, and nothing could appear more provoked. I told him, for his fair means and his foul, they were equally contemned by me; that for my going to England, I was resolved on it, come what would; and that as to treating him not like a husband, and not showing myself a mother to my children, there might be something more in it than he understood at present; but, for his further consideration, I thought fit to tell him thus much, that he neither was my lawful husband, nor they lawful children, and that I had reason to regard neither of them more than I did.\n\nI confess I was moved to pity him when I spoke it, for he turned pale as death, and stood mute as one thunderstruck, and once or twice I thought he would have fainted; in short, it put him in a fit something like an apoplex; he trembled, a sweat or dew ran off his face, and yet he was cold as a clod, so that I was forced to run and fetch something for him to keep life in him. When he recovered of that, he grew sick and vomited, and in a little after was put to bed, and the next morning was, as he had been indeed all night, in a violent fever.\n\nHowever, it went off again, and he recovered, though but slowly, and when he came to be a little better, he told me I had given him a mortal wound with my tongue, and he had only one thing to ask before he desired an explanation. I interrupted him, and told him I was sorry I had gone so far, since I saw what disorder it put him into, but I desired him not to talk to me of explanations, for that would but make things worse.\n\nThis heightened his impatience, and, indeed, perplexed him beyond all bearing; for now he began to suspect that there was some mystery yet unfolded, but could not make the least guess at the real particulars of it; all that ran in his brain was, that I had another husband alive, which I could not say in fact might not be true, but I assured him, however, there was not the least of that in it; and indeed, as to my other husband, he was effectually dead in law to me, and had told me I should look on him as such, so I had not the least uneasiness on that score.\n\nBut now I found the thing too far gone to conceal it much longer, and my husband himself gave me an opportunity to ease myself of the secret, much to my satisfaction. He had laboured with me three or four weeks, but to no purpose, only to tell him whether I had spoken these words only as the effect of my passion, to put him in a passion, or whether there was anything of truth in the bottom of them. But I continued inflexible, and would explain nothing, unless he would first consent to my going to England, which he would never do, he said, while he lived; on the other hand, I said it was in my power to make him willing when I pleased—nay, to make him entreat me to go; and this increased his curiosity, and made him importunate to the highest degree, but it was all to no purpose.\n\nAt length he tells all this story to his mother, and sets her upon me to get the main secret out of me, and she used her utmost skill with me indeed; but I put her to a full stop at once by telling her that the reason and mystery of the whole matter lay in herself, and that it was my respect to her that had made me conceal it; and that, in short, I could go no farther, and therefore conjured her not to insist upon it.\n\nShe was struck dumb at this suggestion, and could not tell what to say or to think; but, laying aside the supposition as a policy of mine, continued her importunity on account of her son, and, if possible, to make up the breach between us two. As to that, I told her that it was indeed a good design in her, but that it was impossible to be done; and that if I should reveal to her the truth of what she desired, she would grant it to be impossible, and cease to desire it. At last I seemed to be prevailed on by her importunity, and told her I dared trust her with a secret of the greatest importance, and she would soon see that this was so, and that I would consent to lodge it in her breast, if she would engage solemnly not to acquaint her son with it without my consent.\n\nShe was long in promising this part, but rather than not come at the main secret, she agreed to that too, and after a great many other preliminaries, I began, and told her the whole story. First I told her how much she was concerned in all the unhappy breach which had happened between her son and me, by telling me her own story and her London name; and that the surprise she saw I was in was upon that occasion. Then I told her my own story, and my name, and assured her, by such other tokens as she could not deny, that I was no other, nor more or less, than her own child, her daughter, born of her body in Newgate; the same that had saved her from the gallows by being in her belly, and the same that she left in such-and-such hands when she was transported.\n\nIt is impossible to express the astonishment she was in; she was not inclined to believe the story, or to remember the particulars, for she immediately foresaw the confusion that must follow in the family upon it. But everything concurred so exactly with the stories she had told me of herself, and which, if she had not told me, she would perhaps have been content to have denied, that she had stopped her own mouth, and she had nothing to do but to take me about the neck and kiss me, and cry most vehemently over me, without speaking one word for a long time together. At last she broke out: “Unhappy child!” says she, “what miserable chance could bring thee hither? and in the arms of my own son, too! Dreadful girl,” says she, “why, we are all undone! Married to thy own brother! Three children, and two alive, all of the same flesh and blood! My son and my daughter lying together as husband and wife! All confusion and distraction for ever! Miserable family! what will become of us? What is to be said? What is to be done?” And thus she ran on for a great while; nor had I any power to speak, or if I had, did I know what to say, for every word wounded me to the soul. With this kind of amazement on our thoughts we parted for the first time, though my mother was more surprised than I was, because it was more news to her than to me. However, she promised again to me at parting, that she would say nothing of it to her son, till we had talked of it again.\n\nIt was not long, you may be sure, before we had a second conference upon the same subject; when, as if she had been willing to forget the story she had told me of herself, or to suppose that I had forgot some of the particulars, she began to tell them with alterations and omissions; but I refreshed her memory and set her to rights in many things which I supposed she had forgot, and then came in so opportunely with the whole history, that it was impossible for her to go from it; and then she fell into her rhapsodies again, and exclamations at the severity of her misfortunes. When these things were a little over with her, we fell into a close debate about what should be first done before we gave an account of the matter to my husband. But to what purpose could be all our consultations? We could neither of us see our way through it, nor see how it could be safe to open such a scene to him. It was impossible to make any judgment, or give any guess at what temper he would receive it in, or what measures he would take upon it; and if he should have so little government of himself as to make it public, we easily foresaw that it would be the ruin of the whole family, and expose my mother and me to the last degree; and if at last he should take the advantage the law would give him, he might put me away with disdain and leave me to sue for the little portion that I had, and perhaps waste it all in the suit, and then be a beggar; the children would be ruined too, having no legal claim to any of his effects; and thus I should see him, perhaps, in the arms of another wife in a few months, and be myself the most miserable creature alive.\n\nMy mother was as sensible of this as I; and, upon the whole, we knew not what to do. After some time we came to more sober resolutions, but then it was with this misfortune too, that my mother’s opinion and mine were quite different from one another, and indeed inconsistent with one another; for my mother’s opinion was, that I should bury the whole thing entirely, and continue to live with him as my husband till some other event should make the discovery of it more convenient; and that in the meantime she would endeavour to reconcile us together again, and restore our mutual comfort and family peace; that we might lie as we used to do together, and so let the whole matter remain a secret as close as death. “For, child,” says she, “we are both undone if it comes out.”\n\nTo encourage me to this, she promised to make me easy in my circumstances, as far as she was able, and to leave me what she could at her death, secured for me separately from my husband; so that if it should come out afterwards, I should not be left destitute, but be able to stand on my own feet and procure justice from him.\n\nThis proposal did not agree at all with my judgment of the thing, though it was very fair and kind in my mother; but my thoughts ran quite another way.\n\nAs to keeping the thing in our own breasts, and letting it all remain as it was, I told her it was impossible; and I asked her how she could think I could bear the thoughts of lying with my own brother. In the next place, I told her that her being alive was the only support of the discovery, and that while she owned me for her child, and saw reason to be satisfied that I was so, nobody else would doubt it; but that if she should die before the discovery, I should be taken for an impudent creature that had forged such a thing to go away from my husband, or should be counted crazed and distracted. Then I told her how he had threatened already to put me into a madhouse, and what concern I had been in about it, and how that was the thing that drove me to the necessity of discovering it to her as I had done.\n\nFrom all which I told her, that I had, on the most serious reflections I was able to make in the case, come to this resolution, which I hoped she would like, as a medium between both, viz. that she should use her endeavours with her son to give me leave to go to England, as I had desired, and to furnish me with a sufficient sum of money, either in goods along with me, or in bills for my support there, all along suggesting that he might one time or other think it proper to come over to me.\n\nThat when I was gone, she should then, in cold blood, and after first obliging him in the solemnest manner possible to secrecy, discover the case to him, doing it gradually, and as her own discretion should guide her, so that he might not be surprised with it, and fly out into any passions and excesses on my account, or on hers; and that she should concern herself to prevent his slighting the children, or marrying again, unless he had a certain account of my being dead.\n\nThis was my scheme, and my reasons were good; I was really alienated from him in the consequences of these things; indeed, I mortally hated him as a husband, and it was impossible to remove that riveted aversion I had to him. At the same time, it being an unlawful, incestuous living, added to that aversion, and though I had no great concern about it in point of conscience, yet everything added to make cohabiting with him the most nauseous thing to me in the world; and I think verily it was come to such a height, that I could almost as willingly have embraced a dog as have let him offer anything of that kind to me, for which reason I could not bear the thoughts of coming between the sheets with him. I cannot say that I was right in point of policy in carrying it such a length, while at the same time I did not resolve to discover the thing to him; but I am giving an account of what was, not of what ought or ought not to be.\n\nIn their directly opposite opinion to one another my mother and I continued a long time, and it was impossible to reconcile our judgments; many disputes we had about it, but we could never either of us yield our own, or bring over the other.\n\nI insisted on my aversion to lying with my own brother, and she insisted upon its being impossible to bring him to consent to my going from him to England; and in this uncertainty we continued, not differing so as to quarrel, or anything like it, but so as not to be able to resolve what we should do to make up that terrible breach that was before us.\n\nAt last I resolved on a desperate course, and told my mother my resolution, viz. that, in short, I would tell him of it myself. My mother was frighted to the last degree at the very thoughts of it; but I bid her be easy, told her I would do it gradually and softly, and with all the art and good-humour I was mistress of, and time it also as well as I could, taking him in good-humour too. I told her I did not question but, if I could be hypocrite enough to feign more affection to him than I really had, I should succeed in all my design, and we might part by consent, and with a good agreement, for I might live him well enough for a brother, though I could not for a husband.\n\nAll this while he lay at my mother to find out, if possible, what was the meaning of that dreadful expression of mine, as he called it, which I mentioned before: namely, that I was not his lawful wife, nor my children his legal children. My mother put him off, told him she could bring me to no explanations, but found there was something that disturbed me very much, and she hoped she should get it out of me in time, and in the meantime recommended to him earnestly to use me more tenderly, and win me with his usual good carriage; told him of his terrifying and affrighting me with his threats of sending me to a madhouse, and the like, and advised him not to make a woman desperate on any account whatever.\n\nHe promised her to soften his behaviour, and bid her assure me that he loved me as well as ever, and that he had no such design as that of sending me to a madhouse, whatever he might say in his passion; also he desired my mother to use the same persuasions to me too, that our affections might be renewed, and we might lie together in a good understanding as we used to do.\n\nI found the effects of this treaty presently. My husband’s conduct was immediately altered, and he was quite another man to me; nothing could be kinder and more obliging than he was to me upon all occasions; and I could do no less than make some return to it, which I did as well as I could, but it was but in an awkward manner at best, for nothing was more frightful to me than his caresses, and the apprehensions of being with child again by him was ready to throw me into fits; and this made me see that there was an absolute necessity of breaking the case to him without any more delay, which, however, I did with all the caution and reserve imaginable.\n\nHe had continued his altered carriage to me near a month, and we began to live a new kind of life with one another; and could I have satisfied myself to have gone on with it, I believe it might have continued as long as we had continued alive together. One evening, as we were sitting and talking very friendly together under a little awning, which served as an arbour at the entrance from our house into the garden, he was in a very pleasant, agreeable humour, and said abundance of kind things to me relating to the pleasure of our present good agreement, and the disorders of our past breach, and what a satisfaction it was to him that we had room to hope we should never have any more of it.\n\nI fetched a deep sigh, and told him there was nobody in the world could be more delighted than I was in the good agreement we had always kept up, or more afflicted with the breach of it, and should be so still; but I was sorry to tell him that there was an unhappy circumstance in our case, which lay too close to my heart, and which I knew not how to break to him, that rendered my part of it very miserable, and took from me all the comfort of the rest.\n\nHe importuned me to tell him what it was. I told him I could not tell how to do it; that while it was concealed from him I alone was unhappy, but if he knew it also, we should be both so; and that, therefore, to keep him in the dark about it was the kindest thing that I could do, and it was on that account alone that I kept a secret from him, the very keeping of which, I thought, would first or last be my destruction.\n\nIt is impossible to express his surprise at this relation, and the double importunity which he used with me to discover it to him. He told me I could not be called kind to him, nay, I could not be faithful to him if I concealed it from him. I told him I thought so too, and yet I could not do it. He went back to what I had said before to him, and told me he hoped it did not relate to what I had said in my passion, and that he had resolved to forget all that as the effect of a rash, provoked spirit. I told him I wished I could forget it all too, but that it was not to be done, the impression was too deep, and I could not do it: it was impossible.\n\nHe then told me he was resolved not to differ with me in anything, and that therefore he would importune me no more about it, resolving to acquiesce in whatever I did or said; only begged I should then agree, that whatever it was, it should no more interrupt our quiet and our mutual kindness.\n\nThis was the most provoking thing he could have said to me, for I really wanted his further importunities, that I might be prevailed with to bring out that which indeed it was like death to me to conceal; so I answered him plainly that I could not say I was glad not to be importuned, thought I could not tell how to comply. “But come, my dear,” said I, “what conditions will you make with me upon the opening this affair to you?”\n\n“Any conditions in the world,” said he, “that you can in reason desire of me.” “Well,” said I, “come, give it me under your hand, that if you do not find I am in any fault, or that I am willingly concerned in the causes of the misfortune that is to follow, you will not blame me, use me the worse, do me any injury, or make me be the sufferer for that which is not my fault.”\n\n“That,” says he, “is the most reasonable demand in the world: not to blame you for that which is not your fault. Give me a pen and ink,” says he; so I ran in and fetched a pen, ink, and paper, and he wrote the condition down in the very words I had proposed it, and signed it with his name. “Well,” says he, “what is next, my dear?”\n\n“Why,” says I, “the next is, that you will not blame me for not discovering the secret of it to you before I knew it.”\n\n“Very just again,” says he; “with all my heart”; so he wrote down that also, and signed it.\n\n“Well, my dear,” says I, “then I have but one condition more to make with you, and that is, that as there is nobody concerned in it but you and I, you shall not discover it to any person in the world, except your own mother; and that in all the measures you shall take upon the discovery, as I am equally concerned in it with you, though as innocent as yourself, you shall do nothing in a passion, nothing to my prejudice or to your mother’s prejudice, without my knowledge and consent.”\n\nThis a little amazed him, and he wrote down the words distinctly, but read them over and over before he signed them, hesitating at them several times, and repeating them: “My mother’s prejudice! and your prejudice! What mysterious thing can this be?” However, at last he signed it.\n\n“Well,” says I, “my dear, I’ll ask you no more under your hand; but as you are to hear the most unexpected and surprising thing that perhaps ever befell any family in the world, I beg you to promise me you will receive it with composure and a presence of mind suitable to a man of sense.”\n\n“I’ll do my utmost,” says he, “upon condition you will keep me no longer in suspense, for you terrify me with all these preliminaries.”\n\n“Well, then,” says I, “it is this: as I told you before in a heat, that I was not your lawful wife, and that our children were not legal children, so I must let you know now in calmness and in kindness, but with affliction enough, that I am your own sister, and you my own brother, and that we are both the children of our mother now alive, and in the house, who is convinced of the truth of it, in a manner not to be denied or contradicted.”\n\nI saw him turn pale and look wild; and I said, “Now remember your promise, and receive it with presence of mind; for who could have said more to prepare you for it than I have done?” However, I called a servant, and got him a little glass of rum (which is the usual dram of that country), for he was just fainting away. When he was a little recovered, I said to him, “This story, you may be sure, requires a long explanation, and therefore, have patience and compose your mind to hear it out, and I’ll make it as short as I can”; and with this, I told him what I thought was needful of the fact, and particularly how my mother came to discover it to me, as above. “And now, my dear,” says I, “you will see reason for my capitulations, and that I neither have been the cause of this matter, nor could be so, and that I could know nothing of it before now.”\n\n“I am fully satisfied of that,” says he, “but ’tis a dreadful surprise to me; however, I know a remedy for it all, and a remedy that shall put an end to your difficulties, without your going to England.” “That would be strange,” said I, “as all the rest.” “No, no,” says he, “I’ll make it easy; there’s nobody in the way of it but myself.” He looked a little disordered when he said this, but I did not apprehend anything from it at that time, believing, as it used to be said, that they who do those things never talk of them, or that they who talk of such things never do them.\n\nBut things were not come to their height with him, and I observed he became pensive and melancholy; and in a word, as I thought, a little distempered in his head. I endeavoured to talk him into temper, and to reason him into a kind of scheme for our government in the affair, and sometimes he would be well, and talk with some courage about it; but the weight of it lay too heavy upon his thoughts, and, in short, it went so far that he made attempts upon himself, and in one of them had actually strangled himself and had not his mother come into the room in the very moment, he had died; but with the help of a Negro servant she cut him down and recovered him.\n\nThings were now come to a lamentable height in the family. My pity for him now began to revive that affection which at first I really had for him, and I endeavoured sincerely, by all the kind carriage I could, to make up the breach; but, in short, it had gotten too great a head, it preyed upon his spirits, and it threw him into a long, lingering consumption, though it happened not to be mortal. In this distress I did not know what to do, as his life was apparently declining, and I might perhaps have married again there, very much to my advantage; it had been certainly my business to have stayed in the country, but my mind was restless too, and uneasy; I hankered after coming to England, and nothing would satisfy me without it.\n\nIn short, by an unwearied importunity, my husband, who was apparently decaying, as I observed, was at last prevailed with; and so my own fate pushing me on, the way was made clear for me, and my mother concurring, I obtained a very good cargo for my coming to England.\n\nWhen I parted with my brother (for such I am now to call him), we agreed that after I arrived he should pretend to have an account that I was dead in England, and so might marry again when he would. He promised, and engaged to me to correspond with me as a sister, and to assist and support me as long as I lived; and that if he died before me, he would leave sufficient to his mother to take care of me still, in the name of a sister, and he was in some respects careful of me, when he heard of me; but it was so oddly managed that I felt the disappointments very sensibly afterwards, as you shall hear in its time.\n\nI came away for England in the month of August, after I had been eight years in that country; and now a new scene of misfortunes attended me, which perhaps few women have gone through the life of.\n\nWe had an indifferent good voyage till we came just upon the coast of England, and where we arrived in two-and-thirty days, but were then ruffled with two or three storms, one of which drove us away to the coast of Ireland, and we put in at Kinsdale. We remained there about thirteen days, got some refreshment on shore, and put to sea again, though we met with very bad weather again, in which the ship sprung her mainmast, as they called it, for I knew not what they meant. But we got at last into Milford Haven, in Wales, where, though it was remote from our port, yet having my foot safe upon the firm ground of my native country, the isle of Britain, I resolved to venture it no more upon the waters, which had been so terrible to me; so getting my clothes and money on shore, with my bills of loading and other papers, I resolved to come for London, and leave the ship to get to her port as she could; the port whither she was bound was to Bristol, where my brother’s chief correspondent lived.\n\nI got to London in about three weeks, where I heard a little while after that the ship was arrived in Bristol, but at the same time had the misfortune to know that by the violent weather she had been in, and the breaking of her mainmast, she had great damage on board, and that a great part of her cargo was spoiled.\n\nI had now a new scene of life upon my hands, and a dreadful appearance it had. I was come away with a kind of final farewell. What I brought with me was indeed considerable, had it come safe, and by the help of it, I might have married again tolerably well; but as it was, I was reduced to between two or three hundred pounds in the whole, and this without any hope of recruit. I was entirely without friends, nay, even so much as without acquaintance, for I found it was absolutely necessary not to revive former acquaintances; and as for my subtle friend that set me up formerly for a fortune, she was dead, and her husband also; as I was informed, upon sending a person unknown to inquire.\n\nThe looking after my cargo of goods soon after obliged me to take a journey to Bristol, and during my attendance upon that affair I took the diversion of going to the Bath, for as I was still far from being old, so my humour, which was always gay, continued so to an extreme; and being now, as it were, a woman of fortune though I was a woman without a fortune, I expected something or other might happen in my way that might mend my circumstances, as had been my case before.\n\nThe Bath is a place of gallantry enough; expensive, and full of snares. I went thither, indeed, in the view of taking anything that might offer, but I must do myself justice, as to protest I knew nothing amiss; I meant nothing but in an honest way, nor had I any thoughts about me at first that looked the way which afterwards I suffered them to be guided.\n\nHere I stayed the whole latter season, as it is called there, and contracted some unhappy acquaintances, which rather prompted the follies I fell afterwards into than fortified me against them. I lived pleasantly enough, kept good company, that is to say, gay, fine company; but had the discouragement to find this way of living sunk me exceedingly, and that as I had no settled income, so spending upon the main stock was but a certain kind of bleeding to death; and this gave me many sad reflections in the interval of my other thoughts. However, I shook them off, and still flattered myself that something or other might offer for my advantage.\n\nBut I was in the wrong place for it. I was not now at Redriff, where, if I had set myself tolerably up, some honest sea captain or other might have talked with me upon the honourable terms of matrimony; but I was at the Bath, where men find a mistress sometimes, but very rarely look for a wife; and consequently all the particular acquaintances a woman can expect to make there must have some tendency that way.\n\nI had spent the first season well enough; for though I had contracted some acquaintance with a gentleman who came to the Bath for his diversion, yet I had entered into no felonious treaty, as it might be called. I had resisted some casual offers of gallantry, and had managed that way well enough. I was not wicked enough to come into the crime for the mere vice of it, and I had no extraordinary offers made me that tempted me with the main thing which I wanted.\n\nHowever, I went this length the first season, viz. I contracted an acquaintance with a woman in whose house I lodged, who, though she did not keep an ill house, as we call it, yet had none of the best principles in herself. I had on all occasions behaved myself so well as not to get the least slur upon my reputation on any account whatever, and all the men that I had conversed with were of so good reputation that I had not given the least reflection by conversing with them; nor did any of them seem to think there was room for a wicked correspondence, if they had any of them offered it; yet there was one gentleman, as above, who always singled me out for the diversion of my company, as he called it, which, as he was pleased to say, was very agreeable to him, but at that time there was no more in it.\n\nI had many melancholy hours at the Bath after the company was gone; for though I went to Bristol sometime for the disposing my effects, and for recruits of money, yet I chose to come back to Bath for my residence, because being on good terms with the woman in whose house I lodged in the summer, I found that during the winter I lived rather cheaper there than I could do anywhere else. Here, I say, I passed the winter as heavily as I had passed the autumn cheerfully; but having contracted a nearer intimacy with the said woman in whose house I lodged, I could not avoid communicating to her something of what lay hardest upon my mind and particularly the narrowness of my circumstances, and the loss of my fortune by the damage of my goods at sea. I told her also, that I had a mother and a brother in Virginia in good circumstances; and as I had really written back to my mother in particular to represent my condition, and the great loss I had received, which indeed came to almost £500, so I did not fail to let my new friend know that I expected a supply from thence, and so indeed I did; and as the ships went from Bristol to York River, in Virginia, and back again generally in less time from London, and that my brother corresponded chiefly at Bristol, I thought it was much better for me to wait here for my returns than to go to London, where also I had not the least acquaintance.\n\nMy new friend appeared sensibly affected with my condition, and indeed was so very kind as to reduce the rate of my living with her to so low a price during the winter, that she convinced me she got nothing by me; and as for lodging, during the winter I paid nothing at all.\n\nWhen the spring season came on, she continued to be as kind to me as she could, and I lodged with her for a time, till it was found necessary to do otherwise. She had some persons of character that frequently lodged in her house, and in particular the gentleman who, as I said, singled me out for his companion the winter before; and he came down again with another gentleman in his company and two servants, and lodged in the same house. I suspected that my landlady had invited him thither, letting him know that I was still with her; but she denied it, and protested to me that she did not, and he said the same.\n\nIn a word, this gentleman came down and continued to single me out for his peculiar confidence as well as conversation. He was a complete gentleman, that must be confessed, and his company was very agreeable to me, as mine, if I might believe him, was to him. He made no professions to me but of an extraordinary respect, and he had such an opinion of my virtue, that, as he often professed, he believed if he should offer anything else, I should reject him with contempt. He soon understood from me that I was a widow; that I had arrived at Bristol from Virginia by the last ships; and that I waited at Bath till the next Virginia fleet should arrive, by which I expected considerable effects. I understood by him, and by others of him, that he had a wife, but that the lady was distempered in her head, and was under the conduct of her own relations, which he consented to, to avoid any reflections that might (as was not unusual in such cases) be cast on him for mismanaging her cure; and in the meantime he came to the Bath to divert his thoughts from the disturbance of such a melancholy circumstance as that was.\n\nMy landlady, who of her own accord encouraged the correspondence on all occasions, gave me an advantageous character of him, as a man of honour and of virtue, as well as of great estate. And indeed I had a great deal of reason to say so of him too; for though we lodged both on a floor, and he had frequently come into my chamber, even when I was in bed, and I also into his when he was in bed, yet he never offered anything to me further than a kiss, or so much as solicited me to anything till long after, as you shall hear.\n\nI frequently took notice to my landlady of his exceeding modesty, and she again used to tell me, she believed it was so from the beginning; however, she used to tell me that she thought I ought to expect some gratification from him for my company, for indeed he did, as it were, engross me, and I was seldom from him. I told her I had not given him the least occasion to think I wanted it, or that I would accept of it from him. She told me she would take that part upon her, and she did so, and managed it so dexterously, that the first time we were together alone, after she had talked with him, he began to inquire a little into my circumstances, as how I had subsisted myself since I came on shore, and whether I did not want money. I stood off very boldly. I told him that though my cargo of tobacco was damaged, yet that it was not quite lost; that the merchant I had been consigned to had so honestly managed for me that I had not wanted, and that I hoped, with frugal management, I should make it hold out till more would come, which I expected by the next fleet; that in the meantime I had retrenched my expenses, and whereas I kept a maid last season, now I lived without; and whereas I had a chamber and a dining-room then on the first floor, as he knew, I now had but one room, two pair of stairs, and the like. “But I live,” said I, “as well satisfied now as I did then”; adding, that his company had been a means to make me live much more cheerfully than otherwise I should have done, for which I was much obliged to him; and so I put off all room for any offer for the present. However, it was not long before he attacked me again, and told me he found that I was backward to trust him with the secret of my circumstances, which he was sorry for; assuring me that he inquired into it with no design to satisfy his own curiosity, but merely to assist me, if there was any occasion; but since I would not own myself to stand in need of any assistance, he had but one thing more to desire of me, and that was, that I would promise him that when I was any way straitened, or like to be so, I would frankly tell him of it, and that I would make use of him with the same freedom that he made the offer; adding, that I should always find I had a true friend, though perhaps I was afraid to trust him.\n\nI omitted nothing that was fit to be said by one infinitely obliged, to let him know that I had a due sense of his kindness; and indeed from that time I did not appear so much reserved to him as I had done before, though still within the bounds of the strictest virtue on both sides; but how free soever our conversation was, I could not arrive to that sort of freedom which he desired, viz. to tell him I wanted money, though I was secretly very glad of his offer.\n\nSome weeks passed after this, and still I never asked him for money; when my landlady, a cunning creature, who had often pressed me to it, but found that I could not do it, makes a story of her own inventing, and comes in bluntly to me when we were together. “Oh, widow!” says she, “I have bad news to tell you this morning.” “What is that?” said I; “are the Virginia ships taken by the French?”—for that was my fear. “No, no,” says she, “but the man you sent to Bristol yesterday for money is come back, and says he has brought none.”\n\nNow I could by no means like her project; I thought it looked too much like prompting him, which indeed he did not want, and I clearly saw that I should lose nothing by being backward to ask, so I took her up short. “I can’t image why he should say so to you,” said I, “for I assure you he brought me all the money I sent him for, and here it is,” said I (pulling out my purse with about twelve guineas in it); and added, “I intend you shall have most of it by and by.”\n\nHe seemed distasted a little at her talking as she did at first, as well as I, taking it, as I fancied he would, as something forward of her; but when he saw me give such an answer, he came immediately to himself again. The next morning we talked of it again, when I found he was fully satisfied, and, smiling, said he hoped I would not want money and not tell him of it, and that I had promised him otherwise. I told him I had been very much dissatisfied at my landlady’s talking so publicly the day before of what she had nothing to do with; but I supposed she wanted what I owed her, which was about eight guineas, which I had resolved to give her, and had accordingly given it her the same night she talked so foolishly.\n\nHe was in a might good humour when he heard me say I had paid her, and it went off into some other discourse at that time. But the next morning, he having heard me up about my room before him, he called to me, and I answering, he asked me to come into his chamber. He was in bed when I came in, and he made me come and sit down on his bedside, for he said he had something to say to me which was of some moment. After some very kind expressions, he asked me if I would be very honest to him, and give a sincere answer to one thing he would desire of me. After some little cavil at the word “sincere,” and asking him if I had ever given him any answers which were not sincere, I promised him I would. Why, then, his request was, he said, to let him see my purse. I immediately put my hand into my pocket, and, laughing to him, pulled it out, and there was in it three guineas and a half. Then he asked me if there was all the money I had. I told him No, laughing again, not by a great deal.\n\nWell, then, he said, he would have me promise to go and fetch him all the money I had, every farthing. I told him I would, and I went into my chamber and fetched him a little private drawer, where I had about six guineas more, and some silver, and threw it all down upon the bed, and told him there was all my wealth, honestly to a shilling. He looked a little at it, but did not tell it, and huddled it all into the drawer again, and then reaching his pocket, pulled out a key, and bade me open a little walnut-tree box he had upon the table, and bring him such a drawer, which I did. In which drawer there was a great deal of money in gold, I believe near two hundred guineas, but I knew not how much. He took the drawer, and taking my hand, made me put it in and take a whole handful. I was backward at that, but he held my hand hard in his hand, and put it into the drawer, and made me take out as many guineas almost as I could well take up at once.\n\nWhen I had done so, he made me put them into my lap, and took my little drawer, and poured out all my money among his, and bade me get me gone, and carry it all home into my own chamber.\n\nI relate this story the more particularly because of the good-humour there was in it, and to show the temper with which we conversed. It was not long after this but he began every day to find fault with my clothes, with my laces and headdresses, and, in a word, pressed me to buy better; which, by the way, I was willing enough to do, though I did not seem to be so, for I loved nothing in the world better than fine clothes. I told him I must housewife the money he had lent me, or else I should not be able to pay him again. He then told me, in a few words, that as he had a sincere respect for me, and knew my circumstances, he had not lent me that money, but given it me, and that he thought I had merited it from him by giving him my company so entirely as I had done. After this he made me take a maid, and keep house, and his friend that come with him to Bath being gone, he obliged me to diet him, which I did very willingly, believing, as it appeared, that I should lose nothing by it, nor did the woman of the house fail to find her account in it too.\n\nWe had lived thus near three months, when the company beginning to wear away at the Bath, he talked of going away, and fain he would have me to go to London with him. I was not very easy in that proposal, not knowing what posture I was to live in there, or how he might use me. But while this was in debate he fell very sick; he had gone out to a place in Somersetshire, called Shepton, where he had some business and was there taken very ill, and so ill that he could not travel; so he sent his man back to Bath, to beg me that I would hire a coach and come over to him. Before he went, he had left all his money and other things of value with me, and what to do with them I did not know, but I secured them as well as I could, and locked up the lodgings and went to him, where I found him very ill indeed; however, I persuaded him to be carried in a litter to the Bath, where there was more help and better advice to be had.\n\nHe consented, and I brought him to the Bath, which was about fifteen miles, as I remember. Here he continued very ill of a fever, and kept his bed five weeks, all which time I nursed him and tended him myself, as much and as carefully as if I had been his wife; indeed, if I had been his wife I could not have done more. I sat up with him so much and so often, that at last, indeed, he would not let me sit up any longer, and then I got a pallet-bed into his room, and lay in it just at his bed’s feet.\n\nI was indeed sensibly affected with his condition, and with the apprehension of losing such a friend as he was, and was like to be to me, and I used to sit and cry by him many hours together. However, at last he grew better, and gave hopes that he would recover, as indeed he did, though very slowly.\n\nWere it otherwise than what I am going to say, I should not be backward to disclose it, as it is apparent I have done in other cases in this account; but I affirm, that through all this conversation, abating the freedom of coming into the chamber when I or he was in bed, and abating the necessary offices of attending him night and day when he was sick, there had not passed the least immodest word or action between us. Oh that it had been so to the last!\n\nAfter some time he gathered strength and grew well apace, and I would have removed my pallet-bed, but he would not let me, till he was able to venture himself without anybody to sit up with him, and then I removed to my own chamber.\n\nHe took many occasions to express his sense of my tenderness and concern for him; and when he grew quite well, he made me a present of fifty guineas for my care and, as he called it, for hazarding my life to save his.\n\nAnd now he made deep protestations of a sincere inviolable affection for me, but all along attested it to be with the utmost reserve for my virtue and his own. I told him I was fully satisfied of it. He carried it that length that he protested to me, that if he was naked in bed with me, he would as sacredly preserve my virtue as he would defend it if I was assaulted by a ravisher. I believed him, and told him I did so; but this did not satisfy him, he would, he said, wait for some opportunity to give me an undoubted testimony of it.\n\nIt was a great while after this that I had occasion, on my own business, to go to Bristol, upon which he hired me a coach, and would go with me, and did so; and now indeed our intimacy increased. From Bristol he carried me to Gloucester, which was merely a journey of pleasure, to take the air; and here it was our hap to have no lodging in the inn but in one large chamber with two beds in it. The master of the house going up with us to show his rooms, and coming into that room, said very frankly to him, “Sir, it is none of my business to inquire whether the lady be your spouse or no, but if not, you may lie as honestly in these two beds as if you were in two chambers,” and with that he pulls a great curtain which drew quite across the room and effectually divided the beds. “Well,” says my friend, very readily, “these beds will do, and as for the rest, we are too near akin to lie together, though we may lodge near one another”; and this put an honest face on the thing too. When we came to go to bed, he decently went out of the room till I was in bed, and then went to bed in the bed on his own side of the room, but lay there talking to me a great while.\n\nAt last, repeating his usual saying, that he could lie naked in the bed with me and not offer me the least injury, he starts out of his bed. “And now, my dear,” says he, “you shall see how just I will be to you, and that I can keep my word,” and away he comes to my bed.\n\nI resisted a little, but I must confess I should not have resisted him much if he had not made those promises at all; so after a little struggle, as I said, I lay still and let him come to bed. When he was there he took me in his arms, and so I lay all night with him, but he had no more to do with me, or offered anything to me, other than embracing me, as I say, in his arms, no, not the whole night, but rose up and dressed him in the morning, and left me as innocent for him as I was the day I was born.\n\nThis was a surprising thing to me, and perhaps may be so to others, who know how the laws of nature work; for he was a strong, vigorous, brisk person; nor did he act thus on a principle of religion at all, but of mere affection; insisting on it, that though I was to him the most agreeable woman in the world, yet, because he loved me, he could not injure me.\n\nI own it was a noble principle, but as it was what I never understood before, so it was to me perfectly amazing. We traveled the rest of the journey as we did before, and came back to the Bath, where, as he had opportunity to come to me when he would, he often repeated the moderation, and I frequently lay with him, and he with me, and although all the familiarities between man and wife were common to us, yet he never once offered to go any farther, and he valued himself much upon it. I do not say that I was so wholly pleased with it as he thought I was, for I own much wickeder than he, as you shall hear presently.\n\nWe lived thus near two years, only with this exception, that he went three times to London in that time, and once he continued there four months; but, to do him justice, he always supplied me with money to subsist me very handsomely.\n\nHad we continued thus, I confess we had had much to boast of; but as wise men say, it is ill venturing too near the brink of a command, so we found it; and here again I must do him the justice to own that the first breach was not on his part. It was one night that we were in bed together warm and merry, and having drunk, I think, a little more wine that night, both of us, than usual, although not in the least to disorder either of us, when, after some other follies which I cannot name, and being clasped close in his arms, I told him (I repeat it with shame and horror of soul) that I could find in my heart to discharge him of his engagement for one night and no more.\n\nHe took me at my word immediately, and after that there was no resisting him; neither indeed had I any mind to resist him any more, let what would come of it.\n\nThus the government of our virtue was broken, and I exchanged the place of friend for that unmusical, harsh-sounding title of whore. In the morning we were both at our penitentials; I cried very heartily, he expressed himself very sorry; but that was all either of us could do at that time, and the way being thus cleared, and the bars of virtue and conscience thus removed, we had the less difficult afterwards to struggle with.\n\nIt was but a dull kind of conversation that we had together for all the rest of that week; I looked on him with blushes, and every now and then started that melancholy objection, “What if I should be with child now? What will become of me then?” He encouraged me by telling me, that as long as I was true to him, he would be so to me; and since it was gone such a length (which indeed he never intended), yet if I was with child, he would take care of that, and of me too. This hardened us both. I assured him if I was with child, I would die for want of a midwife rather than name him as the father of it; and he assured me I should never want if I should be with child. These mutual assurances hardened us in the thing, and after this we repeated the crime as often as we pleased, till at length, as I had feared, so it came to pass, and I was indeed with child.\n\nAfter I was sure it was so, and I had satisfied him of it too, we began to think of taking measures for the managing it, and I proposed trusting the secret to my landlady, and asking her advice, which he agreed to. My landlady, a woman (as I found) used to such things, made light of it; she said she knew it would come to that at last, and made us very merry about it. As I said above, we found her an experienced old lady at such work; she undertook everything, engaged to procure a midwife and a nurse, to satisfy all inquiries, and bring us off with reputation, and she did so very dexterously indeed.\n\nWhen I grew near my time she desired my gentleman to go away to London, or make as if he did so. When he was gone, she acquainted the parish officers that there was a lady ready to lie in at her house, but that she knew her husband very well, and gave them, as she pretended, an account of his name, which she called Sir Walter Cleve; telling them he was a very worthy gentleman, and that she would answer for all inquiries, and the like. This satisfied the parish officers presently, and I lay in with as much credit as I could have done if I had really been my Lady Cleve, and was assisted in my travail by three or four of the best citizens’ wives of Bath who lived in the neighbourhood, which, however, made me a little the more expensive to him. I often expressed my concern to him about it, but he bid me not be concerned at it.\n\nAs he had furnished me very sufficiently with money for the extraordinary expenses of my lying in, I had everything very handsome about me, but did not affect to be gay or extravagant neither; besides, knowing my own circumstances, and knowing the world as I had done, and that such kind of things do not often last long, I took care to lay up as much money as I could for a wet day, as I called it; making him believe it was all spent upon the extraordinary appearance of things in my lying in.\n\nBy this means, and including what he had given me as above, I had at the end of my lying in about two hundred guineas by me, including also what was left of my own.\n\nI was brought to bed of a fine boy indeed, and a charming child it was; and when he heard of it he wrote me a very kind, obliging letter about it, and then told me, he thought it would look better for me to come away for London as soon as I was up and well; that he had provided apartments for me at Hammersmith, as if I came thither only from London; and that after a little while I should go back to the Bath, and he would go with me.\n\nI liked this offer very well, and accordingly hired a coach on purpose, and taking my child, and a wet-nurse to tend and suckle it, and a maid-servant with me, away I went for London.\n\nHe met me at Reading in his own chariot, and taking me into that, left the servant and the child in the hired coach, and so he brought me to my new lodgings at Hammersmith; with which I had abundance of reason to be very well pleased, for they were very handsome rooms, and I was very well accommodated.\n\nAnd now I was indeed in the height of what I might call my prosperity, and I wanted nothing but to be a wife, which, however, could not be in this case, there was no room for it; and therefore on all occasions I studied to save what I could, as I have said above, against a time of scarcity, knowing well enough that such things as these do not always continue; that men that keep mistresses often change them, grow weary of them, or jealous of them, or something or other happens to make them withdraw their bounty; and sometimes the ladies that are thus well used are not careful by a prudent conduct to preserve the esteem of their persons, or the nice article of their fidelity, and then they are justly cast off with contempt.\n\nBut I was secured in this point, for as I had no inclination to change, so I had no manner of acquaintance in the whole house, and so no temptation to look any farther. I kept no company but in the family when I lodged, and with the clergyman’s lady at next door; so that when he was absent I visited nobody, nor did he ever find me out of my chamber or parlour whenever he came down; if I went anywhere to take the air, it was always with him.\n\nThe living in this manner with him, and his with me, was certainly the most undesigned thing in the world; he often protested to me, that when he became first acquainted with me, and even to the very night when we first broke in upon our rules, he never had the least design of lying with me; that he always had a sincere affection for me, but not the least real inclination to do what he had done. I assured him I never suspected him; that if I had I should not so easily have yielded to the freedom which brought it on, but that it was all a surprise, and was owing to the accident of our having yielded too far to our mutual inclinations that night; and indeed I have often observed since, and leave it as a caution to the readers of this story, that we ought to be cautious of gratifying our inclinations in loose and lewd freedoms, lest we find our resolutions of virtue fail us in the junction when their assistance should be most necessary.\n\nIt is true, and I have confessed it before, that from the first hour I began to converse with him, I resolved to let him lie with me, if he offered it; but it was because I wanted his help and assistance, and I knew no other way of securing him than that. But when we were that night together, and, as I have said, had gone such a length, I found my weakness; the inclination was not to be resisted, but I was obliged to yield up all even before he asked it.\n\nHowever, he was so just to me that he never upbraided me with that; nor did he ever express the least dislike of my conduct on any other occasion, but always protested he was as much delighted with my company as he was the first hour we came together: I mean, came together as bedfellows.\n\nIt is true that he had no wife, that is to say, she was as no wife to him, and so I was in no danger that way, but the just reflections of conscience oftentimes snatch a man, especially a man of sense, from the arms of a mistress, as it did him at last, though on another occasion.\nOn the other hand, though I was not without secret reproaches of my own conscience for the life I led, and that even in the greatest height of the satisfaction I ever took, yet I had the terrible prospect of poverty and starving, which lay on me as a frightful spectre, so that there was no looking behind me. But as poverty brought me into it, so fear of poverty kept me in it, and I frequently resolved to leave it quite off, if I could but come to lay up money enough to maintain me. But these were thoughts of no weight, and whenever he came to me they vanished; for his company was so delightful, that there was no being melancholy when he was there; the reflections were all the subject of those hours when I was alone.\n\nI lived six years in this happy but unhappy condition, in which time I brought him three children, but only the first of them lived; and though I removed twice in those six years, yet I came back the sixth year to my first lodgings at Hammersmith. Here it was that I was one morning surprised with a kind but melancholy letter from my gentleman, intimating that he was very ill, and was afraid he should have another fit of sickness, but that his wife’s relations being in the house with him, it would not be practicable to have me with him, which, however, he expressed his great dissatisfaction in, and that he wished I could be allowed to tend and nurse him as I did before.\n\nI was very much concerned at this account, and was very impatient to know how it was with him. I waited a fortnight or thereabouts, and heard nothing, which surprised me, and I began to be very uneasy indeed. I think, I may say, that for the next fortnight I was near to distracted. It was my particular difficulty that I did not know directly where he was; for I understood at first he was in the lodgings of his wife’s mother; but having removed myself to London, I soon found, by the help of the direction I had for writing my letters to him, how to inquire after him, and there I found that he was at a house in Bloomsbury, whither he had, a little before he fell sick, removed his whole family; and that his wife and wife’s mother were in the same house, though the wife was not suffered to know that she was in the same house with her husband.\n\nHere I also soon understood that he was at the last extremity, which made me almost at the last extremity too, to have a true account. One night I had the curiosity to disguise myself like a servant-maid, in a round cap and straw hat, and went to the door, as sent by a lady of his neighbourhood, where he lived before, and giving master and mistress’s service, I said I was sent to know how Mr. —— did, and how he had rested that night. In delivering this message I got the opportunity I desired; for, speaking with one of the maids, I held a long gossip’s tale with her, and had all the particulars of his illness, which I found was a pleurisy, attended with a cough and a fever. She told me also who was in the house, and how his wife was, who, by her relation, they were in some hopes might recover her understanding; but as to the gentleman himself, in short she told me the doctors said there was very little hopes of him, that in the morning they thought he had been dying, and that he was but little better then, for they did not expect that he could live over the next night.\n\nThis was heavy news for me, and I began now to see an end of my prosperity, and to see also that it was very well I had played to good housewife, and secured or saved something while he was alive, for that now I had no view of my own living before me.\n\nIt lay very heavy upon my mind, too, that I had a son, a fine lovely boy, about five years old, and no provision made for it, at least that I knew of. With these considerations, and a sad heart, I went home that evening, and began to cast with myself how I should live, and in what manner to bestow myself, for the residue of my life.\n\nYou may be sure I could not rest without inquiring again very quickly what was become of him; and not venturing to go myself, I sent several sham messengers, till after a fortnight’s waiting longer, I found that there was hopes of his life, though he was still very ill; then I abated my sending any more to the house, and in some time after I learned in the neighbourhood that he was about house, and then that he was abroad again.\n\nI made no doubt then but that I should soon hear of him, and began to comfort myself with my circumstances being, as I thought, recovered. I waited a week, and two weeks, and with much surprise and amazement I waited near two months and heard nothing, but that, being recovered, he was gone into the country for the air, and for the better recovery after his distemper. After this it was yet two months more, and then I understood he was come to his city house again, but still I heard nothing from him.\n\nI had written several letters for him, and directed them as usual, and found two or three of them had been called for, but not the rest. I wrote again in a more pressing manner than ever, and in one of them let him know, that I must be forced to wait on him myself, representing my circumstances, the rent of lodgings to pay, and the provision for the child wanting, and my own deplorable condition, destitute of subsistence for his most solemn engagement to take care of and provide for me. I took a copy of this letter, and finding it lay at the house near a month and was not called for, I found means to have the copy of it put into his own hands at a coffee-house, where I had by inquiry found he used to go.\n\nThis letter forced an answer from him, by which, though I found I was to be abandoned, yet I found he had sent a letter to me some time before, desiring me to go down to the Bath again. Its contents I shall come to presently.\n\nIt is true that sick-beds are the time when such correspondences as this are looked on with different countenances, and seen with other eyes than we saw them with, or than they appeared with before. My lover had been at the gates of death, and at the very brink of eternity; and, it seems, had been struck with a due remorse, and with sad reflections upon his past life of gallantry and levity; and among the rest, criminal correspondence with me, which was neither more nor less than a long-continued life of adultery, and represented itself as it really was, not as it had been formerly thought by him to be, and he looked upon it now with a just and religious abhorrence.\n\nI cannot but observe also, and leave it for the direction of my sex in such cases of pleasure, that whenever sincere repentance succeeds such a crime as this, there never fails to attend a hatred of the object; and the more the affection might seem to be before, the hatred will be the more in proportion. It will always be so, indeed it can be no otherwise; for there cannot be a true and sincere abhorrence of the offence, and the love to the cause of it remain; there will, with an abhorrence of the sin, be found a detestation of the fellow-sinner; you can expect no other.\n\nI found it so here, though good manners and justice in this gentleman kept him from carrying it on to any extreme but the short history of his part in this affair was thus: he perceived by my last letter, and by all the rest, which he went for after, that I was not gone to Bath, that his first letter had not come to my hand; upon which he write me this following:—\n\n“MADAM,—I am surprised that my letter, dated the 8th of last month, did not come to your hand; I give you my word it was delivered at your lodgings, and to the hands of your maid.\n\nI need not acquaint you with what has been my condition for some time past; and how, having been at the edge of the grave, I am, by the unexpected and undeserved mercy of Heaven, restored again. In the condition I have been in, it cannot be strange to you that our unhappy correspondence had not been the least of the burthens which lay upon my conscience. I need say no more; those things that must be repented of, must be also reformed.\n\nI wish you would think of going back to the Bath. I enclose you here a bill for £50 for clearing yourself at your lodgings, and carrying you down, and hope it will be no surprise to you to add, that on this account only, and not for any offence given me on your side, I can _see you no more_. I will take due care of the child; leave him where he is, or take him with you, as you please. I wish you the like reflections, and that they may be to your advantage.—I am,” etc.\n\nI was struck with this letter as with a thousand wounds, such as I cannot describe; the reproaches of my own conscience were such as I cannot express, for I was not blind to my own crime; and I reflected that I might with less offence have continued with my brother, and lived with him as a wife, since there was no crime in our marriage on that score, neither of us knowing it.\n\nBut I never once reflected that I was all this while a married woman, a wife to Mr. —— the linen-draper, who, though he had left me by the necessity of his circumstances, had no power to discharge me from the marriage contract which was between us, or to give me a legal liberty to marry again; so that I had been no less than a whore and an adulteress all this while. I then reproached myself with the liberties I had taken, and how I had been a snare to this gentleman, and that indeed I was principal in the crime; that now he was mercifully snatched out of the gulf by a convincing work upon his mind, but that I was left as if I was forsaken of God’s grace, and abandoned by Heaven to a continuing in my wickedness.\n\nUnder these reflections I continued very pensive and sad for near month, and did not go down to the Bath, having no inclination to be with the woman whom I was with before; lest, as I thought, she should prompt me to some wicked course of life again, as she had done; and besides, I was very loth she should know I was cast off as above.\n\nAnd now I was greatly perplexed about my little boy. It was death to me to part with the child, and yet when I considered the danger of being one time or other left with him to keep without a maintenance to support him, I then resolved to leave him where he was; but then I concluded also to be near him myself too, that I then might have the satisfaction of seeing him, without the care of providing for him.\n\nI sent my gentleman a short letter, therefore, that I had obeyed his orders in all things but that of going back to the Bath, which I could not think of for many reasons; that however parting from him was a wound to me that I could never recover, yet that I was fully satisfied his reflections were just, and would be very far from desiring to obstruct his reformation or repentance.\n\nThen I represented my own circumstances to him in the most moving terms that I was able. I told him that those unhappy distresses which first moved him to a generous and an honest friendship for me, would, I hope, move him to a little concern for me now, though the criminal part of our correspondence, which I believed neither of us intended to fall into at the time, was broken off; that I desired to repent as sincerely as he had done, but entreated him to put me in some condition that I might not be exposed to the temptations which the devil never fails to excite us to from the frightful prospect of poverty and distress; and if he had the least apprehensions of my being troublesome to him, I begged he would put me in a posture to go back to my mother in Virginia, from when he knew I came, and that would put an end to all his fears on that account. I concluded, that if he would send me £50 more to facilitate my going away, I would send him back a general release, and would promise never to disturb him more with any importunities; unless it was to hear of the well-doing of the child, whom, if I found my mother living and my circumstances able, I would send for to come over to me, and take him also effectually off his hands.\n\nThis was indeed all a cheat thus far, viz. that I had no intention to go to Virginia, as the account of my former affairs there may convince anybody of; but the business was to get this last £50 of him, if possible, knowing well enough it would be the last penny I was ever to expect.\n\nHowever, the argument I used, namely, of giving him a general release, and never troubling him any more, prevailed effectually with him, and he sent me a bill for the money by a person who brought with him a general release for me to sign, and which I frankly signed, and received the money; and thus, though full sore against my will, a final end was put to this affair.\n\nAnd here I cannot but reflect upon the unhappy consequence of too great freedoms between persons stated as we were, upon the pretence of innocent intentions, love of friendship, and the like; for the flesh has generally so great a share in those friendships, that is great odds but inclination prevails at last over the most solemn resolutions; and that vice breaks in at the breaches of decency, which really innocent friendship ought to preserve with the greatest strictness. But I leave the readers of these things to their own just reflections, which they will be more able to make effectual than I, who so soon forgot myself, and am therefore but a very indifferent monitor.\n\nI was now a single person again, as I may call myself; I was loosed from all the obligations either of wedlock or mistress-ship in the world, except my husband the linen-draper, whom, I having not now heard from in almost fifteen years, nobody could blame me for thinking myself entirely freed from; seeing also he had at his going away told me, that if I did not hear frequently from him, I should conclude he was dead, and I might freely marry again to whom I pleased.\n\nI now began to cast up my accounts. I had by many letters and much importunity, and with the intercession of my mother too, had a second return of some goods from my brother (as I now call him) in Virginia, to make up the damage of the cargo I brought away with me, and this too was upon the condition of my sealing a general release to him, and to send it him by his correspondent at Bristol, which, though I thought hard of, yet I was obliged to promise to do. However, I managed so well in this case, that I got my goods away before the release was signed, and then I always found something or other to say to evade the thing, and to put off the signing it at all; till at length I pretended I must write to my brother, and have his answer, before I could do it.\n\nIncluding this recruit, and before I got the last £50, I found my strength to amount, put all together, to about £400, so that with that I had about £450. I had saved above £100 more, but I met with a disaster with that, which was this—that a goldsmith in whose hands I had trusted it, broke, so I lost £70 of my money, the man’s composition not making above £30 out of his £100. I had a little plate, but not much, and was well enough stocked with clothes and linen.\n\nWith this stock I had the world to begin again; but you are to consider that I was not now the same woman as when I lived at Redriff; for, first of all, I was near twenty years older, and did not look the better for my age, nor for my rambles to Virginia and back again; and though I omitted nothing that might set me out to advantage, except painting, for that I never stooped to, and had pride enough to think I did not want it, yet there would always be some difference seen between five-and-twenty and two-and-forty.\n\nI cast about innumerable ways for my future state of life, and began to consider very seriously what I should do, but nothing offered. I took care to make the world take me for something more than I was, and had it given out that I was a fortune, and that my estate was in my own hands; the last of which was very true, the first of it was as above. I had no acquaintance, which was one of my worst misfortunes, and the consequence of that was, I had no adviser, at least who could assist and advise together; and above all, I had nobody to whom I could in confidence commit the secret of my circumstances to, and could depend upon for their secrecy and fidelity; and I found by experience, that to be friendless is the worst condition, next to being in want that a woman can be reduced to: I say a woman, because ’tis evident men can be their own advisers, and their own directors, and know how to work themselves out of difficulties and into business better than women; but if a woman has no friend to communicate her affairs to, and to advise and assist her, ’tis ten to one but she is undone; nay, and the more money she has, the more danger she is in of being wronged and deceived; and this was my case in the affair of the £100 which I left in the hands of the goldsmith, as above, whose credit, it seems, was upon the ebb before, but I, that had no knowledge of things and nobody to consult with, knew nothing of it, and so lost my money.\n\nIn the next place, when a woman is thus left desolate and void of counsel, she is just like a bag of money or a jewel dropped on the highway, which is a prey to the next comer; if a man of virtue and upright principles happens to find it, he will have it cried, and the owner may come to hear of it again; but how many times shall such a thing fall into hands that will make no scruple of seizing it for their own, to once that it shall come into good hands?\n\nThis was evidently my case, for I was now a loose, unguided creature, and had no help, no assistance, no guide for my conduct; I knew what I aimed at and what I wanted, but knew nothing how to pursue the end by direct means. I wanted to be placed in a settled state of living, and had I happened to meet with a sober, good husband, I should have been as faithful and true a wife to him as virtue itself could have formed. If I had been otherwise, the vice came in always at the door of necessity, not at the door of inclination; and I understood too well, by the want of it, what the value of a settled life was, to do anything to forfeit the felicity of it; nay, I should have made the better wife for all the difficulties I had passed through, by a great deal; nor did I in any of the time that I had been a wife give my husbands the least uneasiness on account of my behaviour.\n\nBut all this was nothing; I found no encouraging prospect. I waited; I lived regularly, and with as much frugality as became my circumstances, but nothing offered, nothing presented, and the main stock wasted apace. What to do I knew not; the terror of approaching poverty lay hard upon my spirits. I had some money, but where to place it I knew not, nor would the interest of it maintain me, at least not in London.\n\nAt length a new scene opened. There was in the house where I lodged a north-country woman that went for a gentlewoman, and nothing was more frequent in her discourse than her account of the cheapness of provisions, and the easy way of living in her country; how plentiful and how cheap everything was, what good company they kept, and the like; till at last I told her she almost tempted me to go and live in her country; for I that was a widow, though I had sufficient to live on, yet had no way of increasing it; and that I found I could not live here under £100 a year, unless I kept no company, no servant, made no appearance, and buried myself in privacy, as if I was obliged to it by necessity.\n\nI should have observed, that she was always made to believe, as everybody else was, that I was a great fortune, or at least that I had three or four thousand pounds, if not more, and all in my own hands; and she was mighty sweet upon me when she thought me inclined in the least to go into her country. She said she had a sister lived near Liverpool, that her brother was a considerable gentleman there, and had a great estate also in Ireland; that she would go down there in about two months, and if I would give her my company thither, I should be as welcome as herself for a month or more as I pleased, till I should see how I liked the country; and if I thought fit to live there, she would undertake they would take care, though they did not entertain lodgers themselves, they would recommend me to some agreeable family, where I should be placed to my content.\n\nIf this woman had known my real circumstances, she would never have laid so many snares, and taken so many weary steps to catch a poor desolate creature that was good for little when it was caught; and indeed I, whose case was almost desperate, and thought I could not be much worse, was not very anxious about what might befall me, provided they did me no personal injury; so I suffered myself, though not without a great deal of invitation and great professions of sincere friendship and real kindness—I say, I suffered myself to be prevailed upon to go with her, and accordingly I packed up my baggage, and put myself in a posture for a journey, though I did not absolutely know whither I was to go.\n\nAnd now I found myself in great distress; what little I had in the world was all in money, except as before, a little plate, some linen, and my clothes; as for my household stuff, I had little or none, for I had lived always in lodgings; but I had not one friend in the world with whom to trust that little I had, or to direct me how to dispose of it, and this perplexed me night and day. I thought of the bank, and of the other companies in London, but I had no friend to commit the management of it to, and keep and carry about with me bank bills, tallies, orders, and such things, I looked upon at as unsafe; that if they were lost, my money was lost, and then I was undone; and, on the other hand, I might be robbed and perhaps murdered in a strange place for them. This perplexed me strangely, and what to do I knew not.\n\nIt came in my thoughts one morning that I would go to the bank myself, where I had often been to receive the interest of some bills I had, which had interest payable on them, and where I had found a clerk, to whom I applied myself, very honest and just to me, and particularly so fair one time that when I had mistold my money, and taken less than my due, and was coming away, he set me to rights and gave me the rest, which he might have put into his own pocket.\n\nI went to him and represented my case very plainly, and asked if he would trouble himself to be my adviser, who was a poor friendless widow, and knew not what to do. He told me, if I desired his opinion of anything within the reach of his business, he would do his endeavour that I should not be wronged, but that he would also help me to a good sober person who was a grave man of his acquaintance, who was a clerk in such business too, though not in their house, whose judgment was good, and whose honesty I might depend upon. “For,” added he, “I will answer for him, and for every step he takes; if he wrongs you, madam, of one farthing, it shall lie at my door, I will make it good; and he delights to assist people in such cases—he does it as an act of charity.”\n\nI was a little at a stand in this discourse; but after some pause I told him I had rather have depended upon him, because I had found him honest, but if that could not be, I would take his recommendation sooner than any one’s else. “I dare say, madam,” says he, “that you will be as well satisfied with my friend as with me, and he is thoroughly able to assist you, which I am not.” It seems he had his hands full of the business of the bank, and had engaged to meddle with no other business than that of his office, which I heard afterwards, but did not understand then. He added, that his friend should take nothing of me for his advice or assistance, and this indeed encouraged me very much.\n\nHe appointed the same evening, after the bank was shut and business over, for me to meet him and his friend. And indeed as soon as I saw his friend, and he began but to talk of the affair, I was fully satisfied that I had a very honest man to deal with; his countenance spoke it, and his character, as I heard afterwards, was everywhere so good, that I had no room for any more doubts upon me.\n\nAfter the first meeting, in which I only said what I had said before, we parted, and he appointed me to come the next day to him, telling me I might in the meantime satisfy myself of him by inquiry, which, however, I knew not how well to do, having no acquaintance myself.\n\nAccordingly I met him the next day, when I entered more freely with him into my case. I told him my circumstances at large: that I was a widow come over from America, perfectly desolate and friendless; that I had a little money, and but a little, and was almost distracted for fear of losing it, having no friend in the world to trust with the management of it; that I was going into the north of England to live cheap, that my stock might not waste; that I would willingly lodge my money in the bank, but that I durst not carry the bills about me, and the like, as above; and how to correspond about it, or with whom, I knew not.\n\nHe told me I might lodge the money in the bank as an account, and its being entered into the books would entitle me to the money at any time, and if I was in the north I might draw bills on the cashier and receive it when I would; but that then it would be esteemed as running cash, and the bank would give no interest for it; that I might buy stock with it, and so it would lie in store for me, but that then if I wanted to dispose if it, I must come up to town on purpose to transfer it, and even it would be with some difficulty I should receive the half-yearly dividend, unless I was here in person, or had some friend I could trust with having the stock in his name to do it for me, and that would have the same difficulty in it as before; and with that he looked hard at me and smiled a little. At last, says he, “Why do you not get a head steward, madam, that may take you and your money together into keeping, and then you would have the trouble taken off your hands?” “Ay, sir, and the money too, it may be,” said I; “for truly I find the hazard that way is as much as ’tis t’other way”; but I remember I said secretly to myself, “I wish you would ask me the question fairly, I would consider very seriously on it before I said No.”\n\nHe went on a good way with me, and I thought once or twice he was in earnest, but to my real affliction, I found at last he had a wife; but when he owned he had a wife he shook his head, and said with some concern, that indeed he had a wife, and no wife. I began to think he had been in the condition of my late lover, and that his wife had been distempered or lunatic, or some such thing. However, we had not much more discourse at that time, but he told me he was in too much hurry of business then, but that if I would come home to his house after their business was over, he would by that time consider what might be done for me, to put my affairs in a posture of security. I told him I would come, and desired to know where he lived. He gave me a direction in writing, and when he gave it me he read it to me, and said, “There ’tis, madam, if you dare trust yourself with me.” “Yes, sir,” said I, “I believe I may venture to trust you with myself, for you have a wife, you say, and I don’t want a husband; besides, I dare trust you with my money, which is all I have in the world, and if that were gone, I may trust myself anywhere.”\n\nHe said some things in jest that were very handsome and mannerly, and would have pleased me very well if they had been in earnest; but that passed over, I took the directions, and appointed to attend him at his house at seven o’clock the same evening.\n\nWhen I came he made several proposals for my placing my money in the bank, in order to my having interest for it; but still some difficulty or other came in the way, which he objected as not safe; and I found such a sincere disinterested honesty in him, that I began to muse with myself, that I had certainly found the honest man I wanted, and that I could never put myself into better hands; so I told him with a great deal of frankness that I had never met with a man or woman yet that I could trust, or in whom I could think myself safe, but that I saw he was so disinterestedly concerned for my safety, that I said I would freely trust him with the management of that little I had, if he would accept to be steward for a poor widow that could give him no salary.\n\nHe smiled and, standing up, with great respect saluted me. He told me he could not but take it very kindly that I had so good an opinion of him; that he would not deceive me, that he would do anything in his power to serve me, and expect no salary; but that he could not by any means accept of a trust, that it might bring him to be suspected of self-interest, and that if I should die he might have disputes with my executors, which he should be very loth to encumber himself with.\n\nI told him if those were all his objections I would soon remove them, and convince him that there was not the least room for any difficulty; for that, first, as for suspecting him, if ever I should do it, now is the time to suspect him, and not put the trust into his hands, and whenever I did suspect him, he could but throw it up then and refuse to go any further. Then, as to executors, I assured him I had no heirs, nor any relations in England, and I would have neither heirs nor executors but himself, unless I should alter my condition before I died, and then his trust and trouble should cease together, which, however, I had no prospect of yet; but I told him if I died as I was, it should be all his own, and he would deserve it by being so faithful to me as I was satisfied he would be.\n\nHe changed his countenance at this discourse, and asked me how I came to have so much good-will for him; and, looking very much pleased, said he might very lawfully wish he was a single man for my sake. I smiled, and told him as he was not, my offer could have no design upon him in it, and to wish, as he did, was not to be allowed, ’twas criminal to his wife.\n\nHe told me I was wrong. “For,” says he, “madam, as I said before, I have a wife and no wife, and ’twould be no sin to me to wish her hanged, if that were all.” “I know nothing of your circumstances that way, sir,” said I; “but it cannot be innocent to wish your wife dead.” “I tell you,” says he again, “she is a wife and no wife; you don’t know what I am, or what she is.”\n\n“That’s true,” said I; “sir, I do not know what you are, but I believe you to be an honest man, and that’s the cause of all my confidence in you.”\n\n“Well, well,” says he, “and so I am, I hope, too. But I am something else too, madam; for,” says he, “to be plain with you, I am a cuckold, and she is a whore.” He spoke it in a kind of jest, but it was with such an awkward smile, that I perceived it was what struck very close to him, and he looked dismally when he said it.\n\n“That alters the case indeed, sir,” said I, “as to that part you were speaking of; but a cuckold, you know, may be an honest man; it does not alter that case at all. Besides, I think,” said I, “since your wife is so dishonest to you, you are too honest to her to own her for your wife; but that,” said I, “is what I have nothing to do with.”\n\n“Nay,” says he, “I do not think to clear my hands of her; for, to be plain with you, madam,” added he, “I am no contented cuckold neither: on the other hand, I assure you it provokes me the highest degree, but I can’t help myself; she that will be a whore, will be a whore.”\n\nI waived the discourse and began to talk of my business; but I found he could not have done with it, so I let him alone, and he went on to tell me all the circumstances of his case, too long to relate here; particularly, that having been out of England some time before he came to the post he was in, she had had two children in the meantime by an officer of the army; and that when he came to England and, upon her submission, took her again, and maintained her very well, yet she ran away from him with a linen-draper’s apprentice, robbed him of what she could come at, and continued to live from him still. “So that, madam,” says he, “she is a whore not by necessity, which is the common bait of your sex, but by inclination, and for the sake of the vice.”\n\nWell, I pitied him, and wished him well rid of her, and still would have talked of my business, but it would not do. At last he looks steadily at me. “Look you, madam,” says he, “you came to ask advice of me, and I will serve you as faithfully as if you were my own sister; but I must turn the tables, since you oblige me to do it, and are so friendly to me, and I think I must ask advice of you. Tell me, what must a poor abused fellow do with a whore? What can I do to do myself justice upon her?”\n\n“Alas! sir,” says I, “’tis a case too nice for me to advise in, but it seems she has run away from you, so you are rid of her fairly; what can you desire more?” “Ay, she is gone indeed,” said he, “but I am not clear of her for all that.”\n\n“That’s true,” says I; “she may indeed run you into debt, but the law has furnished you with methods to prevent that also; you may cry her down, as they call it.”\n\n“No, no,” says he, “that is not the case neither; I have taken care of all that; ’tis not that part that I speak of, but I would be rid of her so that I might marry again.”\n\n“Well, sir,” says I, “then you must divorce her. If you can prove what you say, you may certainly get that done, and then, I suppose, you are free.”\n\n“That’s very tedious and expensive,” says he.\n\n“Why,” says I, “if you can get any woman you like to take your word, I suppose your wife would not dispute the liberty with you that she takes herself.”\n\n“Ay,” says he, “but ’twould be hard to bring an honest woman to do that; and for the other sort,” says he, “I have had enough of her to meddle with any more whores.”\n\nIt occurred to me presently, “I would have taken your word with all my heart, if you had but asked me the question”; but that was to myself. To him I replied, “Why, you shut the door against any honest woman accepting you, for you condemn all that should venture upon you at once, and conclude, that really a woman that takes you now can’t be honest.”\n\n“Why,” says he, “I wish you would satisfy me that an honest woman would take me; I’d venture it”; and then turns short upon me, “Will you take me, madam?”\n\n“That’s not a fair question,” says I, “after what you have said; however, lest you should think I wait only for a recantation of it, I shall answer you plainly, No, not I; my business is of another kind with you, and I did not expect you would have turned my serious application to you, in my own distracted case, into a comedy.”\n\n“Why, madam,” says he, “my case is as distracted as yours can be, and I stand in as much need of advice as you do, for I think if I have not relief somewhere, I shall be made myself, and I know not what course to take, I protest to you.”\n\n“Why, sir,” says I, “’tis easy to give advice in your case, much easier than it is in mine.” “Speak then,” says he, “I beg of you, for now you encourage me.”\n\n“Why,” says I, “if your case is so plain as you say it is, you may be legally divorced, and then you may find honest women enough to ask the question of fairly; the sex is not so scarce that you can want a wife.”\n\n“Well, then,” said he, “I am in earnest; I’ll take your advice; but shall I ask you one question seriously beforehand?”\n\n“Any question,” said I, “but that you did before.”\n\n“No, that answer will not do,” said he, “for, in short, that is the question I shall ask.”\n\n“You may ask what questions you please, but you have my answer to that already,” said I. “Besides, sir,” said I, “can you think so ill of me as that I would give any answer to such a question beforehand? Can any woman alive believe you in earnest, or think you design anything but to banter her?”\n\n“Well, well,” says he, “I do not banter you, I am in earnest; consider of it.”\n\n“But, sir,” says I, a little gravely, “I came to you about my own business; I beg of you to let me know, what you will advise me to do?”\n\n“I will be prepared,” says he, “against you come again.”\n\n“Nay,” says I, “you have forbid my coming any more.”\n\n“Why so?” said he, and looked a little surprised.\n\n“Because,” said I, “you can’t expect I should visit you on the account you talk of.”\n\n“Well,” says he, “you shall promise me to come again, however, and I will not say any more of it till I have gotten the divorce, but I desire you will prepare to be better conditioned when that’s done, for you shall be the woman, or I will not be divorced at all; why, I owe it to your unlooked-for kindness, if it were to nothing else, but I have other reasons too.”\n\nHe could not have said anything in the world that pleased me better; however, I knew that the way to secure him was to stand off while the thing was so remote, as it appeared to be, and that it was time enough to accept of it when he was able to perform it; so I said very respectfully to him, it was time enough to consider of these things when he was in a condition to talk of them; in the meantime, I told him, I was going a great way from him, and he would find objects enough to please him better. We broke off here for the present, and he made me promise him to come again the next day, for his resolutions upon my own business, which after some pressing I did; though had he seen farther into me, I wanted no pressing on that account.\n\nI came the next evening, accordingly, and brought my maid with me, to let him see that I kept a maid, but I sent her away as soon as I was gone in. He would have had me let the maid have stayed, but I would not, but ordered her aloud to come for me again about nine o’clock. But he forbade that, and told me he would see me safe home, which, by the way, I was not very well pleased with, supposing he might do that to know where I lived and inquire into my character and circumstances. However, I ventured that, for all that the people there or thereabout knew of me, was to my advantage; and all the character he had of me, after he had inquired, was that I was a woman of fortune, and that I was a very modest, sober body; which, whether true or not in the main, yet you may see how necessary it is for all women who expect anything in the world, to preserve the character of their virtue, even when perhaps they may have sacrificed the thing itself.\n\nI found, and was not a little pleased with it, that he had provided a supper for me. I found also he lived very handsomely, and had a house very handsomely furnished; all of which I was rejoiced at indeed, for I looked upon it as all my own.\n\nWe had now a second conference upon the subject-matter of the last conference. He laid his business very home indeed; he protested his affection to me, and indeed I had no room to doubt it; he declared that it began from the first moment I talked with him, and long before I had mentioned leaving my effects with him. “’Tis no matter when it began,” thought I; “if it will but hold, ’twill be well enough.” He then told me how much the offer I had made of trusting him with my effects, and leaving them to him, had engaged him. “So I intended it should,” thought I, “but then I thought you had been a single man too.” After we had supped, I observed he pressed me very hard to drink two or three glasses of wine, which, however, I declined, but drank one glass or two. He then told me he had a proposal to make to me, which I should promise him I would not take ill if I should not grant it. I told him I hoped he would make no dishonourable proposal to me, especially in his own house, and that if it was such, I desired he would not propose it, that I might not be obliged to offer any resentment to him that did not become the respect I professed for him, and the trust I had placed in him in coming to his house; and begged of him he would give me leave to go away, and accordingly began to put on my gloves and prepare to be gone, though at the same time I no more intended it than he intended to let me.\n\nWell, he importuned me not to talk of going; he assured me he had no dishonourable thing in his thoughts about me, and was very far from offering anything to me that was dishonourable, and if I thought so, he would choose to say no more of it.\n\nThat part I did not relish at all. I told him I was ready to hear anything that he had to say, depending that he would say nothing unworthy of himself, or unfit for me to hear. Upon this, he told me his proposal was this: that I would marry him, though he had not yet obtained the divorce from the whore his wife; and to satisfy me that he meant honourably, he would promise not to desire me to live with him, or go to bed with him till the divorce was obtained. My heart said yes to this offer at first word, but it was necessary to play the hypocrite a little more with him; so I seemed to decline the motion with some warmth, and besides a little condemning the thing as unfair, told him that such a proposal could be of no signification, but to entangle us both in great difficulties; for if he should not at last obtain the divorce, yet we could not dissolve the marriage, neither could we proceed in it; so that if he was disappointed in the divorce, I left him to consider what a condition we should both be in.\n\nIn short, I carried on the argument against this so far, that I convinced him it was not a proposal that had any sense in it. Well, then he went from it to another, and that was, that I would sign and seal a contract with him, conditioning to marry him as soon as the divorce was obtained, and to be void if he could not obtain it.\n\nI told him such a thing was more rational than the other; but as this was the first time that ever I could imagine him weak enough to be in earnest in this affair, I did not use to say Yes at first asking; I would consider of it.\n\nI played with this lover as an angler does with a trout. I found I had him fast on the hook, so I jested with his new proposal, and put him off. I told him he knew little of me, and bade him inquire about me; I let him also go home with me to my lodging, though I would not ask him to go in, for I told him it was not decent.\n\nIn short, I ventured to avoid signing a contract of marriage, and the reason why I did it was because the lady that had invited me so earnestly to go with her into Lancashire insisted so positively upon it, and promised me such great fortunes, and such fine things there, that I was tempted to go and try. “Perhaps,” said I, “I may mend myself very much”; and then I made no scruple in my thoughts of quitting my honest citizen, whom I was not so much in love with as not to leave him for a richer.\n\nIn a word, I avoided a contract; but told him I would go into the north, that he should know where to write to me by the consequence of the business I had entrusted with him; that I would give him a sufficient pledge of my respect for him, for I would leave almost all I had in the world in his hands; and I would thus far give him my word, that as soon as he had sued out a divorce from his first wife, he would send me an account of it, I would come up to London, and that then we would talk seriously of the matter.\n\nIt was a base design I went with, that I must confess, though I was invited thither with a design much worse than mine was, as the sequel will discover. Well, I went with my friend, as I called her, into Lancashire. All the way we went she caressed me with the utmost appearance of a sincere, undissembled affection; treated me, except my coach-hire, all the way; and her brother brought a gentleman’s coach to Warrington to receive us, and we were carried from thence to Liverpool with as much ceremony as I could desire. We were also entertained at a merchant’s house in Liverpool three or four days very handsomely; I forbear to tell his name, because of what followed. Then she told me she would carry me to an uncle’s house of hers, where we should be nobly entertained. She did so; her uncle, as she called him, sent a coach and four horses for us, and we were carried near forty miles I know not whither.\n\nWe came, however, to a gentleman’s seat, where was a numerous family, a large park, extraordinary company indeed, and where she was called cousin. I told her if she had resolved to bring me into such company as this, she should have let me have prepared myself, and have furnished myself with better clothes. The ladies took notice of that, and told me very genteelly they did not value people in their country so much by their clothes as they did in London; that their cousin had fully informed them of my quality, and that I did not want clothes to set me off; in short, they entertained me, not like what I was, but like what they thought I had been, namely, a widow lady of a great fortune.\n\nThe first discovery I made here was, that the family were all Roman Catholics, and the cousin too, whom I called my friend; however, I must say that nobody in the world could behave better to me, and I had all the civility shown me that I could have had if I had been of their opinion. The truth is, I had not so much principle of any kind as to be nice in point of religion, and I presently learned to speak favourably of the Romish Church; particularly, I told them I saw little but the prejudice of education in all the difference that were among Christians about religion, and if it had so happened that my father had been a Roman Catholic, I doubted not but I should have been as well pleased with their religion as my own.\n\nThis obliged them in the highest degree, and as I was besieged day and night with good company and pleasant discourse, so I had two or three old ladies that lay at me upon the subject of religion too. I was so complaisant, that though I would not completely engage, yet I made no scruple to be present at their mass, and to conform to all their gestures as they showed me the pattern, but I would not come too cheap; so that I only in the main encouraged them to expect that I would turn Roman Catholic, if I was instructed in the Catholic doctrine as they called it, and so the matter rested.\n\nI stayed here about six weeks; and then my conductor led me back to a country village, about six miles from Liverpool, where her brother (as she called him) came to visit me in his own chariot, and in a very good figure, with two footmen in a good livery; and the next thing was to make love to me. As it had happened to me, one would think I could not have been cheated, and indeed I thought so myself, having a safe card at home, which I resolved not to quit unless I could mend myself very much. However, in all appearance this brother was a match worth my listening to, and the least his estate was valued at was £1000 a year, but the sister said it was worth £1500 a year, and lay most of it in Ireland.\n\nI that was a great fortune, and passed for such, was above being asked how much my estate was; and my false friend taking it upon a foolish hearsay, had raised it from £500 to £5000, and by the time she came into the country she called it £15,000. The Irishman, for such I understood him to be, was stark mad at this bait; in short, he courted me, made me presents, and ran in debt like a madman for the expenses of his equipage and of his courtship. He had, to give him his due, the appearance of an extraordinary fine gentleman; he was tall, well-shaped, and had an extraordinary address; talked as naturally of his park and his stables, of his horses, his gamekeepers, his woods, his tenants, and his servants, as if we had been in the mansion-house, and I had seen them all about me.\n\nHe never so much as asked me about my fortune or estate, but assured me that when we came to Dublin he would jointure me in £600 a year good land; and that we could enter into a deed of settlement or contract here for the performance of it.\n\nThis was such language indeed as I had not been used to, and I was here beaten out of all my measures; I had a she-devil in my bosom, every hour telling me how great her brother lived. One time she would come for my orders, how I would have my coaches painted, and how lined; and another time what clothes my page should wear; in short, my eyes were dazzled. I had now lost my power of saying No, and, to cut the story short, I consented to be married; but to be the more private, we were carried farther into the country, and married by a Romish clergyman, who I was assured would marry us as effectually as a Church of England parson.\n\nI cannot say but I had some reflections in this affair upon the dishonourable forsaking my faithful citizen, who loved me sincerely, and who was endeavouring to quit himself of a scandalous whore by whom he had been indeed barbarously used, and promised himself infinite happiness in his new choice; which choice was now giving up herself to another in a manner almost as scandalous as hers could be.\n\nBut the glittering shoe of a great estate, and of fine things, which the deceived creature that was now my deceiver represented every hour to my imagination, hurried me away, and gave me no time to think of London, or of anything there, much less of the obligation I had to a person of infinitely more real merit than what was now before me.\n\nBut the thing was done; I was now in the arms of my new spouse, who appeared still the same as before; great even to magnificence, and nothing less than £1000 a year could support the ordinary equipage he appeared in.\n\nAfter we had been married about a month, he began to talk of my going to West Chester in order to embark for Ireland. However, he did not hurry me, for we stayed near three weeks longer, and then he sent to Chester for a coach to meet us at the Black Rock, as they call it, over against Liverpool. Thither we went in a fine boat they call a pinnace, with six oars; his servants, and horses, and baggage going in the ferry-boat. He made his excuse to me that he had no acquaintance in Chester, but he would go before and get some handsome apartment for me at a private house. I asked him how long we should stay at Chester. He said, not at all, any longer than one night or two, but he would immediately hire a coach to go to Holyhead. Then I told him he should by no means give himself the trouble to get private lodgings for one night or two, for that Chester being a great place, I made no doubt but there would be very good inns and accommodation enough; so we lodged at an inn in the West Street, not far from the Cathedral; I forget what sign it was at.\n\nHere my spouse, talking of my going to Ireland, asked me if I had no affairs to settle at London before we went off. I told him No, not of any great consequence, but what might be done as well by letter from Dublin. “Madam,” says he, very respectfully, “I suppose the greatest part of your estate, which my sister tells me is most of it in money in the Bank of England, lies secure enough, but in case it required transferring, or any way altering its property, it might be necessary to go up to London and settle those things before we went over.”\n\nI seemed to look strange at it, and told him I knew not what he meant; that I had no effects in the Bank of England that I knew of; and I hoped he could not say that I had ever told him I had. No, he said, I had not told him so, but his sister had said the greatest part of my estate lay there. “And I only mentioned it, me dear,” said he, “that if there was any occasion to settle it, or order anything about it, we might not be obliged to the hazard and trouble of another voyage back again”; for he added, that he did not care to venture me too much upon the sea.\n\nI was surprised at this talk, and began to consider very seriously what the meaning of it must be; and it presently occurred to me that my friend, who called him brother, had represented me in colours which were not my due; and I thought, since it was come to that pitch, that I would know the bottom of it before I went out of England, and before I should put myself into I knew not whose hands in a strange country.\n\nUpon this I called his sister into my chamber the next morning, and letting her know the discourse her brother and I had been upon the evening before, I conjured her to tell me what she had said to him, and upon what foot it was that she had made this marriage. She owned that she had told him that I was a great fortune, and said that she was told so at London. “Told so!” says I warmly; “did I ever tell you so?” No, she said, it was true I did not tell her so, but I had said several times that what I had was in my own disposal. “I did so,” returned I very quickly and hastily, “but I never told you I had anything called a fortune; no, not that I had £100, or the value of £100, in the world. Any how did it consist with my being a fortune,” said I, “that I should come here into the north of England with you, only upon the account of living cheap?” At these words, which I spoke warm and high, my husband, her brother (as she called him), came into the room, and I desired him to come and sit down, for I had something of moment to say before them both, which it was absolutely necessary he should hear.\n\nHe looked a little disturbed at the assurance with which I seemed to speak it, and came and sat down by me, having first shut the door; upon which I began, for I was very much provoked, and turning myself to him, “I am afraid,” says I, “my dear” (for I spoke with kindness on his side), “that you have a very great abuse put upon you, and an injury done you never to be repaired in your marrying me, which, however, as I have had no hand in it, I desire I may be fairly acquitted of it, and that the blame may lie where it ought to lie, and nowhere else, for I wash my hands of every part of it.”\n\n“What injury can be done me, my dear,” says he, “in marrying you. I hope it is to my honour and advantage every way.” “I will soon explain it to you,” says I, “and I fear you will have no reason to think yourself well used; but I will convince you, my dear,” says I again, “that I have had no hand in it”; and there I stopped a while.\n\nHe looked now scared and wild, and began, I believe, to suspect what followed; however, looking towards me, and saying only, “Go on,” he sat silent, as if to hear what I had more to say; so I went on. “I asked you last night,” said I, speaking to him, “if ever I made any boast to you of my estate, or ever told you I had any estate in the Bank of England or anywhere else, and you owned I had not, as is most true; and I desire you will tell me here, before your sister, if ever I gave you any reason from me to think so, or that ever we had any discourse about it”; and he owned again I had not, but said I had appeared always as a woman of fortune, and he depended on it that I was so, and hoped he was not deceived. “I am not inquiring yet whether you have been deceived or not,” said I; “I fear you have, and I too; but I am clearing myself from the unjust charge of being concerned in deceiving you.\n\n“I have been now asking your sister if ever I told her of any fortune or estate I had, or gave her any particulars of it; and she owns I never did. Any pray, madam,” said I, turning myself to her, “be so just to me, before your brother, to charge me, if you can, if ever I pretended to you that I had an estate; and why, if I had, should I come down into this country with you on purpose to spare that little I had, and live cheap?” She could not deny one word, but said she had been told in London that I had a very great fortune, and that it lay in the Bank of England.\n\n“And now, dear sir,” said I, turning myself to my new spouse again, “be so just to me as to tell me who has abused both you and me so much as to make you believe I was a fortune, and prompt you to court me to this marriage?” He could not speak a word, but pointed to her; and, after some more pause, flew out in the most furious passion that ever I saw a man in my life, cursing her, and calling her all the whores and hard names he could think of; and that she had ruined him, declaring that she had told him I had £15,000, and that she was to have £500 of him for procuring this match for him. He then added, directing his speech to me, that she was none of his sister, but had been his whore for two years before, that she had had £100 of him in part of this bargain, and that he was utterly undone if things were as I said; and in his raving he swore he would let her heart’s blood out immediately, which frightened her and me too. She cried, said she had been told so in the house where I lodged. But this aggravated him more than before, that she should put so far upon him, and run things such a length upon no other authority than a hearsay; and then, turning to me again, said very honestly, he was afraid we were both undone. “For, to be plain, my dear, I have no estate,” says he; “what little I had, this devil has made me run out in waiting on you and putting me into this equipage.” She took the opportunity of his being earnest in talking with me, and got out of the room, and I never saw her more.\n\nI was confounded now as much as he, and knew not what to say. I thought many ways that I had the worst of it, but his saying he was undone, and that he had no estate neither, put me into a mere distraction. “Why,” says I to him, “this has been a hellish juggle, for we are married here upon the foot of a double fraud; you are undone by the disappointment, it seems; and if I had had a fortune I had been cheated too, for you say you have nothing.”\n\n“You would indeed have been cheated, my dear,” says he, “but you would not have been undone, for £15,000 would have maintained us both very handsomely in this country; and I assure you,” added he, “I had resolved to have dedicated every groat of it to you; I would not have wronged you of a shilling, and the rest I would have made up in my affection to you, and tenderness of you, as long as I lived.”\n\nThis was very honest indeed, and I really believe he spoke as he intended, and that he was a man that was as well qualified to make me happy, as to his temper and behaviour, as any man ever was; but his having no estate, and being run into debt on this ridiculous account in the country, made all the prospect dismal and dreadful, and I knew not what to say, or what to think of myself.\n\nI told him it was very unhappy that so much love, and so much good nature as I discovered in him, should be thus precipitated into misery; that I saw nothing before us but ruin; for as to me, it was my unhappiness that what little I had was not able to relieve us week, and with that I pulled out a bank bill of £20 and eleven guineas, which I told him I had saved out of my little income, and that by the account that creature had given me of the way of living in that country, I expected it would maintain me three or four years; that if it was taken from me, I was left destitute, and he knew what the condition of a woman among strangers must be, if she had no money in her pocket; however, I told him, if he would take it, there it was.\n\nHe told me with a great concern, and I thought I saw tears stand in his eyes, that he would not touch it; that he abhorred the thoughts of stripping me and make me miserable; that, on the contrary, he had fifty guineas left, which was all he had in the world, and he pulled it out and threw it down on the table, bidding me take it, though he were to starve for want of it.\n\nI returned, with the same concern for him, that I could not bear to hear him talk so; that, on the contrary, if he could propose any probable method of living, I would do anything that became me on my part, and that I would live as close and as narrow as he could desire.\n\nHe begged of me to talk no more at that rate, for it would make him distracted; he said he was bred a gentleman, though he was reduced to a low fortune, and that there was but one way left which he could think of, and that would not do, unless I could answer him one question, which, however, he said he would not press me to. I told him I would answer it honestly; whether it would be to his satisfaction or not, that I could not tell.\n\n“Why, then, my dear, tell me plainly,” says he, “will the little you have keep us together in any figure, or in any station or place, or will it not?”\n\nIt was my happiness hitherto that I had not discovered myself or my circumstances at all—no, not so much as my name; and seeing these was nothing to be expected from him, however good-humoured and however honest he seemed to be, but to live on what I knew would soon be wasted, I resolved to conceal everything but the bank bill and the eleven guineas which I had owned; and I would have been very glad to have lost that and have been set down where he took me up. I had indeed another bank bill about me of £30, which was the whole of what I brought with me, as well to subsist on in the country, as not knowing what might offer; because this creature, the go-between that had thus betrayed us both, had made me believe strange things of my marrying to my advantage in the country, and I was not willing to be without money, whatever might happen. This bill I concealed, and that made me the freer of the rest, in consideration of his circumstances, for I really pitied him heartily.\n\nBut to return to his question, I told him I never willingly deceived him, and I never would. I was very sorry to tell him that the little I had would not subsist us; that it was not sufficient to subsist me alone in the south country, and that this was the reason that made me put myself into the hands of that woman who called him brother, she having assured me that I might board very handsomely at a town called Manchester, where I had not yet been, for about £6 a year; and my whole income not being about £15 a year, I thought I might live easy upon it, and wait for better things.\n\nHe shook his head and remained silent, and a very melancholy evening we had; however, we supped together, and lay together that night, and when we had almost supped he looked a little better and more cheerful, and called for a bottle of wine. “Come, my dear,” says he, “though the case is bad, it is to no purpose to be dejected. Come, be as easy as you can; I will endeavour to find out some way or other to live; if you can but subsist yourself, that is better than nothing. I must try the world again; a man ought to think like a man; to be discouraged is to yield to the misfortune.” With this he filled a glass and drank to me, holding my hand and pressing it hard in his hand all the while the wine went down, and protesting afterwards his main concern was for me.\n\nIt was really a true, gallant spirit he was of, and it was the more grievous to me. ’Tis something of relief even to be undone by a man of honour, rather than by a scoundrel; but here the greatest disappointment was on his side, for he had really spent a great deal of money, deluded by this madam the procuress; and it was very remarkable on what poor terms he proceeded. First the baseness of the creature herself is to be observed, who, for the getting £100 herself, could be content to let him spend three or four more, though perhaps it was all he had in the world, and more than all; when she had not the least ground, more than a little tea-table chat, to say that I had any estate, or was a fortune, or the like. It is true the design of deluding a woman of fortune, if I had been so, was base enough; the putting the face of great things upon poor circumstances was a fraud, and bad enough; but the case a little differed too, and that in his favour, for he was not a rake that made a trade to delude women, and, as some have done, get six or seven fortunes after one another, and then rifle and run away from them; but he was really a gentleman, unfortunate and low, but had lived well; and though, if I had had a fortune, I should have been enraged at the slut for betraying me, yet really for the man, a fortune would not have been ill bestowed on him, for he was a lovely person indeed, of generous principles, good sense, and of abundance of good-humour.\n\nWe had a great deal of close conversation that night, for we neither of us slept much; he was as penitent for having put all those cheats upon me as if it had been felony, and that he was going to execution; he offered me again every shilling of the money he had about him, and said he would go into the army and seek the world for more.\n\nI asked him why he would be so unkind to carry me into Ireland, when I might suppose he could not have subsisted me there. He took me in his arms. “My dear,” said he, “depend upon it, I never designed to go to Ireland at all, much less to have carried you thither, but came hither to be out of the observation of the people, who had heard what I pretended to, and withal, that nobody might ask me for money before I was furnished to supply them.”\n\n“But where, then,” said I, “were we to have gone next?”\n\n“Why, my dear,” said he, “I’ll confess the whole scheme to you as I had laid it; I purposed here to ask you something about your estate, as you see I did, and when you, as I expected you would, had entered into some account with me of the particulars, I would have made an excuse to you to have put off our voyage to Ireland for some time, and to have gone first towards London.\n\n“Then, my dear,” said he, “I resolved to have confessed all the circumstances of my own affairs to you, and let you know I had indeed made use of these artifices to obtain your consent to marry me, but had now nothing to do but ask to your pardon, and to tell you how abundantly, as I have said above, I would endeavour to make you forget what was past, by the felicity of the days to come.”\n\n“Truly,” said I to him, “I find you would soon have conquered me; and it is my affliction now, that I am not in a condition to let you see how easily I should have been reconciled to you, and have passed by all the tricks you had put upon me, in recompense of so much good-humour. But, my dear,” said I, “what can we do now? We are both undone, and what better are we for our being reconciled together, seeing we have nothing to live on?”\n\nWe proposed a great many things, but nothing could offer where there was nothing to begin with. He begged me at last to talk no more of it, for, he said, I would break his heart; so we talked of other things a little, till at last he took a husband’s leave of me, and so we went to sleep.\n\nHe rose before me in the morning; and indeed, having lain awake almost all night, I was very sleepy, and lay till near eleven o’clock. In this time he took his horses and three servants, and all his linen and baggage, and away he went, leaving a short but moving letter for me on the table, as follows:—\n\n“MY DEAR—I am a dog; I have abused you; but I have been drawn into do it by a base creature, contrary to my principle and the general practice of my life. Forgive me, my dear! I ask your pardon with the greatest sincerity; I am the most miserable of men, in having deluded you. I have been so happy to possess you, and now am so wretched as to be forced to fly from you. Forgive me, my dear; once more I say, forgive me! I am not able to see you ruined by me, and myself unable to support you. Our marriage is nothing; I shall never be able to see you again; I here discharge you from it; if you can marry to your advantage, do not decline it on my account; I here swear to you on my faith, and on the word of a man of honour, I will never disturb your repose if I should know of it, which, however, is not likely. On the other hand, if you should not marry, and if good fortune should befall me, it shall be all yours, wherever you are.\n\nI have put some of the stock of money I have left into your pocket; take places for yourself and your maid in the stage-coach, and go for London; I hope it will bear your charges thither, without breaking into your own. Again I sincerely ask your pardon, and will do so as often as I shall ever think of you.\n\nAdieu, my dear, for ever, I am, your most affectionately, J.E.”\n\nNothing that ever befell me in my life sank so deep into my heart as this farewell. I reproached him a thousand times in my thoughts for leaving me, for I would have gone with him through the world, if I had begged my bread. I felt in my pocket, and there found ten guineas, his gold watch, and two little rings, one a small diamond ring worth only about £6, and the other a plain gold ring.\n\nI sat me down and looked upon these things two hours together, and scarce spoke a word, till my maid interrupted me by telling me my dinner was ready. I ate but little, and after dinner I fell into a vehement fit of crying, every now and then calling him by his name, which was James. “O Jemmy!” said I, “come back, come back. I’ll give you all I have; I’ll beg, I’ll starve with you.” And thus I ran raving about the room several times, and then sat down between whiles, and then walking about again, called upon him to come back, and then cried again; and thus I passed the afternoon, till about seven o’clock, when it was near dusk, in the evening, being August, when, to my unspeakable surprise, he comes back into the inn, but without a servant, and comes directly up into my chamber.\n\nI was in the greatest confusion imaginable, and so was he too. I could not imagine what should be the occasion of it, and began to be at odds with myself whether to be glad or sorry; but my affection biassed all the rest, and it was impossible to conceal my joy, which was too great for smiles, for it burst out into tears. He was no sooner entered the room but he ran to me and took me in his arms, holding me fast, and almost stopping my breath with his kisses, but spoke not a word. At length I began. “My dear,” said I, “how could you go away from me?” to which he gave no answer, for it was impossible for him to speak.\n\nWhen our ecstasies were a little over, he told me he was gone about fifteen miles, but it was not in his power to go any farther without coming back to see me again, and to take his leave of me once more.\n\nI told him how I had passed my time, and how loud I had called him to come back again. He told me he heard me very plain upon Delamere Forest, at a place about twelve miles off. I smiled. “Nay,” says he, “do not think I am in jest, for if ever I heard your voice in my life, I heard you call me aloud, and sometimes I thought I saw you running after me.” “Why,” said I, “what did I say?”—for I had not named the words to him. “You called aloud,” says he, “and said, O Jemmy! O Jemmy! come back, come back.”\n\nI laughed at him. “My dear,” says he, “do not laugh, for, depend upon it, I heard your voice as plain as you hear mine now; if you please, I’ll go before a magistrate and make oath of it.” I then began to be amazed and surprised, and indeed frightened, and told him what I had really done, and how I had called after him, as above.\n\nWhen we had amused ourselves a while about this, I said to him: “Well, you shall go away from me no more; I’ll go all over the world with you rather.” He told me it would be a very difficult thing for him to leave me, but since it must be, he hoped I would make it as easy to me as I could; but as for him, it would be his destruction that he foresaw.\nHowever, he told me that he considered he had left me to travel to London alone, which was too long a journey; and that as he might as well go that way as any way else, he was resolved to see me safe thither, or near it; and if he did go away then without taking his leave, I should not take it ill of him; and this he made me promise.\n\nHe told me how he had dismissed his three servants, sold their horses, and sent the fellows away to seek their fortunes, and all in a little time, at a town on the road, I know not where. “And,” says he, “it cost me some tears all alone by myself, to think how much happier they were than their master, for they could go to the next gentleman’s house to see for a service, whereas,” said he, “I knew not wither to go, or what to do with myself.”\n\nI told him I was so completely miserable in parting with him, that I could not be worse; and that now he was come again, I would not go from him, if he would take me with him, let him go whither he would, or do what he would. And in the meantime I agreed that we would go together to London; but I could not be brought to consent he should go away at last and not take his leave of me, as he proposed to do; but told him, jesting, that if he did, I would call him back again as loud as I did before. Then I pulled out his watch and gave it him back, and his two rings, and his ten guineas; but he would not take them, which made me very much suspect that he resolved to go off upon the road and leave me.\n\nThe truth is, the circumstances he was in, the passionate expressions of his letter, the kind, gentlemanly treatment I had from him in all the affair, with the concern he showed for me in it, his manner of parting with that large share which he gave me of his little stock left—all these had joined to make such impressions on me, that I really loved him most tenderly, and could not bear the thoughts of parting with him.\n\nTwo days after this we quitted Chester, I in the stage-coach, and he on horseback. I dismissed my maid at Chester. He was very much against my being without a maid, but she being a servant hired in the country, and I resolving to keep no servant at London, I told him it would have been barbarous to have taken the poor wench and have turned her away as soon as I came to town; and it would also have been a needless charge on the road, so I satisfied him, and he was easy enough on the score.\n\nHe came with me as far as Dunstable, within thirty miles of London, and then he told me fate and his own misfortunes obliged him to leave me, and that it was not convenient for him to go to London, for reasons which it was of no value to me to know, and I saw him preparing to go. The stage-coach we were in did not usually stop at Dunstable, but I desiring it but for a quarter of an hour, they were content to stand at an inn-door a while, and we went into the house.\n\nBeing in the inn, I told him I had but one favour more to ask of him, and that was, that since he could not go any farther, he would give me leave to stay a week or two in the town with him, that we might in that time think of something to prevent such a ruinous thing to us both, as a final separation would be; and that I had something of moment to offer him, that I had never said yet, and which perhaps he might find practicable to our mutual advantage.\n\nThis was too reasonable a proposal to be denied, so he called the landlady of the house, and told her his wife was taken ill, and so ill that she could not think of going any farther in the stage-coach, which had tired her almost to death, and asked if she could not get us a lodging for two or three days in a private house, where I might rest me a little, for the journey had been too much for me. The landlady, a good sort of woman, well-bred and very obliging, came immediately to see me; told me she had two or three very good rooms in a part of the house quite out of the noise, and if I saw them, she did not doubt but I would like them, and I should have one of her maids, that should do nothing else but be appointed to wait on me. This was so very kind, that I could not but accept of it, and thank her; so I went to look on the rooms and liked them very well, and indeed they were extraordinarily furnished, and very pleasant lodgings; so we paid the stage-coach, took out our baggage, and resolved to stay here a while.\n\nHere I told him I would live with him now till all my money was spent, but would not let him spend a shilling of his own. We had some kind squabble about that, but I told him it was the last time I was like to enjoy his company, and I desired he would let me be master in that thing only, and he should govern in everything else; so he acquiesced.\n\nHere one evening, taking a walk into the fields, I told him I would now make the proposal to him I had told him of; accordingly I related to him how I had lived in Virginia, that I had a mother I believed was alive there still, though my husband was dead some years. I told him that had not my effects miscarried, which, by the way, I magnified pretty much, I might have been fortune good enough to him to have kept us from being parted in this manner. Then I entered into the manner of peoples going over to those countries to settle, how they had a quantity of land given them by the Constitution of the place; and if not, that it might be purchased at so easy a rate this it was not worth naming.\n\nI then gave him a full and distinct account of the nature of planting; how with carrying over but two or three hundred pounds value in English goods, with some servants and tools, a man of application would presently lay a foundation for a family, and in a very few years be certain to raise an estate.\n\nI let him into the nature of the product of the earth; how the ground was cured and prepared, and what the usual increase of it was; and demonstrated to him, that in a very few years, with such a beginning, we should be as certain of being rich as we were now certain of being poor.\n\nHe was surprised at my discourse; for we made it the whole subject of our conversation for near a week together, in which time I laid it down in black and white, as we say, that it was morally impossible, with a supposition of any reasonable good conduct, but that we must thrive there and do very well.\n\nThen I told him what measures I would take to raise such a sum of £300 or thereabouts; and I argued with him how good a method it would be to put an end to our misfortunes and restore our circumstances in the world, to what we had both expected; and I added, that after seven years, if we lived, we might be in a posture to leave our plantations in good hands, and come over again and receive the income of it, and live here and enjoy it; and I gave him examples of some that had done so, and lived now in very good circumstances in London.\n\nIn short, I pressed him so to it, that he almost agreed to it, but still something or other broke it off again; till at last he turned the tables, and he began to talk almost to the same purpose of Ireland.\n\nHe told me that a man that could confine himself to country life, and that could find but stock to enter upon any land, should have farms there for £50 a year, as good as were here let for £200 a year; that the produce was such, and so rich the land, that if much was not laid up, we were sure to live as handsomely upon it as a gentleman of £3000 a year could do in England and that he had laid a scheme to leave me in London, and go over and try; and if he found he could lay a handsome foundation of living suitable to the respect he had for me, as he doubted not he should do, he would come over and fetch me.\n\nI was dreadfully afraid that upon such a proposal he would have taken me at my word, viz. to sell my little income as I called it, and turn it into money, and let him carry it over into Ireland and try his experiment with it; but he was too just to desire it, or to have accepted it if I had offered it; and he anticipated me in that, for he added, that he would go and try his fortune that way, and if he found he could do anything at it to live, then, by adding mine to it when I went over, we should live like ourselves; but that he would not hazard a shilling of mine till he had made the experiment with a little, and he assured me that if he found nothing to be done in Ireland, he would then come to me and join in my project for Virginia.\n\nHe was so earnest upon his project being to be tried first, that I could not withstand him; however, he promised to let me hear from him in a very little time after his arriving there, to let me know whether his prospect answered his design, that if there was not a possibility of success, I might take the occasion to prepare for our other voyage, and then, he assured me, he would go with me to America with all his heart.\n\nI could bring him to nothing further than this. However, those consultations entertained us near a month, during which I enjoyed his company, which indeed was the most entertaining that ever I met in my life before. In this time he let me into the whole story of his own life, which was indeed surprising, and full of an infinite variety sufficient to fill up a much brighter history, for its adventures and incidents, than any I ever saw in print; but I shall have occasion to say more of him hereafter.\n\nWe parted at last, though with the utmost reluctance on my side; and indeed he took his leave very unwillingly too, but necessity obliged him, for his reasons were very good why he would not come to London, as I understood more fully some time afterwards.\n\nI gave him a direction how to write to me, though still I reserved the grand secret, and never broke my resolution, which was not to let him ever know my true name, who I was, or where to be found; he likewise let me know how to write a letter to him, so that, he said, he would be sure to receive it.\n\nI came to London the next day after we parted, but did not go directly to my old lodgings; but for another nameless reason took a private lodging in St. John’s Street, or, as it is vulgarly called, St. Jones’s, near Clerkenwell; and here, being perfectly alone, I had leisure to sit down and reflect seriously upon the last seven months’ ramble I had made, for I had been abroad no less. The pleasant hours I had with my last husband I looked back on with an infinite deal of pleasure; but that pleasure was very much lessened when I found some time after that I was really with child.\n\nThis was a perplexing thing, because of the difficulty which was before me where I should get leave to lie in; it being one of the nicest things in the world at that time of day for a woman that was a stranger, and had no friends, to be entertained in that circumstance without security, which, by the way, I had not, neither could I procure any.\n\nI had taken care all this while to preserve a correspondence with my honest friend at the bank, or rather he took care to correspond with me, for he wrote to me once a week; and though I had not spent my money so fast as to want any from him, yet I often wrote also to let him know I was alive. I had left directions in Lancashire, so that I had these letters, which he sent, conveyed to me; and during my recess at St. Jones’s received a very obliging letter from him, assuring me that his process for a divorce from his wife went on with success, though he met with some difficulties in it that he did not expect.\n\nI was not displeased with the news that his process was more tedious than he expected; for though I was in no condition to have him yet, not being so foolish to marry him when I knew myself to be with child by another man, as some I know have ventured to do, yet I was not willing to lose him, and, in a word, resolved to have him if he continued in the same mind, as soon as I was up again; for I saw apparently I should hear no more from my husband; and as he had all along pressed to marry, and had assured me he would not be at all disgusted at it, or ever offer to claim me again, so I made no scruple to resolve to do it if I could, and if my other friend stood to his bargain; and I had a great deal of reason to be assured that he would stand to it, by the letters he wrote to me, which were the kindest and most obliging that could be.\n\nI now grew big, and the people where I lodged perceived it, and began to take notice of it to me, and, as far as civility would allow, intimated that I must think of removing. This put me to extreme perplexity, and I grew very melancholy, for indeed I knew not what course to take. I had money, but no friends, and was like to have a child upon my hands to keep, which was a difficulty I had never had upon me yet, as the particulars of my story hitherto make appear.\n\nIn the course of this affair I fell very ill, and my melancholy really increased my distemper; my illness proved at length to be only an ague, but my apprehensions were really that I should miscarry. I should not say apprehensions, for indeed I would have been glad to miscarry, but I could never be brought to entertain so much as a thought of endeavouring to miscarry, or of taking any thing to make me miscarry; I abhorred, I say, so much as the thought of it.\n\nHowever, speaking of it in the house, the gentlewoman who kept the house proposed to me to send for a midwife. I scrupled it at first, but after some time consented to it, but told her I had no particular acquaintance with any midwife, and so left it to her.\n\nIt seems the mistress of the house was not so great a stranger to such cases as mine was as I thought at first she had been, as will appear presently, and she sent for a midwife of the right sort—that is to say, the right sort for me.\n\nThe woman appeared to be an experienced woman in her business, I mean as a midwife; but she had another calling too, in which she was as expert as most women if not more. My landlady had told her I was very melancholy, and that she believed that had done me harm; and once, before me, said to her, “Mrs. B——” (meaning the midwife), “I believe this lady’s trouble is of a kind that is pretty much in your way, and therefore if you can do anything for her, pray do, for she is a very civil gentlewoman”; and so she went out of the room.\n\nI really did not understand her, but my Mother Midnight began very seriously to explain what she meant, as soon as she was gone. “Madam,” says she, “you seem not to understand what your landlady means; and when you do understand it, you need not let her know at all that you do so.\n\n“She means that you are under some circumstances that may render your lying in difficult to you, and that you are not willing to be exposed. I need say no more, but to tell you, that if you think fit to communicate so much of your case to me, if it be so, as is necessary, for I do not desire to pry into those things, I perhaps may be in a position to help you and to make you perfectly easy, and remove all your dull thoughts upon that subject.”\n\nEvery word this creature said was a cordial to me, and put new life and new spirit into my heart; my blood began to circulate immediately, and I was quite another body; I ate my victuals again, and grew better presently after it. She said a great deal more to the same purpose, and then, having pressed me to be free with her, and promised in the solemnest manner to be secret, she stopped a little, as if waiting to see what impression it made on me, and what I would say.\n\nI was too sensible to the want I was in of such a woman, not to accept her offer; I told her my case was partly as she guessed, and partly not, for I was really married, and had a husband, though he was in such fine circumstances and so remote at that time, as that he could not appear publicly.\n\nShe took me short, and told me that was none of her business; all the ladies that came under her care were married women to her. “Every woman,” she says, “that is with child has a father for it,” and whether that father was a husband or no husband, was no business of hers; her business was to assist me in my present circumstances, whether I had a husband or no. “For, madam,” says she, “to have a husband that cannot appear, is to have no husband in the sense of the case; and, therefore, whether you are a wife or a mistress is all one to me.”\n\nI found presently, that whether I was a whore or a wife, I was to pass for a whore here, so I let that go. I told her it was true, as she said, but that, however, if I must tell her my case, I must tell it her as it was; so I related it to her as short as I could, and I concluded it to her thus. “I trouble you with all this, madam,” said I, “not that, as you said before, it is much to the purpose in your affair, but this is to the purpose, namely, that I am not in any pain about being seen, or being public or concealed, for ’tis perfectly indifferent to me; but my difficulty is, that I have no acquaintance in this part of the nation.”\n\n“I understand you, madam” says she; “you have no security to bring to prevent the parish impertinences usual in such cases, and perhaps,” says she, “do not know very well how to dispose of the child when it comes.” “The last,” says I, “is not so much my concern as the first.” “Well, madam,” answered the midwife, “dare you put yourself into my hands? I live in such a place; though I do not inquire after you, you may inquire after me. My name is B——; I live in such a street”—naming the street—“at the sign of the Cradle. My profession is a midwife, and I have many ladies that come to my house to lie in. I have given security to the parish in general terms to secure them from any charge from whatsoever shall come into the world under my roof. I have but one question to ask in the whole affair, madam,” says she, “and if that be answered you shall be entirely easy for all the rest.”\n\nI presently understood what she meant, and told her, “Madam, I believe I understand you. I thank God, though I want friends in this part of the world, I do not want money, so far as may be necessary, though I do not abound in that neither”: this I added because I would not make her expect great things. “Well, madam,” says she, “that is the thing indeed, without which nothing can be done in these cases; and yet,” says she, “you shall see that I will not impose upon you, or offer anything that is unkind to you, and if you desire it, you shall know everything beforehand, that you may suit yourself to the occasion, and be neither costly or sparing as you see fit.”\n\nI told her she seemed to be so perfectly sensible of my condition, that I had nothing to ask of her but this, that as I had told her that I had money sufficient, but not a great quantity, she would order it so that I might be at as little superfluous charge as possible.\n\nShe replied that she would bring in an account of the expenses of it in two or three shapes, and like a bill of fare, I should choose as I pleased; and I desired her to do so.\n\nThe next day she brought it, and the copy of her three bills was as follows:—\n\n1. For three months’ lodging in her house, including my diet, at 10s. a week . . . . . . . . . . . 6£, 0s., 0d. 2. For a nurse for the month, and use of childbed linen . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 10s., 0d. 3. For a minister to christen the child, and to the godfathers and clerk . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 10s., 0d. 4. For a supper at the christening if I had five friends at it . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 0s., 0d. For her fees as a midwife, and the taking off the trouble of the parish . . . . . . . . . . . . 3£, 3s., 0d. To her maid servant attending . . . . . . . . 0£, 10s., 0d. -------------- 13£, 13s., 0d.\n\nThis was the first bill; the second was the same terms:—\n\n1. For three months’ lodging and diet, etc., at 20s. per week . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 13£, 0s., 0d. 2. For a nurse for the month, and the use of linen and lace . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 2£, 10s., 0d. 3. For the minister to christen the child, etc., as above . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 2£, 0s., 0d. 4. For supper and for sweetmeats . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3£, 3s., 0d. For her fees as above . . . . . . . . . . . . 5£, 5s., 0d. For a servant-maid . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 0s., 0d. -------------- 26£, 18s., 0d.\n\nThis was the second-rate bill; the third, she said, was for a degree higher, and when the father or friends appeared:—\n\n1. For three months’ lodging and diet, having two rooms and a garret for a servant . . . . . . 30£, 0s., 0d., 2. For a nurse for the month, and the finest suit of childbed linen . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 4£, 4s., 0d. 3. For the minister to christen the child, etc. 2£, 10s., 0d. 4. For a supper, the gentlemen to send in the wine . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6£, 0s., 0d. For my fees, etc. . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10£, 10s., 0d. The maid, besides their own maid, only . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 0£, 10s., 0d. -------------- 53£, 14s., 0d.\n\nI looked upon all three bills, and smiled, and told her I did not see but that she was very reasonable in her demands, all things considered, and for that I did not doubt but her accommodations were good.\n\nShe told me I should be judge of that when I saw them. I told her I was sorry to tell her that I feared I must be her lowest-rated customer. “And perhaps, madam,” said I, “you will make me the less welcome upon that account.” “No, not at all,” said she; “for where I have one of the third sort I have two of the second, and four to one of the first, and I get as much by them in proportion as by any; but if you doubt my care of you, I will allow any friend you have to overlook and see if you are well waited on or no.”\n\nThen she explained the particulars of her bill. “In the first place, madam,” said she, “I would have you observe that here is three months’ keeping; you are but ten shillings a week; I undertake to say you will not complain of my table. I suppose,” says she, “you do not live cheaper where you are now?” “No, indeed,” said I, “not so cheap, for I give six shillings per week for my chamber, and find my own diet as well as I can, which costs me a great deal more.”\n\n“Then, madam,” says she, “if the child should not live, or should be dead-born, as you know sometimes happens, then there is the minister’s article saved; and if you have no friends to come to you, you may save the expense of a supper; so that take those articles out, madam,” says she, “your lying in will not cost you above £5, 3s. in all more than your ordinary charge of living.”\n\nThis was the most reasonable thing that I ever heard of; so I smiled, and told her I would come and be her customer; but I told her also, that as I had two months and more to do, I might perhaps be obliged to stay longer with her than three months, and desired to know if she would not be obliged to remove me before it was proper. No, she said; her house was large, and besides, she never put anybody to remove, that had lain in, till they were willing to go; and if she had more ladies offered, she was not so ill-beloved among her neighbours but she could provide accommodations for twenty, if there was occasion.\n\nI found she was an eminent lady in her way; and, in short, I agreed to put myself into her hands, and promised her. She then talked of other things, looked about into my accommodations where I was, found fault with my wanting attendance and conveniences, and that I should not be used so at her house. I told her I was shy of speaking, for the woman of the house looked stranger, or at least I thought so, since I had been ill, because I was with child; and I was afraid she would put some affront or other upon me, supposing that I had been able to give but a slight account of myself.\n\n“Oh dear,” said she, “her ladyship is no stranger to these things; she has tried to entertain ladies in your condition several times, but she could not secure the parish; and besides, she is not such a nice lady as you take her to be; however, since you are a-going, you shall not meddle with her, but I’ll see you are a little better looked after while you are here than I think you are, and it shall not cost you the more neither.”\n\nI did not understand her at all; however, I thanked her, and so we parted. The next morning she sent me a chicken roasted and hot, and a pint bottle of sherry, and ordered the maid to tell me that she was to wait on me every day as long as I stayed there.\n\nThis was surprisingly good and kind, and I accepted it very willingly. At night she sent to me again, to know if I wanted anything, and how I did, and to order the maid to come to her in the morning with my dinner. The maid had orders to make me some chocolate in the morning before she came away, and did so, and at noon she brought me the sweetbread of a breast of veal, whole, and a dish of soup for my dinner; and after this manner she nursed me up at a distance, so that I was mightily well pleased, and quickly well, for indeed my dejections before were the principal part of my illness.\n\nI expected, as is usually the case among such people, that the servant she sent me would have been some imprudent brazen wench of Drury Lane breeding, and I was very uneasy at having her with me upon that account; so I would not let her lie in that house the first night by any means, but had my eyes about me as narrowly as if she had been a public thief.\n\nMy gentlewoman guessed presently what was the matter, and sent her back with a short note, that I might depend upon the honesty of her maid; that she would be answerable for her upon all accounts; and that she took no servants into her house without very good security for their fidelity. I was then perfectly easy; and indeed the maid’s behaviour spoke for itself, for a modester, quieter, soberer girl never came into anybody’s family, and I found her so afterwards.\n\nAs soon as I was well enough to go abroad, I went with the maid to see the house, and to see the apartment I was to have; and everything was so handsome and so clean and well, that, in short, I had nothing to say, but was wonderfully pleased and satisfied with what I had met with, which, considering the melancholy circumstances I was in, was far beyond what I looked for.\n\nIt might be expected that I should give some account of the nature of the wicked practices of this woman, in whose hands I was now fallen; but it would be too much encouragement to the vice, to let the world see what easy measures were here taken to rid the women’s unwelcome burthen of a child clandestinely gotten. This grave matron had several sorts of practice, and this was one particular, that if a child was born, though not in her house (for she had occasion to be called to many private labours), she had people at hand, who for a piece of money would take the child off their hands, and off from the hands of the parish too; and those children, as she said, were honestly provided for and taken care of. What should become of them all, considering so many, as by her account she was concerned with, I cannot conceive.\n\nI had many times discourses upon that subject with her; but she was full of this argument, that she save the life of many an innocent lamb, as she called them, which would otherwise perhaps have been murdered; and of many women who, made desperate by the misfortune, would otherwise be tempted to destroy their children, and bring themselves to the gallows. I granted her that this was true, and a very commendable thing, provided the poor children fell into good hands afterwards, and were not abused, starved, and neglected by the nurses that bred them up. She answered, that she always took care of that, and had no nurses in her business but what were very good, honest people, and such as might be depended upon.\n\nI could say nothing to the contrary, and so was obliged to say, “Madam, I do not question you do your part honestly, but what those people do afterwards is the main question”; and she stopped my mouth again with saying that she took the utmost care about it.\n\nThe only thing I found in all her conversation on these subjects that gave me any distaste, was, that one time in discouraging about my being far gone with child, and the time I expected to come, she said something that looked as if she could help me off with my burthen sooner, if I was willing; or, in English, that she could give me something to make me miscarry, if I had a desire to put an end to my troubles that way; but I soon let her see that I abhorred the thoughts of it; and, to do her justice, she put it off so cleverly, that I could not say she really intended it, or whether she only mentioned the practice as a horrible thing; for she couched her words so well, and took my meaning so quickly, that she gave her negative before I could explain myself.\n\nTo bring this part into as narrow a compass as possible, I quitted my lodging at St. Jones’s and went to my new governess, for so they called her in the house, and there I was indeed treated with so much courtesy, so carefully looked to, so handsomely provided, and everything so well, that I was surprised at it, and could not at first see what advantage my governess made of it; but I found afterwards that she professed to make no profit of lodgers’ diet, nor indeed could she get much by it, but that her profit lay in the other articles of her management, and she made enough that way, I assure you; for ’tis scarce credible what practice she had, as well abroad as at home, and yet all upon the private account, or, in plain English, the whoring account.\n\nWhile I was in her house, which was near four months, she had no less than twelve ladies of pleasure brought to bed within the doors, and I think she had two-and-thirty, or thereabouts, under her conduct without doors, whereof one, as nice as she was with me, was lodged with my old landlady at St. Jones’s.\n\nThis was a strange testimony of the growing vice of the age, and such a one, that as bad as I had been myself, it shocked my very senses. I began to nauseate the place I was in and, about all, the wicked practice; and yet I must say that I never saw, or do I believe there was to be seen, the least indecency in the house the whole time I was there.\n\nNot a man was ever seen to come upstairs, except to visit the lying-in ladies within their month, nor then without the old lady with them, who made it a piece of honour of her management that no man should touch a woman, no, not his own wife, within the month; nor would she permit any man to lie in the house upon any pretence whatever, no, not though she was sure it was with his own wife; and her general saying for it was, that she cared not how many children were born in her house, but she would have none got there if she could help it.\n\nIt might perhaps be carried further than was needful, but it was an error of the right hand if it was an error, for by this she kept up the reputation, such as it was, of her business, and obtained this character, that though she did take care of the women when they were debauched, yet she was not instrumental to their being debauched at all; and yet it was a wicked trade she drove too.\n\nWhile I was there, and before I was brought to bed, I received a letter from my trustee at the bank, full of kind, obliging things, and earnestly pressing me to return to London. It was near a fortnight old when it came to me, because it had been first sent into Lancashire, and then returned to me. He concludes with telling me that he had obtained a decree, I think he called it, against his wife, and that he would be ready to make good his engagement to me, if I would accept of him, adding a great many protestations of kindness and affection, such as he would have been far from offering if he had known the circumstances I had been in, and which as it was I had been very far from deserving.\n\nI returned an answer to his letter, and dated it at Liverpool, but sent it by messenger, alleging that it came in cover to a friend in town. I gave him joy of his deliverance, but raised some scruples at the lawfulness of his marrying again, and told him I supposed he would consider very seriously upon that point before he resolved on it, the consequence being too great for a man of his judgment to venture rashly upon a thing of that nature; so concluded, wishing him very well in whatever he resolved, without letting him into anything of my own mind, or giving any answer to his proposal of my coming to London to him, but mentioned at a distance my intention to return the latter end of the year, this being dated in April.\n\nI was brought to bed about the middle of May and had another brave boy, and myself in as good condition as usual on such occasions. My governess did her part as a midwife with the greatest art and dexterity imaginable, and far beyond all that ever I had had any experience of before.\n\nHer care of me in my travail, and after in my lying in, was such, that if she had been my own mother it could not have been better. Let none be encouraged in their loose practices from this dexterous lady’s management, for she is gone to her place, and I dare say has left nothing behind her that can or will come up on it.\n\nI think I had been brought to bed about twenty-two days when I received another letter from my friend at the bank, with the surprising news that he had obtained a final sentence of divorce against his wife, and had served her with it on such a day, and that he had such an answer to give to all my scruples about his marrying again, as I could not expect, and as he had no desire of; for that his wife, who had been under some remorse before for her usage of him, as soon as she had the account that he had gained his point, had very unhappily destroyed herself that same evening.\n\nHe expressed himself very handsomely as to his being concerned at her disaster, but cleared himself of having any hand in it, and that he had only done himself justice in a case in which he was notoriously injured and abused. However, he said that he was extremely afflicted at it, and had no view of any satisfaction left in his world, but only in the hope that I would come and relieve him by my company; and then he pressed me violently indeed to give him some hopes that I would at least come up to town and let him see me, when he would further enter into discourse about it.\n\nI was exceedingly surprised at the news, and began now seriously to reflect on my present circumstances, and the inexpressible misfortune it was to me to have a child upon my hands, and what to do in it I knew not. At last I opened my case at a distance to my governess. I appeared melancholy and uneasy for several days, and she lay at me continually to know what trouble me. I could not for my life tell her that I had an offer of marriage, after I had so often told her that I had a husband, so that I really knew not what to say to her. I owned I had something which very much troubled me, but at the same time told her I could not speak of it to any one alive.\n\nShe continued importuning me several days, but it was impossible, I told her, for me to commit the secret to anybody. This, instead of being an answer to her, increased her importunities; she urged her having been trusted with the greatest secrets of this nature, that it was her business to conceal everything, and that to discover things of that nature would be her ruin. She asked me if ever I had found her tattling to me of other people’s affairs, and how could I suspect her? She told me, to unfold myself to her was telling it to nobody; that she was silent as death; that it must be a very strange case indeed that she could not help me out of; but to conceal it was to deprive myself of all possible help, or means of help, and to deprive her of the opportunity of serving me. In short, she had such a bewitching eloquence, and so great a power of persuasion that there was no concealing anything from her.\n\nSo I resolved to unbosom myself to her. I told her the history of my Lancashire marriage, and how both of us had been disappointed; how we came together, and how we parted; how he absolutely discharged me, as far as lay in him, free liberty to marry again, protesting that if he knew it he would never claim me, or disturb or expose me; that I thought I was free, but was dreadfully afraid to venture, for fear of the consequences that might follow in case of a discovery.\n\nThen I told her what a good offer I had; showed her my friend’s two last letters, inviting me to come to London, and let her see with what affection and earnestness they were written, but blotted out the name, and also the story about the disaster of his wife, only that she was dead.\n\nShe fell a-laughing at my scruples about marrying, and told me the other was no marriage, but a cheat on both sides; and that, as we were parted by mutual consent, the nature of the contract was destroyed, and the obligation was mutually discharged. She had arguments for this at the tip of her tongue; and, in short, reasoned me out of my reason; not but that it was too by the help of my own inclination.\n\nBut then came the great and main difficulty, and that was the child; this, she told me in so many words, must be removed, and that so as that it should never be possible for any one to discover it. I knew there was no marrying without entirely concealing that I had had a child, for he would soon have discovered by the age of it that it was born, nay, and gotten too, since my parley with him, and that would have destroyed all the affair.\n\nBut it touched my heart so forcibly to think of parting entirely with the child, and, for aught I knew, of having it murdered, or starved by neglect and ill-usage (which was much the same), that I could not think of it without horror. I wish all those women who consent to the disposing their children out of the way, as it is called, for decency sake, would consider that ’tis only a contrived method for murder; that is to say, a-killing their children with safety.\n\nIt is manifest to all that understand anything of children, that we are born into the world helpless, and incapable either to supply our own wants or so much as make them known; and that without help we must perish; and this help requires not only an assisting hand, whether of the mother or somebody else, but there are two things necessary in that assisting hand, that is, care and skill; without both which, half the children that are born would die, nay, though they were not to be denied food; and one half more of those that remained would be cripples or fools, lose their limbs, and perhaps their sense. I question not but that these are partly the reasons why affection was placed by nature in the hearts of mothers to their children; without which they would never be able to give themselves up, as ’tis necessary they should, to the care and waking pains needful to the support of their children.\n\nSince this care is needful to the life of children, to neglect them is to murder them; again, to give them up to be managed by those people who have none of that needful affection placed by nature in them, is to neglect them in the highest degree; nay, in some it goes farther, and is a neglect in order to their being lost; so that ’tis even an intentional murder, whether the child lives or dies.\n\nAll those things represented themselves to my view, and that is the blackest and most frightful form: and as I was very free with my governess, whom I had now learned to call mother, I represented to her all the dark thoughts which I had upon me about it, and told her what distress I was in. She seemed graver by much at this part than at the other; but as she was hardened in these things beyond all possibility of being touched with the religious part, and the scruples about the murder, so she was equally impenetrable in that part which related to affection. She asked me if she had not been careful and tender to me in my lying in, as if I had been her own child. I told her I owned she had. “Well, my dear,” says she, “and when you are gone, what are you to me? And what would it be to me if you were to be hanged? Do you think there are not women who, as it is their trade and they get their bread by it, value themselves upon their being as careful of children as their own mothers can be, and understand it rather better? Yes, yes, child,” says she, “fear it not; how were we nursed ourselves? Are you sure you was nursed up by your own mother? and yet you look fat and fair, child,” says the old beldam; and with that she stroked me over the face. “Never be concerned, child,” says she, going on in her drolling way; “I have no murderers about me; I employ the best and the honestest nurses that can be had, and have as few children miscarry under their hands as there would if they were all nursed by mothers; we want neither care nor skill.”\n\nShe touched me to the quick when she asked if I was sure that I was nursed by my own mother; on the contrary I was sure I was not; and I trembled, and looked pale at the very expression. “Sure,” said I to myself, “this creature cannot be a witch, or have any conversation with a spirit, that can inform her what was done with me before I was able to know it myself”; and I looked at her as if I had been frightened; but reflecting that it could not be possible for her to know anything about me, that disorder went off, and I began to be easy, but it was not presently.\n\nShe perceived the disorder I was in, but did not know the meaning of it; so she ran on in her wild talk upon the weakness of my supposing that children were murdered because they were not all nursed by the mother, and to persuade me that the children she disposed of were as well used as if the mothers had the nursing of them themselves.\n\n“It may be true, mother,” says I, “for aught I know, but my doubts are very strongly grounded indeed.” “Come, then,” says she, “let’s hear some of them.” “Why, first,” says I, “you give a piece of money to these people to take the child off the parent’s hands, and to take care of it as long as it lives. Now we know, mother,” said I, “that those are poor people, and their gain consists in being quit of the charge as soon as they can; how can I doubt but that, as it is best for them to have the child die, they are not over solicitous about life?”\n\n“This is all vapours and fancy,” says the old woman; “I tell you their credit depends upon the child’s life, and they are as careful as any mother of you all.”\n\n“O mother,” says I, “if I was but sure my little baby would be carefully looked to, and have justice done it, I should be happy indeed; but it is impossible I can be satisfied in that point unless I saw it, and to see it would be ruin and destruction to me, as now my case stands; so what to do I know not.”\n\n“A fine story!” says the governess. “You would see the child, and you would not see the child; you would be concealed and discovered both together. These are things impossible, my dear; so you must e’en do as other conscientious mothers have done before you, and be contented with things as they must be, though they are not as you wish them to be.”\n\nI understood what she meant by conscientious mothers; she would have said conscientious whores, but she was not willing to disoblige me, for really in this case I was not a whore, because legally married, the force of former marriage excepted.\n\nHowever, let me be what I would, I was not come up to that pitch of hardness common to the profession; I mean, to be unnatural, and regardless of the safety of my child; and I preserved this honest affection so long, that I was upon the point of giving up my friend at the bank, who lay so hard at me to come to him and marry him, that, in short, there was hardly any room to deny him.\n\nAt last my old governess came to me, with her usual assurance. “Come, my dear,” says she, “I have found out a way how you shall be at a certainty that your child shall be used well, and yet the people that take care of it shall never know you, or who the mother of the child is.”\n\n“Oh mother,” says I, “if you can do so, you will engage me to you for ever.” “Well,” says she, “are you willing to be a some small annual expense, more than what we usually give to the people we contract with?” “Ay,” says I, “with all my heart, provided I may be concealed.” “As to that,” says the governess, “you shall be secure, for the nurse shall never so much as dare to inquire about you, and you shall once or twice a year go with me and see your child, and see how ’tis used, and be satisfied that it is in good hands, nobody knowing who you are.”\n\n“Why,” said I, “do you think, mother, that when I come to see my child, I shall be able to conceal my being the mother of it? Do you think that possible?”\n\n“Well, well,” says my governess, “if you discover it, the nurse shall be never the wiser; for she shall be forbid to ask any questions about you, or to take any notice. If she offers it, she shall lose the money which you are suppose to give her, and the child shall be taken from her too.”\n\nI was very well pleased with this. So the next week a countrywoman was brought from Hertford, or thereabouts, who was to take the child off our hands entirely for £10 in money. But if I would allow £5 a year more of her, she would be obliged to bring the child to my governess’s house as often as we desired, or we should come down and look at it, and see how well she used it.\n\nThe woman was very wholesome-looking, a likely woman, a cottager’s wife, but she had very good clothes and linen, and everything well about her; and with a heavy heart and many a tear, I let her have my child. I had been down at Hertford, and looked at her and at her dwelling, which I liked well enough; and I promised her great things if she would be kind to the child, so she knew at first word that I was the child’s mother. But she seemed to be so much out of the way, and to have no room to inquire after me, that I thought I was safe enough. So, in short, I consented to let her have the child, and I gave her £10; that is to say, I gave it to my governess, who gave it the poor woman before my face, she agreeing never to return the child back to me, or to claim anything more for its keeping or bringing up; only that I promised, if she took a great deal of care of it, I would give her something more as often as I came to see it; so that I was not bound to pay the £5, only that I promised my governess I would do it. And thus my great care was over, after a manner, which though it did not at all satisfy my mind, yet was the most convenient for me, as my affairs then stood, of any that could be thought of at that time.\n\nI then began to write to my friend at the bank in a more kindly style, and particularly about the beginning of July I sent him a letter, that I proposed to be in town some time in August. He returned me an answer in the most passionate terms imaginable, and desired me to let him have timely notice, and he would come and meet me, two day’s journey. This puzzled me scurvily, and I did not know what answer to make of it. Once I resolved to take the stage-coach to West Chester, on purpose only to have the satisfaction of coming back, that he might see me really come in the same coach; for I had a jealous thought, though I had no ground for it at all, lest he should think I was not really in the country. And it was no ill-grounded thought as you shall hear presently.\n\nI endeavoured to reason myself out of it, but it was in vain; the impression lay so strong on my mind, that it was not to be resisted. At last it came as an addition to my new design of going into the country, that it would be an excellent blind to my old governess, and would cover entirely all my other affairs, for she did not know in the least whether my new lover lived in London or in Lancashire; and when I told her my resolution, she was fully persuaded it was in Lancashire.\n\nHaving taken my measure for this journey I let her know it, and sent the maid that tended me, from the beginning, to take a place for me in the coach. She would have had me let the maid have waited on me down to the last stage, and come up again in the waggon, but I convinced her it would not be convenient. When I went away, she told me she would enter into no measures for correspondence, for she saw evidently that my affection to my child would cause me to write to her, and to visit her too when I came to town again. I assured her it would, and so took my leave, well satisfied to have been freed from such a house, however good my accommodations there had been, as I have related above.\n\nI took the place in the coach not to its full extent, but to a place called Stone, in Cheshire, I think it is, where I not only had no manner of business, but not so much as the least acquaintance with any person in the town or near it. But I knew that with money in the pocket one is at home anywhere; so I lodged there two or three days, till, watching my opportunity, I found room in another stage-coach, and took passage back again for London, sending a letter to my gentleman that I should be such a certain day at Stony-Stratford, where the coachman told me he was to lodge.\n\nIt happened to be a chance coach that I had taken up, which, having been hired on purpose to carry some gentlemen to West Chester who were going for Ireland, was now returning, and did not tie itself to exact times or places as the stages did; so that, having been obliged to lie still on Sunday, he had time to get himself ready to come out, which otherwise he could not have done.\n\nHowever, his warning was so short, that he could not reach to Stony-Stratford time enough to be with me at night, but he met me at a place called Brickhill the next morning, as we were just coming in to tow.\n\nI confess I was very glad to see him, for I had thought myself a little disappointed over-night, seeing I had gone so far to contrive my coming on purpose. He pleased me doubly too by the figure he came in, for he brought a very handsome (gentleman’s) coach and four horses, with a servant to attend him.\n\nHe took me out of the stage-coach immediately, which stopped at an inn in Brickhill; and putting into the same inn, he set up his own coach, and bespoke his dinner. I asked him what he meant by that, for I was for going forward with the journey. He said, No, I had need of a little rest upon the road, and that was a very good sort of a house, though it was but a little town; so we would go no farther that night, whatever came of it.\n\nI did not press him much, for since he had come so to meet me, and put himself to so much expense, it was but reasonable I should oblige him a little too; so I was easy as to that point.\n\nAfter dinner we walked to see the town, to see the church, and to view the fields, and the country, as is usual for strangers to do; and our landlord was our guide in going to see the church. I observed my gentleman inquired pretty much about the parson, and I took the hint immediately that he certainly would propose to be married; and though it was a sudden thought, it followed presently, that, in short, I would not refuse him; for, to be plain, with my circumstances I was in no condition now to say No; I had no reason now to run any more such hazards.\n\nBut while these thoughts ran round in my head, which was the work but of a few moments, I observed my landlord took him aside and whispered to him, though not very softly neither, for so much I overheard: “Sir, if you shall have occasion——” the rest I could not hear, but it seems it was to this purpose: “Sir, if you shall have occasion for a minister, I have a friend a little way off that will serve you, and be as private as you please.” My gentleman answered loud enough for me to hear, “Very well, I believe I shall.”\n\nI was no sooner come back to the inn but he fell upon me with irresistible words, that since he had had the good fortune to meet me, and everything concurred, it would be hastening his felicity if I would put an end to the matter just there. “What do you mean?” says I, colouring a little. “What, in an inn, and upon the road! Bless us all,” said I, as if I had been surprised, “how can you talk so?” “Oh, I can talk so very well,” says he, “I came a-purpose to talk so, and I’ll show you that I did”; and with that he pulls out a great bundle of papers. “You fright me,” said I; “what are all these?” “Don’t be frighted, my dear,” said he, and kissed me. This was the first time that he had been so free to call me “my dear”; then he repeated it, “Don’t be frighted; you shall see what it is all”; then he laid them all abroad. There was first the deed or sentence of divorce from his wife, and the full evidence of her playing the whore; then there were the certificates of the minister and churchwardens of the parish where she lived, proving that she was buried, and intimating the manner of her death; the copy of the coroner’s warrant for a jury to sit upon her, and the verdict of the jury, who brought it in Non compos mentis. All this was indeed to the purpose, and to give me satisfaction, though, by the way, I was not so scrupulous, had he known all, but that I might have taken him without it. However, I looked them all over as well as I could, and told him that this was all very clear indeed, but that he need not have given himself the trouble to have brought them out with him, for it was time enough. Well, he said, it might be time enough for me, but no time but the present time was time enough for him.\n\nThere were other papers rolled up, and I asked him what they were. “Why, ay,” says he, “that’s the question I wanted to have you ask me”; so he unrolls them and takes out a little shagreen case, and gives me out of it a very fine diamond ring. I could not refuse it, if I had a mind to do so, for he put it upon my finger; so I made him a curtsy and accepted it. Then he takes out another ring: “And this,” says he, “is for another occasion,” so he puts that in his pocket. “Well, but let me see it, though,” says I, and smiled; “I guess what it is; I think you are mad.” “I should have been mad if I had done less,” says he, and still he did not show me, and I had a great mind to see it; so I says, “Well, but let me see it.” “Hold,” says he, “first look here”; then he took up the roll again and read it, and behold! it was a licence for us to be married. “Why,” says I, “are you distracted? Why, you were fully satisfied that I would comply and yield at first word, or resolved to take no denial.” “The last is certainly the case,” said he. “But you may be mistaken,” said I. “No, no,” says he, “how can you think so? I must not be denied, I can’t be denied”; and with that he fell to kissing me so violently, I could not get rid of him.\n\nThere was a bed in the room, and we were walking to and again, eager in the discourse; at last he takes me by surprise in his arms, and threw me on the bed and himself with me, and holding me fast in his arms, but without the least offer of any indecency, courted me to consent with such repeated entreaties and arguments, protesting his affection, and vowing he would not let me go till I had promised him, that at last I said, “Why, you resolve not to be denied, indeed, I can’t be denied.” “Well, well,” said I, and giving him a slight kiss, “then you shan’t be denied,” said I; “let me get up.”\n\nHe was so transported with my consent, and the kind manner of it, that I began to think once he took it for a marriage, and would not stay for the form; but I wronged him, for he gave over kissing me, and then giving me two or three kisses again, thanked me for my kind yielding to him; and was so overcome with the satisfaction and joy of it, that I saw tears stand in his eyes.\n\nI turned from him, for it filled my eyes with tears too, and I asked him leave to retire a little to my chamber. If ever I had a grain of true repentance for a vicious and abominable life for twenty-four years past, it was then. Oh, what a felicity is it to mankind, said I to myself, that they cannot see into the hearts of one another! How happy had it been for me if I had been wife to a man of so much honesty, and so much affection from the beginning!\n\nThen it occurred to me, “What an abominable creature am I! and how is this innocent gentleman going to be abused by me! How little does he think, that having divorced a whore, he is throwing himself into the arms of another! that he is going to marry one that has lain with two brothers, and has had three children by her own brother! one that was born in Newgate, whose mother was a whore, and is now a transported thief! one that has lain with thirteen men, and has had a child since he saw me! Poor gentleman!” said I, “what is he going to do?” After this reproaching myself was over, it following thus: “Well, if I must be his wife, if it please God to give me grace, I’ll be a true wife to him, and love him suitably to the strange excess of his passion for me; I will make him amends if possible, by what he shall see, for the cheats and abuses I put upon him, which he does not see.”\n\nHe was impatient for my coming out of my chamber, but finding me long, he went downstairs and talked with my landlord about the parson.\n\nMy landlord, an officious though well-meaning fellow, had sent away for the neighbouring clergyman; and when my gentleman began to speak of it to him, and talk of sending for him, “Sir,” says he to him, “my friend is in the house”; so without any more words he brought them together. When he came to the minister, he asked him if he would venture to marry a couple of strangers that were both willing. The parson said that Mr. —— had said something to him of it; that he hoped it was no clandestine business; that he seemed to be a grave gentleman, and he supposed madam was not a girl, so that the consent of friends should be wanted. “To put you out of doubt of that,” says my gentleman, “read this paper”; and out he pulls the license. “I am satisfied,” says the minister; “where is the lady?” “You shall see her presently,” says my gentleman.\n\nWhen he had said thus he comes upstairs, and I was by that time come out of my room; so he tells me the minister was below, and that he had talked with him, and that upon showing him the license, he was free to marry us with all his heart, “but he asks to see you”; so he asked if I would let him come up.\n\n“’Tis time enough,” said I, “in the morning, is it not?” “Why,” said he, “my dear, he seemed to scruple whether it was not some young girl stolen from her parents, and I assured him we were both of age to command our own consent; and that made him ask to see you.” “Well,” said I, “do as you please”; so up they brings the parson, and a merry, good sort of gentleman he was. He had been told, it seems, that we had met there by accident, that I came in the Chester coach, and my gentleman in his own coach to meet me; that we were to have met last night at Stony-Stratford, but that he could not reach so far. “Well, sir,” says the parson, “every ill turn has some good in it. The disappointment, sir,” says he to my gentleman, “was yours, and the good turn is mine, for if you had met at Stony-Stratford I had not had the honour to marry you. Landlord, have you a Common Prayer Book?”\n\nI started as if I had been frightened. “Lord, sir,” says I, “what do you mean? What, to marry in an inn, and at night too?” “Madam,” says the minister, “if you will have it be in the church, you shall; but I assure you your marriage will be as firm here as in the church; we are not tied by the canons to marry nowhere but in the church; and if you will have it in the church, it will be a public as a county fair; and as for the time of day, it does not at all weigh in this case; our princes are married in their chambers, and at eight or ten o’clock at night.”\n\nI was a great while before I could be persuaded, and pretended not to be willing at all to be married but in the church. But it was all grimace; so I seemed at last to be prevailed on, and my landlord and his wife and daughter were called up. My landlord was father and clerk and all together, and we were married, and very merry we were; though I confess the self-reproaches which I had upon me before lay close to me, and extorted every now and then a deep sigh from me, which my bridegroom took notice of, and endeavoured to encourage me, thinking, poor man, that I had some little hesitations at the step I had taken so hastily.\n\nWe enjoyed ourselves that evening completely, and yet all was kept so private in the inn that not a servant in the house knew of it, for my landlady and her daughter waited on me, and would not let any of the maids come upstairs, except while we were at supper. My landlady’s daughter I called my bridesmaid; and sending for a shopkeeper the next morning, I gave the young woman a good suit of knots, as good as the town would afford, and finding it was a lace-making town, I gave her mother a piece of bone-lace for a head.\n\nOne reason that my landlord was so close was, that he was unwilling the minister of the parish should hear of it; but for all that somebody heard of it, so at that we had the bells set a-ringing the next morning early, and the music, such as the town would afford, under our window; but my landlord brazened it out, that we were married before we came thither, only that, being his former guests, we would have our wedding-supper at his house.\n\nWe could not find in our hearts to stir the next day; for, in short, having been disturbed by the bells in the morning, and having perhaps not slept overmuch before, we were so sleepy afterwards that we lay in bed till almost twelve o’clock.\n\nI begged my landlady that we might not have any more music in the town, nor ringing of bells, and she managed it so well that we were very quiet; but an odd passage interrupted all my mirth for a good while. The great room of the house looked into the street, and my new spouse being belowstairs, I had walked to the end of the room; and it being a pleasant, warm day, I had opened the window, and was standing at it for some air, when I saw three gentlemen come by on horseback and go into an inn just against us.\n\nIt was not to be concealed, nor was it so doubtful as to leave me any room to question it, but the second of the three was my Lancashire husband. I was frightened to death; I never was in such a consternation in my life; I though I should have sunk into the ground; my blood ran chill in my veins, and I trembled as if I had been in a cold fit of ague. I say, there was no room to question the truth of it; I knew his clothes, I knew his horse, and I knew his face.\n\nThe first sensible reflect I made was, that my husband was not by to see my disorder, and that I was very glad of it. The gentlemen had not been long in the house but they came to the window of their room, as is usual; but my window was shut, you may be sure. However, I could not keep from peeping at them, and there I saw him again, heard him call out to one of the servants of the house for something he wanted, and received all the terrifying confirmations of its being the same person that were possible to be had.\n\nMy next concern was to know, if possible, what was his business there; but that was impossible. Sometimes my imagination formed an idea of one frightful thing, sometimes of another; sometimes I thought he had discovered me, and was come to upbraid me with ingratitude and breach of honour; and every moment I fancied he was coming up the stairs to insult me; and innumerable fancies came into my head of what was never in his head, nor ever could be, unless the devil had revealed it to him.\n\nI remained in this fright nearly two hours, and scarce ever kept my eye from the window or door of the inn where they were. At last, hearing a great clatter in the passage of their inn, I ran to the window, and, to my great satisfaction, saw them all three go out again and travel on westward. Had they gone towards London, I should have been still in a fright, lest I should meet him on the road again, and that he should know me; but he went the contrary way, and so I was eased of that disorder.\n\nWe resolved to be going the next day, but about six o’clock at night we were alarmed with a great uproar in the street, and people riding as if they had been out of their wits; and what was it but a hue-and-cry after three highwaymen that had robbed two coaches and some other travellers near Dunstable Hill, and notice had, it seems, been given that they had been seen at Brickhill at such a house, meaning the house where those gentlemen had been.\n\nThe house was immediately beset and searched, but there were witnesses enough that the gentlemen had been gone over three hours. The crowd having gathered about, we had the news presently; and I was heartily concerned now another way. I presently told the people of the house, that I durst to say those were not the persons, for that I knew one of the gentlemen to be a very honest person, and of a good estate in Lancashire.\n\nThe constable who came with the hue-and-cry was immediately informed of this, and came over to me to be satisfied from my own mouth, and I assured him that I saw the three gentlemen as I was at the window; that I saw them afterwards at the windows of the room they dined in; that I saw them afterwards take horse, and I could assure him I knew one of them to be such a man, that he was a gentleman of a very good estate, and an undoubted character in Lancashire, from whence I was just now upon my journey.\n\nThe assurance with which I delivered this gave the mob gentry a check, and gave the constable such satisfaction, that he immediately sounded a retreat, told his people these were not the men, but that he had an account they were very honest gentlemen; and so they went all back again. What the truth of the matter was I knew not, but certain it was that the coaches were robbed at Dunstable Hill, and £560 in money taken; besides, some of the lace merchants that always travel that way had been visited too. As to the three gentlemen, that remains to be explained hereafter.\n\nWell, this alarm stopped us another day, though my spouse was for travelling, and told me that it was always safest travelling after a robbery, for that the thieves were sure to be gone far enough off when they had alarmed the country; but I was afraid and uneasy, and indeed principally lest my old acquaintance should be upon the road still, and should chance to see me.\n\nI never lived four pleasanter days together in my life. I was a mere bride all this while, and my new spouse strove to make me entirely easy in everything. Oh could this state of life have continued, how had all my past troubles been forgot, and my future sorrows avoided! But I had a past life of a most wretched kind to account for, some of it in this world as well as in another.\n\nWe came away the fifth day; and my landlord, because he saw me uneasy, mounted himself, his son, and three honest country fellows with good firearms, and, without telling us of it, followed the coach, and would see us safe into Dunstable. We could do no less than treat them very handsomely at Dunstable, which cost my spouse about ten or twelve shillings, and something he gave the men for their time too, but my landlord would take nothing for himself.\n\nThis was the most happy contrivance for me that could have fallen out; for had I come to London unmarried, I must either have come to him for the first night’s entertainment, or have discovered to him that I had not one acquaintance in the whole city of London that could receive a poor bride for the first night’s lodging with her spouse. But now, being an old married woman, I made no scruple of going directly home with him, and there I took possession at once of a house well furnished, and a husband in very good circumstances, so that I had a prospect of a very happy life, if I knew how to manage it; and I had leisure to consider of the real value of the life I was likely to live. How different it was to be from the loose ungoverned part I had acted before, and how much happier a life of virtue and sobriety is, than that which we call a life of pleasure.\n\nOh had this particular scene of life lasted, or had I learned from that time I enjoyed it, to have tasted the true sweetness of it, and had I not fallen into that poverty which is the sure bane of virtue, how happy had I been, not only here, but perhaps for ever! for while I lived thus, I was really a penitent for all my life past. I looked back on it with abhorrence, and might truly be said to hate myself for it. I often reflected how my lover at the Bath, struck at the hand of God, repented and abandoned me, and refused to see me any more, though he loved me to an extreme; but I, prompted by that worst of devils, poverty, returned to the vile practice, and made the advantage of what they call a handsome face to be the relief to my necessities, and beauty be a pimp to vice.\n\nNow I seemed landed in a safe harbour, after the stormy voyage of life past was at an end, and I began to be thankful for my deliverance. I sat many an hour by myself, and wept over the remembrance of past follies, and the dreadful extravagances of a wicked life, and sometimes I flattered myself that I had sincerely repented.\n\nBut there are temptations which it is not in the power of human nature to resist, and few know what would be their case if driven to the same exigencies. As covetousness is the root of all evil, so poverty is, I believe, the worst of all snares. But I waive that discourse till I come to an experiment.\n\nI lived with this husband with the utmost tranquillity; he was a quiet, sensible, sober man; virtuous, modest, sincere, and in his business diligent and just. His business was in a narrow compass, and his income sufficient to a plentiful way of living in the ordinary way. I do not say to keep an equipage, and make a figure, as the world calls it, nor did I expect it, or desire it; for as I abhorred the levity and extravagance of my former life, so I chose now to live retired, frugal, and within ourselves. I kept no company, made no visits; minded my family, and obliged my husband; and this kind of life became a pleasure to me.\n\nWe lived in an uninterrupted course of ease and content for five years, when a sudden blow from an almost invisible hand blasted all my happiness, and turned me out into the world in a condition the reverse of all that had been before it.\n\nMy husband having trusted one of his fellow-clerks with a sum of money, too much for our fortunes to bear the loss of, the clerk failed, and the loss fell very heavy on my husband, yet it was not so great neither but that, if he had had spirit and courage to have looked his misfortunes in the face, his credit was so good that, as I told him, he would easily recover it; for to sink under trouble is to double the weight, and he that will die in it, shall die in it.\n\nIt was in vain to speak comfortably to him; the wound had sunk too deep; it was a stab that touched the vitals; he grew melancholy and disconsolate, and from thence lethargic, and died. I foresaw the blow, and was extremely oppressed in my mind, for I saw evidently that if he died I was undone.\n\nI had had two children by him and no more, for, to tell the truth, it began to be time for me to leave bearing children, for I was now eight-and-forty, and I suppose if he had lived I should have had no more.\n\nI was now left in a dismal and disconsolate case indeed, and in several things worse than ever. First, it was past the flourishing time with me when I might expect to be courted for a mistress; that agreeable part had declined some time, and the ruins only appeared of what had been; and that which was worse than all this, that I was the most dejected, disconsolate creature alive. I that had encouraged my husband, and endeavoured to support his spirits under his trouble, could not support my own; I wanted that spirit in trouble which I told him was so necessary to him for bearing the burthen.\n\nBut my case was indeed deplorable, for I was left perfectly friendless and helpless, and the loss my husband had sustained had reduced his circumstances so low, that though indeed I was not in debt, yet I could easily foresee that what was left would not support me long; that while it wasted daily for subsistence, I had not way to increase it one shilling, so that it would be soon all spent, and then I saw nothing before me but the utmost distress; and this represented itself so lively to my thoughts, that it seemed as if it was come, before it was really very near; also my very apprehensions doubled the misery, for I fancied every sixpence that I paid for a loaf of bread was the last that I had in the world, and that to-morrow I was to fast, and be starved to death.\n\nIn this distress I had no assistant, no friend to comfort or advise me; I sat and cried and tormented myself night and day, wringing my hands, and sometimes raving like a distracted woman; and indeed I have often wondered it had not affected my reason, for I had the vapours to such a degree, that my understanding was sometimes quite lost in fancies and imaginations.\n\nI lived two years in this dismal condition, wasting that little I had, weeping continually over my dismal circumstances, and, as it were, only bleeding to death, without the least hope or prospect of help from God or man; and now I had cried too long, and so often, that tears were, as I might say, exhausted, and I began to be desperate, for I grew poor apace.\n\nFor a little relief I had put off my house and took lodgings; and as I was reducing my living, so I sold off most of my goods, which put a little money in my pocket, and I lived near a year upon that, spending very sparingly, and eking things out to the utmost; but still when I looked before me, my very heart would sink within me at the inevitable approach of misery and want. Oh let none read this part without seriously reflecting on the circumstances of a desolate state, and how they would grapple with mere want of friends and want of bread; it will certainly make them think not of sparing what they have only, but of looking up to heaven for support, and of the wise man’s prayer, “Give me not poverty, lest I steal.”\n\nLet them remember that a time of distress is a time of dreadful temptation, and all the strength to resist is taken away; poverty presses, the soul is made desperate by distress, and what can be done? It was one evening, when being brought, as I may say, to the last gasp, I think I may truly say I was distracted and raving, when prompted by I know not what spirit, and, as it were, doing I did not know what or why, I dressed me (for I had still pretty good clothes) and went out. I am very sure I had no manner of design in my head when I went out; I neither knew nor considered where to go, or on what business; but as the devil carried me out and laid his bait for me, so he brought me, to be sure, to the place, for I knew not whither I was going or what I did.\n\nWandering thus about, I knew not whither, I passed by an apothecary’s shop in Leadenhall Street, when I saw lie on a stool just before the counter a little bundle wrapped in a white cloth; beyond it stood a maid-servant with her back to it, looking towards the top of the shop, where the apothecary’s apprentice, as I suppose, was standing upon the counter, with his back also to the door, and a candle in his hand, looking and reaching up to the upper shelf for something he wanted, so that both were engaged mighty earnestly, and nobody else in the shop.\n\nThis was the bait; and the devil, who I said laid the snare, as readily prompted me as if he had spoke, for I remember, and shall never forget it, ’twas like a voice spoken to me over my shoulder, “Take the bundle; be quick; do it this moment.” It was no sooner said but I stepped into the shop, and with my back to the wench, as if I had stood up for a cart that was going by, I put my hand behind me and took the bundle, and went off with it, the maid or the fellow not perceiving me, or any one else.\n\nIt is impossible to express the horror of my soul all the while I did it. When I went away I had no heart to run, or scarce to mend my pace. I crossed the street indeed, and went down the first turning I came to, and I think it was a street that went through into Fenchurch Street. From thence I crossed and turned through so many ways and turnings, that I could never tell which way it was, not where I went; for I felt not the ground I stepped on, and the farther I was out of danger, the faster I went, till, tired and out of breath, I was forced to sit down on a little bench at a door, and then I began to recover, and found I was got into Thames Street, near Billingsgate. I rested me a little and went on; my blood was all in a fire; my heart beat as if I was in a sudden fright. In short, I was under such a surprise that I still knew not wither I was going, or what to do.\n\nAfter I had tired myself thus with walking a long way about, and so eagerly, I began to consider and make home to my lodging, where I came about nine o’clock at night.\n\nWhen the bundle was made up for, or on what occasion laid where I found it, I knew not, but when I came to open it I found there was a suit of childbed-linen in it, very good and almost new, the lace very fine; there was a silver porringer of a pint, a small silver mug and six spoons, with some other linen, a good smock, and three silk handkerchiefs, and in the mug, wrapped up in a paper, 18s. 6d. in money.\n\nAll the while I was opening these things I was under such dreadful impressions of fear, and I such terror of mind, though I was perfectly safe, that I cannot express the manner of it. I sat me down, and cried most vehemently. “Lord,” said I, “what am I now? a thief! Why, I shall be taken next time, and be carried to Newgate and be tried for my life!” And with that I cried again a long time, and I am sure, as poor as I was, if I had durst for fear, I would certainly have carried the things back again; but that went off after a while. Well, I went to bed for that night, but slept little; the horror of the fact was upon my mind, and I knew not what I said or did all night, and all the next day. Then I was impatient to hear some news of the loss; and would fain know how it was, whether they were a poor body’s goods, or a rich. “Perhaps,” said I, “it may be some poor widow like me, that had packed up these goods to go and sell them for a little bread for herself and a poor child, and are now starving and breaking their hearts for want of that little they would have fetched.” And this thought tormented me worse than all the rest, for three or four days’ time.\n\nBut my own distresses silenced all these reflections, and the prospect of my own starving, which grew every day more frightful to me, hardened my heart by degrees. It was then particularly heavy upon my mind, that I had been reformed, and had, as I hoped, repented of all my past wickedness; that I had lived a sober, grave, retired life for several years, but now I should be driven by the dreadful necessity of my circumstances to the gates of destruction, soul and body; and two or three times I fell upon my knees, praying to God, as well as I could, for deliverance; but I cannot but say, my prayers had no hope in them. I knew not what to do; it was all fear without, and dark within; and I reflected on my past life as not sincerely repented of, that Heaven was now beginning to punish me on this side of the grave, and would make me as miserable as I had been wicked.\n\nHad I gone on here I had perhaps been a true penitent; but I had an evil counsellor within, and he was continually prompting me to relieve myself by the worst means; so one evening he tempted me again, by the same wicked impulse that had said “Take that bundle,” to go out again and seek for what might happen.\n\nI went out now by daylight, and wandered about I knew not whither, and in search of I knew not what, when the devil put a snare in my way of a dreadful nature indeed, and such a one as I have never had before or since. Going through Aldersgate Street, there was a pretty little child who had been at a dancing-school, and was going home, all alone; and my prompter, like a true devil, set me upon this innocent creature. I talked to it, and it prattled to me again, and I took it by the hand and led it along till I came to a paved alley that goes into Bartholomew Close, and I led it in there. The child said that was not its way home. I said, “Yes, my dear, it is; I’ll show you the way home.” The child had a little necklace on of gold beads, and I had my eye upon that, and in the dark of the alley I stooped, pretending to mend the child’s clog that was loose, and took off her necklace, and the child never felt it, and so led the child on again. Here, I say, the devil put me upon killing the child in the dark alley, that it might not cry, but the very thought frighted me so that I was ready to drop down; but I turned the child about and bade it go back again, for that was not its way home. The child said, so she would, and I went through into Bartholomew Close, and then turned round to another passage that goes into St. John Street; then, crossing into Smithfield, went down Chick Lane and into Field Lane to Holborn Bridge, when, mixing with the crowd of people usually passing there, it was not possible to have been found out; and thus I enterprised my second sally into the world.\n\nThe thoughts of this booty put out all the thoughts of the first, and the reflections I had made wore quickly off; poverty, as I have said, hardened my heart, and my own necessities made me regardless of anything. The last affair left no great concern upon me, for as I did the poor child no harm, I only said to myself, I had given the parents a just reproof for their negligence in leaving the poor little lamb to come home by itself, and it would teach them to take more care of it another time.\n\nThis string of beads was worth about twelve or fourteen pounds. I suppose it might have been formerly the mother’s, for it was too big for the child’s wear, but that perhaps the vanity of the mother, to have her child look fine at the dancing-school, had made her let the child wear it; and no doubt the child had a maid sent to take care of it, but she, careless jade, was taken up perhaps with some fellow that had met her by the way, and so the poor baby wandered till it fell into my hands.\n\nHowever, I did the child no harm; I did not so much as fright it, for I had a great many tender thoughts about me yet, and did nothing but what, as I may say, mere necessity drove me to.\n\nI had a great many adventures after this, but I was young in the business, and did not know how to manage, otherwise than as the devil put things into my head; and indeed he was seldom backward to me. One adventure I had which was very lucky to me. I was going through Lombard Street in the dusk of the evening, just by the end of Three King court, when on a sudden comes a fellow running by me as swift as lightning, and throws a bundle that was in his hand, just behind me, as I stood up against the corner of the house at the turning into the alley. Just as he threw it in he said, “God bless you, mistress, let it lie there a little,” and away he runs swift as the wind. After him comes two more, and immediately a young fellow without his hat, crying “Stop thief!” and after him two or three more. They pursued the two last fellows so close, that they were forced to drop what they had got, and one of them was taken into the bargain, and other got off free.\n\nI stood stock-still all this while, till they came back, dragging the poor fellow they had taken, and lugging the things they had found, extremely well satisfied that they had recovered the booty and taken the thief; and thus they passed by me, for I looked only like one who stood up while the crowd was gone.\n\nOnce or twice I asked what was the matter, but the people neglected answering me, and I was not very importunate; but after the crowd was wholly past, I took my opportunity to turn about and take up what was behind me and walk away. This, indeed, I did with less disturbance than I had done formerly, for these things I did not steal, but they were stolen to my hand. I got safe to my lodgings with this cargo, which was a piece of fine black lustring silk, and a piece of velvet; the latter was but part of a piece of about eleven yards; the former was a whole piece of near fifty yards. It seems it was a mercer’s shop that they had rifled. I say rifled, because the goods were so considerable that they had lost; for the goods that they recovered were pretty many, and I believe came to about six or seven several pieces of silk. How they came to get so many I could not tell; but as I had only robbed the thief, I made no scruple at taking these goods, and being very glad of them too.\n\nI had pretty good luck thus far, and I made several adventures more, though with but small purchase, yet with good success, but I went in daily dread that some mischief would befall me, and that I should certainly come to be hanged at last. The impression this made on me was too strong to be slighted, and it kept me from making attempts that, for ought I knew, might have been very safely performed; but one thing I cannot omit, which was a bait to me many a day. I walked frequently out into the villages round the town, to see if nothing would fall in my way there; and going by a house near Stepney, I saw on the window-board two rings, one a small diamond ring, and the other a gold ring, to be sure laid there by some thoughtless lady, that had more money than forecast, perhaps only till she washed her hands.\n\nI walked several times by the window to observe if I could see whether there was anybody in the room or no, and I could see nobody, but still I was not sure. It came presently into my thoughts to rap at the glass, as if I wanted to speak with somebody, and if anybody was there they would be sure to come to the window, and then I would tell them to remove those rings, for that I had seen two suspicious fellows take notice of them. This was a ready thought. I rapped once or twice and nobody came, when, seeing the coast clear, I thrust hard against the square of the glass, and broke it with very little noise, and took out the two rings, and walked away with them very safe. The diamond ring was worth about £3, and the other about 9s.\n\nI was now at a loss for a market for my goods, and especially for my two pieces of silk. I was very loth to dispose of them for a trifle, as the poor unhappy thieves in general do, who, after they have ventured their lives for perhaps a thing of value, are fain to sell it for a song when they have done; but I was resolved I would not do thus, whatever shift I made, unless I was driven to the last extremity. However, I did not well know what course to take. At last I resolved to go to my old governess, and acquaint myself with her again. I had punctually supplied the £5 a year to her for my little boy as long as I was able, but at last was obliged to put a stop to it. However, I had written a letter to her, wherein I had told her that my circumstances were reduced very low; that I had lost my husband, and that I was not able to do it any longer, and so begged that the poor child might not suffer too much for its mother’s misfortunes.\n\nI now made her a visit, and I found that she drove something of the old trade still, but that she was not in such flourishing circumstances as before; for she had been sued by a certain gentleman who had had his daughter stolen from him, and who, it seems, she had helped to convey away; and it was very narrowly that she escaped the gallows. The expense also had ravaged her, and she was become very poor; her house was but meanly furnished, and she was not in such repute for her practice as before; however, she stood upon her legs, as they say, and as she was a stirring, bustling woman, and had some stock left, she was turned pawnbroker, and lived pretty well.\n\nShe received me very civilly, and with her usual obliging manner told me she would not have the less respect for me for my being reduced; that she had taken care my boy was very well looked after, though I could not pay for him, and that the woman that had him was easy, so that I needed not to trouble myself about him till I might be better able to do it effectually.\n\nI told her that I had not much money left, but that I had some things that were money’s worth, if she could tell me how I might turn them into money. She asked me what it was I had. I pulled out the string of gold beads, and told her it was one of my husband’s presents to me; then I showed her the two parcels of silk, which I told her I had from Ireland, and brought up to town with me; and the little diamond ring. As to the small parcel of plate and spoons, I had found means to dispose of them myself before; and as for the childbed-linen I had, she offered me to take it herself, believing it to have been my own. She told me that she was turned pawnbroker, and that she would sell those things for me as pawn to her; and so she sent presently for proper agents that bought them, being in her hands, without any scruple, and gave good prices too.\n\nI now began to think this necessary woman might help me a little in my low condition to some business, for I would gladly have turned my hand to any honest employment if I could have got it. But here she was deficient; honest business did not come within her reach. If I had been younger, perhaps she might have helped me to a spark, but my thoughts were off that kind of livelihood, as being quite out of the way after fifty, which was my case, and so I told her.\n\nShe invited me at last to come, and be at her house till I could find something to do, and it should cost me very little, and this I gladly accepted of. And now living a little easier, I entered into some measures to have my little son by my last husband taken off; and this she made easy too, reserving a payment only of £5 a year, if I could pay it. This was such a help to me, that for a good while I left off the wicked trade that I had so newly taken up; and gladly I would have got my bread by the help of my needle if I could have got work, but that was very hard to do for one that had no manner of acquaintance in the world.\n\nHowever, at last I got some quilting work for ladies’ beds, petticoats, and the like; and this I liked very well, and worked very hard, and with this I began to live; but the diligent devil, who resolved I should continue in his service, continually prompted me to go out and take a walk, that is to say, to see if anything would offer in the old way.\n\nOne evening I blindly obeyed his summons, and fetched a long circuit through the streets, but met with no purchase, and came home very weary and empty; but not content with that, I went out the next evening too, when going by an alehouse I saw the door of a little room open, next the very street, and on the table a silver tankard, things much in use in public-houses at that time. It seems some company had been drinking there, and the careless boys had forgot to take it away.\n\nI went into the box frankly, and setting the silver tankard on the corner of the bench, I sat down before it, and knocked with my foot; a boy came presently, and I bade him fetch me a pint of warm ale, for it was cold weather; the boy ran, and I heard him go down the cellar to draw the ale. While the boy was gone, another boy came into the room, and cried, “D’ ye call?” I spoke with a melancholy air, and said, “No, child; the boy is gone for a pint of ale for me.”\n\nWhile I sat here, I heard the woman in the bar say, “Are they all gone in the five?” which was the box I sat in, and the boy said, “Yes.” “Who fetched the tankard away?” says the woman. “I did,” says another boy; “that’s it,” pointing, it seems, to another tankard, which he had fetched from another box by mistake; or else it must be, that the rogue forgot that he had not brought it in, which certainly he had not.\n\nI heard all this, much to my satisfaction, for I found plainly that the tankard was not missed, and yet they concluded it was fetched away; so I drank my ale, called to pay, and as I went away I said, “Take care of your plate, child,” meaning a silver pint mug, which he brought me drink in. The boy said, “Yes, madam, very welcome,” and away I came.\n\nI came home to my governess, and now I thought it was a time to try her, that if I might be put to the necessity of being exposed, she might offer me some assistance. When I had been at home some time, and had an opportunity of talking to her, I told her I had a secret of the greatest consequence in the world to commit to her, if she had respect enough for me to keep it a secret. She told me she had kept one of my secrets faithfully; why should I doubt her keeping another? I told her the strangest thing in the world had befallen me, and that it had made a thief of me, even without any design, and so told her the whole story of the tankard. “And have you brought it away with you, my dear?” says she. “To be sure I have,” says I, and showed it her. “But what shall I do now,” says I; “must not carry it again?”\n\n“Carry it again!” says she. “Ay, if you are minded to be sent to Newgate for stealing it.” “Why,” says I, “they can’t be so base to stop me, when I carry it to them again?” “You don’t know those sort of people, child,” says she; “they’ll not only carry you to Newgate, but hang you too, without any regard to the honesty of returning it; or bring in an account of all the other tankards they have lost, for you to pay for.” “What must I do, then?” says I. “Nay,” says she, “as you have played the cunning part and stole it, you must e’en keep it; there’s no going back now. Besides, child,” says she, “don’t you want it more than they do? I wish you could light of such a bargain once a week.”\n\nThis gave me a new notion of my governess, and that since she was turned pawnbroker, she had a sort of people about her that were none of the honest ones that I had met with there before.\n\nI had not been long there but I discovered it more plainly than before, for every now and then I saw hilts of swords, spoons, forks, tankards, and all such kind of ware brought in, not to be pawned, but to be sold downright; and she bought everything that came without asking any questions, but had very good bargains, as I found by her discourse.\n\nI found also that in following this trade she always melted down the plate she bought, that it might not be challenged; and she came to me and told me one morning that she was going to melt, and if I would, she would put my tankard in, that it might not be seen by anybody. I told her, with all my heart; so she weighed it, and allowed me the full value in silver again; but I found she did not do the same to the rest of her customers.\n\nSome time after this, as I was at work, and very melancholy, she begins to ask me what the matter was, as she was used to do. I told her my heart was heavy; I had little work, and nothing to live on, and knew not what course to take. She laughed, and told me I must go out again and try my fortune; it might be that I might meet with another piece of plate. “O mother!” says I, “that is a trade I have no skill in, and if I should be taken I am undone at once.” Says she, “I could help you to a schoolmistress that shall make you as dexterous as herself.” I trembled at that proposal, for hitherto I had had no confederates, nor any acquaintance among that tribe. But she conquered all my modesty, and all my fears; and in a little time, by the help of this confederate, I grew as impudent a thief, and as dexterous as ever Moll Cutpurse was, though, if fame does not belie her, not half so handsome.\n\nThe comrade she helped me to dealt in three sorts of craft, viz. shoplifting, stealing of shop-books and pocket-books, and taking off gold watches from the ladies’ sides; and this last she did so dexterously that no woman ever arrived to the performance of that art so as to do it like her. I liked the first and the last of these things very well, and I attended her some time in the practice, just as a deputy attends a midwife, without any pay.\n\nAt length she put me to practice. She had shown me her art, and I had several times unhooked a watch from her own side with great dexterity. At last she showed me a prize, and this was a young lady big with child, who had a charming watch. The thing was to be done as she came out of church. She goes on one side of the lady, and pretends, just as she came to the steps, to fall, and fell against the lady with so much violence as put her into a great fright, and both cried out terribly. In the very moment that she jostled the lady, I had hold of the watch, and holding it the right way, the start she gave drew the hook out, and she never felt it. I made off immediately, and left my schoolmistress to come out of her pretended fright gradually, and the lady too; and presently the watch was missed. “Ay,” says my comrade, “then it was those rogues that thrust me down, I warrant ye; I wonder the gentlewoman did not miss her watch before, then we might have taken them.”\n\nShe humoured the thing so well that nobody suspected her, and I was got home a full hour before her. This was my first adventure in company. The watch was indeed a very fine one, and had a great many trinkets about it, and my governess allowed us £20 for it, of which I had half. And thus I was entered a complete thief, hardened to the pitch above all the reflections of conscience or modesty, and to a degree which I must acknowledge I never thought possible in me.\n\nThus the devil, who began, by the help of an irresistible poverty, to push me into this wickedness, brought me on to a height beyond the common rate, even when my necessities were not so great, or the prospect of my misery so terrifying; for I had now got into a little vein of work, and as I was not at a loss to handle my needle, it was very probable, as acquaintance came in, I might have got my bread honestly enough.\n\nI must say, that if such a prospect of work had presented itself at first, when I began to feel the approach of my miserable circumstances—I say, had such a prospect of getting my bread by working presented itself then, I had never fallen into this wicked trade, or into such a wicked gang as I was now embarked with; but practice had hardened me, and I grew audacious to the last degree; and the more so because I had carried it on so long, and had never been taken; for, in a word, my new partner in wickedness and I went on together so long, without being ever detected, that we not only grew bold, but we grew rich, and we had at one time one-and-twenty gold watches in our hands.\n\nI remember that one day being a little more serious than ordinary, and finding I had so good a stock beforehand as I had, for I had near £200 in money for my share, it came strongly into my mind, no doubt from some kind spirit, if such there be, that at first poverty excited me, and my distresses drove me to these dreadful shifts; so seeing those distresses were now relieved, and I could also get something towards a maintenance by working, and had so good a bank to support me, why should I now not leave off, as they say, while I was well? that I could not expect to go always free; and if I was once surprised, and miscarried, I was undone.\n\nThis was doubtless the happy minute, when, if I had hearkened to the blessed hint, from whatsoever had it came, I had still a cast for an easy life. But my fate was otherwise determined; the busy devil that so industriously drew me in had too fast hold of me to let me go back; but as poverty brought me into the mire, so avarice kept me in, till there was no going back. As to the arguments which my reason dictated for persuading me to lay down, avarice stepped in and said, “Go on, go on; you have had very good luck; go on till you have gotten four or five hundred pounds, and then you shall leave off, and then you may live easy without working at all.”\n\nThus I, that was once in the devil’s clutches, was held fast there as with a charm, and had no power to go without the circle, till I was engulfed in labyrinths of trouble too great to get out at all.\n\nHowever, these thoughts left some impression upon me, and made me act with some more caution than before, and more than my directors used for themselves. My comrade, as I called her, but rather she should have been called my teacher, with another of her scholars, was the first in the misfortune; for, happening to be upon the hunt for purchase, they made an attempt upon a linen-draper in Cheapside, but were snapped by a hawk’s-eyed journeyman, and seized with two pieces of cambric, which were taken also upon them.\n\nThis was enough to lodge them both in Newgate, where they had the misfortune to have some of their former sins brought to remembrance. Two other indictments being brought against them, and the facts being proved upon them, they were both condemned to die. They both pleaded their bellies, and were both voted quick with child; though my tutoress was no more with child than I was.\n\nI went frequently to see them, and condole with them, expecting that it would be my turn next; but the place gave me so much horror, reflecting that it was the place of my unhappy birth, and of my mother’s misfortunes, and that I could not bear it, so I was forced to leave off going to see them.\n\nAnd oh! could I have but taken warning by their disasters, I had been happy still, for I was yet free, and had nothing brought against me; but it could not be, my measure was not yet filled up.\n\nMy comrade, having the brand of an old offender, was executed; the young offender was spared, having obtained a reprieve, but lay starving a long while in prison, till at last she got her name into what they call a circuit pardon, and so came off.\n\nThis terrible example of my comrade frighted me heartily, and for a good while I made no excursions; but one night, in the neighbourhood of my governess’s house, they cried “Fire.” My governess looked out, for we were all up, and cried immediately that such a gentlewoman’s house was all of a light fire atop, and so indeed it was. Here she gives me a job. “Now, child,” says she, “there is a rare opportunity, for the fire being so near that you may go to it before the street is blocked up with the crowd.” She presently gave me my cue. “Go, child,” says she, “to the house, and run in and tell the lady, or anybody you see, that you come to help them, and that you came from such a gentlewoman (that is, one of her acquaintance farther up the street).” She gave me the like cue to the next house, naming another name that was also an acquaintance of the gentlewoman of the house.\n\nAway I went, and, coming to the house, I found them all in confusion, you may be sure. I ran in, and finding one of the maids, “Lord! sweetheart,” says I, “how came this dismal accident? Where is your mistress? Any how does she do? Is she safe? And where are the children? I come from Madam —— to help you.” Away runs the maid. “Madam, madam,” says she, screaming as loud as she could yell, “here is a gentlewoman come from Madam —— to help us.” The poor woman, half out of her wits, with a bundle under her arm, an two little children, comes toward me. “Lord! madam,” says I, “let me carry the poor children to Madam ——,” she desires you to send them; she’ll take care of the poor lambs;’ and immediately I takes one of them out of her hand, and she lifts the other up into my arms. “Ay, do, for God’s sake,” says she, “carry them to her. Oh! thank her for her kindness.” “Have you anything else to secure, madam?” says I; “she will take care of it.” “Oh dear! ay,” says she, “God bless her, and thank her. Take this bundle of plate and carry it to her too. Oh, she is a good woman. Oh Lord! we are utterly ruined, utterly undone!” And away she runs from me out of her wits, and the maids after her; and away comes I with the two children and the bundle.\n\nI was no sooner got into the street but I saw another woman come to me. “Oh!” says she, “mistress,” in a piteous tone, “you will let fall the child. Come, this is a sad time; let me help you”; and immediately lays hold of my bundle to carry it for me. “No,” says I; “if you will help me, take the child by the hand, and lead it for me but to the upper end of the street; I’ll go with you and satisfy you for your pains.”\n\nShe could not avoid going, after what I said; but the creature, in short, was one of the same business with me, and wanted nothing but the bundle; however, she went with me to the door, for she could not help it. When we were come there I whispered her, “Go, child,” said I, “I understand your trade; you may meet with purchase enough.”\n\nShe understood me and walked off. I thundered at the door with the children, and as the people were raised before by the noise of the fire, I was soon let in, and I said, “Is madam awake? Pray tell her Mrs. —— desires the favour of her to take the two children in; poor lady, she will be undone, their house is all of a flame,” They took the children in very civilly, pitied the family in distress, and away came I with my bundle. One of the maids asked me if I was not to leave the bundle too. I said, “No, sweetheart, ’tis to go to another place; it does not belong to them.”\n\nI was a great way out of the hurry now, and so I went on, clear of anybody’s inquiry, and brought the bundle of plate, which was very considerable, straight home, and gave it to my old governess. She told me she would not look into it, but bade me go out again to look for more.\n\nShe gave me the like cue to the gentlewoman of the next house to that which was on fire, and I did my endeavour to go, but by this time the alarm of fire was so great, and so many engines playing, and the street so thronged with people, that I could not get near the house whatever I would do; so I came back again to my governess’s, and taking the bundle up into my chamber, I began to examine it. It is with horror that I tell what a treasure I found there; ’tis enough to say, that besides most of the family plate, which was considerable, I found a gold chain, an old-fashioned thing, the locket of which was broken, so that I suppose it had not been used some years, but the gold was not the worse for that; also a little box of burying-rings, the lady’s wedding-ring, and some broken bits of old lockets of gold, a gold watch, and a purse with about £24 value in old pieces of gold coin, and several other things of value.\n\nThis was the greatest and the worst prize that ever I was concerned in; for indeed, though, as I have said above, I was hardened now beyond the power of all reflection in other cases, yet it really touched me to the very soul when I looked into this treasure, to think of the poor disconsolate gentlewoman who had lost so much by the fire besides; and who would think, to be sure, that she had saved her plate and best things; how she would be surprised and afflicted when she should find that she had been deceived, and should find that the person that took her children and her goods, had not come, as was pretended, from the gentlewoman in the next street, but that the children had been put upon her without her own knowledge.\n\nI say, I confess the inhumanity of this action moved me very much, and made me relent exceedingly, and tears stood in my eyes upon that subject; but with all my sense of its being cruel and inhuman, I could never find in my heart to make any restitution. The reflection wore off, and I began quickly to forget the circumstances that attended the taking them.\n\nNor was this all; for though by this job I was become considerably richer than before, yet the resolution I had formerly taken, of leaving off this horrid trade when I had gotten a little more, did not return, but I must still get farther, and more; and the avarice joined so with the success, that I had no more thought of coming to a timely alteration of life, though without it I could expect no safety, no tranquillity in the possession of what I had so wickedly gained; but a little more, and a little more, was the case still.\n\nAt length, yielding to the importunities of my crime, I cast off all remorse and repentance, and all the reflections on that head turned to no more than this, that I might perhaps come to have one booty more that might complete my desires; but though I certainly had that one booty, yet every hit looked towards another, and was so encouraging to me to go on with the trade, that I had no gust to the thought of laying it down.\n\nIn this condition, hardened by success, and resolving to go on, I fell into the snare in which I was appointed to meet with my last reward for this kind of life. But even this was not yet, for I met with several successful adventures more in this way of being undone.\n\nI remained still with my governess, who was for a while really concerned for the misfortune of my comrade that had been hanged, and who, it seems, knew enough of my governess to have sent her the same way, and which made her very uneasy; indeed, she was in a very great fright.\n\nIt is true that when she was gone, and had not opened mouth to tell what she knew, my governess was easy as to that point, and perhaps glad she was hanged, for it was in her power to have obtained a pardon at the expense of her friends; but on the other hand, the loss of her, and the sense of her kindness in not making her market of what she knew, moved my governess to mourn very sincerely for her. I comforted her as well as I could, and she in return hardened me to merit more completely the same fate.\n\nHowever, as I have said, it made me the more wary, and particularly I was very shy of shoplifting, especially among the mercers and drapers, who are a set of fellows that have their eyes very much about them. I made a venture or two among the lace folks and the milliners, and particularly at one shop where I got notice of two young women who were newly set up, and had not been bred to the trade. There I think I carried off a piece of bone-lace, worth six or seven pounds, and a paper of thread. But this was but once; it was a trick that would not serve again.\n\nIt was always reckoned a safe job when we heard of a new shop, and especially when the people were such as were not bred to shops. Such may depend upon it that they will be visited once or twice at their beginning, and they must be very sharp indeed if they can prevent it.\n\nI made another adventure or two, but they were but trifles too, though sufficient to live on. After this nothing considerable offering for a good while, I began to think that I must give over the trade in earnest; but my governess, who was not willing to lose me, and expected great things of me, brought me one day into company with a young woman and a fellow that went for her husband, though as it appeared afterwards, she was not his wife, but they were partners, it seems, in the trade they carried on, and partners in something else. In short, they robbed together, lay together, were taken together, and at last were hanged together.\n\nI came into a kind of league with these two by the help of my governess, and they carried me out into three or four adventures, where I rather saw them commit some coarse and unhandy robberies, in which nothing but a great stock of impudence on their side, and gross negligence on the people’s side who were robbed, could have made them successful. So I resolved from that time forward to be very cautious how I adventured upon anything with them; and indeed, when two or three unlucky projects were proposed by them, I declined the offer, and persuaded them against it. One time they particularly proposed robbing a watchmaker of three gold watches, which they had eyed in the daytime, and found the place where he laid them. One of them had so many keys of all kinds, that he made no question to open the place where the watchmaker had laid them; and so we made a kind of an appointment; but when I came to look narrowly into the thing, I found they proposed breaking open the house, and this, as a thing out of my way, I would not embark in, so they went without me. They did get into the house by main force, and broke up the locked place where the watches were, but found but one of the gold watches, and a silver one, which they took, and got out of the house again very clear. But the family, being alarmed, cried out “Thieves,” and the man was pursued and taken; the young woman had got off too, but unhappily was stopped at a distance, and the watches found upon her. And thus I had a second escape, for they were convicted, and both hanged, being old offenders, though but young people. As I said before that they robbed together and lay together, so now they hanged together, and there ended my new partnership.\n\nI began now to be very wary, having so narrowly escaped a scouring, and having such an example before me; but I had a new tempter, who prompted me every day—I mean my governess; and now a prize presented, which as it came by her management, so she expected a good share of the booty. There was a good quantity of Flanders lace lodged in a private house, where she had gotten intelligence of it, and Flanders lace being prohibited, it was a good booty to any custom-house officer that could come at it. I had a full account from my governess, as well of the quantity as of the very place where it was concealed, and I went to a custom-house officer, and told him I had such a discovery to make to him of such a quantity of lace, if he would assure me that I should have my due share of the reward. This was so just an offer, that nothing could be fairer; so he agreed, and taking a constable and me with him, we beset the house. As I told him I could go directly to the place, he left it to me; and the hole being very dark, I squeezed myself into it, with a candle in my hand, and so reached the pieces out to him, taking care as I gave him some so to secure as much about myself as I could conveniently dispose of. There was near £300 worth of lace in the hole, and I secured about £50 worth of it to myself. The people of the house were not owners of the lace, but a merchant who had entrusted them with it; so that they were not so surprised as I thought they would be.\n\nI left the officer overjoyed with his prize, and fully satisfied with what he had got, and appointed to meet him at a house of his own directing, where I came after I had disposed of the cargo I had about me, of which he had not the least suspicion. When I came to him he began to capitulate with me, believing I did not understand the right I had to a share in the prize, and would fain have put me off with £20, but I let him know that I was not so ignorant as he supposed I was; and yet I was glad, too, that he offered to bring me to a certainty.\n\nI asked £100, and he rose up to £30; I fell to £80, and he rose again to £40; in a word, he offered £50, and I consented, only demanding a piece of lace, which I thought came to about £8 or £9, as if it had been for my own wear, and he agreed to it. So I got £50 in money paid me that same night, and made an end of the bargain; nor did he ever know who I was, or where to inquire for me, so that if it had been discovered that part of the goods were embezzled, he could have made no challenge upon me for it.\n\nI very punctually divided this spoil with my governess, and I passed with her from this time for a very dexterous manager in the nicest cases. I found that this last was the best and easiest sort of work that was in my way, and I made it my business to inquire out prohibited goods, and after buying some, usually betrayed them, but none of these discoveries amounted to anything considerable, not like that I related just now; but I was willing to act safe, and was still cautious of running the great risks which I found others did, and in which they miscarried every day.\n\nThe next thing of moment was an attempt at a gentlewoman’s good watch. It happened in a crowd, at a meeting-house, where I was in very great danger of being taken. I had full hold of her watch, but giving a great jostle, as if somebody had thrust me against her, and in the juncture giving the watch a fair pull, I found it would not come, so I let it go that moment, and cried out as if I had been killed, that somebody had trod upon my foot, and that there were certainly pickpockets there, for somebody or other had given a pull at my watch; for you are to observe that on these adventures we always went very well dressed, and I had very good clothes on, and a gold watch by my side, as like a lady as other fold.\n\nI had no sooner said so, but the other gentlewoman cried out “A pickpocket” too, for somebody, she said, had tried to pull her watch away.\n\nWhen I touched her watch I was close to her, but when I cried out I stopped as it were short, and the crowd bearing her forward a little, she made a noise too, but it was at some distance from me, so that she did not in the least suspect me; but when she cried out “A pickpocket,” somebody cried, “Ay, and here has been another! this gentlewoman has been attempted too.”\n\nAt that very instance, a little farther in the crowd, and very luckily too, they cried out “A pickpocket,” again, and really seized a young fellow in the very act. This, though unhappy for the wretch, was very opportunely for my case, though I had carried it off handsomely enough before; but now it was out of doubt, and all the loose part of the crowd ran that way, and the poor boy was delivered up to the rage of the street, which is a cruelty I need not describe, and which, however, they are always glad of, rather than to be sent to Newgate, where they lie often a long time, till they are almost perished, and sometimes they are hanged, and the best they can look for, if they are convicted, is to be transported.\n\nThis was a narrow escape to me, and I was so frighted that I ventured no more at gold watches a great while. There was indeed a great many concurring circumstances in this adventure which assisted to my escape; but the chief was, that the woman whose watch I had pulled at was a fool; that is to say, she was ignorant of the nature of the attempt, which one would have thought she should not have been, seeing she was wise enough to fasten her watch so that it could not be slipped up. But she was in such a fright that she had no thought about her proper for the discovery; for she, when she felt the pull, screamed out, and pushed herself forward, and put all the people about her into disorder, but said not a word of her watch, or of a pickpocket, for at least two minutes’ time, which was time enough for me, and to spare. For as I had cried out behind her, as I have said, and bore myself back in the crowd as she bore forward, there were several people, at least seven or eight, the throng being still moving on, that were got between me and her in that time, and then I crying out “A pickpocket,” rather sooner than she, or at least as soon, she might as well be the person suspected as I, and the people were confused in their inquiry; whereas, had she with a presence of mind needful on such an occasion, as soon as she felt the pull, not screamed out as she did, but turned immediately round and seized the next body that was behind her, she had infallibly taken me.\n\nThis is a direction not of the kindest sort to the fraternity, but ’tis certainly a key to the clue of a pickpocket’s motions, and whoever can follow it will as certainly catch the thief as he will be sure to miss if he does not.\n\nI had another adventure, which puts this matter out of doubt, and which may be an instruction for posterity in the case of a pickpocket. My good old governess, to give a short touch at her history, though she had left off the trade, was, as I may say, born a pickpocket, and, as I understood afterwards, had run through all the several degrees of that art, and yet had never been taken but once, when she was so grossly detected, that she was convicted and ordered to be transported; but being a woman of a rare tongue, and withal having money in her pocket, she found means, the ship putting into Ireland for provisions, to get on shore there, where she lived and practised her old trade for some years; when falling into another sort of bad company, she turned midwife and procuress, and played a hundred pranks there, which she gave me a little history of in confidence between us as we grew more intimate; and it was to this wicked creature that I owed all the art and dexterity I arrived to, in which there were few that ever went beyond me, or that practised so long without any misfortune.\n\nIt was after those adventures in Ireland, and when she was pretty well known in that country, that she left Dublin and came over to England, where, the time of her transportation being not expired, she left her former trade, for fear of falling into bad hands again, for then she was sure to have gone to wreck. Here she set up the same trade she had followed in Ireland, in which she soon, by her admirable management and good tongue, arrived to the height which I have already described, and indeed began to be rich, though her trade fell off again afterwards, as I have hinted before.\n\nI mentioned thus much of the history of this woman here, the better to account for the concern she had in the wicked life I was now leading, into all the particulars of which she led me, as it were, by the hand, and gave me such directions, and I so well followed them, that I grew the greatest artist of my time and worked myself out of every danger with such dexterity, that when several more of my comrades ran themselves into Newgate presently, and by that time they had been half a year at the trade, I had now practised upwards of five years, and the people at Newgate did not so much as know me; they had heard much of me indeed, and often expected me there, but I always got off, though many times in the extremest danger.\n\nOne of the greatest dangers I was now in, was that I was too well known among the trade, and some of them, whose hatred was owing rather to envy than any injury I had done them, began to be angry that I should always escape when they were always catched and hurried to Newgate. These were they that gave me the name of Moll Flanders; for it was no more of affinity with my real name or with any of the name I had ever gone by, than black is of kin to white, except that once, as before, I called myself Mrs. Flanders; when I sheltered myself in the Mint; but that these rogues never knew, nor could I ever learn how they came to give me the name, or what the occasion of it was.\n\nI was soon informed that some of these who were gotten fast into Newgate had vowed to impeach me; and as I knew that two or three of them were but too able to do it, I was under a great concern about it, and kept within doors for a good while. But my governess—whom I always made partner in my success, and who now played a sure game with me, for that she had a share of the gain and no share in the hazard—I say, my governess was something impatient of my leading such a useless, unprofitable life, as she called it; and she laid a new contrivance for my going abroad, and this was to dress me up in men’s clothes, and so put me into a new kind of practice.\n\nI was tall and personable, but a little too smooth-faced for a man; however, I seldom went abroad but in the night, it did well enough; but it was a long time before I could behave in my new clothes—I mean, as to my craft. It was impossible to be so nimble, so ready, so dexterous at these things in a dress so contrary to nature; and I did everything clumsily, so I had neither the success nor the easiness of escape that I had before, and I resolved to leave it off; but that resolution was confirmed soon after by the following accident.\n\nAs my governess disguised me like a man, so she joined me with a man, a young fellow that was nimble enough at his business, and for about three weeks we did very well together. Our principal trade was watching shopkeepers’ counters, and slipping off any kind of goods we could see carelessly laid anywhere, and we made several good bargains, as we called them, at this work. And as we kept always together, so we grew very intimate, yet he never knew that I was not a man, nay, though I several times went home with him to his lodgings, according as our business directed, and four or five times lay with him all night. But our design lay another way, and it was absolutely necessary to me to conceal my sex from him, as appeared afterwards. The circumstances of our living, coming in late, and having such and such business to do as required that nobody should be trusted with the coming into our lodgings, were such as made it impossible to me to refuse lying with him, unless I would have owned my sex; and as it was, I effectually concealed myself. But his ill, and my good fortune, soon put an end to this life, which I must own I was sick of too, on several other accounts. We had made several prizes in this new way of business, but the last would be extraordinary. There was a shop in a certain street which had a warehouse behind it that looked into another street, the house making the corner of the turning.\n\nThrough the window of the warehouse we saw, lying on the counter or showboard, which was just before it, five pieces of silks, besides other stuffs, and though it was almost dark, yet the people, being busy in the fore-shop with customers, had not had time to shut up those windows, or else had forgot it.\n\nThis the young fellow was so overjoyed with, that he could not restrain himself. It lay all within his reach he said, and he swore violently to me that he would have it, if he broke down the house for it. I dissuaded him a little, but saw there was no remedy; so he ran rashly upon it, slipped out a square of the sash window dexterously enough, and without noise, and got out four pieces of the silks, and came with them towards me, but was immediately pursued with a terrible clutter and noise. We were standing together indeed, but I had not taken any of the goods out of his hand, when I said to him hastily, “You are undone, fly, for God’s sake!” He ran like lightning, and I too, but the pursuit was hotter after him because he had the goods, than after me. He dropped two of the pieces, which stopped them a little, but the crowd increased and pursued us both. They took him soon after with the other two pieces upon him, and then the rest followed me. I ran for it and got into my governess’s house whither some quick-eyed people followed me so warmly as to fix me there. They did not immediately knock, at the door, by which I got time to throw off my disguise and dress me in my own clothes; besides, when they came there, my governess, who had her tale ready, kept her door shut, and called out to them and told them there was no man come in there. The people affirmed there did a man come in there, and swore they would break open the door.\n\nMy governess, not at all surprised, spoke calmly to them, told them they should very freely come and search her house, if they should bring a constable, and let in none but such as the constable would admit, for it was unreasonable to let in a whole crowd. This they could not refuse, though they were a crowd. So a constable was fetched immediately, and she very freely opened the door; the constable kept the door, and the men he appointed searched the house, my governess going with them from room to room. When she came to my room she called to me, and said aloud, “Cousin, pray open the door; here’s some gentlemen that must come and look into your room.”\n\nI had a little girl with me, which was my governess’s grandchild, as she called her; and I bade her open the door, and there sat I at work with a great litter of things about me, as if I had been at work all day, being myself quite undressed, with only night-clothes on my head, and a loose morning-gown wrapped about me. My governess made a kind of excuse for their disturbing me, telling me partly the occasion of it, and that she had no remedy but to open the doors to them, and let them satisfy themselves, for all she could say to them would not satisfy them. I sat still, and bid them search the room if they pleased, for if there was anybody in the house, I was sure they were not in my room; and as for the rest of the house, I had nothing to say to that, I did not understand what they looked for.\n\nEverything looked so innocent and so honest about me, that they treated me civiller than I expected, but it was not till they had searched the room to a nicety, even under the bed, in the bed, and everywhere else where it was possible anything could be hid. When they had done this, and could find nothing, they asked my pardon for troubling me, and went down.\n\nWhen they had thus searched the house from bottom to top, and then top to bottom, and could find nothing, they appeased the mob pretty well; but they carried my governess before the justice. Two men swore that they saw the man whom they pursued go into her house. My governess rattled and made a great noise that her house should be insulted, and that she should be used thus for nothing; that if a man did come in, he might go out again presently for aught she knew, for she was ready to make oath that no man had been within her doors all that day as she knew of (and that was very true indeed); that it might be indeed that as she was abovestairs, any fellow in a fright might find the door open and run in for shelter when he was pursued, but that she knew nothing of it; and if it had been so, he certainly went out again, perhaps at the other door, for she had another door into an alley, and so had made his escape and cheated them all.\n\nThis was indeed probable enough, and the justice satisfied himself with giving her an oath that she had not received or admitted any man into her house to conceal him, or protect or hide him from justice. This oath she might justly take, and did so, and so she was dismissed.\n\nIt is easy to judge what a fright I was in upon this occasion, and it was impossible for my governess ever to bring me to dress in that disguise again; for, as I told her, I should certainly betray myself.\nMy poor partner in this mischief was now in a bad case, for he was carried away before my Lord Mayor, and by his worship committed to Newgate, and the people that took him were so willing, as well as able, to prosecute him, that they offered themselves to enter into recognisances to appear at the sessions and pursue the charge against him.\n\nHowever, he got his indictment deferred, upon promise to discover his accomplices, and particularly the man that was concerned with him in his robbery; and he failed not to do his endeavour, for he gave in my name, whom he called Gabriel Spencer, which was the name I went by to him; and here appeared the wisdom of my concealing my name and sex from him, which, if he had ever known I had been undone.\n\nHe did all he could to discover this Gabriel Spencer; he described me, he discovered the place where he said I lodged, and, in a word, all the particulars that he could of my dwelling; but having concealed the main circumstances of my sex from him, I had a vast advantage, and he never could hear of me. He brought two or three families into trouble by his endeavouring to find me out, but they knew nothing of me, any more than that I had a fellow with me that they had seen, but knew nothing of. And as for my governess, though she was the means of his coming to me, yet it was done at second-hand, and he knew nothing of her.\n\nThis turned to his disadvantage; for having promised discoveries, but not being able to make it good, it was looked upon as trifling with the justice of the city, and he was the more fiercely pursued by the shopkeepers who took him.\n\nI was, however, terribly uneasy all this while, and that I might be quite out of the way, I went away from my governess’s for a while; but not knowing wither to wander, I took a maid-servant with me, and took the stage-coach to Dunstable, to my old landlord and landlady, where I had lived so handsomely with my Lancashire husband. Here I told her a formal story, that I expected my husband every day from Ireland, and that I had sent a letter to him that I would meet him at Dunstable at her house, and that he would certainly land, if the wind was fair, in a few days, so that I was come to spend a few days with them till he should come, for he was either come post, or in the West Chester coach, I knew not which; but whichsoever it was, he would be sure to come to that house to meet me.\n\nMy landlady was mighty glad to see me, and my landlord made such a stir with me, that if I had been a princess I could not have been better used, and here I might have been welcome a month or two if I had thought fit.\n\nBut my business was of another nature. I was very uneasy (though so well disguised that it was scarce possible to detect me) lest this fellow should somehow or other find me out; and though he could not charge me with this robbery, having persuaded him not to venture, and having also done nothing in it myself but run away, yet he might have charged me with other things, and have bought his own life at the expense of mine.\n\nThis filled me with horrible apprehensions. I had no recourse, no friend, no confidante but my old governess, and I knew no remedy but to put my life in her hands, and so I did, for I let her know where to send to me, and had several letters from her while I stayed here. Some of them almost scared me out my wits but at last she sent me the joyful news that he was hanged, which was the best news to me that I had heard a great while.\n\nI had stayed here five weeks, and lived very comfortably indeed (the secret anxiety of my mind excepted); but when I received this letter I looked pleasantly again, and told my landlady that I had received a letter from my spouse in Ireland, that I had the good news of his being very well, but had the bad news that his business would not permit him to come away so soon as he expected, and so I was like to go back again without him.\n\nMy landlady complimented me upon the good news however, that I had heard he was well. “For I have observed, madam,” says she, “you hadn’t been so pleasant as you used to be; you have been over head and ears in care for him, I dare say,” says the good woman; “’tis easy to be seen there’s an alteration in you for the better,” says she. “Well, I am sorry the esquire can’t come yet,” says my landlord; “I should have been heartily glad to have seen him. But I hope, when you have certain news of his coming, you’ll take a step hither again, madam,” says he; “you shall be very welcome whenever you please to come.”\n\nWith all these fine compliments we parted, and I came merry enough to London, and found my governess as well pleased as I was. And now she told me she would never recommend any partner to me again, for she always found, she said, that I had the best luck when I ventured by myself. And so indeed I had, for I was seldom in any danger when I was by myself, or if I was, I got out of it with more dexterity than when I was entangled with the dull measures of other people, who had perhaps less forecast, and were more rash and impatient than I; for though I had as much courage to venture as any of them, yet I used more caution before I undertook a thing, and had more presence of mind when I was to bring myself off.\n\nI have often wondered even at my own hardiness another way, that when all my companions were surprised and fell so suddenly into the hand of justice, and that I so narrowly escaped, yet I could not all this while enter into one serious resolution to leave off this trade, and especially considering that I was now very far from being poor; that the temptation of necessity, which is generally the introduction of all such wickedness, was now removed; for I had near £500 by me in ready money, on which I might have lived very well, if I had thought fit to have retired; but I say, I had not so much as the least inclination to leave off; no, not so much as I had before when I had but £200 beforehand, and when I had no such frightful examples before my eyes as these were. From hence ’tis evident to me, that when once we are hardened in crime, no fear can affect us, no example give us any warning.\n\nI had indeed one comrade whose fate went very near me for a good while, though I wore it off too in time. That case was indeed very unhappy. I had made a prize of a piece of very good damask in a mercer’s shop, and went clear off myself, but had conveyed the piece to this companion of mine when we went out of the shop, and she went one way and I went another. We had not been long out of the shop but the mercer missed his piece of stuff, and sent his messengers, one one way and one another, and they presently seized her that had the piece, with the damask upon her. As for me, I had very luckily stepped into a house where there was a lace chamber, up one pair of stairs, and had the satisfaction, or the terror indeed, of looking out of the window upon the noise they made, and seeing the poor creature dragged away in triumph to the justice, who immediately committed her to Newgate.\n\nI was careful to attempt nothing in the lace chamber, but tumbled their goods pretty much to spend time; then bought a few yards of edging and paid for it, and came away very sad-hearted indeed for the poor woman, who was in tribulation for what I only had stolen.\n\nHere again my old caution stood me in good stead; namely, that though I often robbed with these people, yet I never let them know who I was, or where I lodged, nor could they ever find out my lodging, though they often endeavoured to watch me to it. They all knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, though even some of them rather believed I was she than knew me to be so. My name was public among them indeed, but how to find me out they knew not, nor so much as how to guess at my quarters, whether they were at the east end of the town or the west; and this wariness was my safety upon all these occasions.\n\nI kept close a great while upon the occasion of this woman’s disaster. I knew that if I should do anything that should miscarry, and should be carried to prison, she would be there and ready to witness against me, and perhaps save her life at my expense. I considered that I began to be very well known by name at the Old Bailey, though they did not know my face, and that if I should fall into their hands, I should be treated as an old offender; and for this reason I was resolved to see what this poor creature’s fate should be before I stirred abroad, though several times in her distress I conveyed money to her for her relief.\n\nAt length she came to her trial. She pleaded she did not steal the thing, but that one Mrs. Flanders, as she heard her called (for she did not know her), gave the bundle to her after they came out of the shop, and bade her carry it home to her lodging. They asked her where this Mrs. Flanders was, but she could not produce her, neither could she give the least account of me; and the mercer’s men swearing positively that she was in the shop when the goods were stolen, that they immediately missed them, and pursued her, and found them upon her, thereupon the jury brought her in guilty; but the Court, considering that she was really not the person that stole the goods, an inferior assistant, and that it was very possible she could not find out this Mrs. Flanders, meaning me, though it would save her life, which indeed was true—I say, considering all this, they allowed her to be transported, which was the utmost favour she could obtain, only that the Court told her that if she could in the meantime produce the said Mrs. Flanders, they would intercede for her pardon; that is to say, if she could find me out, and hand me, she should not be transported. This I took care to make impossible to her, and so she was shipped off in pursuance of her sentence a little while after.\n\nI must repeat it again, that the fate of this poor woman troubled me exceedingly, and I began to be very pensive, knowing that I was really the instrument of her disaster; but the preservation of my own life, which was so evidently in danger, took off all my tenderness; and seeing that she was not put to death, I was very easy at her transportation, because she was then out of the way of doing me any mischief, whatever should happen.\n\nThe disaster of this woman was some months before that of the last-recited story, and was indeed partly occasion of my governess proposing to dress me up in men’s clothes, that I might go about unobserved, as indeed I did; but I was soon tired of that disguise, as I have said, for indeed it exposed me to too many difficulties.\n\nI was now easy as to all fear of witnesses against me, for all those that had either been concerned with me, or that knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, were either hanged or transported; and if I should have had the misfortune to be taken, I might call myself anything else, as well as Moll Flanders, and no old sins could be placed into my account; so I began to run a-tick again with the more freedom, and several successful adventures I made, though not such as I had made before.\n\nWe had at that time another fire happened not a great way off from the place where my governess lived, and I made an attempt there, as before, but as I was not soon enough before the crowd of people came in, and could not get to the house I aimed at, instead of a prize, I got a mischief, which had almost put a period to my life and all my wicked doings together; for the fire being very furious, and the people in a great fright in removing their goods, and throwing them out of window, a wench from out of a window threw a feather-bed just upon me. It is true, the bed being soft, it broke no bones; but as the weight was great, and made greater by the fall, it beat me down, and laid me dead for a while. Nor did the people concern themselves much to deliver me from it, or to recover me at all; but I lay like one dead and neglected a good while, till somebody going to remove the bed out of the way, helped me up. It was indeed a wonder the people in the house had not thrown other goods out after it, and which might have fallen upon it, and then I had been inevitably killed; but I was reserved for further afflictions.\n\nThis accident, however, spoiled my market for that time, and I came home to my governess very much hurt and bruised, and frighted to the last degree, and it was a good while before she could set me upon my feet again.\n\nIt was now a merry time of the year, and Bartholomew Fair was begun. I had never made any walks that way, nor was the common part of the fair of much advantage to me; but I took a turn this year into the cloisters, and among the rest I fell into one of the raffling shops. It was a thing of no great consequence to me, nor did I expect to make much of it; but there came a gentleman extremely well dressed and very rich, and as ’tis frequent to talk to everybody in those shops, he singled me out, and was very particular with me. First he told me he would put in for me to raffle, and did so; and some small matter coming to his lot, he presented it to me (I think it was a feather muff); then he continued to keep talking to me with a more than common appearance of respect, but still very civil, and much like a gentleman.\n\nHe held me in talk so long, till at last he drew me out of the raffling place to the shop-door, and then to a walk in the cloister, still talking of a thousand things cursorily without anything to the purpose. At last he told me that, without compliment, he was charmed with my company, and asked me if I durst trust myself in a coach with him; he told me he was a man of honour, and would not offer anything to me unbecoming him as such. I seemed to decline it a while, but suffered myself to be importuned a little, and then yielded.\n\nI was at a loss in my thoughts to conclude at first what this gentleman designed; but I found afterwards he had had some drink in his head, and that he was not very unwilling to have some more. He carried me in the coach to the Spring Garden, at Knightsbridge, where we walked in the gardens, and he treated me very handsomely; but I found he drank very freely. He pressed me also to drink, but I declined it.\n\nHitherto he kept his word with me, and offered me nothing amiss. We came away in the coach again, and he brought me into the streets, and by this time it was near ten o’clock at night, and he stopped the coach at a house where, it seems, he was acquainted, and where they made no scruple to show us upstairs into a room with a bed in it. At first I seemed to be unwilling to go up, but after a few words I yielded to that too, being willing to see the end of it, and in hope to make something of it at last. As for the bed, etc., I was not much concerned about that part.\n\nHere he began to be a little freer with me than he had promised; and I by little and little yielded to everything, so that, in a word, he did what he pleased with me; I need say no more. All this while he drank freely too, and about one in the morning we went into the coach again. The air and the shaking of the coach made the drink he had get more up in his head than it was before, and he grew uneasy in the coach, and was for acting over again what he had been doing before; but as I thought my game now secure, I resisted him, and brought him to be a little still, which had not lasted five minutes but he fell fast asleep.\n\nI took this opportunity to search him to a nicety. I took a gold watch, with a silk purse of gold, his fine full-bottom periwig and silver-fringed gloves, his sword and fine snuff-box, and gently opening the coach door, stood ready to jump out while the coach was going on; but the coach stopped in the narrow street beyond Temple Bar to let another coach pass, I got softly out, fastened the door again, and gave my gentleman and the coach the slip both together, and never heard more of them.\n\nThis was an adventure indeed unlooked for, and perfectly undesigned by me; though I was not so past the merry part of life, as to forget how to behave, when a fop so blinded by his appetite should not know an old woman from a young. I did not indeed look so old as I was by ten or twelve years; yet I was not a young wench of seventeen, and it was easy enough to be distinguished. There is nothing so absurd, so surfeiting, so ridiculous, as a man heated by wine in his head, and wicked gust in his inclination together; he is in the possession of two devils at once, and can no more govern himself by his reason than a mill can grind without water; his vice tramples upon all that was in him that had any good in it, if any such thing there was; nay, his very sense is blinded by its own rage, and he acts absurdities even in his views; such a drinking more, when he is drunk already; picking up a common woman, without regard to what she is or who she is, whether sound or rotten, clean or unclean, whether ugly or handsome, whether old or young, and so blinded as not really to distinguish. Such a man is worse than a lunatic; prompted by his vicious, corrupted head, he no more knows what he is doing than this wretch of mine knew when I picked his pocket of his watch and his purse of gold.\n\nThese are the men of whom Solomon says, “They go like an ox to the slaughter, till a dart strikes through their liver”; an admirable description, by the way, of the foul disease, which is a poisonous deadly contagion mingling with the blood, whose centre or foundation is in the liver; from whence, by the swift circulation of the whole mass, that dreadful nauseous plague strikes immediately through his liver, and his spirits are infected, his vitals stabbed through as with a dart.\n\nIt is true this poor unguarded wretch was in no danger from me, though I was greatly apprehensive at first of what danger I might be in from him; but he was really to be pitied in one respect, that he seemed to be a good sort of man in himself; a gentleman that had no harm in his design; a man of sense, and of a fine behaviour, a comely handsome person, a sober solid countenance, a charming beautiful face, and everything that could be agreeable; only had unhappily had some drink the night before, had not been in bed, as he told me when we were together; was hot, and his blood fired with wine, and in that condition his reason, as it were asleep, had given him up.\n\nAs for me, my business was his money, and what I could make of him; and after that, if I could have found out any way to have done it, I would have sent him safe home to his house and to his family, for ’twas ten to one but he had an honest, virtuous wife and innocent children, that were anxious for his safety, and would have been glad to have gotten him home, and have taken care of him till he was restored to himself. And then with what shame and regret would he look back upon himself! how would he reproach himself with associating himself with a whore! picked up in the worst of all holes, the cloister, among the dirt and filth of all the town! how would he be trembling for fear he had got the pox, for fear a dart had struck through his liver, and hate himself every time he looked back upon the madness and brutality of his debauch! how would he, if he had any principles of honour, as I verily believe he had—I say, how would he abhor the thought of giving any ill distemper, if he had it, as for aught he knew he might, to his modest and virtuous wife, and thereby sowing the contagion in the life-blood of his posterity.\n\nWould such gentlemen but consider the contemptible thoughts which the very women they are concerned with, in such cases as these, have of them, it would be a surfeit to them. As I said above, they value not the pleasure, they are raised by no inclination to the man, the passive jade thinks of no pleasure but the money; and when he is, as it were, drunk in the ecstasies of his wicked pleasure, her hands are in his pockets searching for what she can find there, and of which he can no more be sensible in the moment of his folly that he can forethink of it when he goes about it.\n\nI knew a woman that was so dexterous with a fellow, who indeed deserved no better usage, that while he was busy with her another way, conveyed his purse with twenty guineas in it out of his fob-pocket, where he had put it for fear of her, and put another purse with gilded counters in it into the room of it. After he had done, he says to her, “Now han’t you picked my pocket?” She jested with him, and told him she supposed he had not much to lose; he put his hand to his fob, and with his fingers felt that his purse was there, which fully satisfied him, and so she brought off his money. And this was a trade with her; she kept a sham gold watch, that is, a watch of silver gilt, and a purse of counters in her pocket to be ready on all such occasions, and I doubt not practiced it with success.\n\nI came home with this last booty to my governess, and really when I told her the story, it so affected her that she was hardly able to forbear tears, to know how such a gentleman ran a daily risk of being undone every time a glass of wine got into his head.\n\nBut as to the purchase I got, and how entirely I stripped him, she told me it pleased her wonderfully. “Nay child,” says she, “the usage may, for aught I know, do more to reform him than all the sermons that ever he will hear in his life.” And if the remainder of the story be true, so it did.\n\nI found the next day she was wonderful inquisitive about this gentleman; the description I had given her of him, his dress, his person, his face, everything concurred to make her think of a gentleman whose character she knew, and family too. She mused a while, and I going still on with the particulars, she starts up; says she, “I’ll lay £100 I know the gentleman.”\n\n“I am sorry you do,” says I, “for I would not have him exposed on any account in the world; he has had injury enough already by me, and I would not be instrumental to do him any more.” “No, no,” says she, “I will do him no injury, I assure you, but you may let me satisfy my curiosity a little, for if it is he, I warrant you I find it out.” I was a little startled at that, and told her, with an apparent concern in my face, that by the same rule he might find me out, and then I was undone. She returned warmly, “Why, do you think I will betray you, child? No, no,” says she, “not for all he is worth in the world. I have kept your counsel in worse things than these; sure you may trust me in this.” So I said no more at that time.\n\nShe laid her scheme another way, and without acquainting me of it, but she was resolved to find it out if possible. So she goes to a certain friend of hers who was acquainted in the family that she guessed at, and told her friend she had some extraordinary business with such a gentleman (who, by the way, was no less than a baronet, and of a very good family), and that she knew not how to come at him without somebody to introduce her. Her friend promised her very readily to do it, and accordingly goes to the house to see if the gentleman was in town.\n\nThe next day she come to my governess and tells her that Sir —— was at home, but that he had met with a disaster and was very ill, and there was no speaking with him. “What disaster?” says my governess hastily, as if she was surprised at it. “Why,” says her friend, “he had been at Hampstead to visit a gentleman of his acquaintance, and as he came back again he was set upon and robbed; and having got a little drink too, as they suppose, the rogues abused him, and he is very ill.” “Robbed!” says my governess, “and what did they take from him?” “Why,” says her friend, “they took his gold watch and his gold snuff-box, his fine periwig, and what money he had in his pocket, which was considerable, to be sure, for Sir —— never goes without a purse of guineas about him.”\n“Pshaw!” says my old governess, jeering, “I warrant you he has got drunk now and got a whore, and she has picked his pocket, and so he comes home to his wife and tells her he has been robbed. That’s an old sham; a thousand such tricks are put upon the poor women every day.”\n\n“Fie!” says her friend, “I find you don’t know Sir ——; why he is as civil a gentleman, there is not a finer man, nor a soberer, graver, modester person in the whole city; he abhors such things; there’s nobody that knows him will think such a thing of him.” “Well, well,” says my governess, “that’s none of my business; if it was, I warrant I should find there was something of that kind in it; your modest men in common opinion are sometimes no better than other people, only they keep a better character, or, if you please, are the better hypocrites.”\n“No, no,” says her friend, “I can assure you Sir —— is no hypocrite, he is really an honest, sober gentleman, and he has certainly been robbed.” “Nay,” says my governess, “it may be he has; it is no business of mine, I tell you; I only want to speak with him; my business is of another nature.” “But,” says her friend, “let your business be of what nature it will, you cannot see him yet, for he is not fit to be seen, for he is very ill, and bruised very much.” “Ay,” says my governess, “nay, then he has fallen into bad hands, to be sure.” And then she asked gravely, “Pray, where is he bruised?” “Why, in the head,” says her friend, “and one of his hands, and his face, for they used him barbarously.” “Poor gentleman,” says my governess, “I must wait, then, till he recovers”; and adds, “I hope it will not be long, for I want very much to speak with him.”\n\nAway she comes to me and tells me this story. “I have found out your fine gentleman, and a fine gentleman he was,” says she; “but, mercy on him, he is in a sad pickle now. I wonder what the d—l you have done to him; why, you have almost killed him.” I looked at her with disorder enough. “I killed him!” says I; “you must mistake the person; I am sure I did nothing to him; he was very well when I left him,” said I, “only drunk and fast asleep.” “I know nothing of that,” says she, “but he is in a sad pickle now”; and so she told me all that her friend had said to her. “Well, then,” says I, “he fell into bad hands after I left him, for I am sure I left him safe enough.”\n\nAbout ten days after, or a little more, my governess goes again to her friend, to introduce her to this gentleman; she had inquired other ways in the meantime, and found that he was about again, if not abroad again, so she got leave to speak with him.\n\nShe was a woman of a admirable address, and wanted nobody to introduce her; she told her tale much better than I shall be able to tell it for her, for she was a mistress of her tongue, as I have said already. She told him that she came, though a stranger, with a single design of doing him a service and he should find she had no other end in it; that as she came purely on so friendly an account, she begged promise from him, that if he did not accept what she should officiously propose he would not take it ill that she meddled with what was not her business. She assured him that as what she had to say was a secret that belonged to him only, so whether he accepted her offer or not, it should remain a secret to all the world, unless he exposed it himself; nor should his refusing her service in it make her so little show her respect as to do him the least injury, so that he should be entirely at liberty to act as he thought fit.\n\nHe looked very shy at first, and said he knew nothing that related to him that required much secrecy; that he had never done any man any wrong, and cared not what anybody might say of him; that it was no part of his character to be unjust to anybody, nor could he imagine in what any man could render him any service; but that if it was so disinterested a service as she said, he could not take it ill from any one that they should endeavour to serve him; and so, as it were, left her a liberty either to tell him or not to tell, as she thought fit.\n\nShe found him so perfectly indifferent, that she was almost afraid to enter into the point with him; but, however, after some other circumlocutions she told him that by a strange and unaccountable accident she came to have a particular knowledge of the late unhappy adventure he had fallen into, and that in such a manner, that there was nobody in the world but herself and him that were acquainted with it, no, not the very person that was with him.\n\nHe looked a little angrily at first. “What adventure?” said he. “Why,” said she, “of your being robbed coming from Knightbr——; Hampstead, sir, I should say,” says she. “Be not surprised, sir,” says she, “that I am able to tell you every step you took that day from the cloister in Smithfield to the Spring Garden at Knightsbridge, and thence to the —— in the Strand, and how you were left asleep in the coach afterwards. I say, let not this surprise you, for, sir, I do not come to make a booty of you, I ask nothing of you, and I assure you the woman that was with you knows nothing who you are, and never shall; and yet perhaps I may serve you further still, for I did not come barely to let you know that I was informed of these things, as if I wanted a bribe to conceal them; assure yourself, sir,” said she, “that whatever you think fit to do or say to me, it shall be all a secret as it is, as much as if I were in my grave.”\n\nHe was astonished at her discourse, and said gravely to her, “Madam, you are a stranger to me, but it is very unfortunate that you should be let into the secret of the worst action of my life, and a thing that I am so justly ashamed of, that the only satisfaction of it to me was, that I thought it was known only to God and my own conscience.” “Pray, sir,” says she, “do not reckon the discovery of it to me to be any part of your misfortune. It was a thing, I believe, you were surprised into, and perhaps the woman used some art to prompt you to it; however, you will never find any just cause,” said she, “to repent that I came to hear of it; nor can your own mouth be more silent in it that I have been, and ever shall be.”\n\n“Well,” says he, “but let me do some justice to the woman too; whoever she is, I do assure you she prompted me to nothing, she rather declined me. It was my own folly and madness that brought me into it all, ay, and brought her into it too; I must give her her due so far. As to what she took from me, I could expect no less from her in the condition I was in, and to this hour I know not whether she robbed me or the coachman; if she did it, I forgive her, and I think all gentlemen that do so should be used in the same manner; but I am more concerned for some other things that I am for all that she took from me.”\n\nMy governess now began to come into the whole matter, and he opened himself freely to her. First she said to him, in answer to what he had said about me, “I am glad, sir, you are so just to the person that you were with; I assure you she is a gentlewoman, and no woman of the town; and however you prevailed with her so far as you did, I am sure ’tis not her practice. You ran a great venture indeed, sir; but if that be any part of your care, I am persuaded you may be perfectly easy, for I dare assure you no man has touched her, before you, since her husband, and he has been dead now almost eight years.”\n\nIt appeared that this was his grievance, and that he was in a very great fright about it; however, when my governess said this to him, he appeared very well pleased, and said, “Well, madam, to be plain with you, if I was satisfied of that, I should not so much value what I lost; for, as to that, the temptation was great, and perhaps she was poor and wanted it.” “If she had not been poor, sir ——,” says my governess, “I assure you she would never have yielded to you; and as her poverty first prevailed with her to let you do as you did, so the same poverty prevailed with her to pay herself at last, when she saw you were in such a condition, that if she had not done it, perhaps the next coachman might have done it.”\n\n“Well,” says he, “much good may it do her. I say again, all the gentlemen that do so ought to be used in the same manner, and then they would be cautious of themselves. I have no more concern about it, but on the score which you hinted at before, madam.” Here he entered into some freedoms with her on the subject of what passed between us, which are not so proper for a woman to write, and the great terror that was upon his mind with relation to his wife, for fear he should have received any injury from me, and should communicate it farther; and asked her at last if she could not procure him an opportunity to speak with me. My governess gave him further assurances of my being a woman clear from any such thing, and that he was as entirely safe in that respect as he was with his own lady; but as for seeing me, she said it might be of dangerous consequence; but, however, that she would talk with me, and let him know my answer, using at the same time some arguments to persuade him not to desire it, and that it could be of no service to him, seeing she hoped he had no desire to renew a correspondence with me, and that on my account it was a kind of putting my life in his hands.\n\nHe told her he had a great desire to see me, that he would give her any assurances that were in his power, not to take any advantages of me, and that in the first place he would give me a general release from all demands of any kind. She insisted how it might tend to a further divulging the secret, and might in the end be injurious to him, entreating him not to press for it; so at length he desisted.\n\nThey had some discourse upon the subject of the things he had lost, and he seemed to be very desirous of his gold watch, and told her if she could procure that for him, he would willingly give as much for it as it was worth. She told him she would endeavour to procure it for him, and leave the valuing it to himself.\n\nAccordingly the next day she carried the watch, and he gave her thirty guineas for it, which was more than I should have been able to make of it, though it seems it cost much more. He spoke something of his periwig, which it seems cost him threescore guineas, and his snuff-box, and in a few days more she carried them too; which obliged him very much, and he gave her thirty more. The next day I sent him his fine sword and cane gratis, and demanded nothing of him, but I had no mind to see him, unless it had been so that he might be satisfied I knew who he was, which he was not willing to.\n\nThen he entered into a long talk with her of the manner how she came to know all this matter. She formed a long tale of that part; how she had it from one that I had told the whole story to, and that was to help me dispose of the goods; and this confidante brought the things to her, she being by profession a pawnbroker; and she hearing of his worship’s disaster, guessed at the thing in general; that having gotten the things into her hands, she had resolved to come and try as she had done. She then gave him repeated assurances that it should never go out of her mouth, and though she knew the woman very well, yet she had not let her know, meaning me, anything of it; that is to say, who the person was, which, by the way, was false; but, however, it was not to his damage, for I never opened my mouth of it to anybody.\n\nI had a great many thoughts in my head about my seeing him again, and was often sorry that I had refused it. I was persuaded that if I had seen him, and let him know that I knew him, I should have made some advantage of him, and perhaps have had some maintenance from him; and though it was a life wicked enough, yet it was not so full of danger as this I was engaged in. However, those thoughts wore off, and I declined seeing him again, for that time; but my governess saw him often, and he was very kind to her, giving her something almost every time he saw her. One time in particular she found him very merry, and as she thought he had some wine in his head, and he pressed her again very earnestly to let him see that woman that, as he said, had bewitched him so that night, my governess, who was from the beginning for my seeing him, told him he was so desirous of it that she could almost yield of it, if she could prevail upon me; adding that if he would please to come to her house in the evening, she would endeavour it, upon his repeated assurances of forgetting what was past.\n\nAccordingly she came to me, and told me all the discourse; in short, she soon biassed me to consent, in a case which I had some regret in my mind for declining before; so I prepared to see him. I dressed me to all the advantage possible, I assure you, and for the first time used a little art; I say for the first time, for I had never yielded to the baseness of paint before, having always had vanity enough to believe I had no need of it.\n\nAt the hour appointed he came; and as she observed before, so it was plain still, that he had been drinking, though very far from what we call being in drink. He appeared exceeding pleased to see me, and entered into a long discourse with me upon the old affair. I begged his pardon very often for my share of it, protested I had not any such design when first I met him, that I had not gone out with him but that I took him for a very civil gentleman, and that he made me so many promises of offering no uncivility to me.\n\nHe alleged the wine he drank, and that he scarce knew what he did, and that if it had not been so, I should never have let him take the freedom with me that he had done. He protested to me that he never touched any woman but me since he was married to his wife, and it was a surprise upon him; complimented me upon being so particularly agreeable to him, and the like; and talked so much of that kind, till I found he had talked himself almost into a temper to do the same thing over again. But I took him up short. I protested I had never suffered any man to touch me since my husband died, which was near eight years. He said he believed it to be so truly; and added that madam had intimated as much to him, and that it was his opinion of that part which made his desire to see me again; and that since he had once broke in upon his virtue with me, and found no ill consequences, he could be safe in venturing there again; and so, in short, it went on to what I expected, and to what will not bear relating.\n\nMy old governess had foreseen it, as well as I, and therefore led him into a room which had not a bed in it, and yet had a chamber within it which had a bed, whither we withdrew for the rest of the night; and, in short, after some time being together, he went to bed, and lay there all night. I withdrew, but came again undressed in the morning, before it was day, and lay with him the rest of the time.\n\nThus, you see, having committed a crime once is a sad handle to the committing of it again; whereas all the regret and reflections wear off when the temptation renews itself. Had I not yielded to see him again, the corrupt desire in him had worn off, and ’tis very probable he had never fallen into it with anybody else, as I really believe he had not done before.\n\nWhen he went away, I told him I hoped he was satisfied he had not been robbed again. He told me he was satisfied in that point, and could trust me again, and putting his hand in his pocket, gave me five guineas, which was the first money I had gained that way for many years.\n\nI had several visits of the like kind from him, but he never came into a settled way of maintenance, which was what I would have best pleased with. Once, indeed, he asked me how I did to live. I answered him pretty quick, that I assured him I had never taken that course that I took with him, but that indeed I worked at my needle, and could just maintain myself; that sometime it was as much as I was able to do, and I shifted hard enough.\n\nHe seemed to reflect upon himself that he should be the first person to lead me into that, which he assured me he never intended to do himself; and it touched him a little, he said, that he should be the cause of his own sin and mine too. He would often make just reflections also upon the crime itself, and upon the particular circumstances of it with respect to himself; how wine introduced the inclinations, how the devil led him to the place, and found out an object to tempt him, and he made the moral always himself.\n\nWhen these thoughts were upon him he would go away, and perhaps not come again in a month’s time or longer; but then as the serious part wore off, the lewd part would wear in, and then he came prepared for the wicked part. Thus we lived for some time; though he did not keep, as they call it, yet he never failed doing things that were handsome, and sufficient to maintain me without working, and, which was better, without following my old trade.\n\nBut this affair had its end too; for after about a year, I found that he did not come so often as usual, and at last he left if off altogether without any dislike to bidding adieu; and so there was an end of that short scene of life, which added no great store to me, only to make more work for repentance.\n\nHowever, during this interval I confined myself pretty much at home; at least, being thus provided for, I made no adventures, no, not for a quarter of a year after he left me; but then finding the fund fail, and being loth to spend upon the main stock, I began to think of my old trade, and to look abroad into the street again; and my first step was lucky enough.\n\nI had dressed myself up in a very mean habit, for as I had several shapes to appear in, I was now in an ordinary stuff-gown, a blue apron, and a straw hat and I placed myself at the door of the Three Cups Inn in St. John Street. There were several carriers used the inn, and the stage-coaches for Barnet, for Totteridge, and other towns that way stood always in the street in the evening, when they prepared to set out, so that I was ready for anything that offered, for either one or other. The meaning was this; people come frequently with bundles and small parcels to those inns, and call for such carriers or coaches as they want, to carry them into the country; and there generally attend women, porters’ wives or daughters, ready to take in such things for their respective people that employ them.\n\nIt happened very oddly that I was standing at the inn gate, and a woman that had stood there before, and which was the porter’s wife belonging to the Barnet stage-coach, having observed me, asked if I waited for any of the coaches. I told her Yes, I waited for my mistress, that was coming to go to Barnet. She asked me who was my mistress, and I told her any madam’s name that came next me; but as it seemed, I happened upon a name, a family of which name lived at Hadley, just beyond Barnet.\n\nI said no more to her, or she to me, a good while; but by and by, somebody calling her at a door a little way off, she desired me that if anybody called for the Barnet coach, I would step and call her at the house, which it seems was an alehouse. I said Yes, very readily, and away she went.\n\nShe was no sooner gone but comes a wench and a child, puffing and sweating, and asks for the Barnet coach. I answered presently, “Here.” “Do you belong to the Barnet coach?” says she. “Yes, sweetheart,” said I; “what do ye want?” “I want room for two passengers,” says she. “Where are they, sweetheart?” said I. “Here’s this girl, pray let her go into the coach,” says she, “and I’ll go and fetch my mistress.” “Make haste, then, sweetheart,” says I, “for we may be full else.” The maid had a great bundle under her arm; so she put the child into the coach, and I said, “You had best put your bundle into the coach too.” “No,” says she, “I am afraid somebody should slip it away from the child.” “Give to me, then,” said I, “and I’ll take care of it.” “Do, then,” says she, “and be sure you take of it.” “I’ll answer for it,” said I, “if it were for £20 value.” “There, take it, then,” says she, and away she goes.\n\nAs soon as I had got the bundle, and the maid was out of sight, I goes on towards the alehouse, where the porter’s wife was, so that if I had met her, I had then only been going to give her the bundle, and to call her to her business, as if I was going away, and could stay no longer; but as I did not meet her, I walked away, and turning into Charterhouse Lane, then crossed into Bartholomew Close, so into Little Britain, and through the Bluecoat Hospital, into Newgate Street.\n\nTo prevent my being known, I pulled off my blue apron, and wrapped the bundle in it, which before was made up in a piece of painted calico, and very remarkable; I also wrapped up my straw hat in it, and so put the bundle upon my head; and it was very well that I did thus, for coming through the Bluecoat Hospital, who should I meet but the wench that had given me the bundle to hold. It seems she was going with her mistress, whom she had been gone to fetch, to the Barnet coaches.\n\nI saw she was in haste, and I had no business to stop her; so away she went, and I brought my bundle safe home to my governess. There was no money, nor plate, or jewels in the bundle, but a very good suit of Indian damask, a gown and a petticoat, a laced-head and ruffles of very good Flanders lace, and some linen and other things, such as I knew very well the value of.\n\nThis was not indeed my own invention, but was given me by one that had practised it with success, and my governess liked it extremely; and indeed I tried it again several times, though never twice near the same place; for the next time I tried it in White Chapel, just by the corner of Petticoat Lane, where the coaches stand that go out to Stratford and Bow, and that side of the country, and another time at the Flying Horse, without Bishopgate, where the Cheston coaches then lay; and I had always the good luck to come off with some booty.\n\nAnother time I placed myself at a warehouse by the waterside, where the coasting vessels from the north come, such as from Newcastle-upon-Tyne, Sunderland, and other places. Here, the warehouses being shut, comes a young fellow with a letter; and he wanted a box and a hamper that was come from Newcastle-upon-Tyne. I asked him if he had the marks of it; so he shows me the letter, by virtue of which he was to ask for it, and which gave an account of the contents, the box being full of linen, and the hamper full of glass ware. I read the letter, and took care to see the name, and the marks, the name of the person that sent the goods, the name of the person that they were sent to; then I bade the messenger come in the morning, for that the warehouse-keeper would not be there any more that night.\n\nAway went I, and getting materials in a public house, I wrote a letter from Mr. John Richardson of Newcastle to his dear cousin Jemmy Cole, in London, with an account that he sent by such a vessel (for I remembered all the particulars to a title), so many pieces of huckaback linen, so many ells of Dutch holland and the like, in a box, and a hamper of flint glasses from Mr. Henzill’s glasshouse; and that the box was marked I. C. No. 1, and the hamper was directed by a label on the cording.\n\nAbout an hour after, I came to the warehouse, found the warehouse-keeper, and had the goods delivered me without any scruple; the value of the linen being about £22.\n\nI could fill up this whole discourse with the variety of such adventures, which daily invention directed to, and which I managed with the utmost dexterity, and always with success.\n\nAt length—as when does the pitcher come safe home that goes so very often to the well?—I fell into some small broils, which though they could not affect me fatally, yet made me known, which was the worst thing next to being found guilty that could befall me.\n\nI had taken up the disguise of a widow’s dress; it was without any real design in view, but only waiting for anything that might offer, as I often did. It happened that while I was going along the street in Covent Garden, there was a great cry of “Stop thief! Stop thief!” some artists had, it seems, put a trick upon a shopkeeper, and being pursued, some of them fled one way, and some another; and one of them was, they said, dressed up in widow’s weeds, upon which the mob gathered about me, and some said I was the person, others said no. Immediately came the mercer’s journeyman, and he swore aloud I was the person, and so seized on me. However, when I was brought back by the mob to the mercer’s shop, the master of the house said freely that I was not the woman that was in his shop, and would have let me go immediately; but another fellow said gravely, “Pray stay till Mr. ——” (meaning the journeyman) “comes back, for he knows her.” So they kept me by force near half an hour. They had called a constable, and he stood in the shop as my jailer; and in talking with the constable I inquired where he lived, and what trade he was; the man not apprehending in the least what happened afterwards, readily told me his name, and trade, and where he lived; and told me as a jest, that I might be sure to hear of his name when I came to the Old Bailey.\n\nSome of the servants likewise used me saucily, and had much ado to keep their hands off me; the master indeed was civiller to me than they, but he would not yet let me go, though he owned he could not say I was in his shop before.\n\nI began to be a little surly with him, and told him I hoped he would not take it ill if I made myself amends upon him in a more legal way another time; and desired I might send for friends to see me have right done me. No, he said, he could give no such liberty; I might ask it when I came before the justice of peace; and seeing I threatened him, he would take care of me in the meantime, and would lodge me safe in Newgate. I told him it was his time now, but it would be mine by and by, and governed my passion as well as I was able. However, I spoke to the constable to call me a porter, which he did, and then I called for pen, ink, and paper, but they would let me have none. I asked the porter his name, and where he lived, and the poor man told it me very willingly. I bade him observe and remember how I was treated there; that he saw I was detained there by force. I told him I should want his evidence in another place, and it should not be the worse for him to speak. The porter said he would serve me with all his heart. “But, madam,” says he, “let me hear them refuse to let you go, then I may be able to speak the plainer.”\n\nWith that I spoke aloud to the master of the shop, and said, “Sir, you know in your own conscience that I am not the person you look for, and that I was not in your shop before, therefore I demand that you detain me here no longer, or tell me the reason of your stopping me.” The fellow grew surlier upon this than before, and said he would do neither till he thought fit. “Very well,” said I to the constable and to the porter; “you will be pleased to remember this, gentlemen, another time.” The porter said, “Yes, madam”; and the constable began not to like it, and would have persuaded the mercer to dismiss him, and let me go, since, as he said, he owned I was not the person. “Good, sir,” says the mercer to him tauntingly, “are you a justice of peace or a constable? I charged you with her; pray do you do your duty.” The constable told him, a little moved, but very handsomely, “I know my duty, and what I am, sir; I doubt you hardly know what you are doing.” They had some other hard words, and in the meantime the journeyman, impudent and unmanly to the last degree, used me barbarously, and one of them, the same that first seized upon me, pretended he would search me, and began to lay hands on me. I spit in his face, called out to the constable, and bade him to take notice of my usage. “And pray, Mr. Constable,” said I, “ask that villain’s name,” pointing to the man. The constable reproved him decently, told him that he did not know what he did, for he knew that his master acknowledged I was not the person that was in his shop; “and,” says the constable, “I am afraid your master is bringing himself, and me too, into trouble, if this gentlewoman comes to prove who she is, and where she was, and it appears that she is not the woman you pretend to.” “Damn her,” says the fellow again, with a impudent, hardened face, “she is the lady, you may depend upon it; I’ll swear she is the same body that was in the shop, and that I gave the pieces of satin that is lost into her own hand. You shall hear more of it when Mr. William and Mr. Anthony (those were other journeymen) come back; they will know her again as well as I.”\n\nJust as the insolent rogue was talking thus to the constable, comes back Mr. William and Mr. Anthony, as he called them, and a great rabble with them, bringing along with them the true widow that I was pretended to be; and they came sweating and blowing into the shop, and with a great deal of triumph, dragging the poor creature in the most butcherly manner up towards their master, who was in the back shop, and cried out aloud, “Here’s the widow, sir; we have catched her at last.” “What do ye mean by that?” says the master. “Why, we have her already; there she sits,” says he, “and Mr. ——,” says he, “can swear this is she.” The other man, whom they called Mr. Anthony, replied, “Mr. —— may say what he will, and swear what he will, but this is the woman, and there’s the remnant of satin she stole; I took it out of her clothes with my own hand.”\n\nI sat still now, and began to take a better heart, but smiled and said nothing; the master looked pale; the constable turned about and looked at me. “Let ’em alone, Mr. Constable,” said I; “let “em go on.” The case was plain and could not be denied, so the constable was charged with the right thief, and the mercer told me very civilly he was sorry for the mistake, and hoped I would not take it ill; that they had so many things of this nature put upon them every day, that they could not be blamed for being very sharp in doing themselves justice. “Not take it ill, sir!” said I; “how can I take it well! If you had dismissed me when your insolent fellow seized on me it the street, and brought me to you, and when you yourself acknowledged I was not the person, I would have put it by, and not taken it ill, because of the many ill things I believe you have put upon you daily; but your treatment of me since has been insufferable, and especially that of your servant; I must and will have reparation for that.”\n\nThen he began to parley with me, said he would make me any reasonable satisfaction, and would fain have had me tell him what it was I expected. I told him that I should not be my own judge, the law should decide it for me; and as I was to be carried before a magistrate, I should let him hear there what I had to say. He told me there was no occasion to go before the justice now, I was at liberty to go where I pleased; and so, calling to the constable, told him he might let me go, for I was discharged. The constable said calmly to him, “sir, you asked me just now if I knew whether I was a constable or justice, and bade me do my duty, and charged me with this gentlewoman as a prisoner. Now, sir, I find you do not understand what is my duty, for you would make me a justice indeed; but I must tell you it is not in my power. I may keep a prisoner when I am charged with him, but ’tis the law and the magistrate alone that can discharge that prisoner; therefore ’tis a mistake, sir; I must carry her before a justice now, whether you think well of it or not.” The mercer was very high with the constable at first; but the constable happening to be not a hired officer, but a good, substantial kind of man (I think he was a corn-handler), and a man of good sense, stood to his business, would not discharge me without going to a justice of the peace; and I insisted upon it too. When the mercer saw that, “Well,” says he to the constable, “you may carry her where you please; I have nothing to say to her.” “But, sir,” says the constable, “you will go with us, I hope, for ’tis you that charged me with her.” “No, not I,” says the mercer; “I tell you I have nothing to say to her.” “But pray, sir, do,” says the constable; “I desire it of you for your own sake, for the justice can do nothing without you.” “Prithee, fellow,” says the mercer, “go about your business; I tell you I have nothing to say to the gentlewoman. I charge you in the king’s name to dismiss her.” “Sir,” says the constable, “I find you don’t know what it is to be constable; I beg of you don’t oblige me to be rude to you.” “I think I need not; you are rude enough already,” says the mercer. “No, sir,” says the constable, “I am not rude; you have broken the peace in bringing an honest woman out of the street, when she was about her lawful occasion, confining her in your shop, and ill-using her here by your servants; and now can you say I am rude to you? I think I am civil to you in not commanding or charging you in the king’s name to go with me, and charging every man I see that passes your door to aid and assist me in carrying you by force; this you cannot but know I have power to do, and yet I forbear it, and once more entreat you to go with me.” Well, he would not for all this, and gave the constable ill language. However, the constable kept his temper, and would not be provoked; and then I put in and said, “Come, Mr. Constable, let him alone; I shall find ways enough to fetch him before a magistrate, I don’t fear that; but there’s the fellow,” says I, “he was the man that seized on me as I was innocently going along the street, and you are a witness of the violence with me since; give me leave to charge you with him, and carry him before the justice.” “Yes, madam,” says the constable; and turning to the fellow “Come, young gentleman,” says he to the journeyman, “you must go along with us; I hope you are not above the constable’s power, though your master is.”\n\nThe fellow looked like a condemned thief, and hung back, then looked at his master, as if he could help him; and he, like a fool, encourage the fellow to be rude, and he truly resisted the constable, and pushed him back with a good force when he went to lay hold on him, at which the constable knocked him down, and called out for help; and immediately the shop was filled with people, and the constable seized the master and man, and all his servants.\n\nThis first ill consequence of this fray was, that the woman they had taken, who was really the thief, made off, and got clear away in the crowd; and two other that they had stopped also; whether they were really guilty or not, that I can say nothing to.\n\nBy this time some of his neighbours having come in, and, upon inquiry, seeing how things went, had endeavoured to bring the hot-brained mercer to his senses, and he began to be convinced that he was in the wrong; and so at length we went all very quietly before the justice, with a mob of about five hundred people at our heels; and all the way I went I could hear the people ask what was the matter, and other reply and say, a mercer had stopped a gentlewoman instead of a thief, and had afterwards taken the thief, and now the gentlewoman had taken the mercer, and was carrying him before the justice. This pleased the people strangely, and made the crowd increase, and they cried out as they went, “Which is the rogue? which is the mercer?” and especially the women. Then when they saw him they cried out, “That’s he, that’s he”; and every now and then came a good dab of dirt at him; and thus we marched a good while, till the mercer thought fit to desire the constable to call a coach to protect himself from the rabble; so we rode the rest of the way, the constable and I, and the mercer and his man.\n\nWhen we came to the justice, which was an ancient gentleman in Bloomsbury, the constable giving first a summary account of the matter, the justice bade me speak, and tell what I had to say. And first he asked my name, which I was very loth to give, but there was no remedy, so I told him my name was Mary Flanders, that I was a widow, my husband being a sea captain, died on a voyage to Virginia; and some other circumstances I told which he could never contradict, and that I lodged at present in town with such a person, naming my governess; but that I was preparing to go over to America, where my husband’s effects lay, and that I was going that day to buy some clothes to put myself into second mourning, but had not yet been in any shop, when that fellow, pointing to the mercer’s journeyman, came rushing upon me with such fury as very much frighted me, and carried me back to his master’s shop, where, though his master acknowledged I was not the person, yet he would not dismiss me, but charged a constable with me.\n\nThen I proceeded to tell how the journeyman treated me; how they would not suffer me to send for any of my friends; how afterwards they found the real thief, and took the very goods they had lost upon her, and all the particulars as before.\n\nThen the constable related his case: his dialogue with the mercer about discharging me, and at last his servant’s refusing to go with him, when he had charged him with him, and his master encouraging him to do so, and at last his striking the constable, and the like, all as I have told it already.\n\nThe justice then heard the mercer and his man. The mercer indeed made a long harangue of the great loss they have daily by lifters and thieves; that it was easy for them to mistake, and that when he found it he would have dismissed me, etc., as above. As to the journeyman, he had very little to say, but that he pretended other of the servants told him that I was really the person.\n\nUpon the whole, the justice first of all told me very courteously I was discharged; that he was very sorry that the mercer’s man should in his eager pursuit have so little discretion as to take up an innocent person for a guilty person; that if he had not been so unjust as to detain me afterward, he believed I would have forgiven the first affront; that, however, it was not in his power to award me any reparation for anything, other than by openly reproving them, which he should do; but he supposed I would apply to such methods as the law directed; in the meantime he would bind him over.\n\nBut as to the breach of the peace committed by the journeyman, he told me he should give me some satisfaction for that, for he should commit him to Newgate for assaulting the constable, and for assaulting me also.\n\nAccordingly he sent the fellow to Newgate for that assault, and his master gave bail, and so we came away; but I had the satisfaction of seeing the mob wait upon them both, as they came out, hallooing and throwing stones and dirt at the coaches they rode in; and so I came home to my governess.\n\nAfter this hustle, coming home and telling my governess the story, she falls a-laughing at me. “Why are you merry?” says I; “the story has not so much laughing room in it as you imagine; I am sure I have had a great deal of hurry and fright too, with a pack of ugly rogues.” “Laugh!” says my governess; “I laugh, child, to see what a lucky creature you are; why, this job will be the best bargain to you that ever you made in your life, if you manage it well. I warrant you,” says she, “you shall make the mercer pay you £500 for damages, besides what you shall get out of the journeyman.”\n\nI had other thoughts of the matter than she had; and especially, because I had given in my name to the justice of peace; and I knew that my name was so well known among the people at Hick’s Hall, the Old Bailey, and such places, that if this cause came to be tried openly, and my name came to be inquired into, no court would give much damages, for the reputation of a person of such a character. However, I was obliged to begin a prosecution in form, and accordingly my governess found me out a very creditable sort of a man to manage it, being an attorney of very good business, and of a good reputation, and she was certainly in the right of this; for had she employed a pettifogging hedge solicitor, or a man not known, and not in good reputation, I should have brought it to but little.\n\nI met this attorney, and gave him all the particulars at large, as they are recited above; and he assured me it was a case, as he said, that would very well support itself, and that he did not question but that a jury would give very considerable damages on such an occasion; so taking his full instructions he began the prosecution, and the mercer being arrested, gave bail. A few days after his giving bail, he comes with his attorney to my attorney, to let him know that he desired to accommodate the matter; that it was all carried on in the heat of an unhappy passion; that his client, meaning me, had a sharp provoking tongue, that I used them ill, gibing at them, and jeering them, even while they believed me to be the very person, and that I had provoked them, and the like.\n\nMy attorney managed as well on my side; made them believe I was a widow of fortune, that I was able to do myself justice, and had great friends to stand by me too, who had all made me promise to sue to the utmost, and that if it cost me a thousand pounds I would be sure to have satisfaction, for that the affronts I had received were insufferable.\nHowever, they brought my attorney to this, that he promised he would not blow the coals, that if I inclined to accommodation, he would not hinder me, and that he would rather persuade me to peace than to war; for which they told him he should be no loser; all which he told me very honestly, and told me that if they offered him any bribe, I should certainly know it; but upon the whole he told me very honestly that if I would take his opinion, he would advise me to make it up with them, for that as they were in a great fright, and were desirous above all things to make it up, and knew that, let it be what it would, they would be allotted to bear all the costs of the suit; he believed they would give me freely more than any jury or court of justice would give upon a trial. I asked him what he thought they would be brought to. He told me he could not tell as to that, but he would tell me more when I saw him again.\n\nSome time after this, they came again to know if he had talked with me. He told them he had; that he found me not so averse to an accommodation as some of my friends were, who resented the disgrace offered me, and set me on; that they blowed the coals in secret, prompting me to revenge, or do myself justice, as they called it; so that he could not tell what to say to it; he told them he would do his endeavour to persuade me, but he ought to be able to tell me what proposal they made. They pretended they could not make any proposal, because it might be made use of against them; and he told them, that by the same rule he could not make any offers, for that might be pleaded in abatement of what damages a jury might be inclined to give. However, after some discourse and mutual promises that no advantage should be taken on either side, by what was transacted then or at any other of those meetings, they came to a kind of a treaty; but so remote, and so wide from one another, that nothing could be expected from it; for my attorney demanded £500 and charges, and they offered £50 without charges; so they broke off, and the mercer proposed to have a meeting with me myself; and my attorney agreed to that very readily.\n\nMy attorney gave me notice to come to this meeting in good clothes, and with some state, that the mercer might see I was something more than I seemed to be that time they had me. Accordingly I came in a new suit of second mourning, according to what I had said at the justice’s. I set myself out, too, as well as a widow’s dress in second mourning would admit; my governess also furnished me with a good pearl necklace, that shut in behind with a locket of diamonds, which she had in pawn; and I had a very good figure; and as I stayed till I was sure they were come, I came in a coach to the door, with my maid with me.\n\nWhen I came into the room the mercer was surprised. He stood up and made his bow, which I took a little notice of, and but a little, and went and sat down where my own attorney had pointed to me to sit, for it was his house. After a little while the mercer said, he did not know me again, and began to make some compliments his way. I told him, I believed he did not know me at first, and that if he had, I believed he would not have treated me as he did.\n\nHe told me he was very sorry for what had happened, and that it was to testify the willingness he had to make all possible reparation that he had appointed this meeting; that he hoped I would not carry things to extremity, which might be not only too great a loss to him, but might be the ruin of his business and shop, in which case I might have the satisfaction of repaying an injury with an injury ten times greater; but that I would then get nothing, whereas he was willing to do me any justice that was in his power, without putting himself or me to the trouble or charge of a suit at law.\n\nI told him I was glad to hear him talk so much more like a man of sense than he did before; that it was true, acknowledgment in most cases of affronts was counted reparation sufficient; but this had gone too far to be made up so; that I was not revengeful, nor did I seek his ruin, or any man’s else, but that all my friends were unanimous not to let me so far neglect my character as to adjust a thing of this kind without a sufficient reparation of honour; that to be taken up for a thief was such an indignity as could not be put up; that my character was above being treated so by any that knew me, but because in my condition of a widow I had been for some time careless of myself, and negligent of myself, I might be taken for such a creature, but that for the particular usage I had from him afterwards,—and then I repeated all as before; it was so provoking I had scarce patience to repeat it.\n\nWell, he acknowledged all, and was might humble indeed; he made proposals very handsome; he came up to £100 and to pay all the law charges, and added that he would make me a present of a very good suit of clothes. I came down to £300, and I demanded that I should publish an advertisement of the particulars in the common newspapers.\n\nThis was a clause he never could comply with. However, at last he came up, by good management of my attorney, to £150 and a suit of black silk clothes; and there I agree, and as it were, at my attorney’s request, complied with it, he paying my attorney’s bill and charges, and gave us a good supper into the bargain.\n\nWhen I came to receive the money, I brought my governess with me, dressed like an old duchess, and a gentleman very well dressed, who we pretended courted me, but I called him cousin, and the lawyer was only to hint privately to him that his gentleman courted the widow.\n\nHe treated us handsomely indeed, and paid the money cheerfully enough; so that it cost him £200 in all, or rather more. At our last meeting, when all was agreed, the case of the journeyman came up, and the mercer begged very hard for him; told me he was a man that had kept a shop of his own, and been in good business, had a wife, and several children, and was very poor; that he had nothing to make satisfaction with, but he should come to beg my pardon on his knees, if I desired it, as openly as I pleased. I had no spleen at the saucy rogue, nor were his submissions anything to me, since there was nothing to be got by him, so I thought it was as good to throw that in generously as not; so I told him I did not desire the ruin of any man, and therefore at his request I would forgive the wretch; it was below me to seek any revenge.\n\nWhen we were at supper he brought the poor fellow in to make acknowledgment, which he would have done with as much mean humility as his offence was with insulting haughtiness and pride, in which he was an instance of a complete baseness of spirit, impious, cruel, and relentless when uppermost and in prosperity, abject and low-spirited when down in affliction. However, I abated his cringes, told him I forgave him, and desired he might withdraw, as if I did not care for the sight of him, though I had forgiven him.\n\nI was now in good circumstances indeed, if I could have known my time for leaving off, and my governess often said I was the richest of the trade in England; and so I believe I was, for I had £700 by me in money, besides clothes, rings, some plate, and two gold watches, and all of them stolen, for I had innumerable jobs besides these I have mentioned. Oh! had I even now had the grace of repentance, I had still leisure to have looked back upon my follies, and have made some reparation; but the satisfaction I was to make for the public mischiefs I had done was yet left behind; and I could not forbear going abroad again, as I called it now, than any more I could when my extremity really drove me out for bread.\n\nIt was not long after the affair with the mercer was made up, that I went out in an equipage quite different from any I had ever appeared in before. I dressed myself like a beggar woman, in the coarsest and most despicable rags I could get, and I walked about peering and peeping into every door and window I came near; and indeed I was in such a plight now that I knew as ill how to behave in as ever I did in any. I naturally abhorred dirt and rags; I had been bred up tight and cleanly, and could be no other, whatever condition I was in; so that this was the most uneasy disguise to me that ever I put on. I said presently to myself that this would not do, for this was a dress that everybody was shy and afraid of; and I thought everybody looked at me, as if they were afraid I should come near them, lest I should take something from them, or afraid to come near me, lest they should get something from me. I wandered about all the evening the first time I went out, and made nothing of it, but came home again wet, draggled, and tired. However, I went out again the next night, and then I met with a little adventure, which had like to have cost me dear. As I was standing near a tavern door, there comes a gentleman on horseback, and lights at the door, and wanting to go into the tavern, he calls one of the drawers to hold his horse. He stayed pretty long in the tavern, and the drawer heard his master call, and thought he would be angry with him. Seeing me stand by him, he called to me, “Here, woman,” says he, “hold this horse a while, till I go in; if the gentleman comes, he’ll give you something.” “Yes,” says I, and takes the horse, and walks off with him very soberly, and carried him to my governess.\n\nThis had been a booty to those that had understood it; but never was poor thief more at a loss to know what to do with anything that was stolen; for when I came home, my governess was quite confounded, and what to do with the creature, we neither of us knew. To send him to a stable was doing nothing, for it was certain that public notice would be given in the _Gazette_, and the horse described, so that we durst not go to fetch it again.\n\nAll the remedy we had for this unlucky adventure was to go and set up the horse at an inn, and send a note by a porter to the tavern, that the gentleman’s horse that was lost such a time was left at such an inn, and that he might be had there; that the poor woman that held him, having led him about the street, not being able to lead him back again, had left him there. We might have waited till the owner had published and offered a reward, but we did not care to venture the receiving the reward.\n\nSo this was a robbery and no robbery, for little was lost by it, and nothing was got by it, and I was quite sick of going out in a beggar’s dress; it did not answer at all, and besides, I thought it was ominous and threatening.\n\nWhile I was in this disguise, I fell in with a parcel of folks of a worse kind than any I ever sorted with, and I saw a little into their ways too. These were coiners of money, and they made some very good offers to me, as to profit; but the part they would have had me have embarked in was the most dangerous part. I mean that of the very working the die, as they call it, which, had I been taken, had been certain death, and that at a stake—I say, to be burnt to death at a stake; so that though I was to appearance but a beggar, and they promised mountains of gold and silver to me to engage, yet it would not do. It is true, if I had been really a beggar, or had been desperate as when I began, I might perhaps have closed with it; for what care they to die that can’t tell how to live? But at present this was not my condition, at least I was for no such terrible risks as those; besides, the very thoughts of being burnt at a stake struck terror into my very soul, chilled my blood, and gave me the vapours to such a degree, as I could not think of it without trembling.\n\nThis put an end to my disguise too, for as I did not like the proposal, so I did not tell them so, but seemed to relish it, and promised to meet again. But I durst see them no more; for if I had seen them, and not complied, though I had declined it with the greatest assurance of secrecy in the world, they would have gone near to have murdered me, to make sure work, and make themselves easy, as they call it. What kind of easiness that is, they may best judge that understand how easy men are that can murder people to prevent danger.\n\nThis and horse-stealing were things quite out of my way, and I might easily resolve I would have no more to say to them; my business seemed to lie another way, and though it had hazard enough in it too, yet it was more suitable to me, and what had more of art in it, and more room to escape, and more chances for a-coming off if a surprise should happen.\n\nI had several proposals made also to me about that time, to come into a gang of house-breakers; but that was a thing I had no mind to venture at neither, any more than I had at the coining trade. I offered to go along with two men and a woman, that made it their business to get into houses by stratagem, and with them I was willing enough to venture. But there were three of them already, and they did not care to part, nor I to have too many in a gang, so I did not close with them, but declined them, and they paid dear for their next attempt.\n\nBut at length I met with a woman that had often told me what adventures she had made, and with success, at the waterside, and I closed with her, and we drove on our business pretty well. One day we came among some Dutch people at St. Catherine’s, where we went on pretence to buy goods that were privately got on shore. I was two or three times in a house where we saw a good quantity of prohibited goods, and my companion once brought away three pieces of Dutch black silk that turned to good account, and I had my share of it; but in all the journeys I made by myself, I could not get an opportunity to do anything, so I laid it aside, for I had been so often, that they began to suspect something, and were so shy, that I saw nothing was to be done.\n\nThis baulked me a little, and I resolved to push at something or other, for I was not used to come back so often without purchase; so the next day I dressed myself up fine, and took a walk to the other end of the town. I passed through the Exchange in the Strand, but had no notion of finding anything to do there, when on a sudden I saw a great cluttering in the place, and all the people, shopkeepers as well as others, standing up and staring; and what should it be but some great duchess come into the Exchange, and they said the queen was coming. I set myself close up to a shop-side with my back to the counter, as if to let the crowd pass by, when keeping my eye upon a parcel of lace which the shopkeeper was showing to some ladies that stood by me, the shopkeeper and her maid were so taken up with looking to see who was coming, and what shop they would go to, that I found means to slip a paper of lace into my pocket and come clear off with it; so the lady-milliner paid dear enough for her gaping after the queen.\n\nI went off from the shop, as if driven along by the throng, and mingling myself with the crowd, went out at the other door of the Exchange, and so got away before they missed their lace; and because I would not be followed, I called a coach and shut myself up in it. I had scarce shut the coach doors up, but I saw the milliner’s maid and five or six more come running out into the street, and crying out as if they were frightened. They did not cry “Stop thief!” because nobody ran away, but I could hear the word “robbed,” and “lace,” two or three times, and saw the wench wringing her hands, and run staring to and again, like one scared. The coachman that had taken me up was getting up into the box, but was not quite up, so that the horse had not begun to move; so that I was terrible uneasy, and I took the packet of lace and laid it ready to have dropped it out at the flap of the coach, which opens before, just behind the coachman; but to my great satisfaction, in less than a minute the coach began to move, that is to say, as soon as the coachman had got up and spoken to his horses; so he drove away without any interruption, and I brought off my purchase, which was worth near £20.\n\nThe next day I dressed up again, but in quite different clothes, and walked the same way again, but nothing offered till I came into St. James’s Park, where I saw abundance of fine ladies in the Park, walking in the Mall, and among the rest there was a little miss, a young lady of about twelve or thirteen years old, and she had a sister, as I suppose it was, with her, that might be about nine years old. I observed the biggest had a fine gold watch on, and a good necklace of pearl, and they had a footman in livery with them; but as it is not usual for the footman to go behind the ladies in the Mall, so I observed the footman stopped at their going into the Mall, and the biggest of the sisters spoke to him, which I perceived was to bid him be just there when they came back.\n\nWhen I heard her dismiss the footman, I stepped up to him and asked him, what little lady that was? and held a little chat with him about what a pretty child it was with her, and how genteel and well-carriaged the lady, the eldest, would be: how womanish, and how grave; and the fool of a fellow told me presently who she was; that she was Sir Thomas ——’s eldest daughter, of Essex, and that she was a great fortune; that her mother was not come to town yet; but she was with Sir William ——’s lady, of Suffolk, at her lodging in Suffolk Street, and a great deal more; that they had a maid and a woman to wait on them, besides Sir Thomas’s coach, the coachman, and himself; and that young lady was governess to the whole family, as well here as at home too; and, in short, told me abundance of things enough for my business.\n\nI was very well dressed, and had my gold watch as well as she; so I left the footman, and I puts myself in a rank with this young lady, having stayed till she had taken one double turn in the Mall, and was going forward again; by and by I saluted her by her name, with the title of Lady Betty. I asked her when she heard from her father; when my lady her mother would be in town, and how she did.\n\nI talked so familiarly to her of her whole family that she could not suspect but that I knew them all intimately. I asked her why she would come abroad without Mrs. Chime with her (that was the name of her woman) to take of Mrs. Judith, that was her sister. Then I entered into a long chat with her about her sister, what a fine little lady she was, and asked her if she had learned French, and a thousand such little things to entertain her, when on a sudden we saw the guards come, and the crowd ran to see the king go by to the Parliament House.\n\nThe ladies ran all to the side of the Mall, and I helped my lady to stand upon the edge of the boards on the side of the Mall, that she might be high enough to see; and took the little one and lifted her quite up; during which, I took care to convey the gold watch so clean away from the Lady Betty, that she never felt it, nor missed it, till all the crowd was gone, and she was gotten into the middle of the Mall among the other ladies.\n\nI took my leave of her in the very crowd, and said to her, as if in haste, “Dear Lady Betty, take care of your little sister.” And so the crowd did as it were thrust me away from her, and that I was obliged unwillingly to take my leave.\n\nThe hurry in such cases is immediately over, and the place clear as soon as the king is gone by; but as there is always a great running and clutter just as the king passes, so having dropped the two little ladies, and done my business with them without any miscarriage, I kept hurrying on among the crowd, as if I ran to see the king, and so I got before the crowd and kept so till I came to the end of the Mall, when the king going on towards the Horse Guards, I went forward to the passage, which went then through against the lower end of the Haymarket, and there I bestowed a coach upon myself, and made off, and I confess I have not yet been so good as my word, viz. to go and visit my Lady Betty.\n\nI was once of the mind to venture staying with Lady Betty till she missed the watch, and so have made a great outcry about it with her, and have got her into the coach, and put myself in the coach with her, and have gone home with her; for she appeared so fond of me, and so perfectly deceived by my so readily talking to her of all her relations and family, that I thought it was very easy to push the thing farther, and to have got at least the necklace of pearl; but when I considered that though the child would not perhaps have suspected me, other people might, and that if I was searched I should be discovered, I thought it was best to go off with what I had got, and be satisfied.\n\nI came accidentally afterwards to hear, that when the young lady missed her watch, she made a great outcry in the Park, and sent her footman up and down to see if he could find me out, she having described me so perfectly that he knew presently that it was the same person that had stood and talked so long with him, and asked him so many questions about them; but I gone far enough out of their reach before she could come at her footman to tell him the story.\n\nI made another adventure after this, of a nature different from all I had been concerned in yet, and this was at a gaming-house near Covent Garden.\n\nI saw several people go in and out; and I stood in the passage a good while with another woman with me, and seeing a gentleman go up that seemed to be of more than ordinary fashion, I said to him, “Sir, pray don’t they give women leave to go up?” “Yes, madam,” says he, “and to play too, if they please.” “I mean so, sir,” said I. And with that he said he would introduce me if I had a mind; so I followed him to the door, and he looking in, “There, madam,” says he, “are the gamesters, if you have a mind to venture.” I looked in and said to my comrade aloud, “Here’s nothing but men; I won’t venture among them.” At which one of the gentlemen cried out, “You need not be afraid, madam, here’s none but fair gamesters; you are very welcome to come and set what you please.” So I went a little nearer and looked on, and some of them brought me a chair, and I sat down and saw the box and dice go round apace; then I said to my comrade, “The gentlemen play too high for us; come, let us go.”\n\nThe people were all very civil, and one gentleman in particular encouraged me, and said, “Come, madam, if you please to venture, if you dare trust me, I’ll answer for it you shall have nothing put upon you here.” “No, sir,” said I, smiling, “I hope the gentlemen would not cheat a woman.” But still I declined venturing, though I pulled out a purse with money in it, that they might see I did not want money.\n\nAfter I had sat a while, one gentleman said to me, jeering, “Come, madam, I see you are afraid to venture for yourself; I always had good luck with the ladies, you shall set for me, if you won’t set for yourself.” I told him, “Sir, I should be very loth to lose your money,” though I added, “I am pretty lucky too; but the gentlemen play so high, that I dare not indeed venture my own.”\n\n“Well, well,” says he, “there’s ten guineas, madam; set them for me.” So I took his money and set, himself looking on. I ran out nine of the guineas by one and two at a time, and then the box coming to the next man to me, my gentleman gave me ten guineas more, and made me set five of them at once, and the gentleman who had the box threw out, so there was five guineas of his money again. He was encouraged at this, and made me take the box, which was a bold venture. However, I held the box so long that I had gained him his whole money, and had a good handful of guineas in my lap, and which was the better luck, when I threw out, I threw but at one or two of those that had set me, and so went off easy.\n\nWhen I was come this length, I offered the gentleman all the gold, for it was his own; and so would have had him play for himself, pretending I did not understand the game well enough. He laughed, and said if I had but good luck, it was no matter whether I understood the game or no; but I should not leave off. However, he took out the fifteen guineas that he had put in at first, and bade me play with the rest. I would have told them to see how much I had got, but he said, “No, no, don’t tell them, I believe you are very honest, and ’tis bad luck to tell them”; so I played on.\n\nI understood the game well enough, though I pretended I did not, and played cautiously. It was to keep a good stock in my lap, out of which I every now and then conveyed some into my pocket, but in such a manner, and at such convenient times, as I was sure he could not see it.\n\nI played a great while, and had very good luck for him; but the last time I held the box, they set me high, and I threw boldly at all; I held the box till I gained near fourscore guineas, but lost above half of it back in the last throw; so I got up, for I was afraid I should lose it all back again, and said to him, “Pray come, sir, now, and take it and play for yourself; I think I have done pretty well for you.” He would have had me play on, but it grew late, and I desired to be excused. When I gave it up to him, I told him I hoped he would give me leave to tell it now, that I might see what I had gained, and how lucky I had been for him; when I told them, there were threescore and three guineas. “Ay,” says I, “if it had not been for that unlucky throw, I had got you a hundred guineas.” So I gave him all the money, but he would not take it till I had put my hand into it, and taken some for myself, and bid me please myself. I refused it, and was positive I would not take it myself; if he had a mind to anything of that kind, it should be all his own doings.\n\nThe rest of the gentlemen seeing us striving cried, “Give it her all”; but I absolutely refused that. Then one of them said, “D—n ye, Jack, halve it with her; don’t you know you should be always upon even terms with the ladies.” So, in short, he divided it with me, and I brought away thirty guineas, besides about forty-three which I had stole privately, which I was sorry for afterward, because he was so generous.\n\nThus I brought home seventy-three guineas, and let my old governess see what good luck I had at play. However, it was her advice that I should not venture again, and I took her counsel, for I never went there any more; for I knew as well as she, if the itch of play came in, I might soon lose that, and all the rest of what I had got.\n\nFortune had smiled upon me to that degree, and I had thriven so much, and my governess too, for she always had a share with me, that really the old gentlewoman began to talk of leaving off while we were well, and being satisfied with what we had got; but, I know not what fate guided me, I was as backward to it now as she was when I proposed it to her before, and so in an ill hour we gave over the thoughts of it for the present, and, in a word, I grew more hardened and audacious than ever, and the success I had made my name as famous as any thief of my sort ever had been at Newgate, and in the Old Bailey.\n\nI had sometime taken the liberty to play the same game over again, which is not according to practice, which however succeeded not amiss; but generally I took up new figures, and contrived to appear in new shapes every time I went abroad.\n\nIt was not a rumbling time of the year, and the gentlemen being most of them gone out of town, Tunbridge, and Epsom, and such places were full of people. But the city was thin, and I thought our trade felt it a little, as well as other; so that at the latter end of the year I joined myself with a gang who usually go every year to Stourbridge Fair, and from thence to Bury Fair, in Suffolk. We promised ourselves great things there, but when I came to see how things were, I was weary of it presently; for except mere picking of pockets, there was little worth meddling with; neither, if a booty had been made, was it so easy carrying it off, nor was there such a variety of occasion for business in our way, as in London; all that I made of the whole journey was a gold watch at Bury Fair, and a small parcel of linen at Cambridge, which gave me an occasion to take leave of the place. It was on old bite, and I thought might do with a country shopkeeper, though in London it would not.\n\nI bought at a linen-draper’s shop, not in the fair, but in the town of Cambridge, as much fine holland and other things as came to about seven pounds; when I had done, I bade them be sent to such an inn, where I had purposely taken up my being the same morning, as if I was to lodge there that night.\n\nI ordered the draper to send them home to me, about such an hour, to the inn where I lay, and I would pay him his money. At the time appointed the draper sends the goods, and I placed one of our gang at the chamber door, and when the innkeeper’s maid brought the messenger to the door, who was a young fellow, an apprentice, almost a man, she tells him her mistress was asleep, but if he would leave the things and call in about an hour, I should be awake, and he might have the money. He left the parcel very readily, and goes his way, and in about half an hour my maid and I walked off, and that very evening I hired a horse, and a man to ride before me, and went to Newmarket, and from thence got my passage in a coach that was not quite full to St. Edmund’s Bury, where, as I told you, I could make but little of my trade, only at a little country opera-house made a shift to carry off a gold watch from a lady’s side, who was not only intolerably merry, but, as I thought, a little fuddled, which made my work much easier.\n\nI made off with this little booty to Ipswich, and from thence to Harwich, where I went into an inn, as if I had newly arrived from Holland, not doubting but I should make some purchase among the foreigners that came on shore there; but I found them generally empty of things of value, except what was in their portmanteaux and Dutch hampers, which were generally guarded by footmen; however, I fairly got one of their portmanteaux one evening out of the chamber where the gentleman lay, the footman being fast asleep on the bed, and I suppose very drunk.\n\nThe room in which I lodged lay next to the Dutchman’s, and having dragged the heavy thing with much ado out of the chamber into mine, I went out into the street, to see if I could find any possibility of carrying it off. I walked about a great while, but could see no probability either of getting out the thing, or of conveying away the goods that were in it if I had opened it, the town being so small, and I a perfect stranger in it; so I was returning with a resolution to carry it back again, and leave it where I found it. Just in that very moment I heard a man make a noise to some people to make haste, for the boat was going to put off, and the tide would be spent. I called to the fellow, “What boat is it, friend,” says I, “that you belong to?” “The Ipswich wherry, madam,” says he. “When do you go off?” says I. “This moment, madam,” says he; “do you want to go thither?” “Yes,” said I, “if you can stay till I fetch my things.” “Where are your things, madam?” says he. “At such an inn,” said I. “Well, I’ll go with you, madam,” says he, very civilly, “and bring them for you.” “Come away, then,” says I, and takes him with me.\n\nThe people of the inn were in a great hurry, the packet-boat from Holland being just come in, and two coaches just come also with passengers from London, for another packet-boat that was going off for Holland, which coaches were to go back next day with the passengers that were just landed. In this hurry it was not much minded that I came to the bar and paid my reckoning, telling my landlady I had gotten my passage by sea in a wherry.\n\nThese wherries are large vessels, with good accommodation for carrying passengers from Harwich to London; and though they are called wherries, which is a word used in the Thames for a small boat rowed with one or two men, yet these are vessels able to carry twenty passengers, and ten or fifteen tons of goods, and fitted to bear the sea. All this I had found out by inquiring the night before into the several ways of going to London.\n\nMy landlady was very courteous, took my money for my reckoning, but was called away, all the house being in a hurry. So I left her, took the fellow up to my chamber, gave him the trunk, or portmanteau, for it was like a trunk, and wrapped it about with an old apron, and he went directly to his boat with it, and I after him, nobody asking us the least question about it; as for the drunken Dutch footman he was still asleep, and his master with other foreign gentlemen at supper, and very merry below, so I went clean off with it to Ipswich; and going in the night, the people of the house knew nothing but that I was gone to London by the Harwich wherry, as I had told my landlady.\n\nI was plagued at Ipswich with the custom-house officers, who stopped my trunk, as I called it, and would open and search it. I was willing, I told them, they should search it, but husband had the key, and he was not yet come from Harwich; this I said, that if upon searching it they should find all the things be such as properly belonged to a man rather than a woman, it should not seem strange to them. However, they being positive to open the trunk I consented to have it be broken open, that is to say, to have the lock taken off, which was not difficult.\n\nThey found nothing for their turn, for the trunk had been searched before, but they discovered several things very much to my satisfaction, as particularly a parcel of money in French pistoles, and some Dutch ducatoons or rix-dollars, and the rest was chiefly two periwigs, wearing-linen, and razors, wash-balls, perfumes, and other useful things necessary for a gentleman, which all passed for my husband’s, and so I was quit to them.\n\nIt was now very early in the morning, and not light, and I knew not well what course to take; for I made no doubt but I should be pursued in the morning, and perhaps be taken with the things about me; so I resolved upon taking new measures. I went publicly to an inn in the town with my trunk, as I called it, and having taken the substance out, I did not think the lumber of it worth my concern; however, I gave it the landlady of the house with a charge to take great care of it, and lay it up safe till I should come again, and away I walked in to the street.\n\nWhen I was got into the town a great way from the inn, I met with an ancient woman who had just opened her door, and I fell into chat with her, and asked her a great many wild questions of things all remote to my purpose and design; but in my discourse I found by her how the town was situated, that I was in a street that went out towards Hadley, but that such a street went towards the water-side, such a street towards Colchester, and so the London road lay there.\n\nI had soon my ends of this old woman, for I only wanted to know which was the London road, and away I walked as fast as I could; not that I intended to go on foot, either to London or to Colchester, but I wanted to get quietly away from Ipswich.\n\nI walked about two or three miles, and then I met a plain countryman, who was busy about some husbandry work, I did not know what, and I asked him a great many questions first, not much to the purpose, but at last told him I was going for London, and the coach was full, and I could not get a passage, and asked him if he could tell me where to hire a horse that would carry double, and an honest man to ride before me to Colchester, that so I might get a place there in the coaches. The honest clown looked earnestly at me, and said nothing for above half a minute, when, scratching his poll, “A horse, say you and to Colchester, to carry double? why yes, mistress, alack-a-day, you may have horses enough for money.” “Well, friend,” says I, “that I take for granted; I don’t expect it without money.” “Why, but, mistress,” says he, “how much are you willing to give?” “Nay,” says I again, “friend, I don’t know what your rates are in the country here, for I am a stranger; but if you can get one for me, get it as cheap as you can, and I’ll give you somewhat for your pains.”\n\n“Why, that’s honestly said too,” says the countryman. “Not so honest, neither,” said I to myself, “if thou knewest all.” “Why, mistress,” says he, “I have a horse that will carry double, and I don’t much care if I go myself with you,” and the like. “Will you?” says I; “well, I believe you are an honest man; if you will, I shall be glad of it; I’ll pay you in reason.” “Why, look ye, mistress,” says he, “I won’t be out of reason with you, then; if I carry you to Colchester, it will be worth five shillings for myself and my horse, for I shall hardly come back to-night.”\n\nIn short, I hired the honest man and his horse; but when we came to a town upon the road (I do not remember the name of it, but it stands upon a river), I pretended myself very ill, and I could go no farther that night but if he would stay there with me, because I was a stranger, I would pay him for himself and his horse with all my heart.\nThis I did because I knew the Dutch gentlemen and their servants would be upon the road that day, either in the stagecoaches or riding post, and I did not know but the drunken fellow, or somebody else that might have seen me at Harwich, might see me again, and so I thought that in one day’s stop they would be all gone by.\n\nWe lay all that night there, and the next morning it was not very early when I set out, so that it was near ten o’clock by the time I got to Colchester. It was no little pleasure that I saw the town where I had so many pleasant days, and I made many inquiries after the good old friends I had once had there, but could make little out; they were all dead or removed. The young ladies had been all married or gone to London; the old gentleman and the old lady that had been my early benefactress all dead; and which troubled me most, the young gentleman my first lover, and afterwards my brother-in-law, was dead; but two sons, men grown, were left of him, but they too were transplanted to London.\n\nI dismissed my old man here, and stayed incognito for three or four days in Colchester, and then took a passage in a waggon, because I would not venture being seen in the Harwich coaches. But I needed not have used so much caution, for there was nobody in Harwich but the woman of the house could have known me; nor was it rational to think that she, considering the hurry she was in, and that she never saw me but once, and that by candlelight, should have ever discovered me.\n\nI was now returned to London, and though by the accident of the last adventure I got something considerable, yet I was not fond of any more country rambles, nor should I have ventured abroad again if I had carried the trade on to the end of my days. I gave my governess a history of my travels; she liked the Harwich journey well enough, and in discoursing of these things between ourselves she observed, that a thief being a creature that watches the advantages of other people’s mistakes, ’tis impossible but that to one that is vigilant and industrious many opportunities must happen, and therefore she thought that one so exquisitely keen in the trade as I was, would scarce fail of something extraordinary wherever I went.\n\nOn the other hand, every branch of my story, if duly considered, may be useful to honest people, and afford a due caution to people of some sort or other to guard against the like surprises, and to have their eyes about them when they have to do with strangers of any kind, for ’tis very seldom that some snare or other is not in their way. The moral, indeed, of all my history is left to be gathered by the senses and judgment of the reader; I am not qualified to preach to them. Let the experience of one creature completely wicked, and completely miserable, be a storehouse of useful warning to those that read.\n\nI am drawing now towards a new variety of the scenes of life. Upon my return, being hardened by a long race of crime, and success unparalleled, at least in the reach of my own knowledge, I had, as I have said, no thoughts of laying down a trade which, if I was to judge by the example of other, must, however, end at last in misery and sorrow.\n\nIt was on the Christmas day following, in the evening, that, to finish a long train of wickedness, I went abroad to see what might offer in my way; when going by a working silversmith’s in Foster Lane, I saw a tempting bait indeed, and not be resisted by one of my occupation, for the shop had nobody in it, as I could see, and a great deal of loose plate lay in the window, and at the seat of the man, who usually, as I suppose, worked at one side of the shop.\n\nI went boldly in, and was just going to lay my hand upon a piece of plate, and might have done it, and carried it clear off, for any care that the men who belonged to the shop had taken of it; but an officious fellow in a house, not a shop, on the other side of the way, seeing me go in, and observing that there was nobody in the shop, comes running over the street, and into the shop, and without asking me what I was, or who, seizes upon me, an cries out for the people of the house.\n\nI had not, as I said above, touched anything in the shop, and seeing a glimpse of somebody running over to the shop, I had so much presence of mind as to knock very hard with my foot on the floor of the house, and was just calling out too, when the fellow laid hands on me.\n\nHowever, as I had always most courage when I was in most danger, so when the fellow laid hands on me, I stood very high upon it, that I came in to buy half a dozen of silver spoons; and to my good fortune, it was a silversmith’s that sold plate, as well as worked plate for other shops. The fellow laughed at that part, and put such a value upon the service that he had done his neighbour, that he would have it be that I came not to buy, but to steal; and raising a great crowd. I said to the master of the shop, who by this time was fetched home from some neighbouring place, that it was in vain to make noise, and enter into talk there of the case; the fellow had insisted that I came to steal, and he must prove it, and I desired we might go before a magistrate without any more words; for I began to see I should be too hard for the man that had seized me.\n\nThe master and mistress of the shop were really not so violent as the man from t’other side of the way; and the man said, “Mistress, you might come into the shop with a good design for aught I know, but it seemed a dangerous thing for you to come into such a shop as mine is, when you see nobody there; and I cannot do justice to my neighbour, who was so kind to me, as not to acknowledge he had reason on his side; though, upon the whole, I do not find you attempted to take anything, and I really know not what to do in it.” I pressed him to go before a magistrate with me, and if anything could be proved on me that was like a design of robbery, I should willingly submit, but if not, I expected reparation.\n\nJust while we were in this debate, and a crowd of people gathered about the door, came by Sir T. B., an alderman of the city, and justice of the peace, and the goldsmith hearing of it, goes out, and entreated his worship to come in and decide the case.\n\nGive the goldsmith his due, he told his story with a great deal of justice and moderation, and the fellow that had come over, and seized upon me, told his with as much heat and foolish passion, which did me good still, rather than harm. It came then to my turn to speak, and I told his worship that I was a stranger in London, being newly come out of the north; that I lodged in such a place, that I was passing this street, and went into the goldsmith’s shop to buy half a dozen of spoons. By great luck I had an old silver spoon in my pocket, which I pulled out, and told him I had carried that spoon to match it with half a dozen of new ones, that it might match some I had in the country.\nThat seeing nobody I the shop, I knocked with my foot very hard to make the people hear, and had also called aloud with my voice; ’tis true, there was loose plate in the shop, but that nobody could say I had touched any of it, or gone near it; that a fellow came running into the shop out of the street, and laid hands on me in a furious manner, in the very moments while I was calling for the people of the house; that if he had really had a mind to have done his neighbour any service, he should have stood at a distance, and silently watched to see whether I had touched anything or no, and then have clapped in upon me, and taken me in the fact. “That is very true,” says Mr. Alderman, and turning to the fellow that stopped me, he asked him if it was true that I knocked with my foot? He said, yes, I had knocked, but that might be because of his coming. “Nay,” says the alderman, taking him short, “now you contradict yourself, for just now you said she was in the shop with her back to you, and did not see you till you came upon her.” Now it was true that my back was partly to the street, but yet as my business was of a kind that required me to have my eyes every way, so I really had a glance of him running over, as I said before, though he did not perceive it.\n\nAfter a full hearing, the alderman gave it as his opinion that his neighbour was under a mistake, and that I was innocent, and the goldsmith acquiesced in it too, and his wife, and so I was dismissed; but as I was going to depart, Mr. Alderman said, “But hold, madam, if you were designing to buy spoons, I hope you will not let my friend here lose his customer by the mistake.” I readily answered, “No, sir, I’ll buy the spoons still, if he can match my odd spoon, which I brought for a pattern”; and the goldsmith showed me some of the very same fashion. So he weighed the spoons, and they came to five-and-thirty shillings, so I pulls out my purse to pay him, in which I had near twenty guineas, for I never went without such a sum about me, whatever might happen, and I found it of use at other times as well as now.\n\nWhen Mr. Alderman saw my money, he said, “Well, madam, now I am satisfied you were wronged, and it was for this reason that I moved you should buy the spoons, and stayed till you had bought them, for if you had not had money to pay for them, I should have suspected that you did not come into the shop with an intent to buy, for indeed the sort of people who come upon these designs that you have been charged with, are seldom troubled with much gold in their pockets, as I see you are.”\nI smiled, and told his worship, that then I owed something of his favour to my money, but I hoped he saw reason also in the justice he had done me before. He said, yes, he had, but this had confirmed his opinion, and he was fully satisfied now of my having been injured. So I came off with flying colours, though from an affair in which I was at the very brink of destruction.\n\nIt was but three days after this, that not at all made cautious by my former danger, as I used to be, and still pursuing the art which I had so long been employed in, I ventured into a house where I saw the doors open, and furnished myself, as I though verily without being perceived, with two pieces of flowered silks, such as they call brocaded silk, very rich. It was not a mercer’s shop, nor a warehouse of a mercer, but looked like a private dwelling-house, and was, it seems, inhabited by a man that sold goods for the weavers to the mercers, like a broker or factor.\n\nThat I may make short of this black part of this story, I was attacked by two wenches that came open-mouthed at me just as I was going out at the door, and one of them pulled me back into the room, while the other shut the door upon me. I would have given them good words, but there was no room for it, two fiery dragons could not have been more furious than they were; they tore my clothes, bullied and roared as if they would have murdered me; the mistress of the house came next, and then the master, and all outrageous, for a while especially.\n\nI gave the master very good words, told him the door was open, and things were a temptation to me, that I was poor and distressed, and poverty was when many could not resist, and begged him with tears to have pity on me. The mistress of the house was moved with compassion, and inclined to have let me go, and had almost persuaded her husband to it also, but the saucy wenches were run, even before they were sent, and had fetched a constable, and then the master said he could not go back, I must go before a justice, and answered his wife that he might come into trouble himself if he should let me go.\n\nThe sight of the constable, indeed, struck me with terror, and I thought I should have sunk into the ground. I fell into faintings, and indeed the people themselves thought I would have died, when the woman argued again for me, and entreated her husband, seeing they had lost nothing, to let me go. I offered him to pay for the two pieces, whatever the value was, though I had not got them, and argued that as he had his goods, and had really lost nothing, it would be cruel to pursue me to death, and have my blood for the bare attempt of taking them. I put the constable in mind that I had broke no doors, nor carried anything away; and when I came to the justice, and pleaded there that I had neither broken anything to get in, nor carried anything out, the justice was inclined to have released me; but the first saucy jade that stopped me, affirming that I was going out with the goods, but that she stopped me and pulled me back as I was upon the threshold, the justice upon that point committed me, and I was carried to Newgate. That horrid place! my very blood chills at the mention of its name; the place where so many of my comrades had been locked up, and from whence they went to the fatal tree; the place where my mother suffered so deeply, where I was brought into the world, and from whence I expected no redemption but by an infamous death: to conclude, the place that had so long expected me, and which with so much art and success I had so long avoided.\n\nI was not fixed indeed; ’tis impossible to describe the terror of my mind, when I was first brought in, and when I looked around upon all the horrors of that dismal place. I looked on myself as lost, and that I had nothing to think of but of going out of the world, and that with the utmost infamy: the hellish noise, the roaring, swearing, and clamour, the stench and nastiness, and all the dreadful crowd of afflicting things that I saw there, joined together to make the place seem an emblem of hell itself, and a kind of an entrance into it.\n\nNow I reproached myself with the many hints I had had, as I have mentioned above, from my own reason, from the sense of my good circumstances, and of the many dangers I had escaped, to leave off while I was well, and how I had withstood them all, and hardened my thoughts against all fear. It seemed to me that I was hurried on by an inevitable and unseen fate to this day of misery, and that now I was to expiate all my offences at the gallows; that I was now to give satisfaction to justice with my blood, and that I was come to the last hour of my life and of my wickedness together. These things poured themselves in upon my thoughts in a confused manner, and left me overwhelmed with melancholy and despair.\n\nThem I repented heartily of all my life past, but that repentance yielded me no satisfaction, no peace, no, not in the least, because, as I said to myself, it was repenting after the power of further sinning was taken away. I seemed not to mourn that I had committed such crimes, and for the fact as it was an offence against God and my neighbour, but I mourned that I was to be punished for it. I was a penitent, as I thought, not that I had sinned, but that I was to suffer, and this took away all the comfort, and even the hope of my repentance in my own thoughts.\n\nI got no sleep for several nights or days after I came into that wretched place, and glad I would have been for some time to have died there, though I did not consider dying as it ought to be considered neither; indeed, nothing could be filled with more horror to my imagination than the very place, nothing was more odious to me than the company that was there. Oh! if I had but been sent to any place in the world, and not to Newgate, I should have thought myself happy.\n\nIn the next place, how did the hardened wretches that were there before me triumph over me! What! Mrs. Flanders come to Newgate at last? What! Mrs. Mary, Mrs. Molly, and after that plain Moll Flanders? They thought the devil had helped me, they said, that I had reigned so long; they expected me there many years ago, and was I come at last? Then they flouted me with my dejections, welcomed me to the place, wished me joy, bid me have a good heart, not to be cast down, things might not be so bad as I feared, and the like; then called for brandy, and drank to me, but put it all up to my score, for they told me I was but just come to the college, as they called it, and sure I had money in my pocket, though they had none.\n\nI asked one of this crew how long she had been there. She said four months. I asked her how the place looked to her when she first came into it. “Just as it did now to you,” says she, “dreadful and frightful”; that she thought she was in hell; “and I believe so still,” adds she, “but it is natural to me now, I don’t disturb myself about it.” “I suppose,” says I, “you are in no danger of what is to follow?” “Nay,” says she, “for you are mistaken there, I assure you, for I am under sentence, only I pleaded my belly, but I am no more with child than the judge that tried me, and I expect to be called down next sessions.” This “calling down” is calling down to their former judgment, when a woman has been respited for her belly, but proves not to be with child, or if she has been with child, and has been brought to bed. “Well,” says I, “are you thus easy?” “Ay,” says she, “I can’t help myself; what signifies being sad? If I am hanged, there’s an end of me,” says she; and away she turns dancing, and sings as she goes the following piece of Newgate wit—\n\n“If I swing by the string, I shall hear the bell ring, And then there’s an end of poor Jenny.”\n\nI mention this because it would be worth the observation of any prisoner, who shall hereafter fall into the same misfortune, and come to that dreadful place of Newgate, how time, necessity, and conversing with the wretches that are there familiarizes the place to them; how at last they become reconciled to that which at first was the greatest dread upon their spirits in the world, and are as impudently cheerful and merry in their misery as they were when out of it.\n\nI cannot say, as some do, this devil is not so black as he is painted; for indeed no colours can represent the place to the life, not any soul conceive aright of it but those who have been sufferers there. But how hell should become by degree so natural, and not only tolerable, but even agreeable, is a thing unintelligible but by those who have experienced it, as I have.\n\nThe same night that I was sent to Newgate, I sent the news of it to my old governess, who was surprised at it, you may be sure, and spent the night almost as ill out of Newgate, as I did in it.\n\nThe next morning she came to see me; she did what she could to comfort me, but she saw that was to no purpose; however, as she said, to sink under the weight was but to increase the weight; she immediately applied herself to all the proper methods to prevent the effects of it, which we feared, and first she found out the two fiery jades that had surprised me. She tampered with them, offered them money, and, in a word, tried all imaginable ways to prevent a prosecution; she offered one of the wenches £100 to go away from her mistress, and not to appear against me, but she was so resolute, that though she was but a servant maid at £3 a year wages or thereabouts, she refused it, and would have refused it, as my governess said she believed, if she had offered her £500. Then she attacked the other maid; she was not so hard-hearted in appearance as the other, and sometimes seemed inclined to be merciful; but the first wench kept her up, and changed her mind, and would not so much as let my governess talk with her, but threatened to have her up for tampering with the evidence.\n\nThen she applied to the master, that is to say, the man whose goods had been stolen, and particularly to his wife, who, as I told you, was inclined at first to have some compassion for me; she found the woman the same still, but the man alleged he was bound by the justice that committed me, to prosecute, and that he should forfeit his recognisance.\n\nMy governess offered to find friends that should get his recognisances off of the file, as they call it, and that he should not suffer; but it was not possible to convince him that could be done, or that he could be safe any way in the world but by appearing against me; so I was to have three witnesses of fact against me, the master and his two maids; that is to say, I was as certain to be cast for my life as I was certain that I was alive, and I had nothing to do but to think of dying, and prepare for it. I had but a sad foundation to build upon, as I said before, for all my repentance appeared to me to be only the effect of my fear of death, not a sincere regret for the wicked life that I had lived, and which had brought this misery upon me, for the offending my Creator, who was now suddenly to be my judge.\n\nI lived many days here under the utmost horror of soul; I had death, as it were, in view, and thought of nothing night and day, but of gibbets and halters, evil spirits and devils; it is not to be expressed by words how I was harassed, between the dreadful apprehensions of death and the terror of my conscience reproaching me with my past horrible life.\n\nThe ordinary of Newgate came to me, and talked a little in his way, but all his divinity ran upon confessing my crime, as he called it (though he knew not what I was in for), making a full discovery, and the like, without which he told me God would never forgive me; and he said so little to the purpose, that I had no manner of consolation from him; and then to observe the poor creature preaching confession and repentance to me in the morning, and find him drunk with brandy and spirits by noon, this had something in it so shocking, that I began to nauseate the man more than his work, and his work too by degrees, for the sake of the man; so that I desired him to trouble me no more.\n\nI know not how it was, but by the indefatigable application of my diligent governess I had no bill preferred against me the first sessions, I mean to the grand jury, at Guildhall; so I had another month or five weeks before me, and without doubt this ought to have been accepted by me, as so much time given me for reflection upon what was past, and preparation for what was to come; or, in a word, I ought to have esteemed it as a space given me for repentance, and have employed it as such, but it was not in me. I was sorry (as before) for being in Newgate, but had very few signs of repentance about me.\n\nOn the contrary, like the waters in the cavities and hollows of mountains, which petrify and turn into stone whatever they are suffered to drop on, so the continual conversing with such a crew of hell-hounds as I was, had the same common operation upon me as upon other people. I degenerated into stone; I turned first stupid and senseless, then brutish and thoughtless, and at last raving mad as any of them were; and, in short, I became as naturally pleased and easy with the place, as if indeed I had been born there.\n\nIt is scarce possible to imagine that our natures should be capable of so much degeneracy, as to make that pleasant and agreeable that in itself is the most complete misery. Here was a circumstance that I think it is scarce possible to mention a worse: I was as exquisitely miserable as, speaking of common cases, it was possible for any one to be that had life and health, and money to help them, as I had.\n\nI had weight of guilt upon me enough to sink any creature who had the least power of reflection left, and had any sense upon them of the happiness of this life, of the misery of another; then I had at first remorse indeed, but no repentance; I had now neither remorse nor repentance. I had a crime charged on me, the punishment of which was death by our law; the proof so evident, that there was no room for me so much as to plead not guilty. I had the name of an old offender, so that I had nothing to expect but death in a few weeks’ time, neither had I myself any thoughts of escaping; and yet a certain strange lethargy of soul possessed me. I had no trouble, no apprehensions, no sorrow about me, the first surprise was gone; I was, I may well say, I know not how; my senses, my reason, nay, my conscience, were all asleep; my course of life for forty years had been a horrid complication of wickedness, whoredom, adultery, incest, lying, theft; and, in a word, everything but murder and treason had been my practice from the age of eighteen, or thereabouts, to three-score; and now I was engulfed in the misery of punishment, and had an infamous death just at the door, and yet I had no sense of my condition, no thought of heaven or hell at least, that went any farther than a bare flying touch, like the stitch or pain that gives a hint and goes off. I neither had a heart to ask God’s mercy, nor indeed to think of it. And in this, I think, I have given a brief description of the completest misery on earth.\n\nAll my terrifying thoughts were past, the horrors of the place were become familiar, and I felt no more uneasiness at the noise and clamours of the prison, than they did who made that noise; in a word, I was become a mere Newgate-bird, as wicked and as outrageous as any of them; nay, I scarce retained the habit and custom of good breeding and manners, which all along till now ran through my conversation; so thorough a degeneracy had possessed me, that I was no more the same thing that I had been, than if I had never been otherwise than what I was now.\n\nIn the middle of this hardened part of my life I had another sudden surprise, which called me back a little to that thing called sorrow, which indeed I began to be past the sense of before. They told me one night that there was brought into the prison late the night before three highwaymen, who had committed robbery somewhere on the road to Windsor, Hounslow Heath, I think it was, and were pursued to Uxbridge by the country, and were taken there after a gallant resistance, in which I know not how many of the country people were wounded, and some killed.\n\nIt is not to be wondered that we prisoners were all desirous enough to see these brave, topping gentlemen, that were talked up to be such as their fellows had not been known, and especially because it was said they would in the morning be removed into the press-yard, having given money to the head master of the prison, to be allowed the liberty of that better part of the prison. So we that were women placed ourselves in the way, that we would be sure to see them; but nothing could express the amazement and surprise I was in, when the very first man that came out I knew to be my Lancashire husband, the same who lived so well at Dunstable, and the same who I afterwards saw at Brickhill, when I was married to my last husband, as has been related.\n\nI was struck dumb at the sight, and knew neither what to say nor what to do; he did not know me, and that was all the present relief I had. I quitted my company, and retired as much as that dreadful place suffers anybody to retire, and I cried vehemently for a great while. “Dreadful creature that I am,” said I, “how many poor people have I made miserable? How many desperate wretches have I sent to the devil?” He had told me at Chester he was ruined by that match, and that his fortunes were made desperate on my account; for that thinking I had been a fortune, he was run into debt more than he was able to pay, and that he knew not what course to take; that he would go into the army and carry a musket, or buy a horse and take a tour, as he called it; and though I never told him that I was a fortune, and so did not actually deceive him myself, yet I did encourage the having it thought that I was so, and by that means I was the occasion originally of his mischief.\n\nThe surprise of the thing only struck deeper into my thoughts, and gave me stronger reflections than all that had befallen me before. I grieved day and night for him, and the more for that they told me he was the captain of the gang, and that he had committed so many robberies, that Hind, or Whitney, or the Golden Farmer were fools to him; that he would surely be hanged if there were no more men left in the country he was born in; and that there would abundance of people come in against him.\nI was overwhelmed with grief for him; my own case gave me no disturbance compared to this, and I loaded myself with reproaches on his account. I bewailed his misfortunes, and the ruin he was now come to, at such a rate, that I relished nothing now as I did before, and the first reflections I made upon the horrid, detestable life I had lived began to return upon me, and as these things returned, my abhorrence of the place I was in, and of the way of living in it, returned also; in a word, I was perfectly changed, and become another body.\n\nWhile I was under these influences of sorrow for him, came notice to me that the next sessions approaching there would be a bill preferred to the grand jury against me, and that I should be certainly tried for my life at the Old Bailey. My temper was touched before, the hardened, wretched boldness of spirit which I had acquired abated, and conscious in the prison, guilt began to flow in upon my mind. In short, I began to think, and to think is one real advance from hell to heaven. All that hellish, hardened state and temper of soul, which I have said so much of before, is but a deprivation of thought; he that is restored to his power of thinking, is restored to himself.\n\nAs soon as I began, I say, to think, the first think that occurred to me broke out thus: “Lord! what will become of me? I shall certainly die! I shall be cast, to be sure, and there is nothing beyond that but death! I have no friends; what shall I do? I shall be certainly cast! Lord, have mercy upon me! What will become of me?” This was a sad thought, you will say, to be the first, after so long a time, that had started into my soul of that kind, and yet even this was nothing but fright at what was to come; there was not a word of sincere repentance in it all. However, I was indeed dreadfully dejected, and disconsolate to the last degree; and as I had no friend in the world to communicate my distressed thoughts to, it lay so heavy upon me, that it threw me into fits and swoonings several times a day. I sent for my old governess, and she, give her her due, acted the part of a true friend. She left no stone unturned to prevent the grand jury finding the bill. She sought out one or two of the jurymen, talked with them, and endeavoured to possess them with favourable dispositions, on account that nothing was taken away, and no house broken, etc.; but all would not do, they were over-ruled by the rest; the two wenches swore home to the fact, and the jury found the bill against me for robbery and house-breaking, that is, for felony and burglary.\n\nI sunk down when they brought me news of it, and after I came to myself again, I thought I should have died with the weight of it. My governess acted a true mother to me; she pitied me, she cried with me, and for me, but she could not help me; and to add to the terror of it, ’twas the discourse all over the house that I should die for it. I could hear them talk it among themselves very often, and see them shake their heads and say they were sorry for it, and the like, as is usual in the place. But still nobody came to tell me their thoughts, till at last one of the keepers came to me privately, and said with a sigh, “Well, Mrs. Flanders, you will be tried on Friday” (.); “what do you intend to do?” I turned as white as a clout, and said, “God knows what I shall do; for my part, I know not what to do.” “Why,” says he, “I won’t flatter you, I would have you prepare for death, for I doubt you will be cast; and as they say you are an old offender, I doubt you will find but little mercy. They say,” added he, “your case is very plain, and that the witnesses swear so home against you, there will be no standing it.”\n\nThis was a stab into the very vitals of one under such a burthen as I was oppressed with before, and I could not speak to him a word, good or bad, for a great while; but at last I burst out into tears, and said to him, “Lord! Mr. ——, what must I do?” “Do!” says he, “send for the ordinary; send for a minister and talk with him; for, indeed, Mrs. Flanders, unless you have very good friends, you are no woman for this world.”\n\nThis was plain dealing indeed, but it was very harsh to me, at least I thought it so. He left me in the greatest confusion imaginable, and all that night I lay awake. And now I began to say my prayers, which I had scarce done before since my last husband’s death, or from a little while after. And truly I may well call it saying my prayers, for I was in such a confusion, and had such horror upon my mind, that though I cried, and repeated several times the ordinary expression of “Lord, have mercy upon me!” I never brought myself to any sense of my being a miserable sinner, as indeed I was, and of confessing my sins to God, and begging pardon for the sake of Jesus Christ. I was overwhelmed with the sense of my condition, being tried for my life, and being sure to be condemned, and then I was as sure to be executed, and on this account I cried out all night, “Lord, what will become of me? Lord! what shall I do? Lord! I shall be hanged! Lord, have mercy upon me!” and the like.\n\nMy poor afflicted governess was now as much concerned as I, and a great deal more truly penitent, though she had no prospect of being brought to trial and sentence. Not but that she deserved it as much as I, and so she said herself; but she had not done anything herself for many years, other than receiving what I and others stole, and encouraging us to steal it. But she cried, and took on like a distracted body, wringing her hands, and crying out that she was undone, that she believed there was a curse from heaven upon her, that she should be damned, that she had been the destruction of all her friends, that she had brought such a one, and such a one, and such a one to the gallows; and there she reckoned up ten or eleven people, some of which I have given account of, that came to untimely ends; and that now she was the occasion of my ruin, for she had persuaded me to go on, when I would have left off. I interrupted her there. “No, mother, no,” said I, “don’t speak of that, for you would have had me left off when I got the mercer’s money again, and when I came home from Harwich, and I would not hearken to you; therefore you have not been to blame; it is I only have ruined myself, I have brought myself to this misery”; and thus we spent many hours together.\n\nWell, there was no remedy; the prosecution went on, and on the Thursday I was carried down to the sessions-house, where I was arraigned, as they called it, and the next day I was appointed to be tried. At the arraignment I pleaded “Not guilty,” and well I might, for I was indicted for felony and burglary; that is, for feloniously stealing two pieces of brocaded silk, value £46, the goods of Anthony Johnson, and for breaking open his doors; whereas I knew very well they could not pretend to prove I had broken up the doors, or so much as lifted up a latch.\n\nOn the Friday I was brought to my trial. I had exhausted my spirits with crying for two or three days before, so that I slept better the Thursday night than I expected, and had more courage for my trial than indeed I thought possible for me to have.\n\nWhen the trial began, the indictment was read, I would have spoke, but they told me the witnesses must be heard first, and then I should have time to be heard. The witnesses were the two wenches, a couple of hard-mouthed jades indeed, for though the thing was truth in the main, yet they aggravated it to the utmost extremity, and swore I had the goods wholly in my possession, that I had hid them among my clothes, that I was going off with them, that I had one foot over the threshold when they discovered themselves, and then I put t’ other over, so that I was quite out of the house in the street with the goods before they took hold of me, and then they seized me, and brought me back again, and they took the goods upon me. The fact in general was all true, but I believe, and insisted upon it, that they stopped me before I had set my foot clear of the threshold of the house. But that did not argue much, for certain it was that I had taken the goods, and I was bringing them away, if I had not been taken.\n\nBut I pleaded that I had stole nothing, they had lost nothing, that the door was open, and I went in, seeing the goods lie there, and with design to buy. If, seeing nobody in the house, I had taken any of them up in my hand it could not be concluded that I intended to steal them, for that I never carried them farther than the door to look on them with the better light.\n\nThe Court would not allow that by any means, and made a kind of a jest of my intending to buy the goods, that being no shop for the selling of anything, and as to carrying them to the door to look at them, the maids made their impudent mocks upon that, and spent their wit upon it very much; told the Court I had looked at them sufficiently, and approved them very well, for I had packed them up under my clothes, and was a-going with them.\n\nIn short, I was found guilty of felony, but acquitted of the burglary, which was but small comfort to me, the first bringing me to a sentence of death, and the last would have done no more. The next day I was carried down to receive the dreadful sentence, and when they came to ask me what I had to say why sentence should not pass, I stood mute a while, but somebody that stood behind me prompted me aloud to speak to the judges, for that they could represent things favourably for me. This encouraged me to speak, and I told them I had nothing to say to stop the sentence, but that I had much to say to bespeak the mercy of the Court; that I hoped they would allow something in such a case for the circumstances of it; that I had broken no doors, had carried nothing off; that nobody had lost anything; that the person whose goods they were was pleased to say he desired mercy might be shown (which indeed he very honestly did); that, at the worst, it was the first offence, and that I had never been before any court of justice before; and, in a word, I spoke with more courage that I thought I could have done, and in such a moving tone, and though with tears, yet not so many tears as to obstruct my speech, that I could see it moved others to tears that heard me.\n\nThe judges sat grave and mute, gave me an easy hearing, and time to say all that I would, but, saying neither Yes nor No to it, pronounced the sentence of death upon me, a sentence that was to me like death itself, which, after it was read, confounded me. I had no more spirit left in me, I had no tongue to speak, or eyes to look up either to God or man.\n\nMy poor governess was utterly disconsolate, and she that was my comforter before, wanted comfort now herself; and sometimes mourning, sometimes raging, was as much out of herself, as to all outward appearance, as any mad woman in Bedlam. Nor was she only disconsolate as to me, but she was struck with horror at the sense of her own wicked life, and began to look back upon it with a taste quite different from mine, for she was penitent to the highest degree for her sins, as well as sorrowful for the misfortune. She sent for a minister, too, a serious, pious, good man, and applied herself with such earnestness, by his assistance, to the work of a sincere repentance, that I believe, and so did the minister too, that she was a true penitent; and, which is still more, she was not only so for the occasion, and at that juncture, but she continued so, as I was informed, to the day of her death.\n\nIt is rather to be thought of than expressed what was now my condition. I had nothing before me but present death; and as I had no friends to assist me, or to stir for me, I expected nothing but to find my name in the dead warrant, which was to come down for the execution, the Friday afterwards, of five more and myself.\n\nIn the meantime my poor distressed governess sent me a minister, who at her request first, and at my own afterwards, came to visit me. He exhorted me seriously to repent of all my sins, and to dally no longer with my soul; not flattering myself with hopes of life, which, he said, he was informed there was no room to expect, but unfeignedly to look up to God with my whole soul, and to cry for pardon in the name of Jesus Christ. He backed his discourses with proper quotations of Scripture, encouraging the greatest sinner to repent, and turn from their evil way, and when he had done, he kneeled down and prayed with me.\n\nIt was now that, for the first time, I felt any real signs of repentance. I now began to look back upon my past life with abhorrence, and having a kind of view into the other side of time, and things of life, as I believe they do with everybody at such a time, began to look with a different aspect, and quite another shape, than they did before. The greatest and best things, the views of felicity, the joy, the griefs of life, were quite other things; and I had nothing in my thoughts but what was so infinitely superior to what I had known in life, that it appeared to me to be the greatest stupidity in nature to lay any weight upon anything, though the most valuable in this world.\nThe word eternity represented itself with all its incomprehensible additions, and I had such extended notions of it, that I know not how to express them. Among the rest, how vile, how gross, how absurd did every pleasant thing look!—I mean, that we had counted pleasant before—especially when I reflected that these sordid trifles were the things for which we forfeited eternal felicity.\n\nWith these reflections came, of mere course, severe reproaches of my own mind for my wretched behaviour in my past life; that I had forfeited all hope of any happiness in the eternity that I was just going to enter into, and on the contrary was entitled to all that was miserable, or had been conceived of misery; and all this with the frightful addition of its being also eternal.\n\nI am not capable of reading lectures of instruction to anybody, but I relate this in the very manner in which things then appeared to me, as far as I am able, but infinitely short of the lively impressions which they made on my soul at that time; indeed, those impressions are not to be explained by words, or if they are, I am not mistress of words enough to express them. It must be the work of every sober reader to make just reflections on them, as their own circumstances may direct; and, without question, this is what every one at some time or other may feel something of; I mean, a clearer sight into things to come than they had here, and a dark view of their own concern in them.\n\nBut I go back to my own case. The minister pressed me to tell him, as far as I thought convenient, in what state I found myself as to the sight I had of things beyond life. He told me he did not come as ordinary of the place, whose business it is to extort confessions from prisoners, for private ends, or for the further detecting of other offenders; that his business was to move me to such freedom of discourse as might serve to disburthen my own mind, and furnish him to administer comfort to me as far as was in his power; and assured me, that whatever I said to him should remain with him, and be as much a secret as if it was known only to God and myself; and that he desired to know nothing of me, but as above to qualify him to apply proper advice and assistance to me, and to pray to God for me.\n\nThis honest, friendly way of treating me unlocked all the sluices of my passions. He broke into my very soul by it; and I unravelled all the wickedness of my life to him. In a word, I gave him an abridgment of this whole history; I gave him a picture of my conduct for fifty years in miniature.\n\nI hid nothing from him, and he in return exhorted me to sincere repentance, explained to me what he meant by repentance, and then drew out such a scheme of infinite mercy, proclaimed from heaven to sinners of the greatest magnitude, that he left me nothing to say, that looked like despair, or doubting of being accepted; and in this condition he left me the first night.\n\nHe visited me again the next morning, and went on with his method of explaining the terms of divine mercy, which according to him consisted of nothing more, or more difficult, than that of being sincerely desirous of it, and willing to accept it; only a sincere regret for, and hatred of, those things I had done, which rendered me so just an object of divine vengeance. I am not able to repeat the excellent discourses of this extraordinary man; ’tis all that I am able to do, to say that he revived my heart, and brought me into such a condition that I never knew anything of in my life before. I was covered with shame and tears for things past, and yet had at the same time a secret surprising joy at the prospect of being a true penitent, and obtaining the comfort of a penitent—I mean, the hope of being forgiven; and so swift did thoughts circulate, and so high did the impressions they had made upon me run, that I thought I could freely have gone out that minute to execution, without any uneasiness at all, casting my soul entirely into the arms of infinite mercy as a penitent.\n\nThe good gentleman was so moved also in my behalf with a view of the influence which he saw these things had on me, that he blessed God he had come to visit me, and resolved not to leave me till the last moment; that is, not to leave visiting me.\n\nIt was no less than twelve days after our receiving sentence before any were ordered for execution, and then upon a Wednesday the dead warrant, as they call it, came down, and I found my name was among them. A terrible blow this was to my new resolutions; indeed my heart sank within me, and I swooned away twice, one after another, but spoke not a word. The good minister was sorely afflicted for me, and did what he could to comfort me with the same arguments, and the same moving eloquence that he did before, and left me not that evening so long as the prisonkeepers would suffer him to stay in the prison, unless he would be locked up with me all night, which he was not willing to be.\n\nI wondered much that I did not see him all the next day, it being the day before the time appointed for execution; and I was greatly discouraged, and dejected in my mind, and indeed almost sank for want of the comfort which he had so often, and with such success, yielded me on his former visits. I waited with great impatience, and under the greatest oppressions of spirits imaginable, till about four o’clock he came to my apartment; for I had obtained the favour, by the help of money, nothing being to be done in that place without it, not to be kept in the condemned hole, as they call it, among the rest of the prisoners who were to die, but to have a little dirty chamber to myself.\n\nMy heart leaped within me for joy when I heard his voice at the door, even before I saw him; but let any one judge what kind of motion I found in my soul, when after having made a short excuse for his not coming, he showed me that his time had been employed on my account; that he had obtained a favourable report from the Recorder to the Secretary of State in my particular case, and, in short, that he had brought me a reprieve.\n\nHe used all the caution that he was able in letting me know a thing which it would have been a double cruelty to have concealed; and yet it was too much for me; for as grief had overset me before, so did joy overset me now, and I fell into a much more dangerous swooning than I did at first, and it was not without a great difficulty that I was recovered at all.\n\nThe good man having made a very Christian exhortation to me, not to let the joy of my reprieve put the remembrance of my past sorrow out of my mind, and having told me that he must leave me, to go and enter the reprieve in the books, and show it to the sheriffs, stood up just before his going away, and in a very earnest manner prayed to God for me, that my repentance might be made unfeigned and sincere; and that my coming back, as it were, into life again, might not be a returning to the follies of life which I had made such solemn resolutions to forsake, and to repent of them. I joined heartily in the petition, and must needs say I had deeper impressions upon my mind all that night, of the mercy of God in sparing my life, and a greater detestation of my past sins, from a sense of the goodness which I had tasted in this case, than I had in all my sorrow before.\n\nThis may be thought inconsistent in itself, and wide from the business of this book; particularly, I reflect that many of those who may be pleased and diverted with the relation of the wild and wicked part of my story may not relish this, which is really the best part of my life, the most advantageous to myself, and the most instructive to others. Such, however, will, I hope, allow me the liberty to make my story complete. It would be a severe satire on such to say they do not relish the repentance as much as they do the crime; and that they had rather the history were a complete tragedy, as it was very likely to have been.\n\nBut I go on with my relation. The next morning there was a sad scene indeed in the prison. The first thing I was saluted with in the morning was the tolling of the great bell at St. Sepulchre’s, as they call it, which ushered in the day. As soon as it began to toll, a dismal groaning and crying was heard from the condemned hole, where there lay six poor souls who were to be executed that day, some from one crime, some for another, and two of them for murder.\n\nThis was followed by a confused clamour in the house, among the several sorts of prisoners, expressing their awkward sorrows for the poor creatures that were to die, but in a manner extremely differing one from another. Some cried for them; some huzzaed, and wished them a good journey; some damned and cursed those that had brought them to it—that is, meaning the evidence, or prosecutors—many pitying them, and some few, but very few, praying for them.\n\nThere was hardly room for so much composure of mind as was required for me to bless the merciful Providence that had, as it were, snatched me out of the jaws of this destruction. I remained, as it were, dumb and silent, overcome with the sense of it, and not able to express what I had in my heart; for the passions on such occasions as these are certainly so agitated as not to be able presently to regulate their own motions.\n\nAll the while the poor condemned creatures were preparing to their death, and the ordinary, as they call him, was busy with them, disposing them to submit to their sentence—I say, all this while I was seized with a fit of trembling, as much as I could have been if I had been in the same condition, as to be sure the day before I expected to be; I was so violently agitated by this surprising fit, that I shook as if it had been in the cold fit of an ague, so that I could not speak or look but like one distracted. As soon as they were all put into carts and gone, which, however, I had not courage enough to see—I say, as soon as they were gone, I fell into a fit of crying involuntarily, and without design, but as a mere distemper, and yet so violent, and it held me so long, that I knew not what course to take, nor could I stop, or put a check to it, no, not with all the strength and courage I had.\nThis fit of crying held me near two hours, and, as I believe, held me till they were all out of the world, and then a most humble, penitent, serious kind of joy succeeded; a real transport it was, or passion of joy and thankfulness, but still unable to give vent to it by words, and in this I continued most part of the day.\n\nIn the evening the good minister visited me again, and then fell to his usual good discourses. He congratulated my having a space yet allowed me for repentance, whereas the state of those six poor creatures was determined, and they were now past the offers of salvation; he earnestly pressed me to retain the same sentiments of the things of life that I had when I had a view of eternity; and at the end of all told me I should not conclude that all was over, that a reprieve was not a pardon, that he could not yet answer for the effects of it; however, I had this mercy, that I had more time given me, and that it was my business to improve that time.\n\nThis discourse, though very seasonable, left a kind of sadness on my heart, as if I might expect the affair would have a tragical issue still, which, however, he had no certainty of; and I did not indeed, at that time, question him about it, he having said that he would do his utmost to bring it to a good end, and that he hoped he might, but he would not have me be secure; and the consequence proved that he had reason for what he said.\n\nIt was about a fortnight after this that I had some just apprehensions that I should be included in the next dead warrant at the ensuing sessions; and it was not without great difficulty, and at last a humble petition for transportation, that I avoided it, so ill was I beholding to fame, and so prevailing was the fatal report of being an old offender; though in that they did not do me strict justice, for I was not in the sense of the law an old offender, whatever I was in the eye of the judge, for I had never been before them in a judicial way before; so the judges could not charge me with being an old offender, but the Recorder was pleased to represent my case as he thought fit.\n\nI had now a certainty of life indeed, but with the hard conditions of being ordered for transportation, which indeed was hard condition in itself, but not when comparatively considered; and therefore I shall make no comments upon the sentence, nor upon the choice I was put to. We shall all choose anything rather than death, especially when ’tis attended with an uncomfortable prospect beyond it, which was my case.\n\nThe good minister, whose interest, though a stranger to me, had obtained me the reprieve, mourned sincerely for this part. He was in hopes, he said, that I should have ended my days under the influence of good instruction, that I should not have been turned loose again among such a wretched crew as they generally are, who are thus sent abroad, where, as he said, I must have more than ordinary secret assistance from the grace of God, if I did not turn as wicked again as ever.\n\nI have not for a good while mentioned my governess, who had during most, if not all, of this part been dangerously sick, and being in as near a view of death by her disease as I was by my sentence, was a great penitent—I say, I have not mentioned her, nor indeed did I see her in all this time; but being now recovering, and just able to come abroad, she came to see me.\n\nI told her my condition, and what a different flux and reflux of tears and hopes I had been agitated with; I told her what I had escaped, and upon what terms; and she was present when the minister expressed his fears of my relapsing into wickedness upon my falling into the wretched companies that are generally transported. Indeed I had a melancholy reflection upon it in my own mind, for I knew what a dreadful gang was always sent away together, and I said to my governess that the good minister’s fears were not without cause. “Well, well,” says she, “but I hope you will not be tempted with such a horrid example as that.” And as soon as the minister was gone, she told me she would not have me discouraged, for perhaps ways and means might be found out to dispose of me in a particular way, by myself, of which she would talk further to me afterward.\n\nI looked earnestly at her, and I thought she looked more cheerful than she usually had done, and I entertained immediately a thousand notions of being delivered, but could not for my life image the methods, or think of one that was in the least feasible; but I was too much concerned in it to let her go from me without explaining herself, which, though she was very loth to do, yet my importunity prevailed, and, while I was still pressing, she answered me in a few words, thus: “Why, you have money, have you not? Did you ever know one in your life that was transported and had a hundred pounds in his pocket, I’ll warrant you, child?” says she.\n\nI understood her presently, but told her I would leave all that to her, but I saw no room to hope for anything but a strict execution of the order, and as it was a severity that was esteemed a mercy, there was no doubt but it would be strictly observed. She said no more but this: “We will try what can be done,” and so we parted for that night.\n\nI lay in the prison near fifteen weeks after this order for transportation was signed. What the reason of it was, I know not, but at the end of this time I was put on board of a ship in the Thames, and with me a gang of thirteen as hardened vile creatures as ever Newgate produced in my time; and it would really well take up a history longer than mine to describe the degrees of impudence and audacious villainy that those thirteen were arrived to, and the manner of their behaviour in the voyage; of which I have a very diverting account by me, which the captain of the ship who carried them over gave me the minutes of, and which he caused his mate to write down at large.\n\nIt may perhaps be thought trifling to enter here into a relation of all the little incidents which attended me in this interval of my circumstances; I mean, between the final order of my transportation and the time of my going on board the ship; and I am too near the end of my story to allow room for it; but something relating to me and my Lancashire husband I must not omit.\n\nHe had, as I have observed already, been carried from the master’s side of the ordinary prison into the press-yard, with three of his comrades, for they found another to add to them after some time; here, for what reason I knew not, they were kept in custody without being brought to trial almost three months. It seems they found means to bribe or buy off some of those who were expected to come in against them, and they wanted evidence for some time to convict them. After some puzzle on this account, at first they made a shift to get proof enough against two of them to carry them off; but the other two, of which my Lancashire husband was one, lay still in suspense. They had, I think, one positive evidence against each of them, but the law strictly obliging them to have two witnesses, they could make nothing of it. Yet it seems they were resolved not to part with the men neither, not doubting but a further evidence would at last come in; and in order to this, I think publication was made, that such prisoners being taken, any one that had been robbed by them might come to the prison and see them.\n\nI took this opportunity to satisfy my curiosity, pretending that I had been robbed in the Dunstable coach, and that I would go to see the two highwaymen. But when I came into the press-yard, I so disguised myself, and muffled my face up so, that he could see little of me, and consequently knew nothing of who I was; and when I came back, I said publicly that I knew them very well.\n\nImmediately it was rumoured all over the prison that Moll Flanders would turn evidence against one of the highwaymen, and that I was to come off by it from the sentence of transportation.\n\nThey heard of it, and immediately my husband desired to see this Mrs. Flanders that knew him so well, and was to be an evidence against him; and accordingly I had leave given to go to him. I dressed myself up as well as the best clothes that I suffered myself ever to appear in there would allow me, and went to the press-yard, but had for some time a hood over my face. He said little to me at first, but asked me if I knew him. I told him, Yes, very well; but as I concealed my face, so I counterfeited my voice, that he had not the least guess at who I was. He asked me where I had seen him. I told him between Dunstable and Brickhill; but turning to the keeper that stood by, I asked if I might not be admitted to talk with him alone. He said Yes, yes, as much as I pleased, and so very civilly withdrew.\n\nAs soon as he was gone, I had shut the door, I threw off my hood, and bursting out into tears, “My dear,” says I, “do you not know me?” He turned pale, and stood speechless, like one thunderstruck, and, not able to conquer the surprise, said no more but this, “Let me sit down”; and sitting down by a table, he laid his elbow upon the table, and leaning his head on his hand, fixed his eyes on the ground as one stupid. I cried so vehemently, on the other hand, that it was a good while ere I could speak any more; but after I had given some vent to my passion by tears, I repeated the same words, “My dear, do you not know me?” At which he answered, Yes, and said no more a good while.\n\nAfter some time continuing in the surprise, as above, he cast up his eyes towards me and said, “How could you be so cruel?” I did not readily understand what he meant; and I answered, “How can you call me cruel? What have I been cruel to you in?” “To come to me,” says he, “in such a place as this, is it not to insult me? I have not robbed you, at least not on the highway.”\n\nI perceived by this that he knew nothing of the miserable circumstances I was in, and thought that, having got some intelligence of his being there, I had come to upbraid him with his leaving me. But I had too much to say to him to be affronted, and told him in few words, that I was far from coming to insult him, but at best I came to condole mutually; that he would be easily satisfied that I had no such view, when I should tell him that my condition was worse than his, and that many ways. He looked a little concerned at the general expression of my condition being worse than his, but, with a kind smile, looked a little wildly, and said, “How can that be? When you see me fettered, and in Newgate, and two of my companions executed already, how can your your condition be worse than mine?”\n\n“Come, my dear,” says I, “we have a long piece of work to do, if I should be to relate, or you to hear, my unfortunate history; but if you are disposed to hear it, you will soon conclude with me that my condition is worse than yours.” “How is that possible,” says he again, “when I expect to be cast for my life the very next sessions?” “Yes,” says I, “’tis very possible, when I shall tell you that I have been cast for my life three sessions ago, and am under sentence of death; is not my case worse than yours?”\n\nThen indeed, he stood silent again, like one struck dumb, and after a while he starts up. “Unhappy couple!” says he. “How can this be possible?” I took him by the hand. “Come, my dear,” said I, “sit down, and let us compare our sorrows. I am a prisoner in this very house, and in much worse circumstances than you, and you will be satisfied I do not come to insult you, when I tell you the particulars.” And with this we sat down together, and I told him so much of my story as I thought was convenient, bringing it at last to my being reduced to great poverty, and representing myself as fallen into some company that led me to relieve my distresses by way that I had been utterly unacquainted with, and that they making an attempt at a tradesman’s house, I was seized upon for having been but just at the door, the maid-servant pulling me in; that I neither had broke any lock nor taken anything away, and that notwithstanding that, I was brought in guilty and sentenced to die; but that the judges, having been made sensible of the hardship of my circumstances, had obtained leave to remit the sentence upon my consenting to be transported.\n\nI told him I fared the worse for being taken in the prison for one Moll Flanders, who was a famous successful thief, that all of them had heard of, but none of them had ever seen; but that, as he knew well, was none of my name. But I placed all to the account of my ill fortune, and that under this name I was dealt with as an old offender, though this was the first thing they had ever known of me. I gave him a long particular of things that had befallen me since I saw him, but I told him if I had seen him since he might think I had, and then gave him an account how I had seen him at Brickhill; how furiously he was pursued, and how, by giving an account that I knew him, and that he was a very honest gentleman, one Mr. ——, the hue-and-cry was stopped, and the high constable went back again.\n\nHe listened most attentively to all my story, and smiled at most of the particulars, being all of them petty matters, and infinitely below what he had been at the head of; but when I came to the story of Brickhill, he was surprised. “And was it you, my dear,” said he, “that gave the check to the mob that was at our heels there, at Brickhill?” “Yes,” said I, “it was I indeed.” And then I told him the particulars which I had observed him there. “Why, then,” said he, “it was you that saved my life at that time, and I am glad I owe my life to you, for I will pay the debt to you now, and I’ll deliver you from the present condition you are in, or I will die in the attempt.”\n\nI told him, by no means; it was a risk too great, not worth his running the hazard of, and for a life not worth his saving. ’Twas no matter for that, he said, it was a life worth all the world to him; a life that had given him a new life; “for,” says he, “I was never in real danger of being taken, but that time, till the last minute when I was taken.” Indeed, he told me his danger then lay in his believing he had not been pursued that way; for they had gone off from Hockey quite another way, and had come over the enclosed country into Brickhill, not by the road, and were sure they had not been seen by anybody.\n\nHere he gave me a long history of his life, which indeed would make a very strange history, and be infinitely diverting. He told me he took to the road about twelve years before he married me; that the woman which called him brother was not really his sister, or any kin to him, but one that belonged to their gang, and who, keeping correspondence with him, lived always in town, having good store of acquaintance; that she gave them a perfect intelligence of persons going out of town, and that they had made several good booties by her correspondence; that she thought she had fixed a fortune for him when she brought me to him, but happened to be disappointed, which he really could not blame her for; that if it had been his good luck that I had had the estate, which she was informed I had, he had resolved to leave off the road and live a retired, sober life but never to appear in public till some general pardon had been passed, or till he could, for money, have got his name into some particular pardon, that so he might have been perfectly easy; but that, as it had proved otherwise, he was obliged to put off his equipage and take up the old trade again.\n\nHe gave me a long account of some of his adventures, and particularly one when he robbed the West Chester coaches near Lichfield, when he got a very great booty; and after that, how he robbed five graziers, in the west, going to Burford Fair in Wiltshire to buy sheep. He told me he got so much money on those two occasions, that if he had known where to have found me, he would certainly have embraced my proposal of going with me to Virginia, or to have settled in a plantation on some other parts of the English colonies in America.\n\nHe told me he wrote two or three letters to me, directed according to my order, but heard nothing from me. This I indeed knew to be true, but the letters coming to my hand in the time of my latter husband, I could do nothing in it, and therefore chose to give no answer, that so he might rather believe they had miscarried.\n\nBeing thus disappointed, he said, he carried on the old trade ever since, though when he had gotten so much money, he said, he did not run such desperate risks as he did before. Then he gave me some account of several hard and desperate encounters which he had with gentlemen on the road, who parted too hardly with their money, and showed me some wounds he had received; and he had one or two very terrible wounds indeed, as particularly one by a pistol bullet, which broke his arm, and another with a sword, which ran him quite through the body, but that missing his vitals, he was cured again; one of his comrades having kept with him so faithfully, and so friendly, as that he assisted him in riding near eighty miles before his arm was set, and then got a surgeon in a considerable city, remote from that place where it was done, pretending they were gentlemen travelling towards Carlisle and that they had been attacked on the road by highwaymen, and that one of them had shot him into the arm and broke the bone.\n\nThis, he said, his friend managed so well, that they were not suspected at all, but lay still till he was perfectly cured. He gave me so many distinct accounts of his adventures, that it is with great reluctance that I decline the relating them; but I consider that this is my own story, not his.\n\nI then inquired into the circumstances of his present case at that time, and what it was he expected when he came to be tried. He told me that they had no evidence against him, or but very little; for that of three robberies, which they were all charged with, it was his good fortune that he was but in one of them, and that there was but one witness to be had for that fact, which was not sufficient, but that it was expected some others would come in against him; that he thought indeed, when he first saw me, that I had been one that came of that errand; but that if somebody came in against him, he hoped he should be cleared; that he had had some intimation, that if he would submit to transport himself, he might be admitted to it without a trial, but that he could not think of it with any temper, and thought he could much easier submit to be hanged.\n\nI blamed him for that, and told him I blamed him on two accounts; first, because if he was transported, there might be a hundred ways for him that was a gentleman, and a bold enterprising man, to find his way back again, and perhaps some ways and means to come back before he went. He smiled at that part, and said he should like the last the best of the two, for he had a kind of horror upon his mind at his being sent over to the plantations, as Romans sent condemned slaves to work in the mines; that he thought the passage into another state, let it be what it would, much more tolerable at the gallows, and that this was the general notion of all the gentlemen who were driven by the exigence of their fortunes to take the road; that at the place of execution there was at least an end of all the miseries of the present state, and as for what was to follow, a man was, in his opinion, as likely to repent sincerely in the last fortnight of his life, under the pressures and agonies of a jail and the condemned hole, as he would ever be in the woods and wilderness of America; that servitude and hard labour were things gentlemen could never stoop to; that it was but the way to force them to be their own executioners afterwards, which was much worse; and that therefore he could not have any patience when he did but think of being transported.\n\nI used the utmost of my endeavour to persuade him, and joined that known woman’s rhetoric to it—I mean, that of tears. I told him the infamy of a public execution was certainly a greater pressure upon the spirits of a gentleman than any of the mortifications that he could meet with abroad could be; that he had at least in the other a chance for his life, whereas here he had none at all; that it was the easiest thing in the world for him to manage the captain of a ship, who were, generally speaking, men of good-humour and some gallantry; and a small matter of conduct, especially if there was any money to be had, would make way for him to buy himself off when he came to Virginia.\n\nHe looked wistfully at me, and I thought I guessed at what he meant, that is to say, that he had no money; but I was mistaken, his meaning was another way. “You hinted just now, my dear,” said he, “that there might be a way of coming back before I went, by which I understood you that it might be possible to buy it off here. I had rather give £200 to prevent going, than £100 to be set at liberty when I came there.” “That is, my dear,” said I, “because you do not know the place so well as I do.” “That may be,” said he; “and yet I believe, as well as you know it, you would do the same, unless it is because, as you told me, you have a mother there.”\n\nI told him, as to my mother, it was next to impossible but that she must be dead many years before; and as for any other relations that I might have there, I knew them not now; that since the misfortunes I had been under had reduced me to the condition I had been in for some years, I had not kept up any correspondence with them; and that he would easily believe, I should find but a cold reception from them if I should be put to make my first visit in the condition of a transported felon; that therefore, if I went thither, I resolved not to see them; but that I had many views in going there, if it should be my fate, which took off all the uneasy part of it; and if he found himself obliged to go also, I should easily instruct him how to manage himself, so as never to go a servant at all, especially since I found he was not destitute of money, which was the only friend in such a condition.\n\nHe smiled, and said he did not tell me he had money. I took him up short, and told him I hoped he did not understand by my speaking, that I should expect any supply from him if he had money; that, on the other hand, though I had not a great deal, yet I did not want, and while I had any I would rather add to him than weaken him in that article, seeing, whatever he had, I knew in the case of transportation he would have occasion of it all.\n\nHe expressed himself in a most tender manner upon that head. He told me what money he had was not a great deal, but that he would never hide any of it from me if I wanted it, and that he assured me he did not speak with any such apprehensions; that he was only intent upon what I had hinted to him before he went; that here he knew what to do with himself, but that there he should be the most ignorant, helpless wretch alive.\n\nI told him he frighted and terrified himself with that which had no terror in it; that if he had money, as I was glad to hear he had, he might not only avoid the servitude supposed to be the consequence of transportation, but begin the world upon a new foundation, and that such a one as he could not fail of success in, with the common application usual in such cases; that he could not but call to mind that it was what I had recommended to him many years before and had proposed it for our mutual subsistence and restoring our fortunes in the world; and I would tell him now, that to convince him both of the certainty of it and of my being fully acquainted with the method, and also fully satisfied in the probability of success, he should first see me deliver myself from the necessity of going over at all, and then that I would go with him freely, and of my own choice, and perhaps carry enough with me to satisfy him that I did not offer it for want of being able to live without assistance from him, but that I thought our mutual misfortunes had been such as were sufficient to reconcile us both to quitting this part of the world, and living where nobody could upbraid us with what was past, or we be in any dread of a prison, and without agonies of a condemned hole to drive us to it; this where we should look back on all our past disasters with infinite satisfaction, when we should consider that our enemies should entirely forget us, and that we should live as new people in a new world, nobody having anything to say to us, or we to them.\n\nI pressed this home to him with so many arguments, and answered all his own passionate objections so effectually that he embraced me, and told me I treated him with such sincerity and affection as overcame him; that he would take my advice, and would strive to submit to his fate in hope of having the comfort of my assistance, and of so faithful a counsellor and such a companion in his misery. But still he put me in mind of what I had mentioned before, namely, that there might be some way to get off before he went, and that it might be possible to avoid going at all, which he said would be much better. I told him he should see, and be fully satisfied, that I would do my utmost in that part too, and if it did not succeed, yet that I would make good the rest.\n\nWe parted after this long conference with such testimonies of kindness and affection as I thought were equal, if not superior, to that at our parting at Dunstable; and now I saw more plainly than before, the reason why he declined coming at that time any farther with me toward London than Dunstable, and why, when we parted there, he told me it was not convenient for him to come part of the way to London to bring me going, as he would otherwise have done. I have observed that the account of his life would have made a much more pleasing history than this of mine; and, indeed, nothing in it was more strange than this part, viz. that he carried on that desperate trade full five-and-twenty years and had never been taken, the success he had met with had been so very uncommon, and such that sometimes he had lived handsomely, and retired in place for a year or two at a time, keeping himself and a man-servant to wait on him, and had often sat in the coffee-houses and heard the very people whom he had robbed give accounts of their being robbed, and of the place and circumstances, so that he could easily remember that it was the same.\n\nIn this manner, it seems, he lived near Liverpool at the time he unluckily married me for a fortune. Had I been the fortune he expected, I verily believe, as he said, that he would have taken up and lived honestly all his days.\n\nHe had with the rest of his misfortunes the good luck not to be actually upon the spot when the robbery was done which he was committed for, and so none of the persons robbed could swear to him, or had anything to charge upon him. But it seems as he was taken with the gang, one hard-mouthed countryman swore home to him, and they were like to have others come in according to the publication they had made; so that they expected more evidence against him, and for that reason he was kept in hold.\n\nHowever, the offer which was made to him of admitting him to transportation was made, as I understood, upon the intercession of some great person who pressed him hard to accept of it before a trial; and indeed, as he knew there were several that might come in against him, I thought his friend was in the right, and I lay at him night and day to delay it no longer.\n\nAt last, with much difficulty, he gave his consent; and as he was not therefore admitted to transportation in court, and on his petition, as I was, so he found himself under a difficulty to avoid embarking himself as I had said he might have done; his great friend, who was his intercessor for the favour of that grant, having given security for him that he should transport himself, and not return within the term.\n\nThis hardship broke all my measures, for the steps I took afterwards for my own deliverance were hereby rendered wholly ineffectual, unless I would abandon him, and leave him to go to America by himself; than which he protested he would much rather venture, although he were certain to go directly to the gallows.\n\nI must now return to my case. The time of my being transported according to my sentence was near at hand; my governess, who continued my fast friend, had tried to obtain a pardon, but it could not be done unless with an expense too heavy for my purse, considering that to be left naked and empty, unless I had resolved to return to my old trade again, had been worse than my transportation, because there I knew I could live, here I could not. The good minister stood very hard on another account to prevent my being transported also; but he was answered, that indeed my life had been given me at his first solicitations, and therefore he ought to ask no more. He was sensibly grieved at my going, because, as he said, he feared I should lose the good impressions which a prospect of death had at first made on me, and which were since increased by his instructions; and the pious gentleman was exceedingly concerned about me on that account.\n\nOn the other hand, I really was not so solicitous about it as I was before, but I industriously concealed my reasons for it from the minister, and to the last he did not know but that I went with the utmost reluctance and affliction.\n\nIt was in the month of February that I was, with seven other convicts, as they called us, delivered to a merchant that traded to Virginia, on board a ship, riding, as they called it, in Deptford Reach. The officer of the prison delivered us on board, and the master of the vessel gave a discharge for us.\n\nWe were for that night clapped under hatches, and kept so close that I thought I should have been suffocated for want of air; and the next morning the ship weighed, and fell down the river to a place they call Bugby’s Hole, which was done, as they told us, by the agreement of the merchant, that all opportunity of escape should be taken from us. However, when the ship came thither and cast anchor, we were allowed more liberty, and particularly were permitted to come up on the deck, but not up on the quarter-deck, that being kept particularly for the captain and for passengers.\n\nWhen by the noise of the men over my head, and the motion of the ship, I perceived that they were under sail, I was at first greatly surprised, fearing we should go away directly, and that our friends would not be admitted to see us any more; but I was easy soon after, when I found they had come to an anchor again, and soon after that we had notice given by some of the men where we were, that the next morning we should have the liberty to come up on deck, and to have our friends come and see us if we had any.\n\nAll that night I lay upon the hard boards of the deck, as the passengers did, but we had afterwards the liberty of little cabins for such of us as had any bedding to lay in them, and room to stow any box or trunk for clothes and linen, if we had it (which might well be put in), for some of them had neither shirt nor shift or a rag of linen or woollen, but what was on their backs, or a farthing of money to help themselves; and yet I did not find but they fared well enough in the ship, especially the women, who got money from the seamen for washing their clothes, sufficient to purchase any common things that they wanted.\n\nWhen the next morning we had the liberty to come up on the deck, I asked one of the officers of the ship, whether I might not have the liberty to send a letter on shore, to let my friends know where the ship lay, and to get some necessary things sent to me. This was, it seems, the boatswain, a very civil, courteous sort of man, who told me I should have that, or any other liberty that I desired, that he could allow me with safety. I told him I desired no other; and he answered that the ship’s boat would go up to London the next tide, and he would order my letter to be carried.\n\nAccordingly, when the boat went off, the boatswain came to me and told me the boat was going off, and that he went in it himself, and asked me if my letter was ready he would take care of it. I had prepared myself, you may be sure, pen, ink, and paper beforehand, and I had gotten a letter ready directed to my governess, and enclosed another for my fellow-prisoner, which, however, I did not let her know was my husband, not to the last. In that to my governess, I let her know where the ship lay, and pressed her earnestly to send me what things I knew she had got ready for me for my voyage.\n\nWhen I gave the boatswain the letter, I gave him a shilling with it, which I told him was for the charge of a messenger or porter, which I entreated him to send with the letter as soon as he came on shore, that if possible I might have an answer brought back by the same hand, that I might know what was become of my things; “for sir,” says I, “if the ship should go away before I have them on board, I am undone.”\n\nI took care, when I gave him the shilling, to let him see that I had a little better furniture about me than the ordinary prisoners, for he saw that I had a purse, and in it a pretty deal of money; and I found that the very sight of it immediately furnished me with very different treatment from what I should otherwise have met with in the ship; for though he was very courteous indeed before, in a kind of natural compassion to me, as a woman in distress, yet he was more than ordinarily so afterwards, and procured me to be better treated in the ship than, I say, I might otherwise have been; as shall appear in its place.\n\nHe very honestly had my letter delivered to my governess’s own hands, and brought me back an answer from her in writing; and when he gave me the answer, gave me the shilling again. “There,” says he, “there’s your shilling again too, for I delivered the letter myself.” I could not tell what to say, I was so surprised at the thing; but after some pause, I said, “Sir, you are too kind; it had been but reasonable that you had paid yourself coach-hire, then.”\n\n“No, no,” says he, “I am overpaid. What is the gentlewoman? Your sister.”\n\n“No, sir,” says I, “she is no relation to me, but she is a dear friend, and all the friends I have in the world.” “Well,” says he, “there are few such friends in the world. Why, she cried after you like a child.” “Ay,” says I again, “she would give a hundred pounds, I believe, to deliver me from this dreadful condition I am in.”\n\n“Would she so?” says he. “For half the money I believe I could put you in a way how to deliver yourself.” But this he spoke softly, that nobody could hear.\n\n“Alas! sir,” said I, “but then that must be such a deliverance as, if I should be taken again, would cost me my life.” “Nay,” said he, “if you were once out of the ship, you must look to yourself afterwards; that I can say nothing to.” So we dropped the discourse for that time.\n\nIn the meantime, my governess, faithful to the last moment, conveyed my letter to the prison to my husband, and got an answer to it, and the next day came down herself to the ship, bringing me, in the first place, a sea-bed as they call it, and all its furniture, such as was convenient, but not to let the people think it was extraordinary. She brought with her a sea-chest—that is, a chest, such as are made for seamen, with all the conveniences in it, and filled with everything almost that I could want; and in one of the corners of the chest, where there was a private drawer, was my bank of money—this is to say, so much of it as I had resolved to carry with me; for I ordered a part of my stock to be left behind me, to be sent afterwards in such goods as I should want when I came to settle; for money in that country is not of much use where all things are brought for tobacco, much more is it a great loss to carry it from hence.\n\nBut my case was particular; it was by no means proper to me to go thither without money or goods, and for a poor convict, that was to be sold as soon as I came on shore, to carry with me a cargo of goods would be to have notice taken of it, and perhaps to have them seized by the public; so I took part of my stock with me thus, and left the other part with my governess.\n\nMy governess brought me a great many other things, but it was not proper for me to look too well provided in the ship, at least till I knew what kind of a captain we should have. When she came into the ship, I thought she would have died indeed; her heart sank at the sight of me, and at the thoughts of parting with me in that condition, and she cried so intolerably, I could not for a long time have any talk with her.\n\nI took that time to read my fellow-prisoner’s letter, which, however, greatly perplexed me. He told me he was determined to go, but found it would be impossible for him to be discharged time enough for going in the same ship, and which was more than all, he began to question whether they would give him leave to go in what ship he pleased, though he did voluntarily transport himself; but that they would see him put on board such a ship as they should direct, and that he would be charged upon the captain as other convict prisoners were; so that he began to be in despair of seeing me till he came to Virginia, which made him almost desperate; seeing that, on the other hand, if I should not be there, if any accident of the sea or of mortality should take me away, he should be the most undone creature there in the world.\n\nThis was very perplexing, and I knew not what course to take. I told my governess the story of the boatswain, and she was mighty eager with me treat with him; but I had no mind to it, till I heard whether my husband, or fellow-prisoner, so she called him, could be at liberty to go with me or no. At last I was forced to let her into the whole matter, except only that of his being my husband. I told her I had made a positive bargain or agreement with him to go, if he could get the liberty of going in the same ship, and that I found he had money.\n\nThen I read a long lecture to her of what I proposed to do when we came there, how we could plant, settle, and, in short, grow rich without any more adventures; and, as a great secret, I told her that we were to marry as soon as he came on board.\n\nShe soon agreed cheerfully to my going when she heard this, and she made it her business from that time to get him out of the prison in time, so that he might go in the same ship with me, which at last was brought to pass, though with great difficulty, and not without all the forms of a transported prisoner-convict, which he really was not yet, for he had not been tried, and which was a great mortification to him. As our fate was now determined, and we were both on board, actually bound to Virginia, in the despicable quality of transported convicts destined to be sold for slaves, I for five years, and he under bonds and security not to return to England any more, as long as he lived, he was very much dejected and cast down; the mortification of being brought on board, as he was, like a prisoner, piqued him very much, since it was first told him he should transport himself, and so that he might go as a gentleman at liberty. It is true he was not ordered to be sold when he came there, as we were, and for that reason he was obliged to pay for his passage to the captain, which we were not; as to the rest, he was as much at a loss as a child what to do with himself, or with what he had, but by directions.\n\nOur first business was to compare our stock. He was very honest to me, and told me his stock was pretty good when he came into the prison, but the living there as he did in a figure like a gentleman, and, which was ten times as much, the making of friends, and soliciting his case, had been very expensive; and, in a word, all his stock that he had left was £108, which he had about him all in gold.\n\nI gave him an account of my stock as faithfully, that is to say, of what I had taken to carry with me, for I was resolved, whatever should happen, to keep what I had left with my governess in reserve; that in case I should die, what I had with me was enough to give him, and that which was left in my governess’s hands would be her own, which she had well deserved of me indeed.\n\nMy stock which I had with me was £246 some odd shillings; so that we had £354 between us, but a worse gotten estate was scarce ever put together to begin the world with.\n\nOur greatest misfortune as to our stock was that it was all in money, which every one knows is an unprofitable cargo to be carried to the plantations. I believe his was really all he had left in the world, as he told me it was; but I, who had between £700 and £800 in bank when this disaster befell me, and who had one of the faithfullest friends in the world to manage it for me, considering she was a woman of manner of religious principles, had still £300 left in her hand, which I reserved as above; besides, some very valuable things, as particularly two gold watches, some small pieces of plate, and some rings—all stolen goods. The plate, rings, and watches were put in my chest with the money, and with this fortune, and in the sixty-first year of my age, I launched out into a new world, as I may call it, in the condition (as to what appeared) only of a poor, naked convict, ordered to be transported in respite from the gallows. My clothes were poor and mean, but not ragged or dirty, and none knew in the whole ship that I had anything of value about me.\n\nHowever, as I had a great many very good clothes and linen in abundance, which I had ordered to be packed up in two great boxes, I had them shipped on board, not as my goods, but as consigned to my real name in Virginia; and had the bills of loading signed by a captain in my pocket; and in these boxes was my plate and watches, and everything of value except my money, which I kept by itself in a private drawer in my chest, which could not be found, or opened, if found, without splitting the chest to pieces.\n\nIn this condition I lay for three weeks in the ship, not knowing whether I should have my husband with me or no, and therefore not resolving how or in what manner to receive the honest boatswain’s proposal, which indeed he thought a little strange at first.\n\nAt the end of this time, behold my husband came on board. He looked with a dejected, angry countenance, his great heart was swelled with rage and disdain; to be dragged along with three keepers of Newgate, and put on board like a convict, when he had not so much as been brought to a trial. He made loud complaints of it by his friends, for it seems he had some interest; but his friends got some check in their application, and were told he had had favour enough, and that they had received such an account of him, since the last grant of his transportation, that he ought to think himself very well treated that he was not prosecuted anew. This answer quieted him at once, for he knew too much what might have happened, and what he had room to expect; and now he saw the goodness of the advice to him, which prevailed with him to accept of the offer of a voluntary transportation. And after this his chagrin at these hell-hounds, as he called them, was a little over, he looked a little composed, began to be cheerful, and as I was telling him how glad I was to have him once more out of their hands, he took me in his arms, and acknowledged with great tenderness that I had given him the best advice possible. “My dear,” says he, “thou has twice saved my life; from henceforward it shall be all employed for you, and I’ll always take your advice.”\n\nThe ship began now to fill; several passengers came on board, who were embarked on no criminal account, and these had accommodations assigned them in the great cabin, and other parts of the ship, whereas we, as convicts, were thrust down below, I know not where. But when my husband came on board, I spoke to the boatswain, who had so early given me hints of his friendship in carrying my letter. I told him he had befriended me in many things, and I had not made any suitable return to him, and with that I put a guinea into his hand. I told him that my husband was now come on board; that though we were both under the present misfortune, yet we had been persons of a different character from the wretched crew that we came with, and desired to know of him, whether the captain might not be moved to admit us to some conveniences in the ship, for which we would make him what satisfaction he pleased, and that we would gratify him for his pains in procuring this for us. He took the guinea, as I could see, with great satisfaction, and assured me of his assistance.\n\nThen he told us he did not doubt but that the captain, who was one of the best-humoured gentlemen in the world, would be easily brought to accommodate us as well as we could desire, and, to make me easy, told me he would go up the next tide on purpose to speak to the captain about it. The next morning, happening to sleep a little longer than ordinary, when I got up, and began to look abroad, I saw the boatswain among the men in his ordinary business. I was a little melancholy at seeing him there, and going forward to speak to him, he saw me, and came towards me, but not giving him time to speak first, I said, smiling, “I doubt, sir, you have forgot us, for I see you are very busy.” He returned presently, “Come along with me, and you shall see.” So he took me into the great cabin, and there sat a good sort of a gentlemanly man for a seaman, writing, and with a great many papers before him.\n\n“Here,” says the boatswain to him that was a-writing, “is the gentlewoman that the captain spoke to you of”; and turning to me, he said, “I have been so far from forgetting your business, that I have been up at the captain’s house, and have represented faithfully to the captain what you said, relating to you being furnished with better conveniences for yourself and your husband; and the captain has sent this gentleman, who is mate of the ship, down with me, on purpose to show you everything, and to accommodate you fully to your content, and bid me assure you that you shall not be treated like what you were at first expected to be, but with the same respect as other passengers are treated.”\n\nThe mate then spoke to me, and, not giving me time to thank the boatswain for his kindness, confirmed what the boatswain had said, and added that it was the captain’s delight to show himself kind and charitable, especially to those that were under any misfortunes, and with that he showed me several cabins built up, some in the great cabin, and some partitioned off, out of the steerage, but opening into the great cabin on purpose for the accommodation of passengers, and gave me leave to choose where I would. However, I chose a cabin which opened into the steerage, in which was very good conveniences to set our chest and boxes, and a table to eat on.\n\nThe mate then told me that the boatswain had given so good a character of me and my husband, as to our civil behaviour, that he had orders to tell me we should eat with him, if we thought fit, during the whole voyage, on the common terms of passengers; that we might lay in some fresh provisions, if we pleased; or if not, he should lay in his usual store, and we should have share with him. This was very reviving news to me, after so many hardships and afflictions as I had gone through of late. I thanked him, and told him the captain should make his own terms with us, and asked him leave to go and tell my husband of it, who was not very well, and was not yet out of his cabin. Accordingly I went, and my husband, whose spirits were still so much sunk with the indignity (as he understood it) offered him, that he was scarce yet himself, was so revived with the account that I gave him of the reception we were like to have in the ship, that he was quite another man, and new vigour and courage appeared in his very countenance. So true is it, that the greatest of spirits, when overwhelmed by their afflictions, are subject to the greatest dejections, and are the most apt to despair and give themselves up.\n\nAfter some little pause to recover himself, my husband came up with me, and gave the mate thanks for the kindness, which he had expressed to us, and sent suitable acknowledgment by him to the captain, offering to pay him by advance, whatever he demanded for our passage, and for the conveniences he had helped us to. The mate told him that the captain would be on board in the afternoon, and that he would leave all that till he came. Accordingly, in the afternoon the captain came, and we found him the same courteous, obliging man that the boatswain had represented him to be; and he was so well pleased with my husband’s conversation, that, in short, he would not let us keep the cabin we had chosen, but gave us one that, as I said before, opened into the great cabin.\n\nNor were his conditions exorbitant, or the man craving and eager to make a prey of us, but for fifteen guineas we had our whole passage and provisions and cabin, ate at the captain’s table, and were very handsomely entertained.\n\nThe captain lay himself in the other part of the great cabin, having let his round house, as they call it, to a rich planter who went over with his wife and three children, who ate by themselves. He had some other ordinary passengers, who quartered in the steerage, and as for our old fraternity, they were kept under the hatches while the ship lay there, and came very little on the deck.\n\nI could not refrain acquainting my governess with what had happened; it was but just that she, who was so really concerned for me, should have part in my good fortune. Besides, I wanted her assistance to supply me with several necessaries, which before I was shy of letting anybody see me have, that it might not be public; but now I had a cabin and room to set things in, I ordered abundance of good things for our comfort in the voyage, as brandy, sugar, lemons, etc., to make punch, and treat our benefactor, the captain; and abundance of things for eating and drinking in the voyage; also a larger bed, and bedding proportioned to it; so that, in a word, we resolved to want for nothing in the voyage.\n\nAll this while I had provided nothing for our assistance when we should come to the place and begin to call ourselves planters; and I was far from being ignorant of what was needful on that occasion; particularly all sorts of tools for the planter’s work, and for building; and all kinds of furniture for our dwelling, which, if to be bought in the country, must necessarily cost double the price.\n\nSo I discoursed that point with my governess, and she went and waited upon the captain, and told him that she hoped ways might be found out for her two unfortunate cousins, as she called us, to obtain our freedom when we came into the country, and so entered into a discourse with him about the means and terms also, of which I shall say more in its place; and after thus sounding the captain, she let him know, though we were unhappy in the circumstances that occasioned our going, yet that we were not unfurnished to set ourselves to work in the country, and we resolved to settle and live there as planters, if we might be put in a way how to do it. The captain readily offered his assistance, told her the method of entering upon such business, and how easy, nay, how certain it was for industrious people to recover their fortunes in such a manner. “Madam,” says he, “’tis no reproach to any many in that country to have been sent over in worse circumstances than I perceive your cousins are in, provided they do but apply with diligence and good judgment to the business of that place when they come there.”\n\nShe then inquired of him what things it was necessary we should carry over with us, and he, like a very honest as well as knowing man, told her thus: “Madam, your cousins in the first place must procure somebody to buy them as servants, in conformity to the conditions of their transportation, and then, in the name of that person, they may go about what they will; they may either purchase some plantations already begun, or they may purchase land of the Government of the country, and begin where they please, and both will be done reasonably.” She bespoke his favour in the first article, which he promised to her to take upon himself, and indeed faithfully performed it, and as to the rest, he promised to recommend us to such as should give us the best advice, and not to impose upon us, which was as much as could be desired.\n\nShe then asked him if it would not be necessary to furnish us with a stock of tools and materials for the business of planting, and he said, “Yes, by all means.” And then she begged his assistance in it. She told him she would furnish us with everything that was convenient whatever it cost her. He accordingly gave her a long particular of things necessary for a planter, which, by his account, came to about fourscore or a hundred pounds. And, in short, she went about as dexterously to buy them, as if she had been an old Virginia merchant; only that she bought, by my direction, above twice as much of everything as he had given her a list of.\n\nThese she put on board in her own name, took his bills of loading for them, and endorsed those bills of loading to my husband, insuring the cargo afterwards in her own name, by our order; so that we were provided for all events, and for all disasters.\n\nI should have told you that my husband gave her all his whole stock of £108, which, as I have said, he had about him in gold, to lay out thus, and I gave her a good sum besides; so that I did not break into the stock which I had left in her hands at all, but after we had sorted out our whole cargo, we had yet near £200 in money, which was more than enough for our purpose.\n\nIn this condition, very cheerful, and indeed joyful at being so happily accommodated as we were, we set sail from Bugby’s Hole to Gravesend, where the ship lay about ten more days, and where the captain came on board for good and all. Here the captain offered us a civility, which indeed we had no reason to expect, namely, to let us go on shore and refresh ourselves, upon giving our words in a solemn manner that we would not go from him, and that we would return peaceably on board again. This was such an evidence of his confidence in us, that it overcame my husband, who, in a mere principle of gratitude, told him, as he could not be in any capacity to make a suitable return for such a favour, so he could not think of accepting of it, nor could he be easy that the captain should run such a risk. After some mutual civilities, I gave my husband a purse, in which was eighty guineas, and he put in into the captain’s hand. “There, captain,” says he, “there’s part of a pledge for our fidelity; if we deal dishonestly with you on any account, ’tis your own.” And on this we went on shore.\n\nIndeed, the captain had assurance enough of our resolutions to go, for that having made such provision to settle there, it did not seem rational that we would choose to remain here at the expense and peril of life, for such it must have been if we had been taken again. In a word, we went all on shore with the captain, and supped together in Gravesend, where we were very merry, stayed all night, lay at the house where we supped, and came all very honestly on board again with him in the morning. Here we bought ten dozen bottles of good beer, some wine, some fowls, and such things as we thought might be acceptable on board.\n\nMy governess was with us all this while, and went with us round into the Downs, as did also the captain’s wife, with whom she went back. I was never so sorrowful at parting with my own mother as I was at parting with her, and I never saw her more. We had a fair easterly wind sprung up the third day after we came to the Downs, and we sailed from thence the 10th of April. Nor did we touch any more at any place, till, being driven on the coast of Ireland by a very hard gale of wind, the ship came to an anchor in a little bay, near the mouth of a river, whose name I remember not, but they said the river came down from Limerick, and that it was the largest river in Ireland.\n\nHere, being detained by bad weather for some time, the captain, who continued the same kind, good-humoured man as at first, took us two on shore with him again. He did it now in kindness to my husband indeed, who bore the sea very ill, and was very sick, especially when it blew so hard. Here we bought in again a store of fresh provisions, especially beef, pork, mutton, and fowls, and the captain stayed to pickle up five or six barrels of beef to lengthen out the ship’s store. We were here not above five days, when the weather turning mild, and a fair wind, we set sail again, and in two-and-forty days came safe to the coast of Virginia.\n\nWhen we drew near to the shore, the captain called me to him, and told me that he found by my discourse I had some relations in the place, and that I had been there before, and so he supposed I understood the custom in their disposing the convict prisoners when they arrived. I told him I did not, and that as to what relations I had in the place, he might be sure I would make myself known to none of them while I was in the circumstances of a prisoner, and that as to the rest, we left ourselves entirely to him to assist us, as he was pleased to promise us he would do. He told me I must get somebody in the place to come and buy us as servants, and who must answer for us to the governor of the country, if he demanded us. I told him we should do as he should direct; so he brought a planter to treat with him, as it were, for the purchase of these two servants, my husband and me, and there we were formally sold to him, and went ashore with him. The captain went with us, and carried us to a certain house, whether it was to be called a tavern or not I know not, but we had a bowl of punch there made of rum, etc., and were very merry. After some time the planter gave us a certificate of discharge, and an acknowledgment of having served him faithfully, and we were free from him the next morning, to go wither we would.\n\nFor this piece of service the captain demanded of us six thousand weight of tabacco, which he said he was accountable for to his freighter, and which we immediately bought for him, and made him a present of twenty guineas besides, with which he was abundantly satisfied.\n\nIt is not proper to enter here into the particulars of what part of the colony of Virginia we settled in, for divers reasons; it may suffice to mention that we went into the great river Potomac, the ship being bound thither; and there we intended to have settled first, though afterwards we altered our minds.\n\nThe first thing I did of moment after having gotten all our goods on shore, and placed them in a storehouse, or warehouse, which, with a lodging, we hired at the small place or village where we landed—I say, the first thing was to inquire after my mother, and after my brother (that fatal person whom I married as a husband, as I have related at large). A little inquiry furnished me with information that Mrs. ——, that is, my mother, was dead; that my brother (or husband) was alive, which I confess I was not very glad to hear; but which was worse, I found he was removed from the plantation where he lived formerly, and where I lived with him, and lived with one of his sons in a plantation just by the place where we landed, and where we had hired a warehouse.\n\nI was a little surprised at first, but as I ventured to satisfy myself that he could not know me, I was not only perfectly easy, but had a great mind to see him, if it was possible to so do without his seeing me. In order to that I found out by inquiry the plantation where he lived, and with a woman of that place whom I got to help me, like what we call a chairwoman, I rambled about towards the place as if I had only a mind to see the country and look about me. At last I came so near that I saw the dwellinghouse. I asked the woman whose plantation that was; she said it belonged to such a man, and looking out a little to our right hands, “there,” says she, “is the gentleman that owns the plantation, and his father with him.” “What are their Christian names?” said I. “I know not,” says she, “what the old gentleman’s name is, but the son’s name is Humphrey; and I believe,” says she, “the father’s is so too.” You may guess, if you can, what a confused mixture of joy and fight possessed my thoughts upon this occasion, for I immediately knew that this was nobody else but my own son, by that father she showed me, who was my own brother. I had no mask, but I ruffled my hood so about my face, that I depended upon it that after above twenty years’ absence, and withal not expecting anything of me in that part of the world, he would not be able to know anything of me. But I need not have used all that caution, for the old gentleman was grown dim-sighted by some distemper which had fallen upon his eyes, and could but just see well enough to walk about, and not run against a tree or into a ditch. The woman that was with me had told me that by a mere accident, knowing nothing of what importance it was to me. As they drew near to us, I said, “Does he know you, Mrs. Owen?” (so they called the woman). “Yes,” said she, “if he hears me speak, he will know me; but he can’t see well enough to know me or anybody else”; and so she told me the story of his sight, as I have related. This made me secure, and so I threw open my hoods again, and let them pass by me. It was a wretched thing for a mother thus to see her own son, a handsome, comely young gentleman in flourishing circumstances, and durst not make herself known to him, and durst not take any notice of him. Let any mother of children that reads this consider it, and but think with what anguish of mind I restrained myself; what yearnings of soul I had in me to embrace him, and weep over him; and how I thought all my entrails turned within me, that my very bowels moved, and I knew not what to do, as I now know not how to express those agonies! When he went from me I stood gazing and trembling, and looking after him as long as I could see him; then sitting down to rest me, but turned from her, and lying on my face, wept, and kissed the ground that he had set his foot on.\n\nI could not conceal my disorder so much from the woman but that she perceived it, and thought I was not well, which I was obliged to pretend was true; upon which she pressed me to rise, the ground being damp and dangerous, which I did accordingly, and walked away.\n\nAs I was going back again, and still talking of this gentleman and his son, a new occasion of melancholy offered itself thus. The woman began, as if she would tell me a story to divert me: “There goes,” says she, “a very odd tale among the neighbours where this gentleman formerly live.” “What was that?” said I. “Why,” says she, “that old gentleman going to England, when he was a young man, fell in love with a young lady there, one of the finest women that ever was seen, and married her, and brought her over hither to his mother who was then living. He lived here several years with her,” continued she, “and had several children by her, of which the young gentleman that was with him now was one; but after some time, the old gentlewoman, his mother, talking to her of something relating to herself when she was in England, and of her circumstances in England, which were bad enough, the daughter-in-law began to be very much surprised and uneasy; and, in short, examining further into things, it appeared past all contradiction that the old gentlewoman was her own mother, and that consequently that son was his wife’s own brother, which struck the whole family with horror, and put them into such confusion that it had almost ruined them all. The young woman would not live with him; the son, her brother and husband, for a time went distracted; and at last the young woman went away for England, and has never been heard of since.”\n\nIt is easy to believe that I was strangely affected with this story, but ’tis impossible to describe the nature of my disturbance. I seemed astonished at the story, and asked her a thousand questions about the particulars, which I found she was thoroughly acquainted with. At last I began to inquire into the circumstances of the family, how the old gentlewoman, I mean my mother, died, and how she left what she had; for my mother had promised me very solemnly, that when she died she would do something for me, and leave it so, as that, if I was living, I should one way or other come at it, without its being in the power of her son, my brother and husband, to prevent it. She told me she did not know exactly how it was ordered, but she had been told that my mother had left a sum of money, and had tied her plantation for the payment of it, to be made good to the daughter, if ever she could be heard of, either in England or elsewhere; and that the trust was left with this son, who was the person that we saw with his father.\n\nThis was news too good for me to make light of, and, you may be sure, filled my heart with a thousand thoughts, what course I should take, how, and when, and in what manner I should make myself known, or whether I should ever make myself know or no.\n\nHere was a perplexity that I had not indeed skill to manage myself in, neither knew I what course to take. It lay heavy upon my mind night and day. I could neither sleep nor converse, so that my husband perceived it, and wondered what ailed me, strove to divert me, but it was all to no purpose. He pressed me to tell him what it was troubled me, but I put it off, till at last, importuning me continually, I was forced to form a story, which yet had a plain truth to lay it upon too. I told him I was troubled because I found we must shift our quarters and alter our scheme of settling, for that I found I should be known if I stayed in that part of the country; for that my mother being dead, several of my relations were come into that part where we then was, and that I must either discover myself to them, which in our present circumstances was not proper on many accounts, or remove; and which to do I knew not, and that this it was that made me so melancholy and so thoughtful.\n\nHe joined with me in this, that it was by no means proper for me to make myself known to anybody in the circumstances in which we then were; and therefore he told me he would be willing to remove to any other part of the country, or even to any other country if I thought fit. But now I had another difficulty, which was, that if I removed to any other colony, I put myself out of the way of ever making a due search after those effects which my mother had left. Again I could never so much as think of breaking the secret of my former marriage to my new husband; it was not a story, as I thought, that would bear telling, nor could I tell what might be the consequences of it; and it was impossible to search into the bottom of the thing without making it public all over the country, as well who I was, as what I now was also.\n\nIn this perplexity I continued a great while, and this made my spouse very uneasy; for he found me perplexed, and yet thought I was not open with him, and did not let him into every part of my grievance; and he would often say, he wondered what he had done that I would not trust him with whatever it was, especially if it was grievous and afflicting. The truth is, he ought to have been trusted with everything, for no man in the world could deserve better of a wife; but this was a thing I knew not how to open to him, and yet having nobody to disclose any part of it to, the burthen was too heavy for my mind; for let them say what they please of our sex not being able to keep a secret, my life is a plain conviction to me of the contrary; but be it our sex, or the man’s sex, a secret of moment should always have a confidant, a bosom friend, to whom we may communicate the joy of it, or the grief of it, be it which it will, or it will be a double weight upon the spirits, and perhaps become even insupportable in itself; and this I appeal to all human testimony for the truth of.\n\nAnd this is the cause why many times men as well as women, and men of the greatest and best qualities other ways, yet have found themselves weak in this part, and have not been able to bear the weight of a secret joy or of a secret sorrow, but have been obliged to disclose it, even for the mere giving vent to themselves, and to unbend the mind oppressed with the load and weights which attended it. Nor was this any token of folly or thoughtlessness at all, but a natural consequence of the thing; and such people, had they struggled longer with the oppression, would certainly have told it in their sleep, and disclosed the secret, let it have been of what fatal nature soever, without regard to the person to whom it might be exposed. This necessity of nature is a thing which works sometimes with such vehemence in the minds of those who are guilty of any atrocious villainy, such as secret murder in particular, that they have been obliged to discover it, though the consequence would necessarily be their own destruction. Now, though it may be true that the divine justice ought to have the glory of all those discoveries and confessions, yet ’tis as certain that Providence, which ordinarily works by the hands of nature, makes use here of the same natural causes to produce those extraordinary effects.\n\nI could give several remarkable instances of this in my long conversation with crime and with criminals. I knew one fellow that, while I was in prison in Newgate, was one of those they called then night-fliers. I know not what other word they may have understood it by since, but he was one who by connivance was admitted to go abroad every evening, when he played his pranks, and furnished those honest people they call thief-catchers with business to find out the next day, and restore for a reward what they had stolen the evening before. This fellow was as sure to tell in his sleep all that he had done, and every step he had taken, what he had stolen, and where, as sure as if he had engaged to tell it waking, and that there was no harm or danger in it, and therefore he was obliged, after he had been out, to lock himself up, or be locked up by some of the keepers that had him in fee, that nobody should hear him; but, on the other hand, if he had told all the particulars, and given a full account of his rambles and success, to any comrade, any brother thief, or to his employers, as I may justly call them, then all was well with him, and he slept as quietly as other people.\n\nAs the publishing this account of my life is for the sake of the just moral of very part of it, and for instruction, caution, warning, and improvement to every reader, so this will not pass, I hope, for an unnecessary digression concerning some people being obliged to disclose the greatest secrets either of their own or other people’s affairs.\n\nUnder the certain oppression of this weight upon my mind, I laboured in the case I have been naming; and the only relief I found for it was to let my husband into so much of it as I thought would convince him of the necessity there was for us to think of settling in some other part of the world; and the next consideration before us was, which part of the English settlements we should go to. My husband was a perfect stranger to the country, and had not yet so much as a geographical knowledge of the situation of the several places; and I, that, till I wrote this, did not know what the word geographical signified, had only a general knowledge from long conversation with people that came from or went to several places; but this I knew, that Maryland, Pennsylvania, East and West Jersey, New York, and New England lay all north of Virginia, and that they were consequently all colder climates, to which for that very reason, I had an aversion. For that as I naturally loved warm weather, so now I grew into years I had a stronger inclination to shun a cold climate. I therefore considered of going to Carolina, which is the only southern colony of the English on the continent of America, and hither I proposed to go; and the rather because I might with great ease come from thence at any time, when it might be proper to inquire after my mother’s effects, and to make myself known enough to demand them.\n\nWith this resolution I proposed to my husband our going away from where we was, and carrying all our effects with us to Carolina, where we resolved to settle; for my husband readily agreed to the first part, viz. that was not at all proper to stay where we was, since I had assured him we should be known there, and the rest I effectually concealed from him.\n\nBut now I found a new difficulty upon me. The main affair grew heavy upon my mind still, and I could not think of going out of the country without somehow or other making inquiry into the grand affair of what my mother had done for me; nor could I with any patience bear the thought of going away, and not make myself known to my old husband (brother), or to my child, his son; only I would fain have had this done without my new husband having any knowledge of it, or they having any knowledge of him, or that I had such a thing as a husband.\n\nI cast about innumerable ways in my thoughts how this might be done. I would gladly have sent my husband away to Carolina with all our goods, and have come after myself, but this was impracticable; he would never stir without me, being himself perfectly unacquainted with the country, and with the methods of settling there or anywhere else. Then I thought we would both go first with part of our goods, and that when we were settled I should come back to Virginia and fetch the remainder; but even then I knew he would never part with me, and be left there to go on alone. The case was plain; he was bred a gentleman, and by consequence was not only unacquainted, but indolent, and when we did settle, would much rather go out into the woods with his gun, which they call there hunting, and which is the ordinary work of the Indians, and which they do as servants; I say, he would rather do that than attend the natural business of his plantation.\n\nThese were therefore difficulties insurmountable, and such as I knew not what to do in. I had such strong impressions on my mind about discovering myself to my brother, formerly my husband, that I could not withstand them; and the rather, because it ran constantly in my thoughts, that if I did not do it while he lived, I might in vain endeavour to convince my son afterward that I was really the same person, and that I was his mother, and so might both lose the assistance and comfort of the relation, and the benefit of whatever it was my mother had left me; and yet, on the other hand, I could never think it proper to discover myself to them in the circumstances I was in, as well relating to the having a husband with me as to my being brought over by a legal transportation as a criminal; on both which accounts it was absolutely necessary to me to remove from the place where I was, and come again to him, as from another place and in another figure.\n\nUpon those considerations, I went on with telling my husband the absolute necessity there was of our not settling in Potomac River, at least that we should be presently made public there; whereas if we went to any other place in the world, we should come in with as much reputation as any family that came to plant; that, as it was always agreeable to the inhabitants to have families come among them to plant, who brought substance with them, either to purchase plantations or begin new ones, so we should be sure of a kind, agreeable reception, and that without any possibility of a discovery of our circumstances.\n\nI told him in general, too, that as I had several relations in the place where we were, and that I durst not now let myself be known to them, because they would soon come into a knowledge of the occasion and reason of my coming over, which would be to expose myself to the last degree, so I had reason to believe that my mother, who died here, had left me something, and perhaps considerable, which it might be very well worth my while to inquire after; but that this too could not be done without exposing us publicly, unless we went from hence; and then, wherever we settled, I might come, as it were, to visit and to see my brother and nephews, make myself known to them, claim and inquire after what was my due, be received with respect, and at the same time have justice done me with cheerfulness and good will; whereas, if I did it now, I could expect nothing but with trouble, such as exacting it by force, receiving it with curses and reluctance, and with all kinds of affronts, which he would not perhaps bear to see; that in case of being obliged to legal proofs of being really her daughter, I might be at loss, be obliged to have recourse to England, and it may be to fail at last, and so lose it, whatever it might be. With these arguments, and having thus acquainted my husband with the whole secret so far as was needful of him, we resolved to go and seek a settlement in some other colony, and at first thoughts, Carolina was the place we pitched upon.\n\nIn order to this we began to make inquiry for vessels going to Carolina, and in a very little while got information, that on the other side the bay, as they call it, namely, in Maryland, there was a ship which came from Carolina, laden with rice and other goods, and was going back again thither, and from thence to Jamaica, with provisions. On this news we hired a sloop to take in our goods, and taking, as it were, a final farewell of Potomac River, we went with all our cargo over to Maryland.\n\nThis was a long and unpleasant voyage, and my spouse said it was worse to him than all the voyage from England, because the weather was but indifferent, the water rough, and the vessel small and inconvenient. In the next place, we were full a hundred miles up Potomac River, in a part which they call Westmoreland County, and as that river is by far the greatest in Virginia, and I have heard say it is the greatest river in the world that falls into another river, and not directly into the sea, so we had base weather in it, and were frequently in great danger; for though we were in the middle, we could not see land on either side for many leagues together. Then we had the great river or bay of Chesapeake to cross, which is where the river Potomac falls into it, near thirty miles broad, and we entered more great vast waters whose names I know not, so that our voyage was full two hundred miles, in a poor, sorry sloop, with all our treasure, and if any accident had happened to us, we might at last have been very miserable; supposing we had lost our goods and saved our lives only, and had then been left naked and destitute, and in a wild, strange place not having one friend or acquaintance in all that part of the world. The very thought of it gives me some horror, even since the danger is past.\n\nWell, we came to the place in five days’ sailing; I think they call it Philip’s Point; and behold, when we came thither, the ship bound to Carolina was loaded and gone away but three days before. This was a disappointment; but, however, I, that was to be discouraged with nothing, told my husband that since we could not get passage to Carolina, and that the country we was in was very fertile and good, we would, if he liked of it, see if we could find out anything for our tune where we was, and that if he liked things we would settle here.\n\nWe immediately went on shore, but found no conveniences just at that place, either for our being on shore or preserving our goods on shore, but was directed by a very honest Quaker, whom we found there, to go to a place about sixty miles east; that is to say, nearer the mouth of the bay, where he said he lived, and where we should be accommodated, either to plant, or to wait for any other place to plant in that might be more convenient; and he invited us with so much kindness and simple honesty, that we agreed to go, and the Quaker himself went with us.\n\nHere we bought us two servants, viz. an English woman-servant just come on shore from a ship of Liverpool, and a Negro man-servant, things absolutely necessary for all people that pretended to settle in that country. This honest Quaker was very helpful to us, and when we came to the place that he proposed to us, found us out a convenient storehouse for our goods, and lodging for ourselves and our servants; and about two months or thereabouts afterwards, by his direction, we took up a large piece of land from the governor of that country, in order to form our plantation, and so we laid the thoughts of going to Carolina wholly aside, having been very well received here, and accommodated with a convenient lodging till we could prepare things, and have land enough cleared, and timber and materials provided for building us a house, all which we managed by the direction of the Quaker; so that in one year’s time we had nearly fifty acres of land cleared, part of it enclosed, and some of it planted with tabacco, though not much; besides, we had garden ground and corn sufficient to help supply our servants with roots and herbs and bread.\n\nAnd now I persuaded my husband to let me go over the bay again, and inquire after my friends. He was the willinger to consent to it now, because he had business upon his hands sufficient to employ him, besides his gun to divert him, which they call hunting there, and which he greatly delighted in; and indeed we used to look at one another, sometimes with a great deal of pleasure, reflecting how much better that was, not than Newgate only, but than the most prosperous of our circumstances in the wicked trade that we had been both carrying on.\nOur affair was in a very good posture; we purchased of the proprietors of the colony as much land for £35, paid in ready money, as would make a sufficient plantation to employ between fifty and sixty servants, and which, being well improved, would be sufficient to us as long as we could either of us live; and as for children, I was past the prospect of anything of that kind.\n\nBut out good fortune did not end here. I went, as I have said, over the bay, to the place where my brother, once a husband, lived; but I did not go to the same village where I was before, but went up another great river, on the east side of the river Potomac, called Rappahannock River, and by this means came on the back of his plantation, which was large, and by the help of a navigable creek, or little river, that ran into the Rappahannock, I came very near it.\n\nI was now fully resolved to go up point-blank to my brother (husband), and to tell him who I was; but not knowing what temper I might find him in, or how much out of temper rather, I might make him by such a rash visit, I resolved to write a letter to him first, to let him know who I was, and that I was come not to give him any trouble upon the old relation, which I hoped was entirely forgot, but that I applied to him as a sister to a brother, desiring his assistance in the case of that provision which our mother, at her decease, had left for my support, and which I did not doubt but he would do me justice in, especially considering that I was come thus far to look after it.\n\nI said some very tender, kind things in the letter about his son, which I told him he knew to be my own child, and that as I was guilty of nothing in marrying him, any more than he was in marrying me, neither of us having then known our being at all related to one another, so I hoped he would allow me the most passionate desire of once seeing my one and only child, and of showing something of the infirmities of a mother in preserving a violent affect for him, who had never been able to retain any thought of me one way or other.\n\nI did believe that, having received this letter, he would immediately give it to his son to read, I having understood his eyes being so dim, that he could not see to read it; but it fell out better than so, for as his sight was dim, so he had allowed his son to open all letters that came to his hand for him, and the old gentleman being from home, or out of the way when my messenger came, my letter came directly to my son’s hand, and he opened and read it.\n\nHe called the messenger in, after some little stay, and asked him where the person was who gave him the letter. The messenger told him the place, which was about seven miles off, so he bid him stay, and ordering a horse to be got ready, and two servants, away he came to me with the messenger. Let any one judge the consternation I was in when my messenger came back, and told me the old gentleman was not at home, but his son was come along with him, and was just coming up to me. I was perfectly confounded, for I knew not whether it was peace or war, nor could I tell how to behave; however, I had but a very few moments to think, for my son was at the heels of the messenger, and coming up into my lodgings, asked the fellow at the door something. I suppose it was, for I did not hear it so as to understand it, which was the gentlewoman that sent him; for the messenger said, “There she is, sir”; at which he comes directly up to me, kisses me, took me in his arms, and embraced me with so much passion that he could not speak, but I could feel his breast heave and throb like a child, that cries, but sobs, and cannot cry it out.\n\nI can neither express nor describe the joy that touched my very soul when I found, for it was easy to discover that part, that he came not as a stranger, but as a son to a mother, and indeed as a son who had never before known what a mother of his own was; in short, we cried over one another a considerable while, when at last he broke out first. “My dear mother,” says he, “are you still alive? I never expected to have seen your face.” As for me, I could say nothing a great while.\n\nAfter we had both recovered ourselves a little, and were able to talk, he told me how things stood. As to what I had written to his father, he told me he had not showed my letter to his father, or told him anything about it; that what his grandmother left me was in his hands, and that he would do me justice to my full satisfaction; that as to his father, he was old and infirm both in body and mind; that he was very fretful and passionate, almost blind, and capable of nothing; and he questioned whether he would know how to act in an affair which was of so nice a nature as this; and that therefore he had come himself, as well to satisfy himself in seeing me, which he could not restrain himself from, as also to put it into my power to make a judgment, after I had seen how things were, whether I would discover myself to his father or no.\n\nThis was really so prudently and wisely managed, that I found my son was a man of sense, and needed no direction from me. I told him I did not wonder that his father was as he had described him, for that his head was a little touched before I went away; and principally his disturbance was because I could not be persuaded to conceal our relation and to live with him as my husband, after I knew that he was my brother; that as he knew better than I what his father’s present condition was, I should readily join with him in such measure as he would direct; that I was indifferent as to seeing his father, since I had seen him first, and he could not have told me better news than to tell me that what his grandmother had left me was entrusted in his hands, who, I doubted not, now he knew who I was, would, as he said, do me justice. I inquired then how long my mother had been dead, and where she died, and told so many particulars of the family, that I left him no room to doubt the truth of my being really and truly his mother.\n\nMy son then inquired where I was, and how I had disposed myself. I told him I was on the Maryland side of the bay, at the plantation of a particular friend who came from England in the same ship with me; that as for that side of the bay where he was, I had no habitation. He told me I should go home with him, and live with him, if I pleased, as long as I lived; that as to his father, he knew nobody, and would never so much as guess at me. I considered of that a little, and told him, that though it was really no concern to me to live at a distance from him, yet I could not say it would be the most comfortable thing in the world to me to live in the house with him, and to have that unhappy object always before me, which had been such a blow to my peace before; that though I should be glad to have his company (my son), or to be as near him as possible while I stayed, yet I could not think of being in the house where I should be also under constant restraint for fear of betraying myself in my discourse, nor should I be able to refrain some expressions in my conversing with him as my son, that might discover the whole affair, which would by no means be convenient.\n\nHe acknowledged that I was right in all this. “But then, dear mother,” says he, “you shall be as near me as you can.” So he took me with him on horseback to a plantation next to his own, and where I was as well entertained as I could have been in his own. Having left me there he went away home, telling me we would talk of the main business the next day; and having first called me his aunt, and given a charge to the people, who it seems were his tenants, to treat me with all possible respect. About two hours after he was gone, he sent me a maid-servant and a Negro boy to wait on me, and provisions ready dressed for my supper; and thus I was as if I had been in a new world, and began secretly now to wish that I had not brought my Lancashire husband from England at all.\n\nHowever, that wish was not hearty neither, for I loved my Lancashire husband entirely, as indeed I had ever done from the beginning; and he merited from me as much as it was possible for a man to do; but that by the way.\n\nThe next morning my son came to visit me again almost as soon as I was up. After a little discourse, he first of all pulled out a deerskin bag, and gave it me, with five-and-fifty Spanish pistoles in it, and told me that was to supply my expenses from England, for though it was not his business to inquire, yet he ought to think I did not bring a great deal of money out with me, it not being usual to bring much money into that country. Then he pulled out his grandmother’s will, and read it over to me, whereby it appeared that she had left a small plantation, as he called it, on York River, that is, where my mother lived, to me, with the stock of servants and cattle upon it, and given it in trust to this son of mine for my use, whenever he should hear of my being alive, and to my heirs, if I had any children, and in default of heirs, to whomsoever I should by will dispose of it; but gave the income of it, till I should be heard of, or found, to my said son; and if I should not be living, then it was to him, and his heirs.\n\nThis plantation, though remote from him, he said he did not let out, but managed it by a head-clerk (steward), as he did another that was his father’s, that lay hard by it, and went over himself three or four times a year to look after it. I asked him what he thought the plantation might be worth. He said, if I would let it out, he would give me about £60 a year for it; but if I would live on it, then it would be worth much more, and, he believed, would bring me in about £150 a year. But seeing I was likely either to settle on the other side of the bay, or might perhaps have a mind to go back to England again, if I would let him be my steward he would manage it for me, as he had done for himself, and that he believed he should be able to send me as much tobacco to England from it as would yield me about £100 a year, sometimes more.\n\nThis was all strange news to me, and things I had not been used to; and really my heart began to look up more seriously than I think it ever did before, and to look with great thankfulness to the hand of Providence, which had done such wonders for me, who had been myself the greatest wonder of wickedness perhaps that had been suffered to live in the world. And I must again observe, that not on this occasion only, but even on all other occasions of thankfulness, my past wicked and abominable life never looked so monstrous to me, and I never so completely abhorred it, and reproached myself with it, as when I had a sense upon me of Providence doing good to me, while I had been making those vile returns on my part.\n\nBut I leave the reader to improve these thoughts, as no doubt they will see cause, and I go on to the fact. My son’s tender carriage and kind offers fetched tears from me, almost all the while he talked with me. Indeed, I could scarce discourse with him but in the intervals of my passion; however, at length I began, and expressing myself with wonder at my being so happy to have the trust of what I had left, put into the hands of my own child, I told him, that as to the inheritance of it, I had no child but him in the world, and was now past having any if I should marry, and therefore would desire him to get a writing drawn, which I was ready to execute, by which I would, after me, give it wholly to him and to his heirs. And in the meantime, smiling, I asked him what made him continue a bachelor so long. His answer was kind and ready, that Virginia did not yield any great plenty of wives, and that since I talked of going back to England, I should send him a wife from London.\n\nThis was the substance of our first day’s conversation, the pleasantest day that ever passed over my head in my life, and which gave me the truest satisfaction. He came every day after this, and spent a great part of his time with me, and carried me about to several of his friends’ houses, where I was entertained with great respect. Also I dined several times at his own house, when he took care always to see his half-dead father so out of the way that I never saw him, or he me. I made him one present, and it was all I had of value, and that was one of the gold watches, of which I mentioned above, that I had two in my chest, and this I happened to have with me, and I gave it him at his third visit. I told him I had nothing of any value to bestow but that, and I desired he would now and then kiss it for my sake. I did not indeed tell him that I had stole it from a gentlewoman’s side, at a meeting-house in London. That’s by the way.\n\nHe stood a little while hesitating, as if doubtful whether to take it or no; but I pressed it on him, and made him accept it, and it was not much less worth than his leather pouch full of Spanish gold; no, though it were to be reckoned as if at London, whereas it was worth twice as much there, where I gave it him. At length he took it, kissed it, told me the watch should be a debt upon him that he would be paying as long as I lived.\n\nA few days after he brought the writings of gift, and the scrivener with them, and I signed them very freely, and delivered them to him with a hundred kisses; for sure nothing ever passed between a mother and a tender, dutiful child with more affection. The next day he brings me an obligation under his hand and seal, whereby he engaged himself to manage and improve the plantation for my account, and with his utmost skill, and to remit the produce to my order wherever I should be; and withal, to be obliged himself to make up the produce £100 a year to me. When he had done so, he told me that as I came to demand it before the crop was off, I had a right to produce of the current year, and so he paid me £100 in Spanish pieces of eight, and desired me to give him a receipt for it as in full for that year, ending at Christmas following; this being about the latter end of August.\n\nI stayed here about five weeks, and indeed had much ado to get away then. Nay, he would have come over the bay with me, but I would by no means allow him to it. However, he would send me over in a sloop of his own, which was built like a yacht, and served him as well for pleasure as business. This I accepted of, and so, after the utmost expressions both of duty and affection, he let me come away, and I arrived safe in two days at my friend’s the Quaker’s.\n\nI brought over with me for the use of our plantation, three horses, with harness and saddles, some hogs, two cows, and a thousand other things, the gift of the kindest and tenderest child that ever woman had. I related to my husband all the particulars of this voyage, except that I called my son my cousin; and first I told him that I had lost my watch, which he seemed to take as a misfortune; but then I told him how kind my cousin had been, that my mother had left me such a plantation, and that he had preserved it for me, in hopes some time or other he should hear from me; then I told him that I had left it to his management, that he would render me a faithful account of its produce; and then I pulled him out the £100 in silver, as the first year’s produce; and then pulling out the deerskin purse with the pistoles, “And here, my dear,” says I, “is the gold watch.” My husband—so is Heaven’s goodness sure to work the same effects in all sensible minds where mercies touch the heart—lifted up both hands, and with an ecstacy of joy, “What is God a-doing,” says he, “for such an ungrateful dog as I am!” Then I let him know what I had brought over in the sloop, besides all this; I mean the horses, hogs, and cows, and other stores for our plantation; all which added to his surprise, and filled his heart with thankfulness; and from this time forward I believe he was as sincere a penitent, and as thoroughly a reformed man, as ever God’s goodness brought back from a profligate, a highwayman, and a robber. I could fill a larger history than this with the evidence of this truth, and but that I doubt that part of the story will not be equally diverting as the wicked part, I have had thoughts of making a volume of it by itself.\n\nAs for myself, as this is to be my own story, not my husband’s, I return to that part which related to myself. We went on with our plantation, and managed it with the help and diversion of such friends as we got there by our obliging behaviour, and especially the honest Quaker, who proved a faithful, generous, and steady friend to us; and we had very good success, for having a flourishing stock to begin with, as I have said, and this being now increased by the addition of £150 sterling in money, we enlarged our number of servants, built us a very good house, and cured every year a great deal of land. The second year I wrote to my old governess, giving her part with us of the joy of our success, and order her how to lay out the money I had left with her, which was £250 as above, and to send it to us in goods, which she performed with her usual kindness and fidelity, and this arrived safe to us.\n\nHere we had a supply of all sorts of clothes, as well for my husband as for myself; and I took especial care to buy for him all those things that I knew he delighted to have; as two good long wigs, two silver-hilted swords, three or four fine fowling-pieces, a fine saddle with holsters and pistols very handsome, with a scarlet cloak; and, in a word, everything I could think of to oblige him, and to make him appear, as he really was, a very fine gentleman. I ordered a good quantity of such household stuff as we yet wanted, with linen of all sorts for us both. As for myself, I wanted very little of clothes or linen, being very well furnished before. The rest of my cargo consisted in iron-work of all sorts, harness for horses, tools, clothes for servants, and woollen cloth, stuffs, serges, stockings, shoes, hats, and the like, such as servants wear; and whole pieces also to make up for servants, all by direction of the Quaker; and all this cargo arrived safe, and in good condition, with three woman-servants, lusty wenches, which my old governess had picked for me, suitable enough to the place, and to the work we had for them to do; one of which happened to come double, having been got with child by one of the seamen in the ship, as she owned afterwards, before the ship got so far as Gravesend; so she brought us a stout boy, about seven months after her landing.\n\nMy husband, you may suppose, was a little surprised at the arriving of all this cargo from England; and talking with me after he saw the account of this particular, “My dear,” says he, “what is the meaning of all this? I fear you will run us too deep in debt: when shall we be able to make return for it all?” I smiled, and told him that it was all paid for; and then I told him, that what our circumstances might expose us to, I had not taken my whole stock with me, that I had reserved so much in my friend’s hands, which now we were come over safe, and was settled in a way to live, I had sent for, as he might see.\n\nHe was amazed, and stood a while telling upon his fingers, but said nothing. At last he began thus: “Hold, let’s see,” says he, telling upon his fingers still, and first on his thumb; “there’s £246 in money at first, then two gold watches, diamond rings, and plate,” says he, upon the forefinger. Then upon the next finger, “Here’s a plantation on York River, £100 a year, then £150 in money, then a sloop load of horses, cows, hogs, and stores”; and so on to the thumb again. “And now,” says he, “a cargo cost £250 in England, and worth here twice the money.” “Well,” says I, “what do you make of all that?” “Make of it?” says he; “why, who says I was deceived when I married a wife in Lancashire? I think I have married a fortune, and a very good fortune too,” says he.\n\nIn a word, we were now in very considerable circumstances, and every year increasing; for our new plantation grew upon our hands insensibly, and in eight years which we lived upon it, we brought it to such pitch, that the produce was at least £300 sterling a year; I mean, worth so much in England.\n\nAfter I had been a year at home again, I went over the bay to see my son, and to receive another year’s income of my plantation; and I was surprised to hear, just at my landing there, that my old husband was dead, and had not been buried above a fortnight. This, I confess, was not disagreeable news, because now I could appear as I was, in a married condition; so I told my son before I came from him, that I believed I should marry a gentleman who had a plantation near mine; and though I was legally free to marry, as to any obligation that was on me before, yet that I was shy of it, lest the blot should some time or other be revived, and it might make a husband uneasy. My son, the same kind, dutiful, and obliging creature as ever, treated me now at his own house, paid me my hundred pounds, and sent me home again loaded with presents.\n\nSome time after this, I let my son know I was married, and invited him over to see us, and my husband wrote a very obliging letter to him also, inviting him to come and see him; and he came accordingly some months after, and happened to be there just when my cargo from England came in, which I let him believe belonged all to my husband’s estate, not to me.\n\nIt must be observed that when the old wretch my brother (husband) was dead, I then freely gave my husband an account of all that affair, and of this cousin, as I had called him before, being my own son by that mistaken unhappy match. He was perfectly easy in the account, and told me he should have been as easy if the old man, as we called him, had been alive. “For,” said he, “it was no fault of yours, nor of his; it was a mistake impossible to be prevented.” He only reproached him with desiring me to conceal it, and to live with him as a wife, after I knew that he was my brother; that, he said, was a vile part. Thus all these difficulties were made easy, and we lived together with the greatest kindness and comfort imaginable.";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-370"]="나의 유명한 몰 플랜더스의 행운과 불운, 기타 등등.\n뉴게이트에서 태어나, 어린 시절을 제외하고도 예순 해 동안 끊임없는 파란만장한 삶을 살았으니, 열두 해를 창녀로, 다섯 번을 아내로(그중 한 번은 자신의 친오라비의 아내로), 열두 해를 도둑으로, 여덟 해를 버지니아의 유배 중범죄자로 지내다가 마침내 부유해져서 정직하게 살았고 참회자로 죽었다. 그녀 자신의 비망록에서 옮겨 적은 글…\n\n대니얼 디포 지음\n\n# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE\n\n저자 서문\n근래 세상은 소설과 로맨스에 너무도 푹 빠져 있어서, 인물의 이름과 그 밖의 정황이 감추어져 있는 개인의 사사로운 이야기를 진짜라고 받아들이기란 어려운 일이다. 그러니 이러한 까닭으로 우리는 이어지는 글에 대한 판단을 독자에게 맡기고, 그가 좋을 대로 받아들이도록 내버려 두는 데에 만족할 수밖에 없다.\n\n여기서 저자는 자기 자신의 이야기를 쓰고 있는 것으로 가정되며, 그녀는 자기 기록의 맨 처음에서 본명을 감추는 편이 적절하다고 여기는 이유를 밝히고 있으므로, 그 점에 관해서는 더 말할 필요가 없겠다.\n\n이 이야기의 원본이 새로운 말로 옮겨지고, 우리가 여기서 다루는 그 유명한 부인의 문체가 다소 손질되었다는 것은 사실이다. 특히 그녀는 자기 이야기를 처음 털어놓았을 때보다 한결 점잖은 말투로 풀어내도록 다듬어졌으니, 처음 손에 들어온 원고는 훗날 참회하고 겸손해진 척하는 사람이 아니라 아직 뉴게이트에 갇혀 있는 사람에 더 가까운 언어로 쓰여 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그녀의 이야기를 완성하여 여러분이 지금 보는 모습으로 만들어 낸 붓은, 그것을 남에게 보일 만한 옷차림으로 입히고 읽을 만한 언어로 말하게 하는 데에 적잖은 어려움을 겪었다. 젊은 시절부터 방탕에 물들었던, 아니 방탕과 악덕의 소생인 한 여자가 자기의 온갖 악행을 낱낱이 이야기하고, 더 나아가 예순 해 동안 그녀가 거쳐 온 특정한 계기와 정황까지 시시콜콜 풀어 내려갈 때, 저자는 그것을 단정하게 감싸서, 특히 음란한 독자들이 그것을 제멋대로 나쁜 쪽으로 이용할 여지를 주지 않도록 하느라 적잖은 고생을 했을 것이 분명하다.\n\n그러나 이 이야기를 새로 단장하면서, 음탕한 관념이나 외설스러운 표현을 조금도 주지 않도록, 그녀의 표현 가운데 가장 고약한 대목에 이르기까지도 온갖 주의를 다 기울였다. 이 목적을 위해, 점잖게 이야기할 수 없는 그녀의 방탕한 삶 가운데 일부는 아예 빼 버렸고, 그 밖의 여러 부분도 매우 많이 줄였다. 남은 것은 가장 정숙한 독자나 점잖은 청자의 비위도 거스르지 않기를 바라며, 가장 고약한 이야기에서도 최선의 쓸모를 끌어냈으니, 그 교훈은 이야기가 독자를 다른 길로 기울게 할 만한 대목에서조차 그를 진지하게 붙들어 두기를 바란다. 회개한 사악한 삶의 이야기를 들려주자면, 그 사악한 부분을 실제 이야기가 허용하는 만큼 사악하게 그려 내야 하는 법이니, 그래야만 회개의 부분—이는 똑같은 기백과 생기로 그려진다면 분명 가장 훌륭하고 가장 빛나는 부분이거니와—을 돋보이게 하고 거기에 아름다움을 부여할 수 있는 것이다.\n\n회개의 부분을 그려 낼 때는 범죄의 부분에서와 똑같은 생기, 똑같은 광채와 아름다움이 있을 수 없다는 주장이 있다. 그 주장에 어떤 진실이 있다면, 그것은 읽을 때 똑같은 맛과 풍미가 없기 때문이라고 말할 수밖에 없으며, 사실 그 차이가 소재의 실제 가치에 있다기보다는 독자의 취향과 입맛에 있다는 것은 너무도 옳은 말이다.\n\n그러나 이 책은 주로 그것을 읽을 줄 알고, 이야기가 처음부터 끝까지 권하는 좋은 쓸모를 끌어낼 줄 아는 이들에게 권하는 것이므로, 그런 독자들은 우화보다는 교훈에, 이야기보다는 그 적용에, 묘사되는 인물의 삶보다는 글쓴이의 의도에 더 흡족해하기를 바라는 바이다.\n\n이 이야기에는 즐거운 사건이 가득하며, 그 모두가 유익하게 적용되어 있다. 그것들을 들려주는 과정에 솜씨 있게 부여된 흐뭇한 전환이 있어, 자연스럽게 이렇게든 저렇게든 독자를 가르친다. 콜체스터의 젊은 신사와 보낸 그녀의 방탕한 삶의 첫 부분은, 그 죄를 폭로하고, 비슷한 처지에 놓인 모든 이에게 그러한 일의 파멸적인 결말과, 두 당사자의 어리석고 분별없으며 가증스러운 행실을 경고하기 위해, 너무도 알맞은 전환을 많이 담고 있어서, 그녀가 자신의 어리석음과 사악함에 대해 생생하게 묘사한 모든 것을 충분히 속죄하고 있다.\n\n바스에서 그녀의 정부情夫가 보인 회개, 그리고 그가 병으로 앓아눕는 정당한 경각심에 이끌려 어떻게 그녀를 버리게 되었는가, 가장 다정한 벗 사이의 합법적인 친밀함에 대해서까지 거기서 주어진 정당한 경계, 그리고 신의 도우심 없이는 그들이 가장 엄숙한 덕의 결심조차 어떻게 지켜 내지 못하는가—이런 부분들은, 올바른 분별력을 지닌 이에게는, 이를 끌어들이는 그 모든 연애의 사슬 같은 이야기보다 더 진정한 아름다움을 담고 있는 것으로 보일 것이다.\n\n한마디로, 이야기 전체에서 거기에 들어 있던 온갖 경박함과 방종함이 세심하게 걸러진 것과 마찬가지로, 그 모두가 더할 나위 없는 주의를 기울여 덕스럽고 종교적인 쓸모에 적용되었다. 누구도 명백한 부당함을 저지르지 않고서는 이 책에, 혹은 이를 출간하는 우리의 의도에 어떤 비난도 던질 수 없다.\n\n연극을 옹호하는 이들은 어느 시대에나, 그들의 희곡이 유익하며 가장 문명화되고 가장 종교적인 정부에서도 허용되어야 마땅하다고 사람들을 설득하기 위해 이것을 큰 논거로 삼아 왔으니, 곧 그것이 덕스러운 목적에 적용되며, 가장 생생한 재현을 통해 덕과 고매한 원칙을 권하고, 온갖 종류의 악덕과 풍속의 타락을 막고 폭로하기를 결코 게을리하지 않는다는 것이다. 만약 그들이 정말로 그렇게 하고, 무대에서의 연기의 기준으로 그 규칙을 한결같이 지킨다는 것이 사실이라면, 그들을 위해 많은 말을 할 수 있을 것이다.\n\n이 책의 무한한 다채로움 속에서 이 근본 원칙은 가장 엄격하게 지켜진다. 어느 부분에서든 사악한 행위는 하나도 빠짐없이 처음부터 끝까지 불행하고 비참하게 그려지며, 무대에 오른 그 어떤 극악무도한 악당도 불행한 결말에 이르거나 회개하는 자가 되고, 언급된 어떤 나쁜 것도 이야기 가운데서조차 단죄되지 않는 것이 없으며, 어떤 덕스럽고 올바른 것도 그에 합당한 칭송을 함께 지니지 않는 것이 없다. 사례라든가, 나쁜 무리라든가, 외설스러운 언어 따위, 그토록 많은 정당한 반대에 부딪히는 그런 사물의 재현조차 권하기 위해 세워진 규칙에, 이보다 더 정확하게 부합할 수 있는 것이 무엇이겠는가?\n\n이러한 바탕 위에서 이 책은, 그 어느 부분에서든 무언가 배울 점이 있고, 어떤 올바르고 종교적인 추론을 끌어낼 수 있는 작품으로서 독자에게 권하는 바이니, 독자가 기꺼이 그것을 활용하고자 한다면 무언가 가르침을 얻게 될 것이다.\n\n이 이름난 부인이 인류를 약탈하며 벌인 온갖 행적은, 정직한 사람들에게 그런 자들을 경계하라는 그만큼 많은 경고로서 서 있으며, 어떤 수법으로 무고한 사람들이 꾀임에 빠져 약탈당하고 강탈당하는지, 그리하여 어떻게 그것을 피해야 하는지를 그들에게 넌지시 일러 준다. 어머니의 허영으로 곱게 차려입혀 무용 학교에 가던 어린 무고한 아이를 그녀가 털어먹은 일은, 앞으로 그런 사람들에게 좋은 경계가 될 것이며, 공원에서 젊은 부인의 옆구리에서 금시계를 빼낸 일도 마찬가지다.\n\n세인트존 거리의 마차 옆에서 어떤 덜렁대는 계집아이에게서 보따리를 빼앗은 일, 화재 현장에서 그리고 다시 하리치에서 챙긴 노획물—이 모두가, 그런 경우에 온갖 종류의 갑작스러운 봉변 앞에서 정신을 더 차리라는 훌륭한 경고를 우리에게 준다.\n\n마침내 버지니아에서 유배된 남편과 더불어 술 끊고 부지런히 살림을 꾸려 나간 일은, 유배의 비참함 때문이든 다른 재난 때문이든 타향에서 재기를 도모할 수밖에 없는 모든 불운한 이들에게 가르침이 풍성한 이야기이니, 근면과 노력은 세상의 가장 외진 곳에서조차 마땅한 보답을 받으며, 어떤 처지도 너무 낮거나, 너무 비천하거나, 너무 가망 없을 수 없어서, 지칠 줄 모르는 근면이 우리를 거기에서 건져 내는 데 큰 몫을 하고, 머지않아 가장 미천한 자도 다시 세상에 모습을 드러내게 일으켜 세우며, 그의 삶에 새로운 처지를 마련해 준다는 것을 그들에게 알려 준다.\n\n이 책에서 우리가 손에 이끌려 다다르게 되는 진지한 추론은 이 몇 가지인데, 이것만으로도 누구든 이 책을 세상에 권하는 것을 정당화하기에, 더더욱 그것을 출간하는 것을 정당화하기에 충분하다.\n\n이 이야기가 어느 정도 짐작하게 해 주고 그 일부를 들여다보게 해 주는, 가장 아름다운 두 부분이 아직 뒤에 남아 있으나, 둘 중 어느 것도 같은 책에 담기에는 너무 길고, 사실 내가 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 그 자체로 한 권의 책에 해당한다. 곧, 첫째, 그녀가 부르는 대로 하자면 그녀의 후견인의 삶인데, 이 여자는 몇 해 사이에 귀부인, 창녀, 포주, 산파와 이른바 산파장, 전당포 주인, 아이 받아 키우는 자, 도둑과 도둑의 장물—다시 말해 훔친 물건—을 받는 자, 한마디로 그녀 자신이 도둑이요 도둑을 길러 내는 자 등등, 온갖 두드러진 단계를 두루 거친 듯하면서도, 끝내는 회개한 자가 되었다.\n\n둘째는 유배된 그녀 남편의 삶인데, 노상강도였던 그는 길에서 열두 해 동안 성공적인 악행의 삶을 살았던 듯하며, 끝내는 죄수가 아니라 자원 유배자로 빠져나올 만큼 잘 풀려서, 그의 삶에는 믿기 어려운 다채로움이 담겨 있다.\n\n그러나 앞서 말했듯이, 이것들은 여기에 끌어들이기에는 너무 긴 것이라, 따로 펴낸다고 약속할 수도 없다.\n\n사실 이 이야기가 그녀 자신이 부르는 대로 그 유명한 몰 플랜더스의 삶의 끝까지 온전히 이어졌다고는 말할 수 없으니, 죽은 뒤에 자기 삶을 쓸 수 있지 않은 한, 누구도 자기 삶을 그 끝까지 온전히 쓸 수는 없는 노릇이기 때문이다. 그러나 제삼자의 손으로 쓰인 그녀 남편의 삶이 두 사람 모두에 대한 충실한 기록을 주어, 그들이 그 나라에서 얼마나 오래 함께 살았는지, 그리고 약 여덟 해 뒤에 어떻게 둘 다 다시 잉글랜드로 돌아왔는지를 전해 주는데, 그동안 그들은 매우 부유해졌고, 그곳에서 그녀는 아주 늙도록 산 듯하나, 처음만큼 그리 대단한 회개자는 아니었던 듯하며, 다만 실은 늘 자신의 지난 삶과 그 모든 부분을 혐오스럽게 이야기했던 것만은 분명한 듯하다.\n\n메릴랜드와 버지니아에서의 그녀의 마지막 장면에서는 여러 유쾌한 일이 일어나, 그녀 삶의 그 부분을 매우 흐뭇하게 만들지만, 그것들은 그녀 자신이 들려준 일들과 같은 우아함으로 이야기되지는 않으므로, 우리가 여기서 끊는 편이 그래도 더 나을 것이다.\n몰 플랜더스\n\n# MOLL FLANDERS\n\n내 본명은 뉴게이트와 올드베일리의 기록부나 명부에 너무도 잘 알려져 있고, 또 내 행적과 관련하여 그곳에 아직 결판나지 않은 중대한 일들이 몇 가지 걸려 있는지라, 내가 이 글에 내 이름이나 내 가문의 내력을 적어 넣으리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이다. 어쩌면 내가 죽은 뒤에는 더 잘 알려질지도 모르겠으나, 지금으로서는 적절치 않을 터이니, 인물이나 죄목에 예외나 유보를 두지 않은 일반사면령이 내린다 해도 마찬가지일 것이다.\n\n내게 해를 끼칠 처지에서 벗어난(내가 종종 그렇게 가리라 예상했던 대로, 계단과 밧줄을 밟고 이 세상을 하직한) 내 가장 고약한 동료들 가운데 더러가 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알고 있었다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하니, 그러므로 내가 지금 누구인가뿐 아니라 지난날 누구였는가를 감히 밝힐 수 있을 때까지는, 그 이름으로 나 자신을 일컫도록 허락해 주시기 바란다.\n\n내가 듣기로 우리 이웃 나라 가운데 하나에서는—그것이 프랑스인지 아니면 다른 어디인지는 모르겠으나—국왕의 명령이 있어서, 어떤 범죄자가 사형이나 갤리선 노역이나 유배형에 처해지면, 그들이 자식을 남길 경우, 그런 아이들은 대개 부모의 가난이나 재산 몰수로 의지할 데가 없게 마련이므로, 그 즉시 정부의 보호 아래 거두어져 고아원이라 불리는 시설에 넣어지고, 거기서 길러지고 입혀지고 먹여지고 가르침을 받으며, 나가도 될 나이가 되면 직업이나 일자리에 배치되어, 정직하고 부지런한 처신으로 스스로 잘 먹고살 수 있게 된다고 한다.\n\n이것이 우리나라의 관습이었다면, 나는 내 운명이 그러했듯이 벗도 없고, 옷도 없고, 세상에 도움도 도와줄 이도 없는 가엾고 외로운 계집아이로 남겨지지 않았을 것이며, 그로 인해 나는 내 처지를 이해하거나 그것을 바로잡을 방도를 알 수 있기도 전에 너무나 큰 곤경에 내몰렸을 뿐 아니라, 그 자체로 수치스러울 뿐더러 그 통상적인 흐름이 영혼과 육체를 다 함께 급속히 파멸로 이끄는 삶의 길로 끌려들어 갔던 것이다.\n\n그러나 여기서는 사정이 달랐다. 내 어머니는 입에 올리기도 민망한 어떤 좀도둑질로 중범죄 유죄판결을 받았으니, 곧 칩사이드의 어떤 포목상에게서 고운 홀란드 천 세 필을 슬쩍 빌릴 기회를 잡았던 것이다. 그 정황은 너무 길어서 되풀이할 수 없거니와, 나는 그 이야기를 하도 여러 갈래로 들은 터라, 어느 것이 올바른 이야기인지 도무지 확신할 수가 없다.\n\n어찌 되었든, 그들 모두가 한결같이 동의하는 바는 이러하니, 내 어머니는 임신을 사유로 호소했고, 임신한 것이 확인되어 약 일곱 달 동안 형 집행이 유예되었다는 것이다. 그동안 나를 세상에 낳고 다시 거동하게 되자, 그들의 말마따나 다시 이전의 판결을 받으러 불려 나갔으나, 식민지로 유배되는 은전을 입었고, 생후 반년쯤 된 나를 남겨 두고 갔으니, 여러분도 짐작하겠지만 나쁜 손에 맡겨진 채였다.\n\n이는 내 생애의 첫 시기와 너무 가까운 일이라 전해 들은 말로밖에는 나 자신에 관해 아무것도 이야기할 수 없으니, 다만 내가 그토록 불행한 곳에서 태어난 까닭에 갓난아이 시절 양육을 의지할 교구가 없었다는 것만 언급해 두면 충분하다. 또 내가 어떻게 목숨을 부지했는지도 도무지 알 길이 없으니, 다만 들은 바로는 내 어머니의 어떤 친척이 한동안 나를 유모로 거두어 데려갔다는 것뿐, 누구의 비용으로, 혹은 누구의 지시로 그리되었는지는 전혀 알지 못한다.\n\n내가 나 자신에 관해 기억해 낼 수 있는, 혹은 알아낼 수 있었던 최초의 일은, 내가 집시 혹은 이집트인이라 불리는 그 무리 가운데 떠돌아다녔다는 것이다. 그러나 내가 그들 틈에 있었던 것은 아주 잠깐이었으리라 생각되니, 그들이 데리고 다니는 모든 아이에게 아주 어릴 적부터 하듯이 내 살갗이 거뭇거뭇하게 물들거나 검게 칠해지지는 않았기 때문이다. 또 내가 어떻게 그들 틈에 끼게 되었는지, 어떻게 그들에게서 벗어났는지도 알 수 없다.\n\n그 사람들이 나를 버리고 간 곳은 에식스주의 콜체스터였다. 내 머릿속에는 내가 거기서 그들을 떠났다(다시 말해, 몸을 숨기고 그들과 더는 가지 않으려 했다)는 생각이 들지만, 그 일을 소상히 말할 수는 없다. 다만 이것만은 기억하니, 콜체스터의 어떤 교구 관리에게 붙들렸을 때, 나는 집시들과 함께 이 마을에 들어왔으나 그들과 더는 가려 하지 않았고, 그래서 그들이 나를 버려두고 갔으며, 그들이 어디로 갔는지는 모르겠다고 진술했다. 그들로서도 그것을 내게 기대할 수는 없었으니, 그들이 그 무리를 수소문하러 온 고을에 사람을 보냈으나, 끝내 찾아내지 못한 듯했기 때문이다.\n\n이제 나는 보살핌을 받을 길에 들어섰다. 법적으로는 내가 이 마을의 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 어느 교구의 부담도 아니었으나, 내 처지가 알려지고, 또 내가 세 살이 채 안 되어 무슨 일을 하기에는 너무 어리다는 것이 드러나자, 동정심에 이끌린 마을의 치안판사들이 나를 돌보도록 명했고, 나는 마치 그곳에서 태어나기라도 한 것처럼 그들 자신의 아이가 되었다.\n\n그들이 나를 위해 마련한 조처 가운데, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 어느 여인에게 유모살이로 맡겨진 것은 내게 다행한 일이었다. 그 여인은 사실 가난하긴 했으나 한때는 더 나은 처지에 있었고, 나 같은 처지로 여겨지는 아이들을 받아, 어느 정도 나이가 되어 일자리에 가거나 제 밥벌이를 할 만하다 싶을 때까지 온갖 필요한 것을 갖춰 데리고 있으면서 근근이 생계를 꾸려 가던 사람이었다.\n\n이 여인은 또한 작은 학교도 운영하고 있었으니, 그것을 꾸려 아이들에게 읽기와 바느질을 가르쳤다. 그리고 앞서 말했듯이 전에는 넉넉한 형편으로 살았던 터라, 자신이 거두는 아이들을 무던히 정성을 들여서, 또한 무던히 솜씨를 부려 길러 냈다.\n\n그러나 무엇보다 값진 것은, 그녀가 아이들을 매우 신앙심 깊게 길러 냈다는 점이니, 그녀 자신이 매우 진중하고 경건한 여인으로, 살림 솜씨가 좋고 청결하며, 매우 예의 바르고 행실이 단정했던 까닭이다. 그래서 한마디로, 소박한 음식과 거친 잠자리와 변변찮은 옷차림은 각오해야 했지만, 우리는 마치 무용 학교에라도 다닌 것처럼 예의 바르고 품위 있게 길러졌다.\n\n나는 여기서 여덟 살이 될 때까지 지냈는데, 그때 치안판사들이(그들을 그렇게 불렀던 것 같다) 나더러 일자리에 나가라는 명을 내렸다는 소식에 겁에 질리고 말았다. 어디로 가게 되든 나는 심부름이나 다니고 어느 부엌데기의 종노릇이나 하는 것 말고는 변변한 일을 할 수 없었는데, 그들은 이 점을 자주 일러 주었고, 그것이 나를 몹시 두렵게 했다. 어렸지만 나는 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 일자리에 나가는 것(다시 말해 하인이 되는 것)이 진저리나게 싫었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 우리가 부르는 대로 유모에게, 만약 그녀가 허락만 해 준다면 일자리에 나가지 않고도 밥벌이를 할 수 있을 것 같다고 말했다. 그녀가 내게 바늘로 바느질하는 법과, 그 도시의 주된 생업인 털실 잣는 법을 가르쳐 준 터라, 나를 데리고 있어 주기만 한다면 그녀를 위해 일하겠노라고, 아주 열심히 일하겠노라고 말했다.\n\n나는 거의 날마다 그녀에게 열심히 일하겠다는 이야기를 했다. 한마디로, 온종일 일하고 우는 것밖에는 하지 않았으니, 이것이 그 착하고 다정한 여인의 마음을 너무도 아프게 한 나머지, 마침내 그녀는 나를 걱정하기 시작했다. 그녀가 나를 무척 아꼈기 때문이다.\n\n그 일이 있은 뒤 어느 날, 우리 가엾은 아이들이 모두 일하고 있는 방으로 그녀가 들어와서는, 여느 때처럼 선생으로서 앉던 자리가 아니라, 마치 일부러 나를 지켜보고 내가 일하는 모습을 보려는 듯, 바로 내 맞은편에 앉았다. 나는 그녀가 시킨 일을 하고 있었으니, 내 기억으로는 그녀가 맡아 짓기로 한 셔츠 몇 벌에 표시를 새기는 일이었다. 한참 뒤에 그녀가 내게 말을 걸기 시작했다. \"이 어리석은 것아.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"넌 늘 울기만 하는구나\"(그때도 나는 울고 있었다). \"어디 말해 보렴, 무엇 때문에 우는 게냐?\" \"사람들이 절 데려가서,\" 내가 말했다. \"일자리에 보내려 하는데, 전 집안일을 할 줄 모르거든요.\" \"얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네 말대로 집안일을 할 줄 모른다 해도, 차차 배우게 될 게다. 처음부터 힘든 일을 시키지는 않을 거야.\" \"아뇨, 시킬 거예요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 제가 못 하면 절 때릴 거고, 하녀들도 절 때려서 힘든 일을 시킬 거예요. 전 조그만 계집아이라 그런 일은 못 한단 말이에요.\" 그러고는 다시 울기 시작해서, 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 할 수 없을 지경이 되었다.\n\n이것이 어머니처럼 다정한 내 유모의 마음을 움직여서, 그녀는 그때부터 나를 아직은 일자리에 보내지 않기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 그녀는 내게 울지 말라고, 시장 어른께 말씀드릴 테니 내가 더 클 때까지는 일자리에 나가지 않아도 된다고 했다. 그러나 이 말로는 내 마음이 풀리지 않았으니, 일자리에 나갈 생각을 하는 것이 내게는 너무도 무서운 일이라, 설령 그녀가 내가 스무 살이 될 때까지는 가지 않아도 된다고 장담했다 한들, 내게는 마찬가지였을 것이다. 결국 언젠가는 그렇게 되리라는 바로 그 두려움 때문에, 나는 그동안 내내 울었을 것이 분명하다.\n\n내가 여전히 진정되지 않는 것을 보고, 그녀는 나에게 화를 내기 시작했다. \"대체 어쩌라는 게냐?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"더 클 때까지는 일자리에 나가지 않아도 된다고 하지 않았느냐?\" \"네,\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 결국엔 가야 하잖아요.\" \"아니, 그래서 뭐?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 애가 미쳤나? 그럼 넌 뭐가 되고 싶은 게냐—귀부인이라도 되고 싶은 게야?\" \"네.\" 내가 말하고는, 다시 목 놓아 울 만큼 서럽게 울었다.\n\n이 말에 그 노부인은 나를 두고 웃음을 터뜨렸으니, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 당연한 노릇이었다. \"그래, 마님, 황송하옵니다.\" 그녀가 나를 놀리며 말했다. \"귀부인이 되시겠다고요. 한데 어떻게 귀부인이 되시려나? 원, 손가락 끝으로 그리하시려나?\"\n\n\"네.\" 나는 또 아주 천진하게 대답했다.\n\n\"원, 네가 벌면 얼마나 벌겠느냐?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"일해서 얼마나 받느냔 말이다.\"\n\n\"3펜스요.\" 내가 말했다. \"실을 자으면요. 그리고 보통 바느질을 하면 4펜스고요.\"\n\n\"저런! 가엾은 귀부인 같으니.\" 그녀가 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"그걸로 네가 뭘 어쩌겠다는 게냐?\"\n\n\"그걸로 제가 먹고살 수 있어요.\" 내가 말했다. \"할머니가 절 데리고 살게만 해 주신다면요.\" 그리고 이 말을 어찌나 가엾게 애원하는 투로 했던지, 나중에 그녀가 내게 들려준 바로는, 그 가엾은 여인의 마음이 나를 향해 애틋해졌다고 한다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그걸로는 너 하나 건사하면서 옷까지 사 입히진 못한단다. 그럼 이 조그만 귀부인 마님 옷은 누가 사 드려야 하나?\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하며 줄곧 나를 보고 미소 지었다.\n\n\"그럼 제가 더 열심히 일할게요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그래서 다 할머니께 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것! 그걸로는 너도 못 건사한단다.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"겨우 끼니나 댈까 말까 한 걸.\"\n\n\"그럼 전 끼니를 안 먹을게요.\" 내가 다시 아주 천진하게 말했다. \"그저 할머니랑 살게만 해 주세요.\"\n\n\"원, 끼니도 안 먹고 어떻게 살겠느냐?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"살 수 있어요.\" 나는 또 대답했는데, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 영락없이 어린아이 같은 투였고, 여전히 서럽게 울고 있었다.\n\n이 모든 것에 내 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있었던 것은 아니다. 여러분도 쉬이 알 수 있듯이, 그것은 온통 천성에서 우러난 것이었다. 그러나 거기에 어찌나 많은 순진함과 어찌나 많은 절절함이 어려 있었던지, 한마디로 그 착하고 어머니 같은 분도 따라 울게 만들었으니, 마침내 그녀는 나만큼이나 줄줄 울다가, 나를 데리고 교실 밖으로 이끌어 냈다. \"자,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"넌 일자리에 안 나가도 된다. 나랑 같이 살자꾸나.\" 그리고 이 말이 당장은 내 마음을 가라앉혀 주었다.\n\n그 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 그녀가 시장을 찾아뵈러 가서 자기 일과 관련된 이런저런 이야기를 하다가, 마침내 내 이야기가 나오자, 마음씨 착한 내 유모는 시장에게 그 전말을 죄다 들려주었다. 그는 그 이야기가 어찌나 흡족했던지, 자기 부인과 두 딸을 불러서 듣게 했고, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그들 사이에 적잖은 웃음을 자아냈다.\n\n그런데 채 한 주도 지나기 전에, 난데없이 시장 부인과 두 딸이 내 늙은 유모를 찾아, 또 그 학교와 아이들을 보러 집으로 찾아왔다. 그들이 잠시 둘러본 뒤, \"그래, ○○ 부인,\" 시장 부인이 내 유모에게 말했다. \"어디, 귀부인이 되겠다는 그 조그만 계집아이가 어느 아이인가요?\" 나는 그 말을 들었고, 처음에는 까닭도 모른 채 끔찍하게 겁이 났다. 그런데 시장 부인이 내게로 다가왔다. \"자, 아가씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"무슨 일을 하고 있는 거니?\" 아가씨라는 말은 우리 학교에서는 좀처럼 들어 본 적 없는 말이라, 나는 그녀가 나를 뭐라고 그런 고약한 이름으로 부르는 건가 의아했다. 어쨌든 나는 일어서서 인사를 올렸고, 그녀는 내 손에서 일감을 받아 들고 들여다보더니 아주 잘했다고 했다. 그러고는 내 손을 하나 들어 올렸다. \"어머나,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"누가 알겠어요, 이 아이가 귀부인이 될지도 모르죠. 귀부인의 손을 가졌네요.\" 그녀가 말했다. 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 이 말이 나를 무척이나 흐뭇하게 했다. 그러나 시장 부인은 거기서 그치지 않고, 내게 일감을 돌려주면서 주머니에 손을 넣어 1실링을 주고는, 일에 마음을 쏟아 일을 잘 배우라고, 그러면 누가 알겠느냐, 귀부인이 될지도 모른다고 했다.\n\n그런데 이러는 내내 마음씨 착한 늙은 유모도, 시장 부인도, 그 밖의 사람들도 모두 나를 전혀 이해하지 못했으니, 그들이 귀부인이라는 말로 뜻하는 것과 내가 뜻하는 것이 사뭇 달랐던 까닭이다. 아, 내가 귀부인이 된다는 것으로 이해한 것은 그저 스스로 일해서 밥벌이를 하고, 일자리에 나가는 그 끔찍한 도깨비 없이도 먹고살 만큼 벌 수 있게 되는 것뿐이었던 반면, 그들이 뜻한 것은 거창하고 부유하고 드높게 사는 것이었으니, 나로서는 알 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n자, 시장 부인이 돌아간 뒤, 그 두 딸이 들어와서는 그들 역시 귀부인을 찾았고, 내게 한참 동안 이야기를 건넸으며, 나는 내 천진한 투로 그들에게 대답했다. 그러나 그들이 정말 귀부인이 되기로 마음먹었느냐고 물을 때면, 나는 늘 그렇다고 대답했다. 마침내 그중 하나가 귀부인이 무엇이냐고 내게 물었다. 그 물음에 나는 무척 당황했다. 그러나 어쨌든 나는 부정의 방식으로 나 자신을 설명했으니, 곧 일자리에 나가 집안일을 하지 않는 사람이 귀부인이라고 했다. 그들은 기꺼이 나와 스스럼없이 굴었고, 그들에게 건네는 내 조그만 재잘거림을 좋아했으니, 그것이 그들에게는 제법 흐뭇했던 모양이라, 그들 역시 내게 돈을 주었다.\n\n그 돈으로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 모조리 내가 부르던 대로 유모 선생님께 드리면서, 지금처럼 내가 귀부인이 되면 내가 버는 것을 모두 드리겠다고 말했다. 이 말과 그 밖의 내 이야기 몇 마디로, 내 늙은 스승은 내가 귀부인이 된다는 것으로 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그리고 내가 그것을 그저 제 손으로 일해서 밥벌이를 할 수 있게 되는 것 이상으로는 여기지 않는다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했고, 마침내 그녀는 정말 그런 것이 아니냐고 내게 물었다.\n\n나는 그렇다고, 그렇게 하는 것이 귀부인이 되는 것이라고 우겨 가며 말했다. \"보세요,\" 내가 말했다. \"이런 사람이 있잖아요.\" 그러면서 레이스를 손질하고 귀부인들의 레이스 머릿수건을 빨아 주는 어떤 여자의 이름을 댔다. \"그 사람이,\" 내가 말했다. \"귀부인이고, 사람들이 그 여자를 마님이라고 불러요.\"\n\n\"가엾은 것아,\" 마음씨 착한 늙은 유모가 말했다. \"넌 머지않아 그런 귀부인은 될 수 있겠구나. 그 여자는 평판이 나쁜 사람이라, 사생아를 두셋이나 낳았단다.\"\n\n나는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 하나도 이해하지 못했으나, 이렇게 대답했다. \"어쨌든 사람들이 그 여자를 마님이라고 부르고, 그 여자는 일자리에 나가지도 않고 집안일도 안 하잖아요.\" 그러고는 그 여자가 귀부인이라고, 나도 그런 귀부인이 되겠다고 우겼다.\n\n이 모든 이야기가 또 그 부인들에게 분명 다시 전해졌고, 그들은 그것으로 흥겨워했으며, 이따금 그 젊은 아가씨들, 곧 시장의 딸들이 나를 보러 와서는 그 조그만 귀부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻곤 했으니, 그것이 나를 적잖이 우쭐하게 만들었다.\n\n이 일은 한참 동안 이어졌고, 나는 이 젊은 아가씨들의 잦은 방문을 받았으며, 더러는 다른 이들을 데려오기도 했으니, 그리하여 나는 그 일로 거의 온 마을에 알려지게 되었다.\n\n이제 나는 열 살쯤 되었고, 제법 여자티가 나기 시작했다. 무척 의젓하고 겸손했으며 매우 예의 발랐고, 또 부인들이 내가 예쁘다고, 아주 잘생긴 여자가 될 거라고 말하는 것을 자주 들었던 터라, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그들이 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고 적잖이 우쭐해졌다. 그러나 그 우쭐함이 아직은 내게 나쁜 영향을 미치지는 않았다. 다만, 그들이 내게 돈을 자주 주었고 나는 그것을 내 늙은 유모에게 주었는데, 그 정직한 여인은 내게 어찌나 의로웠던지, 그것을 죄다 다시 나를 위해 써서, 머릿수건이며 아마포며 장갑이며 리본을 사 주었으니, 나는 아주 말끔하게 다니고 늘 깨끗했다. 그것만은 내가 꼭 했던 일이라, 누더기를 걸치고 있더라도 늘 깨끗이 하든지, 아니면 내 손으로 그것을 물에 담가 빨든지 했기 때문이다. 어쨌든 내 말은, 나의 착한 유모가 내게 돈이 생기면 아주 정직하게 그것을 나를 위해 써 주었고, 늘 부인들에게 이것이며 저것이 그들의 돈으로 산 것이라고 말해 주었다는 것이다. 그래서 그들은 종종 더 많은 돈을 주었으니, 마침내 내가 이해한 바로는 치안판사들이 정말로 나더러 일자리에 나가라고 부르기에 이르렀다. 그러나 그때쯤 나는 스스로 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이 되어 있었고, 부인들도 내게 어찌나 다정했던지, 내가 능히 제 앞가림을 할 수 있다는 것이—다시 말해, 유모가 그것으로 나를 건사할 수 있을 만큼 내가 유모를 위해 벌 수 있다는 것이—분명했다. 그래서 그녀는 그들에게, 허락만 해 준다면 자기가 부르는 대로 그 귀부인을 데리고 있으면서 자기 조수로 삼아 아이들을 가르치게 하겠다고 말했으니, 그것은 내가 능히 할 수 있는 일이었다. 아직 무척 어렸지만, 나는 일하는 손이 야무지고 바느질 솜씨가 좋았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 마을 부인들의 친절은 여기서 끝나지 않았으니, 내가 전처럼 공적인 부조로 부양되지 않는다는 것을 알게 되자, 그들은 전보다 더 자주 내게 돈을 주었다. 그리고 내가 자라자 그들은 내게 해 줄 일감을—이를테면 지을 아마포며, 손질할 레이스며, 매만질 머릿수건 따위를—가져다주었고, 그 일을 해 준 대가를 치를 뿐 아니라 어떻게 하는지를 가르쳐 주기까지 했으니, 그리하여 이제 나는 내가 그 말을 이해하는 대로 정말로 귀부인이 되어, 내 옷가지를 스스로 마련하고 유모에게 내 양육비를 치를 뿐 아니라, 미리 주머니에 돈까지 모아 두게 되었다.\n\n부인들은 또한 자기들 옷이나 자식들 옷을 자주 내게 주었으니, 양말이며, 속치마며, 가운이며, 이것저것 갖가지였다. 그리고 이런 것들을 내 늙은 여인이 마치 친어머니라도 되는 양 나를 위해 챙겨 주어, 나를 위해 간수해 두고, 내게 그것들을 깁고 뒤집고 매만져서 가장 알뜰하게 쓰도록 시켰으니, 그녀가 보기 드물게 살림 솜씨 좋은 사람이었기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 부인들 가운데 하나가 나를 어찌나 마음에 들어 했던지, 한 달 동안 자기 딸들 틈에 두겠다며 나를 자기 집으로 데려가려 했다. 그런데 이것은 그녀로서는 더없이 친절한 일이었지만, 내 늙은 착한 여인이 그녀에게 말했듯이, 그녀가 나를 아주 영영 데리고 있기로 마음먹은 것이 아니라면, 그 조그만 귀부인에게 득보다 해를 더 끼치게 될 터였다. \"그렇군요,\" 부인이 말했다. \"그 말이 맞아요. 그러니 그저 한 주만 집으로 데려가서, 내 딸들과 이 아이가 어떻게 지내는지, 또 내가 이 아이 성품을 어떻게 보는지 살펴보고서, 그때 더 말씀드리지요. 그리고 그동안 누가 늘 하던 대로 이 아이를 보러 오거든, 그저 우리 집으로 보냈다고만 말씀하시면 돼요.\"\n\n이는 제법 신중하게 처리된 일이었고, 나는 그 부인의 집으로 갔다. 그런데 거기서 나는 젊은 아가씨들이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 또 그들도 내가 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 떠나오기가 여간 힘들지 않았고, 그들도 나와 헤어지기를 그토록 아쉬워했다.\n\n그러나 결국 나는 떠나왔고, 내 정직한 늙은 여인과 거의 한 해를 더 살았으며, 이제는 그녀에게 큰 도움이 되기 시작했다. 나는 거의 열네 살이 되어, 나이에 비해 키가 컸고 제법 여자티가 났기 때문이다. 그러나 그 부인의 집에서 품위 있는 생활을 맛본 터라, 나는 예전처럼 내 옛 거처가 그리 편치만은 않았고, 정말로 귀부인이 되는 것이 멋진 일이라고 생각했으니, 이제는 귀부인에 대해 전과는 사뭇 다른 관념을 갖게 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 내 말마따나 귀부인이 되는 것이 멋진 일이라고 생각했으므로, 나는 귀부인들 틈에 있기를 좋아했고, 그래서 다시 그곳에 가 있기를 간절히 바랐다.\n\n내가 열네 살하고도 석 달쯤 되었을 무렵, 나의 착한 유모를—오히려 어머니라 불러야 마땅하겠지만—병이 들어 세상을 떠났다. 그때 나는 정말이지 처량한 처지에 놓였으니, 가엾은 사람의 식솔이란 일단 무덤으로 옮겨지고 나면 끝맺는 데 별 부산을 떨 것도 없는 까닭에, 그 가엾고 착한 여인이 묻히자마자, 그녀가 거두던 교구 아이들은 곧바로 교구 위원들 손에 다른 데로 옮겨졌고, 학교는 문을 닫았으며, 그곳 아이들은 다른 데로 보내질 때까지 그저 집에 머무는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되었다. 그리고 그녀가 남긴 것으로 말하자면, 그녀의 딸—여섯이나 일곱 아이를 둔 기혼녀였다—이 와서 그것을 단번에 모조리 쓸어 갔고, 세간을 옮기면서 내게는 그저 농지거리나 하면서, 그 조그만 귀부인은 좋을 대로 제 살림이나 차리면 되겠다고 말하는 것 말고는 아무 할 말이 없었다.\n\n나는 거의 넋이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질렸고, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 말하자면 문밖 넓은 세상으로 쫓겨난 신세였다. 게다가 더더욱 고약한 것은, 그 늙고 정직한 여인이 내 돈 22실링을 손에 쥐고 있었다는 점인데, 그것은 그 조그만 귀부인이 이 세상에 가진 전 재산이었다. 그래서 내가 딸에게 그것을 달라고 했더니, 그녀는 나를 윽박지르고 비웃으면서, 그 일은 자기와 상관없다고 했다.\n\n그 착하고 가엾은 여인이 자기 딸에게 그 일을 말해 두었던 것은 사실이었으니, 그 돈이 어디어디 있고, 그것이 그 아이의 돈이라고 했으며, 한두 차례 나를 불러 그것을 주려고도 했다. 그러나 나는 불행히도 어디 다른 데에 나가 있었고, 내가 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 그 일을 말할 수 있는 처지가 아니었다. 그래도 딸은 나중에 정직하게도 그 돈을 내게 주었으니, 처음에는 그 일로 나를 모질게 대했지만 말이다.\n\n이제 나는 정말이지 가엾은 귀부인이 되었고, 바로 그날 밤 넓은 세상으로 내쫓길 판이었다. 딸이 세간을 죄다 옮겨 가 버려서, 나는 가서 묵을 거처는커녕 먹을 빵 한 조각도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 내 처지를 알고 있던 이웃 사람들 가운데 더러가 나를 몹시 가엾게 여겨, 앞서 말했듯이 내가 한 주 동안 머물렀던 집의 부인에게 이 사정을 알린 모양이었다. 그러자 그녀는 즉시 하녀를 보내 나를 데려오게 했고, 보내지 않았는데도 두 딸이 하녀와 함께 왔다. 그래서 나는 짐 보따리를 다 챙겨, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 기쁜 마음으로 그들을 따라갔다. 내 처지의 두려움이 내게 어찌나 깊은 자국을 남겼던지, 이제 나는 귀부인이 되고 싶지도 않았고, 기꺼이 하인이 되려 했으며, 그들이 적당하다고 여기는 어떤 종류의 하인이라도 되려 했다.\n\n그러나 나의 새롭고 너그러운 여주인은—그녀는 재산 면에서도, 또 다른 모든 면에서도, 정직함 한 가지만 빼고는, 내가 전에 함께 지냈던 착한 여인을 능가했다. 정직함으로 말하자면, 비록 이 부인이 더없이 올바른 분이긴 했어도, 나는 어떤 경우든 첫 번째 분이 비록 가난했을망정 누구라도 그럴 수 있는 만큼이나 한 점 흐트러짐 없이 정직했다는 것을 잊지 말고 말해 두어야겠다.\n\n앞서 말했듯이, 이 착한 부인이 나를 데려가자마자, 첫 번째 부인, 곧 전임 시장 부인이 나를 보살피라고 두 딸을 보냈다. 그리고 내가 그 조그만 귀부인이던 시절에 나를 눈여겨보고 일감을 주었던 또 다른 집안에서도 그녀를 뒤따라 나를 부르러 보냈으니, 그리하여 우리네 말마따나 나는 무척이나 귀염을 받았다. 아니, 그들은 적잖이 화까지 냈으니, 특히 시장 부인 마님은 자기 친구가 자기에게서—그녀가 부르는 대로 하자면—나를 데려갔다고 했다. 그녀의 말로는, 내가 마땅히 자기 사람이라는 것이었으니, 나를 맨 처음 눈여겨본 사람이 자기였기 때문이라고 했다. 그러나 나를 데리고 있던 이들은 나를 내놓으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 다른 누구의 집에서 더없이 좋은 대접을 받았다 한들, 지금 있는 곳보다 더 나을 수는 없었을 것이다.\n\n나는 여기서 열일고여덟 살이 될 때까지 지냈고, 여기서 상상할 수 있는 온갖 교육의 혜택을 다 누렸다. 부인은 자기 딸들에게 춤과 프랑스어와 글쓰기를 가르칠 선생들과, 그 밖에 음악을 가르칠 선생들을 집으로 불러들였는데, 나는 늘 그들과 함께였으므로 그들만큼이나 빨리 배웠다. 비록 선생들이 나를 가르치도록 정해진 것은 아니었으나, 나는 그들이 지도와 가르침으로 배우는 모든 것을 흉내와 물음으로 배웠으니, 그리하여 한마디로 나는 그들 누구 못지않게 춤을 추고 프랑스어를 하게 되었고, 노래는 훨씬 더 잘하게 되었으니, 그들 누구보다 내 목소리가 더 나았기 때문이다. 하프시코드나 스피넷 연주만큼은 그리 쉽게 익히지 못했으니, 내게는 연습할 악기가 없어서, 그들이 손을 놓는 짬에만 그들의 악기를 만질 수 있었는데 그마저도 일정치 않았기 때문이다. 그래도 나는 그것 또한 제법 잘 익혔고, 마침내 젊은 아가씨들이 악기를 둘, 곧 하프시코드와 스피넷을 마련하자, 그들이 직접 나를 가르쳤다. 그러나 춤으로 말하자면, 그들은 내가 컨트리 댄스를 배우는 것을 좀처럼 막을 수 없었으니, 짝수를 맞추느라 늘 내가 필요했기 때문이다. 또 다른 한편으로, 그들은 내가 배우기를 바라는 만큼이나 자기들이 배운 모든 것을 진심으로 기꺼이 내게 가르쳐 주려 했다.\n\n이런 식으로 나는, 앞서 말했듯이, 내가 함께 살던 이들만큼이나 어엿한 귀부인이었더라도 누릴 수 있었을 온갖 교육의 혜택을 다 누렸다. 그리고 어떤 것에서는, 비록 그들이 내 윗사람이긴 했어도, 내가 내 아가씨들보다 나았다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 타고난 천부의 선물이었으니, 그들의 재산을 다 들여도 갖출 수 없는 것이었다. 첫째로, 나는 보아하니 그들 누구보다 잘생겼고, 둘째로, 몸매가 더 나았으며, 셋째로, 노래를 더 잘했으니, 이 말은 내 목소리가 더 좋았다는 뜻이다. 이 모든 것에 대해, 여러분도 부디 내가 나 자신에 대한 자만을 말하는 것이 아니라, 그 집안을 아는 모든 이의 견해를 말하는 것이라고 인정해 주기를 바란다.\n\n이 모든 것에 더해 나는 우리 여자들이 흔히 지니는 허영을 지니고 있었으니, 곧 실제로 매우 잘생겼다고, 혹은 여러분 좋을 대로 말하자면 굉장한 미인으로 여겨지자, 나는 그것을 잘 알았고, 다른 누가 나를 보는 만큼이나 나 자신을 좋게 여겼으며, 특히 누가 그 이야기를 하는 것을 듣기 좋아했는데, 그런 일이 때때로 내게 일어나지 않을 수 없었고, 그것이 내게는 큰 만족이었다.\n\n여기까지 나는 나 자신에 대해 순탄한 이야기를 들려주었고, 내 삶의 이 모든 부분에서 나는 매우 훌륭한 집안에서—덕과 진중함으로, 또 온갖 값진 것으로 어디서나 이름나고 존경받는 집안에서—산다는 평판을 누렸을 뿐 아니라, 매우 진중하고 정숙하며 덕스러운 처녀라는 평판도 누렸으니, 나는 늘 그러한 사람이었다. 또한 나는 아직 다른 무엇을 생각할 계기도 없었고, 사악함에 대한 유혹이 무엇인지 알 일도 없었다.\n\n그러나 내가 너무 우쭐해했던 그것이 곧 내 파멸이었으니, 아니 차라리 내 허영이 그 원인이었다. 내가 머물던 집의 부인에게는 아들이 둘 있었는데, 매우 장래가 촉망되는 자질과 더없이 훌륭한 행실을 지닌 젊은 신사들이었으나, 그 둘 다와 매우 친하게 지내게 된 것이 내 불운이었다. 그런데 그들은 나를 대하는 처신이 사뭇 달랐다.\n\n큰아들은 도시 물정도 시골 물정도 잘 아는 쾌활한 신사였으니, 비록 짓궂은 짓을 저지를 만큼 경박하긴 했어도, 자기 쾌락의 대가를 너무 비싸게 치르지 않을 만큼은 사리 분별이 있었다. 그는 모든 여자에게 통하는 그 불행한 덫으로 시작했으니, 곧 기회 있을 때마다 내가—그의 표현대로 하자면—얼마나 예쁜지, 얼마나 상냥한지, 얼마나 몸가짐이 좋은지 따위를 짚어 주는 것이었다. 그리고 이것을 어찌나 교묘하게 꾸몄던지, 마치 자고새를 그물로 잡으러 나갈 때처럼 여자를 자기 그물에 옭아넣는 법을 잘 아는 사람 같았다. 그는 내가 곁에 없을 때, 그러나 멀리 있지 않아 분명히 듣게 될 줄 알 때를 골라, 그의 누이들에게 이런 말을 하곤 했기 때문이다. 그의 누이들은 그에게 나직이 되받아 말하곤 했다. \"쉿, 오라버니, 저 애가 듣겠어요. 바로 옆방에 있단 말이에요.\" 그러면 그는 그 말을 슬쩍 흘려넘기고 더 나직이 이야기하면서, 마치 몰랐다는 듯이 자기가 잘못했다고 인정하기 시작했다. 그러다가는 또 자기를 잊은 척 다시 큰 소리로 말했으니, 그것을 듣는 것이 그토록 흐뭇했던 나는, 기회 있을 때마다 영락없이 그 말에 귀를 기울이곤 했다.\n\n이렇게 미끼를 단단히 끼우고, 그것을 내 앞에 드리울 방법을 아주 손쉽게 찾아낸 다음, 그는 더 노골적인 수를 두었다. 어느 날, 내가 그의 누이 방에서 그녀의 몸단장을 거들고 있을 때, 그가 그 방 앞을 지나다가 쾌활한 기색으로 들어왔다. \"오, 베티 양.\" 그가 내게 말했다. \"잘 지내요, 베티 양? 두 볼이 화끈거리지 않아요, 베티 양?\" 나는 인사를 올리며 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다. \"왜 그런 소릴 하세요, 오라버니?\" 아가씨가 말했다. \"있잖아,\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 아래층에서 반 시간이나 저 애 얘기를 했거든.\" \"글쎄요,\" 누이가 말했다. \"오라버니가 저 애 험담을 할 리는 없으니, 무슨 얘길 했든 상관없겠죠.\" \"아니,\" 그가 말했다. \"험담을 하기는커녕, 우린 좋은 얘길 잔뜩 했고, 베티 양에 대해 멋진 얘기가 많이 나왔다고, 정말이라니까. 특히, 저 애가 콜체스터에서 제일 예쁜 처녀라고 말이야. 한마디로, 마을 사람들이 저 애의 건강을 위해 축배를 들기 시작했다고.\"\n\n\"오라버니도 참 별나시네요.\" 누이가 말했다. \"베티는 딱 한 가지가 모자랄 뿐이지만, 그게 모자라느니 차라리 전부 다 모자라는 게 나아요. 요즘 시장이 우리 여자들에게 불리하니까요. 젊은 여자가 미모며, 가문이며, 교양이며, 재치며, 분별이며, 몸가짐이며, 정숙함이며, 이런 걸 죄다 극진히 갖추고 있다 해도, 돈이 없으면 아무것도 아니에요. 그 모든 게 다 있어도 헛일이라고요. 요즘은 돈만이 여자를 내세워 주거든요. 남자들이 패를 죄다 제 손아귀에 쥐고 노름을 하니까요.\"\n\n곁에 있던 그녀의 동생이 외쳤다. \"그만, 누님, 너무 앞서가시네요. 난 누님 법칙의 예외라고요. 정말이지, 누님이 말씀하시는 것처럼 그렇게 다 갖춘 여자를 내가 만난다면, 정말이지, 돈 따위로는 골머리를 앓지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n\"오,\" 누이가 말했다. \"그럼 넌 돈 없는 여자한텐 마음을 두지 않도록 조심하겠구나.\"\n\n\"그것도 모르는 일이죠.\" 동생이 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 누님,\" 큰아들이 말했다. \"왜 그렇게 남자들이 재산만 노린다고 외쳐 대는 거예요? 누님은 다른 건 몰라도 재산이 모자란 사람은 아니잖아요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 오라버니.\" 아가씨가 아주 똑 부러지게 받아쳤다. \"내가 돈은 있고 미모가 모자란다는 거잖아요. 하지만 요즘 세상 돌아가는 걸 보면, 미모 없이도 돈만 있으면 되니까, 난 이웃들보다 나은 셈이죠.\"\n\n\"글쎄요,\" 동생이 말했다. \"하지만 누님이 말하는 그 이웃들도 누님과 어깨를 나란히 할 수 있어요. 미모는 때때로 돈을 제치고 남편을 가로채니까요. 그리고 하녀가 어쩌다 여주인보다 더 잘생긴 경우엔, 그 하녀가 종종 그만큼 좋은 혼처를 잡아서, 여주인보다 앞서 마차를 타고 다니기도 한단 말이에요.\"\n\n이제는 내가 물러나 그들을 떠날 때라고 생각해서 그렇게 했으나, 그들의 이야기를 죄다 들을 만큼은 멀리 가지 않았으니, 거기서 나는 나 자신에 대한 멋진 말을 잔뜩 들었고, 그것이 내 허영을 부추기는 데에는 도움이 되었으나, 내가 곧 알게 되었듯이 이 집안에서 내 입지를 키우는 길은 아니었다. 누이와 동생이 그 일로 심하게 다투었기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 나 때문에 누이에게 매우 불쾌한 말을 몇 마디 했던 터라, 나는 그녀가 그 후 나를 대하는 태도로 보아 그 일을 마음에 두고 있음을 쉬이 알 수 있었으니, 그것은 사실 내게 매우 부당한 일이었다. 나는 그녀가 자기 동생에 대해 의심하는 그런 일을 추호도 생각해 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 사실은, 큰아들이 그 멀찍하고 에두르는 방식으로 농담인 양 많은 말을 했던 것을, 내가 어리석게도 진심으로 믿었거나, 그가 결코 의도하지 않았을, 어쩌면 생각조차 한 적 없었을 것에 대한 희망으로 나 자신을 우쭐하게 했던 것이다.\n\n어느 날 그가 누이들이 으레 앉아서 일하던 방을 향해 계단을 뛰어 올라왔는데, 그가 자주 그러던 일이었다. 그리고 들어오기 전에 그들을 부르는 것 또한 그의 버릇이었던 터라, 마침 거기 혼자 있던 나는 문가로 다가가서 말했다. \"도련님, 아가씨들은 여기 안 계세요. 정원으로 산책 나가셨어요.\" 이 말을 하려고 문 쪽으로 한 걸음 나아가는데, 그가 막 문에 다다라서는, 마치 우연인 양 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았다. \"오, 베티 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 있었군요? 그게 더 잘됐네요. 난 누이들보다 당신하고 얘기하고 싶었거든요.\" 그러고는 나를 품에 안은 채 서너 번 입을 맞췄다.\n\n나는 빠져나오려고 몸부림쳤으나, 그 몸부림조차 시늉뿐이었고, 그는 나를 단단히 붙들고 숨이 거의 차오를 때까지 계속 입을 맞추다가, 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 베티, 난 당신을 사랑하고 있어요.\"\n\n그의 말은, 고백하건대, 내 피를 달아오르게 했다. 내 정신이 죄다 가슴께로 몰려들어 나를 적잖이 어지럽게 했으니, 그는 그것을 내 얼굴에서 쉬이 알아보았을 것이다. 그는 그 후에도 자기가 나를 사랑한다고 여러 차례 되풀이했고, 내 마음은 내가 그것을 좋아한다고 목소리처럼 또렷이 말하고 있었다. 아니, 그가 \"난 당신을 사랑하고 있어요\" 하고 말할 때마다, 내 붉어진 두 볼이 또렷이 대답했다. \"정말 그러시다면 좋겠어요, 도련님.\"\n\n그러나 그때는 그 밖에 다른 일은 없었다. 그저 불시의 일이었고, 그가 가고 나자 나는 곧 마음을 추슬렀다. 그는 나와 더 오래 머물렀을 테지만, 마침 창밖을 내다보다가 누이들이 정원을 올라오는 것을 보고는 작별을 고했고, 다시 내게 입을 맞추고는, 자기는 아주 진지하며 머지않아 곧 자기 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 말하고 떠나갔으니, 비록 얼떨떨하긴 했어도 나를 한없이 기쁘게 한 채였다. 그리고 여기에 한 가지 불행만 끼어 있지 않았다면 나는 옳게 본 것이었으련만, 그 착오는 바로 여기에 있었으니, 베티 양은 진심이었고 그 신사는 진심이 아니었다는 것이다.\n\n이때부터 내 머릿속은 야릇한 것들로 가득 찼고, 정말이지 나는 내가 아니었다고 말할 수 있다. 그런 신사가 내게 나를 사랑하고 있다는 둥, 그가 말하는 대로 내가 그토록 매혹적인 여인이라는 둥 이야기하는 것—이것은 내가 도무지 어찌 감당해야 할지 모를 일이었고, 내 허영은 더할 나위 없이 드높아졌다. 사실 내 머릿속은 자만으로 가득 차 있었으나, 시류의 사악함을 전혀 몰랐던 터라, 내 안전이나 내 정조에 대해서는 한 가닥 생각도 없었다. 그래서 만약 그 젊은 주인이 처음 마주쳤을 때 그것을 시도했더라면, 그는 나를 두고 마음 내키는 대로 어떤 자유든 누릴 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자기에게 유리한 것을 알아보지 못했으니, 그것이 그때만큼은 내 다행이었다.\n\n이 공세가 있은 뒤 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 나를 다시 붙잡을 기회를, 그것도 거의 같은 자세로 붙잡을 기회를 찾아냈다. 사실 이번에는 그의 쪽에 더 많은 꿍꿍이가 있었으나, 내 쪽은 그렇지 않았다. 일은 이러했다. 젊은 아가씨들은 모두 어머니와 함께 방문을 나갔고, 그의 형제는 마을 밖에 나가 있었으며, 그의 아버지로 말하자면 한 주 전부터 런던에 가 있었다. 그는 나를 어찌나 잘 지켜보았던지, 내가 어디 있는지 알고 있었으나, 나는 그가 집에 있는 줄조차 모르고 있었다. 그가 잰걸음으로 계단을 올라와, 내가 일하고 있는 것을 보고는 곧장 내게로 방에 들어와, 전에 했던 그대로 나를 품에 안고 거의 십오 분 동안 줄곧 입을 맞추기 시작했다.\n\n내가 있던 방은 그의 손아래 누이의 방이었는데, 집 안에는 아래층의 하녀들 말고는 아무도 없었던 터라, 그는 어쩌면 더 거리낌이 없었을지 모른다. 한마디로, 그는 정말로 내게 진심으로 굴기 시작했다. 어쩌면 그는 내가 좀 지나치게 호락호락하다고 여겼을지 모르니, 하느님은 아시거니와, 그가 그저 나를 품에 안고 입을 맞추는 동안 나는 그에게 아무런 저항도 하지 않았기 때문이다. 사실 나는 그것이 너무도 흐뭇해서 그에게 그리 저항할 수가 없었다.\n\n그러나 어쨌든 그런 일에 지쳤다는 듯이, 우리는 자리에 앉았고, 거기서 그는 나와 한참 동안 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 내게 흠뻑 반했다고, 또 자기가 얼마나 나를 사랑하는지 내게 말하기 전까지는 밤이고 낮이고 도무지 쉴 수가 없다고, 그리고 만약 내가 그를 마주 사랑할 수 있어서 그를 행복하게 해 준다면, 내가 그의 목숨을 구하는 셈이 될 거라고, 그 밖에도 그런 멋진 말을 많이 했다. 나는 그에게 다시 별말을 하지 않았으나, 내가 바보라는 것을, 그가 무엇을 뜻하는지 조금도 알아차리지 못하고 있다는 것을 쉬이 드러내고 말았다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 방 안을 거닐다가 내 손을 잡았고, 나는 그와 함께 거닐었다. 그러다가 이내 기회를 틈타, 그는 나를 침대 위로 밀어 넘어뜨리고는 거기서 더없이 격렬하게 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 그의 공을 정당히 인정해 주자면, 그는 내게 어떤 무례한 짓도 시도하지 않았고, 그저 오래도록 입을 맞추기만 했다. 이러고 나서 그는 누가 계단을 올라오는 소리를 들은 듯하여 침대에서 일어나 나를 일으켜 세우고는, 나에 대한 사랑을 한껏 늘어놓으면서도, 그것이 모두 정직한 애정일 뿐이고 내게 나쁜 뜻은 없다고 말했다. 그러고는 그 말과 함께 내 손에 5기니를 쥐여 주고 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n나는 그 돈에, 앞서 그 사랑에 그랬던 것보다 더 얼떨떨해졌고, 어찌나 우쭐해졌던지 내가 딛고 선 땅이 어딘지도 거의 알 수 없을 지경이었다. 내가 이 대목을 더 소상히 적는 것은, 만약 내 이야기가 어떤 순진한 젊은이의 손에 읽히게 된다면, 그가 이것을 보고 자기 자신의 미모를 일찍 깨닫는 데 따르는 화를 경계하는 법을 배우기를 바라기 때문이다. 젊은 여자가 일단 제가 잘생겼다고 여기게 되면, 자기를 사랑한다고 말하는 어떤 남자의 진심도 결코 의심하지 않는 법이다. 자기가 그를 사로잡을 만큼 매혹적이라고 믿으면, 그 효과를 기대하는 것이 자연스러운 노릇이기 때문이다.\n\n이 젊은 신사는 내 허영을 부추긴 만큼이나 자기 욕정도 달구어 놓았으니, 마치 기회가 있었는데도 그것을 붙잡지 못한 것을 뉘우치기라도 한 듯, 반 시간쯤 뒤에 다시 올라와서는, 다만 운만 조금 덜 떼고서, 전처럼 나를 상대로 일을 벌이기 시작했다.\n\n그리고 우선, 방에 들어오자 그는 몸을 돌려 문을 닫았다. \"베티 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"아까는 누가 계단을 올라오나 했는데, 그게 아니었어요. 하지만,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"그들이 나를 당신과 한 방에서 보더라도, 당신한테 입 맞추는 걸 들키진 않을 테니까.\" 나는 그에게 누가 계단을 올라올 리는 없다고 말했으니, 집 안에는 요리사와 다른 하녀 말고는 아무도 없을 텐데, 그들은 그 계단으로는 결코 올라오지 않기 때문이라고 했다. \"그래, 내 사랑.\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도 확실히 해 두는 게 좋지.\" 그러고는 자리에 앉았고, 우리는 이야기를 나누기 시작했다. 그리고 이제, 비록 나는 그의 첫 번째 방문 때부터 여전히 온통 달아올라 있어서 말을 거의 하지 않았지만, 그는 말하자면 내 입에 말을 넣어 주듯이, 자기가 얼마나 열렬히 나를 사랑하는지, 또 이 재산을 물려받기 전까지는 그런 일을 입에 올릴 수 없으나, 그때는 기필코 나를 행복하게 해 주고 자기 자신도 행복해지기로 마음먹었다고—다시 말해, 나와 결혼하겠다고—그 밖에도 그런 멋진 말을 잔뜩 늘어놓았다. 가엾은 바보였던 나는 그 속내를 알아차리지 못했으나, 마치 혼인으로 이어지는 사랑 말고는 그 어떤 종류의 사랑도 없다는 듯이 굴었다. 그래서 만약 그가 그것을 입에 올렸더라면, 나는 싫다고 말할 여지도, 또 그럴 힘도 없었을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 아직 거기까지는 이르지 않았다.\n\n우리가 앉은 지 오래지 않아, 그는 일어나서 입맞춤으로 내 숨통을 아예 막으며 나를 다시 침대 위로 밀어 넘어뜨렸다. 그러나 그때는 둘 다 한껏 달아올라 있었던 터라, 그는 점잖게 입에 담을 수 있는 선을 넘어 나에게 더 나아갔으니, 그 순간 그가 실제로 한 것보다 훨씬 더한 것을 시도했다 한들 나로서는 그를 거절할 힘도 없었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 비록 그가 나를 상대로 이런 자유를 누리긴 했어도, 사람들이 마지막 호의라 부르는 그것에까지 이르지는 않았으니, 그의 공을 정당히 인정해 주자면, 그는 그것을 시도하지 않았다. 그리고 그는 이 자제를, 그 후 다른 경우에 나를 상대로 누린 자기의 온갖 자유를 위한 구실로 삼았다. 이 일이 끝나자 그는 잠깐만 더 머물렀으나, 내 손에 거의 한 줌이나 되는 금화를 쥐여 주고는, 나를 향한 자기의 정열을, 또 세상 어떤 여자보다 나를 사랑한다는 것을 수천 번이나 다짐하면서 나를 떠났다.\n\n내가 이제 생각이라는 것을 하기 시작했다 해도 이상할 것은 없을 터이나, 아아! 그것은 견실한 성찰이라고는 거의 없는 생각이었다. 나는 한없이 풍성한 허영과 자만은 지녔으나, 덕이라고는 아주 조금밖에 지니지 못했다. 사실 나는 이따금 젊은 주인이 무엇을 노리는지 혼자 따져 보기는 했으나, 그 멋진 말과 금화 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 그가 나와 결혼할 작정인지, 결혼하지 않을 작정인지는 내게 별로 중대한 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 또한 그가 머지않아 듣게 될 것처럼 내게 일종의 격식 차린 제안을 하기에 이르기 전까지는, 내 생각이 나 자신을 위해 어떤 조건을 따져 두어야 할 필요성을 내게 일러 주지조차 않았다.\n\n그리하여 나는 추호의 거리낌도 없이 스스로를 파멸당할 채비에 내맡겼으니, 나는 덕보다 허영이 앞서는 모든 젊은 여자에게 좋은 경계가 되는 본보기다. 양쪽 다 그토록 어리석은 적은 일찍이 없었다. 만약 내가 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 처신하고, 덕과 명예가 요구하는 대로 저항했더라면, 이 신사는 자기 속셈을 이룰 가망이 없음을 알고 공세를 그만두었거나, 아니면 떳떳하고 명예로운 혼인의 제안을 했을 것이다. 그랬더라면 누가 그를 탓했든, 아무도 나를 탓할 수는 없었을 것이다. 한마디로, 만약 그가 나를 알았더라면, 또 그가 노리던 그 하찮은 것이 얼마나 쉽게 손에 넣을 수 있는 것인지 알았더라면, 그는 더는 골머리를 앓지 않고 내게 네댓 기니를 주고는 다음번에 나를 찾아왔을 때 동침했을 것이다. 그리고 만약 내가 그의 속내를, 또 그가 나를 손에 넣기를 얼마나 어렵게 여기는지 알았더라면, 나는 그와 내 나름의 조건을 정할 수 있었을 것이다. 당장의 혼인을 조건으로 못 박지는 못했더라도, 혼인할 때까지의 생계비를 조건으로 삼을 수는 있었을 것이고, 원하는 것을 얻었을 것이다. 그는 앞으로 물려받을 것은 차치하고도 이미 차고 넘치게 부유했기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 이런 생각 따위는 죄다 내팽개친 듯했고, 오직 내 미모에 대한, 또 그런 신사에게 사랑받는다는 자만에 사로잡혀 있을 뿐이었다. 그 금화로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 들여다보며 몇 시간이고 보냈고, 하루에도 천 번씩 그 기니를 세고 또 셌다. 일찍이 어떤 가엾고 헛된 것도 나만큼 그 이야기의 구석구석에 푹 빠져들지는 못했을 터이니, 내 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지, 또 내 파멸이 문 앞에 얼마나 가까이 와 있는지는 헤아리지 못했다. 사실, 나는 그 파멸을 피하려 애쓰기는커녕 오히려 그것을 바랐던 것 같다.\n\n그러나 그러는 동안에도, 나는 집안의 누구에게도 나를 의심할 빌미를, 혹은 내가 이 젊은 신사와 조금이라도 내통하고 있다고 상상할 빌미를 조금도 주지 않을 만큼은 영리했다. 사람들 앞에서 나는 그를 거의 쳐다보지도 않았고, 누가 우리 가까이 있을 때 그가 내게 말을 걸어도 대꾸하지 않았다. 그래도 우리는 이따금 작은 만남을 가져, 한두 마디 나눌 짬과, 때로는 입맞춤을 나눌 짬을 얻긴 했으나, 의도된 그 못된 짓을 벌일 마땅한 기회는 없었다. 게다가 그는, 만약 내 속내를 알았더라면 필요하지도 않았을 만큼 빙빙 에둘렀는데, 그래서 그 일이 그에게 어려워 보였고, 그가 정말로 그것을 어렵게 만든 셈이었다.\n\n그러나 악마는 지칠 줄 모르는 유혹자인지라, 자기가 부추기는 그 사악함을 위한 기회를 결코 찾아내지 못하는 법이 없다. 어느 날 저녁, 내가 그의 두 손아래 누이와 그와 함께 정원에 있으면서, 다들 더없이 천진하게 흥겨워하고 있을 때, 그는 내 손에 쪽지 하나를 슬쩍 건넬 방도를 찾아냈다. 그 쪽지로 그는 내게, 내일 사람들 앞에서 마을로 자기 심부름을 좀 다녀와 달라고 청할 테니, 가는 길 어디쯤에서 자기를 보게 될 거라는 뜻을 알렸다.\n\n이에 따라, 식사 후에 그는 누이들이 모두 곁에 있는데도 내게 아주 점잖게 말했다. \"베티 양, 부탁 하나 해야겠는데.\" \"그게 뭔데요?\" 그의 둘째 누이가 말했다. \"아니, 누이야,\" 그가 아주 점잖게 말했다. \"오늘 베티 양을 내줄 수 없거든, 다른 때라도 괜찮아.\" 그러자 그들은 베티를 충분히 내줄 수 있다고 말했고, 둘째 누이는 물어봐서 미안하다고 했으나, 그것은 그저 으레 하는 말일 뿐 아무 뜻도 없었다. \"그래도, 오라버니,\" 맏누이가 말했다. \"베티 양한테 그게 뭔지 말해 줘야죠. 우리가 들으면 안 되는 무슨 은밀한 일이거든, 저 애를 밖으로 불러내시든지요. 저기 있잖아요.\" \"원, 누이야,\" 그 신사가 아주 점잖게 말했다. \"무슨 소리야? 난 그저 저 애더러 큰길에 좀 가 달라는 거라고\"(그러고는 깃 장식 하나를 꺼냈다), \"어느 가게에 말이야.\" 그러고는 자기가 값을 매겨 둔 고운 목수건 두 장에 대해 한참 이야기를 늘어놓더니, 자기가 보여 준 그 깃 장식에 맞는 목 장식을 사러 가는 척 심부름을 가서, 그들이 그 목수건을 내 돈에 내줄지 알아봐 달라고, 1실링을 더 부르면서 그들과 흥정해 보라고 했다. 그러고는 또 다른 심부름들을 보태고, 그렇게 해서 내가 분명 한참 걸려 있게끔 그런 자질구레한 볼일을 계속 만들어 냈다.\n\n내게 심부름을 다 일러 준 다음, 그는 그들 모두가 아는 어느 집안을 방문하러 갈 참이라며 한참 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 거기에 이러이러한 신사들이 올 것이고, 다들 얼마나 흥겨울지 따위를 말하고는, 아주 격식을 차려 누이들에게 함께 가자고 청했다. 그러나 그들도 그만큼 격식을 차려 거절했으니, 그날 오후에 자기들을 찾아오기로 기별이 와 있는 손님들 때문이라는 것이었다. 그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 그것은 그가 일부러 꾸며 둔 일이었다.\n\n그가 그들에게 말을 마치고 내게 심부름을 다 일러 주기가 무섭게, 그의 하인이 올라와서, W—— H—— 경의 마차가 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다고 알렸다. 그러자 그는 뛰어 내려갔다가 곧장 다시 올라왔다. \"아아!\" 그가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"내 즐거움이 단번에 죄다 망가지는군. W—— 경께서 나를 데려가려고 마차를 보내셨는데, 무슨 긴한 일로 나와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하시네.\" 알고 보니 이 W—— 경은 마을에서 삼 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 신사였는데, 그가 전날 일부러 그에게 말을 넣어, 어떤 특별한 일에 쓰게 자기에게 마차를 빌려 달라고 했고, 세 시쯤에 자기를 데리러 오도록 정해 두었던 것이며, 정말 그대로 마차가 온 것이었다.\n\n그는 곧장 자기의 가장 좋은 가발과 모자와 칼을 가져오라 이르고는, 하인에게 그 다른 곳으로 가서 자기 사정을 전하라고 명했으니—다시 말해, 그는 핑계를 대어 하인을 멀리 보내 버린 것이었다—마차에 오를 채비를 했다. 그는 가려다가 잠시 멈춰 서서 자기 볼일에 대해 내게 무척 진지하게 이야기하더니, 기회를 틈타 아주 나직이 내게 말했다. \"어서 빠져나와요, 내 사랑, 할 수 있는 한 빨리.\" 나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마치 그가 사람들 앞에서 한 말에 대답하기라도 하듯 인사를 올렸다. 십오 분쯤 뒤에 나도 나갔다. 나는 전과 다름없는 차림이었으나, 다만 주머니에 두건과 가면과 부채와 장갑 한 켤레가 들어 있을 뿐이었으니, 그래서 집 안에서는 추호의 의심도 사지 않았다. 그는 내가 반드시 지나가게 될 줄 아는 어느 뒷골목에서 마차에 탄 채 나를 기다리고 있었고, 마부에게 어디로 갈지 일러 두었으니, 곧 마일엔드라는 어떤 곳이었다. 거기에는 그의 심복이 하나 살고 있었는데, 우리는 그 집으로 들어갔고, 거기에는 우리가 마음껏 사악하게 굴 수 있는 온갖 편의가 다 갖추어져 있었다.\n\n우리가 함께 있게 되자, 그는 내게 아주 점잖게 이야기하기 시작했으니, 나를 배신하려고 거기 데려온 것이 아니라고, 나를 향한 자기의 정열이 나를 욕보이는 것을 결코 용납하지 않는다고, 자기는 그 즉시 나와 결혼하기로 마음먹었다고…\n썬 그가 자기 영지를 물려받게 되면, 그동안에 내가 자기 청을 들어주기만 한다면 나를 아주 떳떳하게 거두어 주겠노라고, 자신의 진실함과 나를 향한 애정을 두고 천 번이고 맹세하면서, 결코 나를 버리지 않겠노라고, 말하자면 굳이 늘어놓을 필요도 없는 서두를 천 번이나 더 늘어놓았다.\n\n그러나 그가 어서 말하라고 다그치자, 나는 그토록 거듭된 맹세를 들은 뒤이니 나를 향한 그의 사랑이 진실한가를 의심할 까닭은 없으나, 다만— 하고 거기서 말을 멈추었으니, 나머지는 그가 짐작하도록 내버려 두려는 듯했다. \"다만 무엇이오, 내 사랑?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 짐작이 가오. 혹시 아이라도 들어서면 어쩌나, 그것 아니오? 아니, 그렇다면,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 당신을 보살피고 당신을 돌보아 주겠소, 아이까지도 말이오. 그리고 내가 농담을 하는 게 아니라는 걸 당신이 알 수 있도록,\" 하고 그가 말하더니, \"여기 당신을 위한 계약금이 있소,\" 하면서 백 기니가 든 비단 지갑을 꺼내어 내게 주었다. \"그리고 당신과 혼인할 때까지 해마다 이만한 것을 또 주리다,\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n지갑을 본 데다 그의 제안의 불길까지 겹쳐, 얼굴빛이 붉어졌다 가셨다 하니, 나는 한마디도 할 수가 없었고, 그는 그것을 쉽사리 알아챘다. 그리하여 지갑을 내 품속에 넣어 주자, 나는 더는 그에게 저항하지 않고, 그가 하고 싶은 대로, 또 하고 싶은 만큼 자주 하도록 내버려 두었다. 이렇게 나는 단숨에 나 자신의 파멸을 완성하고 말았으니, 이날부터 정조와 정숙함에 버림받은 나에게는, 하느님의 축복에든 사람의 도움에든 나를 천거할 만한 값진 것이 아무것도 남지 않게 되었다.\n\n그러나 일은 여기서 끝나지 않았다. 나는 마을로 돌아가, 그가 공공연히 시킨 심부름을 처리하고, 아무도 내가 오래 걸렸다고 여기기 전에 집에 돌아왔다. 그분으로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그러겠노라고 내게 말한 대로 밤늦도록 밖에 머물러 있었고, 그의 일로든 내 일로든 집안에는 추호의 의심도 없었다.\n\n이후로 우리는 우리의 죄를 되풀이할 기회를 자주 가졌으니—주로 그의 궁리에 의해서였다—특히 집안에서, 그의 어머니와 아가씨들이 방문을 나갔을 때였는데, 그는 그것을 어찌나 빈틈없이 살피던지 한 번도 놓치는 법이 없었다. 늘 그들이 언제 나가는지를 미리 알고 있다가, 어김없이 나를 아주 안전하게 단둘이 붙들었으니, 그리하여 우리는 반년 가까이 우리의 사악한 쾌락을 마음껏 누렸다. 그러면서도 무엇보다 나를 흡족하게 한 것은, 내가 아이를 배지 않았다는 사실이었다.\n\n그러나 이 반년이 다 가기 전에, 이야기 첫머리에서 얼마간 언급한 바 있는 그의 동생이 내게 수작을 걸기 시작했으니, 어느 날 저녁 정원에서 나를 홀로 발견하고는 같은 종류의 이야기를 꺼내며, 나를 사랑한다는 정직하고 진실한 고백을 늘어놓더니, 한마디로 그 어떤 다른 제안을 내게 하기에 앞서, 떳떳하고 명예롭게 나와 혼인하겠노라고 청해 왔다.\n\n이제 나는 어쩔 줄을 몰랐고, 그와 같은 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적도 없는 궁지로 내몰렸으니, 적어도 나로서는 처음이었다. 나는 완강하게 그 제안에 맞섰고, 이제 논거로 나 자신을 무장하기 시작했다. 나는 그에게 이 혼인의 불균형을, 내가 집안에서 받게 될 대접을, 그토록 너그러운 마음으로 나를 집에 거두어 준, 그것도 내가 그토록 비천한 처지에 있을 때 거두어 준 그의 훌륭한 부모님께 그것이 얼마나 배은망덕한 일이 되겠는지를 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다. 한마디로, 나는 그의 뜻을 단념시키기 위해 상상할 수 있는 온갖 말을 다 했으니, 진실을 말하는 것만은 빼고서였다. 진실을 말했더라면 정말이지 모든 일에 종지부를 찍었겠으나, 그것을 입에 올린다는 것은 감히 생각조차 할 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 여기서 정말이지 내가 예상치 못한 사정이 벌어져 나를 곤경에 빠뜨렸으니, 이 젊은 신사는 솔직하고 정직한 만큼, 나에 대해서도 그처럼 솔직하고 정직한 것 외에는 아무것도 꾸미지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 자신의 결백함을 알고 있던 터라, 베티 아씨에게 호감을 품고 있다는 것을 집안에서 비밀로 하는 데에 자기 형만큼 조심하지 않았다. 그리고 그 일에 관해 나와 이야기를 나누었다는 것까지는 그들에게 알리지 않았으나, 그의 누이들이 그가 나를 사랑한다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 말을 흘렸고, 그의 어머니도 그것을 보았으니, 그들은 그것을 내게 내색하지는 않았어도 그에게는 내색했으며, 그리하여 나는 곧 나를 대하는 그들의 태도가 전보다 더 달라진 것을 알아차렸다.\n\n먹구름은 보았으나 폭풍은 미처 내다보지 못했다. 다시 말하거니와, 나를 대하는 그들의 태도가 달라졌다는 것, 그리고 그것이 날마다 점점 더 나빠진다는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었으니, 마침내 나는 하인들 사이에서 머지않아 내가 떠나 달라는 요구를 받게 되리라는 정보를 얻었다.\n\n나는 그 소식에 놀라지 않았으니, 달리 거두어질 길이 있다는 것에 충분히 만족하고 있었기 때문이며, 더욱이 날마다 아이를 배리라 예상할 만한 이유가 있었고, 그렇게 되면 어떤 핑계도 댈 수 없이 떠날 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 생각하면 더욱 그러했다.\n\n얼마 뒤 작은 신사가 기회를 잡아, 나를 향한 자신의 호감이 집안에 새어 나갔다고 내게 말했다. 그것을 두고 나를 탓하는 것은 아니라고, 그것이 어떻게 새어 나갔는지는 자기도 잘 안다고 그는 말했다. 자기의 솔직한 말버릇이 그 빌미가 되었으니, 나에 대한 존경을 마땅히 했어야 할 만큼 비밀로 하지 않았기 때문이라고 했고, 그 까닭인즉, 만일 내가 자기를 받아들이는 데 동의한다면, 자기는 모두에게 내놓고 나를 사랑하노라고, 나와 혼인할 작정이라고 말하기로 마음을 정한 지점에 와 있기 때문이라고 했다. 아버지와 어머니가 그것을 못마땅해하며 매정하게 굴지도 모르는 것은 사실이지만, 자기는 법률을 배워 익혔으니 이제 생계를 꾸려 갈 길이 있어, 내가 기대할 만큼 나를 떳떳하게 부양하는 일은 두렵지 않다고 했다. 그리고 한마디로, 내가 자기를 부끄러워하지 않으리라 믿는 만큼, 자기도 나를 부끄러워하지 않기로 결심했으며, 아내로 맞은 뒤에 떳떳이 인정하기로 결심한 사람을 지금 인정하기를 두려워하는 것은 떳떳치 못한 일로 여긴다고 했다. 그러니 나는 그저 그에게 손을 내밀어 주기만 하면 되고, 나머지는 모두 자기가 책임지겠노라고 했다.\n\n이제 나는 정말이지 끔찍한 처지에 놓였고, 큰형과 그토록 쉽게 굴복한 것을 진심으로 뉘우쳤으니, 그것은 양심의 가책 때문이 아니라 내가 누렸을지도 모르나 이제는 불가능하게 만들어 버린 행복을 떠올렸기 때문이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 씨름할 만한 양심의 거리낌이야 그리 크지 않았으나, 그래도 한 형제에게는 창녀가 되고 다른 형제에게는 아내가 된다는 것은 도무지 생각할 수가 없었다. 그러나 그때, 큰형이 자기 영지를 물려받게 되면 나를 아내로 삼겠노라 약속했던 일이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그러나 나는 곧, 종종 떠올리곤 했던 사실, 곧 그가 나를 정부로 정복한 뒤로는 나를 아내로 삼겠다는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없다는 것을 기억해 냈다. 그리고 정말이지 지금까지는, 비록 그것을 종종 생각했다고 말은 했어도, 그것이 내게 조금도 거리낌을 주지는 않았으니, 그가 나를 향한 애정을 조금도 줄이는 기색이 없었던 것과 마찬가지로 그 너그러움도 줄이지 않았기 때문이다. 다만 그는 분별 있게도, 자기가 준 것을 단 한 푼이라도 옷에 들이거나 조금이라도 유난스러운 티를 내지 말라고 내게 당부했으니, 그러면 집안에 필시 의심을 사게 되는 까닭이었다. 내가 그런 물건을 평범한 방법으로는 결코 손에 넣을 수 없고 오직 어떤 은밀한 친분으로만 손에 넣을 수 있다는 것은 누구나 알 터라, 그들이 곧바로 그것을 의심했을 것이기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이제 나는 큰 곤경에 처해 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 가장 큰 어려움은 이것이었으니, 작은 형제가 나를 바짝 에워싸 공략할 뿐 아니라 그것이 드러나도록 내버려 두었다는 점이다. 그는 누이의 방으로, 또 어머니의 방으로 들어와 앉아서는, 그들의 면전에서, 그들이 모두 거기 있는데도, 나에 관해 또 내게 다정한 말을 수천 가지나 늘어놓곤 했다. 이것이 어찌나 공공연해졌던지 온 집안이 그 일을 입에 올렸고, 그의 어머니는 그를 나무랐으며, 나를 대하는 그들의 태도는 완전히 달라진 듯했다. 한마디로, 그의 어머니가 마치 나를 집안에서 내보낼 작정이라는 듯한 말을, 쉬운 우리말로 하자면 나를 문밖으로 내쫓을 작정이라는 듯한 말을 흘렸던 것이다.\n이제 나는 이것이 그의 형에게 비밀일 수 없으리라고 확신했으니, 다만 그가, 사실 아직 아무도 그렇게 여기지 않았듯이, 막내 형제가 그 일에 관해 내게 무슨 제안을 했으리라고는 생각지 못했을 뿐이리라. 그러나 일이 더 멀리 번져 가리라는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었던 것처럼, 그것을 그에게 말하든지 아니면 그가 그것을 내게 말하든지 하지 않을 수 없으리라는 것도 알았으나, 어느 쪽을 먼저 해야 할지는 알 수 없었다. 다시 말해, 내가 그에게 그것을 털어놓아야 할지, 아니면 그가 내게 털어놓을 때까지 내버려 두어야 할지를 알 수 없었던 것이다.\n\n진지하게 숙고한 끝에—이제 정말이지 나는 비로소 일을 매우 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했으니, 그때까지는 한 번도 그래 본 적이 없었다—다시 말하거니와, 진지하게 숙고한 끝에, 나는 내가 먼저 그에게 그것을 말하기로 결심했다. 그리고 머지않아 기회가 왔으니, 바로 다음 날 그의 형이 무슨 볼일로 런던에 갔고, 집안 식구들은 전에 그랬던 것처럼, 또 실로 흔히 그랬던 것처럼 방문을 나갔던 터라, 그가 늘 하던 대로 베티 아씨와 한두 시간을 보내러 왔던 것이다.\n\n그가 와서 잠시 앉아 있자, 그는 내 얼굴빛에 변화가 있다는 것을, 내가 평소처럼 그에게 거리낌 없이 즐겁게 굴지 않는다는 것을, 특히 내가 울고 있었다는 것을 쉽게 알아챘다. 그는 곧 그것을 눈치채고는, 무슨 일이냐고, 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는 것이냐고 아주 다정한 말로 물었다. 나는 할 수만 있다면 그것을 얼버무리고 싶었으나, 감출 수가 없었다. 그리하여 될 수 있는 한 털어놓고 싶었던 그것을 끄집어내려는 숱한 채근을 견딘 끝에, 나는 그에게 정말로 무언가 나를 괴롭히는 것이 있으며, 그것은 그에게 숨길 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이지만, 그래도 어떻게 그것을 그에게 말해야 할지 알 수가 없다고 했다. 그것은 나를 놀라게 했을 뿐 아니라 크게 당혹스럽게 한 일이라, 그가 일러 주지 않는 한 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠다고 했다. 그는 더없이 다정하게, 그것이 무엇이든 그 때문에 괴로워하지 말라고, 자기가 온 세상으로부터 나를 지켜 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n그래서 나는 에둘러 말을 꺼내, 아씨들이 우리 관계에 관해 무슨 은밀한 정보를 얻은 것 같아 두렵다고 그에게 말했다. 오래전부터 나를 대하는 그들의 행실이 매우 달라진 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었고, 이제는 내가 조금도 빌미를 준 적이 없는데도 그들이 자주 나를 트집 잡고, 때로는 아주 대놓고 나와 다투는 지경에 이르렀기 때문이다. 또 전에는 늘 큰아씨와 함께 잠자리에 들었으나, 근래에는 혼자, 아니면 하녀 중 하나와 함께 자게 되었으며, 그들이 나에 관해 여러 차례 아주 매정하게 이야기하는 것을 우연히 엿들었다고 했다. 그러나 그 모든 것을 확증해 준 것은, 하인 중 하나가 내가 쫓겨나게 되어 있다는 말을 들었다고, 내가 더는 집에 있는 것이 집안을 위해 안전치 않다는 말을 들었다고 내게 일러 준 것이었다.\n\n그는 이 모든 말을 듣고 빙그레 웃었고, 나는 만일 무언가 발각된다면 나는 영영 끝장이며, 그것이 비록 나처럼 그를 파멸시키지는 않더라도 그에게까지 해가 되리라는 것을 뻔히 알 텐데, 어떻게 그것을 그토록 가볍게 여길 수 있느냐고 물었다. 나는 그를 나무랐으니, 그도 다른 모든 사내붙이와 마찬가지로, 한 여자의 평판과 명예를 제 손아귀에 넣고 나면 흔히 그것을 농담거리로 삼고, 적어도 그것을 하찮은 것으로 여기며, 제 욕심을 채운 여자들의 파멸을 아무 값어치도 없는 것으로 친다고 했다.\n\n그는 내가 격앙되고 진지해진 것을 보고는 곧바로 어조를 바꾸었다. 내가 자기를 그렇게 생각한다니 안타깝다고, 자기는 그런 생각의 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없으며 자기 자신의 평판만큼이나 내 평판을 아끼려 애써 왔다고 했다. 우리 관계가 어찌나 솜씨 있게 다루어졌던지 집안의 어느 누구도 그것을 조금도 의심하지 못한다고 확신한다고, 내가 내 생각을 말했을 때 자기가 빙그레 웃은 것은, 우리가 서로 통한다는 사실이 알려지거나 짐작되지조차 않았다는 것을 근래에 확인한 데서 온 것이라고 했다. 그리고 자기가 어찌하여 마음을 놓을 만한지를 내게 말해 주면, 나도 자기처럼 빙그레 웃게 될 것이니, 그것이 분명 내게 충분한 만족을 주리라 확신한다고 했다.\n\n\"그건 제가 알 수 없는 수수께끼예요,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 문밖으로 쫓겨나게 되었다는 것이 어찌하여 제게 만족스러운 일이 된다는 건지 말이에요. 우리 관계가 발각된 게 아니라면, 전에는 마치 제가 자기네 친자식이라도 되는 양 그토록 다정하게 대해 주던 온 집안 식구가 이제 와 저를 대하는 낯빛을 바꾸고 지금처럼 저를 대하게 만들 만한 무슨 다른 짓을 제가 했는지 모르겠으니까요.\"\n\n\"아니, 이봐요, 아가씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"그들이 당신 때문에 마음이 편치 않은 것, 그건 사실이오. 하지만 그들이 일의 진상을, 그러니까 당신과 나에 관련된 진상을 조금이라도 의심한다는 건, 사실과는 거리가 멀어서, 그들은 내 동생 로빈을 의심하고 있소. 한마디로, 그들은 그 녀석이 당신에게 구애하고 있다고 철석같이 믿고 있소. 아니, 그 바보 녀석이 제 입으로 그것을 그들 머릿속에 집어넣어 주기까지 했으니, 그 일을 두고 끊임없이 그들을 놀리며 제 꼴을 웃음거리로 만들고 있단 말이오. 솔직히 나는 그 녀석이 그러는 게 잘못이라 생각하오. 그게 그들의 심기를 거슬러 당신에게 매정하게 굴게 만든다는 걸 뻔히 알 텐데 말이오. 하지만 그게 내겐 만족스러운 일이오, 그들이 나를 조금도 의심하지 않는다는 확신을 주니까. 그리고 이게 당신에게도 만족스러운 일이 되기를 바라오.\"\n\n\"한편으로는 그래요,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 제 처지와는 전혀 무관한 일이고, 비록 그 일로도 마음을 졸이긴 했지만, 그게 저를 괴롭히는 가장 큰 일은 아니에요.\" \"그럼, 그게 뭐요?\" 하고 그가 말했다. 그 말에 나는 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게 아무 말도 할 수가 없었다. 그는 할 수 있는 한 나를 달래려 애썼으나, 마침내는 그것이 무엇인지 말하라고 몹시 다그치기 시작했다. 마침내 나는 그에게도 마땅히 말해야 할 것 같다고, 그도 그것을 알 권리가 얼마간 있다고 대답했다. 게다가 이 일에 대해 그의 지시가 필요하니, 내가 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모를 만큼 당혹스러운 처지에 있었기 때문이다. 그러고 나서 나는 그에게 모든 일을 다 이야기했다. 나는 그의 동생이 얼마나 경솔하게 처신했는지를, 자신을 그토록 공공연히 드러냈는지를 이야기했으니, 만일 그가 그런 일은 마땅히 그래야 하듯 비밀로 했더라면, 나는 아무 이유도 대지 않고 그저 그를 딱 잘라 거절할 수 있었을 것이고, 그러면 그도 머지않아 구애를 그쳤을 것이라고 했다. 그러나 그는 우선 내가 자기를 거절하지 않으리라고 믿는 허영을 부렸고, 그러고는 나를 차지하겠다는 결심을 온 집안에 떠벌리는 무람없는 짓을 했다고 했다.\n\n나는 그에게 내가 얼마나 그에 맞서 버텼는지를 말하고, 그의 제안이 얼마나 진실하고 명예로운 것이었는지를 말했다. \"하지만,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"제 처지는 갑절로 어려워질 거예요. 지금은 그가 저를 차지하고 싶어 한다고 해서 저를 못살게 굴지만, 제가 그를 거절했다는 걸 알게 되면 더 못살게 굴 테니까요. 그러면 그들은 곧 거기에 뭔가 다른 게 있다고 말할 테고, 그러면 제가 벌써 다른 누군가와 혼인했다거나, 아니면 저를 위해 이만큼 분에 넘치는 혼처를 결코 마다할 리가 없다는 말이 튀어나올 거예요.\"\n\n이 말은 정말이지 그를 몹시 놀라게 했다. 그는 그것이 정말이지 내가 처리하기에 까다로운 대목이라고, 내가 어떻게 거기서 빠져나갈지 알 수가 없다고 했으나, 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려 보고 다음에 만날 때 그것에 관해 어떤 결심에 이르렀는지 내게 알려 주겠다고 했다. 그리고 그동안에는 그의 동생에게 동의도 하지 말고, 그렇다고 딱 잘라 거절하지도 말고, 한동안 그를 애태우는 상태로 붙들어 두라고 당부했다.\n\n그가 내게 동의하지 말라고 말하는 데에 나는 흠칫하는 시늉을 했다. 나는 그에게, 내게 줄 동의 따위는 없다는 것을 그가 잘 알지 않느냐고 말했다. 그가 나와 혼인하겠노라 스스로 맹세했고, 그와 동시에 내 동의도 그에게 매여 버렸으며, 그가 줄곧 나를 자기 아내라 일러 왔으니, 나는 마치 예식이 치러지기라도 한 것처럼 나 자신을 실질적인 그의 아내로 여겨 왔다고 했다. 그리고 내가 그렇게 한 것은 바로 그의 입에서 나온 말 때문이니, 그가 줄곧 나더러 나 자신을 그의 아내라 부르라고 설득했기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n\"좋소, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"이제 그것 때문에 마음 쓰지 마오. 내가 당신의 남편이 아니라 해도, 나는 남편이나 다름없이 당신에게 잘해 주겠소. 그러니 지금은 그런 일로 괴로워 말고, 내가 이 일을 좀 더 들여다보게 해 주오. 그러면 다음에 만날 때 더 많은 말을 할 수 있을 거요.\"\n\n그는 이 말로 할 수 있는 한 나를 달랬으나, 나는 그가 매우 생각에 잠겨 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 내게 매우 다정하게 굴며 나에게 천 번이고, 아니 그 이상 입을 맞추고, 내게 돈까지 주었으나, 우리가 함께 있던 두 시간 남짓 내내 그 이상은 아무것도 청하지 않았으니, 평소에 어떠했는지, 또 우리에게 어떤 기회가 있었는지를 생각하면 그때 나는 정말이지 몹시 의아했다.\n\n그의 형은 닷새인가 엿새가 지나도록 런던에서 돌아오지 않았고, 그러고도 그와 이야기할 기회를 얻기까지 이틀이 더 걸렸다. 그러나 마침내 그를 단둘이 붙들어 그 일에 관해 매우 단도직입으로 이야기를 꺼냈고, 같은 날 저녁(우리는 함께 긴 이야기를 나누었으니) 그들이 나눈 이야기를 모두 내게 되풀이해 들려줄 기회를 얻었는데, 내가 기억하는 한 그것은 대략 다음과 같았다. 그는 동생에게, 네가 떠난 뒤로 너에 관한 괴이한 소식을 들었다고, 곧 네가 베티 아씨에게 구애한다는 소식을 들었다고 했다. \"음,\" 하고 동생이 조금 성난 투로 말했다, \"그래요, 그러고 있어요. 그래서 어떻단 거예요? 그게 누구하고 무슨 상관이람?\" \"아니,\" 하고 형이 말했다, \"성내지 마라, 로빈. 나는 그 일에 끼어들 생각이 없고, 그 일로 너한테 화를 낼 생각도 없다. 다만 그들이 그 일로 신경을 쓰고 있고, 그 가엾은 아이를 그 일로 모질게 대했다는 걸 알았는데, 그건 마치 내게 한 짓처럼 여겨지더구나.\" \"그들이라니, 누굴 말하는 거예요?\" 하고 로빈이 말했다. \"내 어머니하고 누이들 말이다,\" 하고 형이 말했다. \"하지만 들어 봐,\" 하고 형이 말했다, \"너 진심이냐? 정말로 이 아이를 사랑하느냐? 나한테는 터놓고 말해도 된다, 알잖니.\" \"그럼, 그렇다면,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"형한테는 터놓고 말할게요. 나는 그 아이를 세상의 어떤 여자보다도 사랑하고, 그들이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든 그 아이를 차지할 거예요. 그 아이도 나를 거절하지 않을 거라고 믿어요.\"\n\n그가 내게 이 말을 했을 때 나는 가슴이 철렁했으니, 비록 내가 그를 거절하지 않으리라 여기는 것이 더없이 이치에 맞는 일이었으나, 나는 내 양심 속으로 그를 거절할 수밖에 없다는 것을 알았고, 그래야만 하는 데에서 내 파멸을 보았기 때문이다. 그러나 그때는 달리 말하는 것이 내가 할 일임을 알았던 터라, 나는 그의 이야기를 이렇게 가로막았다.\n\n\"흥!\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"그분은 제가 자기를 거절하지 못하리라 여기나요? 그래도 제가 거절할 수 있다는 걸 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 통째로 들려줄 테니, 그러고 나서 하고 싶은 말을 하구려.\"\n\n그러고는 말을 이어, 자기가 이렇게 대답했노라고 내게 말했다. \"하지만 동생아, 그 아이는 아무것도 가진 게 없다는 걸 너도 알잖니, 게다가 넌 재산 많은 아가씨를 여럿 얻을 수도 있고.\"\n\n\"그딴 건 아무래도 좋아요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"나는 그 아이를 사랑하고, 혼인하면서 내 주머니를 흐뭇하게 할 뿐 내 마음은 흐뭇하게 하지 못하는 짓은 결코 하지 않을 거예요.\" \"그러니, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다, \"그를 막을 도리가 없소.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 그분을 막을 수 있다는 걸 보시게 될 거예요. 전에는 배우지 못했지만 이제는 아니라고 말하는 법을 배웠으니, 이 나라에서 가장 훌륭한 영주가 지금 제게 혼인하자고 청한대도, 저는 아주 기꺼이 그에게 아니라고 말할 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 하지만, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"당신이 그에게 뭐라고 말할 수 있겠소? 전에 우리가 그 이야기를 할 때 당신이 말했듯이, 그가 당신에게 그 일에 관해 숱한 질문을 던질 테고, 온 집안이 그게 대체 무슨 영문인가 의아해할 텐데.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 하고 내가 빙그레 웃으며 말했다, \"저는 그분에게, 또 그들에게도, 제가 벌써 그분의 형과 혼인했다고 말함으로써 단번에 그들 모두의 입을 막을 수 있어요.\"\n\n그도 그 말에 살짝 웃었으나, 나는 그것이 그를 흠칫하게 했다는 것을, 그리고 그가 그 말이 일으킨 동요를 감추지 못한다는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"아니, 그게 어떤 의미에서는 사실일지 몰라도, 당신이 그런 대답을 하겠다고 말하는 건 농담일 뿐이라고 생각하오. 그건 여러모로 온당치 않을 수 있소.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 아니에요,\" 하고 내가 즐거이 말했다, \"저는 당신의 동의 없이 그 비밀이 새어 나가게 하는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않아요.\"\n\n\"하지만 그렇다면, 당신이 명백히 그토록 이로울 혼처에 한사코 반대하는 것을 그들이 알게 되었을 때, 당신은 그에게, 혹은 그들에게 뭐라고 말할 수 있겠소?\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아니,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 무슨 말이 궁할까요? 우선, 저는 그들에게 아무런 이유도 댈 의무가 없어요. 다른 한편으로는, 제가 벌써 혼인했다고 말하고 거기서 멈출 수 있는데, 그러면 그분에게도 단호한 종지부가 될 거예요. 그 뒤로는 그분이 한마디도 더 물어볼 까닭이 없을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 온 집안이, 심지어 아버지와 어머니까지도 그 일로 당신을 들볶을 거고, 당신이 그들을 딱 잘라 거절하면 그들이 당신에게 언짢아할 테고, 게다가 의심까지 품을 거요.\"\n\n\"아니,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"그럼 제가 어쩌란 말이에요? 저더러 어떻게 하란 말이에요? 저는 전에도 충분히 곤경에 처해 있었고, 말씀드렸듯이 전에도 당혹스러운 처지에 있었기에, 당신의 조언을 얻고자 그 사정을 당신께 알린 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 그것에 관해 매우 많이 헤아려 보았다는 건 당신도 분명히 알 거요. 그리고 비록 그것이 내게는 굴욕스러운 점이 무척 많은 조언이고, 처음에는 당신에게 이상하게 들릴지도 모르나, 모든 것을 헤아려 볼 때, 나는 당신에게 그가 하는 대로 내버려 두는 것보다 더 나은 길이 보이지 않소. 그리고 그가 진심이고 진지하다는 걸 알게 되거든, 그와 혼인하구려.\"\n\n그 말에 나는 그에게 공포로 가득 찬 눈길을 던졌고, 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질려, 앉아 있던 의자에서 막 쓰러질 지경이 되었다. 그때 그가 흠칫 놀라며 \"내 사랑,\" 하고 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"당신 왜 그러오? 어디로 가는 거요?\" 하면서 그런 말을 잔뜩 늘어놓고, 흔들어 깨우고 나를 불러, 나를 조금 제정신으로 돌아오게 했으나, 내가 온전히 정신을 차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸고, 그 뒤로도 여러 분 동안 말을 할 수가 없었다.\n\n내가 온전히 정신을 차리자 그가 다시 말을 꺼냈다. \"내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 한 말에 당신은 어찌 그리 놀랐소? 그것을 진지하게 헤아려 봐 주었으면 하오. 이 일에서 집안이 어떤 태도인지는 당신도 뻔히 알 거요. 그리고 만일 그게 내 동생의 처지가 아니라 내 처지라면 그들은 펄펄 뛸 거요. 그리고 내가 보기에는, 그게 나의 파멸이자 당신의 파멸이기도 할 거요.\"\n\n\"흥!\" 하고 내가 여전히 성난 투로 말했다, \"당신의 그 모든 맹세와 서약이 집안의 못마땅함에 흔들려야 한단 말인가요? 제가 늘 그 점을 당신에게 이의로 내세우지 않았던가요? 그런데 당신은 그것을 자신이 초연한 것인 양, 대수롭잖게 여기는 양 가벼이 여겼잖아요. 그런데 이제 와서 이 꼴이 되었군요.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"이게 당신의 신의이고 명예이고, 당신의 사랑이며, 당신 약속의 견고함인가요?\"\n\n내 온갖 책망에도 그는 더없이 침착함을 잃지 않았으니, 나도 그 책망을 조금도 아끼지 않았다. 그러나 그는 마침내 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"내 사랑, 나는 아직 당신과 한 약속을 하나도 어기지 않았소. 내가 내 영지를 물려받게 되면 당신과 혼인하겠다고 말하긴 했지만, 보다시피 내 아버지는 정정하고 건강한 사람이라 앞으로도 삼십 년은 더 사실 테고, 그래도 이 고을 주변의 여러 사람보다 더 늙으신 것도 아니오. 그리고 당신은 그것이 내 파멸이 될 수 있다는 걸 알았기에 더 일찍 혼인하자고 청한 적도 없소. 그 밖의 모든 일에 관해서는, 나는 당신에게 어떤 것도 모자라게 한 적이 없으니, 당신은 아무것도 부족함이 없었소.\"\n\n나는 이 말을 한마디도 부인할 수 없었고, 그 전반에 대해 할 말이 없었다. \"하지만 그렇다면,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"당신은 저를 떠나지 않았으면서, 어떻게 저더러 당신을 떠나라는 그토록 끔찍한 짓을 하라고 설득할 수 있나요? 당신 쪽에 그토록 많은 사랑이 있었던 곳에서, 제 쪽에는 어떤 애정도, 어떤 사랑도 없었다고 보시는 건가요? 제가 아무런 보답도 하지 않았던가요? 제가 제 진실함과 제 열정의 증거를 하나도 보이지 않았던가요? 제가 당신께 바친 명예와 정숙함의 희생이, 제가 끊을 수 없을 만큼 강한 끈으로 당신께 매여 있다는 증거가 아니란 말인가요?\"\n\n\"하지만 이봐요, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"당신은 안전한 처지에 들어설 수 있고, 단번에 명예롭고 영화롭게 모습을 드러낼 수 있으며, 우리가 한 일에 대한 기억은 마치 그런 일이 일어난 적도 없었던 것처럼 영원한 침묵 속에 싸 둘 수 있소. 당신은 언제까지나 나의 존경과 나의 진실한 애정을 받을 테고, 다만 그때는 그것이 떳떳한 것이 되어 내 동생에게도 더없이 떳떳한 것이 될 거요. 당신은 나의 사랑하는 누이가 될 거요, 지금 당신이 나의 사랑하는——\" 하고 그는 거기서 멈췄다.\n\n\"사랑하는 창녀라고,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"말을 이었더라면 그렇게 말씀하셨겠죠. 아예 그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋았어요. 하지만 저는 당신을 알아요. 그래도 저는 당신께 청하니, 당신이 저와 나눈 그 긴 이야기들을, 저더러 나 자신을 떳떳한 여자로 믿게 하려고 들인 그 숱한 시간의 수고를 기억해 주세요. 제가 비록 세상의 눈에는 아니어도 뜻으로는 당신의 아내라는 것을, 그리고 우리 사이에 오간 것이 마치 우리가 교구 목사 앞에서 공공연히 혼례를 올리기라도 한 것처럼 실질적인 혼인이었다는 것을요. 이것이 당신이 제게 한 당신 자신의 말이었다는 걸, 당신도 알고 있고 기억하지 않을 수 없을 거예요.\"\n\n나는 이것이 그에게 좀 너무 따끔하다는 것을 알았으나, 뒤이은 말로 그것을 보충했다. 그는 한동안 미동도 없이 서서 아무 말이 없었고, 나는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"당신은,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"더없이 큰 부당함을 저지르지 않고서는, 제가 그 모든 설득에 굴복한 것이 의심할 여지 없는 사랑 없이, 그 뒤로 어떤 일이 일어나든 다시 흔들리지 않을 사랑 없이 그리한 것이라고 믿을 수 없어요. 만일 당신이 저를 그토록 명예롭지 못하게 생각한다면, 저는 묻지 않을 수 없어요. 제 어떤 행실이 그런 억측의 빌미를 주었는지를요.\n\n그러니, 제가 만일 제 애정의 채근에 굴복했다면, 그리고 제가 정말로, 그 본질에서, 당신의 아내라고 믿도록 설득당했다면, 이제 와서 저는 그 모든 논거를 거짓이라 하고, 나 자신을 당신의 창녀라, 혹은 같은 말이지만 정부라 불러야 하나요? 그리고 당신은 저를 당신의 동생에게 넘기시겠다는 건가요? 당신이 제 애정을 넘길 수 있나요? 당신이 저더러 당신을 사랑하기를 그치고 그를 사랑하라 명할 수 있나요? 제가 청한다고 그런 변화를 일으키는 것이 제 힘으로 되는 일이라 보시나요? 아니에요, 나리,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"분명히 알아 두세요, 그것은 불가능해요. 그리고 당신 쪽의 변화가 어떠하든, 저는 언제까지나 진실할 거예요. 그리고 일이 이 불행한 지경에까지 이르렀으니, 저는 당신 동생의 아내가 되느니 차라리 당신의 창녀가 되는 편이 훨씬 나아요.\"\n\n그는 이 마지막 이야기의 감화에 흐뭇해하고 마음이 움직인 듯했으니, 자기는 전에 섰던 자리에 그대로 서 있다고, 자기는 여태껏 한 어떤 약속에서도 내게 신의를 저버린 적이 없다고 했다. 다만 내 앞에 놓인 이 일에서, 특히 나를 위해서 너무나 끔찍한 일들이 숱하게 눈앞에 떠올랐던 터라, 다른 길을 그 무엇으로도 따를 수 없을 만큼 효험 있는 처방으로 여겼던 것이라고 했다. 그는 이것이 우리를 완전히 갈라놓는 것은 아닐 테고, 우리가 평생 벗으로 사랑할 수 있으며, 어쩌면 일이 어찌 되느냐에 따라서는 지금의 처지에서보다 더 큰 만족을 누리며 그럴 수도 있으리라 생각한다고 했다. 또 자기로 말하자면, 발설되기만 하면 우리 둘 다의 파멸일 수밖에 없는 비밀을 누설하는 일에 관해서는 내가 자기에게서 아무것도 염려할 게 없으리라 감히 말한다고 했다. 그리고 다만 그 일에 걸림돌이 될 만한 질문이 하나 있는데, 만일 그 질문에 부정으로 답한다면, 자기는 아무래도 이것이 내가 취할 수 있는 유일한 길이라고 여길 수밖에 없다고 했다.\n\n나는 곧 그의 질문이 무엇인지 짐작했으니, 곧 내가 아이를 배지 않았다고 확신하느냐는 것이었다. 그것에 관해서라면 그가 마음 쓸 필요가 없다고, 나는 아이를 배지 않았다고 그에게 말했다. \"그렇다면, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"지금은 더 이야기할 겨를이 없소. 그것을 헤아려 보고, 그것에 관해 곰곰이 생각해 보구려. 나는 그래도 그것이 당신이 취할 수 있는 가장 나은 길이라는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없소.\" 그러고는 그가 작별을 고했으니, 그것도 더욱 황급히 그러했던 것은, 그가 떠나려고 막 일어선 바로 그 순간에 그의 어머니와 누이들이 대문에서 종을 울렸기 때문이다.\n\n그는 나를 더없이 어지러운 심사 속에 남겨 두고 떠났고, 그는 다음 날 그것을 쉽게 알아챘으며, 그 주 내내 그러했으니, 우리가 이야기를 나눈 것이 화요일 저녁에 지나지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 그 주 내내 나를 찾아올 기회가 없었고, 그다음 일요일이 되어서야, 내가 몸이 좋지 않아 교회에 가지 않고, 그도 비슷한 핑계를 대고 집에 남았다.\n\n이제 그는 다시 나를 단둘이 한 시간 반 동안 차지했고, 우리는 그 모든 논거로 처음부터 다시 빠져들었으니, 적어도 거의 똑같은 것이라 그것을 되풀이해 봐야 아무 소용이 없을 정도였다. 마침내 나는 그에게 격앙되어, 내가 두 형제와 동침할 생각을 조금이라도 품으리라 짐작할 수 있다니 내 정숙함을 대체 어떻게 보느냐고 물으며, 그런 일은 결코 있을 수 없다고 단언했다. 나는 덧붙이기를, 설령 그가 나를 다시는 보지 않겠노라 말한다 해도—죽음 외에는 그보다 더 끔찍한 것이 없겠지만—나는 나 자신에게 그토록 명예롭지 못하고 그에게 그토록 비열한 생각은 결코 품을 수 없다고 했다. 그러니 나는 그에게, 만일 나를 향한 존경이나 애정이 한 톨이라도 남아 있거든, 그것에 관해 내게 더는 말하지 말든지, 아니면 칼을 뽑아 나를 죽여 달라고 간청했다. 그는 자신이 일컫는바 내 완고함에 놀란 듯했고, 내가 이 일로 나 자신에게도, 또 자기에게도 매정하게 군다고 했다. 그것은 우리 둘 다에게 뜻밖에 닥친 위기라 어느 쪽도 미리 내다볼 수 없었던 것이지만, 자기는 우리 둘 다를 파멸에서 구할 다른 길이 보이지 않으니 그래서 그게 더욱 매정하게 여겨진다고 했다. 그러나 만일 그것에 관해 더는 말해서는 안 된다면, 하고 그는 여느 때 없는 냉랭함으로 덧붙이기를, 우리에게 달리 이야기할 게 있는지 모르겠다고 했다. 그러고는 작별을 고하려 일어섰다. 나도 마치 같은 무심함으로 그러는 양 일어섰으나, 그가 이를테면 작별의 입맞춤을 하려고 다가오자, 나는 그토록 격렬한 울음을 터뜨려, 말을 하려 해도 할 수가 없었고, 그저 그의 손을 꼭 쥐어 작별을 고하는 듯했으나, 격하게 울고 말았다.\n\n그는 이것에 적잖이 마음이 움직였다. 그리하여 그는 다시 앉아, 내 격정의 지나침을 가라앉히려고 다정한 말을 잔뜩 했으나, 그래도 자기가 제안한 일의 불가피함을 거듭 다그쳤다. 그러면서도 줄곧, 만일 내가 정 거절한다면 그래도 자기가 나를 돌보아 주겠노라 고집하면서도, 그가 정작 가장 중요한 점에서는 나를 마다하리라는 것을—아니, 정부로서조차도—내게 뻔히 보여 주었으니, 자기가 아는 한 자기 동생의 아내가 될지도 모르는 여자와는 동침하지 않는 것을 명예의 한 점으로 삼았던 것이다.\n\n그저 그를 정인으로 잃는다는 것은 내게 그리 큰 슬픔이 아니었고, 정작 슬픔은 그의 사람을 잃는다는 것, 정말이지 내가 미칠 듯이 사랑했던 그 사람을 잃는다는 것이었으며, 또 내가 품었던, 그리고 늘 그 위에 내 희망을 쌓아 올렸던, 언젠가 그를 내 남편으로 삼으리라는 그 모든 기대를 잃는다는 것이었다. 이런 것들이 어찌나 내 마음을 짓눌렀던지, 한마디로 나는 몹시 앓아눕고 말았다. 한마디로, 내 마음의 고뇌가 나를 고열에 빠뜨렸고, 집안의 누구도 내가 살아남으리라 여기지 않은 채 오랜 시일이 흘렀다.\n\n나는 정말이지 매우 쇠약해졌고, 자주 헛소리를 하며 정신이 오락가락했다. 그러나 무엇보다 내게 절박했던 것은, 정신이 오락가락할 때 그에게 해가 될 무슨 말이라도 하지 않을까 하는 두려움이었다. 나는 그를 보고 싶어 마음이 괴로웠고, 그도 나를 보고 싶어 그러했으니, 그가 정말로 나를 더없이 열렬히 사랑했기 때문이다. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 어느 쪽에서든 그것을 바랄 여지가 조금도 없었고, 그것을 점잖게 차릴 여지조차 없었다.\n\n나는 거의 다섯 주 동안 자리보전을 했고, 비록 열의 기세가 세 주 만에 누그러지긴 했으나, 그래도 여러 차례 도졌다. 의사들은 두세 번이나, 자기들로서는 더 해 줄 것이 없으니 자연과 병이 싸워 결판을 내도록 맡길 수밖에 없고, 다만 강심제로 자연을 북돋워 그 싸움을 버티게 할 따름이라고 했다. 다섯 주가 지난 끝에 나는 차도가 있었으나, 어찌나 쇠약하고, 어찌나 변해 버리고, 어찌나 우울하며, 또 어찌나 더디게 회복되던지, 의사들은 내가 폐병에 걸리지나 않을까 염려했다. 그리고 무엇보다 나를 분하게 한 것은, 그들이 내 마음이 짓눌려 있고 무언가 나를 괴롭히고 있으며, 한마디로 내가 사랑에 빠져 있다는 것을 자기네 소견으로 내놓았다는 점이다. 그러자 온 집안이 나를 다그쳐, 내가 사랑에 빠졌는지 아닌지, 또 누구와 그러한지 말하라고 캐물었으나, 나는 그럴 만도 했듯이, 사랑에 빠진 일이 전혀 없다고 잡아뗐다.\n\n이 일로 어느 날 식탁에서 나를 두고 한바탕 다툼이 벌어졌으니, 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓을 뻔했고, 한동안은 정말로 그러했다. 마침 아버지만 빼고 식구가 모두 식탁에 있었는데, 나로 말하자면 몸이 아파 내 방에 있었다. 그 이야기의 발단은, 그들이 막 점심을 끝냈을 때, 내게 먹을 것을 좀 보냈던 노부인이 하녀를 불러 올려보내, 내가 더 먹겠는지 물어보라고 한 것이었다. 그러나 하녀는 내가 이미 보낸 것의 절반도 먹지 않았다는 말을 가지고 내려왔다.\n\n\"저런,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다, \"저 가엾은 아이! 영 낫지 못할까 봐 걱정이구나.\"\n\n\"낫다니요!\" 하고 큰아들이 말했다, \"베티 아씨가 어떻게 나을 수가 있겠어요? 사랑에 빠졌다는데요.\"\n\n\"난 그런 건 조금도 믿지 않는다,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"전 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠네요,\" 하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그 애가 그토록 예쁘고, 그토록 매혹적이라느니 어쩌니 하며, 그것도 그 애가 듣는 데서 그렇게들 떠들어 댔으니, 그게 그 애 머리를 돌려놓은 거라고 봐요. 그런 짓거리 뒤에 무슨 망상이 따라붙을지 누가 알겠어요? 저로서는 도무지 영문을 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"이봐, 누이, 그래도 그 애가 아주 예쁘다는 건 인정해야지,\" 하고 큰아들이 말했다.\n\n\"그래요, 게다가 누이보다 훨씬 더 예쁘죠,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그게 누이의 분한 점이고요.\"\n\n\"됐어, 됐어, 그건 문제가 아니야,\" 하고 그의 누이가 말했다, \"그 애가 제법 반반한 건 맞고, 자기도 그걸 아주 잘 알아. 굳이 그런 말을 해 줘서 우쭐하게 만들 필요는 없잖아.\"\n\n\"우리가 그 애가 우쭐하다는 이야기를 하는 게 아니라,\" 하고 큰아들이 말했다, \"사랑에 빠졌다는 이야기를 하는 거잖아. 어쩌면 자기 자신과 사랑에 빠졌는지도 모르지. 내 누이들 생각이 그런 모양이니까.\"\n\n\"그 애가 나하고 사랑에 빠졌으면 좋겠네,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그럼 내가 얼른 그 애의 고통을 덜어 줄 텐데.\"\n\n\"그게 무슨 소리냐, 얘야,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말을 하느냐?\"\n\n\"아니, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 다시, 아주 정직하게 말했다, \"그 가엾은 아이를 사랑 때문에, 그것도 손만 뻗으면 닿을 데 있는 사람 때문에 죽게 내버려 두라는 말씀이세요?\"\n\n\"어머, 오라버니!\" 하고 둘째 딸이 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말을 해요? 세상에 한 푼도 없는 계집애를 거두겠다는 거예요?\"\n\n\"이봐, 얘야,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"아름다움은 곧 지참금이고, 거기에 명랑함까지 더하면 두 곱절의 지참금이야. 네 지참금으로 그 애가 가진 그 두 가지의 절반이라도 네게 있었으면 싶구나.\" 그리하여 그녀의 입이 막혀 버렸다.\n\n\"내가 보기엔,\" 하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"베티가 사랑에 빠진 게 아니라면, 오라버니가 빠진 거예요. 어째서 베티에게 속내를 털어놓지 않았는지 모르겠네. 장담하건대 그 애는 아니라고 하지 않을걸요.\"\n\n\"청을 받았을 때 굴복하는 자들은,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"한 번도 굴복하라는 청을 받지 못한 자들보다 한 걸음 앞서 있고, 청을 받기도 전에 굴복하는 자들보다는 두 걸음 앞서 있지. 그게 누이에 대한 답이야.\"\n\n이 말이 누이를 발끈하게 했고, 그녀는 격노하여, 이제는 그 계집—나를 말함이다—이 집안에서 나가야 할 때가 되었다고 말했다. 그리고 그 애가 쫓아낼 만한 상태가 아니라 그렇지, 그 애가 거동할 수 있게 되는 대로 아버지와 어머니가 그것을 고려해 주시기를 바란다고 했다.\n\n로빈은 그것은 이 집안의 주인과 안주인이 결정할 일이지, 큰누이처럼 분별이 모자란 사람에게 가르침을 받을 일은 아니라고 대꾸했다.\n\n그것은 훨씬 더 격해졌으니, 누이는 악다구니를 했고 로빈은 비아냥거리며 빈정댔으나, 그 일로 가엾은 베티는 집안에서 입지가 몹시 좁아졌다. 나는 그 이야기를 전해 듣고 진심으로 울었고, 노부인이 내게로 올라왔으니, 누군가 내가 그 일로 몹시 상심하고 있다고 그녀에게 일러 준 것이었다. 나는 그녀에게 하소연하기를, 의사들이 아무런 근거도 없이 내게 그런 진단을 내리다니 정말이지 너무하다고, 게다가 내가 집안에서 처한 사정을 생각하면 그것은 더더욱 너무한 일이라고 했다. 나는 그녀의 존경을 잃을 만한 짓이라곤 아무것도 하지 않았기를, 또 그녀의 아들들과 딸들 사이의 다툼에 어떤 빌미도 주지 않았기를 바란다고 했고, 나는 사랑에 빠지기보다는 관桁을 생각해야 할 처지라고 하면서, 다른 누구의 잘못도 아닌 오직 나 자신의 잘못이 아니고서는 그녀의 평가 속에서 손해를 보지 않게 해 달라고 간청했다.\n\n그녀는 내 말의 정당함을 헤아렸으나, 그들 사이에 그런 소란이 일었고 작은아들이 그렇게 헛소리 같은 식으로 떠들어 댄 터이니, 나더러 그녀에게 충직하여 단 한 가지 질문에만 진실하게 답해 달라고 했다. 나는 진심으로, 더없이 솔직하고 진실하게 그러겠노라고 했다. 그래서 그 질문인즉, 그녀의 아들 로버트와 나 사이에 무슨 일이 있느냐는 것이었다. 나는 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 진실의 맹세를 다해, 또 그럴 만도 했듯이, 그런 일은 없으며 일찍이 없었다고 그녀에게 말했다. 나는 그녀에게, 로버트 도련님이 그분의 버릇인 줄 그녀도 알다시피 허튼소리를 하고 농을 친 것이며, 나는 늘 그것을, 짐작건대 그분이 의도한 대로, 아무 뜻도 없는 거칠고 가벼운 말투로 받아들였다고 했다. 그리고 그녀가 그것으로 이해한 그런 일은 우리 사이에 티끌만큼도 없다고 거듭 단언했으며, 그것을 암시한 자들은 내게 큰 해를 끼쳤고 로버트 도련님께도 아무런 득이 되지 않았다고 했다.\n\n노부인은 충분히 흡족해하며 내게 입을 맞추고, 명랑하게 말을 건네며, 몸조심하고 부족함이 없도록 하라고 이르고는 작별을 고했다. 그러나 그녀가 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 아들과 그의 모든 누이가 한데 모여 으르렁대고 있었다. 그들은 격노하여 펄펄 뛰었으니, 그가 그들이 못생겼다고, 한 번도 정인을 가져 본 적이 없다고, 한 번도 그런 청을 받아 본 적이 없다고, 오히려 거의 먼저 청할 만큼 그토록 들이댔다고 면박을 준 까닭이었다. 그는 베티 아씨라는 주제를 두고 그들을 비아냥거렸으니, 그 애가 얼마나 예쁜지, 얼마나 명랑한지, 그 애가 그들보다 노래를 얼마나 더 잘하고 춤을 얼마나 더 잘 추는지, 또 그 애가 얼마나 더 예쁜지를 떠들어 댔다. 그러면서 그는 그들을 약 올릴 만한 심술궂은 말이라면 하나도 빼놓지 않았고, 정말이지 그들을 너무 심하게 몰아붙였다. 노부인은 그것이 한창일 때 아래층으로 내려와, 그것을 그치게 하려고, 자기가 나와 나눈 이야기를 그들에게 모두 들려주었으며, 로버트 도련님과 나 사이에는 아무 일도 없다고 내가 어떻게 답했는지를 말했다.\n\n\"거기선 그 애가 틀렸어요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"우리 사이에 많은 일이 없었던 거라면, 우리는 지금보다 더 가까이 있었을 테니까요. 나는 그 애를 끔찍이 사랑한다고 그 애에게 말했어요,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 내가 진심이라는 걸 도무지 그 말괄량이에게 믿게 할 수가 없었죠.\" \"그걸 어떻게 믿게 하겠니,\" 하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"제정신인 사람이라면 아무도, 네가 사정을 그토록 잘 아는 가엾은 아이에게 그런 식으로 말하는 걸 진심이라고는 믿지 못할 거다.\n\n하지만 얘야,\" 하고 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"네가 진심이라는 걸 그 애에게 믿게 할 수 없었다고 하니, 우리는 그것을 어떻게 믿어야 하느냐? 네가 이야기 중에 어찌나 횡설수설하는지, 네가 진심인지 농담인지 아무도 모르겠구나. 하지만 그 애가, 제 입으로 고백한 바로는, 진실하게 답했다는 걸 알았으니, 너도 그래 주었으면 한다. 내가 믿고 의지할 수 있도록 진지하게 말해 다오. 거기에 뭔가 있느냐, 없느냐? 너 진심이냐, 아니냐? 정말로 정신이 나간 거냐, 아니냐? 그건 무거운 질문이니, 우리 마음을 좀 놓이게 해 주었으면 한다.\"\n\n\"맹세코, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"이 일을 에두르거나 더 거짓말을 늘어놓아 봐야 헛일이에요. 저는 교수대로 끌려가는 사람만큼이나 진심이에요. 베티 아씨가 저를 사랑한다고, 저와 혼인하겠다고 말해만 준다면, 저는 내일 아침 식사도 거른 채 그 애를 맞아, 아침밥을 먹는 대신 '검은 머리 파뿌리 되도록'이라고 말할 거예요.\"\n\n\"그래,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"그렇다면 아들 하나를 잃은 셈이로구나.\" 그녀는 그것을 매우 애통한 어조로, 그것을 크게 근심하는 사람처럼 말했다.\n\n\"잃은 게 아니길 바라요, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"좋은 아내가 찾아낸 사내는 잃은 게 아니니까요.\"\n\n\"하지만 얘야,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다, \"그 애는 거지나 다름없잖니.\"\n\n\"아니, 그렇다면, 어머니, 그 애는 그만큼 자비가 더 필요하겠네요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"제가 그 애를 교구의 손에서 거두어 데려갈 테니, 그 애와 저는 함께 동냥이나 하지요.\"\n\n\"그런 일로 농담을 하는 건 못쓴다,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다.\n\n\"농담이 아니에요, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"저희가 가서 용서를 구할게요, 어머니. 그리고 어머니의 축복을, 또 아버지의 축복을 구할게요.\"\n\n\"이건 다 도리에 어긋나는 짓이다, 얘야,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 진심이라면 넌 끝장난 거야.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않을까 봐 걱정이에요,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"사실 저는 정말로 그 애가 저를 받아들이지 않을까 봐 걱정이거든요. 제 누이의 그 모든 윽박지름과 호통 끝에, 아무래도 저는 도무지 그 애를 설득해 낼 수 없을 것 같아요.\"\n\n\"그것참 그럴듯한 이야기네요. 그 애도 그렇게 정신이 나간 건 아니에요. 베티 아씨는 바보가 아니거든요,\" 하고 작은딸이 말했다, \"그 애가 다른 사람들 못지않게 아니라고 말하는 법을 배웠다고 생각하나요?\"\n\n\"아니지, 농담 잘하는 아가씨,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"베티 아씨는 바보가 아니야. 하지만 베티 아씨가 어딘가 다른 데 매여 있을지도 모르지, 그럼 어쩔 테냐?\"\n\n\"아니,\" 하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그건 우리도 뭐라 할 말이 없네요. 그럼 그 상대가 누구여야 하죠? 그 애는 도통 문밖에 나가는 법이 없으니, 그건 분명 오라버니들 사이일 텐데요.\"\n\n\"그건 내가 할 말이 없어,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"나는 충분히 심문을 받았어. 저기 내 형님이 있잖아. 우리 사이여야 한다면, 형님에게 한번 캐 보든지.\"\n\n이 말이 큰형을 정통으로 찔렀고, 그는 로빈이 무언가 알아챈 것이라고 단정했다. 그러나 그는 동요한 기색을 드러내지 않으려 자제했다. \"이봐,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"네 이야기를 내게 뒤집어씌워 망신 줄 생각 마라. 분명히 말하건대, 나는 그런 물건은 취급하지 않아. 나는 베티 아씨에게도, 이 교구의 어느 베티 아씨에게도 할 말이 없어.\" 그러고는 일어나 휙 나가 버렸다.\n\n\"맞아요,\" 하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"내 큰오라버니를 위해선 내가 장담할 수 있어요. 그분은 세상 물정을 더 잘 아니까요.\"\n\n이렇게 그 이야기는 끝났으나, 그것은 큰형을 완전히 당혹스럽게 만들어 놓았다. 그는 동생이 모든 것을 알아챈 것이라고 단정했고, 거기에 내가 관련되었는지 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다. 그러나 온갖 수를 써 보아도 그것을 알아내려 나를 만날 수가 없었다. 마침내 그는 어찌나 당혹스러웠던지 아주 자포자기하여, 어떻게 되든 내 방으로 들어와 나를 보기로 결심했다. 이를 위해 그는 일을 이렇게 꾸몄으니, 어느 날 점심 식사 뒤에, 큰누이가 위층으로 올라가는 것을 볼 수 있을 때까지 지켜보다가 그녀를 뒤따라 달려갔다. \"이봐요, 누이,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"그 아픈 여자가 어디 있죠? 한번 들여다봐도 될까요?\" \"그럼요,\" 하고 누이가 말했다, \"괜찮을 거예요. 하지만 제가 먼저 좀 가게 해 줘요, 가서 일러 줄 테니.\" 그리하여 그녀는 문간으로 달려가 내게 알리고, 곧 다시 그를 불렀다. \"오라버니,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다, \"들어와도 좋대요.\" 그리하여 그가 들어왔으니, 꼭 그 똑같은 떠들썩한 투였다. \"음,\" 하고 그가 들어오면서 문간에서 말했다, \"사랑에 빠졌다는 그 아픈 양반이 어디 있나? 좀 어떠세요, 베티 아씨?\" 나는 의자에서 일어서려 했으나, 어찌나 쇠약했던지 한참 동안 그럴 수가 없었다. 그가, 또 그의 누이도 그것을 보았고, 그녀가 말했다. \"자, 일어서려고 애쓰지 말아요. 내 오라버니는 격식을 바라지 않으니까요, 더구나 지금 그토록 쇠약한데.\" \"그래요, 그래요, 베티 아씨, 부디 그대로 앉아 계세요,\" 하고 그가 말하더니, 내 맞은편 의자에 앉아, 자못 흥겨운 듯한 기색을 보였다.\n\n그는 누이에게, 또 내게 횡설수설하는 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았으니, 때로는 이 이야기를, 때로는 저 이야기를 했는데, 누이를 즐겁게 하려는 속셈이었고, 이따금씩 그것을 그 옛이야기로 돌려, 내게 향하게 했다. \"가엾은 베티 아씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"사랑에 빠진다는 건 딱한 일이에요. 아니, 그게 아씨를 이렇게 딱하게 만들어 놓았으니.\" 마침내 나도 조금 입을 열었다. \"이렇게 흥겨워하시는 걸 보니 기쁘네요, 나리,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"하지만 의사라면 환자를 놀려 먹는 것보다는 더 나은 할 일을 찾았어야 할 것 같은데요. 제가 다른 병으로 앓았던 거라면, 저는 그 속담을 너무 잘 알기에 의사를 제게 오게 두지도 않았을 거예요.\" \"무슨 속담요?\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"오! 이제 생각나는군요. 뭐더라—\n\n사랑이 병일 때는, 의사도 멍청이라.\n\n그거 아니에요, 베티 아씨?\" 나는 빙그레 웃으며 아무 말도 하지 않았다. \"아니,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내 생각엔 그 결과가 그것이 사랑임을 입증한 것 같소. 의사가 아씨에게 별 도움이 못 된 모양이니 말이오. 차도가 매우 더디다고들 하던데. 거기 뭔가 있는 게 아닌가 싶소, 베티 아씨. 아씨가 불치병을 앓고 있는 게 아닌가 싶은데, 그게 바로 사랑이오.\" 나는 빙그레 웃으며 말했다, \"아니에요, 정말로, 나리, 그건 제 병이 아니에요.\"\n\n우리는 그런 이야기를 잔뜩 나누었고, 때로는 그만큼이나 시답잖은 다른 이야기들도 나누었다. 이윽고 그는 내게 노래를 한 곡 불러 달라고 청했는데, 그 말에 나는 빙그레 웃으며 내 노래하던 시절은 끝났다고 했다. 마침내 그는 내게 자기가 플루트를 불어 줄까 하고 물었고, 그의 누이는 그것이 내게 해로울 것 같다고, 내 머리가 그것을 견디지 못할 거라고 했다. 나는 고개를 숙여 인사하고, 아니, 그것은 내게 해롭지 않을 거라고 했다. \"그리고, 부디, 아씨,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"그것을 막지 말아 주세요. 저는 플루트 소리를 무척 좋아하니까요.\" 그러자 그의 누이가 말했다. \"그래요, 그럼 부세요, 오라버니.\" 그 말에 그는 자기 골방의 열쇠를 꺼냈다. \"사랑하는 누이,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 영 게을러서 그러는데, 내 골방에 좀 가서 플루트를 가져다주겠어요? 그게 그런 서랍에 있어요,\" 하고 그것이 거기 없는 것이 확실한 곳을 일러 주었으니, 그녀가 그것을 찾느라 한동안 헤매게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n그녀가 가자마자, 그는 자기 동생이 나에 관해 한 이야기를 모두, 또 그것을 자기에게 들이댄 일과 그 일에 대한 자기의 근심을 내게 들려주었으니, 그것이 바로 그가 이렇게 나를 찾아오기를 꾸민 까닭이었다. 나는 그에게, 그의 동생에게든 다른 누구에게든 단 한 번도 입을 연 적이 없다고 단언했다. 나는 그에게 내가 처한 끔찍한 위급함을 이야기했으니, 그를 향한 내 사랑과, 그가 그 애정을 잊고 다른 이에게 옮기라고 청한 것이 나를 쓰러뜨렸다고, 그리고 나는 천 번이고, 회복하느니 차라리 죽어, 전에 씨름했던 것과 똑같은 사정과 다시 씨름하지 않기를 바랐다고 했다. 또 그가 삶을 향해 뒷걸음질친 것이 내 회복이 더딘 큰 까닭이었다고 했다. 나는 덧붙이기를, 몸이 낫는 대로 집안을 떠나야 하리라는 것을 내다보고 있다고, 그리고 그의 동생과 혼인하는 일로 말하자면, 그와 나 사이에 있었던 일을 생각하면 그런 생각만 해도 진저리가 난다고, 그러니 그 일에 관해서는 결코 그의 동생을 다시 보지 않으리라는 것을 그가 믿어도 좋다고 했다. 그리고 만일 그가 나와 한 그 모든 맹세와 서약과 약속을 깨뜨리겠다면, 그것은 그의 양심과 명예와 그 자신 사이의 일이지만, 그가 나더러 나 자신을 그의 아내라 부르도록 설득하고, 그가 나를 아내로 쓰도록 허락한 이 몸이, 그가 내게 어떠하든, 아내가 마땅히 그러해야 하듯이 그에게 충실하지 않았다고는 결코 말할 수 없을 거라고 했다.\n\n그는 막 대꾸하려던 참이었고, 내가 설득되지 못해 안타깝다고 말하고 더 말을 이으려던 참이었으나, 그는 누이가 오는 소리를 들었고, 나도 그러했다. 그래도 나는 이 몇 마디 말을 대답으로 억지로 끄집어냈으니, 곧 나는 결코 한 형제를 사랑하면서 다른 형제와 혼인하도록 설득될 수 없다는 것이었다. 그는 고개를 가로저으며 \"그럼 나는 끝장이로군,\" 하고 자기 자신을 두고 말했고, 바로 그 순간 누이가 방으로 들어와 그에게 플루트를 찾을 수 없었다고 했다. \"음,\" 하고 그가 흥겹게 말했다, \"이 게으름은 안 되겠군.\" 그러고는 일어나 직접 그것을 찾으러 갔으나, 그 역시 그것 없이 돌아왔다. 그것을 찾을 수 없어서가 아니라, 그의 마음이 조금 어지러워 불고 싶은 생각이 없었기 때문이다. 게다가 그가 누이에게 보낸 심부름은 다른 식으로 답을 얻은 셈이었으니, 그는 다만 내게 말할 기회를 원했던 것이고, 비록 그리 흡족하지는 못했어도 그것을 얻었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 나는 그에게 거리낌 없이, 또 앞서 이야기한 것처럼 그토록 정직하고 솔직하게 내 속내를 말한 데에 큰 만족이 있었다. 그리고 비록 그것이 내가 바라던 식으로는, 다시 말해 그 사람을 내게 더 묶어 두는 식으로는 전혀 작용하지 않았으나, 그래도 그것은 그가, 곧이곧대로 명예를 저버리고 그토록 자주 맹세로 다짐했던바 결코 나를 버리지 않고 자기 영지를 물려받는 대로 나를 아내로 삼겠다던 신사의 신의를 모두 내던지지 않고서는, 나를 떠날 가능성을 그에게서 모조리 앗아 갔다.\n\n그로부터 그리 여러 주가 지나지 않아 나는 다시 집안을 돌아다니게 되었고 차츰 건강해지기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 여전히 우울하고, 말이 없으며, 활기가 없고, 칩거했으니, 그 까닭을 아는 그 사람만 빼고는 온 집안이 의아해했다. 그래도 그가 그것을 조금이라도 알은체하기까지는 한참이 걸렸고, 나도 그만큼 말을 꺼리며, 그에게 공손히 처신하되 어떤 종류의 사사로운 말도 결코 그에게 한마디 건네려 하지 않았다. 그리고 이것이 열엿새 주인가 열일곱 주 동안 이어졌으니, 그리하여 내가 죄도 없는 다른 일로 그들이 품은 못마땅함 탓에 날마다 집안에서 내쫓길 것을 예상했던 것처럼, 나는 이 신사에게서 그 모든 엄숙한 맹세와 서약에도 불구하고 더는 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 채, 그저 파멸하여 버림받으리라 예상했다.\n\n마침내 내가 떠나는 일에 관해 집안에서 내 입으로 길을 텄으니, 어느 날 노부인과 이 세상에서의 내 처지에 관해, 또 내 병이 어떻게 내 마음에 무거움을 남겨 내가 예전의 그 사람이 아니게 되었는지에 관해 진지하게 이야기를 나누던 중, 노부인이 말했다. \"베티야, 내가 내 아들에 관해 네게 한 말이 네게 어떤 영향을 미쳐, 네가 그 애 때문에 우울한 게 아닌가 걱정이로구나. 부디, 온당치 않은 일이 아니라면, 너희 둘 사이가 어떻게 되어 있는지 내게 알려 주겠니? 로빈으로 말하자면, 내가 그것에 관해 말을 꺼내면 그저 농을 치고 비아냥거리기만 하니 말이다.\" \"사실은, 마님,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"그 일은 제가 바라지 않는 쪽으로 되어 있어요. 그리고 그 일로 제게 무슨 일이 닥치든, 저는 마님께 그것을 매우 진실하게 말씀드리겠어요. 로버트 도련님이 여러 차례 제게 혼인을 청하셨는데, 그건 제 가련한 처지를 생각하면 제가 기대할 까닭이 전혀 없는 일이었어요. 하지만 저는 늘 그분께 맞섰고, 그것도 어쩌면, 마님의 가문 어느 분에게나 제가 마땅히 가져야 할 도리를 생각하면, 제게 어울리지 않을 만큼 단호한 말로 그리했어요. 하지만,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"마님, 저는 마님과 마님의 온 집안에 진 은혜를 그토록 저버려, 마님께 언짢은 일이 될 수밖에 없는 줄 아는 일에 동의하겠다고 나설 수는 도무지 없었어요. 그리고 이것을 저는 그분께 제 논거로 삼았으며, 제가 그토록 거역할 수 없는 숱한 은의로 매여 있는 마님과, 또 그분의 아버님의 동의를 얻지 못하는 한, 저는 결코 그런 종류의 생각을 품지 않겠노라고 그분께 단호히 말씀드렸어요.\"\n\n\"그게 정말이냐, 베티 아씨?\" 하고 노부인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 너는 우리가 네게 대했던 것보다 우리에게 훨씬 더 의로웠던 게로구나. 우리는 모두 너를 내 아들에게 놓인 일종의 덫처럼 여겼고, 나는 그것이 두려워 네게 떠나라는 제안을 할 생각이었거든. 하지만 아직 그것을 네게 말하지 않은 건, 네가 아직 온전히 낫지 않았다고 생각했고, 또 너를 너무 슬프게 하여 다시 쓰러뜨리지나 않을까 두려웠기 때문이다. 우리는 모두 여전히 너를 존중하니까, 그래도 그것이 내 아들의 파멸이 될 만큼은 아니지만 말이다. 하지만 네 말대로라면, 우리는 모두 네게 큰 잘못을 저지른 게로구나.\"\n\n\"제가 드린 말씀의 진실에 관해서라면, 마님,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"마님의 아드님께 직접 여쭤보시면 돼요. 그분이 제게 조금이라도 의로우시다면, 제가 말씀드린 그대로 그 이야기를 마님께 해 드릴 거예요.\"\n\n노부인은 자기 딸들에게로 가서, 내가 그녀에게 말한 그대로 그 이야기를 모두 들려주었다. 그러자 그들은, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 또 나도 그러리라 믿었다시피 그것에 놀랐다. 한 사람은 도무지 그런 줄은 생각도 못 했다고 했고, 또 한 사람은 로빈이 바보라고 했으며, 세 번째 딸은 한마디도 믿지 못하겠다고, 장담하건대 로빈은 그 이야기를 다른 식으로 할 거라고 했다. 그러나 내가 그녀의 아들에게 그동안 있었던 일을 알릴 조금의 기회라도 얻기 전에 그 일의 진상을 끝까지 캐기로 마음먹은 노부인은, 그녀의 아들과 당장 이야기하기로 또한 결심하고는, 그 일로 그를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그가 자기의 자질구레한 볼일로 고을의 한 변호사 집에 가 있을 뿐이었던 터라, 그녀가 사람을 보내자 그는 곧바로 돌아왔다.\n\n그가 그들에게로 올라오자—그들은 모두 여전히 함께 있었으니—\"앉아라, 로빈,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다, \"너와 이야기 좀 해야겠다.\" \"기꺼이 그러죠, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 자못 흥겨운 얼굴로 말했다. \"좋은 아내에 관한 이야기였으면 좋겠네요, 제가 그 일로 몹시 갈팡질팡하고 있거든요.\" \"그게 어찌 된 일이냐?\" 하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"베티 아씨를 차지하기로 결심했다고 하지 않았느냐?\" \"그랬죠, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"하지만 누군가가 결혼 예고를 막아 버렸어요.\" \"예고를 막다니!\" 하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"그게 누구냐?\" \"바로 베티 아씨 자신이에요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다. \"어째서?\" 하고 그의 어머니가 말했다. \"그럼 그 애에게 청을 했단 말이냐?\" \"예, 정말로요, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그 애가 앓고 난 뒤로 다섯 번이나 격식을 갖춰 들이댔지만 번번이 퇴짜를 맞았어요. 그 말괄량이가 어찌나 꿋꿋한지, 제가 실질적으로 들어줄 수 없는 조건이 아니고서는 어떤 조건으로도 항복도 굴복도 하지 않으려 해요.\" \"분명히 말해 보아라,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"놀랍구나. 네 말을 알아듣지 못하겠다. 네가 진심은 아니길 바란다.\"\n\n\"아니, 어머니,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"그 일은 제겐 충분히 분명해요, 저절로 분명해지죠. 그 애가 저를 안 받겠다고 하잖아요. 그거면 충분히 분명하지 않나요? 제 생각엔 분명한 데다, 꽤 매몰차기까지 해요.\" \"그래, 하지만,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 들어줄 수 없는 조건이라니 무슨 말이냐? 그 애가 뭘 원하는 거냐—재산 설정이냐? 그 애의 과부 재산이야 마땅히 그 애의 지참금에 따라야 할 텐데, 대체 그 애가 네게 무슨 재산을 가져온다는 거냐?\" \"아니, 재산으로 말하자면,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그 애는 충분히 부유해요. 그 점에 관해선 저는 만족해요. 하지만 그 애의 조건을 들어줄 수 없는 건 바로 저예요, 그리고 그 애는 그것 없이는 저를 받지 않겠다고 단호해요.\"\n\n여기서 누이들이 끼어들었다. \"어머니,\" 하고 둘째 딸이 말했다, \"오라버니와는 진지하게 이야기할 수가 없어요. 무엇에든 곧이곧대로 답하는 법이 없으니까요. 그냥 내버려 두고, 더는 그 이야기를 꺼내지 마세요. 거기에 뭔가 있다고 보신다면, 그 애를 오라버니의 길에서 치워 버리는 방법쯤은 아시잖아요.\" 로빈은 누이의 무례함에 조금 발끈했으나, 그래도 예의를 잃지 않으면서 그녀에게 맞받았다. \"어머니, 도무지 맞설 수 없는 부류가 둘 있어요,\" 하고 그가 어머니를 향해 돌아서며 말했다, \"곧 현명한 사람과 바보지요. 제가 그 둘을 한꺼번에 상대해야 하다니 좀 가혹하네요.\"\n\n그러자 작은딸이 끼어들었다. \"우리가 정말 바보임이 틀림없겠네요,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다, \"오라버니가 베티 아씨에게 혼인하자고 진지하게 청했고 그 애가 거절했다는 걸 우리가 믿으리라고 오라버니가 생각한다면 말이에요.\" \"답하라, 그러나 답하지 말라, 솔로몬이 이르셨지,\" 하고 그녀의 오라버니가 대꾸했다. \"네 오라버니가 어머니께 그 애에게 다섯 번이나 청했다고, 그리고 정말로 그 애가 단호히 자기를 거절했다고 말씀드렸을진대, 어머니께서도 그것을 의심하지 않으셨는데 동생이 그 진실을 의심할 까닭은 없을 것 같구나.\" \"어머니는, 보다시피, 그걸 이해하지 못하셨던 거예요,\" 하고 둘째 딸이 말했다. \"분명히 말해 달라고 청하시는 것과, 못 믿겠다고 말씀하시는 것 사이엔,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"얼마간 차이가 있지.\"\n\n\"그래, 하지만, 얘야,\" 하고 노부인이 말했다, \"네가 우리에게 그 수수께끼를 알려 줄 마음이 있다면, 그 까다로운 조건이라는 게 대체 무엇이었느냐?\" \"예, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"여기 이 약 올리는 사람들이 끼어들어 저를 들볶지만 않았어도 진작 그렇게 했을 거예요. 그 조건인즉, 제가 아버지와 어머니를 그 일에 동의하시게끔 모셔 오라는 것이고, 그게 없이는 그 애가 결코 다시는 그 일로 저를 만나지 않겠노라고 단언해요. 그리고 이 조건은, 말씀드렸듯이, 아무래도 제가 결코 들어줄 수 없을 것 같네요. 이제 제 성난 누이들이 답을 얻고 조금 부끄러워졌으면 좋겠네요. 만일 그렇지 않다면, 저는 더 들을 때까지 더는 할 말이 없어요.\"\n\n이 대답은 그들 모두에게 놀라운 것이었으나, 어머니에게는 덜 그러했으니, 내가 그녀에게 했던 말 때문이었다. 딸들로 말하자면, 그들은 한참을 말없이 서 있었다. 그러나 어머니는 얼마간 격해져 말했다. \"음, 나는 전에도 이 말을 들었으나 믿을 수가 없었다. 하지만 그게 사실이라면, 우리는 모두 베티에게 잘못을 저지른 게로구나, 그리고 그 애는 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 더 훌륭하게 처신했어.\" \"정말이지,\" 하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그게 사실이라면 그 애는 정말 훌륭하게 행동한 거예요.\" \"내 고백하건대,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"그 애가 그토록 어리석게 그 애에게 마음을 빼앗긴 게 그 애 잘못은 아니지. 하지만 그 애에게 그런 대답을 했다는 건, 네 아버지와 내게 내가 뭐라 표현할 수 없을 만큼 큰 도리를 보인 거다. 나는 그 애를 아는 한 그 일로 그 애를 더 귀히 여길 거야.\" \"하지만 저는 안 그럴 거예요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"어머니가 동의해 주시지 않는 한은요.\" \"그건 한동안 헤아려 보마,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"분명히 말하건대, 다른 걸림돌만 없다면, 그 애의 이런 처신이 나를 동의하게 만드는 데 큰 몫을 할 게다.\" \"그게 그것을 완전히 뚫고 나가 주었으면 좋겠네요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"저를 부유하게 만드는 일에 쓰시는 만큼의 생각을 저를 편안하게 만드는 일에 쓰신다면, 어머니는 곧 거기에 동의하실 거예요.\"\n\n\"그런데, 로빈,\" 하고 어머니가 다시 말했다, \"너 정말로 진심이냐? 네가 짐짓 그러는 것만큼 그토록 간절히 그 애를 원하는 게냐?\" \"정말로요, 어머니,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"제가 그토록 말씀을 드린 끝에 어머니가 그 점을 두고 저를 캐물으시다니 너무하신 것 같아요. 그 애를 차지하겠다고는 말하지 않겠어요. 보시다시피 어머니의 동의 없이는 그 애를 차지할 수 없는데, 어떻게 그 점을 결심할 수 있겠어요? 게다가 저는 꼭 혼인해야 할 의무도 없고요. 하지만 이것만은 진심으로 말하겠어요, 곧 어쩔 수만 있다면 저는 결코 다른 누구도 맞지 않겠다는 거예요. 그러니 어머니가 저를 위해 결정하시면 돼요. 베티 아니면 아무도 없다는 게 제 말이고, 둘 중 어느 쪽이냐 하는 문제는 어머니의 가슴속에서 결정하실 일이에요, 다만 여기 이 명랑한 제 누이들에게는 거기에 한 표도 없다는 조건으로요.\"\n\n이 모든 것이 내게는 끔찍하기만 했으니, 어머니가 마음이 기울기 시작했고, 로빈이 그 점을 두고 어머니를 몰아붙였기 때문이다. 한편 어머니는 맏아들과 의논했는데, 그는 어머니를 설득하기 위해 세상의 온갖 논거를 다 동원했으니, 동생이 나를 열렬히 사랑한다는 것이며, 내가 그토록 미묘한 명예의 문제를 두고 내 이익을 거절함으로써 그 집안에 보인 갸륵한 배려라는 것이며, 그 밖에도 수천 가지 그런 말을 늘어놓았다. 그리고 아버지로 말하자면, 그는 공무와 돈벌이에 분주하여 집에 좀처럼 붙어 있지 않았고, 큰 기회만을 골똘히 생각하는 사람이라, 그런 일은 모두 아내에게 맡겨 두었다.\n\n여러분도 쉽게 믿을 수 있겠지만, 음모가 이렇게 그들 생각에 들통이 나고, 모두가 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 안다고 여기게 되자, 아무도 의심하지 않던 맏아들로서는 전보다 더 자유롭게 내게 접근하는 것이 그리 어렵거나 위험한 일이 아니었다. 아니, 어머니가 그가 바라던 바로 그대로, 그에게 베티 양과 이야기해 보라고 제안하기까지 했다. \"얘야, 어쩌면 말이다,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다. \"네가 나보다 이 일을 더 깊이 들여다볼 수 있을지도 모르고, 저 애가 로빈 말마따나 정말 그렇게 단호했는지 아닌지 네가 한번 봐 다오.\" 이는 그가 바랄 수 있는 그대로였으니, 그는 마치 어머니의 청에 못 이겨 나와 이야기하는 척하며, 어머니가 나를 자기 방으로 데려가, 자기 아들이 자기 부탁으로 내게 볼일이 좀 있다고 하면서 그에게 아주 솔직하게 대하라 이르고는, 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔고, 그는 어머니가 나가자 가서 문을 닫았다.\n\n그는 내게로 돌아와 나를 두 팔로 안고 아주 다정하게 입을 맞추었으나, 내게 할 긴 이야기가 있다고, 그리고 일이 이제 그 갈림길에 이르러, 내가 살아 있는 한 스스로를 행복하게 만들 수도 비참하게 만들 수도 있는 지경이 되었다고 말했다. 일이 이제 너무 멀리 와 버려서, 만약 내가 그의 바람에 따르지 못한다면 우리 둘 다 파멸하리라는 것이었다. 그러고는 그가 로빈이라 부르는 동생과 어머니, 누이들, 그리고 자기 자신 사이에 오간 이야기를 앞서 적은 그대로 죄다 털어놓았다. \"그러니 사랑하는 아가야,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"좋은 가문에, 좋은 형편에, 온 집안의 동의를 얻어 한 신사와 결혼하여 세상이 네게 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 누리는 것이 어떤 것일지, 그리고 다른 한편으로 명예를 잃은 여자의 어두운 처지로 가라앉는 것이 어떤 것일지를 생각해 보아라. 나는 살아 있는 동안 네게 남몰래 벗이 되어 주겠지만, 늘 의심을 받을 터이니, 너는 나를 만나기를 두려워할 것이고, 나도 너를 인정하기를 두려워하게 될 것이다.\"\n\n그는 내게 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 이렇게 말을 이어 갔다. \"아가야, 우리 사이에 일어난 일은, 우리 둘 다 그렇게 하기로 합의하는 한, 묻어 두고 잊어버릴 수 있다. 네가 내 제수가 되면 나는 더 가까운 친밀함을 바라는 마음 없이 언제까지나 너의 진실한 벗이 되어 줄 것이며, 우리는 잘못을 저질렀다는 서로 간의 비난 없이 정직한 교제의 온갖 즐거움을 누리게 될 것이다. 부디 이를 생각해 보고, 네 자신의 안전과 번영을 가로막지 말기를 바란다. 그리고 내가 진심임을 네게 확인시켜 주고자,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다. \"내가 너와 누린 자유에 대해 얼마간 보상하는 뜻으로 여기 현금 500파운드를 네게 주겠다. 그 자유야 우리 삶의 어리석은 짓 가운데 하나로 여겨야 할 것이고, 우리가 그 일을 뉘우치게 되기를 바라야 할 일이다.\"\n\n그는 이 말을 내가 도저히 표현할 수 없을 만큼 한결 더 마음을 움직이는 어조로, 또 내가 되풀이할 수 없을 만큼 한층 더 강력한 논거를 곁들여 했으므로, 나는 다만 이 이야기를 읽는 이들에게 이렇게 짐작해 주기를 권할 뿐이다. 곧 그가 한 시간 반이 넘도록 나를 붙들고 이런 이야기를 하는 동안, 그가 내 온갖 반론에 일일이 답하고, 인간의 재치와 기교가 고안해 낼 수 있는 온갖 논거로 자기 이야기를 단단히 다졌다고 말이다.\n\n그러나 그가 한 말 가운데 어느 것도 내게 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각하게 할 만큼 깊은 인상을 주지는 못했다고 하겠으니, 다만 마지막에 그가 아주 솔직하게, 만약 내가 거절한다면 안타깝지만 우리가 전처럼 그런 처지로는 더는 함께 지낼 수 없노라고 덧붙이기 전까지는 그러했다. 그는 여전히 변함없이 나를 사랑하고, 내가 여전히 변함없이 그의 마음에 든다 해도, 덕에 대한 분별이 아직 그에게서 그토록 멀리 떠나가지는 않았으므로, 자기 동생이 아내로 삼으려 구애하는 여자와 동침하는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없다는 것이었다. 또 이 일에서 거절을 받고 내게 작별을 고하게 된다면, 나를 부양하겠다던 처음의 약속에 근거하여 생계의 측면에서 나를 위해 무엇을 해 주든 간에, 자기로서는 더 이상 나를 만날 수 없노라고 어쩔 수 없이 말해야만 한다는 점에 내가 놀라지 않기를 바란다고, 그리고 사실 나로서도 그에게 그 이상을 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다.\n\n나는 이 마지막 대목을 어느 정도 놀라움과 동요의 기색을 띤 채 받아들였고, 주저앉지 않으려 무던히 애를 써야 했으니, 사실 나는 상상하기 쉽지 않을 만큼 터무니없이 그를 사랑하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 내 동요를 알아챘다. 그는 내게 이를 진지하게 생각해 보라고 간청하며, 이것이야말로 우리의 서로에 대한 애정을 지킬 수 있는 유일한 길이라고, 이런 처지에서 우리는 더없이 뜨거운 열정으로, 그러면서도 정당한 비난에서 자유롭고 다른 사람들의 의심에서도 자유로운, 더럽혀지지 않은 친족의 사랑으로 벗처럼 사랑할 수 있을 것이라고 장담했다. 또 자기는 자신의 행복이 내 덕분임을 언제까지나 인정할 것이며, 살아 있는 한 내게 빚진 자가 되어 숨이 붙어 있는 한 그 빚을 갚아 나가겠노라고 했다. 한마디로 이렇게 그는 나를 그 문제에 대해 일종의 망설임에 이르도록 몰아갔으니, 한편으로는 위험을 생생한 모습으로 그려 보였고, 실은 내 상상 속에서, 넓은 세상으로 내쳐져 한낱 버림받은 창녀가—다름 아닌 바로 그것이었으니—될 것이며, 어쩌면 그런 처지로 폭로되어, 나 자신을 부양할 변변한 것도 없이, 그 고을 밖에는 온 세상에 벗 하나, 아는 이 하나 없는데, 그 고을에 머무는 척할 수도 없는 신세가 되리라는 생각으로 한층 부풀려졌다. 이 모든 것이 나를 극도로 겁에 질리게 했고, 그는 기회 있을 때마다 그것을 그릴 수 있는 가장 음울한 빛깔로 내게 절절히 들이대기를 잊지 않았다. 다른 한편으로 그는 내가 살아가게 될 편안하고 번영하는 삶을 펼쳐 보이는 데에도 소홀하지 않았다.\n\n내가 애정에서, 또 지난날의 약속에서 끌어와 내세울 수 있는 모든 것에 대해, 그는 이제 우리 앞에 다른 방도를 취할 수밖에 없는 절박함이 놓여 있다고 말하며 답했다. 그리고 결혼하겠다던 자기 약속으로 말하자면, 일의 본성상 그것은 끝이 났다고, 그의 약속이 모두 가리키던 그 시점이 오기도 전에 내가 그의 동생의 아내가 될 개연성이 생겼기 때문이라고 했다. 이렇게 하여, 한마디로 그는 나를 내 이성에서 끌어내 설득해 버렸다고 하겠으니, 그는 내 모든 논거를 정복했고, 나는 전에는 생각지 못했던 위험을 보기 시작했으니, 그것은 곧 두 사람 모두에게 버림받아 세상에 홀로 남겨져 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 하는 위험이었다.\n\n이것이, 그리고 그의 설득이 마침내 나로 하여금 승낙하게 만들었으니, 다만 어찌나 마지못해 한 일인지 내가 마치 곰이 말뚝으로 끌려가듯 교회로 가게 되리라는 것은 누구라도 쉽게 알 수 있었다. 내게는 또 작은 걱정이 하나 있었으니, 그건 그렇고 내가 조금도 애정을 품지 않았던 내 새 신랑이 우리가 처음으로 함께 잠자리에 들 때 또 다른 명목으로 나를 따져 물을 만큼 능란하지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 그러나 그가 의도적으로 그랬는지 아닌지는 모르겠으나, 그의 형이 그를 잠자리에 들기 전에 몹시 취하게 만들도록 신경을 써 둔 덕분에, 나는 첫날밤에 술 취한 잠자리 동무라는 만족을 누렸다. 그가 어떻게 그리했는지는 모르겠으나, 나는 그가 분명 그렇게 꾸민 것이라고, 그래서 동생이 처녀와 결혼한 여자의 차이를 도무지 판단할 수 없게 한 것이라고 결론지었으며, 실제로 그는 그것에 대해 어떤 관념도 품지 않았고, 그 일로 마음을 어지럽히지도 않았다.\n\n여기서 내가 멈췄던 데로 조금 거슬러 올라가야겠다. 맏아들은 이렇게 나를 다루고 나서, 다음으로 할 일은 자기 어머니를 다루는 것이었으니, 그는 어머니가 그 일에 묵묵히 수긍하고 잠자코 따르게 만들기까지, 그것도 아버지에게는 우편으로 편지를 보내는 것 말고는 알리지도 않은 채 그리되게 하기까지 손을 놓지 않았다. 그리하여 어머니는 우리가 남몰래 결혼하고 아버지는 나중에 자기가 알아서 구슬리도록 맡기는 데에 동의했다.\n\n그러고 나서 그는 동생을 구슬려, 자기가 그에게 얼마나 큰 도움을 주었는지, 어떻게 어머니가 동의하도록 만들었는지를 설득했으니, 그것은 사실이긴 했으나 실은 동생을 위해 한 일이 아니라 자기 자신을 위해 한 일이었다. 이렇게 그는 부지런히 동생을 속였고, 자기 창녀를 동생의 품에 아내로 떠넘겨 놓고는 충실한 벗이라는 감사 인사까지 받았다. 이처럼 이해관계란 어김없이 온갖 종류의 애정을 몰아내며, 사람이란 자기 안위를 지키기 위해 명예와 정의, 인간애, 심지어 기독교 신앙마저 이토록 자연스레 내던지는 법이다.\n\n이제 나는 우리가 늘 부르던 대로 로빈 도련님에게로 돌아가야겠는데, 그는 앞서 말한 대로 어머니의 동의를 얻고 나서 그 소식으로 잔뜩 부푼 채 내게 와, 그 사연을 죄다 들려주었으니, 그 진심이 너무도 또렷이 드러나 보여서, 내가 이토록 정직한 신사를 농락하는 도구가 되어야 한다는 것이 마음 아팠음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다. 그러나 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그는 나를 아내로 삼으려 했고, 비록 그를 떨쳐 낼 다른 방도가 없었다 해도, 내가 그의 형의 창녀라고 그에게 말해야 할 의무는 없었으니, 그리하여 나는 그를 흡족하게 하며 차차 그 일에 응했고, 그렇게 우리는 결혼하게 되었다.\n\n정숙함이 결혼 침상의 비밀을 드러내는 것을 금하나, 앞서 말한 대로, 내 남편이 잠자리에 들 때 어찌나 취해 있었던지 아침에 나와 무슨 일이 있었는지 없었는지조차 기억하지 못해, 내가 실은 없었던 일을 두고 있었다고 그에게 말할 수밖에 없었던 것보다 내 처지에 더 알맞게 일어날 수 있었던 일은 없었을 것이니, 그래야만 그가 다른 무엇에 대해서도 캐묻지 않으리라고 확신할 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 남편과 함께 산 다섯 해 동안의 그 집안이나 나 자신에 관한 더 자세한 내막에 들어가는 것은 지금 다루는 이야기와는 거의 상관이 없으니, 다만 내가 그에게서 아이 둘을 낳았다는 것, 그리고 다섯 해가 끝날 무렵 그가 죽었다는 것만 짚어 두면 되겠다. 그는 정말로 내게 매우 좋은 남편이었고, 우리는 아주 사이좋게 함께 살았으나, 그가 집안에서 많은 것을 받지도 못했고, 그 살아 있던 짧은 동안에 이렇다 할 것을 별로 모으지도 못했던 터라, 내 형편도 대단치 않았고, 이 혼인으로 내 처지가 크게 나아지지도 않았다. 다만 나는 그 동생과 결혼하는 데 동의해 준 대가로 맏아들이 내게 주겠다 한 500파운드의 차용증서를 간직해 두었던지라, 이것에다 그가 전에 내게 준 돈에서 모아 둔 것, 그리고 남편에게서 그만큼 더해진 것을 합쳐, 나는 주머니에 약 1200파운드를 지닌 과부로 남게 되었다.\n\n내 두 아이는 정말 다행스럽게도 남편의 부모가 떠맡아 주었으니, 그건 그렇고 그것이 그들이 베티 양에게서 얻은 전부였다.\n\n고백하건대 나는 남편을 잃은 것에 대해 마땅한 만큼 슬퍼하지 못했으며, 실은 내가 마땅히 사랑했어야 할 만큼, 혹은 그에게서 받은 좋은 대접에 걸맞을 만큼 그를 사랑한 적이 있었다고도 말할 수 없으니, 그는 어떤 여자라도 바랄 만한 다정하고 친절하며 쾌활한 남자였기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 형이 적어도 우리가 시골에 있는 동안에는 늘 내 눈앞에 있었던 것이 내게는 끊임없는 올가미였으니, 나는 남편과 잠자리에 들 때마다 그의 형의 품에 안기기를 바라곤 했다. 그리고 그의 형은 우리가 결혼한 뒤로는 그런 식의 친절을 조금도 내게 베푼 적이 없고, 꼭 시아주버니가 마땅히 처신해야 할 그대로 행동했으나, 나로서는 그에게 그렇게 처신하기란 도무지 불가능했다. 한마디로 나는 날마다 욕망 속에서 그와 간통과 근친상간을 저질렀으니, 의심할 바 없이 그것은 죄의 본질에 있어서 내가 실제로 그 짓을 한 것이나 진배없이 범죄였다.\n\n남편이 죽기 전에 그의 맏형이 결혼했고, 그때 우리는 런던으로 옮겨 간 터라, 노부인이 와서 결혼식에 참석하라는 편지를 보내 왔다. 남편은 갔으나, 나는 몸이 편치 않아 도저히 여행을 할 수 없다는 핑계를 대고 남았으니, 한마디로 나는 그가 다른 여자에게 넘겨지는 광경을 차마 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다, 비록 내가 그를 결코 차지할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었지만 말이다.\n\n이제 나는 앞서 말한 대로 세상에 풀려나 자유로운 몸이 되었고, 모두가 나를 두고 그렇게 말했듯이 여전히 젊고 어여뻤으며, 단언컨대 나 스스로도 그렇게 여겼고, 주머니에 그런대로 괜찮은 재산을 지니고 있었던 터라, 나 자신을 적잖이 높이 쳤다. 나는 꽤 상당한 상인 여럿에게서 구애를 받았는데, 특히 어느 포목상에게서 매우 열렬한 구애를 받았으니, 남편이 죽은 뒤 나는 그의 누이가 내 아는 사람인지라 그의 집에 하숙을 들었던 것이다.\n여기서 나는 명랑하게 지내며 사람들 사이에 어울려 나설 온갖 자유와 온갖 기회를 마음껏 누렸으니, 내 집주인의 누이가 세상에서 가장 분방하고 가장 명랑한 사람 가운데 하나였고, 처음에 내가 생각했던 만큼 자기 정조의 주인은 아니었기 때문이다. 그녀는 나를 거친 무리의 세계로 끌어들였고, 심지어 자기가 비위를 맞추어 줄 만큼 마음에 드는 여러 사람을 집으로 데려와 자기의 예쁜 과부를—그녀는 나를 그렇게 부르기를 즐겼다—구경시켰으니, 그 이름이 머지않아 사람들 사이에 내게 따라붙었다. 자, 명성과 바보들이 모여 한 자리를 이루는 법이라, 나는 여기서 굉장히 떠받들렸고, 흠모하는 자들이, 또 스스로 연인이라 일컫는 자들이 넘쳐 났다. 그러나 그들 모두 가운데 점잖은 청혼은 하나도 찾아볼 수 없었다. 그들의 흔해 빠진 속셈으로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있던 터라 다시는 그런 부류의 올가미에 끌려들지 않았다. 내 처지는 달라져 있었으니, 나는 주머니에 돈이 있었고, 그들에게 할 말이 아무것도 없었다. 나는 사랑이라 불리는 그 속임수에 한 번 속았으나, 그 노름은 이제 끝났다. 나는 이제 결혼이 아니면 아무것도 아니요, 잘 결혼하든가 아니면 아예 안 하든가 하기로 작정했다.\n\n나는 실로 흥과 재치가 넘치는 남자들, 풍채 좋고 멋진 남자들과 어울리기를 좋아했고, 다른 이들과도 그러했듯 그런 이들과도 자주 즐겼으나, 정확한 관찰을 통해 알게 된 바는, 가장 빛나는 남자들이 가장 시시한 용건을 들고 온다는 것이었으니—즉 내가 노리는 바에 관해서는 가장 시시했다는 말이다. 다른 한편으로 가장 그럴듯한 청혼을 들고 온 자들은 세상에서 가장 따분하고 가장 마음에 안 드는 부류였다. 나는 상인을 마다하지는 않았으나, 그러면서도 제발이지 무언가 신사다운 데가 있는 상인을 원했다. 즉 남편이 나를 궁정이나 연극 구경에 데려가고 싶을 때 칼을 차도 어색하지 않고 다른 어떤 남자 못지않게 신사처럼 보이는 사람, 외투에 앞치마 끈 자국이 남았다거나 가발에 모자 자국이 났다거나 하지 않은 사람, 칼이 그에게 채워진 것이 아니라 그가 칼에 매여 끌려가는 듯이 보이지 않는 사람, 얼굴에 자기 장사를 써 붙이고 다니지 않는 사람을 원했던 것이다.\n\n자, 마침내 나는 이 양서류 같은 존재, 신사 상인이라 불리는 이 뭍에도 물에도 사는 것을 찾아냈다. 그리고 내 어리석음에 대한 마땅한 벌로, 나는, 이를테면 내가 나 자신을 위해 놓아 둔 바로 그 올가미에 걸려들고 말았다. 나 자신을 위해서라고 말한 까닭은, 고백하건대 나는 함정에 빠진 것이 아니라 나 자신을 배반한 것이기 때문이다.\n\n이 사람도 포목상이었으니, 내 동료가 자기 오라비와 흥정을 붙여 주려 했으나, 막상 본론에 이르자, 보아하니 그것은 아내가 아니라 정부情婦로 삼겠다는 것이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 자기를 건사할 돈이 있는 여자라면 결코 정부로 살림을 차려져서는 안 된다는 이 신념을 굳게 지켰다.\n\n이렇게 나의 원칙이 아니라 자존심이, 나의 덕이 아니라 돈이 나를 정숙하게 지켜 주었다. 다만 결국 드러난 바로는, 내 여자 동료에게 그녀의 오라비에게 팔려 가는 편이, 난봉꾼이요 신사요 가게 주인이요 거지인 것을 한데 합쳐 놓은 그런 상인에게 내 손으로 나 자신을 팔아넘기는 것보다 차라리 훨씬 나았으리라는 것을 나는 알게 되었다.\n\n그러나 나는 (신사에 대한 환상에 사로잡혀) 일찍이 어떤 여자도 겪지 못한 가장 천박한 방식으로 나 자신을 망치는 길로 떠밀려 갔으니, 내 새 남편은 단번에 목돈을 손에 넣자 어찌나 흥청망청 돈을 써 댔던지, 내가 가진 모든 것과 그가 전에 가졌던 모든 것이—그에게 입에 올릴 만한 것이 있었다 한들—일 년도 버텨 내지 못할 지경이었다.\n\n그는 한 석 달 동안은 나를 무척 좋아했고, 내가 그 덕에 얻은 것이라곤, 내 돈이 나 자신에게 많이 쓰이는 것을 보는 즐거움과, 또 말하자면 그 돈을 쓰는 데 나도 얼마간 한몫 거든 것이었다. \"자, 여보,\" 하루는 그가 내게 말했다. \"한 일주일쯤 시골로 한 바퀴 다녀올까?\" \"그래요, 여보,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"어디로 가시려고요?\" \"어디든 상관없소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"하지만 한 일주일쯤 귀족 행세를 해 보고 싶구려. 옥스퍼드로 갑시다,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"어떻게요,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"어떻게 가지요? 나는 말을 탈 줄도 모르고, 마차로 가기엔 너무 멀어요.\" \"너무 멀다니!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"여섯 마리 끄는 마차에 너무 먼 곳이란 없소. 내가 당신을 모시고 나가면, 당신은 공작부인처럼 여행하게 될 거요.\" \"흠,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"여보, 그건 한바탕 장난이지만, 당신이 그러고 싶다면 나는 상관없어요.\" 자, 날이 정해지고, 우리에게는 호화로운 마차와 아주 좋은 말들, 마부 하나, 말몰이꾼 하나, 그리고 아주 좋은 제복을 입은 하인 둘이 있었으며, 말을 탄 신사 하나와 또 다른 말에 모자에 깃털을 꽂은 시동 하나가 딸려 있었다. 하인들은 모두 그를 나리라 불렀고, 여관 주인들도, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 똑같이 그리했으며, 나는 마님이신 백작부인이었으니, 이렇게 우리는 옥스퍼드로 여행했고, 참으로 즐거운 여정이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 살아 있는 어떤 거지도 내 남편보다 나리 노릇을 더 잘 알지는 못했기 때문이다. 우리는 옥스퍼드의 진귀한 것을 죄다 구경했고, 나리의 보살핌에 맡겨진 어린 조카를 대학에 넣는 일과 그들이 그 아이의 가정교사가 되는 일에 대해 두세 명의 대학 특별연구원들과 이야기를 나누었다. 우리는 또 적어도 나리의 전속 목사가 되어 띠를 두르게 되기를 바라는 여러 가난한 학자들을 놀려 먹으며 재미를 보았다. 이렇게 씀씀이에 관한 한 정말로 귀족처럼 살고 나서, 우리는 노샘프턴으로 떠났고, 한마디로 열이틀쯤 떠돌다가 약 93파운드의 비용을 치르고서 다시 집으로 돌아왔다.\n\n허영은 멋쟁이의 완성이다. 내 남편은 이 탁월함을 지니고 있었으니, 비용 따위는 아무것도 대수롭게 여기지 않았다. 그리고 그의 이력이란, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 거기에 담긴 무게가 매우 가벼우니, 약 두 해 하고도 석 달 만에 그가 파산했다는 것, 그리고 운 좋게 민트로 피해 들어가지도 못하고, 보석으로 풀려나기에는 너무 무거운 소송으로 체포되어 임시 구류소에 갇히는 신세가 되었고, 그래서 내게 사람을 보내 자기에게 오라고 했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다.\n\n내게는 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 모든 것이 파탄으로 치닫고 있음을 한동안 전부터 예견하고 있었고, 많지는 않았으나 나 자신을 위해 할 수만 있다면 무언가 남겨 두려고 신경을 써 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 나를 부르자, 그는 내가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 점잖게 처신하며, 자기가 바보짓을 했다고, 막을 수도 있었을 텐데 방심하다 봉변을 당했다고 솔직하게 말했다. 이제 자기로서는 도저히 버텨 낼 수 없음을 내다보았으니, 그러므로 나더러 집으로 가서 밤중에 집 안에 있는 값나가는 것은 무엇이든 다 빼내어 안전한 곳에 챙겨 두라고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는, 만약 내가 가게에서 물품으로 백 파운드든 이백 파운드든 빼낼 수 있다면 그리하라고 하면서, \"다만,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 무엇을 가져가는지, 어디로 가져가는지, 내게는 아무것도 알리지 마시오. 나로 말하자면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 집에서 빠져나가 떠나기로 작정했으니 말이오. 그리고 당신이 다시는 내 소식을 듣지 못하더라도, 여보,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 당신이 잘되기를 비오. 다만 내가 당신에게 끼친 해가 안타까울 뿐이오.\" 그는 헤어질 때 정말로 내게 아주 멋진 말을 몇 마디 해 주었으니, 앞서 말했듯이 그는 신사였고, 그가 신사라는 데서 내가 얻은 이로움이란 그것이 전부였다. 즉 그는 마지막까지도 모든 경우에 나를 아주 멋지고 예의 바르게 대해 주었다는 것, 다만 내가 가진 모든 것을 다 써 버리고, 나로 하여금 입에 풀칠할 무언가를 위해 채권자들의 것을 훔치도록 남겨 두었다는 것뿐이다.\n\n그래도 나는, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 그가 시킨 대로 했다. 이렇게 그에게 작별을 고하고 나서, 나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했으니, 그가 그날 밤인가 그다음 날 밤에 집달리의 집에서 빠져나가 프랑스로 건너갈 방도를 찾아냈고, 나머지 채권자들로 말하자면 저마다 할 수 있는 대로 다투어 챙겼기 때문이다. 어떻게 된 일인지 나는 알 수 없었으니, 이것 말고는 아무것도 알아낼 수 없었기 때문이다. 곧 그가 새벽 세 시쯤 집에 돌아와 나머지 물품을 민트로 옮기게 하고 가게를 닫아걸게 했으며, 끌어모을 수 있는 돈을 다 마련하여, 앞서 말했듯이 프랑스로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 그에게서 편지를 한두 통 받았을 뿐 그 이상은 없었다는 것이다. 나는 그가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그를 보지 못했으니, 그가 내게 앞서 말한 대로 지시를 해 두었고, 나도 내 시간을 최대한 활용한 터라, 다시 집으로 돌아갈 볼일이 더는 없었던 데다, 채권자들에게 거기서 붙들릴지도 모를 일이었기 때문이다. 머지않아 파산 명령이 내려졌으니, 그들이 위원들의 명령으로 나를 붙들 수도 있었던 것이다. 그러나 내 남편은 어찌나 날렵하게 집달리의 집에서 빠져나왔던지, 거의 집 꼭대기에서 다른 건물 꼭대기로 더없이 필사적인 방식으로 몸을 내려뜨린 다음 거기서 뛰어내렸는데, 그것은 거의 두 층 높이여서 실로 목이 부러지고도 남을 정도였으나, 그는 집으로 돌아와 채권자들이 압류하러 오기 전에—즉 그들이 파산 명령을 받아 내어 관리들을 보내 점유할 채비를 갖추기 전에—자기 물품을 빼돌렸다.\n\n내 남편은 내게 어찌나 정중했던지—여전히 말하건대 그는 신사다운 데가 많았으니—프랑스에서 내게 처음 쓴 편지에서, 자기가 30파운드에 고운 홀란드 천 스무 필을 잡혔는데 그것이 실은 90파운드짜리였다고 알려 주고는, 그 돈을 치르고 찾아오라는 전당표와 지시서를 동봉해 주었다. 나는 그대로 했고, 시간을 두고 그것들을 잘라 기회가 닿는 대로 개인 가정에 조금씩 조금씩 팔아, 끝내 100파운드가 넘게 거기서 벌었다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것에다, 또 내가 전에 챙겨 둔 모든 것을 다 합쳐도, 셈을 따져 보니 내 처지가 매우 달라졌고 내 재산이 크게 줄었음을 알게 되었다. 홀란드 천과 내가 전에 빼낸 고운 모슬린 한 보따리, 그리고 약간의 은그릇과 다른 물건들까지 다 합쳐도, 나는 도무지 500파운드도 그러모으기 어려웠기 때문이다. 그리고 내 처지는 매우 묘했으니, 아이는 없었으나(내 신사 포목상에게서 하나 낳았으나 묻어 주었다), 나는 마법에 걸린 과부와도 같았다. 남편이 있으되 남편이 없는 셈이었고, 다시 결혼하는 척할 수도 없었으니, 내 남편이 쉰 해를 더 산다 해도 잉글랜드를 두 번 다시는 보지 못하리라는 것을 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러니 이렇게, 어떤 청혼이 내게 들어오든 간에, 나는 결혼이 막혀 있는 처지였다. 그리고 내가 처한 형편에서 의논할 벗이 하나도 없었으니, 적어도 내 처지의 비밀을 감히 털어놓을 만한 이는 하나도 없었다. 만약 위원들에게 내가 있는 곳이 알려진다면, 나는 끌려가 선서를 하고 심문을 받아 내가 챙겨 둔 모든 것을 다 빼앗기게 될 터였기 때문이다.\n\n이런 우려에서, 내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 나를 아는 곳에서 완전히 벗어나 다른 이름으로 행세하는 것이었다. 나는 이를 확실히 해냈으니, 나 역시 민트로 들어가 아주 으슥한 곳에 하숙을 정하고, 과부 차림으로 꾸미고는 나 자신을 플랜더스 부인이라 일컬었다.\n\n그러나 여기서 나는 나 자신을 감추었고, 새로 사귄 이들이 나에 대해 아무것도 몰랐는데도, 머지않아 내 주위에 많은 사람이 모여들었다. 그곳에 흔히 모여드는 부류의 사람들 가운데 여자가 드물어서인지, 아니면 다른 경우보다 그곳의 비참함 속에서 어떤 위안이 더 절실히 필요해서인지, 나는 곧 마음에 드는 여자가 그곳의 고난받는 자들 사이에서 더없이 값진 존재라는 것을, 그리고 채권자들에게 1파운드당 반 크라운을 갚을 돈도 없는 자들, 황소 간판 술집에서 외상으로 끼니를 때우며 빚을 지는 자들조차도, 여자가 마음에 들면 저녁 한 끼 살 돈은 그래도 찾아내곤 한다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그러나 나는 아직 나 자신을 안전하게 지켰으니, 다만 마치 로체스터 경의 정부처럼, 그의 곁에 있기는 좋아하면서도 그 이상은 허락하지 않아, 즐거움은 없이 창녀라는 추문만 떠안게 되는 꼴이 되어 가기 시작했다. 그래서 이런 사정으로, 그곳에도, 실은 그 무리에게도 신물이 난 나는 자리를 옮길 생각을 하기 시작했다.\n\n복잡하게 얽힌 처지에 짓눌린 사람들, 파멸을 몇 단계 넘어선 지경으로 떨어진 사람들, 그 식솔들이 그들 자신에게는 공포의 대상이요 다른 사람들에게는 적선의 대상이 되어 버린 사람들이, 그러면서도 동전 한 푼이라도 남아 있는 한, 아니 그것마저 떨어진 뒤에도, 자기 자신을 술에 빠뜨리려 애쓰고, 이제야말로 기억해야 마땅할 지난 일들을 잊으려 기를 쓰며, 회개거리를 더 늘리고, 지난 죄에 대한 처방이랍시고 계속 죄를 지어 나가는 것을 보는 일은, 내게 참으로 기묘한 성찰의 거리였다.\n\n그러나 설교는 내 재주가 못 된다. 이 사람들은 나 같은 자가 보기에도 너무 사악했다. 그들이 죄짓는 방식에는 어딘가 끔찍하고 어처구니없는 데가 있었으니, 그것이 죄다 그들 자신에게마저 억지로 가하는 것이었기 때문이다. 그들은 양심에 거슬러 행동했을 뿐 아니라 본성에도 거슬러 행동했으니, 자기들 처지가 끊임없이 안겨 주는 그 성찰을 익사시키려고 자기 기질을 강간하다시피 했던 것이다. 그리고 그들이 억지로 지어 보이는 미소에도 불구하고, 한숨이 어떻게 그들의 노래를 가로막고, 창백함과 고뇌가 어떻게 그들의 이마에 들어앉는지를 보기란 더없이 쉬운 일이었다. 아니, 때로는 음란한 향연이나 사악한 포옹을 위해 돈을 다 써 버린 다음 바로 그들의 입에서 그것이 터져 나오기도 했다. 나는 그들이 돌아서며 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는, \"나는 어찌 이리 개 같은 놈인가! 그래, 베티, 여보, 그래도 당신의 건강을 위해 한잔하리다.\" 하고 외치는 소리를 들은 적이 있으니, 이는 어쩌면 자기 자신과 서너 명의 아이들을 위해 반 크라운조차 없을지 모를 그 정직한 아내를 두고 하는 말이었다. 이튿날 아침이면 그들은 다시 참회에 잠긴다. 그리고 어쩌면 가엾은 울고 있는 아내가 그에게 건너와, 채권자들이 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지, 자기와 아이들이 어떻게 문밖으로 쫓겨났는지에 대한 소식이나 그 밖의 어떤 끔찍한 소식을 전한다. 그러면 이것이 그의 자책에 더해진다. 그러나 그가 거의 미칠 지경이 되도록 그것을 곰곰이 생각하고 골똘히 들여다본 끝에, 자기를 지탱해 줄 아무런 원칙도 없고, 자기를 위로해 줄 무엇도 안에든 위에든 없으며, 다만 사방이 온통 어둠뿐임을 깨닫고는, 그는 다시 똑같은 위안으로 달아난다. 즉 술로 그것을 잊어버리고, 방탕으로 그것을 떨쳐 내려 하다가, 꼭 자기와 똑같은 처지에 있는 사람들의 무리에 끼어들어 그 죄를 되풀이하니, 이렇게 그는 날마다 파멸로 가는 길을 한 걸음씩 더 나아간다.\n\n나는 아직 이런 작자들만큼 사악하지는 않았다. 오히려 나는 여기서 내가 무엇을 해야 할지, 내 형편이 어떠한지, 어떤 방도를 취해야 할지를 매우 진지하게 따져 보기 시작했다. 나는 벗이 없다는 것을, 아니, 온 세상에 벗이나 친척이 단 하나도 없다는 것을 알고 있었고, 내게 남은 그 얼마 안 되는 것마저 눈에 띄게 줄어들고 있어서, 그것이 다 떨어지고 나면 내 앞에는 비참함과 굶주림밖에 없으리라는 것을 알았다. 이런 생각들에서, 또 내가 있는 그곳과 늘 내 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 광경들에 진저리를 치며, 나는 떠나기로 결심했다.\n\n나는 매우 진중하고 좋은 부류의 여자 한 사람과 친분을 맺었는데, 그녀 또한 나처럼 과부였으나 더 나은 형편에 있었다. 그녀의 남편은 상선의 선장이었는데, 서인도제도에서 항해를 마치고 돌아오던 길에 난파하는 불행을 겪었으니, 만약 무사히 돌아왔더라면 매우 수지맞는 항해였을 그것이, 그 손실로 그를 어찌나 영락하게 만들었던지, 그때 목숨은 건졌으나 그것이 그의 가슴을 무너뜨려 끝내 그를 죽음에 이르게 했다. 그리고 그의 미망인은 채권자들에게 쫓겨 민트로 피신할 수밖에 없었다. 그녀는 곧 벗들의 도움으로 일을 수습하여 다시 자유의 몸이 되었고, 내가 어떤 특정한 소송 때문이라기보다는 오히려 몸을 숨기려고 그곳에 있다는 것을 알고, 또 내가 그곳과 그 무리에 대한 마땅한 혐오에서 자기와 뜻이 맞는다는 것을, 아니 오히려 그녀가 나와 뜻이 맞는다는 것을 알고는, 내가 세상에서 내 마음에 들게 자리 잡을 어떤 채비를 갖출 수 있을 때까지 자기 집에 함께 가 지내자고 청했다. 게다가 그녀가 사는 그 동네에서라면 십중팔구 어떤 훌륭한 선장이 나를 마음에 들어 하여 구애할 것이라고도 말했다.\n\n나는 그녀의 제안을 받아들여 반년 동안 그녀와 함께 지냈고, 더 오래 있었을 테지만, 그 사이에 그녀가 내게 제안했던 일이 그녀 자신에게 일어나, 그녀가 자기에게 매우 유리하게 결혼했다. 그러나 누구의 운수가 오르고 있든 간에, 내 운수는 기우는 듯했으니, 나는 갑판장 두세 명이나 그 비슷한 작자들 말고는 당장 아무것도 찾지 못했다. 그러나 선장들로 말하자면, 그들은 대체로 두 부류였으니, 첫째, 좋은 일자리, 즉 좋은 배를 가진 자들로서, 이익을 보고서야, 즉 좋은 재산을 보고서야 결혼하기로 작정한 자들이었고, 둘째, 일자리가 없어서 배를 구하는 데 도움이 될 아내를 원하는 자들이었다. 내 말은 (첫째) 얼마간 돈이 있어서, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면, 그들 자신이 배의 상당한 지분을 보유하게 해 주어, 그리하여 선주들이 끼어들도록 부추길 수 있는 아내, 혹은 (둘째) 돈은 없더라도 해운에 관여하는 벗들이 있어서, 그 젊은이를 좋은 배에 넣는 데 도움을 줄 수 있는 아내를 원했다는 것이니, 그들에게는 그것이 지참금이나 다름없는 것이었다. 그런데 이 어느 쪽도 내 경우는 아니었으니, 나는 처치 곤란하게 묵혀질 처지의 사람처럼 보였다.\n\n나는 곧 경험으로 이 사실을 깨달았으니, 곧 혼인에 관해서는 사정이 달라졌다는 것, 그리고 내가 런던에서는 시골에서 발견했던 것을 기대할 수 없다는 것이었다. 즉 이곳에서 결혼이란 이해관계를 맺고 사업을 꾸려 나가기 위한 정략적 책략의 결과물이며, 사랑은 그 문제에 아무런 몫도, 혹은 아주 미미한 몫밖에 차지하지 못한다는 것이었다.\n\n콜체스터의 내 시누이가 말했던 대로, 미모, 재치, 예절, 분별, 좋은 기질, 좋은 처신, 교양, 덕, 신앙심, 혹은 몸이든 마음이든 그 어떤 다른 자질도 사람을 천거할 힘이 없으며, 오로지 돈만이 여자를 마음에 들게 만든다는 것이었다. 남자들은 정부를 고를 때는 정말로 애정의 입맛에 따랐으니, 창녀에게는 어여쁘고 몸매가 좋으며 좋은 자태와 우아한 처신을 갖추는 것이 요긴했으나, 아내로 말하자면, 어떤 흉한 외모도 그 욕망을 거스르지 않고, 어떤 나쁜 자질도 그 판단을 거스르지 않으니, 돈이 곧 관건이었다. 지참금은 굽지도 흉측하지도 않았고, 아내가 어떠하든 간에 돈만은 언제나 마음에 드는 법이었다.\n\n다른 한편으로, 시장이 매우 불행하게도 남자들 쪽으로 기울어 있던지라, 나는 여자들이 싫다고 말할 특권을 잃어버렸음을 알게 되었다. 즉 이제 여자가 청혼을 받는 것 자체가 은혜였고, 만약 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 거절하는 척하는 오만함을 부린다면, 그녀에게는 두 번 거절할 기회는커녕, 그 잘못 디딘 발걸음을 되돌려 거절하는 척했을 뿐인 것을 받아들일 기회조차 결코 주어지지 않았다. 남자들은 어디서나 선택의 여지가 어찌나 많았던지 여자들의 처지가 매우 불행했으니, 남자들은 마치 집집마다 문을 두드리는 듯했고, 어떤 남자가 천만다행으로 한 집에서 거절당한다 해도 그는 다음 집에서는 틀림없이 받아들여졌기 때문이다.\n\n이뿐만 아니라 나는 또 알게 되었으니, 남자들은 실제로 요구할 재산도 없고 받을 자격도 없으면서, 거리낌 없이 자기를 그럴듯하게 꾸미고는, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 재산 사냥에 나섰으며, 그것도 어찌나 거들먹거리며 해 댔던지, 여자는 자기에게 구애하는 자의 인품이나 재산을 캐물어 보는 것조차 거의 허락되지 않았다. 나는 이 본보기를 내 옆집에 사는 한 젊은 아가씨에게서 보았으니, 나는 그녀와 친분을 맺어 둔 터였다. 그녀는 어느 젊은 선장에게서 구애를 받았는데, 거의 2000파운드에 가까운 재산을 지녔으면서도 다만 그의 이웃 몇몇에게 그의 인품이나 품행이나 재산에 대해 물어보았을 뿐인데, 그는 다음번 방문 때 이를 빌미 삼아, 자기는 그것을 정말로 매우 언짢게 여기며 더는 그녀에게 방문하는 수고를 끼치지 않겠노라고 그녀에게 알렸다. 나는 이 일을 전해 들었고, 또 그녀와 친분을 트기 시작한 터라, 이 일로 그녀를 보러 갔다. 그녀는 그 일에 대해 나와 흉금을 터놓고 이야기하며 매우 거리낌 없이 속내를 털어놓았다. 나는 곧, 그녀가 자기가 매우 부당하게 대접받았다고 여기면서도 그것을 분개할 힘이 없으며, 그를 잃었다는 것에, 특히 자기보다 재산이 적은 다른 여자가 그를 차지했다는 것에 몹시 약이 올라 있음을 알아챘다.\n\n나는 내가 비열함이라 부른 그런 짓에 맞서 그녀의 마음을 단단히 다잡아 주었다. 나는 그녀에게, 내가 세상에서 아무리 낮은 처지에 있다 해도, 내가 그의 재산과 인품을 알아볼 자유도 없이 오로지 그의 자기 추천만 믿고 그를 받아들여야 마땅하다고 생각하는 남자라면 경멸했을 것이라고 말했다. 또 나는 그녀에게, 그녀는 좋은 재산을 지녔으니 시대의 재앙에 몸을 굽힐 필요가 없다고, 우리처럼 천거할 만한 돈이 거의 없는 여자들을 남자들이 모욕할 수 있다는 것만으로도 족한데, 만약 그녀가 그런 모욕을 분개하지 않고 자기에게 넘어가도록 내버려 둔다면, 그녀는 모든 경우에 헐값으로 매겨질 것이고, 그 동네 모든 여자의 멸시를 받게 될 것이라고 말했다. 또 여자란 자기를 함부로 대한 남자에게 복수할 기회가 없을 리 없으며, 그런 작자를 굴복시킬 방도야 얼마든지 있으니, 그렇지 않다면 여자란 정녕 세상에서 가장 불행한 존재일 것이라고도 했다.\n\n나는 그녀가 그 이야기에 매우 흡족해함을 알았고, 그녀는 진지하게 내게, 그에게 자기의 정당한 분노를 절감하게 하여 그를 다시 끌어들이거나, 아니면 자기 복수를 되도록 공공연하게 하는 만족을 얻거나 했으면 정말 좋겠다고 말했다.\n\n나는 그녀에게, 만약 내 조언을 따르겠다면, 이 두 가지 일에서 어떻게 그녀의 소원을 이룰 수 있을지 일러 주겠다고 했다. 그리고 내가 그 남자를 다시 그녀의 문 앞으로 데려와 들여보내 달라고 빌게 만들겠노라고 장담했다. 그녀는 그 말에 미소 지으며, 만약 그가 자기 문 앞에 온다면, 자기 분노가 그를 거기 오래 세워 둘 만큼 그리 크지는 않다는 것을 내게 분명히 내비쳤다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 내 조언의 제안에 매우 기꺼이 귀 기울였으니, 그래서 나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기 자신에게 정의를 베푸는 것이라고 말했다. 즉 여러 사람에게서, 그가 부인들 사이에서 자기가 그녀를 버렸다고 떠벌리며 거절의 우위를 자기에게 돌리는 척한다는 말을 들었으니, 그녀는 여자들 사이에 이것을 잘 퍼뜨리도록—그녀가 사는 곳처럼 집안 소식에 그토록 열을 올리는 동네에서야 그럴 기회가 없을 리 없었으니—신경 쓰라는 것이었다. 곧 자기가 그의 형편을 알아본 결과, 그가 재산에 관해서 자처하던 그런 사람이 아님을 알게 되었다고 말이다. \"사람들에게 이렇게 알려지게 하세요, 아가씨,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"당신이 그가 당신이 기대한 그런 사람이 아니라는 것을 잘 전해 들었고, 그래서 그와 엮이는 것이 안전하지 않다고 생각했다고요. 그가 성질이 고약하다는 말을 들었고, 그가 여러 경우에 여자들을 함부로 대한 것을 자랑했다고, 특히 그가 품행이 방탕했다고요.\" 운운. 그중 마지막 것은 정말 얼마간 진실이 담겨 있었으나, 동시에 나는 그녀가 그 대목 때문에 그를 더 나쁘게 여기는 것 같지는 않다는 것을 알아챘다.\n\n내가 이것을 그녀의 머릿속에 심어 주자, 그녀는 더없이 선뜻 받아들였다. 그녀는 즉시 도구가 될 사람을 찾는 일에 착수했고, 그 일에는 거의 어려움이 없었으니, 동네의 수다쟁이 두엇에게 자기 이야기를 대강 들려준 것이, 그 일대 온 동네 다과상의 화젯거리가 되어 내가 어디를 방문하든 그 이야기와 마주쳤기 때문이다. 또 내가 그 젊은 아가씨 본인과 아는 사이라는 것이 알려진 터라, 사람들이 매우 자주 내 의견을 물었고, 나는 필요한 온갖 과장을 곁들여 그것을 뒷받침하며 그의 인품을 더없이 음울한 빛깔로 그려 냈다. 그러고는 일종의 은밀한 정보로서, 다른 수다쟁이들은 전혀 모르는 것이라며 이렇게 덧붙였으니, 곧 내가 듣기로 그가 형편이 매우 좋지 않다는 것, 그가 지휘하는 배의 선주들과의 이해관계를 유지하려면 재산이 절실하다는 것, 그 자신의 지분 값이 치러지지 않았고 만약 곧 치러지지 않으면 선주들이 그를 배에서 내쫓을 것이며, 그 일등 항해사가 그 배를 지휘하게 될 듯한데, 그 항해사가 선장이 사기로 약속했던 그 지분을 사겠다고 제안했다는 것이었다.\n\n나는, 고백하건대 내가 그 악당이라 부른 자에게 진심으로 약이 올라 있던 터라, 그가 플리머스에 살아 있는 아내를 하나, 또 서인도제도에 다른 아내를 하나 두고 있다는 소문도 들었다고 덧붙였으니, 그런 부류의 신사들에게는 그것이 그리 드문 일이 아니라는 것을 그들 모두 알고 있었다.\n\n이는 우리 둘 다 바라던 대로 먹혀들었으니, 즉시 옆집의 그 젊은 아가씨는—그녀에게는 그녀와 그녀의 재산을 둘 다 다스리는 아버지와 어머니가 있었는데—집 안에 갇히게 되었고, 그녀의 아버지는 그가 집에 드나드는 것을 금했다. 또 그가 드나들던 다른 한 곳에서도, 그 여자가 아무리 이상한 일일지언정 싫다고 말할 용기를 냈으니, 그는 어디를 가 보아도 자기 거만함을 두고, 또 여자들에게 자기 인품을 캐물을 여지를 주지 않으려 한다는 둥 하는 말로 비난받았다.\n\n자, 이쯤 되자 그는 자기 실수를 깨닫기 시작했고, 강 이쪽의 모든 여자에게 경보를 울려 놓은 터라, 강 건너 래트클리프로 가서 그곳의 부인 몇몇에게 접근했다. 그러나 그곳의 젊은 여자들 역시 그 시절의 풍조대로 청혼받기를 꽤 마다하지 않았으나, 그의 운수가 어찌나 사나웠던지 그의 평판이 강을 건너 그를 따라가, 그의 명성이 그곳에서도 우리 쪽에서나 다름없었으니, 그리하여 그가 아내야 얼마든지 구할 수 있었을지언정, 그가 원하던 바, 곧 좋은 재산을 지닌 여자들 사이에서는 그런 일이 일어나지 않았다.\n\n그러나 이것이 다가 아니었다. 그녀는 또 다른 일을 자기 손으로 매우 교묘하게 꾸몄으니, 친척인 데다 실은 유부남인 어떤 젊은 신사를 시켜, 매우 멋진 사륜마차에 좋은 제복을 갖춘 채 일주일에 두세 번 자기를 찾아오게 했다. 그러자 그녀의 두 앞잡이와 나 또한 즉시 온 동네에 소문을 퍼뜨렸으니, 이 신사가 그녀에게 구애하러 온다는 것, 그가 연 1000파운드의 수입을 가진 신사라는 것, 그가 그녀에게 반했다는 것, 그리고 그 신사가 레드리프로 마차를 끌고 그녀를 찾아오기에는 거리가 너무 좁고 다니기 어려워 불편하므로, 그녀가 시내에 있는 숙모 댁으로 가려 한다는 것이었다.\n\n이것은 즉시 먹혀들었다. 그 선장은 어느 자리에서나 비웃음거리가 되어 목이라도 맬 지경이었다. 그는 그녀에게 다시 다가갈 온갖 방도를 다 써 보았고, 세상에서 가장 절절한 편지를 그녀에게 써 보내며 자기의 지난 경솔함을 변명했다. 한마디로, 그는 갖은 애를 써서, 그의 말마따나 자기 평판을 깨끗이 하기 위해 다시 그녀를 찾아뵐 허락을 얻어 냈다.\n\n이 만남에서 그녀는 그에게 흠뻑 복수를 했으니, 그녀는 그에게, 자기를 대체 어떤 여자로 여겼기에 결혼처럼 그토록 중대한 협상에 어떤 남자든 그 형편을 충분히 알아보지도 않고 받아들이리라 생각했느냐고 말했다. 또 만약 그가 자기를 윽박질러 혼인으로 몰아넣을 수 있다고, 자기가 이웃 여자들이 처할 법한 그런 처지, 즉 처음 나타나는 점잖은 기독교도면 누구든 덥석 잡을 그런 처지에 있다고 생각했다면 그것은 오산이라고 했다. 또 한마디로, 그의 인품이 실제로 나빴거나, 아니면 그가 이웃들에게 몹시 부당한 평판을 입은 것이라고, 그리고 그가 자기가 정당하게 편견을 갖게 된 몇 가지 점을 해명하지 못한다면, 자기는 그에게 더 할 말이 없으며, 다만 자기 자신에게 정의를 베풀어, 자기는 그에게든 다른 어떤 남자에게든 싫다고 말하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 알게 하는 만족을 그에게 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n그러면서 그녀는, 자기가 그의 인품에 대해 들은, 아니 오히려 내 도움으로 자기가 지어낸 것을 그에게 말했다. 즉 그가 지휘하는 배에서 자기 소유라고 자처하던 지분의 값을 치르지 않았다는 것, 선주들이 그를 지휘에서 내쫓고 그 자리에 항해사를 앉히기로 결정했다는 것, 그의 품행에 관해 일어난 추문, 곧 그가 이러이러한 여자들과 엮였다고 비난받았다는 것, 그리고 플리머스와 서인도제도에 아내를 두고 있다는 것 등을 말했다. 그러고는 그에게, 만약 이런 일들이 해명되지 않는다면, 자기가 그를 거절하고, 그동안 그것이 그토록 중대한 점들인 만큼 그에 대한 해명을 요구하는 것이 정당한 이유가 있는 것이 아니냐고, 그것을 부인할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 말에 어찌나 어리둥절했던지 한마디도 대답하지 못했고, 그녀는 그의 동요를 보고 그 모든 것이 사실인 양 거의 믿게 될 지경이었으니, 동시에 그 모든 소문을 일으킨 것이 바로 자기 자신이라는 것을 알면서도 그러했다.\n\n얼마 후 그는 조금 정신을 차렸고, 그때부터 구애에서 살아 있는 누구보다도 가장 겸손하고 가장 조신하며 가장 끈질긴 남자가 되었다.\n\n그녀는 자기 장난을 꽤 멀리까지 끌고 갔다. 그녀는 그에게, 자기가 그런 대접을 감내할 수 있거나 감내해야 할 만큼 그토록 막다른 지경에 몰려 있다고 생각하느냐고, 그리고 자기에게는 그보다 더 멀리서까지 찾아올 만한 가치가 있다고 여기는 이들이 없지 않다는 것을 보지 못하느냐고 물었으니, 이는 그녀가 거짓 구실로 자기를 찾아오게 만든 그 신사를 두고 하는 말이었다. 그녀는 이런 술책으로 그를, 자기 형편에 관해서든 자기 처신에 관해서든 그녀를 만족시킬 온갖 조치에 따르도록 굴복시켰다. 그는 그녀에게 자기가 배의 지분 값을 치렀다는 반박할 수 없는 증거를 가져왔고, 선주들에게서, 그들이 그를 배의 지휘에서 물러나게 하고 일등 항해사를 그 자리에 앉히려 한다는 소문이 거짓이며 근거 없다는 증명서를 가져왔으니, 한마디로 그는 이전의 그와는 완전히 정반대가 되었다.\n\n이렇게 하여 나는 그녀에게 이를 납득시켰으니, 곧 남자들이 결혼이라는 일에서 우리 여성을 이용해 이득을 본다면, 그렇게 고를 여지가 많다는 것과 여자들이 그토록 만만하다는 것을 전제로 그러는 것이거니와, 그것은 오로지 이 한 가지, 곧 여자들이 자기 입지를 지키고 자기 몫을 해낼 용기가 없다는 데서 비롯된 것일 뿐이라는 것이었다. 그리고 로체스터 경의 말마따나,\n\n\"여자가 아무리 파멸했다 한들, 자기를 망친 자, 곧 남자에게 복수할 수 있는 법이라네.\"\n\n이런 일들이 있은 뒤, 이 젊은 아가씨는 자기 몫을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 그를 차지하기로 작정했고 실은 그를 차지하는 것이 자기 계획의 주된 목표였으면서도, 그가 자기를 얻는 일을 그에게는 세상에서 가장 어려운 일로 만들어 버렸다. 그리고 그녀가 이를 해낸 것은 거만하고 새침한 처신으로가 아니라 정당한 책략으로였으니, 그에게 판세를 뒤집어 그 자신의 수법을 그에게 되돌려 놓은 것이었다. 즉 그가 일종의 거만한 처신으로 자기를 인품 따위는 문제 삼을 수 없는 위치에 두고, 자기 인품을 캐묻는 것을 일종의 모욕으로 여기는 척했던 만큼, 그녀는 바로 그 문제를 두고 그와 절교했으며, 그가 자기 일에 관해서는 온갖 캐물음에 다 따르도록 만드는 동시에, 자기 자신의 일을 그가 들여다보는 것에 대해서는 보란 듯이 문을 닫아걸었던 것이다.\n\n그에게는 그녀를 아내로 얻는 것만으로 족했다. 그녀가 가진 것에 관해서는, 그녀가 그에게 솔직히 말하기를, 그가 자기 형편을 아는 만큼 자기도 그의 형편을 아는 것이 마땅하다고 했다. 그리고 그가 동시에 그녀의 형편을 다만 세간의 소문으로만 알고 있었으면서도, 그녀에 대한 자기 열정을 어찌나 여러 번 다짐했던지, 그로서는 자기의 그 대단한 청에 그녀의 손을 달라는 것 말고는 더 바랄 것이 없다는 둥, 연인들의 관례에 따른 그런 종류의 횡설수설을 늘어놓을 수밖에 없었다. 한마디로, 그는 그녀의 재산에 관해 더 물어볼 여지를 스스로 남겨 두지 않았고, 그녀는 신중한 여자답게 이를 이용했으니, 자기 재산의 일부를 그가 전혀 알지 못하게 수탁자들에게 맡겨 두어, 그것이 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 완전히 벗어나 있게 하고, 그로 하여금 나머지에 매우 흡족해하도록 만들었다.\n\n사실 그녀는 그 밖에도 형편이 꽤 좋았으니, 즉 그녀에게는 현금 1400파운드가량이 있어 그것을 그에게 주었고, 나머지는 얼마간 지난 뒤에 자기 몫의 부수입으로 드러내 보였는데, 그것이 그의 것이 되지는 않더라도 그녀의 특정한 비용이라는 항목에서 그의 부담을 덜어 줄 수 있는 만큼, 그는 그것을 대단한 은혜로 받아들여야 했다. 그리고 덧붙여야 할 것은, 이런 처신으로 그 신사 자신이 그녀를 얻고자 청할 때 더 겸손해졌을 뿐 아니라, 그녀를 차지한 뒤에도 그녀에게 훨씬 더 깍듯한 남편이 되었다는 것이다. 나는 여기서 부인들에게, 그들이 미리부터 남자들에게 모욕당하기를 그토록 감수함으로써 스스로를 아내의 통상적인 지위보다도—내가 편파적이지 않다고 한다면, 그 지위는 이미 충분히 낮거니와—얼마나 더 낮은 자리에 두는지, 또 자기 자신의 굴욕을 스스로 마련하는지를 일깨워 주지 않을 수 없으니, 고백하건대 나는 그래야 할 아무런 필요성도 찾을 수 없다.\n\n그러므로 이 이야기는 부인들에게, 그 우위가 남자들이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 그쪽에 있지 않다는 것을 보여 주는 데 쓸모가 있을 것이다. 그리고 남자들이 우리 가운데서 너무도 많이 고를 수 있다는 것이, 그리고 스스로를 욕되게 하고 값싸고 만만하게 굴며 청혼받기를 거의 기다리지도 않는 여자들이 더러 있다는 것이 사실이라 할지라도, 만약 그들이, 이를테면 얻을 만한 가치가 있는 여자를 얻으려 한다면, 그런 여자들은 여전히 좀처럼 손에 넣기 어렵다는 것을, 그리고 그렇지 않은 여자들은, 막상 얻고 보면 어떤 결함이 있어서, 오히려 까다롭게 구는 부인들을 천거해 주는 셈이지, 남자들이 그 만만한 구애를 계속하고 첫 부름에 응할 똑같이 값진 아내를 기대하도록 부추기는 것은 아니라는 것을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n부인들이 자기 입지를 지키고, 자칭 연인들에게 자기들이 무시당하는 것을 분개할 줄 안다는 것을, 그리고 자기들이 싫다고 말하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 보여 줌으로써 언제나 남자들보다 우위를 점한다는 것보다 더 확실한 것은 없다. 내가 보기에, 남자들은 여자의 수를 들먹이며 우리를 잔뜩 모욕하니, 곧 전쟁과 바다와 장사와 그 밖의 사건들이 남자들을 너무도 많이 데려가 버려서 양성의 수에 균형이 맞지 않으며, 그러므로 여자들이 불리하다는 것이다. 그러나 나는 여자의 수가 그토록 많다거나 남자의 수가 그토록 적다고는 결코 인정할 수 없다. 다만 그들이 내게 진실을 말하라고 한다면, 여자들의 불리함이란 남자들에게는 끔찍한 추문거리이거니와, 그것은 여기에, 오로지 여기에만 있으니, 곧 시대가 너무도 사악하고 그 성이 너무도 방탕하여, 한마디로 정숙한 여자가 엮여도 좋을 만한 남자의 수가 정말로 적으며, 여자가 모험을 걸어 볼 만한 남자란 여기저기 띄엄띄엄 있을 뿐이라는 것이다. 그러나 그것의 귀결조차도 결국 이 정도에 지나지 않으니, 곧 여자들은 더욱더 까다로워야 마땅하다는 것이다. 청혼하는 남자의 진정한 인품을 우리가 어찌 알겠는가? 이런 경우에 여자가 더 만만해야 한다고 말하는 것은, 위험이 크기 때문에 우리가 더 선뜻 모험을 걸어야 한다고 말하는 것이니, 내 식의 추론으로는 그것은 참으로 어처구니없는 일이다.\n\n오히려 여자들은, 배신당할 위험이 클수록 만 배는 더 조심스럽고 신중해야 할 까닭이 있다. 그리고 부인들이 이를 헤아려 신중한 쪽을 택한다면, 다가오는 온갖 속임수를 죄다 간파해 낼 것이니, 한마디로 요즘 인품을 내세울 만한 남자의 삶이란 정말 드물기 때문이다. 그러니 부인들이 조금만이라도 알아본다면, 곧 남자들을 분간하여 스스로를 구해 낼 수 있을 것이다. 자기 자신의 안전 따위는 생각할 가치도 없다고 여기는 여자들로 말하자면, 자기의 온전한 처지를 견디지 못하고, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면, 처음 나타나는 점잖은 기독교도면 누구든 잡기로 작정하여, 마치 말이 전투로 돌진하듯 혼인으로 내달리는 그런 여자들에게 나는 이 말밖에 할 것이 없으니, 곧 그들은 다른 정신 나간 사람들 틈에 끼어 기도나 받아야 할 부류의 부인들이며, 내게는 그들이 마치 십만 개의 꽝에 상이 하나뿐인 복권에 자기 전 재산을 거는 사람들처럼 보인다는 것이다.\n\n상식이 있는 남자라면 여자가 첫 공격에 자기를 내주지 않는다고 해서, 혹은 그의 사람됨이나 인품을 알아보지도 않고 그의 청을 받아들이지 않는다고 해서 그녀를 더 낮게 보지는 않을 것이다. 오히려 그는, 요즘 남자들의 행태로 보아, 그녀를 세상에서 가장 미련한 피조물이라 여길 것이 틀림없다. 한마디로, 자기 목숨이 단 하나뿐이면서 그 목숨을 단번에 던져 버리고, 혼인을 죽음처럼 어둠 속의 도약으로 만드는 여자라면, 그는 그녀의 능력에 대해, 아니 그녀의 분별 하나하나에 대해 매우 경멸스러운 견해를 가질 수밖에 없을 것이다.\n\n나는 이 점에서 우리 여성의 처신이 조금은 다스려졌으면 하고 진심으로 바라니, 이것이야말로 삶의 온갖 부분 가운데 지금 이 시대에 우리가 가장 크게 해를 입고 있는 일이라고 나는 생각한다. 그것은 다름 아닌 용기의 부족이요, 아예 결혼하지 못할까 하는 두려움이요, 노처녀라 불리는 그 무시무시한 삶의 처지에 대한 두려움이니, 이에 관해서는 따로 들려줄 이야기가 있다. 이것이, 다시 말하건대, 여자의 올가미이다. 그러나 부인들이 일단 그 두려움을 넘어서서 제대로 처신하기만 한다면, 자기 행복에 그토록 절대적으로 긴요한 일에서 자기 입지를 지킴으로써, 지금처럼 자기를 내던지는 것보다 더 확실하게 그것을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 비록 그들이 달리 할 수 있는 것만큼 빨리 결혼하지 못한다 해도, 더 안전하게 결혼함으로써 그 손해를 메우게 될 것이다. 나쁜 남편을 얻는 여자는 언제나 너무 일찍 결혼한 것이요, 좋은 남편을 얻는 여자는 결코 너무 늦게 결혼한 것이 아니다. 한마디로, 흉한 외모나 잃어버린 평판을 가진 경우를 제외하면, 처신만 잘한다면 언젠가는 안전하게 결혼할 수 없는 여자란 없으나, 만약 그녀가 서둘러 몸을 내던진다면, 십중팔구가 아니라 만 번 가운데 한 번 빼고는 그녀는 파멸하고 만다.\n\n그러나 나는 이제 내 자신의 경우로 오는데, 거기에는 이때 적잖은 미묘함이 있었다. 내가 처한 형편으로 보아 좋은 남편의 청혼은 내게 세상에서 가장 긴요한 일이었으나, 나는 곧 값싸고 만만하게 구는 것이 그 길이 아님을 알게 되었다. 머지않아 그 과부에게 재산이 없다는 것이 알려지기 시작했고, 이 말은 곧 나에 대한 온갖 나쁜 말을 다 하는 셈이었으니, 나는 혼인에 관한 모든 이야기에서 빠지기 시작했기 때문이다. 교양 있고, 어여쁘고, 재치 있고, 정숙하고, 마음에 드는 것—이 모든 것이 내 인품으로 인정되었으나, 그것이 정당한지 아닌지는 여기서 따질 바가 아니거니와—다시 말하건대, 이 모든 것도 그 찌꺼기 없이는 소용이 없었으니, 이제 그것이 덕 그 자체보다도 더 값진 것이 되어 버린 것이다. 한마디로, 그들은 그 과부에게 돈이 없다고 말했다.\n\n그리하여 나는 내 현재 형편의 상태에 관해, 내 처지를 바꾸어, 나를 알지 못하는 어떤 다른 곳에서 새로이 모습을 드러내는 것이, 또 필요하다면 다른 이름으로 행세하는 것조차도 절대적으로 긴요하다고 결심했다.\n\n나는 내 생각을 가까운 벗인 그 선장 부인에게 털어놓았으니, 그녀가 선장과 겪은 일에서 내가 그토록 충실히 도와준 사람이요, 또 그녀도 내가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 똑같은 식으로 기꺼이 나를 도와줄 사람이었기 때문이다. 나는 거리낌 없이 그녀에게 내 형편을 털어놓았다. 내 밑천은 얼마 안 되었으니, 지난번 일이 끝났을 때 나는 약 540파운드밖에 마련하지 못했고, 그중 얼마는 써 버린 터였다. 그래도 내게는 약 460파운드가 남아 있었고, 매우 값비싼 옷이 많이 있었으며, 금시계 하나와 약간의 보석이 있었는데 그것들은 대단한 값어치는 아니었고, 처분하지 않은 아마포가 약 30파운드에서 40파운드어치 남아 있었다.\n\n내 소중하고 충실한 벗, 그 선장의 아내는 내가 위의 그 일에서 자기에게 베푼 도움을 어찌나 절감했던지, 내게 한결같은 벗이 되어 주었을 뿐 아니라, 내 형편을 알기에 손에 돈이 들어올 때마다 자주 내게 선물을 주었으니, 그것이 충분히 한 사람의 생활비에 이르렀던지라, 나는 내 돈을 한 푼도 쓰지 않았다. 그리고 마침내 그녀는 내게 이 불행한 제안을 했으니, 곧 앞서 말한 대로 우리가 지켜보았듯이, 남자들이 정작 자기 재산은 하나도 없으면서 거리낌 없이 재산 있는 여자를 얻을 자격이 있는 사람인 양 자기를 꾸미는 만큼, 그들의 수법 그대로 그들을 상대하여, 할 수만 있다면 그 속이는 자를 속이는 것이 마땅하다는 것이었다.\n\n한마디로 그 선장 부인이 이 계획을 내 머릿속에 심어 주며, 만약 내가 자기 지시를 따른다면 내가 분명 재산 있는 남편을 얻되, 그에게 나 자신의 재산이 없다고 나를 책망할 여지를 조금도 남기지 않을 것이라고 말했다. 나는, 그럴 만한 이유가 있었던 터라, 그녀에게 나 자신을 온전히 그녀의 지시에 맡기겠노라고, 그리고 그 일에서 그녀가 시키는 대로가 아니고서는 말할 혀도 내디딜 발도 갖지 않겠노라고 말했으니, 그녀가 나를 몰아넣은 온갖 어려움에서 그녀가 나를 빠져나오게 해 주리라 믿었던 것이고, 그녀는 그것을 책임지겠노라고 했다.\n\n그녀가 내게 시킨 첫 단계는 그녀를 사촌이라 부르며 그녀가 일러 준 대로 시골에 있는 그녀의 친척 집으로 가는 것이었는데, 그곳으로 그녀가 자기 남편을 데려와 나를 방문하게 했고, 나를 사촌이라 부르며 일을 이리저리 잘 꾸며서, 그녀의 남편과 그녀가 함께 내게 시내로 와서 자기들과 함께 지내자고 더없이 열렬히 청했으니, 그들은 이제 전에 살던 곳과는 사뭇 다른 곳에 살고 있었기 때문이다. 다음으로 그녀는 자기 남편에게, 내게 적어도 1500파운드의 재산이 있으며, 내 친척 몇몇이 죽고 나면 훨씬 더 많이 갖게 될 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n남편에게 이 말을 해 두는 것으로 충분했으니, 내 쪽에서는 아무것도 할 필요가 없었다. 나는 다만 가만히 앉아 일이 어떻게 되는지 기다리기만 하면 되었으니, 즉시 온 동네에, 아무개 선장 댁에 있는 그 젊은 과부가 재산가라는 것, 그녀에게 적어도 1500파운드, 어쩌면 훨씬 더 많은 재산이 있다는 것, 그리고 선장이 그렇게 말했다는 소문이 쫙 퍼졌기 때문이다. 그리고 누가 언제든 선장에게 나에 대해 물으면, 그는 그 일을 한마디도 알지 못하면서도, 자기 아내가 그렇게 말했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르면서도, 거리낌 없이 그것을 단언했다. 그리고 이 점에서 그는 아무런 해도 생각하지 않았으니, 그것을 아내에게서 들었기에 정말로 그렇다고 믿었기 때문이다. 노름판에 재산이 걸려 있다고 생각하기만 하면 그런 작자들은 그토록 빈약한 기초 위에 집을 짓는 법이다. 이 재산이라는 평판으로 나는 곧 흠모하는 자들이 넘쳐 나는 축복을 누리는 나 자신을 발견했고, 그들이 그토록 드물다고들 했건만 내가 남자를 골라잡을 수 있게 되었음을 알았으니, 그건 그렇고 이는 내가 앞서 한 말을 확증해 준다.\n이것이 내 처지인지라, 교묘한 노름을 벌여야 했던 나로서는 이제 그들 모두 가운데서 내 목적에 가장 알맞을 남자를 골라내는 일밖에 할 것이 없었다. 즉 재산에 대한 풍문을 가장 곧이곧대로 믿고 그 자세한 내막을 너무 깊이 캐묻지 않을 법한 남자를 말이다. 그리고 내가 이를 해내지 못한다면 나는 아무것도 못 하는 것이었으니, 내 경우는 많은 캐물음을 견뎌 내지 못할 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 그가 내게 구애하는 방식을 보고 내린 판단으로, 큰 어려움 없이 내 남자를 골라냈다. 나는 그가 자기는 세상 그 무엇보다 나를 사랑한다고, 만약 내가 그를 행복하게 해 준다면 그것으로 족하다고 다짐하고 맹세하도록 내버려 두었으니, 그 모든 것이 내가 매우 부유하다는 가정 위에, 아니 충분한 확신 위에 놓인 것임을 나는 알고 있었다, 비록 나 자신은 그것에 대해 그에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었지만 말이다.\n\n이 사람이 내 남자였다. 그러나 나는 그를 속속들이 시험해 보아야 했으니, 실로 내 안전은 거기에 달려 있었다. 만약 그가 주춤한다면, 그가 나를 잡았다가 망하는 것이 확실하듯, 나도 망하는 것이 확실하다는 것을 나는 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 그의 재산에 대해 얼마간 까다롭게 굴지 않는다면, 그것은 그로 하여금 내 재산에 대해 얼마간 까다롭게 굴도록 이끄는 길이었다. 그러므로 나는 우선 기회 있을 때마다 그의 진심을 의심하는 척하며, 어쩌면 그가 오직 내 재산 때문에 내게 구애하는 것이리라고 말했다. 그는 앞서 말한 대로 우레와 같은 다짐으로 그 점에 대해 내 입을 막았으나, 나는 여전히 의심하는 척했다.\n\n어느 날 아침 그는 자기 다이아몬드 반지를 빼더니, 내 방 들창 유리에 이런 시구를 썼다—\n\n\"내가 사랑하는 이는 당신, 오직 당신뿐.\"\n\n나는 그것을 읽고, 그에게 반지를 빌려 달라 하여 그 아래에 이렇게 썼다—\n\n\"그리고 누구나 사랑할 때 그렇게 말하지요.\"\n\n그는 다시 자기 반지를 받아 또 이런 한 줄을 썼다—\n\n\"덕만이 홀로 하나의 재산이라네.\"\n\n나는 다시 그것을 빌려 그 아래에 썼다—\n\n\"하지만 돈이 곧 덕이요, 황금이 곧 운명이지요.\"\n\n그는 내가 그토록 잽싸게 자기에게 응수하는 것을 보고 불처럼 새빨개졌고, 일종의 격분에 차서 자기가 나를 정복하겠노라고 말하더니 다시 이렇게 썼다—\n\n\"나는 당신의 황금을 경멸하오, 그래도 나는 사랑하오.\"\n\n나는, 여러분도 보다시피, 시의 마지막 한 수에 모든 것을 걸었으니, 그의 마지막 구절 아래에 대담하게 이렇게 썼기 때문이다—\n\n\"나는 가난해요. 당신이 얼마나 다정할지 어디 봅시다.\"\n\n이것은 내게 서글픈 진실이었다. 그가 나를 믿었는지 아닌지 나로서는 알 수 없었으니, 그때 나는 그가 믿지 않았으리라 짐작했다. 그러나 그는 내게로 날아와 나를 두 팔로 안고, 더없이 열렬하게, 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 열정으로 내게 입 맞추며, 펜과 잉크를 가져오라 할 때까지 나를 꼭 붙들었다. 그러고는 들창에 한 자 한 자 더디게 쓰기를 기다릴 수 없다고 하며, 종잇조각 하나를 꺼내 다시 쓰기 시작했다—\n\n\"가난하든 어떻든 내 사람이 되어 주오.\"\n\n나는 그의 펜을 받아 즉시 그를 따라 이렇게 썼다—\n\n\"그래도 당신은 속으로 내가 거짓말하기를 바라겠지요.\"\n\n그는 그것이 매정하다고 말했으니, 그것이 옳지 않은 데다, 자기로 하여금 나를 반박하게 만드는 것인데, 그것은 자기 애정에 못지않게 예절에도 맞지 않는 일이라는 것이었다. 그래서 내가 그를 자기도 모르게 이 시 짓는 끼적임 속으로 끌어들였으니, 부디 자기에게 그것을 중단하도록 강요하지 말아 달라고 청하며, 그는 다시 이렇게 썼다—\n\n\"우리의 다툼은 오직 사랑에 관한 것만으로 합시다.\"\n\n나는 다시 썼다—\n\n\"미워하지 않는 여자라면 충분히 사랑하는 것이지요.\"\n\n그는 이것을 호의로 받아들이고는, 그리하여 몽둥이를, 즉 펜을 내려놓았다. 다시 말하건대, 그는 그것을 호의로 받아들였으니, 만약 그가 속사정을 다 알았더라면 그것은 대단한 호의였을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그것을 내가 의도한 대로 받아들였으니, 곧 내가 그와 계속 나아갈 마음이 있다고 그가 생각하도록 한 것이었고, 실로 나로서는 그렇게 할 온갖 이유가 다 있었으니, 그는 내가 일찍이 만나 본 가장 쾌활하고 명랑한 부류의 남자였기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 종종 나 자신을 돌이켜보며 어떻게\n그런 사람을 속이는 것은 갑절로 죄스러운 일이었다. 그러나 내 처지에 어울리는 안정된 자리를 마련하라고 나를 다그치던 그 절박함이 내게는 그렇게 할 권리가 되어 주었다. 또한 나에 대한 그의 애정과 그 너그러운 성품은, 그를 함부로 대하지 말아야 한다는 쪽으로 작용했을지언정, 나로서는 오히려 그가 그 어떤 불같은 성미의 못난 사내보다—여자를 평생 비참하게 만드는 것밖에는 내세울 것이 없는 그런 격정 말고는 내세울 것이 아무것도 없는 사내보다—이 실망을 더 잘 견뎌 내리라는 쪽으로 강하게 작용했다.\n\n게다가 비록 내가 그가 짐작한 대로 그토록 자주 내 가난을 두고 그에게 농을 던지긴 했으나, 그것이 사실로 밝혀졌을 때에 그는 온갖 종류의 이의를 미리 봉해 둔 셈이었다. 그가 농으로 말했든 진심으로 말했든, 그는 내 지참금을 전혀 따지지 않고 나를 맞겠다고 선언했고, 내가 농으로 말했든 진심으로 말했든, 나는 내가 무척 가난하다고 선언해 두었던 까닭이다. 그러니 한마디로 나는 그를 양쪽으로 단단히 옭아 둔 셈이었고, 비록 그가 나중에 속았다고 말할 수는 있을지언정, 내가 그를 속였다고는 결코 말할 수 없을 터였다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 나를 바싹 따라다녔고, 나는 그를 잃을까 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 알았으므로, 분별이 아니었다면 권했을 것보다 더 오래 그에게 무심한 척하는 역할을 이어 갔다. 그러나 나는 끝내 내 처지를 그에게 털어놓을 수밖에 없게 될 때, 이 신중함과 무심함이 내게 얼마나 큰 유리함을 줄 것인가를 헤아렸다. 그리고 더더욱 조심스럽게 일을 다루었으니, 그가 거기서, 마땅히 그래야 하듯이, 내가 돈이 더 많거나 분별이 더 깊어서 도무지 함부로 나서지 않는 것이라고 미루어 짐작한다는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n어느 날 우리가 그 화제에 꽤 가까이 다가가 이야기를 나눈 뒤에, 나는 마음 놓고 그에게 이렇게 말했다. 그가 연인으로서 내게 한 가지 호의, 곧 내 재산을 캐묻지 않고 나를 맞겠다는 호의를 베푼 것은 사실이며, 나도 이에 합당한 보답으로, 곧 이치에 닿는 한도 안에서 그의 재산에 대해서도 되도록 캐묻지 않겠으나, 다만 몇 가지 질문은 하도록 허락해 주기 바라며, 그것에 답하고 말고는 그가 알맞다고 여기는 대로 하면 되고, 설령 전혀 답하지 않더라도 나는 언짢아하지 않겠다고. 그 질문 가운데 하나는 우리가 어떻게, 또 어디서 살 것인가에 관한 것이었으니, 내가 듣기로 그가 버지니아에 큰 농장을 가지고 있고, 거기 가서 살겠다는 말을 했다기에, 나는 유배되고 싶지는 않다고 그에게 말했다.\n\n이 이야기를 시작으로 그는 자진하여 자기의 온갖 사정을 내게 털어놓기 시작했고, 솔직하고 거리낌 없는 태도로 자기의 모든 형편을 들려주었으니, 그로써 나는 그가 세상살이에 아주 넉넉한 처지라는 것을 알게 되었다. 다만 그의 재산 가운데 큰 몫은 버지니아에 둔 세 농장으로 이루어져 있었는데, 그것이 대체로 말해 연 삼백 파운드에 이르는 아주 좋은 수입을 가져다주었으나, 만약 그가 거기 살면서 직접 경영한다면 그 네 배는 벌어들이리라는 것이었다. '아주 좋아.' 하고 나는 속으로 생각했다. '당신이 원하는 때에 곧바로 나를 거기로 데려가도 좋아, 비록 내가 미리 그렇게 말해 주지는 않겠지만.'\n\n나는 그가 버지니아에서 어떤 모습으로 행세할까를 두고 그를 몹시 놀려 댔으나, 그가 내 청이라면 무엇이든 다 들어주리라는 것을 알았다. 다만 그는 내가 자기 농장을 깎아내리는 것을 그리 달가워하지 않는 눈치였으므로, 나는 말머리를 돌렸다. 나는 거기 가서 살지 않을 만한 그럴듯한 까닭이 있다고 그에게 말했으니, 그의 농장이 거기서 그만큼 값나가는 것이라면, 나는 그가 말한 대로 연 천이백 파운드짜리 재산을 가진 신사에게 어울릴 만한 재산을 갖추지 못했기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n그는 너그럽게 대답하기를, 내 재산이 얼마인지 묻지 않겠으며, 처음부터 그러지 않겠다고 내게 말했으니 그 말을 그대로 지키겠다고 했다. 그러나 그것이 얼마이든 간에, 내가 더없이 기꺼이 원하여 스스로 택하는 것이 아니라면, 나더러 자기와 함께 버지니아로 가자고 결코 청하지 않을 것이며, 나 없이 자기 혼자 거기로 가지도 않겠노라고 다짐했다.\n\n이 모든 것이 내가 바라던 그대로였음은 여러분도 짐작할 수 있을 것이며, 사실 이보다 더 더할 나위 없이 흡족하게 일이 풀릴 수는 없었을 것이다. 나는 이 대목까지 일종의 무심함으로 끌고 나갔는데, 그는 처음보다도 그것을 더 자주 의아해했으나, 그 무심함이야말로 그의 구애를 떠받쳐 주는 유일한 버팀목이었다. 내가 이 일을 굳이 언급하는 것은, 다시 한번 부인들에게 넌지시 일러 주고자 함이니, 바로 그런 무심함을 부릴 용기의 부족이야말로 우리 여자들을 그토록 값싸게 만들고, 지금처럼 함부로 대접받게끔 만드는 까닭이다. 만약 부인들이, 오로지 제 잘난 점만 내세우며 거들먹거리는 허세 부리는 멋쟁이 하나쯤 이따금 잃는 모험을 무릅쓸 수 있다면, 분명 덜 무시당하고 더 많이 구애받을 것이다. 내가 정말로, 또 사실 그대로 내 대단한 재산이 얼마인지를, 그가 천오백 파운드를 기대하고 있을 때 사실 내가 가진 것은 통틀어 오백 파운드도 채 못 된다는 것을 드러냈더라면, 그래도 나는 그를 너무도 단단히 낚아 두었고 너무도 오래 어르고 다루었던 터라, 그가 나를 가장 나쁜 처지에 있다 해도 맞아 주었으리라고 확신했다. 게다가 그가 진실을 알게 되었을 때, 그것이 그에게는 다른 경우보다 덜 놀라운 일이었으니, 끝까지 무심한 태도로 일관한 나에게 그가 조금도 탓을 돌릴 데가 없었으므로, 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 다만 정말로 더 많을 줄 알았으나 설령 더 적다 한들 자기의 거래를 후회하지는 않으며, 다만 자기가 뜻했던 만큼 나를 잘 건사하지 못하게 될 뿐이라고만 했다.\n\n요컨대 우리는 결혼했고, 적어도 그 남자에 관한 한, 내 쪽에서는 더없이 행복한 결혼이었다고 단언한다. 그는 일찍이 어떤 여자가 가져 본 적 없을 만큼 더없이 좋은 성품의 사내였기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 형편은 내가 짐작했던 것만큼 좋지는 않았고, 반대편으로는 그 또한 이 결혼으로 자기가 기대했던 만큼 처지를 끌어올리지는 못했다.\n\n우리가 결혼했을 때, 나는 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 밑천을 그에게 가져다주고, 그것이 그게 전부임을 그가 알게 하느라 단단히 애를 먹었다. 그러나 그래야만 할 까닭이 있었으므로, 나는 어느 날 우리 둘만 있을 때를 틈타 그 일을 두고 그와 짤막한 이야기를 나누었다. \"여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"우리가 결혼한 지 보름이 되었어요. 이제 당신이 무언가를 가진 아내를 얻은 건지, 아무것도 없는 아내를 얻은 건지 알려 드릴 때가 되지 않았나요?\" \"그건 당신 좋을 때 하구려, 여보.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 내가 사랑하는 아내를 얻은 것으로 흡족하오. 나는 그 일을 두고 당신을 그리 캐묻지 않았잖소.\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"맞아요.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"하지만 그 일로 제게 큰 걱정거리가 하나 있는데, 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 도무지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이오, 여보?\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그게요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"제게는 좀 난처한 일이고, 당신에게는 더 난처한 일이에요. 제가 듣자니 ……대위가\" (내 친구의 남편을 두고 한 말이었다) \"당신에게, 제가 내세운 적도 없는 큰돈을 제가 가졌다고 말했다던데, 저는 분명 그에게 그렇게 하라고 시킨 적이 없거든요.\"\n\n\"음,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"……대위가 내게 그렇게 말했을지도 모르지. 그런데 그게 어떻단 말이오? 당신이 그만큼 가지지 않았다면, 그건 그 사람 탓으로 돌리면 그만이고, 당신은 자기가 무엇을 가졌는지 내게 말한 적이 없으니, 설령 당신이 아무것도 가진 게 없다 한들 내가 당신을 탓할 까닭이 없소.\"\n\n\"그게 너무도 옳고,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"너무도 너그러운 말씀이라, 제가 가진 게 얼마 안 된다는 것이 제게는 갑절로 괴로운 일이 되네요.\"\n\n\"가진 게 적을수록,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리 둘 다에게 더 나쁜 일이지. 하지만 당신이 말하는 그 괴로움이, 지참금이 없다고 내가 당신에게 매정하게 굴까 봐 두려워서 생긴 것은 아니길 바라오. 아니, 아니지, 가진 게 없다면 솔직하게, 그것도 단번에 내게 말해 주오. 어쩌면 내가 대위에게 나를 속였다고 말할 수는 있겠지만, 당신이 나를 속였다고는 결코 말할 수 없소. 당신이 자기 손으로 직접 가난하다고 적어 주지 않았소? 그러니 나는 당신이 그러려니 하고 여겨야 마땅하지.\"\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"여보, 결혼 전에 당신을 속이는 일에 제가 끼어들지 않았던 것이 다행이에요. 결혼한 뒤에 제가 당신을 속인다면, 그게 그나마 덜 나쁜 일일 테니까요. 제가 가난한 것은 너무도 사실이지만, 그렇다고 아무것도 없을 만큼 가난하지는 않답니다.\" 그러고서 나는 은행권 몇 장을 꺼내어 그에게 백육십 파운드쯤을 건넸다. \"여기 좀 있어요, 여보.\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고 이게 전부도 아니랍니다.\"\n\n내가 앞서 한 말로 그를 아무것도 기대하지 않을 지경까지 바싹 몰아 두었던 터라, 그 돈은 액수 자체로는 적었으나 그에게는 갑절로 반가웠다. 그는 그것이 자기가 기대했던 것보다 많다고 인정했고, 내가 그에게 한 이야기로 미루어 내 고운 옷가지며 금시계며 다이아몬드 반지 한두 개가 내 재산의 전부려니 했었노라고 했다.\n\n나는 이삼 일 동안 그가 그 백육십 파운드로 흐뭇해하도록 내버려 두었다가, 그날 밖에 나갔다 들어와서는 마치 그것을 가지러 갔다 온 것처럼 금화로 백 파운드를 더 집에 들고 와서, 그에게 줄 지참금이 조금 더 있다고 말했다. 그러고서 한 일주일쯤 뒤에 나는 다시 백팔십 파운드를 더, 그리고 아마포로 육십 파운드어치쯤을 가져다주었는데, 그 아마포는 내가 그에게 금화로 준 백 파운드와 더불어, 육백 파운드짜리 빚에 대한 변제조로 어쩔 수 없이 받아 둔 것이라고 믿게 했으니, 그것은 일 파운드에 오 실링 남짓밖에 안 되는 데다 그나마 값도 후하게 쳐 준 것이었다.\n\n\"그러니 이제, 여보,\" 하고 나는 그에게 말했다. \"참으로 안타깝게도 이게 전부이며, 제 재산을 몽땅 당신에게 드렸다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요.\" 나는 덧붙이기를, 내 육백 파운드를 가진 그 사람이 나를 등쳐먹지만 않았던들 나는 그에게 천 파운드의 값어치가 되었을 테지만, 형편이 이러하니 나는 그에게 충실하게 굴어 나 자신을 위해서는 한 푼도 남겨 두지 않았으며, 더 있었더라면 그것도 그가 가졌으리라고 했다.\n\n그는 그 태도에 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 또 그 액수에 어찌나 흡족했던지—그는 그것이 아예 아무것도 아닐까 봐 끔찍한 공포에 휩싸여 있었던 까닭에—더없이 고마워하며 그것을 받아들였다. 이렇게 하여 나는, 돈도 없으면서 재산가로 행세하고 재산이 있는 척하여 한 남자를 속여 결혼하는 사기를 무사히 넘겼으니, 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 나는 이것이야말로 여자가 밟을 수 있는 가장 위험한 발걸음 가운데 하나이며, 그 일로 뒷날 함부로 대접받을 위험을 가장 크게 무릅쓰는 짓이라고 여긴다.\n\n내 남편은, 공정하게 말하자면, 한없이 좋은 천성을 지닌 사람이었으나 결코 바보는 아니었다. 그리고 만약 내가 그가 기대했던 것을 가져다주었더라면 누렸을 그런 생활 방식에 자기 수입이 걸맞지 않다는 것을 깨닫고, 또 버지니아에 있는 농장에서 들어오는 수입에 실망한 터라, 그는 여러 번 자기 소유지에서 살고자 버지니아로 건너가고 싶은 마음을 내비쳤다. 그리고 그곳의 생활 방식이 얼마나 비용이 적게 들고, 얼마나 풍족하며, 얼마나 즐거운지 따위를 종종 한껏 추켜세우곤 했다.\n\n나는 곧바로 이 속뜻을 알아채기 시작했고, 어느 날 아침 그를 아주 솔직하게 붙들고 그렇다고 말했다. 곧 나는 그의 소유지가 이렇게 먼 거리에서는 그가 현지에 살면서 거두어들일 수익에 견주어 아무런 보탬이 못 된다는 것을 알았으며, 그가 거기 가서 살고 싶은 마음이 있음을 알았다고 했다. 그러고서 나는 덧붙이기를, 그가 아내를 얻는 일에 실망했음을 헤아리고 있으며, 그쪽으로 그의 기대가 채워지지 못한 것을 알았으니, 그 보상으로 내가 해 줄 수 있는 최소한의 일로서, 나는 더없이 기꺼이 그와 함께 버지니아로 건너가 거기서 살겠다고 그에게 말한다고 했다.\n\n그는 내가 자기에게 그러한 제안을 하는 것을 두고 천 마디 다정한 말을 내게 늘어놓았다. 그는 비록 재산에 대한 기대에는 실망했을지언정 아내에 대해서는 실망하지 않았으며, 나는 아내가 될 수 있는 모든 것을 그에게 다 갖추어 준 사람이고, 이것저것 낱낱이 헤아려 합쳐 보면 전체로는 만족하고도 남는다고 했다. 다만 이 제안이 너무도 다정하여, 자기로서는 이루 다 말로 표현할 수 없을 지경이라고 했다.\n\n이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 우리는 가기로 합의했다. 그는 거기에 아주 좋은 집이 한 채 있고, 가구도 잘 갖추어져 있으며, 어머니가 살아 계셔서 그 집에 살고 계시고, 누이가 하나 있는데 그것이 자기가 가진 친척의 전부라고 했다. 또 자기가 거기 도착하는 즉시 어머니는 다른 집으로 옮겨 가실 텐데, 그 집은 어머니가 살아 계시는 동안에는 어머니의 것이고 돌아가신 뒤에는 자기 것이 되며, 그리하여 나는 그 집 전체를 나 혼자 차지하게 되리라고 했다. 그리고 나는 이 모든 것이 그가 말한 그대로임을 알게 되었다.\n\n이 대목의 이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 우리는 우리가 타고 갈 배에 집에 쓸 좋은 가구를 잔뜩 실었고, 아마포와 그 밖의 필수품도 넉넉히 갖추었으며, 팔 만한 좋은 짐도 실었다. 그리고 우리는 떠났다.\n\n길고도 위험으로 가득했던 우리 항해의 형편을 일일이 늘어놓는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니니, 나도 항해 일지를 적지 않았고 내 남편도 마찬가지였다. 내가 말할 수 있는 것은 다만 끔찍한 항로를 지나며 무시무시한 폭풍에 두 번이나 혼이 났고, 한 번은 그보다 더 끔찍한 일을 겪었으니, 곧 한 해적이 배에 올라타 우리 식량을 거의 다 앗아 갔던 것이다. 그리고 나로서는 무엇보다 견딜 수 없었을 일은, 그들이 한때 내 남편을 데려가 자기들과 함께 가게 하려 했던 것이나, 간곡한 애원에 못 이겨 그를 두고 가도록 설득되었던 것이다. 다시 말해, 이 모든 끔찍한 일들을 겪은 끝에 우리는 버지니아의 요크강에 다다랐고, 우리 농장에 이르러서는 내 남편의 어머니로부터 표현할 수 있는 온갖 다정함과 애정의 표시를 받으며 맞아들여졌다.\n\n우리는 여기서 다 함께 살았으니, 내 시어머니는 내 간청에 따라 그 집에 계속 머무셨다. 그분은 헤어지기에는 너무도 다정한 어머니였기 때문이다. 내 남편 역시 처음과 다름없이 한결같았고, 나는 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 행복한 존재라고 여기던 차에, 기이하고도 놀라운 사건 하나가 그 모든 행복에 한순간에 종지부를 찍었으며, 내 처지를 세상에서 가장 비참하지는 않을지언정 가장 견디기 힘든 것으로 만들어 버렸다.\n\n내 어머니는 무척이나 명랑하고 성품 좋은 노부인이었으니—그분을 노부인이라 불러도 좋을 것이, 그 아들이 서른 살이 넘었기 때문이다—다시 말해 그분은 매우 유쾌하고 좋은 말벗이었으며, 특히 나를 즐겁게 해 주려고 우리가 사는 그 고장과 그곳 사람들에 관한 온갖 이야기로 나를 곧잘 즐겁게 해 주곤 했다.\n\n그중에서도 그분은 이 식민지 주민의 대부분이 잉글랜드에서 매우 보잘것없는 형편으로 이곳에 건너왔다는 것을 종종 내게 들려주었다. 곧, 대체로 말해 그들은 두 부류로 나뉘는데, 첫째는 하인으로 팔리려고 선장들에게 실려 온 자들이라고 했다. \"여보야, 우리는 그런 사람들을 그렇게 부르지만,\" 하고 그분은 말했다. \"더 정확히는 노예라고 불러야 마땅하단다.\" 둘째는, 중범죄나 그 밖에 사형으로 다스려지는 죄로 유죄판결을 받은 뒤에 뉴게이트와 그 밖의 감옥에서 유배된 자들이라고 했다.\n\n\"그들이 이곳에 오면,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"우리는 가리지 않는단다. 농장주들이 그들을 사들이고, 그들은 기한이 다 찰 때까지 밭에서 함께 일하지. 기한이 끝나면,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"그들에게 스스로 농사를 짓도록 장려해 준단다. 나라에서 그들에게 일정한 면적의 땅을 떼어 주거든. 그러면 그들은 일에 나서서 땅을 개간하고 손질한 다음, 거기에 자기들이 쓸 담배와 옥수수를 심지. 그리고 장인들과 상인들이 작물이 자라기도 전에 그 작물을 담보 삼아 연장이며 옷이며 그 밖의 필수품을 외상으로 대 주니까, 그들은 또다시 해마다 전해보다 조금씩 더 심고, 그래서 눈앞에 있는 그 작물로 필요한 것은 무엇이든 사들이게 되는 거야.\n\n\"그렇게 해서, 얘야,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"뉴게이트의 새 같은 자도 큰 인물이 된단다. 그리고 우리한테는,\" 하고 그분이 이어 말했다. \"손에 낙인이 찍힌 치안판사며, 민병대 장교며, 자기가 사는 고을의 행정관이 여럿 있단다.\"\n\n그분은 그 이야기의 그 대목을 계속 이어 가다가, 거기서 자기 자신의 몫이 끼어드는 바람에 말을 멈추었고, 무던히 좋은 성품다운 거리낌 없는 태도로 자기 자신이 바로 그 둘째 부류의 주민 가운데 하나라고 내게 말했다. 곧, 어떤 특정한 사건에서 너무 멀리 모험을 감행하는 바람에 죄인이 되어, 떳떳이 드러난 채로 건너오게 되었다는 것이었다. \"그리고 여기 그 표시가 있단다, 얘야.\" 하고 그분이 말했다. 그러고서 장갑을 벗으며 \"여기 좀 보렴.\" 하고 손바닥을 뒤집어, 무척이나 곱고 흰 팔과 손을 보여 주었으나, 그런 경우에 으레 그렇듯이 손 안쪽에 낙인이 찍혀 있었다.\n\n이 이야기는 내게 무척이나 가슴을 울렸으나, 내 어머니는 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말했다. \"딸아, 이상한 일로 여길 것 없단다. 내가 말했듯이, 이 고장에서 가장 훌륭한 사람들 가운데 더러도 손에 낙인이 찍혀 있고, 그들은 그것을 인정하기를 부끄러워하지 않으니까. 저기 ……소령은,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"이름난 소매치기였고, ……라는 판사는 들치기였는데, 둘 다 손에 낙인이 찍혔단다. 그들 같은 사람을 여럿 더 댈 수도 있지.\"\n\n우리는 이런 종류의 이야기를 자주 나누었고, 그분은 그와 비슷한 예를 숱하게 들려주었다. 얼마쯤 지나서, 그분이 불과 몇 주 전에 유배되어 온 어떤 사람의 이야기를 들려주고 있을 때, 나는 정겨운 태도로 그분 자신의 이야기를 좀 들려 달라고 청하기 시작했다. 그러자 그분은 더없이 꾸밈없고 진솔하게 그 이야기를 들려주었으니, 자기가 젊은 시절에 런던에서 매우 나쁜 무리에 빠지게 된 사정이었다. 그것은 그분의 어머니가 뉴게이트에 갇혀 비참하게 굶주리고 있던 어떤 친척 여자에게 먹을 것과 그 밖의 도움을 가져다주라고 그분을 자주 보냈던 데서 비롯된 것이었는데, 그 친척 여자는 나중에 교수형을 선고받았으나 임신을 사유로 형 집행이 유예되었다가 끝내 감옥에서 죽고 말았다.\n\n여기서 내 시어머니는 그 끔찍한 곳에서 벌어지는 사악한 짓거리들에 대해, 또 그곳이 다른 온 도시를 합친 것보다 더 많은 젊은이를 어떻게 파멸시키는가에 대해 길게 늘어놓았다. \"그리고 얘야,\" 하고 내 어머니가 말했다. \"너는 어쩌면 그것을 잘 모를 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그것에 대해 아무것도 들어 본 적이 없을 수도 있겠지. 하지만 이건 분명하단다,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"여기 사는 우리는 모두 안다, 뉴게이트라는 그 감옥 하나가 만들어 내는 도둑과 악당이 이 나라의 온갖 악당들의 패거리와 단체를 다 합친 것보다 더 많다는 것을. 바로 그 저주받은 곳이,\" 하고 내 어머니가 말했다. \"이 식민지의 절반을 사람으로 채운 게야.\"\n\n여기서 그분은 자기 이야기를 어찌나 길게, 또 어찌나 시시콜콜하게 이어 갔던지, 나는 매우 불안해지기 시작했다. 그러다가 그분의 이름을 말해야 하는 어떤 특정한 대목에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그 자리에서 푹 꺼져 들어가는 것만 같았다. 그분은 내가 정신이 온전치 못한 것을 알아채고, 어디 편치 않으냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다. 나는 그분이 들려준 그 우울한 이야기와 그분이 겪은 그 끔찍한 일들에 너무도 마음이 흔들려 그만 압도되었다고 말하며, 그 이야기를 더는 하지 말아 달라고 간청했다. \"아니, 얘야,\" 하고 그분이 무척 다정하게 말했다. \"무엇 때문에 이런 일들이 너를 괴롭혀야 한단 말이냐? 이 일들은 네가 태어나기 한참 전의 일이고, 지금은 내게 아무런 괴로움도 주지 않는단다. 아니, 오히려 나는 그 일들을 각별한 흡족함으로 돌아본다, 그것들이 나를 이곳에 데려다준 수단이 되었으니 말이다.\"\n그러고서 그분은 어떻게 자기가 무척 운 좋게 좋은 집안에 들어가게 되었는지를 이어서 들려주었다. 거기서 처신을 잘하던 차에 여주인이 죽자 주인이 자기와 결혼했고, 그 사람과의 사이에서 내 남편과 그 누이를 낳았으며, 남편이 죽은 뒤로는 자기의 부지런함과 알뜰한 살림 솜씨로 농장을 그때만큼 일구어 냈으니, 재산의 대부분은 남편의 것이 아니라 자기가 모은 것이라고 했다. 그분은 십육 년 넘게 과부로 지내 온 터였다.\n\n나는 이 대목의 이야기를 거의 귀담아듣지 못했으니, 자리를 물러나 내 격정을 풀어 놓고 싶은 마음이 간절했기 때문이며, 곧이어 그렇게 했다. 그리고 누구든 한번 헤아려 보라, 이 사람이 다름 아닌 바로 내 친어머니이며, 나는 이제 아이를 둘이나 낳았고 또 하나를 내 친오라비의 씨로 배고 있으며, 여전히 밤마다 그와 한자리에 눕는다는 것을 곱씹게 되었을 때, 내 마음이 겪었을 그 고통이 어떠했겠는가를.\n\n나는 이제 세상의 모든 여자 가운데 가장 불행한 존재였다. 아아! 그 이야기를 내가 결코 듣지 않았더라면, 모든 것이 무사했을 것을. 내 남편과 한자리에 누운 것이 죄가 될 수 없었을 것이니, 그가 내 친척이라는 것에 관해서는 내가 아무것도 몰랐기 때문이다.\n\n나는 이제 마음에 너무도 무거운 짐을 지고 있어서 도무지 잠을 이룰 수가 없었다. 그것을 털어놓는다면 내게 얼마간 위안이 되겠으나, 그래 봐야 아무 소용이 없으리라는 것을 알았다. 그러면서도 그것을 감추기란 거의 불가능에 가까웠다. 아니, 나는 분명 잠결에 그것을 말해 버려, 내가 원하든 원치 않든 남편에게 그것을 털어놓고 말리라고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 만약 내가 그것을 드러낸다면, 내가 기대할 수 있는 최소한의 일은 남편을 잃는 것이었으니, 그는 자기가 내 오라비였음을 안 뒤에도 내 남편으로 머물기에는 너무도 까다롭고 너무도 정직한 사람이었다. 그리하여 나는 더할 나위 없는 지경으로 어찌할 바를 몰랐다.\n\n내 눈앞에 어떤 난관들이 펼쳐졌을지는 누구든 헤아려 보도록 맡겨 두겠다. 나는 내 고국에서 떨어져 어마어마하게 먼 거리에 있었고, 돌아갈 길은 내게 건너지 못할 것이었다. 나는 아주 잘 살고 있었으나, 그 자체로 견딜 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다. 만약 내가 어머니에게 내 정체를 드러낸다 해도, 그 세세한 정황을 그분께 납득시키기는 어려울 터였으며, 나로서는 그것을 증명할 길이 없었다. 다른 한편으로, 만약 그분이 나를 의심하거나 미심쩍어한다면 나는 끝장이었으니, 그저 그런 암시만 흘려도 그것이 곧바로 나를 남편에게서 떼어 놓을 터인데, 그렇게 되면 어머니도 그도 얻지 못한 채, 그는 남편도 오라비도 아닌 처지가 되고 말 것이었다. 그리하여 한편의 놀라움과 다른 한편의 불확실함 사이에서, 나는 영락없이 끝장날 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그러는 동안, 나는 그 사실을 너무도 확실히 알고 있었던 까닭에, 정직한 아내의 외양을 한 채로 버젓이 드러난 근친상간과 매음 속에서 살고 있는 셈이었다. 그리고 비록 내가 그 죄에 대해 그리 마음이 찔리지는 않았다 해도, 그 행위 자체에는 본성을 거스르는 무언가 충격적인 것이 있어서, 그가 스스로 그렇게 여기던 대로의 내 남편을 내게 메스껍기까지 한 존재로 만들어 버렸다.\n\n그러나 더없이 차분하게 헤아린 끝에, 나는 그것을 모조리 감추고 어머니에게든 남편에게든 조금도 드러내지 않는 것이 절대로 필요하다고 결심했다. 그리하여 나는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 압박을 받으며 삼 년을 더 살았으나, 더는 아이를 낳지 않았다.\n\n이 동안 내 어머니는 자기의 지난 모험에 관한 옛이야기를 종종 들려주곤 했는데, 그러나 그것은 결코 내게 즐거운 일이 아니었다. 비록 그분이 분명한 말로 들려준 것은 아니었으나, 내가 직접 들은 것, 곧 내 첫 스승들에 관한 이야기와 합쳐 보면, 그분이 젊은 시절에 창녀이자 도둑이었음을 나는 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그분이 그 두 가지 모두를 진심으로 뉘우치며 살아왔으며, 이제는 매우 경건하고 절제 있고 신앙심 깊은 여인이 되었음을 정말로 믿었다.\n\n자, 그분의 지난 삶이 그때 어떠했든 간에, 분명한 것은 내 삶이 내게 매우 괴로웠다는 것이다. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 가장 고약한 종류의 매음 속에 살고 있었고, 그것에서 좋은 것을 기대할 수 없었던 만큼, 정말로 거기서 좋은 결말이 나오지도 않았으니, 나의 그 겉으로만 번지르르하던 번영은 모조리 닳아 없어져 비참과 파멸로 끝나고 말았다. 사실 그 지경에 이르기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸으니, 무슨 나쁜 운명에 이끌렸던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그 뒤로 우리에게는 모든 일이 어긋나기 시작했고, 더 나쁜 것은, 내 남편이 이상하리만치 달라져 제멋대로 굴고, 시샘이 많아지고, 매정해졌으며, 나 또한 그의 처사가 부당하고 무례한 만큼이나 그것을 견디지 못해 안달이 났다는 것이다. 이런 일들이 너무 멀리 나아간 나머지, 우리는 마침내 서로 너무도 사이가 나빠졌고, 그래서 나는 그가 내게 했던 한 가지 약속을 요구하기에 이르렀으니, 그것은 내가 그와 함께 잉글랜드를 떠나오는 데 동의했을 때 그가 기꺼이 나와 맺었던 약속, 곧 만약 내가 그 고장이 내게 맞지 않는다거나 거기 사는 것이 싫다는 것을 알게 되면, 그가 자기 일을 정리하도록 일 년 전에 미리 알리고서 내가 원할 때 다시 잉글랜드로 떠나올 수 있게 한다는 것이었다.\n\n다시 말해, 나는 이제 그에게 이 약속을 요구했는데, 솔직히 고백하건대 나는 그것을 세상에서 가장 정중한 말투로 하지도 않았다. 다만 나는 그가 나를 함부로 대했고, 내가 친구들에게서 멀리 떨어져 있어 나 자신에게 아무런 정당함도 베풀 수 없으며, 그가 까닭 없이 시샘을 부린다고 우겨 댔으니, 내 행실에는 나무랄 데가 없어 그가 그럴 구실이 전혀 없었으며, 잉글랜드로 옮겨 가면 그에게서 그럴 빌미를 모조리 없애 줄 것이라고 했다.\n\n나는 너무도 단호하게 그것을 우겨 댔으므로, 그는 자기 말을 지키든 어기든 둘 중 하나로 결판을 내지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 그리고 이것은, 그가 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨를 다 부리고, 자기 어머니며 그 밖의 사람들을 동원하여 내 결심을 돌리도록 나를 설득하려 했음에도 그러했다. 사실 그 일의 근원은 내 마음속에 있었고, 바로 그것이 그의 모든 노력을 헛되게 만들었으니, 내 마음은 남편으로서의 그에게서 떠나 있었기 때문이다. 나는 그와 한자리에 눕는다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 났고, 그가 나를 건드리지 못하게 하려고 병이며 기분이며 갖가지 핑계를 천 가지나 둘러댔으니, 무엇보다 그의 씨를 또 배는 것이 두려웠던 까닭이며, 그렇게 되면 내가 잉글랜드로 건너가는 것을 분명 가로막거나 적어도 늦출 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 마침내 나는 그의 비위를 어찌나 거슬렀던지, 그는 무모하고도 운명적인 결심을 품게 되었다. 요컨대 나를 잉글랜드에 보내지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그리고 비록 그가 내게 약속하기는 했으나, 내가 그것을 바라는 것은 당치 않은 일이고, 그것은 자기 사업을 망치고 온 집안을 뒤흔들어, 거의 자기를 세상에서 파멸시키는 짓이 될 터이니, 따라서 나는 그것을 그에게 요구해서는 안 되며, 제 집안과 남편의 번영을 소중히 여기는 아내라면 세상에 그 누구도 그런 일을 우겨 대지 않으리라고 했다.\n\n이 말이 나를 다시 곤경에 빠뜨렸으니, 내가 그 일을 차분히 헤아려 보고, 내 남편을 정말 있는 그대로—제 자식들을 위해 재산을 쌓는 본업에서 부지런하고 신중한 사람으로—여겨 보고, 또 그가 자기가 처한 그 끔찍한 정황에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것을 생각하니, 나는 내 제안이 매우 당치 않은 것이며, 제 집안의 안녕을 진심으로 마음에 둔 아내라면 결코 바라지 않았을 일임을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n그러나 내 불만은 다른 성질의 것이었다. 나는 더는 그를 남편으로 여기지 않았고, 가까운 친척으로, 내 친어머니의 아들로 여겼으며, 어떻게든 그에게서 벗어나기로 결심했으나, 어떤 방도로 그렇게 할지는 알 수 없었고, 그것이 가능할 것 같지도 않았다.\n\n심술궂은 세상은 우리 여자들을 두고, 우리가 어떤 일에 마음을 정하면 그 결심에서 돌이키게 하기란 불가능하다고들 말한다. 요컨대 나는 내 항해를 성사시킬 방도를 곰곰이 궁리하기를 결코 멈추지 않았고, 마침내 남편에게 그 없이 혼자 가겠다는 제안을 하는 데까지 이르렀다.\n이것이 그를 더없이 화나게 했고, 그는 나를 매정한 아내일 뿐 아니라 천륜을 저버린 어미라고 불렀으며, 어떻게 내가 두 아이를(하나는 죽었으므로) 어미 없이 남겨 두어 낯선 사람들 손에 길러지게 하고 두 번 다시 보지 않으려는 그런 생각을 끔찍해하지도 않고 품을 수 있느냐고 물었다. 만약 사정이 제대로 된 것이었다면 나도 그렇게 하지 않았으리라는 것은 사실이지만, 이제 그도 그 아이들도 두 번 다시 보지 않는 것이 내 진정한 바람이었다. 그리고 천륜을 저버렸다는 비난에 관해서는, 나는 그 모든 관계가 세상에서 가장 극심하게 천륜을 거스르는 것임을 아는 한, 나 자신에게 손쉽게 대답할 수 있었다.\n\n그러나 내 남편을 어떻게도 움직일 수 없다는 것만은 분명했다. 그는 나와 함께 가지도 않으려 했고, 나 혼자 가게 두지도 않으려 했으며, 내가 있던 그 고장의 사정을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 잘 알듯이, 그의 동의 없이 움직인다는 것은 전적으로 내 힘 밖의 일이었다.\n\n우리는 그 일로 집안싸움을 숱하게 벌였고, 그 싸움은 차츰 위험한 지경에까지 자라나기 시작했다. 내가 (그렇게 불리던) 남편에게 애정으로는 완전히 멀어져 있었던 까닭에, 나는 내 말을 조심하지 않고 때때로 그에게 부아를 돋우는 말을 했으며, 요컨대 그가 나와 갈라서게 하려고 할 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다 했으니, 그것이야말로 세상의 그 무엇보다도 내가 가장 바라던 일이었다.\n\n그는 내 처사를 무척 언짢게 여겼는데, 사실 그럴 만도 했으니, 끝내 나는 그와 한자리에 눕기를 거부했고, 사사건건 그 균열을 극단으로 몰고 갔던 까닭이다. 그는 한번은 내가 미친 것 같다고 하면서, 내가 행실을 고치지 않으면 나를 요양 보내겠다고 했으니, 다시 말해 정신병원에 넣겠다는 것이었다. 나는 그에게, 내가 미친 것과는 거리가 멀다는 것을 알게 될 것이며, 나를 죽이는 것은 그의 힘으로도, 그 어떤 악당의 힘으로도 할 수 없는 일이라고 말했다. 동시에 고백하건대, 나는 그가 나를 정신병원에 넣을 생각을 한다는 데에 진심으로 겁이 났으니, 그렇게 되면 어떤 계기가 생기든 간에 진실을 터뜨릴 모든 가능성이 단번에 무너질 터였기 때문이다. 그렇게 되면 그 누구도 그 말을 한마디도 곧이듣지 않을 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 이 일은, 무슨 결과가 닥치든 간에, 내 사정을 모조리 털어놓기로 결심하게 만들었다. 그러나 어떤 방도로 그것을 할지, 또 누구에게 할지는 풀 수 없는 난제였고, 결심하기까지 여러 달이 걸렸다. 그러는 사이 남편과 또 한 차례 다툼이 일어났는데, 그것이 어찌나 미친 듯한 극단으로 치달았던지, 나는 거의 그 모든 것을 그의 면전에 대고 다 말해 버릴 뻔했다. 그러나 비록 세세한 데까지는 가지 않도록 억눌렀지만, 나는 그를 더없는 혼란에 빠뜨릴 만큼은 말해 버렸고, 끝내는 모든 이야기를 끄집어내고 말았다.\n\n그는 내가 잉글랜드로 가려고 그토록 단호한 데 대해 차분히 따지는 것으로 말문을 열었다. 나는 그것을 변호했고, 모든 집안 다툼에서 으레 그렇듯이 거친 말 한마디가 또 다른 거친 말을 불러왔는데, 그는 나더러 자기를 남편 대하듯 대하지 않고, 자기 자식들을 두고 어미인 양 말하지도 않는다고 했다. 요컨대 나는 아내로서 대접받을 자격이 없으며, 자기는 나에게 가능한 온갖 좋은 방도를 다 써 보았고, 남편으로서 또 기독교인으로서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 온갖 다정함과 차분함으로 나를 타일렀건만, 나는 그에게 그토록 비열한 보답을 하여, 그를 사람이라기보다 개처럼, 남편이라기보다 가장 멸시받을 낯선 자처럼 대했다고 했다. 그리고 자기는 나에게 폭력을 쓰기가 무척 꺼려지지만, 요컨대 이제는 그럴 필요를 느끼며, 앞으로는 나를 본분으로 되돌릴 만한 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없게 되었다고 했다.\n\n이제 내 피는 극도로 끓어올랐으니, 비록 그가 한 말이 모두 옳다는 것을 알았어도, 그보다 더 격분한 모습은 없을 듯했다. 나는 그에게, 그의 좋은 방도든 고약한 방도든 나는 똑같이 경멸한다고 말했다. 잉글랜드로 가는 일에 관해서는, 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 그렇게 하기로 결심했고, 또 그를 남편 대하듯 하지 않고 자식들에게 어미 노릇을 보이지 않는 것에 관해서는, 거기에 그가 지금으로서는 헤아리지 못하는 무언가가 더 있을지도 모른다고 했다. 그러나 그가 더 깊이 생각해 보도록, 나는 이만큼은 말해 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼으니, 곧 그는 내 적법한 남편이 아니며 저들도 적법한 자식들이 아니고, 따라서 나는 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽도 이제껏 여겨 온 것보다 더 위하지 않을 까닭이 있다고 했다.\n\n고백하건대 나는 그 말을 하면서 그가 가엾어 마음이 움직였으니, 그가 죽은 사람처럼 핼쑥해져서 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 말없이 서 있었기 때문이며, 한두 번은 그가 그대로 까무러칠 것만 같았다. 요컨대 그것은 그를 거의 졸중 같은 발작에 빠뜨렸으니, 그는 부들부들 떨었고, 얼굴에서는 땀인지 이슬인지가 흘러내렸으나, 그러면서도 그는 흙덩이처럼 차가웠다. 그리하여 나는 달려가서 그의 목숨을 부지할 만한 무언가를 가져와야만 했다. 그가 거기서 회복되자 메스꺼워하며 토했고, 조금 뒤에 잠자리에 뉘어졌으며, 이튿날 아침에는, 실은 밤새 그러했듯이, 격렬한 열에 들떠 있었다.\n\n그러나 그것은 다시 가라앉았고 그는 회복되었으나, 그저 더디게 나아갔을 뿐이다. 그리고 그가 조금 차도가 있게 되자, 그는 내가 내 혀로 자기에게 치명적인 상처를 입혔다고 말하며, 해명을 요구하기에 앞서 한 가지만 묻고 싶다고 했다. 나는 그의 말을 가로막고, 내 말이 그를 그토록 어지럽게 만든 것을 보니 내가 그렇게까지 멀리 나간 것이 유감이라고 말하면서, 다만 내게 해명 이야기는 하지 말아 달라고 했으니, 그래 봐야 일을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이었기 때문이다.\n\n이 말이 그의 조바심을 한층 돋우었고, 사실 그를 견딜 수 없을 만큼 어지럽혀 놓았다. 이제 그는 아직 밝혀지지 않은 어떤 비밀이 있다고 의심하기 시작했으나, 그 실제 정황에 대해서는 조금도 짐작할 수가 없었다. 그의 머릿속을 온통 맴도는 것은, 내게 다른 남편이 살아 있다는 것뿐이었는데, 사실 그것이 사실이 아닐 수는 없다고 나는 말할 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 나는 그에게 거기에는 그런 것이 조금도 없다고 단언했다. 그리고 사실 내 다른 남편으로 말하자면, 그는 법적으로 내게 사실상 죽은 사람이었고, 나더러 자기를 그런 사람으로 여기라고 그가 말해 두었던 터라, 나는 그 점에 대해서는 조금도 거리낌이 없었다.\n\n그러나 이제 나는 그 일이 더 오래 감추기에는 너무 멀리 나갔다는 것을 알았고, 내 남편 스스로 그 비밀을 털어놓을 기회를 내게 마련해 주었으니, 나로서는 무척 흡족한 일이었다. 그는 서너 주 동안 나를 다그쳤으나 아무 소용이 없었고, 다만 내가 그 말들을 그저 내 격정의 발로로, 그를 격분시키려고 한 것인지, 아니면 그 밑바닥에 무언가 진실이 있는지를 말해 달라고 했을 뿐이다. 그러나 나는 한결같이 완강했고, 그가 먼저 내가 잉글랜드로 가는 데 동의하지 않는 한 아무것도 해명하지 않으려 했는데, 그는 자기가 살아 있는 한 결코 그러지 않겠다고 했다. 다른 한편으로 나는, 내가 원할 때면 그를 기꺼이 응하게 만들—아니, 그가 나더러 가 달라고 애원하게 만들—힘이 내게 있다고 말했다. 그리고 이것이 그의 호기심을 키워, 그를 더없이 끈질기게 졸라 대게 만들었으나, 그것은 모두 아무 소용이 없었다.\n\n마침내 그는 이 모든 이야기를 자기 어머니에게 털어놓고, 나에게서 그 핵심 비밀을 캐내라고 어머니를 부추겼으며, 그분은 정말로 내게 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨를 다 썼다. 그러나 나는, 이 일 전체의 까닭과 비밀이 바로 그분 자신에게 있으며, 그분을 위하는 마음 때문에 내가 그것을 감추어 왔노라고 말함으로써 그분을 단번에 딱 멈춰 세웠다. 그러고서 요컨대 나는 더는 나아갈 수 없으니, 그러므로 그것을 우겨 대지 말아 달라고 그분께 간곡히 청했다.\n\n그분은 이 암시에 말문이 막혀, 무어라 말해야 할지 무어라 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그분은 그 짐작을 내 술책쯤으로 제쳐 두고, 자기 아들을 위하여, 또 할 수만 있다면 우리 둘 사이의 균열을 메우려고 계속 졸라 댔다. 그것에 관해서, 나는 그것이 그분으로서는 참으로 좋은 뜻이지만 이루어지기란 불가능한 일이며, 만약 그분이 바라는 그 진실을 내가 드러낸다면, 그분도 그것이 불가능함을 인정하고 더는 바라지 않게 되리라고 말했다. 마침내 나는 그분의 끈질긴 청에 못 이긴 척하며, 더없이 중대한 비밀 하나를 그분께 감히 맡겨 보겠고, 그분도 곧 그것이 그러함을 알게 될 것이며, 만약 그분이 내 동의 없이는 그것을 아들에게 알리지 않겠다고 엄숙하게 약속해 준다면, 그것을 그분의 가슴속에 묻어 두는 데 동의하겠다고 말했다.\n\n그분은 그 대목을 약속하기까지 오래 머뭇거렸으나, 그 핵심 비밀에 다가가지 못하느니 차라리 그것에도 응하기로 했다. 그리고 그 밖에 숱한 예비적인 말들을 늘어놓은 끝에, 나는 입을 열어 모든 이야기를 그분께 들려주었다. 먼저 나는, 그분이 자기 자신의 이야기와 런던에서 쓰던 이름을 내게 들려준 것으로 인해, 자기 아들과 나 사이에 일어난 그 모든 불행한 균열에 그분이 얼마나 깊이 얽혀 있는지를 말했으며, 그분이 본 그 내 놀라움이 바로 그 때문이었다고 했다. 그러고서 나는 내 자신의 이야기와 내 이름을 말했고, 그분이 부인할 수 없는 그 밖의 여러 표시로써, 내가 다름 아니라 바로 그분 자신의 아이, 그분의 딸, 뉴게이트에서 그분의 몸을 빌려 태어난 바로 그 아이이며, 뱃속에 있음으로써 그분을 교수대에서 구해 낸 바로 그 아이, 그분이 유배될 때 이러저러한 사람들 손에 남겨 두고 떠난 바로 그 아이임을 단언했다.\n\n그분이 빠진 그 경악을 표현하기란 불가능하다. 그분은 그 이야기를 믿으려 들지도, 그 세세한 정황을 떠올리려 들지도 않았으니, 그로 인해 집안에 닥칠 혼란을 곧바로 내다보았기 때문이다. 그러나 모든 것이 그분이 자기 자신에 관해 내게 들려준 이야기들과 어찌나 정확히 들어맞던지—만약 그분이 그것을 내게 들려주지 않았더라면 아마 기꺼이 부인했을 그 이야기들과 말이다—그분은 제 입으로 제 입을 막아 버린 셈이 되어, 이제 그분이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 내 목을 끌어안고 입을 맞추며, 한참 동안 한마디도 못 한 채 나를 끌어안고 더없이 격렬하게 우는 것뿐이었다. 마침내 그분이 터뜨렸다. \"불행한 아이로구나!\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"무슨 비참한 운명이 너를 이리로 데려왔단 말이냐? 그것도 내 친아들의 품으로! 끔찍한 계집아이야,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"아니, 우리는 모두 끝장이로구나! 네 친오라비와 결혼하다니! 아이가 셋이고, 둘이 살아 있고, 모두 같은 살붙이라니! 내 아들과 내 딸이 부부로 한자리에 눕다니! 영영 온통 혼란과 광란뿐이로구나! 비참한 집안이로다! 우리는 어찌 될 것인가? 무슨 말을 해야 하나? 무엇을 해야 하나?\" 그러고서 그분은 한참 동안 이렇게 쏟아 냈다. 나로서는 입을 뗄 기력도 없었고, 설령 있었다 한들 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 한마디 한마디가 내 영혼을 후벼 팠기 때문이다. 이런 종류의 경악을 마음에 안은 채, 우리는 처음으로 헤어졌으나, 내 어머니가 나보다 더 놀랐으니, 그것이 나에게보다 그분에게 더 새로운 소식이었던 까닭이다. 그러나 헤어지면서 그분은 내게 다시 약속하기를, 우리가 그것을 다시 의논하기 전까지는 아들에게 그것에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않겠다고 했다.\n\n여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 우리가 같은 화제로 두 번째 회담을 갖기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그때 그분은, 마치 자기가 내게 들려준 자기 자신의 이야기를 잊고 싶기라도 한 듯, 또는 내가 그 세세한 정황 가운데 일부를 잊었으리라 짐작하기라도 한 듯, 그것을 군데군데 바꾸고 빼면서 들려주기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 그분의 기억을 일깨우고, 그분이 잊었으리라 짐작되는 여러 가지를 바로잡아 주었으며, 그러고는 그 모든 이야기를 어찌나 때맞춰 들이밀었던지, 그분으로서는 도저히 거기서 벗어날 수가 없었다. 그러자 그분은 다시 격정에 휩싸여, 자기 불운의 가혹함을 두고 탄식을 쏟아 냈다. 이런 일들이 그분에게서 조금 가라앉았을 때, 우리는 그 일을 내 남편에게 알리기에 앞서 먼저 무엇을 해야 할지를 두고 빈틈없는 논의에 들어갔다. 그러나 우리의 모든 의논이 무슨 소용이 있었겠는가? 우리 둘 다 그 일을 헤쳐 나갈 길도 보이지 않았고, 그에게 그런 장면을 펼쳐 보이는 것이 어떻게 안전할 수 있을지도 알 수 없었다. 그가 그것을 어떤 마음가짐으로 받아들일지, 또 그 일로 어떤 조치를 취할지 판단하거나 짐작하기란 불가능했다. 그리고 만약 그가 자제력이 너무도 모자라 그것을 공공연히 드러낸다면, 그것이 온 집안의 파멸이 될 것이며, 어머니와 나를 더없는 지경으로 망신시키리라는 것을 우리는 쉽게 내다보았다. 또 만약 그가 끝내 법이 그에게 줄 이점을 취한다면, 그는 나를 멸시하며 내쫓고 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 재산을 받으려고 소송이나 하게 내버려 둘 수 있을 터인데, 어쩌면 그것을 소송에 몽땅 탕진하고는 거지 신세가 될지도 모를 일이었다. 아이들도 그의 재산에 대해 아무런 법적 권리가 없으니 마찬가지로 파멸할 것이었다. 그리하여 나는 어쩌면 몇 달 안에 그가 다른 아내의 품에 안긴 것을 보게 되고, 나 자신은 살아 있는 가장 비참한 존재가 될 것이었다.\n\n내 어머니도 나만큼이나 이것을 절감하고 있었으며, 통틀어 우리는 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 얼마쯤 지나서 우리는 좀 더 차분한 결론에 이르렀으나, 그때는 또 이런 불행이 따랐으니, 곧 내 어머니의 의견과 내 의견이 서로 전혀 달랐고, 사실 서로 양립할 수 없었다는 것이다. 내 어머니의 의견은, 내가 이 모든 일을 통째로 묻어 두고, 어떤 다른 사건이 그 폭로를 더 알맞게 만들어 줄 때까지 그를 내 남편으로 삼아 계속 함께 살아야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그러는 동안 그분이 우리 둘을 다시 화해시켜 우리의 서로에 대한 위안과 집안의 평화를 되찾도록 애써 보겠으며, 우리가 예전처럼 한자리에 누워 그 모든 일이 죽음처럼 단단히 비밀로 남도록 하자는 것이었다. \"왜냐하면, 얘야,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"그것이 새어 나가면 우리는 둘 다 끝장이니까.\"\n\n나를 이 쪽으로 부추기려고, 그분은 자기 힘이 닿는 한 내 형편을 편안하게 해 주고, 자기가 죽을 때 내 남편과는 따로 떼어 내게 확보해 둘 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 남겨 주겠다고 약속했다. 그리하여 나중에 그것이 새어 나오더라도 내가 빈털터리로 남겨지지 않고, 내 두 발로 설 수 있으며 그에게서 정당함을 얻어 낼 수 있게 하겠다는 것이었다.\n\n이 제안은 비록 내 어머니로서는 무척 공정하고 다정한 것이었으나, 그 일에 대한 내 판단과는 도무지 맞지 않았다. 내 생각은 전혀 다른 쪽으로 흐르고 있었다.\n\n그 일을 우리 둘의 가슴속에만 간직하고 모든 것을 있는 그대로 내버려 두는 것에 관해서는, 나는 그것이 불가능하다고 그분께 말했다. 그리고 나는 그분께, 내 친오라비와 한자리에 눕는다는 생각을 내가 어찌 견딜 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 다음으로 나는, 그분이 살아 계신 것만이 이 폭로의 유일한 버팀목이며, 그분이 나를 자기 자식으로 인정하고 내가 그러함을 흡족히 여길 만한 까닭을 보는 한 다른 누구도 그것을 의심하지 않겠지만, 만약 그분이 그 폭로 전에 돌아가신다면, 나는 남편에게서 벗어나려고 그런 일을 꾸며 낸 뻔뻔한 계집으로 여겨지거나, 정신이 나가고 실성한 사람으로 취급되리라고 그분께 말했다. 그러고서 나는 그가 이미 나를 정신병원에 넣겠다고 위협했던 일, 또 그 일로 내가 얼마나 마음을 졸였던가, 그리고 바로 그것이 내가 한 대로 그분께 그것을 폭로할 수밖에 없도록 나를 내몬 까닭이었음을 그분께 말했다.\n\n이 모든 것으로부터 나는 그분께, 내가 이 일에 대해 할 수 있는 가장 진지한 숙고 끝에 이 결심에 이르렀으니, 그분이 양쪽의 절충안으로서 그것을 마음에 들어 하기를 바란다고 말했다. 곧, 그분이 자기 아들에게 애써 권하여, 내가 바란 대로 잉글랜드로 가도록 나를 허락하게 하고, 함께 가져갈 물품으로든 거기서 나를 부양할 어음으로든 내게 넉넉한 돈을 마련해 주게 하되, 그가 언젠가 내게로 건너오는 것이 알맞다고 여길지도 모른다는 것을 줄곧 넌지시 비치게 한다는 것이었다.\n\n그리고 내가 떠난 뒤에, 그분이 그때 가서 냉정한 마음으로, 그리고 먼저 그에게 가능한 가장 엄숙한 방식으로 비밀을 지키겠다는 다짐을 받아 낸 다음, 그 일을 그에게 드러내되, 그가 그것에 놀라 나를 두고든 그분을 두고든 어떤 격정이나 무모한 짓에 빠져들지 않도록, 차근차근, 그리고 그분 자신의 분별이 이끄는 대로 하라고 했다. 또한 그분이 마음을 써서, 그가 내가 죽었다는 확실한 소식을 듣지 않는 한, 그가 아이들을 소홀히 하거나 다시 결혼하는 것을 막아 달라고 했다.\n\n이것이 내 계책이었고, 내 이유들은 그럴듯했다. 나는 이 일들이 빚어낸 결과로 정말로 그에게서 마음이 떠나 있었으며, 사실 나는 남편으로서의 그를 죽도록 미워했고, 그에게 박힌 그 못 같은 혐오감을 떼어 내기란 불가능했다. 동시에 그것이 적법하지 못한 근친상간의 동거였다는 사실이 그 혐오감에 더해졌으니, 비록 내가 양심상으로는 그것에 대해 그리 크게 마음 쓰지 않았다 해도, 모든 것이 합쳐져 그와 함께 사는 것을 내게는 세상에서 가장 메스꺼운 일로 만들었다. 그리고 정말이지 나는, 그가 내게 그런 종류의 무언가를 청하게 두느니 차라리 거의 기꺼이 개를 끌어안는 편이 나을 지경에까지 이르렀다고 생각한다. 그런 까닭에 나는 그와 한 이불 속에 든다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다. 동시에 내가 그 일을 그에게 드러내기로 작정하지도 않은 채 그것을 그토록 멀리 끌고 간 것이, 처세상 옳았다고는 말할 수 없다. 그러나 나는 마땅히 어떠했어야 했는가, 어떠해서는 안 되었는가가 아니라, 무엇이 실제로 있었는가를 이야기하고 있는 것이다.\n\n서로 정반대인 의견 속에서 내 어머니와 나는 오랫동안 머물렀고, 우리의 판단을 일치시키기란 불가능했다. 우리는 그 일로 숱한 언쟁을 벌였으나, 우리 둘 중 어느 쪽도 자기 의견을 굽히지도, 상대를 자기 쪽으로 끌어오지도 결코 못했다.\n\n나는 내 친오라비와 한자리에 눕는 것에 대한 혐오감을 우겨 댔고, 그분은 그가 나를 자기 곁에서 떠나 잉글랜드로 가도록 동의하게 만들기란 불가능하다는 것을 우겨 댔다. 그리고 이 불확실함 속에서 우리는, 다투거나 그 비슷한 무엇에 이를 만큼 갈라서지는 않았으나, 우리 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 균열을 메우려면 무엇을 해야 할지 결단을 내리지 못한 채로 머물러 있었다.\n\n마침내 나는 한 가지 필사적인 방도를 정하고, 내 결심을 어머니에게 말했으니, 곧 요컨대 내가 그것을 그에게 직접 말하겠다는 것이었다. 내 어머니는 그 생각만으로도 더없이 겁에 질렸다. 그러나 나는 그분을 안심시키며, 차근차근, 부드럽게, 또 내가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨와 좋은 기분으로 그렇게 할 것이며, 시기 또한 되도록 잘 골라 그를 좋은 기분일 때 붙들겠다고 말했다. 나는, 만약 내가 실제 가진 것보다 더 많은 애정을 그에게 꾸며 보일 만큼 충분히 위선을 부릴 수만 있다면, 내 모든 계획에서 성공하여, 우리가 합의로, 또 원만한 약조로 갈라설 수 있으리라고 믿어 의심치 않는다고 했다. 비록 남편으로서는 못 하더라도, 오라비로서는 그를 그럭저럭 위할 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 내내 그는 자기 어머니에게 매달려, 앞서 내가 말한 그 끔찍한 표현—그가 그렇게 부른 대로—곧 내가 그의 적법한 아내가 아니며 내 아이들도 그의 적법한 자식들이 아니라는 그 말의 뜻이 가능하면 무엇인지 알아내려 했다. 내 어머니는 그를 따돌렸으니, 자기는 나에게서 어떤 해명도 끌어낼 수 없으나, 무언가 나를 몹시 괴롭히는 것이 있음을 알았으며, 머지않아 그것을 내게서 끌어내기를 바란다고 그에게 말했다. 그러면서 그러는 동안 그에게, 나를 더 다정하게 대하고 그 평소의 좋은 처신으로 나를 다독이라고 간곡히 권했으며, 그가 나를 정신병원에 보내겠다는 위협 따위로 나를 겁주고 떨게 한 일을 들어, 어떤 까닭으로든 여자를 절망에 빠뜨리지 말라고 충고했다.\n\n그는 자기 행동을 누그러뜨리겠다고 그분께 약속했고, 자기가 여전히 나를 사랑하며, 격정에 못 이겨 무어라고 말했든 간에 나를 정신병원에 보낼 그런 생각은 없다는 것을 내게 확실히 전해 달라고 했다. 또 그는 내 어머니에게, 우리의 애정이 되살아나 우리가 예전처럼 좋은 양해 속에 한자리에 누울 수 있도록, 나에게도 똑같은 설득을 해 달라고 청했다.\n\n나는 곧바로 이 화해의 효과를 보았다. 내 남편의 처신은 즉시 달라졌고, 그는 내게 완전히 다른 사람이 되었다. 그가 사사건건 내게 보이는 태도보다 더 다정하고 더 정성스러울 수는 없었다. 나로서도 거기에 얼마간 보답하지 않을 수 없어 되도록 그렇게 했으나, 그것은 잘해야 어색한 모양새일 뿐이었으니, 그의 애무보다 더 끔찍한 것은 내게 없었고, 그의 씨를 또 밴다는 걱정이 나를 발작에 빠뜨릴 지경이었기 때문이다. 그리고 이것이 나로 하여금, 더는 미루지 말고 그 일을 그에게 터놓을 절대적인 필요가 있음을 알게 했으니, 그러나 나는 그것을 상상할 수 있는 온갖 신중함과 조심성으로 했다.\n\n그는 거의 한 달 가까이 나에게 달라진 처신을 이어 갔고, 우리는 서로 새로운 종류의 삶을 살기 시작했다. 만약 내가 그것을 계속해 나가도 좋다고 스스로 흡족해할 수만 있었다면, 그것은 우리가 함께 살아 있는 한 이어졌으리라고 나는 믿는다. 어느 날 저녁, 우리 집에서 정원으로 나가는 입구에 정자 노릇을 하던 자그마한 차양 아래 함께 앉아 무척 다정하게 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 그는 매우 즐겁고 흐뭇한 기분에 잠겨, 우리의 지금 이 좋은 화합의 기쁨과 지난 균열의 어지러움에 관해, 또 우리가 두 번 다시 그런 일을 겪지 않으리라고 바랄 여지가 있다는 것이 자기에게 얼마나 큰 흡족함인지에 관해, 다정한 말을 한껏 내게 늘어놓았다.\n\n나는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고서, 우리가 늘 지켜 온 그 좋은 화합에서 나보다 더 기뻐할 사람도, 그 균열에 나보다 더 마음 아파할 사람도, 또 앞으로도 그러할 사람도 세상에 없다고 그에게 말했다. 다만 안타깝게도 우리 처지에는 한 가지 불행한 정황이 있어, 그것이 내 가슴 너무도 가까이 박혀 있고 그것을 어떻게 그에게 터놓아야 할지 알 수 없으며, 그것이 내 몫의 화합을 무척 비참하게 만들고 나머지 모든 위안마저 내게서 앗아 간다고 했다.\n\n그는 그것이 무엇인지 말해 달라고 졸랐다. 나는 그것을 어떻게 말해야 할지 알 수 없다고 했으니, 그것이 그에게 감추어져 있는 동안에는 나 혼자만 불행하지만, 만약 그도 그것을 알게 되면 우리 둘 다 그렇게 될 터이며, 따라서 그것에 관해 그를 어둠 속에 두는 것이야말로 내가 할 수 있는 가장 다정한 일이라고 했다. 그리고 바로 그 까닭으로만 나는 그에게 비밀을 간직하는 것인데, 그 비밀을 간직하는 일 자체가 머지않아 내 파멸이 되리라고 나는 생각한다고 했다.\n\n이 이야기에 그가 빠진 놀라움과, 그것을 자기에게 털어놓으라며 그가 내게 부린 갑절의 채근을 표현하기란 불가능하다. 그는 만약 내가 그것을 자기에게 감춘다면, 나를 자기에게 다정하다고 할 수 없으며, 아니, 충실하다고도 할 수 없다고 했다. 나는 나도 그렇게 생각한다고, 그러면서도 그것을 할 수가 없다고 했다. 그는 앞서 내가 자기에게 한 말로 되돌아가, 그것이 내가 격정에 못 이겨 한 말과 관련된 것이 아니기를 바라며, 자기는 그 모든 것을 무모하고 격분한 마음의 소산으로 여겨 잊기로 작정했다고 했다. 나는 나도 그 모든 것을 잊을 수 있기를 바라지만, 그것은 될 일이 아니며, 그 흔적이 너무 깊이 박혀 도저히 그럴 수가 없으니, 불가능하다고 했다.\n\n그러자 그는, 자기는 어떤 일로도 나와 다투지 않기로 작정했으며, 따라서 그 일로 더는 나를 채근하지 않고 내가 무엇을 하든 무슨 말을 하든 그대로 받아들이기로 했다고 하면서, 다만 그것이 무엇이든 간에 더는 우리의 평온과 우리 서로에 대한 다정함을 어지럽히지 않기로 그때 합의해 달라고만 청했다.\n\n이것은 그가 내게 할 수 있는 말 가운데 가장 부아를 돋우는 것이었으니, 사실 나는 그의 더한 채근을 바라고 있었기 때문이다. 그래야 정말로 감추는 것이 내게 죽음과도 같던 그것을 끄집어내도록 못 이긴 척할 수 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그에게 솔직하게, 채근받지 않게 되어 기쁘다고는 할 수 없으나, 그렇다고 어떻게 응해야 할지도 모르겠다고 대답했다. \"그런데 여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"이 일을 당신에게 터놓는다면, 당신은 나와 어떤 조건을 맺어 주겠어요?\"\n\n\"세상의 그 어떤 조건이든,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 이치에 맞게 내게 바랄 수 있는 것이라면 다 좋소.\" \"좋아요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그럼, 이렇게 당신 손으로 적어 주세요. 만약 당신이 내게 아무런 잘못이 없고, 내가 앞으로 닥칠 그 불행의 원인에 스스로 즐겨 가담한 것이 아니라는 것을 알게 되면, 나를 탓하지도, 나를 더 나쁘게 대하지도, 내게 어떤 해도 끼치지도, 내 잘못이 아닌 일로 나를 고통받게 하지도 않겠다고요.\"\n\n\"그것이야말로,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"세상에서 가장 이치에 맞는 요구로구려. 당신 잘못이 아닌 일로 당신을 탓하지 않겠다니 말이오. 펜과 잉크를 가져다주오.\" 하고 그가 말했다. 그래서 나는 안으로 달려 들어가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 가져왔고, 그는 내가 제안한 바로 그 말 그대로 그 조건을 적은 다음, 자기 이름으로 서명했다. \"자,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"다음은 무엇이오, 여보?\"\n\n\"그게요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"다음은, 내가 그 비밀을 알기 전에 그것을 당신에게 드러내지 않았다고 해서 나를 탓하지 않겠다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"그것도 지극히 옳소,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"기꺼이 그러리다.\" 그러고서 그는 그것도 적고서 서명했다.\n\n\"좋아요, 여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그럼 당신과 맺을 조건이 하나 더 있어요. 그건, 이 일에 관련된 사람은 당신과 나뿐이니, 당신 어머니 말고는 세상 어느 누구에게도 그것을 드러내지 않겠다는 거예요. 그리고 그 폭로로 인해 당신이 취할 모든 조치에서, 나도 당신과 똑같이 거기 얽혀 있되 당신만큼이나 결백한 만큼, 당신은 내가 알고 동의하지 않고서는 격정에 휩싸여 아무 일도 하지 않고, 나에게 해가 되는 일도, 당신 어머니에게 해가 되는 일도 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 거예요.\"\n\n이 말이 그를 좀 놀라게 했고, 그는 그 말을 또박또박 적었으나, 서명하기 전에 그것을 몇 번이고 거듭 읽으며 여러 번 머뭇거렸고, 그것을 되뇌었다. \"내 어머니에게 해가 된다고! 또 당신에게 해가 된다고! 이게 도대체 무슨 알 수 없는 일일까?\" 그러나 마침내 그는 서명했다.\n\n\"좋아요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"여보, 당신 손으로 적어 달라고 더는 청하지 않을게요. 다만 당신은 아마 세상의 그 어떤 집안에 일찍이 닥쳤던 것 가운데 가장 뜻밖이고 가장 놀라운 일을 듣게 될 테니, 부디 그것을 침착하게, 또 분별 있는 사람에게 어울리는 마음가짐으로 받아들이겠다고 약속해 주세요.\"\n\n\"내 힘껏 그러리다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"다만 더는 나를 애태우지 않는다는 조건으로 말이오. 이 모든 사전 절차로 당신은 나를 겁나게 하는구려.\"\n\n\"그럼, 좋아요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"이거예요. 앞서 내가 격해서 당신에게, 내가 당신의 적법한 아내가 아니고 우리 아이들도 적법한 자식들이 아니라고 말했듯이, 이제 나는 당신에게 차분하게, 또 다정하게, 그러나 그만큼의 비통함을 안고 알려야겠어요. 나는 당신의 친누이이고, 당신은 내 친오라비이며, 우리는 둘 다 지금 살아 계셔 이 집에 있는 우리 어머니의 자식들이고, 그분은 부인하거나 반박할 수 없는 방식으로 그것이 진실임을 확신하고 계세요.\"\n\n나는 그가 핼쑥해지고 얼이 빠진 표정을 짓는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 말했다. \"이제 당신의 약속을 기억하고, 그것을 침착한 마음으로 받아들여요. 내가 한 것보다 더, 당신을 이 일에 대비시키려고 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있었겠어요?\" 그러나 나는 하인을 불러 그에게 럼주를 작은 잔으로 한 잔 가져오게 했으니(그것이 그 고장에서 흔히 마시는 독한 술이다), 그가 막 까무러치려던 참이었기 때문이다. 그가 조금 정신을 차리자 나는 그에게 말했다. \"이 이야기는, 당신도 분명 알겠지만, 긴 해명이 필요한 일이에요. 그러니 참고서 마음을 가다듬어 끝까지 들어요. 나는 되도록 그것을 짧게 할게요.\" 그러고서 나는 그 사실 가운데 필요하다고 여긴 것을, 특히 내 어머니가 어떻게 그것을 내게 드러내게 되었는지를 앞서 말한 대로 그에게 들려주었다. \"그러니 이제, 여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신은 내가 조건을 단 까닭을, 또 내가 이 일의 원인이 된 적도 없고 그럴 수도 없었으며, 지금 이전에는 그것에 대해 아무것도 알 수 없었다는 것을 알게 될 거예요.\"\n\n\"나도 그것은 충분히 납득하오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그러나 이건 내게 끔찍한 충격이오. 하지만 나는 이 모든 것에 대한 해결책을 알고 있고, 당신이 잉글랜드로 가지 않고도 당신의 어려움을 끝낼 해결책을 알고 있소.\" \"그건 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"참 이상한 일이겠네요.\" \"아니, 아니오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 그것을 쉽게 만들어 주리다. 그것을 가로막는 사람은 나 말고는 아무도 없으니까.\" 그는 이 말을 하면서 좀 흐트러진 기색이었으나, 나는 그때는 그 말에서 아무것도 짐작하지 못했다. 흔히들 하는 말마따나, 그런 일을 하는 자들은 결코 그것을 입에 올리지 않으며, 그런 일을 입에 올리는 자들은 결코 그것을 하지 않는다고 믿었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 일은 아직 그에게서 극에 다다른 것이 아니었으니, 나는 그가 시름에 잠겨 우울해지는 것을 보았고, 한마디로 내가 보기에 머리가 조금 이상해진 것 같았다. 나는 그를 다독여 진정시키고, 이 일에서 우리가 처신할 일종의 방안에 대해 이치를 따져 가며 그를 설득해 보려 애썼다. 때때로 그는 멀쩡하여, 그 일을 두고 얼마간 용기를 내어 이야기하기도 했다. 그러나 그 무게가 그의 생각을 너무도 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 요컨대 일이 너무 멀리 나아가 그는 자기 자신에게 위해를 가하려 했으며, 그 가운데 한 번은 실제로 목을 매었으니, 만약 바로 그 순간에 그의 어머니가 방에 들어오지 않았더라면 죽고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그분은 흑인 하인의 도움으로 그를 끌어 내려 살려 냈다.\n\n이제 집안은 통탄할 지경에 이르렀다. 그에 대한 내 연민이 이제 처음에 내가 정말로 그에게 품었던 그 애정을 되살리기 시작했고, 나는 진심으로, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 다정한 처신으로 그 균열을 메우려 애썼다. 그러나 요컨대 그것은 너무도 크게 부풀어 올라, 그의 기운을 갉아먹었고, 그를 길고 더디게 시들어 가는 폐병에 빠뜨렸으니, 다만 그것이 치명적인 데까지는 이르지 않았을 뿐이다. 이 곤경 속에서 나는 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 그의 목숨이 눈에 띄게 기울어 가고 있었던 까닭이며, 어쩌면 나는 거기서 다시 결혼하여 내게 무척 이로울 수도 있었을 것이다. 분명 그 고장에 머무는 것이 내가 할 일이었으나, 내 마음 역시 들떠 있고 편치 않았다. 나는 잉글랜드로 오기를 갈망했고, 그것 없이는 아무것도 나를 만족시키지 못했다.\n\n요컨대 지칠 줄 모르는 채근으로, 내가 본 대로 눈에 띄게 쇠해 가던 내 남편은 마침내 설득되었다. 그리하여 나 자신의 운명이 나를 떠밀어 길이 내게 환히 열렸고, 어머니도 거기에 동의하여, 나는 잉글랜드로 오는 데 쓸 아주 좋은 짐을 얻어 냈다.\n\n내 오라비(이제 나는 그를 그렇게 불러야 한다)와 헤어질 때, 우리는 내가 도착한 뒤에 그가 내가 잉글랜드에서 죽었다는 소식을 받은 척하여, 자기가 원할 때 다시 결혼할 수 있게 하기로 합의했다. 그는 나와 누이로서 서신을 주고받고, 내가 사는 동안 나를 돕고 부양하기로 약속하고 다짐했다. 또 만약 자기가 나보다 먼저 죽으면, 자기 어머니에게 넉넉한 것을 남겨, 여전히 누이라는 이름으로 나를 보살피게 하겠다고 했다. 그리고 그는 내 소식을 들으면 어떤 면으로는 나를 챙겨 주었으나, 그것이 어찌나 이상하게 처리되었던지, 나는 뒤에 그 실망을 무척 절절히 느꼈으니, 그것은 때가 되면 여러분이 듣게 될 것이다.\n\n나는 그 고장에서 여덟 해를 지낸 뒤, 팔월에 잉글랜드를 향해 떠나왔다. 그리고 이제 새로운 불운의 장면이 나를 기다리고 있었으니, 그것은 아마 평생을 두고 그것을 겪어 본 여자가 거의 없을 만한 것이었다.\n\n우리는 잉글랜드 해안에 막 다다를 때까지 그럭저럭 괜찮은 항해를 했고, 서른두 날 만에 거기 이르렀으나, 그때 두세 차례의 폭풍에 시달렸다. 그 가운데 하나가 우리를 아일랜드 해안으로 떠밀어, 우리는 킨세일에 입항했다. 우리는 거기서 열사흘쯤 머물며 뭍에서 얼마간 원기를 회복하고 다시 바다로 나갔으나, 또다시 매우 험한 날씨를 만났고, 그 속에서 배가, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면, 큰돛대가 부러졌으니, 나는 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 알지 못했다. 그러나 우리는 마침내 웨일스의 밀퍼드헤이븐으로 들어갔는데, 비록 그곳이 우리 항구에서 멀리 떨어져 있긴 했어도, 내 발을 내 고국, 브리튼섬의 굳은 땅 위에 무사히 디딘 터라, 나는 나에게 그토록 끔찍했던 그 물 위에 두 번 다시 그것을 내맡기지 않기로 결심했다. 그래서 나는 내 옷가지와 돈을, 또 내 선하증권과 그 밖의 서류를 뭍에 내려놓고서, 런던으로 가기로 결심했고, 배는 제 항구로 알아서 가도록 내버려 두었다. 배가 향하던 항구는 브리스틀이었는데, 거기에 내 오라비의 주된 거래처가 살고 있었다.\n\n나는 한 삼 주 만에 런던에 이르렀고, 얼마 뒤에 거기서 그 배가 브리스틀에 도착했다는 것을 들었으나, 동시에 그 배가 겪은 험한 날씨와 큰돛대가 부러진 일로 인해 선상에 큰 손상을 입었으며, 그 짐의 큰 몫이 못 쓰게 되었다는 불운한 소식을 알게 되었다.\n\n이제 나는 내 손에 새로운 인생의 장면을 떠안았는데, 그것은 끔찍한 모습을 띠고 있었다. 나는 일종의 마지막 작별을 고하고 떠나온 터였다. 내가 가지고 온 것은, 만약 그것이 무사히 왔더라면 정말로 상당한 것이었고, 그 도움으로 나는 그런대로 잘 다시 결혼할 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 사정이 이러하니, 나는\n전체 자산이 다 합쳐 봐야 이삼백 파운드 남짓으로 줄어들었고, 그나마도 다시 채울 가망이 전혀 없었다. 나는 완전히 벗 하나 없는 신세였으니, 아니, 아는 사람조차 하나 없었다. 옛 친지들을 다시 살려 내지 않는 것이 절대적으로 필요하다는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다. 또 예전에 나를 재산가 행세 시켜 주었던 그 약삭빠른 벗으로 말하자면, 내가 사람을 시켜 은밀히 알아보게 한 바로는, 그녀는 죽었고 그 남편 또한 죽었다고 했다.\n\n얼마 지나지 않아 내 화물을 챙기러 가야 할 일이 생겨 브리스틀로 길을 떠나게 되었는데, 그 일을 처리하는 동안 나는 바스로 가는 기분 전환을 누렸다. 나는 아직 늙으려면 멀었고, 늘 명랑하던 내 기질도 여전히 극단적이라 할 만큼 명랑함을 잃지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 비록 재산 없는 여자였으나 마치 재산가 여자인 양 처신하고 있던 터라, 예전에 그랬듯이 내 처지를 나아지게 해 줄 무슨 일인가가 내 길에 일어날지도 모른다고 기대했다.\n\n바스는 풍류가 넘치는 곳이었다. 돈이 많이 들고, 함정으로 가득한 곳이었다. 나는 사실 무엇이든 기회가 닿는 대로 잡아 보겠다는 속셈으로 그곳에 갔으나, 나 자신을 위해 공정하게 밝혀 두자면, 나쁜 짓이라곤 전혀 염두에 두지 않았다고 단언해야겠다. 나는 오로지 정직한 방향만을 생각했고, 처음에는 훗날 내가 그렇게 끌려가도록 내버려 둔 그쪽으로 향하는 생각이라곤 조금도 품지 않았다.\n\n이곳에서 나는 그들이 말하는 이른바 후반 사교철 내내 머물렀는데, 불운한 친지 몇을 사귀었으니, 그들은 훗날 내가 빠져든 어리석은 짓들에 맞서도록 나를 다잡아 주기는커녕 오히려 그 짓들을 부추겼다. 나는 제법 즐겁게 살았고, 좋은 무리와, 다시 말해 명랑하고 멋들어진 무리와 어울렸다. 그러나 이런 식의 생활이 나를 엄청나게 가라앉히고 있다는 것을, 또 정해진 수입이 없는 처지라 밑천을 헐어 쓰는 것은 일종의 확실한 출혈사에 다름없다는 것을 깨닫는 낙심이 따랐다. 이것은 다른 생각들 사이사이에 내게 많은 서글픈 성찰을 안겨 주었다. 그러나 나는 그 생각들을 떨쳐 내고, 그래도 무슨 일인가가 내게 이롭게 일어날지 모른다며 여전히 스스로를 다독였다.\n\n그러나 나는 그러기에는 잘못된 곳에 있었다. 이제 내가 있는 곳은 레드리프가 아니었으니, 거기서라면 내가 그럭저럭 살림을 차려 놓았을 경우 어떤 정직한 선장쯤이 명예로운 혼인의 조건을 걸고 내게 말을 걸어왔을 수도 있었다. 그러나 나는 바스에 있었고, 거기서 남자들은 더러 정부情婦를 구할지언정 아내를 찾는 일은 매우 드물었다. 따라서 여자가 그곳에서 사귈 수 있다고 기대할 만한 특별한 친지란 모두 그쪽으로 기우는 어떤 성향을 띠게 마련이었다.\n\n첫 사교철은 그런대로 잘 보냈다. 비록 기분 전환차 바스에 온 어느 신사와 친분을 트긴 했어도, 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 중범죄적 거래에는 발을 들이지 않았기 때문이다. 나는 우연히 던져진 몇몇 풍류의 제안을 물리쳤고, 그쪽으로는 그런대로 잘 처신했다. 나는 단지 악덕 그 자체를 위해 그 죄에 빠져들 만큼 사악하지는 않았고, 또 내가 원하던 그 본질적인 것으로 나를 유혹할 만한 특별한 제안을 받지도 않았다.\n\n그러나 첫 사교철에 이만큼은 나아갔으니, 곧 내가 묵던 집의 안주인인 어떤 여자와 친분을 텄던 것이다. 그 여자는 우리가 말하는 이른바 못된 집을 운영하지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 자기 자신이 그리 훌륭한 원칙을 지닌 사람도 아니었다. 나는 어떤 경우에도 무슨 까닭으로든 내 평판에 조금의 흠집도 입지 않을 만큼 처신을 잘해 왔고, 내가 어울린 남자들은 모두 평판이 너무나 좋아서, 그들과 어울린다 하여 조금의 손가락질도 받지 않았다. 또 그들 가운데 누구도, 설령 사악한 관계를 청했더라도 그럴 여지가 있다고 여기는 듯하지 않았다. 그러나 앞서 말한 한 신사가 있었으니, 그는 늘 자기 말마따나 내 동반의 즐거움을 위해 나를 콕 집어 따로 골라냈고, 그것이 자기에게 매우 흡족하다고 기꺼이 말하곤 했으나, 그때까지는 그 이상의 것은 없었다.\n\n무리가 떠나고 나자 바스에서 나는 우울한 시간을 많이 보냈다. 비록 내 물품을 처분하고 돈을 다시 채우려고 이따금 브리스틀에 다녀오긴 했어도, 거처로는 바스로 돌아오기를 택했으니, 여름에 내가 묵던 집의 여자와 사이가 좋았던 덕에 겨우내 다른 어느 곳보다 오히려 더 싸게 지낼 수 있다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 여기서, 다시 말하건대, 나는 가을을 명랑하게 보냈던 만큼이나 겨울을 무겁게 보냈다. 그러나 내가 묵던 집의 그 여자와 더 가까운 친밀함을 맺은 터라, 내 마음을 가장 짓누르던 것들, 특히 내 형편의 옹색함과, 바다에서 물품이 상하는 바람에 재산을 잃은 일을 그녀에게 어느 정도 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 또한 버지니아에 넉넉하게 사는 어머니와 오라비가 있다고 말했다. 그리고 실제로 어머니에게 따로 편지를 써 보내 내 처지와, 거의 오백 파운드에 달하던 그 막대한 손실을 알린 터였으므로, 거기서 도움을 받기를 기대하고 있다는 사실도 새 벗에게 빠짐없이 알렸고, 사실 그러기도 했다. 또 배들이 브리스틀에서 버지니아의 요크강으로 갔다가 다시 돌아오는 데에 대개 런던에서 가는 것보다 시간이 덜 걸렸고, 내 오라비도 주로 브리스틀에서 서신을 주고받았으므로, 나는 아는 사람이라곤 조금도 없는 런던으로 가느니 여기서 내 회신을 기다리는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했다.\n\n새 벗은 내 처지에 마음으로 깊이 마음 쓰는 듯했고, 실로 너무도 친절하여 겨우내 그녀 집에서 지내는 비용을 너무나 낮은 값으로 깎아 주었으니, 그 정도면 그녀가 나로 인해 아무것도 벌지 못한다는 것을 내게 납득시켰다. 그리고 숙박료로 말하자면, 겨우내 나는 한 푼도 내지 않았다.\n\n봄철이 다가오자 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 계속 내게 친절하게 굴었고, 나는 한동안 그녀 집에 묵었으니, 달리 해야 할 필요가 생길 때까지였다. 그녀 집에는 신분 있는 사람들이 자주 묵었는데, 특히 앞서 말했듯 지난겨울 나를 자기 동반으로 콕 집어 골라냈던 그 신사가 있었다. 그가 다시 내려왔는데, 다른 신사 한 명을 일행으로, 또 하인 둘을 데리고 와서 같은 집에 묵었다. 나는 안주인이 내가 아직 자기 집에 있다는 것을 알려 그를 그리로 초대한 것이 아닌가 의심했으나, 그녀는 그것을 부인하며 자기는 그러지 않았다고 내게 맹세했고, 그도 같은 말을 했다.\n\n한마디로, 이 신사는 내려와서 대화뿐 아니라 자신의 각별한 신뢰의 대상으로도 나를 계속 콕 집어 골라냈다. 그가 완벽한 신사였다는 것은 인정해야겠고, 그의 동반은 내게 매우 흡족했으며, 그의 말을 믿어도 된다면 내 동반 또한 그에게 그러했다. 그는 내게 각별한 존경 외에는 아무런 마음도 고백하지 않았고, 내 덕성에 대해 어찌나 높이 여겼던지, 그가 종종 공언했듯이, 만약 자기가 그 외의 다른 무엇을 청한다면 내가 경멸하며 그를 물리치리라고 믿었다. 그는 곧 내게서, 내가 과부라는 것, 지난번 배편으로 버지니아에서 브리스틀에 도착했다는 것, 그리고 상당한 자산이 도착하기를 기대하며 다음 버지니아 선단이 들어올 때까지 바스에서 기다리고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 나는 그에게서, 또 그를 아는 다른 이들에게서, 그에게 아내가 있으나 그 부인이 정신이 온전치 못하여 자기 친정 친척들의 보살핌 아래 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 그것에 동의한 것은, 그런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯, 아내의 치료를 잘못 관리했다고 자기에게 쏟아질 수 있는 비난을 피하기 위함이었고, 그러는 동안 그는 그런 우울한 사정의 심란함에서 마음을 돌리려고 바스에 온 것이었다.\n\n내 안주인은 제 발로 나서서 온갖 경우에 이 교제를 부추겼으니, 그를 명예와 덕을 갖춘 사람이자 또한 큰 재산가라고 내게 유리하게 평해 주었다. 그리고 실로 나도 그를 그렇게 말할 까닭이 충분했다. 우리 둘이 같은 층에 묵었고, 그가 자주 내 방에 들어왔으니 심지어 내가 잠자리에 들었을 때에도 그러했고, 나 또한 그가 잠자리에 들었을 때 그의 방에 들어갔으나, 그는 입맞춤 이상의 그 무엇도 내게 청한 적이 없었고, 앞으로 여러분이 듣게 되겠지만 그로부터 한참 뒤까지 무슨 일을 하자고 조르는 일조차 없었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 그의 지나친 정숙함을 안주인에게 자주 언급했고, 그녀는 또 처음부터 그러했으리라 믿는다고 내게 말하곤 했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 내가 동반해 준 대가로 그에게서 어떤 사례를 기대해야 마땅하다고 말하곤 했으니, 실로 그가 말하자면 나를 독차지하다시피 했고 나는 좀처럼 그의 곁을 떠나지 않았기 때문이다. 나는 그가 내가 그것을 원한다거나 그에게서 그것을 받겠다고 생각할 만한 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없다고 그녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 그 일을 자기가 맡겠다고 했고, 실제로 그렇게 했으며, 어찌나 능란하게 일을 처리했던지, 그녀가 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤 우리 둘만 처음 같이 있게 되었을 때, 그가 내 형편을 조금 캐묻기 시작했으니, 곧 뭍에 오른 뒤로 어떻게 지내 왔는지, 돈이 부족하지는 않은지 따위였다. 나는 매우 당당하게 버텼다. 내 담뱃잎 화물이 상하기는 했으나 아주 잃어버린 것은 아니라고, 내가 위탁받았던 상인이 나를 위해 너무도 정직하게 처리해 주어 부족함이 없었다고, 그리고 알뜰하게 꾸려 나가면 다음 선단 편에 기대하는 더 많은 물품이 올 때까지 버틸 수 있으리라 바란다고 말했다. 또 그동안 지출을 줄여, 지난 사교철에는 하녀를 두었으나 이제는 없이 산다고, 또 그때는 그도 알다시피 일층에 침실과 식당을 두었으나 이제는 계단 두 층을 올라간 방 하나만 둔다는 둥의 이야기를 했다. \"그래도 나는,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"지금도 그때만큼이나 흡족하게 살고 있어요.\" 그러면서 그의 동반이 안 그랬을 경우보다 훨씬 더 명랑하게 살게 해 준 방편이 되었으니 그 점은 그에게 무척 고맙다고 덧붙였다. 그렇게 하여 나는 당장은 무슨 제안이 들어올 여지를 모두 밀어내 버렸다. 그러나 머지않아 그는 다시 나를 공략하며, 내가 내 형편의 속사정을 그에게 믿고 털어놓기를 꺼리는 것을 알았는데 그것이 안타깝다고 말했다. 그러면서 자기가 그것을 캐물은 것은 결코 자기 호기심을 채우려는 의도가 아니라 오로지, 만약 필요한 일이 있다면, 나를 돕기 위함이었다고 단언했다. 그러나 내가 어떤 도움도 필요하다고 인정하려 들지 않으니, 자기에게 더 바라는 것은 단 한 가지뿐이라며, 그것은 내가 어떤 식으로든 궁색해지거나 그리될 성싶을 때 솔직하게 그것을 자기에게 말해 주고, 또 자기가 제안한 그 거리낌 없는 마음 그대로 자기를 부려 달라고 약속해 달라는 것이라고 했다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, 비록 내가 그를 믿기를 두려워했을지언정, 내게 진정한 벗이 있다는 것을 언제나 알게 될 것이라고 했다.\n\n나는 한없이 큰 은혜를 입은 사람으로서 마땅히 해야 할 말이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않고, 그의 친절을 합당하게 헤아리고 있음을 그에게 알렸다. 그리고 실로 그때부터 나는 전처럼 그에게 그리 서먹하게 굴지 않았으나, 여전히 양쪽 다 가장 엄격한 덕의 테두리 안에 머물렀다. 그러나 우리 대화가 아무리 거리낌 없었어도, 나는 그가 바라던 그런 종류의 거리낌 없음, 곧 그에게 돈이 필요하다고 말하는 데까지는 다다를 수 없었으니, 속으로는 그의 제안이 매우 기쁘면서도 그러했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤 몇 주가 지났으나, 나는 여전히 그에게 돈을 청한 적이 없었다. 그러던 차에 교활한 인물인 내 안주인이 종종 내게 그렇게 하라고 졸랐으나 내가 그러지 못하는 것을 알고는, 제가 지어낸 이야기 하나를 꾸며서, 우리가 함께 있을 때 불쑥 내게 들어왔다. \"어머, 과부댁!\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 안 좋은 소식을 전해 드릴 게 있어요.\" \"무슨 일인데요?\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"버지니아 배들이 프랑스인들에게 나포되기라도 했나요?\" 그것이 내 걱정거리였기 때문이다. \"아뇨, 아뇨,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"어제 돈 가지러 브리스틀에 보내신 그 사람이 돌아왔는데, 한 푼도 못 가져왔다지 뭐예요.\"\n\n이제 나는 그녀의 꿍꿍이가 도무지 마음에 들지 않았다. 그것은 그를 너무 부추기는 것처럼 보였는데, 실로 그는 부추길 필요가 없는 사람이었고, 또 내가 청하기를 꺼린다 하여 잃을 것이 전혀 없다는 것을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있었으므로, 그녀의 말을 짧게 잘랐다. \"그 사람이 왜 당신한테 그런 소릴 했는지 도무지 모르겠네요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"장담컨대 그 사람은 내가 가져오라 한 돈을 죄다 가져왔고, 여기 이렇게 있는걸요,\" 하고 나는 (기니 화폐 열두 닢쯤 든 지갑을 꺼내며) 말했다. 그러고는 \"이따 그중 대부분은 당신께 드릴 작정이에요\" 하고 덧붙였다.\n\n그는 처음에 그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것을 나와 마찬가지로 조금 못마땅해하는 듯, 내가 짐작한 대로 그녀의 주제넘은 짓쯤으로 여기는 눈치였다. 그러나 내가 그런 대답을 하는 것을 보자, 그는 곧바로 다시 평소의 그로 돌아왔다. 이튿날 아침 우리는 그 일을 다시 이야기했는데, 그때 나는 그가 완전히 만족했다는 것을 알았고, 그는 빙긋 웃으며 내가 돈이 부족해도 자기에게 말하지 않는 일이 없기를 바란다고, 또 내가 그러지 않겠노라 약속하지 않았느냐고 했다. 나는 어제 안주인이 자기와 아무 상관도 없는 일을 그렇게 드러내 놓고 떠벌린 것이 무척 못마땅했다고 말했다. 다만 짐작건대 그녀는 내가 그녀에게 진 빚, 곧 여덟 기니쯤 되던 그것을 받고 싶었던 모양이라고, 그래서 나는 그것을 줄 작정을 했고 그녀가 그토록 어리석게 떠들던 바로 그날 밤에 마침내 그것을 주었노라고 했다.\n\n내가 그녀에게 갚았다는 말을 듣자 그는 무척 기분이 좋아졌고, 그때는 그 일이 다른 이야기로 흘러가 버렸다. 그러나 이튿날 아침, 그가 자기보다 먼저 내가 방에서 일어나 부산을 떠는 소리를 듣고는 나를 불렀고, 내가 대답하자 자기 방으로 들어오라고 했다. 내가 들어갔을 때 그는 잠자리에 있었는데, 자기에게 좀 긴요한 할 말이 있다며 나를 침대 곁으로 와서 앉게 했다. 무척 다정한 말 몇 마디를 건넨 뒤, 그는 내게 자기에게 매우 정직해 줄 수 있겠는지, 자기가 청하는 한 가지에 진실한 대답을 해 줄 수 있겠는지 물었다. \"진실한\"이라는 말을 두고 내가 잠시 트집을 잡으며, 내가 그에게 진실하지 않은 대답을 한 적이 있느냐고 물은 뒤, 나는 그러겠노라 약속했다. 그러자 그의 청은 이러했으니, 내 지갑을 보여 달라는 것이었다. 나는 곧바로 주머니에 손을 넣어, 그에게 웃어 보이며 지갑을 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 삼 기니 반이 들어 있었다. 그러자 그는 그것이 내가 가진 돈 전부냐고 물었다. 나는 다시 웃으며, 아니라고, 어림도 없다고 말했다.\n\n그러자 그는 자기에게, 내가 가진 돈을 한 닢까지 죄다 가져오겠다고 약속하라고 했다. 나는 그러겠다 하고 내 방으로 가서 작은 개인 서랍을 가져왔는데, 거기엔 육 기니쯤이 더 있었고 은화도 좀 있었다. 나는 그것을 모두 침대 위에 쏟아 놓고, 이것이 내 재산 전부라고, 정직하게 일 실링까지 다 합쳐 그렇다고 말했다. 그는 그것을 잠시 들여다보았으나 세어 보지는 않았고, 도로 모조리 서랍에 쓸어 담았다. 그러고는 자기 주머니에 손을 뻗어 열쇠 하나를 꺼내더니, 탁자 위에 있던 작은 호두나무 상자를 열어 어떤 서랍을 가져오라고 했고, 나는 그렇게 했다. 그 서랍 안에는 금화로 된 돈이 많이 들어 있었으니, 아마 이백 기니 가까이 되었을 테지만 얼마인지는 알지 못했다. 그는 그 서랍을 받아 들더니, 내 손을 잡아 그 안에 넣게 하고는 한 움큼 가득 집으라고 했다. 나는 그러기를 꺼렸으나, 그가 내 손을 자기 손으로 꽉 잡아 서랍 안에 넣고는, 한 번에 집어 올릴 수 있는 만큼의 기니를 거의 다 꺼내게 했다.\n\n내가 그렇게 하자, 그는 그것을 내 무릎 위에 놓게 하고, 내 작은 서랍을 가져다 내 돈을 모조리 자기 돈 속에 쏟아붓더니, 나더러 그만 가라고, 그것을 죄다 내 방으로 가져가라고 했다.\n\n내가 이 이야기를 이토록 시시콜콜 늘어놓는 것은 거기에 깃든 화기애애함 때문이며, 또 우리가 어떤 기분으로 대화를 나누었는지 보여 주기 위함이다. 이 일이 있은 지 오래지 않아 그는 날마다 내 옷을, 내 레이스와 머리 장식을 트집 잡기 시작했고, 한마디로 더 좋은 것을 사라고 졸랐다. 한데 말이 난 김에 말하자면, 나는 비록 그래 보이지 않으려 했어도 내심 기꺼이 그러고 싶었으니, 세상에서 고운 옷보다 더 좋아하는 것이 없었기 때문이다. 나는 그가 빌려준 돈을 알뜰히 아껴 써야지, 안 그러면 그에게 다시 갚을 수가 없을 거라고 말했다. 그러자 그는 몇 마디로, 자기는 내게 진심 어린 존경을 품고 있고 내 형편도 아는지라, 그 돈을 빌려준 것이 아니라 준 것이며, 또 내가 그토록 온전히 동반해 줌으로써 그에게서 그것을 받을 자격을 얻었다고 생각한다고 말했다. 이 일이 있은 뒤 그는 나로 하여금 하녀를 두고 살림을 꾸리게 했고, 바스에 함께 온 그의 벗이 떠나자 자기 식사를 내가 차려 주도록 하였다. 나는 그것으로 손해 볼 것이 없으리라 믿었고 실제로 그러했으므로 기꺼이 그렇게 했으며, 그 집 여자 또한 거기서 제 몫의 이득을 챙기는 데에 빠짐이 없었다.\n\n우리가 이렇게 산 지 거의 석 달이 되었을 즈음, 바스에서 사람들이 하나둘 빠져나가기 시작하자 그는 떠날 이야기를 꺼냈고, 나더러 자기와 함께 런던에 가자고 간곡히 청했다. 나는 그 제안이 그리 편치 않았으니, 거기서 내가 어떤 처지로 살게 될지, 그가 나를 어떻게 대할지 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 일을 두고 옥신각신하는 사이에 그가 몹시 앓아누웠다. 그는 무슨 볼일이 있어 서머싯셔의 셉턴이라는 곳에 나갔다가 거기서 매우 심하게 앓게 되었으니, 어찌나 심했던지 길을 떠날 수도 없었다. 그래서 그는 자기 하인을 바스로 돌려보내, 내가 마차를 빌려 자기에게 와 주기를 청했다. 그는 떠나기 전에 자기 돈과 그 밖의 값진 것들을 모두 내게 맡겨 두었는데, 나는 그것들을 어찌해야 할지 몰랐으나 할 수 있는 한 잘 간수해 두고는, 숙소를 잠가 두고 그에게로 갔다. 가 보니 그는 실로 매우 심하게 앓고 있었다. 그러나 나는 그를 설득하여 가마에 실어 바스로 옮기게 하였으니, 거기에는 손쓸 방도도 더 많고 더 나은 의술도 구할 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그는 동의했고, 나는 그를 바스로 데려왔으니, 내 기억으로는 십오 마일쯤 되는 거리였다. 여기서 그는 열병으로 계속 심하게 앓아 다섯 주일 동안 자리에 누워 있었는데, 그동안 내내 나는 마치 그의 아내라도 되는 양 손수, 그만큼이나 정성스럽게 그를 간호하고 돌보았다. 실로 내가 그의 아내였더라도 그 이상은 할 수 없었을 것이다. 나는 그토록 자주, 그토록 오래 그의 곁을 지켜 밤을 새웠으니, 마침내는 그가 더는 밤을 새우지 못하게 하기에 이르렀고, 그러자 나는 간이침대 하나를 그의 방에 들여놓고 바로 그의 침대 발치에서 잤다.\n\n나는 실로 그의 처지에, 또 그토록 좋은 벗이며 앞으로도 내게 그러할 그를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움에 마음이 사무치게 움직여, 그의 곁에 앉아 몇 시간씩 내리 울곤 했다. 그러나 마침내 그는 차도를 보였고 회복하리라는 희망을 주었으며, 실로 비록 아주 더디기는 했어도 회복했다.\n\n지금 내가 하려는 말과 사정이 달랐다면, 이 기록에서 다른 경우들에 그래 왔음이 분명하듯이, 나는 그것을 밝히기를 망설이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 단언하건대, 이 모든 교제를 통틀어, 나나 그가 잠자리에 있을 때 방에 드나든 거리낌 없음과, 그가 앓을 때 밤낮으로 시중든 불가피한 수발을 제하고는, 우리 사이에 조금이라도 정숙하지 못한 말이나 행동이 오간 적이 없었다. 아, 그것이 끝까지 그러했더라면!\n\n얼마 뒤 그는 기력을 차려 빠르게 나아 갔고, 나는 내 간이침대를 치우려 했으나, 그는 곁에서 밤새워 줄 사람 없이도 혼자 견딜 수 있게 될 때까지 그러지 못하게 했고, 그제야 나는 내 방으로 옮겼다.\n\n그는 여러 기회에 나의 다정함과 자기를 향한 마음 씀에 대한 고마움을 표하였고, 완전히 나은 뒤에는 내게 오십 기니를 선물로 주었으니, 나를 돌봐 준 것과, 자기 말마따나 자기를 구하려 목숨을 건 것에 대한 것이었다.\n\n그리고 이제 그는 나를 향한 진실하고 깨뜨릴 수 없는 애정을 깊이 맹세했으나, 시종 그것이 내 덕성과 자기 자신의 덕성을 위해 더할 나위 없는 절제를 지킨 것임을 다짐했다. 나는 그것을 충분히 믿는다고 그에게 말했다. 그는 그것을 어찌나 멀리까지 끌고 갔던지, 만약 자기가 나와 함께 벌거벗고 침대에 눕는다 해도, 마치 내가 능욕자에게 겁탈당할 때 막아 주기라도 하듯 신성하게 내 덕성을 지켜 주겠노라 맹세할 정도였다. 나는 그를 믿었고, 그렇다고 그에게 말했다. 그러나 이것으로도 그는 만족하지 못하여, 자기가 그것을 의심할 여지 없이 증명해 보일 어떤 기회를 기다리겠노라 했다.\n\n이 일이 있은 지 한참 뒤, 내 볼일로 브리스틀에 갈 일이 생겼는데, 그러자 그는 나를 위해 마차를 빌려 나와 함께 가겠다고 했고 실제로 그렇게 했다. 그리하여 이제 우리의 친밀함은 실로 깊어졌다. 브리스틀에서 그는 나를 글로스터로 데려갔는데, 그저 바람을 쐬려는 유흥의 여정이었다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 여관에 침대 둘이 놓인 큰 방 하나밖에 묵을 곳이 없는 처지가 되었다. 집주인이 우리와 함께 올라가 방들을 보여 주다가 그 방에 들어와서는, 그에게 매우 거리낌 없이 말했다. \"나리, 이 부인이 나리의 부인이신지 아닌지 캐묻는 건 제 알 바 아닙니다만, 혹 아니시라면, 두 방에 따로 드신 거나 진배없이 이 두 침대에서 점잖게 주무실 수 있습죠.\" 그러면서 그는 방을 가로질러 완전히 쳐지는 큰 휘장을 끌어당겨, 침대들을 효과적으로 갈라놓았다. \"그렇군,\" 하고 내 벗이 선뜻 말했다. \"이 침대들이면 되겠소. 그리고 나머지로 말하자면, 우리는 함께 자기엔 너무 가까운 친척이라, 그저 서로 가까이 묵을 따름이오.\" 그리고 이 말은 그 일에도 정직한 낯을 씌워 주었다. 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 그는 내가 침대에 들 때까지 점잖게 방을 나가 있다가, 들어와서는 방의 자기 쪽 침대에 누웠는데, 거기 누운 채로 한참 동안 내게 말을 걸었다.\n\n마침내 그는 자기가 나와 함께 벌거벗고 침대에 누워도 조금의 해코지도 하지 않을 수 있다는, 늘 하던 그 말을 되뇌더니, 벌떡 자기 침대에서 일어났다. \"자, 이제, 여보,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신께 얼마나 정직한지, 또 내가 약속을 지킬 수 있다는 걸 보여 주리다.\" 그러더니 그는 내 침대로 건너왔다.\n\n나는 조금 물리쳤으나, 고백하건대 그가 애당초 그런 약속들을 하지 않았더라면 나는 그를 그리 심하게 물리치지 않았을 것이다. 그래서 앞서 말했듯 잠깐의 실랑이 끝에, 나는 가만히 누워 그가 침대에 들도록 두었다. 그는 거기 와서 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 그리하여 나는 밤새 그와 함께 누웠으나, 그는 내게 그 이상은 아무 짓도 하지 않았고, 말했다시피 두 팔로 끌어안는 것 외에 그 무엇도 청하지 않았으니, 밤새도록 그러했고, 다만 아침에 일어나 옷을 차려입고는, 나를 내가 태어난 날만큼이나 그에게 순결한 채로 남겨 두고 떠났다.\n\n이것은 내게 놀라운 일이었고, 자연의 법칙이 어떻게 작용하는지 아는 다른 이들에게도 아마 그러할 것이다. 그는 건장하고 정력이 넘치며 혈기 왕성한 사람이었으니 말이다. 또 그가 이렇게 처신한 것은 결코 신앙심의 원칙에서가 아니라 순전한 애정에서였으니, 비록 내가 세상에서 가장 마음에 드는 여자일지언정, 자기는 나를 사랑하기에 나를 해칠 수 없노라고 우겼다.\n\n나는 그것이 고귀한 원칙임을 인정하지만, 그것은 내가 전에 한 번도 이해해 본 적 없는 것이었던 만큼, 내게는 더할 나위 없이 놀라운 일이었다. 우리는 나머지 여정을 전과 다름없이 다녔고 바스로 돌아왔다. 거기서 그는 마음 내킬 때면 언제든 내게 올 수 있었으므로 종종 그 절제를 되풀이했고, 나는 자주 그와 함께, 그는 나와 함께 누웠으며, 비록 부부 사이의 온갖 친밀함이 우리에게 예사로운 일이었으나, 그는 단 한 번도 그 이상으로 나아가려 하지 않았고, 그 점을 두고 스스로를 무척 대견해했다. 그렇다고 내가 그가 생각한 만큼 그것에 온전히 흡족했다고는 말하지 않겠으니, 곧 여러분이 듣게 되겠지만 나는 그보다 훨씬 더 사악했음을 인정하기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 이렇게 거의 두 해를 살았는데, 다만 이런 예외가 있었으니, 그가 그동안 세 번 런던에 갔고, 한번은 거기서 넉 달을 머물렀다는 것이다. 그러나 그를 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그는 늘 나를 매우 넉넉하게 먹여 살릴 돈을 대 주었다.\n\n우리가 계속 이렇게 살았더라면, 고백하건대 우리는 자랑할 거리가 많았을 것이다. 그러나 현명한 사람들이 말하듯, 계율의 벼랑 끝에 너무 가까이 다가가는 것은 위험한 짓이거니와, 우리가 바로 그러했다. 그리고 여기서 다시 나는 그를 위해 공정히 인정해야 하니, 첫 번째 허물은 그의 편에서 비롯된 것이 아니었다. 어느 날 밤 우리가 함께 침대에 누워 따뜻하고 흥겨워하다가, 둘 다 그날 밤 평소보다 술을 좀 더, 비록 조금도 둘 중 누구의 정신을 흩뜨릴 정도는 아니었으나, 마신 듯한데, 차마 입에 담을 수 없는 몇몇 객쩍은 짓 끝에, 그의 두 팔에 꼭 안긴 채로, 나는 그에게 (영혼의 수치와 전율 속에 거듭 말하거니와) 단 하룻밤만, 그 이상은 결코 아니되, 그의 맹세를 풀어 줄 마음이 든다고 말하고 말았다.\n\n그는 곧바로 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였고, 그 뒤로는 그를 물리칠 길이 없었다. 실로 나 또한 어떤 결과가 닥치든 더는 그를 물리칠 마음이 없었다.\n\n이리하여 우리 덕성의 다스림은 깨어졌고, 나는 벗이라는 자리를, 창녀라는 저 가락도 안 맞고 귀에 거슬리는 호칭과 맞바꾸었다. 아침에 우리는 둘 다 참회에 잠겼으니, 나는 매우 진심으로 울었고 그는 매우 미안한 마음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그것이 그때 우리 둘이 할 수 있는 전부였고, 이렇게 길이 트이고 덕성과 양심의 빗장이 이렇게 치워지자, 그 뒤로 우리가 씨름해야 할 어려움은 한결 줄어들었다.\n\n그 주의 남은 날 내내 우리가 함께 나눈 대화는 그저 따분한 종류의 것이었다. 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 바라보았고, 이따금씩 \"혹시 내가 지금 아이를 배면 어쩌지? 그러면 나는 어찌 되는 거지?\" 하는 그 우울한 걱정을 불쑥불쑥 꺼냈다. 그는 내가 자기에게 진실하기만 하다면 자기도 내게 그러할 것이라고, 또 일이 이렇게까지 멀리 갔으니(실로 그는 결코 그럴 작정은 아니었다) 만약 내가 아이를 배더라도 그것도 나도 잘 보살피겠노라 말하며 나를 다독였다. 이것은 우리 둘을 다 무디게 만들었다. 나는 만약 아이를 밴다면 산파가 없어 죽을지언정 그를 아비로 지목하지는 않겠노라 그에게 다짐했고, 그는 내가 아이를 배더라도 결코 궁하게 두지 않겠노라 내게 다짐했다. 이 서로의 다짐이 우리를 그 일에 무디게 만들었고, 그 뒤로 우리는 내키는 대로 그 죄를 거듭 저질렀으니, 마침내 내가 두려워하던 대로 그 일이 닥쳐와, 나는 실로 아이를 배게 되었다.\n\n그것이 확실하다는 것을 알고, 또 그것을 그에게도 납득시킨 뒤, 우리는 그 일을 어떻게 처리할지 방도를 강구할 생각을 하기 시작했다. 나는 안주인에게 그 비밀을 털어놓고 그녀의 조언을 구하자고 제안했고, 그도 그에 동의했다. (내가 알게 된 바로는) 그런 일에 익숙한 여자인 내 안주인은 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 그녀는 결국 그렇게 될 줄 알고 있었다고 말하며, 그 일을 두고 우리를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었다. 앞서 말했듯, 우리는 그녀가 그런 일에 노련한 노부인임을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 모든 일을 떠맡았으니, 산파와 유모를 구해 오고, 온갖 캐묻는 것을 무마하고, 우리를 평판에 흠 없이 빠져나오게 해 주겠노라 약속했고, 실로 매우 능란하게 그렇게 해냈다.\n\n내 몸 풀 때가 가까워지자, 그녀는 내 신사더러 런던으로 떠나거나, 아니면 떠나는 척하라고 청했다. 그가 떠나자, 그녀는 자기 집에 곧 몸 풀 한 부인이 있는데, 자기는 그 남편을 매우 잘 안다며 교구 관리들에게 알렸고, 자기가 꾸며 댄 대로 그 이름을 일러 주었으니, 월터 클리브 경이라 했다. 그녀는 그가 매우 훌륭한 신사라고, 또 자기가 온갖 캐묻는 것에 책임지고 답하겠노라는 둥의 말을 했다. 이것으로 교구 관리들은 곧바로 만족했고, 나는 실제로 내가 클리브 부인이라도 되었을 만큼이나 떳떳하게 몸을 풀었으며, 산고를 치를 때 인근에 사는 바스의 가장 점잖은 시민들의 아내 서넛의 도움을 받았으니, 다만 그 일로 그가 나 때문에 좀 더 비용을 들이게 되긴 했다. 나는 그것에 대해 종종 그에게 걱정을 내비쳤으나, 그는 그런 일로 걱정 말라고 일렀다.\n\n그가 내 몸 푸는 데에 드는 특별한 비용을 위해 돈을 매우 넉넉하게 대 준 덕에, 나는 주변의 모든 것을 매우 단정하게 갖추었으나, 그렇다고 화려하거나 사치스럽게 보이려 하지는 않았다. 게다가 내 형편을 알고, 또 내가 겪어 온 만큼 세상을 알며, 이런 종류의 일들이 오래 가지 않는 경우가 많다는 것을 아는지라, 나는 이른바 비 오는 날을 대비하여 할 수 있는 한 많은 돈을 챙겨 두려 애썼고, 그것이 다 내 몸 푸는 일의 그 특별한 겉치레에 쓰인 것처럼 그가 믿게 했다.\n\n이런 방편으로, 또 앞서 말한 대로 그가 내게 준 것까지 포함하여, 나는 몸 풀고 났을 즈음 내 손에 이백 기니쯤을 지니게 되었으니, 그중에는 본래 내 것에서 남은 것도 포함되어 있었다.\n\n나는 실로 잘생긴 사내아이를 낳았으니, 매력 넘치는 아이였다. 그가 그 소식을 듣고는 그것에 관해 매우 다정하고 자상한 편지를 내게 써 보냈고, 그러고는 내가 일어나 건강을 되찾는 대로 런던으로 떠나오는 편이 내게 더 좋아 보인다고 말했다. 자기가 해머스미스에 내가 묵을 거처를 마련해 두었으니, 마치 내가 런던에서 막 그리로 온 것처럼 하라고, 또 얼마 지나면 내가 다시 바스로 가야 할 텐데 그때는 자기가 나와 함께 가겠노라 했다.\n\n나는 이 제안이 무척 마음에 들었고, 그리하여 일부러 마차 한 대를 빌려, 내 아이와, 그 아이를 돌보고 젖 먹일 유모와, 하녀 하나를 데리고 런던으로 떠났다.\n\n그는 자기 마차를 타고 레딩에서 나를 맞았고, 나를 그 마차에 태우더니, 하인과 아이는 빌린 마차에 남겨 둔 채, 그렇게 나를 해머스미스의 새 거처로 데려갔다. 그곳에 대해 나는 매우 흡족해할 까닭이 충분했으니, 매우 단정한 방들이었고 나는 매우 잘 갖춰진 살림을 누렸기 때문이다.\n\n그리고 이제 나는 실로 내가 번영이라 부를 만한 것의 절정에 있었고, 아내가 되는 것 말고는 부족한 것이 없었다. 그러나 이 경우에 그것은 될 수 없는 일이었고, 그럴 여지가 없었다. 그러므로 나는 앞서 말했듯, 모든 기회마다 궁핍의 때를 대비하여 모을 수 있는 것을 모으려 애썼으니, 이런 일들이 늘 이어지지는 않는다는 것을, 정부情婦를 두는 남자들은 흔히 그들을 갈아 치우고, 싫증을 내거나 질투하게 되며, 혹은 무슨 일인가가 생겨 그 너그러움을 거두게 마련이라는 것을, 또 더러 이렇게 좋은 대접을 받는 여자들이 신중한 처신으로 제 몸의 존중이나 정절이라는 미묘한 조목을 지키는 데에 소홀하여, 그러면 경멸 속에 마땅히 버림받는다는 것을 충분히 잘 알았기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 나는 이 점에서는 안전했으니, 나는 갈아 치울 마음이 없었던 데다, 온 집 안에 아는 사람이라곤 전혀 없어서 더 멀리 눈 돌릴 유혹도 없었기 때문이다. 나는 묵는 집의 식구들과, 옆집 목사 부인 말고는 어떤 무리와도 어울리지 않았다. 그래서 그가 없을 때면 나는 누구도 찾아가지 않았고, 그가 내려올 때면 그는 언제나 나를 내 방이나 응접실에서 보았다. 어디든 바람을 쐬러 갈 때면 그것은 늘 그와 함께였다.\n\n이렇게 그와 함께, 또 그가 나와 함께 산 것은 분명 세상에서 가장 의도치 않은 일이었다. 그는 종종 내게 맹세하기를, 처음 나와 알게 되었을 때부터, 심지어 우리가 처음으로 우리 규율을 깨뜨린 바로 그날 밤까지도, 자기는 나와 동침할 의도를 조금도 품은 적이 없었다고, 자기는 늘 나를 진실하게 사랑했으나 자기가 한 그 짓을 하려는 실제 의향은 조금도 없었다고 했다. 나는 그를 한 번도 의심한 적이 없다고, 만약 의심했더라면 그 일을 불러온 거리낌 없음에 그토록 쉽게 굴복하지 않았을 것이라고, 그러나 그것은 모두 불시의 일이었으며, 그날 밤 우리가 서로의 의향에 너무 멀리 굴복하고 만 우연에서 비롯된 것이라고 그에게 다짐했다. 그리고 실로 나는 그 뒤로 종종 깨달았으니, 이 이야기의 독자 여러분에게 경계로 남겨 두거니와, 우리는 헤프고 음란한 거리낌 없음 속에서 우리 의향을 채우는 것을 조심해야 마땅하다는 것이다. 자칫하면 덕성의 도움이 가장 절실히 필요한 그 길목에서, 덕을 지키려던 우리의 결심이 우리를 저버리는 것을 보게 될 테니 말이다.\n\n사실, 그리고 내가 앞서 고백했듯이, 그와 처음 대화를 나누기 시작한 그 시각부터, 나는 그가 청한다면 그가 나와 동침하도록 허락하기로 작정했다. 그러나 그것은 내가 그의 도움과 부조를 원했고, 그를 붙잡아 둘 다른 방도를 알지 못했기 때문이었다. 그러나 그날 밤 우리가 함께 있다가, 앞서 말했듯 그토록 멀리까지 나아갔을 때, 나는 내 나약함을 깨달았다. 그 의향은 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 것이어서, 나는 그가 청하기도 전에 모든 것을 내어 줄 수밖에 없었다.\n\n그러나 그는 내게 너무도 공정하여 그 일로 나를 한 번도 나무라지 않았고, 그 어떤 다른 경우에도 내 처신에 대해 조금의 못마땅함도 드러낸 적이 없었으며, 도리어 우리가 처음 함께한 그 시각만큼이나 늘 내 동반에 기뻐한다고 맹세했다. 함께했다는 것은, 곧 잠자리를 함께하는 사이로서 함께했다는 뜻이다.\n\n그에게 아내가 없었다는 것은, 곧 그녀가 그에게 아내나 다름없는 존재가 아니었다는 것은 사실이고, 그래서 나는 그쪽으로는 아무 위험이 없었다. 그러나 양심의 정당한 성찰은 흔히 한 남자를, 특히 분별 있는 남자를, 정부의 품에서 낚아채는 법이니, 비록 다른 계기에서이긴 했으나, 마침내 그에게도 그러했다. 다른 한편으로, 나 또한 내가 살아온 삶에 대해 양심의 은밀한 책망이 없지 않았고, 그것은 내가 누린 가장 큰 만족의 절정에서조차 그러했으나, 가난과 굶주림이라는 끔찍한 전망이 무서운 유령처럼 나를 짓눌렀으므로, 뒤돌아볼 수가 없었다. 그러나 가난이 나를 그 길로 끌어들였듯, 가난에 대한 두려움이 나를 그 안에 붙들어 두었고, 나는 종종 나를 부양할 만큼의 돈을 모을 수만 있다면 그것을 아주 끊어 버리리라 결심했다. 그러나 이런 것들은 무게 없는 생각들이어서, 그가 내게 올 때면 모조리 사라졌다. 그의 동반이 어찌나 즐거웠던지 그가 곁에 있을 때면 우울해질 수가 없었다. 그런 성찰은 모두 내가 혼자 있던 그 시간들의 몫이었다.\n\n나는 이 행복하면서도 불행한 처지로 여섯 해를 살았으니, 그동안 그에게 아이 셋을 낳아 주었으나 그중 첫째만 살았다. 그리고 비록 그 여섯 해 사이에 두 번 거처를 옮겼어도, 여섯 해째에 나는 해머스미스의 첫 거처로 돌아왔다. 바로 여기서 나는 어느 날 아침, 내 신사가 보낸 다정하면서도 우울한 편지에 놀라게 되었다. 그가 매우 아프며 또 한 차례 병이 도질까 두렵다는 것, 그러나 그의 처가 친척들이 집에 그와 함께 있어 나를 그의 곁에 둘 수 없겠다는 것을 넌지시 알리는 내용이었는데, 그래도 그는 그 점에 대한 큰 불만을 드러내며, 전처럼 내가 그를 돌보고 간호하도록 허락받을 수 있으면 좋겠다고 했다.\n\n나는 이 소식에 무척 마음이 쓰였고, 그가 어떤 상태인지 알고 싶어 몹시 안달이 났다. 나는 보름 남짓 기다렸으나 아무 소식도 듣지 못했고, 그것이 나를 놀라게 하여 실로 매우 불안해지기 시작했다. 다음 보름 동안 나는 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었다고 말할 수 있을 것 같다. 내게 각별히 힘들었던 것은 그가 정확히 어디에 있는지 알지 못한다는 것이었다. 처음에 나는 그가 처가 어머니의 거처에 있다고 알았으나, 내가 런던으로 거처를 옮긴 뒤로, 그에게 편지를 부치려고 받아 둔 주소의 도움으로, 곧 그를 수소문할 방도를 찾아냈고, 그리하여 그가 블룸즈베리의 어느 집에 있다는 것을 알아냈다. 그는 앓아눕기 조금 전에 온 식솔을 그리로 옮겼고, 그의 처와 처가 어머니도 같은 집에 있었으나, 다만 그 처는 자기가 남편과 같은 집에 있다는 것을 알지 못하도록 되어 있었다.\n\n여기서 나는 또한 곧, 그가 마지막 고비에 이르렀음을 알게 되었고, 그것은 나 또한 그에 대한 참된 소식을 얻고자 거의 마지막 고비에 이르게 했다. 어느 날 밤 나는 호기심에 이끌려 동그란 모자에 밀짚모자를 쓴 하녀 차림으로 변장하여 그 집 문으로 갔다. 그가 전에 살던 동네의 어느 부인이 보내서 온 것처럼, 주인 내외께 안부를 여쭙는다며, 나는 ―― 씨께서 어떠하신지, 또 간밤에 어떻게 주무셨는지 알아보러 왔다고 말했다. 이 전갈을 전하면서 나는 바라던 기회를 얻었으니, 하녀 가운데 하나와 이야기를 나누다가 그녀와 오래도록 수다를 떨어, 그의 병에 관한 온갖 자세한 사정을 죄다 알아냈다. 알고 보니 그것은 늑막염으로, 기침과 열을 동반한 것이었다. 그녀는 또한 누가 집에 있는지, 그의 처는 어떠한지도 일러 주었는데, 그녀의 말로는 처가 정신을 되찾을 가망이 좀 있다고들 한다고 했다. 그러나 신사 자신에 관해서는, 한마디로 의사들이 그에게 희망이 거의 없다고 했다는 것, 아침에는 그가 거의 죽어 가는 줄 알았다는 것, 그리고 그때도 좀 나아졌을 뿐이어서 다음 날 밤을 넘기지 못하리라 본다는 것이었다.\n\n이것은 내게 무거운 소식이었고, 나는 이제 내 번영의 끝을 보기 시작했으며, 또한 그가 살아 있는 동안 내가 알뜰한 살림꾼 노릇을 하여 무언가를 챙기거나 모아 둔 것이 참으로 다행이었음을 보게 되었으니, 이제 내 앞에는 내 살길에 대한 어떤 전망도 없었기 때문이다.\n\n또한 내게는 다섯 살쯤 된, 잘생기고 사랑스러운 사내아이 하나가 있는데, 적어도 내가 아는 한, 그 아이를 위해 마련된 것이 아무것도 없다는 사실이 내 마음을 몹시 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 이런 생각들로 슬픈 마음을 안고 그날 저녁 나는 집으로 돌아와, 내 남은 평생을 어떻게 살아야 할지, 또 어떤 식으로 내 몸을 의탁해야 할지 혼자 궁리하기 시작했다.\n\n여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 나는 그가 어떻게 되었는지를 다시 한번 매우 서둘러 알아보지 않고는 가만히 있을 수가 없었다. 그리고 감히 내가 직접 가지는 못한 채, 나는 여러 차례 가짜 심부름꾼을 보냈고, 그리하여 다시 보름을 더 기다린 끝에, 비록 그가 여전히 매우 아프기는 해도 살아날 가망이 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그제야 나는 그 집에 더 사람을 보내는 일을 줄였고, 얼마 뒤 동네에서 그가 집 안을 돌아다닌다는 것을, 그리고 다시 바깥출입을 한다는 것을 알게 되었다.\n\n그래서 나는 머지않아 그에게서 소식을 들으리라 의심치 않았고, 내가 생각하기에 내 형편이 회복되었다며 스스로 마음을 다독이기 시작했다. 나는 한 주, 또 두 주를 기다렸고, 그러다 무척 놀랍고 어이없게도 거의 두 달을 기다렸으나, 그가 회복하여 바람을 쐬러, 또 병을 앓고 난 뒤 더 잘 추스르려 시골로 갔다는 것 말고는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다. 그 뒤로 또 두 달이 더 지나서야, 나는 그가 다시 그의 시내 집으로 돌아왔다는 것을 알게 되었으나, 그래도 여전히 그에게서는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다.\n\n나는 그에게 편지를 여러 통 써서 늘 하던 대로 주소를 적어 보냈는데, 그중 두세 통은 그가 찾아갔으나 나머지는 그러지 않았음을 알게 되었다. 나는 어느 때보다 더 다급한 투로 다시 편지를 썼고, 그중 한 통에서는, 나로서는 내가 직접 그를 찾아갈 수밖에 없겠다고 알리며, 내 형편을, 내야 할 거처 임대료를, 아이에게 필요한 양육비가 부족하다는 것을, 또 그가 나를 보살피고 돌봐 주겠다던 그토록 엄숙한 맹세에도 불구하고 생계 수단마저 없는 내 비참한 처지를 호소했다. 나는 이 편지의 사본을 떠 두었고, 그 편지가 그 집에 거의 한 달이나 놓여 있으면서도 찾아가지 않는 것을 알자, 수소문 끝에 그가 자주 다닌다는 것을 알아낸 어느 커피점에서, 그 사본을 그의 손에 직접 쥐여 줄 방도를 찾아냈다.\n\n이 편지는 그에게서 답장을 끌어냈는데, 그 답장으로 나는 비록 내가 버림받게 되리라는 것을 알았으나, 또한 그가 얼마 전에 내게 편지 한 통을 보내, 다시 바스로 내려가기를 청했다는 것도 알게 되었다. 그 내용은 곧이어 말하겠다.\n\n사실 병상은 이런 종류의 교제가 다른 낯빛으로 비치고, 우리가 전에 보던 것과는, 또는 전에 보이던 것과는 다른 눈으로 보이게 되는 때이다. 내 연인은 죽음의 문턱에, 영원의 벼랑 끝에 다다랐던 것이고, 보아하니 마땅한 회한에, 또 풍류와 경박함으로 지나온 자기 삶에 대한 슬픈 성찰에 사로잡혔던 것이다. 그 가운데서도 특히 나와의 그 죄스러운 교제가, 그것은 다름 아닌 오래 이어진 간음의 삶이었거니와, 그것이 본래 그러했던 모습 그대로, 곧 전에 그가 여기던 것과는 다른 모습으로 드러났고, 이제 그는 그것을 정당하고 신앙심 어린 혐오로 바라보았다.\n\n나는 또한 다음을 언급하지 않을 수 없으니, 그런 쾌락의 경우에 처한 내 동성同性들에게 길잡이로 남겨 두거니와, 이와 같은 죄 뒤에 진실한 회개가 뒤따를 때면, 그 대상에 대한 미움이 반드시 함께 따라온다는 것이다. 그리고 전에 그 애정이 커 보였을수록, 그 미움도 그에 비례하여 더 커질 것이다. 언제나 그러할 것이며, 실로 달리 될 수가 없다. 그 죄에 대한 참되고 진실한 혐오가 있으면서, 동시에 그 원인에 대한 사랑이 남아 있을 수는 없기 때문이다. 죄에 대한 혐오와 더불어 함께 죄지은 자에 대한 증오가 따라오게 마련이니, 그 밖의 것은 기대할 수 없다.\n\n여기서도 나는 그러함을 알았으니, 다만 이 신사의 경우 좋은 예의와 정의감이 그가 그것을 어떤 극단으로까지 밀고 가지 못하게 막았다. 이 일에서 그가 맡은 몫의 짧은 내력은 이러했다. 그는 내 마지막 편지로, 또 그가 나중에 찾아간 나머지 모든 편지로, 내가 바스로 가지 않았다는 것을, 자기 첫 편지가 내 손에 닿지 않았다는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 내게 다음과 같이 써 보냈다.\n\n\"부인, 지난달 8일 자로 보낸 내 편지가 당신 손에 닿지 않았다니 놀랍소. 내 장담하건대 그것은 당신 거처로, 또 당신 하녀의 손에 전해졌소.\n\n지난 한동안 내가 어떤 처지에 있었는지, 또 어떻게 내가 무덤의 벼랑 끝에 이르렀다가 하늘의 뜻밖이며 분에 넘치는 자비로 다시 회복되었는지를 굳이 당신께 알릴 필요는 없겠소. 내가 처해 있던 그 처지에서, 우리의 불행한 교제가 내 양심을 짓누른 짐 가운데 결코 가장 작은 것이 아니었다는 사실이 당신께는 이상하게 들리지 않을 것이오. 더 말할 필요는 없겠소. 회개해야 마땅한 그런 일들은 또한 바로잡혀야 마땅한 법이오.\n\n당신이 바스로 돌아가는 것을 생각해 주기 바라오. 여기 당신 거처의 셈을 치르고 당신을 그리로 내려보낼 비용으로 오십 파운드짜리 어음을 동봉하오. 그리고 다음을 덧붙이는 것이 당신께 놀라움이 되지 않기를 바라거니와, 오로지 이런 까닭에서이지 당신 편에서 내게 무슨 잘못을 저질러서가 아니라, 나는 더는 당신을 볼 수 없소. 아이는 내가 마땅히 보살피겠소. 당신 좋을 대로, 아이를 있는 곳에 두든 데려가든 하시오. 당신께도 그와 같은 성찰이 있기를, 또 그것이 당신께 이로움이 되기를 바라오. 그럼 이만,\" 운운.\n\n나는 이 편지에 천 군데를 찔린 듯한 상처를 입었으니, 차마 형언할 수 없는 그런 것이었다. 내 양심의 책망은 이루 말할 수 없는 것이었으니, 나는 내 죄에 눈멀어 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 나는, 차라리 내 오라비와 계속 머물러 아내로서 그와 사는 편이 죄가 덜했으리라 성찰했다. 우리 둘 다 그것을 알지 못했으니, 그 점에서는 우리의 혼인에 죄가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 나는 이 모든 동안 내가 유부녀라는 것을, ―― 씨, 곧 그 포목상의 아내라는 것을 단 한 번도 성찰하지 못했다. 그는 비록 자기 형편의 어쩔 수 없는 사정으로 나를 떠났으나, 우리 사이에 맺어진 혼인 계약에서 나를 풀어 줄 권한도, 내가 다시 혼인할 법적 자유를 줄 권한도 없었다. 그러니 나는 이 모든 동안 다름 아닌 한낱 창녀요 간음한 여자에 지나지 않았던 것이다. 그리하여 나는 내가 누렸던 그 거리낌 없음을 두고, 또 내가 이 신사에게 어떻게 덫이 되었는지를 두고 나 자신을 책망했으며, 실로 내가 그 죄의 주범이었음을, 이제 그는 마음에 일어난 깨우침의 역사役事로 자비롭게 그 구렁에서 낚여 올려졌으나, 나는 마치 하느님의 은총에서 버림받은 듯, 또 내 사악함에 머물도록 하늘이 내버려 둔 듯 남겨졌음을 책망했다.\n\n이런 성찰 속에 나는 거의 한 달 동안 매우 시름에 잠겨 슬프게 지냈고, 바스로 내려가지 않았으니, 전에 함께 지내던 그 여자와 함께 있고 싶은 마음이 없었기 때문이다. 내 생각에, 그녀가 전에 그랬듯이 나를 다시 무슨 사악한 삶의 길로 부추길까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 나는 그녀가 앞서 말한 대로 내가 버림받았다는 것을 아는 것이 무척이나 싫었다.\n\n그리고 이제 나는 내 어린 아들 일로 몹시 골치가 아팠다. 그 아이와 헤어지는 것은 내게 죽음 같은 일이었으나, 언젠가 그 아이를 부양할 생계 수단도 없이 그 아이만 떠안게 될 위험을 생각하니, 그제야 나는 그 아이를 있는 곳에 두기로 결심했다. 그러나 그러면서도 나 또한 그 아이 가까이에 있어, 그 아이를 부양하는 짐은 지지 않으면서도 그 아이를 보는 만족은 누릴 수 있게 하리라 작정했다.\n\n그리하여 나는 내 신사에게 짤막한 편지를 보내, 바스로 돌아가는 것 말고는 모든 일에서 그의 분부를 따랐다고, 그것만은 여러 까닭으로 도저히 생각할 수 없었다고 말했다. 또 그와 헤어지는 것은 내가 결코 회복할 수 없는 상처이긴 하나, 그래도 나는 그의 성찰이 정당하다는 것에 온전히 수긍하며, 그의 개심이나 회개를 가로막을 마음은 추호도 없다고 했다.\n\n그러고 나서 나는 내가 할 수 있는 가장 가슴 저미는 말로 내 형편을 그에게 호소했다. 나는 처음에 그를 움직여 나를 향한 너그럽고 정직한 우정을 품게 한 그 불행한 곤경이, 비록 우리 교제의 죄스러운 부분은, 그것은 우리 둘 다 그때는 빠질 작정이 아니었다고 믿거니와, 끊어졌을지언정, 이제 나를 향한 작은 마음 씀을 그에게 일으켜 주기를 바란다고 말했다. 또 나도 그가 한 만큼 진실하게 회개하기를 바라나, 다만 가난과 곤경이라는 끔찍한 전망에서 악마가 결코 우리를 부추기기를 게을리하지 않는 그 유혹에 내가 내몰리지 않도록, 나를 어떤 처지에 놓아 달라고 그에게 간청했다. 그리고 만약 그가 내가 자기에게 성가신 존재가 될까 조금이라도 염려한다면, 그가 알다시피 내가 떠나온 곳, 버지니아의 어머니에게로 돌아갈 수 있게 나를 채비시켜 주기를 빌었으니, 그러면 그 점에 대한 그의 모든 두려움이 끝날 것이라고 했다. 끝으로 나는, 만약 그가 내가 떠나는 일을 수월케 하도록 오십 파운드를 더 보내 준다면, 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서를 되돌려 보내겠노라고, 또 아이의 잘 지내는 소식을 듣고자 하는 것 말고는 다시는 어떤 성가신 졸라댐으로도 그를 어지럽히지 않겠노라 약속했다. 그 아이는, 만약 내 어머니가 살아 계시고 내 형편이 허락한다면, 내가 데려와 내게 오게 하여, 또한 그것을 그의 손에서 완전히 덜어 주겠노라 했다.\n\n이것은 실로 여기까지는 모두 거짓이었으니, 곧 나는 버지니아로 갈 의도가 전혀 없었던 것이다. 그곳에서의 내 지난 일에 대한 기록이 누구든 그 점을 납득시켜 줄 터이다. 그러나 핵심은 가능하다면 그에게서 이 마지막 오십 파운드를 받아 내는 것이었으니, 그것이 내가 그에게서 기대할 수 있는 마지막 한 푼이 되리라는 것을 충분히 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 내가 쓴 논리, 곧 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서를 주고 다시는 그를 성가시게 하지 않겠다는 그 논리가 그에게 효과적으로 먹혀들었고, 그는 사람을 시켜 내게 그 돈의 어음을 보내왔는데, 그 사람은 내가 서명할 전면적인 면제 증서를 함께 가져왔다. 나는 그것에 선선히 서명하고 돈을 받았다. 그리하여, 비록 내 뜻과는 너무도 어긋났으나, 이 일에는 마침내 종지부가 찍혔다.\n\n그리고 여기서 나는, 우리처럼 처지가 정해진 사람들 사이에서, 순수한 의도라느니, 우정의 사랑이라느니 하는 핑계로 너무 큰 거리낌 없음을 누리는 것의 불행한 결말을 성찰하지 않을 수 없다. 살이라는 것은 대개 그런 우정에서 차지하는 몫이 너무도 커서, 가장 엄숙한 결심조차 결국에는 의향이 이겨 버릴 공산이 매우 크기 때문이다. 또 악덕은 정숙함의 갈라진 틈으로 비집고 들어오는 법이니, 실로 순수한 우정이라면 그 정숙함을 더없이 엄격하게 지켜야 마땅한 것이다. 그러나 나는 이런 일들을 읽는 독자 여러분에게 그들 자신의 정당한 성찰을 맡기거니와, 그들은 나보다 그것을 더 잘 효력 있게 만들 수 있을 것이다. 나는 그토록 빨리 나 자신을 잊어버린 사람이니, 그러므로 그저 매우 시원찮은 훈계자일 따름이다.\n\n이제 나는 다시, 나 자신을 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 홀몸이 되었다. 나는 세상의 혼인이든 정부 노릇이든 그 모든 의무에서 풀려났으니, 다만 내 남편인 그 포목상만은 예외였다. 그러나 거의 십오 년 동안 그에게서 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터라, 내가 그에게서 완전히 풀려났다고 여긴들 누구도 나를 탓할 수 없을 것이다. 게다가 그는 떠날 때 내게, 만약 그에게서 자주 소식을 듣지 못하거든 그가 죽은 줄로 알라고, 그러면 나는 누구든 마음에 드는 사람과 자유로이 다시 혼인해도 된다고 말했던 것이다.\n\n이제 나는 내 셈속을 따져 보기 시작했다. 나는 여러 통의 편지와 많은 졸라댐으로, 또 어머니의 중재까지 곁들여, 버지니아에 있는 오라비에게서(나는 이제 그를 그렇게 부른다) 물품의 두 번째 회송을 받아, 내가 가지고 떠나온 화물의 손해를 메웠다. 그런데 이것 또한 내가 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서에 봉인하여 브리스틀에 있는 그의 거래처를 통해 보내 준다는 조건이었으니, 비록 나는 그것을 가혹하게 여겼으나, 그렇게 하겠노라 약속할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 나는 이 경우에 너무도 잘 처신하여, 면제 증서에 서명하기 전에 내 물품을 먼저 빼냈고, 그런 다음 늘 무슨 핑계인가를 찾아내어 그 일을 피하고 서명 자체를 미루었다. 그러다 마침내 나는 그것을 할 수 있으려면 오라비에게 편지를 써서 그의 답을 받아야 한다고 둘러댔다.\n\n이 보충분을 합쳐, 또 마지막 오십 파운드를 받기 전에, 나는 내 자산이 죄다 합쳐 사백 파운드쯤에 이른다는 것을 알았고, 그래서 그것까지 더하면 사백오십 파운드쯤 되었다. 나는 그 위로 백 파운드를 더 모아 두었으나, 그것을 두고 한 가지 재난을 겪었으니, 이러했다. 그 돈을 맡겨 둔 어느 금세공인이 파산하는 바람에, 나는 내 돈 칠십 파운드를 잃었다. 그자의 화의和議로 그의 백 파운드에서 삼십 파운드밖에 돌아오지 않았기 때문이다. 나는 약간의 은붙이가 있었으나 많지는 않았고, 옷가지와 아마포는 그런대로 넉넉히 갖추고 있었다.\n\n이 밑천을 가지고 나는 다시 세상을 시작해야 했다. 그러나 여러분은, 내가 이제 레드리프에 살던 때의 그 여자와 같지 않았음을 헤아려야 한다. 우선 무엇보다, 나는 나이가 거의 스무 살은 더 들었고, 그 나이로도, 또 버지니아를 오간 떠돌이살이로도 더 나아 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 비록 나는 나를 돋보이게 할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않았으나, 다만 화장만은 예외였으니, 나는 한 번도 거기까지 몸을 낮춘 적이 없었고, 그런 것은 필요 없다고 여길 만큼의 자존심이 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 스물다섯과 마흔둘 사이에는 언제나 어떤 차이가 보이게 마련이었다.\n\n나는 앞으로의 삶의 처지를 두고 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 방도를 궁리했고, 무엇을 해야 할지 매우 진지하게 생각하기 시작했으나, 아무것도 손에 잡히지 않았다. 나는 세상이 나를 실제보다 무언가 더 대단한 사람으로 여기도록 신경 썼고, 내가 재산가이며 내 재산이 내 손안에 있다고 소문이 나게 했다. 뒤엣것은 매우 사실이었으나, 앞엣것은 앞서 말한 대로였다. 나는 아는 사람이 하나도 없었으니, 그것이 내 가장 큰 불운의 하나였고, 그 결과로 나는 조언자가, 적어도 함께 거들고 조언해 줄 수 있는 조언자가 하나도 없었다. 그리고 무엇보다, 내 형편의 비밀을 믿고 털어놓을 수 있고 그 비밀과 신의를 의지할 수 있는 사람이 하나도 없었다. 나는 경험으로 알았으니, 벗이 없다는 것은 여자가 내몰릴 수 있는 처지 가운데, 곤궁에 처한 것 다음으로 가장 나쁜 것이다. 내가 여자라고 말하는 것은, 분명 남자라면 제 스스로의 조언자가, 제 스스로의 인도자가 될 수 있고, 여자보다 더 잘 곤경에서 빠져나오고 일거리 속으로 들어가는 법을 알기 때문이다. 그러나 여자에게 제 일을 털어놓고 조언과 도움을 구할 벗이 하나도 없다면, 십중팔구 그녀는 파멸하고 만다. 아니, 게다가 돈이 많을수록 그녀는 더 부당한 일을 당하고 속아 넘어갈 위험에 처한다. 앞서 말한 대로 내가 그 금세공인의 손에 맡겨 둔 백 파운드의 일이 바로 내 경우였으니, 보아하니 그자의 신용은 그 전부터 기울고 있었으나, 사정에 어두운 데다 상의할 사람도 없던 나는 그것을 전혀 알지 못했고, 그래서 내 돈을 잃은 것이다.\n\n다음으로, 여자가 이렇게 외롭게 버려지고 조언도 없이 남겨지면, 그녀는 큰길에 떨어진 돈주머니나 보석과 꼭 같아서, 다음에 오는 자에게 먹잇감이 된다. 만약 덕 있고 올곧은 원칙을 지닌 사람이 그것을 우연히 줍는다면 그는 그것을 알리게 하여 주인이 다시 그 소식을 듣게 할 것이다. 그러나 그런 것이 단 한 번 좋은 손에 들어갈 동안, 몇 번이나 거리낌 없이 그것을 제 것으로 채갈 손에 떨어지겠는가?\n\n이것이 분명 내 경우였으니, 나는 이제 풀려난, 이끌어 줄 이 없는 존재였고, 내 처신을 위한 도움도, 부조도, 인도자도 없었다. 나는 내가 무엇을 노리는지, 무엇을 원하는지는 알았으나, 곧바른 방도로 그 목적을 이루는 법은 전혀 알지 못했다. 나는 안정된 살림의 처지에 놓이기를 원했고, 만약 내가 술 절제할 줄 알고 마음씨 좋은 남편을 만났더라면, 덕성 그 자체가 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼이나 충실하고 진실한 아내가 되었을 것이다. 만약 내가 그렇지 못했다면, 그 악덕은 언제나 의향의 문이 아니라 필요의 문으로 들어온 것이었다. 또 나는 그것의 결핍을 통해, 안정된 삶의 가치가 무엇인지를 너무도 잘 이해한 터라, 그 행복을 잃을 만한 일은 무엇도 하지 않았을 것이다. 아니, 나는 내가 겪어 온 그 모든 고난 덕에 도리어 훨씬 더 나은 아내가 되었을 것이며, 또 내가 아내였던 그 어느 때에도, 내 처신으로 남편들에게 조금의 불편도 끼친 적이 없었다.\n\n그러나 이 모든 것이 부질없었으니, 나는 어떤 고무적인 전망도 찾지 못했다. 나는 기다렸고, 규칙적으로, 또 내 형편에 걸맞은 만큼 알뜰하게 살았으나, 아무것도 손에 잡히지 않았고, 아무것도 나타나지 않았으며, 밑천은 빠르게 줄어들었다. 무엇을 해야 할지 나는 알지 못했고, 다가오는 가난의 두려움이 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 돈이 좀 있긴 했으나 어디에 둘지 알지 못했고, 그 이자로는 나를 부양할 수도 없었으니, 적어도 런던에서는 그러했다.\n\n마침내 새로운 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 내가 묵던 집에 양갓집 부인 행세를 하는 북부 지방 여자가 하나 있었는데, 그녀의 이야기에서 자기 고장의 물가가 싸다는 것과 살림하기 수월하다는 것에 관한 자랑보다 더 자주 나오는 것이 없었으니, 모든 것이 얼마나 푸짐하고 얼마나 싼지, 얼마나 좋은 무리와 어울리는지 따위였다. 마침내 나는 그녀에게, 당신이 나를 거의 당신 고장에 가서 살고 싶게 만든다고 말했다. 나로 말하자면, 비록 살아갈 만큼은 충분히 있는 과부였으나, 그것을 불릴 길이 전혀 없었던 데다, 여기서는 어떤 무리와도 어울리지 않고, 하인도 두지 않고, 겉치레도 하지 않고, 마치 필요에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 은둔에 파묻혀 살지 않는 한, 한 해에 백 파운드 아래로는 살 수 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n진작 말해 두었어야 할 것이, 그녀는 다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로, 내가 큰 재산가라고, 적어도 삼사천 파운드는, 어쩌면 그 이상은 가지고 있고 그것이 다 내 손안에 있다고 늘 믿게 되어 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 내가 조금이라도 자기 고장에 갈 마음이 있다고 여기자 내게 무척 살갑게 굴었다. 그녀는 리버풀 근처에 사는 자매가 하나 있다고, 자기 오라비는 거기서 상당한 신사이며 아일랜드에도 큰 재산이 있다고, 자기는 두 달쯤 뒤에 그리로 내려갈 텐데, 만약 내가 거기까지 동행해 준다면, 내가 그 고장을 어떻게 여기는지 보게 될 때까지 한 달이고 그 이상이고 내 좋을 대로, 자기만큼이나 환영받을 것이라고 했다. 그리고 만약 내가 거기서 살 만하다고 여긴다면, 비록 자기네는 직접 하숙을 치지는 않으나, 어떤 마음 맞는 집안에 나를 추천하여 내 마음에 들게 자리 잡게 해 주도록 자기네가 책임지고 보살피겠노라고 장담했다.\n\n만약 이 여자가 내 진짜 형편을 알았더라면, 잡고 보니 별 쓸모도 없는 가엾고 외로운 신세 하나를 잡으려고 그토록 많은 덫을 놓고 그토록 많은 고단한 발품을 팔지는 않았을 것이다. 실로 나는, 내 처지가 거의 가망 없는 데다 이보다 더 나빠질 수도 없으리라 여긴 터라, 그들이 내게 몸으로 해코지만 하지 않는다면 무슨 일이 닥치든 그리 마음 졸이지 않았다. 그래서 나는, 비록 많은 권유와 진실한 우정과 참된 친절에 대한 거창한 다짐이 없지는 않았으나, 다시 말하건대, 나는 그녀와 함께 가도록 설득당하기에 나를 내맡겼고, 그리하여 비록 어디로 가는지 확실히 알지도 못한 채로, 짐을 꾸리고 여정에 오를 채비를 갖추었다.\n\n그리고 이제 나는 큰 곤경에 처한 나 자신을 발견했다. 세상에 내가 가진 보잘것없는 것은 앞서 말한 약간의 은붙이와 얼마간의 아마포와 내 옷가지를 빼고는 모두 돈이었다. 살림살이로 말하자면 거의 없거나 아예 없었으니, 나는 늘 셋방에서만 살아왔기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그 보잘것없는 것을 믿고 맡길 벗도, 그것을 어떻게 처분할지 일러 줄 벗도 세상에 하나 없었고, 이것이 밤낮으로 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 은행과, 또 런던의 다른 회사들을 떠올렸으나, 그 운용을 맡길 벗이 없었다. 그리고 은행 어음이며 증서며 지급 명령서 따위를 내가 지니고 다니는 것은 안전치 못하게 여겨졌으니, 만약 그것들을 잃으면 내 돈도 잃는 것이고 그러면 나는 끝장이며, 다른 한편으로 낯선 곳에서 그것들 때문에 강도를 당하거나 어쩌면 살해당할 수도 있었기 때문이다. 이것이 나를 이상하게 괴롭혔고, 무엇을 해야 할지 나는 알지 못했다.\n\n어느 날 아침 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐으니, 내가 직접 은행에 가 보자는 것이었다. 나는 이자가 붙는 어음 몇 장이 있어 그 이자를 받으러 거기에 자주 갔었고, 거기서 내가 찾아가 상대한 어떤 서기가 내게 매우 정직하고 공정했으니, 특히 한번은 어찌나 공정했던지, 내가 돈을 잘못 세어 받을 몫보다 적게 받고 나오려 하자, 그가 나를 바로잡아 나머지를 마저 주었던 것이다. 그것을 제 주머니에 넣을 수도 있었을 텐데 말이다.\n\n나는 그를 찾아가 내 사정을 아주 솔직하게 털어놓고, 가엾게도 벗 없는 과부이며 무엇을 해야 할지 모르는 나의 조언자가 되어 주는 수고를 해 주겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 만약 자기 업무 범위 안에 드는 무엇이든 내가 그의 의견을 바란다면, 내가 부당한 일을 당하지 않도록 힘을 다하겠노라고, 그러나 또한 자기가 아는 점잖고 진중한 사람 하나를 소개해 주겠다고 했다. 그자는 비록 자기네 회사 소속은 아니나 그런 업무의 서기이며, 판단이 뛰어나고 그 정직함은 내가 의지해도 좋다고 했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 그 사람을, 또 그가 밟는 모든 절차를 보증하겠습니다. 부인, 만약 그가 부인께 단 한 푼이라도 부당한 짓을 한다면, 그것은 제 책임이 될 것이고, 제가 그것을 갚아 드리겠습니다. 그리고 그 사람은 그런 경우에 사람들을 돕기를 좋아합니다. 자선을 베푸는 셈 치고 그 일을 하는 거지요.\"\n\n나는 이 말에 잠시 머뭇거렸으나, 잠깐 멈칫한 뒤에 그에게, 나는 차라리 당신을 의지하고 싶다고, 당신이 정직하다는 것을 알았기 때문이라고, 그러나 만약 그럴 수 없다면 당신의 추천을 받아들이겠노라고 말했다.\nn의 누구의 것보다도 먼저 말이다. \"감히 말씀드리건대, 부인,\" 그가 말했다, \"제 친구에게도 저에게만큼이나 만족하실 겁니다. 그 사람은 부인을 도울 능력이 충분하지만, 저는 그렇지 못하니까요.\" 보아하니 그는 은행 업무로 손이 가득 차서, 자기 직무 외에는 어떤 일에도 관여하지 않기로 약조했던 모양인데, 이는 내가 나중에야 들어 알게 된 일이지 그때는 이해하지 못했다. 그는 덧붙이기를, 자기 친구는 조언이나 도움에 대해 내게서 한 푼도 받지 않을 것이라고 했으니, 이는 참으로 나를 크게 안심시켰다.\n\n그는 은행 문을 닫고 업무가 끝난 뒤인 바로 그날 저녁에, 나더러 자기와 자기 친구를 만나러 오라고 일러 주었다. 그리고 정말이지 나는 그의 친구를 보자마자, 그가 그 일에 관해 말을 꺼내기 시작하자마자, 내가 매우 정직한 사람을 상대하게 되었다는 것을 충분히 알았으니, 그의 얼굴 생김새가 그것을 말해 주었고, 또 내가 나중에 들은 바로 그의 평판이 어디서나 너무도 좋았던지라, 나는 더 이상 의심을 품을 여지가 없었다.\n\n첫 만남에서 나는 앞서 했던 말을 되풀이했을 뿐이었고, 우리는 헤어졌으며, 그는 나더러 이튿날 자기에게 오라고 약속을 정하면서, 그동안 내가 수소문하여 그에 관해 안심해도 좋다고 일러 주었으나, 나로서는 아는 사람도 없는 터라 그 일을 어떻게 잘 해낼지 알 수가 없었다.\n\n그리하여 나는 이튿날 그를 만났고, 그제야 좀 더 거리낌 없이 내 사정 안으로 들어갔다. 나는 내 처지를 죽 늘어놓았으니, 곧 나는 아메리카에서 건너온 과부로 완전히 의지가지없고 벗도 없는 신세이며, 약간의 돈이 있으나 그저 약간일 뿐인데, 그 관리를 믿고 맡길 벗이 세상에 하나도 없는 까닭에 그것을 잃을까 두려워 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이라고 말했다. 또 나는 밑천이 축나지 않도록 물가가 싼 잉글랜드 북부로 가서 살려 하며, 기꺼이 그 돈을 은행에 맡기고 싶으나 어음을 몸에 지니고 다닐 엄두는 나지 않는다는 둥, 앞서 말한 것과 같은 사정을 늘어놓았으며, 그 일을 어떻게, 또 누구와 더불어 연락해 처리할지 알 수 없다고 했다.\n\n그는 내게 그 돈을 계좌로 은행에 맡기면 그것이 장부에 기재되어 언제든 그 돈에 대한 권리를 갖게 되고, 내가 북부에 있더라도 출납계 앞으로 어음을 발행하여 원할 때 받을 수 있다고 일러 주었으나, 다만 그리되면 그것은 유통 현금으로 간주되어 은행이 이자를 주지 않으리라고 했다. 또 그 돈으로 주식을 살 수도 있으니 그러면 그것이 나를 위해 비축되어 있겠으나, 다만 그것을 처분하고자 하면 일부러 상경하여 명의를 옮겨야 하고, 게다가 내가 직접 이곳에 있거나 그 주식을 자기 명의로 두고 나를 대신해 그 일을 해 줄 믿을 만한 벗이 없는 한 반년치 배당금을 받기조차 다소 어려울 터인데, 그 또한 앞서 말한 것과 같은 어려움이 따를 것이라고 했다. 그러고는 나를 빤히 쳐다보며 살짝 미소를 지었다. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"부인, 어찌하여 부인과 부인의 돈을 함께 거두어 맡아 줄 집사 우두머리 하나를 얻지 않으시오? 그러면 그 수고를 부인 손에서 덜어 낼 수 있을 텐데요.\" \"그럼요, 어르신, 그리고 어쩌면 돈까지도 덜어 가겠지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"정말이지 그쪽 방도의 위험이 이쪽 방도의 위험만큼이나 크다는 걸 알겠으니까요.\" 그러나 나는 속으로 가만히 이렇게 말한 것이 기억난다. \"당신이 그 질문을 점잖게 내게 던져 주면 좋으련만, 그러면 아니라고 말하기 전에 아주 진지하게 생각해 볼 텐데.\"\n\n그는 나와 더불어 꽤 멀리까지 이야기를 끌고 갔고, 나는 한두 번쯤 그가 진심이라고 생각했으나, 정말로 나를 비통하게 한 것은, 마침내 그에게 아내가 있다는 것을 알게 된 일이었다. 그러나 아내가 있음을 인정하면서 그는 고개를 가로젓고는 다소 근심스레, 정말이지 자기에게는 아내가 있으되 아내가 없다고 말했다. 나는 그가 내 죽은 정부情夫와 같은 처지에 놓여 있어, 그의 아내가 병들었거나 미쳤거나 그 비슷한 무엇이리라 생각하기 시작했다. 어쨌든 그때는 더 많은 이야기를 나누지 못했으나, 그는 내게 지금은 일이 너무 바쁘지만, 업무가 끝난 뒤에 자기 집으로 와 준다면 그때까지 내 일을 안전한 자세로 놓아 주기 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 궁리해 보겠다고 했다. 나는 가겠노라 말하고 그가 어디에 사는지 알고 싶다고 했다. 그는 종이에 주소를 적어 주었고, 그것을 건네면서 내게 읽어 주고는 말했다. \"여기 있습니다, 부인, 저를 믿고 부인 자신을 맡기실 엄두가 나신다면요.\" \"예, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"제 몸을 어르신께 맡겨도 좋을 듯합니다. 어르신께는 아내가 있다 하셨고 저는 남편을 원하지 않으니까요. 게다가 제 돈을, 제가 세상에 가진 전부를 어르신께 맡길 엄두가 나니, 그것이 사라진다 한들 제 몸이야 어디에든 맡길 수 있겠지요.\"\n\n그는 농담조로 몇 마디 했는데, 그것이 매우 멋지고 점잖아서, 진심이었다면 나를 흡족하게 했을 법한 말이었다. 그러나 그 일은 그냥 지나갔고, 나는 주소를 받아들고 같은 날 저녁 일곱 시에 그의 집으로 찾아가기로 약속을 정했다.\n\n내가 갔더니 그는 내 돈을 은행에 맡겨 이자를 받게 할 여러 방안을 내놓았으나, 여전히 이런저런 어려움이 끼어들었고, 그는 그것이 안전하지 않다며 반대했다. 그러고는 나는 그에게서 너무도 진실하고 사심 없는 정직함을 발견하여, 속으로 곰곰이 생각하기를, 내가 분명 내가 원하던 정직한 사람을 찾아냈으며 결코 이보다 더 나은 손에 나 자신을 맡길 수 없으리라고 여기게 되었다. 그리하여 나는 매우 솔직하게 그에게 말하기를, 나는 아직껏 믿을 만하거나 그와 더불어 내가 안전하다고 여길 만한 남자도 여자도 만나 본 적이 없으나, 그가 내 안전을 그토록 사심 없이 염려하는 것을 보았으니, 만약 그가 봉급 한 푼 못 주는 가엾은 과부의 집사가 되기를 받아들여 준다면 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것의 관리를 기꺼이 그에게 맡기겠다고 했다.\n\n그는 미소를 짓고는 일어서서 매우 정중하게 내게 인사를 했다. 그는 내가 자기를 그토록 좋게 여겨 주니 그저 매우 고맙게 받아들일 수밖에 없다고 했고, 나를 속이지 않을 것이며, 봉급은 바라지 않고 자기 힘닿는 데까지 무엇이든 해 나를 섬기겠노라 했다. 그러나 결코 그러한 신탁을 받아들일 수는 없으니, 그것이 자기를 사리사욕의 혐의로 끌고 갈 수 있고, 또 내가 죽기라도 하면 내 유언집행인들과 분쟁이 생길 수 있는데, 그런 짐을 스스로 떠안는 것은 몹시 꺼려진다고 했다.\n\n나는 그에게 그것이 그의 반대의 전부라면 곧 그것을 제거하고, 조금도 어려움의 여지가 없음을 납득시켜 주겠다고 했다. 왜냐하면 첫째, 그를 의심하는 일에 관해서라면, 내가 만약 언제고 그를 의심한다면 바로 지금이 그를 의심하여 신탁을 그의 손에 맡기지 않을 때이며, 또 내가 언제든 그를 의심하게 되면 그는 그때 그것을 던져 버리고 더 나아가기를 거절하면 그만이기 때문이었다. 그다음, 유언집행인에 관해서라면, 나는 그에게 잉글랜드에 상속인도 친척도 없으며, 내가 죽기 전에 처지가 바뀌지 않는 한 그 자신 말고는 상속인도 유언집행인도 두지 않을 것이고, 그렇게 처지가 바뀌면 그의 신탁과 수고도 함께 끝날 것이라고 단언했으나, 다만 그런 전망은 아직 없다고 했다. 그러나 내가 지금 이대로 죽는다면 그것은 모두 그 자신의 것이 될 것이며, 내가 확신하는 만큼 그가 내게 충실히 해 줄 터이니 마땅히 그것을 받을 자격이 있으리라고 일러 주었다.\n\n그는 이 말에 낯빛이 바뀌더니, 어쩌다 내가 자기에게 그토록 큰 호의를 품게 되었느냐고 물었고, 매우 흡족한 표정으로, 나를 위해서라면 자기가 독신이기를 바라는 것도 지극히 떳떳한 일이겠다고 말했다. 나는 미소 지으며, 그가 독신이 아닌 이상 내 제안에는 그를 향한 어떤 속셈도 담길 수 없으며, 그가 그러듯 바라는 것은 허락될 수 없으니, 그의 아내에게 죄가 되는 일이라고 말했다.\n\n그는 내가 틀렸다고 했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 그가 말했다, \"부인, 앞서 말씀드렸듯 제게는 아내가 있으되 아내가 없으니, 만약 그게 다라면 그 여자가 교수형당하기를 바란다 해도 제게 죄가 되지 않을 겁니다.\" \"그쪽 방면으로 어르신 사정을 전혀 모릅니다, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 아내가 죽기를 바라는 것이 결백한 일일 수는 없지요.\" \"다시 말씀드리지만,\" 그가 거듭 말했다, \"그 여자는 아내이면서 아내가 아니랍니다. 부인은 제가 어떤 사람인지, 그 여자가 어떤 사람인지 모르세요.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신, 어르신이 어떤 분인지는 모르나, 정직한 분이라 믿으며, 그것이 제가 어르신을 그토록 신뢰하는 까닭이랍니다.\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요,\" 그가 말했다. \"저 역시 그렇기를 바라지요. 하지만 저는 또 다른 무엇이기도 하답니다, 부인. 까놓고 말씀드리자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 오쟁이 진 사내요, 그 여자는 창녀랍니다.\" 그는 일종의 농담조로 그렇게 말했으나, 어찌나 어색한 미소를 띠었던지, 나는 그것이 그에게 매우 가깝게 와닿는 일임을 알아챘고, 그는 그 말을 할 때 음울한 표정이었다.\n\n\"그러면 정말 사정이 달라지는군요, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신이 말씀하시던 그 부분에 관해서는요. 하지만 오쟁이 진 사내라도, 아시다시피, 정직한 사람일 수 있지요. 그건 그 점을 조금도 바꾸지 않으니까요. 게다가 제 생각엔,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신의 아내가 어르신에게 그토록 부정하다면, 어르신은 그 여자를 아내로 인정하기엔 너무 정직하신 분입니다만, 그건,\" 내가 말했다, \"제가 상관할 바가 아니지요.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그 여자에게서 손을 털 생각이 없습니다. 까놓고 말씀드리자면, 부인,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"저는 또 만족하는 오쟁이 진 사내도 아니랍니다. 오히려 그 일은 저를 더없이 분노케 하지요. 그러나 어쩔 도리가 없으니, 창녀가 되려는 여자는 기어이 창녀가 되고 마는 법이지요.\"\n\n나는 그 이야기를 비껴가며 내 일을 꺼내려 했으나, 그가 그것을 끝낼 수 없으리라는 것을 알아채고는 그냥 내버려 두었고, 그는 자기 사정의 온갖 정황을 죽 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 여기 옮기기에는 너무 길다. 특히 그가 지금의 자리에 오기 얼마 전 한동안 잉글랜드를 떠나 있는 사이, 그 여자가 어떤 군 장교와의 사이에서 아이 둘을 낳았다는 것, 그리고 그가 잉글랜드에 돌아와 그 여자가 잘못을 빌자 다시 거두어들여 매우 잘 부양해 주었건만, 그 여자는 어떤 포목상 도제와 함께 그에게서 달아나며 손에 닿는 대로 그의 것을 훔쳐 갔고, 여전히 그를 떠나 살고 있다는 것이었다. \"그러니, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 여자는 흔히 여자들을 꾀는 미끼인 궁핍 때문이 아니라, 천성으로, 또 그 악덕 그 자체를 위하여 창녀랍니다.\"\n\n나는 그가 가여워, 그가 그 여자를 잘 떼어 내기를 바랐고, 여전히 내 일을 이야기하려 했으나, 도무지 되지 않았다. 마침내 그가 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다. \"보세요, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"부인은 제게 조언을 구하러 오셨고, 저는 부인이 제 친누이라도 되는 양 충실히 부인을 섬길 겁니다. 하지만 부인이 저더러 그러게 하시고, 또 제게 그토록 다정하시니, 이번엔 제가 처지를 뒤바꿔 부인께 조언을 구해야겠습니다. 말씀해 주시오, 가엾게 능멸당한 사내가 창녀를 두고 어찌해야 하겠습니까? 그 여자에게 정당한 응징을 하려면 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"아아! 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"그건 제가 조언하기엔 너무 미묘한 경우입니다만, 보아하니 그 여자가 어르신에게서 달아났으니 어르신은 깨끗이 그 여자를 떼어 내신 셈이지요. 더 무엇을 바라시겠어요?\" \"그래요, 그 여자는 정말 가 버렸지요,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 저는 그 여자에게서 자유롭지 못하답니다.\"\n\n\"그건 사실이지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 여자가 정말 어르신을 빚더미에 앉힐 수는 있겠지만, 법이 그것 또한 막을 방도를 어르신께 마련해 주었으니, 이른바 공시 거부를 하시면 됩니다.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것도 아니랍니다. 그 부분은 다 대비해 두었으니, 제가 말하는 건 그 부분이 아닙니다. 다만 저는 다시 결혼할 수 있도록 그 여자를 떼어 내고 싶은 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그럼, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"그렇다면 그 여자와 이혼하셔야지요. 어르신이 하신 말씀을 입증할 수 있다면 분명 그 일을 해내실 수 있을 테고, 그러면, 짐작건대, 어르신은 자유의 몸이 되시겠지요.\"\n\n\"그건 아주 지루하고 돈이 많이 드는 일이지요,\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"왜요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신이 마음에 드는 여자를 구해 어르신 말을 믿게 할 수 있다면, 짐작건대 어르신의 아내도 자기가 누리는 그 자유를 어르신께서 누린다고 다투지는 못할 겁니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지요,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 정직한 여자를 끌어들여 그리하게 하는 건 어려운 일이지요. 그리고 그 다른 부류로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 그 여자 하나로 창녀라면 더 상대하기에 진절머리가 나거든요.\"\n\n문득 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"당신이 그저 그 질문을 내게 던지기만 했다면, 나는 진심으로 당신의 말을 믿어 주었을 텐데.\" 그러나 그것은 속으로 한 말이었다. 그에게는 이렇게 답했다. \"왜요, 어르신은 어떤 정직한 여자가 어르신을 받아들이든 그 문을 닫아 버리시는군요. 어르신께 모험을 걸 모든 여자를 단번에 단죄하시고, 정말이지 지금 어르신을 받아들이는 여자는 정직할 수 없다고 결론지으시니까요.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"정직한 여자가 저를 받아 줄 거라고 부인이 저를 납득시켜 주신다면 좋겠군요. 그러면 모험을 걸 텐데요.\" 그러고는 갑자기 내게로 돌아서며, \"부인, 저를 받아 주시겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그건 공정한 질문이 아니지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신이 하신 말씀 뒤에 말이에요. 하지만 제가 그 말을 거두기만 기다린다고 여기실까 봐, 분명히 답해 드리지요. 아니요, 저는 아닙니다. 어르신과는 다른 종류의 볼일이 있으며, 저는 어르신이 제 절박한 처지에 관한 진지한 청을 희극으로 바꾸어 놓으시리라고는 예상하지 못했습니다.\"\n\n\"왜요, 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"제 처지도 부인 처지만큼이나 절박하고, 부인 못지않게 조언이 절실합니다. 어딘가에서 구원을 얻지 못한다면 제가 미쳐 버릴 것 같으니, 단언컨대 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠거든요.\"\n\n\"왜요, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신 처지에선 조언을 드리기가 쉽지요, 제 처지에서보다 훨씬 쉽답니다.\" \"그럼 말씀해 주시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"부탁드립니다, 이제 부인이 저를 북돋아 주시니까요.\"\n\n\"왜요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신 처지가 말씀하시는 만큼 분명하다면, 어르신은 합법적으로 이혼하실 수 있고, 그러면 점잖게 청혼할 정직한 여자를 얼마든지 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다. 여자라는 성이 그리 귀하지 않으니 아내가 없어 곤란하실 일은 없지요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다, 그럼,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 진심입니다. 부인 조언을 따르지요. 하지만 그에 앞서 진지하게 한 가지만 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"무슨 질문이든요,\" 내가 말했다. \"전에 하셨던 그것만 빼고요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 그 답으론 안 됩니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"왜냐하면, 한마디로, 바로 그것이 제가 여쭐 질문이니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 질문이든 마음껏 하셔도 좋지만, 그것에 대해선 이미 제 답을 들으셨지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"게다가, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신은 제가 그런 질문에 미리 어떤 답이라도 내놓으리만치 저를 그토록 나쁘게 여기실 수 있나요? 살아 있는 어떤 여자가 어르신이 진심이라고 믿거나, 어르신이 그 여자를 놀려먹는 것 말고 다른 무엇을 꾀한다고 여기겠어요?\"\n\n\"그래요, 그래요,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 부인을 놀리는 게 아니라 진심입니다. 한번 생각해 보세요.\"\n\n\"하지만, 어르신,\" 내가 다소 진지하게 말했다. \"저는 제 볼일로 어르신을 찾아왔답니다. 부탁드리니, 제게 무엇을 하라고 조언하실지 알려 주세요.\"\n\n\"준비해 두겠습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"부인이 다시 오실 때까지요.\"\n\n\"아니요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신은 제가 더 오는 것을 금하셨잖아요.\"\n\n\"어찌하여요?\" 그가 말하며 다소 놀란 표정을 지었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신이 말씀하시는 그런 명목으로 제가 어르신을 찾아오리라 기대하실 수는 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"좋습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도 부인은 제게 다시 오겠다고 약속하셔야 합니다. 그리고 이혼을 얻어 낼 때까지는 그 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않겠습니다. 다만 그 일이 끝나면 부인이 더 나은 처지가 되도록 준비해 주시기 바랍니다. 부인이 그 여자가 되어 주셔야 하니까요. 그러지 않으면 저는 아예 이혼하지 않겠습니다. 다른 까닭이 없다 해도, 부인의 뜻밖의 친절에 제가 빚을 진 것이거니와, 저는 다른 이유들도 있답니다.\"\n\n그는 세상 그 무엇으로도 나를 그보다 더 기쁘게 할 수 없었다. 그러나 나는, 그를 붙드는 길은 일이 그렇게 멀어 보이는 동안 거리를 두는 것이며, 그가 그것을 이행할 능력이 되었을 때 받아들여도 늦지 않다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 그에게 매우 정중하게, 그가 이런 일들을 이야기할 처지가 되었을 때 그것을 생각해도 늦지 않다고 했고, 그동안 나는 그에게서 멀리 떠나갈 것이며, 그는 나보다 더 마음에 드는 상대를 얼마든지 찾을 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 우리는 지금으로서는 여기서 이야기를 끊었고, 그는 내게 이튿날 다시 와서 내 일에 관한 그의 결정을 들으라고 약속하게 했으니, 나는 몇 차례 채근을 받은 끝에 그리했으나, 그가 내 속을 더 깊이 들여다보았더라면 그 일에 관해서라면 나는 채근이 전혀 필요 없었던 것이다.\n\n그리하여 나는 이튿날 저녁에 갔고, 하녀를 두고 있음을 그에게 보이려고 하녀를 데려갔으나, 안으로 들어가자마자 곧 돌려보냈다. 그는 내게 하녀를 머물게 하라고 했으나 나는 그러지 않고, 큰 소리로 아홉 시쯤 다시 나를 데리러 오라고 하녀에게 일렀다. 그러나 그가 그것을 막으며, 나를 집까지 안전히 바래다주겠다고 했는데, 사실 나는 그것이 그리 달갑지 않았으니, 그가 내가 어디 사는지 알아내어 내 평판과 처지를 캐묻기 위해 그러는 것일지 모른다고 생각했기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그것을 무릅썼으니, 그곳이나 그 인근 사람들이 나에 관해 아는 바라곤 죄다 내게 유리한 것이었기 때문이다. 그가 수소문한 끝에 나에 관해 얻은 평판이라곤, 내가 재산 있는 여자이며 매우 정숙하고 진중한 사람이라는 것뿐이었으니, 그것이 대체로 사실이든 아니든, 무릇 세상에서 무언가를 기대하는 모든 여자가, 어쩌면 그 정조라는 것 자체는 이미 희생했을지언정, 정조 있다는 평판만큼은 지키는 것이 얼마나 긴요한 일인지 여러분도 알 수 있을 것이다.\n\n나는 그가 나를 위해 만찬을 마련해 둔 것을 알았고, 그것이 적잖이 기뻤다. 또 그가 매우 훌륭하게 살고 있으며, 매우 훌륭하게 꾸민 집을 가지고 있다는 것도 알았으니, 나는 그 모두가 참으로 기뻤다. 그 모두를 다 내 것으로 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 이제 지난번 회담의 주제를 두고 두 번째 회담을 가졌다. 그는 정말이지 자기 속내를 아주 곧장 들이밀었다. 그는 내게 자기 애정을 단언했고, 정말이지 나는 그것을 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그는 그것이 내가 그와 처음 이야기를 나눈 그 순간부터, 내가 그에게 내 재물을 맡기겠다는 이야기를 꺼내기 훨씬 전부터 비롯되었노라 단언했다. \"언제 비롯되었든 무슨 상관이랴,\" 나는 생각했다. \"그것이 변치만 않는다면 그것으로 충분하지.\" 그러고 나서 그는 내 재물을 자기에게 믿고 맡겨 그에게 남기겠다는 내 제안이 자기를 얼마나 사로잡았는지 말했다. \"그러라고 그런 거였지,\" 나는 생각했다. \"하지만 그땐 당신도 독신인 줄 알았단 말이야.\" 만찬을 마친 뒤 나는 그가 포도주를 두세 잔 마시라고 나를 몹시 채근하는 것을 알아챘으나, 사양하고 한두 잔만 마셨다. 그러고 나서 그는 내게 한 가지 제안할 것이 있다며, 혹 그것을 들어주지 못하더라도 언짢게 여기지 않겠다고 약속해 달라고 했다. 나는 그가 내게 불명예스러운 제안을 하지 않기를 바라며, 특히 자기 집에서는 더더욱 그렇기를 바란다고 했고, 만약 그런 것이라면 부디 그것을 제안하지 말아 달라고, 그래야 내가 그에게 표한 존경과 그의 집으로 찾아옴으로써 그에게 두었던 신뢰에 어울리지 않는 어떤 분노도 그에게 드러낼 필요가 없으리라고 했으며, 부디 떠나도 좋다고 허락해 달라 청하고는, 그러면서 장갑을 끼고 떠날 채비를 하기 시작했으나, 정작 그때 나는 그가 나를 떠나보낼 마음이 없는 것 못지않게 떠날 마음이 조금도 없었다.\n\n자, 그는 떠난다는 말은 하지 말라고 나를 졸랐다. 그는 나에 관해 어떤 불명예스러운 것도 마음에 품지 않았으며, 불명예스러운 무언가를 내게 제안하는 것과는 거리가 멀다고 단언했고, 만약 내가 그렇게 여긴다면 그것에 관해서는 더 이상 말하지 않는 편을 택하겠노라 했다.\n\n그 부분이 나는 도무지 마뜩잖았다. 나는 그가 자기 자신에게 부끄러운 것이나 내가 듣기에 부적절한 것은 아무것도 말하지 않으리라 믿고서, 그가 하려는 말이라면 무엇이든 들을 준비가 되어 있다고 했다. 그러자 그는 내게 자기 제안이 이러하다고 말했다. 곧 그가 아직 창녀인 자기 아내에게서 이혼을 얻어 내지 못했더라도 나와 결혼하자는 것이었다. 그리고 자기가 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있음을 내게 납득시키기 위해, 이혼이 성사될 때까지는 나더러 자기와 함께 살거나 잠자리를 같이하자고 청하지 않겠노라 약속하겠다고 했다. 내 마음은 그 제안에 첫마디부터 그러자고 답했으나, 그와 좀 더 위선을 떨 필요가 있었다. 그래서 나는 다소 발끈하며 그 제안을 사양하는 척했고, 게다가 그것을 온당치 못한 일이라며 약간 책망하면서, 그러한 제안은 우리 둘을 크나큰 곤경에 옭아매는 것 말고는 아무 의미도 없으리라고 했다. 왜냐하면 그가 끝내 이혼을 얻어 내지 못한다면 우리는 그 혼인을 무를 수도 없고, 그렇다고 그것을 밀고 나아갈 수도 없을 터이니, 만약 그가 이혼에 실패한다면 우리 둘이 어떤 처지에 놓일지 한번 생각해 보라고 했다.\n\n요컨대 나는 이에 반대하는 논변을 멀리까지 끌고 가서, 그것이 도무지 사리에 닿는 제안이 아님을 그에게 납득시켰다. 그러자 그는 거기서 또 다른 제안으로 옮겨 갔으니, 곧 내가 그와 더불어 계약서에 서명하고 봉인하여, 이혼이 성사되는 즉시 그와 결혼하기로 조건을 정하되, 그가 이혼을 얻어 내지 못하면 그것을 무효로 한다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 그런 것은 다른 제안보다 한결 합리적이라고 했으나, 다만 그가 이 일에 진심일 만큼 어수룩하리라고 내가 상상한 것은 이번이 처음인 데다, 나는 첫 청에 그러자고 말하는 버릇이 없으니 그것을 생각해 보겠노라 했다.\n\n나는 이 연인을 낚시꾼이 송어를 다루듯 가지고 놀았다. 나는 그를 단단히 낚싯바늘에 꿰어 두었음을 알았기에, 그의 새 제안을 농담으로 받아넘기며 그를 미루어 두었다. 나는 그에게, 그가 나를 거의 모르니 나에 관해 수소문해 보라고 했다. 또 그가 내 숙소까지 나를 바래다 가게는 했으나 안으로 들어오라고 청하지는 않았으니, 그것이 점잖지 못하다고 했기 때문이다.\n\n요컨대 나는 과감히 혼인 계약서 서명을 피했는데, 내가 그리한 까닭은, 나더러 함께 랭커셔로 가자고 그토록 간곡히 청했던 그 부인이 어찌나 단호하게 그 일을 우기며, 그곳에서 그토록 큰 재산이며 그토록 좋은 것들을 약속했던지, 가서 한번 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 동했기 때문이다. \"어쩌면,\" 나는 생각했다, \"내 처지를 크게 나아지게 할 수도 있겠지.\" 그러자 나는 마음속에서 내 정직한 시민과 헤어지는 데 아무 거리낌도 두지 않았으니, 나는 그를, 더 부유한 자를 위해 그를 떠나지 못할 만큼 깊이 사랑하지는 않았던 것이다.\n\n한마디로 나는 계약을 피했다. 다만 그에게, 나는 북부로 갈 것이며, 내가 그에게 맡긴 일의 진행을 통해 어디로 편지를 보내면 될지 알게 될 것이고, 내가 그를 존중한다는 충분한 담보를 그에게 줄 터이니, 곧 내가 세상에 가진 거의 전부를 그의 손에 맡겨 두겠노라고 했다. 또 이만큼은 그에게 약속하겠으니, 그가 자기 첫 아내에게서 이혼을 청구해 그 소식을 내게 보내는 즉시 내가 런던으로 올라올 것이고, 그러면 그때 우리가 그 일을 진지하게 의논하자고 했다.\n\n내가 품고 간 것은 비열한 속셈이었으니, 고백건대 그러했다. 다만 뒤에 드러나듯이, 나는 내 것보다 훨씬 더 고약한 속셈을 품은 자에게 그리로 초대받은 것이었다. 자, 나는 내가 벗이라 부르던 그 여자와 함께 랭커셔로 갔다. 가는 내내 그 여자는 더없이 진실하고 꾸밈없는 애정을 풍기며 나를 어루만져 주었고, 가는 길 내내 마찻삯만 빼고 모든 것을 대접해 주었다. 그리고 그 여자의 오라비가 우리를 맞으러 워링턴까지 신사의 마차를 가지고 왔고, 우리는 거기서 내가 바랄 수 있는 만큼의 격식을 다 갖추어 리버풀까지 실려 갔다. 우리는 또한 리버풀의 어느 상인 집에서 사나흘간 매우 훌륭한 대접을 받았다. 뒤에 벌어진 일 때문에 그의 이름은 밝히지 않겠다. 그러고 나서 그 여자는 자기 외삼촌 댁으로 나를 데려갈 텐데, 거기서 우리가 귀하게 대접받을 것이라고 했다. 그 여자는 그리했으니, 그 여자가 외삼촌이라 부르는 이가 우리를 위해 사두마차를 보내왔고, 우리는 어딘지도 모를 곳으로 사십 마일 가까이 실려 갔다.\n\n그러나 우리는 어느 신사의 저택에 다다랐으니, 그곳에는 식솔이 많고, 큰 사냥터가 있으며, 정말이지 비범한 사람들이 모여 있었고, 거기서 그 여자는 사촌이라 불렸다. 나는 그 여자에게, 만약 이런 사람들 틈으로 나를 데려올 작정이었다면 내가 채비를 갖추고 더 나은 옷을 마련하게 해 주었어야 했다고 말했다. 부인들이 그 점을 알아채고는 매우 점잖게 내게 말하기를, 자기네 고장에서는 런던에서처럼 사람을 옷으로 그리 따지지 않으며, 자기들 사촌이 내 신분에 관해 충분히 일러 주었으니 나를 돋보이게 할 옷 따위는 필요 없다고 했다. 요컨대 그들은 나를 실제의 나가 아니라 그들이 생각하는 나, 곧 큰 재산을 지닌 과부 부인으로 여겨 대접해 주었다.\n\n내가 여기서 처음으로 알아낸 사실은, 그 집안이 모두 로마 가톨릭교도이며, 내가 벗이라 부르던 그 사촌 또한 그러하다는 것이었다. 그러나 세상 누구도 내게 그보다 더 잘 대해 줄 수 없었다고 말해야겠으니, 내가 그들과 같은 견해를 지녔더라도 받았을 만한 온갖 정중한 대접을 다 받았던 것이다. 사실 나는 신앙 문제에 까다로울 만큼 어떤 종류의 신조도 갖고 있지 않았던 터라, 곧 로마 교회를 호의적으로 말하는 법을 익혔다. 특히 나는 그들에게, 종교를 둘러싼 그리스도교도들 사이의 온갖 차이에서 교육의 편견 말고는 거의 아무것도 보이지 않으며, 만약 어쩌다 내 아버지가 로마 가톨릭교도였다면 나도 내 종교만큼이나 그들의 종교에 흡족해했으리라는 데 의심이 없다고 말했다.\n\n이는 그들을 더없이 흡족하게 했고, 나는 밤낮으로 좋은 사람들과 유쾌한 담소에 둘러싸였을 뿐 아니라, 종교 문제로 나를 물고 늘어지는 나이 든 부인이 두셋 있었다. 나는 어찌나 고분고분했던지, 완전히 발을 들이지는 않으면서도 그들의 미사에 참석하고, 그들이 본을 보여 주는 대로 그들의 온갖 몸짓을 따라 하는 데 거리낌이 없었으나, 너무 헐값에 넘어가려 하지는 않았다. 그리하여 대체로 나는, 그들이 가톨릭 교리라 부르는 것을 배운다면 로마 가톨릭교도가 되리라는 기대를 그들이 품도록 부추기는 데 그쳤고, 그렇게 그 일은 그대로 머물렀다.\n\n나는 여기서 여섯 주쯤 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 나를 인도하던 그 여자가 나를 리버풀에서 여섯 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 시골 마을로 도로 데려갔는데, 거기서 그 여자의 (그 여자가 부르는 대로 하자면) 오라비가 좋은 제복을 입은 종복 둘을 거느리고 자기 사두마차를 타고 매우 훌륭한 풍채로 나를 찾아왔다. 그리고 그다음 일은 내게 구애하는 것이었다. 내게 일어났던 일들로 보아, 사람들은 내가 속아 넘어갈 리 없으리라 여길 테고, 정말이지 나 자신도 그렇게 생각했으니, 집에 안전한 패를 쥐고 있어, 내 처지를 크게 나아지게 할 수 없는 한 그것을 버리지 않으리라 작정하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 모든 정황으로 보아 이 오라비는 내가 귀 기울일 만한 가치가 있는 혼처였으니, 그의 재산이 적게 잡아도 연 1000파운드로 평가되었으나, 그 누이는 연 1500파운드의 가치가 있으며 그 대부분이 아일랜드에 있다고 했다.\n\n큰 재산가이며 또 그런 사람으로 통하던 나는, 내 재산이 얼마인지 따위를 질문받을 처지를 넘어선 사람이었다. 그리고 내 거짓 벗은 어리석은 소문을 곧이듣고서 그것을 500파운드에서 5000파운드로 부풀렸으며, 그 고장에 들어올 무렵에는 1만 5000파운드라 일컫고 있었다. 그 아일랜드인은—내가 알기로 그는 그러했다—이 미끼에 완전히 미쳐 버렸다. 요컨대 그는 내게 구애했고, 내게 선물을 했으며, 자기 행차와 구애에 드는 비용 때문에 미친 사람처럼 빚을 졌다. 그는, 공정히 말하자면, 비범하게 멋진 신사의 풍모를 지니고 있었으니, 키가 크고 몸매가 훤칠했으며 비범한 화술을 지녔다. 그는 자기 사냥터며 마구간이며 자기 말들, 사냥터지기들, 숲, 소작인들, 하인들에 관해, 마치 우리가 그 저택 안에 있어 내가 그것들을 죄다 내 둘레에서 본 것처럼 자연스럽게 이야기했다.\n\n그는 내 재산이나 자산에 관해서는 묻지도 않은 채, 다만 우리가 더블린에 가면 내게 연 600파운드의 좋은 땅을 과부 재산으로 떼어 주겠으며, 그것의 이행을 위해 여기서 양도 증서나 계약을 맺을 수 있다고 단언했다.\n\n이는 정말이지 내가 익숙지 않았던 종류의 말이었고, 나는 여기서 내 온갖 계산이 다 무너지고 말았다. 내 품속에는 암녀석 하나가 들어앉아, 시시각각 자기 오라비가 얼마나 호화롭게 사는지 내게 일러 주었다. 한번은 내 마차를 어떻게 칠하고 어떻게 안감을 댈지 내 분부를 받으러 왔고, 또 한번은 내 시동이 어떤 옷을 입을지 물었다. 요컨대 내 눈은 현혹되었다. 나는 이제 아니라고 말하는 힘을 잃어버렸고, 이야기를 줄이자면, 결혼하기로 승낙하고 말았다. 다만 좀 더 은밀히 하려고 우리는 시골 더 깊숙이 실려 가, 어느 로마 가톨릭 성직자에게 결혼식을 올렸는데, 그가 잉글랜드 교회 목사 못지않게 우리를 확실히 맺어 주리라는 보장을 받았다.\n\n이 일에서 나를 진심으로 사랑했던, 또 자기를 정말이지 야만스럽게 굴려먹은 어떤 수치스러운 창녀에게서 자기 몸을 떼어 내려 애쓰며 자신의 새로운 선택에서 무한한 행복을 기약했던 그 충실한 시민을 불명예스럽게 저버린 데 대해, 약간의 자책이 없었다고는 할 수 없다. 그런데 그 선택받은 여자는 이제, 그의 아내가 했을 법한 만큼이나 거의 수치스러운 방식으로 자기를 다른 자에게 내주고 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 이제 나를 속이는 자가 된, 속아 넘어간 그 피조물이 시시각각 내 상상 앞에 그려 보인, 큰 재산이며 좋은 것들의 번쩍이는 신발이 나를 휩쓸어 가, 런던이나 그곳의 무엇을 생각할 겨를을 조금도 주지 않았으며, 지금 내 앞에 있는 것보다 무한히 더 참된 가치를 지닌 사람에게 내가 진 의무는 더더욱 생각할 겨를을 주지 않았다.\n\n그러나 일은 이미 벌어졌다. 나는 이제 새 남편의 품에 안겼고, 그는 여전히 예전과 다름없어 보였으니, 호화롭다 할 만큼 화려했으며, 그가 차린 통상의 행차를 떠받치는 데는 적어도 연 1000파운드는 들 것 같았다.\n\n우리가 결혼한 지 한 달쯤 되었을 때, 그는 아일랜드로 떠나는 배를 타기 위해 내가 웨스트체스터로 가는 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다. 그러나 그는 나를 서두르게 하지 않았으니, 우리는 세 주가량을 더 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 그는 사람들이 블랙록이라 부르는, 리버풀 맞은편의 그곳으로 우리를 맞으러 마차를 보내 달라고 체스터로 사람을 보냈다. 우리는 거기로, 사람들이 피니스라 부르는, 노가 여섯 개 달린 멋진 배를 타고 갔고, 그의 하인들과 말들과 짐은 나룻배로 갔다. 그는 내게 체스터에 아는 사람이 없으니, 먼저 가서 어느 개인 집에 나를 위해 훌륭한 거처를 구해 두겠다고 양해를 구했다. 나는 그에게 체스터에 얼마나 머물 것이냐고 물었다. 그는 전혀 머물지 않을 것이며, 하룻밤이나 이틀 밤보다 더 길지는 않을 것이고, 곧장 홀리헤드로 갈 마차를 빌리겠다고 했다. 그래서 나는 그에게, 하룻밤이나 이틀 밤을 위해 개인 숙소를 구하느라 수고할 일이 결코 아니니, 체스터가 큰 곳인 만큼 분명 매우 좋은 여관과 넉넉한 숙박 시설이 있을 것이라고 했다. 그리하여 우리는 대성당에서 멀지 않은 웨스트 거리의 어느 여관에 묵었는데, 어떤 간판을 단 곳이었는지는 잊어버렸다.\n\n여기서 내 남편은 내가 아일랜드로 가는 일을 이야기하다가, 떠나기 전에 런던에서 정리할 볼일이 없느냐고 물었다. 나는 없다고, 대단한 것은 아무것도 없으며, 있다 해도 더블린에서 편지로 처리해도 될 만한 것뿐이라고 했다. \"부인,\" 그가 매우 정중하게 말했다. \"짐작건대 부인 재산의 대부분은, 내 누이 말로는 그 대부분이 잉글랜드 은행에 돈으로 있다는데, 충분히 안전하게 있겠지만, 혹 그것을 명의 이전하거나 어떤 식으로든 그 소유 형태를 바꿀 필요가 있다면, 우리가 건너가기 전에 런던으로 올라가 그런 일들을 정리하는 것이 좋을지도 모르겠소.\"\n\n나는 그것을 의아하게 바라보는 척하며, 그에게 그게 무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 했다. 내가 아는 한 잉글랜드 은행에 아무런 재물도 없으며, 그에게 그런 게 있다고 말한 적이 없음을 그도 분명 말할 수 있으리라 바란다고 했다. 그렇다고, 그는 내가 자기에게 그렇게 말하지는 않았으나, 자기 누이가 내 재산의 대부분이 거기 있다고 말했다고 했다. \"그래서 그저 그 이야기를 꺼낸 것뿐이오, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것을 정리하거나 그에 관해 무언가 처리할 일이 있다면, 우리가 다시 건너왔다 가는 위험과 수고를 겪지 않아도 되게 말이오.\" 그러고는 자기는 나를 바다에 너무 많이 모험시키고 싶지 않다고 덧붙였다.\n\n나는 이 말에 깜짝 놀라, 그 뜻이 대체 무엇일까 매우 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했다. 그러자 곧 이런 생각이 떠올랐으니, 그를 오라비라 부르던 내 벗이 나를 내게 어울리지 않는 빛깔로 그려 보였구나 하는 것이었다. 그리고 나는, 일이 이 지경에 이르렀으니, 잉글랜드를 떠나기 전에, 또 낯선 나라에서 누구의 손인지도 모를 손에 나 자신을 맡기기 전에, 이 일의 밑바닥을 알아내야겠다고 생각했다.\n\n그리하여 나는 이튿날 아침 그의 누이를 내 방으로 불러, 전날 저녁 그 오라비와 내가 나눈 이야기를 알려 주고는, 그 여자가 그에게 무슨 말을 했으며 무엇을 근거로 이 혼사를 성사시켰는지 말해 달라고 다그쳤다. 그 여자는 자기가 그에게 내가 큰 재산가라고 말했다고 인정하면서, 런던에서 그렇게 들었노라고 했다. \"그렇게 들었다고요!\" 내가 발끈하며 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 그렇게 말한 적이 있던가요?\" 아니요, 그 여자는 내가 자기에게 그렇게 말하지 않은 건 사실이나, 내 것이 내 마음대로 처분할 수 있는 것이라고 내가 여러 번 말했다고 했다. \"그랬지요,\" 나는 매우 재빠르고 다급하게 받아쳤다. \"하지만 내가 당신에게 재산이라 할 만한 무엇이 있다고 말한 적은 없어요. 아니, 100파운드가, 아니 100파운드 값어치가 세상에 있다고 한 적조차 없지요. 게다가,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 그저 물가 싸게 살겠다는 까닭 하나로 당신과 함께 여기 잉글랜드 북부로 내려왔다는 것이, 내가 재산가라는 것과 어떻게 아귀가 맞는단 말인가요?\" 내가 발끈하여 높은 소리로 이 말들을 내뱉을 때, 내 남편, 곧 (그 여자가 부르는 대로 하자면) 그 오라비가 방으로 들어왔고, 나는 그에게 와서 앉으라고 청했으니, 둘 앞에서 할 중대한 말이 있고, 그도 그것을 꼭 들어야 했기 때문이다.\n\n그는 내가 그 말을 내뱉는 그 단호함에 다소 동요한 표정으로, 먼저 문을 닫고는 와서 내 곁에 앉았다. 그러자 나는—몹시 화가 나 있던 터라—그에게로 몸을 돌리며 입을 열었다. \"여보,\" 내가 말했다(그의 편에서는 다정하게 말했으니), \"당신은 매우 큰 기만을 당하셨고, 나와 결혼함으로써 결코 돌이킬 수 없는 손해를 입으신 것 같아 두렵습니다. 다만 나는 거기 손을 댄 바 없으니, 부디 그것에 대해 깨끗이 면책되고, 그 책임이 마땅히 있어야 할 곳에 놓이고 다른 어디에도 놓이지 않기를 바랍니다. 나는 그 모든 부분에서 손을 씻으니까요.\"\n\n\"여보, 내가 당신과 결혼하여 무슨 손해를 입을 수 있단 말이오,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것은 모든 면에서 내 명예요 이득이라고 믿는데요.\" \"곧 설명해 드리지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"그리고 당신은 자신이 잘 대접받았다고 여길 만한 까닭이 없으리라 두렵습니다. 하지만 나는 거기 손을 댄 바 없음을 당신께 납득시켜 드리겠어요, 여보,\" 내가 다시 말했고, 거기서 잠시 멈추었다.\n\n이제 그는 겁먹고 혼이 나간 표정으로, 무슨 말이 뒤따를지 짐작하기 시작한 듯했다. 그러나 그는 나를 향해 그저 \"계속하시오\" 하고는, 내가 더 할 말을 들으려는 듯 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그래서 나는 계속했다. \"어젯밤 당신에게 물었지요,\" 내가 그에게 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 내 재산을 자랑한 적이 있는지, 혹은 잉글랜드 은행이나 다른 어디에 재산이 있다고 말한 적이 있는지 말이에요. 그리고 당신은 내가 그러지 않았다고 인정하셨고, 그건 더없는 사실이지요. 그러니 부디 여기 당신 누이 앞에서 말씀해 주세요, 내가 당신에게 그렇게 여길 만한 어떤 빌미라도 준 적이 있는지, 혹은 우리가 그에 관해 어떤 이야기라도 나눈 적이 있는지를요.\" 그러자 그는 다시 내가 그러지 않았음을 인정하면서도, 다만 내가 늘 재산 있는 여자로 보였고, 자기는 내가 그러하리라 믿고 의지했으며, 속지 않았기를 바란다고 했다. \"당신이 속았는지 아닌지를 지금 따지려는 게 아니에요,\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 속았을까 두렵고, 나 또한 그렇지요. 다만 나는 당신을 속이는 데 가담했다는 부당한 혐의를 벗으려는 거랍니다.\n\n\"나는 방금 당신 누이에게, 내가 가진 재산이나 자산에 관해 자기에게 말한 적이 있는지, 혹은 그에 관한 어떤 세부라도 일러 준 적이 있는지 물었어요. 그리고 그 사람은 내가 결코 그러지 않았음을 인정했지요. 그러니 부디, 부인,\" 내가 그 여자에게 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"당신 오라비 앞에서 내게 공정하게, 할 수 있다면, 내가 당신에게 재산이 있는 체한 적이 있다고 책잡아 보세요. 또 내게 재산이 있었다면, 어찌하여 내가 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 아끼고 물가 싸게 살겠다는 일부러의 까닭으로 당신과 함께 이 고장으로 내려왔겠어요?\" 그 여자는 한마디도 부인하지 못하면서, 다만 내게 매우 큰 재산이 있으며 그것이 잉글랜드 은행에 있다고 런던에서 들었노라고 했다.\n\n\"그러니 이제, 여보,\" 내가 다시 새 남편에게 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"부디 내게 공정하게 말해 주세요. 도대체 누가 당신과 나를 그토록 농락하여, 당신이 나를 재산가로 믿게 하고 이 혼사로 내게 구애하도록 부추겼나요?\" 그는 한마디도 못 하고 그저 그 여자를 가리켰다. 그러고는 좀 더 멈칫하더니, 내 평생 본 적 없는 더없이 사나운 격분에 휩싸여, 그 여자를 저주하고 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 창녀니 하는 모진 이름으로 불러대며, 그 여자가 자기를 망쳐 놓았다고 했고, 그 여자가 자기에게 내게 1만 5000파운드가 있다고 말했으며, 이 혼사를 맺어 준 대가로 자기에게서 500파운드를 받기로 되어 있었다고 폭로했다. 그러고는 내게 말머리를 돌려, 그 여자는 자기 누이가 전혀 아니고 두 해 전부터 자기 창녀였으며, 그 여자가 이 거래의 일부로 자기에게서 100파운드를 받았고, 만약 일이 내가 말한 대로라면 자기는 완전히 끝장이라고 했다. 그리고 그는 길길이 날뛰며 당장 그 여자의 심장의 피를 뽑아내겠다고 맹세했으니, 그것이 그 여자도 나도 겁에 질리게 했다. 그 여자는 울며, 내가 묵던 집에서 그렇게 들었다고 했다. 그러나 이는 그를 전보다 더 격앙시켰으니, 그 여자가 그토록 그를 속여먹고, 한낱 소문 말고는 다른 아무 근거도 없이 일을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 갔다는 것이었다. 그러고는 다시 내게 몸을 돌려 매우 정직하게, 우리 둘 다 끝장난 것 같아 두렵다고 했다. \"왜냐하면, 까놓고 말해서, 여보, 나는 재산이 없소,\" 그가 말했다. \"내게 있던 그 얼마 안 되는 것을, 이 악마가 당신 시중을 들고 나를 이런 행차로 차려 입히느라 다 써 버리게 만들었단 말이오.\"\n그 여자는 그가 나와 열심히 이야기하는 틈을 타 방에서 빠져나갔고, 나는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 보지 못했다.\n\n이제 나는 그 못지않게 어쩔 줄 몰라, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 나는 여러모로 내 쪽이 더 나쁜 처지라고 생각했으나, 그가 자기는 끝장났고 게다가 재산도 없다는 말은 나를 그야말로 제정신이 아닌 지경으로 몰아넣었다. \"이거 참,\" 내가 그에게 말했다. \"이건 지옥 같은 농간이었군요. 우리는 여기서 이중의 사기를 바탕으로 결혼한 셈이니까요. 보아하니 당신은 실망으로 끝장났고, 만약 내게 재산이 있었다면 나 또한 속았을 테지요. 당신은 가진 게 없다 하시니 말이에요.\"\n\n\"당신은 정말 속았을 거요, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 끝장나지는 않았을 거요. 1만 5000파운드면 이 고장에서 우리 둘이 아주 훌륭하게 살림을 꾸렸을 테니까. 그리고 단언컨대,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"나는 그 한 푼 한 푼을 다 당신에게 바치기로 작정했었소. 당신에게서 1실링도 빼앗지 않았을 것이고, 나머지는 내가 사는 동안 당신을 향한 애정과 당신을 향한 정성으로 채워 주었을 거요.\"\n\n이것은 정말이지 매우 정직한 말이었고, 나는 그가 마음먹은 대로 말했으며, 또 그가 성품과 행실에 관한 한 일찍이 어떤 남자 못지않게 나를 행복하게 해 줄 자질을 갖춘 사람이라고 진정 믿는다. 그러나 그가 재산이 없고, 이 고장에서 이 우스꽝스러운 명목으로 빚을 진 탓에, 모든 전망이 암담하고 끔찍해졌으며, 나는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 나 자신을 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다.\n\n나는 그에게, 그에게서 발견한 그토록 많은 사랑과 그토록 많은 선량함이 이렇듯 불행으로 곤두박질치다니 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이라고 했다. 또 우리 앞에 파멸 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다고 했으니, 내 처지로 말하자면, 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것이 우리를 한 주도 구해 내지 못한다는 것이 내 불행이라고 했다. 그러면서 나는 20파운드짜리 은행 어음 한 장과 11기니를 꺼내 보이며, 이것은 내 보잘것없는 수입에서 모은 것이고, 그 피조물이 그 고장의 생활 방식에 관해 내게 일러 준 바로는 이것으로 서너 해는 살 수 있으리라 예상했다고 했다. 또 이것을 내게서 빼앗아 가면 나는 빈털터리가 될 것이며, 낯선 사람들 틈에서 주머니에 돈 한 푼 없는 여자의 처지가 어떠해야 하는지 그도 알 것이라고 했다. 그러나 나는 그에게, 그가 그것을 가져가겠다면 여기 있다고 했다.\n\n그는 매우 근심스러운 빛으로, 그리고 그의 눈에 눈물이 고이는 것을 본 듯도 한데, 그것에 손대지 않겠다고 했다. 자기는 나를 발가벗겨 비참하게 만든다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 난다고 했고, 오히려 자기에게 50기니가 남았는데 그것이 세상에서 가진 전부라며, 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 던지고는, 자기가 그것이 없어 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 내가 그것을 가지라고 했다.\n\n나는 그에 대한 같은 근심으로 받아쳐, 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 차마 들을 수 없다고 했다. 오히려 만약 그가 살아갈 어떤 그럴듯한 방도라도 내놓을 수 있다면 나는 내 쪽에서 마땅한 무엇이든 하겠으며, 그가 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠듯하고 옹색하게 살겠다고 했다.\n\n그는 내게 더 이상 그런 식으로 말하지 말아 달라고 빌었으니, 그것이 자기를 제정신이 아니게 만든다고 했다. 그는 자기가 비록 낮은 처지로 영락했으나 신사로 길러졌으며, 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 길은 단 하나밖에 남지 않았는데, 그것도 내가 한 가지 질문에 답해 주지 못하면 소용이 없다고 했다. 다만 그 질문을 내게 강요하지는 않겠다고 했다. 나는 정직하게 답하겠으나, 그것이 그를 만족시킬지 아닐지는 알 수 없다고 했다.\n\n\"그럼, 여보, 솔직히 말해 주시오,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것으로 우리가 어떤 모양새로든, 어떤 처지나 어떤 곳에서든 함께 살아갈 수 있겠소, 없겠소?\"\n\n지금껏 내 다행이었던 것은, 나 자신이나 내 사정을 전혀 드러내지 않았다는 것, 아니, 내 이름조차 드러내지 않았다는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 아무리 쾌활하고 아무리 정직해 보인다 한들, 그에게서 기대할 수 있는 것이라곤 내가 곧 거덜 나리라 아는 것에 기대어 사는 일밖에 없음을 알았기에, 나는 이미 인정한 은행 어음과 11기니 말고는 모든 것을 숨기기로 작정했다. 그리고 나는 차라리 그것마저 잃고, 그가 나를 거두었던 그 자리에 도로 내려놓이기를 진정 바랐을 것이다. 사실 내게는 30파운드짜리 은행 어음이 한 장 더 있었는데, 그것이 내가 그 고장에서 살아갈 밑천으로, 또 무슨 일이 생길지 모른다는 생각에서 지니고 온 전부였다. 왜냐하면 이 피조물, 곧 우리 둘을 이렇게 배신한 그 중개인이, 내가 그 고장에서 유리하게 결혼한다는 둥 이상한 것들을 내게 믿게 만들었던 터라, 나는 무슨 일이 생기든 돈 없이 지내고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 이 어음을 나는 숨겼고, 그의 처지를 헤아려 나머지에 대해서는 더 후하게 굴 수 있었으니, 나는 정말로 그가 진심으로 가여웠기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그의 질문으로 돌아가, 나는 그에게 결코 일부러 그를 속인 적이 없으며 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않겠다고 했다. 매우 유감스럽게도 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것으로는 우리가 살아갈 수 없으며, 그것은 남부 지방에서 나 혼자 살아가기에도 충분치 않다고 말했다. 그리고 바로 그것이 내가 그를 오라비라 부르던 그 여자의 손에 나 자신을 맡기게 된 까닭이라고 했으니, 그 여자가 내게, 아직 가 본 적은 없으나 맨체스터라는 고장에서 연 6파운드 정도로 매우 훌륭하게 하숙할 수 있다고 단언했기 때문이며, 내 전체 수입이 연 15파운드가 채 안 되는지라, 나는 그것으로 편안히 살며 더 나은 것을 기다릴 수 있으리라 생각했던 것이다.\n\n그는 고개를 가로젓고 잠자코 있었으며, 우리는 매우 우울한 저녁을 보냈다. 그러나 우리는 함께 저녁을 먹고 그날 밤 함께 누웠는데, 저녁을 거의 다 먹었을 무렵 그는 낯빛이 조금 나아지고 한결 밝아져서 포도주 한 병을 가져오라고 했다. \"자, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"처지가 나쁘기는 해도 풀이 죽어 있어 봐야 아무 소용없소. 자, 되도록 마음 편히 가지시오. 나는 이러저러한 방도를 찾아 살아갈 길을 마련해 보겠소. 당신이 당신 한 몸이라도 건사할 수 있다면, 그게 아무것도 없는 것보다는 낫지. 나는 세상에 다시 나가 부딪쳐 봐야 하오. 사내라면 사내답게 생각해야지. 풀이 죽는다는 건 불운에 항복하는 것이오.\" 그러면서 그는 잔을 채워 내게 권했고, 포도주가 넘어가는 내내 내 손을 잡고 자기 손으로 그것을 꼭 쥐었으며, 그러고 나서 자기의 가장 큰 근심은 나라고 단언했다.\n\n그는 정말이지 참되고 의협심 있는 기질의 사람이었고, 그래서 나는 더욱 비통했다. 비열한 자에게 망하느니 명예로운 사람에게 망하는 것이 그래도 다소 위안이 되는 법이다. 그러나 여기서 가장 큰 실망은 그의 편에 있었으니, 그는 이 마담, 곧 그 뚜쟁이에게 속아 정말로 많은 돈을 써 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 얼마나 가련한 조건으로 일을 진행했는지가 매우 주목할 만했다. 첫째로 그 피조물 자신의 비열함을 눈여겨봐야 하니, 그 여자는 자기가 100파운드를 챙기려고, 그가 어쩌면 세상에 가진 전부, 아니 전부 이상을 서너 곱절 더 써 버리게 내버려 둘 수 있었던 것이다. 그러면서도 그 여자는, 한낱 차탁의 잡담 말고는, 내게 무슨 재산이 있다거나 내가 재산가라거나 하는 따위를 말할 손톱만 한 근거조차 없었던 것이다. 재산 있는 여자를—내가 만약 그랬더라면—농락하려는 속셈은 정말 충분히 비열한 일이었음이 사실이다. 가난한 처지에 큰 것들의 겉모습을 씌우는 것은 사기였고, 충분히 고약한 일이었다. 그러나 경우가 조금 다르기도 했으니, 그것도 그에게 유리한 쪽으로 그러했다. 왜냐하면 그는 여자를 농락하는 것을 업으로 삼고, 어떤 자들이 하듯 잇따라 예닐곱 명의 재산가를 손에 넣은 뒤 다 털어먹고 달아나는 그런 난봉꾼이 아니었기 때문이다. 그는 정말로 신사였고, 불운하고 처지가 낮으나 한때 넉넉히 살았던 사람이었다. 그리고 만약 내게 재산이 있었다면 나는 나를 배신한 그 잡년에게 격분했겠지만, 정말이지 그 남자로 말하자면, 재산이 그에게 잘못 주어진 셈은 아니었을 것이다. 그는 정말로 사랑스러운 사람으로, 너그러운 신조와 분별을 지녔으며 더없이 쾌활한 기질이었기 때문이다.\n\n우리는 그날 밤 매우 허심탄회한 이야기를 많이 나누었으니, 둘 다 잠을 거의 자지 못했기 때문이다. 그는 마치 그것이 중죄여서 자기가 처형당하러 가기라도 하는 양, 내게 그 온갖 기만을 씌운 것을 뉘우쳤다. 그는 자기가 몸에 지닌 돈을 한 푼도 남김없이 다시 내게 내밀며, 자기는 군대에 들어가 더 많은 것을 찾아 세상으로 나가겠다고 했다.\n\n나는 그에게, 거기서 나를 부양할 수 없었으리라 짐작되는데 어찌하여 그토록 매정하게 나를 아일랜드로 데려가려 했느냐고 물었다. 그는 나를 품에 안았다. \"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"믿으시오, 나는 애당초 아일랜드에 갈 생각이 전혀 없었고, 당신을 거기로 데려갈 생각은 더더욱 없었소. 다만 내가 행세하던 바를 들은 사람들의 눈을 피하려고 여기로 온 것이고, 게다가 내가 그들에게 갚을 채비가 되기 전에 아무도 내게 돈을 요구하지 못하게 하려던 것이었소.\"\n\n\"그럼 우리는,\" 내가 말했다. \"그다음에 어디로 갔어야 하나요?\"\n\n\"여보, 그게 말이오,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 짜 놓았던 계략 전부를 당신에게 털어놓지요. 나는 여기서 당신에게 당신 재산에 관해 무언가 물을 작정이었소. 보다시피 그렇게 했고요. 그리고 당신이, 내가 예상한 대로, 그 세부에 관해 내게 무언가 설명을 늘어놓기 시작하면, 나는 핑계를 대어 우리의 아일랜드행을 한동안 미루고, 먼저 런던 쪽으로 갈 작정이었소.\n\n\"그러고 나서, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 내 처지의 온갖 정황을 당신에게 고백하고, 내가 정말로 당신의 결혼 승낙을 얻으려고 이런 술책을 썼음을 알리되, 이제는 당신의 용서를 구하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없으며, 앞서 말했듯이 다가올 나날의 행복으로 지난 일을 잊게 하려고 얼마나 넘치도록 애쓰겠는지를 당신에게 말할 작정이었소.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 내가 그에게 말했다. \"당신은 곧 나를 정복했을 거예요. 그리고 내가 얼마나 쉽게 당신과 화해하고, 그 많은 쾌활함에 대한 보답으로 당신이 내게 씌운 온갖 술책을 다 눈감아 주었을지 당신에게 보여 줄 처지가 못 되는 것이 지금 내 비통함이랍니다. 하지만, 여보,\" 내가 말했다. \"이제 우리가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요? 우리 둘 다 끝장났는데, 살아갈 게 아무것도 없는 마당에 서로 화해한들 무슨 보탬이 되겠어요?\"\n\n우리는 많은 것을 궁리했으나, 시작할 밑천이 아무것도 없는 마당에 무엇 하나 떠오를 수가 없었다. 그는 마침내 더는 그 이야기를 하지 말아 달라고 빌었으니, 그것이 자기 가슴을 찢어 놓는다고 했다. 그래서 우리는 잠시 다른 이야기를 조금 하다가, 마침내 그가 남편으로서의 작별을 내게 고했고, 그렇게 우리는 잠이 들었다.\n\n그는 아침에 나보다 먼저 일어났다. 그리고 정말이지 나는 밤새 거의 뜬눈으로 누워 있던 터라 몹시 졸려서, 열한 시 가까이까지 누워 있었다. 그사이 그는 자기 말들과 하인 셋, 그리고 자기 모든 내의와 짐을 챙겨 떠나 버렸으니, 탁자 위에 다음과 같은 짧지만 가슴 뭉클한 편지를 내게 남겨 두고서였다.\n\n\"내 사랑—나는 개 같은 놈이오. 나는 당신을 능멸했소. 하지만 나는 내 신조와 내 평생의 처신에 어긋나게, 어떤 비열한 피조물에게 꾐을 받아 그 일에 끌려든 것이오. 용서해 주오, 내 사랑! 더없이 진심으로 당신의 용서를 비오. 나는 당신을 농락한, 사내들 가운데 가장 비참한 자요. 나는 당신을 차지하는 더없는 복을 누렸으나, 이제 당신에게서 달아날 수밖에 없는 더없이 가련한 신세가 되었소. 용서해 주오, 내 사랑. 다시 한번 말하니, 용서해 주오! 나는 당신이 나로 인해 파멸하고, 나 자신은 당신을 부양할 수 없는 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없소. 우리의 혼인은 아무것도 아니오. 나는 다시는 당신을 볼 수 없을 것이오. 나는 여기서 당신을 그것에서 풀어 주오. 당신이 유리하게 결혼할 수 있다면, 나 때문에 사양하지 마오. 나는 여기서 내 신의를 걸고, 명예로운 사내의 말로써 당신에게 맹세하니, 설령 그 일을 알게 되더라도—그럴 법하지는 않으나—결코 당신의 안식을 흩뜨리지 않겠소. 한편 당신이 결혼하지 않고, 또 내게 행운이 닥친다면, 그것은 당신이 어디에 있든 모두 당신 것이 될 것이오.\n\n나는 내게 남은 돈 밑천 가운데 얼마를 당신 주머니에 넣어 두었소. 당신과 당신 하녀의 역마차 자리를 잡아 런던으로 가시오. 당신 자신의 돈을 헐지 않고도 거기까지 가는 비용은 댈 수 있기를 바라오. 다시 한번 진심으로 당신의 용서를 비며, 당신을 생각할 때마다 거듭 그리하겠소.\n\n안녕히, 내 사랑, 영원히,\n당신을 더없이 다정히 사랑하는,\n\nJ.E.\"\n내 평생 내게 닥친 그 무엇도 이 작별만큼 내 가슴 깊이 가라앉은 것은 없었다. 나는 마음속으로 그가 나를 떠난 것을 천 번이고 책망했으니, 빌어먹는 한이 있어도 나는 세상 끝까지 그와 함께 갔을 것이기 때문이다. 나는 주머니를 더듬어 거기서 10기니와 그의 금시계, 그리고 작은 반지 둘을 찾아냈는데, 하나는 6파운드쯤 나가는 작은 다이아몬드 반지였고, 다른 하나는 무늬 없는 금반지였다.\n\n나는 앉아서 이 물건들을 두 시간이나 들여다보며 거의 한마디도 하지 않았는데, 마침내 하녀가 점심이 준비되었다고 일러 주어 나를 일깨웠다. 나는 거의 먹지 못했고, 점심 뒤에는 격렬하게 우는 발작에 빠져, 시시각각 그의 이름을 불러댔으니, 그 이름은 제임스였다. \"오, 제미!\" 내가 말했다. \"돌아와요, 돌아와요. 내가 가진 걸 다 줄게요. 빌어먹고, 당신과 함께 굶을게요.\" 그러고는 이렇게 방 안을 몇 차례 미친 듯이 돌아다니다가, 사이사이 주저앉았다가, 다시 서성이며 그에게 돌아오라고 부르짖었다가, 그러고는 다시 울었다. 그렇게 나는 오후를 보냈으니, 일곱 시쯤, 때가 팔월이라 저녁 어스름이 가까웠을 무렵, 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 그가 여관으로 도로 돌아왔는데, 다만 하인 없이, 곧장 내 방으로 올라왔다.\n\n나는 상상할 수 있는 더없는 혼란에 빠졌고, 그 또한 그러했다. 나는 그것이 무슨 까닭일까 짐작할 수 없었고, 기뻐해야 할지 슬퍼해야 할지 나 자신과 다투기 시작했으나, 내 애정이 나머지 모든 것을 압도했고, 미소로는 담을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 큰 그 기쁨을 감추기란 불가능했으니, 그것이 눈물로 터져 나왔던 것이다. 그는 방에 들어서기 무섭게 내게 달려와 나를 품에 안고는, 나를 꼭 붙들고 입맞춤으로 내 숨이 거의 막힐 지경이었으나, 한마디도 하지 못했다. 마침내 내가 입을 열었다. \"여보,\" 내가 말했다. \"어떻게 나를 떠나갈 수 있었어요?\" 그는 거기에 아무 대답도 하지 못했으니, 그로서는 도무지 말을 할 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n우리의 황홀경이 좀 가라앉자, 그는 내게 열다섯 마일쯤 갔으나, 나를 다시 보고 한 번 더 작별을 고하러 돌아오지 않고는 도무지 더 갈 수가 없었다고 했다.\n\n나는 그에게 내가 어떻게 시간을 보냈는지, 또 얼마나 큰 소리로 그에게 돌아오라고 불렀는지 말했다. 그는 열두 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 곳, 델러미어 숲에서 내 소리를 아주 또렷이 들었다고 했다. 나는 미소 지었다. \"아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 농담하는 줄 알지 마오. 내 평생 당신 목소리를 들은 적이 있다면, 나는 당신이 나를 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 들었고, 때로는 당신이 내 뒤를 쫓아 달려오는 것이 보이는 것 같았소.\" \"그럼,\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 뭐라고 했나요?\"—나는 그에게 그 말을 입에 올린 적이 없었으니까. \"당신은 큰 소리로,\" 그가 말했다. \"오, 제미! 오, 제미! 돌아와요, 돌아와요, 하고 말했소.\"\n\n나는 그를 보고 웃었다. \"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"웃지 마오. 분명히 말하건대, 나는 지금 당신이 내 목소리를 듣는 것만큼이나 또렷이 당신 목소리를 들었소. 원한다면 치안판사 앞에 나가 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠소.\" 그러자 나는 어안이 벙벙하고 놀라며, 정말이지 겁이 나기 시작하여, 앞서와 같이 내가 실제로 무엇을 했는지, 또 어떻게 그의 뒤를 따라 불렀는지 그에게 말했다.\n\n우리는 이 일을 두고 한동안 신기해하다가, 내가 그에게 말했다. \"자, 당신은 다시는 내게서 떠나가지 마세요. 차라리 내가 당신과 함께 온 세상을 돌아다닐게요.\" 그는 나를 떠나는 것이 자기로서는 매우 어려운 일일 것이나, 그래야만 한다면 부디 내가 되도록 마음 편히 받아들이기를 바란다고 했다. 다만 자기로 말하자면, 그것이 자기 파멸이 되리라는 것을 내다본다고 했다. 그러나 그는, 나를 혼자 런던까지 가도록 내버려 두었던 것을 헤아려 보았는데, 그것은 너무 긴 여정이며, 자기도 어차피 그 길로 가나 다른 길로 가나 마찬가지이니, 나를 거기까지, 혹은 그 근처까지 안전히 바래다주기로 작정했다고 했다. 그리고 그때 자기가 작별 인사 없이 떠나가더라도 내가 언짢게 여기지 말기를 바란다고 했고, 이것을 내게 약속하게 했다.\n\n그는 어떻게 자기 하인 셋을 내보내고 그들의 말을 팔았으며, 그 친구들을 제 운수 찾아 떠나보냈는지, 그것도 길가의 어느 고을에서—어딘지는 모르겠다—삽시간에 그리했는지를 내게 말했다. \"그리고,\" 그가 말했다. \"나 홀로 얼마간 눈물을 흘렸으니, 그들이 자기 주인보다 얼마나 더 행복한지 생각하니 말이오. 그들은 옆 신사의 집으로 가서 일자리를 알아볼 수 있지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 어디로 가야 할지, 나 자신을 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었으니 말이오.\"\n\n나는 그에게, 그와 헤어지는 것이 너무도 완전히 비참하여 더 나빠질 수도 없으며, 이제 그가 다시 돌아왔으니, 그가 나를 데려가 준다면 어디를 가든, 무엇을 하든, 나는 그에게서 떠나지 않겠다고 했다. 그러는 한편 나는 우리가 함께 런던으로 가는 데 동의했다. 그러나 나는 그가 자기 말대로 끝내 떠나가며 내게 작별을 고하지 않는다는 것에는 도무지 응할 수 없어, 농담조로, 만약 그가 그러면 앞서처럼 큰 소리로 그를 도로 불러대겠다고 했다. 그러고는 그의 시계를 꺼내 그에게 돌려주었고, 그의 반지 둘과 그의 10기니도 돌려주었으나, 그는 그것을 받으려 하지 않았으니, 그것이 나로 하여금 그가 길에서 떠나가 나를 버릴 작정이구나 하고 몹시 의심하게 만들었다.\n\n사실 그가 처한 정황, 그의 편지에 담긴 절절한 표현, 이 모든 일에서 내가 그에게서 받은 친절하고 신사다운 대접, 거기서 그가 내게 보인 근심, 그리고 자기에게 남은 그 적은 밑천에서 그토록 큰 몫을 내게 나눠 주며 헤어지던 그 태도—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 내게 그런 깊은 인상을 남긴 터라, 나는 정말로 그를 더없이 애틋이 사랑하게 되었고, 그와 헤어진다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다.\n\n이 일이 있고 이틀 뒤 우리는 체스터를 떠났으니, 나는 역마차로, 그는 말을 타고 갔다. 나는 체스터에서 하녀를 내보냈다. 그는 내가 하녀 없이 지내는 것을 몹시 반대했으나, 그 아이가 그 고장에서 고용한 하인인 데다 나는 런던에서 하인을 두지 않기로 작정했던 터라, 나는 그에게, 그 가엾은 계집아이를 데려갔다가 도시에 닿자마자 내쫓는 것은 야박한 일이 될 것이며, 또 그것은 길에서 쓸데없는 비용이 되리라고 했다. 그렇게 나는 그를 납득시켰고, 그도 그 점에 관해 충분히 마음을 놓았다.\n\n그는 런던에서 삼십 마일 안쪽인 던스터블까지 나와 함께 왔고, 그러고는 운명과 자신의 불운 때문에 나를 떠날 수밖에 없으며, 자기가 런던으로 가는 것은 적절치 않다고, 내가 알아 봐야 아무 쓸모없는 이유들 때문에 그러하다고 했다. 그리고 나는 그가 떠날 채비를 하는 것을 보았다. 우리가 탄 역마차는 보통 던스터블에 서지 않았으나, 내가 십오 분만 서 달라고 청하니, 그들은 잠시 여관 문 앞에 서 있는 데 응했고, 우리는 그 집 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n여관에 들어, 나는 그에게 단 한 가지 청이 더 있다고 했으니, 곧 그가 더 멀리 갈 수 없다면, 그와 함께 이 고을에서 한두 주 머물도록 허락해 달라는 것이었다. 그동안 우리 둘 모두에게 최종 이별만큼 파멸적인 일을 막을 무언가를 궁리해 볼 수 있도록 말이다. 또 나는 그에게, 아직 한 번도 말한 적 없는 중대한 무언가를 내놓을 것이 있는데, 어쩌면 그가 우리 서로의 이익을 위해 실행할 만하다고 여길지도 모른다고 했다.\n\n이것은 거절하기에는 너무도 사리에 맞는 제안이었던지라, 그는 그 집 안주인을 불러, 자기 아내가 병이 났는데 너무 심해서 역마차로 더 가는 것은 생각도 할 수 없다고, 마차가 나를 거의 죽을 지경으로 지치게 했다고 말하고는, 내가 좀 쉴 수 있도록 어느 개인 집에 이삼 일 묵을 거처를 구해 줄 수 없겠느냐고, 여정이 내게 너무 벅찼다고 물었다. 그 안주인은 마음씨 좋은 여자로, 예의 바르고 매우 친절했는데, 곧장 나를 보러 와서는, 집 안의 한쪽, 소음에서 완전히 벗어난 곳에 매우 좋은 방이 두셋 있으니, 보면 분명 마음에 들 것이며, 자기 하녀 가운데 하나를 붙여, 오직 나를 시중드는 일만 맡게 하겠다고 했다. 이는 너무도 친절하여, 나는 그것을 받아들여 감사할 수밖에 없었다. 그래서 나는 방들을 보러 갔고 매우 마음에 들었으니, 정말이지 그것들은 유난히 잘 꾸며져 있었고 매우 쾌적한 거처였다. 그리하여 우리는 역마차 삯을 치르고 짐을 내려, 여기서 한동안 머물기로 작정했다.\n\n여기서 나는 그에게, 이제 내 돈이 다 떨어질 때까지 그와 함께 살되, 그가 자기 돈은 1실링도 쓰지 못하게 하겠다고 했다. 우리는 그 일을 두고 다정한 실랑이를 좀 벌였으나, 나는 그에게, 이것이 내가 그의 벗을 누릴 마지막 기회일 듯하니, 그 한 가지 일에서만큼은 부디 내가 주인이 되게 해 달라고, 다른 모든 일에서는 그가 다스리면 된다고 했다. 그래서 그는 응낙했다.\n\n여기서 어느 날 저녁, 들판으로 산책을 나가, 나는 그에게 이제 내가 말했던 그 제안을 하겠노라고 했다. 그리하여 나는 그에게, 내가 버지니아에서 어떻게 살았는지, 내게 어머니가 있었는데 내 생각에는\n내 남편은 몇 해 전에 죽었으나 그녀는 거기서 아직 살아 있다고 했다. 나는 그에게, 만약 내 재산이 잘못되지만 않았더라면—덧붙여 말하자면 나는 이것을 꽤나 부풀려 말했다—내가 이런 식으로 우리가 헤어지지 않을 만큼 그에게 넉넉한 재산이 되었을지도 모른다고 했다. 그러고는 사람들이 그런 나라들로 정착하러 건너가는 방식, 그곳의 제도에 따라 어떻게 일정량의 땅을 받게 되는지, 그렇지 않다 하더라도 입에 올릴 가치도 없을 만큼 헐한 값으로 땅을 살 수 있다는 이야기를 풀어 놓았다.\n\n그런 다음 나는 그에게 농장 경영의 본질에 관해 빠짐없이 또렷하게 설명해 주었다. 곧, 영국 상품으로 이삼백 파운드어치만 가지고 건너가서, 약간의 하인과 연장을 갖추면, 부지런한 사람은 곧 한 가문의 토대를 닦을 수 있고, 몇 해 안에 틀림없이 재산을 일굴 수 있다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 그에게 땅에서 나는 산물의 본질, 토지가 어떻게 손질되고 마련되는지, 그리고 그 통상적인 소출이 얼마나 되는지를 일러 주었고, 그러한 시작으로 몇 해 안에 우리가 지금 가난할 것이 확실한 만큼이나 부유해질 것이 확실하다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보였다.\n\n그는 내 이야기에 놀랐으니, 우리는 거의 일주일 내내 이것을 우리 대화의 온전한 주제로 삼았고, 그동안 나는 우리 말마따나 흑백으로 또렷이 적어 보였는데, 곧 어느 정도 분별 있는 처신을 전제한다면, 우리가 거기서 번창하여 아주 잘 살게 되지 않는 것은 도덕적으로 불가능하다는 것이었다.\n\n그러고는 나는 그에게 그런 300파운드 안팎의 돈을 마련하기 위해 내가 어떤 방도를 취하겠는지 일러 주었고, 우리의 불운을 끝장내고 우리 둘 다 본디 기대했던 세상에서의 처지를 회복하는 데 그것이 얼마나 훌륭한 방책인지를 그와 따져 보았다. 그리고 덧붙이기를, 칠 년 뒤에 우리가 살아 있다면, 우리 농장을 좋은 사람들 손에 맡겨 두고 다시 건너와서 그 수입을 받아, 여기서 살며 그것을 누릴 형편이 될 수도 있다고 했다. 또 그렇게 하고서 지금 런던에서 아주 좋은 처지로 살아가는 몇몇 사람들의 예를 그에게 들어 주었다.\n\n요컨대 나는 그를 어찌나 다그쳤던지 그가 거의 동의할 지경이었으나, 그래도 이런저런 것이 자꾸 그것을 깨뜨려 놓았으니, 마침내 그가 처지를 뒤바꾸어 거의 같은 취지로 아일랜드에 관해 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다.\n\n그는 시골 생활에 자신을 가둘 수 있고, 어떤 땅이든 들어가 부칠 밑천만 마련할 수 있는 사람이라면, 거기서는 연 50파운드면 여기서 연 200파운드에 세놓는 것만큼 좋은 농장을 얻을 수 있다고 했다. 소출이 그러하고 땅이 어찌나 기름진지, 많이 모아 두지 않더라도 우리가 잉글랜드에서 연 3000파운드를 가진 신사가 살 수 있는 만큼이나 번듯하게 거기서 살 수 있는 것이 확실하다고 했다. 그리고 자기는 나를 런던에 남겨 두고 건너가서 시험해 볼 계획을 세웠으며, 만약 나에 대한 자신의 존중에 걸맞은 살림의 그럴듯한 토대를 닦을 수 있겠다 싶으면—그렇게 되리라 그는 의심치 않았거니와—건너와서 나를 데려가겠다고 했다.\n\n나는 이런 제안을 두고 그가 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들일까 봐, 다시 말해 내가 말하던 대로 그 보잘것없는 내 수입을 팔아 돈으로 바꾸어, 그가 그것을 아일랜드로 가지고 건너가 그것으로 자신의 시험을 해 보게 할까 봐 끔찍이도 두려웠다. 그러나 그는 너무도 올바른 사람이라 그것을 바라지도 않았고, 설령 내가 권했더라도 받아들이지 않았을 것이며, 오히려 그 점에서 나를 앞질렀으니, 그가 덧붙이기를, 자기가 가서 그 방면으로 운을 시험해 보고, 거기서 먹고살 만한 무언가를 할 수 있겠다 싶으면, 그때 내가 건너갈 때 내 것을 거기에 보태어 우리답게 살자고 했다. 다만 작은 것으로 시험을 마치기 전에는 내 돈에서 단 한 푼도 모험을 걸지 않겠다고 했고, 만약 아일랜드에서 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도 없으면 그때는 내게 와서 버지니아에 관한 내 계획에 합류하겠노라고 다짐했다.\n\n그는 자신의 계획을 먼저 시험해 보아야 한다는 데 어찌나 간곡했던지 나로서는 그를 막을 수가 없었다. 그래도 그는 거기 도착한 뒤 아주 짧은 시일 안에 자기 소식을 내게 알려, 자신의 전망이 자기 계획에 부합하는지 알게 해 주겠다고 약속했으니, 만약 성공의 가능성이 없다면 내가 그 기회를 틈타 우리의 다른 항해를 준비할 수 있도록 하기 위함이었다. 그러고는 그때는 진심으로 기꺼이 나와 함께 아메리카로 가겠노라고 다짐했다.\n\n나는 그에게서 이 이상은 끌어낼 수 없었다. 그래도 이런 의논들은 거의 한 달 동안 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었으니, 그동안 나는 그의 벗 됨을 누렸는데, 그것은 참으로 내 평생 그전에 만나 본 가운데 가장 즐거운 것이었다. 이 동안 그는 자기 자신의 삶의 이야기를 죄다 나에게 들려주었으니, 그것은 참으로 놀라웠고, 무한한 다채로움으로 가득 차서, 그 모험과 사건들로 말하자면 내가 일찍이 활자로 본 어떤 이야기보다도 훨씬 더 빛나는 역사를 채우고도 남을 만했다. 그러나 그에 관해서는 앞으로 더 이야기할 기회가 있을 것이다.\n\n우리는 마침내 헤어졌으니, 비록 내 쪽으로는 더할 나위 없이 마음이 내키지 않았으나 그러했고, 사실 그 역시 매우 마지못해 작별을 고했으나, 형편이 그를 어쩔 수 없게 했으니, 그가 런던에 오지 않으려 한 데에는 매우 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었던바, 이는 얼마 뒤에 내가 더 온전히 알게 되었다.\n\n나는 그에게 내게 편지하는 법을 일러 주었으나, 그래도 그 큰 비밀만은 간직했고, 결코 내 결심을 깨뜨리지 않았으니, 곧 그에게 내 진짜 이름이며, 내가 누구인지, 어디서 찾을 수 있는지를 결코 알리지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그 역시 자기에게 편지 쓰는 법을 내게 알려 주었으니, 그렇게 하면 자기가 그것을 틀림없이 받게 될 거라고 했다.\n\n우리가 헤어진 다음 날 나는 런던에 왔으나, 곧장 내 옛 거처로 가지는 않았다. 다만 또 다른, 이름 댈 수 없는 이유로, 클러큰웰 근처의 세인트존 거리, 혹은 속되게 일컫는 대로 하자면 세인트존스 거리에 사사로운 거처를 잡았다. 그리고 여기서, 완전히 혼자가 되어, 나는 차분히 앉아 지난 일곱 달 동안 내가 한 떠돌이 생활을 진지하게 되돌아볼 여유를 가졌으니, 그동안 나는 그에 못지않게 바깥으로 나돌아 다녔던 것이다. 마지막 남편과 함께 보낸 즐거운 시간들을 나는 한없는 기쁨으로 돌이켜 보았다. 그러나 그 기쁨은 얼마 뒤에 내가 정말로 아이를 가졌다는 것을 알게 되었을 때 무척이나 줄어들었다.\n\n이는 골치 아픈 일이었으니, 어디서 해산할 허락을 얻을 것인가 하는 어려움이 내 앞에 놓여 있었기 때문이다. 그 시절에 낯선 데다 의지할 벗도 없는 여자가 보증 없이 그런 처지로 받아들여진다는 것은 세상에서 가장 까다로운 일 가운데 하나였으니, 덧붙여 말하자면 나는 보증이 없었고, 마련할 수도 없었다.\n\n그러는 동안 내내 나는 은행에 있는 정직한 내 벗과의 서신 왕래를 이어 가도록 마음을 써 왔는데, 아니 오히려 그가 나와 서신을 주고받으려고 마음을 썼다고 해야겠으니, 그는 내게 일주일에 한 번씩 편지를 썼던 것이다. 그리고 내가 그에게서 무언가를 받아야 할 만큼 내 돈을 빨리 써 버린 것은 아니었으나, 그래도 나는 종종 편지를 써서 내가 살아 있음을 그에게 알렸다. 나는 랭커셔에 일러 둔 바가 있어서, 그가 보내는 이 편지들이 내게 전달되도록 해 두었다. 그리고 세인트존스에 은거하는 동안 나는 그에게서 매우 정중한 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 그는 아내와의 이혼 소송이 성공적으로 진행되고 있다고 확언하면서도, 자기가 예상치 못한 몇 가지 어려움에 부딪혔다고 했다.\n\n나는 그의 소송이 그가 예상한 것보다 더 더디다는 소식에 언짢지 않았으니, 비록 내가 아직 그를 맞아들일 처지가 못 되었음에도—내가 다른 남자의 아이를 가진 줄 뻔히 알면서, 내가 아는 몇몇이 감히 그러했던 것처럼 그와 결혼할 만큼 어리석지는 않았으므로—그래도 나는 그를 잃고 싶지 않았고, 한마디로 내가 다시 거동할 수 있게 되는 즉시, 그가 같은 마음을 이어 간다면 그를 맞아들이기로 작정했기 때문이다. 보아하니 내 남편에게서는 더 이상 소식이 없을 것이 분명했고, 그가 줄곧 결혼하라고 다그쳤고, 그 일을 두고 자기는 조금도 언짢아하지 않을 것이며, 결코 다시 나를 자기 사람이라 주장하지 않겠다고 다짐했던 터라, 나는 할 수만 있다면, 그리고 내 다른 벗이 자기 약속을 지킨다면 그렇게 하기로 작정하는 데 거리낌이 없었다. 그리고 그가 내게 쓴 편지들로 미루어, 그가 그 약속을 지키리라고 확신할 만한 충분한 이유가 있었으니, 그 편지들은 더없이 다정하고 정중한 것이었다.\n\n이제 나는 배가 불러 왔고, 내가 묵던 집 사람들이 그것을 알아채고 내게 그 일을 들먹이기 시작했으며, 예의가 허락하는 한도 안에서, 내가 거처를 옮길 생각을 해야 한다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다. 이 일은 나를 극도의 곤혹에 빠뜨렸고, 나는 몹시 우울해졌으니, 참으로 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 돈은 있었으나 벗이 없었고, 거두어 키워야 할 아이가 손에 들어올 판이었는데, 이는 지금까지 내 이야기의 세세한 대목들이 드러내듯이 내가 일찍이 겪어 본 적 없는 어려움이었다.\n\n이 일을 겪는 와중에 나는 몹시 앓아눕게 되었고, 내 우울이 정말로 내 병을 키웠다. 내 병은 마침내 학질에 지나지 않는 것으로 드러났으나, 내가 정말로 걱정한 것은 유산하지나 않을까 하는 것이었다. 걱정이라고 말해서는 안 되겠으니, 사실 나는 유산했다면 기뻤을 것이기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 유산을 꾀하려 한다거나, 유산하게 하는 무언가를 먹으려 한다는 생각만은 결코 품을 수 없었으니, 다시 말하건대 나는 그런 생각조차 끔찍이 싫어했다.\n\n그러나 집에서 그 이야기를 하던 중에, 그 집을 꾸려 가던 점잖은 부인이 내게 산파를 부르자고 권했다. 처음엔 망설였으나 얼마 뒤 그러기로 했고, 다만 나는 어느 산파와도 특별히 아는 사이가 아니라고 일러 두고 그 일을 그녀에게 맡겼다.\n\n보아하니 그 집 안주인은 내 처지 같은 경우에, 처음 내가 짐작했던 것만큼 그리 낯선 사람이 아니었으니, 이는 곧 드러날 것이거니와, 그녀는 제대로 된 부류의 산파를—다시 말해 나에게 제대로 된 부류의 산파를—불러 주었다.\n\n그 여자는 자기 일에, 곧 산파로서 노련한 여자로 보였다. 그러나 그녀에게는 또 다른 직업도 있었으니, 그 일에서도 그녀는 웬만한 여자들 못지않게, 아니 그 이상으로 능란했다. 내 안주인은 그녀에게 내가 몹시 우울하다고, 그리고 그것이 내게 해를 끼친 것 같다고 일러 두었다. 그리고 한번은 내 앞에서 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다. \"B 부인\" (산파를 가리킨 것이었다), \"이 부인의 골칫거리는 아무래도 당신 영역에 꽤 들어맞는 종류인 것 같으니, 그러니 당신이 이분을 위해 무얼 해 줄 수 있거든 부디 그렇게 해 주세요. 아주 점잖은 부인이거든요.\" 그러고는 그녀가 방에서 나갔다.\n\n나는 정말로 그녀의 말을 알아듣지 못했으나, 나의 그 한밤의 산파 어멈은 그녀가 나가자마자 자기가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 아주 진지하게 설명하기 시작했다. \"부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"댁의 안주인이 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 못 알아들으신 것 같군요. 그리고 알아듣게 되시더라도, 댁이 알아들었다는 걸 그분에게 전혀 알릴 필요는 없어요.\n\n\"그분 말씀은, 댁이 해산을 어렵게 만들 만한 어떤 처지에 놓여 있고, 그것이 드러나기를 바라지 않으신다는 거예요. 더 말할 것도 없고, 다만 이 말씀만 드리죠. 만약 댁이 댁의 사정을, 그게 그렇다면, 필요한 만큼 저에게 털어놓는 게 좋겠다 싶으시면—저는 그런 일들을 캐고 들 마음은 없으니까요—제가 어쩌면 댁을 도와드리고, 댁을 완전히 마음 편하게 해 드리며, 그 일에 대한 댁의 온갖 울적한 생각을 없애 드릴 수 있는 처지에 있을지도 몰라요.\"\n\n이 사람이 한 마디 한 마디가 내게는 강심제와 같아서, 내 가슴에 새 생명과 새 기운을 불어넣었다. 내 피가 곧장 돌기 시작했고, 나는 아주 딴사람이 되었다. 나는 다시 음식을 먹었고, 그러고 나서 곧 좋아졌다. 그녀는 같은 취지로 한참을 더 말했고, 그러고는 내게 자기에게 터놓으라고 다그치며 더없이 엄숙한 태도로 비밀을 지키겠노라 약속한 다음, 마치 그것이 내게 어떤 인상을 주는지, 내가 무슨 말을 할지 보려고 기다리기라도 하듯 잠시 멈추었다.\n\n나는 그런 여자가 내게 얼마나 필요한지를 너무도 절감하던 터라 그녀의 제안을 받아들이지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 그녀에게, 내 사정은 일부는 그녀가 짐작한 대로이고 일부는 그렇지 않으니, 나는 실은 결혼한 몸이고 남편이 있으나, 다만 그가 그때 너무도 좋은 처지에 있고 너무도 멀리 떨어져 있어서 공공연히 나설 수가 없는 형편이라고 했다.\n\n그녀는 내 말을 끊고는, 그것은 자기가 알 바 아니라고 했다. 자기 보살핌 아래 오는 부인들은 모두 자기에게는 결혼한 여자라는 것이었다. \"아이를 가진 여자라면 누구든,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 아이의 아비가 있는 법이지요.\" 그리고 그 아비가 남편이든 남편이 아니든 그것은 자기가 알 바 아니며, 자기가 할 일은 내가 남편이 있든 없든 지금의 처지에서 나를 돕는 것이라고 했다. \"왜냐하면, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"나설 수 없는 남편을 두었다는 것은, 이 경우의 의미로 보자면 남편이 없는 것과 마찬가지거든요. 그러니 댁이 아내든 정부情婦든 저에게는 다 매한가지예요.\"\n\n나는 곧, 내가 창녀든 아내든 여기서는 창녀로 통해야 한다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 그래서 그 점은 그냥 넘겼다. 나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 말한 대로 그것은 사실이지만, 그래도 내가 내 사정을 그녀에게 말해야 한다면 있는 그대로 말해야 한다고 했다. 그래서 나는 되도록 짧게 그것을 그녀에게 들려주었고, 이렇게 매듭지었다. \"이 모든 걸로 댁을 번거롭게 하는 건, 부인,\" 내가 말했다, \"댁이 전에 말씀하신 대로 그것이 댁의 일과 크게 상관있어서가 아니라, 바로 이 점이 상관있기 때문이에요. 곧, 저는 남에게 보이든, 공공연히 드러나든 감추어지든 그 일로는 조금도 괴롭지 않으니, 그건 제게 완전히 아무래도 좋은 일이거든요. 다만 제 어려움은, 이 고장에 제가 아는 사람이 하나도 없다는 거예요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 부인.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그런 경우에 흔히 있는 교구의 성가신 참견을 막아 줄 보증을 마련하지 못하시는 거죠. 그리고 어쩌면,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"아이가 태어났을 때 그 아이를 어찌 처리할지도 잘 모르시는 거고요.\" \"뒤엣것은,\" 내가 말했다, \"앞엣것만큼 제 걱정거리는 아니에요.\"\n\"그럼, 부인,\" 산파가 대답했다, \"감히 댁을 제 손에 맡기시겠어요? 저는 어디어디에 살아요. 비록 저는 댁에 대해 캐묻지 않겠지만, 댁은 저에 대해 알아보셔도 돼요. 제 이름은 B예요. 저는 어느 거리에 살죠\"—거리 이름을 댔다—\"요람 간판이 걸린 데예요. 제 직업은 산파이고, 우리 집에 와서 해산하는 부인들이 많답니다. 저는 교구에 일반적인 조건으로 보증을 서서, 제 지붕 아래에서 세상에 나오는 누구든 그들에게 비용 부담을 지우지 않도록 해 놓았어요. 이 모든 일에서 제가 여쭐 게 딱 한 가지 있어요, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 한 가지에만 답이 되면 나머지는 모두 완전히 마음 놓으셔도 돼요.\"\n\n나는 곧 그녀가 무슨 뜻인지 알아차리고 이렇게 말했다. \"부인, 무슨 뜻인지 알 것 같아요. 고맙게도, 비록 이 세상 이 고장에는 제가 벗이 없으나, 돈은, 필요할 만큼은, 부족하지 않아요. 그렇다고 그것이 넉넉하다는 것도 아니지만요.\" 이렇게 덧붙인 것은 그녀가 큰 것을 기대하지 않게 하기 위함이었다. \"그럼, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그게 정말 핵심이지요. 그게 없으면 이런 경우에 아무것도 할 수가 없거든요. 그렇지만,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"제가 댁에게 바가지를 씌우거나, 댁에게 야박한 걸 들이밀지 않는다는 걸 보시게 될 거예요. 그리고 댁이 원하신다면, 모든 걸 미리 다 아시게 해서, 댁이 형편에 맞추어, 보시기에 좋을 대로 많이 쓰지도 적게 쓰지도 않게 하실 수 있어요.\"\n나는 그녀에게, 당신은 내 처지를 너무도 완전히 잘 알아주는 것 같으니, 내가 당신에게 청할 것은 다만 이것뿐이라고 했다. 곧, 내가 당신에게 말한 대로, 나에게 돈은 충분히 있으나 많은 양은 아니니, 내가 되도록 군더더기 비용을 적게 들이도록 잘 마련해 달라는 것이었다.\n\n그녀는, 그 비용 내역을 두세 가지 모양으로, 차림표처럼 가져올 테니, 내가 좋을 대로 고르라고 대답했다. 나는 그렇게 해 달라고 했다.\n이튿날 그녀가 그것을 가져왔는데, 그녀의 세 가지 계산서 사본은 다음과 같았다.\n\n1. 식사 포함, 그녀의 집에서 석 달 묵는 값, 주당 10실링……6파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2. 한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 산욕용 침구 사용료……1파운드 10실링 0펜스\n\n3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사에게, 그리고 대부들과 서기에게……1파운드 10실링 0펜스\n4. 세례식에 벗 다섯을 청할 경우의 만찬 비용……1파운드 0실링 0펜스\n\n산파로서의 그녀의 수고비, 그리고 교구의 성가신 일을 떠맡아 주는 값……3파운드 3실링 0펜스\n시중드는 그녀의 하녀에게……0파운드 10실링 0펜스\n\n―――――― 13파운드 13실링 0펜스\n이것이 첫째 계산서였고, 둘째 것은 같은 항목이었다.\n\n1. 석 달간의 숙식 등, 주당 20실링……13파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2. 한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 침구와 레이스 사용료……2파운드 10실링 0펜스\n\n3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사 등, 위와 같음……2파운드 0실링 0펜스\n4. 만찬과 과자류……3파운드 3실링 0펜스\n\n위와 같은 그녀의 수고비……5파운드 5실링 0펜스\n하녀에게……1파운드 0실링 0펜스\n\n―――――― 26파운드 18실링 0펜스\n이것이 둘째 등급의 계산서였고, 셋째 것은, 그녀의 말로는, 한 단계 더 높은 것으로, 아비나 벗들이 나설 경우의 것이었다.\n\n1. 석 달간의 숙식, 방 두 칸과 하인용 다락 한 칸……30파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2. 한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 가장 고운 산욕용 침구……4파운드 4실링 0펜스\n\n3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사 등……2파운드 10실링 0펜스\n4. 만찬, 포도주는 신사 측에서 보내올 것……6파운드 0실링 0펜스\n\n나의 수고비 등……10파운드 10실링 0펜스\n저쪽 하녀 말고 따로 둘 하녀……0파운드 10실링 0펜스 ―――――― 53파운드 14실링 0펜스\n\n나는 세 계산서를 모두 들여다보고는 미소 지으며, 모든 것을 따져 볼 때 그녀의 요구가 매우 합당해 보이고, 그러니 그녀의 시설이 좋으리라는 것도 의심치 않는다고 말했다.\n그녀는 그건 내가 직접 보면 판단할 수 있을 거라고 했다. 나는 그녀에게, 안타깝지만 아무래도 내가 그녀의 가장 낮은 등급의 손님이 되어야 할 것 같다고 했다. \"그리고 어쩌면, 부인,\" 내가 말했다, \"그 때문에 저를 덜 반기시게 될지도 모르겠네요.\" \"아니에요, 전혀 그렇지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"셋째 부류의 손님이 한 분 계실 때 둘째 부류는 두 분, 첫째 부류는 그 네 배가 계시거든요. 그리고 그분들에게서 비례해서 따지면 다른 어느 분에게서만큼이나 벌어요. 그렇지만 제가 댁을 잘 보살피는지 미덥지 않으시거든, 댁의 벗 누구든 와서 댁이 잘 시중받는지 아닌지 지켜보도록 허락해 드릴게요.\"\n\n그러고는 그녀가 계산서의 세부 항목을 설명했다. \"우선, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"여기 석 달치 비용이 있다는 걸 보셨으면 해요. 댁은 주당 10실링밖에 안 들어요. 단언컨대 댁은 제 밥상에 불평하지 않으실 거예요. 짐작건대,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"지금 계신 데서도 이보다 싸게 살지는 못하시죠?\" \"정말 그래요.\" 내가 말했다. \"이렇게 싸지 않아요. 방 하나에 주당 6실링을 내고, 게다가 제 끼니는 제가 할 수 있는 대로 알아서 마련해야 하는데, 그게 훨씬 더 많이 들거든요.\"\n\"그렇다면, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"만약 아이가 살지 못하거나, 댁도 아시다시피 가끔 그런 일이 있듯이 사산되거든, 그러면 목사 항목이 절약되지요. 그리고 댁을 찾아올 벗이 없으면 만찬 비용을 아끼실 수 있고요. 그러니 그 항목들을 빼면, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"댁의 해산 비용은 댁의 평소 생활비보다 다 합쳐 5파운드 3실링 넘게는 들지 않을 거예요.\"\n\n이것은 내가 일찍이 들어 본 가운데 가장 합당한 것이었다. 그래서 나는 미소 지으며 그녀에게, 가서 그녀의 손님이 되겠다고 했다. 다만 나는 또한, 내게는 아직 두 달 남짓이 남았으니 아마도 석 달보다 더 오래 그녀에게 묵게 될지도 모른다고 하면서, 알맞은 때가 되기 전에 그녀가 나를 옮겨야 할 일은 없겠는지 알고 싶다고 했다. 아니라고, 그녀가 말했다. 자기 집은 넓고, 게다가 해산한 사람은 본인이 가고 싶어 할 때까지 누구도 옮기게 하는 법이 없으며, 더 많은 부인이 찾아온다 해도, 자기는 이웃들 사이에서 그리 밉보이지 않아서 필요하다면 스무 명도 거처를 마련해 줄 수 있다고 했다.\n\n나는 그녀가 자기 분야에서 이름난 부인이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 한마디로, 나는 나 자신을 그녀의 손에 맡기기로 하고 그녀에게 약속했다. 그러자 그녀는 다른 것들에 관해 이야기하다가, 내가 지금 묵는 곳의 시설을 둘러보고는, 내게 시중과 편의가 부족하다는 것을 흠잡으며, 자기 집에서는 이런 대접을 받지 않을 거라고 했다. 나는 말을 꺼내기가 조심스러웠다고 했으니, 그 집 여주인이 내가 아이를 가진 까닭에 내가 앓아누운 뒤로 더 서먹하게 굴거나, 적어도 내게는 그렇게 보였기 때문이며, 또 내가 나 자신에 관해 변변히 설명도 못 하는 처지라 여겨서, 그녀가 이런저런 모욕이라도 줄까 봐 두려웠던 것이다.\n\n\"아이고,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 마님은 이런 일에 낯선 분이 아니에요. 댁 같은 처지의 부인들을 여러 번 받아 보려 했지만, 교구를 무마하질 못했던 거죠. 게다가 그분은 댁이 생각하시는 것만큼 까다로운 부인도 아니에요. 그래도, 댁이 떠나실 참이니, 그분 일에 끼어들지는 마시고, 댁이 여기 계시는 동안 제 생각보다 댁이 좀 더 잘 보살핌을 받도록 제가 살펴 드릴게요. 그렇다고 그 때문에 댁에게 비용이 더 드는 것도 아니고요.\"\n나는 그녀의 말을 전혀 알아듣지 못했다. 그래도 나는 그녀에게 고맙다고 했고, 그렇게 우리는 헤어졌다. 이튿날 아침 그녀는 김이 오르는 구운 닭 한 마리와 셰리주 한 병을 보내고는, 하녀를 시켜 내게 전하기를, 내가 거기 머무는 동안 자기가 날마다 시중을 들겠다고 했다.\n\n이것은 놀랄 만큼 좋고 친절한 일이라, 나는 매우 기꺼이 그것을 받아들였다. 밤에 그녀는 다시 사람을 보내어, 내게 필요한 것은 없는지, 몸은 어떤지 묻고는, 아침에 내 끼니와 함께 하녀를 자기에게 보내라고 일렀다. 하녀는 떠나오기 전 아침에 내게 초콜릿을 좀 타 주라는 지시를 받았고 그대로 했으며, 정오에는 송아지 가슴살 췌장 통째와 수프 한 접시를 내 끼니로 가져왔다. 이런 식으로 그녀는 멀찍이서 나를 보살펴 길러 주었으니, 나는 무척 흡족했고 곧 좋아졌다. 사실 그전의 내 의기소침함이 내 병의 주된 부분이었던 것이다.\n\n나는 그런 사람들 사이에서 흔히 그렇듯이, 그녀가 내게 보낸 하인이 드루어리 레인 출신의 어떤 경솔하고 뻔뻔한 계집아이일 거라고 짐작했고, 그 때문에 그녀를 곁에 두는 것이 몹시 마음이 편치 않았다. 그래서 나는 첫날 밤에는 도무지 그녀를 그 집에서 재우려 하지 않았고, 마치 그녀가 공공연한 도둑이라도 되는 양 빈틈없이 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다.\n\n나의 그 점잖은 부인은 곧 무슨 일인지 알아채고는, 짤막한 쪽지와 함께 그녀를 돌려보냈으니, 곧 자기 하녀의 정직함은 믿어도 된다는 것, 무슨 일이든 자기가 그녀를 책임지겠다는 것, 그리고 자기는 그 충실함에 대한 매우 좋은 보증 없이는 어떤 하인도 집에 들이지 않는다는 것이었다. 그제야 나는 완전히 마음이 놓였다. 그리고 사실 그 하녀의 행실이 스스로를 증명했으니, 그보다 더 얌전하고 조용하고 차분한 계집아이가 누구의 집에 들어온 적이 없었고, 나는 나중에도 그녀가 과연 그러함을 알게 되었다.\n\n내가 바깥출입을 할 만큼 좋아지자마자, 나는 그 하녀와 함께 그 집을 보러 가서, 내가 쓰게 될 처소를 살펴보았다. 모든 것이 어찌나 보기 좋고 깨끗하고 훌륭한지, 한마디로 나는 흠잡을 데가 없었고, 내가 마주한 것에 놀랄 만큼 기쁘고 만족스러웠으니, 내가 처한 우울한 처지를 생각하면 그것은 내가 기대했던 것을 훨씬 넘어서는 것이었다.\n이제 내가 그 손에 떨어진 이 여자의 사악한 행위의 본질에 관해 어느 정도 설명하리라 기대할 법도 하다. 그러나 은밀히 밴 아이라는 여자들의 달갑잖은 짐을 여기서 어떤 손쉬운 방도로 떨쳐 버리는지를 세상에 보여 주는 것은, 그 악덕을 너무 부추기는 일이 될 것이다. 이 점잖은 부인은 여러 가지 일을 했는데, 그중 하나가 이런 것이었다. 곧, 아이가 태어나면, 비록 자기 집에서가 아니더라도(그녀는 여러 사사로운 해산에 불려 갈 일이 있었으므로), 약간의 돈을 받고 그 아이를 그들의 손에서, 그리고 교구의 손에서도 떠맡아 줄 사람들을 대기시켜 두었던 것이다. 그리고 그런 아이들은, 그녀의 말로는, 정직하게 거두어져 보살핌을 받는다고 했다. 그녀의 말대로라면 그녀가 관여한 그 많은 아이가 다 어찌 되었을지, 나로서는 도무지 짐작이 가지 않는다.\n\n나는 여러 번 그 일을 두고 그녀와 이야기를 나누었으나, 그녀는 늘 이 논리로 가득 차 있었다. 곧, 자기가 그녀의 말마따나 죄 없는 어린 양들의 목숨을 숱하게 구해 주었으니, 그러지 않았다면 그 양들은 어쩌면 죽임을 당했을 거라는 것, 또 불운에 절망한 나머지 그러지 않았다면 자기 아이를 없애고 스스로를 교수대로 끌고 갈 유혹에 빠졌을 숱한 여자들을 구해 주었다는 것이었다. 나는 그녀에게, 그것이 사실이며 매우 칭찬할 만한 일이라는 것을 인정했으니, 다만 그 가엾은 아이들이 그 뒤에 좋은 손에 떨어져, 자기들을 길러 주는 유모들에게 학대받거나 굶주리거나 방치되지 않는다는 조건에서였다. 그녀는, 자기는 늘 그 점에 마음을 쓰고, 자기 일에는 아주 선량하고 정직한, 믿을 만한 사람들 외에는 유모로 두지 않는다고 대답했다.\n\n나는 그에 반대되는 말을 할 수 없었으니, 그래서 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었다. \"부인, 댁이 댁의 몫을 정직하게 하시는 건 의심치 않아요. 하지만 그 사람들이 그 뒤에 무얼 하는지가 가장 큰 문제지요.\" 그러자 그녀는 자기가 그 점에 더없이 마음을 쓴다는 말로 다시 내 입을 막아 버렸다.\n\n이런 일들을 두고 나눈 그녀의 온갖 이야기 가운데 나에게 거슬리는 것이라곤 단 하나였으니, 그것은 한번 내가 만삭이 다 되어 가고 예정일이 다가온다는 이야기를 하던 중에, 그녀가 무언가 이런 식으로 들리는 말을 한 것이었다. 곧, 내가 원한다면 자기가 더 빨리 내 짐을 덜어 줄 수도 있다는 것, 쉬운 말로 하자면, 내가 그런 식으로 내 골칫거리를 끝장내고 싶다면 유산하게 하는 무언가를 줄 수 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 곧 그녀에게, 내가 그런 생각을 끔찍이 싫어한다는 것을 똑똑히 보여 주었다. 그리고 그녀를 위해 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀는 그것을 어찌나 능란하게 둘러댔던지, 나로서는 그녀가 정말로 그것을 의도한 것인지, 아니면 그저 그런 행위를 끔찍한 일로서 언급한 것뿐인지 단언할 수가 없었다. 그녀가 어찌나 말을 잘 감추고 내 뜻을 어찌나 빨리 알아챘던지, 내가 미처 내 뜻을 밝히기도 전에 그녀가 부정의 뜻을 내비쳤기 때문이다.\n\n이 대목을 되도록 짧게 줄이자면, 나는 세인트존스의 거처를 떠나 나의 새 후견인—집 안에서 그들이 그녀를 그렇게 불렀던 것이다—에게 갔다. 거기서 나는 참으로 어찌나 정중한 대접을 받고, 어찌나 세심한 보살핌을 받고, 어찌나 훌륭한 시중을 받고, 모든 것이 어찌나 좋았던지, 나는 그것에 놀랐고, 내 후견인이 거기서 무슨 이득을 얻는지 처음에는 알 수가 없었다. 그러나 나중에 알게 된바, 그녀는 묵는 이들의 식사로는 이윤을 남기지 않겠노라 공언했고, 사실 그것으로는 많이 벌 수도 없었으며, 그녀의 이윤은 그녀가 꾸리는 다른 항목들에 있었으니, 단언컨대 그녀는 그쪽으로 충분히 벌었다. 그녀가 집 안에서뿐 아니라 집 밖에서도 한 일이 어찌나 많은지 거의 믿기지 않을 정도였는데, 그 모든 것이 그 사사로운 쪽 일, 쉬운 말로 하자면 매춘 쪽 일이었던 것이다.\n\n내가 그녀의 집에 있던 동안—거의 넉 달이었다—그녀는 적어도 열두 명의 환락의 여인들을 그 문 안에서 해산하게 했고, 문밖에서 그녀가 거느린 이가 서른두 명 안팎쯤 되었던 것 같은데, 그중 한 명은, 나에게 그러했듯이 까다로운 부인이었으나, 세인트존스의 내 옛 안주인 집에 묵고 있었다.\n\n이것은 이 시대에 점점 자라나는 악덕에 대한 기이한 증거였으니, 나 자신이 그토록 못된 사람이었음에도 내 분별을 충격에 빠뜨릴 만한 것이었다. 나는 내가 있는 그곳이, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 사악한 행위가 역겨워지기 시작했다. 그러면서도 한마디 해 두어야겠으니, 내가 거기 있던 내내 그 집에서 조금이라도 점잖지 못한 일을 본 적이 없으며, 그런 일이 보일 만한 것이 있었으리라고도 생각지 않는다.\n\n해산한 부인들이 산욕 한 달 안에 있을 때 문병하는 경우를 제외하고는, 어떤 남자도 이층으로 올라오는 것이 보인 적이 없었고, 그때에도 그 노부인이 함께 따라붙지 않은 적이 없었으니, 그녀는 어떤 남자도, 아니 자기 아내라 할지라도, 산욕 한 달 안에는 여자에게 손대게 하지 않는다는 것을 자기 운영의 명예로 삼았다. 또한 그녀는 어떤 핑계로도 남자가 그 집에서 자는 것을 허락하지 않았으니, 아니, 설령 그것이 그의 친아내인 것이 확실하다 해도 그러했다. 그리고 그에 대한 그녀의 입버릇은, 자기 집에서 얼마나 많은 아이가 태어나든 상관하지 않으나, 자기가 막을 수만 있다면 거기서 어떤 아이도 배지 않게 하겠다는 것이었다.\n\n어쩌면 이것은 필요 이상으로 지나쳤을지도 모르나, 설령 그것이 오류라 하더라도 좋은 쪽으로 기운 오류였으니, 이로써 그녀는 자기 일의, 그것이 어떠하든, 평판을 지켜 냈고, 다음과 같은 평판을 얻었기 때문이다. 곧, 그녀가 여자들이 타락한 뒤에는 그들을 보살펴 주었으되, 그들이 타락하는 데에는 조금도 손을 보태지 않았다는 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀가 꾸려 가는 것 역시 사악한 장사였다.\n\n내가 거기 있는 동안, 그리고 내가 해산하기 전에, 나는 은행에 있는 나의 수탁인에게서 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 다정하고 정중한 말들로 가득했고, 런던으로 돌아오라고 간곡히 다그치는 것이었다. 그것이 내게 왔을 때는 부친 지 거의 보름이 다 된 것이었으니, 먼저 랭커셔로 보내졌다가 다시 내게로 돌려보내졌기 때문이다. 그는 끝맺으며 내게 이르기를, 자기가, 그가 그렇게 불렀던 것 같은데, 아내에 대한 판결을 얻어 냈으며, 내가 자기를 받아 준다면 나에 대한 자기 약속을 지킬 준비가 되어 있다고 하면서, 다정함과 애정을 거듭 맹세하는 숱한 말들을 덧붙였는데, 만약 그가 내가 처해 온 사정을 알았더라면 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 그런 것들이었고, 또 현재의 나로서는 도무지 받을 자격이 없는 것들이었다.\n\n나는 그의 편지에 답장을 보내며 그것을 리버풀에서 쓴 것으로 날짜를 적었으나, 인편으로 부치면서, 그것이 시내의 어떤 벗에게 봉투에 넣어 보내져 왔다고 둘러댔다. 나는 그가 풀려난 것을 축하해 주면서도, 그가 다시 결혼하는 것이 적법한지에 대해 몇 가지 거리낌을 내비쳤고, 그에게 이르기를, 그가 그것을 작정하기 전에 그 점을 아주 진지하게 생각해 보리라 짐작한다고, 그 결과가 너무도 중대하여 그만한 분별을 지닌 사람이 그런 성질의 일을 경솔하게 모험할 수는 없는 법이라고 했다. 그러고는, 그가 무엇을 작정하든 잘되기를 빈다는 말로 끝맺으면서도, 내 마음속 생각은 조금도 그에게 들이지 않았고, 그에게 가서 함께하라는 그의 제안에도 아무 답을 주지 않았으며, 다만 멀찍이서 그해 말에 돌아갈 생각이라는 것만 언급했으니, 이 편지의 날짜는 4월이었다.\n\n나는 5월 중순쯤 해산하여 또 하나의 늠름한 사내아이를 낳았고, 나 자신도 그런 경우에 늘 그렇듯이 좋은 상태였다. 내 후견인은 산파로서 자기 몫을, 상상할 수 있는 가장 빼어난 솜씨와 능란함으로, 내가 일찍이 겪어 본 그 어떤 것도 훨씬 넘어서게 해냈다.\n\n내 산고 중에, 그리고 산후 산욕 중에 그녀가 나를 보살핀 것은, 그녀가 내 친어머니였다 해도 그보다 더 나을 수 없었을 정도였다. 이 능란한 부인의 솜씨를 보고 누구도 자기네 방종한 행위에 용기를 얻지 말지니, 그녀는 자기가 갈 데로 갔고, 단언컨대 그 뒤를 이을 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 남기지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n내가 해산한 지 스무이틀쯤 되었을 때, 나는 은행에 있는 내 벗에게서 또 한 통의 편지를 받았는데, 거기에는 놀라운 소식이 담겨 있었다. 곧, 자기가 아내에 대한 이혼 최종 판결을 얻어 냈고, 그것을 어느 날 그녀에게 송달했으며, 또 그가 다시 결혼하는 것에 대한 내 온갖 거리낌에 대해, 내가 기대할 수도 없었고 그 자신도 바라지 않았던 그런 답을 줄 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다. 곧, 전부터 그를 대한 자기 처사에 어느 정도 회한을 품고 있던 그의 아내가, 그가 뜻을 이루었다는 소식을 듣자마자 바로 그날 저녁, 매우 불행하게도 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 것이었다.\n\n그는 그녀의 참변에 대해 자기가 마음 아파하고 있다는 것을 매우 점잖게 표현하면서도, 자기는 거기에 아무런 손도 대지 않았으며, 자기는 다만 누가 봐도 부당하게 해를 입고 학대받은 일에서 자기 자신에 대한 정당함을 도모했을 뿐이라고 변명했다. 그러나 그는 그 일로 극도로 괴로워하고 있으며, 이 세상에서 자기에게 남은 어떤 위안의 전망도 없되, 다만 내가 와서 내 벗 됨으로 자기를 위로해 주리라는 희망만 있을 뿐이라고 했다. 그러고는 정말이지 격렬하게 나를 다그쳐, 적어도 내가 시내로 올라와 자기를 보게 해 주리라는 어떤 희망이라도 달라고 했으니, 그러면 그때 그 일에 관해 더 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다.\n\n나는 그 소식에 몹시 놀랐고, 이제 진지하게 내 현재의 처지를, 그리고 거두어 키워야 할 아이가 손에 있다는 것이 내게 얼마나 이루 말할 수 없는 불운인지를 곰곰이 되돌아보기 시작했으니, 그 일을 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 마침내 나는 내 후견인에게 멀찍이서 내 사정을 털어놓았다. 나는 며칠 동안 우울하고 불안한 기색을 보였고, 그녀는 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는지 알아내려고 끊임없이 졸라 댔다. 내가 그토록 여러 번 남편이 있다고 그녀에게 말해 놓은 마당에, 청혼을 받았다고는 죽어도 그녀에게 말할 수가 없었으니, 정말이지 그녀에게 무어라 해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 나는 나를 몹시 괴롭히는 무언가가 있다는 것은 인정했으나, 동시에 그녀에게, 그것을 살아 있는 누구에게도 말할 수 없다고 했다.\n\n그녀는 며칠을 두고 계속 졸라 댔으나, 나는 그녀에게, 그 비밀을 누구에게도 털어놓는 것은 불가능하다고 했다. 이것은 그녀에 대한 답이 되기는커녕 그녀의 졸라 댐을 더 키웠다. 그녀는, 자기가 이런 성질의 가장 큰 비밀들을 맡아 온 사람이라는 것, 모든 것을 감추는 것이 자기 일이라는 것, 그리고 그런 성질의 일들을 발설하는 것은 자기 파멸이 되리라는 것을 내세웠다. 그녀는 내게, 자기가 다른 사람들의 일을 두고 내게 수다 떠는 것을 본 적이 있느냐고, 그런데 어찌 자기를 의심할 수 있느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 내게, 자기에게 털어놓는 것은 아무에게도 말하지 않는 것과 같다고, 자기는 죽음처럼 입이 무겁다고, 자기가 도와줄 수 없는 사정이라면 그것은 정말이지 아주 기이한 경우일 수밖에 없다고, 그러나 그것을 감추는 것은 나 자신에게서 온갖 가능한 도움이나 도움의 방도를 빼앗는 것이요, 그녀에게서는 나를 도울 기회를 빼앗는 것이라고 했다. 한마디로, 그녀에게는 사람을 호리는 그런 능변과 설득의 힘이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그녀에게 무엇 하나 감출 수가 없었다.\n\n그래서 나는 그녀에게 속을 털어놓기로 작정했다. 나는 그녀에게 내 랭커셔 결혼의 내력을, 그리고 우리 둘 다 어떻게 낭패를 보았는지, 우리가 어떻게 함께 살게 되었고 어떻게 헤어졌는지, 그가 어떻게 자기 깜냥껏 나를 완전히 풀어 주어 다시 결혼할 자유를 주었는지, 자기가 그것을 알게 되더라도 결코 나를 자기 사람이라 주장하지도, 나를 성가시게 하거나 들추어내지도 않겠다고 다짐했는지를 들려주었다. 또 나는 내가 자유로운 몸이라고 생각했으나, 발각될 경우에 뒤따를지도 모를 결과가 두려워서 감히 모험을 걸기가 끔찍이도 겁이 났다고 했다.\n\n그러고는 나는 그녀에게, 내가 얼마나 좋은 청혼을 받았는지를 말했다. 나는 그녀에게 내 벗의 마지막 두 편지를 보여 주어, 그것이 나를 런던으로 오라고 청하는 것임을, 그리고 그것이 얼마나 애정과 간곡함으로 쓰였는지를 보게 했으나, 이름은 지워 버렸고, 또 그의 아내의 참변에 관한 이야기도, 다만 그녀가 죽었다는 것 외에는 지워 버렸다.\n\n그녀는 결혼에 대한 내 거리낌을 두고 웃음을 터뜨리며, 그 다른 쪽 것은 결혼이 아니라 양쪽 모두에 대한 기만이었다고 했다. 그리고 우리가 상호 합의로 헤어졌으니, 그 계약의 본질은 깨어진 것이요, 그 의무는 서로 면제된 것이라고 했다. 그녀에게는 이에 대한 논거들이 혀끝에 맺혀 있었으니, 한마디로 그녀는 따져서 나를 내 이성에서 벗어나게 했다. 물론 그것은 내 자신의 마음이 기우는 것이 거든 덕택이기도 했지만 말이다.\n\n그러나 그때 크고 주된 어려움이 닥쳤으니, 그것은 곧 아이였다. 이것은, 그녀가 노골적으로 내게 말하기를, 치워 버려야 하며, 그것도 누구도 결코 그것을 알아낼 수 없도록 해야 한다는 것이었다. 나는 내게 아이가 있었다는 것을 완전히 감추지 않고는 결혼이 있을 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그가 곧 그 나이로 미루어 그 아이가 내가 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 태어났음을, 아니, 잉태되기까지 했음을 알아챌 것이고, 그러면 그 일이 다 깨어져 버릴 터였기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 그 아이와 완전히 헤어진다는 것, 그리고 내가 알 수 없는 노릇이지만, 그 아이가 죽임을 당하거나, 방치와 학대로 굶어 죽는다는 것(이는 거의 같은 일이거니와)을 생각하면, 내 가슴이 어찌나 세차게 미어지던지, 나는 그것을 끔찍함 없이는 생각할 수가 없었다. 나는, 점잖음을 위해서라며, 흔히 일컫는 대로 자기 아이를 어딘가로 치워 버리는 데 동의하는 모든 여자가, 그것이 그저 살인을 위해 꾸며진 방편일 뿐임을, 다시 말해 안전하게 자기 아이를 죽이는 일임을 헤아려 주기를 바란다.\n\n아이에 관해 무엇이든 아는 모든 이에게 분명한 사실이거니와, 우리는 무력하게, 자기 자신의 필요를 채우기는커녕 그것을 알릴 능력조차 없이 세상에 태어나며, 도움 없이는 죽을 수밖에 없다. 그리고 이 도움에는 어머니든 다른 누구든 거들어 주는 손이 필요할 뿐 아니라, 그 거들어 주는 손에는 두 가지가 필요하니, 곧 보살핌과 솜씨다. 이 둘이 다 없으면, 태어나는 아이의 절반은 죽을 것이요, 아니, 설령 먹을 것을 거부당하지 않는다 하더라도 그러할 것이며, 살아남은 자들 가운데서도 절반은 또 불구가 되거나 바보가 되어, 사지를 잃거나, 어쩌면 정신을 잃게 될 것이다. 나는 의심치 않거니와, 이런 것들이야말로 자연이 어머니의 가슴속에 자기 아이에 대한 애정을 심어 둔 이유의 일부이니, 그것 없이는 어머니들이 자기 아이를 지탱하는 데 필요한 보살핌과 뜬눈의 수고에, 마땅히 그래야 하듯이 자기 자신을 내던질 수 없을 것이다.\n\n이 보살핌이 아이의 생명에 필요한 것이므로, 그들을 방치하는 것은 곧 그들을 죽이는 것이다. 다시, 자연이 그 속에 심어 둔 그런 필요한 애정이라곤 조금도 지니지 못한 사람들에게 그들을 맡겨 다루게 하는 것은, 그들을 가장 높은 정도로 방치하는 것이다. 아니, 어떤 경우에는 그것이 더 나아가, 그들을 잃게 할 목적의 방치이니, 그리하여 그것은 아이가 살든 죽든 의도적인 살인이나 다름없다.\n\n이 모든 것이 내 눈앞에 떠올랐으니, 그것도 가장 검고 가장 무시무시한 모양으로 떠올랐다. 그리고 나는 이제 어머니라고 부르는 법을 익힌 내 후견인에게 거리낌이 없던 터라, 그녀에게 내가 그 일을 두고 품고 있던 온갖 어두운 생각을 다 털어놓고, 내가 얼마나 큰 고통 속에 있는지를 말했다. 그녀는 이 대목에서는 다른 대목에서보다 훨씬 더 진지한 기색이었다. 그러나 그녀는 이런 일들에 도무지 마음을 움직일 가망이 없을 만큼 굳어 있어서, 신앙의 부분이며 살인에 대한 거리낌에 꿈쩍도 하지 않았듯이, 애정에 관한 그 부분에서도 똑같이 뚫고 들어갈 수가 없었다. 그녀는 내게, 마치 내가 자기 친자식이라도 되는 듯이 산욕 중에 자기가 나를 세심하고 자상하게 보살피지 않았느냐고 물었다. 나는 그녀가 그러했음을 인정한다고 했다. \"그래요, 내 사랑,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그런데 댁이 가 버리고 나면, 댁이 내게 뭐가 되죠? 그리고 댁이 교수형을 당한다 한들 그게 내게 무슨 상관이겠어요? 그게 자기네 장사고 그걸로 밥벌이를 하느라, 친어머니가 할 수 있는 만큼이나 아이들을 알뜰히 보살피는 걸 자부하고, 어쩌면 더 잘 아는 여자들이 없다고 생각하세요? 아니에요, 아니고말고요, 아가,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"걱정 마요. 우리 자신은 어떻게 길러졌나요? 댁이 친어머니 손에 길러진 게 확실해요? 그런데도 댁은 토실토실하고 고운 걸요, 아가,\" 그 늙은 마귀할멈은 그렇게 말하며 내 얼굴을 쓰다듬었다. \"조금도 걱정 마요, 아가,\" 그녀는 자기 특유의 익살스러운 투로 이어 갔다, \"내 곁에는 살인자라곤 없어요. 나는 구할 수 있는 가장 솜씨 좋고 가장 정직한 유모들만 쓰고, 그들 손에서 잘못되는 아이가, 죄다 친어머니가 젖을 먹였을 때만큼이나 적답니다. 우리에겐 보살핌도 솜씨도 모자라지 않아요.\"\n\n내가 친어머니 손에 길러진 게 확실하냐고 그녀가 물었을 때, 그녀는 내 아픈 데를 찔렀다. 도리어 나는 그렇지 않았다는 것을 확실히 알고 있었으니, 나는 바로 그 말에 몸을 떨며 새파랗게 질렸다. \"분명,\" 나는 속으로 중얼거렸다, \"이 사람이 마녀이거나 어떤 영(靈)과 통하는 게 아닐진대, 내가 나 자신을 알 수 있기도 전에 내게 무슨 일이 있었는지를 어찌 알 수 있겠어.\" 그리고 나는 마치 겁에 질리기라도 한 듯이 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀가 나에 관해 무엇 하나 알 수 있을 리 없다는 데 생각이 미치자 그 동요는 가셨고, 나는 마음이 놓이기 시작했으나, 그것이 곧바로는 아니었다.\n\n그녀는 내가 빠진 동요를 알아챘으나 그 까닭은 알지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 아이들이 친어머니 손에 다 길러지지 않는다 해서 그들이 죽임을 당한다고 짐작하는 내 어리석음을 두고 제멋대로 떠들어 댔으며, 자기가 처리하는 아이들은 그 어머니들이 직접 길렀더라도 그러했을 만큼이나 잘 대우받는다고 나를 설득하려 들었다.\n\n\"그게 사실일지도 모르죠, 어머니,\" 내가 말했다, \"제가 알 수 없는 노릇이지만요. 하지만 제 의심은 정말이지 매우 강하게 근거 지어진 거예요.\" \"그럼, 어디,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그중 몇 개 들어 봅시다.\" \"왜냐하면, 우선,\" 내가 말했다, \"어머니는 그 사람들에게 약간의 돈을 주어, 그 아이를 부모의 손에서 떠맡아, 그 아이가 사는 동안 내내 보살피게 하잖아요. 그런데 우리가 알다시피, 어머니,\" 내가 말했다, \"그들은 가난한 사람들이고, 그들의 벌이는 되도록 빨리 그 부담에서 벗어나는 데 있어요. 그러니 그들로서는 그 아이가 죽는 게 가장 좋은 일이고 보면, 그들이 그 목숨에 그리 마음을 쓰지 않으리라는 걸 제가 어찌 의심하지 않을 수 있겠어요?\"\n\n\"그건 다 헛것이고 공상이에요.\" 그 노파가 말했다. \"내 말하건대, 그들의 신용은 그 아이의 목숨에 달려 있어서, 그들은 댁들 누구의 친어머니 못지않게 알뜰히 보살핀다니까요.\"\n\n\"오 어머니,\" 내가 말했다, \"제 어린것이 알뜰히 보살핌을 받고 마땅한 대우를 받는다는 것만 제가 확신할 수 있다면, 저는 정말이지 행복할 거예요. 하지만 그 점에서 제가 흡족해질 수 있으려면 제가 그 아이를 직접 보는 수밖에 없는데, 지금 제 처지에서 그 아이를 보는 것은 제게 파멸이요 멸망일 테니, 그러니 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\"\n\n\"별 이야기를 다!\" 후견인이 말했다. \"댁은 아이를 보고도 싶고, 아이를 보고 싶지 않기도 하고, 감추어지고도 싶고 드러나고도 싶고, 둘 다를 한꺼번에 바라는군요. 그건 불가능한 일이에요, 내 사랑. 그러니 댁은 댁보다 앞서 다른 양심적인 어머니들이 해 온 그대로 해야 하고, 일이 댁의 바람대로는 아니더라도 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로인 데에 만족해야 해요.\"\n\n나는 그녀가 양심적인 어머니들이라는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아들었다. 그녀는 양심적인 창녀들이라고 말하려 했던 것이나, 나를 언짢게 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 사실 이 경우에 나는 창녀가 아니었으니, 앞선 결혼의 효력을 제외하면 적법하게 결혼한 몸이었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 내가 무엇이었든 간에, 나는 그 직업에 흔한 그런 모진 경지에까지는 이르지 못했으니, 다시 말해 천륜을 저버리고 내 아이의 안위를 아랑곳하지 않는 경지 말이다. 그리고 나는 이 정직한 애정을 어찌나 오래 간직했던지, 은행에 있는 내 벗을 거의 포기할 지경에까지 이르렀다. 그는 자기에게 와서 결혼하자고 어찌나 모질게 나를 다그쳤던지, 한마디로 그를 거절할 여지가 거의 없었는데도 말이다.\n\n마침내 나의 늙은 후견인이 여느 때의 그 자신만만함으로 내게 와서 말했다. \"자, 내 사랑,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"내가 한 가지 방도를 찾아냈어요. 댁의 아이가 잘 대우받으리라는 걸 댁이 확실히 알 수 있으면서도, 그 아이를 보살피는 사람들은 댁을 결코 알지 못하고, 그 아이의 어미가 누구인지도 결코 알지 못하게 할 방도 말이에요.\"\n\n\"오 어머니,\" 내가 말했다, \"그렇게 해 주실 수 있다면, 저는 영원히 어머니께 매인 몸이 될 거예요.\" \"자,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"우리가 보통 계약하는 사람들에게 주는 것보다, 댁이 매년 약간의 비용을 더 댈 용의가 있어요?\" \"그럼요,\" 내가 말했다, \"기꺼이요, 다만 제가 감추어질 수만 있다면요.\" \"그 점은,\" 후견인이 말했다, \"댁은 안전할 거예요. 유모는 감히 댁에 대해 캐물을 엄두조차 못 낼 테고, 댁은 일 년에 한두 번 나와 함께 가서 댁의 아이를 보고, 그 아이가 어떻게 대우받는지 보고, 그 아이가 좋은 손에 있다는 걸, 댁이 누구인지 아무도 모르는 채로, 확인할 수 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 내가 말했다, \"어머니, 제가 제 아이를 보러 가면, 제가 그 아이의 어미라는 걸 감출 수 있을 거라고 생각하세요? 그게 가능하다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"걱정 마요, 걱정 마요,\" 나의 후견인이 말했다, \"댁이 그걸 드러낸다 한들, 유모는 조금도 눈치채지 못할 거예요. 그 여자는 댁에 대해 어떤 것도 묻거나 어떤 것도 알은체하는 게 금지될 테니까요. 만약 그 여자가 그러려 들면, 댁이 줄 것으로 되어 있는 돈을 잃게 될 테고, 그 아이도 그 여자에게서 거두어들이게 될 거예요.\"\n\n나는 이것이 매우 흡족했다. 그래서 그다음 주에 하트퍼드 근처 어디에선가 어떤 시골 여자가 데려와졌는데, 그 여자는 돈 10파운드를 받고 그 아이를 우리 손에서 완전히 떠맡기로 되어 있었다. 그러나 만약 내가 그 여자에게 매년 5파운드를 더 주겠다면, 그 여자는 우리가 바랄 때마다 그 아이를 내 후견인의 집으로 데려오거나, 우리가 내려가서 그 아이를 보고 그 여자가 그 아이를 얼마나 잘 대우하는지 보게 할 의무를 지기로 했다.\n\n그 여자는 매우 건강해 보였고, 그럴듯한 여자였으며, 한 오두막 농부의 아내였으나, 옷가지며 속옷이 매우 좋았고, 자기 몸에 두른 모든 것이 단정했다. 나는 무거운 마음과 숱한 눈물로 그 여자에게 내 아이를 내주었다. 나는 하트퍼드에 내려가 그 여자와 그 여자의 거처를 보았는데, 그것이 제법 마음에 들었다. 그리고 나는 그 여자에게, 그 아이에게 잘해 주기만 한다면 후하게 해 주겠노라 약속했으니, 그래서 그 여자는 첫마디부터 내가 그 아이의 어미인 것을 알았다. 그러나 그 여자는 어찌나 외진 데에 있고, 나에 대해 알아볼 여지라곤 없어 보였던지, 나는 내가 충분히 안전하다고 생각했다. 그래서 한마디로, 나는 그 여자에게 그 아이를 내주는 데 동의했고, 그 여자에게 10파운드를 주었다. 다시 말해, 나는 그것을 내 후견인에게 주었고, 그녀가 내 면전에서 그 가난한 여자에게 주었으니, 그 여자는 결코 그 아이를 내게 돌려보내지도, 그 아이를 거두고 기르는 값으로 더 이상 무엇을 요구하지도 않기로 합의했다. 다만 나는, 그 여자가 그 아이를 무척 알뜰히 보살피거든, 내가 그 아이를 보러 갈 때마다 무언가 더 주겠노라 약속했을 뿐이다. 그러니 나는 그 5파운드를 낼 의무가 있는 것은 아니었고, 다만 내 후견인에게 그렇게 하겠노라 약속했을 뿐이다. 이리하여 나의 큰 걱정은, 어떻게든 끝이 났으니, 비록 그것이 내 마음을 조금도 흡족케 하지는 못했으나, 그래도 그 당시 내 사정이 처한 형편으로는, 그때 떠올릴 수 있었던 어떤 방도보다도 내게 가장 편한 것이었다.\n\n그제야 나는 은행에 있는 내 벗에게 더 다정한 투로 편지를 쓰기 시작했고, 특히 7월 초쯤 그에게 편지 한 통을 보내어, 8월 어느 무렵에 시내에 가 있을 작정이라고 했다. 그는 상상할 수 있는 가장 정열적인 말로 내게 답장을 보내어, 미리 알맞게 기별을 달라고, 그러면 자기가 이틀 길을 와서 나를 마중하겠노라고 했다. 이것은 나를 고약하게 난감하게 했으니, 나는 거기에 무어라 답해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 한때 나는 일부러 웨스트체스터까지 역마차를 타고 갔다가 돌아오는 만족을 누리려고 작정했으니, 그가 내가 정말로 같은 마차로 들어오는 것을 보게 하려 함이었다. 나는, 비록 그럴 아무런 근거도 없었으나, 그가 내가 정말로 그 시골에 있던 것이 아니라고 여길까 봐 시샘 어린 생각이 들었던 것이다. 그리고 그것은 곧 듣게 되겠지만, 근거 없는 생각도 아니었다.\n\n나는 그런 생각에서 따져 벗어나려 애썼으나 헛일이었다. 그 인상이 내 마음에 어찌나 강하게 박혀 있던지 저항할 수가 없었다. 마침내 그 생각이, 시골로 내려간다는 내 새 계획에 보태어, 나의 늙은 후견인에게 더없이 훌륭한 눈가림이 되어 내 다른 모든 일을 완전히 가려 주리라는 데에까지 미쳤으니, 그녀는 내 새 연인이 런던에 사는지 랭커셔에 사는지 도무지 알지 못했기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 그녀에게 내 결심을 말했을 때, 그녀는 그것이 랭커셔에 있는 일이라고 굳게 믿었다.\n\n이 여행을 위해 채비를 마치고서 나는 그녀에게 그것을 알렸고, 처음부터 나를 시중들던 그 하녀를 보내어 마차에 내 자리를 잡아 두게 했다. 그녀는 그 하녀가 마지막 역참까지 나를 시중들며 내려갔다가 짐마차로 다시 올라오게 하라고 하려 했으나, 나는 그것이 편치 않을 거라고 그녀를 설득했다. 내가 떠날 때 그녀는 내게, 서신 왕래를 위한 어떤 방도도 정하지 않겠다고 했으니, 내 아이에 대한 애정이 내가 다시 시내에 오면 그녀에게 편지하고 그녀를 찾게 만들리라는 것을 그녀가 빤히 알았기 때문이다. 나는 그녀에게 정말 그러리라고 확언하고는, 그렇게 작별을 고했으니, 앞에서 이야기한 대로 거기서의 내 시설이 아무리 좋았다 한들, 그런 집에서 벗어나게 된 것이 매우 흡족했다.\n\n나는 마차 자리를 끝까지 가는 것으로 잡지 않고, 체셔에 있는, 내 생각엔 그곳인 것 같은데, 스톤이라는 곳까지만 잡았으니, 거기에는 내가 무슨 볼일이 있기는커녕, 그 마을이나 그 근처의 누구와도 조금도 아는 사이조차 아니었다. 그러나 나는, 주머니에 돈이 있으면 어디서든 제집 같다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 거기서 이삼 일을 묵으며 기회를 엿보다가, 다른 역마차에 자리가 난 것을 알고 런던으로 다시 돌아가는 차편을 잡고는, 내 신사에게 편지를 보내어, 내가 어느 날 스토니스트랫퍼드에 가 있겠다고 했으니, 마부가 내게 거기서 묵을 거라고 일러 주었던 것이다.\n\n내가 잡아탄 것은 마침 임시로 빌린 마차였는데, 아일랜드로 가려고 웨스트체스터로 가는 어떤 신사들을 모시려고 일부러 빌린 것이라, 이제 돌아가는 길이었고, 역마차들처럼 정확한 시각이나 장소에 매여 있지 않았다. 그래서 마부는 일요일에 가만히 묵을 수밖에 없었던 덕에, 나설 채비를 할 시간이 있었던바, 그렇지 않았다면 그러지 못했을 것이다.\n\n그러나 그가 받은 기별이 너무 촉박했던 터라, 그는 밤에 나와 함께 있을 만큼 제때에 스토니스트랫퍼드에 닿을 수가 없었고, 다만 이튿날 아침, 우리가 막 마을로 들어서던 참에, 브릭힐이라는 곳에서 나를 만났다.\n\n고백하건대 나는 그를 보아 매우 기뻤으니, 일부러 그 먼 데까지 가서 내가 오는 것을 꾸며 놓은 마당에, 전날 밤 조금 낭패를 본 것 같았기 때문이다. 그는 또한 자기가 타고 온 차림새로 나를 갑절로 기쁘게 했으니, 그가 매우 멋진 (신사용) 마차와 말 네 필을 데리고, 자기를 시중드는 하인까지 거느리고 왔기 때문이다.\n\n그는 곧 나를 역마차에서 내리게 했으니, 그 마차는 브릭힐의 한 여인숙에 멈추었다. 그러고는 같은 여인숙에 들어, 자기 마차를 세워 두고 저녁을 시켰다. 나는 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었으니, 나는 여정을 계속 나아가고 싶었기 때문이다. 그는 아니라고, 내가 길에서 약간 쉴 필요가 있다고, 그리고 이 집은 비록 작은 마을이긴 해도 매우 좋은 부류의 집이라고 했다. 그러니 무슨 일이 있어도 그날 밤은 더 이상 가지 말자는 것이었다.\n\n나는 그를 그리 다그치지 않았으니, 그가 나를 마중하러 그렇게 와서 그토록 많은 비용을 들인 마당에, 나도 그를 좀 받들어 주는 것이 마땅했기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그 점에 대해 마음이 편했다.\n\n저녁을 들고 나서 우리는 마을을 구경하러, 교회를 보러, 들판과 시골을 둘러보러 거닐었으니, 낯선 이들이 으레 하는 대로였고, 우리 여인숙 주인이 교회 구경을 가는 데 우리 길잡이가 되어 주었다. 나는 내 신사가 그 목사에 대해 꽤나 캐묻는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 곧 그가 분명 결혼하자고 제안하리라는 낌새를 챘다. 그리고 그것은 갑작스러운 생각이었으나, 곧이어 다음과 같은 생각이 따라왔으니, 한마디로 나는 그를 거절하지 않으리라는 것이었다. 솔직히 말해, 내 처지로는 나는 이제 안 된다고 말할 형편이 못 되었고, 더 이상 그런 모험을 무릅쓸 까닭이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 이런 생각들이 내 머릿속을 맴도는 동안—그것은 그저 몇 순간의 일이었지만—나는 우리 여인숙 주인이 그를 한쪽으로 데려가 귀엣말을 하는 것을 눈여겨보았는데, 그것도 그리 나직하지도 않아서, 나는 이만큼은 엿들었던 것이다. \"나리, 혹시 필요하시거든……\" 그 나머지는 듣지 못했으나, 보아하니 이런 취지였던 것 같다. \"나리, 혹시 목사가 필요하시거든, 조금 떨어진 데에 나리를 도와줄 제 벗이 있는데, 나리께서 바라시는 만큼 은밀히 해 드릴 겁니다.\" 내 신사는 내가 들을 수 있을 만큼 큰 소리로 대답했다. \"아주 좋네, 아마 그래야 할 것 같으이.\"\n\n내가 여인숙으로 돌아오자마자 그는 거역할 수 없는 말로 나를 몰아붙였으니, 자기가 다행히 나를 만났고 모든 것이 들어맞은 마당에, 만약 내가 바로 거기서 그 일을 매듭짓겠다면 그것이 자기 행복을 앞당기는 것이 되리라고 했다. \"무슨 말씀이세요?\" 내가 얼굴을 약간 붉히며 말했다. \"아니, 여인숙에서, 그것도 길 위에서요! 맙소사,\" 마치 내가 놀라기라도 한 듯이 내가 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요?\" \"오, 나는 그런 말 아주 잘할 수 있다오,\" 그가 말했다, \"일부러 그런 말을 하려고 온 거고, 내가 그랬다는 걸 보여 주리다.\" 그러면서 그는 서류 한 뭉치를 꺼냈다. \"겁나게 하시네요,\" 내가 말했다. \"이게 다 뭐예요?\" \"겁내지 마요, 내 사랑,\" 그가 말하며 내게 입을 맞추었다. 그가 나를 \"내 사랑\"이라고 부를 만큼 거리낌 없었던 것은 이때가 처음이었다. 그러고는 그가 거듭 말했다. \"겁내지 마요. 이게 다 뭔지 보게 될 테니.\" 그러고는 그가 그것들을 죄다 펼쳐 놓았다. 먼저 그의 아내로부터의 이혼 증서, 곧 판결문이 있었고, 그녀가 창녀짓을 했다는 충분한 증거가 있었다. 다음으로는 그녀가 살던 교구의 목사와 교구 위원들의 증명서들이 있었으니, 그녀가 매장되었음을 입증하고 그녀의 사인을 넌지시 알리는 것이었다. 그리고 그녀를 두고 배심을 열라는 검시관의 영장 사본, 그리고 그것을 심신상실이라 판정한 배심의 평결이 있었다.\n이 모든 것은 정말이지 핵심에 닿는 것이었고, 나를 흡족케 하려는 것이었다. 비록, 덧붙여 말하자면, 그가 모든 걸 알았더라면, 나는 그것 없이도 그를 받아들였을 만큼 그리 까다롭지 않았을 테지만 말이다. 그래도 나는 할 수 있는 대로 그것들을 다 훑어보고는, 그에게 이것이 정말이지 다 매우 명백하나, 그가 굳이 그것들을 가지고 오는 수고를 할 필요는 없었을 거라고, 그것은 천천히 해도 될 일이었다고 했다. 그래, 그는 그것이 나에게는 천천히 해도 될 일일지 모르나, 자기에게는 지금 이 순간 말고는 어떤 때도 충분히 이른 때가 아니라고 했다.\n\n다른 서류들이 둘둘 말려 있어서, 나는 그에게 그것들이 무엇이냐고 물었다. \"아, 그래요,\" 그가 말했다, \"그게 바로 내가 당신이 물어 주기를 바라던 거요.\" 그러고는 그가 그것들을 펼쳐, 작은 가죽 상자 하나를 꺼내더니, 그 안에서 매우 고운 다이아몬드 반지 하나를 내게 건넸다. 설령 그러고 싶었다 한들 나는 그것을 거절할 수가 없었으니, 그가 그것을 내 손가락에 끼워 주었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그에게 살짝 무릎을 굽혀 절하고 그것을 받았다. 그러고는 그가 또 다른 반지를 꺼냈다. \"그리고 이건,\" 그가 말했다, \"다른 경우를 위한 거요.\" 그러면서 그가 그것을 자기 주머니에 넣었다. \"어머, 그래도 좀 보여 주세요,\" 내가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그게 뭔지 짐작이 가요. 당신 미쳤군요.\" \"이보다 덜했다면 내가 미친 거였을 거요,\" 그가 말했으나, 여전히 내게 보여 주지 않았고, 나는 그것을 무척이나 보고 싶었다. 그래서 내가 말했다. \"그래도 좀 보여 주세요.\" \"가만,\" 그가 말했다, \"먼저 이걸 봐요.\" 그러고는 그가 다시 그 두루마리를 집어 들어 읽었는데, 보라! 그것은 우리가 결혼해도 좋다는 허가서였다. \"아니,\" 내가 말했다, \"당신 정신 나갔어요? 아니, 당신은 내가 첫마디에 응하고 따르리라고 완전히 확신했거나, 아니면 어떤 거절도 받지 않겠다고 작정했군요.\" \"뒤엣것이 분명 맞소,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 잘못 짚었을 수도 있잖아요,\" 내가 말했다. \"아니, 아니오,\" 그가 말했다, \"어찌 그렇게 생각할 수 있겠소? 나는 거절당해서는 안 되고, 거절당할 수가 없소.\" 그러면서 그가 어찌나 격렬하게 내게 입맞추기 시작했던지, 나는 그에게서 벗어날 수가 없었다.\n\n방 안에 침대 하나가 있었고, 우리는 이야기에 열을 올리며 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 마침내 그가 불시에 나를 두 팔로 끌어안아 침대 위로 던지고는 자기도 함께 쓰러졌으니, 나를 두 팔로 꼭 붙든 채로, 그러나 조금의 무례한 짓도 없이, 거듭되는 간청과 논변으로 나에게 응하라고 졸랐으며, 자기 애정을 맹세하고, 내가 약속하기 전에는 결코 나를 놓아주지 않겠노라 다짐하니, 마침내 내가 말했다. \"원, 당신은 정말 거절당하지 않기로 작정했고, 나는 거절할 수가 없네요.\" \"좋아요, 좋아,\" 내가 그에게 가볍게 입맞추며 말했다, \"그럼 당신은 거절당하지 않을 거예요,\" 내가 말했다, \"이제 좀 일으켜 주세요.\"\n\n그는 내 승낙과, 그 다정한 태도에 어찌나 도취했던지, 한순간 나는 그가 그것을 결혼으로 여겨 그 의식을 기다리지도 않으려는 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 그를 오해했으니, 그는 내게 입맞추기를 그만두었고, 그러고는 다시 두세 번 입맞추며, 자기에게 다정하게 응해 준 것에 고마워했으며, 그것의 만족과 기쁨에 어찌나 북받쳤던지, 나는 그의 눈에 눈물이 고이는 것을 보았다.\n\n나는 그에게서 돌아섰으니, 그것이 내 눈에도 눈물을 가득 고이게 했기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 그에게 잠시 내 방으로 물러가게 해 달라고 청했다. 만약 내가 지난 스물네 해 동안의 악덕에 찌든 가증스러운 삶에 대해 한 톨이라도 참된 회개를 품은 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 그때였다. 오, 사람들이 서로의 마음속을 들여다볼 수 없다는 것이 인류에게 얼마나 다행한 일인가, 하고 나는 속으로 중얼거렸다. 내가 처음부터 그토록 정직하고 그토록 애정 어린 남자의 아내였더라면 내게 얼마나 행복한 일이었을까!\n\n그러자 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"나는 얼마나 가증스러운 인간인가! 그리고 이 순진한 신사가 나에게 얼마나 속아 넘어가려는가! 자기가 한 창녀와 이혼하고서, 또 다른 창녀의 품에 자기를 내던지고 있다는 걸 그가 얼마나 모르고 있는가! 형제 둘과 동침하고, 자기 친오라비에게서 세 아이를 낳은 여자와 결혼하려 한다는 걸! 뉴게이트에서 태어났고, 그 어미는 창녀였으며 지금은 유배된 도둑인 여자와! 열세 명의 남자와 동침했고, 그를 본 뒤로 아이까지 낳은 여자와! 가엾은 신사여,\" 내가 말했다, \"그가 무슨 짓을 하려는 것인가?\" 이렇게 나 자신을 책망하기를 마치자, 이런 생각이 뒤따랐다. \"그래, 내가 그의 아내가 되어야 한다면, 신께서 내게 은총을 베푸시기를 바라거니와, 나는 그에게 참된 아내가 되어, 나를 향한 그의 그 기이하게 넘치는 정념에 걸맞게 그를 사랑하리라. 가능하다면, 그가 보게 될 것으로써, 그가 보지 못하는 내가 그에게 저지른 속임과 학대를 보상해 주리라.\"\n\n그는 내가 방에서 나오기를 애타게 기다렸으나, 내가 더디 나오는 것을 보고는 아래층으로 내려가 우리 여인숙 주인과 그 목사에 대해 이야기를 나누었다.\n\n주제넘긴 하나 선의는 있는 사람인 우리 여인숙 주인은 이웃 성직자를 부르러 사람을 보내 둔 터였다. 그래서 내 신사가 그에게 그 일을 이야기하며 그를 부르러 보내자고 말을 꺼내자, \"나리,\" 그가 그에게 말했다, \"제 벗이 집 안에 있습니다.\" 그러고는 더 말할 것도 없이 그가 두 사람을 만나게 해 주었다. 그 목사를 만나자, 그는 둘 다 원하는 한 쌍의 낯선 이들을 결혼시킬 엄두를 내겠느냐고 물었다. 그 목사는, 아무개 씨가 자기에게 그 일을 얼마간 말했다고, 자기는 이것이 은밀한 일이 아니기를 바란다고, 그가 점잖은 신사로 보이고, 부인도 처녀는 아닌 듯하니 벗들의 동의가 필요하지는 않을 거라 짐작한다고 했다. \"그 점에 대해 의심을 거두시도록,\" 내 신사가 말했다, \"이 서류를 읽어 보시오.\"\n\n그가 면허장을 꺼냈다. \"되었습니다.\" 목사가 말했다. \"신부는 어디 계십니까?\" \"곧 보시게 될 겁니다.\" 내 신사가 말했다. 이렇게 말하고 그는 위층으로 올라왔는데, 그때쯤 나는 이미 내 방에서 나와 있었다. 그래서 그는 내게 목사가 아래층에 와 있으며, 자기가 그와 이야기를 나누었고, 면허장을 보여 주자 기꺼이 우리의 혼인을 주관하겠다고 했으나 \"당신을 한번 뵙기를 청한다\"고 전했다. 그러면서 그를 올라오게 해도 되겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"내일 아침이라도 늦지 않잖아요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 말이오, 여보.\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 혹시 부모에게서 빼내 온 어린 처녀가 아닌가 미심쩍어하는 눈치라, 우리 둘 다 스스로 동의할 수 있는 나이라고 장담해 두었소. 그래서 당신을 보자고 한 거요.\" \"그럼.\" 내가 말했다. \"좋으실 대로 하세요.\" 그리하여 목사를 올려보냈는데, 그는 쾌활하고 사람 좋은 신사였다. 듣자 하니 그는 우리가 이곳에서 우연히 만났으며, 내가 체스터 마차를 타고 왔고 내 신사는 나를 맞으러 자기 마차를 타고 왔으며, 어젯밤 스토니스트랫퍼드에서 만나기로 했으나 그가 거기까지 닿지 못했다는 이야기를 들었던 모양이다. \"허허, 선생.\" 목사가 말했다. \"모든 궂은일에는 좋은 구석이 있는 법이지요. 차질은 선생 몫이었으나,\" 그가 내 신사를 향해 말했다. \"그 덕은 제 차지가 되었으니, 만약 두 분이 스토니스트랫퍼드에서 만나셨더라면 제가 혼례를 주관하는 영광을 누리지 못했을 테니 말입니다. 주인장, 공동기도서 한 권 있소?\"\n\n나는 깜짝 놀란 듯이 움찔했다. \"어머, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"무슨 말씀이세요? 여관에서, 그것도 밤에 혼례를 올리다니요?\" \"부인.\" 목사가 말했다. \"교회에서 올리시겠다면 그리하셔도 됩니다. 허나 여기서 올리는 혼인도 교회에서 올리는 것만큼 굳건하다는 것을 장담하지요. 교회법이 우리더러 교회 밖에서는 혼인을 주관하지 말라고 묶어 둔 게 아닙니다. 교회에서 올리시겠다면, 그것은 마치 고을 장날처럼 떠들썩한 공개 행사가 될 것입니다. 시각으로 말하자면, 이 경우에는 전혀 문제 될 게 없습니다. 우리 군주들도 밤 여덟 시나 열 시에 침소에서 혼례를 올리니까요.\"\n\n나는 한참 동안 좀처럼 설득되지 않았고, 교회가 아니면 도무지 혼인할 마음이 없는 척했다. 그러나 그것은 죄다 시늉에 지나지 않았으니, 마침내 나는 못 이기는 척 넘어가는 듯했고, 주인 내외와 그 딸이 불려 올라왔다. 주인은 아버지 노릇이며 서기 노릇이며 죄다 한 몸으로 겸했고, 우리는 혼례를 올렸으며, 더없이 즐거웠다. 다만 고백하건대 전부터 나를 짓누르던 자책감이 바짝 들러붙어 이따금 깊은 한숨을 자아냈는데, 신랑은 그것을 알아채고 나를 다독이려 애쓰면서, 가엾게도 내가 그토록 성급하게 내디딘 걸음에 작은 망설임을 품고 있다고 여겼다.\n\n그날 저녁 우리는 더할 나위 없이 흡족하게 보냈고, 그러면서도 여관 안에서 모든 것이 어찌나 은밀하게 지켜졌던지 집 안의 하인 누구도 그 일을 알지 못했으니, 주인 아내와 그 딸이 나를 시중들면서 우리가 저녁을 드는 동안을 빼고는 하녀 누구도 위층에 올라오지 못하게 했기 때문이다. 나는 주인집 딸을 신부 들러리라 불렀고, 이튿날 아침 가게 주인을 불러 그 처녀에게 고을에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 매듭 장식 한 벌을 사 주었으며, 이곳이 레이스 만드는 고을임을 알고서 그 어미에게는 머리에 두를 본레이스 한 조각을 주었다.\n\n주인이 그토록 입을 다물었던 까닭 하나는, 교구 목사가 그 일을 듣지 않기를 바랐기 때문이다. 그러나 그런데도 누군가 그 소문을 들었던지, 이튿날 이른 아침 종을 울리게 했고, 고을에서 구할 수 있는 만큼의 악대가 우리 창 아래에서 연주를 했다. 그러나 주인은 뻔뻔스레 우리가 이곳에 오기 전에 이미 혼인한 사이이며, 다만 자기네 옛 손님인지라 혼례 만찬을 자기 집에서 든 것뿐이라고 둘러댔다.\n\n우리는 도저히 이튿날 떠날 마음이 나지 않았으니, 한마디로 아침에 종소리에 시달린 데다 아마 그 전에 잠을 그리 많이 못 잤던 터라, 그 뒤로 어찌나 졸렸던지 거의 정오까지 침대에 누워 있었다.\n\n나는 주인 아내에게 고을에 더 이상 음악도 종소리도 없게 해 달라고 청했고, 그녀가 일을 어찌나 잘 처리했던지 우리는 매우 조용히 지낼 수 있었다. 그러나 묘한 사건 하나가 한동안 내 즐거움을 송두리째 망쳐 놓았다. 집의 큰 방은 거리를 내려다보았는데, 새 남편이 아래층에 있던 차에 나는 방 끝까지 걸어가 보았고, 따뜻하고 화창한 날이라 창문을 열고 바람을 쐬려고 거기 서 있다가, 신사 셋이 말을 타고 지나와 우리 맞은편 여관으로 들어가는 것을 보았다.\n\n그것은 감출 수도 없었고, 의심하여 따져 볼 여지를 남길 만큼 모호하지도 않았으니, 그 셋 중 둘째가 바로 내 랭커셔 남편이었다. 나는 기절할 만큼 놀랐다. 평생 그런 경악에 빠져 본 적이 없었다. 땅속으로 꺼져 버리는 것만 같았고, 핏줄 속의 피가 싸늘하게 식었으며, 학질로 오한이 든 사람처럼 부들부들 떨렸다. 거듭 말하거니와 그 사실을 의심할 여지가 없었으니, 나는 그의 옷을 알아보았고, 그의 말을 알아보았으며, 그의 얼굴을 알아보았다.\n\n내가 맨 처음 떠올린 분별 있는 생각은, 남편이 곁에 없어 내가 흐트러진 꼴을 보지 못한다는 것이었고, 나는 그것이 무척 다행스러웠다. 신사들은 집에 든 지 얼마 되지 않아 으레 그러하듯 자기네 방 창가로 다가왔는데, 짐작하다시피 내 창문은 닫혀 있었다. 그래도 나는 그들을 훔쳐보지 않을 수 없었고, 거기서 나는 다시 그를 보았으며, 그가 집 하인 하나를 불러 무언가 필요한 것을 청하는 소리를 들었고, 그가 바로 그 사람이라는 두려운 확증을 얻을 수 있는 한 죄다 얻고야 말았다.\n\n그다음 내 관심사는 가능하다면 그가 그곳에 무슨 볼일로 왔는지를 아는 것이었으나, 그것은 불가능했다. 때로 내 상상은 어떤 무시무시한 일을 그려 냈다가, 때로는 또 다른 일을 그려 냈다. 때로 나는 그가 나를 알아채고 배은망덕과 신의 저버림을 나무라러 온 것이라고 생각했고, 시시각각 그가 나를 모욕하러 계단을 올라오는 것만 같았으며, 그의 머릿속에는 도무지 들어 있지도 않았고 들어 있을 수도 없는—악마가 그에게 일러 주지 않은 다음에야—온갖 망상이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다.\n\n나는 거의 두 시간 동안 이런 공포에 사로잡혀, 그들이 든 여관의 창문이나 문에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 못했다. 마침내 그들 여관의 통로에서 요란한 소리가 들리기에 나는 창가로 달려갔고, 더없이 흡족하게도 셋이 모두 다시 나와 서쪽으로 길을 떠나는 것을 보았다. 만약 그들이 런던 쪽으로 갔더라면, 나는 길에서 그를 다시 마주쳐 그가 나를 알아볼까 봐 여전히 공포에 떨었을 것이나, 그는 반대쪽으로 갔고, 그리하여 나는 그 흐트러짐에서 놓여났다.\n\n우리는 이튿날 떠나기로 했으나, 밤 여섯 시쯤 거리에서 일어난 엄청난 소란에 화들짝 놀랐으니, 사람들이 마치 정신 나간 사람들처럼 말을 몰고 다녔다. 알고 보니 그것은 던스터블 언덕 근처에서 마차 두 대와 다른 길손 몇몇을 턴 노상강도 셋을 쫓는 추적의 외침이었고, 듣자 하니 그들이 브릭힐의 어느 집에서 목격되었다는 제보가 있었다는데, 바로 그 신사들이 들었던 집을 가리킨 것이었다.\n\n그 집은 즉시 포위되어 수색당했으나, 그 신사들이 세 시간도 더 전에 떠났다는 증인이 충분했다. 군중이 몰려든 덕분에 우리는 곧 소식을 들었고, 나는 이제 또 다른 까닭으로 진심으로 마음이 쓰였다. 나는 곧장 집 사람들에게, 그들이 범인이 아닐 거라고 감히 말할 수 있다고, 왜냐하면 그 신사들 중 한 사람이 매우 정직한 사람이며 랭커셔에 좋은 재산을 지닌 이임을 알기 때문이라고 일렀다.\n\n추적의 외침을 따라온 치안관은 즉시 이 말을 전해 듣고, 내 입으로 직접 확인하고자 나에게로 건너왔다. 나는 그에게, 창가에 있을 때 신사 셋을 보았고, 그 뒤에 그들이 식사한 방의 창가에서도 보았으며, 그 뒤에 그들이 말에 오르는 것까지 보았다고, 그리고 그들 중 한 사람이 바로 그런 사람—매우 좋은 재산을 지닌 신사이며 랭커셔에서 의심할 바 없는 평판을 지닌 이로, 내가 바로 지금 그곳에서 여행해 오는 길이라고—임을 분명히 안다고 장담했다.\n\n내가 이 말을 전한 그 단호함이 폭도 무리에게 제동을 걸었고, 치안관에게도 큰 확신을 주었던지, 그는 즉시 후퇴 신호를 울려 사람들에게 이들은 그 범인이 아니며 도리어 매우 정직한 신사들이라는 정보를 얻었노라 일렀고, 그리하여 그들은 모두 다시 돌아갔다. 이 일의 진상이 어떠했는지는 알 수 없으나, 던스터블 언덕에서 마차들이 털리고 현금 560파운드를 빼앗긴 것만은 분명했으며, 그 밖에 늘 그 길로 다니는 레이스 상인들도 봉변을 당했다. 그 신사 셋에 관해서는 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 일로 남겨 둔다.\n\n그래, 이 소동 때문에 우리는 또 하루를 묶이고 말았으니, 내 남편은 떠나자는 쪽이었고, 강도 사건이 난 뒤에 여행하는 것이 늘 가장 안전하다고, 도둑들은 한 지방을 발칵 뒤집어 놓은 뒤에는 으레 멀리 달아나 버리기 마련이라고 내게 일렀다. 그러나 나는 겁이 나고 마음이 불안했으니, 사실 무엇보다 내 옛 정인이 아직 길에 있다가 어쩌다 나를 보게 될까 봐서였다.\n\n내 평생 그토록 즐거운 나흘을 연달아 보낸 적이 없었다. 이 내내 나는 영락없는 신부였고, 새 남편은 모든 면에서 나를 더없이 편안하게 해 주려 애썼다. 아, 이 삶의 형편이 그대로 이어질 수만 있었다면, 지난날의 온갖 곤경이 죄다 잊히고 앞날의 슬픔도 면할 수 있었으련만! 그러나 나에게는 가장 비참한 종류의 지난 삶이 있어 셈을 치러야 했으니, 그중 더러는 저세상에서뿐 아니라 이 세상에서도 마찬가지였다.\n\n우리는 닷새째 되는 날 떠났다. 주인은 내가 불안해하는 것을 보았던지 자기 자신과 아들, 그리고 좋은 총기를 갖춘 정직한 시골뜨기 셋을 말에 태우고, 우리에게는 알리지도 않은 채 마차를 뒤따라와 우리가 던스터블까지 무사히 가는 것을 지켜보았다. 우리로서는 던스터블에서 그들을 후하게 대접하지 않을 수 없었으니, 그것이 남편에게 한 열에서 열두 실링쯤 들었고, 사람들에게는 시간을 내준 값으로 얼마간 더 주었으나, 주인은 자기 몫으로는 아무것도 받으려 하지 않았다.\n\n이것은 나에게 일어날 수 있는 가장 다행스러운 방책이었으니, 만약 내가 혼인하지 않은 채 런던에 왔더라면, 첫날밤 묵을 곳을 그에게 청하든가, 아니면 런던 시내 전체에 신부가 남편과 함께 첫날밤 묵을 곳을 마련해 줄 만한 아는 사람이 단 하나도 없다는 것을 그에게 털어놓아야 했을 것이다. 그러나 이제 나는 결혼한 지 오래된 부인인지라 거리낌 없이 곧장 그와 함께 집으로 갔고, 거기서 단번에 잘 꾸며진 집과 매우 좋은 형편의 남편을 차지하게 되어, 꾸려 갈 줄만 안다면 매우 행복한 삶의 전망을 갖게 되었으며, 내가 살아가게 될 삶의 진정한 가치를 곰곰이 헤아릴 여유도 생겼다. 그 삶이 내가 전에 연기했던 방종하고 절제 없는 부분과 얼마나 달랐는지, 그리고 덕스럽고 절제 있는 삶이 우리가 쾌락의 삶이라 부르는 것보다 얼마나 더 행복한지를.\n\n아, 이 한 시절의 삶이 이어졌더라면, 혹은 내가 그것을 누리던 그때부터 그 참된 달콤함을 맛볼 줄 배웠더라면, 그리고 덕의 확실한 독약인 그 가난에 빠지지 않았더라면, 나는 여기서뿐 아니라 어쩌면 영원토록 얼마나 행복했을 것인가! 이렇게 사는 동안 나는 정말로 지난 삶 전체를 뉘우치는 참회자였으니까. 나는 그 삶을 혐오스럽게 돌아보았고, 그로 인해 진정 나 자신을 미워했다고 해도 좋을 것이다. 나는 바스에서의 내 정인이 신의 손에 일격을 맞고 뉘우쳐 나를 버렸으며, 나를 극진히 사랑했으면서도 더는 보려 하지 않았던 일을 자주 떠올렸다. 그러나 나는 그 악마들 가운데 가장 고약한 가난에 부추김을 받아 그 비열한 짓으로 되돌아갔고, 사람들이 곱다고 하는 얼굴을 밑천 삼아 내 궁핍을 메우고, 미모로 하여금 악덕의 포주 노릇을 하게 했던 것이다.\n\n이제 나는 지난 삶의 험한 항해가 끝나고 안전한 항구에 닿은 듯했고, 내가 건짐받은 것에 감사하기 시작했다. 나는 혼자서 여러 시간을 앉아, 지난날의 어리석음과 사악한 삶의 끔찍한 방탕을 떠올리며 울었고, 때로는 내가 진심으로 뉘우쳤다고 스스로를 다독였다.\n\n그러나 인간의 본성으로는 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 유혹이 있는 법이며, 똑같은 곤경에 내몰린다면 자기가 어찌 될지 아는 이는 드물다. 탐욕이 모든 악의 뿌리이듯이, 가난은 내가 믿건대 모든 덫 가운데 가장 고약한 것이다. 그러나 그 이야기는 한 실례를 들 데에 이를 때까지 미뤄 두겠다.\n\n나는 이 남편과 더없는 평온 속에서 살았다. 그는 조용하고 분별 있고 절제 있는 사람이었으며, 덕스럽고 겸손하고 진실했고, 자기 일에 부지런하고 공정했다. 그의 일은 규모가 크지 않았으나, 그 수입은 평범한 방식으로 넉넉하게 살아가기에 충분했다. 세상에서 일컫는 대로 마차를 거느리고 위세를 부리며 산다는 말은 아니며, 나 또한 그것을 기대하지도 바라지도 않았으니, 지난 삶의 경박함과 방탕을 혐오한 만큼 이제는 은둔하여 검소하게, 우리끼리만 살기를 택했기 때문이다. 나는 어울려 다니는 무리도 두지 않고 방문도 하지 않았으며, 집안을 살피고 남편을 받들었다. 이런 식의 삶이 나에게는 즐거움이 되었다.\n\n우리는 다섯 해 동안 끊김 없는 안락과 만족의 길을 걸었는데, 그때 거의 보이지도 않는 손이 가한 갑작스러운 일격이 내 모든 행복을 송두리째 날려 버리고, 나를 그 이전의 모든 것과는 정반대인 처지로 세상에 내던졌다.\n\n남편이 동료 서기 한 사람에게 우리 형편으로는 그 손실을 감당하기에 너무 큰 액수의 돈을 맡겼는데, 그 서기가 파산하는 바람에 그 손실이 남편에게 매우 무겁게 떨어졌다. 그래도 그것이 그토록 엄청난 것은 아니었으니, 만약 그가 자기 불운을 정면으로 마주할 기개와 용기가 있었더라면, 그의 신용은 매우 좋았으므로 내가 그에게 말했듯이 쉽게 회복할 수 있었을 것이다. 곤경에 짓눌려 가라앉는 것은 그 무게를 곱절로 만드는 일이며, 그 안에서 죽으려는 자는 그 안에서 죽고야 마는 법이니까.\n\n그에게 위로의 말을 건네는 것은 부질없는 일이었다. 상처가 너무 깊이 박혔던 것이다. 그것은 급소를 찌른 일격이었다. 그는 우울하고 의기소침해졌고, 거기서 무기력에 빠져 죽고 말았다. 나는 그 일격을 미리 내다보았고 마음이 극도로 짓눌렸으니, 그가 죽으면 내가 끝장이라는 것을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다.\n\n나는 그에게서 자식 둘을 보았을 뿐 더는 두지 않았으니, 사실을 말하자면 내가 아이 낳기를 그만둘 때가 되었던 까닭이다. 나는 이제 마흔여덟이었으니, 설령 그가 살았더라도 더는 낳지 못했으리라 짐작한다.\n\n나는 이제 정말로 음울하고 의기소침한 처지에, 여러 면에서 그 어느 때보다 못한 형편에 남겨졌다. 첫째로, 정부情婦로 구애받기를 기대할 만한 한창때가 나에게는 이미 지나가 버렸으니, 그 흡족한 부분은 얼마 전부터 기울었고 한때의 모습은 폐허만 남아 있었다. 그리고 이 모든 것보다 더 고약한 것은, 내가 살아 있는 가장 의기소침하고 낙담한 인간이라는 점이었다. 남편을 격려하고 그 곤경 속에서 그의 기운을 떠받치려 애썼던 내가, 정작 내 기운은 떠받치지 못했다. 곤경 속에서 그 짐을 견디는 데 그토록 긴요하다고 그에게 일렀던 그 기개가 나에게는 없었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 내 처지는 정말로 한심했으니, 나는 완전히 벗도 없고 도움도 없이 남겨졌고, 남편이 입은 손실로 그 형편이 어찌나 낮아졌던지, 비록 내가 빚을 진 것은 아니었으나, 남은 것으로는 나를 오래 부양하지 못하리라는 것을 쉽게 내다볼 수 있었다. 그것이 날마다 먹고사느라 줄어드는 동안, 나에게는 그것을 단 한 실링이라도 불릴 길이 없었으니, 머지않아 죄다 써 버릴 것이며, 그러면 내 앞에 보이는 것이라곤 더없이 극심한 곤궁뿐이었다. 그리고 이것이 내 생각 속에 어찌나 생생하게 떠올랐던지, 그것이 실제로는 그리 가깝지도 않은데 마치 벌써 닥쳐온 듯했다. 게다가 바로 그 불안이 비참함을 곱절로 만들었으니, 빵 한 덩이 값으로 치르는 6펜스마다 그것이 이 세상에서 내가 가진 마지막 돈이며, 내일이면 굶고 굶어 죽을 것만 같았다.\n\n이 곤경 속에서 나에게는 거들어 줄 이도, 위로하거나 충고해 줄 벗도 없었다. 나는 앉아서 밤낮으로 울며 스스로를 괴롭혔고, 두 손을 쥐어짜며 때로는 실성한 여자처럼 헛소리를 했다. 사실 그것이 내 이성을 망가뜨리지 않은 것이 종종 의아할 정도였으니, 우울증이 어찌나 심했던지 내 분별이 때로는 망상과 상상 속에 아주 빠져 버리곤 했기 때문이다.\n\n나는 이 음울한 형편 속에서 두 해를 살았으니, 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 축내면서 음울한 처지를 두고 끊임없이 울었고, 말하자면 하느님께서든 사람에게서든 도움의 희망이나 전망이라곤 조금도 없이 그저 피 흘리며 죽어 가고 있을 뿐이었다. 이제 나는 너무 오래, 너무 자주 울었던지라 눈물이 말하자면 바닥나 버렸고, 빠르게 가난해지는 통에 절망에 빠지기 시작했다.\n\n얼마간의 구제책으로 나는 집을 처분하고 셋방을 얻었다. 살림을 줄여 나가면서 살림살이도 대부분 팔아 치워 호주머니에 약간의 돈을 넣었고, 매우 아껴 쓰며 물건을 끝까지 늘려 가면서 거의 일 년을 그것으로 버텼다. 그러나 앞날을 내다볼 때면 비참함과 궁핍이 어쩔 수 없이 다가오는 통에 그저 가슴이 철렁 내려앉곤 했다. 아, 누구든 황량한 처지의 형편을, 그리고 벗도 빵도 없는 그 적나라한 궁핍과 어떻게 씨름하게 될지를 진지하게 헤아리지 않고서는 이 대목을 읽지 말기를. 그러면 그들은 분명 자기가 가진 것을 아낄 생각만 하는 것이 아니라, 떠받쳐 달라고 하늘을 우러러볼 것이며, 저 현자의 기도를 떠올릴 것이다. \"제게 가난을 주지 마옵소서. 도둑질을 할까 두렵나이다.\"\n\n곤경의 때는 무서운 유혹의 때이며, 저항할 모든 힘이 빼앗겨 버린다는 것을 그들이 기억하기를. 가난이 짓누르고, 영혼이 곤경에 절망하게 되니, 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?\n어느 날 저녁이었으니, 말하자면 마지막 숨에 이르도록 내몰려, 진정 나는 실성하여 헛소리를 했다고 해도 좋을 그때, 나는 무슨 영靈에 부추김을 받았는지, 말하자면 내가 무엇을 왜 하는지도 모른 채, 옷을 차려입고(나에게는 아직 꽤 좋은 옷이 있었으니) 밖으로 나갔다. 나갈 때 내 머릿속에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 전혀 없었다는 것만은 분명하다. 어디로 갈지, 무슨 볼일로 갈지 알지도 따지지도 않았다. 다만 악마가 나를 끌어내어 미끼를 놓았던지라, 그가 분명 나를 그 자리로 데려갔으니, 내가 어디로 가는지 무엇을 하는지 알지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n이렇게 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 떠돌다가 나는 레든홀 거리의 약종상 가게 앞을 지나게 되었는데, 계산대 바로 앞 걸상 위에 흰 천에 싸인 작은 보따리가 놓인 것이 보였다. 그 너머에는 하녀 하나가 등을 돌리고 서서 가게 위쪽을 바라보고 있었는데, 거기서는 짐작건대 약종상 견습공이 계산대 위에 올라서서 역시 문을 등진 채 손에 촛불을 들고, 필요한 무언가를 찾느라 맨 위 선반을 올려다보며 손을 뻗고 있었다. 그래서 두 사람 다 어찌나 골똘히 매여 있던지, 가게 안에 다른 사람은 아무도 없었다.\n\n이것이 미끼였다. 그리고 내가 덫을 놓았다고 말한 그 악마가, 마치 입으로 말하기라도 한 듯 선뜻 나를 부추겼으니, 내가 기억하기로—그리고 결코 잊지 못하리니—그것은 마치 내 어깨 너머로 들려오는 목소리 같았다. \"보따리를 집어라. 어서. 지금 당장 해치워라.\" 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 나는 가게 안으로 들어섰고, 마치 지나가는 짐수레를 피해 비켜선 듯이 그 계집애에게 등을 돌린 채 손을 뒤로 돌려 보따리를 집어 들고는 그것을 들고 빠져나왔으니, 하녀도 그 녀석도, 다른 누구도 나를 알아채지 못했다.\n\n그 일을 하는 내내 내 영혼의 공포가 어떠했는지는 이루 말할 수 없다. 빠져나올 때 나는 뛸 엄두도, 걸음을 빨리할 엄두도 거의 나지 않았다. 그래도 길을 건너 처음 나오는 길모퉁이로 접어들었는데, 그것은 펜처치 거리로 통하는 길이었던 것 같다. 거기서부터 나는 길을 건너고 어찌나 많은 길과 모퉁이를 돌았던지, 그것이 어느 길이었는지도, 내가 어디로 갔는지도 도무지 알 수 없었다. 디딘 땅도 느껴지지 않았고, 위험에서 멀어질수록 나는 더 빨리 걸어, 마침내 지치고 숨이 차서 어느 집 문간의 작은 벤치에 주저앉을 수밖에 없었다. 그제야 나는 정신을 차리기 시작했고, 빌링스게이트 근처 템스 거리에 와 있다는 것을 알았다. 나는 잠시 쉬었다가 다시 걸었다. 온몸의 피가 불타올랐고, 갑작스레 놀란 사람처럼 심장이 두근거렸다. 한마디로 나는 어찌나 경악에 빠져 있었던지, 여전히 어디로 가는지도, 무엇을 해야 할지도 알지 못했다.\n\n이렇게 한참을 빙 돌아 그토록 다급하게 걸어 지친 뒤에야, 나는 정신을 차리고 셋방으로 발길을 돌렸고, 밤 아홉 시쯤 거기에 닿았다.\n\n그 보따리가 언제 꾸려진 것인지, 무슨 일로 내가 발견한 그 자리에 놓였던 것인지는 알 수 없었으나, 풀어 보니 그 안에는 매우 좋고 거의 새것인 산모용 아마포 한 벌이 들어 있었고, 레이스가 매우 고왔다. 1파인트들이 은 죽그릇 하나, 작은 은잔 하나와 숟가락 여섯 개, 그 밖에 아마포 약간, 좋은 속옷 한 벌과 비단 손수건 세 장이 있었으며, 그 잔 안에는 종이에 싼 현금 18실링 6펜스가 들어 있었다.\n\n이 물건들을 풀어 보는 내내 나는 너무도 무서운 두려움에 사로잡혀, 비록 완전히 안전한데도 마음에 어찌나 큰 공포가 일었던지 그 모양을 이루 표현할 수가 없다. 나는 주저앉아 더없이 격렬하게 울었다. \"맙소사.\" 내가 말했다. \"이제 나는 무엇인가? 도둑이라니! 아이고, 다음번에는 붙들려 뉴게이트로 끌려가 목숨을 걸고 재판을 받겠지!\" 그러고 나서 다시 오래 울었으니, 분명 나는 그토록 가난했음에도 두려움 때문에 엄두만 났더라면 틀림없이 그 물건들을 도로 가져갔을 것이다. 그러나 그것도 한참 뒤에는 사그라졌다. 그래, 그날 밤 나는 잠자리에 들었으나 거의 잠을 이루지 못했다. 그 짓의 공포가 내 마음을 짓눌렀고, 나는 밤새도록, 그리고 이튿날 온종일 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 했는지 알지 못했다. 그러고 나서 나는 그 분실에 관한 소식을 듣고 싶어 안달이 났고, 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지, 그것이 가난한 사람의 물건인지 부자의 물건인지 몹시 알고 싶었다. \"어쩌면,\" 내가 말했다. \"그건 나 같은 어느 가난한 과부의 것인지도 몰라. 자기와 가엾은 자식의 빵 한 조각을 위해 이 물건들을 꾸려 팔러 가던 길에, 이제 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 잃고 그것 때문에 굶주리며 애간장을 태우고 있는지도 모르지.\" 그리고 이 생각이 서너 날 동안 나를 다른 무엇보다 더 괴롭혔다.\n\n그러나 나 자신의 곤경이 이 모든 성찰을 잠재웠고, 날마다 더 무서워지는 내 굶주림의 전망이 차츰차츰 내 마음을 굳게 만들었다. 그때 특히 내 마음을 무겁게 짓누른 것은, 내가 한때 거듭났고 바라건대 지난날의 온갖 사악함을 뉘우쳤으며, 여러 해 동안 절제 있고 진중하고 은둔하는 삶을 살았는데, 이제는 내 처지의 무서운 궁핍에 떠밀려 영혼과 육체가 다 함께 파멸의 문으로 내몰리게 되었다는 것이었다. 두세 번 나는 무릎을 꿇고 힘닿는 대로 하느님께 건져 주시기를 빌었으나, 고백건대 내 기도에는 아무런 희망이 담겨 있지 않았다. 나는 어찌해야 할지 알지 못했다. 밖은 온통 두려움이요, 안은 캄캄했다. 나는 지난 삶을 진심으로 뉘우치지 못한 것으로 돌아보았고, 이제 하늘이 무덤 이편에서 나를 벌하기 시작했으며, 내가 사악했던 만큼 나를 비참하게 만들려 하신다고 헤아렸다.\n\n여기서 멈추었더라면 나는 어쩌면 참된 참회자가 되었을 것이다. 그러나 내 안에는 사악한 조언자가 있어, 끊임없이 가장 고약한 수단으로 스스로를 구제하라고 부추겼다. 그리하여 어느 날 저녁 그는 \"저 보따리를 집어라\"고 했던 바로 그 사악한 충동으로 다시 나를 꾀어, 무슨 일이 닥치는지 다시 나가 찾아보게 했다.\n\n이번에 나는 대낮에 나가, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채, 무엇을 찾는지도 모른 채 떠돌았는데, 그때 악마가 내 길에 정말로 무서운 종류의 덫을, 전에도 그 뒤로도 결코 겪어 보지 못한 그런 덫을 놓았다. 올더스게이트 거리를 지나는데, 무용 학교에 갔다가 혼자서 집으로 가던 예쁜 어린아이 하나가 있었고, 내 부추기는 자가 영락없는 악마답게 나를 이 무고한 어린것에게 들이밀었다. 내가 말을 걸자 그것도 나에게 재잘거렸고, 나는 그 손을 잡고 바살러뮤클로스로 통하는 포장된 골목에 이를 때까지 데리고 가다가 그 안으로 이끌었다. 아이는 그 길이 집으로 가는 길이 아니라고 했다. 나는 말했다. \"맞단다, 얘야. 내가 집으로 가는 길을 알려 주마.\" 아이는 금구슬로 된 작은 목걸이를 하고 있었는데, 나는 거기에 눈독을 들이고 있었다. 골목의 어둠 속에서 나는 헐거워진 아이의 나막신을 고쳐 주는 척하며 몸을 숙여 그 목걸이를 벗겨 냈고, 아이는 그것을 전혀 느끼지 못했다. 그리하여 다시 아이를 데리고 갔다. 여기서, 거듭 말하거니와, 악마는 아이가 울지 못하도록 그 어두운 골목에서 죽여 버리라고 나를 부추겼으나, 그 생각만으로도 어찌나 겁이 났던지 나는 그 자리에 쓰러질 뻔했다. 그래서 나는 아이를 돌려세워 이쪽은 집으로 가는 길이 아니니 도로 돌아가라고 일렀다. 아이는 그러겠다고 했고, 나는 바살러뮤클로스로 빠져나와 세인트존 거리로 통하는 또 다른 길로 돌아 들어갔다. 그러고는 스미스필드로 건너가 칙 골목을 내려가 필드 골목을 거쳐 홀번 다리에 이르렀는데, 거기서 늘 그곳을 지나는 사람들의 무리에 섞이니 도무지 발각될 수가 없었다. 그리하여 나는 세상으로 나서는 두 번째 출격을 감행했다.\n\n이 노획물 생각이 첫 번째 일에 대한 생각을 죄다 몰아냈고, 내가 했던 성찰도 금세 닳아 없어졌다. 앞서 말했듯이 가난이 내 마음을 굳게 만들었고, 나 자신의 궁핍이 무엇에도 아랑곳하지 않게 했다. 이 마지막 일은 나에게 큰 거리낌을 남기지 않았으니, 그 가엾은 아이에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았던지라, 나는 그저 그 가엾은 어린양을 혼자 집에 가게 내버려 둔 부모의 부주의에 정당한 책망을 내린 것뿐이며, 그것이 다음번에는 아이를 더 잘 돌보도록 그들을 가르치리라고 혼자 되뇌었다.\n\n이 구슬 줄은 한 열두 파운드에서 열네 파운드쯤 나가는 것이었다. 짐작건대 그것은 본디 어미의 것이었을 텐데, 아이가 차기에는 너무 컸으니, 아마 무용 학교에서 제 아이를 곱게 보이려는 어미의 허영이 아이에게 그것을 차게 했을 것이다. 분명 아이에게는 돌보라고 딸려 보낸 하녀가 있었을 테지만, 그 칠칠치 못한 계집은 어쩌면 길에서 만난 어떤 사내에게 정신이 팔려 있었을 것이고, 그래서 그 가엾은 아기가 떠돌다가 내 손에 떨어진 것이다.\n\n그래도 나는 아이에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았다. 겁조차 주지 않았으니, 나에게는 아직 다정한 마음이 많이 남아 있었고, 말하자면 순전한 궁핍이 나를 몰아붙인 것 외에는 아무것도 하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이 일이 있은 뒤로 나는 숱한 모험을 겪었으나, 이 일에 아직 풋내기였던지라 악마가 내 머릿속에 일을 집어넣어 주는 것 외에는 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알지 못했다. 사실 그는 좀처럼 나에게 게으르지 않았다. 한번은 나에게 매우 운 좋은 모험이 있었다. 어스름한 저녁녘에 롬바드 거리를 지나 스리킹스 마당 끄트머리 바로 옆을 지나는데, 별안간 한 사내가 번개처럼 잽싸게 내 곁을 달려 지나가며, 골목으로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이 집 벽에 기대어 선 내 바로 뒤에 손에 쥐고 있던 보따리 하나를 던졌다. 그것을 던져 넣으면서 그는 \"복 받으시오, 아주머니. 잠시만 거기 그대로 두시오\"라고 말하고는 바람처럼 잽싸게 달아났다. 그 뒤로 둘이 더 쫓아왔고, 곧이어 모자도 안 쓴 젊은 사내가 \"도둑 잡아라!\" 외치며 따라왔으며, 그 뒤로 두셋이 더 따라왔다. 그들은 뒤의 두 녀석을 어찌나 바짝 쫓았던지, 그 둘은 손에 넣었던 것을 떨굴 수밖에 없었고, 그중 하나는 덤으로 붙잡혔으며, 다른 하나는 무사히 달아났다.\n\n나는 이 내내 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 가만히 서 있었는데, 마침내 그들이 붙잡은 가엾은 녀석을 끌고, 찾아낸 물건들을 질질 끌면서, 노획물을 되찾고 도둑을 잡은 것에 더없이 흡족해하며 돌아왔다. 그리하여 그들은 내 곁을 지나갔으니, 나는 군중이 지나가는 동안 그저 비켜선 사람으로만 보였기 때문이다.\n\n한두 번 나는 무슨 일이냐고 물었으나 사람들은 내 물음에 답하지 않았고, 나도 그리 끈질기게 묻지 않았다. 그러나 군중이 완전히 지나간 뒤에 나는 기회를 잡아 몸을 돌려 내 뒤에 있던 것을 집어 들고 걸어가 버렸다. 이번에는 정말로 전보다 덜 동요하며 했으니, 이 물건들은 내가 훔친 것이 아니라 내 손에 훔쳐다 바쳐진 것이었기 때문이다. 나는 이 짐을 들고 무사히 셋방에 닿았는데, 그것은 고운 검정 광택 명주 한 필과 우단 한 필이었다. 후자는 한 열한 야드쯤 되는 한 필의 일부였고, 전자는 쉰 야드 가까이 되는 온전한 한 필이었다. 듣자 하니 그들이 턴 곳은 어느 포목상 가게였던 모양이다. 턴 곳이라 말하는 까닭은, 그들이 잃은 물건이 어찌나 상당했던지, 되찾은 물건만도 꽤 많아서 내 생각에 명주 여섯이나 일곱 필쯤 되었기 때문이다. 그들이 어떻게 그토록 많이 손에 넣었는지는 알 수 없으나, 나는 도둑을 털었을 뿐이므로 이 물건들을 취하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었고, 도리어 매우 기뻐했다.\n\n여기까지 나는 꽤 운이 좋았고, 비록 얻는 것은 적었으나 좋은 성과를 거두며 몇 번 더 모험을 했다. 그러나 나는 무슨 화가 닥쳐 끝내는 틀림없이 교수형을 당하게 되리라는 두려움 속에 날마다 살았다. 이것이 내게 남긴 인상은 너무 강해서 가벼이 넘길 수가 없었으니, 그것이 어쩌면 매우 안전하게 해치울 수 있었을지도 모를 시도들을 못 하게 막았다. 그러나 한 가지는 빼놓을 수 없으니, 그것은 여러 날 동안 나에게 미끼가 되었던 일이다. 나는 마을 둘레의 촌락들로 자주 걸어 나가, 거기서 무언가 내 손에 떨어질 것이 없는지 살피곤 했다. 스테프니 근처의 어느 집을 지나는데, 창문 받침대 위에 반지 두 개가 놓인 것이 보였다. 하나는 작은 다이아몬드 반지였고, 다른 하나는 금반지였는데, 분명 신중함보다 돈이 더 많은 어느 분별없는 부인이, 아마 손을 씻을 동안만 거기에 놓아둔 것이었다.\n\n나는 그 방에 누가 있는지 없는지 알 수 있을까 하여 창문 옆을 여러 번 걸어 보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았고, 그래도 확신할 수가 없었다. 곧 유리를 두드려 누군가와 이야기하고 싶은 척하면 되겠다는 생각이 떠올랐으니, 만약 누가 있다면 분명 창가로 나올 테고, 그러면 내가 수상쩍은 사내 둘이 그 반지를 눈여겨보더라고 일러 주며 치우라고 하면 될 일이었다. 이것은 척척 떠오른 생각이었다. 한두 번 두드렸으나 아무도 나오지 않았고, 주위에 인기척이 없는 것을 보고 나는 유리창을 향해 세게 밀어 아주 작은 소리만 내며 깨뜨려, 반지 두 개를 꺼내 들고 매우 무사히 걸어가 버렸다. 다이아몬드 반지는 한 3파운드쯤 나갔고, 다른 하나는 한 9실링쯤 나갔다.\n\n이제 나는 내 물건들을 처분할 시장이, 특히 명주 두 필을 처분할 데가 막막했다. 가엾고 불행한 도둑들이 흔히 그러하듯—목숨을 걸고 어쩌면 값나가는 것을 손에 넣고도 결국 헐값에 팔아넘기듯—나는 그것들을 푼돈에 처분하기가 몹시 싫었다. 나는 마지막 막다른 지경에 내몰리지 않는 한, 무슨 변통을 하든 그렇게는 하지 않으리라 작정했다. 그러나 어떤 길을 잡아야 할지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다. 마침내 나는 옛 후견인에게 가서 다시 인사를 나누기로 했다. 나는 형편이 닿는 한 어린 아들을 위해 그녀에게 해마다 5파운드를 어김없이 보냈으나, 끝내 그것을 멈출 수밖에 없었다. 그래도 나는 그녀에게 편지를 써서, 내 형편이 매우 낮아졌으며, 남편을 잃었고, 더는 그것을 댈 수 없게 되었으니, 그 가엾은 아이가 제 어미의 불운으로 너무 큰 고생을 하지 않게 해 달라고 청해 두었다.\n\n이제 나는 그녀를 찾아갔고, 그녀가 여전히 옛 장사 비슷한 것을 하고 있으나 예전처럼 번창하는 형편은 아니라는 것을 알았다. 어느 신사가 딸을 도둑맞았는데, 듣자 하니 그녀가 그 아이를 빼돌리는 것을 거든 모양이라 그 신사에게 고소를 당했고, 가까스로 교수대를 면했던 것이다. 그 비용 또한 그녀를 거덜 내어 그녀는 매우 가난해졌고, 집은 보잘것없이 꾸려져 있었으며, 그 솜씨로 예전 같은 평판도 얻지 못했다. 그래도 그녀는 사람들 말마따나 제 두 발로 버티고 서 있었으니, 부산스럽고 분주한 여자인 데다 밑천이 얼마간 남아 있어 전당포 주인으로 돌아서서 꽤 잘살고 있었다.\n\n그녀는 나를 매우 정중하게 맞이했고, 늘 그렇듯 친절한 태도로, 내가 형편이 기울었다고 해서 나를 덜 존중하지는 않겠다고 말했다. 또 내가 비용을 댈 수 없는데도 내 아들이 매우 잘 보살핌받도록 보살펴 두었으며, 그 아이를 맡은 여자가 마음 편히 지내고 있으니, 내가 그것을 제대로 댈 수 있을 만큼 형편이 나아질 때까지는 그 아이 일로 마음 쓸 필요가 없다고 했다.\n\n나는 그녀에게 돈은 별로 남지 않았으나, 돈만큼 값나가는 물건이 얼마 있는데, 그것을 어떻게 돈으로 바꿀 수 있는지 일러 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 내가 무엇을 가졌느냐고 물었다. 나는 그 금구슬 줄을 꺼내 그것이 남편이 내게 준 선물 가운데 하나라고 말했다. 그러고 나서 명주 두 꾸러미를 보여 주며 그것은 아일랜드에서 얻어 마을로 가져온 것이라고 말했고, 작은 다이아몬드 반지도 보여 주었다. 작은 은붙이와 숟가락 꾸러미는 전에 내가 직접 처분할 방도를 찾아냈고, 산모용 아마포는 그녀가 내 것인 줄 믿고 자기가 받아 가겠노라 제안했다. 그녀는 자기가 전당포 주인으로 돌아섰으니 그 물건들을 자기에게 저당 잡힌 셈으로 해서 팔아 주겠노라 했다. 그리하여 그녀는 곧 적당한 중개인들을 불렀고, 그들은 그녀 손에 들어온 물건인지라 아무 거리낌 없이 그것들을 사 갔으며, 값도 후하게 쳐 주었다.\n\n이제 나는 이 요긴한 여자가 내 낮은 처지에 무슨 벌이라도 조금 거들어 줄 수 있지 않을까 생각하기 시작했으니, 얻을 수만 있다면 어떤 정직한 일에라도 기꺼이 손을 댈 마음이었기 때문이다. 그러나 여기서 그녀는 부족했다. 정직한 일은 그녀의 손이 닿는 데 있지 않았다. 내가 더 젊었더라면 어쩌면 그녀가 한량 하나를 붙여 주었을지도 모르나, 내 생각은 그런 식의 생계에서 떠나 있었으니, 내 경우인 쉰을 넘긴 뒤에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 길이었다. 나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 무슨 할 일을 찾을 때까지 자기 집에 와서 지내라고, 그래도 비용은 거의 들지 않을 거라고 청했고, 나는 이것을 기꺼이 받아들였다. 이제 형편이 조금 편해진 나는 마지막 남편에게서 본 어린 아들을 떠맡길 방도를 강구하기 시작했고, 이 일 또한 그녀가 수월하게 만들어 주었으니, 내가 댈 수 있다면 해마다 5파운드만 치르는 것으로 정해 주었다. 이것이 나에게 어찌나 큰 도움이 되었던지, 한동안 나는 그토록 막 손댔던 그 사악한 장사를 그만두었다. 일감을 구할 수만 있었다면 나는 기꺼이 바늘 솜씨로 빵을 벌었겠으나, 세상에 아무런 연줄도 없는 사람에게는 그것이 몹시 어려운 일이었다.\n\n그래도 마침내 나는 부인들의 침대보며 페티코트며 그런 것들을 누비는 일감을 좀 얻었고, 이것이 나는 매우 마음에 들어 무척 열심히 일했으며, 이것으로 나는 살아가기 시작했다. 그러나 내가 자기 일에 계속 머물기를 작정한 그 부지런한 악마는 끊임없이 나를 부추겨 밖에 나가 한 바퀴 산책을 하라고, 다시 말해 옛 방식대로 무언가 손에 들어올 것이 없는지 살펴보라고 했다.\n\n어느 날 저녁 나는 분별없이 그 부름에 따라 길을 따라 멀리 한 바퀴 돌았으나 아무 소득이 없었고, 매우 지치고 빈손으로 집에 돌아왔다. 그래도 그것으로 성에 차지 않아 이튿날 저녁에도 나갔는데, 어느 맥줏집을 지나는데 거리 바로 옆 작은 방의 문이 열려 있고, 탁자 위에 은 큰 잔 하나가 놓인 것이 보였으니, 그 시절 술집에서 많이 쓰던 물건이었다. 듣자 하니 어떤 패거리가 거기서 술을 마셨던 모양인데, 부주의한 사환 녀석들이 그것을 치우는 것을 잊었던 것이다.\n\n나는 거리낌 없이 그 칸막이 자리로 들어가 은 큰 잔을 의자 모퉁이에 올려놓고 그 앞에 앉아 발로 바닥을 굴렀다. 곧 사환 하나가 왔고, 나는 추운 날씨이니 따뜻한 맥주 한 파인트를 가져오라고 일렀다. 사환이 달려갔고, 나는 그가 맥주를 따르러 지하실로 내려가는 소리를 들었다. 그 녀석이 간 동안 다른 사환 하나가 방으로 들어와 \"부르셨어요?\" 하고 소리쳤다. 나는 침울한 기색으로 말했다. \"아니란다, 얘야. 그 애가 내게 맥주 한 파인트를 가지러 갔단다.\"\n\n이렇게 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 술청에 있던 여자가 \"5번 자리 손님들 다 갔니?\" 하고 묻는 소리를 들었는데, 그것이 바로 내가 앉은 칸이었고, 사환은 \"예\" 하고 답했다. \"그 큰 잔은 누가 치웠지?\" 여자가 물었다. \"제가요.\" 다른 사환이 말했다. \"저거예요.\" 짐작건대 그 녀석이 다른 칸에서 잘못 가져온 또 다른 큰 잔을 가리킨 것이었다. 아니면, 그 녀석이 그것을 들여오지 않았으면서도 들여온 줄로 잊어버린 것이 분명했으니, 사실 그는 분명 그러지 않았던 것이다.\n\n나는 이 모든 것을 매우 흡족하게 들었으니, 그 큰 잔이 없어졌다고 여겨지지 않으면서도 치워졌다고 결론지어진 것이 분명히 드러났기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 맥주를 마시고 셈을 청했으며, 나가면서 \"은붙이 잘 챙기렴, 얘야\" 하고 말했으니, 그가 내게 술을 가져다준 1파인트들이 은잔을 두고 한 말이었다. 사환은 \"예, 부인. 또 오십시오\" 하고 말했고, 나는 그곳을 빠져나왔다.\n\n나는 후견인에게 돌아왔고, 이제 그녀를 시험해 볼 때라고 생각했으니, 혹시 내가 드러나야 할 궁지에 몰릴 경우 그녀가 내게 무슨 도움을 줄 수 있을까 해서였다. 집에 돌아와 얼마간 지내다가 그녀와 이야기할 기회가 생겼을 때, 나는 그녀에게 세상에서 더없이 중대한 비밀 하나를 맡기고 싶은데, 만약 그녀가 그것을 비밀로 지켜 줄 만큼 나를 존중한다면 그러겠다고 말했다. 그녀는 자기가 내 비밀 하나를 충실히 지켜 주지 않았느냐고, 그런데 내가 어찌 또 하나를 지킬 것을 의심하느냐고 말했다. 나는 세상에서 가장 기이한 일이 나에게 닥쳤으며, 그것이 아무 꿍꿍이도 없이 나를 도둑으로 만들었다고 말하고는, 그 큰 잔에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들려주었다. \"그래서 그걸 들고 왔단 말이냐, 얘야?\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그럼요, 가져왔지요.\" 내가 말하며 그것을 보여 주었다. \"그런데 이제 어쩌죠?\" 내가 말했다. \"도로 가져다주면 안 될까요?\"\n\n\"도로 가져다줘!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그래, 그걸 훔친 죄로 뉴게이트에 끌려가고 싶다면 그러려무나.\" \"아니,\" 내가 말했다. \"그걸 도로 가져다주는데 설마 나를 붙잡을 만큼 비열하지는 않겠지요?\" \"넌 그런 부류의 사람들을 모르는구나, 얘야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그자들은 너를 뉴게이트로 끌고 갈 뿐 아니라, 도로 돌려준 정직함 따위는 거들떠보지도 않고 교수형에 처할 거다. 아니면 자기네가 잃어버린 다른 잔들을 죄다 셈에 올려 너더러 물어내라고 할걸.\" \"그럼 어쩌면 좋아요?\" 내가 말했다. \"에이,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"교활한 짓을 부려 훔쳤으니, 차라리 그냥 가지고 있으려무나. 이제 와서 물릴 수도 없는 노릇이다. 게다가, 얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"네가 그자들보다 그게 더 필요하지 않으냐? 일주일에 한 번씩 그런 횡재를 만났으면 좋겠구나.\"\n\n이 일로 나는 내 후견인에 대해 새로운 생각을 품게 되었으니, 그녀가 전당포 주인으로 돌아선 뒤로 내가 전에 거기서 만났던 정직한 부류와는 거리가 먼 그런 사람들을 둘레에 두고 있다는 것이었다.\n\n거기 머문 지 얼마 되지 않아 나는 그것을 전보다 더 또렷이 알게 되었으니, 이따금 칼자루며 숟가락이며 포크며 큰 잔이며 그런 온갖 물건이 저당 잡히려는 것이 아니라 아예 팔리려고 들어오는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 들어오는 것은 무엇이든 아무것도 묻지 않고 사들였으나, 그녀의 말로 미루어 짐작건대 매우 좋은 값에 사들였다.\n\n또한 나는 그녀가 이 장사를 하면서 사들인 은붙이를 항상 녹여 버린다는 것을 알았으니, 임자가 나서서 따지지 못하게 하려는 것이었다. 어느 날 아침 그녀는 나에게 와서 녹이려는 참인데, 원한다면 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않도록 내 큰 잔도 넣어 주겠노라 했다. 나는 진심으로 그러시라고 말했고, 그녀는 그것의 무게를 달아 그 값을 다시 은으로 온전히 쳐 주었다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 다른 손님들에게는 그렇게 해 주지 않는다는 것을 알았다.\n\n이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 내가 일을 하며 매우 침울해하고 있는데 그녀가 늘 그러듯 무슨 일이냐고 묻기 시작했다. 나는 마음이 무겁다고, 일감도 적고 먹고살 것도 없으며 어떤 길을 잡아야 할지 모르겠다고 말했다. 그녀는 웃으며, 다시 밖에 나가 운수를 시험해 보아야 한다고, 어쩌면 또 다른 은붙이를 만날지도 모르지 않느냐고 말했다. \"어머, 어머니!\" 내가 말했다. \"그건 제가 솜씨가 없는 장사인 데다, 붙잡히기라도 하면 단번에 끝장이에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"내가 너를 자기만큼 능숙하게 만들어 줄 여선생 하나를 붙여 줄 수 있단다.\" 나는 그 제안에 몸서리를 쳤으니, 그때까지 나에게는 한패도 없었고 그 부류 가운데 아는 사람도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그녀는 내 부끄러움도, 내 두려움도 죄다 정복해 버렸다. 그리하여 머지않아 이 한패의 도움으로 나는 몰 컷퍼스 못지않게 뻔뻔한 도둑이자 그 못지않게 능숙한 도둑이 되었으니, 다만 소문이 그녀를 헐뜯는 게 아니라면 그 절반만큼도 곱지는 못했다.\n\n* 몰 컷퍼스: 17세기 런던에서 악명 높았던 여성 소매치기이자 장물아비 메리 프리스의 별명.\n그녀가 붙여 준 동무는 세 가지 종류의 기술을 부렸으니, 곧 가게에서 물건 슬쩍하기, 가게 장부와 수첩 훔치기, 그리고 부인들의 옆구리에서 금시계 빼내기였다. 이 마지막 것을 그녀는 어찌나 능숙하게 해냈던지, 어떤 여자도 그 솜씨에 이르러 그녀처럼 해낸 적이 없었다. 나는 이 가운데 첫째와 마지막 것이 매우 마음에 들었고, 마치 부산파가 산파를 따라다니듯 보수도 없이 한동안 그녀를 따라다니며 실습을 했다.\n\n마침내 그녀가 나를 실전에 내보냈다. 그녀가 자기 기술을 보여 준 터라, 나는 여러 번 그녀 자신의 옆구리에서 시계를 매우 능숙하게 풀어낸 적이 있었다. 마침내 그녀가 나에게 한 표적을 보여 주었으니, 그것은 매혹적인 시계를 찬, 만삭의 젊은 부인이었다. 그 일은 그녀가 교회에서 나올 때 해치워야 했다. 그녀는 그 부인의 한쪽 옆으로 다가가, 부인이 막 계단에 이르렀을 때 넘어지는 척하며 어찌나 세차게 부인에게 부딪쳤던지 부인을 크게 놀라게 했고, 둘 다 무섭게 비명을 질렀다. 그녀가 부인을 떠밀친 바로 그 순간, 나는 시계를 붙들었고, 그것을 올바른 방향으로 잡고 있던 터라 부인이 흠칫하는 통에 고리가 빠졌으며, 부인은 그것을 전혀 느끼지 못했다. 나는 즉시 그 자리를 떴고, 내 여선생은 천천히 그 꾸며 낸 놀람에서 헤어나도록 내버려 두었으며, 부인도 마찬가지였다. 이윽고 시계가 없어진 것이 드러났다. \"아이고,\" 내 동무가 말했다. \"그렇다면 분명 나를 떠민 그 도둑놈들이었어요. 부인께서 진작 시계가 없어진 걸 알아채지 못하신 게 안타깝네요. 그랬으면 그놈들을 잡을 수도 있었을 텐데요.\"\n\n그녀가 그 일을 어찌나 잘 둘러댔던지 아무도 그녀를 의심하지 않았고, 나는 그녀보다 꼬박 한 시간 먼저 집에 와 있었다. 이것이 패거리와 함께한 내 첫 모험이었다. 그 시계는 정말로 매우 고운 것이었고, 거기에 자질구레한 장식이 많이 달려 있었는데, 내 후견인은 그것의 값으로 우리에게 20파운드를 쳐 주었고, 그중 절반을 내가 가졌다. 그리하여 나는 양심이나 부끄러움의 온갖 성찰을 죄다 넘어설 만큼 굳어진, 그것도 나 스스로 결코 나에게 가능하리라 생각해 본 적 없는 정도로 굳어진, 완벽한 도둑으로 입문했다.\n\n이리하여, 거역할 수 없는 가난의 도움으로 나를 이 사악함에 밀어 넣기 시작했던 악마는, 정작 내 궁핍이 그리 심하지도 않고 비참함의 전망도 그리 무섭지 않게 되었을 때조차, 나를 보통의 도를 넘어선 높이로까지 끌고 갔다. 이제 나는 일감의 작은 길에 접어들었고, 바늘을 다루는 데 막힘이 없던 터라, 연줄이 들어옴에 따라 충분히 정직하게 빵을 벌 수 있었을 법한데도 말이다.\n\n말하지 않을 수 없는 것은, 만약 처음에, 내 비참한 처지가 다가오는 것을 느끼기 시작했을 때, 그런 일감의 전망이 나타났더라면—다시 말해 일해서 빵을 버는 그런 전망이 그때 나타났더라면—나는 결코 이 사악한 장사에, 혹은 지금 내가 발을 들인 그런 사악한 패거리에 빠지지 않았으리라는 것이다. 그러나 실습이 나를 굳게 만들었고, 나는 마지막 한도까지 대담해졌다. 더더욱 그러했던 까닭은, 내가 그것을 그토록 오래 해 오면서 한 번도 붙잡힌 적이 없었기 때문이다. 한마디로, 사악함의 새 동업자와 나는 한 번도 발각되지 않고 그토록 오래 함께해 나갔던지라, 우리는 대담해졌을 뿐 아니라 부유해졌으니, 한때 우리 손에 금시계 스물한 개를 쥐고 있기도 했다.\n\n기억하건대 어느 날 나는 여느 때보다 조금 진지해져서, 내가 그토록 좋은 밑천을 미리 쟁여 두었음을 깨닫고—내 몫으로 현금이 거의 200파운드나 있었으니—이런 생각이 강하게 떠올랐다. 의심할 바 없이 어떤 선한 영靈에게서 왔을 것이니, 만약 그런 것이 있다면 말이다. 곧 처음에는 가난이 나를 부추겼고, 내 곤경이 나를 이 무서운 변통으로 내몰았으나, 이제 그 곤경이 가셨고, 또 일해서 생계에 보탤 것도 얻을 수 있으며, 나를 떠받쳐 줄 그토록 좋은 밑천도 있으니, 어찌하여 사람들 말마따나 형편이 좋을 때 그만두지 않겠는가? 늘 자유로이 다닐 것을 기대할 수는 없으며, 한번 들켜 일을 그르치면 나는 끝장이라는 생각이었다.\n\n이것은 의심할 바 없이 그 다행스러운 순간이었으니, 만약 내가 그 복된 암시에—그것이 어디서 왔든—귀를 기울였더라면, 나에게는 아직 편안한 삶을 향한 한 번의 기회가 있었다. 그러나 내 운명은 달리 정해져 있었다. 그토록 부지런히 나를 끌어들인 그 분주한 악마는 나를 너무 단단히 붙들고 있어 돌아가게 놓아주지 않았다. 가난이 나를 진창에 빠뜨렸듯이, 탐욕이 거기에 나를 붙들어, 끝내 돌아갈 길이 없게 되었다. 그만 손을 떼라고 내 이성이 일러 준 논거들에 대해서는, 탐욕이 끼어들어 이렇게 말했다. \"계속해, 계속해. 넌 운이 아주 좋았어. 사오백 파운드를 손에 넣을 때까지 계속하다가, 그러고 나서 그만두면, 그때는 아예 일하지 않고도 편안히 살 수 있어.\"\n\n이리하여, 한때 악마의 손아귀에 든 나는 마치 주문에 걸린 듯 거기에 단단히 붙들려, 그 원 밖으로 나갈 힘이 없었고, 마침내 도무지 빠져나올 수 없을 만큼 거대한 곤경의 미궁에 휩싸이고 말았다.\n\n그래도 이 생각들은 나에게 얼마간 인상을 남겨, 전보다 좀 더 조심스럽게, 또 내 길잡이들이 자기들끼리 부리는 것보다 더 조심스럽게 처신하게 만들었다. 내가 동무라고 불렀으나 차라리 내 선생이라 불렀어야 할 그 여자가, 그녀의 다른 제자 하나와 더불어 맨 먼저 불운을 맞았다. 노획물을 사냥하러 나섰다가, 그들이 칩사이드의 어느 포목상에게 시도를 했는데, 매의 눈을 가진 점원에게 덜미를 잡혀, 그들에게서 함께 압수된 케임브릭 천 두 필과 함께 붙들렸기 때문이다.\n\n이것만으로도 둘 다 뉴게이트에 갇히기에 충분했고, 거기서 그들은 지난날의 죄 가운데 몇 가지가 다시 들춰지는 불운을 겪었다. 그들에 대해 다른 두 건의 기소가 제기되었고, 그 사실이 입증되어 둘 다 사형을 선고받았다. 둘 다 임신을 사유로 호소했고, 둘 다 만삭으로 판정받았으나, 사실 내 여선생은 나만큼이나 임신한 상태가 아니었다.\n\n나는 그들을 보러, 그리고 그들을 위로하러 자주 갔으니, 다음번이 내 차례가 되리라 예상했기 때문이다. 그러나 그곳은 나에게 너무도 큰 공포를 주었으니, 그곳이 내 불운한 출생의 자리요 내 어머니의 불운의 자리임을 떠올리면 도무지 견딜 수가 없어, 나는 그들을 보러 가는 것을 그만둘 수밖에 없었다.\n\n아, 그들의 재난에서 경계를 받아들일 수만 있었더라면, 나는 여전히 행복했을 것이다. 나는 아직 자유로웠고, 나에게 제기된 죄목도 없었으니까. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 내 분량이 아직 다 차지 않았던 것이다.\n\n내 동무는 상습범의 낙인이 찍혀 처형되었고, 어린 범죄자는 사형 집행 유예를 얻어 목숨을 건졌으나, 오래도록 옥에서 굶주리며 누워 있다가 마침내 사람들이 순회사면이라 부르는 것에 이름을 올려 빠져나왔다.\n\n내 동무의 이 끔찍한 본보기는 나를 진심으로 겁먹게 했고, 한동안 나는 출격을 하지 않았다. 그러나 어느 날 밤, 내 후견인 집 근처에서 사람들이 \"불이야\" 하고 외쳤다. 우리는 모두 깨어 있던 터라 내 후견인이 내다보고는, 곧 어느 부인의 집 꼭대기가 온통 불길에 휩싸였다고 외쳤는데, 정말로 그러했다. 여기서 그녀가 나에게 한 가지 일거리를 주었다. \"자, 얘야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"둘도 없는 기회다. 불이 어찌나 가까운지, 거리가 군중으로 막히기 전에 거기 갈 수 있겠어.\" 그녀는 곧 내게 할 말을 일러 주었다. \"가거라, 얘야.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그 집으로 가서, 뛰어 들어가 부인이나 보이는 누구에게든, 거들러 왔다고, 아무개 부인이 보내서 왔다고 말하렴.\" 곧 거리 위쪽에 사는 그녀의 아는 사람 하나를 가리킨 것이었다. 그녀는 옆집에도 똑같은 말을 일러 주며, 그 집 부인이 또한 아는 사람인 다른 이름 하나를 댔다.\n\n나는 떠났고, 그 집에 이르니 짐작하다시피 모두가 아수라장이었다. 나는 뛰어 들어가 하녀 하나를 붙잡고 말했다. \"어머나! 얘야,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 끔찍한 변고가 어쩌다 났니? 마님은 어디 계셔? 어떠셔? 무사하셔? 아이들은 어디 있고? 나는 아무개 마님이 보내서 너희를 도우러 왔단다.\" 하녀가 달려갔다. \"마님, 마님,\" 그녀가 있는 힘껏 비명을 지르며 외쳤다. \"여기 아무개 마님이 보내서 우리를 도우러 온 부인이 계세요.\" 정신이 반쯤 나간 가엾은 여자가 팔에 보따리 하나를 끼고 어린아이 둘을 데리고 내게로 다가왔다. \"어머나! 부인,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 가엾은 아이들을 제가 아무개 마님께 데려다드릴게요. 마님이 아이들을 보내 달라 하셨어요. 가엾은 어린양들을 돌봐 주시겠대요.\" 그러고는 곧 내가 아이 하나를 그녀 손에서 받아 들자, 그녀는 다른 아이를 들어 내 품에 안겼다. \"그래요, 제발 그래 주세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아이들을 마님께 데려다주세요. 아! 친절을 베풀어 주셔서 고맙다고 전해 주세요.\" \"또 챙겨야 할 게 있나요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다. \"마님이 돌봐 주실 거예요.\" \"아이고! 그래요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"마님께 복이 있기를, 고맙기도 하셔라. 이 은붙이 보따리도 가져가서 마님께 전해 주세요. 아, 정말 좋은 분이셔. 아이고 맙소사! 우리는 아주 망했어요, 아주 끝장이라고요!\" 그러고는 정신이 나간 채 내게서 달아났고, 하녀들도 그 뒤를 따랐으며, 나는 두 아이와 보따리를 안고 그곳을 빠져나왔다.\n\n거리로 나서자마자 또 다른 여자가 내게로 다가오는 것이 보였다. \"어머!\" 그녀가 가엾어하는 어조로 말했다. \"아주머니, 아이를 떨어뜨리시겠어요. 자, 참 딱한 때로군요. 제가 거들어 드릴게요.\" 그러고는 곧 그것을 들어 주겠다며 내 보따리를 붙들었다. \"아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"거들고 싶거든, 아이 손을 잡고 이 거리 위쪽 끝까지만 끌어 주세요. 같이 가서 수고한 값을 치러 드릴게요.\"\n\n내가 한 말을 듣고 그녀는 따라오지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 한마디로 그 인간은 나와 같은 장사를 하는 자였고, 보따리 말고는 아무것도 바라는 게 없었다. 그래도 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 나와 함께 그 문까지 갔다. 거기에 이르자 나는 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"가 보세요, 얘.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신 장사를 알겠어요. 노획물이야 얼마든지 만날 수 있을 테니.\"\n\n그녀는 내 말을 알아듣고 가 버렸다. 나는 아이들을 데리고 문을 쾅쾅 두드렸고, 사람들은 이미 불 소동으로 깨어 있던 터라 곧 안으로 들여보내 주었다. 나는 말했다. \"마님 깨어 계세요? 아무개 부인이 이 두 아이를 받아 주십사 청하신다고 전해 주세요. 가엾은 부인, 큰일 났어요. 그 집이 온통 불길에 휩싸였답니다.\" 그들은 매우 정중하게 아이들을 받아들이고 곤경에 빠진 그 가족을 가엾어했으며, 나는 보따리를 들고 빠져나왔다. 하녀 하나가 나에게 보따리도 두고 가는 거 아니냐고 물었다. 나는 말했다. \"아니란다, 얘야. 그건 다른 데로 갈 거야. 그 집 것이 아니거든.\"\n\n나는 이제 그 소동에서 멀찍이 벗어나 있던지라, 누구의 추궁도 받지 않고 계속 걸어가, 매우 상당한 은붙이 보따리를 곧장 집으로 가져와 내 옛 후견인에게 건넸다. 그녀는 그것을 들여다보지 않겠노라며, 더 찾으러 다시 나가 보라고 일렀다.\n\n그녀는 불이 난 집 옆집 부인에게도 똑같은 말을 일러 주었고, 나는 가 보려고 애썼으나 이때쯤에는 불 경보가 어찌나 커지고, 어찌나 많은 소방기가 물을 뿜어 대며, 거리가 어찌나 사람들로 붐볐던지, 무슨 수를 써도 그 집 근처에 갈 수가 없었다. 그래서 나는 다시 후견인 집으로 돌아와 보따리를 내 방으로 가지고 올라가 살펴보기 시작했다. 거기서 내가 어떤 보물을 찾아냈는지는 몸서리치며 이야기하거니와, 상당했던 그 집안 은붙이의 대부분 말고도, 금사슬 하나—구식 물건으로 거기 달린 로켓이 부서져 있어 몇 해 동안 쓰이지 않았던 듯하나, 그렇다고 금이 못해진 것은 아니었다—와 장례용 반지가 든 작은 상자 하나, 부인의 결혼반지, 그리고 부서진 옛 금 로켓 조각 몇 개, 금시계 하나, 그리고 옛 금화 조각으로 24파운드 값어치쯤 든 지갑 하나, 그 밖에 값나가는 여러 물건을 찾아냈다고 말하는 것으로 족하다.\n\n이것은 내가 손댄 가운데 가장 크고 가장 고약한 노획물이었다. 정말이지, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 다른 경우들에서는 이제 온갖 성찰의 힘을 넘어설 만큼 굳어져 있었으나, 이 보물을 들여다보았을 때만은 정말로 영혼 깊은 데까지 마음이 찔렸으니, 그 불로 그토록 많은 것을 잃은 데다, 분명 자기는 은붙이와 가장 좋은 물건들을 건졌으리라 믿을 그 가엾고 의기소침한 부인을 생각하면, 자기가 속았음을 알게 되고, 자기 아이들과 물건을 가져간 사람이 가장한 대로 옆 거리 부인에게서 온 것이 아니라, 아이들이 제 모르게 그 부인에게 떠맡겨졌음을 알게 될 때 그녀가 얼마나 놀라고 비통해할지를 생각하면 그러했다.\n\n거듭 말하거니와, 고백건대 이 짓의 비인간성이 나를 무척 흔들어 놓았고 몹시 마음이 누그러지게 했으며, 그 일로 내 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그러나 그것이 잔인하고 비인간적이라는 온갖 자각에도 불구하고, 나는 도무지 무언가를 돌려주려는 마음을 낼 수가 없었다. 그 성찰은 닳아 없어졌고, 나는 그것들을 가져올 때 따랐던 정황을 곧 잊어버리기 시작했다.\n\n이것이 다가 아니었으니, 이 일거리로 내가 전보다 상당히 부유해졌는데도, 전에 품었던 결심, 곧 조금만 더 손에 넣으면 이 끔찍한 장사를 그만두겠다던 결심이 돌아오지 않았고, 도리어 나는 여전히 더 멀리, 더 많이 나아가야만 했다. 탐욕이 성공과 어찌나 한데 얽혔던지, 나는 제때에 삶을 바꿀 생각이 더는 들지 않았으니, 그것 없이는 그토록 사악하게 얻은 것을 지키는 데 아무런 안전도, 아무런 평온도 기대할 수 없는데도, 조금만 더, 조금만 더가 여전히 내 형편이었다.\n\n마침내 내 죄의 끈질긴 부추김에 굴복하여, 나는 온갖 회한과 참회를, 그리고 그 문제에 관한 온갖 성찰을 죄다 떨쳐 버렸으니, 그것들은 결국 이런 것 이상이 되지 못했다. 곧 어쩌면 내 욕망을 채워 줄 노획물 하나를 더 손에 넣게 될지도 모른다는 것. 그러나 분명히 그 하나를 손에 넣었어도, 한 번 한 번 손에 넣을 때마다 또 다른 것을 향하게 되었고, 그것이 나에게 어찌나 그 장사를 계속하도록 부추겼던지, 나는 그것을 그만둘 생각에는 도무지 구미가 당기지 않았다.\n\n이런 형편으로, 성공에 굳어져 계속하기로 작정한 채, 나는 이런 삶에 대한 마지막 보답을 만나도록 정해진 그 덫에 빠지고 말았다. 그러나 그조차 아직은 아니었으니, 나는 이렇게 끝장으로 치닫는 길에서 성공적인 모험을 몇 차례 더 만났기 때문이다.\n\n나는 여전히 내 후견인과 함께 머물렀는데, 그녀는 한동안 교수형을 당한 내 동무의 불운을 정말로 걱정했다. 듣자 하니 그 여자는 내 후견인을 같은 길로 보낼 만큼 그녀에 대해 충분히 알고 있었던지라, 그것이 그녀를 매우 불안하게 만들었으니, 그녀는 정말로 큰 공포에 휩싸여 있었다.\n\n사실 그 여자가 떠나면서도 자기가 아는 것을 발설하려고 입을 열지 않았던지라, 내 후견인은 그 점에 관해서는 마음이 놓였고, 어쩌면 그녀가 교수형을 당한 것이 다행이라 여겼을 것이다. 그녀로서는 제 동무들을 팔아넘기는 대가로 사면을 얻어 낼 수도 있었으니까. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 그녀를 잃은 일과, 자기가 아는 것을 미끼로 팔아넘기지 않은 그 친절함을 떠올리니, 내 후견인은 그녀를 위해 매우 진심으로 애도하게 되었다. 나는 힘닿는 대로 그녀를 위로했고, 그녀는 그 보답으로 나를 굳게 만들어 더더욱 온전히 똑같은 운명을 받을 만하게 했다.\n\n그래도 앞서 말했듯이 그것은 나를 더욱 조심스럽게 만들었으니, 특히 나는 가게 물건 슬쩍하기를 매우 꺼렸고, 특히 눈을 사방에 두고 있는 부류인 포목상과 옷감상 사이에서는 더욱 그러했다. 나는 레이스 장수들과 모자 장수들 사이에서 한두 번 모험을 했는데, 특히 새로 차린 데다 그 장사로 자란 사람들이 아닌 젊은 여자 둘이 있다는 것을 알게 된 어느 가게에서 그랬다. 거기서 내 생각에 나는 예닐곱 파운드 값어치의 본레이스 한 조각과 실 한 꾸러미를 들고 나왔던 것 같다. 그러나 이것은 단 한 번뿐이었으니, 두 번은 통하지 않을 수법이었다.\n\n새 가게가 있다는 소문을 들으면, 특히 그 사람들이 가게 장사로 자란 사람들이 아닐 때는, 그것은 늘 안전한 일거리로 여겨졌다. 그런 사람들은 시작할 무렵에 한두 번 봉변을 당하리라 믿어도 좋으며, 그것을 막을 수 있다면 그들은 정말로 매우 빈틈없는 사람임이 분명하다.\n\n나는 한두 번 더 모험을 했으나, 그것들도 자질구레한 것이었고, 다만 먹고살기에는 충분했다. 이 뒤로 한동안 이렇다 할 것이 나타나지 않자, 나는 정말로 이 장사를 그만두어야겠다고 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 나를 잃고 싶어 하지 않고 나에게 큰 것을 기대하던 내 후견인이, 어느 날 나를 한 젊은 여자와 어울리게 했으니\n그 여자의 남편으로 통하던 사내가 하나 있었는데, 나중에 드러난 바로는 그녀는 그의 아내가 아니라 동업자였던 듯하니, 그들이 벌이는 장사의 동업자였고, 또 다른 일에서도 동업자였다. 한마디로 그들은 함께 도둑질하고, 함께 자고, 함께 붙잡혔으며, 끝내 함께 교수형을 당했다.\n\n나는 후견인의 주선으로 이 두 사람과 일종의 동맹을 맺게 되었고, 그들은 나를 서너 번의 모험에 데리고 나갔는데, 거기서 나는 그들이 저지르는 거칠고 서툰 도둑질을 그저 지켜보았을 뿐이니, 그들 쪽의 어마어마한 뻔뻔함과 털리는 사람들 쪽의 지독한 부주의가 아니었더라면 결코 성공할 수 없었을 짓들이었다. 그래서 나는 그때부터 그들과 함께 무엇이든 도모하는 데 무척 신중하게 굴기로 마음먹었으며, 실제로 그들이 운수 사나운 계획을 두세 가지 내놓았을 때 나는 그 제안을 거절했고, 그들도 그만두도록 설득했다. 한번은 그들이 특별히, 낮 동안 눈여겨 보아두고 그것을 두는 자리까지 알아낸 어느 시계 장수에게서 금시계 세 개를 훔치자고 제안했다. 그들 가운데 하나가 온갖 종류의 열쇠를 어찌나 많이 가졌던지, 그 시계 장수가 시계를 둔 곳을 여는 일쯤은 의심할 것도 없다고 했다. 그리하여 우리는 일종의 약속을 잡았다. 그러나 내가 그 일을 자세히 들여다보니, 그들이 그 집을 부수고 들어가자는 것임을 알게 되었고, 이는 내 방식과는 어긋나는 일인지라 나는 거기에 끼어들려 하지 않았으므로, 그들은 나 없이 갔다. 그들은 힘으로 그 집에 들어가 시계가 든 잠긴 곳을 부수었으나, 금시계 하나와 은시계 하나밖에 찾지 못해 그것을 챙겼고, 다시 아주 깨끗이 집에서 빠져나왔다. 그러나 식구들이 놀라 \"도둑이야\" 하고 외치는 바람에, 사내는 쫓겨 붙잡혔다. 젊은 여자도 빠져나오기는 했으나, 불행히도 멀리서 붙들렸고 그 몸에서 시계가 나왔다. 이리하여 나는 두 번째로 벗어났으니, 그들은 유죄판결을 받고 둘 다 교수형에 처해졌는데, 비록 나이는 젊었으나 상습범이었던 까닭이다. 앞서 그들이 함께 도둑질하고 함께 잤다고 말한 대로, 이제 그들은 함께 교수형을 당했으며, 그렇게 나의 새로운 동업은 끝이 났다.\n\n나는 그토록 아슬아슬하게 호된 일을 면한 데다 그런 본보기까지 눈앞에 둔 터라, 이제 무척 조심스러워지기 시작했다. 그러나 내게는 날마다 나를 부추기는 새로운 유혹자가 있었으니, 곧 내 후견인 말이다. 그리고 이제 한 건의 노획물이 나타났는데, 이것이 그녀의 주선으로 굴러들어 온 것인지라 그녀는 노획물의 좋은 몫을 기대하고 있었다. 어느 사사로운 집에 플랜더스 레이스가 상당량 보관되어 있다는 정보를 그녀가 얻었던 것인데, 플랜더스 레이스는 금지 품목이었으므로 그것에 손댈 수 있는 세관 관리에게는 좋은 노획물이었다. 나는 후견인에게서 그 양과 더불어 그것이 숨겨진 바로 그 장소까지 소상히 들었고, 그래서 어느 세관 관리를 찾아가, 만일 내 정당한 상금 몫을 받게 되리라고 보장해 준다면 그만한 양의 레이스에 관해 이러이러한 정보를 알려 주겠다고 말했다. 이는 더없이 정당한 제안이라 더 공정할 수 없었으므로, 그는 동의했고, 순경 한 명과 나를 데리고 가 그 집을 에워쌌다. 내가 그 장소로 곧장 갈 수 있다고 말하자, 그는 그것을 내게 맡겼다. 구멍이 아주 어두웠으므로 나는 손에 촛불을 들고 그 안으로 비집고 들어가, 그에게 천 조각들을 꺼내 건넸는데, 몇 개를 건네면서도 내가 편히 처분할 수 있을 만큼은 내 몸에 챙겨 두도록 주의를 기울였다. 그 구멍에는 300파운드어치에 가까운 레이스가 있었고, 나는 그중 50파운드어치쯤을 내 것으로 챙겼다. 그 집 사람들은 레이스의 임자가 아니라, 그것을 그들에게 맡겨 둔 어느 상인이 임자였다. 그래서 그들은 내가 예상했던 만큼 놀라지는 않았다.\n\n나는 그 관리를 두고 나왔는데, 그는 노획물에 더없이 기뻐하며 자기가 챙긴 것에 흡족해했고, 자기가 일러 준 그 자신의 집에서 나와 만나기로 약속했다. 나는 내 몸에 지닌 짐을 처분한 뒤에 그리로 갔는데, 그는 그것에 대해 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 내가 그에게 갔을 때 그는, 내가 노획물의 몫에 대해 마땅히 누릴 권리를 모를 것이라 믿고서 나와 흥정을 시작하더니, 기어이 20파운드로 나를 떼어 버리려 했다. 그러나 나는 그가 짐작하는 만큼 무지하지 않다는 것을 그에게 알려 주었다. 그러면서도 그가 내게 확실한 금액을 제시해 주려 한 것이 다행스럽기도 했다.\n\n나는 100파운드를 요구했고, 그는 30파운드까지 올렸다. 나는 80파운드로 내려갔고, 그는 다시 40파운드로 올렸다. 한마디로, 그가 50파운드를 제시했고 나는 동의했는데, 다만 내가 쓰려는 것인 양 8, 9파운드쯤 되리라 짐작되는 레이스 한 조각을 요구했고, 그도 거기에 동의했다. 그리하여 나는 바로 그날 밤에 50파운드를 현금으로 받았고 그 거래를 끝냈다. 그는 내가 누구인지, 나를 어디서 수소문해야 하는지도 끝내 알지 못했으니, 설령 물품의 일부가 빼돌려진 것이 발각되었다 한들 그것을 두고 나를 추궁할 수 없었을 것이다.\n\n나는 이 노획물을 후견인과 아주 정확하게 나누었고, 이때부터 그녀에게 나는 가장 까다로운 일도 능숙하게 처리하는 솜씨꾼으로 통했다. 나는 이 마지막 일이 내가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 낫고 가장 손쉬운 종류임을 알게 되었고, 그래서 금지 품목을 알아내는 것을 내 일거리로 삼아, 더러 그것을 사들인 뒤 으레 밀고하곤 했으나, 이런 밀고들은 방금 이야기한 것처럼 대단한 액수에 이르지는 못했다. 다만 나는 안전하게 행동하기를 바랐고, 남들이 하다가 날마다 실패하는 것을 본 그 큰 위험을 무릅쓰는 일에는 여전히 조심스러웠다.\n\n다음으로 중요한 일은 어느 귀부인의 좋은 시계를 노린 시도였다. 그것은 어느 예배당에서 군중 속에서 일어났는데, 거기서 나는 붙잡힐 큰 위험에 처했다. 나는 그녀의 시계를 단단히 움켜쥐었으나, 누가 나를 그녀에게 떠밀기라도 한 듯 크게 부딪치며 그 순간에 시계를 한껏 당겼는데도, 그것이 빠지지 않는 것을 알고 그 즉시 손을 놓고는, 죽기라도 한 듯이 소리쳤다. 누가 내 발을 밟았다고, 그리고 분명 여기에 소매치기가 있으니 누군가가 내 시계를 잡아당겼다고 외쳤던 것이다. 여러분이 알아 두어야 할 것은, 이런 모험에서 우리는 늘 옷을 아주 잘 차려입고 다녔다는 점이니, 나도 좋은 옷을 입고 옆구리에 금시계를 차서, 다른 사람들 못지않게 귀부인과 흡사했던 것이다.\n\n내가 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게, 그 다른 귀부인도 \"소매치기야\" 하고 외쳤으니, 누군가가 자기 시계를 잡아당기려 했다는 것이었다.\n\n내가 그녀의 시계에 손댔을 때 나는 그녀에게 바싹 붙어 있었으나, 내가 소리쳤을 때는 말하자면 우뚝 멈추어 섰고, 군중이 그녀를 조금 앞으로 떠밀어 그녀도 소리를 냈는데, 그것은 나와는 다소 떨어진 곳이어서 그녀는 나를 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 그런데 그녀가 \"소매치기야\" 하고 외치자, 누군가가 외치기를 \"그래, 여기도 또 한 사람 당했어! 이 귀부인도 당할 뻔했다고\" 했다.\n\n바로 그 순간에, 군중 속에서 조금 더 안쪽에서, 또한 아주 운 좋게도, 사람들이 다시 \"소매치기야\" 하고 외치며 정말로 한 젊은 사내를 현행범으로 붙들었다. 이는 그 가엾은 놈에게는 불운한 일이었으나 내 처지에는 더없이 때맞은 일이었으니, 비록 그 전에도 이미 내가 그 일을 제법 솜씨 있게 넘기긴 했어도, 이제는 의심의 여지가 없어졌고, 군중 가운데 떠도는 무리가 모두 그쪽으로 달려갔으며, 그 가엾은 소년은 길거리의 분노에 내맡겨졌으니, 그것은 내가 굳이 묘사할 필요 없는 잔혹함이거니와, 그런데도 그들은 뉴게이트로 보내지는 것보다는 차라리 그쪽을 늘 반긴다. 뉴게이트에서는 거의 다 죽어 갈 때까지 오래 갇혀 있기 일쑤이고, 때로는 교수형을 당하며, 유죄판결을 받을 경우 그들이 바랄 수 있는 가장 나은 것이라야 유배형이기 때문이다.\n\n이는 내게 아슬아슬한 모면이었고, 나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 한참 동안 다시는 금시계에 손대지 못했다. 사실 이 모험에는 내가 벗어나는 데 도움을 준 여러 정황이 겹쳐 있었으나, 가장 큰 것은 내가 시계를 잡아당겼던 그 여자가 어리석은 사람이었다는 점이다. 다시 말해, 그녀는 그런 시도의 본질을 몰랐는데, 시계를 슬쩍 빼낼 수 없도록 단단히 매어 둘 만큼 영리했던 것을 보면, 모르리라고는 생각하기 어려운 일이었다. 그러나 그녀는 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 범인을 가려내는 데 알맞은 생각이라곤 도무지 하지 못했다. 그녀는 당기는 것을 느끼자 비명을 지르며 앞으로 떠밀고 나가 주위 사람들을 온통 혼란에 빠뜨렸을 뿐, 적어도 두 사람 정도 셀 동안은 자기 시계에 대해서도, 소매치기에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않았으니, 그것은 내게 충분하고도 남는 시간이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 그녀의 뒤에서 소리쳤고, 그녀가 앞으로 떠밀려 가는 동안 나는 군중 속에서 뒤로 물러났으므로, 그 시간 동안 사람들이, 적어도 일고여덟은, 여전히 움직이는 군중 속에서 나와 그녀 사이에 끼어들게 되었다. 그러고 나서 내가 그녀보다 오히려 먼저, 적어도 그만큼 빨리 \"소매치기야\" 하고 외쳤으니, 나만큼이나 그녀도 의심받는 사람이 될 수 있었고, 사람들은 누구를 찾아야 할지 갈팡질팡하게 되었다. 반면, 만일 그녀가 그런 경우에 필요한 침착함을 지녀, 당기는 것을 느끼자마자 그렇게 비명을 지르는 대신 곧장 돌아서서 바로 뒤에 있는 사람을 붙들었더라면, 그녀는 틀림없이 나를 잡았을 것이다.\n\n이는 그 패거리에게 그리 친절한 종류의 안내는 못 되지만, 소매치기의 동작을 푸는 단서의 열쇠임은 분명하니, 이를 따를 줄 아는 자라면 누구든 도둑을 틀림없이 잡을 것이요, 따르지 못하면 틀림없이 놓칠 것이다.\n\n내게는 또 다른 모험이 있었으니, 이것이 이 일을 의심의 여지 없게 만들어 주며, 소매치기의 경우에 대해 후세에 교훈이 될 만하다. 나의 착하고 늙은 후견인은 그 내력을 잠깐 짚어 두자면, 비록 그 장사에서는 손을 뗐어도, 말하자면 타고난 소매치기였고, 나중에 들어 알게 된 바로는 그 기술의 온갖 단계를 두루 거쳤으면서도 단 한 번밖에 붙잡힌 적이 없었는데, 그때 어찌나 호되게 발각되었던지 유죄판결을 받고 유배형에 처해질 처지였다. 그러나 보기 드물게 입담이 좋은 여자인 데다 주머니에 돈까지 지니고 있었던 터라, 배가 식량을 싣느라 아일랜드에 들렀을 때 그곳에 상륙할 방도를 찾아냈고, 거기서 몇 해 동안 살면서 옛 장사를 했다. 그러다가 또 다른 종류의 나쁜 무리에 끼어들어 산파 겸 포주가 되었고, 거기서 백 가지 농간을 부렸는데, 우리가 차츰 친해지면서 그녀는 우리 사이의 은밀한 이야기로 그 내력을 조금 들려주었다. 그리고 내가 다다른 그 모든 기술과 솜씨는 바로 이 사악한 여자 덕분이었으니, 그 기술에서 나를 넘어선 자도, 아무런 불운 없이 그토록 오래 그 짓을 한 자도 거의 없었다.\n\n그녀가 더블린을 떠나 잉글랜드로 건너온 것은 아일랜드에서 그런 모험을 겪고 그 나라에서 제법 이름이 알려진 뒤였으니, 잉글랜드에서는 유배 기간이 아직 끝나지 않은 터라 다시 나쁜 손에 떨어질까 두려워 이전의 장사를 그만두었다. 그랬다가는 영락없이 파멸할 것이 분명했기 때문이다. 여기서 그녀는 아일랜드에서 따르던 것과 똑같은 장사를 차렸는데, 그 일에서 곧 그 감탄할 만한 수완과 좋은 입담으로, 내가 이미 묘사한 그 정점에 다다라, 실로 부자가 되기 시작했으나, 앞서 넌지시 비친 대로 그 장사는 나중에 다시 기울었다.\n\n내가 여기서 이 여자의 내력을 이만큼 언급한 것은, 그녀가 내가 이제 이끌어 가던 그 사악한 삶에 관여한 바를 더 잘 설명하기 위해서이니, 그녀는 그 온갖 세세한 데까지 말하자면 내 손을 잡고 나를 이끌었고, 또 어찌나 잘 일러 주었던지, 나도 그것을 어찌나 잘 따랐던지, 나는 내 시대의 가장 위대한 솜씨꾼이 되어, 어떤 위험에서든 솜씨 좋게 빠져나왔다. 그리하여 내 동료들 가운데 여럿이 그 장사를 한 지 반년 만에 곧장 뉴게이트로 끌려들어 갈 때, 나는 이제 오 년 넘게 그 짓을 해 왔건만 뉴게이트의 사람들은 나를 알지조차 못했다. 사실 그들은 내 소문을 많이 들었고 종종 내가 거기 오리라 기다렸으나, 나는 여러 번 극도의 위험에 처하면서도 늘 빠져나갔다.\n\n내가 이제 처한 가장 큰 위험 가운데 하나는, 내가 그 장사판에서 너무 잘 알려졌다는 것이었으니, 그들 가운데 더러는, 내가 그들에게 끼친 어떤 해 때문이라기보다 오히려 시샘 탓에 나를 미워하던 자들이, 자기들은 늘 붙잡혀 뉴게이트로 끌려가는데 나는 늘 빠져나가는 것에 화를 내기 시작했다. 내게 몰 플랜더스라는 이름을 붙여 준 것이 바로 이들이었으니, 그 이름은 내 본명이나 내가 여태껏 써 온 어떤 이름과도, 검은빛이 흰빛과 닮지 않은 만큼이나 아무런 연고가 없었는데, 다만 앞서처럼 한 번, 곧 내가 민트에 몸을 숨겼을 때 나 자신을 플랜더스 부인이라 일컬은 적은 있었다. 그러나 그것을 이 악당들은 전혀 알지 못했고, 그들이 어떻게 그 이름을 붙이게 되었는지, 무슨 계기였는지 나도 끝내 알 수 없었다.\n\n뉴게이트에 단단히 갇힌 자들 가운데 더러가 나를 고발하겠노라 다짐했다는 것을 나는 곧 알게 되었다. 그들 가운데 두셋은 그럴 만한 능력이 너무도 충분하다는 것을 내가 알았던 터라, 나는 그 일로 크게 근심하여 한동안 집 안에 틀어박혀 있었다. 그러나 내 후견인은 내가 늘 그 성공의 동업자로 삼았던 사람이요, 이제 나와 더불어 안전한 노름을 하던 사람이었으니, 이득에는 몫이 있으되 위험에는 몫이 없었기 때문이다. 말하자면 내 후견인은 내가 그녀의 표현대로 그토록 쓸모없고 이문도 없는 삶을 사는 데 다소 안달이 났던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 내가 바깥에 나갈 새로운 계책을 꾸몄으니, 그것은 나를 남자 옷으로 차려입혀 새로운 종류의 일에 투입하는 것이었다.\n\n나는 키가 크고 풍채가 좋았으나, 남자치고는 얼굴이 조금 너무 매끈했다. 그래도 나는 좀처럼 밤에밖에는 나가지 않았으므로 그런대로 잘 되었다. 그러나 새 옷차림으로 처신하는 데는, 내 말은 내 솜씨에 관한 한, 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 본성에 그토록 어긋나는 옷차림으로는 이런 일에서 그토록 날래고, 그토록 재빠르고, 그토록 능숙하기가 불가능했고, 나는 무엇이든 서투르게 했으니, 예전에 누리던 성공도, 빠져나오는 수월함도 누리지 못했다. 그래서 나는 그것을 그만두기로 마음먹었는데, 그 결심은 곧이어 다음의 사건으로 굳어졌다.\n\n내 후견인은 나를 남자처럼 변장시켰을 뿐 아니라, 자기 일에 제법 날랜 어느 젊은 사내와 나를 짝지어 주었고, 약 삼 주 동안 우리는 함께 아주 잘 해 나갔다. 우리의 주된 장사는 가게 주인들의 계산대를 살피다가, 아무 데나 부주의하게 놓인 온갖 물건을 슬쩍하는 것이었고, 우리는 이 일로, 우리가 부르는 대로 하자면, 여러 번 좋은 거래를 했다. 그리고 우리는 늘 함께 다녔으므로 무척 친밀해졌으나, 그는 내가 남자가 아님을 끝내 알지 못했다. 아니, 내가 일의 사정에 따라 여러 번 그를 따라 그의 숙소로 가, 너덧 번은 그와 밤새 함께 잤는데도 그러했다. 그러나 우리의 속셈은 다른 데 있었고, 나로서는 그에게 내 성별을 감추는 것이 절대적으로 필요했으니, 그것은 나중에 드러났다. 우리의 생활 형편, 곧 늦게 돌아오는 것, 또 누구도 우리 숙소에 드나드는 것을 믿고 맡길 수 없게 하는 이런저런 일을 해야 했던 사정은, 내가 내 성별을 인정하지 않는 한 그와 함께 자기를 거절하기란 불가능하게 만들었다. 그리고 그런 형편에서 나는 나 자신을 감쪽같이 감추었다. 그러나 그의 불운과 나의 행운이 곧 이 삶에 종지부를 찍었으니, 사실 나도 여러 다른 이유로 이 삶에 진저리가 났음을 인정해야겠다. 우리는 이 새로운 방식의 장사로 여러 건의 노획물을 챙겼으나, 마지막 것은 유난한 것이 될 참이었다. 어느 거리에 가게가 하나 있었는데, 그 뒤로 다른 거리 쪽으로 면한 창고가 있어, 그 집이 모퉁이를 이루고 있었다.\n\n창고의 창문을 통해, 그 바로 앞에 있는 계산대 혹은 진열대 위에 다른 천들과 더불어 비단 다섯 필이 놓여 있는 것이 보였는데, 거의 어두워졌는데도 사람들이 앞쪽 가게에서 손님을 보느라 분주해 그 창문들을 닫을 틈이 없었거나, 아니면 잊어버린 모양이었다.\n\n이것에 그 젊은 사내는 어찌나 기뻐했던지 자신을 억누르지 못했다. 그것이 죄다 손 닿는 데 있다고 그는 말했고, 집을 부수어서라도 그것을 손에 넣겠다고 내게 사납게 맹세했다. 나는 조금 말렸으나 어쩔 도리가 없음을 알았다. 그래서 그는 무모하게 달려들어, 미닫이창의 한 칸을 제법 솜씨 좋게, 소리도 없이 빼내고는 비단 네 필을 꺼내 그것을 들고 내 쪽으로 왔으나, 곧장 무시무시한 소동과 소음과 함께 추격당했다. 우리는 사실 함께 서 있었으나, 내가 그의 손에서 물건을 하나도 받지 않았을 때, 나는 그에게 다급히 말했다. \"넌 끝장났어, 제발 도망쳐!\" 그는 번개처럼 달렸고, 나도 그러했으나, 그가 물건을 지녔던 탓에 추격은 나보다 그를 향해 더 거셌다. 그는 비단 두 필을 떨어뜨려 그들을 잠시 멈칫하게 했으나, 군중은 불어나 우리 둘을 다 쫓았다. 그들은 곧 그를, 나머지 두 필을 지닌 채로 붙들었고, 그런 다음 나머지가 나를 쫓아왔다. 나는 죽어라 달려 후견인의 집으로 들어갔는데, 눈썰미 좋은 자들 몇이 어찌나 바싹 쫓아왔던지 나를 거기에 들어가는 것까지 봐 두었다. 그들이 곧장 문을 두드리지는 않은 덕에 나는 변장을 벗어던지고 내 옷으로 갈아입을 시간을 벌었다. 게다가 그들이 거기에 이르렀을 때, 미리 둘러댈 말을 준비해 둔 내 후견인이 문을 닫아 둔 채 그들에게 큰 소리로, 거기 들어온 남자는 없다고 말했다. 사람들은 분명히 남자가 거기 들어갔다고 우기며, 문을 부수고 들어가겠다고 으름장을 놓았다.\n\n내 후견인은 조금도 당황하지 않고 그들에게 차분히 말하기를, 순경을 데려온다면 자기 집을 아주 기꺼이 와서 뒤져도 좋으나, 순경이 들이는 자 외에는 아무도 들이지 않겠다고 했으니, 군중 전부를 들이는 것은 도리에 어긋나기 때문이라고 했다. 비록 그들이 군중이긴 했으나, 이를 그들도 거절할 수 없었다. 그리하여 곧장 순경이 불려 왔고, 그녀는 아주 기꺼이 문을 열어 주었다. 순경이 문을 지키고, 그가 지정한 자들이 집을 뒤졌으며, 내 후견인은 방마다 그들을 따라다녔다. 내 방에 이르자 그녀는 나를 불러 큰 소리로 말했다. \"사촌, 어서 문 좀 열어요. 여기 신사분들이 사촌 방을 들여다봐야 한다는군요.\"\n\n내게는 어린 계집아이가 하나 같이 있었는데, 후견인이 부르는 대로 하자면 그녀의 손녀였다. 나는 그 아이에게 문을 열라고 일렀고, 거기에 나는 온갖 물건을 잔뜩 늘어놓은 채 마치 하루 종일 일이라도 하고 있었던 양 앉아 있었으니, 나 자신은 옷도 제대로 갖춰 입지 않고 머리에 잠잘 때 쓰는 것만 두른 채, 헐렁한 아침 가운을 몸에 두르고 있었다. 내 후견인은 그들이 나를 방해한 데 대해 일종의 변명을 하며, 그 까닭을 내게 얼마간 일러 주고, 그들에게 문을 열어 주어 그들이 스스로 만족하게 하는 수밖에 없으니, 자기가 무슨 말을 해도 그들이 만족하지 않을 것이라고 했다. 나는 가만히 앉아, 원한다면 방을 뒤지라고 일렀으니, 집 안에 누가 있다 한들 내 방에는 없는 것이 분명하기 때문이라 했다. 그리고 집의 나머지에 대해서는 나는 할 말이 없으며, 그들이 무엇을 찾는지 알지 못한다고 했다.\n\n내 주위의 모든 것이 어찌나 결백하고 정직해 보였던지, 그들은 내가 예상했던 것보다 더 정중하게 나를 대했으나, 그것은 그들이 방을, 침대 밑이며 침대 속이며 무엇이든 숨길 수 있는 다른 모든 곳까지 샅샅이 뒤진 뒤의 일이었다. 그들은 이렇게 하고도 아무것도 찾아내지 못하자, 나를 성가시게 한 데 대해 용서를 구하고 아래층으로 내려갔다.\n\n그들이 이렇게 집을 아래에서 위로, 다시 위에서 아래로 뒤지고도 아무것도 찾아내지 못하자, 군중을 제법 잘 달랬다. 그러나 그들은 내 후견인을 치안판사 앞으로 데려갔다. 두 사내가 자기들이 쫓던 그 남자가 그녀의 집으로 들어가는 것을 보았다고 맹세했다. 내 후견인은 자기 집이 모욕당하고 자기가 아무 까닭 없이 이런 취급을 받는 데 대해 떠들썩하게 야단법석을 떨었으니, 설령 남자가 들어왔다 한들 자기가 알기로는 곧장 다시 나갔을 수도 있으며, 자기가 아는 한 그날 하루 종일 자기 집 문 안에 든 남자는 없었다고 맹세할 수 있다(그리고 그것은 실로 사실이었다)고 했다. 자기가 위층에 있는 동안 겁에 질린 어떤 사내가 쫓기다가 문이 열린 것을 보고 피신하러 뛰어들었을 수는 있겠으나 자기는 그것을 전혀 모르며, 설령 그러했다 한들 그는 분명 다시, 어쩌면 다른 문으로 나갔을 것이니, 자기 집에는 골목으로 통하는 다른 문이 또 있어, 그렇게 빠져나가 그들 모두를 따돌렸으리라고 했다.\n\n이것은 사실 제법 그럴듯했고, 치안판사는 그녀에게, 누군가를 숨겨 주거나, 보호하거나, 법망에서 가려 주기 위해 자기 집에 어떤 남자도 받아들이거나 들이지 않았다는 맹세를 받는 것으로 만족했다. 이 맹세는 그녀가 정당하게 할 수 있는 것이었고, 또 그렇게 했으므로, 그녀는 방면되었다.\n\n이 일로 내가 얼마나 겁에 질렸는지는 짐작하기 어렵지 않으니, 내 후견인은 결코 다시는 나를 그런 변장으로 차려입게 할 수 없었다. 내가 그녀에게 말했듯이, 나는 틀림없이 내 정체를 들킬 것이기 때문이었다. 이 못된 짓의 가엾은 내 동업자는 이제 딱한 처지에 놓였으니, 그는 시장 각하 앞으로 끌려가 그 각하에 의해 뉴게이트에 수감되었고, 그를 붙든 사람들은 그를 기소할 의향도 능력도 어찌나 충분했던지, 자진하여 개정 기일에 출두해 그에 대한 고소를 밀고 나가겠노라 서약하겠다고 나섰다.\n\n그러나 그는 자기 공범들을, 특히 그 도둑질에 자기와 함께 가담한 사내를 밝히겠다는 약속으로 자기의 기소를 미루었고, 또 그 일에 애쓰기를 마다하지 않았으니, 그가 내 이름을 댔는데, 그는 나를 그에게 통하던 이름인 게이브리얼 스펜서라고 불렀다. 여기서 내가 그에게 내 이름과 성별을 감춘 지혜가 드러났으니, 만일 그가 그것을 알았더라면 나는 끝장났을 것이다.\n\n그는 이 게이브리얼 스펜서를 밝혀내려 할 수 있는 모든 짓을 다 했다. 그는 나를 묘사하고, 자기가 말하는 내 숙소의 장소를 밝혔으며, 한마디로 내가 사는 곳에 대해 알 수 있는 온갖 세세한 것을 밝혔다. 그러나 내가 그에게 성별이라는 핵심 정황을 감추었던 터라, 나는 막대한 이점이 있었고, 그는 끝내 내 소식을 들을 수 없었다. 그는 나를 찾아내려는 노력으로 두세 가구를 곤경에 빠뜨렸으나, 그들은 나에 대해, 내게 자기들이 본 적 있는 사내 하나가 같이 있었다는 것 외에는 아무것도 몰랐고, 그조차도 알지 못했다. 그리고 내 후견인으로 말하자면, 비록 그녀가 그를 내게 데려온 매개이긴 했으나, 그것은 한 다리 건너 이루어진 일이라 그는 그녀에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다.\n\n이는 그에게 불리하게 돌아갔으니, 밝혀내겠다고 약속해 놓고도 그것을 이루지 못하자, 그것이 이 도시의 사법을 우롱하는 것으로 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 자기를 붙든 가게 주인들에게 더욱 사납게 추궁당했다.\n\n그러나 나는 이 내내 끔찍하게 불안했으니, 아예 멀찍이 떨어져 있고자, 한동안 내 후견인의 집을 떠났다. 그러나 어디로 떠돌아야 할지 몰라, 하녀 하나를 데리고 역마차를 타고 던스터블의, 옛 집주인 내외에게로 갔으니, 거기서 나는 내 랭커셔 남편과 그토록 풍족하게 살았던 것이다. 여기서 나는 그녀에게 그럴듯한 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 내가 날마다 남편이 아일랜드에서 오기를 기다리고 있으며, 그에게 던스터블의 그녀 집에서 만나자고 편지를 보냈고, 바람만 순조롭다면 그가 며칠 안에 틀림없이 상륙할 것이므로, 그가 올 때까지 그들과 며칠 묵으러 왔노라고 했다. 그가 역마로 오는지, 아니면 웨스트 체스터 마차로 오는지는 모르겠으나, 어느 쪽이든 그는 틀림없이 나를 만나러 그 집으로 올 것이라고 했다.\n\n내 집주인 여자는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐했고, 집주인 남자는 나를 어찌나 떠받들었던지, 내가 공주였다 한들 그보다 더 나은 대접을 받지는 못했을 것이며, 내가 마음만 먹었다면 한두 달은 환대받을 수 있었을 것이다.\n\n그러나 내 볼일은 다른 성질의 것이었다. 나는 무척 불안했으니(비록 들킬 가능성이 거의 없을 만큼 잘 변장하긴 했어도), 이 사내가 어떻게든 나를 찾아낼까 두려웠던 것이다. 그가 나더러 모험에 나서지 말라고 설득했고, 또 나 자신은 그 일에서 달아난 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았던 터라, 비록 그가 나를 이 도둑질로 고발할 수는 없었어도, 다른 일들로 나를 고발하여 내 목숨을 대가로 자기 목숨을 살 수는 있었을 것이다.\n\n이것이 나를 끔찍한 불안으로 채웠다. 내게는 의지할 데도, 벗도, 늙은 후견인 외에는 속을 터놓을 사람도 없었으므로, 나는 내 목숨을 그녀의 손에 맡기는 수밖에 다른 방도를 알지 못했고, 그래서 그렇게 했으니, 그녀에게 내게 연락할 곳을 알려 두었고, 내가 여기 머무는 동안 그녀에게서 여러 통의 편지를 받았다. 그중 더러는 나를 거의 혼비백산하게 했으나, 마침내 그녀는 그가 교수형을 당했다는 기쁜 소식을 보내왔으니, 그것은 내가 오랫동안 들은 것 가운데 가장 반가운 소식이었다.\n\n나는 여기서 다섯 주를 머물렀고 실로 아주 편안하게 지냈으나(내 마음의 은밀한 불안만 빼면), 이 편지를 받자 나는 다시 즐거운 낯이 되어, 집주인 여자에게 아일랜드에 있는 남편에게서 편지를 받았는데, 그가 아주 잘 있다는 좋은 소식이 있었으나, 그의 일이 그가 기대했던 만큼 빨리 떠나오는 것을 허락지 않는다는 나쁜 소식이 있어, 나도 그를 두고 다시 돌아가야 할 것 같다고 말했다.\n\n내 집주인 여자는 그래도 그가 잘 있다는 소식을 들은 데 대해 내게 좋은 소식이라며 인사치레를 했다. \"제가 보기에 마님은,\" 하고 그녀가 말하기를, \"여느 때만큼 즐거워 보이지 않으셨거든요. 그분 걱정에 머리끝까지 푹 빠져 계셨던 게 분명해요.\" 하고 그 착한 여자가 말했다. \"마님이 한결 나아지신 게 한눈에 보인다니까요.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"원, 그 나리께서 아직 못 오신다니 안됐습니다.\" 하고 내 집주인 남자가 말했다. \"저도 진심으로 그분을 뵙고 싶었는데 말이지요. 하지만 그분이 오신다는 확실한 소식을 들으시거든, 마님, 다시 이리로 한 걸음 해 주시기를 바랍니다.\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"언제든 오고 싶으실 때 오시면 더없이 환영합니다.\"\n\n이 모든 멋진 인사치레와 함께 우리는 헤어졌고, 나는 제법 흥겹게 런던으로 돌아와, 내 후견인이 나만큼이나 기뻐하는 것을 보았다. 그리고 이제 그녀는 내게 다시는 어떤 동업자도 추천하지 않겠다고 말했으니, 자기가 보기에 내가 혼자 모험에 나설 때 늘 운이 가장 좋더라는 것이었다. 그리고 실로 그러했으니, 나는 혼자 있을 때는 좀처럼 어떤 위험에도 처하지 않았고, 설령 처한다 해도, 어쩌면 나보다 앞을 덜 내다보고 더 무모하고 더 성마른 다른 사람들의 굼뜬 셈법에 얽혀 있을 때보다 더 솜씨 좋게 거기서 빠져나왔다. 모험에 나서는 용기로 말하자면 나도 그들 누구 못지않게 지녔으나, 어떤 일을 벌이기 전에 더 많은 신중을 기했고, 나 자신을 빼낼 때 더 많은 침착함을 지녔기 때문이다.\n\n나는 종종 또 다른 점에서 내 자신의 대담함에 놀라곤 했으니, 곧 내 동료들이 모두 불시에 당하여 그토록 갑작스레 법의 손에 떨어지고, 나는 그토록 아슬아슬하게 벗어났는데도, 이 내내 이 장사를 그만두겠다는 진지한 결심 하나 품지 못했다는 것이며, 더구나 내가 이제 결코 가난하지 않다는 점, 또 대개 그런 온갖 사악함의 입구가 되는 궁핍의 유혹이 이제 사라졌다는 점을 생각하면 더욱 그러했다. 나는 손에 500파운드에 가까운 현금을 지니고 있었으니, 은퇴하기로 마음먹었더라면 그것으로 아주 잘 살 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 말하건대, 내게는 그만두려는 마음이 조금도 없었다. 아니, 손에 겨우 200파운드를 지녔던 예전, 그리고 이런 무시무시한 본보기들을 눈앞에 두지 않았던 예전만큼도 없었다. 이로 미루어 내게 분명해진 바는, 일단 우리가 죄에 굳어지고 나면 어떤 두려움도 우리를 움직이지 못하고, 어떤 본보기도 우리에게 아무런 경고가 되지 못한다는 것이다.\n\n사실 내게는 그 운명이 한동안 내 마음을 깊이 아프게 한 동료가 하나 있었으니, 그것도 차츰 닳아 없어지긴 했다. 그 경우는 실로 무척 불행했다. 나는 어느 포목상에서 아주 좋은 다마스크 천 한 필을 노획물로 챙겨, 나 자신은 무사히 빠져나왔으나, 가게에서 나올 때 그 천을 이 동료에게 건넸고, 그녀는 한쪽 길로, 나는 다른 길로 갔다. 우리가 가게에서 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 포목상이 자기 천이 없어진 것을 알아채고, 심부름꾼들을 보내, 한 사람은 이쪽으로 한 사람은 저쪽으로 보냈는데, 그들은 곧 천을 지닌 그녀를, 다마스크를 몸에 지닌 채 붙들었다. 나로 말하자면, 운 좋게도 위층 한 층에 레이스 방이 있는 어느 집으로 발을 들여놓아, 그들이 일으키는 소동을 창밖으로 내다보며, 그 가엾은 것이 의기양양하게 치안판사에게 끌려가는 것을, 그리고 그가 곧장 그녀를 뉴게이트에 수감하는 것을 지켜보는 만족, 아니 실로 공포를 누렸다.\n\n나는 레이스 방에서는 아무것도 시도하지 않도록 조심하고, 다만 시간을 끌려고 그들의 물건을 제법 뒤적거리기만 했다. 그런 다음 가장자리 장식 몇 야드를 사서 그 값을 치르고, 실로 무척 슬픈 마음으로 나왔으니, 그 가엾은 여자는 나 혼자 훔친 것 때문에 고난에 처한 것이었다.\n\n여기서 또 나의 오랜 신중함이 큰 보탬이 되었으니, 곧 비록 내가 이 사람들과 자주 도둑질을 했어도, 결코 그들에게 내가 누구인지, 어디서 묵는지 알려 주지 않았다는 점이다. 그들은 종종 나를 미행해 내 숙소를 알아내려 했으나 끝내 찾아내지 못했다. 그들은 모두 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알았으나, 그들 가운데 더러는 내가 그 여자임을 알았다기보다 오히려 그러리라 믿는 정도였다. 사실 내 이름은 그들 사이에 널리 알려졌으나, 어떻게 나를 찾아내야 할지 그들은 알지 못했고, 내가 묵는 곳이 도시의 동쪽 끝에 있는지 서쪽에 있는지조차 짐작하지 못했다. 그리고 이 조심성이 이 모든 경우에 나의 안전이었다.\n\n나는 이 여자의 재난을 빌미로 한참 동안 몸을 사렸다. 만일 내가 무슨 일을 벌이다 실패해 감옥에 끌려간다면, 그녀가 거기 있다가 기꺼이 나에 대해 증언하고, 어쩌면 내 목숨을 대가로 자기 목숨을 구하리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 나는 내 이름이 올드베일리에서 제법 잘 알려지기 시작했음을, 비록 그들이 내 얼굴은 몰랐어도 그러함을, 그리고 만일 내가 그들의 손에 떨어진다면 상습범으로 취급되리라는 것을 생각했다. 이런 까닭에 나는 그 가엾은 것의 운명이 어찌 될지 보고 나서야 바깥에 나서기로 마음먹었으나, 그녀가 곤경에 처한 동안 여러 번 그녀를 구제하려고 돈을 전해 주었다.\n\n마침내 그녀는 재판에 나섰다. 그녀는 자기가 그 물건을 훔치지 않았으며, 다만 (자기가 알지는 못하나) 사람들이 플랜더스 부인이라 부르는 것을 들은 어떤 여자가 가게에서 나온 뒤에 자기에게 그 꾸러미를 건네며, 그것을 자기 숙소로 가져다 달라고 일렀노라 항변했다. 그들이 이 플랜더스 부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 그녀는 나를 내놓지도 못했고, 나에 대해 조금도 설명하지 못했다. 그리고 포목상의 점원들이 물건이 도난당했을 때 그녀가 가게에 있었으며, 자기들이 곧장 그것이 없어진 것을 알아채고 그녀를 쫓아가 그 몸에서 찾아냈노라 단호히 맹세하자, 그에 따라 배심원단은 그녀에게 유죄 평결을 내렸다. 그러나 법정은, 그녀가 사실 물건을 훔친 당사자가 아니라 종범에 불과하며, 비록 제 목숨을 구하는 일일지언정 이 플랜더스 부인, 곧 나를 찾아내는 것이 그녀로서는 도무지 불가능했으리라는 점을 고려하니, 실로 그것은 사실이었다. 말하건대, 이 모든 것을 고려하여 그들은 그녀에게 유배형을 허락했으니, 그것이 그녀가 얻을 수 있는 최대의 은전이었다. 다만 법정은 그녀에게, 그동안에 그 플랜더스 부인을 내놓을 수 있다면 그녀의 사면을 위해 주선해 주겠노라 일렀으니, 다시 말해 그녀가 나를 찾아내 넘긴다면 유배되지 않으리라는 것이었다. 이것을 나는 그녀에게 불가능하도록 단단히 해 두었고, 그래서 그녀는 얼마 뒤에 그 판결에 따라 배에 실려 떠났다.\n\n다시 한번 거듭 말하건대, 이 가엾은 여자의 운명은 나를 몹시 괴롭혔고, 나는 무척 수심에 잠기기 시작했으니, 내가 실로 그녀 재난의 도구였음을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 그토록 명백히 위태로운 내 자신의 목숨을 지키려는 마음이 내 온갖 측은함을 거두어 갔다. 그리고 그녀가 사형에 처해지지는 않은 것을 보고, 나는 그녀의 유배에 무척 마음이 놓였으니, 무슨 일이 생기든 그녀가 그때는 내게 해를 끼칠 길에서 벗어나 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 여자의 재난은 방금 이야기한 사건보다 몇 달 앞선 일이었고, 실로 내 후견인이 나를 남자 옷으로 차려입혀 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 돌아다니게 하자고 제안한 한 가지 빌미였으니, 실로 나는 그렇게 했다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이 나는 곧 그 변장에 진력이 났으니, 실로 그것은 나를 너무 많은 어려움에 내몰았기 때문이다.\n\n이제 나는 나에 대한 증인에 대한 온갖 두려움에서 벗어나 마음이 편했으니, 나와 관련되었거나 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알던 자들은 모두 교수형을 당하거나 유배되었기 때문이다. 그리고 설령 불운하게 붙잡힌다 해도, 나는 몰 플랜더스 못지않게 다른 무엇으로든 나를 일컬을 수 있었고, 어떤 옛 죄도 내 앞으로 돌려질 수 없었다. 그래서 나는 더 거리낌 없이 다시 외상 장사를 시작했고, 여러 차례 성공적인 모험을 했으나, 예전에 했던 것만큼은 아니었다.\n\n그 무렵 우리에게는 또 다른 화재가, 내 후견인이 사는 곳에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 일어났는데, 나는 예전처럼 거기서 한 건을 시도했으나, 군중이 몰려들기 전에 미처 도착하지 못해 내가 노린 집에 다다를 수 없었던 탓에, 노획물 대신 봉변을 당했으니, 그것은 내 목숨과 내 온갖 사악한 짓에 거의 종지부를 찍을 뻔했다. 불길이 어찌나 사나웠던지, 사람들이 크게 겁에 질려 가재도구를 옮기고 창밖으로 내던지던 차에, 창밖에서 어느 계집아이가 깃털 침대를 바로 내 위로 던진 것이다. 사실 침대가 푹신해 뼈가 부러지지는 않았으나, 그 무게가 큰 데다 떨어지면서 더 커진 탓에 나를 내리눌러, 한동안 죽은 듯이 쓰러뜨렸다. 사람들은 그것에서 나를 빼내거나 나를 되살리는 데 그다지 마음 쓰지도 않았고, 나는 죽은 자처럼 한참 동안 버려진 채 누워 있다가, 누군가가 그 침대를 치우러 와서 나를 일으켜 주었다. 실로, 그 집 사람들이 그 위로 다른 가재도구를 더 던지지 않은 것이 천만다행이었으니, 그것이 그 위에 떨어졌더라면 나는 영락없이 죽었을 것이다. 그러나 나는 더 큰 고난을 위해 남겨진 몸이었다.\n\n그러나 이 사고는 그때의 내 장사를 망쳐 놓았고, 나는 몹시 다치고 멍이 든 채, 더없이 겁에 질려 내 후견인에게 돌아왔으며, 그녀가 나를 다시 일으켜 세우기까지는 한참이 걸렸다.\n\n이제는 한 해 중 흥겨운 철이라 바살러뮤 시장이 시작되었다. 나는 그쪽으로는 한 번도 발걸음을 한 적이 없었고, 시장의 흔한 부분은 내게 별 이득이 되지도 않았다. 그러나 나는 올해 회랑 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸고, 그러던 중 추첨 가게 중 하나에 들어가게 되었다. 그것은 내게 그리 대수로운 일이 아니었고, 거기서 별 재미를 보리라 기대하지도 않았다. 그러나 더없이 잘 차려입고 무척 부유한 신사 하나가 들어왔는데, 그런 가게에서는 누구에게나 말을 거는 일이 흔하므로, 그는 나를 콕 집어 무척 각별하게 굴었다. 먼저 그는 내 몫으로 추첨에 돈을 걸어 주겠다며 그렇게 했고, 사소한 것이 그의 몫으로 떨어지자 그것을 내게 선사했는데(내 생각에 깃털 토시였던 것 같다), 그러고는 예사롭지 않게 공손한 태도로, 그러면서도 여전히 무척 정중하게, 신사답게 내게 계속 말을 걸었다.\n\n그는 어찌나 오래 나를 이야기에 붙들어 두었던지, 마침내 나를 추첨 가게에서 가게 문간으로, 그러고는 회랑의 산책로로 끌어내, 별 요점도 없이 천 가지 일을 두서없이 이야기했다. 마침내 그는, 입에 발린 말이 아니라 내 동행에 매혹되었노라 말하며, 자기와 함께 마차에 몸을 맡길 용기가 있느냐고 물었다. 그는 자기가 명예를 아는 사람이며, 그런 사람으로서 어울리지 않는 어떤 짓도 내게 하지 않겠노라 말했다. 나는 잠시 사양하는 척하다가, 조금 조르는 것을 못 이긴 척하고는 응했다.\n\n나는 처음에 이 신사가 무엇을 노리는지 도무지 종잡을 수 없었으나, 나중에 알고 보니 그는 머릿속에 술기운이 좀 있었고, 더 마시고 싶은 마음도 그리 없지 않았다. 그는 나이츠브리지의 스프링 가든으로 나를 마차에 태워 데려갔는데, 거기서 우리는 정원을 거닐었고, 그는 나를 무척 후하게 대접했다. 그러나 나는 그가 무척 거리낌 없이 술을 마시는 것을 알았다. 그는 내게도 마시기를 권했으나 나는 사양했다.\n\n여기까지 그는 내게 약속을 지켰고, 어떤 잘못된 것도 내게 제안하지 않았다. 우리는 다시 마차로 떠나왔고, 그는 나를 거리로 데리고 나왔는데, 이때쯤에는 밤 열 시 무렵이었다. 그가 마차를 어느 집 앞에 세웠는데, 그가 아는 듯한 집이었고, 거기서는 거리낌 없이 우리를 위층의, 침대가 있는 방으로 안내했다. 처음에 나는 올라가기를 꺼리는 척했으나, 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤 그것에도 응했으니, 그 끝을 보고 싶었고, 끝내 거기서 무언가를 챙기리라는 희망에서였다. 침대 따위에 관해서는, 나는 그 부분에 대해 별로 개의치 않았다.\n\n여기서 그는 약속했던 것보다 조금 더 거리낌 없이 굴기 시작했고, 나는 조금씩 모든 것에 응했으니, 한마디로 그는 나를 제멋대로 다루었다. 더 말할 필요는 없겠다. 이 내내 그는 거리낌 없이 술도 마셨고, 새벽 한 시 무렵 우리는 다시 마차에 올랐다. 바깥 공기와 마차의 흔들림이 그가 마신 술을 전보다 더 머리로 솟구치게 했고, 그는 마차 안에서 거북해하며 자기가 조금 전에 하던 짓을 다시 한번 벌이려 했다. 그러나 나는 이제 내 노름이 안전하다고 여겼던 터라 그를 뿌리쳤고, 그를 좀 잠잠하게 만들었는데, 그것은 오 분도 채 가지 않아 그가 깊이 잠들어 버렸다.\n\n나는 이 기회를 잡아 그를 샅샅이 뒤졌다. 나는 금시계 하나와 금화가 든 비단 지갑 하나, 그의 멋진 풍성한 가발과 은실로 가장자리를 두른 장갑, 그의 칼과 멋진 코담배갑을 챙기고는, 마차 문을 살며시 열어 마차가 가는 동안 뛰어내릴 채비를 했다. 그러나 마차가 템플 바 너머 좁은 거리에서 다른 마차를 지나가게 하려고 멈춘 덕에, 나는 살그머니 내려 문을 다시 잠그고는, 내 신사와 마차를 한꺼번에 따돌렸으며, 그들에 관해서는 두 번 다시 아무것도 듣지 못했다.\n\n이것은 실로 예기치 못한, 그리고 내가 전혀 계획하지 않은 모험이었다. 비록 내가 인생의 흥겨운 시절을 그토록 다 지나치지는 않아, 식욕에 눈이 멀어 늙은 여자를 젊은 여자와 구별하지 못하는 멋쟁이를 어떻게 대해야 할지는 잊지 않았어도 말이다. 사실 나는 내 나이보다 열에서 열두 살은 젊어 보였으나, 그래도 열일곱 살짜리 젊은 계집은 아니었으니, 충분히 구별될 만했다. 머릿속에 술이 오른 데다 마음속에 사악한 욕망이 함께 오른 남자만큼 터무니없고, 물리고, 우스꽝스러운 것은 없으니, 그는 한꺼번에 두 악마에게 사로잡혀, 물 없이 방아를 돌릴 수 없는 것 못지않게 자기 이성으로 자신을 다스리지 못한다. 그의 악덕은 그 안에 무언가 좋은 것이 있었다면, 만일 그런 것이 있었다면, 그 좋은 것을 죄다 짓밟는다. 아니, 그의 분별마저 제 분노로 눈이 멀어, 그는 자기 견해에서조차 터무니없는 짓을 하니, 이미 취했는데도 더 마시는 짓, 흔해 빠진 여자를, 그 여자가 무엇이고 누구인지, 멀쩡한지 병들었는지, 깨끗한지 더러운지, 추한지 고운지, 늙었는지 젊었는지 아랑곳없이 집어 들고는, 눈이 멀어 실로 분간조차 못 하는 짓이 그것이다. 그런 남자는 미치광이보다 더하니, 자기의 사악하고 타락한 머리에 부추김을 받아, 마치 이 가엾은 내 사내가 내가 그의 주머니에서 시계와 금화 지갑을 털어 갈 때 알지 못했던 것처럼, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알지 못한다.\n\n이들이야말로 솔로몬이 이른 자들이니, \"소가 도살장으로 가듯, 화살이 그 간을 꿰뚫을 때까지 그들은 간다.\" 이는 말이 나온 김에 말하건대, 저 더러운 병에 대한 감탄할 만한 묘사이니, 그것은 핏속에 섞이는 독하고 치명적인 전염병으로, 그 중심 혹은 근원이 간에 있으며, 거기서 온몸의 빠른 순환을 통해 그 무시무시하고 메스꺼운 역병이 곧장 그의 간을 꿰뚫고, 그의 정기가 감염되며, 그의 급소가 화살에 찔리듯 꿰뚫린다.\n\n사실 이 가엾고 방심한 사내는 내게서 아무런 위험도 없었으나, 비록 처음에 나는 그에게서 내가 어떤 위험에 처할지 무척 걱정했어도 말이다. 그러나 한 가지 점에서 그는 실로 가엾이 여길 만했으니, 그 자신은 선량한 부류의 사람으로 보였던 것이다. 그 속셈에 아무런 악의도 없는 신사요, 분별 있는 사람이며, 처신이 훌륭하고, 풍채가 잘생기고 의젓하며, 침착하고 진중한 낯빛에, 매혹적이고 아름다운 얼굴에, 호감 가는 온갖 것을 다 갖춘 사람이었다. 다만 불행히도 그 전날 밤 술을 좀 마셨고, 우리가 함께 있을 때 그가 말한 대로 잠자리에 들지 못했으며, 몸이 달아오르고 피가 술로 끓어, 그런 상태에서 그의 이성이 말하자면 잠들어 그를 내맡겨 버린 것이었다.\n\n나로 말하자면, 내 볼일은 그의 돈이요, 그에게서 챙길 수 있는 것이었다. 그리고 그 뒤에, 만일 어떻게든 할 길을 찾을 수만 있었다면, 나는 그를 그의 집으로, 그의 가족에게로 무사히 돌려보냈을 것이니, 십중팔구 그에게는 정직하고 정숙한 아내와 죄 없는 자식들이 있어, 그의 안위를 걱정하며 그를 집으로 데려와 그가 제정신을 되찾을 때까지 돌보고 싶어 했을 것이기 때문이다. 그러면 그는 얼마나 부끄러움과 후회로 자신을 돌아보겠는가! 창녀와, 그것도 온 도시의 진흙과 오물 속, 그 모든 소굴 가운데 가장 고약한 회랑에서 집어 든 창녀와 어울린 것을 두고 얼마나 자신을 책망하겠는가! 매독에 걸렸을까 봐, 화살이 자기 간을 꿰뚫었을까 봐 얼마나 벌벌 떨겠으며, 자기 방탕의 광기와 야만을 돌아볼 때마다 얼마나 자신을 미워하겠는가! 만일 그에게 명예의 원칙이 조금이라도 있었다면, 실로 나는 그에게 있었으리라 믿거니와, 말하건대 그가 어떤 몹쓸 병에 걸렸다면, 그로서는 알 수 없으나 걸렸을 수도 있으니, 그 병을 자기의 정숙하고 덕스러운 아내에게 옮겨, 그리하여 자기 후손의 생피에 그 전염병의 씨를 뿌린다는 생각을 그는 얼마나 혐오하겠는가.\n\n그런 신사들이, 이런 경우에 자기들이 상대하는 바로 그 여자들이 그들에 대해 품는 경멸스러운 생각을 한번 헤아려 본다면, 그것은 그들에게 진저리 나는 일이 될 것이다. 앞서 말했듯이, 그들은 쾌락을 중히 여기지 않고, 남자에 대한 어떤 끌림으로 달뜨지도 않으며, 그 수동적인 계집은 돈 말고는 어떤 쾌락도 생각지 않으니, 그가 말하자면 자기의 사악한 쾌락의 황홀경에 취해 있을 때, 그녀의 손은 그의 주머니에 들어가 거기서 찾아낼 수 있는 것을 뒤지고 있는데, 그는 그 어리석음의 순간에 그것을 미리 헤아릴 수 없었던 것 못지않게 그 순간에도 그것을 조금도 느끼지 못한다.\n\n나는 어떤 사내에게, 실로 더 나은 대접을 받을 자격이 없는 사내에게 어찌나 능숙했던지, 그가 다른 식으로 그녀와 분주한 동안에 이십 기니가 든 그의 지갑을, 그가 그녀를 경계해 넣어 둔 시계 주머니에서 빼내고, 그 자리에 도금한 가짜 돈이 든 다른 지갑을 넣어 둔 여자를 하나 알았다. 일을 다 마친 뒤 그가 그녀에게 말하기를, \"자, 너 내 주머니 털지 않았겠지?\" 했다. 그녀는 그와 농을 주고받으며, 그가 잃을 것이 많지는 않았으리라 짐작한다고 말했다. 그가 시계 주머니에 손을 넣어 손가락으로 자기 지갑이 거기 있는 것을 느끼고는 완전히 안심했으니, 그렇게 그녀는 그의 돈을 챙겼다. 그리고 이것이 그녀에게는 장사였으니, 그녀는 가짜 금시계, 곧 은에 도금한 시계와 가짜 돈이 든 지갑을 주머니에 지녀 그런 온갖 경우에 대비해 두었고, 나는 그녀가 그것을 성공적으로 써먹었으리라 의심치 않는다.\n\n나는 이 마지막 노획물을 들고 내 후견인에게 돌아왔는데, 실로 내가 그 이야기를 들려주자 그녀는 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지 눈물을 참기 어려울 지경이었으니, 그런 신사가 술 한 잔이 머리에 오를 때마다 날마다 파멸할 위험을 무릅쓴다는 것을 알고서였다.\n\n그러나 내가 챙긴 노획물에 관해, 그리고 내가 그를 얼마나 깡그리 털었는지에 관해서는, 그녀가 그것이 자기를 놀랍도록 기쁘게 한다고 말했다. \"아니, 얘야,\" 하고 그녀가 말하기를, \"그렇게 당해 본 것이, 내가 알기로는, 그가 평생 듣게 될 그 모든 설교보다 그를 더 바로잡아 줄지도 모른단다.\" 그리고 이 이야기의 나머지가 사실이라면, 정말로 그러했다.\n\n이튿날 나는 그녀가 이 신사에 대해 놀랍도록 캐묻고 다니는 것을 알게 되었으니, 내가 그녀에게 들려준 그에 대한 묘사, 곧 그의 옷차림이며, 그의 풍채며, 그의 얼굴이며, 모든 것이 한데 들어맞아, 그녀는 자기가 그 인품을, 또 그 가문까지 아는 어떤 신사를 떠올리게 되었다. 그녀가 잠시 곰곰이 생각하는 사이, 내가 여전히 그 세세한 것을 이어 가자, 그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말하기를, \"내가 100파운드를 걸고 말하건대, 나는 그 신사를 알아.\"\n\n\"그러지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"세상 어떤 일이 있어도 그분이 망신당하는 건 바라지 않으니까요. 그분은 이미 저 때문에 충분히 봉변을 당하셨고, 저는 그분께 더는 어떤 짓도 하는 도구가 되고 싶지 않아요.\" \"아니, 아니야,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"장담컨대 나는 그에게 어떤 봉변도 끼치지 않을 거란다. 다만 내 호기심을 좀 풀게 해 다오. 그가 그 사람이라면, 내가 틀림없이 알아낼 테니.\" 나는 그 말에 조금 흠칫 놀라, 얼굴에 역력한 근심을 띤 채 그녀에게 말하기를, 같은 이치로 그가 나를 찾아낼 수도 있고, 그러면 나는 끝장이라고 했다. 그녀가 발끈하여 대꾸하기를, \"원, 얘야, 내가 너를 팔아넘기리라 생각하느냐? 아니, 아니다,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그가 세상에서 가진 그 모든 것을 준다 해도 그러지 않아. 나는 이보다 더 고약한 일에서도 네 비밀을 지켜 주었는데, 이런 일에서야 분명 나를 믿을 수 있지 않겠느냐.\" 그래서 나는 그때는 더 말하지 않았다.\n\n그녀는 다른 식으로 계책을 세웠고, 내게 알리지도 않은 채, 그러나 가능하다면 그것을 알아내기로 마음먹었다. 그래서 그녀는 자기가 짐작하는 그 가문에 정통한 어떤 친구에게 가, 그 친구에게 자기가 그런 신사와(그런데 그는 다름 아닌 준남작이요, 무척 좋은 가문 출신이었다) 특별한 볼일이 있는데, 자기를 소개해 줄 누군가 없이는 그에게 다가갈 방도를 모르겠다고 말했다. 그녀의 친구는 무척 선뜻 그렇게 해 주겠다고 약속했고, 그에 따라 그 신사가 시내에 있는지 알아보러 그 집으로 갔다.\n\n이튿날 그녀는 내 후견인에게 와서, 아무개 경이 댁에 계시기는 한데, 봉변을 당해 무척 편찮으셔서 말을 나눌 수가 없다고 전했다. \"무슨 봉변요?\" 하고 내 후견인이, 마치 그것에 놀라기라도 한 듯 다급히 물었다. \"글쎄,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말하기를, \"그분이 햄스테드에 아는 신사를 찾아뵈러 가셨다가, 돌아오시는 길에 습격당해 강도를 만나셨다지 뭐예요. 게다가 그분도 술을 좀 드신 터라, 그놈들이 그분을 함부로 다뤄서, 무척 편찮으시대요.\" \"강도라니!\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그래, 그놈들이 그분에게서 무얼 가져갔답니까?\" \"그게,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말하기를, \"그분의 금시계와 금 코담배갑, 그분의 멋진 가발, 그리고 주머니에 지니셨던 돈을 가져갔다는데, 그게 분명 적지 않은 액수였을 거예요. 아무개 경께서는 늘 기니가 든 지갑을 지니고 다니시거든요.\"\n\n\"피!\" 하고 내 늙은 후견인이 비웃으며 말했다. \"장담컨대 그분이 이제 술에 취해 창녀를 하나 집어 들었는데, 그 여자가 그분의 주머니를 털었고, 그래서 그분이 아내에게 돌아와 강도를 당했다고 한 게지요. 그건 케케묵은 속임수예요. 가엾은 여자들에게 그런 수작이 날마다 천 가지는 행해진다니까요.\" \"원, 별말씀을!\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"아무개 경을 모르시는군요. 그분으로 말하면 더없이 점잖은 신사라, 온 도시를 통틀어 그분보다 더 훌륭한 사람도, 더 침착하고 진중하고 정숙한 사람도 없어요. 그분은 그런 일을 질색하신답니다. 그분을 아는 사람이라면 누구도 그분을 두고 그런 생각은 안 할 거예요.\" \"그래요, 그래요,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그건 내가 알 바 아니지요. 내 일이라면야 장담컨대 거기에 그런 종류의 무언가가 있다는 걸 알아낼 테지만요. 흔히들 정숙하다는 남자들이 때로는 다른 사람들보다 나을 것도 없고, 다만 더 나은 평판을 지킬 뿐이거나, 굳이 말하자면 더 능란한 위선자일 뿐이지요.\" \"아니, 아니에요,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"장담하건대 아무개 경은 위선자가 아니에요. 그분은 정말로 정직하고 침착한 신사이고, 그분은 분명 강도를 당하신 거예요.\" \"원,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 그건 내 일이 아니라니까요. 나는 그저 그분과 이야기를 나누고 싶을 뿐이에요. 내 볼일은 다른 성질의 것이거든요.\" \"하지만,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"볼일이 무슨 성질의 것이든 아직은 그분을 뵐 수 없어요. 그분은 뵐 만한 상태가 아니거든요. 무척 편찮으시고, 멍도 심하게 드셨어요.\" \"이런,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"아니, 그렇다면 그분이 분명 나쁜 손에 떨어지셨군요.\" 그러고는 진중하게 물었다. \"그래, 그분이 어디를 다치셨답니까?\" \"글쎄, 머리하고,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"한쪽 손, 그리고 얼굴이에요. 그놈들이 그분을 무지막지하게 다뤘거든요.\" \"가엾은 신사로군요,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 나는 그분이 회복하실 때까지 기다려야겠네요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"오래 걸리지 않기를 바라요. 나는 그분과 무척 이야기를 나누고 싶거든요.\"\n\n그녀는 내게 와서 이 이야기를 들려주었다. \"내가 네 멋진 신사를 알아냈는데, 정말 멋진 신사더구나,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 가엾기도 해라, 지금은 딱한 꼴이 되었지 뭐냐. 도대체 네가 그에게 무슨 짓을 했는지 모르겠구나. 원, 네가 그를 거의 죽여 놓았어.\" 나는 적잖이 당황하여 그녀를 바라보았다. \"제가 그분을 죽였다고요!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"사람을 잘못 보신 게 틀림없어요. 분명 저는 그분께 아무 짓도 안 했는걸요. 제가 떠날 때 그분은 아주 멀쩡하셨어요.\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그저 취해서 곤히 잠드셨을 뿐이라고요.\" \"그건 나도 모르겠다만,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"어쨌든 지금은 딱한 꼴이란다.\" 그러고는 그녀의 친구가 자기에게 한 말을 죄다 들려주었다. \"그렇다면,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 떠난 뒤에 그분이 나쁜 손에 떨어지신 거예요. 제가 떠날 때 분명 그분을 멀쩡하게 두고 왔으니까요.\"\n\n열흘쯤 뒤에, 아니면 그보다 조금 더 지나, 내 후견인은 다시 그녀의 친구에게 가, 자기를 이 신사에게 소개해 달라고 했다. 그녀는 그동안 다른 경로로도 알아보아, 그가 다시 거동하게 되었고, 아직 바깥출입까지는 아니어도 그러함을 알아냈던 터라, 그와 이야기를 나눌 허락을 얻었다.\n\n그녀는 응대가 감탄할 만한 여자라 자기를 소개해 줄 사람이 따로 필요 없었으니, 그녀는 내가 그녀를 대신해 이야기할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 그 이야기를 풀어냈는데, 앞서 이미 말했듯이 그녀는 입담의 명수였기 때문이다. 그녀는 그에게, 비록 낯선 사람이긴 하나 오로지 그에게 도움이 되고자 하는 단 하나의 뜻으로 왔으며, 그것 말고 다른 속셈이 없음을 알게 되리라고 말했다. 그리고 자기가 순전히 그토록 우정 어린 까닭으로 온 것이니, 만일 그가 자기가 주제넘게 제안하는 바를 받아들이지 않더라도, 자기가 자기 일도 아닌 데에 참견한 것을 언짢게 여기지 말아 달라고 그에게 약속을 청했다. 그녀는 그에게, 자기가 할 말은 오직 그에게만 관계되는 비밀이니만큼, 그가 자기 제안을 받아들이든 받아들이지 않든, 그가 스스로 그것을 폭로하지 않는 한 온 세상에 비밀로 남으리라고 장담했다. 또 그가 그 일에서 자기의 도움을 거절한다 해도 그녀가 그토록 옹졸하게 자기 존중심을 드러내어 그에게 조금이라도 봉변을 끼치는 일은 없을 것이니, 그가 옳다고 여기는 대로 행할 완전한 자유를 누리게 되리라고 했다.\n\n그는 처음에 무척 경계하는 낯빛으로, 자기에게 그토록 비밀을 요하는 무슨 일이 있는지 모르겠다고 했다. 자기는 어느 누구에게도 잘못한 적이 없으며, 누가 자기에 대해 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않는다고, 누구에게든 부당하게 구는 것은 자기 인품의 일부가 아니며, 어떤 사람이 무슨 일로 자기에게 도움을 줄 수 있을지 상상도 못 하겠다고 했다. 그러나 만일 그것이 그녀의 말마따나 그토록 사심 없는 도움이라면, 누가 자기에게 도움을 주려 애쓰는 것을 언짢게 여길 수는 없겠노라고 했다. 그러고는 말하자면 그녀에게, 옳다고 여기는 대로 자기에게 말하든 말든 하라는 자유를 남겼다.\n\n그녀는 그가 어찌나 더없이 무심한지, 그와 본론으로 들어가기가 거의 두려웠으나, 그래도 몇 마디 다른 에두른 말을 한 뒤에 그에게 말하기를, 이상하고 설명하기 어려운 우연으로, 자기가 근래 그가 빠졌던 그 불행한 모험에 대해 각별히 알게 되었으며, 그것도 온 세상에 자기와 그 말고는, 아니 그와 함께 있던 바로 그 사람조차도 그것을 아는 이가 없을 만큼 그러하다고 했다.\n\n그는 처음에 조금 화난 듯이 바라보았다. \"무슨 모험요?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그게,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"나이츠브——에서 돌아오시다 강도를 만나신 일 말입니다. 햄스테드라고 해야겠군요, 나리.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"놀라지 마십시오, 나리,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그날 나리께서 스미스필드의 회랑에서 나이츠브리지의 스프링 가든으로, 거기서 다시 스트랜드의 아무개 집으로 옮기신 걸음걸음을, 그리고 그 뒤에 어떻게 마차 안에 잠든 채 남겨지셨는지를 죄다 말씀드릴 수 있다고 해서 말입니다. 말씀드리건대, 이것에 놀라지 마십시오. 나리, 저는 나리를 등쳐 먹으러 온 것이 아니라, 나리께 아무것도 청하지 않으며, 장담컨대 나리와 함께 있던 그 여자는 나리가 누구신지 전혀 모르며 앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 것입니다. 그러면서도 어쩌면 저는 나리께 더 큰 도움을 드릴 수 있을지도 모릅니다. 저는 단지 제가 이런 일들을 전해 들었다는 것을 알려 드리러, 마치 그것을 감춰 주는 대가로 뇌물이라도 바라는 양 온 것이 아니니까요. 나리,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"장담하건대, 나리께서 제게 무엇을 하시거나 말씀하시는 것이 옳다고 여기시든, 그것은 지금처럼 죄다 비밀로 남을 것입니다. 마치 제가 무덤 속에 있기라도 한 것처럼요.\"\n\n그는 그녀의 말에 깜짝 놀라, 진중하게 그녀에게 말했다. \"부인, 부인은 제게 낯선 분이지만, 제 평생 가장 못된 행실의, 그리고 제가 너무도 당연히 부끄러워하는 일의 비밀을 부인께서 알게 되셨다는 것은 무척 불운한 일입니다. 그 일에서 제게 유일한 위안이라면, 그것이 오직 하느님과 제 양심만 아는 일이라 여겼다는 것이었으니까요.\" \"이런, 나리,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그것이 제게 알려진 것을 나리의 불행의 일부로 여기지 마십시오. 그것은, 제 생각에, 나리께서 얼떨결에 빠져드신 일이고, 어쩌면 그 여자가 무슨 술수를 써서 나리를 부추겼을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 나리께서는 제가 그것을 전해 듣게 된 것을 후회하실 어떤 정당한 까닭도 결코 찾지 못하실 것입니다.\" 하고 그녀가 말했다. \"또한 나리 자신의 입도, 제가 그 일에서 입을 다물어 온 만큼, 그리고 앞으로도 언제나 다물 만큼보다 더 입이 무거울 수는 없을 것입니다.\"\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"하지만 그 여자에게도 좀 공평하게 대해 줍시다. 그녀가 누구든, 장담하건대 그녀는 제게 아무것도 부추기지 않았고, 오히려 저를 마다했습니다. 그 모든 일에 저를 빠뜨린 것은, 그래요, 그녀까지 빠뜨린 것은 제 자신의 어리석음과 광기였습니다. 그 점만큼은 그녀에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려야겠지요. 그녀가 제게서 가져간 것으로 말하자면, 제가 처한 형편에서 그녀에게 그보다 못한 것을 기대할 수도 없었고, 지금 이 순간까지도 저는 그녀가 저를 턴 것인지 마부가 턴 것인지 모릅니다. 만일 그녀가 그랬다면 저는 그녀를 용서하며, 그렇게 구는 모든 신사도 마찬가지로 당해야 마땅하다고 생각합니다. 그러나 저는 그녀가 제게서 가져간 그 모든 것보다 다른 어떤 것들이 더 마음에 걸립니다.\"\n\n이제 내 후견인은 그 모든 일의 본론으로 들어가기 시작했고, 그는 그녀에게 거리낌 없이 속을 털어놓았다. 먼저 그녀는 그가 나에 대해 한 말에 답하여 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 나리께서 함께 계셨던 그 사람에게 그토록 공평하게 대하시니 기쁩니다. 장담하건대 그녀는 양갓집 여자이지 거리의 여자가 아닙니다. 그리고 나리께서 그녀를 어떻게 그토록 넘어가게 하셨든, 그것이 그녀의 버릇이 아님은 분명합니다. 나리께서는 실로 큰 위험을 무릅쓰셨습니다, 나리. 그러나 그것이 나리 걱정의 일부라면, 장담컨대 나리께서는 완전히 마음 놓으셔도 됩니다. 감히 장담하건대, 나리 이전에는 그녀의 남편 이후로 어떤 남자도 그녀에게 손댄 적이 없으며, 그가 죽은 지도 이제 거의 여덟 해입니다.\"\n\n이것이 그의 근심이었고, 그가 그것에 대해 무척 큰 두려움에 떨고 있었던 것이 드러났다. 그러나 내 후견인이 그에게 이렇게 말하자, 그는 무척 흡족해하며 말했다. \"그렇군요, 부인, 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 제가 그 점에 대해 확신할 수만 있다면, 제가 잃은 것은 그리 대수롭지 않게 여길 것입니다. 그것으로 말하자면, 유혹이 컸고, 어쩌면 그녀는 가난해서 그것이 필요했을 테니까요.\" \"그녀가 가난하지 않았더라면, 아무개 경,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"장담하건대 그녀는 결코 나리께 응하지 않았을 것입니다. 그리고 그녀의 가난이 처음에 그녀를 움직여 나리께서 하시는 대로 내버려 두게 한 것처럼, 바로 그 가난이 끝에 가서 그녀를 움직여, 나리께서 그런 형편에 계신 것을 보고 스스로 제 값을 챙기게 한 것입니다. 만일 그녀가 그러지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 다음 마부가 그랬을 테니까요.\"\n\n\"그렇군요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그녀에게 큰 복이 되기를. 다시 말하건대, 그렇게 구는 모든 신사는 마찬가지로 당해 마땅하며, 그러면 그들도 자신을 조심하게 되겠지요. 저는 그것에 대해서는 더 개의치 않습니다. 다만 부인께서 앞서 넌지시 비치신 그 점에 관해서는 다릅니다.\" 여기서 그는 우리 사이에 오간 일을 두고 그녀와 좀 거리낌 없는 이야기를 나누었는데, 그것은 여자가 적기에는 그리 적절치 않은 것이며, 또 자기 아내와 관련하여 그의 마음에 얹힌 큰 두려움, 곧 자기가 나에게서 무슨 몹쓸 것을 옮았을까, 그래서 그것을 더 옮기게 될까 하는 두려움에 관한 것이었다. 그러고는 마침내 그녀에게, 자기가 나와 이야기 나눌 기회를 주선해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 물었다. 내 후견인은 그에게, 내가 그런 일에서 깨끗한 여자이며, 그가 그 점에 관해서는 자기 아내와 함께 있는 것만큼이나 완전히 안전하다고 거듭 장담했다. 그러나 나를 만나는 것으로 말하자면, 그녀는 그것이 위험한 결과를 부를 수 있다고 했다. 그러나 어쨌든 자기가 나와 이야기해 보고 그에게 내 대답을 알려 주겠다고 하면서, 동시에 그것을 바라지 말라고 그를 설득할 몇 가지 논거를 댔으니, 곧 그가 나와 다시 왕래를 트고 싶어 하지 않기를 바라는 만큼, 그것이 그에게 아무런 도움도 되지 않을 것이며, 또 내 처지에서는 그것이 일종의 내 목숨을 그의 손에 맡기는 일이라고 했다.\n\n그는 그녀에게, 자기가 무척 나를 만나고 싶으며, 나를 이용해 어떤 잇속도 챙기지 않겠다는, 자기 힘이 닿는 어떤 보장이든 그녀에게 주겠다고 했다. 또 무엇보다 먼저 자기가 내게 온갖 종류의 청구에 대한 전면적인 면책을 주겠노라 했다. 그녀는 그것이 어떻게 그 비밀을 더 퍼뜨리는 쪽으로 이어질 수 있는지, 그리고 끝에 가서는 그에게 해가 될 수 있는지를 강조하며, 그것을 조르지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 그리하여 마침내 그는 단념했다.\n\n그들은 그가 잃은 물건들을 두고 얼마간 이야기를 나누었고, 그는 그 금시계를 무척 되찾고 싶어 했고, 만약 그녀가 그것을 구해다 줄 수 있다면 값이 나가는 만큼 기꺼이 치르겠노라고 했다. 그녀는 그것을 구해 보겠노라 했고, 값은 그가 매기도록 그에게 맡기겠다고 했다.\n\n이렇게 하여 이튿날 그녀가 시계를 가져갔고, 그는 그 값으로 삼십 기니를 주었는데, 그것은 내가 받아 낼 수 있었을 것보다 더 많은 액수였으니, 아무래도 처음 산 값은 훨씬 더 나갔던 모양이었다. 그는 자기 가발에 대해서도 무어라 말했는데, 그것은 예순 기니나 들었던 듯했고, 또 코담뱃갑에 대해서도 말하기에, 며칠 더 지나 그녀가 그것들마저 가져다주었으니, 그는 무척 고마워하며 삼십 기니를 더 주었다. 이튿날 나는 그의 멋진 검과 지팡이를 거저 돌려보냈고 아무것도 요구하지 않았으나, 그를 보고 싶은 마음은 없었으니, 다만 그가 자기가 누구인지를 내가 안다는 사실을 확인하고 싶어 했더라면 모를 일이었지만, 그는 그것을 원치 않았다.\n\n그러자 그는 그녀가 어떻게 이 모든 일을 알게 되었는지를 두고 긴 이야기를 나누었다. 그녀는 그 대목에 관해 긴 이야기를 지어냈으니, 내가 전말을 다 털어놓은 어떤 사람에게서 들었고, 그 사람이 내가 물건을 처분하는 것을 도와주기로 한 사람이며, 그 심복이 자기에게 물건을 가져왔는데, 자기는 직업이 전당포 주인인지라, 그분의 봉변을 전해 듣고 일을 대충 짐작했고, 물건이 자기 손에 들어온 터라 이렇게 한번 시도해 보기로 결심했노라고 했다. 그러고는 그것이 결코 자기 입 밖으로 새어 나가지 않으리라고 거듭 다짐하면서, 그 여자(나를 가리키는 것이었다)를 매우 잘 알지만 그녀에게는 그 일에 관해, 다시 말해 그 사람이 누구인지에 관해 아무것도 알리지 않았노라고 했으니, 그 말은 사실 거짓이었으나, 어찌 되었든 그에게 해가 될 것은 없었으니, 나는 그 일에 관해 누구에게도 입을 연 적이 없었기 때문이다.\n\n그를 다시 만나는 문제를 두고 내 머릿속에는 갖가지 생각이 오갔고, 그것을 거절했던 일을 자주 후회했다. 만약 내가 그를 만나, 내가 그를 안다는 것을 알게 했더라면, 그에게서 무언가 득을 보았을 테고, 어쩌면 그에게서 생계를 얻었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었으니, 그것은 충분히 사악한 삶이긴 했어도, 내가 발을 들인 이 일만큼 위험으로 가득 차 있지는 않았던 것이다. 그러나 그런 생각들도 점차 사그라들어, 나는 이번만은 그를 다시 만나기를 마다했다. 그런데 내 후견인은 그를 자주 만났고, 그는 그녀에게 매우 친절하여, 만날 때마다 거의 빠짐없이 무언가를 주었다. 특히 한번은 그녀가 그를 보니 무척 흥겨워하고 있었는데, 그녀가 보기에 술기운이 좀 있는 듯했고, 그가 그날 밤 자기를 그토록 홀려 놓은 그 여자를 다시 보게 해 달라고 또 한 번 몹시 간곡하게 졸라 대자, 처음부터 내가 그를 만나는 쪽이었던 내 후견인은, 그가 그토록 간절히 바라니 만약 자기가 나를 설득할 수만 있다면 거의 응해 줄 수도 있겠노라 하고는, 지난 일을 잊겠다고 거듭 다짐만 한다면 저녁에 자기 집으로 와 주시면 한번 애써 보겠노라고 덧붙였다.\n\n이렇게 하여 그녀는 나를 찾아와 그 모든 이야기를 전했고, 한마디로, 전에 거절했던 일을 마음 한구석에 다소 후회하고 있던 터라, 그녀는 이내 나를 동의 쪽으로 기울게 만들었다. 그리하여 나는 그를 만날 채비를 했다. 단언하건대 나는 할 수 있는 한 가장 돋보이게 차려입었고, 난생처음 약간의 기교를 부렸으니, 처음이라 함은 전에는 화장이라는 천한 짓에 한 번도 굴한 적이 없었기 때문이니, 늘 그런 것은 필요 없다고 믿을 만한 허영심이 충분히 있었던 것이다.\n\n약속한 시각에 그가 왔고, 그녀가 앞서 보았던 대로 여전히 분명했으니, 그가 술을 마셨다는 것이었는데, 다만 우리가 흔히 취했다고 하는 정도와는 거리가 멀었다. 그는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하는 듯했고, 나와 더불어 지난 일을 두고 긴 이야기에 들어갔다. 나는 거기서 내가 맡은 몫에 대해 그에게 여러 번 용서를 빌었고, 처음 그를 만났을 때 그런 의도는 조금도 없었으며, 다만 그가 매우 점잖은 신사라고 여겼고 또 내게 어떤 무례도 범하지 않겠다고 그토록 여러 번 약속했기에 함께 나섰던 것이라고 항변했다.\n\n그는 자기가 마신 술을 핑계 삼으며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지조차 거의 몰랐고, 만약 그렇지만 않았더라면 내가 결코 그토록 마음대로 하게 두지 않았으리라고 했다. 그는 아내와 결혼한 이래 나 말고는 어떤 여자도 손댄 적이 없으며, 그것은 자기에게도 뜻밖의 일이었노라고 항변했고, 내가 자기에게 그토록 각별히 마음에 든다고 추어올리며 그런 따위의 말을 늘어놓았으니, 마침내 나는 그가 같은 일을 다시 한번 벌일 만한 기분으로 스스로를 거의 몰아갔다는 것을 알아챘다. 그러나 나는 그를 단번에 막아섰다. 나는 남편이 죽은 이래—그것이 근 여덟 해 전이었으니—어떤 남자도 나를 손대게 한 적이 없다고 항변했다. 그는 정말 그러리라 믿는다고 했고, 부인(후견인)도 그에게 그쯤 넌지시 일러 주었으며, 바로 그 점에 대한 자기의 생각 때문에 나를 다시 보고 싶었던 것이라고 덧붙였다. 그러고는 이미 한 번 나와 더불어 자기의 덕을 깨뜨렸어도 아무 나쁜 결과가 없었으니, 다시 한번 거기에 모험을 걸어도 안전할 수 있겠다고 하더니, 한마디로, 그것은 내가 예상한 데로, 그리고 차마 옮길 수 없는 데로 흘러갔다.\n\n내 늙은 후견인은 나만큼이나 그것을 미리 내다보았던 터라, 그를 침대가 없는 방으로 이끌었는데, 그 방 안쪽으로는 침대가 있는 작은 방이 또 있어서, 우리는 그날 밤의 나머지를 보내려고 그리로 물러갔다. 한마디로, 얼마 동안 함께 있다가 그는 잠자리에 들었고 밤새 거기 누워 있었다. 나는 물러났다가, 날이 새기 전 아침에 잠옷 차림으로 다시 와서 남은 시간을 그와 함께 누워 있었다.\n\n이렇듯, 여러분도 보다시피, 죄를 한 번 저지른다는 것은 그것을 다시 저지르는 데에 슬픈 빌미가 되니, 유혹이 새로이 찾아들면 온갖 후회와 반성은 다 닳아 없어지는 법이다. 만약 내가 그를 다시 만나는 데 응하지 않았더라면, 그 안의 타락한 욕망은 사그라들었을 테고, 그가 다른 누구와도 그런 일에 빠지지 않았으리라는 것은 십중팔구 옳은 말이니, 내가 정말로 믿건대 그는 전에도 그런 적이 없었던 것이다.\n\n그가 떠날 때, 나는 그가 다시 강탈당하지 않았다는 것을 확인했기를 바란다고 말했다. 그는 그 점에 대해서는 만족하며 나를 다시 믿을 수 있노라 했고, 주머니에 손을 넣어 다섯 기니를 주었으니, 그것은 여러 해 만에 내가 그런 식으로 벌어들인 첫 돈이었다.\n\n나는 그에게서 비슷한 종류의 방문을 여러 번 받았으나, 그는 끝내 안정된 생계 지원의 길로는 들어서지 않았으니, 그것이야말로 내가 가장 흡족해했을 일이었다. 사실 한번은 그가 내게 어떻게 살아가느냐고 물었다. 나는 꽤 재빠르게 대답하기를, 단언하건대 그에게 한 그런 짓을 다른 누구에게도 한 적이 없으며, 다만 정말로 바느질로 일하여 겨우 제 한 몸을 건사할 수 있을 뿐이고, 때로는 그것이 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 전부라서 꽤나 빠듯하게 버텨 간다고 했다.\n\n그는 나를 그 길로 처음 끌어들인 사람이 자기였다는 사실을 두고 스스로를 책망하는 듯했는데, 자기로서는 결코 그럴 작정이 아니었노라고 내게 다짐했다. 그는 자기 죄와 내 죄의 원인이 자기여야 한다는 것이 마음에 좀 걸린다고 했다. 그는 또한 그 죄 자체에 대해, 그리고 자기 자신과 관련된 그 죄의 특정한 정황들에 대해 자주 정당한 성찰을 하곤 했으니, 술이 어떻게 그 욕망을 불러들였는지, 악마가 어떻게 그를 그 자리로 이끌어 유혹할 대상을 찾아냈는지를 두고 늘 스스로 교훈을 끌어내곤 했다.\n\n이런 생각들이 그를 사로잡을 때면 그는 떠나가서, 어쩌면 한 달이 넘도록 다시 오지 않기도 했다. 그러나 그러다가 진지한 부분이 닳아 없어지면 음탕한 부분이 다시 자라나, 그때는 그 사악한 짓을 할 채비를 하고 오는 것이었다. 우리는 한동안 이렇게 살았으니, 그가 이른바 첩살림을 차려 준 것은 아니었으나, 그래도 그는 점잖은 일들을, 내가 일하지 않고도 살아갈 만큼, 더 나아가 옛 장사를 다시 따르지 않고도 살아갈 만큼 충분히 베풀기를 한 번도 거른 적이 없었다.\n\n그러나 이 관계 또한 끝이 있었으니, 한 해쯤 지나자 그가 예전만큼 자주 오지 않는다는 것을 알게 되었고, 마침내 그는 작별 인사도 마다하는 기색 없이 아예 발길을 끊어 버렸다. 그리하여 내 삶의 그 짧은 한 장면은 끝났으니, 그것은 내게 별다른 재산을 보태 주지 않았고, 다만 회개해야 할 거리만 더 늘려 놓았을 뿐이다.\n\n그래도 이 동안에는 나는 꽤 집 안에 틀어박혀 지냈으니, 적어도 이렇게 살림이 마련되어 있는 터라 아무 모험도 벌이지 않았고, 그가 나를 떠난 뒤로 석 달이 넘도록 그러했다. 그러나 밑천이 바닥나는 것을 알고, 또 본전을 헐어 쓰기는 싫었던지라, 나는 옛 장사를 다시 생각하고, 또다시 거리로 나가 두리번거리기 시작했으니, 첫걸음은 꽤나 운이 좋았다.\n\n나는 아주 초라한 옷차림으로 차려입었으니, 내게는 모습을 꾸밀 여러 형태가 있었던 터라, 이번에는 평범한 모직 가운에 파란 앞치마, 그리고 밀짚모자 차림으로, 세인트존 거리의 스리컵스 여관 문간에 자리를 잡았다. 그 여관에는 여러 짐꾼이 드나들었고, 바닛이며 토터리지며 그쪽 방면의 다른 고을로 가는 역마차들이 저녁이면 출발 채비를 할 때 늘 거리에 서 있었으니, 그래서 나는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 어느 것에든 손쓸 채비가 되어 있었다. 그 까닭은 이러하니, 사람들이 더러 보따리며 작은 꾸러미를 들고 그런 여관들로 와서 자기가 원하는 짐꾼이나 마차를 불러 그것을 시골로 실어 보내곤 하는데, 거기에는 대개 여자들—짐꾼의 아내나 딸들—이 대기하고 있다가, 저마다 자기를 고용한 사람들을 위해 그런 물건들을 맡아 받곤 했던 것이다.\n\n참으로 묘하게도, 내가 여관 문간에 서 있을 때, 전부터 거기 서 있던 한 여자—바닛 역마차에 속한 짐꾼의 아내였다—가 나를 눈여겨보더니, 어느 마차를 기다리느냐고 물었다. 나는 그렇다고, 바닛으로 가시려고 오시는 내 마님을 기다린다고 했다. 그녀가 내 마님이 누구냐고 묻기에, 나는 마침 떠오르는 아무 부인의 이름이나 댔는데, 공교롭게도 내가 댄 이름은, 바닛 바로 너머 해들리에 그 성을 쓰는 가문이 살고 있는 그런 이름이었던 모양이었다.\n\n나는 한참 동안 그녀에게 더 말하지 않았고, 그녀도 내게 더 말하지 않았다. 그런데 이윽고 누군가가 조금 떨어진 어느 문간에서 그녀를 부르자, 그녀는 만약 누가 바닛 마차를 찾거든 그 집—아무래도 선술집인 듯했다—으로 가서 자기를 불러 달라고 내게 청했다. 나는 매우 선뜻 그러겠다고 했고, 그녀는 가 버렸다.\n\n그녀가 가자마자 한 계집아이가 아이 하나를 데리고 씩씩거리며 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리면서 와서는 바닛 마차를 찾았다. 나는 즉시 \"여기요\" 하고 대답했다. \"바닛 마차에 속한 사람이에요?\" 하고 그녀가 물었다. \"그렇단다, 얘야.\" 내가 말했다. \"무엇이 필요하니?\" \"승객 두 사람 자리가 필요해요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그분들이 어디 계시니, 얘야?\" 내가 말했다. \"여기 이 여자아이요, 부디 마차에 태워 주세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 가서 마님을 모셔 올게요.\" \"그럼 서두르렴, 얘야.\" 내가 말했다. \"안 그러면 자리가 다 찰지도 모르니까.\" 그 하녀는 겨드랑이에 큼직한 보따리를 끼고 있었다. 그래서 그녀가 아이를 마차에 태우자, 나는 \"네 보따리도 마차에 넣는 게 좋겠구나\" 하고 말했다. \"아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"누가 아이한테서 슬쩍 채 갈까 봐 걱정돼요.\" \"그럼 내게 다오.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 잘 봐주마.\" \"그래 주세요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 꼭 잘 챙겨 주세요.\" \"내가 책임지마.\" 내가 말했다. \"이게 이십 파운드짜리라 해도 말이다.\" \"그럼 자, 받으세요.\" 그녀가 말하고는 가 버렸다.\n\n보따리를 손에 넣자마자, 그리고 그 하녀가 보이지 않게 되자, 나는 짐꾼의 아내가 있는 선술집 쪽으로 걸어갔으니, 만약 그녀를 만났더라면 그저 보따리를 건네주고, 떠나야 해서 더는 있을 수 없는 양 그녀에게 일을 보라고 부르려던 것처럼 보였을 터였다. 그러나 그녀를 만나지 않았으므로, 나는 그대로 걸어가, 차터하우스 골목으로 꺾어 들어 바살러뮤 클로즈로 가로질러 갔고, 그리하여 리틀브리튼으로, 또 블루코트 자선원을 지나 뉴게이트 거리로 들어섰다.\n\n신원이 들통나지 않도록, 나는 파란 앞치마를 벗어, 전에는 무늬 입힌 옥양목 조각으로 싸여 있어 매우 눈에 띄던 그 보따리를 거기에 쌌다. 또 밀짚모자도 거기에 싸 넣고, 그리하여 그 보따리를 머리에 이었으니, 이렇게 한 것이 참으로 다행이었던 것이, 블루코트 자선원을 지나오다가 하필 누구를 만났겠는가, 다름 아닌 그 보따리를 내게 맡겼던 그 계집아이였다. 아무래도 그녀는 모셔 오러 갔던 마님과 함께 바닛 마차로 가는 길인 듯했다.\n\n그녀가 바쁘다는 것을 알았고, 나로서도 그녀를 붙들 까닭이 없었으니, 그녀는 그대로 가 버렸고, 나는 보따리를 무사히 내 후견인에게 가져왔다. 그 보따리에는 돈도, 은붙이도, 보석도 없었으나, 매우 좋은 인도산 다마스크 한 벌—가운과 페티코트—과 아주 좋은 플랜더스 레이스로 된 레이스 머리 장식과 소맷동, 그리고 약간의 아마포와 그 밖에 내가 그 값어치를 아주 잘 아는 물건들이 들어 있었다.\n\n이것은 사실 내가 고안한 것이 아니라, 그것을 성공적으로 써먹어 본 어떤 사람이 내게 일러 준 것이었고, 내 후견인은 그것을 무척 마음에 들어 했다. 사실 나는 그 수법을 여러 번 다시 써먹었으나, 같은 장소 근처에서는 결코 두 번 하지 않았으니, 다음번에는 화이트채플에서, 바로 페티코트 골목 모퉁이 곁, 스트랫퍼드며 보며 그쪽 방면 시골로 나가는 마차들이 서 있는 데서 해 보았고, 또 한 번은 비숍게이트 바깥의 플라잉호스에서, 그 무렵 체스턴행 마차들이 서던 데서 해 보았다. 그리고 나는 늘 운이 좋아 무언가 노획물을 들고 빠져나왔다.\n\n또 한 번은 강가의 어느 창고 옆에 자리를 잡았으니, 그곳은 뉴캐슬어폰타인이며 선덜랜드며 그 밖의 데에서 오는 북쪽의 연안 배들이 닿는 데였다. 여기서, 창고가 닫혀 있을 때, 한 젊은이가 편지를 들고 와서는, 뉴캐슬어폰타인에서 온 상자 하나와 광주리 하나를 찾았다. 나는 그에게 그것의 표지를 가지고 있느냐고 물었고, 그러자 그는 그것을 청하는 근거가 될 편지를 보여 주었는데, 거기에는 내용물에 대한 설명이 적혀 있었으니, 상자에는 아마포가 가득하고 광주리에는 유리 그릇이 가득하다는 것이었다. 나는 그 편지를 읽고, 이름과 표지를, 곧 물건을 보낸 사람의 이름과 보내진 사람의 이름을 잘 봐 두었다. 그러고는 그 심부름꾼에게 아침에 다시 오라고 일렀으니, 창고지기가 그날 밤에는 더는 거기 있지 않을 것이기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n나는 떠나가, 어느 선술집에서 필기구를 구해, 뉴캐슬의 존 리처드슨 씨가 런던에 있는 친애하는 사촌 제미 콜에게 보내는 편지를 한 통 썼으니, 그가 아무개 배편으로(나는 그 세세한 사항을 토씨 하나까지 다 기억하고 있었다) 허커백 아마포 몇 필, 네덜란드산 홀란드 천 몇 엘 따위를 상자 하나에 담아 보내고, 또 헨질 씨의 유리 공장에서 부싯돌 유리잔 한 광주리를 보낸다는 사연을 적었으며, 그 상자에는 I. C. 1호라고 표시되어 있고 광주리는 묶음줄에 붙인 꼬리표로 표시되어 있다고 했다.\n\n한 시간쯤 뒤, 나는 창고로 가서 창고지기를 만나, 아무 의심도 받지 않고 물건을 넘겨받았으니, 아마포의 값어치는 약 이십이 파운드였다.\n\n나는 이 글 전체를, 날마다 떠오르는 착상이 이끄는 대로 따른, 또 내가 더할 나위 없는 솜씨로, 그리고 늘 성공적으로 해치운 그런 갖가지 모험들로 가득 채울 수도 있을 것이다.\n\n마침내—그토록 자주 우물에 가는 항아리가 어찌 성하게 집으로 돌아오겠는가?—나는 몇 가지 자잘한 곤경에 빠지게 되었는데, 그것들이 나를 치명적으로 해칠 수는 없었으나, 그래도 나를 사람들에게 알려지게 만들었으니, 그것은 유죄판결을 받는 일 다음으로 내게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 고약한 일이었다.\n\n나는 과부의 상복으로 변장을 했으니, 무슨 정해진 속셈이 있어서가 아니라, 그저 흔히 그러했듯이 무슨 일이 생길까 하여 기다리던 것이었다. 마침 내가 코번트가든의 거리를 따라 걸어가고 있을 때 \"도둑이야! 도둑 잡아라!\" 하는 큰 외침이 일었으니, 아무래도 어떤 솜씨꾼들이 어느 가게 주인에게 수작을 부린 듯했고, 쫓기게 되자 그들 가운데 더러는 이쪽으로, 더러는 저쪽으로 달아났는데, 그중 하나가 과부 상복 차림이었다는 것이었다. 그러자 군중이 내 주위로 몰려들어, 더러는 내가 그 사람이라 하고, 더러는 아니라고 했다. 곧이어 그 포목상의 직공이 와서는, 내가 그 사람이라고 큰 소리로 맹세하더니 나를 붙들었다. 그러나 군중에게 이끌려 그 포목상의 가게로 되돌려졌을 때, 그 집 주인은 내가 자기 가게에 있던 그 여자가 아니라고 선뜻 말하며 나를 즉시 놓아주려 했으나, 또 다른 자가 자못 진지하게 말하기를 \"아무개 씨가\"(그 직공을 가리키는 것이었다) \"돌아올 때까지 기다리시지요, 그가 저 여자를 압니다\" 하는 것이었다. 그리하여 그들은 나를 거의 반 시간이나 억지로 붙들어 두었다. 그들이 치안관 한 사람을 불렀고, 그가 내 간수처럼 가게 안에 서 있었으니, 그 치안관과 이야기하면서 나는 그가 어디 사는지, 무슨 직업인지를 물었고, 그 사내는 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어날지 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채 선뜻 자기 이름과 직업, 사는 곳을 일러 주었고, 또 농담 삼아, 내가 올드베일리에 나오게 되면 틀림없이 자기 이름을 듣게 될 것이라고 했다.\n\n하인들 가운데 더러도 나를 건방지게 다루며 손을 대지 않으려고 무던히도 애를 썼고, 주인은 사실 그들보다는 내게 점잖게 굴었으나, 그래도 나를 놓아주려 하지 않았으니, 비록 그는 내가 전에 자기 가게에 있었다고는 말할 수 없노라고 인정하면서도 그러했다.\n\n나는 그에게 조금 퉁명스러워지기 시작하여, 훗날 더 합법적인 방식으로 그에게 앙갚음하더라도 언짢게 여기지 말기를 바란다고 하고, 내가 정당한 대접을 받는 것을 지켜봐 줄 벗들을 부르러 사람을 보내게 해 달라고 청했다. 안 된다고, 그는 그런 자유를 줄 수 없으니, 치안판사 앞에 나가서 청하라고 했다. 그러고는 내가 자기를 위협하는 것을 보고는, 그동안 자기가 나를 단속해 두겠으며, 뉴게이트에 안전하게 가둬 두겠노라고 했다. 나는 지금은 그의 때이지만, 머지않아 내 때가 올 것이라고 말하며, 할 수 있는 한 내 노여움을 다스렸다. 그래도 나는 치안관에게 짐꾼을 한 사람 불러 달라고 했고, 그는 그렇게 해 주었으며, 그러고서 나는 펜과 잉크와 종이를 달라고 했으나 그들은 아무것도 주려 하지 않았다. 나는 그 짐꾼에게 이름과 사는 곳을 물었고, 그 가엾은 사내는 매우 선뜻 일러 주었다. 나는 그에게, 내가 여기서 어떻게 대접받는지를 똑똑히 보고 기억해 두라고, 내가 여기 억지로 붙들려 있는 것을 그가 보았노라고 일렀다. 나는 다른 곳에서 그의 증언이 필요할 것이며, 입을 여는 것이 그에게 나쁠 것 없으리라고 말했다. 그 짐꾼은 진심으로 나를 돕겠노라 했다. \"하지만 마님,\" 그가 말했다. \"저들이 마님을 못 가게 거절하는 걸 제가 듣게 해 주십쇼, 그래야 제가 더 분명하게 말할 수 있을 테니까요.\"\n\n그 말에 나는 가게 주인에게 큰 소리로 말하기를 \"선생, 당신은 양심으로 알고 있겠지만 나는 당신이 찾는 그 사람이 아니고, 전에 당신 가게에 있지도 않았소. 그러니 나를 더는 여기 붙들어 두지 말든가, 아니면 나를 멈춰 세운 이유를 말하시오\" 하였다. 그 사내는 이 말에 전보다 더 퉁명스러워져서, 자기 마음 내키기 전에는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않겠노라 했다. \"아주 좋소.\" 나는 치안관과 짐꾼에게 말했다. \"여러분, 훗날 이것을 꼭 기억해 주시기 바라오.\" 짐꾼은 \"예, 마님\" 하였고, 치안관은 그것이 마음에 들지 않기 시작하여, 포목상에게 자기가 인정했듯이 내가 그 사람이 아닌 이상 나를 풀어 보내라고 설득하려 했다. \"거참, 선생,\" 포목상이 그에게 비꼬듯 말했다. \"당신이 치안판사요, 치안관이요? 내가 이 여자를 당신에게 맡겼으니, 부디 당신 할 일이나 하시오.\" 치안관은 조금 마음이 상했으나 매우 점잖게 말하기를 \"나는 내 할 일과 내가 무엇인지를 압니다, 선생. 정작 당신이야말로 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 모르는 것 같소\" 하였다.\n그들은 또 몇 마디 거친 말을 주고받았고, 그러는 동안 그 직공은 끝까지 뻔뻔하고 사내답지 못하게 굴며 나를 야만스럽게 다루었으니, 그들 가운데 하나—처음에 나를 붙든 바로 그자였다—가 나를 수색하겠노라 하면서 내게 손을 대기 시작했다. 나는 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉고, 치안관을 불러, 내가 어떤 대접을 받는지 똑똑히 봐 두라고 일렀다. \"그리고 치안관 선생,\" 나는 그 사내를 가리키며 말했다. \"저 악당의 이름을 좀 물어봐 주시오.\" 치안관은 그를 점잖게 꾸짖으며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르고 있다고, 자기 주인조차 내가 가게에 있던 사람이 아니라고 인정한 것을 자기도 알고 있다고 말했다. \"그리고,\" 치안관이 말했다. \"만약 이 부인이 자기가 누구이며 어디 있었는지를 증명하게 되고, 자네가 우기는 그 여자가 아니라는 것이 드러나면, 자네 주인이 자기 자신과 나까지도 곤경에 빠뜨리는 게 아닐까 걱정일세.\" \"빌어먹을 년,\" 그 사내가 다시, 뻔뻔하고 모질어진 얼굴로 말했다. \"저 여자가 그 마님이오, 틀림없소이다. 내 맹세컨대 가게에 있던 바로 그 몸뚱이요, 없어진 새틴 조각들을 바로 저 여자 손에 내가 건네줬단 말이오. 윌리엄 씨와 앤서니 씨가(그들은 다른 직공들이었다) 돌아오면 더 들으시게 될 거요. 그들도 나만큼이나 저 여자를 다시 알아볼 테니.\"\n\n그 무례한 악당이 치안관에게 이렇게 지껄이고 있을 바로 그때, 그가 부른 대로 윌리엄 씨와 앤서니 씨가 한 무리의 군중과 함께 돌아왔으니, 그들은 내가 행세한 것으로 되어 있던 그 진짜 과부를 데리고 왔다. 그들은 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 헐떡거리며 가게로 들어와, 자못 의기양양하게, 그 가엾은 것을 더없이 백정 같은 꼴로 끌고서 뒤쪽 가게에 있던 주인 앞으로 데려가더니, 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"여기 과부가 있습니다, 선생. 우리가 마침내 그 여자를 잡았습니다.\" \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\" 주인이 말했다. \"아니, 우리는 이미 그 여자를 잡았다, 저기 앉아 있지 않으냐.\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 아무개 씨가\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 여자가 그 여자라고 맹세할 수 있소.\" 그들이 앤서니 씨라 부른 다른 사내가 대꾸하기를 \"아무개 씨야 무어라 말하든, 무어라 맹세하든 마음대로 하시구려, 하지만 이 여자가 그 여자고, 저기 그녀가 훔친 새틴 자투리가 있소. 내가 내 손으로 그녀 옷에서 꺼냈단 말이오\" 하였다.\n\n나는 이제 가만히 앉아, 한결 마음이 든든해지기 시작했으나, 미소만 지을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 주인은 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 치안관은 돌아서서 나를 바라보았다. \"저들을 내버려 두시오, 치안관 선생.\" 내가 말했다. \"저들이 계속하게 두시오.\" 사정은 명백하여 부인할 수가 없었으니, 치안관은 진짜 도둑을 떠맡게 되었고, 포목상은 내게 매우 점잖게 말하기를, 그 잘못에 대해 미안하며, 내가 그것을 언짢게 여기지 않기를 바란다고, 자기들은 날마다 이런 종류의 일을 하도 많이 당하는 터라, 스스로 정당함을 지키느라 몹시 날카롭게 군다고 해서 탓할 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다. \"언짢게 여기지 말라니, 선생!\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 어찌 좋게 여길 수 있겠소! 만약 당신의 무례한 자가 거리에서 나를 붙들어 당신에게 데려왔을 때, 또 당신 자신이 내가 그 사람이 아니라고 인정했을 때 나를 풀어 주었더라면, 나는 그것을 넘겨 버리고 언짢게 여기지 않았을 것이오, 당신들이 날마다 이런 고약한 일을 많이 당하리라 믿기에 말이오. 하지만 그 뒤로 당신이 나를 대접한 꼴은 참을 수 없는 것이었고, 특히 당신 하인이 그러했으니, 나는 그것에 대해 반드시 배상을 받아야겠고, 또 받을 것이오.\"\n\n그러자 그는 나와 흥정을 시작하여, 무엇이든 합당한 보상을 해 주겠노라 하며, 내가 무엇을 기대하는지 부디 말해 달라고 했다. 나는 내가 내 일의 심판관이 될 수는 없으니, 법이 그것을 나 대신 판결할 것이며, 내가 치안판사 앞에 끌려가게 될 터이니, 내가 할 말은 거기서 그에게 들려주겠노라고 했다. 그는 이제 치안판사 앞에 갈 필요가 없으며, 내가 어디든 가고 싶은 데로 갈 자유가 있다고 하고는, 치안관을 불러, 나를 풀어 주어도 된다고, 나는 방면되었다고 일렀다. 치안관은 그에게 차분히 말하기를 \"선생, 당신은 방금 내가 내 자신이 치안관인지 치안판사인지나 아느냐고 묻고, 내 할 일이나 하라고 하면서 이 부인을 죄수로 내게 맡겼소. 한데 선생, 보아하니 당신은 내 할 일이 무엇인지를 모르는 것 같소, 정작 나를 치안판사로 만들려 하다니 말이오. 하지만 내 말해 두건대 그건 내 권한 밖이오. 죄수를 떠맡았을 때 그를 붙들어 둘 수는 있어도, 그 죄수를 방면할 수 있는 것은 법과 치안판사뿐이오. 그러니 그건 잘못이오, 선생. 당신이 좋게 여기든 아니든 나는 이제 이 여자를 치안판사 앞으로 데려가야 하오.\" 포목상은 처음에는 치안관에게 매우 거만하게 굴었으나, 그 치안관이 마침 삯을 받고 일하는 자가 아니라 착실하고 든실한 부류의 사람이었고(내 생각에 곡물 중개상이었던 듯하다) 분별 있는 사람이었던지라, 자기 할 일을 굽히지 않고, 치안판사에게 가지 않고는 나를 풀어 주려 하지 않았다. 그리고 나 또한 그것을 고집했다.\n포목상은 그것을 보고 \"좋소,\" 하고 치안관에게 말했다. \"당신 좋을 대로 이 여자를 데려가시오. 나는 이 여자한테 할 말이 없으니.\" \"하지만 선생,\" 치안관이 말했다. \"당신도 우리와 함께 가셔야 하오, 이 여자를 내게 맡긴 게 바로 당신이니 말이오.\" \"아니, 나는 안 가오.\" 포목상이 말했다. \"내 말하지 않았소, 이 여자한테 할 말이 없다고.\" \"하지만 부디, 선생, 가셔야 하오.\" 치안관이 말했다. \"당신 자신을 위해서라도 청하는 거요, 당신 없이는 치안판사가 아무것도 할 수 없으니 말이오.\" \"여보게, 이 사람아,\" 포목상이 말했다. \"자네 할 일이나 보러 가게. 내 말하지 않았나, 이 부인한테 할 말이 없다고. 국왕의 이름으로 자네에게 명하니, 이 여자를 풀어 주게.\" \"선생,\" 치안관이 말했다. \"보아하니 당신은 치안관이 된다는 게 무엇인지를 모르는구려. 부디 내가 당신에게 무례하게 굴도록 만들지 마시오.\" \"그럴 필요 없을 것 같네, 자네는 이미 충분히 무례하니.\" 포목상이 말했다. \"아니오, 선생,\" 치안관이 말했다. \"나는 무례하지 않소. 당신이야말로 자기 떳떳한 일을 보던 정직한 여인을 거리에서 데려와 가게에 가두고, 여기서 당신 하인들을 시켜 학대하여 평온을 깨뜨려 놓고서, 이제 와 내가 당신에게 무례하다고 할 수 있소? 내 생각엔, 국왕의 이름으로 당신에게 나와 함께 가라 명하지도 않고, 당신 문 앞을 지나는 사람마다 붙들어 당신을 억지로 끌고 가는 일을 돕고 거들라고 명하지도 않으니, 나는 당신에게 점잖은 거요. 내게 그럴 권한이 있다는 것을 당신도 모르지 않을 텐데, 그래도 나는 그것을 삼가고, 다시 한번 당신에게 나와 함께 가 주기를 청하는 거요.\" 그래도 그는 이 모든 말에도 가려 하지 않았고, 치안관에게 험한 말을 퍼부었다. 그러나 치안관은 성미를 가라앉히고 도발에 넘어가려 하지 않았다. 그러자 내가 끼어들어 말했다. \"자, 치안관 선생, 저 사람은 내버려 두시오. 나는 저 사람을 치안판사 앞에 데려갈 방도를 얼마든지 찾아낼 테니, 그건 걱정하지 않소. 한데 저기 저 사내,\" 내가 말했다. \"저자가 바로 내가 아무 죄 없이 거리를 걸어갈 때 나를 붙든 자이고, 그 뒤로 내게 가한 폭력은 당신이 증인이오. 부디 나로 하여금 저자를 당신에게 맡기게 하여, 치안판사 앞으로 데려가게 해 주시오.\" \"예, 마님.\" 치안관이 말하고는, 그 사내를 향해 돌아서서 그 직공에게 말했다. \"이리 오게, 젊은 양반, 자네는 우리와 함께 가야겠네. 자네 주인은 몰라도 자네는 치안관의 권한 위에 있지는 않을 테니 말이야.\"\n\n그 사내는 사형선고를 받은 도둑 같은 꼴로 뒷걸음질을 치더니, 마치 도와줄 수 있기라도 한 양 제 주인을 바라보았고, 그 주인은 어리석게도 그 사내가 무례하게 굴도록 부추겼으니, 그자는 정말로 치안관에게 맞서서, 치안관이 그를 붙들려고 다가서자 제법 거센 힘으로 그를 밀쳐 냈고, 그러자 치안관이 그를 때려눕히고 도와달라고 외쳤다. 그러자 즉시 가게가 사람들로 가득 찼고, 치안관은 주인과 그 사내와 그의 하인들을 모조리 붙잡았다.\n\n이 소동의 첫 번째 나쁜 결과는 이러했으니, 그들이 붙들었던, 정말로 도둑이었던 그 여자가 빠져나가 군중 속으로 감쪽같이 달아나 버린 것이었고, 그들이 멈춰 세웠던 다른 두 사람도 마찬가지였으니, 그들이 정말로 죄가 있었는지 없었는지는 내가 무어라 말할 수 없다.\n\n이쯤 되자 그의 이웃 가운데 더러가 들어와, 사정을 알아보고는 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지를 보고서, 그 머리에 피가 솟구친 포목상을 정신 차리게 하려 애썼고, 그는 자기가 그르다는 것을 차츰 깨닫기 시작했다. 그리하여 마침내 우리는 다 함께 매우 조용히 치안판사 앞으로 갔으니, 약 오백 명쯤 되는 군중을 뒤에 줄줄이 거느린 채였다. 그리고 가는 내내 나는 사람들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 소리며, 다른 이들이 대꾸하여, 어느 포목상이 도둑 대신 어느 부인을 붙들었다가 그 뒤에 진짜 도둑을 잡았는데, 이제 그 부인이 포목상을 잡아 치안판사 앞으로 끌고 가는 길이라고 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 이것이 사람들을 묘하게 흡족하게 만들어 군중이 더 불어났고, 그들은 가면서 \"어느 놈이 악당이냐? 어느 놈이 포목상이냐?\" 하고 외쳤으니, 특히 여자들이 그러했다. 그러다 그를 보면 \"저놈이다, 저놈이야\" 하고 외쳤고, 이따금 그에게 흙덩이가 제대로 한 방씩 날아들었다. 이렇게 우리는 한참을 행진했으니, 마침내 포목상이 군중에게서 제 몸을 지키려고 치안관에게 마차를 한 대 불러 달라고 청하는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그리하여 치안관과 나, 그리고 포목상과 그의 사내가 남은 길을 마차로 갔다.\n\n블룸즈베리에 있는 한 연로한 신사였던 치안판사 앞에 이르자, 치안관이 먼저 일의 개요를 보고하니, 치안판사는 내게 말해 보라고, 할 말이 있으면 하라고 했다. 그리고 먼저 그가 내 이름을 물었는데, 나는 그것을 대기가 무척 싫었으나 달리 도리가 없었으니, 내 이름은 메리 플랜더스이고, 나는 과부이며, 내 남편은 선장이었는데 버지니아로 가는 항해 중에 죽었노라고 했다. 또 그가 결코 반박할 수 없을 그 밖의 몇 가지 정황도 말했고, 지금은 아무개라는 사람과 함께 시내에 묵고 있다고 하며 내 후견인의 이름을 댔다. 그러나 나는 내 남편의 재산이 놓여 있는 아메리카로 건너갈 채비를 하던 중이었으며, 그날은 약식 상복을 갖춰 입을 옷을 좀 사러 가던 길로, 아직 어느 가게에도 들어가지 않았는데, 그때 저 사내가—포목상의 직공을 가리키며—나를 몹시 놀라게 할 만큼 사납게 달려들어, 나를 제 주인의 가게로 도로 끌고 갔으며, 거기서 그의 주인이 내가 그 사람이 아니라고 인정했음에도 그는 나를 풀어 주려 하지 않고 치안관에게 나를 맡겼다고 했다.\n\n그러고서 나는 그 직공이 나를 어떻게 다루었는지, 그들이 어떻게 내가 벗들 가운데 누구에게도 사람을 보내지 못하게 했는지, 그 뒤에 그들이 어떻게 진짜 도둑을 찾아내어 자기들이 잃은 바로 그 물건을 그 여자에게서 발견했는지, 그리고 앞서 말한 대로 자초지종을 낱낱이 이야기해 나갔다.\n\n그러자 치안관이 자기 쪽 사정을 이야기했으니, 나를 풀어 주는 문제를 두고 포목상과 주고받은 대화며, 마침내 자기가 그 하인을 떠맡겼는데도 그 하인이 함께 가기를 거부한 일이며, 그 주인이 그렇게 하도록 부추긴 일이며, 끝내 그가 치안관을 때린 일 따위를, 모두 내가 이미 말한 대로 늘어놓았다.\n\n그러자 치안판사는 포목상과 그의 사내의 말을 들었다. 포목상은 사실 자기들이 날치기와 도둑들 때문에 날마다 입는 큰 손실에 대해 길게 일장연설을 늘어놓았으니, 자기들로서는 잘못 알기가 쉬운 일이며, 자기가 그것을 알아챘을 때 나를 풀어 주려 했다는 것 따위, 위에서 말한 대로였다. 그 직공으로 말하자면, 다른 하인들이 나더러 정말 그 사람이라고 일러 주었다고 우긴 것 외에는, 할 말이 거의 없었다.\n\n대체로 보아, 치안판사는 무엇보다 먼저 내게 매우 정중하게, 나는 방면되었다고 했다. 그는 포목상의 사내가 그 열띤 추격 가운데 너무도 분별이 없어, 죄 있는 사람 대신 죄 없는 사람을 붙든 것이 매우 유감스럽다고 했고, 만약 그가 그 뒤에 나를 붙들어 두는 부당한 짓만 하지 않았더라면 내가 그 첫 모욕은 용서했으리라 믿는다고 했다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 그들을 공개적으로 꾸짖는 것 외에는—그것은 자기가 하겠다고 했다—내게 어떤 배상도 내려 줄 권한이 자기에게는 없다고 했으나, 내가 법이 일러 주는 방도를 따르리라 짐작한다고 했다. 그동안 그는 그를 보석으로 묶어 두겠노라 했다.\n\n그러나 그 직공이 저지른 평온 침해에 대해서는, 그것에 대해 내게 어느 정도 만족을 주겠노라 하면서, 치안관을 폭행한 죄로, 또 나를 폭행한 죄로 그를 뉴게이트에 가두겠다고 했다.\n\n이렇게 하여 그는 그 폭행죄로 그 사내를 뉴게이트로 보냈고, 그의 주인은 보석금을 내어, 우리는 그곳을 떠났다. 그러나 나는 그 두 사람이 나올 때 군중이 그들을 떠받들어 모시는 꼴을 보는 만족을 누렸으니, 그들이 탄 마차에 함성을 지르고 돌과 흙을 던져 댔던 것이다. 그리하여 나는 내 후견인에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n이 소동을 치르고 집에 돌아와 내 후견인에게 그 이야기를 들려주자, 그녀는 나를 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"왜 그리 즐거워하세요?\" 내가 말했다. \"이 이야기에는 당신이 생각하는 만큼 웃을 거리가 그리 많지 않아요. 정말이지 나는 한 무리의 고약한 악당들 때문에 무던히도 부산을 떨고 겁도 났단 말이에요.\" \"웃다니!\" 내 후견인이 말했다. \"얘야, 나는 네가 얼마나 운 좋은 것인지가 보여서 웃는 거란다. 아니, 이 일은 네가 잘만 다루면 평생 해 본 거래 가운데 가장 수지맞는 거래가 될 거야. 내 장담하건대,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"너는 그 포목상에게서 손해배상으로 오백 파운드를 받아 내게 될 거야, 그 직공한테서 받아 낼 것은 별도로 하고 말이지.\"\n\n나는 이 일에 대해 그녀와는 다른 생각을 품고 있었으니, 특히 내가 치안판사에게 내 이름을 대 버린 까닭이었다. 그리고 나는 내 이름이 힉스홀이며 올드베일리며 그런 데의 사람들 사이에 너무도 잘 알려져 있어서, 만약 이 송사가 공개적으로 재판에 부쳐지고 내 이름이 캐어지게 되면, 어떤 법정도 그런 평판을 지닌 사람의 명예에 대해 큰 배상금을 매겨 주지는 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다. 그러나 어쩔 수 없이 정식으로 소송을 시작하게 되었고, 그리하여 내 후견인이 그것을 맡아 처리할 매우 믿을 만한 부류의 사람을 찾아내 주었으니, 일거리가 아주 많고 평판도 좋은 사무변호사였다. 그리고 그녀가 이 점에서 옳았던 것이 분명하니, 만약 그녀가 어떤 삼류 엉터리 변호사나, 알려지지도 않고 평판도 좋지 않은 사람을 썼더라면, 나는 이 일에서 별것을 얻어 내지 못했을 것이다.\n\n나는 이 변호사를 만나, 위에 적힌 그대로 자초지종을 빠짐없이 일러 주었고, 그는 내게 단언하기를, 그의 말마따나 이것은 그 자체로 충분히 떠받쳐질 만한 사건이며, 이런 경우에 배심원단이 상당한 배상금을 매겨 주리라는 데 의심하지 않는다고 했다. 그리하여 그는 내 자세한 지시를 받아 소송을 시작했고, 포목상은 체포되어 보석금을 냈다. 그가 보석금을 낸 며칠 뒤, 그는 자기 변호사와 함께 내 변호사를 찾아와, 이 일을 원만히 매듭짓고 싶다는 뜻을 알리며, 모든 것이 불행한 격정의 열기 속에서 벌어진 일이고, 그의 의뢰인—곧 나를 가리키는 것이었다—이 날카롭고 도발적인 혀를 지녀, 그들이 나를 바로 그 사람이라 믿고 있던 동안에조차 내가 그들을 함부로 대하며 빈정대고 조롱하여 자기들을 도발했다는 둥 그런 식으로 말했다.\n\n내 변호사도 내 쪽에서 못지않게 잘 다루었으니, 그들로 하여금 내가 재산 있는 과부이며, 스스로 정당함을 지킬 능력이 있고, 또 나를 후원해 줄 큰 벗들이 있어, 그들 모두가 끝까지 소송하라고 내게 다짐을 받았으며, 설령 천 파운드가 든다 해도 나는 반드시 배상을 받고야 말 것이라고 믿게 만들었으니, 내가 받은 모욕이 참을 수 없는 것이었기 때문이라고 했다. 그러나 그들은 내 변호사를 이 지경까지 끌고 갔으니, 곧 그가 불씨를 부채질하지 않겠노라고, 만약 내가 화해 쪽으로 기운다면 막지 않겠노라고, 그리고 차라리 나를 전쟁보다 평화 쪽으로 설득하겠노라고 약속하게 한 것이었고, 그들은 그 대가로 그가 손해 볼 일은 없으리라고 했다. 그는 이 모든 것을 내게 매우 정직하게 일러 주었고, 만약 그들이 자기에게 어떤 뇌물이라도 내민다면 내가 반드시 알게 될 것이라고 했다. 그러나 대체로 그는 내게 매우 정직하게 말하기를, 내가 자기 의견을 받아들이겠다면, 그들과 화해하라고 권하겠노라 했으니, 그들이 큰 겁에 질려 무엇보다도 이 일을 매듭짓기를 바라고, 또 일이 어찌 되든 자기들이 소송 비용을 모두 떠안게 되리라는 것을 알고 있는 터라, 어떤 배심원단이나 법정이 재판에서 매겨 줄 것보다 더 후하게 내게 선뜻 내어 주리라 믿는다고 했다. 나는 그들이 어느 선까지 끌려올 것 같으냐고 물었다. 그는 그것까지는 알 수 없으나, 다시 나를 만날 때 더 일러 주겠노라고 했다.\n\n이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 그들은 그가 나와 이야기를 나누었는지 알아보러 다시 왔다. 그는 그렇게 했노라고, 내가 일부 내 벗들만큼 화해에 그리 반대하지는 않더라고 했으니, 그 벗들은 내게 가해진 치욕에 분개하여 나를 부추기고, 은밀히 불씨를 부채질하며 내게 복수를, 곧 그들의 말마따나 스스로 정당함을 지키기를 다그치고 있어서, 그로서도 그것에 대해 무어라 말해야 할지 모르겠노라고 했다. 그는 그들에게 자기가 나를 설득해 보도록 애쓰겠으나, 그들이 어떤 제안을 하는지를 내게 일러 줄 수 있어야 한다고 했다. 그들은 어떤 제안도 할 수 없다고 우겼으니, 그것이 자기들에게 불리하게 쓰일 수 있기 때문이라 했고, 그는 그들에게, 같은 이치로 자기도 어떤 제안을 내놓을 수 없다고 했으니, 그것이 배심원단이 매기려 할 배상금을 깎는 데에 변명거리로 내세워질 수 있기 때문이라 했다. 그러나 얼마간 이야기를 나누고, 그때든 그 밖의 다른 모임에서든 거기서 오간 것을 두고 어느 쪽도 이익을 취하지 않겠다고 서로 약속한 뒤, 그들은 일종의 협상에 이르렀다. 그러나 그것이 너무도 멀고, 서로 너무도 동떨어진 터라 거기서는 아무것도 기대할 수 없었으니, 내 변호사는 오백 파운드에 비용을 요구했고, 그들은 비용 없이 오십 파운드를 내놓았던 것이다. 그리하여 협상은 결렬되었고, 포목상은 나 자신과 직접 만남을 갖자고 제안했으며, 내 변호사는 그것에 매우 선뜻 동의했다.\n\n내 변호사는 내게 이 만남에 좋은 옷을 입고, 어느 정도 위엄을 갖춰 오라고 일러 주었으니, 그들이 나를 붙들었던 그때 보이던 모습보다 내가 무언가 더 나은 사람임을 포목상이 알아보게 하려는 것이었다. 그리하여 나는, 치안판사 앞에서 한 말대로, 약식 상복 새 옷 한 벌을 차려입고 갔다. 또 약식 상복의 과부 차림이 허락하는 한 최대한 나 자신을 돋보이게 꾸몄으니, 내 후견인도 자기가 전당으로 잡아 둔, 뒤에서 다이아몬드 로켓으로 잠그는 좋은 진주 목걸이를 내게 갖춰 주었다. 그리하여 나는 매우 좋은 자태를 갖추었고, 그들이 온 것이 확실해질 때까지 기다렸다가, 내 하녀를 데리고 마차를 타고 문 앞에 다다랐다.\n\n내가 방으로 들어서자 포목상은 깜짝 놀랐다. 그는 일어서서 허리를 굽혀 인사했고, 나는 그것을 조금, 그저 조금만 알은체하고는, 가서 내 변호사가 내게 앉으라고 가리킨 자리에 앉았으니, 그 집은 그의 집이었기 때문이다. 잠시 뒤 포목상은 나를 다시 알아보지 못하겠노라 하면서, 자기 식으로 몇 마디 인사치레를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 나는 그가 처음에는 나를 알아보지 못한 듯하며, 만약 알아보았더라면 나를 그런 식으로 대접하지는 않았으리라 믿는다고 말했다.\n\n그는 일어난 일에 대해 매우 미안하다고, 가능한 한 모든 배상을 하려는 자기 뜻을 증언하고자 이 만남을 마련한 것이라고 했다. 그는 내가 일을 극단으로 몰고 가지 않기를 바란다고 했으니, 그것은 자기에게 너무 큰 손실이 될 뿐 아니라 자기 사업과 가게의 파멸이 될 수도 있고, 그렇게 되면 나는 상해를 열 배나 더 큰 상해로 갚는 만족이야 누릴 수 있겠으나, 그때는 내가 아무것도 얻지 못할 것이라 했다. 반면에 자기는 소송의 수고나 비용을 자기 자신이나 내게 끼치지 않고도, 자기 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 정당함이든 내게 베풀 뜻이 있다고 했다.\n\n나는 그가 전보다 훨씬 분별 있는 사람답게 말하는 것을 들으니 기쁘다고 했으며, 대개의 경우 모욕에 대한 사죄가 충분한 배상으로 쳐지는 것은 사실이나, 이번 일은 그렇게 매듭짓기에는 너무 멀리 갔다고 했다. 나는 복수심이 있는 것도 아니고, 그의 파멸이나 다른 누구의 파멸을 바라는 것도 아니지만, 내 벗들이 한결같이, 내가 충분한 명예 회복 없이 이런 종류의 일을 매듭지을 만큼 내 체면을 그토록 소홀히 하도록 내버려 두지 않으려 한다고 했다. 도둑으로 붙들린다는 것은 도무지 참아 넘길 수 없는 모욕이며, 내 체면은 나를 아는 어떤 이에게도 그런 식으로 대접받을 만한 것이 아니지만, 다만 과부의 처지에서 한동안 내 몸을 돌보지 않고 소홀히 한 까닭에 그런 부류로 오인될 수도 있었던 것이라고 했다. 그러나 그 뒤에 그에게서 받은 그 각별한 대접으로 말하자면—그러고서 나는 앞서와 똑같이 모든 것을 되풀이했으니, 그것은 너무도 부아가 치미는 일이라 되풀이하기에도 참을성이 거의 남아 있지 않았다.\n\n그래, 그는 모든 것을 인정했고, 정말이지 무척 겸손해졌다. 그는 매우 점잖은 제안들을 내놓았으니, 백 파운드까지 올리고 모든 소송 비용을 치르겠다고 했으며, 거기에 매우 좋은 옷 한 벌을 선물하겠다고 덧붙였다. 나는 삼백 파운드로 내려왔고, 또 그 자초지종을 일반 신문에 광고로 실을 것을 요구했다.\n\n이것은 그가 도저히 응할 수 없는 조항이었다. 그러나 마침내 그는 내 변호사의 능숙한 처리로 백오십 파운드와 검은 비단옷 한 벌까지 올라왔고, 거기서 나는 동의했으니, 말하자면 내 변호사의 청에 따라 그것에 응한 셈이었다. 그는 내 변호사의 비용과 수임료를 치렀고, 덤으로 우리에게 좋은 저녁까지 대접했다.\n\n돈을 받으러 갈 때, 나는 내 후견인을 늙은 공작부인처럼 차려입혀 데려갔고, 또 매우 잘 차려입은 한 신사를 데려갔으니, 우리는 그가 내게 구애하는 사람인 양 꾸몄으나 나는 그를 사촌이라 불렀고, 변호사는 그저 그 신사가 그 과부에게 구애한다고 그에게 은밀히 귀띔만 하기로 했다.\n\n그는 정말이지 우리를 점잖게 대접했고, 돈도 꽤 흔쾌히 치렀으니, 그리하여 그에게는 통틀어 이백 파운드, 아니 그보다 좀 더 들었다. 마지막 만남에서, 모든 것이 합의되었을 때, 그 직공의 일이 거론되었고, 포목상은 그를 위해 무척 간곡히 빌었다. 그는 내게, 그자가 한때 자기 가게를 차려 제법 장사도 했던 사람이며, 아내와 여러 자식이 있고 몹시 가난하다고, 배상할 거리가 아무것도 없으나, 내가 원한다면 내가 바라는 만큼 공개적으로 무릎 꿇고 내게 용서를 빌 것이라고 했다. 나는 그 건방진 악당에게 별다른 앙심도 없었고, 그에게서 얻어 낼 것이 아무것도 없는 터라 그의 굴복도 내게 아무것도 아니었으니, 인색하게 구느니 그것을 너그러이 던져 주는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다. 그리하여 나는, 나는 어떤 사람의 파멸도 바라지 않으니, 그의 청에 따라 그 가엾은 자를 용서하겠노라고, 무슨 복수를 꾀하는 것은 내게 어울리지 않는 일이라고 했다.\n\n저녁을 들고 있을 때 그는 그 가엾은 사내를 데려와 사죄하게 했는데, 그자는 모욕적인 거만함과 오만으로 죄를 지었던 것만큼이나 비루한 비굴함으로 사죄하려 들었으니, 이 점에서 그는 영혼이 완전히 천박한 자의 본보기였다. 곧 우위에 서서 번성할 때는 불경하고 잔인하며 가차 없다가, 곤경에 빠져 처지면 비굴하고 풀이 죽는 그런 자였다. 그래도 나는 그의 굽실거림을 그치게 하고, 용서한다고 말한 뒤, 비록 용서는 했어도 그 꼴을 보고 싶지 않은 양 그가 물러가기를 청했다.\n\n이제 나는 정말이지 형편이 좋았으니, 만약 손 떼야 할 때를 알 수만 있었더라면 말이다. 내 후견인은 자주 내가 잉글랜드에서 이 장사를 하는 자들 가운데 가장 부유하다고 했고, 나도 그렇게 믿었으니, 옷가지며 반지며 약간의 은붙이며 금시계 두 개 외에도 현금으로 칠백 파운드를 지니고 있었고, 그것들은 모두 훔친 것이었으니, 내가 언급한 것들 외에도 무수한 일거리가 있었기 때문이다. 아아! 만약 내가 그때라도 회개의 은총을 받았더라면, 나는 아직 내 어리석음을 돌아보고 얼마간 속죄할 겨를이 있었을 것이다. 그러나 내가 끼친 공공의 해악에 대해 치러야 할 대가는 아직 뒤에 남아 있었고, 나는 다시 밖으로 나가는 것을—이제 나는 그것을 그렇게 불렀거니와—참을 수가 없었으니, 정말로 궁핍에 내몰려 끼니를 위해 거리로 떠밀려 나가던 그 어느 때보다도 더 그러했다.\n\n포목상과의 일이 매듭지어진 지 얼마 안 되어, 나는 전에 한 번도 보인 적 없는 전혀 다른 차림새로 나갔다. 나는 구할 수 있는 가장 거칠고 가장 천한 누더기를 입고 거지 여인처럼 차려입었으며, 가까이 가는 문이며 창마다 들여다보고 엿보며 돌아다녔다. 사실 나는 이제 그런 꼴이라, 그 어느 때 못지않게 어떻게 처신해야 할지를 도무지 알 수 없었다. 나는 본래 더러움과 누더기를 끔찍이 싫어했으니, 단정하고 청결하게 자라난 터라 어떤 처지에 있든 달리될 수가 없었고, 그래서 이것은 내가 여태껏 걸쳐 본 변장 가운데 가장 거북한 것이었다. 나는 곧 혼잣말로, 이건 안 되겠다고 했으니, 이것은 누구나 꺼리고 두려워하는 차림이었고, 누구나 마치 내가 자기들에게서 무언가 빼앗아 갈까 봐 내가 가까이 올까 두려워하는 양, 아니면 자기들이 내게서 무언가 옮을까 두려워하여 내게 가까이 오기를 두려워하는 양 나를 쳐다보는 것 같았기 때문이다. 처음 나간 날 나는 저녁 내내 돌아다녔으나 아무 소득도 얻지 못하고, 젖고 진창에 더럽혀지고 지친 채 다시 집으로 돌아왔다.\n그래도 나는 이튿날 밤 다시 나갔는데, 그때 작은 모험 하나를 만났으니, 그것은 하마터면 내게 큰 대가를 치르게 할 뻔했다. 내가 어느 선술집 문간 가까이에 서 있을 때, 한 신사가 말을 타고 와서 문 앞에서 내렸고, 선술집에 들어가고 싶었던 그는 급사 하나를 불러 자기 말을 붙들고 있게 했다. 그는 선술집에 꽤 오래 머물렀고, 급사는 주인이 부르는 소리를 듣고는 주인이 자기에게 화를 낼까 봐 걱정했다. 그는 내가 자기 곁에 서 있는 것을 보고 내게 소리쳤다. \"여기요, 아주머니,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 들어갔다 올 동안 이 말 좀 잠깐 붙들고 계세요. 그 신사분이 나오면 무어라도 좀 주실 겁니다.\" \"그러지요,\" 내가 말하고는 말을 받아, 매우 점잖은 걸음으로 그 말을 끌고 가서, 내 후견인에게 데려갔다.\n\n이것은 그것을 다룰 줄 아는 자들에게는 노획물이었겠으나, 훔친 것을 두고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라 이만큼 쩔쩔맨 가엾은 도둑은 일찍이 없었을 것이다. 집에 돌아왔을 때 내 후견인은 완전히 당황했고, 우리 둘 다 그 짐승을 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다. 마구간에 보내는 것은 아무 소용이 없었으니, 틀림없이 가제트에 공고가 실리고 그 말의 생김새가 적혀 나올 터라, 우리는 감히 그것을 다시 가지러 갈 수도 없었다.\n* 가제트: 영국의 관보를 겸한 신문으로, 분실물이나 현상수배 공고가 실렸다.\n\n이 운 나쁜 모험에 대해 우리가 쓸 수 있는 유일한 방책은, 가서 그 말을 어느 여관에 맡겨 두고, 짐꾼 편에 선술집으로 쪽지를 한 장 보내, 아무 때에 잃어버린 그 신사의 말이 아무 여관에 맡겨져 있으니 거기서 찾아갈 수 있다는 것, 그리고 그 말을 붙들고 있던 가엾은 여인이 그것을 거리로 끌고 다녔으나 도로 끌고 갈 수가 없어서 거기에 두고 갔다는 것을 알리는 일이었다. 우리는 주인이 공고를 내고 사례금을 내걸 때까지 기다릴 수도 있었으나, 그 사례금을 받는 모험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았다.\n\n그리하여 이것은 강도질이면서 강도질이 아니었으니, 그것으로 잃은 것도 거의 없고 얻은 것도 없었다. 그리고 나는 거지 옷차림으로 나가는 것에 아주 진저리가 났으니, 그것은 도무지 들어맞지 않았고, 게다가 불길하고 위협적인 일로 여겨졌다.\n\n이렇게 변장하고 있는 동안, 나는 여태껏 내가 어울려 본 어떤 무리보다도 더 고약한 부류의 패거리와 엮이게 되었고, 그들의 수법도 조금 들여다보게 되었다. 이들은 화폐 위조범들이었는데, 이익으로 치자면 내게 매우 좋은 제안을 했으나, 그들이 내게 발을 들이게 하려던 부분은 가장 위험한 부분이었다. 곧 그들의 말마따나 다이 그 자체를 다루는 일이었으니, 만약 내가 붙잡혔더라면 그것은 확실한 죽음이었고, 그것도 화형주에서—다시 말해, 화형주에 묶여 불에 타 죽는 것이었다. 그리하여 비록 내가 겉보기에는 거지에 지나지 않았고 그들이 끌어들이려고 내게 산더미 같은 금은을 약속했어도, 그것은 될 일이 아니었다. 사실 만약 내가 정말로 거지였거나, 처음 시작하던 때처럼 절박했더라면, 어쩌면 그것에 응했을지도 모른다. 어찌 살아야 할지 모르는 자들이 죽는 것을 무어 그리 두려워하겠는가? 그러나 지금은 그것이 내 처지가 아니었으니, 적어도 나는 그런 끔찍한 위험은 무릅쓸 마음이 없었다. 게다가 화형주에서 불에 타 죽는다는 생각만으로도 내 영혼 깊숙이 공포가 사무쳐, 피가 얼어붙고, 몸서리치지 않고는 그것을 생각할 수 없을 만큼 신경이 곤두섰다.\n* 다이: 화폐를 찍어 내는 데 쓰는 주형(鑄型). 당시 화폐 위조는 대역죄에 준하여 여성의 경우 화형에 처해졌다.\n\n이것은 또한 내 변장에도 종지부를 찍었으니, 나는 그 제안이 마음에 들지 않았으나 그들에게 그렇다고 말하지는 않고, 마음에 드는 척하며 다시 만나기로 약속했다. 그러나 나는 감히 그들을 다시 만날 수 없었으니, 만약 내가 그들을 만나고도 응하지 않았다면, 비록 세상에서 가장 굳은 비밀 엄수의 다짐을 하며 거절했더라도, 그들은 확실히 일을 마무리 짓고 그들의 말마따나 마음을 편케 하려고 나를 죽이려 들었을 것이기 때문이다. 그것이 어떤 종류의 마음 편함인지는, 위험을 막으려고 사람을 죽일 수 있는 자들이 얼마나 마음이 편한지를 아는 이들이 가장 잘 판단할 것이다.\n\n이것도 말 도둑질도 도무지 내 길과는 동떨어진 일이라, 나는 그들과 더는 상관하지 않겠다고 쉽사리 결심할 수 있었다. 내 일은 다른 길에 놓여 있는 듯했으니, 비록 그것에도 위험이 충분히 들어 있긴 했어도, 그래도 그것이 내게 더 잘 맞았고, 그 안에 솜씨가 더 들어 있으며, 빠져나갈 여지도 더 많고, 불시의 봉변이 닥치더라도 모면할 가망이 더 많았던 것이다.\n\n또한 그 무렵 나는 가택 침입강도단에 들어오라는 제안도 여러 번 받았으나, 그것도 화폐 위조 장사 못지않게 내가 무릅쓰고 싶지 않은 일이었다. 나는 계략을 써서 남의 집에 들어가는 것을 업으로 삼던 두 사내와 한 여자를 따라가겠노라 제안했고, 그들과 함께라면 기꺼이 모험을 무릅쓸 만했다. 그러나 그들은 이미 셋인 데다, 그들도 갈라서기를 꺼렸고 나도 한 패거리에 사람이 너무 많은 것을 바라지 않았으니, 그리하여 나는 그들과 손잡지 않고 마다했는데, 그들은 다음번 시도에서 톡톡히 대가를 치렀다.\n\n그러나 마침내 나는, 강가에서 어떤 모험을 했고 또 성공했는지를 내게 자주 들려주던 한 여자를 만나, 그녀와 손잡았고, 우리는 우리 일을 꽤 잘 꾸려 나갔다. 어느 날 우리는 세인트캐서린의 어떤 네덜란드인들 틈에 끼어들었으니, 우리는 몰래 뭍에 들여온 물건을 산다는 핑계로 갔던 것이다. 나는 두세 번 어느 집에 들어가, 거기서 상당한 양의 금제품(禁制品)을 보았고, 내 동료는 한번은 네덜란드산 검은 비단 세 필을 가지고 나와 톡톡히 재미를 보았으며, 나도 그것에서 내 몫을 챙겼다. 그러나 나 혼자 한 그 모든 나들이에서는 무언가를 할 기회를 도무지 잡지 못해서, 나는 그 일을 접어 두었으니, 내가 하도 자주 드나든 터라 그들이 무언가 의심하기 시작하여 어찌나 경계하는지, 더는 할 수 있는 일이 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n이것이 나를 좀 김새게 했고, 나는 무엇이든 한 건 해야겠다고 결심했으니, 그토록 자주 빈손으로 돌아오는 데에 익숙하지 않았던 것이다. 그리하여 이튿날 나는 곱게 차려입고, 시내의 다른 쪽 끝으로 산책을 나갔다. 나는 스트랜드의 익스체인지를 지나가고 있었으나, 거기서 무슨 할 일을 찾으리라는 생각은 없었는데, 그때 별안간 그곳이 크게 부산해지더니, 가게 주인들이며 다른 이들이며 사람들이 모두 일어서서 멀거니 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그것이 무엇인고 하니, 어떤 대단한 공작부인이 익스체인지에 든 것이었고, 사람들은 여왕이 오신다고들 했다. 나는 마치 군중을 지나가게 하려는 양 등을 계산대 쪽으로 돌리고 어느 가게 옆에 바짝 붙어 섰는데, 가게 주인이 내 곁에 서 있던 어떤 부인들에게 보여 주고 있던 한 묶음의 레이스에 눈을 둔 채였으니, 가게 주인과 그 하녀가 누가 오는지, 또 그들이 어느 가게로 갈지 보느라 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있던지, 나는 레이스 한 묶음을 내 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣고 그것을 가지고 감쪽같이 빠져나올 방도를 찾았다. 그리하여 그 부인 모자상은 여왕을 향해 입을 헤벌리고 바라본 대가를 톡톡히 치른 셈이었다.\n\n나는 마치 무리에 떠밀려 가는 양 가게를 벗어나, 군중 속에 섞여들어 익스체인지의 다른 문으로 나갔고, 그리하여 그들이 레이스가 없어진 것을 알아채기 전에 빠져나왔다. 그리고 미행당하지 않으려고 마차를 한 대 불러 그 안에 들어가 몸을 숨겼다. 마차 문을 닫자마자, 나는 그 모자상의 하녀와 대여섯 사람이 거리로 뛰어나와 겁에 질린 듯 외쳐 대는 것을 보았다. 아무도 달아나지 않았으니 \"도둑이야!\" 하고 외치지는 않았으나, \"강도당했어\"라는 말과 \"레이스\"라는 말을 두세 번 들을 수 있었고, 그 계집아이가 두 손을 비틀며 겁에 질린 사람처럼 이리저리 멀거니 뛰어다니는 것을 보았다. 나를 태운 마부가 마부석에 올라타려 했으나 아직 다 올라타지 못해서, 말이 움직이기 시작하지 않았다. 그래서 나는 몹시 불안했고, 그 레이스 묶음을 집어 들어, 마부 바로 뒤, 앞쪽으로 열리는 마차의 덮개 밖으로 떨어뜨릴 채비를 해 두었다. 그러나 무척 다행스럽게도, 채 일 분도 못 되어 마차가 움직이기 시작했으니, 다시 말해 마부가 올라타 자기 말들에게 말을 건네자마자였다. 그리하여 그는 아무 방해도 없이 마차를 몰아갔고, 나는 내 노획물을 가지고 빠져나왔으니, 그것은 이십 파운드 가까이 나가는 것이었다.\n\n이튿날 나는 다시 차려입었으나, 전혀 다른 옷이었고, 같은 길로 다시 걸어 나갔다. 그러나 세인트제임스 공원에 이를 때까지는 아무 일도 생기지 않았는데, 거기서 나는 공원에서 몰 거리를 거닐고 있는 수많은 고운 부인을 보았고, 그 가운데 열두세 살쯤 된 어린 아가씨가 있었는데, 그녀에게는 아마 아홉 살쯤 된 여동생인 듯한 아이가 함께 있었다. 큰 아이가 고운 금시계를 차고 있고 좋은 진주 목걸이를 하고 있는 것을 눈여겨보았으며, 그들에게는 제복 차림의 하인 하나가 딸려 있었다. 그러나 하인이 몰에서 부인들의 뒤를 따르는 것은 흔한 일이 아니었던지라, 나는 그 하인이 그들이 몰에 들어설 때 멈춰 서는 것을 보았고, 자매 가운데 큰 아이가 그에게 무어라 말했는데, 내가 알아챈 바로는 그들이 돌아올 때 바로 거기 있으라고 이르는 것이었다.\n\n그녀가 하인을 물리는 것을 듣고, 나는 그에게 다가가, 저 어린 아가씨가 누구냐고 묻고는, 그녀와 함께 있는 아이가 얼마나 예쁜지, 또 그 손위 아가씨가 자라면 얼마나 기품 있고 몸가짐이 단정할지, 얼마나 부인다워질지, 얼마나 진중할지를 두고 그와 잠깐 한담을 나누었다. 그러자 그 어리석은 사내는 대뜸 그녀가 누구인지를 내게 일러 주었으니, 그녀는 에식스의 토머스 ―경의 맏딸이며, 큰 재산을 물려받을 사람이라고, 그녀의 어머니는 아직 시내에 오지 않았으나, 그녀는 서퍽 거리의 숙소에서 서퍽의 윌리엄 ―경의 부인과 함께 있다고 했으며, 그 밖에도 많은 말을 했으니, 그들에게는 시중드는 하녀와 시중드는 여인이 있고, 그 밖에 토머스 경의 마차며, 마부며, 자기 자신이 있다는 것, 그리고 저 어린 아가씨가 여기서나 집에서나 온 집안의 살림을 맡고 있다는 것이었다. 한마디로, 그는 내 일거리에 충분할 만큼 많은 것을 내게 일러 주었다.\n\n나는 매우 잘 차려입고 있었고, 그녀 못지않게 금시계도 차고 있었다. 그래서 나는 그 하인을 떠나, 이 어린 아가씨가 몰에서 한 바퀴를 다 돌고 다시 앞으로 나아가려 할 때까지 기다렸다가, 그녀와 나란히 줄을 섰다. 이윽고 나는 그녀의 이름을 부르며 베티 아가씨라는 호칭으로 인사를 건넸다. 나는 그녀에게 언제 아버지의 소식을 들었는지, 어머니 되시는 마님은 언제 시내에 오시는지, 또 그분은 안녕하신지를 물었다.\n\n나는 그녀의 온 집안에 대해 어찌나 허물없이 이야기했던지, 그녀는 내가 그들 모두와 친밀하게 안다고밖에는 의심할 수 없었다. 나는 그녀에게 어찌하여 동생 주디스 아가씨를 돌볼 차임 부인(그것이 그녀의 시중드는 여인의 이름이었다)도 없이 밖에 나왔느냐고 물었다. 그러고는 그녀의 동생에 대해 긴 한담에 들어가, 얼마나 고운 어린 아가씨인지를 말하고, 프랑스어를 배웠는지 등을 물으며 그녀의 흥을 돋울 자잘한 이야기를 수천 가지나 늘어놓았는데, 그때 별안간 근위병들이 오는 것을 보았고, 군중이 국왕이 의사당으로 가시는 것을 보려고 달려갔다.\n\n부인들이 모두 몰의 가장자리로 달려갔고, 나는 내 아가씨가 충분히 높이 서서 볼 수 있도록 몰 가의 판자 가장자리 위에 올라서게 도와주었으며, 어린 동생을 안아 번쩍 들어 올렸다. 그러는 동안 나는 베티 아가씨에게서 금시계를 어찌나 감쪽같이 빼냈던지, 그녀는 군중이 모두 흩어지고 그녀가 다른 부인들 틈에 끼어 몰 한복판으로 들어설 때까지, 그것을 느끼지도, 없어진 것을 알아채지도 못했다.\n\n나는 바로 그 군중 속에서 그녀에게 작별을 고하며, 마치 바쁜 듯이 그녀에게 말했다. \"베티 아가씨, 어린 동생을 잘 보살피세요.\" 그리하여 군중이 말하자면 나를 그녀에게서 떠밀어 놓았고, 나는 마지못해 작별을 고할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n이런 경우의 부산함은 곧 가라앉고, 국왕이 지나가시면 이내 그 자리가 휑해지는 법이다. 그러나 국왕이 지나가실 바로 그때는 늘 크게 뛰고 부산을 떠는 법이라, 그 두 어린 아가씨를 떨쳐 내고 아무 탈 없이 그들과의 일을 해치운 나는, 마치 국왕을 보려고 달려가는 양 군중 틈을 헤치며 계속 서둘러 나아갔고, 그리하여 군중보다 앞서 나가 몰 끝에 이를 때까지 그렇게 했다. 국왕이 호스가즈 쪽으로 나아가시자, 나는 그 무렵 헤이마켓 아래쪽 끝을 가로질러 나 있던 통로 쪽으로 앞서 나아가, 거기서 나 자신에게 마차 한 대를 베풀고 빠져나왔다. 그리고 고백하건대 나는 아직 내 약속을, 곧 가서 베티 아가씨를 방문하겠다던 약속을 지키지 못했다.\n\n나는 한때, 베티 아가씨가 시계가 없어진 것을 알아챌 때까지 그녀와 함께 머물러 있다가, 그녀와 더불어 그것을 두고 크게 야단법석을 떨고, 그녀를 마차에 태운 뒤 나도 그 마차에 함께 타서, 그녀와 함께 그녀의 집으로 가 볼 마음도 있었다. 그녀가 나를 어찌나 따르고, 또 내가 그녀의 모든 친척과 집안에 대해 그토록 거침없이 이야기한 데에 어찌나 감쪽같이 속아 넘어갔던지, 나는 그 일을 더 밀고 나가 적어도 그 진주 목걸이만은 손에 넣기가 매우 쉬우리라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 비록 그 아이는 어쩌면 나를 의심하지 않았을지 몰라도 다른 사람들은 의심할 수 있고, 또 만약 내가 수색당하면 들통날 것임을 생각하니, 손에 넣은 것을 가지고 빠져나와 만족하는 편이 가장 낫겠다고 여겼다.\n\n나는 그 뒤에 우연히 듣게 되었으니, 그 어린 아가씨가 시계가 없어진 것을 알았을 때 공원에서 크게 야단법석을 떨며, 자기 하인을 이리저리 보내 나를 찾아낼 수 있는지 보게 했고, 그녀가 나를 어찌나 완벽하게 묘사했던지 그가 대뜸 그것이 자기와 그토록 오래 서서 이야기를 나누고 그들에 대해 그토록 많은 것을 물었던 바로 그 사람임을 알아차렸다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 자기 하인에게 다가가 그 이야기를 할 수 있게 되기 전에 이미 그들의 손이 닿을 수 없는 곳까지 충분히 멀리 가 있었다.\n\n나는 이 일이 있은 뒤에 또 한 번 모험을 했는데, 그것은 여태껏 내가 관여한 어떤 것과도 성격이 다른 것이었으니, 이번에는 코번트가든 근처의 어느 도박장에서였다.\n\n나는 여러 사람이 드나드는 것을 보았고, 통로에 꽤\n어떤 여자 한 사람과 함께 있던 중에, 어딘가 보통 이상으로 멋을 부린 듯한 신사 하나가 위층으로 올라가는 것을 보고 내가 그에게 말했다. \"선생님, 여쭙는데, 여자도 올라가게 해 주지 않나요?\" \"그럼요, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다. \"원하시면 노름도 하실 수 있고요.\" \"저도 그런 뜻이에요, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. 그러자 그는 내가 마음이 있다면 안내해 주겠노라 했고, 나는 그를 따라 문간으로 갔다. 그가 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"저기, 부인, 노름꾼들이 있습니다. 한번 걸어 보실 마음이 있다면요.\" 나는 안을 들여다보고는 일행에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"여기는 남자들뿐이네요. 저 사람들 틈에 끼어들지는 않겠어요.\" 그러자 신사들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"겁내실 것 없습니다, 부인. 여기는 점잖은 노름꾼들밖에 없으니, 들어오셔서 마음껏 거셔도 좋습니다.\" 그래서 나는 조금 더 가까이 다가가 구경했고, 그들 중 몇이 내게 의자를 가져다주어 자리에 앉아 주사위 통과 주사위가 빠르게 돌아가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러고는 일행에게 말했다. \"이 신사들은 우리에게는 판돈이 너무 큰 노름을 하네요. 자, 가요.\"\n\n사람들은 모두 매우 정중했고, 한 신사가 특히 나를 부추기며 말했다. \"자, 부인, 한번 걸어 보실 마음이 있고, 또 저를 믿어 주신다면, 여기서 부인께 어떤 속임수도 쓰이지 않으리라는 것을 제가 보증하겠습니다.\" \"아니에요, 선생님.\" 나는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"신사분들이 설마 여자를 속이지는 않으시겠지요.\" 그러면서도 나는 여전히 거는 것을 사양했는데, 다만 돈이 든 지갑을 꺼내 보여서 내가 돈이 없어서가 아님을 그들이 알게 했다.\n\n내가 한참 앉아 있자 한 신사가 빈정대듯 내게 말했다. \"자, 부인, 보아하니 부인 스스로를 위해 거는 것은 겁이 나시는 모양이군요. 저는 늘 숙녀분들과 함께하면 운이 좋았으니, 부인 스스로를 위해 걸지 않으시겠다면 저를 위해 걸어 주시지요.\" 나는 그에게 말했다. \"선생님, 저는 선생님 돈을 잃게 되는 것이 정말 싫습니다.\" 그러면서도 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"저도 운이 꽤 좋은 편이긴 하지만, 신사분들이 판돈을 너무 크게 거셔서 제 돈은 정말 걸 엄두가 안 나는 것이지요.\"\n\n\"좋아요, 좋아요.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 십 기니가 있으니, 부인, 저를 위해 걸어 주십시오.\" 그래서 나는 그의 돈을 받아 들고 걸었고, 그 자신은 지켜보았다. 나는 한두 번에 한 번씩 걸어 아홉 기니를 날렸는데, 그러다 주사위 통이 내 옆자리 사람에게 돌아왔을 때 내 신사가 다시 십 기니를 더 주며 그중 다섯을 한꺼번에 걸게 했고, 주사위 통을 쥔 신사가 끗수가 나빠 그의 돈 다섯 기니가 다시 돌아왔다. 그는 이에 신이 나서 내게 주사위 통을 쥐게 했는데, 이는 대담한 모험이었다. 그러나 나는 주사위 통을 오래도록 쥐고 있어서 그의 돈을 전부 따 주었고, 내 무릎에는 한 움큼의 기니가 그득했으며, 게다가 더 운이 좋았던 것은, 내가 끗수를 내고 나설 때면 내게 돈을 걸어 준 사람들 가운데 한둘에게만 걸어, 그렇게 수월히 빠져나왔다는 점이다.\n\n여기까지 이르자 나는 그 금화가 모두 그의 것이었으므로 그 신사에게 전부 내밀며, 노름을 잘 모른다는 핑계로 그더러 직접 걸라고 하려 했다. 그는 웃으며, 운만 좋다면 노름을 알든 모르든 상관없다고 했고, 그러니 나더러 그만두면 안 된다고 했다. 그래도 그는 처음에 넣었던 십오 기니를 빼내고는 나머지로 노름을 하라고 했다. 나는 내가 얼마나 땄는지 보려고 그것을 헤아려 보려 했으나, 그가 말했다. \"아니, 아니요, 헤아리지 마세요. 부인은 매우 정직한 분이라 믿거니와, 헤아리는 것은 운에 좋지 않으니까요.\" 그래서 나는 노름을 계속했다.\n\n나는 모르는 척했으나 사실 노름을 충분히 알고 있었고, 조심스럽게 했다. 그것은 내 무릎에 두둑한 밑천을 쌓아 두고, 거기서 이따금 얼마씩을 내 호주머니로 슬쩍 옮기되, 그가 결코 보지 못하리라 확신할 만한 방식으로, 또 그만큼 알맞은 때에 하기 위함이었다.\n\n나는 오랫동안 노름을 했고 그를 위해 운이 매우 좋았으나, 마지막으로 주사위 통을 쥐었을 때 그들이 내게 판돈을 크게 걸어 왔고, 나는 대담하게 전부를 걸었다. 나는 주사위 통을 쥐고 거의 팔십 기니에 가깝게 땄으나, 마지막 던지기에서 그 절반 넘게 도로 잃었다. 그래서 나는 전부를 다시 잃을까 두려워 자리에서 일어나 그에게 말했다. \"자, 이제 선생님, 와서 이걸 받으시고 직접 걸어 보세요. 제가 선생님을 위해 꽤 잘해 드린 것 같으니까요.\" 그는 내가 노름을 계속하기를 바랐으나, 시간이 늦어져 나는 양해를 구했다. 그것을 그에게 돌려주면서 나는, 이제는 내가 얼마나 땄는지, 또 그를 위해 얼마나 운이 좋았는지 알 수 있도록 헤아리게 해 주시기를 바란다고 말했다. 헤아려 보니 예순세 기니였다. \"아.\" 내가 말했다. \"그 운 나쁜 던지기만 아니었다면, 선생님께 백 기니는 따 드렸을 텐데요.\" 그래서 나는 그에게 돈을 전부 주었으나, 그는 내가 거기에 손을 넣어 얼마간 내 몫으로 가질 때까지 받으려 하지 않았고, 마음껏 가지라고 했다. 나는 거절하며, 절대로 내가 직접 가지지는 않겠다고 단호히 말했다. 그가 그런 식으로 무언가를 해 주고 싶다면, 그것은 전적으로 그 자신이 알아서 할 일이라고 했다.\n\n나머지 신사들이 우리가 실랑이하는 것을 보고는 외쳤다. \"부인께 전부 드리시오.\" 그러나 나는 그것을 단호히 거절했다. 그러자 그들 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이런, 잭, 부인하고 반씩 나누게. 숙녀분들과는 늘 공평한 조건이어야 한다는 걸 모르나.\" 그래서 한마디로, 그는 그것을 나와 반씩 나누었고, 나는 따로 몰래 훔친 마흔세 기니가량 외에 서른 기니를 가지고 나왔는데, 그가 그토록 너그러웠던 까닭에 나중에 그 일이 마음에 걸렸다.\n\n이렇게 나는 일흔세 기니를 집에 가져와, 늙은 후견인에게 노름에서 내가 얼마나 운이 좋았는지를 보여 주었다. 그러나 다시는 모험하지 말라는 것이 그녀의 충고였고, 나는 그 조언을 받아들여 다시는 그곳에 가지 않았다. 노름의 욕심이 한번 끼어들면, 나도 그녀만큼이나 잘 알았듯이, 그 돈과 내가 그동안 번 나머지 전부를 머지않아 잃을 수도 있기 때문이었다.\n\n운명이 내게 그토록 미소를 지어 주어, 나도 그렇고 내 후견인도 그렇고—그녀는 늘 나와 몫을 나누었으므로—참으로 많이 번 터라, 이 늙은 부인은 우리가 형편이 좋을 때 그만두고, 그동안 번 것에 만족하자는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그러나 어떤 운명이 나를 이끌었는지 모르겠으나, 전에 내가 그녀에게 그것을 제안했을 때 그녀가 그러했던 것처럼, 이제는 내가 그 일에 미적거렸고, 그리하여 불운한 시각에 우리는 당분간 그 생각을 접어 두었으니, 한마디로 나는 그 어느 때보다 더 모질고 대담해졌으며, 그 성공으로 나는 뉴게이트와 올드베일리에서 내 부류의 어떤 도둑 못지않게 이름을 떨치게 되었다.\n\n나는 더러 같은 수법을 다시 써먹는 자유를 누리기도 했는데, 이는 통례에 어긋나는 일이었으나 그래도 그리 나쁘지 않게 먹혀들었다. 그러나 대개는 새로운 모습으로 꾸미고, 밖에 나갈 때마다 새로운 행색으로 나타나도록 궁리했다.\n\n지금은 한 해 중 떠들썩한 철은 아니었고, 신사들 대부분이 시내를 떠난 터라 턴브리지며 엡섬이며 그런 곳들이 사람들로 가득했다. 그러나 시내는 한산했고, 나는 우리 장사도 다른 장사들과 마찬가지로 그 영향을 다소 느낀다고 생각했다. 그래서 그해 말미에 나는 해마다 스타브리지 장에 갔다가 거기서 서퍽주의 베리 장으로 가곤 하는 한 무리에 끼어들었다. 우리는 거기서 큰 수확을 기대했으나, 막상 가서 사정을 보니 나는 금세 싫증이 났다. 단순한 소매치기 외에는 손댈 만한 가치 있는 것이 거의 없었고, 또 노획물이 생긴다 해도 그것을 가지고 빠져나오기가 그리 쉽지 않았으며, 런던에서처럼 우리 식으로 일할 거리가 그렇게 다양하지도 않았다. 이 여정 전체에서 내가 얻은 것이라곤 베리 장에서의 금시계 하나와 케임브리지에서의 자그마한 아마포 꾸러미 하나뿐이었는데, 그것이 내게 그곳을 떠날 구실을 주었다. 그것은 오래된 수법으로, 런던에서는 통하지 않겠지만 시골 가게 주인에게는 먹혀들 만하다고 생각했다.\n\n나는 장터가 아니라 케임브리지 읍내에 있는 어느 포목상에서 고운 홀란드 천과 그 밖의 물건들을 약 칠 파운드어치쯤 사들였다. 다 사고 나서 나는, 마치 그날 밤 거기서 묵을 것처럼 바로 그날 아침에 일부러 잡아 둔 어느 여관으로 그것들을 보내 달라고 했다.\n\n나는 포목상에게 그 물건들을 일정한 시각에, 내가 묵는 여관으로 보내 달라고 하면서, 그러면 그의 돈을 치르겠다고 했다. 약속한 시각에 포목상이 물건을 보내왔는데, 나는 우리 무리 가운데 하나를 방문 앞에 세워 두었다. 여관 하녀가 심부름꾼을, 거의 다 자란 도제 청년을 문간으로 데려오자, 하녀는 그에게 자기 마님이 주무시고 계시니, 물건을 두고 갔다가 한 시간쯤 뒤에 다시 들르면 그때는 내가 깨어 있을 테고 돈을 받을 수 있으리라고 했다. 그는 선뜻 꾸러미를 두고 제 갈 길로 갔고, 반시간쯤 뒤에 내 하녀와 나는 걸어 나갔다. 그리고 바로 그날 저녁 나는 말 한 필과 내 앞에서 말을 몰 사람을 하나 빌려 뉴마켓으로 갔고, 거기서 자리가 다 차지 않은 마차에 편승하여 세인트에드먼즈베리로 갔는데, 앞서 말했듯이 그곳에서는 내 장사로 거의 재미를 보지 못했고, 다만 어느 작은 시골 오페라 극장에서 한 부인의 옆구리에서 금시계를 슬쩍 빼내는 데 그쳤으니, 그 부인은 참을 수 없을 만큼 들떠 있었을 뿐 아니라 내가 보기에 조금 취해 있기까지 해서 내 일이 한결 수월했다.\n\n나는 이 자그마한 노획물을 가지고 입스위치로 빠져나갔고, 거기서 다시 하리치로 갔는데, 거기서 나는 마치 막 홀란드에서 도착한 양 어느 여관에 들었으니, 그곳에 상륙하는 외국인들 틈에서 한몫 챙기리라는 것을 의심치 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 대개 여행 가방과 네덜란드식 바구니에 든 것 외에는 값나가는 물건이 없는 듯했고, 그것들은 대개 하인들이 지키고 있었다. 그러나 어느 날 저녁, 신사가 누워 있던 방에서 그들의 여행 가방 하나를 깨끗이 손에 넣었으니, 하인이 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 짐작건대 몹시 취해 있었던 것이다.\n\n내가 묵던 방은 그 네덜란드인의 방 옆에 있었고, 그 무거운 물건을 무진 애를 써서 그 방에서 내 방으로 끌고 온 다음, 나는 그것을 가지고 빠져나갈 무슨 방도가 있을지 알아보러 거리로 나갔다. 나는 한참을 돌아다녔으나, 그 물건을 가지고 나갈 가망도, 또 설령 그것을 열었다 한들 그 안의 물건들을 빼낼 가망도 보이지 않았으니, 고을이 워낙 작은 데다 나는 그곳에서 완전한 이방인이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그것을 도로 가져다가 발견한 자리에 두기로 마음먹고 돌아오던 참이었다. 바로 그 순간, 한 사내가 어떤 사람들에게 서두르라고, 배가 곧 떠나려 하고 물때가 다 되어 간다고 소리치는 것을 들었다. 나는 그 사내를 불렀다. \"여보세요, 그게 무슨 배인가요? 댁이 타는 배가?\" 내가 물었다. \"입스위치행 거룻배입니다, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다. \"언제 떠나는데요?\" 내가 물었다. \"지금 당장요, 부인.\" 그가 말했다. \"그쪽으로 가시려고요?\" \"그래요.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 짐을 가져올 때까지 기다려 주신다면요.\" \"짐이 어디 있는데요, 부인?\" 그가 물었다. \"어느 여관에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그럼 제가 함께 가서, 부인 짐을 가져다드리지요.\" 그가 매우 정중하게 말했다. \"그럼 어서 가요.\" 내가 말하며 그를 데리고 갔다.\n\n여관 사람들은 무척 분주했으니, 홀란드에서 오는 우편선이 막 들어온 데다, 홀란드로 떠나려는 또 다른 우편선을 타려고 런던에서 온 승객들을 실은 마차 두 대도 막 도착한 참이었기 때문이다. 그 마차들은 막 상륙한 승객들을 태우고 이튿날 돌아갈 예정이었다. 이 부산함 속에서, 내가 계산대로 와 셈을 치르며 여주인에게 거룻배로 뱃길을 잡았노라 말한 것은 그리 주목받지 못했다.\n\n이 거룻배들은 큰 배로, 하리치에서 런던까지 승객을 실어 나르기에 좋은 시설을 갖추고 있다. 그리고 비록 거룻배라 불리지만—이는 템스강에서 한두 사람이 노 저어 가는 작은 배를 가리키는 말이지만—이것들은 스무 명의 승객과 십 톤 내지 십오 톤의 물건을 실을 수 있고 바다를 견디도록 만들어진 배다. 이 모든 것을 나는 전날 밤 런던으로 가는 여러 길을 알아보던 중에 알아낸 것이었다.\n\n내 여주인은 매우 친절하여 셈한 내 돈을 받았으나, 온 집이 부산한 통에 불려 가 버렸다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 두고, 그 사내를 내 방으로 데려가 그 트렁크, 아니 여행 가방을—그것은 트렁크 같았으므로—건넸고, 그것을 낡은 앞치마로 둘둘 싸자 그는 곧장 그것을 가지고 제 배로 갔으며, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐으니, 아무도 우리에게 그것에 대해 조금도 묻지 않았다. 취한 네덜란드 하인으로 말하자면 그는 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 그의 주인은 다른 외국 신사들과 함께 아래층에서 저녁을 들며 매우 흥겨워하고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 가지고 깨끗이 입스위치로 빠져나갔다. 그리고 밤에 떠난지라, 여관 사람들은 내가 여주인에게 말한 대로 하리치 거룻배를 타고 런던으로 갔다는 것밖에는 아무것도 몰랐다.\n\n나는 입스위치에서 세관 관리들 때문에 골치를 앓았으니, 그들이 내가 트렁크라 부르던 것을 막아 세우고 열어 뒤지려 했던 것이다. 나는 그들에게 뒤져도 좋다고, 다만 열쇠는 남편이 가지고 있는데 아직 하리치에서 오지 않았다고 말했다. 내가 이렇게 말한 것은, 그들이 뒤져서 안에 든 물건들이 모두 여자보다는 남자에게 어울리는 것들임을 발견하더라도 이상하게 여기지 않도록 하기 위함이었다. 그러나 그들이 트렁크를 열겠다고 단호히 나오기에, 나는 그것을 부수어 여는 것에, 다시 말해 자물쇠를 떼어 내는 것에 동의했으니, 그것은 어렵지 않은 일이었다.\n\n그들은 자기들 목적에 맞는 것은 아무것도 찾지 못했으니, 그 트렁크는 이미 한 번 뒤져진 터였기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 나로서는 매우 흡족한 여러 물건을 찾아냈는데, 특히 프랑스 피스톨 금화 한 꾸러미와 네덜란드 두카톤화 혹은 릭스달러 약간이 있었고, 나머지는 주로 가발 두 개, 입을 아마포, 면도칼, 비누 덩이, 향수, 그리고 신사에게 필요한 그 밖의 쓸모 있는 물건들이었는데, 그 모두가 내 남편의 것으로 통했고, 그리하여 나는 그들에게서 풀려났다.\n\n이제는 아침 아주 이른 시각이었고 아직 밝지도 않았으며, 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 좋을지 알 수 없었다. 아침이 되면 틀림없이 추격당할 것이고, 어쩌면 물건을 지닌 채 붙잡힐지도 모른다는 생각에 의심의 여지가 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 새로운 조처를 취하기로 마음먹었다. 나는 내가 트렁크라 부르던 것을 가지고 보란 듯이 읍내의 어느 여관으로 갔는데, 알맹이는 이미 빼낸 터라 그 거추장스러운 껍데기는 내가 신경 쓸 가치가 없다고 여겼다. 그래도 나는 그것을 여관 여주인에게 맡기며, 매우 잘 간수하여 내가 다시 올 때까지 안전하게 보관해 두라고 단단히 당부하고는, 거리로 걸어 나갔다.\n\n여관에서 한참 멀리 읍내로 들어갔을 때, 나는 막 문을 연 한 노파를 만나 그녀와 이야기를 나누게 되었고, 내 목적과 의도와는 전혀 동떨어진 여러 가지 엉뚱한 질문을 그녀에게 많이 했다. 그러나 그 이야기 속에서 나는 그녀를 통해 읍내가 어떻게 자리 잡혀 있는지를 알아냈으니, 내가 있는 거리는 해들리 쪽으로 나가는 길이고, 어느 거리는 물가 쪽으로, 어느 거리는 콜체스터 쪽으로 가며, 런던으로 가는 길은 저쪽에 있다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 곧 이 노파에게서 내 목적을 이루었으니, 나는 다만 어느 길이 런던으로 가는 길인지를 알고 싶었을 뿐이었고, 그리하여 나는 될 수 있는 한 빨리 걸어갔다. 런던으로든 콜체스터로든 걸어서 갈 작정이었던 것은 아니고, 다만 입스위치에서 조용히 빠져나가고 싶었던 것이다.\n\n나는 이삼 마일쯤 걸어가다가, 무슨 농사일인지는 모르겠으나 어떤 농사일에 분주한 수수한 시골 사람 하나를 만났다. 나는 처음에는 별 요지 없는 질문을 많이 했으나, 마침내 그에게 런던으로 가는 길인데 마차가 다 차서 자리를 얻지 못했다고 말하고, 둘이 탈 만한 말과 콜체스터까지 내 앞에서 말을 몰아 줄 정직한 사람을 어디서 빌릴 수 있을지 일러 줄 수 있는지 물었으니, 그래야 거기서 마차에 자리를 잡을 수 있을 것이기 때문이었다. 그 정직한 시골뜨기는 나를 골똘히 바라보며 반 분이 넘도록 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 머리를 긁적이며 말했다. \"말이라, 그러니까, 콜체스터까지, 둘이 타고 가게요? 아 그럼요, 아주머니, 저런, 돈만 있으면 말이야 얼마든지 구하시죠.\" \"그럼요, 여보세요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그건 당연하지요. 돈 없이야 바라지도 않아요.\" \"그런데요, 아주머니.\" 그가 말했다. \"얼마나 내실 생각인데요?\" \"아니.\" 내가 다시 말했다. \"여보세요, 여기 시골 삯이 얼마나 되는지 제가 모르거든요. 저는 타지 사람이라서요. 그렇지만 저를 위해 하나 구해 주실 수 있다면, 되도록 싸게 구해 주시면, 수고비로 얼마쯤 드릴게요.\"\n\n\"아, 그것 또한 정직한 말씀이군요.\" 시골 사람이 말했다. \"그렇게 정직하지도 않지.\" 나는 속으로 말했다. \"네가 다 알게 된다면 말이야.\" \"그런데요, 아주머니.\" 그가 말했다. \"둘이 탈 만한 말이 한 마리 있는데, 제가 직접 아주머니와 함께 가도 별 상관없어요.\" 그가 그렇게 말했다. \"그래 주시겠어요?\" 내가 말했다. \"아무렴요, 정직한 분 같으니, 그래 주신다면 정말 기쁘겠어요. 사례는 후하게 해 드릴게요.\" \"그럼, 보세요, 아주머니.\" 그가 말했다. \"저도 아주머니께 무리한 셈을 하지는 않을게요. 콜체스터까지 모셔다드리면, 저랑 제 말 몫으로 오 실링은 받아야겠는데요. 오늘 밤에는 돌아오기 어려울 테니까요.\"\n\n한마디로, 나는 그 정직한 사람과 그의 말을 빌렸다. 그러나 길에 있는 어느 읍에 이르렀을 때(이름은 기억나지 않으나 강가에 있는 곳이다), 나는 몹시 아픈 척하며 그날 밤은 더 갈 수 없으니, 내가 타지 사람이라 그러니 그가 거기서 나와 함께 머물러 준다면, 그와 그의 말 몫을 기꺼이 치르겠노라고 했다. 내가 이렇게 한 것은, 그 네덜란드 신사들과 그 하인들이 그날 역마차로든 파발마로든 길에 나설 것을 알았기 때문이고, 또 그 취한 사내든, 아니면 하리치에서 나를 본 적이 있을 다른 누군가든 나를 다시 알아볼지 모른다 싶었기 때문이니, 그래서 나는 하루를 멈춰 있으면 그들이 모두 지나가 버리리라 생각한 것이다.\n\n우리는 그날 밤을 그곳에서 묵었고, 이튿날 아침 그리 이르지 않게 길을 떠났으므로, 내가 콜체스터에 닿았을 때는 거의 열 시 무렵이었다. 그토록 즐거운 나날을 보냈던 그 읍을 다시 보게 된 것은 적잖은 기쁨이었고, 나는 한때 그곳에 있었던 좋은 옛 친구들을 여럿 수소문했으나 별 소득이 없었으니, 그들은 모두 죽었거나 떠나 버렸다. 젊은 아가씨들은 모두 시집을 갔거나 런던으로 가 버렸고, 일찍이 나의 은인이었던 노신사와 노부인은 다 세상을 떠났으며, 무엇보다 나를 가장 마음 아프게 한 것은, 내 첫 연인이자 훗날 내 시아주버니가 되었던 젊은 신사가 죽었다는 것이었다. 다만 그에게는 다 자란 두 아들이 남아 있었으나, 그들 역시 런던으로 옮겨 가 있었다.\n\n나는 여기서 늙은이를 돌려보내고, 사나흘 동안 신분을 숨긴 채 콜체스터에 머물렀다가, 하리치 마차에 모습을 드러내는 모험을 하고 싶지 않아 짐마차에 편승했다. 그러나 그렇게까지 조심할 필요는 없었으니, 하리치에서 나를 알아볼 수 있을 사람이라곤 여관집 여자뿐이었고, 또 그녀가 그토록 분주했던 데다 나를 단 한 번, 그것도 촛불 아래서밖에 보지 못한 것을 생각하면, 그녀가 나를 알아보았으리라고 여기는 것도 이치에 맞지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n이제 나는 런던으로 돌아왔고, 비록 지난번 모험의 우연으로 상당한 것을 얻기는 했으나, 더는 시골을 떠돌고 싶은 마음이 없었으며, 설령 죽는 날까지 이 장사를 계속한다 해도 다시는 멀리 나가지 않았을 것이다. 나는 후견인에게 내 여행담을 들려주었는데, 그녀는 하리치 여정을 꽤 마음에 들어 했고, 우리끼리 이런 일들을 이야기하던 중에 이렇게 말했다. 도둑이란 남의 실수에서 생기는 기회를 노리는 족속이니, 빈틈없고 부지런한 자에게는 숱한 기회가 생길 수밖에 없는 법이고, 따라서 나처럼 이 장사에 그토록 빼어나게 예리한 자라면 어디를 가든 무언가 비상한 것을 놓치는 일이 좀처럼 없으리라는 것이었다.\n\n다른 한편으로, 내 이야기의 가지가지는, 마땅히 헤아려 본다면 정직한 사람들에게 유익할 수 있고, 이런저런 부류의 사람들에게 비슷한 봉변을 막도록, 또 어떤 종류든 낯선 이들을 상대할 때 정신을 차리고 있도록 마땅한 경계를 줄 수 있으니, 그들의 길목에 무슨 올가미가 놓여 있지 않은 경우란 매우 드물기 때문이다. 사실 내 이야기 전체의 교훈은 독자의 분별과 판단으로 거두어 내도록 남겨 두니, 나는 그들에게 설교할 자격이 없다. 완전히 사악하고 완전히 비참했던 한 인간의 경험이, 이 글을 읽는 이들에게 쓸모 있는 경고의 곳간이 되기를 바랄 뿐이다.\n\n이제 나는 새로운 삶의 장면들로 다가가고 있다. 돌아온 뒤, 길고 긴 범죄의 행로와 적어도 내가 아는 한 견줄 데 없는 성공으로 모질어진 나는, 앞서 말했듯이, 다른 이들의 본보기로 미루어 판단한다면 결국에는 비참과 슬픔으로 끝날 수밖에 없을 그 장사를 그만둘 생각이 조금도 없었다.\n\n그 일은 뒤이은 성탄절에, 저녁에 일어났다. 길고 긴 사악함의 행로를 마무리하려는 듯, 나는 내 길에 무엇이 걸려들지 보러 밖으로 나갔는데, 포스터 거리의 어느 은세공장이를 지나다가 참으로 솔깃한 미끼를, 나 같은 직업을 가진 자로서는 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 미끼를 보았으니, 내가 보기에 가게 안에는 아무도 없었고, 창가와, 짐작건대 그 사내가 으레 가게 한쪽에서 일하던 자리에 풀려나온 은그릇이 잔뜩 놓여 있었던 것이다.\n\n나는 대담하게 안으로 들어가 막 은그릇 하나에 손을 대려던 참이었고, 가게에 딸린 사내들이 그것을 간수하는 정도로 보아 그렇게 했더라면 그것을 깨끗이 가지고 나올 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 길 건너편의, 가게가 아니라 어느 집에 있던 참견 잘하는 사내 하나가 내가 들어가는 것을 보고, 또 가게에 아무도 없는 것을 알아채고는, 길을 가로질러 달려와 가게로 들어와서는, 내가 무엇인지, 누구인지도 묻지 않고 나를 붙들고 집 사람들을 부르며 소리쳤다.\n\n나는 앞서 말했듯이 가게의 무엇에도 손대지 않았는데, 누군가가 가게로 달려오는 것을 언뜻 보고 정신을 바짝 차려 발로 그 집 바닥을 아주 세게 굴렀고, 막 소리를 지르려던 참에 그 사내가 나를 붙잡은 것이었다.\n\n그러나 나는 가장 위험할 때면 늘 가장 용기가 났던지라, 그 사내가 나를 붙잡았을 때 나는 아주 당당하게 버티며, 은수저 여섯 개를 사러 들어왔노라고 우겼다. 그리고 다행히도 그곳은 다른 가게들을 위해 은그릇을 가공할 뿐 아니라 은그릇을 팔기도 하는 은세공장이였다. 그 사내는 그 대목을 비웃으며, 자기가 이웃에게 해 준 봉사를 어찌나 대단하게 여기던지, 내가 사러 온 것이 아니라 훔치러 온 것이라고 우겨 대며 큰 군중을 모았다. 나는, 이때쯤 이웃 어딘가에서 불려 온 가게 주인에게, 여기서 소란을 피우고 이 일을 두고 입씨름하는 것은 부질없다고 말했다. 그 사내가 내가 훔치러 왔다고 우겨 대니 그것을 증명해야 할 것이라고, 그러니 더 말할 것 없이 치안판사 앞으로 가자고 했다. 나를 붙잡은 사내쯤은 충분히 감당해 낼 수 있으리라는 것이 보이기 시작했기 때문이다.\n\n가게의 주인 내외는 길 건너편에서 온 사내만큼 사납지는 정말로 않았다. 그 사내가 말했다. \"아주머니, 댁이 무슨 좋은 뜻으로 가게에 들어왔을 수도 있겠지요, 내가 어찌 알겠소. 하지만 아무도 없는 게 빤히 보이는 우리 같은 가게에 들어온 것은 댁한테 위태로운 일로 보였단 말이오. 또 내게 그렇게 친절을 베푼 이웃에게, 그 사람 쪽에 일리가 있었다는 걸 인정하지 않는다면 도리가 아니지요. 하기야 전체로 보아 댁이 무얼 가져가려 한 건 아닌 듯하니, 나도 정말이지 이 일을 어찌해야 할지 모르겠소.\" 나는 그에게 나와 함께 치안판사 앞으로 가자고 다그쳤고, 만약 내게서 강도질의 의도 비슷한 무엇이라도 증명될 수 있다면 기꺼이 따르겠으나, 그렇지 않다면 배상을 받아야겠다고 했다.\n\n우리가 이렇게 다투고 있고 군중이 문간에 모여들고 있는 바로 그때, 시市의 참사회원이자 치안판사인 T. B. 경이 지나갔는데, 금세공인이 그 소식을 듣고 나가서, 들어오셔서 이 일을 가려 주십사고 그 어른께 간청했다.\n\n금세공인에게 공정을 기하자면, 그는 자기 이야기를 매우 공정하고 절도 있게 풀어냈고, 길을 건너와 나를 붙잡았던 사내는 자기 이야기를 그만큼 격하고 어리석은 격정으로 늘어놓았는데, 그것은 도리어 내게 해가 아니라 득이 되었다. 그러고는 내가 말할 차례가 되어, 나는 그 어른께, 나는 런던에서는 이방인으로 북쪽 지방에서 막 올라온 사람인데, 어느 곳에 묵고 있다고, 이 거리를 지나다가 은수저 여섯 개를 사려고 금세공인의 가게로 들어갔노라고 말했다. 천만다행으로 내게는 호주머니에 낡은 은수저가 하나 있었으니, 나는 그것을 꺼내며, 시골에 있는 내 수저들과 짝이 맞도록 새것 여섯 개와 맞춰 보려고 그 수저를 가지고 다녔노라고 말했다. 가게에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 사람들이 듣도록 발을 아주 세게 굴렀고, 또 큰 소리로 부르기까지 했다고, 가게에 풀려나온 은그릇이 있었던 것은 사실이나 내가 그 어느 것에 손을 댔다거나 가까이 갔다고는 아무도 말할 수 없을 것이라고, 그런데 한 사내가 길에서 가게로 달려 들어와, 내가 바로 집 사람들을 부르고 있던 그 순간에 사납게 나를 붙들었다고 말했다. 만약 그가 정말로 이웃에게 무슨 도움을 줄 마음이었다면, 멀찍이 서서 내가 무엇에 손을 대는지 아닌지를 조용히 지켜보다가, 그런 다음에 나를 덮쳐 현장에서 붙잡았어야 했을 것이라고 했다. \"그것은 매우 옳은 말이오.\" 참사회원께서 말씀하시고는, 나를 막아 세운 사내를 돌아보며, 내가 발을 굴렀다는 것이 사실인지 물었다. 그는 그렇다고, 내가 발을 굴렀다고 했으나, 그것은 자기가 다가왔기 때문일 수도 있다고 했다. \"아니.\" 참사회원께서 그의 말을 가로채며 말씀하셨다. \"이제 자네가 스스로 모순되는 말을 하는군. 방금 자네는 저 부인이 자네에게 등을 돌린 채 가게 안에 있어서, 자네가 들이닥칠 때까지 자네를 보지 못했다고 하지 않았나.\" 그런데 내 등이 거리 쪽으로 어느 정도 향해 있었던 것은 사실이나, 내 일이 사방으로 눈을 두어야 하는 종류의 것인지라, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 정말로 그가 달려오는 것을 흘긋 보았으니, 다만 그가 그것을 알아채지 못했을 뿐이다.\n\n충분한 심리 끝에 참사회원께서는, 그 이웃이 착오를 범한 것이며 나는 무죄라는 견해를 내놓으셨고, 금세공인도, 그의 아내도 그에 수긍하여, 그리하여 나는 풀려났다. 그러나 내가 막 떠나려 하는데 참사회원께서 말씀하셨다. \"그런데 잠깐, 부인, 만약 부인이 수저를 살 작정이셨다면, 이 착오 때문에 여기 내 친구가 손님을 놓치게 하지는 않으시리라 믿소.\" 나는 선뜻 대답했다. \"네, 그럼요, 본보기로 가져온 제 짝 안 맞는 수저에 맞춰 줄 수만 있다면 그래도 수저를 사겠어요.\" 그러자 금세공인이 바로 그 같은 모양의 수저 몇 개를 보여 주었다. 그래서 그가 수저의 무게를 다니 서른다섯 실링이 나왔고, 나는 값을 치르려고 지갑을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 스무 기니 가까이 들어 있었으니, 나는 무슨 일이 있을지 몰라 늘 그만한 돈을 지니고 다녔고, 지금뿐 아니라 다른 때에도 그것이 쓸모 있음을 알았기 때문이다.\n\n내 돈을 보고 참사회원께서 말씀하셨다. \"자, 부인, 이제 나는 부인이 부당한 일을 당했다고 확신하오. 내가 부인더러 수저를 사라고 권하고, 사실 때까지 머무른 것도 바로 이런 까닭이었소. 만약 부인에게 값을 치를 돈이 없었다면, 나는 부인이 살 마음으로 가게에 들어온 것이 아니라고 의심했을 것이오. 사실 부인이 뒤집어쓸 뻔한 그런 짓을 꾸미고 다니는 부류의 사람들은, 내가 보는 부인과는 달리 호주머니에 금화가 그리 많은 법이 좀처럼 없으니까요.\" 나는 미소를 지으며 그 어른께, 그렇다면 제가 받은 호의의 얼마쯤은 제 돈 덕분이로군요, 하지만 그 전에 제게 베풀어 주신 공정함에도 일리가 있음을 보셨기를 바란다고 말했다. 그는 그렇다고, 그러나 이번 일이 자기 견해를 굳혀 주었고, 이제는 내가 부당한 일을 당했음을 충분히 확신한다고 했다. 그리하여 나는, 파멸의 벼랑 끝에 섰던 일에서 보란 듯이 빠져나왔다.\n\n이 일이 있은 지 겨우 사흘 뒤, 나는 예전의 위험으로도 평소처럼 조금도 조심해지지 않은 채, 그토록 오래 종사해 온 그 기술을 여전히 좇아, 문이 열려 있는 것을 본 어느 집으로 들어갔고, 정말이지 들키지 않았다고 믿으며, 그들이 비단 능직이라 부르는 매우 값진 꽃무늬 비단 두 필을 챙겼다. 그곳은 포목상도 아니고 포목상의 창고도 아니었으며, 개인 살림집처럼 보였고, 또 그렇게 보아하니 직조공들의 물건을 포목상들에게 팔아 주는, 중개인이나 거간 같은 자가 사는 집이었다.\n\n이 이야기의 이 어두운 부분을 짧게 줄이자면, 내가 막 문으로 나서려는데 두 계집이 입에 거품을 물고 내게 달려들어, 하나는 나를 방 안으로 끌어들이고 다른 하나는 내게 문을 닫아 버렸다. 나는 좋은 말로 달래려 했으나 그럴 틈이 없었으니, 불을 뿜는 두 마리 용도 그들보다 더 사납지는 못했을 것이다. 그들은 내 옷을 찢고, 마치 나를 죽이려는 듯이 윽박지르며 고래고래 소리를 질렀다. 다음으로 집 안주인이 왔고, 그다음에 주인이 왔으며, 특히 한동안은 모두가 길길이 날뛰었다.\n\n나는 주인에게 아주 좋은 말로, 문이 열려 있었고 물건들이 내게 유혹이 되었다고, 나는 가난하고 곤궁한데 가난이란 많은 이들이 떨치지 못하는 것이라고 말하며, 눈물을 흘리며 나를 가엾이 여겨 달라고 그에게 빌었다. 집 안주인은 동정심이 일어 나를 보내 주려는 쪽으로 기울었고, 자기 남편도 거의 그렇게 설득할 뻔했으나, 그 건방진 계집들이 시키지도 않았는데 벌써 달려 나가 순경을 데려왔으니, 그러자 주인은, 이제 돌이킬 수 없게 되었다고, 나를 치안판사 앞으로 데려가야 한다고 말하며, 자기가 나를 보내 준다면 자기가 곤경에 빠질 수도 있노라고 아내에게 대답했다.\n\n순경을 본 것은 정말이지 나를 공포로 찔렀고, 나는 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 것만 같았다. 나는 까무러쳐 쓰러졌고, 정말이지 그 집 사람들마저 내가 죽으려나 보다고 여겼는데, 그러자 그 여자가 다시 나를 위해 변호하며, 잃은 것이 없으니 나를 보내 주자고 자기 남편에게 간청했다. 나는, 비록 그 물건들을 가지지는 못했으나 그 값이 얼마든 그 두 필 값을 치르겠다고 그에게 제의하면서, 그는 물건을 도로 가졌고 정말로 잃은 것이 없으니, 그것들을 가져가려고 한 단순한 시도 때문에 나를 죽음으로 몰아 내 피를 보겠다는 것은 잔인한 일이라고 따졌다. 나는 순경에게, 내가 문을 부수지도, 아무것도 가지고 나가지도 않았음을 일깨웠고, 치안판사 앞에 이르러 거기서 내가 들어오려고 무엇을 부수지도, 아무것도 가지고 나오지도 않았다고 항변하자, 치안판사는 나를 풀어 주려는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그러나 처음 나를 막아 세웠던 그 건방진 계집이, 내가 물건을 가지고 나가고 있었는데 자기가 나를 막아 세워 문지방을 막 넘으려는 나를 도로 끌어들였노라고 단언하자, 치안판사는 그 점을 두고 나를 구류하였고, 나는 뉴게이트로 끌려갔다. 그 끔찍한 곳! 그 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 내 피가 다 얼어붙는다. 내 동료들 가운데 그토록 많은 이가 갇혀 있었고, 거기서 운명의 나무로 끌려갔던 곳, 내 어머니가 그토록 깊은 고초를 겪었고, 내가 세상에 태어났으며, 거기서 나로서는 치욕스러운 죽음 외에는 아무 구원도 기대할 수 없었던 곳. 결론을 짓자면, 그토록 오랫동안 나를 기다려 왔고, 내가 그토록 많은 술수와 성공으로 그토록 오래 피해 왔던 그곳이었다.\n\n나는 정말이지 마음을 다잡지 못했다. 처음 그곳에 끌려들었을 때, 또 그 음산한 곳의 온갖 끔찍한 것을 둘러보았을 때 내 마음의 공포는 말로 다 할 수 없다. 나는 나 자신을 끝장난 존재로 여겼고, 이제는 세상을 떠나는 것밖에는, 그것도 더할 나위 없는 치욕 속에 떠나는 것밖에는 생각할 것이 없다고 여겼다. 지옥 같은 소음, 고함, 욕설과 아우성, 악취와 더러움, 그리고 거기서 내가 본 마음을 짓누르는 온갖 무시무시한 무리—이 모든 것이 한데 합쳐져 그곳을 지옥 그 자체의 표상이요, 일종의 지옥으로 들어가는 입구처럼 보이게 했다.\n\n이제 나는, 앞서 말했듯이 내 자신의 이성에서, 또 내 좋은 형편을 헤아리던 데서, 또 내가 빠져나온 숱한 위험을 떠올리던 데서 형편이 좋을 때 그만두라는 숱한 암시를 받았건만, 어떻게 그 모두를 물리치고 온갖 두려움에 맞서 내 마음을 굳혀 왔던가를 자책했다. 마치 내가 피할 수 없는 보이지 않는 운명에 떠밀려 이 비참의 날에 이른 것만 같았고, 이제는 교수대에서 내 모든 죄를 속죄해야 하며, 이제 내 피로 정의에 보상해야 하고, 내 삶과 내 사악함의 마지막 시각에 함께 다다른 것만 같았다. 이런 것들이 어지러이 내 생각 속으로 쏟아져 들어와, 나를 우울과 절망에 짓눌린 채로 남겨 두었다.\n\n그때 나는 지난 내 삶 전체를 진심으로 뉘우쳤으나, 그 뉘우침은 내게 아무런 흡족함도, 아무런 평안도 주지 못했으니, 조금도 그러지 못했다. 왜냐하면 내가 스스로에게 말했듯이, 그것은 더 죄지을 힘이 빼앗긴 뒤의 뉘우침이었기 때문이다. 나는 그런 죄를 저질렀다는 것을, 또 그 행위가 하느님과 내 이웃에 대한 죄였다는 점을 슬퍼하는 것이 아니라, 그 때문에 벌받게 되었다는 것을 슬퍼하는 듯했다. 내 생각에 나는, 죄지은 것을 뉘우치는 참회자가 아니라, 벌받게 된 것을 뉘우치는 참회자였고, 이것이 내 마음속에서 뉘우침의 모든 위안을, 아니 그 희망마저 앗아 가 버렸다.\n\n그 비참한 곳에 들어온 뒤로 여러 밤, 여러 날 동안 나는 잠을 이루지 못했고, 한동안은 차라리 거기서 죽었으면 좋겠다는 마음이었으나, 그러면서도 죽음을 마땅히 헤아려야 할 만큼 헤아린 것도 아니었다. 정말이지 내 상상에 그곳보다 더 공포로 가득한 것은 없었고, 거기 있던 무리보다 더 역겨운 것도 없었다. 아! 세상 그 어디로든, 뉴게이트만 아닌 곳으로 보내졌더라면, 나는 나 자신을 행복하게 여겼으련만.\n\n다음으로, 나보다 먼저 그곳에 있던 그 모진 인간들이 얼마나 나를 두고 의기양양해했던가! 아니! 플랜더스 부인이 마침내 뉴게이트에 들어왔다고? 아니! 메리 부인이, 몰리 부인이, 그러고도 그저 몰 플랜더스가? 그들은, 내가 그토록 오래 활개를 쳤으니 악마가 나를 도왔던 것이라고들 했다. 그들은 여러 해 전부터 나를 거기서 기다렸는데, 마침내 내가 왔느냐는 것이었다. 그러고는 내 풀 죽은 모습을 두고 나를 놀려 대고, 이곳에 온 것을 환영하며, 잘 왔다고 인사하고, 기운을 내라고, 풀 죽지 말라고, 일이 내가 두려워하는 것만큼 나쁘지 않을 수도 있다는 둥 말했다. 그러고는 브랜디를 청해 내게 건배했으나, 그것을 모두 내 외상으로 달아 놓았으니, 그들 말로는 내가 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 이제 막 이 대학에 들어왔으니, 자기들에게는 없어도 내게는 분명 호주머니에 돈이 있으리라는 것이었다.\n\n나는 이 패거리 가운데 하나에게 거기 온 지 얼마나 되었느냐고 물었다. 그녀는 넉 달이라고 했다. 나는 그녀에게, 처음 들어왔을 때 이곳이 그녀에게 어떻게 보였느냐고 물었다. \"지금 댁한테 보이는 그대로였지.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"무섭고 끔찍했어.\" 자기가 지옥에 와 있다고 여겼다는 것이다. \"지금도 그렇게 믿고 있지.\" 그녀가 덧붙였다. \"하지만 이젠 그게 자연스러워서, 나는 그걸로 마음 쓰지 않아.\" \"그럼 짐작건대.\" 내가 말했다. \"댁은 앞으로 닥칠 일에 대해서는 위험이 없는 거예요?\" \"아니.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"그건 댁이 잘못 안 거야, 정말이지. 나는 이미 선고를 받았는데, 다만 임신을 사유로 호소했을 뿐이거든. 그렇지만 나는 나를 재판한 판사만큼이나 임신한 게 아니라서, 다음 개정기에 다시 불려 나가게 될 거야.\" 이 '불려 나간다'는 것은, 여자가 임신을 사유로 형 집행이 유예되었다가 임신한 것이 아닌 것으로 드러나거나, 혹은 임신했다 해도 이미 해산한 경우에, 앞서의 판결을 받으러 다시 불려 내려가는 것을 말한다. \"그래.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런데 그렇게 마음이 편해요?\" \"그럼.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"달리 어쩌겠어. 슬퍼해 봤자 무슨 소용이야? 교수형을 당하면, 그걸로 내 끝장인걸.\" 그녀가 말하고는, 춤추며 돌아서서 가면서 다음과 같은 뉴게이트 식 익살 한 토막을 노래했다.\n\n\"내가 밧줄에 매달려 흔들리면, 종소리가 들려오겠지, 그러면 가엾은 제니도 그걸로 끝장이라네.\"\n\n내가 이것을 언급하는 까닭은, 훗날 이와 같은 불운에 빠져 뉴게이트라는 그 무시무시한 곳에 오게 될 어떤 죄수에게든, 시간과 필요와 거기 있는 인간들과의 어울림이 어떻게 그곳을 그들에게 익숙하게 만드는지, 처음에는 세상에서 그들의 정신을 가장 짓누르던 것에 어떻게 마침내 그들이 길들여지는지, 또 거기서 벗어나 있을 때 그러했던 만큼이나 그 비참 속에서도 어떻게 뻔뻔스럽게 명랑하고 흥겨워하는지를 눈여겨볼 만한 일이기 때문이다.\n\n나는 더러 사람들이 그러듯 이 악마가 그려진 것만큼 그리 검지는 않다고는 말할 수 없으니, 정말이지 어떤 색채로도 그곳을 실제 그대로 그려 낼 수 없고, 거기서 고초를 겪어 본 이들 외에는 어떤 영혼도 그것을 올바로 헤아릴 수 없기 때문이다. 그러나 지옥이 어떻게 차츰 그토록 자연스러워지고, 견딜 만할 뿐 아니라 심지어 흡족해지기까지 하는지는, 나처럼 그것을 겪어 본 이들 외에는 이해할 수 없는 일이다.\n\n내가 뉴게이트로 보내진 바로 그날 밤, 나는 그 소식을 내 늙은 후견인에게 알렸는데, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그녀는 그 소식에 놀랐고, 내가 뉴게이트 안에서 그러했던 만큼이나 뉴게이트 밖에서 그날 밤을 거의 비참하게 보냈다.\n\n이튿날 아침 그녀가 나를 보러 왔다. 그녀는 나를 위로하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다했으나, 그것이 부질없음을 알았다. 그러나 그녀의 말마따나 그 무게에 짓눌려 주저앉는 것은 그 무게를 더하는 것일 뿐이었다. 그녀는 곧장 우리가 두려워하던 그 결과를 막기 위한 온갖 적절한 방도에 매달렸는데, 먼저 나를 봉변에 빠뜨린 그 사나운 두 계집을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 그들을 구워삶고, 돈을 내밀며, 한마디로 기소를 막을 온갖 상상할 수 있는 수단을 다 써 보았다. 그녀는 그 계집들 가운데 하나에게 자기 여주인을 떠나 나를 거슬러 나서지 말라고 백 파운드를 제의했으나, 그녀는 어찌나 단호하던지, 연봉이 삼 파운드 남짓한 한낱 하녀에 불과한데도 그것을 거절했으니, 내 후견인이 믿기로는, 설령 오백 파운드를 제의했더라도 거절했으리라는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 다른 하녀를 공략했다. 그녀는 겉보기에 다른 쪽만큼 모질지는 않았고, 때로는 자비를 베풀 마음이 있는 듯도 했다. 그러나 첫 번째 계집이 그녀를 부추겨 마음을 돌려놓았고, 내 후견인이 그녀와 이야기하는 것조차 못 하게 했을 뿐 아니라, 증거에 손을 댄다며 그녀를 고발하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다.\n\n그러고 나서 그녀는 주인에게, 다시 말해 물건을 도둑맞은 사내에게, 특히 앞서 여러분에게 말했듯이 처음에 나를 다소 가엾이 여기는 쪽으로 기울었던 그의 아내에게 접근했다. 그녀는 그 여자가 여전히 한결같음을 알았으나, 남자는 자기를 구류한 치안판사에게 매여 기소를 해야 하며, 그렇지 않으면 자기 보석 보증금을 잃게 된다고 주장했다.\n\n내 후견인은, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 그의 보석 보증서를 서류철에서 빼내, 그가 손해를 보지 않도록 해 줄 친구들을 찾아 주겠노라고 제의했다. 그러나 그가 그렇게 될 수 있다는 것을, 혹은 나를 거슬러 나서는 것 외에는 세상 어떤 방법으로도 자기가 안전할 수 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기란 불가능했다. 그리하여 나는 사실관계의 증인 셋을, 곧 주인과 그의 두 하녀를 거스르는 증인으로 두게 되었으니, 다시 말해 나는 내가 살아 있다는 것이 확실한 만큼이나 목숨을 빼앗기는 선고를 받으리라는 것이 확실했고, 죽음을 생각하고 그것을 준비하는 것 외에는 할 일이 없었다. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 의지할 슬픈 토대밖에 가지지 못했으니, 내 모든 뉘우침이 내게는 그저 죽음에 대한 두려움의 결과로만 보였을 뿐, 내가 살아온 사악한 삶에 대한, 그리고 이 비참을 내게 안긴 그 삶에 대한, 또 이제 갑자기 나의 심판자가 되실 나의 창조주를 거스른 데 대한 진정한 회한은 아니었기 때문이다.\n\n나는 여기서 여러 날을 영혼의 더할 나위 없는 공포 속에 살았다. 나는 말하자면 죽음을 눈앞에 두고 있었고, 밤낮으로 교수대와 올가미, 악령과 악마 외에는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 죽음에 대한 무시무시한 두려움과, 내 지난 끔찍한 삶을 책망하는 양심의 공포 사이에서 내가 얼마나 시달렸는지는 말로 표현할 수 없다.\n\n뉴게이트의 교무 사제가 내게 와서 그 나름의 방식으로 잠시 이야기했으나, 그의 모든 신학은 그가 부르는 대로 하자면 내 죄를(비록 그는 내가 무슨 죄로 들어왔는지도 몰랐지만) 고백하고, 낱낱이 털어놓는 따위에 매여 있었으니, 그렇게 하지 않으면 하느님께서 결코 나를 용서하지 않으시리라고 그가 내게 말했다. 그가 요지에 닿는 말을 하도 적게 하는 통에, 나는 그에게서 아무런 위안도 얻지 못했다. 게다가 이 가엾은 자가 아침에는 내게 고백과 회개를 설교하는 것을 보다가, 정오에는 브랜디며 독한 술에 취해 있는 꼴을 보니, 거기에는 어찌나 충격적인 데가 있던지, 나는 그자의 일보다 그자를 더 역겨워하기 시작했고, 그자 때문에 차츰 그자의 일까지도 역겨워졌다. 그래서 나는 그에게 더는 나를 성가시게 하지 말아 달라고 했다.\n\n어찌 된 일인지 모르겠으나, 부지런한 내 후견인의 지칠 줄 모르는 진력 덕분에 나는 첫 개정기에는 기소되지 않았으니, 길드홀의 대배심에 회부되지 않았다는 말이다. 그래서 나는 한 달 내지 다섯 주의 시간을 더 갖게 되었고, 의심할 바 없이 나는 이것을 지나간 일을 성찰하고 다가올 일을 준비할 만큼의 시간을 받은 것으로, 혹은 한마디로 회개를 위해 주어진 말미로 여기고 그렇게 써야 마땅했으나, 내게는 그럴 마음이 없었다. 나는 (전과 같이) 뉴게이트에 있는 것을 슬퍼했을 뿐, 내게 회개의 기색은 거의 없었다.\n\n도리어, 산의 동굴과 구덩이 속의 물이 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 무엇이든 돌처럼 굳히고 돌로 바꾸어 놓듯이, 나처럼 그런 지옥의 사냥개 같은 무리와 끊임없이 어울리는 것은 내게도 다른 사람들에게와 똑같은 작용을 했다. 나는 돌로 굳어 갔으니, 처음에는 멍청하고 무감각해지더니, 그다음에는 짐승같이 분별없어지고, 마침내는 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 미쳐 날뛰게 되었다. 그리하여 한마디로, 나는 정말로 거기서 태어나기라도 한 것처럼 그곳에 자연스럽게 흡족하고 편안해졌다.\n\n우리의 본성이 그토록 타락할 수 있어서, 그 자체로 더할 나위 없는 비참인 것을 즐겁고 흡족하게 만들 수 있다는 것은 좀처럼 상상하기 어려운 일이다. 여기에 더 나쁜 것을 들기란 좀처럼 불가능하리라 여겨지는 처지가 있었으니, 곧 흔한 경우를 두고 말하자면, 생명과 건강과, 나처럼 그들을 도울 돈을 가진 그 누구라도 도달할 수 있는 만큼이나, 나는 더없이 지독하게 비참했던 것이다.\n\n내게는, 조금이라도 성찰의 힘이 남아 있고 이승의 행복과 저승의 비참에 대한 어떤 감각이라도 지닌 인간이라면 누구라도 가라앉히고 말 만큼의 죄의 무게가 얹혀 있었다. 그러고는 처음에는 정말이지 가책이 있었으나 회개는 없었고, 이제는 가책도 회개도 없었다. 내게는 우리 법에 따라 그 형벌이 사형인 죄목이 씌워져 있었고, 그 증거가 어찌나 명백하던지 내가 무죄를 항변할 여지조차 없었다. 나는 상습범이라는 이름이 붙어 있었으므로, 몇 주 안에 죽음 외에는 기대할 것이 없었고, 나 자신도 빠져나갈 생각이 전혀 없었다. 그런데도 어떤 기이한 영혼의 무기력이 나를 사로잡았다. 내게는 아무 근심도, 아무 두려움도, 아무 슬픔도 없었으니, 처음의 놀라움은 가셨다. 나는, 어떻게라고 잘 말할 수 있으니, 나도 어떻게인지 모르는 채로, 내 감각도, 내 이성도, 아니 내 양심마저도 모두 잠들어 있었던 것이다. 사십 년에 걸친 내 삶의 행로는 사악함과 매음과 간통과 근친상간과 거짓말과 도둑질의 끔찍한 뒤엉킴이었고, 한마디로 살인과 반역을 제외한 모든 것이 열여덟 살 무렵부터 예순 살에 이르기까지 내 소행이었다. 그리고 이제 나는 형벌의 비참 속에 잠겨 들었고, 치욕스러운 죽음이 바로 문간에 와 있었건만, 나는 내 처지에 대한 아무런 감각도, 적어도 잠깐 스쳐 가는 가벼운 접촉, 곧 힌트만 주고 사라지는 결림이나 통증 이상으로 천국이나 지옥에 대한 아무런 생각도 없었다. 나는 하느님의 자비를 구할 마음도, 정말이지 그것을 떠올릴 마음도 없었다. 그리고 이로써 나는 이 땅에서 가장 완전한 비참을 간략히 그려 낸 셈이라고 생각한다.\n\n나를 두려움에 떨게 하던 온갖 생각은 지나갔고, 그곳의 끔찍함은 익숙해졌으며, 나는 그 소음을 내는 자들이 그러했던 것 못지않게 감옥의 소음과 아우성에 더는 불편함을 느끼지 않았다. 한마디로, 나는 한낱 뉴게이트의 새가 되어, 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 사악하고 거칠어졌다. 아니, 지금까지 내내 내 대화에 배어 있던 좋은 가정교육과 예의범절의 습관마저 거의 간직하지 못했다. 어찌나 철저한 타락이 나를 사로잡았던지, 나는 마치 지금의 나 외에 다른 무엇이었던 적이 없기라도 한 것처럼, 더는 예전의 나와 같은 존재가 아니었다.\n\n내 삶의 이 모질어진 부분의 한가운데서, 나는 또 한 번 갑작스러운 놀라움을 겪었으니, 그것은 정말이지 그 전에 이미 그 감각을 넘어서 버린 슬픔이라는 것으로 나를 잠시 도로 불러 세웠다. 어느 날 밤 사람들이 내게 말하기를, 그 전날 밤늦게 감옥으로 노상강도 세 명이 끌려 들어왔는데, 그들은 윈저로 가는 길 어딘가에서—내 생각에 그것은 하운슬로 황야였던 듯하다—강도질을 저질렀고, 시골 사람들에게 억스브리지까지 추격당했으며, 거기서 용감하게 저항한 끝에 붙잡혔는데, 그 와중에 시골 사람들 가운데 몇이나 부상당하고 더러는 죽었는지 나는 모른다고 했다.\n\n우리 죄수들이 모두 이 용맹하고 한가락 하는 신사들을, 그 패거리가 일찍이 알려진 바 없는 그런 자들이라고 떠받들어지는 이들을 너무도 보고 싶어 한 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다. 더욱이 그들이 감옥의 우두머리 간수에게 돈을 주어 감옥의 그 더 나은 구역을 쓸 자유를 얻었으므로, 아침에 압류 마당으로 옮겨지리라는 말이 있었기에 더욱 그러했다. 그래서 우리 여자들은 그들을 꼭 보려고 길목에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 맨 처음 나온 사내가 다름 아닌 나의 랭커셔 남편임을, 던스터블에서 그토록 잘 살았던 바로 그 사람이며, 앞서 이야기한 대로 내가 마지막 남편과 결혼했을 때 내가 훗날 브릭힐에서 보았던 바로 그 사람임을 알아보았을 때 내가 빠진 그 경악과 놀라움은 무엇으로도 표현할 수 없다.\n\n나는 그 광경에 말문이 막혔고, 무슨 말을 해야 할지도 무엇을 해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그가 나를 알아보지 못한 것, 그것만이 그 순간 내가 받은 위안의 전부였다. 나는 일행에게서 물러나, 그 끔찍한 곳이 누구에게든 허락하는 만큼 멀찍이 물러나 한참을 격하게 울었다. \"끔찍한 인간이로구나, 나라는 것은.\" 나는 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 많은 가엾은 사람들을 비참하게 만들었던가? 얼마나 많은 절망에 빠진 인간들을 악마에게 보냈던가?\" 그는 체스터에서 내게, 자기가 그 혼인으로 파멸했으며 내 탓으로 자기 처지가 절망에 빠졌노라고 했었으니, 내가 재산가인 줄로 여기고 갚을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 빚을 졌으며, 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠다고 했었다. 군에 들어가 총을 들든지, 아니면 말을 사서 그가 부르는 대로 하자면 한 바퀴 떠돌든지 하겠다고 했었다. 그리고 비록 내가 그에게 내가 재산가라고 말한 적은 결코 없어서 나 자신이 실제로 그를 속인 것은 아니었으나, 나는 내가 그러하다고 여겨지는 것을 부추기기는 했으니, 그런 방식으로 나는 애초에 그의 불행의 빌미가 되었던 것이다.\n\n그 일의 놀라움은 다만 내 생각 속으로 더 깊이 파고들어, 그 전에 내게 닥쳤던 그 모든 것보다 더 강한 성찰을 안겨 주었다. 나는 밤낮으로 그를 위해 슬퍼했고, 그가 그 패거리의 두목이며, 어찌나 많은 강도질을 저질렀던지 하인드나 휘트니나 골든 파머도 그에 비하면 어수룩하다는 말을, 또 그가 태어난 지방에 사람이 더는 남아 있지 않다 해도 그는 틀림없이 교수형을 당하리라는 말을, 또 그를 거슬러 무수히 많은 사람이 나설 것이라는 말을 그들에게 듣고는 더욱 슬퍼했다. 나는 그를 위한 슬픔에 압도되었고, 내 자신의 처지는 이에 비하면 아무런 동요도 주지 못했으며, 나는 그에 대한 자책으로 나 자신을 짓눌렀다. 나는 그의 불행을, 또 그가 이제 다다른 파멸을 어찌나 슬퍼했던지, 이제는 그 전처럼 아무것에도 흥미를 느끼지 못했고, 내가 살아온 끔찍하고 가증스러운 삶에 대한 최초의 성찰이 내게 되돌아오기 시작했으며, 이런 것들이 되돌아옴에 따라 내가 있는 그곳에 대한, 또 거기서 살아가는 방식에 대한 혐오도 되돌아왔다. 한마디로, 나는 완전히 바뀌어 다른 사람이 되었다.\n\n그를 위한 이런 슬픔의 영향 아래 있던 동안, 다음 개정기가 다가오니 대배심에 나를 거스르는 기소장이 제출될 것이며, 올드베일리에서 틀림없이 목숨을 걸고 재판받게 되리라는 통지가 내게 왔다. 내 마음은 이미 누그러진 터였고, 내가 익혀 온 그 모질고 사악한 마음의 대담함이 가라앉았으며, 감옥에서 죄를 의식하면서 죄책감이 내 마음속으로 흘러들기 시작했다. 한마디로 나는 생각하기 시작했고, 생각한다는 것은 지옥에서 천국으로 향하는 하나의 진정한 전진이다. 내가 앞서 그토록 많이 이야기한 그 모든 지옥 같고 모질어진 영혼의 상태와 기질은 다름 아닌 생각의 박탈일 뿐이니, 생각하는 힘을 되찾은 자는 자기 자신을 되찾는 것이다.\n\n이를테면 내가 생각하기 시작하자마자, 맨 처음 떠오른 생각이 이렇게 터져 나왔다. \"주여! 나는 어찌 될까요? 나는 틀림없이 죽겠지요! 나는 틀림없이 선고를 받을 테고, 그 너머에는 죽음밖에 없으니! 내게는 친구도 없는데, 어찌해야 하나요? 나는 틀림없이 선고를 받을 거예요! 주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서! 나는 어찌 될까요?\" 여러분은 이것이 그토록 오랜 시간이 흐른 뒤에 내 영혼에 그런 종류로 떠오른 첫 생각치고는 슬픈 생각이라고 말할 테고, 그런데도 이것조차 다가올 일에 대한 두려움일 뿐이어서, 그 안에는 진심 어린 회개의 말이라곤 한마디도 없었다. 그러나 나는 정말이지 더없이 끔찍하게 풀이 죽고 더할 나위 없이 비탄에 잠겼다. 그리고 내 비참한 생각을 나눌 친구가 세상에 하나도 없었던지라, 그것이 너무도 무겁게 나를 짓눌러, 하루에도 몇 번씩 발작과 졸도에 빠뜨렸다. 나는 내 늙은 후견인을 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 공정하게 말하건대 그녀는 진정한 친구의 몫을 다했다. 그녀는 대배심이 기소장을 인정하지 못하도록 온갖 수단을 다 동원했다. 그녀는 배심원 한둘을 찾아내어 그들과 이야기하고, 아무것도 가져간 것이 없고 집을 부수지도 않았다는 등의 이유로 그들이 호의적인 마음을 품도록 애썼다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었으니, 그들은 나머지 사람들에게 눌렸고, 두 계집이 그 사실관계를 단단히 못 박아 증언하여, 배심은 강도질과 가택 침입, 곧 중죄와 주거 침입 절도로 나를 거스르는 기소장을 인정했다.\n\n그들이 내게 그 소식을 가져왔을 때 나는 주저앉았고, 다시 정신을 차린 뒤에는 그 무게에 짓눌려 죽을 것만 같았다. 내 후견인은 내게 진정한 어머니의 몫을 다했으니, 나를 가엾이 여기고, 나와 함께, 또 나를 위해 울었으나, 나를 도울 수는 없었다. 게다가 그 공포를 더하느라, 온 옥사에 내가 그 일로 죽으리라는 말이 떠돌았다. 나는 그들이 저희끼리 그것을 이야기하는 것을 자주 들을 수 있었고, 그들이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 가엾다는 둥, 그곳에서 으레 그러듯 그런 말을 하는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그러나 여전히 아무도 내게 와서 자기 생각을 말해 주지는 않았는데, 마침내 간수 가운데 하나가 내게 은밀히 와서 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이런, 플랜더스 부인, 금요일에 재판을 받게 됐어요.\" (.) \"어쩌실 작정이오?\" 나는 행주처럼 하얗게 질려 말했다. \"어찌해야 할지는 하느님께서나 아시겠지요. 나로서는 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\" \"이봐요.\" 그가 말했다. \"내 빈말은 안 하리다, 죽음을 준비하시는 게 좋겠소. 선고를 받을 게 걱정이거든요. 게다가 사람들 말로 댁이 상습범이라니, 자비는 거의 못 받지 싶소. 사람들 말이,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"댁 사건은 아주 명백한 데다, 증인들이 어찌나 댁을 단단히 못 박아 증언하는지, 버틸 재간이 없을 거라더군요.\"\n\n이것은 나처럼 그 전에 이미 짓눌려 있던 자의 급소를 찌르는 일격이었고, 나는 한참 동안 그에게 좋은 말이든 나쁜 말이든 한마디도 할 수 없었다. 그러다 마침내 나는 울음을 터뜨리며 그에게 말했다. \"주여! ○○ 씨, 나는 어찌해야 하나요?\" \"어찌하긴!\" 그가 말했다. \"교무 사제를 부르러 보내요. 목사를 불러 그와 이야기를 나누시오. 정말이지, 플랜더스 부인, 매우 좋은 친구들이 있지 않은 한, 댁은 이 세상 사람이 아니거든요.\"\n\n이것은 정말이지 솔직한 처사였으나, 내게는, 적어도 내 생각에는 매우 가혹했다. 그는 나를 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 혼란 속에 남겨 두었고, 그날 밤 내내 나는 깨어 누워 있었다. 그리고 이제 나는 기도를 드리기 시작했는데, 마지막 남편이 죽은 뒤로, 혹은 그 얼마 뒤로는 거의 드린 적이 없는 기도였다. 그리고 정말이지 나는 그것을 기도를 드린다고밖에 부를 수 없으니, 나는 어찌나 혼란에 빠지고 마음에 어찌나 공포가 서려 있던지, 비록 울며 \"주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\"라는 그 흔한 말을 여러 번 되풀이했으나, 정말이지 그러했듯이 내가 비참한 죄인임을, 또 내 죄를 하느님께 고백하고 예수 그리스도를 위하여 용서를 구한다는 그 어떤 감각에도 나 자신을 이르게 하지 못했기 때문이다. 나는 목숨을 걸고 재판받게 되었고, 틀림없이 단죄받으리라는, 그러면 틀림없이 처형되리라는 내 처지에 대한 감각에 압도되어, 이런 까닭에 밤새도록 부르짖었다. \"주여, 나는 어찌 될까요? 주여! 나는 어찌해야 하나요? 주여! 나는 교수형을 당하겠지요! 주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\" 그런 말을 되풀이했다.\n\n내 가엾고 비탄에 잠긴 후견인은 이제 나만큼이나 마음을 졸였고, 비록 재판과 선고에 회부될 가망은 없었으나 훨씬 더 진심으로 뉘우치고 있었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 나만큼 그럴 만하지 않은 것은 아니었고, 그녀 자신도 그렇게 말했다. 다만 그녀는 여러 해 동안, 나와 다른 이들이 훔쳐 온 것을 받고 우리가 그것을 훔치도록 부추기는 것 외에는 직접 한 일이 없을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그녀는 미친 사람처럼 울며 안달했으니, 두 손을 비틀고, 자기는 끝장났다고, 하늘로부터 자기에게 저주가 내린 것이 분명하다고, 자기는 지옥에 떨어질 것이라고, 자기는 자기 친구들을 모두 파멸시켰다고, 이러이러한 자를, 또 이러이러한 자를, 또 이러이러한 자를 교수대로 보냈다고 부르짖었다. 그러고는 거기서 열 명인가 열한 명을 꼽았는데, 그중 더러는 내가 그 비명횡사를 이야기한 바 있고, 또 이제는 자기가 내 파멸의 빌미가 되었으니, 내가 그만두려 했을 때 자기가 나더러 계속하라고 설득했기 때문이라고 했다. 나는 거기서 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"아니에요, 어머니, 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런 말씀 마세요. 어머니는 제가 포목상의 돈을 도로 손에 넣었을 때, 또 제가 하리치에서 돌아왔을 때 제가 그만두기를 바라셨지만, 제가 어머니 말씀을 들으려 하지 않았던걸요. 그러니 어머니는 잘못한 게 없어요. 나 자신을 파멸시킨 건 오직 저예요, 나 자신을 이 비참에 빠뜨린 건 저라고요.\" 이렇게 우리는 여러 시간을 함께 보냈다.\n\n자, 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 기소는 진행되었고, 목요일에 나는 사법회관으로 끌려 내려가 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 기소 인정 신문을 받았으며, 이튿날 재판받도록 정해졌다. 기소 인정 신문에서 나는 \"무죄\"를 항변했는데, 그럴 만도 했으니, 나는 중죄와 주거 침입 절도로, 곧 앤서니 존슨의 소유물인 능직 비단 두 필을, 값으로 사십육 파운드어치를 중죄로 훔치고 그의 문을 부수어 연 죄목으로 기소되었기 때문이다. 그런데 나는 그들이 내가 문을 부수었다거나, 빗장 하나라도 들어 올렸다는 것을 증명하는 척조차 할 수 없음을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n금요일에 나는 재판정에 끌려 나갔다. 나는 그 전 이삼 일 동안 울며 기운을 다 소진한 터라, 목요일 밤에는 예상보다 잘 잤고, 정말이지 내가 가질 수 있으리라 여겼던 것보다 더 큰 용기를 재판에 임해 지니고 있었다.\n\n재판이 시작되어 기소장이 낭독되자 나는 말을 하려 했으나, 그들은 먼저 증인들의 말을 들어야 하며, 그런 다음에 내가 진술할 시간을 가지게 되리라고 했다. 증인들은 그 두 계집으로, 정말이지 입버릇 사나운 한 쌍의 계집이었으니, 그 일이 대체로는 사실이었음에도, 그들은 그것을 극단으로 부풀려, 내가 물건을 온전히 내 손에 쥐고 있었다고, 내가 그것을 옷 속에 숨겼다고, 내가 그것을 가지고 빠져나가고 있었다고, 자기들이 모습을 드러냈을 때 내가 한쪽 발을 문지방 너머에 두고 있었고 그러고 나서 다른 발도 마저 넘겨, 그들이 나를 붙들기 전에 내가 물건을 가지고 집을 완전히 벗어나 거리에 나와 있었다고, 그래서 자기들이 나를 붙잡아 도로 데려왔으며 내게서 물건을 빼앗았다고 맹세했다. 대체로 그 사실은 모두 진실이었으나, 나는 그들이 내가 집 문지방을 채 벗어나기도 전에 나를 막아 세웠다고 믿었고, 또 그렇게 우겼다. 그러나 그것은 별 의미가 없었으니, 내가 물건을 가져갔고, 붙잡히지만 않았다면 그것을 가지고 빠져나가고 있었으리라는 것은 분명했기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 나는, 내가 아무것도 훔치지 않았다고, 그들은 아무것도 잃지 않았다고, 문이 열려 있었고 나는 거기 놓인 물건을 보고 사려는 마음으로 들어갔노라고 항변했다. 집에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 그것들 가운데 무엇을 손에 들었다 한들, 내가 그것을 훔치려 했다고 단정할 수는 없으니, 나는 그것을 더 밝은 빛으로 보려고 문 너머로는 결코 가져가지 않았기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n법정은 그것을 결코 받아들이지 않았고, 내가 물건을 사려 했다는 것을 일종의 농담거리로 삼았으니, 그곳은 무엇을 파는 가게가 아니었기 때문이다. 또 물건을 보려고 문간으로 가져갔다는 것에 대해서는, 하녀들이 거기에 뻔뻔스러운 조롱을 퍼붓고 그 일에 한껏 재치를 부려 댔으니, 내가 그것들을 충분히 보았고 매우 만족스러워했다고, 왜냐하면 내가 그것들을 옷 밑에 챙겨 넣고 그것들을 가지고 가고 있었기 때문이라고 법정에 말했다.\n\n한마디로, 나는 중죄로는 유죄가 인정되었으나 주거 침입 절도로는 무죄가 선고되었는데, 그것은 내게 거의 위안이 되지 못했으니, 앞엣것이 나를 사형 선고로 이끌었고 뒤엣것도 그 이상은 하지 못했을 것이기 때문이다. 이튿날 나는 그 무시무시한 선고를 받으러 끌려 내려갔고, 그들이 ……에 이르렀을 때\n내가 어찌하여 선고가 내려져서는 안 되는지 할 말이 있느냐고 묻기에, 나는 한동안 입을 다물고 서 있었으나, 내 뒤에 서 있던 누군가가 큰 소리로 재판관들에게 말씀을 올리라고, 그분들이 나를 위해 사정을 호의적으로 헤아려 줄 수 있을 것이라고 일러 주었다. 이에 용기를 얻어 나는 입을 열어, 선고를 막을 만한 할 말은 없으나 법정의 자비를 청할 말은 많다고 말했고, 이러한 경우에는 그 정황을 어느 정도 참작해 주시기를 바란다고, 나는 문을 부수지도 않았고 아무것도 가지고 나오지 않았으며, 아무도 무엇 하나 잃지 않았고, 그 물건의 주인이 자비를 베풀어 주기를 바란다고 말해 주었으며(이는 실로 그가 매우 정직하게 그렇게 해 준 것이었다), 아무리 나쁘게 보아도 그것은 초범이며 나는 그 전에 한 번도 어떤 사법 법정에도 선 적이 없다고 말했다. 한마디로, 나는 내가 할 수 있으리라 여겼던 것보다 더 큰 용기를 내어, 그토록 사람을 움직이는 어조로, 비록 눈물을 흘리기는 했으나 말을 가로막을 만큼 많은 눈물은 아니게끔 말했고, 내 말을 듣던 다른 이들마저 눈물짓게 한 것을 볼 수 있었다.\n\n재판관들은 엄숙하고 말없이 앉아, 내 말을 너그럽게 들어 주고 내가 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 시간을 주었으나, 그에 대해 가타부타 한마디도 하지 않은 채 내게 사형 선고를 내렸으니, 그것은 내게 죽음 그 자체와도 같은 선고였고, 그것이 낭독된 뒤에 나는 정신이 아득해졌다. 내게는 더 이상 기력이 남아 있지 않았고, 말할 혀도, 하느님이든 사람이든 우러러볼 눈도 없었다.\n\n가엾은 내 후견인은 더없이 비탄에 잠겼으니, 전에는 나를 위로해 주던 그녀가 이제는 스스로 위로를 필요로 했고, 때로는 슬퍼하고 때로는 광란하며, 겉으로 보기에는 베들럼의 어떤 미친 여자 못지않게 제정신이 아니었다. 그녀는 나 때문에만 비탄에 잠긴 것이 아니라, 자기 자신의 사악한 삶을 자각하는 데서 오는 공포에 사로잡혀, 나와는 사뭇 다른 마음으로 그것을 되돌아보기 시작했으니, 그녀는 그 불운을 슬퍼하는 것에 더하여 자기 죄에 대해 더할 나위 없이 깊이 참회했기 때문이다. 그녀는 또한 목사 한 사람을 불렀으니, 진지하고 경건한 선량한 사람으로, 그의 도움을 받아 진실한 참회의 일에 어찌나 간곡히 매달렸던지, 나는 그녀가 진정한 참회자라고 믿게 되었고 그 목사 역시 그러했다. 더욱이 그녀는 그때 그 순간만 그러했던 것이 아니라, 내가 들은 바로는 죽는 날까지 한결같이 그러했다.\n\n지금 내 처지가 어떠했는지는 말로 표현하기보다 마음으로 헤아릴 일이다. 내 앞에는 닥쳐온 죽음 외에는 아무것도 없었고, 나를 도와주거나 나를 위해 애써 줄 벗 하나 없었으니, 나는 그저 그 이튿주 금요일에 나 말고 다섯 사람을 더 처형하기 위해 내려올 사형집행영장에서 내 이름을 발견하게 되리라는 것밖에는 아무것도 기대할 수 없었다.\n\n그러는 사이, 비탄에 빠진 가엾은 내 후견인이 목사 한 사람을 내게 보냈는데, 그는 처음에는 그녀의 청으로, 나중에는 나 자신의 청으로 나를 찾아왔다. 그는 내게 모든 죄를 진지하게 참회하고 더는 내 영혼을 가지고 미적거리지 말라고, 그가 듣기로는 기대할 여지가 전혀 없다는 목숨에 헛된 희망을 품지 말고, 온 영혼을 다해 거짓 없이 하느님을 우러러보며 예수 그리스도의 이름으로 용서를 빌라고 간곡히 권면했다. 그는 자기 말을 적절한 성경 구절로 뒷받침하여, 아무리 큰 죄인이라도 참회하고 그 악한 길에서 돌아서도록 북돋웠으며, 말을 마치고는 무릎을 꿇고 나와 함께 기도했다.\n\n내가 참회의 어떤 진정한 기미라도 처음 느낀 것은 바로 이때였다. 나는 이제 내 지난 삶을 혐오하며 되돌아보기 시작했고, 시간의 저편과 삶의 일들을 어느 정도 들여다보게 되면서—이런 때에는 누구나 그러하리라 믿거니와—전과는 다른 면모로, 전혀 다른 모습으로 그것들을 바라보기 시작했다. 가장 위대하고 가장 좋은 것들, 행복의 전망, 삶의 기쁨과 슬픔이 전혀 다른 것이 되어 있었고, 내 머릿속에는 내가 살면서 알던 것보다 한없이 우월한 것들밖에 없어서, 이 세상에서 가장 값진 것일지라도 무엇이든 거기에 무게를 두는 것이야말로 천하에 가장 큰 어리석음으로 여겨졌다. 영원이라는 말이 그 헤아릴 수 없는 온갖 의미를 더한 채 스스로를 드러냈고, 나는 그것에 대해 어찌나 광대한 관념을 품게 되었던지 그것을 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모를 지경이었다. 무엇보다, 온갖 즐거운 것들—내 말은, 우리가 전에 즐겁다고 여겼던 것들 말이다—이 얼마나 비루하고 얼마나 천박하고 얼마나 터무니없어 보였던가! 특히 이런 더러운 하찮은 것들이 바로 우리가 영원한 행복을 내던지고 맞바꾼 것들임을 곱씹어 보았을 때 더욱 그러했다.\n\n이런 성찰과 더불어, 당연한 수순으로, 내 지난 삶의 비참한 행실에 대한 내 마음의 준엄한 자책이 찾아왔으니, 내가 막 들어서려 하는 그 영원 속에서 누릴 어떠한 행복의 희망도 다 내던져 버렸으며, 도리어 비참한 모든 것, 일찍이 비참함으로 상상되어 온 모든 것을 받아 마땅하게 되었고, 게다가 이 모든 것이 또한 영원하다는 무서운 사실까지 더해진다는 것이었다.\n\n나는 누구에게 가르침의 강론을 펼칠 만한 사람이 못 되거니와, 다만 그때 내게 사물이 비쳐 보였던 그 모습 그대로를 내가 할 수 있는 한 이야기할 뿐이지만, 그것은 그때 내 영혼에 새겨졌던 생생한 인상에는 한없이 못 미치는 것이니, 실로 그 인상들은 말로 설명할 수 없는 것이거나, 설령 설명할 수 있다 해도 내가 그것을 표현할 만큼 말을 부릴 줄 아는 사람이 못 되는 것이다. 그것들에 대해 저마다 자기 처지가 일러 주는 대로 올바른 성찰을 하는 것은 분별 있는 모든 독자 각자의 몫일 터이며, 의심할 바 없이 이는 누구나 언젠가 한 번쯤은 그 무언가를 느끼게 될 일이니, 내 말은, 여기서 지녔던 것보다 더 또렷하게 다가올 일들을 들여다보고, 그 일들에 얽힌 자기 자신의 처지를 어둡게 내다보는 것 말이다.\n\n그러나 나는 내 이야기로 되돌아간다. 그 목사는 내가 적절하다고 여기는 한도 안에서, 삶 너머의 일들을 내다본 그 시야에 관하여 내가 나 자신을 어떤 상태에 있다고 느끼는지 말해 달라고 다그쳤다. 그는 자신이 사사로운 목적을 위해서나 다른 범죄자들을 더 적발하기 위해서 죄수들에게서 자백을 짜내는 것이 본분인 그곳의 전속 교목으로서 온 것이 아니라고 말했으며, 자기가 할 일은 내가 마음을 털어놓도록 자유롭게 이야기하게 이끌어, 내 마음의 짐을 덜어 주고, 자기가 할 수 있는 한 내게 위안을 베풀 수 있게 하는 것이라고 했다. 그러면서 내가 그에게 하는 어떤 말이든 그에게만 머무를 것이며, 오직 하느님과 나 자신만이 아는 것처럼 비밀로 지켜질 것이라고, 또 자기는 위에서 말한 대로 내게 알맞은 충고와 도움을 베풀고 나를 위해 하느님께 기도하기에 합당하게 하려는 것 말고는 나에 대해 아무것도 알고자 하지 않는다고 다짐해 주었다.\n\n나를 대하는 이 정직하고 다정한 태도가 내 온갖 격정의 수문을 모조리 열어젖혔다. 그는 그것으로 내 영혼 깊은 곳까지 파고들었고, 나는 내 삶의 온갖 사악함을 그에게 낱낱이 풀어놓았다. 한마디로, 나는 그에게 이 모든 이력을 간추려 들려주었고, 오십 년에 걸친 내 행실을 축소판으로 그려 보였다.\n\n나는 그에게 아무것도 숨기지 않았고, 그는 그 답례로 내게 진실한 참회를 권하며 자기가 참회로써 무엇을 뜻하는지 설명해 주었으며, 그런 다음 하늘로부터 가장 극악한 죄인들에게까지 선포된 무한한 자비의 도리를 어찌나 잘 펼쳐 보였던지, 절망이나 받아들여지지 못하리라는 의심 같은 빛을 띤 말은 내게 한마디도 남기지 않았다. 이런 상태로 그는 첫날 밤 나를 두고 떠났다.\n\n그는 이튿날 아침 다시 나를 찾아와, 하느님의 자비의 조건을 설명하는 자기 방식을 이어 갔는데, 그에 따르면 그것은 그것을 진실로 갈망하고 기꺼이 받아들이려는 것, 다만 나를 하느님의 응징에 그토록 마땅한 대상으로 만든 그 행한 일들에 대한 진실한 뉘우침과 미움 외에는 더 많지도, 더 어렵지도 않은 것이었다. 나는 이 비범한 사람의 훌륭한 강론을 되풀이해 옮길 능력이 없으니, 그가 내 마음을 되살려 내, 내가 평생 한 번도 알지 못했던 그런 상태로 나를 이끌었다고 말하는 것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부다. 나는 지난 일들에 대한 부끄러움과 눈물에 뒤덮여 있으면서도, 동시에 진정한 참회자가 되어 참회자의 위안—내 말은, 용서받으리라는 희망 말이다—을 얻게 되리라는 전망에 어떤 은밀하고도 놀라운 기쁨을 느꼈다. 또한 생각이 어찌나 빠르게 돌고, 그것들이 내게 남긴 인상이 어찌나 드높이 차올랐던지, 나는 한 점 불안도 없이 그 순간 당장이라도 형장에 나아가, 참회자로서 내 영혼을 무한한 자비의 품에 온전히 내던질 수 있으리라 여겨졌다.\n\n이 선량한 신사 또한 이런 것들이 내게 미치는 영향을 보고 나를 위하여 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 자기가 나를 찾아오게 된 것을 두고 하느님께 감사하며 마지막 순간까지 나를 떠나지 않기로—다시 말해 나를 찾아오기를 그만두지 않기로—결심했다.\n\n선고를 받고 나서 누군가의 처형이 명해지기까지는 자그마치 열이틀이나 걸렸고, 그러고는 어느 수요일에 그들이 이르는 대로 사형집행영장이 내려왔는데, 그 안에 내 이름이 들어 있는 것을 알게 되었다. 이것은 내 새로운 결심에 무서운 타격이었다. 실로 내 가슴은 무너져 내렸고, 나는 잇따라 두 번이나 까무러쳤으나 한마디도 하지 않았다. 선량한 목사는 나 때문에 몹시 괴로워하며, 전과 똑같은 논리와 똑같이 사람을 움직이는 언변으로 나를 위로하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했고, 밤새도록 나와 함께 갇혀 있으려 하지는 않았으므로—그것은 그가 내키지 않아 한 일이었다—그날 저녁 간수들이 그를 옥 안에 머물게 허락하는 한 오래도록 나를 떠나지 않았다.\n\n이튿날 하루 종일 그를 보지 못한 것이 나는 몹시 의아했으니, 그날이 바로 처형으로 정해진 날의 전날이었기 때문이다. 나는 크게 낙담하여 마음이 풀이 죽었고, 실로 그가 앞서 여러 차례 그토록 큰 효험으로 베풀어 주던 그 위안이 없어 거의 주저앉을 지경이었다. 나는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 정신의 압박 아래, 크나큰 초조함 속에서 네 시쯤까지 기다렸고, 그제야 그가 내 방으로 찾아왔으니, 나는 돈의 힘을 빌려—그곳에서는 돈 없이는 아무것도 되지 않았다—그들이 사형수 굴이라 부르는 곳에서 죽음을 앞둔 다른 죄수들 틈에 갇혀 있지 않고, 더럽고 작으나마 나 혼자 쓰는 방 하나를 가지는 호의를 얻어 두었던 것이다.\n\n그를 보기도 전에 문간에서 그의 목소리를 들었을 때, 내 가슴은 기쁨으로 펄쩍 뛰었다. 그러나 그가 오지 못한 데 대해 짧게 변명을 하고 나서, 자기가 그동안 나를 위해 시간을 써 왔다는 것을, 나의 그 특정한 사건에 대하여 형사법원 기록관으로부터 국무대신에게 가는 호의적인 보고를 얻어 냈다는 것을, 한마디로 나에게 형 집행 유예를 받아 왔다는 것을 보여 주었을 때 내 영혼에 어떤 떨림이 일었는지는 누구든 헤아려 보시기를.\n\n그는 감추었더라면 이중의 잔혹함이 되었을 일을 내게 알리면서도 할 수 있는 온갖 신중을 다 기울였다. 그런데도 그것은 내게 너무 벅찼으니, 전에는 슬픔이 나를 뒤엎었던 것처럼 이제는 기쁨이 나를 뒤엎어, 나는 처음보다 훨씬 더 위태로운 혼절에 빠졌고, 여간한 어려움 끝에야 겨우 정신을 차렸다.\n\n그 선량한 사람은 내게 더없이 그리스도교다운 권면을 하여, 형 집행 유예의 기쁨이 내 지난 슬픔의 기억을 마음에서 몰아내게 하지 말라고 일렀으며, 그 유예를 장부에 등재하고 주(州) 장관들에게 보여 주러 가야 하니 나를 떠나야 한다고 말하고는, 떠나기 직전에 일어서서 더없이 간곡한 태도로 나를 위해 하느님께 기도하여, 내 참회가 거짓 없고 진실하게 되기를, 그리고 이를테면 다시 삶으로 돌아오는 것이 내가 그토록 엄숙하게 버리고 참회하기로 결심한 삶의 어리석음으로 되돌아가는 것이 되지 않기를 빌었다. 나는 그 기원에 진심으로 동참했고, 그날 밤 내내 내 목숨을 살려 주신 하느님의 자비에 대해, 또 이번 일에서 내가 맛본 그 선하심을 자각함으로 인해 내 지난 죄에 대한 더 큰 혐오에 대해, 전에 겪은 그 모든 슬픔 속에서보다 더 깊은 인상을 마음에 새겼다고 말하지 않을 수 없다.\n\n이것은 그 자체로 모순되어 보일 수도 있고, 이 책의 본분에서 동떨어진 것으로 여겨질 수도 있다. 특히 나는, 내 이야기의 거칠고 사악한 부분을 들으며 즐거워하고 기분 전환을 할 만한 많은 이들이 실은 내 삶의 가장 좋은 부분이요, 나 자신에게 가장 이로우며 다른 이들에게 가장 교훈이 되는 이 부분은 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다는 것을 곱씹어 본다. 그러나 그런 이들도 내 이야기를 온전하게 마무리할 자유만은 부디 내게 허락해 주기를 바란다. 그들이 죄악만큼 참회를 달가워하지 않는다고, 또 이 이력이 충분히 그리될 법했던 대로 완전한 비극이 되었더라면 차라리 더 좋아했으리라고 말한다면, 그것은 그들에 대한 가혹한 풍자가 될 것이다.\n\n그러나 나는 내 이야기를 이어 간다. 이튿날 아침 옥 안에는 실로 처참한 광경이 펼쳐졌다. 아침에 나를 가장 먼저 맞이한 것은 그들이 이르는 대로 세인트세펄커 교회의 큰 종이 울리는 소리였으니, 그것이 그날의 시작을 알렸다. 종이 울리기 시작하자마자, 그날 처형될 가엾은 여섯 영혼이 누워 있는 사형수 굴에서 음산한 신음과 울부짖음이 들려왔는데, 더러는 이런 죄로, 더러는 저런 죄로, 그중 둘은 살인죄로 처형될 자들이었다.\n\n이에 뒤이어 옥사 안의 여러 부류의 죄수들 사이에서 어수선한 떠들썩함이 일어, 죽음을 앞둔 그 가엾은 자들에 대한 어설픈 슬픔을 저마다 드러냈으나, 그 방식은 서로 극도로 달랐다. 더러는 그들을 위해 울었고, 더러는 환호하며 잘 가라고 빌어 주었으며, 더러는 그들을 그 지경에 이르게 한 자들—다시 말해 증인이나 고발인을 두고 하는 말이다—을 저주하고 욕했으며, 많은 이가 그들을 가엾이 여겼고, 몇몇은, 그러나 아주 적은 몇몇은 그들을 위해 기도했다.\n\n이를테면 이 파멸의 아가리에서 나를 낚아채 주신 자비로운 섭리에 감사할 만큼의 마음의 평정을 차릴 여유조차 거의 없었다. 나는 이를테면 그것을 자각하는 데 압도되어 벙어리처럼 말없이 있었으며, 내 마음속에 있는 것을 표현할 수가 없었으니, 이런 경우의 격정이란 분명 어찌나 요동치는지 당장은 제 움직임을 다스릴 수 없는 법이기 때문이다.\n\n그러는 내내 그 가엾은 사형수들이 죽음을 준비하고 있었고, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 전속 교목이 그들에게 선고에 순순히 따르도록 권하며 분주히 오가고 있었으니—내 말은, 그러는 내내 나는 떨림의 발작에 사로잡혀, 마치 내가 똑같은 처지에 있기라도 한 것처럼, 분명 그 전날 내가 그러하리라 예상했던 그만큼이나 떨었다. 나는 이 놀라운 발작에 어찌나 격렬하게 흔들렸던지, 학질의 오한이 든 것처럼 몸을 떨어, 실성한 사람처럼이 아니고서는 말도 못 하고 쳐다보지도 못했다. 그들이 모두 수레에 실려 떠나가자—그것을 차마 지켜볼 용기는 내게 없었지만—내 말은, 그들이 떠나가자마자, 나는 나도 모르게 아무런 작정도 없이, 그저 하나의 발작인 양 울음의 발작에 빠져들었으나 어찌나 격렬한지, 또 어찌나 오래도록 나를 붙들었던지, 나는 어찌해야 할지를 알 수 없었고, 내가 지닌 온 힘과 용기를 다해도 그것을 멈추거나 억누를 수가 없었다. 이 울음의 발작은 두 시간 가까이 나를 붙들었으니, 내가 믿기로는 그들이 모두 이 세상을 떠날 때까지 나를 붙들었고, 그런 다음 더없이 겸허하고 참회하는 진지한 종류의 기쁨이 그 뒤를 이었다. 그것은 진정한 황홀경, 곧 기쁨과 감사의 격정이었으나, 여전히 말로는 그것을 터뜨릴 수가 없었고, 나는 그날 대부분을 이런 상태로 보냈다.\n\n저녁이 되어 그 선량한 목사가 다시 나를 찾아와, 늘 하던 그 선한 강론을 시작했다. 그는 나에게는 아직 참회를 위한 여유가 주어졌으나, 그 가엾은 여섯 사람의 처지는 이미 결정되어 이제 구원의 제의가 미치지 못하는 곳에 있게 되었음을 두고 다행이라고 했다. 그는 내가 영원을 내다보았을 때 삶의 일들에 대해 품었던 그 똑같은 마음가짐을 간직하라고 간곡히 권했으며, 그 모든 말 끝에는 내가 모든 것이 끝났다고 단정해서는 안 된다고, 형 집행 유예가 사면은 아니라고, 자기가 아직 그 결과를 장담할 수는 없다고 일렀다. 그러나 나는 더 많은 시간이 주어진 이 자비를 입었으니, 그 시간을 잘 쓰는 것이 내 할 일이라고 했다.\n\n이 강론은 매우 시의적절하기는 했으나, 마치 그 일이 여전히 비극적인 결말을 맞이할 수도 있을 것처럼 내 마음에 일종의 슬픔을 남겼는데, 다만 그것에 대해 그는 아무런 확신이 없었고, 나 역시 그때는 정말로 그것에 대해 그에게 캐묻지 않았으니, 그가 좋은 결말로 이끌기 위해 최선을 다하겠다고, 또 그러기를 바란다고는 했으나 내가 안심하기를 바라지는 않는다고 말했기 때문이며, 그 결과는 그가 한 말에 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었음을 증명해 주었다.\n\n그러고 나서 약 두 주 뒤에, 나는 다가오는 개정기(開廷期)의 다음 사형집행영장에 내가 포함되리라는 어느 정도 근거 있는 염려를 품게 되었고, 여간한 어려움이 아니고서는, 그리고 마침내 유배형을 청하는 겸허한 탄원이 아니고서는 그것을 면할 수 없었으니, 나는 명성에 그토록 못된 빚을 졌고, 상습범이라는 그 치명적인 소문이 그토록 위세를 떨쳤던 것이다. 다만 그 점에서 그들이 내게 엄정한 정의를 베푼 것은 아니었으니, 재판관의 눈에 내가 무엇으로 비쳤든 간에 법의 의미로 보자면 나는 상습범이 아니었고, 전에 사법적인 방식으로 그들 앞에 선 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다. 그러므로 재판관들이 나를 상습범이라고 몰아붙일 수는 없었으나, 형사법원 기록관이 제멋대로 내 사건을 그렇게 진술했던 것이다.\n\n이제 나는 실로 목숨을 부지하게 되었으나, 유배형에 처해진다는 가혹한 조건이 따랐으니, 그것은 그 자체로 보면 실로 가혹한 조건이었으나 견주어 헤아려 보면 그렇지 않았다. 그러므로 나는 그 선고에 대해서도, 내게 주어진 선택에 대해서도 아무 논평을 하지 않으련다. 우리는 누구나 죽음보다는 무엇이든 가리지 않고 택하게 마련이니, 더구나 그 너머에 음울한 전망이 따르는 죽음이라면 더욱 그러한데, 그것이 바로 내 경우였다.\n\n내게는 낯선 사람이면서도 그 영향력으로 형 집행 유예를 얻어 준 그 선량한 목사는 이 대목을 두고 진심으로 슬퍼했다. 그는 내가 좋은 가르침의 영향 아래 생을 마치게 되기를 바랐다고, 이렇게 외국으로 보내지는 자들이 으레 그러한 그 비참한 무리 가운데로 다시 풀려나지 않기를 바랐다고 했으며, 거기서는 하느님의 은총으로부터 예사롭지 않은 은밀한 도움을 받지 않는 한, 내가 다시 전처럼 사악해지고 말 것이라고 했다.\n\n내가 한동안 내 후견인에 대해 언급하지 않았는데, 그녀는 이 부분의 거의 내내, 아니 어쩌면 줄곧 위중하게 앓아, 내가 선고로 인해 죽음에 다가선 만큼이나 자기 병으로 인해 죽음에 가까이 다가서 있었던 터라, 크나큰 참회자가 되어 있었다. 내 말은, 나는 그녀에 대해 언급하지 않았고, 실로 이 모든 동안 그녀를 보지도 못했다는 것이다. 그러나 이제 회복되어 겨우 바깥출입을 할 수 있게 되자 그녀가 나를 보러 왔다.\n\n나는 그녀에게 내 처지를, 그리고 내가 눈물과 희망의 어떤 밀물과 썰물에 시달려 왔는지를 이야기했고, 무엇을 면했으며 어떤 조건으로 그러했는지를 들려주었다. 목사가 내가 으레 유배되는 그 비참한 무리에 휩쓸려 다시 사악함으로 빠져들까 염려하는 말을 했을 때 그녀가 그 자리에 함께 있었다. 실로 나도 내 마음속으로 그것을 두고 우울하게 곱씹었으니, 늘 얼마나 끔찍한 패거리가 한꺼번에 보내지는지를 알고 있었기 때문이며, 나는 후견인에게 그 선량한 목사의 염려가 까닭 없는 것이 아니라고 말했다. \"아니에요, 아니에요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 그런 끔찍한 본보기 따위에 당신이 넘어가지는 않으리라 믿어요.\" 그리고 목사가 가자마자, 그녀는 내가 낙심하지 말기를 바란다고, 어쩌면 나를 특별한 방식으로, 나 혼자 따로 처분할 길과 방도가 찾아질지도 모른다고, 그것에 대해서는 나중에 더 이야기하겠다고 했다.\n\n나는 그녀를 진지하게 바라보았고, 그녀가 여느 때보다 더 명랑해 보인다고 생각했으며, 곧장 풀려나리라는 천 가지 생각을 품었으나, 도무지 그 방법을 그려 볼 수도, 조금이라도 실현 가능한 길 하나를 떠올릴 수도 없었다. 그러나 나는 그 일에 너무도 마음이 쏠려 있던 터라 그녀가 자기 뜻을 밝히지 않은 채 내게서 떠나가게 둘 수가 없었으니, 그녀는 몹시 내키지 않아 했으나 내 간청이 이겨, 내가 여전히 다그치는 동안 그녀는 몇 마디 말로 이렇게 대답했다. \"아니, 당신, 돈이 있잖아요, 안 그래요? 살면서 유배되는 사람이 주머니에 백 파운드를 지니고 있는 걸 본 적이 있어요, 장담컨대, 응?\" 그녀가 말했다.\n\n나는 곧 그녀의 뜻을 알아차렸으나, 그 일은 모두 그녀에게 맡기겠다고, 다만 명령이 엄정하게 집행되는 것 외에는 무엇을 바랄 여지도 보이지 않는다고, 또 그것이 자비로 여겨지는 가혹한 처분인 만큼 분명 엄정하게 지켜지리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다고 말했다. 그녀는 이 말밖에는 더 하지 않았다. \"할 수 있는 일을 한번 해 봅시다.\" 그렇게 우리는 그날 밤 헤어졌다.\n\n나는 이 유배형 명령에 서명이 떨어진 뒤로 거의 열다섯 주를 더 옥에 있었다. 그 까닭이 무엇이었는지는 모르겠으나, 이 기간이 끝날 무렵 나는 템스강에 떠 있는 한 배에 태워졌고, 나와 함께 내 시절 뉴게이트가 배출한 가장 모질고 비루한 무리 열셋이 함께 실렸다. 그 열셋이 다다른 뻔뻔함과 대담무쌍한 악행의 정도와, 항해 중에 그들이 보인 행실의 양상을 묘사하자면 실로 내 이야기보다 더 긴 이력이 필요할 것인데, 나는 그것에 대한 매우 흥미로운 기록을 지니고 있으니, 그들을 실어 나른 그 배의 선장이 내게 그 요점을 일러 주고, 자기 항해사를 시켜 그것을 소상히 받아 적게 한 것이다.\n\n내 처지가 이렇게 바뀌어 가는 이 막간에 내게 일어난 온갖 자질구레한 사건들을 여기서 죄다 늘어놓는 것은 어쩌면 부질없는 일로 여겨질지도 모른다. 내 말은, 내 유배에 대한 최종 명령과 내가 배에 오른 때 사이의 일들 말이다. 게다가 나는 내 이야기의 끝에 너무 가까이 와 있어 그럴 여유가 없다. 그러나 나와 내 랭커셔 남편에 관련된 무언가만은 빠뜨릴 수 없다.\n\n그는, 내가 이미 말했듯이, 일반 감옥의 미결수 쪽에서 그의 동료 셋과 함께 프레스야드로 옮겨졌는데, 얼마 뒤 거기에 한 사람이 더 보태졌다. 무슨 까닭에서인지는 모르겠으나, 그들은 거의 석 달 동안 재판에 회부되지도 않은 채 구금되어 있었다. 보아하니 그들은 자기들에게 불리하게 나설 것으로 예상되던 자들 가운데 몇몇을 매수하거나 돈으로 입막음할 방도를 찾아낸 듯했고, 그래서 한동안 그들을 유죄로 만들 증거가 모자랐던 것이다. 이 때문에 처음에는 한참을 헤맨 끝에, 그들 중 둘에 대해서는 처형할 만한 증거를 그럭저럭 마련해 냈으나, 나머지 둘은—내 랭커셔 남편이 그중 하나였다—여전히 미결 상태로 남아 있었다. 내 생각에 그들 각각에 대해 확실한 증인이 하나씩은 있었으나, 법이 증인 둘을 두도록 엄격히 규정하고 있어 아무것도 어찌하지 못했다. 그러나 보아하니 그들은 그 사람들을 놓아줄 마음도 없어, 끝내는 추가 증거가 들어오리라 의심치 않았던 것이며, 이를 위해 내 생각에 그러한 죄수들이 잡혀 있으니 그들에게 강도를 당한 자는 누구든 옥으로 와서 그들을 볼 수 있다는 공고가 났던 것이다.\n\n나는 이 기회를 틈타 내 호기심을 채우기로 하여, 던스터블 마차에서 강도를 당했으니 그 두 노상강도를 보러 가겠다고 꾸며 댔다. 그러나 프레스야드로 들어갈 때 나는 어찌나 변장을 하고 얼굴을 꽁꽁 싸맸던지, 그가 나를 거의 볼 수 없었고, 따라서 내가 누구인지 전혀 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 돌아와서는, 나는 그들을 아주 잘 안다고 공공연히 말했다.\n\n이내 옥 전체에 몰 플랜더스가 그 노상강도 가운데 하나에게 불리한 증언을 할 것이며, 그로써 유배형 선고를 면하게 되리라는 소문이 퍼졌다.\n\n그들이 그 소문을 들었고, 곧장 내 남편이 자기를 그토록 잘 알고 자기에게 불리한 증인이 되려 한다는 그 플랜더스 부인을 보고 싶다고 청했으며, 그리하여 나는 그에게 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다. 나는 거기서 내가 입고 나선 적 있는 가장 좋은 옷이 허락하는 한 잘 차려입고 프레스야드로 갔으나, 한동안은 얼굴에 두건을 두르고 있었다. 그는 처음에는 내게 별말을 하지 않다가, 자기를 아느냐고 물었다. 나는 그렇다, 아주 잘 안다고 말했으나, 얼굴을 가린 것처럼 목소리도 꾸며 내, 그는 내가 누구인지 조금도 짐작하지 못했다. 그는 어디서 자기를 보았느냐고 물었다. 나는 던스터블과 브릭힐 사이에서라고 말했고, 곁에 서 있던 간수에게 돌아서서, 그와 단둘이 이야기하도록 해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 그러라고, 그러라고, 원하는 만큼 그러라고 하고는 아주 정중하게 물러갔다.\n\n그가 가자마자 나는 문을 닫고 두건을 벗어 던지고는, 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"여보, 나를 모르겠어요?\" 그는 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 말없이 서 있다가, 그 놀라움을 도무지 가라앉히지 못한 채 이 말밖에는 더 하지 못했다. \"좀 앉읍시다.\" 그러고는 탁자 옆에 앉아 탁자 위에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 손에 머리를 기댄 채 멍한 사람처럼 두 눈을 바닥에 박았다. 한편 나는 어찌나 격렬하게 울었던지 한참이 지나서야 다시 말을 꺼낼 수 있었다. 그러나 눈물로 격정을 어느 정도 쏟아 낸 뒤에 나는 똑같은 말을 되풀이했다. \"여보, 나를 모르겠어요?\" 그러자 그는 그렇다고 대답하고는 한참 동안 더 말이 없었다.\n\n앞서 말한 그 놀라움 속에 얼마간 잠겨 있다가, 그는 나를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"어떻게 그리도 잔인할 수 있소?\" 나는 그가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 얼른 알아듣지 못해 이렇게 대답했다. \"어떻게 나를 잔인하다고 하시는 거예요? 내가 당신에게 무슨 잔인한 짓을 했다고요?\" \"이런 곳으로 나를 찾아오는 게,\" 그가 말했다. \"나를 모욕하는 게 아니고 무엇이오? 나는 당신에게서 빼앗은 게 없소, 적어도 노상에서는 말이오.\"\n\n나는 이로써 그가 내가 처한 비참한 사정을 전혀 모르고 있으며, 자기가 거기 있다는 어떤 소식을 듣고서 내가 그를 버리고 떠난 일을 두고 그를 책망하러 온 줄로 여기고 있음을 알아차렸다. 그러나 나는 그에게 할 말이 너무 많았던 터라 화를 낼 수도 없어, 몇 마디로 그에게 말하기를, 나는 결코 그를 모욕하러 온 것이 아니라, 잘해야 서로의 처지를 함께 슬퍼하러 온 것이며, 내 처지가 그의 처지보다, 그것도 여러모로 더 나쁘다고 말해 주면 내게 그런 속셈이 없다는 것을 쉬이 납득하리라고 했다. 그는 내 처지가 자기보다 더 나쁘다는 그 막연한 표현에 다소 걱정스러운 빛을 띠었으나, 다정한 미소를 지으며 다소 얼떨떨한 얼굴로 말했다. \"그게 어찌 그럴 수 있소? 내가 족쇄를 차고 뉴게이트에 갇혀 있고, 내 동료 둘은 이미 처형되었는데, 당신 처지가 어떻게 내 처지보다 더 나쁠 수 있단 말이오?\"\n\n\"이봐요, 여보.\" 내가 말했다. \"내가 내 불운한 이력을 들려주거나 당신이 그걸 듣자면 한참이나 긴 이야기가 될 거예요. 하지만 당신이 들을 마음이 있다면, 내 처지가 당신보다 더 나쁘다는 데 곧 나와 같은 결론에 이르게 될 거예요.\" \"그게 어떻게 가능하단 말이오,\" 그가 다시 말했다. \"나는 바로 다음 개정기에 사형을 선고받을 처지인데?\" \"그래요,\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 아주 가능한 일이에요, 내가 세 번의 개정기 전에 이미 사형을 선고받았고 지금 사형 선고 아래 있다고 당신에게 말하면 말이에요. 내 처지가 당신보다 더 나쁘지 않은가요?\"\n\n그제야 그는 실로 다시 벙어리가 된 사람처럼 말없이 서 있다가, 잠시 뒤 벌떡 일어섰다. \"불행한 부부로구려!\" 그가 말했다. \"어떻게 이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말이오?\" 나는 그의 손을 잡았다. \"이봐요, 여보.\" 내가 말했다. \"앉아서 우리의 슬픔을 견주어 봅시다. 나는 바로 이 옥의 죄수이고, 당신보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 처지에 있어요. 내가 그 자초지종을 말해 주면, 당신은 내가 당신을 모욕하러 온 게 아니라는 걸 납득하게 될 거예요.\" 그리고 이렇게 우리는 함께 앉았고, 나는 내 이야기 가운데 적절하다 여겨지는 만큼을 그에게 들려주어, 마침내 내가 극심한 가난에 내몰린 데까지 이르게 했고, 내가 어떤 무리에 휩쓸려 들어가 도무지 익숙지 않은 방식으로 내 곤궁을 면하려 했다고, 그들이 어떤 상인의 집을 털려 했을 때 나는 막 그 문 앞에 있었다는 이유로—하녀가 나를 안으로 끌어들였던 것이다—붙들렸다고, 나는 자물쇠 하나 부수지 않고 아무것도 가져가지 않았는데도 유죄로 끌려가 사형을 선고받았으나, 재판관들이 내 처지의 가혹함을 알게 되어 내가 유배되는 데 동의하는 조건으로 그 선고를 감면받을 허가를 얻어 냈다고 나 자신을 그려 보였다.\n\n나는 그에게, 내가 옥에서 몰 플랜더스라는 자로 오인된 탓에 더 나쁜 처지에 놓였다고 말했으니, 그자는 그들 모두가 소문은 들었으되 누구도 본 적은 없는, 이름난 데다 성공한 도둑이었으나, 당신도 잘 알다시피 그건 내 이름이 아니었던 것이다. 그러나 나는 그 모든 것을 내 불운의 탓으로 돌렸으니, 이 이름 아래 나는 상습범으로 다루어졌으되 실은 이것이 그들이 나에 대해 알게 된 첫 번째 일이었다. 나는 그를 본 뒤로 내게 닥쳤던 일들을 길게 낱낱이 들려주었는데, 다만 그 뒤로 그를 본 적이 있었다면 그가 내가 그랬으리라 여길 수도 있겠다고 말한 다음, 내가 브릭힐에서 그를 본 일을 들려주었으니, 그가 얼마나 맹렬히 쫓기고 있었는지, 그리고 내가 그를 알며 그가 아무개 씨라는 매우 정직한 신사라고 진술함으로써 어떻게 추격이 멈추고 치안 책임자가 발길을 돌렸는지를 들려주었다.\n\n그는 내 이야기를 죄다 더없이 귀 기울여 들으며 그 자질구레한 대목 대부분에 미소를 지었으니, 그것들이 모두 사소한 일들이요, 그가 우두머리 노릇을 하던 일들에 견주면 한없이 하찮은 것들이었기 때문이다. 그러나 내가 브릭힐 이야기에 이르자 그는 놀라워했다. \"아니, 그게 당신이었소, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"거기 브릭힐에서 우리 뒤를 바짝 쫓던 그 무리를 막아 준 게?\" \"그래요,\" 내가 말했다. \"그게 정말 나였어요.\" 그러고 나서 나는 거기서 그를 두고 눈여겨본 자초지종을 들려주었다. \"그렇다면,\" 그가 말했다. \"그때 내 목숨을 구해 준 게 당신이었구려, 그리고 내가 당신에게 목숨을 빚진 것이 기쁘오, 이제 내가 당신에게 그 빚을 갚을 테니 말이오. 당신을 지금 처한 이 처지에서 구해 내든지, 아니면 그러다 죽든지 하겠소.\"\n\n나는 그에게 결코 그러지 말라고, 그것은 너무 큰 위험이라 그가 무릅쓸 만한 것이 못 되며, 구할 가치도 없는 목숨을 위해 그럴 일이 아니라고 말했다. 그런 건 아무래도 좋다고, 그것은 그에게 온 세상보다 값진 목숨이라고, 자기에게 새 삶을 준 목숨이라고 그는 말했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 잡히던 그 마지막 순간 말고는, 그때 그 한 번 말고는 실제로 잡힐 위험에 처한 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이오.\" 실로 그는 그때 자기 위험은 그쪽 길로 추격당하지 않았으리라 믿었던 데 있었다고 말했으니, 그들이 호키에서 전혀 다른 길로 빠져나가, 큰길이 아니라 울타리 친 들판을 가로질러 브릭힐로 들어왔던 터라, 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않았으리라 확신했던 것이다.\n\n여기서 그는 내게 자기 삶의 긴 내력을 들려주었는데, 실로 그것은 매우 기이한 이력이 될 만했고 한없이 흥미로울 것이었다. 그는 나와 결혼하기 약 열두 해 전부터 노상강도 일에 들어섰다고, 그를 오라비라 부르던 그 여자는 사실 그의 누이도, 그와 아무런 혈연도 아니라 그들 패거리에 속한 자였다고, 그와 연락을 주고받으며 늘 시내에 살았고 아는 사람이 두루 많았다고 말했다. 그녀가 그들에게 시내를 떠나는 사람들에 대한 정확한 정보를 주어, 그녀의 연통 덕분에 그들이 여러 차례 짭짤한 노획물을 거두었다고, 그녀는 나를 그에게 데려오면서 그를 위해 한밑천 잡아 주었다고 여겼으나 그만 어긋나고 말았는데, 그것을 그가 정말로 그녀 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다. 만약 그녀가 들은 대로 내가 재산을 지니고 있었더라면 그것이 그의 행운이 되어, 그는 노상 일을 그만두고 은둔하여 점잖게 살기로 마음먹었으되, 일반사면령이 내리거나 돈으로 자기 이름을 어떤 특별사면에 올려 넣어 완전히 마음 편해질 수 있을 때까지는 결코 공공연히 모습을 드러내지 않으려 했다고, 그러나 사정이 달리 풀린 터라 마차며 거마(車馬)를 처분하고 다시 옛 직업으로 돌아설 수밖에 없었다고 했다.\n\n그는 자기 모험담 가운데 몇 가지를 길게 들려주었는데, 특히 한번은 리치필드 근처에서 웨스트체스터 마차들을 털어 매우 큰 노획물을 거둔 일과, 그 뒤에 윌트셔의 버퍼드 장으로 양을 사러 가던 서부의 목축업자 다섯을 턴 일이 있었다. 그는 이 두 차례에 어찌나 많은 돈을 거두었던지, 만약 나를 어디서 찾을 수 있을지만 알았더라면, 나와 함께 버지니아로 가거나 아메리카에 있는 다른 어느 영국 식민지의 농장에 자리 잡자던 내 제안을 분명 기꺼이 받아들였으리라고 말했다.\n\n그는 내가 일러 준 주소대로 부쳐 내게 두세 통의 편지를 썼으나 나로부터 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다고 말했다. 이것은 실로 사실임을 내가 알고 있었으나, 그 편지들이 내 나중 남편과 살던 때에 내 손에 들어온 터라 나로서는 어찌할 도리가 없어, 차라리 아무 답도 하지 않기로 했던 것이니, 그래야 그가 편지들이 전해지지 못한 것이려니 여기게 될 것이었기 때문이다.\n\n이렇게 어긋나 버린 터라, 그는 그 뒤로 줄곧 옛 일을 이어 왔다고 말했으나, 그만큼 많은 돈을 거둔 뒤로는 전처럼 그토록 무모한 위험을 무릅쓰지는 않았다고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 돈을 너무 모질게 내놓지 않으려는 신사들과 노상에서 벌인 여러 차례의 거칠고 필사적인 격투에 대해 어느 정도 들려주었고, 자기가 입은 상처 몇 군데를 보여 주었다. 그에게는 실로 매우 끔찍한 상처가 한두 군데 있었으니, 특히 권총 탄환에 팔이 부러진 상처와, 칼에 몸을 완전히 꿰뚫린 상처가 있었으나, 그것이 급소를 비껴간 덕에 그는 다시 나았다. 그의 동료 하나가 어찌나 충실하고 다정하게 그의 곁을 지켰던지, 그가 팔을 접합하기 전에 팔십 마일 가까이 말을 타고 가도록 도왔으며, 그런 다음 그 일이 벌어진 곳에서 멀리 떨어진 상당한 규모의 도시에서 외과의를 불러, 자기들은 칼라일 쪽으로 여행하는 신사들인데 노상에서 노상강도들에게 습격당해, 그중 하나가 자기 팔을 쏘아 뼈를 부러뜨렸다고 둘러댔다는 것이다.\n\n이것을 그의 벗이 어찌나 능란하게 처리했던지, 그들은 전혀 의심받지 않은 채, 그가 완전히 나을 때까지 가만히 숨어 지냈다고 그는 말했다. 그가 내게 자기 모험담을 어찌나 많은 개별 이야기로 들려주었던지, 나는 그것들을 옮기기를 단념하는 것이 몹시 아쉽다. 그러나 나는 이것이 그의 이야기가 아니라 내 이야기임을 헤아린다.\n\n그러고 나서 나는 그때 그가 처한 사건의 정황과, 재판에 회부될 때 그가 무엇을 예상하는지를 캐물었다. 그는 자기에게 불리한 증거가 전혀 없거나 있어도 아주 미미하다고 말했으니, 그들 모두가 함께 기소된 세 건의 강도 가운데, 자기는 단 한 건에만 가담했던 것이 다행이었고, 게다가 그 사건에 대해 댈 수 있는 증인이 단 하나뿐이라 그것으로는 부족했기 때문이다. 다만 다른 몇몇이 자기에게 불리하게 나서리라 예상된다고, 처음 나를 보았을 때는 실로 내가 그런 볼일로 온 자인 줄 여겼다고, 그러나 누군가 자기에게 불리하게 나선다 해도 자기는 무죄로 풀려나기를 바란다고 했다. 만약 자청하여 스스로 유배를 떠나는 데 응하면 재판 없이 그것이 허락될 수도 있다는 언질을 받았으나, 도무지 마음 편히 그것을 생각할 수 없었고, 차라리 교수형을 당하는 편이 훨씬 받아들이기 쉬울 것 같다고 했다.\n\n나는 그 점을 두고 그를 나무랐고, 두 가지 까닭에서 그를 나무란다고 말했다. 첫째는, 그가 유배된다면 신사이자 대담하고 진취적인 사람인 그에게는 다시 돌아올 길이 백 가지는 있을 것이며, 어쩌면 떠나기 전에 돌아올 길과 방도가 생길지도 모른다는 것이었다. 그는 그 대목에 미소 지으며, 둘 중에서는 후자가 가장 마음에 든다고 말했으니, 마치 로마인들이 사형을 선고받은 노예들을 광산에서 일하라고 보냈듯이 자기가 농장으로 보내진다는 데 일종의 공포가 마음에 서려 있었기 때문이며, 다른 세상으로 건너가는 길은, 그것이 무엇이든 간에, 교수대에서가 훨씬 더 견딜 만하다고 여겼으며, 이것이야말로 형편의 절박함에 떠밀려 노상에 나선 모든 신사들의 일반적인 생각이라고 했다. 처형장에서는 적어도 현세의 온갖 비참함에 끝이 있고, 그 뒤에 따라올 것으로 말하자면, 자기 생각에 사람이란 옥과 사형수 굴의 압박과 고뇌 아래 삶의 마지막 두 주 동안에도, 아메리카의 숲과 황야에서 평생 그러할 만큼이나 진실하게 참회할 수 있다는 것이며, 종살이와 고된 노역은 신사로서는 결코 몸을 굽힐 수 없는 일이요, 그것은 결국 그들을 나중에 스스로 제 목숨을 끊는 자로 내모는 길에 지나지 않으니 그것이 훨씬 더 나쁘다고, 그러므로 자기는 유배된다는 것을 생각만 해도 도무지 참을 수가 없다고 했다.\n\n나는 그를 설득하려고 온갖 노력을 다 기울였고, 거기에 저 알려진 여인의 수사학—내 말은, 눈물 말이다—을 보탰다. 나는 그에게, 공개 처형의 오명이야말로 신사의 심령에 외국에서 마주칠 수 있는 그 어떤 굴욕보다도 분명 더 큰 압박이 되리라고, 후자에는 적어도 목숨을 부지할 가망이 있으나 여기서는 그런 것이 전혀 없다고, 대체로 사람 좋고 어느 정도 호기로운 부류인 배의 선장을 구슬리는 일은 그에게 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이며, 약간의 처신, 특히 손에 쥘 돈이 좀 있다면 그것으로 그가 버지니아에 이르렀을 때 돈을 치르고 풀려날 길이 열릴 것이라고 말했다.\n\n그는 나를 애틋하게 바라보았고, 나는 그의 뜻을 짐작했다고 여겼으니, 다시 말해 그에게 돈이 없다는 뜻인 줄 알았다. 그러나 나는 잘못 짚었으니, 그의 뜻은 다른 데 있었다. \"여보, 당신이 방금 넌지시 말했잖소,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 떠나기 전에 돌아올 길이 있을지도 모른다고 말이오. 그 말을 나는 여기서 돈을 치르고 그것을 면할 수도 있다는 뜻으로 알아들었소. 나는 거기 이르러 풀려나는 데 백 파운드를 치르느니, 차라리 떠나는 것을 막는 데 이백 파운드를 치르겠소.\" \"그건, 여보,\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 그곳을 나만큼 잘 알지 못하기 때문이에요.\" \"그럴지도 모르지,\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도 나는, 당신이 그곳을 아무리 잘 안다 해도, 당신 역시 똑같이 할 거라고 믿소. 당신이 내게 말했듯이 거기 어머니가 계시다는 것 때문이 아니라면 말이오.\"\n\n나는 그에게, 내 어머니로 말하자면, 그분이 여러 해 전에 이미 돌아가셨을 것이 거의 틀림없다고 말했고, 거기 있을지도 모를 다른 친척들로 말하자면 이제 나는 그들을 알지 못한다고, 내가 겪은 불운들이 나를 여러 해 동안 처해 있던 그 처지로 몰아넣은 뒤로 나는 그들과 아무런 연락도 이어 오지 않았다고, 또 내가 유배된 중범죄자의 처지로 처음 찾아가야 한다면 그들에게서 냉랭한 대접밖에 받지 못하리라는 것을 그가 쉬이 믿으리라고 말했다. 그러므로 내가 만약 그리로 간다 해도 그들을 보지 않기로 마음먹었다고, 다만 만약 그것이 내 운명이 된다면 거기 가는 데에는 그 일의 괴로운 부분을 죄다 덜어 줄 여러 가지 속셈이 있다고, 그리고 그 역시 가야 할 처지에 놓인다면, 내가 그에게 처신하는 법을 쉬이 일러 주어, 결코 종으로는 가지 않게 하겠다고, 특히 내가 보기에 그에게는 돈이 없지 않으니 더욱 그러하다고, 그런 처지에서 돈이야말로 유일한 벗이라고 말했다.\n\n그는 미소 지으며, 자기에게 돈이 있다고는 말하지 않았다고 했다. 나는 그의 말을 얼른 가로막으며, 내가 그렇게 말한 것을 두고, 그에게 돈이 있다 한들 내가 무슨 도움을 바라리라고 여기지는 말기를 바란다고 말했다. 도리어, 내게 돈이 많지는 않으나 그래도 부족하지는 않으며, 내게 무엇이 있는 한 그것을 두고 그를 축내기보다는 차라리 그에게 보태 주겠다고, 그가 무엇을 지녔든 유배의 경우에는 그것이 죄다 필요할 것임을 내가 알기 때문이라고 했다.\n\n그는 그 점에 대해 더없이 다정한 태도로 자기 마음을 드러냈다. 그는 자기가 지닌 돈이 많지는 않으나, 만약 내가 필요로 한다면 그중 어느 것도 결코 내게 감추지 않겠다고, 또 자기는 결코 그런 우려에서 말한 것이 아니라고 다짐했다. 자기는 다만 떠나기 전에 내가 넌지시 비친 그것에만 마음을 쏟고 있다고, 여기서는 자기 한 몸 어찌해야 할지 알지만, 거기서는 살아 있는 가장 무지하고 무력한 가련한 자가 되리라고 했다.\n\n나는 그에게, 아무런 두려울 것도 없는 일을 두고 그가 스스로 겁먹고 떨고 있다고 말했다. 그에게 돈이 있다면—그렇다는 것을 들으니 기쁘거니와—유배의 결과로 여겨지는 그 종살이를 피할 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 새로운 토대 위에서 세상을 다시 시작할 수 있으며, 그런 경우에 으레 들이는 평범한 노력만으로도 실패할 리 없는 그런 시작을 할 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 그것은 내가 여러 해 전에 그에게 권했던 것이요, 우리 둘이 함께 먹고살며 세상에서 우리의 형편을 다시 일으키자고 제안했던 것임을 그가 떠올리지 않을 수 없으리라고 했다. 그리고 이제 그에게 말하건대, 그것의 확실함과 내가 그 방법을 속속들이 알고 있다는 것, 또 내가 성공의 가망을 온전히 확신하고 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기 위하여, 먼저 내가 아예 건너갈 필요에서 나 자신을 벗어나게 하는 것을 그가 보게 될 것이며, 그런 다음 내가 기꺼이, 내 스스로의 선택으로 그와 함께 가겠다고, 그리고 어쩌면 내가 그의 도움 없이는 살아갈 수 없어서 그것을 제안하는 것이 아님을 그가 납득할 만큼 충분한 것을 지니고 가겠다고 했다. 다만 나는 우리가 함께 겪은 불운이 우리 둘로 하여금 이 세상의 이 부분을 떠나, 아무도 우리의 지난 일을 두고 우리를 책망할 수 없고, 우리도 옥을 두려워할 일이 없으며, 사형수 굴의 고뇌에 떠밀릴 일도 없는 곳에서 살아가는 데 우리를 화해시키기에 충분했다고 여긴다고 했다. 그곳에서 우리는 우리의 지난 온갖 재난을 한없는 만족 속에서 되돌아보게 될 것이니, 우리의 원수들이 우리를 까맣게 잊으리라는 것을, 그리고 우리가 새로운 세상에서 새사람으로 살아가, 아무도 우리에게 할 말이 없고 우리도 그들에게 할 말이 없으리라는 것을 헤아릴 때 그러하리라고 했다.\n\n나는 어찌나 많은 논거로 이것을 그에게 절실히 다그쳤던지, 또 그 자신의 온갖 격정 어린 반박에 어찌나 효과적으로 답했던지, 그는 나를 끌어안으며, 내가 자기를 그토록 진실하고 애정 어린 마음으로 대해 자기를 굴복시켰다고 말했다. 자기는 내 충고를 받아들이겠다고, 내 도움의 위안을, 그리고 자기 불행 속에서 그토록 충실한 조언자요 그런 동반자를 얻으리라는 희망을 품고 자기 운명에 순순히 따르고자 애쓰겠다고 했다. 그러나 그는 여전히, 내가 앞서 언급한 것, 곧 떠나기 전에 빠져나갈 어떤 길이 있을지도 모른다는 것, 아예 가지 않는 것이 가능할지도 모른다는 것을 내게 일깨웠고, 그렇게 되면 훨씬 더 나을 것이라고 했다. 나는 그에게, 그 부분에서도 내가 최선을 다하는 것을 보고 온전히 납득하게 될 것이며, 만약 그것이 성공하지 못한다 해도 나머지만은 반드시 이루어 내겠다고 말했다.\n\n이 긴 대화 끝에 우리는 던스터블에서 헤어질 때의 그것과 맞먹는, 아니 어쩌면 그보다 더한 다정함과 애정의 표시를 나누며 헤어졌다. 그리고 이제 나는 전보다 더 분명하게, 그가 그때 런던을 향해 나와 함께 던스터블보다 더 멀리 가기를 마다한 까닭을, 또 거기서 헤어질 때 그가 어찌하여 내게 런던으로 가는 길 일부를 바래다주는 것이 자기에게 편치 않다고—그렇지 않았다면 그리해 주었을 것을—말했는지를 알게 되었다. 나는 그의 삶의 내력이 내 것보다 훨씬 더 재미있는 이력이 되었으리라고 말한 바 있거니와, 실로 그 가운데서도 이 부분, 곧 그가 그 무모한 일을 꼬박 스물다섯 해 동안이나 이어 오면서 한 번도 잡힌 적이 없었다는 것보다 더 기이한 것은 없었으니, 그가 거둔 성공이 어찌나 보기 드문 것이었던지, 때로는 한 번에 한두 해씩 어느 곳에 은거하여 풍족하게 지내며, 자기 시중을 들 하인 하나를 두고 자기를 건사했으며, 종종 커피하우스에 앉아, 자기가 턴 바로 그 사람들이 강도를 당한 일과 그 장소와 정황을 늘어놓는 것을 들으면서 그것이 같은 사건임을 쉬이 떠올리곤 했던 것이다.\n\n이런 식으로, 보아하니 그는 한밑천을 노리고 불운하게 나와 결혼했을 무렵 리버풀 근처에 살고 있었다. 만약 내가 그가 기대했던 그 한밑천이었더라면, 그가 말했듯이, 나는 그가 정말로 손을 씻고 평생 정직하게 살았으리라 믿는다.\n\n그는 그 온갖 불운 가운데서도, 자기가 갇히게 된 그 강도 사건이 벌어졌을 때 실제로 그 자리에 있지 않았던 행운을 누렸으니, 그래서 강도를 당한 사람들 가운데 누구도 그를 두고 맹세할 수 없었고, 그에게 씌울 만한 것도 없었다. 그러나 보아하니 그가 패거리와 함께 잡혔던 터라, 입이 험한 어떤 시골 사람 하나가 그를 두고 단단히 맹세했고, 그들이 낸 공고에 따라 다른 자들도 나설 듯하여, 그래서 그에게 불리한 증거가 더 들어오리라 예상되었고, 그런 까닭에 그는 구금된 채 있었던 것이다.\n\n그러나 그를 유배에 부치도록 허락하는 그 제의는, 내가 알기로 어떤 지체 높은 인물의 알선으로 이루어진 것이었으니, 그 사람이 재판에 앞서 그것을 받아들이라고 그를 모질게 다그쳤던 것이다. 실로 그에게 불리하게 나설 자가 여럿 있을 수 있음을 그가 아는 터라, 나는 그의 벗이 옳았다고 여겨, 더는 미루지 말라고 밤낮으로 그를 졸랐다.\n\n마침내 여간한 어려움 끝에 그는 동의했다. 그런데 그는 나처럼 법정에서, 또 자기 탄원에 따라 유배에 부쳐진 것이 아니었으므로, 내가 그가 그리할 수 있었으리라 말한 대로 스스로 승선하는 것을 피하기가 어려운 처지에 놓이게 되었으니, 그에게 그 허가의 은전을 알선해 준 그 지체 높은 벗이, 그가 스스로 유배를 떠나며 기한 안에 돌아오지 않으리라는 보증을 서 주었기 때문이다.\n\n이 난관이 내 모든 계획을 어그러뜨렸으니, 그 뒤로 나 자신의 구원을 위해 내가 밟은 절차들이 이로 인해 죄다 헛것이 되고 말았기 때문이며, 그를 저버리고 그가 홀로 아메리카로 가도록 내버려 두지 않는 한 그러했는데, 그는 설령 곧장 교수대로 가는 것이 확실하다 할지라도 차라리 그쪽을 무릅쓰겠다고 단언했다.\n\n이제 나는 내 이야기로 돌아가야겠다. 내 선고에 따라 유배될 시기가 가까이 다가와 있었다. 내 변함없는 벗으로 남아 있던 후견인이 사면을 얻어 보려 애썼으나, 내 주머니에 비추어 너무 버거운 비용을 들이지 않고서는 그리할 수 없었으니, 내가 옛 직업으로 다시 돌아갈 결심을 하지 않은 한, 빈털터리에 알몸으로 남겨지는 것은 내 유배보다 더 나쁜 일이었던 까닭이다. 거기서는 내가 살아갈 수 있음을 알았으나 여기서는 그럴 수 없었기 때문이다. 그 선량한 목사도 내가 유배되는 것을 막으려고 다른 명목으로 매우 애썼으나, 실로 내 목숨이 그의 첫 간청으로 이미 주어진 것이니 더는 청해서는 안 된다는 답을 들었다. 그는 내가 떠나는 것을 두고 절실히 슬퍼했으니, 그의 말처럼, 죽음의 전망이 처음에 내게 새긴, 그리고 그 뒤로 그의 가르침으로 더해진 그 좋은 인상들을 내가 잃을까 두려워했기 때문이며, 그 경건한 신사는 그 때문에 나를 두고 몹시 걱정했다.\n\n다른 한편으로, 나는 실로 전만큼 그것을 두고 노심초사하지는 않았으나, 그 까닭만은 목사에게 애써 감추었으니, 끝까지 그는 내가 더없는 마뜩잖음과 비통함 속에 떠나는 줄로만 알고 있었다.\n\n내가 그들이 우리를 일컫는 대로 일곱 명의 다른 죄수와 함께 버지니아로 무역하는 한 상인에게 넘겨져, 그들이 이르는 대로 뎁트퍼드리치에 정박해 있는 한 배에 태워진 것은 이월의 일이었다. 옥의 관리가 우리를 배에 인도했고, 그 배의 선장이 우리에 대한 인수증을 써 주었다.\n\n그날 밤 우리는 승강구 아래 갇혔는데, 어찌나 빽빽하게 가두어 두었던지 나는 공기가 모자라 질식하는 줄로만 알았다. 이튿날 아침 배가 닻을 올려 강을 따라 내려가, 그들이 버그비스홀이라 부르는 곳에 이르렀으니, 우리에게 일러 준 바로는 우리에게서 모든 도망의 기회를 앗아 두려는 그 상인의 합의에 따른 것이었다. 그러나 배가 거기 이르러 닻을 내리자, 우리는 더 많은 자유를 허락받았고, 특히 갑판 위로 올라오는 것은 허락되었으되, 그것은 특별히 선장과 승객들을 위해 마련된 곳이었던 까닭에 후갑판 위로 올라오는 것은 허락되지 않았다.\n\n머리 위 사람들의 소란과 배의 움직임으로 그들이 돛을 올렸음을 알아차렸을 때, 나는 처음에 우리가 곧장 떠나가 우리 벗들이 더는 우리를 보러 오도록 허락되지 않을까 두려워 크게 놀랐다. 그러나 곧 그들이 다시 닻을 내린 것을 알고는 이내 마음이 놓였고, 그 직후에 우리가 어디에 있는지를 몇몇 선원이 우리에게 일러 주며, 이튿날 아침 우리가 갑판 위로 올라오고, 벗이 있다면 그들이 우리를 보러 오게 할 자유를 누리게 되리라고 알려 주었다.\n\n그날 밤 내내 나는 승객들이 그러하듯 갑판의 딱딱한 널빤지 위에 누워 있었으나, 그 뒤로 우리 가운데 거기에 깔 침구를 가진 자들에게는 작은 선실을 쓸 자유와, 가진 것이 있다면 옷이며 속옷을 넣을 상자나 궤짝 하나를 쟁여 둘 자리가 허락되었으니(그것은 충분히 들여놓을 만했다), 그들 가운데 더러는 등에 걸친 것 말고는 셔츠도 슈미즈도, 아마포든 모직이든 헝겊 한 조각도, 제 한 몸 건사할 한 푼의 돈도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그들이 배에서 그런대로 잘 지내지 못하는 것 같지는 않았으니, 특히 여자들은 선원들의 옷을 빨아 주고 돈을 벌어, 자기들에게 필요한 흔한 물건들을 사기에 넉넉했다.\n\n이튿날 아침 우리가 갑판 위로 올라올 자유를 누리게 되었을 때, 나는 배의 한 관리에게, 내가 뭍으로 편지를 한 통 보내 내 벗들에게 배가 어디에 정박해 있는지 알리고, 내게 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 보내 받을 자유가 없겠느냐고 물었다. 보아하니 이 사람은 갑판장으로, 매우 점잖고 정중한 부류의 사람이었는데, 그는 내게 그것이든, 그가 무사히 허락해 줄 수 있는 다른 어떤 자유든 누리게 해 주겠다고 말했다. 나는 다른 것은 바라지 않는다고 말했고, 그는 이튿날 밀물에 배의 보트가 런던으로 올라갈 것이니 내 편지를 실어 보내도록 분부하겠다고 대답했다.\n\n그리하여 보트가 떠날 때, 갑판장이 내게 와서 보트가 떠난다고, 자기도 그 안에 타고 간다고 일러 주며, 내 편지가 준비되었다면 자기가 그것을 맡아 주겠다고 물었다. 여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 나는 미리 펜과 잉크와 종이를 마련해 두었던 터라, 내 후견인에게 보내는 편지 한 통을 준비해 두었고, 그 안에 내 동료 죄수에게 보내는 또 한 통을 동봉했는데, 다만 끝까지 그가 내 남편이라는 것은 그녀에게 알리지 않았다. 후견인에게 보내는 편지에는 배가 어디에 정박해 있는지를 알리고, 내 항해를 위해 그녀가 마련해 두었음을 내가 아는 그 물건들을 보내 달라고 간곡히 졸랐다.\n\n내가 갑판장에게 편지를 건넬 때, 나는 그것과 함께 그에게 일 실링을 주며, 그것은 심부름꾼이나 짐꾼의 삯에 쓰라는 것이라고 말하고, 그가 뭍에 닿는 대로 그 편지를 들려 보내 주기를 간청했으니, 될 수 있으면 같은 사람 손에 답장을 받아 와, 내 물건들이 어찌 되었는지 알 수 있게 하기 위함이었다. \"선생님, 만약에,\" 내가 말했다. \"그 물건들을 배에 들이기 전에 배가 떠나 버리면, 나는 끝장이거든요.\"\n\n내가 그에게 일 실링을 줄 때, 나는 그가 내게 여느 죄수들보다 조금 나은 살림을 갖추고 있음을 보게끔 신경을 썼으니, 그는 내게 지갑이 있고 그 안에 꽤 많은 돈이 들어 있는 것을 보았던 것이다. 그리고 나는 바로 그것을 본 것만으로도 그가 내게, 그러지 않았더라면 배에서 받았을 것과는 사뭇 다른 대접을 즉시 해 주었음을 알게 되었다. 그는 실로 전에도, 곤경에 처한 여인인 내게 일종의 자연스러운 연민으로 매우 정중했으나, 그 뒤로는 예사롭지 않게 더 그러했고, 내가 그러지 않았더라면 받았을 것보다 배에서 더 나은 대접을 받도록 주선해 주었으니, 이는 마땅한 자리에서 드러날 것이다.\n\n그는 매우 정직하게 내 편지를 후견인의 손에 직접 전해 주고, 그녀에게서 글로 쓴 답장을 받아다 주었으며, 그 답장을 내게 건넬 때 일 실링도 도로 주었다. \"여기,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신 일 실링도 도로 받으시오, 편지는 내가 직접 전했으니 말이오.\" 나는 그 일에 어찌나 놀랐던지 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으나, 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다. \"선생님, 너무 친절하시네요. 그렇다면 그 마차 삯이라도 받으시는 게 도리였을 텐데요.\"\n\n\"아니요, 아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 넘치게 받았소. 그 부인은 무엇이오? 당신 언니요?\"\n\n\"아니에요, 선생님,\" 내가 말했다. \"그분은 제 친척이 아니라 소중한 벗이고, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 벗의 전부예요.\" \"허,\" 그가 말했다. \"세상에 그런 벗은 드물지요. 아니, 그 부인은 당신을 두고 어린아이처럼 울던걸요.\" \"그럼요,\" 내가 다시 말했다. \"내가 처한 이 무서운 처지에서 나를 구해 낼 수 있다면 그분은 백 파운드라도 내놓을 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇단 말이오?\" 그가 말했다. \"그 절반이면 내가 당신 스스로 빠져나갈 길을 일러 줄 수 있을 것 같소만.\" 그러나 이 말을 그는 아무도 들을 수 없게 나직이 했다.\n\n\"아아! 선생님,\" 내가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 만약 다시 잡히기라도 하면 제 목숨을 앗아 갈 그런 종류의 탈출일 거예요.\" \"아니지,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신이 일단 배에서 나가면, 그 뒤로는 당신 스스로 알아서 해야지요. 그건 내가 뭐라 할 수 없는 일이오.\" 그래서 우리는 그때는 그 이야기를 그만두었다.\n\n그러는 사이, 마지막 순간까지 충실했던 내 후견인은 내 편지를 옥에 있는 내 남편에게 전해 그에 대한 답장을 받아다 주었고, 이튿날에는 몸소 배로 내려와, 무엇보다 먼저 그들이 이르는 대로 선상 침대 하나와, 요긴하되 사람들이 유별나다고 여기지 않을 만한 그 온갖 부속품을 내게 가져다주었다. 그녀는 선상 궤짝 하나도 가져왔는데, 그것은 선원들을 위해 만들어진 그런 궤짝으로, 온갖 편의 용품이 갖추어져 있고, 내게 필요할 만한 거의 모든 것이 채워져 있었다. 그리고 그 궤짝의 한 귀퉁이, 비밀 서랍이 있는 곳에는 내 은행, 곧 내 돈이 들어 있었으니, 다시 말해 내가 가지고 가기로 마음먹은 만큼의 돈이었다. 나는 내 밑천의 일부를 뒤에 남겨 두어, 거기 이르러 자리를 잡을 때 내게 필요할 물품의 형태로 나중에 부쳐 오도록 분부해 두었으니, 그 나라에서는 모든 것이 담배로 거래되는 터라 돈이 그리 쓸모가 없을뿐더러, 여기서 그것을 가지고 가는 것은 더더욱 큰 손해이기 때문이다.\n\n그러나 내 경우는 사정이 남달랐으니, 내가 돈도 물품도 없이 그리로 가는 것은 결코 마땅치 않았고, 뭍에 닿는 대로 팔려 나갈 가련한 죄수가 한 무더기의 물품을 가지고 간다면 사람들의 눈에 띄어, 어쩌면 관헌에게 압수당할지도 모르는 일이었다. 그래서 나는 내 밑천의 일부를 이렇게 가지고 가고, 나머지 일부는 후견인에게 맡겨 두었다.\n\n후견인은 그 밖에도 많은 물건을 내게 가져다주었으나, 적어도 우리가 어떤 부류의 선장을 만나게 될지 알기 전까지는 내가 배에서 너무 잘 갖춘 티를 내는 것이 마땅치 않았다. 그녀가 배에 들어섰을 때, 나는 그녀가 정말로 죽고 마는 줄 알았다. 그런 처지의 나를 보고, 또 그런 형편의 나와 헤어진다는 생각에 그녀는 가슴이 무너져, 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 울던지, 나는 한참 동안 그녀와 아무런 이야기도 나눌 수 없었다.\n\n나는 그 틈을 타 내 동료 죄수의 편지를 읽었는데, 그것은 나를 몹시 난감하게 했다. 그는 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔으나, 같은 배로 떠날 만큼 제때에 방면되기는 불가능할 것 같다고, 게다가 무엇보다도, 비록 자청하여 스스로 유배를 떠나는 처지이긴 하나, 자기가 원하는 배로 가도록 허락해 줄지가 의심스러워지기 시작했다고, 도리어 그들이 자기를 지정하는 배에 태우는 것을 지켜볼 것이며, 다른 죄수들과 마찬가지로 자기가 그 선장에게 인계되리라고 적어 왔다. 그래서 그는 자기가 버지니아에 이를 때까지 나를 보지 못하게 될까 절망하기 시작했고, 그 때문에 거의 자포자기에 빠졌으니, 다른 한편으로, 만약 내가 거기 있지 않게 된다면, 만약 바다의 어떤 사고나 죽음이 나를 앗아 간다면, 자기는 거기서 이 세상에서 가장 신세 망친 가련한 자가 되고 말리라고 여겼기 때문이다.\n\n이것은 매우 난감한 일이라, 나는 어떤 방도를 취해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 나는 후견인에게 갑판장의 이야기를 들려주었고, 그녀는 내가 그와 흥정해 보기를 몹시 다그쳤다. 그러나 나는 내 남편, 그러니까 그녀가 일컫는 대로 하면 내 동료 죄수가 나와 함께 갈 자유를 얻을 수 있을지 없을지를 듣기 전까지는 그럴 마음이 없었다. 마침내 나는 그를 내 남편이라는 것만 빼고는 그 일 전부를 그녀에게 털어놓을 수밖에 없었다. 나는 그녀에게, 만약 그가 같은 배로 갈 자유를 얻을 수 있다면 함께 가기로 그와 확실한 거래, 곧 약조를 맺었으며, 그에게 돈이 있음을 알게 되었다고 말했다.\n그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게, 우리가 거기 이르면 내가 무엇을 하려는지, 우리가 어떻게 농사를 짓고 자리를 잡으며, 한마디로 더는 어떤 모험도 없이 어떻게 부유해질 것인지를 길게 늘어놓았고, 큰 비밀로서, 그가 배에 오르는 대로 우리가 혼인하기로 되어 있다고 그녀에게 말했다.\n\n그녀는 이 말을 듣자 곧 기꺼이 내가 가는 데 동의했고, 그때부터 그를 제때에 옥에서 빼내어 나와 같은 배로 가게 하는 것을 자기 일로 삼았으니, 마침내 그것이 이루어지긴 했으나, 여간한 어려움이 아니었고, 또 유배된 죄수의 온갖 절차를 다 거치지 않고서는 안 되었는데, 그는 아직 재판을 받지 않았으니 실은 그런 죄수가 아니었던지라, 그것은 그에게 크나큰 굴욕이었다.\n\n이제 우리의 운명이 정해져, 우리 둘 다 배에 올라, 노예로 팔려 갈 운명인 유배된 죄수라는 그 비천한 신분으로, 나는 다섯 해 동안, 그는 보증 아래 실제로 버지니아행에 묶이게 되었으니\n잉글랜드로 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다는 보증을 살아 있는 한 지켜야 한다는 조건이 붙자, 그는 몹시 풀이 죽고 의기소침해졌다. 그가 그렇게 죄수처럼 배에 끌려와 실린 굴욕이 그를 크게 자극했으니, 처음에는 그가 자원하여 유배를 떠나도 되며, 따라서 자유로운 신사로서 갈 수 있다고 들었기 때문이다. 사실 그는 거기에 도착했을 때 우리처럼 팔리도록 명령받지는 않았고, 그런 까닭에 선장에게 뱃삯을 치러야 했으니, 우리는 그러지 않았다. 나머지에 관해서는, 그는 어린아이처럼, 자기 자신을 어찌해야 할지, 가진 것을 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못하고 그저 지시를 따를 뿐이었다.\n\n우리가 맨 먼저 할 일은 우리의 밑천을 견주어 보는 것이었다. 그는 내게 아주 솔직하게, 자기가 감옥에 들어왔을 때만 해도 밑천이 꽤 괜찮았으나, 거기서 신사 행세하며 산 생활과, 그것의 열 배에 달하는, 친구를 사귀고 자기 사정을 청원하는 일이 무척 비용이 많이 들었다고 말했다. 한마디로, 그에게 남은 밑천 전부는 백팔 파운드였고, 그것을 모두 금화로 몸에 지니고 있었다.\n\n나도 마찬가지로 충실하게 내 밑천을 그에게 알려 주었으니, 다시 말해 내가 지니고 가려고 챙긴 것에 관해서 말이다. 무슨 일이 일어나든 후견인에게 맡겨 둔 것은 따로 비축해 두기로 결심한 까닭이었다. 혹여 내가 죽더라도 내가 지닌 것만으로도 그에게 주기에 충분할 터이고, 후견인의 손에 남겨 둔 것은 그녀 자신의 몫이 될 터인데, 실로 그녀는 내게서 그것을 받을 만한 자격이 충분했다.\n\n내가 지니고 있던 밑천은 이백사십육 파운드 남짓의 몇 실링이었으니, 그래서 우리 둘 사이에는 삼백오십사 파운드가 있었던 셈이나, 세상을 새로 시작하는 밑천으로 이보다 더 나쁘게 얻은 재산을 한데 그러모은 일은 좀처럼 없었으리라.\n\n우리 밑천에 관한 가장 큰 불운은 그것이 모두 돈이라는 점이었으니, 누구나 알다시피 식민지로 가져가기에 돈은 이득이 없는 짐이다. 그의 것은, 그가 내게 말한 대로, 정말로 세상에 그가 가진 전부였으리라 믿는다. 그러나 이 재난이 내게 닥쳤을 때 나는 은행에 칠백에서 팔백 파운드 사이의 돈을 두고 있었고, 또 그것을 관리해 줄, 세상에서 가장 충실한 벗 가운데 하나를 두었으니, 그녀가 어느 정도 신앙적 원칙을 지닌 여자임을 생각하면, 앞서 말한 대로 따로 비축해 둔 삼백 파운드가 여전히 그녀의 손에 남아 있었다. 게다가 매우 값나가는 물건 몇 가지도 있었으니, 특히 금시계 두 개, 작은 은그릇 몇 점, 반지 몇 개였는데, 모두 훔친 물건이었다.\n은그릇과 반지와 시계는 돈과 함께 내 궤짝에 넣어 두었고, 이 재산을 지니고, 또 내 나이 예순한 살에, 나는 (겉보기에는) 교수대에서 형 집행이 유예되어 유배되라는 명을 받은 가엾고 헐벗은 중범죄자에 지나지 않는 처지로, 새로운 세계라 부를 만한 곳으로 나아갔다. 내 옷은 초라하고 보잘것없었으나 누덕누덕하거나 더럽지는 않았고, 배 안의 그 누구도 내가 무언가 값진 것을 몸에 지니고 있음을 알지 못했다.\n\n그렇지만 나는 아주 좋은 옷가지가 많고 아마포가 넉넉했던지라, 그것들을 큰 상자 두 개에 꾸려 두라고 일러 두고는, 내 물건으로서가 아니라 버지니아에 있는 내 본명 앞으로 보내는 화물로 배에 실었다. 그리고 어느 선장이 서명한 선하증권을 내 주머니에 지니고 있었다. 또 이 상자들 안에는 내 은그릇과 시계와, 돈을 뺀 온갖 값진 것이 들어 있었으니, 돈만은 따로 내 궤짝의 비밀 서랍에 넣어 두었는데, 그 서랍은 찾아낼 수도 없거니와, 설령 찾아낸다 해도 궤짝을 산산조각 내지 않고서는 열 수 없는 것이었다.\n\n이런 처지로 나는 배 안에서 세 주를 지냈는데, 남편이 나와 함께 갈지 어떨지 알 수 없었던 까닭에, 그 정직한 갑판장의 제안을 어떻게, 무슨 방식으로 받아들일지 결심을 내리지 못하고 있었으니, 사실 그도 처음에는 그것을 조금 이상하게 여겼다.\n\n이 기간이 끝날 무렵, 보라, 내 남편이 배에 올랐다. 그는 풀이 죽고 성난 얼굴빛이었으며, 그 큰 마음은 분노와 멸시로 부풀어 있었다. 재판조차 받지 않았는데도 뉴게이트의 간수 셋에게 끌려와 죄수처럼 배에 실린 것이었다. 그는 친구들을 통해 그 일을 크게 항의했으니, 그에게는 어느 정도 연줄이 있었던 모양이다. 그러나 그의 친구들은 청원하다가 제동이 걸렸고, 그가 충분히 은전을 입었다는 것과, 그의 유배가 마지막으로 허가된 이후로 그에 관해 그런 보고를 받았으니 새로이 기소되지 않은 것만으로도 매우 후한 대접을 받았다고 여겨야 마땅하다는 말을 들었다. 이 대답이 그를 단숨에 잠잠하게 했으니, 그는 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있었는지, 또 그가 무엇을 예상할 만했는지를 너무도 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 그는 자발적인 유배의 제안을 받아들이도록 그를 설득했던, 그 충고의 훌륭함을 깨달았다. 이렇게 그가 지옥의 사냥개들이라 부르던 자들에 대한 그의 울분이 조금 가라앉은 뒤, 그는 다소 차분해 보였고 명랑해지기 시작했다. 내가 그를 다시 한번 그들의 손아귀에서 벗어나게 해서 얼마나 기쁜지 이야기하자, 그는 나를 두 팔로 끌어안고는, 더없는 다정함으로, 내가 그에게 더없이 좋은 충고를 해 주었음을 인정했다. \"여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"당신은 두 번이나 내 목숨을 구했소. 이제부터 그 목숨은 모두 당신을 위해 쓰일 것이고, 나는 언제나 당신의 충고를 따르겠소.\"\n\n이제 배가 차기 시작했다. 범죄와는 무관하게 승선한 여러 승객이 배에 올랐는데, 이들에게는 큰 선실이나 배의 다른 부분에 숙소가 배정되었으나, 우리는 중범죄자라 하여 어딘지도 모를 아래쪽으로 밀쳐 넣어졌다. 그러나 내 남편이 배에 올랐을 때, 나는 일찍이 내 편지를 전해 주며 호의를 넌지시 보였던 그 갑판장에게 말을 걸었다. 나는 그에게, 그가 여러 가지로 나를 도와주었는데도 내가 그에 합당한 보답을 하지 못했다고 말하며, 그러면서 그의 손에 기니 한 닢을 쥐여 주었다. 나는 그에게, 이제 내 남편이 배에 올랐으며, 우리 둘 다 지금의 불운에 처해 있긴 하나, 우리는 함께 끌려온 그 비참한 무리와는 다른 부류의 사람들이었다고 말하고, 우리가 그가 원하는 만큼 사례를 할 터이니, 그리고 그가 우리를 위해 이 일을 주선해 주는 수고에 대해 후하게 보답할 터이니, 선장이 마음을 움직여 우리에게 배 안에서 얼마간 편의를 베풀어 주지 않겠는지 그에게 물어보고 싶다고 했다. 그는 내가 보기에 매우 흡족해하며 기니를 받았고, 자기가 돕겠노라고 내게 장담했다.\n\n그러더니 그는 우리에게, 세상에서 가장 너그러운 신사 가운데 하나인 선장이 우리가 바라는 만큼 기꺼이 우리를 편의 봐주리라는 것을 의심치 않는다고 말했고, 나를 안심시키려고, 그 일을 일부러 선장에게 말하러 다음 물때에 올라가겠다고 했다. 다음 날 아침, 마침 평소보다 조금 늦잠을 자고는, 일어나서 밖을 내다보기 시작했을 때, 나는 갑판장이 사내들 틈에서 평소의 일을 하고 있는 것을 보았다. 그가 거기 있는 것을 보고 나는 조금 우울해져서, 그에게 말을 걸려고 다가가니, 그가 나를 보고 내 쪽으로 왔는데, 그가 먼저 말할 틈을 주지 않고 나는 미소 지으며 말했다. \"선장님, 우리를 잊으신 것 같네요. 보아하니 매우 바쁘신 듯하니 말이에요.\" 그가 곧 대답했다. \"저와 함께 가십시다. 그러면 보실 겁니다.\" 그렇게 그는 나를 큰 선실로 데려갔는데, 거기에는 뱃사람치고는 점잖은 신사다운 사람이 앉아서, 앞에 수많은 서류를 늘어놓고 글을 쓰고 있었다.\n\n\"여기,\" 갑판장이 글을 쓰고 있던 그에게 말했다. \"선장님께서 당신께 말씀하신 그 부인입니다.\" 그러고는 내게 돌아서서 말했다. \"저는 당신의 일을 잊기는커녕, 선장님 댁까지 올라가서, 당신과 당신 남편을 위해 더 나은 편의를 갖춰 주는 일에 관해 당신이 하신 말씀을 선장님께 충실히 전했습니다. 그리고 선장님께서는 이 신사분을, 곧 이 배의 항해사를 일부러 저와 함께 내려보내, 당신께 모든 것을 보여드리고, 당신이 만족하시도록 충분히 편의를 봐드리게 하셨으며, 당신이 처음에 예상되던 그런 대접이 아니라 다른 승객들과 똑같은 존중을 받게 되리라는 것을 당신께 장담하라고 제게 분부하셨습니다.\"\n\n그러자 항해사가 내게 말을 걸었는데, 내가 갑판장의 친절에 감사할 틈도 주지 않고, 갑판장이 한 말을 확인해 주며, 선장은 친절하고 자비로운 모습을 보이기를, 특히 어떤 불운에 처한 이들에게 그러기를 좋아한다고 덧붙였다. 그러면서 그는 내게, 어떤 것은 큰 선실에 만들어지고, 어떤 것은 조타실에서 칸막이로 막되 일부러 승객 편의를 위해 큰 선실 쪽으로 트인, 여러 선실을 보여주며 내가 원하는 곳을 고르게 했다. 그렇지만 나는 조타실 쪽으로 트인 선실을 골랐으니, 거기에는 우리 궤짝과 상자들을 둘 매우 좋은 자리와, 식사할 탁자가 있었다.\n\n그러더니 항해사는 내게, 우리의 점잖은 처신에 관해 갑판장이 나와 내 남편에 대해 너무도 좋게 말해 두었으므로, 우리가 원한다면 항해 내내 일반 승객의 통상적인 조건으로 선장과 함께 식사하게 될 것이라고 전하라는 분부를 받았다고 말했다. 우리가 원한다면 신선한 식량을 좀 사 두어도 좋고, 그러지 않겠다면 선장이 평소의 저장 식량을 마련할 터이니 우리가 그와 나누면 된다고 했다. 이는 근래 내가 겪어 온 그토록 많은 고난과 고초 끝에, 내게 무척이나 생기를 되살려 주는 소식이었다. 나는 그에게 감사를 표하고, 선장이 우리와 자기 마음대로 조건을 정하면 된다고 말했으며, 가서 남편에게 이를 알려도 되겠는지 허락을 청했으니, 남편은 몸이 그리 좋지 않아 아직 선실에서 나오지 않은 터였다. 그래서 나는 갔고, 남편은 (그가 받아들이기로는) 자기에게 가해진 모욕으로 여전히 기운이 너무도 가라앉아서 아직 제정신이 아닐 지경이었는데, 우리가 배에서 받게 될 대접에 관해 내가 전해 준 이야기에 어찌나 생기를 되찾았는지, 완전히 다른 사람이 되어, 새로운 활기와 용기가 바로 그 얼굴빛에 떠올랐다. 가장 큰 기개를 지닌 자도 그 고난에 짓눌리면 가장 큰 의기소침에 빠지기 쉽고, 가장 절망하여 스스로를 내던지기 쉽다는 것은 이토록 참된 일이다.\n\n마음을 추스르려 잠시 멈춘 뒤, 내 남편이 나와 함께 올라가서 우리에게 베풀어 준 친절에 대해 항해사에게 감사를 표하고, 그를 통해 선장에게 합당한 사례의 뜻을 전했으니, 우리 뱃삯과, 그가 우리에게 마련해 준 편의에 대해 무엇이든 요구하는 대로 미리 치르겠노라고 했다. 항해사는 그에게, 선장이 오후에 배에 오를 터이니 그 모든 일은 선장이 올 때까지 두겠다고 말했다. 그리하여 오후에 선장이 왔는데, 우리는 그가 갑판장이 묘사한 그대로 예의 바르고 친절한 사람임을 알았다. 그는 내 남편의 대화에 어찌나 흡족해했는지, 한마디로 우리가 고른 선실을 쓰지 못하게 하고, 앞서 말한 대로 큰 선실 쪽으로 트인 선실을 우리에게 내주었다.\n\n그의 조건도 터무니없지 않았고, 그 사람은 욕심을 부려 우리를 먹잇감으로 삼으려 안달하지도 않았으니, 다만 십오 기니로 우리는 항해 전체와 식량과 선실을 얻었고, 선장의 식탁에서 식사하며 매우 융숭한 대접을 받았다.\n\n선장은 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 자기 후갑판실을, 아내와 세 아이를 데리고 건너가는 어느 부유한 농장주에게 빌려준 터라, 큰 선실의 다른 부분에 자리를 잡았는데, 그 가족은 따로 식사를 했다. 그에게는 다른 평범한 승객도 몇몇 있어 조타실에 묵었고, 우리의 옛 동무들로 말하자면, 배가 거기 정박해 있는 동안 승강구 아래에 갇혀 있어 갑판에는 거의 올라오지 못했다.\n\n나는 일어난 일을 후견인에게 알리지 않을 수 없었으니, 나를 그토록 진심으로 염려해 준 그녀가 내 행운에 한몫을 하는 것은 마땅한 일이었다. 게다가 나는 그녀의 도움을 받아 여러 필수품을 마련해야 했는데, 전에는 그것이 남들 눈에 띄지 않게 하려고, 누군가 내가 그런 것을 지닌 것을 보게 하기를 꺼렸지만, 이제 나는 물건을 둘 선실과 자리가 생겼으므로, 항해 중에 우리가 편하도록 좋은 물건을 잔뜩 주문했으니, 펀치를 만들고 우리의 은인인 선장을 대접할 브랜디, 설탕, 레몬 따위와, 항해 중에 먹고 마실 온갖 것이었다. 또한 더 큰 침대와, 거기에 어울리는 침구도 주문했다. 한마디로, 우리는 항해 중에 아무것도 부족하지 않기로 작정한 것이다.\n\n이 모든 동안에도 나는, 우리가 그곳에 도착해 우리 자신을 농장주라 일컫기 시작할 때 우리에게 도움이 될 것을 아무것도 마련하지 못한 터였다. 그리고 나는 그런 경우에 무엇이 필요한지를 결코 모르지 않았으니, 특히 농장 일과 건축에 쓸 온갖 연장과, 우리 거처에 쓸 온갖 가구가 그것인데, 이는 그 나라에서 사려면 반드시 두 배의 값이 들 터였다.\n\n그래서 나는 그 점을 후견인과 의논했고, 그녀는 가서 선장을 찾아뵙고는, 자기가 사촌이라 부르던 우리 두 불운한 자가 그 나라에 도착했을 때 자유를 얻을 방도가 찾아질 수 있기를 바란다고 말하면서, 그 방법과 조건에 관해서도 그와 의논에 들어갔는데, 그에 관해서는 적당한 자리에서 더 말하겠다. 이렇게 선장의 속을 떠본 뒤, 그녀는 그에게, 우리가 비록 우리를 떠나게 만든 그 정황으로 인해 불행하긴 하나, 그 나라에서 일을 시작할 채비가 없지 않으며, 그곳에 정착해 농장주로 살아갈 길만 마련된다면 그리하기로 작정했다는 것을 알렸다. 선장은 선뜻 도움을 베풀겠다고 하며, 그녀에게 그런 사업에 착수하는 방법과, 부지런한 사람들이 그런 식으로 재산을 회복하기가 얼마나 쉬운지, 아니, 얼마나 확실한지를 일러 주었다. \"부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"그 나라에서는 제가 보기에 당신 사촌들이 처한 것보다 더 나쁜 처지로 보내져 온 것도 누구에게든 결코 수치가 아니랍니다. 그저 그들이 그곳에 도착해서 그 고장의 일에 부지런함과 좋은 분별로 임하기만 한다면 말이지요.\"\n\n그러자 그녀는 그에게, 우리가 가지고 건너가야 할 것이 무엇인지 물었고, 그는 매우 정직할 뿐 아니라 식견 있는 사람으로서 그녀에게 이렇게 일러 주었다. \"부인, 당신 사촌들은 우선 그들의 유배 조건에 따라 자기들을 종으로 사 줄 누군가를 구해야 합니다. 그런 다음 그 사람의 이름으로 그들은 무엇이든 마음대로 할 수 있지요. 이미 시작된 농장을 사들이든, 아니면 그 나라 정부로부터 땅을 사서 원하는 곳에서 시작하든, 둘 다 합당한 값에 할 수 있습니다.\" 그녀는 첫 번째 항목에 관해 그의 호의를 청했고, 그는 그 일을 자기가 떠맡겠노라고 그녀에게 약속했으며, 실로 충실히 그것을 해냈다. 나머지에 관해서는, 우리에게 가장 좋은 조언을 해 줄 만한 이들에게 우리를 추천하고, 우리를 속이지 않게 하겠노라고 약속했으니, 그것은 바랄 수 있는 전부였다.\n\n그러더니 그녀는 그에게, 우리에게 농사일에 쓸 연장과 자재를 한 밑천 갖춰 줄 필요는 없겠는지 물었고, 그는 \"그렇습니다, 무슨 수를 써서라도요\"라고 말했다. 그러자 그녀는 그 일에 그의 도움을 청했다. 그녀는 그에게, 비용이 얼마가 들든 우리에게 편리한 것은 무엇이든 다 갖춰 주겠노라고 말했다. 그래서 그는 그녀에게 농장주에게 필요한 물건의 긴 목록을 일러 주었는데, 그의 셈으로는 그것이 팔십 내지 백 파운드쯤에 이르렀다. 한마디로, 그녀는 마치 노련한 버지니아 상인이라도 되는 양 능란하게 그것들을 사러 다녔으니, 다만 내 지시에 따라 그가 일러 준 목록의 모든 것을 두 배 넘게 사들였다는 점만 달랐다.\n\n이것들을 그녀는 자기 이름으로 배에 실었고, 그것들의 선하증권을 받아 내 남편 앞으로 배서했으며, 그 후 우리 지시에 따라 자기 이름으로 화물에 보험을 들었으니, 그리하여 우리는 온갖 사태와 온갖 재난에 대비가 되었다.\n\n내 남편이 그녀에게 자기 밑천 백팔 파운드 전부를, 앞서 말했듯이 그가 금화로 몸에 지니고 있던 것을, 이렇게 쓰라고 내주었다는 것을 말해 두었어야 했다. 그리고 나도 그 밖에 상당한 액수를 그녀에게 주었으니, 그래서 나는 그녀의 손에 맡겨 둔 밑천에는 전혀 손을 대지 않았으며, 우리 화물을 전부 갖춘 뒤에도 우리에게는 여전히 이백 파운드 가까운 돈이 남아 있었는데, 그것은 우리 목적에 차고도 남았다.\n\n이런 처지로, 매우 명랑하게, 실로 그토록 만족스럽게 편의를 봐주신 것에 기뻐하며, 우리는 버그비스홀에서 그레이브젠드로 출항했고, 배는 그곳에 열흘 남짓 더 정박했으며, 거기서 선장이 아주 눌러앉으려 배에 올랐다. 여기서 선장은 우리가 실로 기대할 까닭이 전혀 없던 호의를 베풀었으니, 곧 우리가 그에게서 달아나지 않고 다시 얌전히 배로 돌아오겠다고 엄숙하게 약속하면, 뭍에 올라 기분을 풀고 오게 해 주겠다는 것이었다. 이것은 우리에 대한 그의 신뢰의 그런 증거였던지라, 내 남편을 감복시켰으니, 그는 순전한 감사의 마음에서, 자기로서는 그런 호의에 합당한 보답을 할 처지가 도무지 못 되므로 그것을 받아들일 생각조차 할 수 없으며, 선장이 그런 위험을 무릅쓰게 하는 것도 마음 편치 않다고 그에게 말했다. 서로 몇 마디 예의를 주고받은 뒤, 나는 남편에게 팔십 기니가 든 지갑 하나를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 선장의 손에 쥐여 주었다. \"자, 선장님,\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 우리 신의의 담보 일부가 있습니다. 우리가 어떤 일로든 당신을 부정하게 대한다면, 그것은 당신 것입니다.\" 그러고 나서 우리는 뭍에 올랐다.\n\n실로 선장은 우리가 떠나리라는 것에 대한 확신이 충분했으니, 우리가 그곳에 정착하려고 그런 채비를 갖춰 둔 마당에, 목숨을 건 비용과 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 여기 남기를 택하리라는 것은 이치에 닿지 않아 보였기 때문이다. 만약 우리가 다시 붙잡힌다면 그것은 분명 그런 꼴이 되었을 터이다. 한마디로, 우리는 선장과 함께 모두 뭍에 올라 그레이브젠드에서 함께 저녁을 먹었고, 거기서 매우 흥겹게 보냈으며, 밤새 머물러 우리가 저녁을 먹은 그 집에서 잤고, 아침에 모두 그와 함께 아주 떳떳이 다시 배에 올랐다. 여기서 우리는 좋은 맥주 열두 다스와 포도주 약간, 닭 몇 마리, 그리고 배 안에서 환영받을 만하다 싶은 것들을 샀다.\n\n내 후견인은 이 모든 동안 우리와 함께 있었고, 우리와 더불어 다운스까지 돌아 나왔으며, 선장의 아내도 그랬으니, 후견인은 그 아내와 함께 돌아갔다. 나는 내 친어머니와 헤어질 때도 그녀와 헤어질 때만큼 슬프지는 않았고, 그 뒤로 다시는 그녀를 보지 못했다. 우리가 다운스에 다다른 지 사흘째 되는 날 순한 동풍이 일었고, 우리는 사월 십 일에 그곳에서 출항했다. 그 뒤로는 어디에도 들르지 않았으나, 매우 거센 강풍에 아일랜드 해안으로 밀려가, 배가 이름은 기억나지 않는 어느 강 어귀 근처의 작은 만에 닻을 내렸다. 사람들 말로는 그 강이 리머릭에서 내려온다 했고, 아일랜드에서 가장 큰 강이라고 했다.\n\n여기서 나쁜 날씨에 한동안 발이 묶이게 되자, 처음과 똑같이 친절하고 너그러운 사람이던 선장이 다시 우리 둘을 데리고 뭍에 올랐다. 그가 이제 이렇게 한 것은 실로 내 남편에 대한 친절에서였으니, 남편은 바다를 몹시 견디지 못해 무척이나 멀미를 했고, 특히 그렇게 바람이 거세게 불 때는 더했다. 여기서 우리는 다시 신선한 식량을, 특히 소고기, 돼지고기, 양고기, 닭을 한 밑천 사들였고, 선장은 배의 저장 식량을 늘리려고 소고기 대여섯 통을 절여 두느라 머물렀다. 우리는 여기서 닷새도 채 있지 않았는데, 날씨가 온화해지고 순풍이 불자 다시 출항했고, 마흔이틀 만에 무사히 버지니아 해안에 다다랐다.\n\n우리가 해안에 가까워졌을 때, 선장이 나를 불러서, 내 말투로 보아 내게 그곳에 친척이 좀 있고 또 전에도 거기 와 본 적이 있는 듯하니, 중범죄자가 도착했을 때 그들을 처분하는 관습을 내가 알고 있으리라 짐작한다고 말했다. 나는 모른다고 했고, 그곳에 내게 어떤 친척이 있든, 내가 죄수의 처지에 있는 동안에는 그 누구에게도 나를 알리지 않으리라는 것만은 그가 분명히 믿어도 좋으며, 나머지에 관해서는 그가 우리에게 약속해 준 대로 우리를 도와주는 것을 그에게 전적으로 맡기겠다고 했다. 그는 내게, 우리를 종으로 사러 와서, 만약 그 나라 총독이 우리를 요구하면 우리에 대해 책임져 줄 누군가를 그곳에서 구해야 한다고 말했다. 나는 그가 지시하는 대로 하겠다고 했다. 그래서 그는 어느 농장주를 데려와, 이를테면 이 두 종, 곧 내 남편과 나를 사들이는 일을 그와 흥정하게 했고, 거기서 우리는 격식대로 그에게 팔려 그와 함께 뭍에 올랐다. 선장이 우리와 함께 가서, 우리를 어떤 집으로 데려갔는데, 그곳을 술집이라 불러야 할지 어떨지는 모르겠으나, 우리는 거기서 럼주 따위로 만든 펀치 한 사발을 마시고 매우 흥겹게 지냈다. 얼마쯤 지난 뒤 그 농장주가 우리에게 방면 증서와, 우리가 자기를 충실히 섬겼다는 확인서를 주었고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 그에게서 풀려나 어디든 가고 싶은 곳으로 갈 수 있었다.\n\n이 일을 봐준 대가로 선장은 우리에게 담배 육천 무게를 요구했는데, 그가 자기 화주에게 그것을 책임져야 한다고 했고, 우리는 그것을 곧바로 사서 그에게 주었으며, 그 밖에 이십 기니를 선물로 안겼으니, 그는 그것으로 더없이 만족스러워했다.\n\n여러 까닭으로, 우리가 버지니아 식민지의 어느 지역에 정착했는지 그 세세한 사정을 여기 적어 넣는 것은 적절치 않다. 다만, 배가 그리로 향하던 까닭에 우리가 큰 강 포토맥강으로 들어갔다는 것만 언급하면 충분하겠다. 그리고 거기서 우리는 처음에 정착하려 했으나, 나중에 마음을 바꾸었다.\n\n우리의 모든 물건을 뭍에 내려, 우리가 상륙한 작은 고장이랄까 마을에서 거처와 함께 빌린 창고, 곧 물품 보관소에 넣어 둔 뒤에 내가 한 첫 번째 중요한 일은, 내 어머니와, 그리고 (앞서 길게 이야기했듯이 내가 남편으로 결혼했던 그 운명의 사람인) 내 오라비의 행방을 수소문하는 것이었다. 조금 알아보니, 이런저런 부인, 곧 내 어머니가 돌아가셨고, 내 오라비(혹은 남편)는 살아 있다는 정보를 얻었는데, 솔직히 그 소식을 듣고 나는 그리 기쁘지 않았다. 그러나 더 나쁜 것은, 그가 예전에 살던, 그리고 내가 그와 함께 살던 농장에서 옮겨, 우리가 상륙해 창고를 빌린 바로 그 자리 근처의 농장에서 자기 아들 중 하나와 살고 있다는 것을 알게 된 일이었다.\n\n나는 처음에는 조금 놀랐으나, 그가 나를 알아볼 수 없으리라고 스스로 마음을 다잡고 나니, 완전히 마음이 놓였을 뿐 아니라, 그가 나를 보지 않고서 그럴 수만 있다면 그를 보고 싶은 마음이 간절해졌다. 그러기 위해 나는 수소문하여 그가 사는 농장을 알아냈고, 우리가 가정부라 부르는 따위로 나를 도와줄 사람으로 구한, 그 고장의 어느 여자와 함께, 마치 그저 그 시골을 구경하며 둘러보고 싶은 양 그 자리 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 다녔다. 마침내 나는 그 살림집이 보일 만큼 가까이 다가갔다. 나는 그 여자에게 저것이 누구의 농장이냐고 물었고, 그녀는 어떤 사람의 것이라고 하더니, 우리 오른쪽을 조금 내다보며 \"저기,\" 하고 말했다. \"저분이 저 농장의 주인이고, 함께 있는 분은 그의 아버지예요.\" \"그분들의 세례명은 무엇인가요?\" 내가 말했다. \"그 노신사분의 이름은 모르겠어요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"하지만 아들의 이름은 험프리예요. 그리고 아버지도 그럴 거예요,\" 그녀가 말했다. 이 일로 인해 얼마나 어지러운 기쁨과 두려움이 뒤섞여 내 생각을 사로잡았는지는, 여러분이 짐작할 수 있다면 짐작해 보시라. 나는 그녀가 가리킨 그 아버지로 보아, 이가 다름 아닌 내 친아들임을 곧바로 알아차렸으니, 그 아버지가 내 친오라비였기 때문이다. 나는 가면이 없었으나, 두건을 얼굴 둘레로 잔뜩 헝클어 두르고는, 이십 년 넘게 떨어져 있었던 데다 세상의 그 지역에서 나에 관한 어떤 것도 예상하지 못할 그가 나를 전혀 알아보지 못하리라 믿었다. 그러나 나는 그 모든 조심을 다 할 필요가 없었으니, 그 노신사는 눈에 어떤 병이 생겨 침침해진 터라, 나무에 부딪히거나 도랑에 빠지지 않고 돌아다닐 수 있을 만큼만 겨우 보일 뿐이었다. 함께 있던 여자가 그저 우연히, 그것이 내게 얼마나 중요한 일인지는 전혀 모르고 그렇게 말해 준 것이었다. 그들이 우리에게 가까이 다가오자, 나는 \"그분이 당신을 알아보나요, 오언 부인?\" 하고 물었다(사람들이 그 여자를 그렇게 불렀다). \"네,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 말하는 걸 들으면 저를 알아보세요. 하지만 저나 다른 누구를 알아볼 만큼 잘 보지는 못하시죠.\" 그러고는 내가 이야기한 대로 그의 시력에 관한 사연을 들려주었다. 이것이 나를 안심시켰고, 그래서 나는 다시 두건을 젖히고는 그들이 내 곁을 지나가게 두었다. 한 어머니가 이렇게 자기 친아들을, 번창하는 형편의 잘생기고 훤칠한 젊은 신사를 보면서도, 감히 자신을 그에게 알리지 못하고, 감히 그를 아는 체하지 못하는 것은 비참한 일이었다. 이 글을 읽는 자식 둔 어머니라면 누구든 이를 헤아려, 내가 어떤 마음의 고뇌로 나 자신을 억눌렀는지, 그를 끌어안고 그 위에 눈물 흘리고 싶은 어떤 영혼의 갈망이 내 안에 있었는지, 또 어떻게 내 온 오장이 안에서 뒤집히고, 바로 그 창자가 움직이는 듯했으며, 어쩔 줄을 몰랐는지를 그저 생각해 보시라. 지금도 나는 그 고통을 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠다! 그가 내게서 멀어져 갈 때 나는 응시하며 떨면서 서서, 보일 수 있는 한 멀리까지 그를 바라보았고, 그러고는 쉬려고 주저앉되, 그녀에게서 돌아앉아 얼굴을 땅에 대고 엎드려 울며, 그가 발을 디뎠던 땅에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n나는 내 흐트러진 모습을 그 여자에게 충분히 감출 수 없어서 그녀가 알아차렸고, 내가 몸이 좋지 않은 줄로 여겼는데, 나는 그것이 사실인 양 꾸며 댈 수밖에 없었다. 그러자 그녀는 땅이 축축하고 위험하다며 일어나라고 재촉했고, 나는 그에 따라 일어나 걸어갔다.\n\n다시 돌아가면서 여전히 이 신사와 그 아들에 관해 이야기하던 중에, 새로운 우울거리가 이렇게 떠올랐다. 그 여자가, 마치 나를 기분 전환시켜 주려고 이야기 하나를 들려주려는 양 입을 열었다. \"저기,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 신사분이 예전에 살던 곳의 이웃들 사이에 아주 묘한 이야기가 돌고 있어요.\" \"그게 무엇인가요?\" 내가 말했다. \"글쎄요,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저 노신사분이 젊었을 때 잉글랜드에 갔다가, 거기서 어느 젊은 부인과 사랑에 빠졌대요. 일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 아리따운 여자 중 하나였다지요. 그래서 그 여자와 결혼해서, 그때 살아 계시던 자기 어머니에게로 이리 데려왔어요. 그는 그 여자와 여기서 여러 해를 살았고,\" 그녀가 이어 말했다. \"그 여자에게서 여러 아이를 얻었는데, 지금 그와 함께 있던 그 젊은 신사도 그중 하나예요. 그런데 얼마 뒤, 그의 어머니인 그 노부인이 며느리에게, 그녀가 잉글랜드에 있었을 때의 자신에 관한 일과, 꽤나 나빴던 잉글랜드에서의 자기 처지에 관한 무언가를 이야기하다가, 며느리가 무척이나 놀라고 불안해하기 시작했대요. 한마디로, 일을 더 캐물어 보니, 그 노부인이 바로 자기 친어머니이고, 따라서 그 아들이 자기 아내의 친오라비라는 것이 의심의 여지 없이 드러났던 거예요. 그것이 온 집안을 공포에 빠뜨리고, 그들을 그런 혼란에 몰아넣어, 하마터면 그들 모두를 파멸시킬 뻔했지요. 그 젊은 여자는 그와 함께 살려 하지 않았고, 그녀의 오라비이자 남편인 그 아들은 한동안 미쳐 버렸으며, 끝내 그 젊은 여자는 잉글랜드로 떠나서, 그 뒤로 소식이 끊겼답니다.\"\n\n내가 이 이야기에 이상하리만치 동요했으리라는 것은 쉽게 믿어질 터이나, 내 동요의 본질을 묘사하기란 불가능하다. 나는 그 이야기에 놀란 척하며, 그녀에게 세세한 정황에 관해 수천 가지 질문을 던졌는데, 그녀가 그것을 속속들이 알고 있음을 알게 되었다. 마침내 나는 그 집안의 정황을 캐묻기 시작했으니, 그 노부인, 곧 내 어머니가 어떻게 돌아가셨는지, 가진 것을 어떻게 남기셨는지를 물었다. 내 어머니는 자신이 죽을 때 나를 위해 무언가를 해 두겠노라고, 그래서 내가 살아 있다면 어떤 식으로든 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있도록, 그녀의 아들, 곧 내 오라비이자 남편이 그것을 막을 힘이 없도록 그렇게 남겨 두겠노라고 내게 매우 엄숙하게 약속한 터였기 때문이다. 그녀는 그것이 정확히 어떻게 처리되었는지는 모르겠으나, 내 어머니가 돈을 한몫 남기고, 그 지급을 위해 자기 농장을 담보로 묶어 두어, 잉글랜드에서든 다른 어디에서든 그 딸의 소식을 듣게 되면 딸에게 틀림없이 건네지도록 했다고 들었으며, 그 신탁이 이 아들에게 맡겨졌는데, 그가 바로 우리가 그 아버지와 함께 본 그 사람이라고 했다.\n\n이는 가벼이 여기기에는 내게 너무도 좋은 소식이어서, 여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 내가 어떤 길을 택해야 할지, 어떻게, 언제, 무슨 방식으로 나 자신을 알릴지, 아니 도대체 나 자신을 알리기는 할지 어떨지, 수천 가지 생각으로 내 마음을 가득 채웠다.\n\n여기 실로 나 자신이 헤쳐 나갈 재간이 없는 난처함이 있었으니, 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지도 알지 못했다. 그것은 밤낮으로 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다. 나는 잠을 잘 수도, 이야기를 나눌 수도 없었으니, 그래서 남편이 그것을 알아차리고 내게 무슨 일인지 의아해하며 나를 기분 전환시키려 애썼으나, 모두 헛수고였다. 그는 내게 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는지 말하라고 다그쳤지만, 나는 그것을 미루다가, 마침내 그가 끊임없이 졸라 대는 통에, 그래도 분명한 진실을 바탕에 깔 수 있는 이야기 하나를 지어낼 수밖에 없었다. 나는 그에게, 우리가 거처를 옮기고 정착 계획을 바꿔야 한다는 것을 알게 되어 괴롭다고 말했으니, 내가 그 지역에 머무른다면 알려지게 되리라는 것을 알았기 때문이라고 했다. 내 어머니가 돌아가신 까닭에 내 친척 여럿이 우리가 그때 있던 지역으로 들어왔으므로, 나는 그들에게 나 자신을 드러내든지—이는 여러 까닭으로 우리의 지금 처지에서 적절치 않았다—아니면 옮겨 가야 하는데, 어느 쪽을 해야 할지 모르겠으며, 바로 이것이 나를 그토록 우울하고 그토록 수심에 잠기게 한다고 했다.\n\n그는, 우리가 그때 처한 그 정황에서 내가 누구에게든 나 자신을 알리는 것은 결코 적절치 않다는 점에서 나와 뜻을 같이했다. 그러므로 그는 내게, 내가 좋다고 여긴다면 그 나라의 다른 어느 지역으로든, 심지어 다른 어느 나라로든 기꺼이 옮겨 가겠다고 말했다. 그러나 이제 나는 또 다른 어려움에 부딪혔으니, 곧 내가 다른 어느 식민지로든 옮겨 가면, 내 어머니가 남긴 그 재산을 마땅히 찾아볼 길에서 영영 벗어나게 된다는 것이었다. 또한 나는 내 옛 결혼의 비밀을 새 남편에게 털어놓을 생각은 도무지 할 수조차 없었으니, 그것은 내 생각에 입에 올릴 만한 이야기가 아니었고, 그 결과가 어찌 될지도 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 지금 누구이며 지난날 누구였는지를 온 나라에 공공연히 드러내지 않고서는 그 일의 밑바닥을 캐는 것이 불가능했다.\n\n이런 난처함 속에서 나는 오랫동안 지냈고, 이것이 내 남편을 무척 불안하게 했으니, 그는 내가 난처해하는 것을 알면서도, 내가 자기에게 솔직하지 않고 내 고민의 구석구석을 다 알려 주지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 종종, 무엇이 되었든, 특히 그것이 괴롭고 고통스러운 일이라면, 내가 자기를 믿고 털어놓지 않는데 자기가 무슨 잘못을 했는지 의아하다고 말하곤 했다. 사실 그는 모든 것을 믿고 털어놓을 만한 사람이었으니, 세상의 어떤 남자도 아내에게서 그보다 더 나은 대접을 받을 자격이 없었을 터이기 때문이다. 그러나 이는 내가 그에게 어떻게 털어놓아야 할지 모르는 일이었고, 그러면서도 그 일부나마 털어놓을 사람이 아무도 없으니, 그 짐은 내 마음에 너무도 무거웠다. 우리 여자들이 비밀을 지키지 못한다고 사람들이 무어라 떠들든, 내 삶은 그 반대의 분명한 증거이지만, 우리 여자의 일이든 남자의 일이든, 중대한 비밀에는 언제나 그것의 기쁨이든 슬픔이든, 어느 쪽이든 그것을 나눌 수 있는 속내 털어놓을 이가, 가슴을 터놓을 벗이 있어야 하니, 그러지 않으면 그것은 정신에 이중의 무게가 되고, 어쩌면 그 자체로 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 버린다. 이것의 진실에 관해서는 모든 인간의 증언에 호소하는 바이다.\n\n그리고 이것이 바로, 여자뿐 아니라 남자도, 다른 면에서는 가장 위대하고 훌륭한 자질을 지닌 남자조차도, 여러 번 이 점에서 자신이 약함을 깨닫고, 비밀스러운 기쁨이나 비밀스러운 슬픔의 무게를 견디지 못해, 그저 자기 속을 토해 내고, 그에 따르는 짐과 무게에 짓눌린 마음을 풀어 놓기 위해서라도 그것을 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 까닭이다. 그리고 이것은 결코 어리석음이나 경솔함의 표시가 아니라, 그 일의 자연스러운 귀결이었다. 그런 사람들은, 그 짓눌림과 더 오래 싸웠더라면, 필시 잠결에 그것을 말해 버려, 그것이 아무리 치명적인 성질의 것이라 해도, 또 그것이 드러날 상대가 누구인지도 아랑곳없이, 그 비밀을 털어놓고 말았을 것이다. 이 본성의 필연은, 어떤 극악무도한 악행, 특히 은밀한 살인을 저지른 자들의 마음속에서 때로 그토록 격렬하게 작용하는 것이어서, 그 결과가 필연적으로 자신의 파멸이 되리라는 것을 알면서도 그것을 드러낼 수밖에 없게 만든다. 그런데, 그 모든 발각과 고백의 영광이 신의 정의에 돌아가야 한다는 것이 참일지라도, 통상 본성의 손을 빌려 일하는 섭리가 여기서 바로 그 자연적 원인을 이용해 그런 비범한 결과를 빚어낸다는 것 또한 그만큼 확실하다.\n\n나는 범죄와, 또 범죄자들과 오래 어울리며 이에 관한 주목할 만한 사례를 여럿 들 수 있다. 내가 뉴게이트 감옥에 있을 때, 그때 사람들이 야간 비행자라 부르던 자들 가운데 하나인 어느 사내를 알았다. 그 뒤로 그것을 무슨 다른 말로 이해해 왔는지는 모르겠으나, 그는 묵인 아래 매일 저녁 밖으로 나가도록 허락받은 자로, 그때 자기 못된 짓을 저지르고는, 사람들이 도둑잡이라 부르는 그 정직한 자들에게, 이튿날 알아내어 보상을 받고 전날 저녁에 도둑맞은 것을 되찾아 줄 일거리를 마련해 주었다. 이 사내는, 마치 깨어 있을 때 그것을 말하기로 약속이라도 한 듯이, 또 거기에 아무 해도 위험도 없는 듯이, 잠결에 자기가 한 모든 일과 자기가 밟은 모든 단계, 무엇을 어디서 훔쳤는지를 어김없이 말해 버렸다. 그러므로 그는 밖에 다녀온 뒤에는, 아무도 그가 하는 말을 듣지 못하도록, 스스로를 가두거나, 그를 삯 받고 맡은 간수 중 누군가에게 가두어지게 할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 만약 그가 자기 떠돌이질과 성과의 전말을 어느 동무에게든, 어느 도둑 형제에게든, 혹은 정당히 그렇게 부를 만한 그의 고용주들에게든 낱낱이 털어놓고 죄다 이야기하고 나면, 그에게는 모든 것이 무사했고, 그는 다른 사람들처럼 조용히 잤다.\n\n내 삶에 관한 이 기록을 출간하는 것은 그 모든 부분의 올바른 교훈을 위해서이고, 모든 독자에게 가르침과 경계와 경고와 향상을 주기 위해서이므로, 어떤 사람들이 자기 자신의 일이든 남의 일이든 가장 큰 비밀을 털어놓을 수밖에 없게 되는 것에 관한 이 이야기도, 바라건대 불필요한 여담으로 치부되지는 않을 것이다.\n\n내 마음을 짓누르는 이 무게의 확실한 짓눌림 아래, 나는 앞서 말한 그 일로 괴로워했다. 그리고 그에 대해 내가 찾은 유일한 위안은, 우리가 세상의 다른 어느 지역에 정착할 것을 생각해야 할 필요가 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기에 충분하리라 여겨지는 만큼만 그것을 남편에게 털어놓는 것이었다. 그래서 우리 앞에 놓인 다음 고려 사항은, 잉글랜드 정착지 가운데 어느 지역으로 갈 것인가였다. 내 남편은 그 나라에 완전히 낯선 자라, 여러 고장의 위치에 관해 지리적 지식조차 아직 없었다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 이 글을 쓰기 전까지 지리적이라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지도 몰랐던 터라, 여러 곳에서 오거나 여러 곳으로 가는 사람들과 오래 이야기를 나눈 데서 얻은 일반적인 지식밖에 없었다. 그러나 이것만은 알았으니, 메릴랜드, 펜실베이니아, 동서 저지, 뉴욕, 뉴잉글랜드가 모두 버지니아 북쪽에 있고, 따라서 모두 더 추운 기후라는 것이었는데, 바로 그 까닭으로 나는 그곳들이 싫었다. 나는 본디 따뜻한 날씨를 좋아한 데다, 이제 나이가 들수록 추운 기후를 피하고 싶은 마음이 더 강해진 까닭이었다. 그래서 나는 캐롤라이나로 가는 것을 고려했으니, 그곳은 아메리카 대륙에 있는 잉글랜드인의 유일한 남쪽 식민지였고, 나는 이리로 가기를 제안했다. 더욱이 그곳에서라면 내 어머니의 재산을 알아보고, 그것을 요구할 만큼 충분히 나 자신을 알리는 것이 적절할 때, 언제든 매우 손쉽게 거기서 올 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n이 결심으로 나는 남편에게, 우리가 있던 곳을 떠나, 우리의 모든 재산을 가지고 캐롤라이나로 가서 거기에 정착하자고 제안했다. 남편은 첫 번째 부분, 곧 우리가 있던 곳에 머무르는 것이 전혀 적절치 않다는 것에 선뜻 동의했으니, 내가 그에게 우리가 거기서 알려지게 될 것이라고 장담한 까닭이었고, 나머지는 그에게 완전히 숨겼다.\n\n그러나 이제 나는 새로운 어려움에 부딪힌 것을 깨달았다. 그 주된 일이 여전히 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀으니, 내 어머니가 나를 위해 해 둔 것이라는 그 큰일을 어떤 식으로든 알아보지 않고서는 그 나라를 떠날 생각을 할 수 없었다. 또 나는 내 옛 남편(오라비)이나, 내 자식인 그의 아들에게 나 자신을 알리지 않은 채 떠난다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수 없었다. 다만 나는 내 새 남편이 그것을 전혀 알지 못하게, 또 그들이 그를 전혀 알지 못하게, 그리고 내게 남편이라는 것이 있다는 것조차 알지 못하게 이 일을 하고 싶었다.\n\n나는 이것이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있을지 머릿속으로 수없이 많은 길을 궁리했다. 나는 기꺼이 남편을 우리 모든 물건과 함께 캐롤라이나로 먼저 보내고, 나 자신은 뒤따라가고 싶었으나, 이것은 실행 불가능한 일이었다. 그는 그 나라에도, 거기서든 다른 어디서든 정착하는 방법에도 완전히 낯선 자라, 나 없이는 결코 움직이려 하지 않을 터였다. 그다음에 나는, 우리 둘이 먼저 물건의 일부를 가지고 함께 가고, 정착하고 나면 내가 버지니아로 돌아와 나머지를 가져오면 되겠다고 생각했으나, 그때도 그가 결코 나와 떨어져, 혼자 일을 꾸려 나가도록 거기 남겨지지 않으리라는 것을 알았다. 사정은 분명했다. 그는 신사로 자란지라, 따라서 일에 낯설 뿐 아니라 게을렀고, 우리가 정착한다 해도, 그곳에서 사냥이라 부르는 것, 곧 본디 인디언들의 일이며 그들이 종으로서 하는 그 일을 하려고 총을 들고 숲으로 나가기를 훨씬 더 좋아할 터였다. 다시 말해, 그는 자기 농장의 마땅한 일을 돌보기보다 그러기를 더 좋아할 것이었다.\n\n그러므로 이것들은 극복할 수 없는 어려움이었고, 나는 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이었다. 나는 한때 내 남편이었던 내 오라비에게 나 자신을 드러내는 것에 관해 마음에 그토록 강한 인상을 받아, 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 더욱이, 그가 살아 있는 동안 그것을 하지 않으면, 나중에 내가 정말로 바로 그 사람이며 그의 어머니라는 것을 내 아들에게 납득시키려 해 봐야 헛수고일지 모르고, 그리하여 그 혈연의 도움과 위안도, 내 어머니가 내게 남긴 것이 무엇이든 그 혜택도 둘 다 잃을지 모른다는 생각이 끊임없이 내 머릿속을 맴돌았기 때문이다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 나는 내가 처한 정황에서, 곧 내게 남편이 있다는 것에 관해서나, 내가 범죄자로서 합법적 유배에 의해 끌려왔다는 것에 관해서나, 그들에게 나 자신을 드러내는 것이 적절하다고는 결코 생각할 수 없었다. 이 두 가지 까닭으로, 내가 있던 곳에서 옮겨 가서, 다른 곳에서 다른 모습으로 다시 그에게 오는 것이 내게는 절대적으로 필요했다.\n\n이러한 고려에서, 나는 남편에게, 우리가 포토맥강에 정착해서는 안 될 절대적인 필요가 있다는 것, 적어도 거기서는 우리가 곧 공공연히 알려지게 되리라는 것을 계속 이야기했다. 반면에 우리가 세상의 다른 어느 곳으로 가면, 우리는 농사지으러 온 어느 집안 못지않은 평판을 가지고 들어가게 될 것이라고 했다. 살림을 가지고 와서 농장을 사들이든 새로 시작하든 농사지으러 자기들 틈에 들어오는 집안을 갖는 것은 늘 주민들에게 반가운 일이므로, 우리는 우리 정황이 발각될 가능성 없이, 친절하고 반가운 영접을 분명히 받게 되리라고 했다.\n\n나는 또 그에게 대략, 우리가 있던 곳에 내 친척이 여럿 있는데, 그들이 곧 내가 건너온 계기와 까닭을 알게 되어 나를 더없이 욕보이게 될 터이므로 이제 그들에게 나 자신을 알릴 엄두를 낼 수 없으며, 또 여기서 돌아가신 내 어머니가 내게 무언가를, 어쩌면 상당한 것을 남기셨으리라 믿을 만한 까닭이 있으니, 그것을 알아볼 만한 가치가 충분할지 모른다고 말했다. 그러나 이것 또한 우리가 여기를 떠나지 않고서는 우리를 공공연히 드러내지 않고서는 할 수 없는 일이며, 그런 뒤에 우리가 어디에 정착하든, 나는 이를테면 내 오라비와 조카들을 방문하고 만나러 와서, 그들에게 나 자신을 알리고, 내 몫인 것을 청구하고 알아보며, 존중받으면서 동시에 흔쾌히, 선의로 정의를 받을 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 반면에 만약 지금 그것을 한다면, 나는 힘으로 그것을 강요하고, 저주와 마지못함과 온갖 종류의 모욕 속에서 그것을 받는 따위의 곤경 외에는 아무것도 기대할 수 없으며, 그는 어쩌면 그 꼴을 차마 보지 못할 것이라고 했다. 또 내가 정말로 그녀의 딸이라는 것을 법적으로 입증해야 하는 경우, 나는 갈피를 잡지 못하고 잉글랜드에 의지할 수밖에 없게 되며, 어쩌면 끝내 실패하여, 그것이 무엇이든 그것을 잃게 될지 모른다고 했다. 이러한 논거로, 그리고 이렇게 그에게 필요한 만큼 그 비밀의 전모를 알린 뒤, 우리는 다른 어느 식민지에서 정착지를 찾으러 가기로 결심했고, 첫 생각에 캐롤라이나가 우리가 정한 곳이었다.\n\n이를 위해 우리는 캐롤라이나로 가는 배를 수소문하기 시작했고, 머지않아, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 만 건너편, 곧 메릴랜드에, 쌀과 그 밖의 물건을 싣고 캐롤라이나에서 온 배가 한 척 있는데, 다시 그리로 돌아갔다가 거기서 식량을 싣고 자메이카로 가려 한다는 정보를 얻었다. 이 소식을 듣고 우리는 우리 물건을 실을 외돛배 한 척을 빌려, 이를테면 포토맥강에 마지막 작별을 고하고는, 우리의 모든 화물과 함께 메릴랜드로 건너갔다.\n\n이것은 길고 불쾌한 항해였고, 내 남편은 잉글랜드에서 온 항해 전체보다 자기에게는 더 고약했다고 말했으니, 날씨가 시원찮고, 물결이 거칠고, 배가 작고 불편했기 때문이다. 다음으로, 우리는 포토맥강을 백 마일이나 거슬러 올라간, 웨스트모얼랜드 카운티라 부르는 지역에 있었는데, 그 강은 버지니아에서 단연 가장 큰 강이고, 내가 듣기로는 곧장 바다로 흘러드는 것이 아니라 다른 강으로 흘러드는 강 중에서 세계에서 가장 큰 강이라 하니, 그래서 우리는 그 강에서 험한 날씨를 만나 자주 큰 위험에 처했다. 우리가 한가운데 있으면서도 몇 리그씩이나 양쪽 어느 편의 뭍도 볼 수 없었기 때문이다. 그다음에는 큰 강 혹은 만인 체서피크를 건너야 했는데, 그곳은 포토맥강이 흘러드는 데로, 폭이 거의 삼십 마일이었고, 우리는 이름도 모르는 더 큰 망망대해로 들어섰으니, 그리하여 우리의 항해는 보잘것없는 형편없는 외돛배에 우리의 온 보물을 싣고 꼬박 이백 마일에 이르렀던 것이다. 우리에게 어떤 사고라도 일어났더라면, 끝내 우리는 매우 비참해졌을지 모른다. 우리가 물건을 잃고 목숨만 건졌다고 치면, 그러고 나서 헐벗고 빈털터리로, 세상의 그 지역에 벗이나 아는 이 하나 없이, 거칠고 낯선 곳에 버려졌을 터이기 때문이다. 위험이 지나간 지금조차, 그 생각만 해도 나는 얼마간 끔찍하다.\n\n자, 우리는 닷새 항해 끝에 그곳에 다다랐으니, 그곳을 필립스포인트라 부르는 듯하다. 그런데 보라, 우리가 거기 도착했을 때, 캐롤라이나행 배는 짐을 싣고 떠나 버린 지 사흘밖에 안 된 터였다. 이것은 실망스러운 일이었다. 그러나 그래도 무엇에도 좌절하지 않을 나는 남편에게, 우리가 캐롤라이나행 배편을 얻을 수 없는 데다, 우리가 있는 그 고장이 매우 비옥하고 좋으니, 그가 좋다면 우리가 있는 데서 우리 형편에 맞는 무언가를 찾아낼 수 있을지 알아보고, 그가 마음에 든다면 여기에 정착하자고 말했다.\n\n우리는 곧바로 뭍에 올랐으나, 바로 그 자리에서는 우리가 뭍에 있을 편의도, 우리 물건을 뭍에 보존할 편의도 찾지 못했고, 다만 거기서 만난 매우 정직한 퀘이커 한 사람의 안내로, 동쪽으로 육십 마일쯤 떨어진, 다시 말해 만 어귀에 더 가까운 어느 곳으로 가라는 말을 들었다. 그가 거기 산다며, 거기서라면 우리가 농사짓든, 아니면 더 편리할지 모를 다른 농사 자리를 기다리든, 편의를 얻으리라고 했다. 그가 우리를 그토록 친절하고 꾸밈없는 정직함으로 초대한지라, 우리는 가기로 했고, 그 퀘이커가 직접 우리와 함께 갔다.\n\n여기서 우리는 종 둘을 샀으니, 곧 리버풀 배에서 막 뭍에 내린 잉글랜드인 하녀 하나와 흑인 사내종 하나였는데, 그 나라에 정착하려는 모든 사람에게 절대적으로 필요한 것이었다. 이 정직한 퀘이커는 우리에게 매우 도움이 되었으니, 우리가 그가 제안한 곳에 다다르자, 우리 물건을 둘 편리한 창고와, 우리 자신과 우리 종들을 위한 숙소를 찾아 주었다. 그리고 그 후 두 달쯤 지나, 그의 안내에 따라, 우리는 우리 농장을 일굴 요량으로 그 나라 총독에게서 큰 땅 한 뙈기를 받았으며, 그리하여 우리는 캐롤라이나로 갈 생각을 완전히 접었다. 여기서 우리는 매우 잘 영접받았고, 우리가 일을 채비하고, 땅을 충분히 개간하고, 집 지을 목재와 자재를 마련할 때까지 편리한 숙소를 얻었으니, 이 모든 것을 우리는 퀘이커의 안내에 따라 처리했다. 그리하여 일 년 사이에 우리는 거의 오십 에이커의 땅을 개간했고, 그 일부에 울타리를 두르고, 일부에는 담배를 심었으니, 많지는 않았다. 게다가 우리는 종들에게 뿌리채소와 푸성귀와 빵을 대주는 데 보탬이 될 만큼의 채마밭과 곡식도 두었다.\n\n그리고 이제 나는 남편을 설득해 다시 만을 건너가 내 친구들의 행방을 알아보러 가게 해 달라고 했다. 그는 이제 그것에 더 기꺼이 동의했으니, 그를 흥겹게 해 줄 그의 총—거기서 사냥이라 부르며 그가 무척 즐기던 것—말고도, 그를 충분히 바쁘게 할 일거리가 그의 손에 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 실로 우리는 때때로 무척이나 흐뭇하게 서로를 바라보며, 그것이 뉴게이트보다 나을 뿐 아니라, 우리 둘 다 벌이던 그 못된 장사에서 가장 번창했던 우리 형편보다도 얼마나 더 나은지를 곱씹곤 했다. 우리 일은 매우 좋은 상태에 있었다. 우리는 식민지의 소유주들에게서, 현금으로 치른 삼십오 파운드로, 종 오십에서 육십 명을 부릴 만한 충분한 농장이 될 만큼의 땅을 사들였는데, 그 땅을 잘 일구면 우리 둘 중 누구든 살아 있는 한 우리에게 충분할 터였다. 그리고 자식으로 말하자면, 나는 그런 것에 대한 가망은 이미 지난 나이였다.\n\n그러나 우리의 행운은 여기서 끝나지 않았다. 나는 앞서 말한 대로 만을 건너, 한때 남편이었던 내 오라비가 사는 곳으로 갔다. 그러나 전에 갔던 그 마을로 가지 않고, 포토맥강 동쪽에 있는, 래퍼해녹강이라 부르는 또 다른 큰 강을 거슬러 올라갔으니, 이렇게 해서 그의 농장 뒤편으로 나오게 되었는데, 그 농장은 컸고, 래퍼해녹으로 흘러드는 항행 가능한 샛강, 곧 작은 강의 도움으로, 나는 그것에 매우 가까이 다가갔다.\n\n나는 이제 내 오라비(남편)에게 곧장 정면으로 올라가서, 내가 누구인지 그에게 말하기로 완전히 결심했다. 그러나 내가 그를 어떤 기분으로 마주하게 될지, 아니 그런 무모한 방문으로 내가 그를 얼마나 언짢게 만들지 알 수 없었으므로, 나는 먼저 그에게 편지를 써서, 내가 누구인지, 그리고 내가 옛 혈연을 들어—그것은 완전히 잊혔기를 바란다고—그에게 어떤 폐를 끼치러 온 것이 아니라, 우리 어머니가 돌아가실 때 내 부양을 위해 남겨 둔 그 재산의 일로 그의 도움을 청하며 누이가 오라비에게 하듯 호소하는 것이라고 알리기로 결심했으니, 더욱이 내가 그것을 알아보러 이토록 멀리까지 온 것을 생각하면, 그가 그 일에서 내게 마땅히 정당하게 대해 주리라는 것을 의심치 않았다.\n\n나는 편지에 그의 아들에 관해 매우 다정하고 친절한 말을 좀 적었으니, 그에게, 그 아이가 내 친자식임을 그가 안다는 것과, 또 내가 그를 남편으로 맞은 데 죄가 없는 것이 그가 나를 아내로 맞은 데 죄가 없는 것과 마찬가지이며, 그때 우리 둘 다 서로가 도무지 혈연 사이임을 알지 못했으므로, 그러니 단 하나뿐인 내 자식을 한 번이라도 보고 싶은 더없이 애틋한 소망과, 나에 관해 이렇다 저렇다 어떤 생각도 결코 품을 수 없었던 그에게 격렬한 애정을 간직하는, 어미로서의 어떤 연약함을 보이는 것을 그가 허락해 주기를 바란다고 말했다.\n\n나는 그가 이 편지를 받으면, 그것을 곧바로 아들에게 읽으라고 주리라 믿었으니, 그의 눈이 너무 침침해 그것을 읽을 수 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 일은 그보다 더 잘 풀렸으니, 그의 시력이 침침했던 까닭에 그는 자기 앞으로 온 모든 편지를 아들이 열어 보도록 허락해 둔 터였고, 내 심부름꾼이 왔을 때 그 노신사가 집에 없거나 자리를 비운 까닭에, 내 편지가 곧장 내 아들의 손에 들어가, 그가 그것을 열어 읽었다.\n\n그는 잠시 머문 뒤 심부름꾼을 불러들여, 그에게 그 편지를 준 사람이 어디 있는지 물었다. 심부름꾼이 그 장소를 일러 주었는데, 칠 마일쯤 떨어진 곳이었다. 그래서 그는 그에게 기다리라 하고, 말 한 필과 종 둘을 채비시키더니, 심부름꾼과 함께 내게로 왔다. 내 심부름꾼이 돌아와, 노신사는 집에 없으나 그 아들이 함께 와서 막 내게로 올라오는 중이라고 말했을 때, 내가 어떤 경악에 빠졌을지 누구든 헤아려 보시라. 나는 그것이 화평인지 전쟁인지 알 수 없어 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐고, 어떻게 처신해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 나는 생각할 겨를이 아주 잠깐밖에 없었으니, 내 아들이 심부름꾼의 바로 뒤를 따라와, 내 숙소로 올라오면서 문간에 있던 자에게 무언가를 물었기 때문이다. 짐작건대, 그것은 누가 그를 보냈는가 하는 그 부인이 누구냐는 것이었으리라. 내가 알아들을 만큼 똑똑히 듣지는 못했으나 말이다. 심부름꾼이 \"저기 계십니다, 나리\" 하고 말했고, 그러자 그는 곧장 내게로 올라와, 내게 입을 맞추고, 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았으며, 어찌나 격정에 차서 나를 부둥켜안았는지 말을 잇지 못했다. 다만 나는 그의 가슴이 마치, 울다가 흐느끼되 끝내 울음을 터뜨리지 못하는 아이처럼 들썩이며 두근거리는 것을 느낄 수 있었다.\n\n내가 알아차렸을 때 내 영혼을 사무치게 건드린 그 기쁨을 나는 표현할 수도 묘사할 수도 없으니, 그 점만은 쉽게 알아차릴 수 있었거니와, 그가 낯선 자로서가 아니라 어머니에게 오는 아들로서, 실로 자기 친어머니가 어떤 분인지 전에는 결코 알지 못했던 아들로서 왔다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 한마디로, 우리는 한참 동안 서로 부둥켜안고 울었는데, 마침내 그가 먼저 입을 열었다. \"사랑하는 어머니,\" 그가 말했다. \"아직 살아 계셨군요? 저는 어머니 얼굴을 다시 뵈리라고는 한 번도 기대하지 못했어요.\" 나로 말하자면, 한참 동안 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n우리 둘이 조금 마음을 추스르고 이야기할 수 있게 된 뒤, 그는 내게 일이 어떻게 되어 있는지 말해 주었다. 내가 그의 아버지에게 써 보낸 것에 관해서는, 자기가 내 편지를 아버지에게 보이지도, 그에 관해 아버지에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다고 했다. 그의 할머니가 내게 남긴 것은 자기 손에 있으며, 내가 완전히 만족하도록 내게 정당하게 해 주겠다고 했다. 그의 아버지로 말하자면, 늙고 몸과 마음이 다 쇠약하며, 매우 짜증을 잘 내고 성을 잘 내며, 거의 눈이 멀어, 아무것도 할 수 없는 형편이라 했다. 그리고 그는 아버지가 이토록 미묘한 성질의 일에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알기나 할지 의심스럽다고 했다. 그러므로 그는, 도무지 참을 수 없어 나를 직접 만나 흡족해하려는 까닭에서도, 또 일이 어떻게 되어 있는지 내가 본 뒤에 아버지에게 나 자신을 드러낼지 말지를 내가 판단할 수 있게 해 주려는 까닭에서도, 자기가 직접 왔다고 했다.\n\n이것은 실로 너무도 신중하고 슬기롭게 처리되었던지라, 나는 내 아들이 분별 있는 사람이며 내게서 어떤 지시도 받을 필요가 없음을 알았다. 나는 그에게, 그의 아버지가 그가 묘사한 그대로인 것이 놀랍지 않으니, 내가 떠나기 전에도 그의 머리가 조금 이상했었다고 말했다. 그리고 그의 동요의 주된 까닭은, 내가 그를 내 오라비임을 안 뒤에도, 우리 혈연을 감추고 그를 내 남편으로 삼아 함께 살자는 설득에 내가 응할 수 없었기 때문이라고 했다. 그가 그의 아버지의 지금 형편을 나보다 더 잘 아니, 나는 그가 지시하는 대로의 조치에 선뜻 따르겠다고 했다. 또 내가 그를 먼저 보았으니 그의 아버지를 보는 것에는 무관심하며, 그의 할머니가 내게 남긴 것이 그의 손에 맡겨져 있다는 말보다 그가 내게 더 좋은 소식을 들려줄 수는 없었을 것이라고 했다. 이제 그가 내가 누구인지 알았으니, 그가 말한 대로 내게 정당하게 해 주리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다. 그러고 나서 나는 내 어머니가 돌아가신 지 얼마나 되었는지, 어디서 돌아가셨는지를 물었고, 그 집안에 관해 너무도 많은 세세한 사정을 이야기한지라, 그에게 내가 정말로 참되이 그의 어머니라는 진실을 의심할 여지를 조금도 남기지 않았다.\n\n그러자 내 아들은 내가 어디 있는지, 어떻게 지내 왔는지 물었다. 나는 그에게, 나는 만의 메릴랜드 쪽에, 나와 같은 배로 잉글랜드에서 온 어느 각별한 친구의 농장에 있으며, 그가 있는 만의 그쪽으로 말하자면 내게는 거처가 없다고 말했다. 그는 내게, 내가 원한다면 자기와 함께 집으로 가서 평생 함께 살자고, 그의 아버지로 말하자면 아무도 알아보지 못하니 나를 짐작조차 하지 못할 것이라고 했다. 나는 그것을 조금 헤아려 보고 그에게 말하기를, 그와 떨어져 사는 것이 내게 실로 아무 문제가 아니긴 하나, 그와 한집에 살면서, 전에 내 평안에 그토록 큰 타격이었던 그 불행한 존재를 늘 눈앞에 두는 것이 내게 세상에서 가장 마음 편한 일이라고는 할 수 없겠다고 했다. 비록 그(내 아들)와 함께 있거나, 내가 머무는 동안 가능한 한 그와 가까이 있는 것은 기쁘겠으나, 내가 말로 나 자신을 들킬까 두려워 끊임없이 조심해야 하는 그 집에 있을 생각은 할 수 없으며, 또 그를 내 아들로 여기고 이야기하다가 그 일의 전모를 드러낼 만한 어떤 표현을 삼가지 못할 터인데, 그것은 결코 적절치 않은 일이라고 했다.\n\n그는 이 모든 점에서 내가 옳다고 인정했다. \"하지만 그렇다면, 사랑하는 어머니,\" 그가 말했다. \"되도록 제 곁에 가까이 계세요.\" 그래서 그는 나를 말에 태워 자기 농장 바로 옆 농장으로 데려갔는데, 거기서 나는 그의 집에서 받았을 만큼 잘 대접받았다. 나를 거기 남겨 두고 그는 집으로 돌아가면서, 우리가 그 주된 일은 이튿날 이야기하자고 했고, 먼저 나를 자기 고모라 부르고, 그곳 사람들—이들은 그의 소작인인 듯했다—에게 나를 더없이 정중하게 대접하라고 분부했다. 그가 떠난 지 두 시간쯤 뒤에, 그는 내 시중을 들 하녀 하나와 흑인 사내아이 하나, 그리고 내 저녁거리로 이미 요리해 둔 음식을 보내 주었다. 그리하여 나는 마치 새로운 세계에라도 있는 듯했고, 이제 내심 내가 잉글랜드에서 내 랭커셔 남편을 데려오지 않았더라면 하고 바라기 시작했다.\n\n그렇지만 그 바람도 진심은 아니었으니, 나는 내 랭커셔 남편을 온전히 사랑했고, 실로 처음부터 줄곧 그래 왔으며, 그는 한 남자가 할 수 있는 만큼 내게서 사랑받을 자격이 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 이야기는 그저 곁들여 말해 둔다.\n\n이튿날 아침 내 아들은 내가 일어나자마자 거의 곧바로 다시 나를 찾아왔다. 조금 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 무엇보다 먼저 사슴 가죽 주머니 하나를 꺼내 그 안에 스페인 피스톨 쉰다섯 닢을 넣어 내게 주면서, 그것은 내가 잉글랜드에서 오는 데 든 비용을 대 주려는 것이라고 했다. 그것을 캐묻는 것이 자기 할 일은 아니지만, 그래도 내가 돈을 많이 가지고 나오지는 않았으리라 여겨야 마땅하니, 그 나라로 돈을 많이 가지고 들어오는 일이 흔치 않기 때문이라고 했다. 그런 다음 그는 자기 할머니의 유언장을 꺼내 내게 죽 읽어 주었는데, 그에 따르면 그녀는 요크강에, 곧 내 어머니가 살던 곳에 있는, 그가 부르는 대로 작은 농장 하나를, 그 위의 종들과 가축들의 밑천과 함께 내게 남겼고, 그것을 내 아들인 이 사람에게 신탁으로 맡겨, 그가 내가 살아 있다는 것을 듣게 될 때마다 내가 쓰도록 하고, 내게 자식이 있다면 내 상속인에게, 상속인이 없으면 내가 유언으로 처분하는 누구에게든 주도록 했음이 드러났다. 다만 내 소식을 듣거나 나를 찾아낼 때까지의 그 수입은 앞서 말한 내 아들에게 주고, 만약 내가 살아 있지 않다면 그것은 그와 그의 상속인에게 가도록 했다.\n\n이 농장은 그에게서 멀리 떨어져 있긴 하나, 그는 그것을 세놓지 않고 수석 사무원(집사)을 두어 관리하는데, 그 바로 곁에 있는 그의 아버지 소유의 또 다른 농장도 그렇게 관리한다고 했으며, 자기가 직접 일 년에 서너 번 건너가 그것을 돌본다고 했다. 나는 그에게 그 농장이 얼마나 가치가 있겠는지 물었다. 그는, 내가 그것을 세놓는다면 자기가 그것에 대해 일 년에 육십 파운드쯤 주겠으나, 내가 거기서 산다면 훨씬 더 가치가 있어, 자기 생각에는 내게 일 년에 백오십 파운드쯤을 안겨 줄 것이라고 했다. 그러나 내가 만의 다른 쪽에 정착할 듯하거나, 어쩌면 다시 잉글랜드로 돌아갈 마음이 있을지 모르니, 내가 자기를 내 집사로 삼아 준다면, 자기 것을 관리하듯 그것을 나를 위해 관리하겠으며, 내게 보내 줄 수 있으리라 믿는다고 했으니\n그 농장에서 잉글랜드로 담배를 실어 보내면 내게 한 해 100파운드쯤, 때로는 그보다 더 많은 수입을 안겨 주리라는 것이었다.\n\n이것은 내게 죄다 낯선 소식이요, 내가 익숙하지 못한 일들이었다. 그리하여 실로 내 마음은, 내 평생 어느 때보다도 진지하게 위를 우러르기 시작하였고, 나 같은 자에게—어쩌면 세상에 살도록 용납된 자들 가운데 가장 큰 사악함의 경이였을 나에게—그토록 놀라운 일들을 베풀어 주신 섭리의 손길을 깊은 감사로 우러러보기 시작하였다. 그리고 다시 한번 짚어 두지 않을 수 없으니, 이 경우만이 아니라 감사할 만한 다른 모든 경우에도, 지난날의 사악하고 가증스러운 내 삶이 그토록 흉측하게 보인 적이 없었으며, 내가 그것을 그토록 온전히 혐오하고 그 일로 스스로를 책망한 적이 없었으니, 그것은 바로 내가 섭리께서 내게 선을 베풀고 계신다는 것을 절감하면서도, 내 쪽에서는 그토록 비열하게 갚아 온 것을 깨달았을 때였다.\n\n그러나 이런 생각들은 독자 여러분이 알아서 좋은 쓸모로 살려 주시리라 믿고 그 일은 맡겨 두며, 나는 본 이야기로 넘어가겠다. 아들의 다정한 태도와 친절한 제안에, 그가 나와 이야기를 나누는 거의 내내 내게서 눈물이 솟았다. 사실 나는 북받치는 감정이 잦아드는 사이사이가 아니고서는 그와 이야기를 나눌 수조차 없었으나, 그래도 마침내 입을 떼었으니, 내가 남겨 두고 간 것의 관리가 다름 아닌 내 친자식의 손에 맡겨지는 이런 복을 누리게 된 데 대해 놀라움을 표하며, 그 상속에 관해서는, 세상에 그 말고는 자식이 없거니와 이제 설령 내가 결혼한다 해도 더 자식을 둘 나이가 지났으니, 내가 죽은 뒤 그것을 온전히 그와 그의 상속인들에게 넘긴다는 증서를 한 장 작성해 주기를 바라며, 그러면 내가 기꺼이 서명하겠노라고 일렀다. 그리고 그러는 사이에, 나는 미소를 지으며 무엇 때문에 이토록 오래 홀아비로 지냈느냐고 물었다. 그의 대답은 다정하고 거침이 없었으니, 버지니아에서는 아내가 그리 흔하게 나지 않거니와, 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아간다는 말을 하니 런던에서 아내 하나를 보내 달라는 것이었다.\n\n이것이 우리가 나눈 첫날 대화의 요지였으니, 내 평생 머리 위로 지나간 가장 즐거운 날이요, 내게 가장 참된 만족을 안겨 준 날이었다. 그는 이후로 날마다 찾아와 많은 시간을 나와 함께 보냈고, 나를 여러 벗의 집으로 데리고 다녔는데, 그곳에서 나는 큰 존경을 받으며 대접받았다. 또한 나는 여러 차례 그의 집에서 식사를 하였는데, 그럴 때면 그는 반쯤 죽은 자기 아버지를 늘 멀찍이 치워 두어, 나는 한 번도 그를 보지 못하였고 그 또한 나를 보지 못하였다.\n나는 그에게 선물 하나를 주었으니, 그것이 내게 있는 값나가는 전부였다. 그것은 앞서 내 궤짝에 두 개가 있다고 언급한 그 금시계 가운데 하나로, 마침 몸에 지니고 있던 터라 세 번째 방문 때 그에게 주었다. 나는 그것 말고는 줄 만한 값진 것이 아무것도 없으니, 나를 생각하여 이따금 거기에 입맞춤이나 해 달라고 일렀다. 다만 그것을 런던의 어느 예배당에서 어느 귀부인의 옆구리에서 훔쳤다는 말은 정말이지 그에게 하지 않았다. 그건 그렇고.\n\n그는 받아야 할지 말아야 할지 망설이듯 잠시 머뭇거리며 서 있었으나, 나는 그것을 그의 손에 떠안기듯 권하여 기어이 받게 하였으니, 그것은 스페인 금화가 가득 든 그의 가죽 주머니보다 결코 그리 값이 못하지 않았다. 아니, 설령 런던 기준으로 따진다 해도 그러하였거니와, 내가 그것을 그에게 준 이곳에서는 그 곱절의 값어치가 있었다. 마침내 그는 그것을 받아 입을 맞추고는, 이 시계가 내가 살아 있는 한 갚아 나갈 빚이 될 것이라고 말했다.\n\n며칠 뒤 그는 증여 증서를 가져왔고, 대서인도 함께 데려왔으므로, 나는 아주 흔쾌히 서명하여 백 번의 입맞춤과 더불어 그에게 건넸으니, 어머니와 다정하고 효심 깊은 자식 사이에 이보다 더한 애정으로 오간 일은 일찍이 없었으리라. 이튿날 그는 자기 손으로 쓰고 인장을 찍은 증서 한 장을 가져왔는데, 그로써 그는 내 몫으로 농장을 자신의 온 솜씨를 다하여 경영하고 가꾸며, 내가 어디에 있든 내 지시에 따라 그 수익을 송금하고, 아울러 그 수익을 한 해 100파운드로 맞춰 주기로 스스로 의무를 졌다.\n그렇게 해 놓고는, 내가 수확이 끝나기 전에 청구하러 왔으니 올해 수익에 대한 권리가 내게 있다고 말하며, 스페인 8레알 은화로 100파운드를 치러 주고는, 그해 분, 곧 다가오는 크리스마스로 끝나는 한 해 분을 완납받았다는 영수증을 자기에게 써 달라고 하였다. 때는 8월 하순 무렵이었다.\n\n나는 이곳에서 다섯 주가량 머물렀는데, 실은 떠나기가 여간 힘들지 않았다. 아니, 그는 나와 함께 만을 건너오려고까지 하였으나, 나는 결코 그것을 허락하지 않았다. 그래도 그는 자기 소유의 외돛배 한 척에 나를 태워 보내 주었으니, 그것은 요트처럼 지어져 그에게 사업에도 유람에도 두루 쓸모가 있었다. 이것은 내가 받아들였고, 그리하여 더없는 효심과 애정의 표현을 다 나눈 뒤, 그는 나를 떠나보냈으며, 나는 이틀 만에 무사히 내 벗인 퀘이커 여인의 집에 다다랐다.\n\n나는 우리 농장에 쓰려고 마구와 안장을 갖춘 말 세 필, 돼지 몇 마리, 암소 두 마리, 그 밖에도 천 가지나 되는 물건들을 가져왔으니, 모두가 일찍이 어떤 여인도 두지 못했을 만큼 더없이 친절하고 다정한 자식이 준 선물이었다. 나는 남편에게 이번 항해의 모든 세세한 일을 들려주었으나, 다만 아들을 사촌이라 일컬었다. 먼저 시계를 잃어버렸다고 말하니 그는 그것을 불운으로 여기는 듯하였으나, 이어 내 사촌이 얼마나 친절했는지, 어머니가 내게 그런 농장을 남겨 주었고 그가 언젠가 내 소식을 듣게 되기를 바라며 그것을 나를 위해 간수해 왔다는 것을 말해 주었다. 그러고 나서 나는 그것을 그의 경영에 맡겨 두었으며, 그가 그 수익에 대해 충실히 보고해 줄 것이라고 말한 뒤, 첫해 수익으로 100파운드 은화를 꺼내 보였다. 이어 피스톨 금화가 든 사슴가죽 지갑을 꺼내며 \"그리고 여보, 여기 그 금시계가 있어요.\" 하고 말했다. 내 남편은—자비가 마음을 울리는 곳에서는 분별 있는 모든 마음에 한결같은 결과를 빚어내는 것이 하늘의 선하심이 틀림없으니—두 손을 들어 올리며 환희에 겨워 \"나처럼 이렇게 은혜를 모르는 개 같은 놈에게 하느님께서 무엇을 해 주고 계신단 말인가!\" 하고 외쳤다. 그러고 나서 나는 이 모든 것 말고도 그 외돛배에 실어 온 것, 곧 말과 돼지와 암소, 그리고 우리 농장에 쓸 다른 물품들을 알려 주었으니, 이 모두가 그의 놀라움을 한층 더하고 그의 마음을 감사로 가득 채웠다. 그리고 이때부터 줄곧 그는, 방탕아요 노상강도요 강도였던 자를 하느님의 선하심이 돌이켜 데려온 그 누구 못지않게 진실한 참회자요, 더없이 철저하게 거듭난 사람이 되었다고 나는 믿는다. 나는 이 진실의 증거만으로도 이보다 더 큰 이야기책을 채울 수 있으련만, 그 부분의 이야기가 사악한 부분만큼 흥미롭지는 못하리라는 의구심이 들어서 그렇지, 그것만 따로 한 권의 책으로 엮을까 하는 생각을 해 본 적도 있다.\n\n나 자신으로 말하자면, 이것은 내 남편의 이야기가 아니라 내 자신의 이야기여야 하므로, 나는 나 자신과 관련된 부분으로 돌아가겠다. 우리는 우리 농장을 계속 꾸려 나갔으니, 우리의 싹싹한 처신으로 그곳에서 사귄 벗들의 도움과 어울림으로, 특히 우리에게 충실하고 너그럽고 한결같은 벗임을 입증한 그 정직한 퀘이커 여인의 도움으로 그것을 경영하였다. 그리고 우리는 매우 큰 성공을 거두었으니, 앞서 말했듯 번창하는 밑천으로 시작한 데다, 이제 거기에 은화 150파운드가 더해져 불어났으므로, 우리는 하인의 수를 늘리고, 매우 좋은 집을 한 채 지었으며, 해마다 많은 땅을 일구어 놓았다. 둘째 해에 나는 옛 후견인에게 편지를 써, 우리의 성공이 주는 기쁨을 그녀와 나누고, 위에서 말한 250파운드, 곧 내가 그녀에게 맡겨 둔 돈을 어떻게 쓸지 일러 주며, 그것을 물품으로 사서 우리에게 보내 달라고 하였더니, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 친절하고 충실하게 그 일을 해냈고, 그것은 무사히 우리에게 도착하였다.\n\n이로써 우리는 남편과 나 둘 다를 위한 온갖 종류의 옷을 갖추게 되었다. 그리고 나는 그가 갖기를 좋아한다는 것을 내가 아는 온갖 물건을 그를 위해 사는 데 각별히 마음을 썼으니, 곧 좋은 긴 가발 두 개, 은 손잡이가 달린 칼 두 자루, 멋진 새 사냥총 서너 자루, 권총집과 권총이 딸린 아주 잘빠진 안장 하나에 진홍색 망토 하나, 한마디로 그를 흡족하게 하고, 또 그가 실제로 그러하듯 아주 훌륭한 신사로 보이게 할 만한 것이라면 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 것이었다. 나는 우리에게 아직 부족한 살림살이도 넉넉히 주문하였고, 둘이 함께 쓸 온갖 종류의 아마포도 들였다. 나 자신으로 말하자면, 이미 매우 잘 갖추고 있던 터라 옷이나 아마포가 거의 필요치 않았다. 내 나머지 짐은 온갖 종류의 철물, 말에 쓸 마구, 연장, 하인들 옷, 그리고 모직물, 직물, 서지, 양말, 신발, 모자 따위, 곧 하인들이 입는 그런 것들로 이루어졌으며, 하인들 옷을 지을 옷감도 통째로 들였으니, 모두가 그 퀘이커 여인의 지시에 따른 것이었다. 그리고 이 모든 짐이 무사히, 온전한 상태로 도착하였는데, 옛 후견인이 나를 위해 골라 준 건장한 처녀 하인 셋도 함께 왔으니, 그곳에도, 또 우리가 그들에게 시킬 일에도 어지간히 알맞았다. 그중 하나는 마침 배가 부른 채로 왔으니, 그녀가 나중에 실토한 바로는 배가 그레이브젠드에 채 닿기도 전에 배의 선원 하나에게 임신을 당한 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 상륙한 지 일곱 달쯤 뒤에 우리에게 튼튼한 사내아이 하나를 낳아 주었다.\n\n여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 내 남편은 잉글랜드에서 온 이 모든 짐이 도착하자 조금 놀랐다. 그리고 이 짐의 명세서를 본 뒤 나와 이야기를 나누며 \"여보, 이게 다 어찌 된 영문이오? 우리가 너무 깊이 빚을 지게 되는 건 아닌지 걱정이오. 우리가 언제 이걸 다 갚을 수 있겠소?\" 하고 물었다. 나는 미소를 지으며 그것이 죄다 값이 치러진 것이라고 말한 뒤, 우리 처지가 어떤 일을 겪게 할지 몰라 내 밑천을 전부 가지고 오지 않고 벗의 손에 그만큼을 남겨 두었던 것인데, 이제 우리가 무사히 건너와 살아갈 길이 잡혔으니 그것을 가져오라고 사람을 보냈던 것이며, 그가 보다시피 이렇게 도착한 것이라고 일러 주었다.\n\n그는 어안이 벙벙하여 한참 동안 손가락을 꼽아 가며 셈을 하면서도 아무 말이 없었다. 마침내 그가 이렇게 입을 떼었다. \"가만, 어디 봅시다.\" 하면서 그는 여전히 손가락을 꼽되, 우선 엄지부터 꼽으며 \"처음에 돈으로 246파운드, 그다음 금시계 두 개에 다이아몬드 반지들, 그리고 은붙이.\" 하고는 검지를 꼽았다. 그러고는 그다음 손가락을 꼽으며 \"여기 요크강에 있는 한 해 100파운드짜리 농장 하나, 그다음 돈 150파운드, 그다음 말과 암소와 돼지와 물품을 가득 실은 외돛배 한 척.\" 하고 셈하다가 다시 엄지로 돌아왔다. \"그리고 이제,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"잉글랜드에서 250파운드 들인, 여기서는 그 곱절 값이 나가는 짐 한 무더기.\" \"그래서,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그걸 다 합쳐 어떻게 보세요?\" \"어떻게 보냐고?\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"아, 내가 랭커셔에서 아내를 얻을 때 속았다고 누가 그러던가? 나는 한 재산, 그것도 아주 좋은 재산하고 결혼한 셈이지.\" 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n한마디로, 우리는 이제 상당히 넉넉한 형편이 되었고, 해마다 형편이 나아졌다. 우리의 새 농장은 어느새 부쩍부쩍 자라났으니, 우리가 그곳에서 산 여덟 해 동안 그것을 어찌나 일구어 놓았던지, 수익이 적어도 한 해 300파운드, 곧 잉글랜드에서 그만한 값어치에 이를 정도였다.\n\n다시 집에 돌아온 지 한 해가 지난 뒤, 나는 아들을 보고 또 농장의 또 한 해 수익을 받으러 만을 건너갔다. 그런데 그곳에 막 내리자마자, 내 늙은 남편이 죽어 묻힌 지 보름이 채 안 되었다는 소식을 듣고 놀랐다. 솔직히 말해 이것은 거슬리는 소식이 아니었으니, 이제 나는 있는 그대로의 모습, 곧 기혼의 처지로 나설 수 있게 되었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 아들에게서 떠나오기 전에, 내 농장 가까이에 농장을 가진 어떤 신사와 결혼하게 될 것 같다고 말하면서, 비록 이전에 내게 지워졌던 어떤 의무에 관해서는 법적으로 결혼할 자유가 있긴 하나, 그 오점이 언젠가 다시 들춰져 남편을 불편하게 할까 봐 나는 그것을 꺼린다고 일러 두었다. 여전히 한결같이 친절하고 효심 깊고 싹싹한 내 아들은, 이번에는 자기 집에서 나를 대접하고, 100파운드를 치러 주었으며, 선물을 잔뜩 실어 나를 다시 집으로 돌려보냈다.\n이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 나는 아들에게 내가 결혼했다는 것을 알리고 그를 우리에게로 초대하였으며, 남편도 그에게 매우 정중한 편지를 써서 와서 만나 보자고 청하였다. 그리하여 그는 몇 달 뒤 과연 찾아왔는데, 마침 잉글랜드에서 온 내 짐이 들어온 바로 그때였다. 나는 그 짐이 죄다 내 것이 아니라 남편의 재산에 속한 것이라고 그가 믿게 해 두었다.\n\n짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그 늙은 비참한 자, 곧 내 오라비(남편)가 죽었을 때, 나는 그제야 그 모든 일에 관해, 또 앞서 사촌이라 일컬었던 이 사람이 그 잘못된 불행한 결합으로 낳은 내 친아들이라는 것에 관해, 남편에게 거리낌 없이 다 털어놓았다는 점이다. 그는 그 이야기에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았고, 설령 우리가 노인이라 부르던 그 사람이 살아 있었다 해도 자기는 마찬가지로 편안했을 것이라고 말했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그건 당신 잘못도, 그 사람 잘못도 아니었으니까. 그건 막을 도리가 없는 착오였소.\" 그는 다만, 내가 그 사람이 내 오라비인 줄 안 뒤에도 그것을 감추고 아내로 함께 살자고 한 그 사람만은 나무랐으니, 그것이야말로 비열한 짓이었다고 말했다. 이리하여 이 모든 어려움은 다 풀어졌고, 우리는 더없이 친절하고 더없이 안락하게 함께 살았다.\n\n우리는 늙었다. 나는 거의 일흔 살이 되어 잉글랜드로 돌아왔고, 남편은 예순여덟이니, 우리는 정해진 유배 기간을 훨씬 넘겨 마쳤다. 그리고 이제, 우리 둘이 함께 겪어 온 그 모든 고생과 그 모든 비참함에도 불구하고, 우리 둘 다 마음도 몸도 건강하다. 남편은 우리 일을 정리하느라 나보다 얼마간 더 그곳에 남아 있었고, 처음에 나는 그에게로 돌아갈 작정이었으나, 그의 청에 따라 그 결심을 바꾸었으니, 그도 잉글랜드로 건너왔으며, 여기서 우리는 우리가 살아온 사악한 삶에 대한 진실한 참회로 남은 여생을 보내기로 결심하였다.\n1683년에 쓰다";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-370"]=[[["Who was Born in Newgate, and during a Life of continu’d Variety for Threescore Years, besides her Childhood, was Twelve Year a Whore, five times a Wife (whereof once to her own Brother), Twelve Year a Thief, Eight Year a Transported Felon in Virginia, at last grew Rich, liv’d Honest, and dies a Penitent.", "나의 유명한 몰 플랜더스의 행운과 불운, 기타 등등."], ["Written from her own Memorandums .", "뉴게이트에서 태어나, 어린 시절을 제외하고도 예순 해 동안 끊임없는 파란만장한 삶을 살았으니, 열두 해를 창녀로, 다섯 번을 아내로(그중 한 번은 자신의 친오라비의 아내로), 열두 해를 도둑으로, 여덟 해를 버지니아의 유배 중범죄자로 지내다가 마침내 부유해져서 정직하게 살았고 참회자로 죽었다."], [". .", "그녀 자신의 비망록에서 옮겨 적은 글…"]], [["by Daniel Defoe", "대니얼 디포 지음"]], [["# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE", "# THE AUTHOR’S PREFACE"]], [["The world is so taken up of late with novels and romances, that it will be hard for a private history to be taken for genuine, where the names and other circumstances of the person are concealed, and on this account we must be content to leave the reader to pass his own opinion upon the ensuing sheet, and take it just as he pleases.", "저자 서문\n근래 세상은 소설과 로맨스에 너무도 푹 빠져 있어서, 인물의 이름과 그 밖의 정황이 감추어져 있는 개인의 사사로운 이야기를 진짜라고 받아들이기란 어려운 일이다. 그러니 이러한 까닭으로 우리는 이어지는 글에 대한 판단을 독자에게 맡기고, 그가 좋을 대로 받아들이도록 내버려 두는 데에 만족할 수밖에 없다."]], [["The author is here supposed to be writing her own history, and in the very beginning of her account she gives the reasons why she thinks fit to conceal her true name, after which there is no occasion to say any more about that.", "여기서 저자는 자기 자신의 이야기를 쓰고 있는 것으로 가정되며, 그녀는 자기 기록의 맨 처음에서 본명을 감추는 편이 적절하다고 여기는 이유를 밝히고 있으므로, 그 점에 관해서는 더 말할 필요가 없겠다."]], [["It is true that the original of this story is put into new words, and the style of the famous lady we here speak of is a little altered; particularly she is made to tell her own tale in modester words that she told it at first, the copy which came first to hand having been written in language more like one still in Newgate than one grown penitent and humble, as she afterwards pretends to be.", "이 이야기의 원본이 새로운 말로 옮겨지고, 우리가 여기서 다루는 그 유명한 부인의 문체가 다소 손질되었다는 것은 사실이다. 특히 그녀는 자기 이야기를 처음 털어놓았을 때보다 한결 점잖은 말투로 풀어내도록 다듬어졌으니, 처음 손에 들어온 원고는 훗날 참회하고 겸손해진 척하는 사람이 아니라 아직 뉴게이트에 갇혀 있는 사람에 더 가까운 언어로 쓰여 있었기 때문이다."]], [["The pen employed in finishing her story, and making it what you now see it to be, has had no little difficulty to put it into a dress fit to be seen, and to make it speak language fit to be read.", "그녀의 이야기를 완성하여 여러분이 지금 보는 모습으로 만들어 낸 붓은, 그것을 남에게 보일 만한 옷차림으로 입히고 읽을 만한 언어로 말하게 하는 데에 적잖은 어려움을 겪었다."], ["When a woman debauched from her youth, nay, even being the offspring of debauchery and vice, comes to give an account of all her vicious practices, and even to descend to the particular occasions and circumstances by which she ran through in threescore years, an author must be hard put to it wrap it up so clean as not to give room, especially for vicious readers, to turn it to his disadvantage.", "젊은 시절부터 방탕에 물들었던, 아니 방탕과 악덕의 소생인 한 여자가 자기의 온갖 악행을 낱낱이 이야기하고, 더 나아가 예순 해 동안 그녀가 거쳐 온 특정한 계기와 정황까지 시시콜콜 풀어 내려갈 때, 저자는 그것을 단정하게 감싸서, 특히 음란한 독자들이 그것을 제멋대로 나쁜 쪽으로 이용할 여지를 주지 않도록 하느라 적잖은 고생을 했을 것이 분명하다."]], [["All possible care, however, has been taken to give no lewd ideas, no immodest turns in the new dressing up of this story; no, not to the worst parts of her expressions.", "그러나 이 이야기를 새로 단장하면서, 음탕한 관념이나 외설스러운 표현을 조금도 주지 않도록, 그녀의 표현 가운데 가장 고약한 대목에 이르기까지도 온갖 주의를 다 기울였다."], ["To this purpose some of the vicious part of her life, which could not be modestly told, is quite left out, and several other parts are very much shortened.", "이 목적을 위해, 점잖게 이야기할 수 없는 그녀의 방탕한 삶 가운데 일부는 아예 빼 버렸고, 그 밖의 여러 부분도 매우 많이 줄였다."], ["What is left ’tis hoped will not offend the chastest reader or the modest hearer; and as the best use is made even of the worst story, the moral ’tis hoped will keep the reader serious, even where the story might incline him to be otherwise.", "남은 것은 가장 정숙한 독자나 점잖은 청자의 비위도 거스르지 않기를 바라며, 가장 고약한 이야기에서도 최선의 쓸모를 끌어냈으니, 그 교훈은 이야기가 독자를 다른 길로 기울게 할 만한 대목에서조차 그를 진지하게 붙들어 두기를 바란다."], ["To give the history of a wicked life repented of, necessarily requires that the wicked part should be make as wicked as the real history of it will bear, to illustrate and give a beauty to the penitent part, which is certainly the best and brightest, if related with equal spirit and life.", "회개한 사악한 삶의 이야기를 들려주자면, 그 사악한 부분을 실제 이야기가 허용하는 만큼 사악하게 그려 내야 하는 법이니, 그래야만 회개의 부분—이는 똑같은 기백과 생기로 그려진다면 분명 가장 훌륭하고 가장 빛나는 부분이거니와—을 돋보이게 하고 거기에 아름다움을 부여할 수 있는 것이다."]], [["It is suggested there cannot be the same life, the same brightness and beauty, in relating the penitent part as is in the criminal part.", "회개의 부분을 그려 낼 때는 범죄의 부분에서와 똑같은 생기, 똑같은 광채와 아름다움이 있을 수 없다는 주장이 있다."], ["If there is any truth in that suggestion, I must be allowed to say ’tis because there is not the same taste and relish in the reading, and indeed it is too true that the difference lies not in the real worth of the subject so much as in the gust and palate of the reader.", "그 주장에 어떤 진실이 있다면, 그것은 읽을 때 똑같은 맛과 풍미가 없기 때문이라고 말할 수밖에 없으며, 사실 그 차이가 소재의 실제 가치에 있다기보다는 독자의 취향과 입맛에 있다는 것은 너무도 옳은 말이다."]], [["But as this work is chiefly recommended to those who know how to read it, and how to make the good uses of it which the story all along recommends to them, so it is to be hoped that such readers will be more pleased with the moral than the fable, with the application than with the relation, and with the end of the writer than with the life of the person written of.", "그러나 이 책은 주로 그것을 읽을 줄 알고, 이야기가 처음부터 끝까지 권하는 좋은 쓸모를 끌어낼 줄 아는 이들에게 권하는 것이므로, 그런 독자들은 우화보다는 교훈에, 이야기보다는 그 적용에, 묘사되는 인물의 삶보다는 글쓴이의 의도에 더 흡족해하기를 바라는 바이다."]], [["There is in this story abundance of delightful incidents, and all of them usefully applied.", "이 이야기에는 즐거운 사건이 가득하며, 그 모두가 유익하게 적용되어 있다."], ["There is an agreeable turn artfully given them in the relating, that naturally instructs the reader, either one way or other.", "그것들을 들려주는 과정에 솜씨 있게 부여된 흐뭇한 전환이 있어, 자연스럽게 이렇게든 저렇게든 독자를 가르친다."], ["The first part of her lewd life with the young gentleman at Colchester has so many happy turns given it to expose the crime, and warn all whose circumstances are adapted to it, of the ruinous end of such things, and the foolish, thoughtless, and abhorred conduct of both the parties, that it abundantly atones for all the lively description she gives of her folly and wickedness.", "콜체스터의 젊은 신사와 보낸 그녀의 방탕한 삶의 첫 부분은, 그 죄를 폭로하고, 비슷한 처지에 놓인 모든 이에게 그러한 일의 파멸적인 결말과, 두 당사자의 어리석고 분별없으며 가증스러운 행실을 경고하기 위해, 너무도 알맞은 전환을 많이 담고 있어서, 그녀가 자신의 어리석음과 사악함에 대해 생생하게 묘사한 모든 것을 충분히 속죄하고 있다."]], [["The repentance of her lover at the Bath, and how brought by the just alarm of his fit of sickness to abandon her; the just caution given there against even the lawful intimacies of the dearest friends, and how unable they are to preserve the most solemn resolutions of virtue without divine assistance; these are parts which, to a just discernment, will appear to have more real beauty in them, than all the amorous chain of story which introduces it.", "바스에서 그녀의 정부情夫가 보인 회개, 그리고 그가 병으로 앓아눕는 정당한 경각심에 이끌려 어떻게 그녀를 버리게 되었는가, 가장 다정한 벗 사이의 합법적인 친밀함에 대해서까지 거기서 주어진 정당한 경계, 그리고 신의 도우심 없이는 그들이 가장 엄숙한 덕의 결심조차 어떻게 지켜 내지 못하는가—이런 부분들은, 올바른 분별력을 지닌 이에게는, 이를 끌어들이는 그 모든 연애의 사슬 같은 이야기보다 더 진정한 아름다움을 담고 있는 것으로 보일 것이다."]], [["In a word, as the whole relation is carefully garbled of all the levity and looseness that was in it, so it all applied, and with the utmost care, to virtuous and religious uses.", "한마디로, 이야기 전체에서 거기에 들어 있던 온갖 경박함과 방종함이 세심하게 걸러진 것과 마찬가지로, 그 모두가 더할 나위 없는 주의를 기울여 덕스럽고 종교적인 쓸모에 적용되었다."], ["None can, without being guilty of manifest injustice, cast any reproach upon it, or upon our design in publishing it.", "누구도 명백한 부당함을 저지르지 않고서는 이 책에, 혹은 이를 출간하는 우리의 의도에 어떤 비난도 던질 수 없다."]], [["The advocates for the stage have, in all ages, made this the great argument to persuade people that their plays are useful, and that they ought to be allowed in the most civilised and in the most religious government; namely, that they are applied to virtuous purposes, and that by the most lively representations, they fail not to recommend virtue and generous principles, and to discourage and expose all sorts of vice and corruption of manners; and were it true that they did so, and that they constantly adhered to that rule, as the test of their acting on the theatre, much might be said in their favour.", "연극을 옹호하는 이들은 어느 시대에나, 그들의 희곡이 유익하며 가장 문명화되고 가장 종교적인 정부에서도 허용되어야 마땅하다고 사람들을 설득하기 위해 이것을 큰 논거로 삼아 왔으니, 곧 그것이 덕스러운 목적에 적용되며, 가장 생생한 재현을 통해 덕과 고매한 원칙을 권하고, 온갖 종류의 악덕과 풍속의 타락을 막고 폭로하기를 결코 게을리하지 않는다는 것이다. 만약 그들이 정말로 그렇게 하고, 무대에서의 연기의 기준으로 그 규칙을 한결같이 지킨다는 것이 사실이라면, 그들을 위해 많은 말을 할 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Throughout the infinite variety of this book, this fundamental is most strictly adhered to; there is not a wicked action in any part of it, but is first and last rendered unhappy and unfortunate; there is not a superlative villain brought upon the stage, but either he is brought to an unhappy end, or brought to be a penitent; there is not an ill thing mentioned but it is condemned, even in the relation, nor a virtuous, just thing but it carries its praise along with it.", "이 책의 무한한 다채로움 속에서 이 근본 원칙은 가장 엄격하게 지켜진다. 어느 부분에서든 사악한 행위는 하나도 빠짐없이 처음부터 끝까지 불행하고 비참하게 그려지며, 무대에 오른 그 어떤 극악무도한 악당도 불행한 결말에 이르거나 회개하는 자가 되고, 언급된 어떤 나쁜 것도 이야기 가운데서조차 단죄되지 않는 것이 없으며, 어떤 덕스럽고 올바른 것도 그에 합당한 칭송을 함께 지니지 않는 것이 없다."], ["What can more exactly answer the rule laid down, to recommend even those representations of things which have so many other just objections leaving against them? namely, of example, of bad company, obscene language, and the like.", "사례라든가, 나쁜 무리라든가, 외설스러운 언어 따위, 그토록 많은 정당한 반대에 부딪히는 그런 사물의 재현조차 권하기 위해 세워진 규칙에, 이보다 더 정확하게 부합할 수 있는 것이 무엇이겠는가?"]], [["Upon this foundation this book is recommended to the reader as a work from every part of which something may be learned, and some just and religious inference is drawn, by which the reader will have something of instruction, if he pleases to make use of it.", "이러한 바탕 위에서 이 책은, 그 어느 부분에서든 무언가 배울 점이 있고, 어떤 올바르고 종교적인 추론을 끌어낼 수 있는 작품으로서 독자에게 권하는 바이니, 독자가 기꺼이 그것을 활용하고자 한다면 무언가 가르침을 얻게 될 것이다."]], [["All the exploits of this lady of fame, in her depredations upon mankind, stand as so many warnings to honest people to beware of them, intimating to them by what methods innocent people are drawn in, plundered and robbed, and by consequence how to avoid them.", "이 이름난 부인이 인류를 약탈하며 벌인 온갖 행적은, 정직한 사람들에게 그런 자들을 경계하라는 그만큼 많은 경고로서 서 있으며, 어떤 수법으로 무고한 사람들이 꾀임에 빠져 약탈당하고 강탈당하는지, 그리하여 어떻게 그것을 피해야 하는지를 그들에게 넌지시 일러 준다."], ["Her robbing a little innocent child, dressed fine by the vanity of the mother, to go to the dancing-school, is a good memento to such people hereafter, as is likewise her picking the gold watch from the young lady’s side in the Park.", "어머니의 허영으로 곱게 차려입혀 무용 학교에 가던 어린 무고한 아이를 그녀가 털어먹은 일은, 앞으로 그런 사람들에게 좋은 경계가 될 것이며, 공원에서 젊은 부인의 옆구리에서 금시계를 빼낸 일도 마찬가지다."]], [["Her getting a parcel from a hare-brained wench at the coaches in St. John Street; her booty made at the fire, and again at Harwich, all give us excellent warnings in such cases to be more present to ourselves in sudden surprises of every sort.", "세인트존 거리의 마차 옆에서 어떤 덜렁대는 계집아이에게서 보따리를 빼앗은 일, 화재 현장에서 그리고 다시 하리치에서 챙긴 노획물—이 모두가, 그런 경우에 온갖 종류의 갑작스러운 봉변 앞에서 정신을 더 차리라는 훌륭한 경고를 우리에게 준다."]], [["Her application to a sober life and industrious management at last in Virginia, with her transported spouse, is a story fruitful of instruction to all the unfortunate creatures who are obliged to seek their re-establishment abroad, whether by the misery of transportation or other disaster; letting them know that diligence and application have their due encouragement, even in the remotest parts of the world, and that no case can be so low, so despicable, or so empty of prospect, but that an unwearied industry will go a great way to deliver us from it, will in time raise the meanest creature to appear again in the world, and give him a new case for his life.", "마침내 버지니아에서 유배된 남편과 더불어 술 끊고 부지런히 살림을 꾸려 나간 일은, 유배의 비참함 때문이든 다른 재난 때문이든 타향에서 재기를 도모할 수밖에 없는 모든 불운한 이들에게 가르침이 풍성한 이야기이니, 근면과 노력은 세상의 가장 외진 곳에서조차 마땅한 보답을 받으며, 어떤 처지도 너무 낮거나, 너무 비천하거나, 너무 가망 없을 수 없어서, 지칠 줄 모르는 근면이 우리를 거기에서 건져 내는 데 큰 몫을 하고, 머지않아 가장 미천한 자도 다시 세상에 모습을 드러내게 일으켜 세우며, 그의 삶에 새로운 처지를 마련해 준다는 것을 그들에게 알려 준다."]], [["There are a few of the serious inferences which we are led by the hand to in this book, and these are fully sufficient to justify any man in recommending it to the world, and much more to justify the publication of it.", "이 책에서 우리가 손에 이끌려 다다르게 되는 진지한 추론은 이 몇 가지인데, 이것만으로도 누구든 이 책을 세상에 권하는 것을 정당화하기에, 더더욱 그것을 출간하는 것을 정당화하기에 충분하다."]], [["There are two of the most beautiful parts still behind, which this story gives some idea of, and lets us into the parts of them, but they are either of them too long to be brought into the same volume, and indeed are, as I may call them, whole volumes of themselves, viz.: 1.", "이 이야기가 어느 정도 짐작하게 해 주고 그 일부를 들여다보게 해 주는, 가장 아름다운 두 부분이 아직 뒤에 남아 있으나, 둘 중 어느 것도 같은 책에 담기에는 너무 길고, 사실 내가 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 그 자체로 한 권의 책에 해당한다."], ["The life of her governess, as she calls her, who had run through, it seems, in a few years, all the eminent degrees of a gentlewoman, a whore, and a bawd; a midwife and a midwife-keeper, as they are called; a pawnbroker, a childtaker, a receiver of thieves, and of thieves’ purchase, that is to say, of stolen goods; and in a word, herself a thief, a breeder up of thieves and the like, and yet at last a penitent.", "곧, 첫째, 그녀가 부르는 대로 하자면 그녀의 후견인의 삶인데, 이 여자는 몇 해 사이에 귀부인, 창녀, 포주, 산파와 이른바 산파장, 전당포 주인, 아이 받아 키우는 자, 도둑과 도둑의 장물—다시 말해 훔친 물건—을 받는 자, 한마디로 그녀 자신이 도둑이요 도둑을 길러 내는 자 등등, 온갖 두드러진 단계를 두루 거친 듯하면서도, 끝내는 회개한 자가 되었다."]], [["The second is the life of her transported husband, a highwayman, who it seems, lived a twelve years’ life of successful villainy upon the road, and even at last came off so well as to be a volunteer transport, not a convict; and in whose life there is an incredible variety.", "둘째는 유배된 그녀 남편의 삶인데, 노상강도였던 그는 길에서 열두 해 동안 성공적인 악행의 삶을 살았던 듯하며, 끝내는 죄수가 아니라 자원 유배자로 빠져나올 만큼 잘 풀려서, 그의 삶에는 믿기 어려운 다채로움이 담겨 있다."]], [["But, as I have said, these are things too long to bring in here, so neither can I make a promise of the coming out by themselves.", "그러나 앞서 말했듯이, 이것들은 여기에 끌어들이기에는 너무 긴 것이라, 따로 펴낸다고 약속할 수도 없다."]], [["We cannot say, indeed, that this history is carried on quite to the end of the life of this famous Moll Flanders, as she calls herself, for nobody can write their own life to the full end of it, unless they can write it after they are dead.", "사실 이 이야기가 그녀 자신이 부르는 대로 그 유명한 몰 플랜더스의 삶의 끝까지 온전히 이어졌다고는 말할 수 없으니, 죽은 뒤에 자기 삶을 쓸 수 있지 않은 한, 누구도 자기 삶을 그 끝까지 온전히 쓸 수는 없는 노릇이기 때문이다."], ["But her husband’s life, being written by a third hand, gives a full account of them both, how long they lived together in that country, and how they both came to England again, after about eight years, in which time they were grown very rich, and where she lived, it seems, to be very old, but was not so extraordinary a penitent as she was at first; it seems only that indeed she always spoke with abhorrence of her former life, and of every part of it.", "그러나 제삼자의 손으로 쓰인 그녀 남편의 삶이 두 사람 모두에 대한 충실한 기록을 주어, 그들이 그 나라에서 얼마나 오래 함께 살았는지, 그리고 약 여덟 해 뒤에 어떻게 둘 다 다시 잉글랜드로 돌아왔는지를 전해 주는데, 그동안 그들은 매우 부유해졌고, 그곳에서 그녀는 아주 늙도록 산 듯하나, 처음만큼 그리 대단한 회개자는 아니었던 듯하며, 다만 실은 늘 자신의 지난 삶과 그 모든 부분을 혐오스럽게 이야기했던 것만은 분명한 듯하다."]], [["In her last scene, at Maryland and Virginia, many pleasant things happened, which makes that part of her life very agreeable, but they are not told with the same elegancy as those accounted for by herself; so it is still to the more advantage that we break off here.", "메릴랜드와 버지니아에서의 그녀의 마지막 장면에서는 여러 유쾌한 일이 일어나, 그녀 삶의 그 부분을 매우 흐뭇하게 만들지만, 그것들은 그녀 자신이 들려준 일들과 같은 우아함으로 이야기되지는 않으므로, 우리가 여기서 끊는 편이 그래도 더 나을 것이다. 몰 플랜더스"]], [["# MOLL FLANDERS", "# MOLL FLANDERS"]], [["My true name is so well known in the records or registers at Newgate, and in the Old Bailey, and there are some things of such consequence still depending there, relating to my particular conduct, that it is not be expected I should set my name or the account of my family to this work; perhaps, after my death, it may be better known; at present it would not be proper, no not though a general pardon should be issued, even without exceptions and reserve of persons or crimes.", "내 본명은 뉴게이트와 올드베일리의 기록부나 명부에 너무도 잘 알려져 있고, 또 내 행적과 관련하여 그곳에 아직 결판나지 않은 중대한 일들이 몇 가지 걸려 있는지라, 내가 이 글에 내 이름이나 내 가문의 내력을 적어 넣으리라고는 기대할 수 없는 노릇이다. 어쩌면 내가 죽은 뒤에는 더 잘 알려질지도 모르겠으나, 지금으로서는 적절치 않을 터이니, 인물이나 죄목에 예외나 유보를 두지 않은 일반사면령이 내린다 해도 마찬가지일 것이다."]], [["It is enough to tell you, that as some of my worst comrades, who are out of the way of doing me harm (having gone out of the world by the steps and the string, as I often expected to go), knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, so you may give me leave to speak of myself under that name till I dare own who I have been, as well as who I am.", "내게 해를 끼칠 처지에서 벗어난(내가 종종 그렇게 가리라 예상했던 대로, 계단과 밧줄을 밟고 이 세상을 하직한) 내 가장 고약한 동료들 가운데 더러가 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알고 있었다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하니, 그러므로 내가 지금 누구인가뿐 아니라 지난날 누구였는가를 감히 밝힐 수 있을 때까지는, 그 이름으로 나 자신을 일컫도록 허락해 주시기 바란다."]], [["I have been told that in one of our neighbour nations, whether it be in France or where else I know not, they have an order from the king, that when any criminal is condemned, either to die, or to the galleys, or to be transported, if they leave any children, as such are generally unprovided for, by the poverty or forfeiture of their parents, so they are immediately taken into the care of the Government, and put into a hospital called the House of Orphans, where they are bred up, clothed, fed, taught, and when fit to go out, are placed out to trades or to services, so as to be well able to provide for themselves by an honest, industrious behaviour.", "내가 듣기로 우리 이웃 나라 가운데 하나에서는—그것이 프랑스인지 아니면 다른 어디인지는 모르겠으나—국왕의 명령이 있어서, 어떤 범죄자가 사형이나 갤리선 노역이나 유배형에 처해지면, 그들이 자식을 남길 경우, 그런 아이들은 대개 부모의 가난이나 재산 몰수로 의지할 데가 없게 마련이므로, 그 즉시 정부의 보호 아래 거두어져 고아원이라 불리는 시설에 넣어지고, 거기서 길러지고 입혀지고 먹여지고 가르침을 받으며, 나가도 될 나이가 되면 직업이나 일자리에 배치되어, 정직하고 부지런한 처신으로 스스로 잘 먹고살 수 있게 된다고 한다."]], [["Had this been the custom in our country, I had not been left a poor desolate girl without friends, without clothes, without help or helper in the world, as was my fate; and by which I was not only exposed to very great distresses, even before I was capable either of understanding my case or how to amend it, but brought into a course of life which was not only scandalous in itself, but which in its ordinary course tended to the swift destruction both of soul and body.", "이것이 우리나라의 관습이었다면, 나는 내 운명이 그러했듯이 벗도 없고, 옷도 없고, 세상에 도움도 도와줄 이도 없는 가엾고 외로운 계집아이로 남겨지지 않았을 것이며, 그로 인해 나는 내 처지를 이해하거나 그것을 바로잡을 방도를 알 수 있기도 전에 너무나 큰 곤경에 내몰렸을 뿐 아니라, 그 자체로 수치스러울 뿐더러 그 통상적인 흐름이 영혼과 육체를 다 함께 급속히 파멸로 이끄는 삶의 길로 끌려들어 갔던 것이다."]], [["But the case was otherwise here.", "그러나 여기서는 사정이 달랐다."], ["My mother was convicted of felony for a certain petty theft scarce worth naming, viz. having an opportunity of borrowing three pieces of fine holland of a certain draper in Cheapside.", "내 어머니는 입에 올리기도 민망한 어떤 좀도둑질로 중범죄 유죄판결을 받았으니, 곧 칩사이드의 어떤 포목상에게서 고운 홀란드 천 세 필을 슬쩍 빌릴 기회를 잡았던 것이다."], ["The circumstances are too long to repeat, and I have heard them related so many ways, that I can scarce be certain which is the right account.", "그 정황은 너무 길어서 되풀이할 수 없거니와, 나는 그 이야기를 하도 여러 갈래로 들은 터라, 어느 것이 올바른 이야기인지 도무지 확신할 수가 없다."]], [["However it was, this they all agree in, that my mother pleaded her belly, and being found quick with child, she was respited for about seven months; in which time having brought me into the world, and being about again, she was called down, as they term it, to her former judgment, but obtained the favour of being transported to the plantations, and left me about half a year old; and in bad hands, you may be sure.", "어찌 되었든, 그들 모두가 한결같이 동의하는 바는 이러하니, 내 어머니는 임신을 사유로 호소했고, 임신한 것이 확인되어 약 일곱 달 동안 형 집행이 유예되었다는 것이다. 그동안 나를 세상에 낳고 다시 거동하게 되자, 그들의 말마따나 다시 이전의 판결을 받으러 불려 나갔으나, 식민지로 유배되는 은전을 입었고, 생후 반년쯤 된 나를 남겨 두고 갔으니, 여러분도 짐작하겠지만 나쁜 손에 맡겨진 채였다."]], [["This is too near the first hours of my life for me to relate anything of myself but by hearsay; it is enough to mention, that as I was born in such an unhappy place, I had no parish to have recourse to for my nourishment in my infancy; nor can I give the least account how I was kept alive, other than that, as I have been told, some relation of my mother’s took me away for a while as a nurse, but at whose expense, or by whose direction, I know nothing at all of it.", "이는 내 생애의 첫 시기와 너무 가까운 일이라 전해 들은 말로밖에는 나 자신에 관해 아무것도 이야기할 수 없으니, 다만 내가 그토록 불행한 곳에서 태어난 까닭에 갓난아이 시절 양육을 의지할 교구가 없었다는 것만 언급해 두면 충분하다. 또 내가 어떻게 목숨을 부지했는지도 도무지 알 길이 없으니, 다만 들은 바로는 내 어머니의 어떤 친척이 한동안 나를 유모로 거두어 데려갔다는 것뿐, 누구의 비용으로, 혹은 누구의 지시로 그리되었는지는 전혀 알지 못한다."]], [["The first account that I can recollect, or could ever learn of myself, was that I had wandered among a crew of those people they call gypsies, or Egyptians; but I believe it was but a very little while that I had been among them, for I had not had my skin discoloured or blackened, as they do very young to all the children they carry about with them; nor can I tell how I came among them, or how I got from them.", "내가 나 자신에 관해 기억해 낼 수 있는, 혹은 알아낼 수 있었던 최초의 일은, 내가 집시 혹은 이집트인이라 불리는 그 무리 가운데 떠돌아다녔다는 것이다. 그러나 내가 그들 틈에 있었던 것은 아주 잠깐이었으리라 생각되니, 그들이 데리고 다니는 모든 아이에게 아주 어릴 적부터 하듯이 내 살갗이 거뭇거뭇하게 물들거나 검게 칠해지지는 않았기 때문이다. 또 내가 어떻게 그들 틈에 끼게 되었는지, 어떻게 그들에게서 벗어났는지도 알 수 없다."]], [["It was at Colchester, in Essex, that those people left me; and I have a notion in my head that I left them there (that is, that I hid myself and would not go any farther with them), but I am not able to be particular in that account; only this I remember, that being taken up by some of the parish officers of Colchester, I gave an account that I came into the town with the gypsies, but that I would not go any farther with them, and that so they had left me, but whither they were gone that I knew not, nor could they expect it of me; for though they send round the country to inquire after them, it seems they could not be found.", "그 사람들이 나를 버리고 간 곳은 에식스주의 콜체스터였다. 내 머릿속에는 내가 거기서 그들을 떠났다(다시 말해, 몸을 숨기고 그들과 더는 가지 않으려 했다)는 생각이 들지만, 그 일을 소상히 말할 수는 없다. 다만 이것만은 기억하니, 콜체스터의 어떤 교구 관리에게 붙들렸을 때, 나는 집시들과 함께 이 마을에 들어왔으나 그들과 더는 가려 하지 않았고, 그래서 그들이 나를 버려두고 갔으며, 그들이 어디로 갔는지는 모르겠다고 진술했다. 그들로서도 그것을 내게 기대할 수는 없었으니, 그들이 그 무리를 수소문하러 온 고을에 사람을 보냈으나, 끝내 찾아내지 못한 듯했기 때문이다."]], [["I was now in a way to be provided for; for though I was not a parish charge upon this or that part of the town by law, yet as my case came to be known, and that I was too young to do any work, being not above three years old, compassion moved the magistrates of the town to order some care to be taken of me, and I became one of their own as much as if I had been born in the place.", "이제 나는 보살핌을 받을 길에 들어섰다. 법적으로는 내가 이 마을의 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 어느 교구의 부담도 아니었으나, 내 처지가 알려지고, 또 내가 세 살이 채 안 되어 무슨 일을 하기에는 너무 어리다는 것이 드러나자, 동정심에 이끌린 마을의 치안판사들이 나를 돌보도록 명했고, 나는 마치 그곳에서 태어나기라도 한 것처럼 그들 자신의 아이가 되었다."]], [["In the provision they made for me, it was my good hap to be put to nurse, as they call it, to a woman who was indeed poor but had been in better circumstances, and who got a little livelihood by taking such as I was supposed to be, and keeping them with all necessaries, till they were at a certain age, in which it might be supposed they might go to service or get their own bread.", "그들이 나를 위해 마련한 조처 가운데, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 어느 여인에게 유모살이로 맡겨진 것은 내게 다행한 일이었다. 그 여인은 사실 가난하긴 했으나 한때는 더 나은 처지에 있었고, 나 같은 처지로 여겨지는 아이들을 받아, 어느 정도 나이가 되어 일자리에 가거나 제 밥벌이를 할 만하다 싶을 때까지 온갖 필요한 것을 갖춰 데리고 있으면서 근근이 생계를 꾸려 가던 사람이었다."]], [["This woman had also had a little school, which she kept to teach children to read and to work; and having, as I have said, lived before that in good fashion, she bred up the children she took with a great deal of art, as well as with a great deal of care.", "이 여인은 또한 작은 학교도 운영하고 있었으니, 그것을 꾸려 아이들에게 읽기와 바느질을 가르쳤다. 그리고 앞서 말했듯이 전에는 넉넉한 형편으로 살았던 터라, 자신이 거두는 아이들을 무던히 정성을 들여서, 또한 무던히 솜씨를 부려 길러 냈다."]], [["But that which was worth all the rest, she bred them up very religiously, being herself a very sober, pious woman, very house-wifely and clean, and very mannerly, and with good behaviour.", "그러나 무엇보다 값진 것은, 그녀가 아이들을 매우 신앙심 깊게 길러 냈다는 점이니, 그녀 자신이 매우 진중하고 경건한 여인으로, 살림 솜씨가 좋고 청결하며, 매우 예의 바르고 행실이 단정했던 까닭이다."], ["So that in a word, expecting a plain diet, coarse lodging, and mean clothes, we were brought up as mannerly and as genteelly as if we had been at the dancing-school.", "그래서 한마디로, 소박한 음식과 거친 잠자리와 변변찮은 옷차림은 각오해야 했지만, 우리는 마치 무용 학교에라도 다닌 것처럼 예의 바르고 품위 있게 길러졌다."]], [["I was continued here till I was eight years old, when I was terrified with news that the magistrates (as I think they called them) had ordered that I should go to service. I was able to do but very little service wherever I was to go, except it was to run of errands and be a drudge to some cookmaid, and this they told me of often, which put me into a great fright; for I had a thorough aversion to going to service, as they called it (that is, to be a servant), though I was so young; and I told my nurse, as we called her, that I believed I could get my living without going to service, if she pleased to let me; for she had taught me to work with my needle, and spin worsted, which is the chief trade of that city, and I told her that if she would keep me, I would work for her, and I would work very hard.", "나는 여기서 여덟 살이 될 때까지 지냈는데, 그때 치안판사들이(그들을 그렇게 불렀던 것 같다) 나더러 일자리에 나가라는 명을 내렸다는 소식에 겁에 질리고 말았다. 어디로 가게 되든 나는 심부름이나 다니고 어느 부엌데기의 종노릇이나 하는 것 말고는 변변한 일을 할 수 없었는데, 그들은 이 점을 자주 일러 주었고, 그것이 나를 몹시 두렵게 했다. 어렸지만 나는 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 일자리에 나가는 것(다시 말해 하인이 되는 것)이 진저리나게 싫었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 우리가 부르는 대로 유모에게, 만약 그녀가 허락만 해 준다면 일자리에 나가지 않고도 밥벌이를 할 수 있을 것 같다고 말했다. 그녀가 내게 바늘로 바느질하는 법과, 그 도시의 주된 생업인 털실 잣는 법을 가르쳐 준 터라, 나를 데리고 있어 주기만 한다면 그녀를 위해 일하겠노라고, 아주 열심히 일하겠노라고 말했다."]], [["I talked to her almost every day of working hard; and, in short, I did nothing but work and cry all day, which grieved the good, kind woman so much, that at last she began to be concerned for me, for she loved me very well.", "나는 거의 날마다 그녀에게 열심히 일하겠다는 이야기를 했다. 한마디로, 온종일 일하고 우는 것밖에는 하지 않았으니, 이것이 그 착하고 다정한 여인의 마음을 너무도 아프게 한 나머지, 마침내 그녀는 나를 걱정하기 시작했다. 그녀가 나를 무척 아꼈기 때문이다."]], [["One day after this, as she came into the room where all we poor children were at work, she sat down just over against me, not in her usual place as mistress, but as if she set herself on purpose to observe me and see me work.", "그 일이 있은 뒤 어느 날, 우리 가엾은 아이들이 모두 일하고 있는 방으로 그녀가 들어와서는, 여느 때처럼 선생으로서 앉던 자리가 아니라, 마치 일부러 나를 지켜보고 내가 일하는 모습을 보려는 듯, 바로 내 맞은편에 앉았다. 나는 그녀가 시킨 일을 하고 있었으니, 내 기억으로는 그녀가 맡아 짓기로 한 셔츠 몇 벌에 표시를 새기는 일이었다."], ["I was doing something she had set me to; as I remember, it was marking some shirts which she had taken to make, and after a while she began to talk to me.", "한참 뒤에 그녀가 내게 말을 걸기 시작했다. \"이 어리석은 것아.\""], ["“Thou foolish child,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"넌 늘 울기만 하는구나\"(그때도 나는 울고 있었다)."], ["says she, “thou art always crying”", "\"어디 말해 보렴, 무엇 때문에 우는 게냐?\" \"사람들이 절 데려가서,\""], ["(for I was crying then); “prithee, what dost cry for?”", "내가 말했다. \"일자리에 보내려 하는데, 전 집안일을 할 줄 모르거든요.\""], ["“Because they will take me away,”", "\"얘야,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["says I, “and put me to service, and I can’t work housework.”", "\"네 말대로 집안일을 할 줄 모른다 해도, 차차 배우게 될 게다. 처음부터 힘든 일을 시키지는 않을 거야.\""], ["“Well, child,”", "\"아뇨, 시킬 거예요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["says she, “but though you can’t work housework, as you call it, you will learn it in time, and they won’t put you to hard things at first.”", "\"그리고 제가 못 하면 절 때릴 거고, 하녀들도 절 때려서 힘든 일을 시킬 거예요. 전 조그만 계집아이라 그런 일은 못 한단 말이에요.\""], ["“Yes, they will,” says I, “and if I can’t do it they will beat me, and the maids will beat me to make me do great work, and I am but a little girl and I can’t do it”; and then I cried again, till I could not speak any more to her.", "그러고는 다시 울기 시작해서, 그녀에게 더는 아무 말도 할 수 없을 지경이 되었다."]], [["This moved my good motherly nurse, so that she from that time resolved I should not go to service yet; so she bid me not cry, and she would speak to Mr.", "이것이 어머니처럼 다정한 내 유모의 마음을 움직여서, 그녀는 그때부터 나를 아직은 일자리에 보내지 않기로 마음먹었다."], ["Mayor, and I should not go to service till I was bigger.", "그래서 그녀는 내게 울지 말라고, 시장 어른께 말씀드릴 테니 내가 더 클 때까지는 일자리에 나가지 않아도 된다고 했다."], ["Well, this did not satisfy me, for to think of going to service was such a frightful thing to me, that if she had assured me I should not have gone till I was twenty years old, it would have been the same to me; I should have cried, I believe, all the time, with the very apprehension of its being to be so at last.", "그러나 이 말로는 내 마음이 풀리지 않았으니, 일자리에 나갈 생각을 하는 것이 내게는 너무도 무서운 일이라, 설령 그녀가 내가 스무 살이 될 때까지는 가지 않아도 된다고 장담했다 한들, 내게는 마찬가지였을 것이다. 결국 언젠가는 그렇게 되리라는 바로 그 두려움 때문에, 나는 그동안 내내 울었을 것이 분명하다."]], [["When she saw that I was not pacified yet, she began to be angry with me.", "내가 여전히 진정되지 않는 것을 보고, 그녀는 나에게 화를 내기 시작했다."], ["“And what would you have?”", "\"대체 어쩌라는 게냐?\""], ["says she; “don’t I tell you that you shall not go to service till your are bigger?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"더 클 때까지는 일자리에 나가지 않아도 된다고 하지 않았느냐?\""], ["“Ay,”", "\"네,\""], ["said I, “but then I must go at last.”", "내가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 결국엔 가야 하잖아요.\""], ["“Why, what?”", "\"아니, 그래서 뭐?\""], ["said she; “is the girl mad?", "그녀가 말했다. \"이 애가 미쳤나?"], ["What would you be—a gentlewoman?”", "그럼 넌 뭐가 되고 싶은 게냐—귀부인이라도 되고 싶은 게야?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["says I, and cried heartily till I roared out again.", "내가 말하고는, 다시 목 놓아 울 만큼 서럽게 울었다."]], [["This set the old gentlewoman a-laughing at me, as you may be sure it would.", "이 말에 그 노부인은 나를 두고 웃음을 터뜨렸으니, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 당연한 노릇이었다. \"그래, 마님, 황송하옵니다.\""], ["“Well, madam, forsooth,”", "그녀가 나를 놀리며 말했다."], ["says she, gibing at me, “you would be a gentlewoman; and pray how will you come to be a gentlewoman?", "\"귀부인이 되시겠다고요. 한데 어떻게 귀부인이 되시려나?"], ["What! will you do it by your fingers’ end?”", "원, 손가락 끝으로 그리하시려나?\""]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네.\""], ["says I again, very innocently.", "나는 또 아주 천진하게 대답했다."]], [["“Why, what can you earn?”", "\"원, 네가 벌면 얼마나 벌겠느냐?\""], ["says she; “what can you get at your work?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"일해서 얼마나 받느냔 말이다.\""]], [["“Threepence,”", "\"3펜스요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “when I spin, and fourpence when I work plain work.”", "\"실을 자으면요. 그리고 보통 바느질을 하면 4펜스고요.\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"저런!"], ["poor gentlewoman,”", "가엾은 귀부인 같으니.\""], ["said she again, laughing, “what will that do for thee?”", "그녀가 다시 웃으며 말했다. \"그걸로 네가 뭘 어쩌겠다는 게냐?\""]], [["“It will keep me,”", "\"그걸로 제가 먹고살 수 있어요.\""], ["says I, “if you will let me live with you.”", "내가 말했다. \"할머니가 절 데리고 살게만 해 주신다면요.\""], ["And this I said in such a poor petitioning tone, that it made the poor woman’s heart yearn to me, as she told me afterwards.", "그리고 이 말을 어찌나 가엾게 애원하는 투로 했던지, 나중에 그녀가 내게 들려준 바로는, 그 가엾은 여인의 마음이 나를 향해 애틋해졌다고 한다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["says she, “that will not keep you and buy you clothes too; and who must buy the little gentlewoman clothes?”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그걸로는 너 하나 건사하면서 옷까지 사 입히진 못한단다."], ["says she, and smiled all the while at me.", "그럼 이 조그만 귀부인 마님 옷은 누가 사 드려야 하나?\" 그녀가 이렇게 말하며 줄곧 나를 보고 미소 지었다."]], [["“I will work harder, then,”", "\"그럼 제가 더 열심히 일할게요.\""], ["says I, “and you shall have it all.”", "내가 말했다. \"그래서 다 할머니께 드릴게요.\""]], [["“Poor child!", "\"가엾은 것!"], ["it won’t keep you,”", "그걸로는 너도 못 건사한단다.\""], ["says she; “it will hardly keep you in victuals.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"겨우 끼니나 댈까 말까 한 걸.\""]], [["“Then I will have no victuals,”", "\"그럼 전 끼니를 안 먹을게요.\""], ["says I, again very innocently; “let me but live with you.”", "내가 다시 아주 천진하게 말했다. \"그저 할머니랑 살게만 해 주세요.\""]], [["“Why, can you live without victuals?”", "\"원, 끼니도 안 먹고 어떻게 살겠느냐?\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"살 수 있어요.\""], ["again says I, very much like a child, you may be sure, and still I cried heartily.", "나는 또 대답했는데, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 영락없이 어린아이 같은 투였고, 여전히 서럽게 울고 있었다."]], [["I had no policy in all this; you may easily see it was all nature; but it was joined with so much innocence and so much passion that, in short, it set the good motherly creature a-weeping too, and she cried at last as fast as I did, and then took me and led me out of the teaching-room. “Come,” says she, “you shan’t go to service; you shall live with me”; and this pacified me for the present.", "이 모든 것에 내 무슨 꿍꿍이가 있었던 것은 아니다. 여러분도 쉬이 알 수 있듯이, 그것은 온통 천성에서 우러난 것이었다. 그러나 거기에 어찌나 많은 순진함과 어찌나 많은 절절함이 어려 있었던지, 한마디로 그 착하고 어머니 같은 분도 따라 울게 만들었으니, 마침내 그녀는 나만큼이나 줄줄 울다가, 나를 데리고 교실 밖으로 이끌어 냈다. \"자,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"넌 일자리에 안 나가도 된다. 나랑 같이 살자꾸나.\" 그리고 이 말이 당장은 내 마음을 가라앉혀 주었다."]], [["Some time after this, she going to wait on the Mayor, and talking of such things as belonged to her business, at last my story came up, and my good nurse told Mr. Mayor the whole tale.", "그 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 그녀가 시장을 찾아뵈러 가서 자기 일과 관련된 이런저런 이야기를 하다가, 마침내 내 이야기가 나오자, 마음씨 착한 내 유모는 시장에게 그 전말을 죄다 들려주었다."], ["He was so pleased with it, that he would call his lady and his two daughters to hear it, and it made mirth enough among them, you may be sure.", "그는 그 이야기가 어찌나 흡족했던지, 자기 부인과 두 딸을 불러서 듣게 했고, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그들 사이에 적잖은 웃음을 자아냈다."]], [["However, not a week had passed over, but on a sudden comes Mrs. Mayoress and her two daughters to the house to see my old nurse, and to see her school and the children.", "그런데 채 한 주도 지나기 전에, 난데없이 시장 부인과 두 딸이 내 늙은 유모를 찾아, 또 그 학교와 아이들을 보러 집으로 찾아왔다."], ["When they had looked about them a little, “Well, Mrs. ——,”", "그들이 잠시 둘러본 뒤, \"그래, ○○ 부인,\""], ["says the Mayoress to my nurse, “and pray which is the little lass that intends to be a gentlewoman?”", "시장 부인이 내 유모에게 말했다. \"어디, 귀부인이 되겠다는 그 조그만 계집아이가 어느 아이인가요?\""], ["I heard her, and I was terribly frighted at first, though I did not know why neither; but Mrs.", "나는 그 말을 들었고, 처음에는 까닭도 모른 채 끔찍하게 겁이 났다. 그런데 시장 부인이 내게로 다가왔다."], ["Mayoress comes up to me.", "\"자, 아가씨.\""], ["“Well, miss,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “and what are you at work upon?”", "\"무슨 일을 하고 있는 거니?\""], ["The word miss was a language that had hardly been heard of in our school, and I wondered what sad name it was she called me.", "아가씨라는 말은 우리 학교에서는 좀처럼 들어 본 적 없는 말이라, 나는 그녀가 나를 뭐라고 그런 고약한 이름으로 부르는 건가 의아했다."], ["However, I stood up, made a curtsy, and she took my work out of my hand, looked on it, and said it was very well; then she took up one of the hands.", "어쨌든 나는 일어서서 인사를 올렸고, 그녀는 내 손에서 일감을 받아 들고 들여다보더니 아주 잘했다고 했다. 그러고는 내 손을 하나 들어 올렸다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"어머나,\""], ["says she, “the child may come to be a gentlewoman for aught anybody knows; she has a gentlewoman’s hand,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"누가 알겠어요, 이 아이가 귀부인이 될지도 모르죠."], ["says she.", "귀부인의 손을 가졌네요.\""], ["This pleased me mightily, you may be sure; but Mrs.", "그녀가 말했다. 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 이 말이 나를 무척이나 흐뭇하게 했다."], ["Mayoress did not stop there, but giving me my work again, she put her hand in her pocket, gave me a shilling, and bid me mind my work, and learn to work well, and I might be a gentlewoman for aught she knew.", "그러나 시장 부인은 거기서 그치지 않고, 내게 일감을 돌려주면서 주머니에 손을 넣어 1실링을 주고는, 일에 마음을 쏟아 일을 잘 배우라고, 그러면 누가 알겠느냐, 귀부인이 될지도 모른다고 했다."]], [["Now all this while my good old nurse, Mrs. Mayoress, and all the rest of them did not understand me at all, for they meant one sort of thing by the word gentlewoman, and I meant quite another; for alas!", "그런데 이러는 내내 마음씨 착한 늙은 유모도, 시장 부인도, 그 밖의 사람들도 모두 나를 전혀 이해하지 못했으니, 그들이 귀부인이라는 말로 뜻하는 것과 내가 뜻하는 것이 사뭇 달랐던 까닭이다."], ["all I understood by being a gentlewoman was to be able to work for myself, and get enough to keep me without that terrible bugbear going to service, whereas they meant to live great, rich and high, and I know not what.", "아, 내가 귀부인이 된다는 것으로 이해한 것은 그저 스스로 일해서 밥벌이를 하고, 일자리에 나가는 그 끔찍한 도깨비 없이도 먹고살 만큼 벌 수 있게 되는 것뿐이었던 반면, 그들이 뜻한 것은 거창하고 부유하고 드높게 사는 것이었으니, 나로서는 알 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["Well, after Mrs. Mayoress was gone, her two daughters came in, and they called for the gentlewoman too, and they talked a long while to me, and I answered them in my innocent way; but always, if they asked me whether I resolved to be a gentlewoman, I answered Yes.", "자, 시장 부인이 돌아간 뒤, 그 두 딸이 들어와서는 그들 역시 귀부인을 찾았고, 내게 한참 동안 이야기를 건넸으며, 나는 내 천진한 투로 그들에게 대답했다."], ["At last one of them asked me what a gentlewoman was?", "그러나 그들이 정말 귀부인이 되기로 마음먹었느냐고 물을 때면, 나는 늘 그렇다고 대답했다. 마침내 그중 하나가 귀부인이 무엇이냐고 내게 물었다."], ["That puzzled me much; but, however, I explained myself negatively, that it was one that did not go to service, to do housework.", "그 물음에 나는 무척 당황했다. 그러나 어쨌든 나는 부정의 방식으로 나 자신을 설명했으니, 곧 일자리에 나가 집안일을 하지 않는 사람이 귀부인이라고 했다."], ["They were pleased to be familiar with me, and like my little prattle to them, which, it seems, was agreeable enough to them, and they gave me money too.", "그들은 기꺼이 나와 스스럼없이 굴었고, 그들에게 건네는 내 조그만 재잘거림을 좋아했으니, 그것이 그들에게는 제법 흐뭇했던 모양이라, 그들 역시 내게 돈을 주었다."]], [["As for my money, I gave it all to my mistress-nurse, as I called her, and told her she should have all I got for myself when I was a gentlewoman, as well as now.", "그 돈으로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 모조리 내가 부르던 대로 유모 선생님께 드리면서, 지금처럼 내가 귀부인이 되면 내가 버는 것을 모두 드리겠다고 말했다."], ["By this and some other of my talk, my old tutoress began to understand me about what I meant by being a gentlewoman, and that I understood by it no more than to be able to get my bread by my own work; and at last she asked me whether it was not so.", "이 말과 그 밖의 내 이야기 몇 마디로, 내 늙은 스승은 내가 귀부인이 된다는 것으로 무엇을 뜻하는지, 그리고 내가 그것을 그저 제 손으로 일해서 밥벌이를 할 수 있게 되는 것 이상으로는 여기지 않는다는 것을 알아차리기 시작했고, 마침내 그녀는 정말 그런 것이 아니냐고 내게 물었다."]], [["I told her, yes, and insisted on it, that to do so was to be a gentlewoman; “for,”", "나는 그렇다고, 그렇게 하는 것이 귀부인이 되는 것이라고 우겨 가며 말했다. \"보세요,\""], ["says I, “there is such a one,”", "내가 말했다. \"이런 사람이 있잖아요.\""], ["naming a woman that mended lace and washed the ladies’ laced-heads; “she,”", "그러면서 레이스를 손질하고 귀부인들의 레이스 머릿수건을 빨아 주는 어떤 여자의 이름을 댔다. \"그 사람이,\""], ["says I, “is a gentlewoman, and they call her madam.”", "내가 말했다. \"귀부인이고, 사람들이 그 여자를 마님이라고 불러요.\""]], [["“Poor child,”", "\"가엾은 것아,\" 마음씨 착한 늙은 유모가 말했다."], ["says my good old nurse, “you may soon be such a gentlewoman as that, for she is a person of ill fame, and has had two or three bastards.”", "\"넌 머지않아 그런 귀부인은 될 수 있겠구나. 그 여자는 평판이 나쁜 사람이라, 사생아를 두셋이나 낳았단다.\""]], [["I did not understand anything of that; but I answered, “I am sure they call her madam, and she does not go to service nor do housework”; and therefore I insisted that she was a gentlewoman, and I would be such a gentlewoman as that.", "나는 그 말이 무슨 뜻인지 하나도 이해하지 못했으나, 이렇게 대답했다. \"어쨌든 사람들이 그 여자를 마님이라고 부르고, 그 여자는 일자리에 나가지도 않고 집안일도 안 하잖아요.\" 그러고는 그 여자가 귀부인이라고, 나도 그런 귀부인이 되겠다고 우겼다."]], [["The ladies were told all this again, to be sure, and they made themselves merry with it, and every now and then the young ladies, Mr. Mayor’s daughters, would come and see me, and ask where the little gentlewoman was, which made me not a little proud of myself.", "이 모든 이야기가 또 그 부인들에게 분명 다시 전해졌고, 그들은 그것으로 흥겨워했으며, 이따금 그 젊은 아가씨들, 곧 시장의 딸들이 나를 보러 와서는 그 조그만 귀부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻곤 했으니, 그것이 나를 적잖이 우쭐하게 만들었다."]], [["This held a great while, and I was often visited by these young ladies, and sometimes they brought others with them; so that I was known by it almost all over the town.", "이 일은 한참 동안 이어졌고, 나는 이 젊은 아가씨들의 잦은 방문을 받았으며, 더러는 다른 이들을 데려오기도 했으니, 그리하여 나는 그 일로 거의 온 마을에 알려지게 되었다."]], [["I was now about ten years old, and began to look a little womanish, for I was mighty grave and humble, very mannerly, and as I had often heard the ladies say I was pretty, and would be a very handsome woman, so you may be sure that hearing them say so made me not a little proud. However, that pride had no ill effect upon me yet; only, as they often gave me money, and I gave it to my old nurse, she, honest woman, was so just to me as to lay it all out again for me, and gave me head-dresses, and linen, and gloves, and ribbons, and I went very neat, and always clean; for that I would do, and if I had rags on, I would always be clean, or else I would dabble them in water myself; but, I say, my good nurse, when I had money given me, very honestly laid it out for me, and would always tell the ladies this or that was bought with their money; and this made them oftentimes give me more, till at last I was indeed called upon by the magistrates, as I understood it, to go out to service; but then I was come to be so good a workwoman myself, and the ladies were so kind to me, that it was plain I could maintain myself—that is to say, I could earn as much for my nurse as she was able by it to keep me—so she told them that if they would give her leave, she would keep the gentlewoman, as she called me, to be her assistant and teach the children, which I was very well able to do; for I was very nimble at my work, and had a good hand with my needle, though I was yet very young.", "이제 나는 열 살쯤 되었고, 제법 여자티가 나기 시작했다. 무척 의젓하고 겸손했으며 매우 예의 발랐고, 또 부인들이 내가 예쁘다고, 아주 잘생긴 여자가 될 거라고 말하는 것을 자주 들었던 터라, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그들이 그렇게 말하는 것을 듣고 적잖이 우쭐해졌다. 그러나 그 우쭐함이 아직은 내게 나쁜 영향을 미치지는 않았다. 다만, 그들이 내게 돈을 자주 주었고 나는 그것을 내 늙은 유모에게 주었는데, 그 정직한 여인은 내게 어찌나 의로웠던지, 그것을 죄다 다시 나를 위해 써서, 머릿수건이며 아마포며 장갑이며 리본을 사 주었으니, 나는 아주 말끔하게 다니고 늘 깨끗했다. 그것만은 내가 꼭 했던 일이라, 누더기를 걸치고 있더라도 늘 깨끗이 하든지, 아니면 내 손으로 그것을 물에 담가 빨든지 했기 때문이다. 어쨌든 내 말은, 나의 착한 유모가 내게 돈이 생기면 아주 정직하게 그것을 나를 위해 써 주었고, 늘 부인들에게 이것이며 저것이 그들의 돈으로 산 것이라고 말해 주었다는 것이다. 그래서 그들은 종종 더 많은 돈을 주었으니, 마침내 내가 이해한 바로는 치안판사들이 정말로 나더러 일자리에 나가라고 부르기에 이르렀다. 그러나 그때쯤 나는 스스로 솜씨 좋은 일꾼이 되어 있었고, 부인들도 내게 어찌나 다정했던지, 내가 능히 제 앞가림을 할 수 있다는 것이—다시 말해, 유모가 그것으로 나를 건사할 수 있을 만큼 내가 유모를 위해 벌 수 있다는 것이—분명했다. 그래서 그녀는 그들에게, 허락만 해 준다면 자기가 부르는 대로 그 귀부인을 데리고 있으면서 자기 조수로 삼아 아이들을 가르치게 하겠다고 말했으니, 그것은 내가 능히 할 수 있는 일이었다. 아직 무척 어렸지만, 나는 일하는 손이 야무지고 바느질 솜씨가 좋았기 때문이다."]], [["But the kindness of the ladies of the town did not end here, for when they came to understand that I was no more maintained by the public allowance as before, they gave me money oftener than formerly; and as I grew up they brought me work to do for them, such as linen to make, and laces to mend, and heads to dress up, and not only paid me for doing them, but even taught me how to do them; so that now I was a gentlewoman indeed, as I understood that word, I not only found myself clothes and paid my nurse for my keeping, but got money in my pocket too beforehand.", "그러나 마을 부인들의 친절은 여기서 끝나지 않았으니, 내가 전처럼 공적인 부조로 부양되지 않는다는 것을 알게 되자, 그들은 전보다 더 자주 내게 돈을 주었다. 그리고 내가 자라자 그들은 내게 해 줄 일감을—이를테면 지을 아마포며, 손질할 레이스며, 매만질 머릿수건 따위를—가져다주었고, 그 일을 해 준 대가를 치를 뿐 아니라 어떻게 하는지를 가르쳐 주기까지 했으니, 그리하여 이제 나는 내가 그 말을 이해하는 대로 정말로 귀부인이 되어, 내 옷가지를 스스로 마련하고 유모에게 내 양육비를 치를 뿐 아니라, 미리 주머니에 돈까지 모아 두게 되었다."]], [["The ladies also gave me clothes frequently of their own or their children’s; some stockings, some petticoats, some gowns, some one thing, some another, and these my old woman managed for me like a mere mother, and kept them for me, obliged me to mend them, and turn them and twist them to the best advantage, for she was a rare housewife.", "부인들은 또한 자기들 옷이나 자식들 옷을 자주 내게 주었으니, 양말이며, 속치마며, 가운이며, 이것저것 갖가지였다. 그리고 이런 것들을 내 늙은 여인이 마치 친어머니라도 되는 양 나를 위해 챙겨 주어, 나를 위해 간수해 두고, 내게 그것들을 깁고 뒤집고 매만져서 가장 알뜰하게 쓰도록 시켰으니, 그녀가 보기 드물게 살림 솜씨 좋은 사람이었기 때문이다."]], [["At last one of the ladies took so much fancy to me that she would have me home to her house, for a month, she said, to be among her daughters.", "마침내 부인들 가운데 하나가 나를 어찌나 마음에 들어 했던지, 한 달 동안 자기 딸들 틈에 두겠다며 나를 자기 집으로 데려가려 했다. 그런데 이것은 그녀로서는 더없이 친절한 일이었지만, 내 늙은 착한 여인이 그녀에게 말했듯이, 그녀가 나를 아주 영영 데리고 있기로 마음먹은 것이 아니라면, 그 조그만 귀부인에게 득보다 해를 더 끼치게 될 터였다."], ["Now, though this was exceeding kind in her, yet, as my old good woman said to her, unless she resolved to keep me for good and all, she would do the little gentlewoman more harm than good.", "\"그렇군요,\" 부인이 말했다."], ["“Well,”", "\"그 말이 맞아요."], ["says the lady, “that’s true; and therefore I’ll only take her home for a week, then, that I may see how my daughters and she agree together, and how I like her temper, and then I’ll tell you more; and in the meantime, if anybody comes to see her as they used to do, you may only tell them you have sent her out to my house.”", "그러니 그저 한 주만 집으로 데려가서, 내 딸들과 이 아이가 어떻게 지내는지, 또 내가 이 아이 성품을 어떻게 보는지 살펴보고서, 그때 더 말씀드리지요. 그리고 그동안 누가 늘 하던 대로 이 아이를 보러 오거든, 그저 우리 집으로 보냈다고만 말씀하시면 돼요.\""]], [["This was prudently managed enough, and I went to the lady’s house; but I was so pleased there with the young ladies, and they so pleased with me, that I had enough to do to come away, and they were as unwilling to part with me.", "이는 제법 신중하게 처리된 일이었고, 나는 그 부인의 집으로 갔다. 그런데 거기서 나는 젊은 아가씨들이 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 또 그들도 내가 어찌나 마음에 들었던지, 떠나오기가 여간 힘들지 않았고, 그들도 나와 헤어지기를 그토록 아쉬워했다."]], [["However, I did come away, and lived almost a year more with my honest old woman, and began now to be very helpful to her; for I was almost fourteen years old, was tall of my age, and looked a little womanish; but I had such a taste of genteel living at the lady’s house that I was not so easy in my old quarters as I used to be, and I thought it was fine to be a gentlewoman indeed, for I had quite other notions of a gentlewoman now than I had before; and as I thought, I say, that it was fine to be a gentlewoman, so I loved to be among gentlewomen, and therefore I longed to be there again.", "그러나 결국 나는 떠나왔고, 내 정직한 늙은 여인과 거의 한 해를 더 살았으며, 이제는 그녀에게 큰 도움이 되기 시작했다. 나는 거의 열네 살이 되어, 나이에 비해 키가 컸고 제법 여자티가 났기 때문이다. 그러나 그 부인의 집에서 품위 있는 생활을 맛본 터라, 나는 예전처럼 내 옛 거처가 그리 편치만은 않았고, 정말로 귀부인이 되는 것이 멋진 일이라고 생각했으니, 이제는 귀부인에 대해 전과는 사뭇 다른 관념을 갖게 되었기 때문이다. 그리고 내 말마따나 귀부인이 되는 것이 멋진 일이라고 생각했으므로, 나는 귀부인들 틈에 있기를 좋아했고, 그래서 다시 그곳에 가 있기를 간절히 바랐다."]], [["About the time that I was fourteen years and a quarter old, my good nurse, mother I rather to call her, fell sick and died.", "내가 열네 살하고도 석 달쯤 되었을 무렵, 나의 착한 유모를—오히려 어머니라 불러야 마땅하겠지만—병이 들어 세상을 떠났다."], ["I was then in a sad condition indeed, for as there is no great bustle in putting an end to a poor body’s family when once they are carried to the grave, so the poor good woman being buried, the parish children she kept were immediately removed by the church-wardens; the school was at an end, and the children of it had no more to do but just stay at home till they were sent somewhere else; and as for what she left, her daughter, a married woman with six or seven children, came and swept it all away at once, and removing the goods, they had no more to say to me than to jest with me, and tell me that the little gentlewoman might set up for herself if she pleased.", "그때 나는 정말이지 처량한 처지에 놓였으니, 가엾은 사람의 식솔이란 일단 무덤으로 옮겨지고 나면 끝맺는 데 별 부산을 떨 것도 없는 까닭에, 그 가엾고 착한 여인이 묻히자마자, 그녀가 거두던 교구 아이들은 곧바로 교구 위원들 손에 다른 데로 옮겨졌고, 학교는 문을 닫았으며, 그곳 아이들은 다른 데로 보내질 때까지 그저 집에 머무는 것 말고는 할 일이 없게 되었다. 그리고 그녀가 남긴 것으로 말하자면, 그녀의 딸—여섯이나 일곱 아이를 둔 기혼녀였다—이 와서 그것을 단번에 모조리 쓸어 갔고, 세간을 옮기면서 내게는 그저 농지거리나 하면서, 그 조그만 귀부인은 좋을 대로 제 살림이나 차리면 되겠다고 말하는 것 말고는 아무 할 말이 없었다."]], [["I was frighted out of my wits almost, and knew not what to do, for I was, as it were, turned out of doors to the wide world, and that which was still worse, the old honest woman had two-and-twenty shillings of mine in her hand, which was all the estate the little gentlewoman had in the world; and when I asked the daughter for it, she huffed me and laughed at me, and told me she had nothing to do with it.", "나는 거의 넋이 나갈 만큼 겁에 질렸고, 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 말하자면 문밖 넓은 세상으로 쫓겨난 신세였다. 게다가 더더욱 고약한 것은, 그 늙고 정직한 여인이 내 돈 22실링을 손에 쥐고 있었다는 점인데, 그것은 그 조그만 귀부인이 이 세상에 가진 전 재산이었다. 그래서 내가 딸에게 그것을 달라고 했더니, 그녀는 나를 윽박지르고 비웃으면서, 그 일은 자기와 상관없다고 했다."]], [["It was true the good, poor woman had told her daughter of it, and that it lay in such a place, that it was the child’s money, and had called once or twice for me to give it me, but I was, unhappily, out of the way somewhere or other, and when I came back she was past being in a condition to speak of it.", "그 착하고 가엾은 여인이 자기 딸에게 그 일을 말해 두었던 것은 사실이었으니, 그 돈이 어디어디 있고, 그것이 그 아이의 돈이라고 했으며, 한두 차례 나를 불러 그것을 주려고도 했다. 그러나 나는 불행히도 어디 다른 데에 나가 있었고, 내가 돌아왔을 때 그녀는 이미 그 일을 말할 수 있는 처지가 아니었다."], ["However, the daughter was so honest afterwards as to give it me, though at first she used me cruelly about it.", "그래도 딸은 나중에 정직하게도 그 돈을 내게 주었으니, 처음에는 그 일로 나를 모질게 대했지만 말이다."]], [["Now was I a poor gentlewoman indeed, and I was just that very night to be turned into the wide world; for the daughter removed all the goods, and I had not so much as a lodging to go to, or a bit of bread to eat.", "이제 나는 정말이지 가엾은 귀부인이 되었고, 바로 그날 밤 넓은 세상으로 내쫓길 판이었다. 딸이 세간을 죄다 옮겨 가 버려서, 나는 가서 묵을 거처는커녕 먹을 빵 한 조각도 없었기 때문이다."], ["But it seems some of the neighbours, who had known my circumstances, took so much compassion of me as to acquaint the lady in whose family I had been a week, as I mentioned above; and immediately she sent her maid to fetch me away, and two of her daughters came with the maid though unsent.", "그러나 내 처지를 알고 있던 이웃 사람들 가운데 더러가 나를 몹시 가엾게 여겨, 앞서 말했듯이 내가 한 주 동안 머물렀던 집의 부인에게 이 사정을 알린 모양이었다. 그러자 그녀는 즉시 하녀를 보내 나를 데려오게 했고, 보내지 않았는데도 두 딸이 하녀와 함께 왔다."], ["So I went with them, bag and baggage, and with a glad heart, you may be sure.", "그래서 나는 짐 보따리를 다 챙겨, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 기쁜 마음으로 그들을 따라갔다."], ["The fright of my condition had made such an impression upon me, that I did not want now to be a gentlewoman, but was very willing to be a servant, and that any kind of servant they thought fit to have me be.", "내 처지의 두려움이 내게 어찌나 깊은 자국을 남겼던지, 이제 나는 귀부인이 되고 싶지도 않았고, 기꺼이 하인이 되려 했으며, 그들이 적당하다고 여기는 어떤 종류의 하인이라도 되려 했다."]], [["But my new generous mistress, for she exceeded the good woman I was with before, in everything, as well as in the matter of estate; I say, in everything except honesty; and for that, though this was a lady most exactly just, yet I must not forget to say on all occasions, that the first, though poor, was as uprightly honest as it was possible for any one to be.", "그러나 나의 새롭고 너그러운 여주인은—그녀는 재산 면에서도, 또 다른 모든 면에서도, 정직함 한 가지만 빼고는, 내가 전에 함께 지냈던 착한 여인을 능가했다. 정직함으로 말하자면, 비록 이 부인이 더없이 올바른 분이긴 했어도, 나는 어떤 경우든 첫 번째 분이 비록 가난했을망정 누구라도 그럴 수 있는 만큼이나 한 점 흐트러짐 없이 정직했다는 것을 잊지 말고 말해 두어야겠다."]], [["I was no sooner carried away, as I have said, by this good gentlewoman, but the first lady, that is to say, the Mayoress that was, sent her two daughters to take care of me; and another family which had taken notice of me when I was the little gentlewoman, and had given me work to do, sent for me after her, so that I was mightily made of, as we say; nay, and they were not a little angry, especially madam the Mayoress, that her friend had taken me away from her, as she called it; for, as she said, I was hers by right, she having been the first that took any notice of me. But they that had me would not part with me; and as for me, though I should have been very well treated with any of the others, yet I could not be better than where I was.", "앞서 말했듯이, 이 착한 부인이 나를 데려가자마자, 첫 번째 부인, 곧 전임 시장 부인이 나를 보살피라고 두 딸을 보냈다. 그리고 내가 그 조그만 귀부인이던 시절에 나를 눈여겨보고 일감을 주었던 또 다른 집안에서도 그녀를 뒤따라 나를 부르러 보냈으니, 그리하여 우리네 말마따나 나는 무척이나 귀염을 받았다. 아니, 그들은 적잖이 화까지 냈으니, 특히 시장 부인 마님은 자기 친구가 자기에게서—그녀가 부르는 대로 하자면—나를 데려갔다고 했다. 그녀의 말로는, 내가 마땅히 자기 사람이라는 것이었으니, 나를 맨 처음 눈여겨본 사람이 자기였기 때문이라고 했다. 그러나 나를 데리고 있던 이들은 나를 내놓으려 하지 않았다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 다른 누구의 집에서 더없이 좋은 대접을 받았다 한들, 지금 있는 곳보다 더 나을 수는 없었을 것이다."]], [["Here I continued till I was between seventeen and eighteen years old, and here I had all the advantages for my education that could be imagined; the lady had masters home to the house to teach her daughters to dance, and to speak French, and to write, and other to teach them music; and I was always with them, I learned as fast as they; and though the masters were not appointed to teach me, yet I learned by imitation and inquiry all that they learned by instruction and direction; so that, in short, I learned to dance and speak French as well as any of them, and to sing much better, for I had a better voice than any of them. I could not so readily come at playing on the harpsichord or spinet, because I had no instrument of my own to practice on, and could only come at theirs in the intervals when they left it, which was uncertain; but yet I learned tolerably well too, and the young ladies at length got two instruments, that is to say, a harpsichord and a spinet too, and then they taught me themselves. But as to dancing, they could hardly help my learning country-dances, because they always wanted me to make up even number; and, on the other hand, they were as heartily willing to learn me everything that they had been taught themselves, as I could be to take the learning.", "나는 여기서 열일고여덟 살이 될 때까지 지냈고, 여기서 상상할 수 있는 온갖 교육의 혜택을 다 누렸다. 부인은 자기 딸들에게 춤과 프랑스어와 글쓰기를 가르칠 선생들과, 그 밖에 음악을 가르칠 선생들을 집으로 불러들였는데, 나는 늘 그들과 함께였으므로 그들만큼이나 빨리 배웠다. 비록 선생들이 나를 가르치도록 정해진 것은 아니었으나, 나는 그들이 지도와 가르침으로 배우는 모든 것을 흉내와 물음으로 배웠으니, 그리하여 한마디로 나는 그들 누구 못지않게 춤을 추고 프랑스어를 하게 되었고, 노래는 훨씬 더 잘하게 되었으니, 그들 누구보다 내 목소리가 더 나았기 때문이다. 하프시코드나 스피넷 연주만큼은 그리 쉽게 익히지 못했으니, 내게는 연습할 악기가 없어서, 그들이 손을 놓는 짬에만 그들의 악기를 만질 수 있었는데 그마저도 일정치 않았기 때문이다. 그래도 나는 그것 또한 제법 잘 익혔고, 마침내 젊은 아가씨들이 악기를 둘, 곧 하프시코드와 스피넷을 마련하자, 그들이 직접 나를 가르쳤다. 그러나 춤으로 말하자면, 그들은 내가 컨트리 댄스를 배우는 것을 좀처럼 막을 수 없었으니, 짝수를 맞추느라 늘 내가 필요했기 때문이다. 또 다른 한편으로, 그들은 내가 배우기를 바라는 만큼이나 자기들이 배운 모든 것을 진심으로 기꺼이 내게 가르쳐 주려 했다."]], [["By this means I had, as I have said above, all the advantages of education that I could have had if I had been as much a gentlewoman as they were with whom I lived; and in some things I had the advantage of my ladies, though they were my superiors; but they were all the gifts of nature, and which all their fortunes could not furnish. First, I was apparently handsomer than any of them; secondly, I was better shaped; and, thirdly, I sang better, by which I mean I had a better voice; in all which you will, I hope, allow me to say, I do not speak my own conceit of myself, but the opinion of all that knew the family.", "이런 식으로 나는, 앞서 말했듯이, 내가 함께 살던 이들만큼이나 어엿한 귀부인이었더라도 누릴 수 있었을 온갖 교육의 혜택을 다 누렸다. 그리고 어떤 것에서는, 비록 그들이 내 윗사람이긴 했어도, 내가 내 아가씨들보다 나았다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 타고난 천부의 선물이었으니, 그들의 재산을 다 들여도 갖출 수 없는 것이었다. 첫째로, 나는 보아하니 그들 누구보다 잘생겼고, 둘째로, 몸매가 더 나았으며, 셋째로, 노래를 더 잘했으니, 이 말은 내 목소리가 더 좋았다는 뜻이다. 이 모든 것에 대해, 여러분도 부디 내가 나 자신에 대한 자만을 말하는 것이 아니라, 그 집안을 아는 모든 이의 견해를 말하는 것이라고 인정해 주기를 바란다."]], [["I had with all these the common vanity of my sex, viz. that being really taken for very handsome, or, if you please, for a great beauty, I very well knew it, and had as good an opinion of myself as anybody else could have of me; and particularly I loved to hear anybody speak of it, which could not but happen to me sometimes, and was a great satisfaction to me.", "이 모든 것에 더해 나는 우리 여자들이 흔히 지니는 허영을 지니고 있었으니, 곧 실제로 매우 잘생겼다고, 혹은 여러분 좋을 대로 말하자면 굉장한 미인으로 여겨지자, 나는 그것을 잘 알았고, 다른 누가 나를 보는 만큼이나 나 자신을 좋게 여겼으며, 특히 누가 그 이야기를 하는 것을 듣기 좋아했는데, 그런 일이 때때로 내게 일어나지 않을 수 없었고, 그것이 내게는 큰 만족이었다."]], [["Thus far I have had a smooth story to tell of myself, and in all this part of my life I not only had the reputation of living in a very good family, and a family noted and respected everywhere for virtue and sobriety, and for every valuable thing; but I had the character too of a very sober, modest, and virtuous young woman, and such I had always been; neither had I yet any occasion to think of anything else, or to know what a temptation to wickedness meant.", "여기까지 나는 나 자신에 대해 순탄한 이야기를 들려주었고, 내 삶의 이 모든 부분에서 나는 매우 훌륭한 집안에서—덕과 진중함으로, 또 온갖 값진 것으로 어디서나 이름나고 존경받는 집안에서—산다는 평판을 누렸을 뿐 아니라, 매우 진중하고 정숙하며 덕스러운 처녀라는 평판도 누렸으니, 나는 늘 그러한 사람이었다. 또한 나는 아직 다른 무엇을 생각할 계기도 없었고, 사악함에 대한 유혹이 무엇인지 알 일도 없었다."]], [["But that which I was too vain of was my ruin, or rather my vanity was the cause of it.", "그러나 내가 너무 우쭐해했던 그것이 곧 내 파멸이었으니, 아니 차라리 내 허영이 그 원인이었다."], ["The lady in the house where I was had two sons, young gentlemen of very promising parts and of extraordinary behaviour, and it was my misfortune to be very well with them both, but they managed themselves with me in a quite different manner.", "내가 머물던 집의 부인에게는 아들이 둘 있었는데, 매우 장래가 촉망되는 자질과 더없이 훌륭한 행실을 지닌 젊은 신사들이었으나, 그 둘 다와 매우 친하게 지내게 된 것이 내 불운이었다. 그런데 그들은 나를 대하는 처신이 사뭇 달랐다."]], [["The eldest, a gay gentleman that knew the town as well as the country, and though he had levity enough to do an ill-natured thing, yet had too much judgment of things to pay too dear for his pleasures; he began with the unhappy snare to all women, viz. taking notice upon all occasions how pretty I was, as he called it, how agreeable, how well-carriaged, and the like; and this he contrived so subtly, as if he had known as well how to catch a woman in his net as a partridge when he went a-setting; for he would contrive to be talking this to his sisters when, though I was not by, yet when he knew I was not far off but that I should be sure to hear him. His sisters would return softly to him, “Hush, brother, she will hear you; she is but in the next room.” Then he would put it off and talk softlier, as if he had not known it, and begin to acknowledge he was wrong; and then, as if he had forgot himself, he would speak aloud again, and I, that was so well pleased to hear it, was sure to listen for it upon all occasions.", "큰아들은 도시 물정도 시골 물정도 잘 아는 쾌활한 신사였으니, 비록 짓궂은 짓을 저지를 만큼 경박하긴 했어도, 자기 쾌락의 대가를 너무 비싸게 치르지 않을 만큼은 사리 분별이 있었다. 그는 모든 여자에게 통하는 그 불행한 덫으로 시작했으니, 곧 기회 있을 때마다 내가—그의 표현대로 하자면—얼마나 예쁜지, 얼마나 상냥한지, 얼마나 몸가짐이 좋은지 따위를 짚어 주는 것이었다. 그리고 이것을 어찌나 교묘하게 꾸몄던지, 마치 자고새를 그물로 잡으러 나갈 때처럼 여자를 자기 그물에 옭아넣는 법을 잘 아는 사람 같았다. 그는 내가 곁에 없을 때, 그러나 멀리 있지 않아 분명히 듣게 될 줄 알 때를 골라, 그의 누이들에게 이런 말을 하곤 했기 때문이다. 그의 누이들은 그에게 나직이 되받아 말하곤 했다. \"쉿, 오라버니, 저 애가 듣겠어요. 바로 옆방에 있단 말이에요.\" 그러면 그는 그 말을 슬쩍 흘려넘기고 더 나직이 이야기하면서, 마치 몰랐다는 듯이 자기가 잘못했다고 인정하기 시작했다. 그러다가는 또 자기를 잊은 척 다시 큰 소리로 말했으니, 그것을 듣는 것이 그토록 흐뭇했던 나는, 기회 있을 때마다 영락없이 그 말에 귀를 기울이곤 했다."]], [["After he had thus baited his hook, and found easily enough the method how to lay it in my way, he played an opener game; and one day, going by his sister’s chamber when I was there, doing something about dressing her, he comes in with an air of gaiety.", "이렇게 미끼를 단단히 끼우고, 그것을 내 앞에 드리울 방법을 아주 손쉽게 찾아낸 다음, 그는 더 노골적인 수를 두었다. 어느 날, 내가 그의 누이 방에서 그녀의 몸단장을 거들고 있을 때, 그가 그 방 앞을 지나다가 쾌활한 기색으로 들어왔다."], ["“Oh, Mrs. Betty,”", "\"오, 베티 양.\""], ["said he to me, “how do you do, Mrs.", "그가 내게 말했다. \"잘 지내요, 베티 양?"], ["Betty? Don’t your cheeks burn, Mrs.", "두 볼이 화끈거리지 않아요, 베티 양?\""], ["Betty?” I made a curtsy and blushed, but said nothing.", "나는 인사를 올리며 얼굴을 붉혔으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["“What makes you talk so, brother?”", "\"왜 그런 소릴 하세요, 오라버니?\""], ["says the lady.", "아가씨가 말했다."], ["“Why,”", "\"있잖아,\""], ["says he, “we have been talking of her below-stairs this half-hour.”", "그가 말했다. \"우리가 아래층에서 반 시간이나 저 애 얘기를 했거든.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"글쎄요,\""], ["says his sister, “you can say no harm of her, that I am sure, so ’tis no matter what you have been talking about.”", "누이가 말했다. \"오라버니가 저 애 험담을 할 리는 없으니, 무슨 얘길 했든 상관없겠죠.\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says he, “’tis so far from talking harm of her, that we have been talking a great deal of good, and a great many fine things have been said of Mrs.", "그가 말했다. \"험담을 하기는커녕, 우린 좋은 얘길 잔뜩 했고, 베티 양에 대해 멋진 얘기가 많이 나왔다고, 정말이라니까."], ["Betty, I assure you; and particularly, that she is the handsomest young woman in Colchester; and, in short, they begin to toast her health in the town.”", "특히, 저 애가 콜체스터에서 제일 예쁜 처녀라고 말이야. 한마디로, 마을 사람들이 저 애의 건강을 위해 축배를 들기 시작했다고.\""]], [["“I wonder at you, brother,” says the sister. “Betty wants but one thing, but she had as good want everything, for the market is against our sex just now; and if a young woman have beauty, birth, breeding, wit, sense, manners, modesty, and all these to an extreme, yet if she have not money, she’s nobody, she had as good want them all for nothing but money now recommends a woman; the men play the game all into their own hands.”", "\"오라버니도 참 별나시네요.\" 누이가 말했다. \"베티는 딱 한 가지가 모자랄 뿐이지만, 그게 모자라느니 차라리 전부 다 모자라는 게 나아요. 요즘 시장이 우리 여자들에게 불리하니까요. 젊은 여자가 미모며, 가문이며, 교양이며, 재치며, 분별이며, 몸가짐이며, 정숙함이며, 이런 걸 죄다 극진히 갖추고 있다 해도, 돈이 없으면 아무것도 아니에요. 그 모든 게 다 있어도 헛일이라고요. 요즘은 돈만이 여자를 내세워 주거든요. 남자들이 패를 죄다 제 손아귀에 쥐고 노름을 하니까요.\""]], [["Her younger brother, who was by, cried, “Hold, sister, you run too fast; I am an exception to your rule.", "곁에 있던 그녀의 동생이 외쳤다. \"그만, 누님, 너무 앞서가시네요."], ["I assure you, if I find a woman so accomplished as you talk of, I say, I assure you, I would not trouble myself about the money.”", "난 누님 법칙의 예외라고요. 정말이지, 누님이 말씀하시는 것처럼 그렇게 다 갖춘 여자를 내가 만난다면, 정말이지, 돈 따위로는 골머리를 앓지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["says the sister, “but you will take care not to fancy one, then, without the money.”", "누이가 말했다. \"그럼 넌 돈 없는 여자한텐 마음을 두지 않도록 조심하겠구나.\""]], [["“You don’t know that neither,”", "\"그것도 모르는 일이죠.\""], ["says the brother.", "동생이 말했다."]], [["“But why, sister,”", "\"그런데 누님,\""], ["says the elder brother, “why do you exclaim so at the men for aiming so much at the fortune?", "큰아들이 말했다. \"왜 그렇게 남자들이 재산만 노린다고 외쳐 대는 거예요?"], ["You are none of them that want a fortune, whatever else you want.”", "누님은 다른 건 몰라도 재산이 모자란 사람은 아니잖아요.\""]], [["“I understand you, brother,”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 오라버니.\" 아가씨가 아주 똑 부러지게 받아쳤다."], ["replies the lady very smartly; “you suppose I have the money, and want the beauty; but as times go now, the first will do without the last, so I have the better of my neighbours.”", "\"내가 돈은 있고 미모가 모자란다는 거잖아요. 하지만 요즘 세상 돌아가는 걸 보면, 미모 없이도 돈만 있으면 되니까, 난 이웃들보다 나은 셈이죠.\""]], [["“Well,” says the younger brother, “but your neighbours, as you call them, may be even with you, for beauty will steal a husband sometimes in spite of money, and when the maid chances to be handsomer than the mistress, she oftentimes makes as good a market, and rides in a coach before her.”", "\"글쎄요,\" 동생이 말했다. \"하지만 누님이 말하는 그 이웃들도 누님과 어깨를 나란히 할 수 있어요. 미모는 때때로 돈을 제치고 남편을 가로채니까요. 그리고 하녀가 어쩌다 여주인보다 더 잘생긴 경우엔, 그 하녀가 종종 그만큼 좋은 혼처를 잡아서, 여주인보다 앞서 마차를 타고 다니기도 한단 말이에요.\""]], [["I thought it was time for me to withdraw and leave them, and I did so, but not so far but that I heard all their discourse, in which I heard abundance of the fine things said of myself, which served to prompt my vanity, but, as I soon found, was not the way to increase my interest in the family, for the sister and the younger brother fell grievously out about it; and as he said some very disobliging things to her upon my account, so I could easily see that she resented them by her future conduct to me, which indeed was very unjust to me, for I had never had the least thought of what she suspected as to her younger brother; indeed, the elder brother, in his distant, remote way, had said a great many things as in jest, which I had the folly to believe were in earnest, or to flatter myself with the hopes of what I ought to have supposed he never intended, and perhaps never thought of.", "이제는 내가 물러나 그들을 떠날 때라고 생각해서 그렇게 했으나, 그들의 이야기를 죄다 들을 만큼은 멀리 가지 않았으니, 거기서 나는 나 자신에 대한 멋진 말을 잔뜩 들었고, 그것이 내 허영을 부추기는 데에는 도움이 되었으나, 내가 곧 알게 되었듯이 이 집안에서 내 입지를 키우는 길은 아니었다. 누이와 동생이 그 일로 심하게 다투었기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 나 때문에 누이에게 매우 불쾌한 말을 몇 마디 했던 터라, 나는 그녀가 그 후 나를 대하는 태도로 보아 그 일을 마음에 두고 있음을 쉬이 알 수 있었으니, 그것은 사실 내게 매우 부당한 일이었다. 나는 그녀가 자기 동생에 대해 의심하는 그런 일을 추호도 생각해 본 적이 없었기 때문이다. 사실은, 큰아들이 그 멀찍하고 에두르는 방식으로 농담인 양 많은 말을 했던 것을, 내가 어리석게도 진심으로 믿었거나, 그가 결코 의도하지 않았을, 어쩌면 생각조차 한 적 없었을 것에 대한 희망으로 나 자신을 우쭐하게 했던 것이다."]], [["It happened one day that he came running upstairs, towards the room where his sisters used to sit and work, as he often used to do; and calling to them before he came in, as was his way too, I, being there alone, stepped to the door, and said, “Sir, the ladies are not here, they are walked down the garden.” As I stepped forward to say this, towards the door, he was just got to the door, and clasping me in his arms, as if it had been by chance, “Oh, Mrs. Betty,” says he, “are you here? That’s better still; I want to speak with you more than I do with them”; and then, having me in his arms, he kissed me three or four times.", "어느 날 그가 누이들이 으레 앉아서 일하던 방을 향해 계단을 뛰어 올라왔는데, 그가 자주 그러던 일이었다. 그리고 들어오기 전에 그들을 부르는 것 또한 그의 버릇이었던 터라, 마침 거기 혼자 있던 나는 문가로 다가가서 말했다. \"도련님, 아가씨들은 여기 안 계세요. 정원으로 산책 나가셨어요.\" 이 말을 하려고 문 쪽으로 한 걸음 나아가는데, 그가 막 문에 다다라서는, 마치 우연인 양 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았다. \"오, 베티 양.\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 있었군요? 그게 더 잘됐네요. 난 누이들보다 당신하고 얘기하고 싶었거든요.\" 그러고는 나를 품에 안은 채 서너 번 입을 맞췄다."]], [["I struggled to get away, and yet did it but faintly neither, and he held me fast, and still kissed me, till he was almost out of breath, and then, sitting down, says, “Dear Betty, I am in love with you.”", "나는 빠져나오려고 몸부림쳤으나, 그 몸부림조차 시늉뿐이었고, 그는 나를 단단히 붙들고 숨이 거의 차오를 때까지 계속 입을 맞추다가, 자리에 앉으며 말했다. \"사랑하는 베티, 난 당신을 사랑하고 있어요.\""]], [["His words, I must confess, fired my blood; all my spirits flew about my heart and put me into disorder enough, which he might easily have seen in my face.", "그의 말은, 고백하건대, 내 피를 달아오르게 했다. 내 정신이 죄다 가슴께로 몰려들어 나를 적잖이 어지럽게 했으니, 그는 그것을 내 얼굴에서 쉬이 알아보았을 것이다."], ["He repeated it afterwards several times, that he was in love with me, and my heart spoke as plain as a voice, that I liked it; nay, whenever he said, “I am in love with you,”", "그는 그 후에도 자기가 나를 사랑한다고 여러 차례 되풀이했고, 내 마음은 내가 그것을 좋아한다고 목소리처럼 또렷이 말하고 있었다. 아니, 그가 \"난 당신을 사랑하고 있어요\""], ["my blushes plainly replied, “Would you were, sir.”", "하고 말할 때마다, 내 붉어진 두 볼이 또렷이 대답했다. \"정말 그러시다면 좋겠어요, 도련님.\""]], [["However, nothing else passed at that time; it was but a surprise, and when he was gone I soon recovered myself again.", "그러나 그때는 그 밖에 다른 일은 없었다. 그저 불시의 일이었고, 그가 가고 나자 나는 곧 마음을 추슬렀다."], ["He had stayed longer with me, but he happened to look out at the window and see his sisters coming up the garden, so he took his leave, kissed me again, told me he was very serious, and I should hear more of him very quickly, and away he went, leaving me infinitely pleased, though surprised; and had there not been one misfortune in it, I had been in the right, but the mistake lay here, that Mrs.", "그는 나와 더 오래 머물렀을 테지만, 마침 창밖을 내다보다가 누이들이 정원을 올라오는 것을 보고는 작별을 고했고, 다시 내게 입을 맞추고는, 자기는 아주 진지하며 머지않아 곧 자기 소식을 듣게 될 거라고 말하고 떠나갔으니, 비록 얼떨떨하긴 했어도 나를 한없이 기쁘게 한 채였다."], ["Betty was in earnest and the gentleman was not.", "그리고 여기에 한 가지 불행만 끼어 있지 않았다면 나는 옳게 본 것이었으련만, 그 착오는 바로 여기에 있었으니, 베티 양은 진심이었고 그 신사는 진심이 아니었다는 것이다."]], [["From this time my head ran upon strange things, and I may truly say I was not myself; to have such a gentleman talk to me of being in love with me, and of my being such a charming creature, as he told me I was; these were things I knew not how to bear, my vanity was elevated to the last degree. It is true I had my head full of pride, but, knowing nothing of the wickedness of the times, I had not one thought of my own safety or of my virtue about me; and had my young master offered it at first sight, he might have taken any liberty he thought fit with me; but he did not see his advantage, which was my happiness for that time.", "이때부터 내 머릿속은 야릇한 것들로 가득 찼고, 정말이지 나는 내가 아니었다고 말할 수 있다. 그런 신사가 내게 나를 사랑하고 있다는 둥, 그가 말하는 대로 내가 그토록 매혹적인 여인이라는 둥 이야기하는 것—이것은 내가 도무지 어찌 감당해야 할지 모를 일이었고, 내 허영은 더할 나위 없이 드높아졌다. 사실 내 머릿속은 자만으로 가득 차 있었으나, 시류의 사악함을 전혀 몰랐던 터라, 내 안전이나 내 정조에 대해서는 한 가닥 생각도 없었다. 그래서 만약 그 젊은 주인이 처음 마주쳤을 때 그것을 시도했더라면, 그는 나를 두고 마음 내키는 대로 어떤 자유든 누릴 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 그는 자기에게 유리한 것을 알아보지 못했으니, 그것이 그때만큼은 내 다행이었다."]], [["After this attack it was not long but he found an opportunity to catch me again, and almost in the same posture; indeed, it had more of design in it on his part, though not on my part.", "이 공세가 있은 뒤 얼마 지나지 않아, 그는 나를 다시 붙잡을 기회를, 그것도 거의 같은 자세로 붙잡을 기회를 찾아냈다. 사실 이번에는 그의 쪽에 더 많은 꿍꿍이가 있었으나, 내 쪽은 그렇지 않았다."], ["It was thus: the young ladies were all gone a-visiting with their mother; his brother was out of town; and as for his father, he had been in London for a week before.", "일은 이러했다. 젊은 아가씨들은 모두 어머니와 함께 방문을 나갔고, 그의 형제는 마을 밖에 나가 있었으며, 그의 아버지로 말하자면 한 주 전부터 런던에 가 있었다."], ["He had so well watched me that he knew where I was, though I did not so much as know that he was in the house; and he briskly comes up the stairs and, seeing me at work, comes into the room to me directly, and began just as he did before, with taking me in his arms, and kissing me for almost a quarter of an hour together.", "그는 나를 어찌나 잘 지켜보았던지, 내가 어디 있는지 알고 있었으나, 나는 그가 집에 있는 줄조차 모르고 있었다. 그가 잰걸음으로 계단을 올라와, 내가 일하고 있는 것을 보고는 곧장 내게로 방에 들어와, 전에 했던 그대로 나를 품에 안고 거의 십오 분 동안 줄곧 입을 맞추기 시작했다."]], [["It was his younger sister’s chamber that I was in, and as there was nobody in the house but the maids below-stairs, he was, it may be, the ruder; in short, he began to be in earnest with me indeed.", "내가 있던 방은 그의 손아래 누이의 방이었는데, 집 안에는 아래층의 하녀들 말고는 아무도 없었던 터라, 그는 어쩌면 더 거리낌이 없었을지 모른다. 한마디로, 그는 정말로 내게 진심으로 굴기 시작했다."], ["Perhaps he found me a little too easy, for God knows I made no resistance to him while he only held me in his arms and kissed me; indeed, I was too well pleased with it to resist him much.", "어쩌면 그는 내가 좀 지나치게 호락호락하다고 여겼을지 모르니, 하느님은 아시거니와, 그가 그저 나를 품에 안고 입을 맞추는 동안 나는 그에게 아무런 저항도 하지 않았기 때문이다. 사실 나는 그것이 너무도 흐뭇해서 그에게 그리 저항할 수가 없었다."]], [["However, as it were, tired with that kind of work, we sat down, and there he talked with me a great while; he said he was charmed with me, and that he could not rest night or day till he had told me how he was in love with me, and, if I was able to love him again, and would make him happy, I should be the saving of his life, and many such fine things.", "그러나 어쨌든 그런 일에 지쳤다는 듯이, 우리는 자리에 앉았고, 거기서 그는 나와 한참 동안 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 내게 흠뻑 반했다고, 또 자기가 얼마나 나를 사랑하는지 내게 말하기 전까지는 밤이고 낮이고 도무지 쉴 수가 없다고, 그리고 만약 내가 그를 마주 사랑할 수 있어서 그를 행복하게 해 준다면, 내가 그의 목숨을 구하는 셈이 될 거라고, 그 밖에도 그런 멋진 말을 많이 했다."], ["I said little to him again, but easily discovered that I was a fool, and that I did not in the least perceive what he meant.", "나는 그에게 다시 별말을 하지 않았으나, 내가 바보라는 것을, 그가 무엇을 뜻하는지 조금도 알아차리지 못하고 있다는 것을 쉬이 드러내고 말았다."]], [["Then he walked about the room, and taking me by the hand, I walked with him; and by and by, taking his advantage, he threw me down upon the bed, and kissed me there most violently; but, to give him his due, offered no manner of rudeness to me, only kissed a great while. After this he thought he had heard somebody come upstairs, so got off from the bed, lifted me up, professing a great deal of love for me, but told me it was all an honest affection, and that he meant no ill to me; and with that he put five guineas into my hand, and went away downstairs.", "그러고 나서 그는 방 안을 거닐다가 내 손을 잡았고, 나는 그와 함께 거닐었다. 그러다가 이내 기회를 틈타, 그는 나를 침대 위로 밀어 넘어뜨리고는 거기서 더없이 격렬하게 입을 맞췄다. 그러나 그의 공을 정당히 인정해 주자면, 그는 내게 어떤 무례한 짓도 시도하지 않았고, 그저 오래도록 입을 맞추기만 했다. 이러고 나서 그는 누가 계단을 올라오는 소리를 들은 듯하여 침대에서 일어나 나를 일으켜 세우고는, 나에 대한 사랑을 한껏 늘어놓으면서도, 그것이 모두 정직한 애정일 뿐이고 내게 나쁜 뜻은 없다고 말했다. 그러고는 그 말과 함께 내 손에 5기니를 쥐여 주고 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["I was more confounded with the money than I was before with the love, and began to be so elevated that I scarce knew the ground I stood on.", "나는 그 돈에, 앞서 그 사랑에 그랬던 것보다 더 얼떨떨해졌고, 어찌나 우쭐해졌던지 내가 딛고 선 땅이 어딘지도 거의 알 수 없을 지경이었다."], ["I am the more particular in this part, that if my story comes to be read by any innocent young body, they may learn from it to guard themselves against the mischiefs which attend an early knowledge of their own beauty.", "내가 이 대목을 더 소상히 적는 것은, 만약 내 이야기가 어떤 순진한 젊은이의 손에 읽히게 된다면, 그가 이것을 보고 자기 자신의 미모를 일찍 깨닫는 데 따르는 화를 경계하는 법을 배우기를 바라기 때문이다."], ["If a young woman once thinks herself handsome, she never doubts the truth of any man that tells her he is in love with her; for if she believes herself charming enough to captivate him, ’tis natural to expect the effects of it.", "젊은 여자가 일단 제가 잘생겼다고 여기게 되면, 자기를 사랑한다고 말하는 어떤 남자의 진심도 결코 의심하지 않는 법이다. 자기가 그를 사로잡을 만큼 매혹적이라고 믿으면, 그 효과를 기대하는 것이 자연스러운 노릇이기 때문이다."]], [["This young gentleman had fired his inclination as much as he had my vanity, and, as if he had found that he had an opportunity and was sorry he did not take hold of it, he comes up again in half an hour or thereabouts, and falls to work with me again as before, only with a little less introduction.", "이 젊은 신사는 내 허영을 부추긴 만큼이나 자기 욕정도 달구어 놓았으니, 마치 기회가 있었는데도 그것을 붙잡지 못한 것을 뉘우치기라도 한 듯, 반 시간쯤 뒤에 다시 올라와서는, 다만 운만 조금 덜 떼고서, 전처럼 나를 상대로 일을 벌이기 시작했다."]], [["And first, when he entered the room, he turned about and shut the door.", "그리고 우선, 방에 들어오자 그는 몸을 돌려 문을 닫았다. \"베티 양.\""], ["“Mrs.", "그가 말했다."], ["Betty,”", "\"아까는 누가 계단을 올라오나 했는데, 그게 아니었어요. 하지만,\""], ["said he, “I fancied before somebody was coming upstairs, but it was not so; however,”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"그들이 나를 당신과 한 방에서 보더라도, 당신한테 입 맞추는 걸 들키진 않을 테니까.\""], ["adds he, “if they find me in the room with you, they shan’t catch me a-kissing of you.”", "나는 그에게 누가 계단을 올라올 리는 없다고 말했으니, 집 안에는 요리사와 다른 하녀 말고는 아무도 없을 텐데, 그들은 그 계단으로는 결코 올라오지 않기 때문이라고 했다. \"그래, 내 사랑.\""], ["I told him I did not know who should be coming upstairs, for I believed there was nobody in the house but the cook and the other maid, and they never came up those stairs.", "그가 말했다. \"그래도 확실히 해 두는 게 좋지.\""], ["“Well, my dear,”", "그러고는 자리에 앉았고, 우리는 이야기를 나누기 시작했다."], ["says he, “’tis good to be sure, however”; and so he sits down, and we began to talk.", "그리고 이제, 비록 나는 그의 첫 번째 방문 때부터 여전히 온통 달아올라 있어서 말을 거의 하지 않았지만, 그는 말하자면 내 입에 말을 넣어 주듯이, 자기가 얼마나 열렬히 나를 사랑하는지, 또 이 재산을 물려받기 전까지는 그런 일을 입에 올릴 수 없으나, 그때는 기필코 나를 행복하게 해 주고 자기 자신도 행복해지기로 마음먹었다고—다시 말해, 나와 결혼하겠다고—그 밖에도 그런 멋진 말을 잔뜩 늘어놓았다. 가엾은 바보였던 나는 그 속내를 알아차리지 못했으나, 마치 혼인으로 이어지는 사랑 말고는 그 어떤 종류의 사랑도 없다는 듯이 굴었다."], ["And now, though I was still all on fire with his first visit, and said little, he did as it were put words in my mouth, telling me how passionately he loved me, and that though he could not mention such a thing till he came to this estate, yet he was resolved to make me happy then, and himself too; that is to say, to marry me, and abundance of such fine things, which I, poor fool, did not understand the drift of, but acted as if there was no such thing as any kind of love but that which tended to matrimony; and if he had spoke of that, I had no room, as well as no power, to have said no; but we were not come that length yet.", "그래서 만약 그가 그것을 입에 올렸더라면, 나는 싫다고 말할 여지도, 또 그럴 힘도 없었을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 아직 거기까지는 이르지 않았다."]], [["We had not sat long, but he got up, and, stopping my very breath with kisses, threw me upon the bed again; but then being both well warmed, he went farther with me than decency permits me to mention, nor had it been in my power to have denied him at that moment, had he offered much more than he did.", "우리가 앉은 지 오래지 않아, 그는 일어나서 입맞춤으로 내 숨통을 아예 막으며 나를 다시 침대 위로 밀어 넘어뜨렸다. 그러나 그때는 둘 다 한껏 달아올라 있었던 터라, 그는 점잖게 입에 담을 수 있는 선을 넘어 나에게 더 나아갔으니, 그 순간 그가 실제로 한 것보다 훨씬 더한 것을 시도했다 한들 나로서는 그를 거절할 힘도 없었을 것이다."]], [["However, though he took these freedoms with me, it did not go to that which they call the last favour, which, to do him justice, he did not attempt; and he made that self-denial of his a plea for all his freedoms with me upon other occasions after this.", "그러나 비록 그가 나를 상대로 이런 자유를 누리긴 했어도, 사람들이 마지막 호의라 부르는 그것에까지 이르지는 않았으니, 그의 공을 정당히 인정해 주자면, 그는 그것을 시도하지 않았다. 그리고 그는 이 자제를, 그 후 다른 경우에 나를 상대로 누린 자기의 온갖 자유를 위한 구실로 삼았다."], ["When this was over, he stayed but a little while, but he put almost a handful of gold in my hand, and left me, making a thousand protestations of his passion for me, and of his loving me above all the women in the world.", "이 일이 끝나자 그는 잠깐만 더 머물렀으나, 내 손에 거의 한 줌이나 되는 금화를 쥐여 주고는, 나를 향한 자기의 정열을, 또 세상 어떤 여자보다 나를 사랑한다는 것을 수천 번이나 다짐하면서 나를 떠났다."]], [["It will not be strange if I now began to think, but alas!", "내가 이제 생각이라는 것을 하기 시작했다 해도 이상할 것은 없을 터이나, 아아!"], ["it was but with very little solid reflection.", "그것은 견실한 성찰이라고는 거의 없는 생각이었다."], ["I had a most unbounded stock of vanity and pride, and but a very little stock of virtue.", "나는 한없이 풍성한 허영과 자만은 지녔으나, 덕이라고는 아주 조금밖에 지니지 못했다. 사실 나는 이따금 젊은 주인이 무엇을 노리는지 혼자 따져 보기는 했으나, 그 멋진 말과 금화 말고는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다."], ["I did indeed case sometimes with myself what young master aimed at, but thought of nothing but the fine words and the gold; whether he intended to marry me, or not to marry me, seemed a matter of no great consequence to me; nor did my thoughts so much as suggest to me the necessity of making any capitulation for myself, till he came to make a kind of formal proposal to me, as you shall hear presently.", "그가 나와 결혼할 작정인지, 결혼하지 않을 작정인지는 내게 별로 중대한 일로 여겨지지 않았다. 또한 그가 머지않아 듣게 될 것처럼 내게 일종의 격식 차린 제안을 하기에 이르기 전까지는, 내 생각이 나 자신을 위해 어떤 조건을 따져 두어야 할 필요성을 내게 일러 주지조차 않았다."]], [["Thus I gave up myself to a readiness of being ruined without the least concern and am a fair memento to all young women whose vanity prevails over their virtue.", "그리하여 나는 추호의 거리낌도 없이 스스로를 파멸당할 채비에 내맡겼으니, 나는 덕보다 허영이 앞서는 모든 젊은 여자에게 좋은 경계가 되는 본보기다."], ["Nothing was ever so stupid on both sides.", "양쪽 다 그토록 어리석은 적은 일찍이 없었다."], ["Had I acted as became me, and resisted as virtue and honour require, this gentleman had either desisted his attacks, finding no room to expect the accomplishment of his design, or had made fair and honourable proposals of marriage; in which case, whoever had blamed him, nobody could have blamed me.", "만약 내가 마땅히 그래야 할 대로 처신하고, 덕과 명예가 요구하는 대로 저항했더라면, 이 신사는 자기 속셈을 이룰 가망이 없음을 알고 공세를 그만두었거나, 아니면 떳떳하고 명예로운 혼인의 제안을 했을 것이다. 그랬더라면 누가 그를 탓했든, 아무도 나를 탓할 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["In short, if he had known me, and how easy the trifle he aimed at was to be had, he would have troubled his head no farther, but have given me four or five guineas, and have lain with me the next time he had come at me.", "한마디로, 만약 그가 나를 알았더라면, 또 그가 노리던 그 하찮은 것이 얼마나 쉽게 손에 넣을 수 있는 것인지 알았더라면, 그는 더는 골머리를 앓지 않고 내게 네댓 기니를 주고는 다음번에 나를 찾아왔을 때 동침했을 것이다. 그리고 만약 내가 그의 속내를, 또 그가 나를 손에 넣기를 얼마나 어렵게 여기는지 알았더라면, 나는 그와 내 나름의 조건을 정할 수 있었을 것이다."], ["And if I had known his thoughts, and how hard he thought I would be to be gained, I might have made my own terms with him; and if I had not capitulated for an immediate marriage, I might for a maintenance till marriage, and might have had what I would; for he was already rich to excess, besides what he had in expectation; but I seemed wholly to have abandoned all such thoughts as these, and was taken up only with the pride of my beauty, and of being beloved by such a gentleman.", "당장의 혼인을 조건으로 못 박지는 못했더라도, 혼인할 때까지의 생계비를 조건으로 삼을 수는 있었을 것이고, 원하는 것을 얻었을 것이다. 그는 앞으로 물려받을 것은 차치하고도 이미 차고 넘치게 부유했기 때문이다."], ["As for the gold, I spent whole hours in looking upon it; I told the guineas over and over a thousand times a day.", "그러나 나는 이런 생각 따위는 죄다 내팽개친 듯했고, 오직 내 미모에 대한, 또 그런 신사에게 사랑받는다는 자만에 사로잡혀 있을 뿐이었다. 그 금화로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 들여다보며 몇 시간이고 보냈고, 하루에도 천 번씩 그 기니를 세고 또 셌다."], ["Never a poor vain creature was so wrapt up with every part of the story as I was, not considering what was before me, and how near my ruin was at the door; indeed, I think I rather wished for that ruin than studied to avoid it.", "일찍이 어떤 가엾고 헛된 것도 나만큼 그 이야기의 구석구석에 푹 빠져들지는 못했을 터이니, 내 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지, 또 내 파멸이 문 앞에 얼마나 가까이 와 있는지는 헤아리지 못했다. 사실, 나는 그 파멸을 피하려 애쓰기는커녕 오히려 그것을 바랐던 것 같다."]], [["In the meantime, however, I was cunning enough not to give the least room to any in the family to suspect me, or to imagine that I had the least correspondence with this young gentleman.", "그러나 그러는 동안에도, 나는 집안의 누구에게도 나를 의심할 빌미를, 혹은 내가 이 젊은 신사와 조금이라도 내통하고 있다고 상상할 빌미를 조금도 주지 않을 만큼은 영리했다. 사람들 앞에서 나는 그를 거의 쳐다보지도 않았고, 누가 우리 가까이 있을 때 그가 내게 말을 걸어도 대꾸하지 않았다."], ["I scarce ever looked towards him in public, or answered if he spoke to me when anybody was near us; but for all that, we had every now and then a little encounter, where we had room for a word or two, and now and then a kiss, but no fair opportunity for the mischief intended; and especially considering that he made more circumlocution than, if he had known my thoughts, he had occasion for; and the work appearing difficult to him, he really made it so.", "그래도 우리는 이따금 작은 만남을 가져, 한두 마디 나눌 짬과, 때로는 입맞춤을 나눌 짬을 얻긴 했으나, 의도된 그 못된 짓을 벌일 마땅한 기회는 없었다. 게다가 그는, 만약 내 속내를 알았더라면 필요하지도 않았을 만큼 빙빙 에둘렀는데, 그래서 그 일이 그에게 어려워 보였고, 그가 정말로 그것을 어렵게 만든 셈이었다."]], [["But as the devil is an unwearied tempter, so he never fails to find opportunity for that wickedness he invites to.", "그러나 악마는 지칠 줄 모르는 유혹자인지라, 자기가 부추기는 그 사악함을 위한 기회를 결코 찾아내지 못하는 법이 없다."], ["It was one evening that I was in the garden, with his two younger sisters and himself, and all very innocently merry, when he found means to convey a note into my hand, by which he directed me to understand that he would to-morrow desire me publicly to go of an errand for him into the town, and that I should see him somewhere by the way.", "어느 날 저녁, 내가 그의 두 손아래 누이와 그와 함께 정원에 있으면서, 다들 더없이 천진하게 흥겨워하고 있을 때, 그는 내 손에 쪽지 하나를 슬쩍 건넬 방도를 찾아냈다. 그 쪽지로 그는 내게, 내일 사람들 앞에서 마을로 자기 심부름을 좀 다녀와 달라고 청할 테니, 가는 길 어디쯤에서 자기를 보게 될 거라는 뜻을 알렸다."]], [["Accordingly, after dinner, he very gravely says to me, his sisters being all by, “Mrs.", "이에 따라, 식사 후에 그는 누이들이 모두 곁에 있는데도 내게 아주 점잖게 말했다."], ["Betty, I must ask a favour of you.”", "\"베티 양, 부탁 하나 해야겠는데.\""], ["“What’s that?”", "\"그게 뭔데요?\""], ["says his second sister.", "그의 둘째 누이가 말했다."], ["“Nay, sister,”", "\"아니, 누이야,\""], ["says he very gravely, “if you can’t spare Mrs.", "그가 아주 점잖게 말했다."], ["Betty to-day, any other time will do.”", "\"오늘 베티 양을 내줄 수 없거든, 다른 때라도 괜찮아.\""], ["Yes, they said, they could spare her well enough, and the sister begged pardon for asking, which they did but of mere course, without any meaning.", "그러자 그들은 베티를 충분히 내줄 수 있다고 말했고, 둘째 누이는 물어봐서 미안하다고 했으나, 그것은 그저 으레 하는 말일 뿐 아무 뜻도 없었다."], ["“Well, but, brother,”", "\"그래도, 오라버니,\""], ["says the eldest sister, “you must tell Mrs.", "맏누이가 말했다. \"베티 양한테 그게 뭔지 말해 줘야죠."], ["Betty what it is; if it be any private business that we must not hear, you may call her out.", "우리가 들으면 안 되는 무슨 은밀한 일이거든, 저 애를 밖으로 불러내시든지요."], ["There she is.”", "저기 있잖아요.\""], ["“Why, sister,”", "\"원, 누이야,\""], ["says the gentleman very gravely, “what do you mean?", "그 신사가 아주 점잖게 말했다. \"무슨 소리야?"], ["I only desire her to go into the High Street”", "난 그저 저 애더러 큰길에 좀 가 달라는 거라고\"(그러고는 깃 장식 하나를 꺼냈다), \"어느 가게에 말이야.\""], ["(and then he pulls out a turnover), “to such a shop”; and then he tells them a long story of two fine neckcloths he had bid money for, and he wanted to have me go and make an errand to buy a neck to the turnover that he showed, to see if they would take my money for the neckcloths; to bid a shilling more, and haggle with them; and then he made more errands, and so continued to have such petty business to do, that I should be sure to stay a good while.", "그러고는 자기가 값을 매겨 둔 고운 목수건 두 장에 대해 한참 이야기를 늘어놓더니, 자기가 보여 준 그 깃 장식에 맞는 목 장식을 사러 가는 척 심부름을 가서, 그들이 그 목수건을 내 돈에 내줄지 알아봐 달라고, 1실링을 더 부르면서 그들과 흥정해 보라고 했다. 그러고는 또 다른 심부름들을 보태고, 그렇게 해서 내가 분명 한참 걸려 있게끔 그런 자질구레한 볼일을 계속 만들어 냈다."]], [["When he had given me my errands, he told them a long story of a visit he was going to make to a family they all knew, and where was to be such-and-such gentlemen, and how merry they were to be, and very formally asks his sisters to go with him, and they as formally excused themselves, because of company that they had notice was to come and visit them that afternoon; which, by the way, he had contrived on purpose.", "내게 심부름을 다 일러 준 다음, 그는 그들 모두가 아는 어느 집안을 방문하러 갈 참이라며 한참 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 거기에 이러이러한 신사들이 올 것이고, 다들 얼마나 흥겨울지 따위를 말하고는, 아주 격식을 차려 누이들에게 함께 가자고 청했다. 그러나 그들도 그만큼 격식을 차려 거절했으니, 그날 오후에 자기들을 찾아오기로 기별이 와 있는 손님들 때문이라는 것이었다. 그런데 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 그것은 그가 일부러 꾸며 둔 일이었다."]], [["He had scarce done speaking to them, and giving me my errand, but his man came up to tell him that Sir W—— H——’s coach stopped at the door; so he runs down, and comes up again immediately.", "그가 그들에게 말을 마치고 내게 심부름을 다 일러 주기가 무섭게, 그의 하인이 올라와서, W—— H—— 경의 마차가 문 앞에 멈춰 섰다고 알렸다. 그러자 그는 뛰어 내려갔다가 곧장 다시 올라왔다."], ["“Alas!”", "\"아아!\""], ["says he aloud, “there’s all my mirth spoiled at once; sir W—— has sent his coach for me, and desires to speak with me upon some earnest business.”", "그가 큰 소리로 말했다. \"내 즐거움이 단번에 죄다 망가지는군."], ["It seems this Sir W—— was a gentleman who lived about three miles out of town, to whom he had spoken on purpose the day before, to lend him his chariot for a particular occasion, and had appointed it to call for him, as it did, about three o’clock.", "W—— 경께서 나를 데려가려고 마차를 보내셨는데, 무슨 긴한 일로 나와 이야기를 나누고 싶어 하시네.\" 알고 보니 이 W—— 경은 마을에서 삼 마일쯤 떨어진 곳에 사는 신사였는데, 그가 전날 일부러 그에게 말을 넣어, 어떤 특별한 일에 쓰게 자기에게 마차를 빌려 달라고 했고, 세 시쯤에 자기를 데리러 오도록 정해 두었던 것이며, 정말 그대로 마차가 온 것이었다."]], [["Immediately he calls for his best wig, hat, and sword, and ordering his man to go to the other place to make his excuse— that was to say, he made an excuse to send his man away—he prepares to go into the coach.", "그는 곧장 자기의 가장 좋은 가발과 모자와 칼을 가져오라 이르고는, 하인에게 그 다른 곳으로 가서 자기 사정을 전하라고 명했으니—다시 말해, 그는 핑계를 대어 하인을 멀리 보내 버린 것이었다—마차에 오를 채비를 했다."], ["As he was going, he stopped a while, and speaks mighty earnestly to me about his business, and finds an opportunity to say very softly to me, “Come away, my dear, as soon as ever you can.”", "그는 가려다가 잠시 멈춰 서서 자기 볼일에 대해 내게 무척 진지하게 이야기하더니, 기회를 틈타 아주 나직이 내게 말했다. \"어서 빠져나와요, 내 사랑, 할 수 있는 한 빨리.\""], ["I said nothing, but made a curtsy, as if I had done so to what he said in public.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 마치 그가 사람들 앞에서 한 말에 대답하기라도 하듯 인사를 올렸다."], ["In about a quarter of an hour I went out too; I had no dress other than before, except that I had a hood, a mask, a fan, and a pair of gloves in my pocket; so that there was not the least suspicion in the house.", "십오 분쯤 뒤에 나도 나갔다. 나는 전과 다름없는 차림이었으나, 다만 주머니에 두건과 가면과 부채와 장갑 한 켤레가 들어 있을 뿐이었으니, 그래서 집 안에서는 추호의 의심도 사지 않았다."], ["He waited for me in the coach in a back-lane, which he knew I must pass by, and had directed the coachman whither to go, which was to a certain place, called Mile End, where lived a confidant of his, where we went in, and where was all the convenience in the world to be as wicked as we pleased.", "그는 내가 반드시 지나가게 될 줄 아는 어느 뒷골목에서 마차에 탄 채 나를 기다리고 있었고, 마부에게 어디로 갈지 일러 두었으니, 곧 마일엔드라는 어떤 곳이었다. 거기에는 그의 심복이 하나 살고 있었는데, 우리는 그 집으로 들어갔고, 거기에는 우리가 마음껏 사악하게 굴 수 있는 온갖 편의가 다 갖추어져 있었다."]], [["When we were together he began to talk very gravely to me, and to tell me he did not bring me there to betray me; that his passion for me would not suffer him to abuse me; that he resolved to marry me as soon as he came to his estate; that in the meantime, if I would grant his request, he would maintain me very honourably; and made me a thousand protestations of his sincerity and of his affection to me; and that he would never abandon me, and as I may say, made a thousand more preambles than he need to have done.", "우리가 함께 있게 되자, 그는 내게 아주 점잖게 이야기하기 시작했으니, 나를 배신하려고 거기 데려온 것이 아니라고, 나를 향한 자기의 정열이 나를 욕보이는 것을 결코 용납하지 않는다고, 자기는 그 즉시 나와 결혼하기로 마음먹었다고… 썬 그가 자기 영지를 물려받게 되면, 그동안에 내가 자기 청을 들어주기만 한다면 나를 아주 떳떳하게 거두어 주겠노라고, 자신의 진실함과 나를 향한 애정을 두고 천 번이고 맹세하면서, 결코 나를 버리지 않겠노라고, 말하자면 굳이 늘어놓을 필요도 없는 서두를 천 번이나 더 늘어놓았다."]], [["However, as he pressed me to speak, I told him I had no reason to question the sincerity of his love to me after so many protestations, but—and there I stopped, as if I left him to guess the rest.", "그러나 그가 어서 말하라고 다그치자, 나는 그토록 거듭된 맹세를 들은 뒤이니 나를 향한 그의 사랑이 진실한가를 의심할 까닭은 없으나, 다만— 하고 거기서 말을 멈추었으니, 나머지는 그가 짐작하도록 내버려 두려는 듯했다."], ["“But what, my dear?” says he.", "\"다만 무엇이오, 내 사랑?\""], ["“I guess what you mean: what if you should be with child?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 무슨 말을 하려는지 짐작이 가오."], ["Is not that it? Why, then,”", "혹시 아이라도 들어서면 어쩌나, 그것 아니오?"], ["says he, “I’ll take care of you and provide for you, and the child too; and that you may see I am not in jest,”", "아니, 그렇다면,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 당신을 보살피고 당신을 돌보아 주겠소, 아이까지도 말이오."], ["says he, “here’s an earnest for you,”", "그리고 내가 농담을 하는 게 아니라는 걸 당신이 알 수 있도록,\""], ["and with that he pulls out a silk purse, with an hundred guineas in it, and gave it me.", "하고 그가 말하더니, \"여기 당신을 위한 계약금이 있소,\" 하면서 백 기니가 든 비단 지갑을 꺼내어 내게 주었다."], ["“And I’ll give you such another,”", "\"그리고 당신과 혼인할 때까지 해마다 이만한 것을 또 주리다,\""], ["says he, “every year till I marry you.”", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["My colour came and went, at the sight of the purse and with the fire of his proposal together, so that I could not say a word, and he easily perceived it; so putting the purse into my bosom, I made no more resistance to him, but let him do just what he pleased, and as often as he pleased; and thus I finished my own destruction at once, for from this day, being forsaken of my virtue and my modesty, I had nothing of value left to recommend me, either to God’s blessing or man’s assistance.", "지갑을 본 데다 그의 제안의 불길까지 겹쳐, 얼굴빛이 붉어졌다 가셨다 하니, 나는 한마디도 할 수가 없었고, 그는 그것을 쉽사리 알아챘다. 그리하여 지갑을 내 품속에 넣어 주자, 나는 더는 그에게 저항하지 않고, 그가 하고 싶은 대로, 또 하고 싶은 만큼 자주 하도록 내버려 두었다. 이렇게 나는 단숨에 나 자신의 파멸을 완성하고 말았으니, 이날부터 정조와 정숙함에 버림받은 나에게는, 하느님의 축복에든 사람의 도움에든 나를 천거할 만한 값진 것이 아무것도 남지 않게 되었다."]], [["But things did not end here.", "그러나 일은 여기서 끝나지 않았다."], ["I went back to the town, did the business he publicly directed me to, and was at home before anybody thought me long.", "나는 마을로 돌아가, 그가 공공연히 시킨 심부름을 처리하고, 아무도 내가 오래 걸렸다고 여기기 전에 집에 돌아왔다."], ["As for my gentleman, he stayed out, as he told me he would, till late at night, and there was not the least suspicion in the family either on his account or on mine.", "그분으로 말하자면, 그는 자기가 그러겠노라고 내게 말한 대로 밤늦도록 밖에 머물러 있었고, 그의 일로든 내 일로든 집안에는 추호의 의심도 없었다."]], [["We had, after this, frequent opportunities to repeat our crime—chiefly by his contrivance—especially at home, when his mother and the young ladies went abroad a-visiting, which he watched so narrowly as never to miss; knowing always beforehand when they went out, and then failed not to catch me all alone, and securely enough; so that we took our fill of our wicked pleasure for near half a year; and yet, which was the most to my satisfaction, I was not with child.", "이후로 우리는 우리의 죄를 되풀이할 기회를 자주 가졌으니—주로 그의 궁리에 의해서였다—특히 집안에서, 그의 어머니와 아가씨들이 방문을 나갔을 때였는데, 그는 그것을 어찌나 빈틈없이 살피던지 한 번도 놓치는 법이 없었다. 늘 그들이 언제 나가는지를 미리 알고 있다가, 어김없이 나를 아주 안전하게 단둘이 붙들었으니, 그리하여 우리는 반년 가까이 우리의 사악한 쾌락을 마음껏 누렸다. 그러면서도 무엇보다 나를 흡족하게 한 것은, 내가 아이를 배지 않았다는 사실이었다."]], [["But before this half-year was expired, his younger brother, of whom I have made some mention in the beginning of the story, falls to work with me; and he, finding me alone in the garden one evening, begins a story of the same kind to me, made good honest professions of being in love with me, and in short, proposes fairly and honourably to marry me, and that before he made any other offer to me at all.", "그러나 이 반년이 다 가기 전에, 이야기 첫머리에서 얼마간 언급한 바 있는 그의 동생이 내게 수작을 걸기 시작했으니, 어느 날 저녁 정원에서 나를 홀로 발견하고는 같은 종류의 이야기를 꺼내며, 나를 사랑한다는 정직하고 진실한 고백을 늘어놓더니, 한마디로 그 어떤 다른 제안을 내게 하기에 앞서, 떳떳하고 명예롭게 나와 혼인하겠노라고 청해 왔다."]], [["I was now confounded, and driven to such an extremity as the like was never known; at least not to me.", "이제 나는 어쩔 줄을 몰랐고, 그와 같은 일은 일찍이 들어 본 적도 없는 궁지로 내몰렸으니, 적어도 나로서는 처음이었다. 나는 완강하게 그 제안에 맞섰고, 이제 논거로 나 자신을 무장하기 시작했다."], ["I resisted the proposal with obstinacy; and now I began to arm myself with arguments.", "나는 그에게 이 혼인의 불균형을, 내가 집안에서 받게 될 대접을, 그토록 너그러운 마음으로 나를 집에 거두어 준, 그것도 내가 그토록 비천한 처지에 있을 때 거두어 준 그의 훌륭한 부모님께 그것이 얼마나 배은망덕한 일이 되겠는지를 앞에 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["I laid before him the inequality of the match; the treatment I should meet with in the family; the ingratitude it would be to his good father and mother, who had taken me into their house upon such generous principles, and when I was in such a low condition; and, in short, I said everything to dissuade him from his design that I could imagine, except telling him the truth, which would indeed have put an end to it all, but that I durst not think of mentioning.", "한마디로, 나는 그의 뜻을 단념시키기 위해 상상할 수 있는 온갖 말을 다 했으니, 진실을 말하는 것만은 빼고서였다. 진실을 말했더라면 정말이지 모든 일에 종지부를 찍었겠으나, 그것을 입에 올린다는 것은 감히 생각조차 할 수 없었다."]], [["But here happened a circumstance that I did not expect indeed, which put me to my shifts; for this young gentleman, as he was plain and honest, so he pretended to nothing with me but what was so too; and, knowing his own innocence, he was not so careful to make his having a kindness for Mrs.", "그러나 여기서 정말이지 내가 예상치 못한 사정이 벌어져 나를 곤경에 빠뜨렸으니, 이 젊은 신사는 솔직하고 정직한 만큼, 나에 대해서도 그처럼 솔직하고 정직한 것 외에는 아무것도 꾸미지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 그는 자신의 결백함을 알고 있던 터라, 베티 아씨에게 호감을 품고 있다는 것을 집안에서 비밀로 하는 데에 자기 형만큼 조심하지 않았다."], ["Betty a secret in the house, as his brother was. And though he did not let them know that he had talked to me about it, yet he said enough to let his sisters perceive he loved me, and his mother saw it too, which, though they took no notice of it to me, yet they did to him, an immediately I found their carriage to me altered, more than ever before.", "그리고 그 일에 관해 나와 이야기를 나누었다는 것까지는 그들에게 알리지 않았으나, 그의 누이들이 그가 나를 사랑한다는 것을 알아챌 만큼은 말을 흘렸고, 그의 어머니도 그것을 보았으니, 그들은 그것을 내게 내색하지는 않았어도 그에게는 내색했으며, 그리하여 나는 곧 나를 대하는 그들의 태도가 전보다 더 달라진 것을 알아차렸다."]], [["I saw the cloud, though I did not foresee the storm.", "먹구름은 보았으나 폭풍은 미처 내다보지 못했다."], ["It was easy, I say, to see that their carriage to me was altered, and that it grew worse and worse every day; till at last I got information among the servants that I should, in a very little while, be desired to remove.", "다시 말하거니와, 나를 대하는 그들의 태도가 달라졌다는 것, 그리고 그것이 날마다 점점 더 나빠진다는 것은 쉽게 알 수 있었으니, 마침내 나는 하인들 사이에서 머지않아 내가 떠나 달라는 요구를 받게 되리라는 정보를 얻었다."]], [["I was not alarmed at the news, having a full satisfaction that I should be otherwise provided for; and especially considering that I had reason every day to expect I should be with child, and that then I should be obliged to remove without any pretences for it.", "나는 그 소식에 놀라지 않았으니, 달리 거두어질 길이 있다는 것에 충분히 만족하고 있었기 때문이며, 더욱이 날마다 아이를 배리라 예상할 만한 이유가 있었고, 그렇게 되면 어떤 핑계도 댈 수 없이 떠날 수밖에 없으리라는 것을 생각하면 더욱 그러했다."]], [["After some time the younger gentleman took an opportunity to tell me that the kindness he had for me had got vent in the family.", "얼마 뒤 작은 신사가 기회를 잡아, 나를 향한 자신의 호감이 집안에 새어 나갔다고 내게 말했다. 그것을 두고 나를 탓하는 것은 아니라고, 그것이 어떻게 새어 나갔는지는 자기도 잘 안다고 그는 말했다."], ["He did not charge me with it, he said, for he know well enough which way it came out.", "자기의 솔직한 말버릇이 그 빌미가 되었으니, 나에 대한 존경을 마땅히 했어야 할 만큼 비밀로 하지 않았기 때문이라고 했고, 그 까닭인즉, 만일 내가 자기를 받아들이는 데 동의한다면, 자기는 모두에게 내놓고 나를 사랑하노라고, 나와 혼인할 작정이라고 말하기로 마음을 정한 지점에 와 있기 때문이라고 했다. 아버지와 어머니가 그것을 못마땅해하며 매정하게 굴지도 모르는 것은 사실이지만, 자기는 법률을 배워 익혔으니 이제 생계를 꾸려 갈 길이 있어, 내가 기대할 만큼 나를 떳떳하게 부양하는 일은 두렵지 않다고 했다."], ["He told me his plain way of talking had been the occasion of it, for that he did not make his respect for me so much a secret as he might have done, and the reason was, that he was at a point, that if I would consent to have him, he would tell them all openly that he loved me, and that he intended to marry me; that it was true his father and mother might resent it, and be unkind, but that he was now in a way to live, being bred to the law, and he did not fear maintaining me agreeable to what I should expect; and that, in short, as he believed I would not be ashamed of him, so he was resolved not to be ashamed of me, and that he scorned to be afraid to own me now, whom he resolved to own after I was his wife, and therefore I had nothing to do but to give him my hand, and he would answer for all the rest.", "그리고 한마디로, 내가 자기를 부끄러워하지 않으리라 믿는 만큼, 자기도 나를 부끄러워하지 않기로 결심했으며, 아내로 맞은 뒤에 떳떳이 인정하기로 결심한 사람을 지금 인정하기를 두려워하는 것은 떳떳치 못한 일로 여긴다고 했다. 그러니 나는 그저 그에게 손을 내밀어 주기만 하면 되고, 나머지는 모두 자기가 책임지겠노라고 했다."]], [["I was now in a dreadful condition indeed, and now I repented heartily my easiness with the eldest brother; not from any reflection of conscience, but from a view of the happiness I might have enjoyed, and had now made impossible; for though I had no great scruples of conscience, as I have said, to struggle with, yet I could not think of being a whore to one brother and a wife to the other. But then it came into my thoughts that the first brother had promised to made me his wife when he came to his estate; but I presently remembered what I had often thought of, that he had never spoken a word of having me for a wife after he had conquered me for a mistress; and indeed, till now, though I said I thought of it often, yet it gave me no disturbance at all, for as he did not seem in the least to lessen his affection to me, so neither did he lessen his bounty, though he had the discretion himself to desire me not to lay out a penny of what he gave me in clothes, or to make the least show extraordinary, because it would necessarily give jealousy in the family, since everybody know I could come at such things no manner of ordinary way, but by some private friendship, which they would presently have suspected.", "이제 나는 정말이지 끔찍한 처지에 놓였고, 큰형과 그토록 쉽게 굴복한 것을 진심으로 뉘우쳤으니, 그것은 양심의 가책 때문이 아니라 내가 누렸을지도 모르나 이제는 불가능하게 만들어 버린 행복을 떠올렸기 때문이었다. 앞서 말했듯이 씨름할 만한 양심의 거리낌이야 그리 크지 않았으나, 그래도 한 형제에게는 창녀가 되고 다른 형제에게는 아내가 된다는 것은 도무지 생각할 수가 없었다. 그러나 그때, 큰형이 자기 영지를 물려받게 되면 나를 아내로 삼겠노라 약속했던 일이 머릿속에 떠올랐다. 그러나 나는 곧, 종종 떠올리곤 했던 사실, 곧 그가 나를 정부로 정복한 뒤로는 나를 아내로 삼겠다는 말을 한마디도 한 적이 없다는 것을 기억해 냈다. 그리고 정말이지 지금까지는, 비록 그것을 종종 생각했다고 말은 했어도, 그것이 내게 조금도 거리낌을 주지는 않았으니, 그가 나를 향한 애정을 조금도 줄이는 기색이 없었던 것과 마찬가지로 그 너그러움도 줄이지 않았기 때문이다. 다만 그는 분별 있게도, 자기가 준 것을 단 한 푼이라도 옷에 들이거나 조금이라도 유난스러운 티를 내지 말라고 내게 당부했으니, 그러면 집안에 필시 의심을 사게 되는 까닭이었다. 내가 그런 물건을 평범한 방법으로는 결코 손에 넣을 수 없고 오직 어떤 은밀한 친분으로만 손에 넣을 수 있다는 것은 누구나 알 터라, 그들이 곧바로 그것을 의심했을 것이기 때문이다."]], [["But I was now in a great strait, and knew not what to do.", "그러나 이제 나는 큰 곤경에 처해 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["The main difficulty was this: the younger brother not only laid close siege to me, but suffered it to be seen.", "가장 큰 어려움은 이것이었으니, 작은 형제가 나를 바짝 에워싸 공략할 뿐 아니라 그것이 드러나도록 내버려 두었다는 점이다."], ["He would come into his sister’s room, and his mother’s room, and sit down, and talk a thousand kind things of me, and to me, even before their faces, and when they were all there.", "그는 누이의 방으로, 또 어머니의 방으로 들어와 앉아서는, 그들의 면전에서, 그들이 모두 거기 있는데도, 나에 관해 또 내게 다정한 말을 수천 가지나 늘어놓곤 했다. 이것이 어찌나 공공연해졌던지 온 집안이 그 일을 입에 올렸고, 그의 어머니는 그를 나무랐으며, 나를 대하는 그들의 태도는 완전히 달라진 듯했다."], ["This grew so public that the whole house talked of it, and his mother reproved him for it, and their carriage to me appeared quite altered.", "한마디로, 그의 어머니가 마치 나를 집안에서 내보낼 작정이라는 듯한 말을, 쉬운 우리말로 하자면 나를 문밖으로 내쫓을 작정이라는 듯한 말을 흘렸던 것이다."], ["In short, his mother had let fall some speeches, as if she intended to put me out of the family; that is, in English, to turn me out of doors.", "이제 나는 이것이 그의 형에게 비밀일 수 없으리라고 확신했으니, 다만 그가, 사실 아직 아무도 그렇게 여기지 않았듯이, 막내 형제가 그 일에 관해 내게 무슨 제안을 했으리라고는 생각지 못했을 뿐이리라."], ["Now I was sure this could not be a secret to his brother, only that he might not think, as indeed nobody else yet did, that the youngest brother had made any proposal to me about it; but as I easily could see that it would go farther, so I saw likewise there was an absolute necessity to speak of it to him, or that he would speak of it to me, and which to do first I knew not; that is, whether I should break it to him or let it alone till he should break it to me.", "그러나 일이 더 멀리 번져 가리라는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었던 것처럼, 그것을 그에게 말하든지 아니면 그가 그것을 내게 말하든지 하지 않을 수 없으리라는 것도 알았으나, 어느 쪽을 먼저 해야 할지는 알 수 없었다. 다시 말해, 내가 그에게 그것을 털어놓아야 할지, 아니면 그가 내게 털어놓을 때까지 내버려 두어야 할지를 알 수 없었던 것이다."]], [["Upon serious consideration, for indeed now I began to consider things very seriously, and never till now; I say, upon serious consideration, I resolved to tell him of it first; and it was not long before I had an opportunity, for the very next day his brother went to London upon some business, and the family being out a-visiting, just as it had happened before, and as indeed was often the case, he came according to his custom, to spend an hour or two with Mrs.", "진지하게 숙고한 끝에—이제 정말이지 나는 비로소 일을 매우 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했으니, 그때까지는 한 번도 그래 본 적이 없었다—다시 말하거니와, 진지하게 숙고한 끝에, 나는 내가 먼저 그에게 그것을 말하기로 결심했다."], ["Betty.", "그리고 머지않아 기회가 왔으니, 바로 다음 날 그의 형이 무슨 볼일로 런던에 갔고, 집안 식구들은 전에 그랬던 것처럼, 또 실로 흔히 그랬던 것처럼 방문을 나갔던 터라, 그가 늘 하던 대로 베티 아씨와 한두 시간을 보내러 왔던 것이다."]], [["When he came and had sat down a while, he easily perceived there was an alteration in my countenance, that I was not so free and pleasant with him as I used to be, and particularly, that I had been a-crying; he was not long before he took notice of it, and asked me in very kind terms what was the matter, and if anything troubled me.", "그가 와서 잠시 앉아 있자, 그는 내 얼굴빛에 변화가 있다는 것을, 내가 평소처럼 그에게 거리낌 없이 즐겁게 굴지 않는다는 것을, 특히 내가 울고 있었다는 것을 쉽게 알아챘다. 그는 곧 그것을 눈치채고는, 무슨 일이냐고, 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는 것이냐고 아주 다정한 말로 물었다."], ["I would have put it off if I could, but it was not to be concealed; so after suffering many importunities to draw that out of me which I longed as much as possible to disclose, I told him that it was true something did trouble me, and something of such a nature that I could not conceal from him, and yet that I could not tell how to tell him of it neither; that it was a thing that not only surprised me, but greatly perplexed me, and that I knew not what course to take, unless he would direct me.", "나는 할 수만 있다면 그것을 얼버무리고 싶었으나, 감출 수가 없었다. 그리하여 될 수 있는 한 털어놓고 싶었던 그것을 끄집어내려는 숱한 채근을 견딘 끝에, 나는 그에게 정말로 무언가 나를 괴롭히는 것이 있으며, 그것은 그에게 숨길 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이지만, 그래도 어떻게 그것을 그에게 말해야 할지 알 수가 없다고 했다."], ["He told me with great tenderness, that let it be what it would, I should not let it trouble me, for he would protect me from all the world.", "그것은 나를 놀라게 했을 뿐 아니라 크게 당혹스럽게 한 일이라, 그가 일러 주지 않는 한 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠다고 했다. 그는 더없이 다정하게, 그것이 무엇이든 그 때문에 괴로워하지 말라고, 자기가 온 세상으로부터 나를 지켜 주겠노라고 했다."]], [["I then began at a distance, and told him I was afraid the ladies had got some secret information of our correspondence; for that it was easy to see that their conduct was very much changed towards me for a great while, and that now it was come to that pass that they frequently found fault with me, and sometimes fell quite out with me, though I never gave them the least occasion; that whereas I used always to lie with the eldest sister, I was lately put to lie by myself, or with one of the maids; and that I had overheard them several times talking very unkindly about me; but that which confirmed it all was, that one of the servants had told me that she had heard I was to be turned out, and that it was not safe for the family that I should be any longer in the house.", "그래서 나는 에둘러 말을 꺼내, 아씨들이 우리 관계에 관해 무슨 은밀한 정보를 얻은 것 같아 두렵다고 그에게 말했다. 오래전부터 나를 대하는 그들의 행실이 매우 달라진 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었고, 이제는 내가 조금도 빌미를 준 적이 없는데도 그들이 자주 나를 트집 잡고, 때로는 아주 대놓고 나와 다투는 지경에 이르렀기 때문이다. 또 전에는 늘 큰아씨와 함께 잠자리에 들었으나, 근래에는 혼자, 아니면 하녀 중 하나와 함께 자게 되었으며, 그들이 나에 관해 여러 차례 아주 매정하게 이야기하는 것을 우연히 엿들었다고 했다. 그러나 그 모든 것을 확증해 준 것은, 하인 중 하나가 내가 쫓겨나게 되어 있다는 말을 들었다고, 내가 더는 집에 있는 것이 집안을 위해 안전치 않다는 말을 들었다고 내게 일러 준 것이었다."]], [["He smiled when he heard all this, and I asked him how he could make so light of it, when he must needs know that if there was any discovery I was undone for ever, and that even it would hurt him, though not ruin him as it would me.", "그는 이 모든 말을 듣고 빙그레 웃었고, 나는 만일 무언가 발각된다면 나는 영영 끝장이며, 그것이 비록 나처럼 그를 파멸시키지는 않더라도 그에게까지 해가 되리라는 것을 뻔히 알 텐데, 어떻게 그것을 그토록 가볍게 여길 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["I upbraided him, that he was like all the rest of the sex, that, when they had the character and honour of a woman at their mercy, oftentimes made it their jest, and at least looked upon it as a trifle, and counted the ruin of those they had had their will of as a thing of no value.", "나는 그를 나무랐으니, 그도 다른 모든 사내붙이와 마찬가지로, 한 여자의 평판과 명예를 제 손아귀에 넣고 나면 흔히 그것을 농담거리로 삼고, 적어도 그것을 하찮은 것으로 여기며, 제 욕심을 채운 여자들의 파멸을 아무 값어치도 없는 것으로 친다고 했다."]], [["He saw me warm and serious, and he changed his style immediately; he told me he was sorry I should have such a thought of him; that he had never given me the least occasion for it, but had been as tender of my reputation as he could be of his own; that he was sure our correspondence had been managed with so much address, that not one creature in the family had so much as a suspicion of it; that if he smiled when I told him my thoughts, it was at the assurance he lately received, that our understanding one another was not so much as known or guessed at; and that when he had told me how much reason he had to be easy, I should smile as he did, for he was very certain it would give me a full satisfaction.", "그는 내가 격앙되고 진지해진 것을 보고는 곧바로 어조를 바꾸었다. 내가 자기를 그렇게 생각한다니 안타깝다고, 자기는 그런 생각의 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없으며 자기 자신의 평판만큼이나 내 평판을 아끼려 애써 왔다고 했다. 우리 관계가 어찌나 솜씨 있게 다루어졌던지 집안의 어느 누구도 그것을 조금도 의심하지 못한다고 확신한다고, 내가 내 생각을 말했을 때 자기가 빙그레 웃은 것은, 우리가 서로 통한다는 사실이 알려지거나 짐작되지조차 않았다는 것을 근래에 확인한 데서 온 것이라고 했다. 그리고 자기가 어찌하여 마음을 놓을 만한지를 내게 말해 주면, 나도 자기처럼 빙그레 웃게 될 것이니, 그것이 분명 내게 충분한 만족을 주리라 확신한다고 했다."]], [["“This is a mystery I cannot understand,”", "\"그건 제가 알 수 없는 수수께끼예요,\""], ["says I, “or how it should be to my satisfaction that I am to be turned out of doors; for if our correspondence is not discovered, I know not what else I have done to change the countenances of the whole family to me, or to have them treat me as they do now, who formerly used me with so much tenderness, as if I had been one of their own children.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 문밖으로 쫓겨나게 되었다는 것이 어찌하여 제게 만족스러운 일이 된다는 건지 말이에요. 우리 관계가 발각된 게 아니라면, 전에는 마치 제가 자기네 친자식이라도 되는 양 그토록 다정하게 대해 주던 온 집안 식구가 이제 와 저를 대하는 낯빛을 바꾸고 지금처럼 저를 대하게 만들 만한 무슨 다른 짓을 제가 했는지 모르겠으니까요.\""]], [["“Why, look you, child,” says he, “that they are uneasy about you, that is true; but that they have the least suspicion of the case as it is, and as it respects you and I, is so far from being true, that they suspect my brother Robin; and, in short, they are fully persuaded he makes love to you; nay, the fool has put it into their heads too himself, for he is continually bantering them about it, and making a jest of himself. I confess I think he is wrong to do so, because he cannot but see it vexes them, and makes them unkind to you; but ’tis a satisfaction to me, because of the assurance it gives me, that they do not suspect me in the least, and I hope this will be to your satisfaction too.”", "\"아니, 이봐요, 아가씨,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"그들이 당신 때문에 마음이 편치 않은 것, 그건 사실이오. 하지만 그들이 일의 진상을, 그러니까 당신과 나에 관련된 진상을 조금이라도 의심한다는 건, 사실과는 거리가 멀어서, 그들은 내 동생 로빈을 의심하고 있소. 한마디로, 그들은 그 녀석이 당신에게 구애하고 있다고 철석같이 믿고 있소. 아니, 그 바보 녀석이 제 입으로 그것을 그들 머릿속에 집어넣어 주기까지 했으니, 그 일을 두고 끊임없이 그들을 놀리며 제 꼴을 웃음거리로 만들고 있단 말이오. 솔직히 나는 그 녀석이 그러는 게 잘못이라 생각하오. 그게 그들의 심기를 거슬러 당신에게 매정하게 굴게 만든다는 걸 뻔히 알 텐데 말이오. 하지만 그게 내겐 만족스러운 일이오, 그들이 나를 조금도 의심하지 않는다는 확신을 주니까. 그리고 이게 당신에게도 만족스러운 일이 되기를 바라오.\""]], [["“So it is,”", "\"한편으로는 그래요,\""], ["says I, “one way; but this does not reach my case at all, nor is this the chief thing that troubles me, though I have been concerned about that too.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"하지만 그건 제 처지와는 전혀 무관한 일이고, 비록 그 일로도 마음을 졸이긴 했지만, 그게 저를 괴롭히는 가장 큰 일은 아니에요.\" \"그럼, 그게 뭐요?\""], ["“What is it, then?”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he. With which I fell to tears, and could say nothing to him at all.", "그 말에 나는 눈물을 쏟으며 그에게 아무 말도 할 수가 없었다."], ["He strove to pacify me all he could, but began at last to be very pressing upon me to tell what it was.", "그는 할 수 있는 한 나를 달래려 애썼으나, 마침내는 그것이 무엇인지 말하라고 몹시 다그치기 시작했다. 마침내 나는 그에게도 마땅히 말해야 할 것 같다고, 그도 그것을 알 권리가 얼마간 있다고 대답했다."], ["At last I answered that I thought I ought to tell him too, and that he had some right to know it; besides, that I wanted his direction in the case, for I was in such perplexity that I knew not what course to take, and then I related the whole affair to him.", "게다가 이 일에 대해 그의 지시가 필요하니, 내가 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모를 만큼 당혹스러운 처지에 있었기 때문이다. 그러고 나서 나는 그에게 모든 일을 다 이야기했다."], ["I told him how imprudently his brother had managed himself, in making himself so public; for that if he had kept it a secret, as such a thing ought to have been, I could but have denied him positively, without giving any reason for it, and he would in time have ceased his solicitations; but that he had the vanity, first, to depend upon it that I would not deny him, and then had taken the freedom to tell his resolution of having me to the whole house.", "나는 그의 동생이 얼마나 경솔하게 처신했는지를, 자신을 그토록 공공연히 드러냈는지를 이야기했으니, 만일 그가 그런 일은 마땅히 그래야 하듯 비밀로 했더라면, 나는 아무 이유도 대지 않고 그저 그를 딱 잘라 거절할 수 있었을 것이고, 그러면 그도 머지않아 구애를 그쳤을 것이라고 했다. 그러나 그는 우선 내가 자기를 거절하지 않으리라고 믿는 허영을 부렸고, 그러고는 나를 차지하겠다는 결심을 온 집안에 떠벌리는 무람없는 짓을 했다고 했다."]], [["I told him how far I had resisted him, and told him how sincere and honourable his offers were.", "나는 그에게 내가 얼마나 그에 맞서 버텼는지를 말하고, 그의 제안이 얼마나 진실하고 명예로운 것이었는지를 말했다. \"하지만,\""], ["“But,”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"제 처지는 갑절로 어려워질 거예요."], ["says I, “my case will be doubly hard; for as they carry it ill to me now, because he desires to have me, they’ll carry it worse when they shall find I have denied him; and they will presently say, there’s something else in it, and then out it comes that I am married already to somebody else, or that I would never refuse a match so much above me as this was.”", "지금은 그가 저를 차지하고 싶어 한다고 해서 저를 못살게 굴지만, 제가 그를 거절했다는 걸 알게 되면 더 못살게 굴 테니까요. 그러면 그들은 곧 거기에 뭔가 다른 게 있다고 말할 테고, 그러면 제가 벌써 다른 누군가와 혼인했다거나, 아니면 저를 위해 이만큼 분에 넘치는 혼처를 결코 마다할 리가 없다는 말이 튀어나올 거예요.\""]], [["This discourse surprised him indeed very much.", "이 말은 정말이지 그를 몹시 놀라게 했다."], ["He told me that it was a critical point indeed for me to manage, and he did not see which way I should get out of it; but he would consider it, and let me know next time we met, what resolution he was come to about it; and in the meantime desired I would not give my consent to his brother, nor yet give him a flat denial, but that I would hold him in suspense a while.", "그는 그것이 정말이지 내가 처리하기에 까다로운 대목이라고, 내가 어떻게 거기서 빠져나갈지 알 수가 없다고 했으나, 그것을 곰곰이 헤아려 보고 다음에 만날 때 그것에 관해 어떤 결심에 이르렀는지 내게 알려 주겠다고 했다. 그리고 그동안에는 그의 동생에게 동의도 하지 말고, 그렇다고 딱 잘라 거절하지도 말고, 한동안 그를 애태우는 상태로 붙들어 두라고 당부했다."]], [["I seemed to start at his saying I should not give him my consent.", "그가 내게 동의하지 말라고 말하는 데에 나는 흠칫하는 시늉을 했다. 나는 그에게, 내게 줄 동의 따위는 없다는 것을 그가 잘 알지 않느냐고 말했다."], ["I told him he knew very well I had no consent to give; that he had engaged himself to marry me, and that my consent was the same time engaged to him; that he had all along told me I was his wife, and I looked upon myself as effectually so as if the ceremony had passed; and that it was from his own mouth that I did so, he having all along persuaded me to call myself his wife.", "그가 나와 혼인하겠노라 스스로 맹세했고, 그와 동시에 내 동의도 그에게 매여 버렸으며, 그가 줄곧 나를 자기 아내라 일러 왔으니, 나는 마치 예식이 치러지기라도 한 것처럼 나 자신을 실질적인 그의 아내로 여겨 왔다고 했다. 그리고 내가 그렇게 한 것은 바로 그의 입에서 나온 말 때문이니, 그가 줄곧 나더러 나 자신을 그의 아내라 부르라고 설득했기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["“Well, my dear,” says he, “don’t be concerned at that now; if I am not your husband, I’ll be as good as a husband to you; and do not let those things trouble you now, but let me look a little farther into this affair, and I shall be able to say more next time we meet.”", "\"좋소, 내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"이제 그것 때문에 마음 쓰지 마오. 내가 당신의 남편이 아니라 해도, 나는 남편이나 다름없이 당신에게 잘해 주겠소. 그러니 지금은 그런 일로 괴로워 말고, 내가 이 일을 좀 더 들여다보게 해 주오. 그러면 다음에 만날 때 더 많은 말을 할 수 있을 거요.\""]], [["He pacified me as well as he could with this, but I found he was very thoughtful, and that though he was very kind to me and kissed me a thousand times, and more I believe, and gave me money too, yet he offered no more all the while we were together, which was above two hours, and which I much wondered at indeed at that time, considering how it used to be, and what opportunity we had.", "그는 이 말로 할 수 있는 한 나를 달랬으나, 나는 그가 매우 생각에 잠겨 있다는 것을 알아챘다. 그리고 그가 내게 매우 다정하게 굴며 나에게 천 번이고, 아니 그 이상 입을 맞추고, 내게 돈까지 주었으나, 우리가 함께 있던 두 시간 남짓 내내 그 이상은 아무것도 청하지 않았으니, 평소에 어떠했는지, 또 우리에게 어떤 기회가 있었는지를 생각하면 그때 나는 정말이지 몹시 의아했다."]], [["His brother did not come from London for five or six days, and it was two days more before he got an opportunity to talk with him; but then getting him by himself he began to talk very close to him about it, and the same evening got an opportunity (for we had a long conference together) to repeat all their discourse to me, which, as near as I can remember, was to the purpose following.", "그의 형은 닷새인가 엿새가 지나도록 런던에서 돌아오지 않았고, 그러고도 그와 이야기할 기회를 얻기까지 이틀이 더 걸렸다. 그러나 마침내 그를 단둘이 붙들어 그 일에 관해 매우 단도직입으로 이야기를 꺼냈고, 같은 날 저녁(우리는 함께 긴 이야기를 나누었으니) 그들이 나눈 이야기를 모두 내게 되풀이해 들려줄 기회를 얻었는데, 내가 기억하는 한 그것은 대략 다음과 같았다."], ["He told him he heard strange news of him since he went, viz. that he made love to Mrs.", "그는 동생에게, 네가 떠난 뒤로 너에 관한 괴이한 소식을 들었다고, 곧 네가 베티 아씨에게 구애한다는 소식을 들었다고 했다."], ["Betty.", "\"음,\""], ["“Well,” says his brother a little angrily, “and so I do.", "하고 동생이 조금 성난 투로 말했다, \"그래요, 그러고 있어요."], ["And what then?", "그래서 어떻단 거예요?"], ["What has anybody to do with that?”", "그게 누구하고 무슨 상관이람?\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says his brother, “don’t be angry, Robin; I don’t pretend to have anything to do with it; nor do I pretend to be angry with you about it.", "하고 형이 말했다, \"성내지 마라, 로빈. 나는 그 일에 끼어들 생각이 없고, 그 일로 너한테 화를 낼 생각도 없다."], ["But I find they do concern themselves about it, and that they have used the poor girl ill about it, which I should take as done to myself.”", "다만 그들이 그 일로 신경을 쓰고 있고, 그 가엾은 아이를 그 일로 모질게 대했다는 걸 알았는데, 그건 마치 내게 한 짓처럼 여겨지더구나.\""], ["“Whom do you mean by _they_?”", "\"그들이라니, 누굴 말하는 거예요?\""], ["says Robin.", "하고 로빈이 말했다."], ["“I mean my mother and the girls,”", "\"내 어머니하고 누이들 말이다,\""], ["says the elder brother.", "하고 형이 말했다."], ["“But hark ye,”", "\"하지만 들어 봐,\""], ["says his brother, “are you in earnest?", "하고 형이 말했다, \"너 진심이냐?"], ["Do you really love this girl?", "정말로 이 아이를 사랑하느냐?"], ["You may be free with me, you know.”", "나한테는 터놓고 말해도 된다, 알잖니.\""], ["“Why, then,”", "\"그럼, 그렇다면,\""], ["says Robin, “I will be free with you; I do love her above all the women in the world, and I will have her, let them say and do what they will.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"형한테는 터놓고 말할게요. 나는 그 아이를 세상의 어떤 여자보다도 사랑하고, 그들이 무슨 말을 하고 무슨 짓을 하든 그 아이를 차지할 거예요."], ["I believe the girl will not deny me.”", "그 아이도 나를 거절하지 않을 거라고 믿어요.\""]], [["It struck me to the heart when he told me this, for though it was most rational to think I would not deny him, yet I knew in my own conscience I must deny him, and I saw my ruin in my being obliged to do so; but I knew it was my business to talk otherwise then, so I interrupted him in his story thus.", "그가 내게 이 말을 했을 때 나는 가슴이 철렁했으니, 비록 내가 그를 거절하지 않으리라 여기는 것이 더없이 이치에 맞는 일이었으나, 나는 내 양심 속으로 그를 거절할 수밖에 없다는 것을 알았고, 그래야만 하는 데에서 내 파멸을 보았기 때문이다. 그러나 그때는 달리 말하는 것이 내가 할 일임을 알았던 터라, 나는 그의 이야기를 이렇게 가로막았다."]], [["“Ay!”", "\"흥!\""], ["said I, “does he think I cannot deny him?", "하고 내가 말했다, \"그분은 제가 자기를 거절하지 못하리라 여기나요?"], ["But he shall find I can deny him, for all that.”", "그래도 제가 거절할 수 있다는 걸 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“Well, my dear,”", "\"좋소, 내 사랑,\""], ["says he, “but let me give you the whole story as it went on between us, and then say what you will.”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 우리 사이에 오간 이야기를 통째로 들려줄 테니, 그러고 나서 하고 싶은 말을 하구려.\""]], [["Then he went on and told me that he replied thus: “But, brother, you know she has nothing, and you may have several ladies with good fortunes.”", "그러고는 말을 이어, 자기가 이렇게 대답했노라고 내게 말했다. \"하지만 동생아, 그 아이는 아무것도 가진 게 없다는 걸 너도 알잖니, 게다가 넌 재산 많은 아가씨를 여럿 얻을 수도 있고.\""]], [["“’Tis no matter for that,”", "\"그딴 건 아무래도 좋아요,\""], ["said Robin; “I love the girl, and I will never please my pocket in marrying, and not please my fancy.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"나는 그 아이를 사랑하고, 혼인하면서 내 주머니를 흐뭇하게 할 뿐 내 마음은 흐뭇하게 하지 못하는 짓은 결코 하지 않을 거예요.\""], ["“And so, my dear,”", "\"그러니, 내 사랑,\""], ["adds he, “there is no opposing him.”", "하고 그가 덧붙였다, \"그를 막을 도리가 없소.\""]], [["“Yes, yes,”", "\"아니에요, 아니에요,\""], ["says I, “you shall see I can oppose him; I have learnt to say No, now though I had not learnt it before; if the best lord in the land offered me marriage now, I could very cheerfully say No to him.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 그분을 막을 수 있다는 걸 보시게 될 거예요. 전에는 배우지 못했지만 이제는 아니라고 말하는 법을 배웠으니, 이 나라에서 가장 훌륭한 영주가 지금 제게 혼인하자고 청한대도, 저는 아주 기꺼이 그에게 아니라고 말할 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Well, but, my dear,”", "\"좋소, 하지만, 내 사랑,\""], ["says he, “what can you say to him?", "하고 그가 말했다, \"당신이 그에게 뭐라고 말할 수 있겠소?"], ["You know, as you said when we talked of it before, he will ask you many questions about it, and all the house will wonder what the meaning of it should be.”", "전에 우리가 그 이야기를 할 때 당신이 말했듯이, 그가 당신에게 그 일에 관해 숱한 질문을 던질 테고, 온 집안이 그게 대체 무슨 영문인가 의아해할 텐데.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says I, smiling, “I can stop all their mouths at one clap by telling him, and them too, that I am married already to his elder brother.”", "하고 내가 빙그레 웃으며 말했다, \"저는 그분에게, 또 그들에게도, 제가 벌써 그분의 형과 혼인했다고 말함으로써 단번에 그들 모두의 입을 막을 수 있어요.\""]], [["He smiled a little too at the word, but I could see it startled him, and he could not hide the disorder it put him into.", "그도 그 말에 살짝 웃었으나, 나는 그것이 그를 흠칫하게 했다는 것을, 그리고 그가 그 말이 일으킨 동요를 감추지 못한다는 것을 알 수 있었다. 그러나 그는 이렇게 대꾸했다."], ["However, he returned, “Why, though that may be true in some sense, yet I suppose you are but in jest when you talk of giving such an answer as that; it may not be convenient on many accounts.”", "\"아니, 그게 어떤 의미에서는 사실일지 몰라도, 당신이 그런 대답을 하겠다고 말하는 건 농담일 뿐이라고 생각하오. 그건 여러모로 온당치 않을 수 있소.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니에요, 아니에요,\""], ["says I pleasantly, “I am not so fond of letting the secret come out without your consent.”", "하고 내가 즐거이 말했다, \"저는 당신의 동의 없이 그 비밀이 새어 나가게 하는 걸 그리 좋아하지 않아요.\""]], [["“But what, then, can you say to him, or to them,”", "\"하지만 그렇다면, 당신이 명백히 그토록 이로울 혼처에 한사코 반대하는 것을 그들이 알게 되었을 때, 당신은 그에게, 혹은 그들에게 뭐라고 말할 수 있겠소?\""], ["says he, “when they find you positive against a match which would be apparently so much to your advantage?”", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says I, “should I be at a loss?", "하고 내가 말했다, \"제가 무슨 말이 궁할까요? 우선, 저는 그들에게 아무런 이유도 댈 의무가 없어요."], ["First of all, I am not obliged to give me any reason at all; on the other hand, I may tell them I am married already, and stop there, and that will be a full stop too to him, for he can have no reason to ask one question after it.”", "다른 한편으로는, 제가 벌써 혼인했다고 말하고 거기서 멈출 수 있는데, 그러면 그분에게도 단호한 종지부가 될 거예요. 그 뒤로는 그분이 한마디도 더 물어볼 까닭이 없을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["says he; “but the whole house will tease you about that, even to father and mother, and if you deny them positively, they will be disobliged at you, and suspicious besides.”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 온 집안이, 심지어 아버지와 어머니까지도 그 일로 당신을 들볶을 거고, 당신이 그들을 딱 잘라 거절하면 그들이 당신에게 언짢아할 테고, 게다가 의심까지 품을 거요.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says I, “what can I do? What would you have me do?", "하고 내가 말했다, \"그럼 제가 어쩌란 말이에요?"], ["I was in straight enough before, and as I told you, I was in perplexity before, and acquainted you with the circumstances, that I might have your advice.”", "저더러 어떻게 하란 말이에요? 저는 전에도 충분히 곤경에 처해 있었고, 말씀드렸듯이 전에도 당혹스러운 처지에 있었기에, 당신의 조언을 얻고자 그 사정을 당신께 알린 거잖아요.\""]], [["“My dear,”", "\"내 사랑,\" 하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 그것에 관해 매우 많이 헤아려 보았다는 건 당신도 분명히 알 거요."], ["says he, “I have been considering very much upon it, you may be sure, and though it is a piece of advice that has a great many mortifications in it to me, and may at first seem strange to you, yet, all things considered, I see no better way for you than to let him go on; and if you find him hearty and in earnest, marry him.”", "그리고 비록 그것이 내게는 굴욕스러운 점이 무척 많은 조언이고, 처음에는 당신에게 이상하게 들릴지도 모르나, 모든 것을 헤아려 볼 때, 나는 당신에게 그가 하는 대로 내버려 두는 것보다 더 나은 길이 보이지 않소. 그리고 그가 진심이고 진지하다는 걸 알게 되거든, 그와 혼인하구려.\""]], [["I gave him a look full of horror at those words, and, turning pale as death, was at the very point of sinking down out of the chair I sat in; when, giving a start, “My dear,”", "그 말에 나는 그에게 공포로 가득 찬 눈길을 던졌고, 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질려, 앉아 있던 의자에서 막 쓰러질 지경이 되었다. 그때 그가 흠칫 놀라며 \"내 사랑,\""], ["says he aloud, “what’s the matter with you?", "하고 큰 소리로 외쳤다, \"당신 왜 그러오?"], ["Where are you a-going?”", "어디로 가는 거요?\""], ["and a great many such things; and with jogging and called to me, fetched me a little to myself, though it was a good while before I fully recovered my senses, and was not able to speak for several minutes more.", "하면서 그런 말을 잔뜩 늘어놓고, 흔들어 깨우고 나를 불러, 나를 조금 제정신으로 돌아오게 했으나, 내가 온전히 정신을 차리기까지는 한참이 걸렸고, 그 뒤로도 여러 분 동안 말을 할 수가 없었다."]], [["When I was fully recovered he began again.", "내가 온전히 정신을 차리자 그가 다시 말을 꺼냈다."], ["“My dear,”", "\"내 사랑,\""], ["says he, “what made you so surprised at what I said?", "하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 한 말에 당신은 어찌 그리 놀랐소? 그것을 진지하게 헤아려 봐 주었으면 하오."], ["I would have you consider seriously of it?", "이 일에서 집안이 어떤 태도인지는 당신도 뻔히 알 거요."], ["You may see plainly how the family stand in this case, and they would be stark mad if it was my case, as it is my brother’s; and for aught I see, it would be my ruin and yours too.”", "그리고 만일 그게 내 동생의 처지가 아니라 내 처지라면 그들은 펄펄 뛸 거요. 그리고 내가 보기에는, 그게 나의 파멸이자 당신의 파멸이기도 할 거요.\""]], [["“Ay!”", "\"흥!\""], ["says I, still speaking angrily; “are all your protestations and vows to be shaken by the dislike of the family?", "하고 내가 여전히 성난 투로 말했다, \"당신의 그 모든 맹세와 서약이 집안의 못마땅함에 흔들려야 한단 말인가요? 제가 늘 그 점을 당신에게 이의로 내세우지 않았던가요?"], ["Did I not always object that to you, and you made light thing of it, as what you were above, and would value; and is it come to this now?”", "그런데 당신은 그것을 자신이 초연한 것인 양, 대수롭잖게 여기는 양 가벼이 여겼잖아요. 그런데 이제 와서 이 꼴이 되었군요.\""], ["said I.", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["“Is this your faith and honour, your love, and the solidity of your promises?”", "\"이게 당신의 신의이고 명예이고, 당신의 사랑이며, 당신 약속의 견고함인가요?\""]], [["He continued perfectly calm, notwithstanding all my reproaches, and I was not sparing of them at all; but he replied at last, “My dear, I have not broken one promise with you yet; I did tell you I would marry you when I was come to my estate; but you see my father is a hale, healthy man, and may live these thirty years still, and not be older than several are round us in town; and you never proposed my marrying you sooner, because you knew it might be my ruin; and as to all the rest, I have not failed you in anything, you have wanted for nothing.”", "내 온갖 책망에도 그는 더없이 침착함을 잃지 않았으니, 나도 그 책망을 조금도 아끼지 않았다. 그러나 그는 마침내 이렇게 대꾸했다. \"내 사랑, 나는 아직 당신과 한 약속을 하나도 어기지 않았소. 내가 내 영지를 물려받게 되면 당신과 혼인하겠다고 말하긴 했지만, 보다시피 내 아버지는 정정하고 건강한 사람이라 앞으로도 삼십 년은 더 사실 테고, 그래도 이 고을 주변의 여러 사람보다 더 늙으신 것도 아니오. 그리고 당신은 그것이 내 파멸이 될 수 있다는 걸 알았기에 더 일찍 혼인하자고 청한 적도 없소. 그 밖의 모든 일에 관해서는, 나는 당신에게 어떤 것도 모자라게 한 적이 없으니, 당신은 아무것도 부족함이 없었소.\""]], [["I could not deny a word of this, and had nothing to say to it in general.", "나는 이 말을 한마디도 부인할 수 없었고, 그 전반에 대해 할 말이 없었다."], ["“But why, then,”", "\"하지만 그렇다면,\""], ["says I, “can you persuade me to such a horrid step as leaving you, since you have not left me?", "하고 내가 말했다, \"당신은 저를 떠나지 않았으면서, 어떻게 저더러 당신을 떠나라는 그토록 끔찍한 짓을 하라고 설득할 수 있나요?"], ["Will you allow no affection, no love on my side, where there has been so much on your side?", "당신 쪽에 그토록 많은 사랑이 있었던 곳에서, 제 쪽에는 어떤 애정도, 어떤 사랑도 없었다고 보시는 건가요?"], ["Have I made you no returns?", "제가 아무런 보답도 하지 않았던가요?"], ["Have I given no testimony of my sincerity and of my passion?", "제가 제 진실함과 제 열정의 증거를 하나도 보이지 않았던가요?"], ["Are the sacrifices I have made of honour and modesty to you no proof of my being tied to you in bonds too strong to be broken?”", "제가 당신께 바친 명예와 정숙함의 희생이, 제가 끊을 수 없을 만큼 강한 끈으로 당신께 매여 있다는 증거가 아니란 말인가요?\""]], [["“But here, my dear,”", "\"하지만 이봐요, 내 사랑,\""], ["says he, “you may come into a safe station, and appear with honour and with splendour at once, and the remembrance of what we have done may be wrapt up in an eternal silence, as if it had never happened; you shall always have my respect, and my sincere affection, only then it shall be honest, and perfectly just to my brother; you shall be my dear sister, as now you are my dear——”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"당신은 안전한 처지에 들어설 수 있고, 단번에 명예롭고 영화롭게 모습을 드러낼 수 있으며, 우리가 한 일에 대한 기억은 마치 그런 일이 일어난 적도 없었던 것처럼 영원한 침묵 속에 싸 둘 수 있소. 당신은 언제까지나 나의 존경과 나의 진실한 애정을 받을 테고, 다만 그때는 그것이 떳떳한 것이 되어 내 동생에게도 더없이 떳떳한 것이 될 거요."], ["and there he stopped.", "당신은 나의 사랑하는 누이가 될 거요, 지금 당신이 나의 사랑하는——\" 하고 그는 거기서 멈췄다."]], [["“Your dear whore,”", "\"사랑하는 창녀라고,\""], ["says I, “you would have said if you had gone on, and you might as well have said it; but I understand you.", "하고 내가 말했다, \"말을 이었더라면 그렇게 말씀하셨겠죠. 아예 그렇게 말씀하셔도 좋았어요."], ["However, I desire you to remember the long discourses you have had with me, and the many hours’ pains you have taken to persuade me to believe myself an honest woman; that I was your wife intentionally, though not in the eyes of the world, and that it was as effectual a marriage that had passed between us as if we had been publicly wedded by the parson of the parish.", "하지만 저는 당신을 알아요. 그래도 저는 당신께 청하니, 당신이 저와 나눈 그 긴 이야기들을, 저더러 나 자신을 떳떳한 여자로 믿게 하려고 들인 그 숱한 시간의 수고를 기억해 주세요."], ["You know and cannot but remember that these have been your own words to me.”", "제가 비록 세상의 눈에는 아니어도 뜻으로는 당신의 아내라는 것을, 그리고 우리 사이에 오간 것이 마치 우리가 교구 목사 앞에서 공공연히 혼례를 올리기라도 한 것처럼 실질적인 혼인이었다는 것을요. 이것이 당신이 제게 한 당신 자신의 말이었다는 걸, 당신도 알고 있고 기억하지 않을 수 없을 거예요.\""]], [["I found this was a little too close upon him, but I made it up in what follows.", "나는 이것이 그에게 좀 너무 따끔하다는 것을 알았으나, 뒤이은 말로 그것을 보충했다."], ["He stood stock-still for a while and said nothing, and I went on thus: “You cannot,”", "그는 한동안 미동도 없이 서서 아무 말이 없었고, 나는 이렇게 말을 이었다. \"당신은,\""], ["says I, “without the highest injustice, believe that I yielded upon all these persuasions without a love not to be questioned, not to be shaken again by anything that could happen afterward.", "하고 내가 말했다, \"더없이 큰 부당함을 저지르지 않고서는, 제가 그 모든 설득에 굴복한 것이 의심할 여지 없는 사랑 없이, 그 뒤로 어떤 일이 일어나든 다시 흔들리지 않을 사랑 없이 그리한 것이라고 믿을 수 없어요."], ["If you have such dishonourable thoughts of me, I must ask you what foundation in any of my behaviour have I given for such a suggestion?", "만일 당신이 저를 그토록 명예롭지 못하게 생각한다면, 저는 묻지 않을 수 없어요. 제 어떤 행실이 그런 억측의 빌미를 주었는지를요."]], [["“If, then, I have yielded to the importunities of my affection, and if I have been persuaded to believe that I am really, and in the essence of the thing, your wife, shall I now give the lie to all those arguments and call myself your whore, or mistress, which is the same thing?", "그러니, 제가 만일 제 애정의 채근에 굴복했다면, 그리고 제가 정말로, 그 본질에서, 당신의 아내라고 믿도록 설득당했다면, 이제 와서 저는 그 모든 논거를 거짓이라 하고, 나 자신을 당신의 창녀라, 혹은 같은 말이지만 정부라 불러야 하나요? 그리고 당신은 저를 당신의 동생에게 넘기시겠다는 건가요?"], ["And will you transfer me to your brother?", "당신이 제 애정을 넘길 수 있나요?"], ["Can you transfer my affection?", "당신이 저더러 당신을 사랑하기를 그치고 그를 사랑하라 명할 수 있나요?"], ["Can you bid me cease loving you, and bid me love him?", "제가 청한다고 그런 변화를 일으키는 것이 제 힘으로 되는 일이라 보시나요? 아니에요, 나리,\""], ["It is in my power, think you, to make such a change at demand? No, sir,”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"분명히 알아 두세요, 그것은 불가능해요."], ["said I, “depend upon it ’tis impossible, and whatever the change of your side may be, I will ever be true; and I had much rather, since it is come that unhappy length, be your whore than your brother’s wife.”", "그리고 당신 쪽의 변화가 어떠하든, 저는 언제까지나 진실할 거예요. 그리고 일이 이 불행한 지경에까지 이르렀으니, 저는 당신 동생의 아내가 되느니 차라리 당신의 창녀가 되는 편이 훨씬 나아요.\""]], [["He appeared pleased and touched with the impression of this last discourse, and told me that he stood where he did before; that he had not been unfaithful to me in any one promise he had ever made yet, but that there were so many terrible things presented themselves to his view in the affair before me, and that on my account in particular, that he had thought of the other as a remedy so effectual as nothing could come up to it. That he thought this would not be entire parting us, but we might love as friends all our days, and perhaps with more satisfaction than we should in the station we were now in, as things might happen; that he durst say, I could not apprehend anything from him as to betraying a secret, which could not but be the destruction of us both, if it came out; that he had but one question to ask of me that could lie in the way of it, and if that question was answered in the negative, he could not but think still it was the only step I could take.", "그는 이 마지막 이야기의 감화에 흐뭇해하고 마음이 움직인 듯했으니, 자기는 전에 섰던 자리에 그대로 서 있다고, 자기는 여태껏 한 어떤 약속에서도 내게 신의를 저버린 적이 없다고 했다. 다만 내 앞에 놓인 이 일에서, 특히 나를 위해서 너무나 끔찍한 일들이 숱하게 눈앞에 떠올랐던 터라, 다른 길을 그 무엇으로도 따를 수 없을 만큼 효험 있는 처방으로 여겼던 것이라고 했다. 그는 이것이 우리를 완전히 갈라놓는 것은 아닐 테고, 우리가 평생 벗으로 사랑할 수 있으며, 어쩌면 일이 어찌 되느냐에 따라서는 지금의 처지에서보다 더 큰 만족을 누리며 그럴 수도 있으리라 생각한다고 했다. 또 자기로 말하자면, 발설되기만 하면 우리 둘 다의 파멸일 수밖에 없는 비밀을 누설하는 일에 관해서는 내가 자기에게서 아무것도 염려할 게 없으리라 감히 말한다고 했다. 그리고 다만 그 일에 걸림돌이 될 만한 질문이 하나 있는데, 만일 그 질문에 부정으로 답한다면, 자기는 아무래도 이것이 내가 취할 수 있는 유일한 길이라고 여길 수밖에 없다고 했다."]], [["I guessed at his question presently, namely, whether I was sure I was not with child?", "나는 곧 그의 질문이 무엇인지 짐작했으니, 곧 내가 아이를 배지 않았다고 확신하느냐는 것이었다."], ["As to that, I told him he need not be concerned about it, for I was not with child.", "그것에 관해서라면 그가 마음 쓸 필요가 없다고, 나는 아이를 배지 않았다고 그에게 말했다."], ["“Why, then, my dear,”", "\"그렇다면, 내 사랑,\""], ["says he, “we have no time to talk further now.", "하고 그가 말했다, \"지금은 더 이야기할 겨를이 없소."], ["Consider of it, and think closely about it; I cannot but be of the opinion still, that it will be the best course you can take.”", "그것을 헤아려 보고, 그것에 관해 곰곰이 생각해 보구려. 나는 그래도 그것이 당신이 취할 수 있는 가장 나은 길이라는 생각을 떨칠 수가 없소.\""], ["And with this he took his leave, and the more hastily too, his mother and sisters ringing at the gate, just at the moment that he had risen up to go.", "그러고는 그가 작별을 고했으니, 그것도 더욱 황급히 그러했던 것은, 그가 떠나려고 막 일어선 바로 그 순간에 그의 어머니와 누이들이 대문에서 종을 울렸기 때문이다."]], [["He left me in the utmost confusion of thought; and he easily perceived it the next day, and all the rest of the week, for it was but Tuesday evening when we talked; but he had no opportunity to come at me all that week, till the Sunday after, when I, being indisposed, did not go to church, and he, making some excuse for the like, stayed at home.", "그는 나를 더없이 어지러운 심사 속에 남겨 두고 떠났고, 그는 다음 날 그것을 쉽게 알아챘으며, 그 주 내내 그러했으니, 우리가 이야기를 나눈 것이 화요일 저녁에 지나지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 그 주 내내 나를 찾아올 기회가 없었고, 그다음 일요일이 되어서야, 내가 몸이 좋지 않아 교회에 가지 않고, 그도 비슷한 핑계를 대고 집에 남았다."]], [["And now he had me an hour and a half again by myself, and we fell into the same arguments all over again, or at least so near the same, as it would be to no purpose to repeat them.", "이제 그는 다시 나를 단둘이 한 시간 반 동안 차지했고, 우리는 그 모든 논거로 처음부터 다시 빠져들었으니, 적어도 거의 똑같은 것이라 그것을 되풀이해 봐야 아무 소용이 없을 정도였다."], ["At last I asked him warmly, what opinion he must have of my modesty, that he could suppose I should so much as entertain a thought of lying with two brothers, and assured him it could never be.", "마침내 나는 그에게 격앙되어, 내가 두 형제와 동침할 생각을 조금이라도 품으리라 짐작할 수 있다니 내 정숙함을 대체 어떻게 보느냐고 물으며, 그런 일은 결코 있을 수 없다고 단언했다. 나는 덧붙이기를, 설령 그가 나를 다시는 보지 않겠노라 말한다 해도—죽음 외에는 그보다 더 끔찍한 것이 없겠지만—나는 나 자신에게 그토록 명예롭지 못하고 그에게 그토록 비열한 생각은 결코 품을 수 없다고 했다."], ["I added, if he was to tell me that he would never see me more, than which nothing but death could be more terrible, yet I could never entertain a thought so dishonourable to myself, and so base to him; and therefore, I entreated him, if he had one grain of respect or affection left for me, that he would speak no more of it to me, or that he would pull his sword out and kill me.", "그러니 나는 그에게, 만일 나를 향한 존경이나 애정이 한 톨이라도 남아 있거든, 그것에 관해 내게 더는 말하지 말든지, 아니면 칼을 뽑아 나를 죽여 달라고 간청했다. 그는 자신이 일컫는바 내 완고함에 놀란 듯했고, 내가 이 일로 나 자신에게도, 또 자기에게도 매정하게 군다고 했다."], ["He appeared surprised at my obstinacy, as he called it; told me I was unkind to myself, and unkind to him in it; that it was a crisis unlooked for upon us both, and impossible for either of us to foresee, but that he did not see any other way to save us both from ruin, and therefore he thought it the more unkind; but that if he must say no more of it to me, he added with an unusual coldness, that he did not know anything else we had to talk of; and so he rose up to take his leave.", "그것은 우리 둘 다에게 뜻밖에 닥친 위기라 어느 쪽도 미리 내다볼 수 없었던 것이지만, 자기는 우리 둘 다를 파멸에서 구할 다른 길이 보이지 않으니 그래서 그게 더욱 매정하게 여겨진다고 했다. 그러나 만일 그것에 관해 더는 말해서는 안 된다면, 하고 그는 여느 때 없는 냉랭함으로 덧붙이기를, 우리에게 달리 이야기할 게 있는지 모르겠다고 했다."], ["I rose up too, as if with the same indifference; but when he came to give me as it were a parting kiss, I burst out into such a passion of crying, that though I would have spoke, I could not, and only pressing his hand, seemed to give him the adieu, but cried vehemently.", "그러고는 작별을 고하려 일어섰다. 나도 마치 같은 무심함으로 그러는 양 일어섰으나, 그가 이를테면 작별의 입맞춤을 하려고 다가오자, 나는 그토록 격렬한 울음을 터뜨려, 말을 하려 해도 할 수가 없었고, 그저 그의 손을 꼭 쥐어 작별을 고하는 듯했으나, 격하게 울고 말았다."]], [["He was sensibly moved with this; so he sat down again, and said a great many kind things to me, to abate the excess of my passion, but still urged the necessity of what he had proposed; all the while insisting, that if I did refuse, he would notwithstanding provide for me; but letting me plainly see that he would decline me in the main point—nay, even as a mistress; making it a point of honour not to lie with the woman that, for aught he knew, might come to be his brother’s wife.", "그는 이것에 적잖이 마음이 움직였다. 그리하여 그는 다시 앉아, 내 격정의 지나침을 가라앉히려고 다정한 말을 잔뜩 했으나, 그래도 자기가 제안한 일의 불가피함을 거듭 다그쳤다. 그러면서도 줄곧, 만일 내가 정 거절한다면 그래도 자기가 나를 돌보아 주겠노라 고집하면서도, 그가 정작 가장 중요한 점에서는 나를 마다하리라는 것을—아니, 정부로서조차도—내게 뻔히 보여 주었으니, 자기가 아는 한 자기 동생의 아내가 될지도 모르는 여자와는 동침하지 않는 것을 명예의 한 점으로 삼았던 것이다."]], [["The bare loss of him as a gallant was not so much my affliction as the loss of his person, whom indeed I loved to distraction; and the loss of all the expectations I had, and which I always had built my hopes upon, of having him one day for my husband.", "그저 그를 정인으로 잃는다는 것은 내게 그리 큰 슬픔이 아니었고, 정작 슬픔은 그의 사람을 잃는다는 것, 정말이지 내가 미칠 듯이 사랑했던 그 사람을 잃는다는 것이었으며, 또 내가 품었던, 그리고 늘 그 위에 내 희망을 쌓아 올렸던, 언젠가 그를 내 남편으로 삼으리라는 그 모든 기대를 잃는다는 것이었다."], ["These things oppressed my mind so much, that, in short, I fell very ill; the agonies of my mind, in a word, threw me into a high fever, and long it was, that none in the family expected my life.", "이런 것들이 어찌나 내 마음을 짓눌렀던지, 한마디로 나는 몹시 앓아눕고 말았다. 한마디로, 내 마음의 고뇌가 나를 고열에 빠뜨렸고, 집안의 누구도 내가 살아남으리라 여기지 않은 채 오랜 시일이 흘렀다."]], [["I was reduced very low indeed, and was often delirious and light-headed; but nothing lay so near me as the fear that, when I was light-headed, I should say something or other to his prejudice.", "나는 정말이지 매우 쇠약해졌고, 자주 헛소리를 하며 정신이 오락가락했다. 그러나 무엇보다 내게 절박했던 것은, 정신이 오락가락할 때 그에게 해가 될 무슨 말이라도 하지 않을까 하는 두려움이었다."], ["I was distressed in my mind also to see him, and so he was to see me, for he really loved me most passionately; but it could not be; there was not the least room to desire it on one side or other, or so much as to make it decent.", "나는 그를 보고 싶어 마음이 괴로웠고, 그도 나를 보고 싶어 그러했으니, 그가 정말로 나를 더없이 열렬히 사랑했기 때문이다. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 어느 쪽에서든 그것을 바랄 여지가 조금도 없었고, 그것을 점잖게 차릴 여지조차 없었다."]], [["It was near five weeks that I kept my bed and though the violence of my fever abated in three weeks, yet it several times returned; and the physicians said two or three times, they could do no more for me, but that they must leave nature and the distemper to fight it out, only strengthening the first with cordials to maintain the struggle.", "나는 거의 다섯 주 동안 자리보전을 했고, 비록 열의 기세가 세 주 만에 누그러지긴 했으나, 그래도 여러 차례 도졌다. 의사들은 두세 번이나, 자기들로서는 더 해 줄 것이 없으니 자연과 병이 싸워 결판을 내도록 맡길 수밖에 없고, 다만 강심제로 자연을 북돋워 그 싸움을 버티게 할 따름이라고 했다."], ["After the end of five weeks I grew better, but was so weak, so altered, so melancholy, and recovered so slowly, that the physicians apprehended I should go into a consumption; and which vexed me most, they gave it as their opinion that my mind was oppressed, that something troubled me, and, in short, that I was in love.", "다섯 주가 지난 끝에 나는 차도가 있었으나, 어찌나 쇠약하고, 어찌나 변해 버리고, 어찌나 우울하며, 또 어찌나 더디게 회복되던지, 의사들은 내가 폐병에 걸리지나 않을까 염려했다. 그리고 무엇보다 나를 분하게 한 것은, 그들이 내 마음이 짓눌려 있고 무언가 나를 괴롭히고 있으며, 한마디로 내가 사랑에 빠져 있다는 것을 자기네 소견으로 내놓았다는 점이다."], ["Upon this, the whole house was set upon me to examine me, and to press me to tell whether I was in love or not, and with whom; but as I well might, I denied my being in love at all.", "그러자 온 집안이 나를 다그쳐, 내가 사랑에 빠졌는지 아닌지, 또 누구와 그러한지 말하라고 캐물었으나, 나는 그럴 만도 했듯이, 사랑에 빠진 일이 전혀 없다고 잡아뗐다."]], [["They had on this occasion a squabble one day about me at table, that had like to have put the whole family in an uproar, and for some time did so.", "이 일로 어느 날 식탁에서 나를 두고 한바탕 다툼이 벌어졌으니, 온 집안을 발칵 뒤집어 놓을 뻔했고, 한동안은 정말로 그러했다."], ["They happened to be all at table but the father; as for me, I was ill, and in my chamber.", "마침 아버지만 빼고 식구가 모두 식탁에 있었는데, 나로 말하자면 몸이 아파 내 방에 있었다."], ["At the beginning of the talk, which was just as they had finished their dinner, the old gentlewoman, who had sent me somewhat to eat, called her maid to go up and ask me if I would have any more; but the maid brought down word I had not eaten half what she had sent me already.", "그 이야기의 발단은, 그들이 막 점심을 끝냈을 때, 내게 먹을 것을 좀 보냈던 노부인이 하녀를 불러 올려보내, 내가 더 먹겠는지 물어보라고 한 것이었다. 그러나 하녀는 내가 이미 보낸 것의 절반도 먹지 않았다는 말을 가지고 내려왔다."]], [["“Alas,”", "\"저런,\""], ["says the old lady, “that poor girl!", "하고 노부인이 말했다, \"저 가엾은 아이!"], ["I am afraid she will never be well.”", "영 낫지 못할까 봐 걱정이구나.\""]], [["“Well!”", "\"낫다니요!\""], ["says the elder brother, “how should Mrs. Betty be well?", "하고 큰아들이 말했다, \"베티 아씨가 어떻게 나을 수가 있겠어요?"], ["They say she is in love.”", "사랑에 빠졌다는데요.\""]], [["“I believe nothing of it,”", "\"난 그런 건 조금도 믿지 않는다,\""], ["says the old gentlewoman.", "하고 노부인이 말했다."]], [["“I don’t know,”", "\"전 뭐라 해야 할지 모르겠네요,\""], ["says the eldest sister, “what to say to it; they have made such a rout about her being so handsome, and so charming, and I know not what, and that in her hearing too, that has turned the creature’s head, I believe, and who knows what possessions may follow such doings?", "하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그 애가 그토록 예쁘고, 그토록 매혹적이라느니 어쩌니 하며, 그것도 그 애가 듣는 데서 그렇게들 떠들어 댔으니, 그게 그 애 머리를 돌려놓은 거라고 봐요. 그런 짓거리 뒤에 무슨 망상이 따라붙을지 누가 알겠어요?"], ["For my part, I don’t know what to make of it.”", "저로서는 도무지 영문을 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“Why, sister, you must acknowledge she is very handsome,”", "\"이봐, 누이, 그래도 그 애가 아주 예쁘다는 건 인정해야지,\""], ["says the elder brother.", "하고 큰아들이 말했다."]], [["“Ay, and a great deal handsomer than you, sister,”", "\"그래요, 게다가 누이보다 훨씬 더 예쁘죠,\""], ["says Robin, “and that’s your mortification.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그게 누이의 분한 점이고요.\""]], [["“Well, well, that is not the question,”", "\"됐어, 됐어, 그건 문제가 아니야,\""], ["says his sister; “that girl is well enough, and she knows it well enough; she need not be told of it to make her vain.”", "하고 그의 누이가 말했다, \"그 애가 제법 반반한 건 맞고, 자기도 그걸 아주 잘 알아. 굳이 그런 말을 해 줘서 우쭐하게 만들 필요는 없잖아.\""]], [["“We are not talking of her being vain,”", "\"우리가 그 애가 우쭐하다는 이야기를 하는 게 아니라,\" 하고 큰아들이 말했다, \"사랑에 빠졌다는 이야기를 하는 거잖아."], ["says the elder brother, “but of her being in love; it may be she is in love with herself; it seems my sisters think so.”", "어쩌면 자기 자신과 사랑에 빠졌는지도 모르지. 내 누이들 생각이 그런 모양이니까.\""]], [["“I would she was in love with me,”", "\"그 애가 나하고 사랑에 빠졌으면 좋겠네,\""], ["says Robin; “I’d quickly put her out of her pain.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그럼 내가 얼른 그 애의 고통을 덜어 줄 텐데.\""]], [["“What d’ye mean by that, son,”", "\"그게 무슨 소리냐, 얘야,\""], ["says the old lady; “how can you talk so?”", "하고 노부인이 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말을 하느냐?\""]], [["“Why, madam,”", "\"아니, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, again, very honestly, “do you think I’d let the poor girl die for love, and of one that is near at hand to be had, too?”", "하고 로빈이 다시, 아주 정직하게 말했다, \"그 가엾은 아이를 사랑 때문에, 그것도 손만 뻗으면 닿을 데 있는 사람 때문에 죽게 내버려 두라는 말씀이세요?\""]], [["“Fie, brother!”, says the second sister, “how can you talk so?", "\"어머, 오라버니!\" 하고 둘째 딸이 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말을 해요?"], ["Would you take a creature that has not a groat in the world?”", "세상에 한 푼도 없는 계집애를 거두겠다는 거예요?\""]], [["“Prithee, child,”", "\"이봐, 얘야,\""], ["says Robin, “beauty’s a portion, and good-humour with it is a double portion; I wish thou hadst half her stock of both for thy portion.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"아름다움은 곧 지참금이고, 거기에 명랑함까지 더하면 두 곱절의 지참금이야. 네 지참금으로 그 애가 가진 그 두 가지의 절반이라도 네게 있었으면 싶구나.\""], ["So there was her mouth stopped.", "그리하여 그녀의 입이 막혀 버렸다."]], [["“I find,”", "\"내가 보기엔,\""], ["says the eldest sister, “if Betty is not in love, my brother is.", "하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"베티가 사랑에 빠진 게 아니라면, 오라버니가 빠진 거예요."], ["I wonder he has not broke his mind to Betty; I warrant she won’t say No.”", "어째서 베티에게 속내를 털어놓지 않았는지 모르겠네. 장담하건대 그 애는 아니라고 하지 않을걸요.\""]], [["“They that yield when they’re asked,”", "\"청을 받았을 때 굴복하는 자들은,\""], ["says Robin, “are one step before them that were never asked to yield, sister, and two steps before them that yield before they are asked; and that’s an answer to you, sister.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"한 번도 굴복하라는 청을 받지 못한 자들보다 한 걸음 앞서 있고, 청을 받기도 전에 굴복하는 자들보다는 두 걸음 앞서 있지. 그게 누이에 대한 답이야.\""]], [["This fired the sister, and she flew into a passion, and said, things were come to that pass that it was time the wench, meaning me, was out of the family; and but that she was not fit to be turned out, she hoped her father and mother would consider of it as soon as she could be removed.", "이 말이 누이를 발끈하게 했고, 그녀는 격노하여, 이제는 그 계집—나를 말함이다—이 집안에서 나가야 할 때가 되었다고 말했다. 그리고 그 애가 쫓아낼 만한 상태가 아니라 그렇지, 그 애가 거동할 수 있게 되는 대로 아버지와 어머니가 그것을 고려해 주시기를 바란다고 했다."]], [["Robin replied, that was business for the master and mistress of the family, who where not to be taught by one that had so little judgment as his eldest sister.", "로빈은 그것은 이 집안의 주인과 안주인이 결정할 일이지, 큰누이처럼 분별이 모자란 사람에게 가르침을 받을 일은 아니라고 대꾸했다."]], [["It ran up a great deal farther; the sister scolded, Robin rallied and bantered, but poor Betty lost ground by it extremely in the family.", "그것은 훨씬 더 격해졌으니, 누이는 악다구니를 했고 로빈은 비아냥거리며 빈정댔으나, 그 일로 가엾은 베티는 집안에서 입지가 몹시 좁아졌다."], ["I heard of it, and I cried heartily, and the old lady came up to me, somebody having told her that I was so much concerned about it.", "나는 그 이야기를 전해 듣고 진심으로 울었고, 노부인이 내게로 올라왔으니, 누군가 내가 그 일로 몹시 상심하고 있다고 그녀에게 일러 준 것이었다."], ["I complained to her, that it was very hard the doctors should pass such a censure upon me, for which they had no ground; and that it was still harder, considering the circumstances I was under in the family; that I hoped I had done nothing to lessen her esteem for me, or given any occasion for the bickering between her sons and daughters, and I had more need to think of a coffin than of being in love, and begged she would not let me suffer in her opinion for anybody’s mistakes but my own.", "나는 그녀에게 하소연하기를, 의사들이 아무런 근거도 없이 내게 그런 진단을 내리다니 정말이지 너무하다고, 게다가 내가 집안에서 처한 사정을 생각하면 그것은 더더욱 너무한 일이라고 했다. 나는 그녀의 존경을 잃을 만한 짓이라곤 아무것도 하지 않았기를, 또 그녀의 아들들과 딸들 사이의 다툼에 어떤 빌미도 주지 않았기를 바란다고 했고, 나는 사랑에 빠지기보다는 관桁을 생각해야 할 처지라고 하면서, 다른 누구의 잘못도 아닌 오직 나 자신의 잘못이 아니고서는 그녀의 평가 속에서 손해를 보지 않게 해 달라고 간청했다."]], [["She was sensible of the justice of what I said, but told me, since there had been such a clamour among them, and that her younger son talked after such a rattling way as he did, she desired I would be so faithful to her as to answer her but one question sincerely.", "그녀는 내 말의 정당함을 헤아렸으나, 그들 사이에 그런 소란이 일었고 작은아들이 그렇게 헛소리 같은 식으로 떠들어 댄 터이니, 나더러 그녀에게 충직하여 단 한 가지 질문에만 진실하게 답해 달라고 했다. 나는 진심으로, 더없이 솔직하고 진실하게 그러겠노라고 했다."], ["I told her I would, with all my heart, and with the utmost plainness and sincerity.", "그래서 그 질문인즉, 그녀의 아들 로버트와 나 사이에 무슨 일이 있느냐는 것이었다."], ["Why, then, the question was, whether there was anything between her son Robert and me.", "나는 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 진실의 맹세를 다해, 또 그럴 만도 했듯이, 그런 일은 없으며 일찍이 없었다고 그녀에게 말했다."], ["I told her with all the protestations of sincerity that I was able to make, and as I might well, do, that there was not, nor ever had been; I told her that Mr.", "나는 그녀에게, 로버트 도련님이 그분의 버릇인 줄 그녀도 알다시피 허튼소리를 하고 농을 친 것이며, 나는 늘 그것을, 짐작건대 그분이 의도한 대로, 아무 뜻도 없는 거칠고 가벼운 말투로 받아들였다고 했다."], ["Robert had rattled and jested, as she knew it was his way, and that I took it always, as I supposed he meant it, to be a wild airy way of discourse that had no signification in it; and again assured her, that there was not the least tittle of what she understood by it between us; and that those who had suggested it had done me a great deal of wrong, and Mr. Robert no service at all.", "그리고 그녀가 그것으로 이해한 그런 일은 우리 사이에 티끌만큼도 없다고 거듭 단언했으며, 그것을 암시한 자들은 내게 큰 해를 끼쳤고 로버트 도련님께도 아무런 득이 되지 않았다고 했다."]], [["The old lady was fully satisfied, and kissed me, spoke cheerfully to me, and bid me take care of my health and want for nothing, and so took her leave.", "노부인은 충분히 흡족해하며 내게 입을 맞추고, 명랑하게 말을 건네며, 몸조심하고 부족함이 없도록 하라고 이르고는 작별을 고했다. 그러나 그녀가 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 아들과 그의 모든 누이가 한데 모여 으르렁대고 있었다."], ["But when she came down she found the brother and all his sisters together by the ears; they were angry, even to passion, at his upbraiding them with their being homely, and having never had any sweethearts, never having been asked the question, and their being so forward as almost to ask first. He rallied them upon the subject of Mrs.", "그들은 격노하여 펄펄 뛰었으니, 그가 그들이 못생겼다고, 한 번도 정인을 가져 본 적이 없다고, 한 번도 그런 청을 받아 본 적이 없다고, 오히려 거의 먼저 청할 만큼 그토록 들이댔다고 면박을 준 까닭이었다."], ["Betty; how pretty, how good-humoured, how she sung better than they did, and danced better, and how much handsomer she was; and in doing this he omitted no ill-natured thing that could vex them, and indeed, pushed too hard upon them.", "그는 베티 아씨라는 주제를 두고 그들을 비아냥거렸으니, 그 애가 얼마나 예쁜지, 얼마나 명랑한지, 그 애가 그들보다 노래를 얼마나 더 잘하고 춤을 얼마나 더 잘 추는지, 또 그 애가 얼마나 더 예쁜지를 떠들어 댔다. 그러면서 그는 그들을 약 올릴 만한 심술궂은 말이라면 하나도 빼놓지 않았고, 정말이지 그들을 너무 심하게 몰아붙였다."], ["The old lady came down in the height of it, and to put a stop it to, told them all the discourse she had had with me, and how I answered, that there was nothing between Mr. Robert and I.", "노부인은 그것이 한창일 때 아래층으로 내려와, 그것을 그치게 하려고, 자기가 나와 나눈 이야기를 그들에게 모두 들려주었으며, 로버트 도련님과 나 사이에는 아무 일도 없다고 내가 어떻게 답했는지를 말했다."]], [["“She’s wrong there,”", "\"거기선 그 애가 틀렸어요,\""], ["says Robin, “for if there was not a great deal between us, we should be closer together than we are.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"우리 사이에 많은 일이 없었던 거라면, 우리는 지금보다 더 가까이 있었을 테니까요."], ["I told her I loved her hugely,”", "나는 그 애를 끔찍이 사랑한다고 그 애에게 말했어요,\""], ["says he, “but I could never make the jade believe I was in earnest.”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"하지만 내가 진심이라는 걸 도무지 그 말괄량이에게 믿게 할 수가 없었죠.\""], ["“I do not know how you should,”", "\"그걸 어떻게 믿게 하겠니,\""], ["says his mother; “nobody in their senses could believe you were in earnest, to talk so to a poor girl, whose circumstances you know so well.", "하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"제정신인 사람이라면 아무도, 네가 사정을 그토록 잘 아는 가엾은 아이에게 그런 식으로 말하는 걸 진심이라고는 믿지 못할 거다."]], [["“But prithee, son,”", "하지만 얘야,\""], ["adds she, “since you tell me that you could not make her believe you were in earnest, what must we believe about it?", "하고 그녀가 덧붙였다, \"네가 진심이라는 걸 그 애에게 믿게 할 수 없었다고 하니, 우리는 그것을 어떻게 믿어야 하느냐? 네가 이야기 중에 어찌나 횡설수설하는지, 네가 진심인지 농담인지 아무도 모르겠구나."], ["For you ramble so in your discourse, that nobody knows whether you are in earnest or in jest; but as I find the girl, by your own confession, has answered truly, I wish you would do so too, and tell me seriously, so that I may depend upon it.", "하지만 그 애가, 제 입으로 고백한 바로는, 진실하게 답했다는 걸 알았으니, 너도 그래 주었으면 한다. 내가 믿고 의지할 수 있도록 진지하게 말해 다오."], ["Is there anything in it or no?", "거기에 뭔가 있느냐, 없느냐?"], ["Are you in earnest or no?", "너 진심이냐, 아니냐?"], ["Are you distracted, indeed, or are you not?", "정말로 정신이 나간 거냐, 아니냐?"], ["’Tis a weighty question, and I wish you would make us easy about it.”", "그건 무거운 질문이니, 우리 마음을 좀 놓이게 해 주었으면 한다.\""]], [["“By my faith, madam,”", "\"맹세코, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, “’tis in vain to mince the matter or tell any more lies about it; I am in earnest, as much as a man is that’s going to be hanged.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"이 일을 에두르거나 더 거짓말을 늘어놓아 봐야 헛일이에요. 저는 교수대로 끌려가는 사람만큼이나 진심이에요."], ["If Mrs. Betty would say she loved me, and that she would marry me, I’d have her tomorrow morning fasting, and say, ‘To have and to hold,’ instead of eating my breakfast.”", "베티 아씨가 저를 사랑한다고, 저와 혼인하겠다고 말해만 준다면, 저는 내일 아침 식사도 거른 채 그 애를 맞아, 아침밥을 먹는 대신 '검은 머리 파뿌리 되도록'이라고 말할 거예요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그래,\""], ["says the mother, “then there’s one son lost”; and she said it in a very mournful tone, as one greatly concerned at it.", "하고 어머니가 말했다, \"그렇다면 아들 하나를 잃은 셈이로구나.\" 그녀는 그것을 매우 애통한 어조로, 그것을 크게 근심하는 사람처럼 말했다."]], [["“I hope not, madam,”", "\"잃은 게 아니길 바라요, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin; “no man is lost when a good wife has found him.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"좋은 아내가 찾아낸 사내는 잃은 게 아니니까요.\""]], [["“Why, but, child,”", "\"하지만 얘야,\""], ["says the old lady, “she is a beggar.”", "하고 노부인이 말했다, \"그 애는 거지나 다름없잖니.\""]], [["“Why, then, madam, she has the more need of charity,”", "\"아니, 그렇다면, 어머니, 그 애는 그만큼 자비가 더 필요하겠네요,\""], ["says Robin; “I’ll take her off the hands of the parish, and she and I’ll beg together.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"제가 그 애를 교구의 손에서 거두어 데려갈 테니, 그 애와 저는 함께 동냥이나 하지요.\""]], [["“It’s bad jesting with such things,”", "\"그런 일로 농담을 하는 건 못쓴다,\""], ["says the mother.", "하고 어머니가 말했다."]], [["“I don’t jest, madam,” says Robin.", "\"농담이 아니에요, 어머니,\""], ["“We’ll come and beg your pardon, madam; and your blessing, madam, and my father’s.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"저희가 가서 용서를 구할게요, 어머니. 그리고 어머니의 축복을, 또 아버지의 축복을 구할게요.\""]], [["“This is all out of the way, son,”", "\"이건 다 도리에 어긋나는 짓이다, 얘야,\""], ["says the mother. “If you are in earnest you are undone.”", "하고 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 진심이라면 넌 끝장난 거야.\""]], [["“I am afraid not,”", "\"그렇지 않을까 봐 걱정이에요,\""], ["says he, “for I am really afraid she won’t have me; after all my sister’s huffing and blustering, I believe I shall never be able to persuade her to it.”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"사실 저는 정말로 그 애가 저를 받아들이지 않을까 봐 걱정이거든요. 제 누이의 그 모든 윽박지름과 호통 끝에, 아무래도 저는 도무지 그 애를 설득해 낼 수 없을 것 같아요.\""]], [["“That’s a fine tale, indeed; she is not so far out of her senses neither.", "\"그것참 그럴듯한 이야기네요. 그 애도 그렇게 정신이 나간 건 아니에요."], ["Mrs. Betty is no fool,”", "베티 아씨는 바보가 아니거든요,\""], ["says the younger sister. “Do you think she has learnt to say No, any more than other people?”", "하고 작은딸이 말했다, \"그 애가 다른 사람들 못지않게 아니라고 말하는 법을 배웠다고 생각하나요?\""]], [["“No, Mrs. Mirth-wit,”", "\"아니지, 농담 잘하는 아가씨,\""], ["says Robin, “Mrs. Betty’s no fool; but Mrs.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"베티 아씨는 바보가 아니야."], ["Betty may be engaged some other way, and what then?”", "하지만 베티 아씨가 어딘가 다른 데 매여 있을지도 모르지, 그럼 어쩔 테냐?\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says the eldest sister, “we can say nothing to that.", "하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그건 우리도 뭐라 할 말이 없네요."], ["Who must it be to, then?", "그럼 그 상대가 누구여야 하죠?"], ["She is never out of the doors; it must be between you.”", "그 애는 도통 문밖에 나가는 법이 없으니, 그건 분명 오라버니들 사이일 텐데요.\""]], [["“I have nothing to say to that,” says Robin.", "\"그건 내가 할 말이 없어,\""], ["“I have been examined enough; there’s my brother.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"나는 충분히 심문을 받았어. 저기 내 형님이 있잖아."], ["If it must be between us, go to work with him.”", "우리 사이여야 한다면, 형님에게 한번 캐 보든지.\""]], [["This stung the elder brother to the quick, and he concluded that Robin had discovered something.", "이 말이 큰형을 정통으로 찔렀고, 그는 로빈이 무언가 알아챈 것이라고 단정했다."], ["However, he kept himself from appearing disturbed.", "그러나 그는 동요한 기색을 드러내지 않으려 자제했다."], ["“Prithee,”", "\"이봐,\""], ["says he, “don’t go to shame your stories off upon me; I tell you, I deal in no such ware; I have nothing to say to Mrs.", "하고 그가 말했다, \"네 이야기를 내게 뒤집어씌워 망신 줄 생각 마라."], ["Betty, nor to any of the Mrs.", "분명히 말하건대, 나는 그런 물건은 취급하지 않아."], ["Bettys in the parish”; and with that he rose up and brushed off.", "나는 베티 아씨에게도, 이 교구의 어느 베티 아씨에게도 할 말이 없어.\" 그러고는 일어나 휙 나가 버렸다."]], [["“No,”", "\"맞아요,\""], ["says the eldest sister, “I dare answer for my brother; he knows the world better.”", "하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"내 큰오라버니를 위해선 내가 장담할 수 있어요. 그분은 세상 물정을 더 잘 아니까요.\""]], [["Thus the discourse ended, but it left the elder brother quite confounded.", "이렇게 그 이야기는 끝났으나, 그것은 큰형을 완전히 당혹스럽게 만들어 놓았다."], ["He concluded his brother had made a full discovery, and he began to doubt whether I had been concerned in it or not; but with all his management he could not bring it about to get at me.", "그는 동생이 모든 것을 알아챈 것이라고 단정했고, 거기에 내가 관련되었는지 아닌지 의심하기 시작했다. 그러나 온갖 수를 써 보아도 그것을 알아내려 나를 만날 수가 없었다."], ["At last he was so perplexed that he was quite desperate, and resolved he would come into my chamber and see me, whatever came of it.", "마침내 그는 어찌나 당혹스러웠던지 아주 자포자기하여, 어떻게 되든 내 방으로 들어와 나를 보기로 결심했다."], ["In order to do this, he contrived it so, that one day after dinner, watching his eldest sister till he could see her go upstairs, he runs after her.", "이를 위해 그는 일을 이렇게 꾸몄으니, 어느 날 점심 식사 뒤에, 큰누이가 위층으로 올라가는 것을 볼 수 있을 때까지 지켜보다가 그녀를 뒤따라 달려갔다."], ["“Hark ye, sister,”", "\"이봐요, 누이,\""], ["says he, “where is this sick woman?", "하고 그가 말했다, \"그 아픈 여자가 어디 있죠?"], ["May not a body see her?”", "한번 들여다봐도 될까요?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그럼요,\""], ["says the sister, “I believe you may; but let me go first a little, and I’ll tell you.”", "하고 누이가 말했다, \"괜찮을 거예요. 하지만 제가 먼저 좀 가게 해 줘요, 가서 일러 줄 테니.\""], ["So she ran up to the door and gave me notice, and presently called to him again.", "그리하여 그녀는 문간으로 달려가 내게 알리고, 곧 다시 그를 불렀다."], ["“Brother,”", "\"오라버니,\""], ["says she, “you may come if you please.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다, \"들어와도 좋대요.\""], ["So in he came, just in the same kind of rant.", "그리하여 그가 들어왔으니, 꼭 그 똑같은 떠들썩한 투였다."], ["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["says he at the door as he came in, “where is this sick body that’s in love?", "하고 그가 들어오면서 문간에서 말했다, \"사랑에 빠졌다는 그 아픈 양반이 어디 있나?"], ["How do ye do, Mrs. Betty?”", "좀 어떠세요, 베티 아씨?\""], ["I would have got up out of my chair, but was so weak I could not for a good while; and he saw it, and his sister too, and she said, “Come, do not strive to stand up; my brother desires no ceremony, especially now you are so weak.”", "나는 의자에서 일어서려 했으나, 어찌나 쇠약했던지 한참 동안 그럴 수가 없었다. 그가, 또 그의 누이도 그것을 보았고, 그녀가 말했다."], ["“No, no, Mrs.", "\"자, 일어서려고 애쓰지 말아요."], ["Betty, pray sit still,”", "내 오라버니는 격식을 바라지 않으니까요, 더구나 지금 그토록 쇠약한데.\""], ["says he, and so sits himself down in a chair over against me, and appeared as if he was mighty merry.", "\"그래요, 그래요, 베티 아씨, 부디 그대로 앉아 계세요,\" 하고 그가 말하더니, 내 맞은편 의자에 앉아, 자못 흥겨운 듯한 기색을 보였다."]], [["He talked a lot of rambling stuff to his sister and to me, sometimes of one thing, sometimes of another, on purpose to amuse his sister, and every now and then would turn it upon the old story, directing it to me.", "그는 누이에게, 또 내게 횡설수설하는 이야기를 잔뜩 늘어놓았으니, 때로는 이 이야기를, 때로는 저 이야기를 했는데, 누이를 즐겁게 하려는 속셈이었고, 이따금씩 그것을 그 옛이야기로 돌려, 내게 향하게 했다."], ["“Poor Mrs. Betty,”", "\"가엾은 베티 아씨,\""], ["says he, “it is a sad thing to be in love; why, it has reduced you sadly.”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"사랑에 빠진다는 건 딱한 일이에요. 아니, 그게 아씨를 이렇게 딱하게 만들어 놓았으니.\""], ["At last I spoke a little.", "마침내 나도 조금 입을 열었다."], ["“I am glad to see you so merry, sir,”", "\"이렇게 흥겨워하시는 걸 보니 기쁘네요, 나리,\""], ["says I; “but I think the doctor might have found something better to do than to make his game at his patients.", "하고 내가 말했다, \"하지만 의사라면 환자를 놀려 먹는 것보다는 더 나은 할 일을 찾았어야 할 것 같은데요."], ["If I had been ill of no other distemper, I know the proverb too well to have let him come to me.”", "제가 다른 병으로 앓았던 거라면, 저는 그 속담을 너무 잘 알기에 의사를 제게 오게 두지도 않았을 거예요.\""], ["“What proverb?”", "\"무슨 속담요?\""], ["says he, “Oh!", "하고 그가 말했다, \"오!"], ["I remember it now.", "이제 생각나는군요."], ["What—", "뭐더라—"]], [["“Where love is the case, The doctor’s an ass.”", "사랑이 병일 때는, 의사도 멍청이라."]], [["Is not that it, Mrs.", "그거 아니에요, 베티 아씨?\""], ["Betty?” I smiled and said nothing.", "나는 빙그레 웃으며 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says he, “I think the effect has proved it to be love, for it seems the doctor has been able to do you but little service; you mend very slowly, they say.", "하고 그가 말했다, \"내 생각엔 그 결과가 그것이 사랑임을 입증한 것 같소. 의사가 아씨에게 별 도움이 못 된 모양이니 말이오."], ["I doubt there’s somewhat in it, Mrs.", "차도가 매우 더디다고들 하던데."], ["Betty; I doubt you are sick of the incurables, and that is love.”", "거기 뭔가 있는 게 아닌가 싶소, 베티 아씨. 아씨가 불치병을 앓고 있는 게 아닌가 싶은데, 그게 바로 사랑이오.\""], ["I smiled and said, “No, indeed, sir, that’s none of my distemper.”", "나는 빙그레 웃으며 말했다, \"아니에요, 정말로, 나리, 그건 제 병이 아니에요.\""]], [["We had a deal of such discourse, and sometimes others that signified as little.", "우리는 그런 이야기를 잔뜩 나누었고, 때로는 그만큼이나 시답잖은 다른 이야기들도 나누었다."], ["By and by he asked me to sing them a song, at which I smiled, and said my singing days were over.", "이윽고 그는 내게 노래를 한 곡 불러 달라고 청했는데, 그 말에 나는 빙그레 웃으며 내 노래하던 시절은 끝났다고 했다."], ["At last he asked me if he should play upon his flute to me; his sister said she believe it would hurt me, and that my head could not bear it.", "마침내 그는 내게 자기가 플루트를 불어 줄까 하고 물었고, 그의 누이는 그것이 내게 해로울 것 같다고, 내 머리가 그것을 견디지 못할 거라고 했다."], ["I bowed, and said, No, it would not hurt me.", "나는 고개를 숙여 인사하고, 아니, 그것은 내게 해롭지 않을 거라고 했다."], ["“And, pray, madam,”", "\"그리고, 부디, 아씨,\""], ["said I, “do not hinder it; I love the music of the flute very much.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"그것을 막지 말아 주세요. 저는 플루트 소리를 무척 좋아하니까요.\""], ["Then his sister said, “Well, do, then, brother.”", "그러자 그의 누이가 말했다. \"그래요, 그럼 부세요, 오라버니.\""], ["With that he pulled out the key of his closet.", "그 말에 그는 자기 골방의 열쇠를 꺼냈다."], ["“Dear sister,”", "\"사랑하는 누이,\""], ["says he, “I am very lazy; do step to my closet and fetch my flute; it lies in such a drawer,”", "하고 그가 말했다, \"내가 영 게을러서 그러는데, 내 골방에 좀 가서 플루트를 가져다주겠어요? 그게 그런 서랍에 있어요,\""], ["naming a place where he was sure it was not, that she might be a little while a-looking for it.", "하고 그것이 거기 없는 것이 확실한 곳을 일러 주었으니, 그녀가 그것을 찾느라 한동안 헤매게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["As soon as she was gone, he related the whole story to me of the discourse his brother had about me, and of his pushing it at him, and his concern about it, which was the reason of his contriving this visit to me.", "그녀가 가자마자, 그는 자기 동생이 나에 관해 한 이야기를 모두, 또 그것을 자기에게 들이댄 일과 그 일에 대한 자기의 근심을 내게 들려주었으니, 그것이 바로 그가 이렇게 나를 찾아오기를 꾸민 까닭이었다."], ["I assured him I had never opened my mouth either to his brother or to anybody else.", "나는 그에게, 그의 동생에게든 다른 누구에게든 단 한 번도 입을 연 적이 없다고 단언했다."], ["I told him the dreadful exigence I was in; that my love to him, and his offering to have me forget that affection and remove it to another, had thrown me down; and that I had a thousand times wished I might die rather than recover, and to have the same circumstances to struggle with as I had before, and that his backwardness to life had been the great reason of the slowness of my recovering.", "나는 그에게 내가 처한 끔찍한 위급함을 이야기했으니, 그를 향한 내 사랑과, 그가 그 애정을 잊고 다른 이에게 옮기라고 청한 것이 나를 쓰러뜨렸다고, 그리고 나는 천 번이고, 회복하느니 차라리 죽어, 전에 씨름했던 것과 똑같은 사정과 다시 씨름하지 않기를 바랐다고 했다. 또 그가 삶을 향해 뒷걸음질친 것이 내 회복이 더딘 큰 까닭이었다고 했다."], ["I added that I foresaw that as soon as I was well, I must quit the family, and that as for marrying his brother, I abhorred the thoughts of it after what had been my case with him, and that he might depend upon it I would never see his brother again upon that subject; that if he would break all his vows and oaths and engagements with me, be that between his conscience and his honour and himself; but he should never be able to say that I, whom he had persuaded to call myself his wife, and who had given him the liberty to use me as a wife, was not as faithful to him as a wife ought to be, whatever he might be to me.", "나는 덧붙이기를, 몸이 낫는 대로 집안을 떠나야 하리라는 것을 내다보고 있다고, 그리고 그의 동생과 혼인하는 일로 말하자면, 그와 나 사이에 있었던 일을 생각하면 그런 생각만 해도 진저리가 난다고, 그러니 그 일에 관해서는 결코 그의 동생을 다시 보지 않으리라는 것을 그가 믿어도 좋다고 했다. 그리고 만일 그가 나와 한 그 모든 맹세와 서약과 약속을 깨뜨리겠다면, 그것은 그의 양심과 명예와 그 자신 사이의 일이지만, 그가 나더러 나 자신을 그의 아내라 부르도록 설득하고, 그가 나를 아내로 쓰도록 허락한 이 몸이, 그가 내게 어떠하든, 아내가 마땅히 그러해야 하듯이 그에게 충실하지 않았다고는 결코 말할 수 없을 거라고 했다."]], [["He was going to reply, and had said that he was sorry I could not be persuaded, and was a-going to say more, but he heard his sister a-coming, and so did I; and yet I forced out these few words as a reply, that I could never be persuaded to love one brother and marry another.", "그는 막 대꾸하려던 참이었고, 내가 설득되지 못해 안타깝다고 말하고 더 말을 이으려던 참이었으나, 그는 누이가 오는 소리를 들었고, 나도 그러했다. 그래도 나는 이 몇 마디 말을 대답으로 억지로 끄집어냈으니, 곧 나는 결코 한 형제를 사랑하면서 다른 형제와 혼인하도록 설득될 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["He shook his head and said, “Then I am ruined,”", "그는 고개를 가로저으며 \"그럼 나는 끝장이로군,\" 하고 자기 자신을 두고 말했고, 바로 그 순간 누이가 방으로 들어와 그에게 플루트를 찾을 수 없었다고 했다."], ["meaning himself; and that moment his sister entered the room and told him she could not find the flute.", "\"음,\" 하고 그가 흥겹게 말했다, \"이 게으름은 안 되겠군.\""], ["“Well,”", "그러고는 일어나 직접 그것을 찾으러 갔으나, 그 역시 그것 없이 돌아왔다."], ["says he merrily, “this laziness won’t do”; so he gets up and goes himself to go to look for it, but comes back without it too; not but that he could have found it, but because his mind was a little disturbed, and he had no mind to play; and, besides, the errand he sent his sister on was answered another way; for he only wanted an opportunity to speak to me, which he gained, though not much to his satisfaction.", "그것을 찾을 수 없어서가 아니라, 그의 마음이 조금 어지러워 불고 싶은 생각이 없었기 때문이다. 게다가 그가 누이에게 보낸 심부름은 다른 식으로 답을 얻은 셈이었으니, 그는 다만 내게 말할 기회를 원했던 것이고, 비록 그리 흡족하지는 못했어도 그것을 얻었기 때문이다."]], [["I had, however, a great deal of satisfaction in having spoken my mind to him with freedom, and with such an honest plainness, as I have related; and though it did not at all work the way I desired, that is to say, to oblige the person to me the more, yet it took from him all possibility of quitting me but by a downright breach of honour, and giving up all the faith of a gentleman to me, which he had so often engaged by, never to abandon me, but to make me his wife as soon as he came to his estate.", "그러나 나는 그에게 거리낌 없이, 또 앞서 이야기한 것처럼 그토록 정직하고 솔직하게 내 속내를 말한 데에 큰 만족이 있었다. 그리고 비록 그것이 내가 바라던 식으로는, 다시 말해 그 사람을 내게 더 묶어 두는 식으로는 전혀 작용하지 않았으나, 그래도 그것은 그가, 곧이곧대로 명예를 저버리고 그토록 자주 맹세로 다짐했던바 결코 나를 버리지 않고 자기 영지를 물려받는 대로 나를 아내로 삼겠다던 신사의 신의를 모두 내던지지 않고서는, 나를 떠날 가능성을 그에게서 모조리 앗아 갔다."]], [["It was not many weeks after this before I was about the house again, and began to grow well; but I continued melancholy, silent, dull, and retired, which amazed the whole family, except he that knew the reason of it; yet it was a great while before he took any notice of it, and I, as backward to speak as he, carried respectfully to him, but never offered to speak a word to him that was particular of any kind whatsoever; and this continued for sixteen or seventeen weeks; so that, as I expected every day to be dismissed the family, on account of what distaste they had taken another way, in which I had no guilt, so I expected to hear no more of this gentleman, after all his solemn vows and protestations, but to be ruined and abandoned.", "그로부터 그리 여러 주가 지나지 않아 나는 다시 집안을 돌아다니게 되었고 차츰 건강해지기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 여전히 우울하고, 말이 없으며, 활기가 없고, 칩거했으니, 그 까닭을 아는 그 사람만 빼고는 온 집안이 의아해했다. 그래도 그가 그것을 조금이라도 알은체하기까지는 한참이 걸렸고, 나도 그만큼 말을 꺼리며, 그에게 공손히 처신하되 어떤 종류의 사사로운 말도 결코 그에게 한마디 건네려 하지 않았다. 그리고 이것이 열엿새 주인가 열일곱 주 동안 이어졌으니, 그리하여 내가 죄도 없는 다른 일로 그들이 품은 못마땅함 탓에 날마다 집안에서 내쫓길 것을 예상했던 것처럼, 나는 이 신사에게서 그 모든 엄숙한 맹세와 서약에도 불구하고 더는 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 채, 그저 파멸하여 버림받으리라 예상했다."]], [["At last I broke the way myself in the family for my removing; for being talking seriously with the old lady one day, about my own circumstances in the world, and how my distemper had left a heaviness upon my spirits, that I was not the same thing I was before, the old lady said, “I am afraid, Betty, what I have said to you about my son has had some influence upon you, and that you are melancholy on his account; pray, will you let me know how the matter stands with you both, if it may not be improper?", "마침내 내가 떠나는 일에 관해 집안에서 내 입으로 길을 텄으니, 어느 날 노부인과 이 세상에서의 내 처지에 관해, 또 내 병이 어떻게 내 마음에 무거움을 남겨 내가 예전의 그 사람이 아니게 되었는지에 관해 진지하게 이야기를 나누던 중, 노부인이 말했다. \"베티야, 내가 내 아들에 관해 네게 한 말이 네게 어떤 영향을 미쳐, 네가 그 애 때문에 우울한 게 아닌가 걱정이로구나."], ["For, as for Robin, he does nothing but rally and banter when I speak of it to him.”", "부디, 온당치 않은 일이 아니라면, 너희 둘 사이가 어떻게 되어 있는지 내게 알려 주겠니? 로빈으로 말하자면, 내가 그것에 관해 말을 꺼내면 그저 농을 치고 비아냥거리기만 하니 말이다.\""], ["“Why, truly, madam,”", "\"사실은, 마님,\""], ["said I, “that matter stands as I wish it did not, and I shall be very sincere with you in it, whatever befalls me for it.", "하고 내가 말했다, \"그 일은 제가 바라지 않는 쪽으로 되어 있어요. 그리고 그 일로 제게 무슨 일이 닥치든, 저는 마님께 그것을 매우 진실하게 말씀드리겠어요."], ["Mr.", "로버트 도련님이 여러 차례 제게 혼인을 청하셨는데, 그건 제 가련한 처지를 생각하면 제가 기대할 까닭이 전혀 없는 일이었어요."], ["Robert has several times proposed marriage to me, which is what I had no reason to expect, my poor circumstances considered; but I have always resisted him, and that perhaps in terms more positive than became me, considering the regard that I ought to have for every branch of your family; but,”", "하지만 저는 늘 그분께 맞섰고, 그것도 어쩌면, 마님의 가문 어느 분에게나 제가 마땅히 가져야 할 도리를 생각하면, 제게 어울리지 않을 만큼 단호한 말로 그리했어요. 하지만,\""], ["said I, “madam, I could never so far forget my obligation to you and all your house, to offer to consent to a thing which I know must needs be disobliging to you, and this I have made my argument to him, and have positively told him that I would never entertain a thought of that kind unless I had your consent, and his father’s also, to whom I was bound by so many invincible obligations.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"마님, 저는 마님과 마님의 온 집안에 진 은혜를 그토록 저버려, 마님께 언짢은 일이 될 수밖에 없는 줄 아는 일에 동의하겠다고 나설 수는 도무지 없었어요. 그리고 이것을 저는 그분께 제 논거로 삼았으며, 제가 그토록 거역할 수 없는 숱한 은의로 매여 있는 마님과, 또 그분의 아버님의 동의를 얻지 못하는 한, 저는 결코 그런 종류의 생각을 품지 않겠노라고 그분께 단호히 말씀드렸어요.\""]], [["“And is this possible, Mrs.", "\"그게 정말이냐, 베티 아씨?\" 하고 노부인이 말했다."], ["Betty?”", "\"그렇다면 너는 우리가 네게 대했던 것보다 우리에게 훨씬 더 의로웠던 게로구나."], ["says the old lady.", "우리는 모두 너를 내 아들에게 놓인 일종의 덫처럼 여겼고, 나는 그것이 두려워 네게 떠나라는 제안을 할 생각이었거든. 하지만 아직 그것을 네게 말하지 않은 건, 네가 아직 온전히 낫지 않았다고 생각했고, 또 너를 너무 슬프게 하여 다시 쓰러뜨리지나 않을까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["“Then you have been much juster to us than we have been to you; for we have all looked upon you as a kind of snare to my son, and I had a proposal to make to you for your removing, for fear of it; but I had not yet mentioned it to you, because I thought you were not thorough well, and I was afraid of grieving you too much, lest it should throw you down again; for we have all a respect for you still, though not so much as to have it be the ruin of my son; but if it be as you say, we have all wronged you very much.”", "우리는 모두 여전히 너를 존중하니까, 그래도 그것이 내 아들의 파멸이 될 만큼은 아니지만 말이다. 하지만 네 말대로라면, 우리는 모두 네게 큰 잘못을 저지른 게로구나.\""]], [["“As to the truth of what I say, madam,”", "\"제가 드린 말씀의 진실에 관해서라면, 마님,\""], ["said I, “refer you to your son himself; if he will do me any justice, he must tell you the story just as I have told it.”", "하고 내가 말했다, \"마님의 아드님께 직접 여쭤보시면 돼요. 그분이 제게 조금이라도 의로우시다면, 제가 말씀드린 그대로 그 이야기를 마님께 해 드릴 거예요.\""]], [["Away goes the old lady to her daughters and tells them the whole story, just as I had told it her; and they were surprised at it, you may be sure, as I believed they would be.", "노부인은 자기 딸들에게로 가서, 내가 그녀에게 말한 그대로 그 이야기를 모두 들려주었다. 그러자 그들은, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 또 나도 그러리라 믿었다시피 그것에 놀랐다."], ["One said she could never have thought it; another said Robin was a fool; a third said she would not believe a word of it, and she would warrant that Robin would tell the story another way.", "한 사람은 도무지 그런 줄은 생각도 못 했다고 했고, 또 한 사람은 로빈이 바보라고 했으며, 세 번째 딸은 한마디도 믿지 못하겠다고, 장담하건대 로빈은 그 이야기를 다른 식으로 할 거라고 했다."], ["But the old gentlewoman, who was resolved to go to the bottom of it before I could have the least opportunity of acquainting her son with what had passed, resolved too that she would talk with her son immediately, and to that purpose sent for him, for he was gone but to a lawyer’s house in the town, upon some petty business of his own, and upon her sending he returned immediately.", "그러나 내가 그녀의 아들에게 그동안 있었던 일을 알릴 조금의 기회라도 얻기 전에 그 일의 진상을 끝까지 캐기로 마음먹은 노부인은, 그녀의 아들과 당장 이야기하기로 또한 결심하고는, 그 일로 그를 부르러 사람을 보냈다. 그가 자기의 자질구레한 볼일로 고을의 한 변호사 집에 가 있을 뿐이었던 터라, 그녀가 사람을 보내자 그는 곧바로 돌아왔다."]], [["Upon his coming up to them, for they were all still together, “Sit down, Robin,”", "그가 그들에게로 올라오자—그들은 모두 여전히 함께 있었으니—\"앉아라, 로빈,\""], ["says the old lady, “I must have some talk with you.”", "하고 노부인이 말했다, \"너와 이야기 좀 해야겠다.\""], ["“With all my heart, madam,”", "\"기꺼이 그러죠, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, looking very merry.", "하고 로빈이 자못 흥겨운 얼굴로 말했다."], ["“I hope it is about a good wife, for I am at a great loss in that affair.”", "\"좋은 아내에 관한 이야기였으면 좋겠네요, 제가 그 일로 몹시 갈팡질팡하고 있거든요.\""], ["“How can that be?”", "\"그게 어찌 된 일이냐?\""], ["says his mother; “did not you say you resolved to have Mrs.", "하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"베티 아씨를 차지하기로 결심했다고 하지 않았느냐?\""], ["Betty?” “Ay, madam,”", "\"그랬죠, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, “but there is one has forbid the banns.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"하지만 누군가가 결혼 예고를 막아 버렸어요.\""], ["“Forbid, the banns!”", "\"예고를 막다니!\""], ["says his mother; “who can that be?”", "하고 그의 어머니가 말했다, \"그게 누구냐?\""], ["“Even Mrs. Betty herself,”", "\"바로 베티 아씨 자신이에요,\""], ["says Robin.", "하고 로빈이 말했다."], ["“How so?”", "\"어째서?\""], ["says his mother.", "하고 그의 어머니가 말했다."], ["“Have you asked her the question, then?”", "\"그럼 그 애에게 청을 했단 말이냐?\""], ["“Yes, indeed, madam,” says Robin.", "\"예, 정말로요, 어머니,\""], ["“I have attacked her in form five times since she was sick, and am beaten off; the jade is so stout she won’t capitulate nor yield upon any terms, except such as I cannot effectually grant.”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그 애가 앓고 난 뒤로 다섯 번이나 격식을 갖춰 들이댔지만 번번이 퇴짜를 맞았어요. 그 말괄량이가 어찌나 꿋꿋한지, 제가 실질적으로 들어줄 수 없는 조건이 아니고서는 어떤 조건으로도 항복도 굴복도 하지 않으려 해요.\""], ["“Explain yourself,”", "\"분명히 말해 보아라,\""], ["says the mother, “for I am surprised; I do not understand you.", "하고 어머니가 말했다, \"놀랍구나. 네 말을 알아듣지 못하겠다."], ["I hope you are not in earnest.”", "네가 진심은 아니길 바란다.\""]], [["“Why, madam,”", "\"아니, 어머니,\""], ["says he, “the case is plain enough upon me, it explains itself; she won’t have me, she says; is not that plain enough?", "하고 그가 말했다, \"그 일은 제겐 충분히 분명해요, 저절로 분명해지죠. 그 애가 저를 안 받겠다고 하잖아요."], ["I think ’tis plain, and pretty rough too.”", "그거면 충분히 분명하지 않나요? 제 생각엔 분명한 데다, 꽤 매몰차기까지 해요.\""], ["“Well, but,”", "\"그래, 하지만,\""], ["says the mother, “you talk of conditions that you cannot grant; what does she want—a settlement?", "하고 어머니가 말했다, \"네가 들어줄 수 없는 조건이라니 무슨 말이냐? 그 애가 뭘 원하는 거냐—재산 설정이냐?"], ["Her jointure ought to be according to her portion; but what fortune does she bring you?”", "그 애의 과부 재산이야 마땅히 그 애의 지참금에 따라야 할 텐데, 대체 그 애가 네게 무슨 재산을 가져온다는 거냐?\" \"아니, 재산으로 말하자면,\""], ["“Nay, as to fortune,”", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"그 애는 충분히 부유해요."], ["says Robin, “she is rich enough; I am satisfied in that point; but ’tis I that am not able to come up to her terms, and she is positive she will not have me without.”", "그 점에 관해선 저는 만족해요. 하지만 그 애의 조건을 들어줄 수 없는 건 바로 저예요, 그리고 그 애는 그것 없이는 저를 받지 않겠다고 단호해요.\""]], [["Here the sisters put in.", "여기서 누이들이 끼어들었다."], ["“Madam,”", "\"어머니,\""], ["says the second sister, “’tis impossible to be serious with him; he will never give a direct answer to anything; you had better let him alone, and talk no more of it to him; you know how to dispose of her out of his way if you thought there was anything in it.”", "하고 둘째 딸이 말했다, \"오라버니와는 진지하게 이야기할 수가 없어요. 무엇에든 곧이곧대로 답하는 법이 없으니까요."], ["Robin was a little warmed with his sister’s rudeness, but he was even with her, and yet with good manners too.", "그냥 내버려 두고, 더는 그 이야기를 꺼내지 마세요. 거기에 뭔가 있다고 보신다면, 그 애를 오라버니의 길에서 치워 버리는 방법쯤은 아시잖아요.\""], ["“There are two sorts of people, madam,”", "로빈은 누이의 무례함에 조금 발끈했으나, 그래도 예의를 잃지 않으면서 그녀에게 맞받았다. \"어머니, 도무지 맞설 수 없는 부류가 둘 있어요,\""], ["says he, turning to his mother, “that there is no contending with; that is, a wise body and a fool; ’tis a little hard I should engage with both of them together.”", "하고 그가 어머니를 향해 돌아서며 말했다, \"곧 현명한 사람과 바보지요. 제가 그 둘을 한꺼번에 상대해야 하다니 좀 가혹하네요.\""]], [["The younger sister then put in.", "그러자 작은딸이 끼어들었다."], ["“We must be fools indeed,”", "\"우리가 정말 바보임이 틀림없겠네요,\""], ["says she, “in my brother’s opinion, that he should think we can believe he has seriously asked Mrs.", "하고 그녀가 말했다, \"오라버니가 베티 아씨에게 혼인하자고 진지하게 청했고 그 애가 거절했다는 걸 우리가 믿으리라고 오라버니가 생각한다면 말이에요.\""], ["Betty to marry him, and that she has refused him.”", "\"답하라, 그러나 답하지 말라, 솔로몬이 이르셨지,\""], ["“Answer, and answer not, say Solomon,”", "하고 그녀의 오라버니가 대꾸했다."], ["replied her brother. “When your brother had said to your mother that he had asked her no less than five times, and that it was so, that she positively denied him, methinks a younger sister need not question the truth of it when her mother did not.”", "\"네 오라버니가 어머니께 그 애에게 다섯 번이나 청했다고, 그리고 정말로 그 애가 단호히 자기를 거절했다고 말씀드렸을진대, 어머니께서도 그것을 의심하지 않으셨는데 동생이 그 진실을 의심할 까닭은 없을 것 같구나.\""], ["“My mother, you see, did not understand it,” says the second sister.", "\"어머니는, 보다시피, 그걸 이해하지 못하셨던 거예요,\""], ["“There’s some difference,”", "하고 둘째 딸이 말했다."], ["says Robin, “between desiring me to explain it, and telling me she did not believe it.”", "\"분명히 말해 달라고 청하시는 것과, 못 믿겠다고 말씀하시는 것 사이엔,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"얼마간 차이가 있지.\""]], [["“Well, but, son,”", "\"그래, 하지만, 얘야,\""], ["says the old lady, “if you are disposed to let us into the mystery of it, what were these hard conditions?”", "하고 노부인이 말했다, \"네가 우리에게 그 수수께끼를 알려 줄 마음이 있다면, 그 까다로운 조건이라는 게 대체 무엇이었느냐?\""], ["“Yes, madam,”", "\"예, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, “I had done it before now, if the teasers here had not worried me by way of interruption.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"여기 이 약 올리는 사람들이 끼어들어 저를 들볶지만 않았어도 진작 그렇게 했을 거예요."], ["The conditions are, that I bring my father and you to consent to it, and without that she protests she will never see me more upon that head; and to these conditions, as I said, I suppose I shall never be able to grant.", "그 조건인즉, 제가 아버지와 어머니를 그 일에 동의하시게끔 모셔 오라는 것이고, 그게 없이는 그 애가 결코 다시는 그 일로 저를 만나지 않겠노라고 단언해요. 그리고 이 조건은, 말씀드렸듯이, 아무래도 제가 결코 들어줄 수 없을 것 같네요."], ["I hope my warm sisters will be answered now, and blush a little; if not, I have no more to say till I hear further.”", "이제 제 성난 누이들이 답을 얻고 조금 부끄러워졌으면 좋겠네요. 만일 그렇지 않다면, 저는 더 들을 때까지 더는 할 말이 없어요.\""]], [["This answer was surprising to them all, though less to the mother, because of what I had said to her.", "이 대답은 그들 모두에게 놀라운 것이었으나, 어머니에게는 덜 그러했으니, 내가 그녀에게 했던 말 때문이었다. 딸들로 말하자면, 그들은 한참을 말없이 서 있었다."], ["As to the daughters, they stood mute a great while; but the mother said with some passion, “Well, I had heard this before, but I could not believe it; but if it is so, then we have all done Betty wrong, and she has behaved better than I ever expected.”", "그러나 어머니는 얼마간 격해져 말했다. \"음, 나는 전에도 이 말을 들었으나 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["“Nay,” says the eldest sister, “if it be so, she has acted handsomely indeed.”", "하지만 그게 사실이라면, 우리는 모두 베티에게 잘못을 저지른 게로구나, 그리고 그 애는 내가 기대했던 것보다 훨씬 더 훌륭하게 처신했어.\""], ["“I confess,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["says the mother, “it was none of her fault, if he was fool enough to take a fancy to her; but to give such an answer to him, shows more respect to your father and me than I can tell how to express; I shall value the girl the better for it as long as I know her.”", "하고 큰딸이 말했다, \"그게 사실이라면 그 애는 정말 훌륭하게 행동한 거예요.\""], ["“But I shall not,”", "\"내 고백하건대,\""], ["says Robin, “unless you will give your consent.”", "하고 어머니가 말했다, \"그 애가 그토록 어리석게 그 애에게 마음을 빼앗긴 게 그 애 잘못은 아니지. 하지만 그 애에게 그런 대답을 했다는 건, 네 아버지와 내게 내가 뭐라 표현할 수 없을 만큼 큰 도리를 보인 거다."], ["“I’ll consider of that a while,”", "나는 그 애를 아는 한 그 일로 그 애를 더 귀히 여길 거야.\""], ["says the mother; “I assure you, if there were not some other objections in the way, this conduct of hers would go a great way to bring me to consent.”", "\"하지만 저는 안 그럴 거예요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"어머니가 동의해 주시지 않는 한은요.\""], ["“I wish it would go quite through it,”", "\"그건 한동안 헤아려 보마,\" 하고 어머니가 말했다, \"분명히 말하건대, 다른 걸림돌만 없다면, 그 애의 이런 처신이 나를 동의하게 만드는 데 큰 몫을 할 게다.\""], ["says Robin; “if you had as much thought about making me easy as you have about making me rich, you would soon consent to it.”", "\"그게 그것을 완전히 뚫고 나가 주었으면 좋겠네요,\" 하고 로빈이 말했다, \"저를 부유하게 만드는 일에 쓰시는 만큼의 생각을 저를 편안하게 만드는 일에 쓰신다면, 어머니는 곧 거기에 동의하실 거예요.\""]], [["“Why, Robin,”", "\"그런데, 로빈,\""], ["says the mother again, “are you really in earnest?", "하고 어머니가 다시 말했다, \"너 정말로 진심이냐?"], ["Would you so fain have her as you pretend?”", "네가 짐짓 그러는 것만큼 그토록 간절히 그 애를 원하는 게냐?\""], ["“Really, madam,”", "\"정말로요, 어머니,\""], ["says Robin, “I think ’tis hard you should question me upon that head after all I have said.", "하고 로빈이 말했다, \"제가 그토록 말씀을 드린 끝에 어머니가 그 점을 두고 저를 캐물으시다니 너무하신 것 같아요."], ["I won’t say that I will have her; how can I resolve that point, when you see I cannot have her without your consent?", "그 애를 차지하겠다고는 말하지 않겠어요. 보시다시피 어머니의 동의 없이는 그 애를 차지할 수 없는데, 어떻게 그 점을 결심할 수 있겠어요?"], ["Besides, I am not bound to marry at all.", "게다가 저는 꼭 혼인해야 할 의무도 없고요."], ["But this I will say, I am in earnest in, that I will never have anybody else if I can help it; so you may determine for me.", "하지만 이것만은 진심으로 말하겠어요, 곧 어쩔 수만 있다면 저는 결코 다른 누구도 맞지 않겠다는 거예요. 그러니 어머니가 저를 위해 결정하시면 돼요."], ["Betty or nobody is the word, and the question which of the two shall be in your breast to decide, madam, provided only, that my good-humoured sisters here may have no vote in it.”", "베티 아니면 아무도 없다는 게 제 말이고, 둘 중 어느 쪽이냐 하는 문제는 어머니의 가슴속에서 결정하실 일이에요, 다만 여기 이 명랑한 제 누이들에게는 거기에 한 표도 없다는 조건으로요.\""]], [["All this was dreadful to me, for the mother began to yield, and Robin pressed her home on it.", "이 모든 것이 내게는 끔찍하기만 했으니, 어머니가 마음이 기울기 시작했고, 로빈이 그 점을 두고 어머니를 몰아붙였기 때문이다."], ["On the other hand, she advised with the eldest son, and he used all the arguments in the world to persuade her to consent; alleging his brother’s passionate love for me, and my generous regard to the family, in refusing my own advantages upon such a nice point of honour, and a thousand such things.", "한편 어머니는 맏아들과 의논했는데, 그는 어머니를 설득하기 위해 세상의 온갖 논거를 다 동원했으니, 동생이 나를 열렬히 사랑한다는 것이며, 내가 그토록 미묘한 명예의 문제를 두고 내 이익을 거절함으로써 그 집안에 보인 갸륵한 배려라는 것이며, 그 밖에도 수천 가지 그런 말을 늘어놓았다."], ["And as to the father, he was a man in a hurry of public affairs and getting money, seldom at home, thoughtful of the main chance, but left all those things to his wife.", "그리고 아버지로 말하자면, 그는 공무와 돈벌이에 분주하여 집에 좀처럼 붙어 있지 않았고, 큰 기회만을 골똘히 생각하는 사람이라, 그런 일은 모두 아내에게 맡겨 두었다."]], [["You may easily believe, that when the plot was thus, as they thought, broke out, and that every one thought they knew how things were carried, it was not so difficult or so dangerous for the elder brother, whom nobody suspected of anything, to have a freer access to me than before; nay, the mother, which was just as he wished, proposed it to him to talk with Mrs.", "여러분도 쉽게 믿을 수 있겠지만, 음모가 이렇게 그들 생각에 들통이 나고, 모두가 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지 안다고 여기게 되자, 아무도 의심하지 않던 맏아들로서는 전보다 더 자유롭게 내게 접근하는 것이 그리 어렵거나 위험한 일이 아니었다. 아니, 어머니가 그가 바라던 바로 그대로, 그에게 베티 양과 이야기해 보라고 제안하기까지 했다."], ["Betty. “For it may be, son,”", "\"얘야, 어쩌면 말이다,\""], ["said she, “you may see farther into the thing than I, and see if you think she has been so positive as Robin says she has been, or no.”", "하고 어머니가 말했다. \"네가 나보다 이 일을 더 깊이 들여다볼 수 있을지도 모르고, 저 애가 로빈 말마따나 정말 그렇게 단호했는지 아닌지 네가 한번 봐 다오.\""], ["This was as well as he could wish, and he, as it were, yielding to talk with me at his mother’s request, she brought me to him into her own chamber, told me her son had some business with me at her request, and desired me to be very sincere with him, and then she left us together, and he went and shut the door after her.", "이는 그가 바랄 수 있는 그대로였으니, 그는 마치 어머니의 청에 못 이겨 나와 이야기하는 척하며, 어머니가 나를 자기 방으로 데려가, 자기 아들이 자기 부탁으로 내게 볼일이 좀 있다고 하면서 그에게 아주 솔직하게 대하라 이르고는, 우리 둘만 남겨 두고 나갔고, 그는 어머니가 나가자 가서 문을 닫았다."]], [["He came back to me and took me in his arms, and kissed me very tenderly; but told me he had a long discourse to hold with me, and it was not come to that crisis, that I should make myself happy or miserable as long as I lived; that the thing was now gone so far, that if I could not comply with his desire, we would both be ruined.", "그는 내게로 돌아와 나를 두 팔로 안고 아주 다정하게 입을 맞추었으나, 내게 할 긴 이야기가 있다고, 그리고 일이 이제 그 갈림길에 이르러, 내가 살아 있는 한 스스로를 행복하게 만들 수도 비참하게 만들 수도 있는 지경이 되었다고 말했다. 일이 이제 너무 멀리 와 버려서, 만약 내가 그의 바람에 따르지 못한다면 우리 둘 다 파멸하리라는 것이었다."], ["Then he told the whole story between Robin, as he called him, and his mother and sisters and himself, as it is above.", "그러고는 그가 로빈이라 부르는 동생과 어머니, 누이들, 그리고 자기 자신 사이에 오간 이야기를 앞서 적은 그대로 죄다 털어놓았다. \"그러니 사랑하는 아가야,\""], ["“And now, dear child,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “consider what it will be to marry a gentleman of a good family, in good circumstances, and with the consent of the whole house, and to enjoy all that the world can give you; and what, on the other hand, to be sunk into the dark circumstances of a woman that has lost her reputation; and that though I shall be a private friend to you while I live, yet as I shall be suspected always, so you will be afraid to see me, and I shall be afraid to own you.”", "\"좋은 가문에, 좋은 형편에, 온 집안의 동의를 얻어 한 신사와 결혼하여 세상이 네게 줄 수 있는 모든 것을 누리는 것이 어떤 것일지, 그리고 다른 한편으로 명예를 잃은 여자의 어두운 처지로 가라앉는 것이 어떤 것일지를 생각해 보아라. 나는 살아 있는 동안 네게 남몰래 벗이 되어 주겠지만, 늘 의심을 받을 터이니, 너는 나를 만나기를 두려워할 것이고, 나도 너를 인정하기를 두려워하게 될 것이다.\""]], [["He gave me no time to reply, but went on with me thus: “What has happened between us, child, so long as we both agree to do so, may be buried and forgotten.", "그는 내게 대답할 틈을 주지 않고 이렇게 말을 이어 갔다. \"아가야, 우리 사이에 일어난 일은, 우리 둘 다 그렇게 하기로 합의하는 한, 묻어 두고 잊어버릴 수 있다."], ["I shall always be your sincere friend, without any inclination to nearer intimacy, when you become my sister; and we shall have all the honest part of conversation without any reproaches between us of having done amiss.", "네가 내 제수가 되면 나는 더 가까운 친밀함을 바라는 마음 없이 언제까지나 너의 진실한 벗이 되어 줄 것이며, 우리는 잘못을 저질렀다는 서로 간의 비난 없이 정직한 교제의 온갖 즐거움을 누리게 될 것이다. 부디 이를 생각해 보고, 네 자신의 안전과 번영을 가로막지 말기를 바란다."], ["I beg of you to consider it, and to not stand in the way of your own safety and prosperity; and to satisfy you that I am sincere,”", "그리고 내가 진심임을 네게 확인시켜 주고자,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다."], ["added he, “I here offer you £500 in money, to make you some amends for the freedoms I have taken with you, which we shall look upon as some of the follies of our lives, which ’tis hoped we may repent of.”", "\"내가 너와 누린 자유에 대해 얼마간 보상하는 뜻으로 여기 현금 500파운드를 네게 주겠다. 그 자유야 우리 삶의 어리석은 짓 가운데 하나로 여겨야 할 것이고, 우리가 그 일을 뉘우치게 되기를 바라야 할 일이다.\""]], [["He spoke this in so much more moving terms than it is possible for me to express, and with so much greater force of argument than I can repeat, that I only recommend it to those who read the story, to suppose, that as he held me above an hour and a half in that discourse, so he answered all my objections, and fortified his discourse with all the arguments that human wit and art could devise.", "그는 이 말을 내가 도저히 표현할 수 없을 만큼 한결 더 마음을 움직이는 어조로, 또 내가 되풀이할 수 없을 만큼 한층 더 강력한 논거를 곁들여 했으므로, 나는 다만 이 이야기를 읽는 이들에게 이렇게 짐작해 주기를 권할 뿐이다. 곧 그가 한 시간 반이 넘도록 나를 붙들고 이런 이야기를 하는 동안, 그가 내 온갖 반론에 일일이 답하고, 인간의 재치와 기교가 고안해 낼 수 있는 온갖 논거로 자기 이야기를 단단히 다졌다고 말이다."]], [["I cannot say, however, that anything he said made impression enough upon me so as to give me any thought of the matter, till he told me at last very plainly, that if I refused, he was sorry to add that he could never go on with me in that station as we stood before; that though he loved me as well as ever, and that I was as agreeable to him as ever, yet sense of virtue had not so far forsaken him as to suffer him to lie with a woman that his brother courted to make his wife; and if he took his leave of me, with a denial in this affair, whatever he might do for me in the point of support, grounded on his first engagement of maintaining me, yet he would not have me be surprised that he was obliged to tell me he could not allow himself to see me any more; and that, indeed, I could not expect it of him.", "그러나 그가 한 말 가운데 어느 것도 내게 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각하게 할 만큼 깊은 인상을 주지는 못했다고 하겠으니, 다만 마지막에 그가 아주 솔직하게, 만약 내가 거절한다면 안타깝지만 우리가 전처럼 그런 처지로는 더는 함께 지낼 수 없노라고 덧붙이기 전까지는 그러했다. 그는 여전히 변함없이 나를 사랑하고, 내가 여전히 변함없이 그의 마음에 든다 해도, 덕에 대한 분별이 아직 그에게서 그토록 멀리 떠나가지는 않았으므로, 자기 동생이 아내로 삼으려 구애하는 여자와 동침하는 것을 차마 견딜 수 없다는 것이었다. 또 이 일에서 거절을 받고 내게 작별을 고하게 된다면, 나를 부양하겠다던 처음의 약속에 근거하여 생계의 측면에서 나를 위해 무엇을 해 주든 간에, 자기로서는 더 이상 나를 만날 수 없노라고 어쩔 수 없이 말해야만 한다는 점에 내가 놀라지 않기를 바란다고, 그리고 사실 나로서도 그에게 그 이상을 기대할 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다."]], [["I received this last part with some token of surprise and disorder, and had much ado to avoid sinking down, for indeed I loved him to an extravagance not easy to imagine; but he perceived my disorder.", "나는 이 마지막 대목을 어느 정도 놀라움과 동요의 기색을 띤 채 받아들였고, 주저앉지 않으려 무던히 애를 써야 했으니, 사실 나는 상상하기 쉽지 않을 만큼 터무니없이 그를 사랑하고 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 내 동요를 알아챘다."], ["He entreated me to consider seriously of it; assured me that it was the only way to preserve our mutual affection; that in this station we might love as friends, with the utmost passion, and with a love of relation untainted, free from our just reproaches, and free from other people’s suspicions; that he should ever acknowledge his happiness owing to me; that he would be debtor to me as long as he lived, and would be paying that debt as long as he had breath.", "그는 내게 이를 진지하게 생각해 보라고 간청하며, 이것이야말로 우리의 서로에 대한 애정을 지킬 수 있는 유일한 길이라고, 이런 처지에서 우리는 더없이 뜨거운 열정으로, 그러면서도 정당한 비난에서 자유롭고 다른 사람들의 의심에서도 자유로운, 더럽혀지지 않은 친족의 사랑으로 벗처럼 사랑할 수 있을 것이라고 장담했다. 또 자기는 자신의 행복이 내 덕분임을 언제까지나 인정할 것이며, 살아 있는 한 내게 빚진 자가 되어 숨이 붙어 있는 한 그 빚을 갚아 나가겠노라고 했다."], ["Thus he wrought me up, in short, to a kind of hesitation in the matter; having the dangers on one side represented in lively figures, and indeed, heightened by my imagination of being turned out to the wide world a mere cast-off whore, for it was no less, and perhaps exposed as such, with little to provide for myself, with no friend, no acquaintance in the whole world, out of that town, and there I could not pretend to stay.", "한마디로 이렇게 그는 나를 그 문제에 대해 일종의 망설임에 이르도록 몰아갔으니, 한편으로는 위험을 생생한 모습으로 그려 보였고, 실은 내 상상 속에서, 넓은 세상으로 내쳐져 한낱 버림받은 창녀가—다름 아닌 바로 그것이었으니—될 것이며, 어쩌면 그런 처지로 폭로되어, 나 자신을 부양할 변변한 것도 없이, 그 고을 밖에는 온 세상에 벗 하나, 아는 이 하나 없는데, 그 고을에 머무는 척할 수도 없는 신세가 되리라는 생각으로 한층 부풀려졌다."], ["All this terrified me to the last degree, and he took care upon all occasions to lay it home to me in the worst colours that it could be possible to be drawn in.", "이 모든 것이 나를 극도로 겁에 질리게 했고, 그는 기회 있을 때마다 그것을 그릴 수 있는 가장 음울한 빛깔로 내게 절절히 들이대기를 잊지 않았다."], ["On the other hand, he failed not to set forth the easy, prosperous life which I was going to live.", "다른 한편으로 그는 내가 살아가게 될 편안하고 번영하는 삶을 펼쳐 보이는 데에도 소홀하지 않았다."]], [["He answered all that I could object from affection, and from former engagements, with telling me the necessity that was before us of taking other measures now; and as to his promises of marriage, the nature of things, he said, had put an end to that, by the probability of my being his brother’s wife, before the time to which his promises all referred.", "내가 애정에서, 또 지난날의 약속에서 끌어와 내세울 수 있는 모든 것에 대해, 그는 이제 우리 앞에 다른 방도를 취할 수밖에 없는 절박함이 놓여 있다고 말하며 답했다. 그리고 결혼하겠다던 자기 약속으로 말하자면, 일의 본성상 그것은 끝이 났다고, 그의 약속이 모두 가리키던 그 시점이 오기도 전에 내가 그의 동생의 아내가 될 개연성이 생겼기 때문이라고 했다."], ["Thus, in a word, I may say, he reasoned me out of my reason; he conquered all my arguments, and I began to see a danger that I was in, which I had not considered of before, and that was, of being dropped by both of them and left alone in the world to shift for myself.", "이렇게 하여, 한마디로 그는 나를 내 이성에서 끌어내 설득해 버렸다고 하겠으니, 그는 내 모든 논거를 정복했고, 나는 전에는 생각지 못했던 위험을 보기 시작했으니, 그것은 곧 두 사람 모두에게 버림받아 세상에 홀로 남겨져 스스로 헤쳐 나가야 하는 위험이었다."]], [["This, and his persuasion, at length prevailed with me to consent, though with so much reluctance, that it was easy to see I should go to church like a bear to the stake.", "이것이, 그리고 그의 설득이 마침내 나로 하여금 승낙하게 만들었으니, 다만 어찌나 마지못해 한 일인지 내가 마치 곰이 말뚝으로 끌려가듯 교회로 가게 되리라는 것은 누구라도 쉽게 알 수 있었다."], ["I had some little apprehensions about me, too, lest my new spouse, who, by the way, I had not the least affection for, should be skillful enough to challenge me on another account, upon our first coming to bed together.", "내게는 또 작은 걱정이 하나 있었으니, 그건 그렇고 내가 조금도 애정을 품지 않았던 내 새 신랑이 우리가 처음으로 함께 잠자리에 들 때 또 다른 명목으로 나를 따져 물을 만큼 능란하지나 않을까 하는 것이었다."], ["But whether he did it with design or not, I know not, but his elder brother took care to make him very much fuddled before he went to bed, so that I had the satisfaction of a drunken bedfellow the first night.", "그러나 그가 의도적으로 그랬는지 아닌지는 모르겠으나, 그의 형이 그를 잠자리에 들기 전에 몹시 취하게 만들도록 신경을 써 둔 덕분에, 나는 첫날밤에 술 취한 잠자리 동무라는 만족을 누렸다."], ["How he did it I know not, but I concluded that he certainly contrived it, that his brother might be able to make no judgment of the difference between a maid and a married woman; nor did he ever entertain any notions of it, or disturb his thoughts about it.", "그가 어떻게 그리했는지는 모르겠으나, 나는 그가 분명 그렇게 꾸민 것이라고, 그래서 동생이 처녀와 결혼한 여자의 차이를 도무지 판단할 수 없게 한 것이라고 결론지었으며, 실제로 그는 그것에 대해 어떤 관념도 품지 않았고, 그 일로 마음을 어지럽히지도 않았다."]], [["I should go back a little here to where I left off.", "여기서 내가 멈췄던 데로 조금 거슬러 올라가야겠다."], ["The elder brother having thus managed me, his next business was to manage his mother, and he never left till he had brought her to acquiesce and be passive in the thing, even without acquainting the father, other than by post letters; so that she consented to our marrying privately, and leaving her to manage the father afterwards.", "맏아들은 이렇게 나를 다루고 나서, 다음으로 할 일은 자기 어머니를 다루는 것이었으니, 그는 어머니가 그 일에 묵묵히 수긍하고 잠자코 따르게 만들기까지, 그것도 아버지에게는 우편으로 편지를 보내는 것 말고는 알리지도 않은 채 그리되게 하기까지 손을 놓지 않았다. 그리하여 어머니는 우리가 남몰래 결혼하고 아버지는 나중에 자기가 알아서 구슬리도록 맡기는 데에 동의했다."]], [["Then he cajoled with his brother, and persuaded him what service he had done him, and how he had brought his mother to consent, which, though true, was not indeed done to serve him, but to serve himself; but thus diligently did he cheat him, and had the thanks of a faithful friend for shifting off his whore into his brother’s arms for a wife.", "그러고 나서 그는 동생을 구슬려, 자기가 그에게 얼마나 큰 도움을 주었는지, 어떻게 어머니가 동의하도록 만들었는지를 설득했으니, 그것은 사실이긴 했으나 실은 동생을 위해 한 일이 아니라 자기 자신을 위해 한 일이었다. 이렇게 그는 부지런히 동생을 속였고, 자기 창녀를 동생의 품에 아내로 떠넘겨 놓고는 충실한 벗이라는 감사 인사까지 받았다."], ["So certainly does interest banish all manner of affection, and so naturally do men give up honour and justice, humanity, and even Christianity, to secure themselves.", "이처럼 이해관계란 어김없이 온갖 종류의 애정을 몰아내며, 사람이란 자기 안위를 지키기 위해 명예와 정의, 인간애, 심지어 기독교 신앙마저 이토록 자연스레 내던지는 법이다."]], [["I must now come back to brother Robin, as we always called him, who having got his mother’s consent, as above, came big with the news to me, and told me the whole story of it, with a sincerity so visible, that I must confess it grieved me that I must be the instrument to abuse so honest a gentleman.", "이제 나는 우리가 늘 부르던 대로 로빈 도련님에게로 돌아가야겠는데, 그는 앞서 말한 대로 어머니의 동의를 얻고 나서 그 소식으로 잔뜩 부푼 채 내게 와, 그 사연을 죄다 들려주었으니, 그 진심이 너무도 또렷이 드러나 보여서, 내가 이토록 정직한 신사를 농락하는 도구가 되어야 한다는 것이 마음 아팠음을 고백하지 않을 수 없다."], ["But there was no remedy; he would have me, and I was not obliged to tell him that I was his brother’s whore, though I had no other way to put him off; so I came gradually into it, to his satisfaction, and behold we were married.", "그러나 달리 어쩔 도리가 없었다. 그는 나를 아내로 삼으려 했고, 비록 그를 떨쳐 낼 다른 방도가 없었다 해도, 내가 그의 형의 창녀라고 그에게 말해야 할 의무는 없었으니, 그리하여 나는 그를 흡족하게 하며 차차 그 일에 응했고, 그렇게 우리는 결혼하게 되었다."]], [["Modesty forbids me to reveal the secrets of the marriage-bed, but nothing could have happened more suitable to my circumstances than that, as above, my husband was so fuddled when he came to bed, that he could not remember in the morning whether he had had any conversation with me or no, and I was obliged to tell him he had, though in reality he had not, that I might be sure he could make to inquiry about anything else.", "정숙함이 결혼 침상의 비밀을 드러내는 것을 금하나, 앞서 말한 대로, 내 남편이 잠자리에 들 때 어찌나 취해 있었던지 아침에 나와 무슨 일이 있었는지 없었는지조차 기억하지 못해, 내가 실은 없었던 일을 두고 있었다고 그에게 말할 수밖에 없었던 것보다 내 처지에 더 알맞게 일어날 수 있었던 일은 없었을 것이니, 그래야만 그가 다른 무엇에 대해서도 캐묻지 않으리라고 확신할 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["It concerns the story in hand very little to enter into the further particulars of the family, or of myself, for the five years that I lived with this husband, only to observe that I had two children by him, and that at the end of five years he died.", "이 남편과 함께 산 다섯 해 동안의 그 집안이나 나 자신에 관한 더 자세한 내막에 들어가는 것은 지금 다루는 이야기와는 거의 상관이 없으니, 다만 내가 그에게서 아이 둘을 낳았다는 것, 그리고 다섯 해가 끝날 무렵 그가 죽었다는 것만 짚어 두면 되겠다."], ["He had been really a very good husband to me, and we lived very agreeably together; but as he had not received much from them, and had in the little time he lived acquired no great matters, so my circumstances were not great, nor was I much mended by the match.", "그는 정말로 내게 매우 좋은 남편이었고, 우리는 아주 사이좋게 함께 살았으나, 그가 집안에서 많은 것을 받지도 못했고, 그 살아 있던 짧은 동안에 이렇다 할 것을 별로 모으지도 못했던 터라, 내 형편도 대단치 않았고, 이 혼인으로 내 처지가 크게 나아지지도 않았다."], ["Indeed, I had preserved the elder brother’s bonds to me, to pay £500, which he offered me for my consent to marry his brother; and this, with what I had saved of the money he formerly gave me, about as much more by my husband, left me a widow with about £1200 in my pocket.", "다만 나는 그 동생과 결혼하는 데 동의해 준 대가로 맏아들이 내게 주겠다 한 500파운드의 차용증서를 간직해 두었던지라, 이것에다 그가 전에 내게 준 돈에서 모아 둔 것, 그리고 남편에게서 그만큼 더해진 것을 합쳐, 나는 주머니에 약 1200파운드를 지닌 과부로 남게 되었다."]], [["My two children were, indeed, taken happily off my hands by my husband’s father and mother, and that, by the way, was all they got by Mrs. Betty.", "내 두 아이는 정말 다행스럽게도 남편의 부모가 떠맡아 주었으니, 그건 그렇고 그것이 그들이 베티 양에게서 얻은 전부였다."]], [["I confess I was not suitably affected with the loss of my husband, nor indeed can I say that I ever loved him as I ought to have done, or as was proportionable to the good usage I had from him, for he was a tender, kind, good-humoured man as any woman could desire; but his brother being so always in my sight, at least while we were in the country, was a continual snare to me, and I never was in bed with my husband but I wished myself in the arms of his brother; and though his brother never offered me the least kindness that way after our marriage, but carried it just as a brother ought to do, yet it was impossible for me to do so to him; in short, I committed adultery and incest with him every day in my desires, which, without doubt, was as effectually criminal in the nature of the guilt as if I had actually done it.", "고백하건대 나는 남편을 잃은 것에 대해 마땅한 만큼 슬퍼하지 못했으며, 실은 내가 마땅히 사랑했어야 할 만큼, 혹은 그에게서 받은 좋은 대접에 걸맞을 만큼 그를 사랑한 적이 있었다고도 말할 수 없으니, 그는 어떤 여자라도 바랄 만한 다정하고 친절하며 쾌활한 남자였기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 형이 적어도 우리가 시골에 있는 동안에는 늘 내 눈앞에 있었던 것이 내게는 끊임없는 올가미였으니, 나는 남편과 잠자리에 들 때마다 그의 형의 품에 안기기를 바라곤 했다. 그리고 그의 형은 우리가 결혼한 뒤로는 그런 식의 친절을 조금도 내게 베푼 적이 없고, 꼭 시아주버니가 마땅히 처신해야 할 그대로 행동했으나, 나로서는 그에게 그렇게 처신하기란 도무지 불가능했다. 한마디로 나는 날마다 욕망 속에서 그와 간통과 근친상간을 저질렀으니, 의심할 바 없이 그것은 죄의 본질에 있어서 내가 실제로 그 짓을 한 것이나 진배없이 범죄였다."]], [["Before my husband died his elder brother was married, and we, being then removed to London, were written to by the old lady to come and be at the wedding.", "남편이 죽기 전에 그의 맏형이 결혼했고, 그때 우리는 런던으로 옮겨 간 터라, 노부인이 와서 결혼식에 참석하라는 편지를 보내 왔다."], ["My husband went, but I pretended indisposition, and that I could not possibly travel, so I stayed behind; for, in short, I could not bear the sight of his being given to another woman, though I knew I was never to have him myself.", "남편은 갔으나, 나는 몸이 편치 않아 도저히 여행을 할 수 없다는 핑계를 대고 남았으니, 한마디로 나는 그가 다른 여자에게 넘겨지는 광경을 차마 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다, 비록 내가 그를 결코 차지할 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었지만 말이다."]], [["I was now, as above, left loose to the world, and being still young and handsome, as everybody said of me, and I assure you I thought myself so, and with a tolerable fortune in my pocket, I put no small value upon myself.", "이제 나는 앞서 말한 대로 세상에 풀려나 자유로운 몸이 되었고, 모두가 나를 두고 그렇게 말했듯이 여전히 젊고 어여뻤으며, 단언컨대 나 스스로도 그렇게 여겼고, 주머니에 그런대로 괜찮은 재산을 지니고 있었던 터라, 나 자신을 적잖이 높이 쳤다."], ["I was courted by several very considerable tradesmen, and particularly very warmly by one, a linen-draper, at whose house, after my husband’s death, I took a lodging, his sister being my acquaintance.", "나는 꽤 상당한 상인 여럿에게서 구애를 받았는데, 특히 어느 포목상에게서 매우 열렬한 구애를 받았으니, 남편이 죽은 뒤 나는 그의 누이가 내 아는 사람인지라 그의 집에 하숙을 들었던 것이다."], ["Here I had all the liberty and all the opportunity to be gay and appear in company that I could desire, my landlord’s sister being one of the maddest, gayest things alive, and not so much mistress of her virtue as I thought at first she had been.", "여기서 나는 명랑하게 지내며 사람들 사이에 어울려 나설 온갖 자유와 온갖 기회를 마음껏 누렸으니, 내 집주인의 누이가 세상에서 가장 분방하고 가장 명랑한 사람 가운데 하나였고, 처음에 내가 생각했던 만큼 자기 정조의 주인은 아니었기 때문이다."], ["She brought me into a world of wild company, and even brought home several persons, such as she liked well enough to gratify, to see her pretty widow, so she was pleased to call me, and that name I got in a little time in public.", "그녀는 나를 거친 무리의 세계로 끌어들였고, 심지어 자기가 비위를 맞추어 줄 만큼 마음에 드는 여러 사람을 집으로 데려와 자기의 예쁜 과부를—그녀는 나를 그렇게 부르기를 즐겼다—구경시켰으니, 그 이름이 머지않아 사람들 사이에 내게 따라붙었다."], ["Now, as fame and fools make an assembly, I was here wonderfully caressed, had abundance of admirers, and such as called themselves lovers; but I found not one fair proposal among them all.", "자, 명성과 바보들이 모여 한 자리를 이루는 법이라, 나는 여기서 굉장히 떠받들렸고, 흠모하는 자들이, 또 스스로 연인이라 일컫는 자들이 넘쳐 났다. 그러나 그들 모두 가운데 점잖은 청혼은 하나도 찾아볼 수 없었다."], ["As for their common design, that I understood too well to be drawn into any more snares of that kind.", "그들의 흔해 빠진 속셈으로 말하자면, 나는 그것을 너무도 잘 알고 있던 터라 다시는 그런 부류의 올가미에 끌려들지 않았다."], ["The case was altered with me: I had money in my pocket, and had nothing to say to them.", "내 처지는 달라져 있었으니, 나는 주머니에 돈이 있었고, 그들에게 할 말이 아무것도 없었다."], ["I had been tricked once by that cheat called love, but the game was over; I was resolved now to be married or nothing, and to be well married or not at all.", "나는 사랑이라 불리는 그 속임수에 한 번 속았으나, 그 노름은 이제 끝났다. 나는 이제 결혼이 아니면 아무것도 아니요, 잘 결혼하든가 아니면 아예 안 하든가 하기로 작정했다."]], [["I loved the company, indeed, of men of mirth and wit, men of gallantry and figure, and was often entertained with such, as I was also with others; but I found by just observation, that the brightest men came upon the dullest errand—that is to say, the dullest as to what I aimed at.", "나는 실로 흥과 재치가 넘치는 남자들, 풍채 좋고 멋진 남자들과 어울리기를 좋아했고, 다른 이들과도 그러했듯 그런 이들과도 자주 즐겼으나, 정확한 관찰을 통해 알게 된 바는, 가장 빛나는 남자들이 가장 시시한 용건을 들고 온다는 것이었으니—즉 내가 노리는 바에 관해서는 가장 시시했다는 말이다."], ["On the other hand, those who came with the best proposals were the dullest and most disagreeable part of the world.", "다른 한편으로 가장 그럴듯한 청혼을 들고 온 자들은 세상에서 가장 따분하고 가장 마음에 안 드는 부류였다."], ["I was not averse to a tradesman, but then I would have a tradesman, forsooth, that was something of a gentleman too; that when my husband had a mind to carry me to the court, or to the play, he might become a sword, and look as like a gentleman as another man; and not be one that had the mark of his apron-strings upon his coat, or the mark of his hat upon his periwig; that should look as if he was set on to his sword, when his sword was put on to him, and that carried his trade in his countenance.", "나는 상인을 마다하지는 않았으나, 그러면서도 제발이지 무언가 신사다운 데가 있는 상인을 원했다. 즉 남편이 나를 궁정이나 연극 구경에 데려가고 싶을 때 칼을 차도 어색하지 않고 다른 어떤 남자 못지않게 신사처럼 보이는 사람, 외투에 앞치마 끈 자국이 남았다거나 가발에 모자 자국이 났다거나 하지 않은 사람, 칼이 그에게 채워진 것이 아니라 그가 칼에 매여 끌려가는 듯이 보이지 않는 사람, 얼굴에 자기 장사를 써 붙이고 다니지 않는 사람을 원했던 것이다."]], [["Well, at last I found this amphibious creature, this land-water thing called a gentleman-tradesman; and as a just plague upon my folly, I was catched in the very snare which, as I might say, I laid for myself.", "자, 마침내 나는 이 양서류 같은 존재, 신사 상인이라 불리는 이 뭍에도 물에도 사는 것을 찾아냈다. 그리고 내 어리석음에 대한 마땅한 벌로, 나는, 이를테면 내가 나 자신을 위해 놓아 둔 바로 그 올가미에 걸려들고 말았다."], ["I said for myself, for I was not trepanned, I confess, but I betrayed myself.", "나 자신을 위해서라고 말한 까닭은, 고백하건대 나는 함정에 빠진 것이 아니라 나 자신을 배반한 것이기 때문이다."]], [["This was a draper, too, for though my comrade would have brought me to a bargain with her brother, yet when it came to the point, it was, it seems, for a mistress, not a wife; and I kept true to this notion, that a woman should never be kept for a mistress that had money to keep herself.", "이 사람도 포목상이었으니, 내 동료가 자기 오라비와 흥정을 붙여 주려 했으나, 막상 본론에 이르자, 보아하니 그것은 아내가 아니라 정부情婦로 삼겠다는 것이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 자기를 건사할 돈이 있는 여자라면 결코 정부로 살림을 차려져서는 안 된다는 이 신념을 굳게 지켰다."]], [["Thus my pride, not my principle, my money, not my virtue, kept me honest; though, as it proved, I found I had much better have been sold by my she-comrade to her brother, than have sold myself as I did to a tradesman that was rake, gentleman, shopkeeper, and beggar, all together.", "이렇게 나의 원칙이 아니라 자존심이, 나의 덕이 아니라 돈이 나를 정숙하게 지켜 주었다. 다만 결국 드러난 바로는, 내 여자 동료에게 그녀의 오라비에게 팔려 가는 편이, 난봉꾼이요 신사요 가게 주인이요 거지인 것을 한데 합쳐 놓은 그런 상인에게 내 손으로 나 자신을 팔아넘기는 것보다 차라리 훨씬 나았으리라는 것을 나는 알게 되었다."]], [["But I was hurried on (by my fancy to a gentleman) to ruin myself in the grossest manner that every woman did; for my new husband coming to a lump of money at once, fell into such a profusion of expense, that all I had, and all he had before, if he had anything worth mentioning, would not have held it out above one year.", "그러나 나는 (신사에 대한 환상에 사로잡혀) 일찍이 어떤 여자도 겪지 못한 가장 천박한 방식으로 나 자신을 망치는 길로 떠밀려 갔으니, 내 새 남편은 단번에 목돈을 손에 넣자 어찌나 흥청망청 돈을 써 댔던지, 내가 가진 모든 것과 그가 전에 가졌던 모든 것이—그에게 입에 올릴 만한 것이 있었다 한들—일 년도 버텨 내지 못할 지경이었다."]], [["He was very fond of me for about a quarter of a year, and what I got by that was, that I had the pleasure of seeing a great deal of my money spent upon myself, and, as I may say, had some of the spending it too.", "그는 한 석 달 동안은 나를 무척 좋아했고, 내가 그 덕에 얻은 것이라곤, 내 돈이 나 자신에게 많이 쓰이는 것을 보는 즐거움과, 또 말하자면 그 돈을 쓰는 데 나도 얼마간 한몫 거든 것이었다."], ["“Come, my dear,”", "\"자, 여보,\""], ["says he to me one day, “shall we go and take a turn into the country for about a week?”", "하루는 그가 내게 말했다. \"한 일주일쯤 시골로 한 바퀴 다녀올까?\""], ["“Ay, my dear,”", "\"그래요, 여보,\""], ["says I, “whither would you go?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"어디로 가시려고요?\""], ["“I care not whither,”", "\"어디든 상관없소,\""], ["says he, “but I have a mind to look like quality for a week.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"하지만 한 일주일쯤 귀족 행세를 해 보고 싶구려."], ["We’ll go to Oxford,”", "옥스퍼드로 갑시다,\""], ["says he. “How,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["says I, “shall we go?", "\"어떻게요,\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["I am no horsewoman, and ’tis too far for a coach.”", "\"어떻게 가지요? 나는 말을 탈 줄도 모르고, 마차로 가기엔 너무 멀어요.\""], ["“Too far!”", "\"너무 멀다니!\""], ["says he; “no place is too far for a coach-and-six.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"여섯 마리 끄는 마차에 너무 먼 곳이란 없소."], ["If I carry you out, you shall travel like a duchess.”", "내가 당신을 모시고 나가면, 당신은 공작부인처럼 여행하게 될 거요.\""], ["“Hum,”", "\"흠,\""], ["says I, “my dear, ’tis a frolic; but if you have a mind to it, I don’t care.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"여보, 그건 한바탕 장난이지만, 당신이 그러고 싶다면 나는 상관없어요.\""], ["Well, the time was appointed, we had a rich coach, very good horses, a coachman, postillion, and two footmen in very good liveries; a gentleman on horseback, and a page with a feather in his hat upon another horse.", "자, 날이 정해지고, 우리에게는 호화로운 마차와 아주 좋은 말들, 마부 하나, 말몰이꾼 하나, 그리고 아주 좋은 제복을 입은 하인 둘이 있었으며, 말을 탄 신사 하나와 또 다른 말에 모자에 깃털을 꽂은 시동 하나가 딸려 있었다."], ["The servants all called him my lord, and the inn-keepers, you may be sure, did the like, and I was her honour the Countess, and thus we traveled to Oxford, and a very pleasant journey we had; for, give him his due, not a beggar alive knew better how to be a lord than my husband.", "하인들은 모두 그를 나리라 불렀고, 여관 주인들도, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 똑같이 그리했으며, 나는 마님이신 백작부인이었으니, 이렇게 우리는 옥스퍼드로 여행했고, 참으로 즐거운 여정이었다. 사실대로 말하자면, 살아 있는 어떤 거지도 내 남편보다 나리 노릇을 더 잘 알지는 못했기 때문이다."], ["We saw all the rarities at Oxford, talked with two or three Fellows of colleges about putting out a young nephew, that was left to his lordship’s care, to the University, and of their being his tutors.", "우리는 옥스퍼드의 진귀한 것을 죄다 구경했고, 나리의 보살핌에 맡겨진 어린 조카를 대학에 넣는 일과 그들이 그 아이의 가정교사가 되는 일에 대해 두세 명의 대학 특별연구원들과 이야기를 나누었다."], ["We diverted ourselves with bantering several other poor scholars, with hopes of being at least his lordship’s chaplains and putting on a scarf; and thus having lived like quality indeed, as to expense, we went away for Northampton, and, in a word, in about twelve days’ ramble came home again, to the tune of about £93 expense.", "우리는 또 적어도 나리의 전속 목사가 되어 띠를 두르게 되기를 바라는 여러 가난한 학자들을 놀려 먹으며 재미를 보았다. 이렇게 씀씀이에 관한 한 정말로 귀족처럼 살고 나서, 우리는 노샘프턴으로 떠났고, 한마디로 열이틀쯤 떠돌다가 약 93파운드의 비용을 치르고서 다시 집으로 돌아왔다."]], [["Vanity is the perfection of a fop.", "허영은 멋쟁이의 완성이다."], ["My husband had this excellence, that he valued nothing of expense; and as his history, you may be sure, has very little weight in it, ’tis enough to tell you that in about two years and a quarter he broke, and was not so happy to get over into the Mint, but got into a sponging-house, being arrested in an action too heavy from him to give bail to, so he sent for me to come to him.", "내 남편은 이 탁월함을 지니고 있었으니, 비용 따위는 아무것도 대수롭게 여기지 않았다. 그리고 그의 이력이란, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 거기에 담긴 무게가 매우 가벼우니, 약 두 해 하고도 석 달 만에 그가 파산했다는 것, 그리고 운 좋게 민트로 피해 들어가지도 못하고, 보석으로 풀려나기에는 너무 무거운 소송으로 체포되어 임시 구류소에 갇히는 신세가 되었고, 그래서 내게 사람을 보내 자기에게 오라고 했다는 것만 말해 두면 충분하다."]], [["It was no surprise to me, for I had foreseen some time that all was going to wreck, and had been taking care to reserve something if I could, though it was not much, for myself. But when he sent for me, he behaved much better than I expected, and told me plainly he had played the fool, and suffered himself to be surprised, which he might have prevented; that now he foresaw he could not stand it, and therefore he would have me go home, and in the night take away everything I had in the house of any value, and secure it; and after that, he told me that if I could get away one hundred or two hundred pounds in goods out of the shop, I should do it; “only,” says he, “let me know nothing of it, neither what you take nor whither you carry it; for as for me,” says he, “I am resolved to get out of this house and be gone; and if you never hear of me more, my dear,” says he, “I wish you well; I am only sorry for the injury I have done you.” He said some very handsome things to me indeed at parting; for I told you he was a gentleman, and that was all the benefit I had of his being so; that he used me very handsomely and with good manners upon all occasions, even to the last, only spent all I had, and left me to rob the creditors for something to subsist on.", "내게는 놀랄 일이 아니었으니, 모든 것이 파탄으로 치닫고 있음을 한동안 전부터 예견하고 있었고, 많지는 않았으나 나 자신을 위해 할 수만 있다면 무언가 남겨 두려고 신경을 써 두었기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 나를 부르자, 그는 내가 예상했던 것보다 훨씬 점잖게 처신하며, 자기가 바보짓을 했다고, 막을 수도 있었을 텐데 방심하다 봉변을 당했다고 솔직하게 말했다. 이제 자기로서는 도저히 버텨 낼 수 없음을 내다보았으니, 그러므로 나더러 집으로 가서 밤중에 집 안에 있는 값나가는 것은 무엇이든 다 빼내어 안전한 곳에 챙겨 두라고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는, 만약 내가 가게에서 물품으로 백 파운드든 이백 파운드든 빼낼 수 있다면 그리하라고 하면서, \"다만,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"당신이 무엇을 가져가는지, 어디로 가져가는지, 내게는 아무것도 알리지 마시오. 나로 말하자면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이 집에서 빠져나가 떠나기로 작정했으니 말이오. 그리고 당신이 다시는 내 소식을 듣지 못하더라도, 여보,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 당신이 잘되기를 비오. 다만 내가 당신에게 끼친 해가 안타까울 뿐이오.\" 그는 헤어질 때 정말로 내게 아주 멋진 말을 몇 마디 해 주었으니, 앞서 말했듯이 그는 신사였고, 그가 신사라는 데서 내가 얻은 이로움이란 그것이 전부였다. 즉 그는 마지막까지도 모든 경우에 나를 아주 멋지고 예의 바르게 대해 주었다는 것, 다만 내가 가진 모든 것을 다 써 버리고, 나로 하여금 입에 풀칠할 무언가를 위해 채권자들의 것을 훔치도록 남겨 두었다는 것뿐이다."]], [["However, I did as he bade me, that you may be sure; and having thus taken my leave of him, I never saw him more, for he found means to break out of the bailiff’s house that night or the next, and go over into France, and for the rest of the creditors scrambled for it as well as they could.", "그래도 나는, 여러분도 짐작하다시피, 그가 시킨 대로 했다. 이렇게 그에게 작별을 고하고 나서, 나는 그를 다시는 보지 못했으니, 그가 그날 밤인가 그다음 날 밤에 집달리의 집에서 빠져나가 프랑스로 건너갈 방도를 찾아냈고, 나머지 채권자들로 말하자면 저마다 할 수 있는 대로 다투어 챙겼기 때문이다."], ["How, I knew not, for I could come at no knowledge of anything, more than this, that he came home about three o’clock in the morning, caused the rest of his goods to be removed into the Mint, and the shop to be shut up; and having raised what money he could get together, he got over, as I said, to France, from whence I had one or two letters from him, and no more.", "어떻게 된 일인지 나는 알 수 없었으니, 이것 말고는 아무것도 알아낼 수 없었기 때문이다. 곧 그가 새벽 세 시쯤 집에 돌아와 나머지 물품을 민트로 옮기게 하고 가게를 닫아걸게 했으며, 끌어모을 수 있는 돈을 다 마련하여, 앞서 말했듯이 프랑스로 건너갔고, 그곳에서 그에게서 편지를 한두 통 받았을 뿐 그 이상은 없었다는 것이다."], ["I did not see him when he came home, for he having given me such instructions as above, and I having made the best of my time, I had no more business back again at the house, not knowing but I might have been stopped there by the creditors; for a commission of bankrupt being soon after issued, they might have stopped me by orders from the commissioners.", "나는 그가 집에 돌아왔을 때 그를 보지 못했으니, 그가 내게 앞서 말한 대로 지시를 해 두었고, 나도 내 시간을 최대한 활용한 터라, 다시 집으로 돌아갈 볼일이 더는 없었던 데다, 채권자들에게 거기서 붙들릴지도 모를 일이었기 때문이다. 머지않아 파산 명령이 내려졌으니, 그들이 위원들의 명령으로 나를 붙들 수도 있었던 것이다."], ["But my husband, having so dexterously got out of the bailiff’s house by letting himself down in a most desperate manner from almost the top of the house to the top of another building, and leaping from thence, which was almost two storeys, and which was enough indeed to have broken his neck, he came home and got away his goods before the creditors could come to seize; that is to say, before they could get out the commission, and be ready to send their officers to take possession.", "그러나 내 남편은 어찌나 날렵하게 집달리의 집에서 빠져나왔던지, 거의 집 꼭대기에서 다른 건물 꼭대기로 더없이 필사적인 방식으로 몸을 내려뜨린 다음 거기서 뛰어내렸는데, 그것은 거의 두 층 높이여서 실로 목이 부러지고도 남을 정도였으나, 그는 집으로 돌아와 채권자들이 압류하러 오기 전에—즉 그들이 파산 명령을 받아 내어 관리들을 보내 점유할 채비를 갖추기 전에—자기 물품을 빼돌렸다."]], [["My husband was so civil to me, for still I say he was much of a gentleman, that in the first letter he wrote me from France, he let me know where he had pawned twenty pieces of fine holland for £30, which were really worth £90, and enclosed me the token and an order for the taking them up, paying the money, which I did, and made in time above £100 of them, having leisure to cut them and sell them, some and some, to private families, as opportunity offered.", "내 남편은 내게 어찌나 정중했던지—여전히 말하건대 그는 신사다운 데가 많았으니—프랑스에서 내게 처음 쓴 편지에서, 자기가 30파운드에 고운 홀란드 천 스무 필을 잡혔는데 그것이 실은 90파운드짜리였다고 알려 주고는, 그 돈을 치르고 찾아오라는 전당표와 지시서를 동봉해 주었다. 나는 그대로 했고, 시간을 두고 그것들을 잘라 기회가 닿는 대로 개인 가정에 조금씩 조금씩 팔아, 끝내 100파운드가 넘게 거기서 벌었다."]], [["However, with all this, and all that I had secured before, I found, upon casting things up, my case was very much altered, any my fortune much lessened; for, including the hollands and a parcel of fine muslins, which I carried off before, and some plate, and other things, I found I could hardly muster up £500; and my condition was very odd, for though I had no child (I had had one by my gentleman draper, but it was buried), yet I was a widow bewitched; I had a husband and no husband, and I could not pretend to marry again, though I knew well enough my husband would never see England any more, if he lived fifty years. Thus, I say, I was limited from marriage, what offer might soever be made me; and I had not one friend to advise with in the condition I was in, least not one I durst trust the secret of my circumstances to, for if the commissioners were to have been informed where I was, I should have been fetched up and examined upon oath, and all I have saved be taken away from me.", "그러나 이 모든 것에다, 또 내가 전에 챙겨 둔 모든 것을 다 합쳐도, 셈을 따져 보니 내 처지가 매우 달라졌고 내 재산이 크게 줄었음을 알게 되었다. 홀란드 천과 내가 전에 빼낸 고운 모슬린 한 보따리, 그리고 약간의 은그릇과 다른 물건들까지 다 합쳐도, 나는 도무지 500파운드도 그러모으기 어려웠기 때문이다. 그리고 내 처지는 매우 묘했으니, 아이는 없었으나(내 신사 포목상에게서 하나 낳았으나 묻어 주었다), 나는 마법에 걸린 과부와도 같았다. 남편이 있으되 남편이 없는 셈이었고, 다시 결혼하는 척할 수도 없었으니, 내 남편이 쉰 해를 더 산다 해도 잉글랜드를 두 번 다시는 보지 못하리라는 것을 나는 너무도 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그러니 이렇게, 어떤 청혼이 내게 들어오든 간에, 나는 결혼이 막혀 있는 처지였다. 그리고 내가 처한 형편에서 의논할 벗이 하나도 없었으니, 적어도 내 처지의 비밀을 감히 털어놓을 만한 이는 하나도 없었다. 만약 위원들에게 내가 있는 곳이 알려진다면, 나는 끌려가 선서를 하고 심문을 받아 내가 챙겨 둔 모든 것을 다 빼앗기게 될 터였기 때문이다."]], [["Upon these apprehensions, the first thing I did was to go quite out of my knowledge, and go by another name.", "이런 우려에서, 내가 맨 먼저 한 일은 나를 아는 곳에서 완전히 벗어나 다른 이름으로 행세하는 것이었다."], ["This I did effectually, for I went into the Mint too, took lodgings in a very private place, dressed up in the habit of a widow, and called myself Mrs. Flanders.", "나는 이를 확실히 해냈으니, 나 역시 민트로 들어가 아주 으슥한 곳에 하숙을 정하고, 과부 차림으로 꾸미고는 나 자신을 플랜더스 부인이라 일컬었다."]], [["Here, however, I concealed myself, and though my new acquaintances knew nothing of me, yet I soon got a great deal of company about me; and whether it be that women are scarce among the sorts of people that generally are to be found there, or that some consolations in the miseries of the place are more requisite than on other occasions, I soon found an agreeable woman was exceedingly valuable among the sons of affliction there, and that those that wanted money to pay half a crown on the pound to their creditors, and that run in debt at the sign of the Bull for their dinners, would yet find money for a supper, if they liked the woman.", "그러나 여기서 나는 나 자신을 감추었고, 새로 사귄 이들이 나에 대해 아무것도 몰랐는데도, 머지않아 내 주위에 많은 사람이 모여들었다. 그곳에 흔히 모여드는 부류의 사람들 가운데 여자가 드물어서인지, 아니면 다른 경우보다 그곳의 비참함 속에서 어떤 위안이 더 절실히 필요해서인지, 나는 곧 마음에 드는 여자가 그곳의 고난받는 자들 사이에서 더없이 값진 존재라는 것을, 그리고 채권자들에게 1파운드당 반 크라운을 갚을 돈도 없는 자들, 황소 간판 술집에서 외상으로 끼니를 때우며 빚을 지는 자들조차도, 여자가 마음에 들면 저녁 한 끼 살 돈은 그래도 찾아내곤 한다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["However, I kept myself safe yet, though I began, like my Lord Rochester’s mistress, that loved his company, but would not admit him farther, to have the scandal of a whore, without the joy; and upon this score, tired with the place, and indeed with the company too, I began to think of removing.", "그러나 나는 아직 나 자신을 안전하게 지켰으니, 다만 마치 로체스터 경의 정부처럼, 그의 곁에 있기는 좋아하면서도 그 이상은 허락하지 않아, 즐거움은 없이 창녀라는 추문만 떠안게 되는 꼴이 되어 가기 시작했다. 그래서 이런 사정으로, 그곳에도, 실은 그 무리에게도 신물이 난 나는 자리를 옮길 생각을 하기 시작했다."]], [["It was indeed a subject of strange reflection to me to see men who were overwhelmed in perplexed circumstances, who were reduced some degrees below being ruined, whose families were objects of their own terror and other people’s charity, yet while a penny lasted, nay, even beyond it, endeavouring to drown themselves, labouring to forget former things, which now it was the proper time to remember, making more work for repentance, and sinning on, as a remedy for sin past.", "복잡하게 얽힌 처지에 짓눌린 사람들, 파멸을 몇 단계 넘어선 지경으로 떨어진 사람들, 그 식솔들이 그들 자신에게는 공포의 대상이요 다른 사람들에게는 적선의 대상이 되어 버린 사람들이, 그러면서도 동전 한 푼이라도 남아 있는 한, 아니 그것마저 떨어진 뒤에도, 자기 자신을 술에 빠뜨리려 애쓰고, 이제야말로 기억해야 마땅할 지난 일들을 잊으려 기를 쓰며, 회개거리를 더 늘리고, 지난 죄에 대한 처방이랍시고 계속 죄를 지어 나가는 것을 보는 일은, 내게 참으로 기묘한 성찰의 거리였다."]], [["But it is none of my talent to preach; these men were too wicked, even for me. There was something horrid and absurd in their way of sinning, for it was all a force even upon themselves; they did not only act against conscience, but against nature; they put a rape upon their temper to drown the reflections, which their circumstances continually gave them; and nothing was more easy than to see how sighs would interrupt their songs, and paleness and anguish sit upon their brows, in spite of the forced smiles they put on; nay, sometimes it would break out at their very mouths when they had parted with their money for a lewd treat or a wicked embrace. I have heard them, turning about, fetch a deep sigh, and cry, “What a dog am I! Well, Betty, my dear, I’ll drink thy health, though”; meaning the honest wife, that perhaps had not a half-crown for herself and three or four children. The next morning they are at their penitentials again; and perhaps the poor weeping wife comes over to him, either brings him some account of what his creditors are doing, and how she and the children are turned out of doors, or some other dreadful news; and this adds to his self-reproaches; but when he has thought and pored on it till he is almost mad, having no principles to support him, nothing within him or above him to comfort him, but finding it all darkness on every side, he flies to the same relief again, viz. to drink it away, debauch it away, and falling into company of men in just the same condition with himself, he repeats the crime, and thus he goes every day one step onward of his way to destruction.", "그러나 설교는 내 재주가 못 된다. 이 사람들은 나 같은 자가 보기에도 너무 사악했다. 그들이 죄짓는 방식에는 어딘가 끔찍하고 어처구니없는 데가 있었으니, 그것이 죄다 그들 자신에게마저 억지로 가하는 것이었기 때문이다. 그들은 양심에 거슬러 행동했을 뿐 아니라 본성에도 거슬러 행동했으니, 자기들 처지가 끊임없이 안겨 주는 그 성찰을 익사시키려고 자기 기질을 강간하다시피 했던 것이다. 그리고 그들이 억지로 지어 보이는 미소에도 불구하고, 한숨이 어떻게 그들의 노래를 가로막고, 창백함과 고뇌가 어떻게 그들의 이마에 들어앉는지를 보기란 더없이 쉬운 일이었다. 아니, 때로는 음란한 향연이나 사악한 포옹을 위해 돈을 다 써 버린 다음 바로 그들의 입에서 그것이 터져 나오기도 했다. 나는 그들이 돌아서며 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고는, \"나는 어찌 이리 개 같은 놈인가! 그래, 베티, 여보, 그래도 당신의 건강을 위해 한잔하리다.\" 하고 외치는 소리를 들은 적이 있으니, 이는 어쩌면 자기 자신과 서너 명의 아이들을 위해 반 크라운조차 없을지 모를 그 정직한 아내를 두고 하는 말이었다. 이튿날 아침이면 그들은 다시 참회에 잠긴다. 그리고 어쩌면 가엾은 울고 있는 아내가 그에게 건너와, 채권자들이 무슨 짓을 하고 있는지, 자기와 아이들이 어떻게 문밖으로 쫓겨났는지에 대한 소식이나 그 밖의 어떤 끔찍한 소식을 전한다. 그러면 이것이 그의 자책에 더해진다. 그러나 그가 거의 미칠 지경이 되도록 그것을 곰곰이 생각하고 골똘히 들여다본 끝에, 자기를 지탱해 줄 아무런 원칙도 없고, 자기를 위로해 줄 무엇도 안에든 위에든 없으며, 다만 사방이 온통 어둠뿐임을 깨닫고는, 그는 다시 똑같은 위안으로 달아난다. 즉 술로 그것을 잊어버리고, 방탕으로 그것을 떨쳐 내려 하다가, 꼭 자기와 똑같은 처지에 있는 사람들의 무리에 끼어들어 그 죄를 되풀이하니, 이렇게 그는 날마다 파멸로 가는 길을 한 걸음씩 더 나아간다."]], [["I was not wicked enough for such fellows as these yet.", "나는 아직 이런 작자들만큼 사악하지는 않았다."], ["On the contrary, I began to consider here very seriously what I had to do; how things stood with me, and what course I ought to take.", "오히려 나는 여기서 내가 무엇을 해야 할지, 내 형편이 어떠한지, 어떤 방도를 취해야 할지를 매우 진지하게 따져 보기 시작했다."], ["I knew I had no friends, no, not one friend or relation in the world; and that little I had left apparently wasted, which when it was gone, I saw nothing but misery and starving was before me.", "나는 벗이 없다는 것을, 아니, 온 세상에 벗이나 친척이 단 하나도 없다는 것을 알고 있었고, 내게 남은 그 얼마 안 되는 것마저 눈에 띄게 줄어들고 있어서, 그것이 다 떨어지고 나면 내 앞에는 비참함과 굶주림밖에 없으리라는 것을 알았다."], ["Upon these considerations, I say, and filled with horror at the place I was in, and the dreadful objects which I had always before me, I resolved to be gone.", "이런 생각들에서, 또 내가 있는 그곳과 늘 내 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 광경들에 진저리를 치며, 나는 떠나기로 결심했다."]], [["I had made an acquaintance with a very sober, good sort of a woman, who was a widow too, like me, but in better circumstances.", "나는 매우 진중하고 좋은 부류의 여자 한 사람과 친분을 맺었는데, 그녀 또한 나처럼 과부였으나 더 나은 형편에 있었다."], ["Her husband had been a captain of a merchant ship, and having had the misfortune to be cast away coming home on a voyage from the West Indies, which would have been very profitable if he had come safe, was so reduced by the loss, that though he had saved his life then, it broke his heart, and killed him afterwards; and his widow, being pursued by the creditors, was forced to take shelter in the Mint.", "그녀의 남편은 상선의 선장이었는데, 서인도제도에서 항해를 마치고 돌아오던 길에 난파하는 불행을 겪었으니, 만약 무사히 돌아왔더라면 매우 수지맞는 항해였을 그것이, 그 손실로 그를 어찌나 영락하게 만들었던지, 그때 목숨은 건졌으나 그것이 그의 가슴을 무너뜨려 끝내 그를 죽음에 이르게 했다. 그리고 그의 미망인은 채권자들에게 쫓겨 민트로 피신할 수밖에 없었다."], ["She soon made things up with the help of friends, and was at liberty again; and finding that I rather was there to be concealed, than by any particular prosecutions and finding also that I agreed with her, or rather she with me, in a just abhorrence of the place and of the company, she invited to go home with her till I could put myself in some posture of settling in the world to my mind; withal telling me, that it was ten to one but some good captain of a ship might take a fancy to me, and court me, in that part of the town where she lived.", "그녀는 곧 벗들의 도움으로 일을 수습하여 다시 자유의 몸이 되었고, 내가 어떤 특정한 소송 때문이라기보다는 오히려 몸을 숨기려고 그곳에 있다는 것을 알고, 또 내가 그곳과 그 무리에 대한 마땅한 혐오에서 자기와 뜻이 맞는다는 것을, 아니 오히려 그녀가 나와 뜻이 맞는다는 것을 알고는, 내가 세상에서 내 마음에 들게 자리 잡을 어떤 채비를 갖출 수 있을 때까지 자기 집에 함께 가 지내자고 청했다. 게다가 그녀가 사는 그 동네에서라면 십중팔구 어떤 훌륭한 선장이 나를 마음에 들어 하여 구애할 것이라고도 말했다."]], [["I accepted her offer, and was with her half a year, and should have been longer, but in that interval what she proposed to me happened to herself, and she married very much to her advantage.", "나는 그녀의 제안을 받아들여 반년 동안 그녀와 함께 지냈고, 더 오래 있었을 테지만, 그 사이에 그녀가 내게 제안했던 일이 그녀 자신에게 일어나, 그녀가 자기에게 매우 유리하게 결혼했다."], ["But whose fortune soever was upon the increase, mine seemed to be upon the wane, and I found nothing present, except two or three boatswains, or such fellows, but as for the commanders, they were generally of two sorts: 1.", "그러나 누구의 운수가 오르고 있든 간에, 내 운수는 기우는 듯했으니, 나는 갑판장 두세 명이나 그 비슷한 작자들 말고는 당장 아무것도 찾지 못했다."], ["Such as, having good business, that is to say, a good ship, resolved not to marry but with advantage, that is, with a good fortune; 2.", "그러나 선장들로 말하자면, 그들은 대체로 두 부류였으니, 첫째, 좋은 일자리, 즉 좋은 배를 가진 자들로서, 이익을 보고서야, 즉 좋은 재산을 보고서야 결혼하기로 작정한 자들이었고, 둘째, 일자리가 없어서 배를 구하는 데 도움이 될 아내를 원하는 자들이었다."], ["Such as, being out of employ, wanted a wife to help them to a ship; I mean (1) a wife who, having some money, could enable them to hold, as they call it, a good part of a ship themselves, so to encourage owners to come in; or (2) a wife who, if she had not money, had friends who were concerned in shipping, and so could help to put the young man into a good ship, which to them is as good as a portion; and neither of these was my case, so I looked like one that was to lie on hand.", "내 말은 (첫째) 얼마간 돈이 있어서, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면, 그들 자신이 배의 상당한 지분을 보유하게 해 주어, 그리하여 선주들이 끼어들도록 부추길 수 있는 아내, 혹은 (둘째) 돈은 없더라도 해운에 관여하는 벗들이 있어서, 그 젊은이를 좋은 배에 넣는 데 도움을 줄 수 있는 아내를 원했다는 것이니, 그들에게는 그것이 지참금이나 다름없는 것이었다. 그런데 이 어느 쪽도 내 경우는 아니었으니, 나는 처치 곤란하게 묵혀질 처지의 사람처럼 보였다."]], [["This knowledge I soon learned by experience, viz.", "나는 곧 경험으로 이 사실을 깨달았으니, 곧 혼인에 관해서는 사정이 달라졌다는 것, 그리고 내가 런던에서는 시골에서 발견했던 것을 기대할 수 없다는 것이었다."], ["that the state of things was altered as to matrimony, and that I was not to expect at London what I had found in the country: that marriages were here the consequences of politic schemes for forming interests, and carrying on business, and that Love had no share, or but very little, in the matter.", "즉 이곳에서 결혼이란 이해관계를 맺고 사업을 꾸려 나가기 위한 정략적 책략의 결과물이며, 사랑은 그 문제에 아무런 몫도, 혹은 아주 미미한 몫밖에 차지하지 못한다는 것이었다."]], [["That as my sister-in-law at Colchester had said, beauty, wit, manners, sense, good humour, good behaviour, education, virtue, piety, or any other qualification, whether of body or mind, had no power to recommend; that money only made a woman agreeable; that men chose mistresses indeed by the gust of their affection, and it was requisite to a whore to be handsome, well-shaped, have a good mien and a graceful behaviour; but that for a wife, no deformity would shock the fancy, no ill qualities the judgment; the money was the thing; the portion was neither crooked nor monstrous, but the money was always agreeable, whatever the wife was.", "콜체스터의 내 시누이가 말했던 대로, 미모, 재치, 예절, 분별, 좋은 기질, 좋은 처신, 교양, 덕, 신앙심, 혹은 몸이든 마음이든 그 어떤 다른 자질도 사람을 천거할 힘이 없으며, 오로지 돈만이 여자를 마음에 들게 만든다는 것이었다. 남자들은 정부를 고를 때는 정말로 애정의 입맛에 따랐으니, 창녀에게는 어여쁘고 몸매가 좋으며 좋은 자태와 우아한 처신을 갖추는 것이 요긴했으나, 아내로 말하자면, 어떤 흉한 외모도 그 욕망을 거스르지 않고, 어떤 나쁜 자질도 그 판단을 거스르지 않으니, 돈이 곧 관건이었다. 지참금은 굽지도 흉측하지도 않았고, 아내가 어떠하든 간에 돈만은 언제나 마음에 드는 법이었다."]], [["On the other hand, as the market ran very unhappily on the men’s side, I found the women had lost the privilege of saying No; that it was a favour now for a woman to have the Question asked, and if any young lady had so much arrogance as to counterfeit a negative, she never had the opportunity given her of denying twice, much less of recovering that false step, and accepting what she had but seemed to decline.", "다른 한편으로, 시장이 매우 불행하게도 남자들 쪽으로 기울어 있던지라, 나는 여자들이 싫다고 말할 특권을 잃어버렸음을 알게 되었다. 즉 이제 여자가 청혼을 받는 것 자체가 은혜였고, 만약 어떤 젊은 아가씨가 거절하는 척하는 오만함을 부린다면, 그녀에게는 두 번 거절할 기회는커녕, 그 잘못 디딘 발걸음을 되돌려 거절하는 척했을 뿐인 것을 받아들일 기회조차 결코 주어지지 않았다."], ["The men had such choice everywhere, that the case of the women was very unhappy; for they seemed to ply at every door, and if the man was by great chance refused at one house, he was sure to be received at the next.", "남자들은 어디서나 선택의 여지가 어찌나 많았던지 여자들의 처지가 매우 불행했으니, 남자들은 마치 집집마다 문을 두드리는 듯했고, 어떤 남자가 천만다행으로 한 집에서 거절당한다 해도 그는 다음 집에서는 틀림없이 받아들여졌기 때문이다."]], [["Besides this, I observed that the men made no scruple to set themselves out, and to go a-fortunehunting, as they call it, when they had really no fortune themselves to demand it, or merit to deserve it; and that they carried it so high, that a woman was scarce allowed to inquire after the character or estate of the person that pretended to her.", "이뿐만 아니라 나는 또 알게 되었으니, 남자들은 실제로 요구할 재산도 없고 받을 자격도 없으면서, 거리낌 없이 자기를 그럴듯하게 꾸미고는, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 재산 사냥에 나섰으며, 그것도 어찌나 거들먹거리며 해 댔던지, 여자는 자기에게 구애하는 자의 인품이나 재산을 캐물어 보는 것조차 거의 허락되지 않았다."], ["This I had an example of, in a young lady in the next house to me, and with whom I had contracted an intimacy; she was courted by a young captain, and though she had near £2000 to her fortune, she did but inquire of some of his neighbours about his character, his morals, or substance, and he took occasion at the next visit to let her know, truly, that he took it very ill, and that he should not give her the trouble of his visits any more.", "나는 이 본보기를 내 옆집에 사는 한 젊은 아가씨에게서 보았으니, 나는 그녀와 친분을 맺어 둔 터였다. 그녀는 어느 젊은 선장에게서 구애를 받았는데, 거의 2000파운드에 가까운 재산을 지녔으면서도 다만 그의 이웃 몇몇에게 그의 인품이나 품행이나 재산에 대해 물어보았을 뿐인데, 그는 다음번 방문 때 이를 빌미 삼아, 자기는 그것을 정말로 매우 언짢게 여기며 더는 그녀에게 방문하는 수고를 끼치지 않겠노라고 그녀에게 알렸다."], ["I heard of it, and I had begun my acquaintance with her, I went to see her upon it.", "나는 이 일을 전해 들었고, 또 그녀와 친분을 트기 시작한 터라, 이 일로 그녀를 보러 갔다."], ["She entered into a close conversation with me about it, and unbosomed herself very freely.", "그녀는 그 일에 대해 나와 흉금을 터놓고 이야기하며 매우 거리낌 없이 속내를 털어놓았다."], ["I perceived presently that though she thought herself very ill used, yet she had no power to resent it, and was exceedingly piqued that she had lost him, and particularly that another of less fortune had gained him.", "나는 곧, 그녀가 자기가 매우 부당하게 대접받았다고 여기면서도 그것을 분개할 힘이 없으며, 그를 잃었다는 것에, 특히 자기보다 재산이 적은 다른 여자가 그를 차지했다는 것에 몹시 약이 올라 있음을 알아챘다."]], [["I fortified her mind against such a meanness, as I called it; I told her, that as low as I was in the world, I would have despised a man that should think I ought to take him upon his own recommendation only, without having the liberty to inform myself of his fortune and of his character; also I told her, that as she had a good fortune, she had no need to stoop to the disaster of the time; that it was enough that the men could insult us that had but little money to recommend us, but if she suffered such an affront to pass upon her without resenting it, she would be rendered low-prized upon all occasions, and would be the contempt of all the women in that part of the town; that a woman can never want an opportunity to be revenged of a man that has used her ill, and that there were ways enough to humble such a fellow as that, or else certainly women were the most unhappy creatures in the world.", "나는 내가 비열함이라 부른 그런 짓에 맞서 그녀의 마음을 단단히 다잡아 주었다. 나는 그녀에게, 내가 세상에서 아무리 낮은 처지에 있다 해도, 내가 그의 재산과 인품을 알아볼 자유도 없이 오로지 그의 자기 추천만 믿고 그를 받아들여야 마땅하다고 생각하는 남자라면 경멸했을 것이라고 말했다. 또 나는 그녀에게, 그녀는 좋은 재산을 지녔으니 시대의 재앙에 몸을 굽힐 필요가 없다고, 우리처럼 천거할 만한 돈이 거의 없는 여자들을 남자들이 모욕할 수 있다는 것만으로도 족한데, 만약 그녀가 그런 모욕을 분개하지 않고 자기에게 넘어가도록 내버려 둔다면, 그녀는 모든 경우에 헐값으로 매겨질 것이고, 그 동네 모든 여자의 멸시를 받게 될 것이라고 말했다. 또 여자란 자기를 함부로 대한 남자에게 복수할 기회가 없을 리 없으며, 그런 작자를 굴복시킬 방도야 얼마든지 있으니, 그렇지 않다면 여자란 정녕 세상에서 가장 불행한 존재일 것이라고도 했다."]], [["I found she was very well pleased with the discourse, and she told me seriously that she would be very glad to make him sensible of her just resentment, and either to bring him on again, or have the satisfaction of her revenge being as public as possible.", "나는 그녀가 그 이야기에 매우 흡족해함을 알았고, 그녀는 진지하게 내게, 그에게 자기의 정당한 분노를 절감하게 하여 그를 다시 끌어들이거나, 아니면 자기 복수를 되도록 공공연하게 하는 만족을 얻거나 했으면 정말 좋겠다고 말했다."]], [["I told her, that if she would take my advice, I would tell her how she should obtain her wishes in both these things; and that I would engage to bring the man to her door again, and make him beg to be let in.", "나는 그녀에게, 만약 내 조언을 따르겠다면, 이 두 가지 일에서 어떻게 그녀의 소원을 이룰 수 있을지 일러 주겠다고 했다. 그리고 내가 그 남자를 다시 그녀의 문 앞으로 데려와 들여보내 달라고 빌게 만들겠노라고 장담했다."], ["She smiled at that, and plainly let me see, that if he came to her door, her resentment was not so great as to give her leave to let him stand long there.", "그녀는 그 말에 미소 지으며, 만약 그가 자기 문 앞에 온다면, 자기 분노가 그를 거기 오래 세워 둘 만큼 그리 크지는 않다는 것을 내게 분명히 내비쳤다."]], [["However, she listened very willingly to my offer of advice; so I told her that the first thing she ought to do was a piece of justice to herself, namely, that whereas she had been told by several people that he had reported among the ladies that he had left her, and pretended to give the advantage of the negative to himself, she should take care to have it well spread among the women—which she could not fail of an opportunity to do in a neighbourhood so addicted to family news as that she live in was—that she had inquired into his circumstances, and found he was not the man as to estate he pretended to be. “Let them be told, madam,” said I, “that you had been well informed that he was not the man that you expected, and that you thought it was not safe to meddle with him; that you heard he was of an ill temper, and that he boasted how he had used the women ill upon many occasions, and that particularly he was debauched in his morals”, etc. The last of which, indeed, had some truth in it; but at the same time I did not find that she seemed to like him much the worse for that part.", "그러나 그녀는 내 조언의 제안에 매우 기꺼이 귀 기울였으니, 그래서 나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 가장 먼저 해야 할 일은 자기 자신에게 정의를 베푸는 것이라고 말했다. 즉 여러 사람에게서, 그가 부인들 사이에서 자기가 그녀를 버렸다고 떠벌리며 거절의 우위를 자기에게 돌리는 척한다는 말을 들었으니, 그녀는 여자들 사이에 이것을 잘 퍼뜨리도록—그녀가 사는 곳처럼 집안 소식에 그토록 열을 올리는 동네에서야 그럴 기회가 없을 리 없었으니—신경 쓰라는 것이었다. 곧 자기가 그의 형편을 알아본 결과, 그가 재산에 관해서 자처하던 그런 사람이 아님을 알게 되었다고 말이다. \"사람들에게 이렇게 알려지게 하세요, 아가씨,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"당신이 그가 당신이 기대한 그런 사람이 아니라는 것을 잘 전해 들었고, 그래서 그와 엮이는 것이 안전하지 않다고 생각했다고요. 그가 성질이 고약하다는 말을 들었고, 그가 여러 경우에 여자들을 함부로 대한 것을 자랑했다고, 특히 그가 품행이 방탕했다고요.\" 운운. 그중 마지막 것은 정말 얼마간 진실이 담겨 있었으나, 동시에 나는 그녀가 그 대목 때문에 그를 더 나쁘게 여기는 것 같지는 않다는 것을 알아챘다."]], [["As I had put this into her head, she came most readily into it.", "내가 이것을 그녀의 머릿속에 심어 주자, 그녀는 더없이 선뜻 받아들였다."], ["Immediately she went to work to find instruments, and she had very little difficulty in the search, for telling her story in general to a couple of gossips in the neighbourhood, it was the chat of the tea-table all over that part of the town, and I met with it wherever I visited; also, as it was known that I was acquainted with the young lady herself, my opinion was asked very often, and I confirmed it with all the necessary aggravations, and set out his character in the blackest colours; but then as a piece of secret intelligence, I added, as what the other gossips knew nothing of, viz.", "그녀는 즉시 도구가 될 사람을 찾는 일에 착수했고, 그 일에는 거의 어려움이 없었으니, 동네의 수다쟁이 두엇에게 자기 이야기를 대강 들려준 것이, 그 일대 온 동네 다과상의 화젯거리가 되어 내가 어디를 방문하든 그 이야기와 마주쳤기 때문이다. 또 내가 그 젊은 아가씨 본인과 아는 사이라는 것이 알려진 터라, 사람들이 매우 자주 내 의견을 물었고, 나는 필요한 온갖 과장을 곁들여 그것을 뒷받침하며 그의 인품을 더없이 음울한 빛깔로 그려 냈다."], ["that I had heard he was in very bad circumstances; that he was under a necessity of a fortune to support his interest with the owners of the ship he commanded; that his own part was not paid for, and if it was not paid quickly, his owners would put him out of the ship, and his chief mate was likely to command it, who offered to buy that part which the captain had promised to take.", "그러고는 일종의 은밀한 정보로서, 다른 수다쟁이들은 전혀 모르는 것이라며 이렇게 덧붙였으니, 곧 내가 듣기로 그가 형편이 매우 좋지 않다는 것, 그가 지휘하는 배의 선주들과의 이해관계를 유지하려면 재산이 절실하다는 것, 그 자신의 지분 값이 치러지지 않았고 만약 곧 치러지지 않으면 선주들이 그를 배에서 내쫓을 것이며, 그 일등 항해사가 그 배를 지휘하게 될 듯한데, 그 항해사가 선장이 사기로 약속했던 그 지분을 사겠다고 제안했다는 것이었다."]], [["I added, for I confess I was heartily piqued at the rogue, as I called him, that I had heard a rumour, too, that he had a wife alive at Plymouth, and another in the West Indies, a thing which they all knew was not very uncommon for such kind of gentlemen.", "나는, 고백하건대 내가 그 악당이라 부른 자에게 진심으로 약이 올라 있던 터라, 그가 플리머스에 살아 있는 아내를 하나, 또 서인도제도에 다른 아내를 하나 두고 있다는 소문도 들었다고 덧붙였으니, 그런 부류의 신사들에게는 그것이 그리 드문 일이 아니라는 것을 그들 모두 알고 있었다."]], [["This worked as we both desire it, for presently the young lady next door, who had a father and mother that governed both her and her fortune, was shut up, and her father forbid him the house.", "이는 우리 둘 다 바라던 대로 먹혀들었으니, 즉시 옆집의 그 젊은 아가씨는—그녀에게는 그녀와 그녀의 재산을 둘 다 다스리는 아버지와 어머니가 있었는데—집 안에 갇히게 되었고, 그녀의 아버지는 그가 집에 드나드는 것을 금했다."], ["Also in one place more where he went, the woman had the courage, however strange it was, to say No; and he could try nowhere but he was reproached with his pride, and that he pretended not to give the women leave to inquire into his character, and the like.", "또 그가 드나들던 다른 한 곳에서도, 그 여자가 아무리 이상한 일일지언정 싫다고 말할 용기를 냈으니, 그는 어디를 가 보아도 자기 거만함을 두고, 또 여자들에게 자기 인품을 캐물을 여지를 주지 않으려 한다는 둥 하는 말로 비난받았다."]], [["Well, by this time he began to be sensible of his mistake; and having alarmed all the women on that side of the water, he went over to Ratcliff, and got access to some of the ladies there; but though the young women there too were, according to the fate of the day, pretty willing to be asked, yet such was his ill-luck, that his character followed him over the water and his good name was much the same there as it was on our side; so that though he might have had wives enough, yet it did not happen among the women that had good fortunes, which was what he wanted.", "자, 이쯤 되자 그는 자기 실수를 깨닫기 시작했고, 강 이쪽의 모든 여자에게 경보를 울려 놓은 터라, 강 건너 래트클리프로 가서 그곳의 부인 몇몇에게 접근했다. 그러나 그곳의 젊은 여자들 역시 그 시절의 풍조대로 청혼받기를 꽤 마다하지 않았으나, 그의 운수가 어찌나 사나웠던지 그의 평판이 강을 건너 그를 따라가, 그의 명성이 그곳에서도 우리 쪽에서나 다름없었으니, 그리하여 그가 아내야 얼마든지 구할 수 있었을지언정, 그가 원하던 바, 곧 좋은 재산을 지닌 여자들 사이에서는 그런 일이 일어나지 않았다."]], [["But this was not all; she very ingeniously managed another thing herself, for she got a young gentleman, who as a relation, and was indeed a married man, to come and visit her two or three times a week in a very fine chariot and good liveries, and her two agents, and I also, presently spread a report all over, that this gentleman came to court her; that he was a gentleman of a £1000 a year, and that he was fallen in love with her, and that she was going to her aunt’s in the city, because it was inconvenient for the gentleman to come to her with his coach in Redriff, the streets being so narrow and difficult.", "그러나 이것이 다가 아니었다. 그녀는 또 다른 일을 자기 손으로 매우 교묘하게 꾸몄으니, 친척인 데다 실은 유부남인 어떤 젊은 신사를 시켜, 매우 멋진 사륜마차에 좋은 제복을 갖춘 채 일주일에 두세 번 자기를 찾아오게 했다. 그러자 그녀의 두 앞잡이와 나 또한 즉시 온 동네에 소문을 퍼뜨렸으니, 이 신사가 그녀에게 구애하러 온다는 것, 그가 연 1000파운드의 수입을 가진 신사라는 것, 그가 그녀에게 반했다는 것, 그리고 그 신사가 레드리프로 마차를 끌고 그녀를 찾아오기에는 거리가 너무 좁고 다니기 어려워 불편하므로, 그녀가 시내에 있는 숙모 댁으로 가려 한다는 것이었다."]], [["This took immediately.", "이것은 즉시 먹혀들었다."], ["The captain was laughed at in all companies, and was ready to hang himself.", "그 선장은 어느 자리에서나 비웃음거리가 되어 목이라도 맬 지경이었다."], ["He tried all the ways possible to come at her again, and wrote the most passionate letters to her in the world, excusing his former rashness; and in short, by great application, obtained leave to wait on her again, as he said, to clear his reputation.", "그는 그녀에게 다시 다가갈 온갖 방도를 다 써 보았고, 세상에서 가장 절절한 편지를 그녀에게 써 보내며 자기의 지난 경솔함을 변명했다. 한마디로, 그는 갖은 애를 써서, 그의 말마따나 자기 평판을 깨끗이 하기 위해 다시 그녀를 찾아뵐 허락을 얻어 냈다."]], [["At this meeting she had her full revenge of him; for she told him she wondered what he took her to be, that she should admit any man to a treaty of so much consequence as that to marriage, without inquiring very well into his circumstances; that if he thought she was to be huffed into wedlock, and that she was in the same circumstances which her neighbours might be in, viz.", "이 만남에서 그녀는 그에게 흠뻑 복수를 했으니, 그녀는 그에게, 자기를 대체 어떤 여자로 여겼기에 결혼처럼 그토록 중대한 협상에 어떤 남자든 그 형편을 충분히 알아보지도 않고 받아들이리라 생각했느냐고 말했다. 또 만약 그가 자기를 윽박질러 혼인으로 몰아넣을 수 있다고, 자기가 이웃 여자들이 처할 법한 그런 처지, 즉 처음 나타나는 점잖은 기독교도면 누구든 덥석 잡을 그런 처지에 있다고 생각했다면 그것은 오산이라고 했다."], ["to take up with the first good Christian that came, he was mistaken; that, in a word, his character was really bad, or he was very ill beholden to his neighbours; and that unless he could clear up some points, in which she had justly been prejudiced, she had no more to say to him, but to do herself justice, and give him the satisfaction of knowing that she was not afraid to say No, either to him or any man else.", "또 한마디로, 그의 인품이 실제로 나빴거나, 아니면 그가 이웃들에게 몹시 부당한 평판을 입은 것이라고, 그리고 그가 자기가 정당하게 편견을 갖게 된 몇 가지 점을 해명하지 못한다면, 자기는 그에게 더 할 말이 없으며, 다만 자기 자신에게 정의를 베풀어, 자기는 그에게든 다른 어떤 남자에게든 싫다고 말하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 알게 하는 만족을 그에게 주겠노라고 했다."]], [["With that she told him what she had heard, or rather raised herself by my means, of his character; his not having paid for the part he pretended to own of the ship he commanded; of the resolution of his owners to put him out of the command, and to put his mate in his stead; and of the scandal raised on his morals; his having been reproached with such-and-such women, and having a wife at Plymouth and in the West Indies, and the like; and she asked him whether he could deny that she had good reason, if these things were not cleared up, to refuse him, and in the meantime to insist upon having satisfaction in points to significant as they were.", "그러면서 그녀는, 자기가 그의 인품에 대해 들은, 아니 오히려 내 도움으로 자기가 지어낸 것을 그에게 말했다. 즉 그가 지휘하는 배에서 자기 소유라고 자처하던 지분의 값을 치르지 않았다는 것, 선주들이 그를 지휘에서 내쫓고 그 자리에 항해사를 앉히기로 결정했다는 것, 그의 품행에 관해 일어난 추문, 곧 그가 이러이러한 여자들과 엮였다고 비난받았다는 것, 그리고 플리머스와 서인도제도에 아내를 두고 있다는 것 등을 말했다. 그러고는 그에게, 만약 이런 일들이 해명되지 않는다면, 자기가 그를 거절하고, 그동안 그것이 그토록 중대한 점들인 만큼 그에 대한 해명을 요구하는 것이 정당한 이유가 있는 것이 아니냐고, 그것을 부인할 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["He was so confounded at her discourse that he could not answer a word, and she almost began to believe that all was true, by his disorder, though at the same time she knew that she had been the raiser of all those reports herself.", "그는 그녀의 말에 어찌나 어리둥절했던지 한마디도 대답하지 못했고, 그녀는 그의 동요를 보고 그 모든 것이 사실인 양 거의 믿게 될 지경이었으니, 동시에 그 모든 소문을 일으킨 것이 바로 자기 자신이라는 것을 알면서도 그러했다."]], [["After some time he recovered himself a little, and from that time became the most humble, the most modest, and most importunate man alive in his courtship.", "얼마 후 그는 조금 정신을 차렸고, 그때부터 구애에서 살아 있는 누구보다도 가장 겸손하고 가장 조신하며 가장 끈질긴 남자가 되었다."]], [["She carried her jest on a great way.", "그녀는 자기 장난을 꽤 멀리까지 끌고 갔다."], ["She asked him, if he thought she was so at her last shift that she could or ought to bear such treatment, and if he did not see that she did not want those who thought it worth their while to come farther to her than he did; meaning the gentleman whom she had brought to visit her by way of sham.", "그녀는 그에게, 자기가 그런 대접을 감내할 수 있거나 감내해야 할 만큼 그토록 막다른 지경에 몰려 있다고 생각하느냐고, 그리고 자기에게는 그보다 더 멀리서까지 찾아올 만한 가치가 있다고 여기는 이들이 없지 않다는 것을 보지 못하느냐고 물었으니, 이는 그녀가 거짓 구실로 자기를 찾아오게 만든 그 신사를 두고 하는 말이었다."], ["She brought him by these tricks to submit to all possible measures to satisfy her, as well of his circumstances as of his behaviour.", "그녀는 이런 술책으로 그를, 자기 형편에 관해서든 자기 처신에 관해서든 그녀를 만족시킬 온갖 조치에 따르도록 굴복시켰다."], ["He brought her undeniable evidence of his having paid for his part of the ship; he brought her certificates from his owners, that the report of their intending to remove him from the command of the ship and put his chief mate in was false and groundless; in short, he was quite the reverse of what he was before.", "그는 그녀에게 자기가 배의 지분 값을 치렀다는 반박할 수 없는 증거를 가져왔고, 선주들에게서, 그들이 그를 배의 지휘에서 물러나게 하고 일등 항해사를 그 자리에 앉히려 한다는 소문이 거짓이며 근거 없다는 증명서를 가져왔으니, 한마디로 그는 이전의 그와는 완전히 정반대가 되었다."]], [["Thus I convinced her, that if the men made their advantage of our sex in the affair of marriage, upon the supposition of there being such choice to be had, and of the women being so easy, it was only owing to this, that the women wanted courage to maintain their ground and to play their part; and that, according to my Lord Rochester,", "이렇게 하여 나는 그녀에게 이를 납득시켰으니, 곧 남자들이 결혼이라는 일에서 우리 여성을 이용해 이득을 본다면, 그렇게 고를 여지가 많다는 것과 여자들이 그토록 만만하다는 것을 전제로 그러는 것이거니와, 그것은 오로지 이 한 가지, 곧 여자들이 자기 입지를 지키고 자기 몫을 해낼 용기가 없다는 데서 비롯된 것일 뿐이라는 것이었다. 그리고 로체스터 경의 말마따나,"]], [["“A woman’s ne’er so ruined but she can Revenge herself on her undoer, Man.”", "\"여자가 아무리 파멸했다 한들, 자기를 망친 자, 곧 남자에게 복수할 수 있는 법이라네.\""]], [["After these things this young lady played her part so well, that though she resolved to have him, and that indeed having him was the main bent of her design, yet she made his obtaining her be to him the most difficult thing in the world; and this she did, not by a haughty reserved carriage, but by a just policy, turning the tables upon him, and playing back upon him his own game; for as he pretended, by a kind of lofty carriage, to place himself above the occasion of a character, and to make inquiring into his character a kind of an affront to him, she broke with him upon that subject, and at the same time that she make him submit to all possible inquiry after his affairs, she apparently shut the door against his looking into her own.", "이런 일들이 있은 뒤, 이 젊은 아가씨는 자기 몫을 어찌나 잘 해냈던지, 그를 차지하기로 작정했고 실은 그를 차지하는 것이 자기 계획의 주된 목표였으면서도, 그가 자기를 얻는 일을 그에게는 세상에서 가장 어려운 일로 만들어 버렸다. 그리고 그녀가 이를 해낸 것은 거만하고 새침한 처신으로가 아니라 정당한 책략으로였으니, 그에게 판세를 뒤집어 그 자신의 수법을 그에게 되돌려 놓은 것이었다. 즉 그가 일종의 거만한 처신으로 자기를 인품 따위는 문제 삼을 수 없는 위치에 두고, 자기 인품을 캐묻는 것을 일종의 모욕으로 여기는 척했던 만큼, 그녀는 바로 그 문제를 두고 그와 절교했으며, 그가 자기 일에 관해서는 온갖 캐물음에 다 따르도록 만드는 동시에, 자기 자신의 일을 그가 들여다보는 것에 대해서는 보란 듯이 문을 닫아걸었던 것이다."]], [["It was enough to him to obtain her for a wife.", "그에게는 그녀를 아내로 얻는 것만으로 족했다."], ["As to what she had, she told him plainly, that as he knew her circumstances, it was but just she should know his; and though at the same time he had only known her circumstances by common fame, yet he had made so many protestations of his passion for her, that he could ask no more but her hand to his grand request, and the like ramble according to the custom of lovers.", "그녀가 가진 것에 관해서는, 그녀가 그에게 솔직히 말하기를, 그가 자기 형편을 아는 만큼 자기도 그의 형편을 아는 것이 마땅하다고 했다. 그리고 그가 동시에 그녀의 형편을 다만 세간의 소문으로만 알고 있었으면서도, 그녀에 대한 자기 열정을 어찌나 여러 번 다짐했던지, 그로서는 자기의 그 대단한 청에 그녀의 손을 달라는 것 말고는 더 바랄 것이 없다는 둥, 연인들의 관례에 따른 그런 종류의 횡설수설을 늘어놓을 수밖에 없었다."], ["In short, he left himself no room to ask any more questions about her estate, and she took the advantage of it like a prudent woman, for she placed part of her fortune so in trustees, without letting him know anything of it, that it was quite out of his reach, and made him be very well content with the rest.", "한마디로, 그는 그녀의 재산에 관해 더 물어볼 여지를 스스로 남겨 두지 않았고, 그녀는 신중한 여자답게 이를 이용했으니, 자기 재산의 일부를 그가 전혀 알지 못하게 수탁자들에게 맡겨 두어, 그것이 그의 손이 닿지 않는 곳에 완전히 벗어나 있게 하고, 그로 하여금 나머지에 매우 흡족해하도록 만들었다."]], [["It is true she was pretty well besides, that is to say, she had about £1400 in money, which she gave him; and the other, after some time, she brought to light as a perquisite to herself, which he was to accept as a mighty favour, seeing though it was not to be his, it might ease him in the article of her particular expenses; and I must add, that by this conduct the gentleman himself became not only the more humble in his applications to her to obtain her, but also was much the more an obliging husband to her when he had her.", "사실 그녀는 그 밖에도 형편이 꽤 좋았으니, 즉 그녀에게는 현금 1400파운드가량이 있어 그것을 그에게 주었고, 나머지는 얼마간 지난 뒤에 자기 몫의 부수입으로 드러내 보였는데, 그것이 그의 것이 되지는 않더라도 그녀의 특정한 비용이라는 항목에서 그의 부담을 덜어 줄 수 있는 만큼, 그는 그것을 대단한 은혜로 받아들여야 했다. 그리고 덧붙여야 할 것은, 이런 처신으로 그 신사 자신이 그녀를 얻고자 청할 때 더 겸손해졌을 뿐 아니라, 그녀를 차지한 뒤에도 그녀에게 훨씬 더 깍듯한 남편이 되었다는 것이다."], ["I cannot but remind the ladies here how much they place themselves below the common station of a wife, which, if I may be allowed not to be partial, is low enough already; I say, they place themselves below their common station, and prepare their own mortifications, by their submitting so to be insulted by the men beforehand, which I confess I see no necessity of.", "나는 여기서 부인들에게, 그들이 미리부터 남자들에게 모욕당하기를 그토록 감수함으로써 스스로를 아내의 통상적인 지위보다도—내가 편파적이지 않다고 한다면, 그 지위는 이미 충분히 낮거니와—얼마나 더 낮은 자리에 두는지, 또 자기 자신의 굴욕을 스스로 마련하는지를 일깨워 주지 않을 수 없으니, 고백하건대 나는 그래야 할 아무런 필요성도 찾을 수 없다."]], [["This relation may serve, therefore, to let the ladies see that the advantage is not so much on the other side as the men think it is; and though it may be true that the men have but too much choice among us, and that some women may be found who will dishonour themselves, be cheap, and easy to come at, and will scarce wait to be asked, yet if they will have women, as I may say, worth having, they may find them as uncomeatable as ever and that those that are otherwise are a sort of people that have such deficiencies, when had, as rather recommend the ladies that are difficult than encourage the men to go on with their easy courtship, and expect wives equally valuable that will come at first call.", "그러므로 이 이야기는 부인들에게, 그 우위가 남자들이 생각하는 것만큼 그리 그쪽에 있지 않다는 것을 보여 주는 데 쓸모가 있을 것이다. 그리고 남자들이 우리 가운데서 너무도 많이 고를 수 있다는 것이, 그리고 스스로를 욕되게 하고 값싸고 만만하게 굴며 청혼받기를 거의 기다리지도 않는 여자들이 더러 있다는 것이 사실이라 할지라도, 만약 그들이, 이를테면 얻을 만한 가치가 있는 여자를 얻으려 한다면, 그런 여자들은 여전히 좀처럼 손에 넣기 어렵다는 것을, 그리고 그렇지 않은 여자들은, 막상 얻고 보면 어떤 결함이 있어서, 오히려 까다롭게 구는 부인들을 천거해 주는 셈이지, 남자들이 그 만만한 구애를 계속하고 첫 부름에 응할 똑같이 값진 아내를 기대하도록 부추기는 것은 아니라는 것을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["Nothing is more certain than that the ladies always gain of the men by keeping their ground, and letting their pretended lovers see they can resent being slighted, and that they are not afraid of saying No.", "부인들이 자기 입지를 지키고, 자칭 연인들에게 자기들이 무시당하는 것을 분개할 줄 안다는 것을, 그리고 자기들이 싫다고 말하기를 두려워하지 않는다는 것을 보여 줌으로써 언제나 남자들보다 우위를 점한다는 것보다 더 확실한 것은 없다. 내가 보기에, 남자들은 여자의 수를 들먹이며 우리를 잔뜩 모욕하니, 곧 전쟁과 바다와 장사와 그 밖의 사건들이 남자들을 너무도 많이 데려가 버려서 양성의 수에 균형이 맞지 않으며, 그러므로 여자들이 불리하다는 것이다."], ["They, I observe, insult us mightily with telling us of the number of women; that the wars, and the sea, and trade, and other incidents have carried the men so much away, that there is no proportion between the numbers of the sexes, and therefore the women have the disadvantage; but I am far from granting that the number of women is so great, or the number of men so small; but if they will have me tell the truth, the disadvantage of the women is a terrible scandal upon the men, and it lies here, and here only; namely, that the age is so wicked, and the sex so debauched, that, in short, the number of such men as an honest woman ought to meddle with is small indeed, and it is but here and there that a man is to be found who is fit for a woman to venture upon.", "그러나 나는 여자의 수가 그토록 많다거나 남자의 수가 그토록 적다고는 결코 인정할 수 없다. 다만 그들이 내게 진실을 말하라고 한다면, 여자들의 불리함이란 남자들에게는 끔찍한 추문거리이거니와, 그것은 여기에, 오로지 여기에만 있으니, 곧 시대가 너무도 사악하고 그 성이 너무도 방탕하여, 한마디로 정숙한 여자가 엮여도 좋을 만한 남자의 수가 정말로 적으며, 여자가 모험을 걸어 볼 만한 남자란 여기저기 띄엄띄엄 있을 뿐이라는 것이다."], ["But the consequence even of that too amounts to no more than this, that women ought to be the more nice; for how do we know the just character of the man that makes the offer?", "그러나 그것의 귀결조차도 결국 이 정도에 지나지 않으니, 곧 여자들은 더욱더 까다로워야 마땅하다는 것이다. 청혼하는 남자의 진정한 인품을 우리가 어찌 알겠는가?"], ["To say that the woman should be the more easy on this occasion, is to say we should be the forwarder to venture because of the greatness of the danger, which, in my way of reasoning, is very absurd.", "이런 경우에 여자가 더 만만해야 한다고 말하는 것은, 위험이 크기 때문에 우리가 더 선뜻 모험을 걸어야 한다고 말하는 것이니, 내 식의 추론으로는 그것은 참으로 어처구니없는 일이다."]], [["On the contrary, the women have ten thousand times the more reason to be wary and backward, by how much the hazard of being betrayed is the greater; and would the ladies consider this, and act the wary part, they would discover every cheat that offered; for, in short, the lives of very few men nowadays will bear a character; and if the ladies do but make a little inquiry, they will soon be able to distinguish the men and deliver themselves.", "오히려 여자들은, 배신당할 위험이 클수록 만 배는 더 조심스럽고 신중해야 할 까닭이 있다. 그리고 부인들이 이를 헤아려 신중한 쪽을 택한다면, 다가오는 온갖 속임수를 죄다 간파해 낼 것이니, 한마디로 요즘 인품을 내세울 만한 남자의 삶이란 정말 드물기 때문이다."], ["As for women that do not think their own safety worth their thought, that, impatient of their perfect state, resolve, as they call it, to take the first good Christian that comes, that run into matrimony as a horse rushes into the battle, I can say nothing to them but this, that they are a sort of ladies that are to be prayed for among the rest of distempered people, and to me they look like people that venture their whole estates in a lottery where there is a hundred thousand blanks to one prize.", "그러니 부인들이 조금만이라도 알아본다면, 곧 남자들을 분간하여 스스로를 구해 낼 수 있을 것이다. 자기 자신의 안전 따위는 생각할 가치도 없다고 여기는 여자들로 말하자면, 자기의 온전한 처지를 견디지 못하고, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면, 처음 나타나는 점잖은 기독교도면 누구든 잡기로 작정하여, 마치 말이 전투로 돌진하듯 혼인으로 내달리는 그런 여자들에게 나는 이 말밖에 할 것이 없으니, 곧 그들은 다른 정신 나간 사람들 틈에 끼어 기도나 받아야 할 부류의 부인들이며, 내게는 그들이 마치 십만 개의 꽝에 상이 하나뿐인 복권에 자기 전 재산을 거는 사람들처럼 보인다는 것이다."]], [["No man of common-sense will value a woman the less for not giving up herself at the first attack, or for accepting his proposal without inquiring into his person or character; on the contrary, he must think her the weakest of all creatures in the world, as the rate of men now goes.", "상식이 있는 남자라면 여자가 첫 공격에 자기를 내주지 않는다고 해서, 혹은 그의 사람됨이나 인품을 알아보지도 않고 그의 청을 받아들이지 않는다고 해서 그녀를 더 낮게 보지는 않을 것이다. 오히려 그는, 요즘 남자들의 행태로 보아, 그녀를 세상에서 가장 미련한 피조물이라 여길 것이 틀림없다."], ["In short, he must have a very contemptible opinion of her capacities, nay, every of her understanding, that, having but one case of her life, shall call that life away at once, and make matrimony, like death, be a leap in the dark.", "한마디로, 자기 목숨이 단 하나뿐이면서 그 목숨을 단번에 던져 버리고, 혼인을 죽음처럼 어둠 속의 도약으로 만드는 여자라면, 그는 그녀의 능력에 대해, 아니 그녀의 분별 하나하나에 대해 매우 경멸스러운 견해를 가질 수밖에 없을 것이다."]], [["I would fain have the conduct of my sex a little regulated in this particular, which is the thing in which, of all the parts of life, I think at this time we suffer most in; ’tis nothing but lack of courage, the fear of not being married at all, and of that frightful state of life called an old maid, of which I have a story to tell by itself. This, I say, is the woman’s snare; but would the ladies once but get above that fear and manage rightly, they would more certainly avoid it by standing their ground, in a case so absolutely necessary to their felicity, that by exposing themselves as they do; and if they did not marry so soon as they may do otherwise, they would make themselves amends by marrying safer. She is always married too soon who gets a bad husband, and she is never married too late who gets a good one; in a word, there is no woman, deformity or lost reputation excepted, but if she manages well, may be married safely one time or other; but if she precipitates herself, it is ten thousand to one but she is undone.", "나는 이 점에서 우리 여성의 처신이 조금은 다스려졌으면 하고 진심으로 바라니, 이것이야말로 삶의 온갖 부분 가운데 지금 이 시대에 우리가 가장 크게 해를 입고 있는 일이라고 나는 생각한다. 그것은 다름 아닌 용기의 부족이요, 아예 결혼하지 못할까 하는 두려움이요, 노처녀라 불리는 그 무시무시한 삶의 처지에 대한 두려움이니, 이에 관해서는 따로 들려줄 이야기가 있다. 이것이, 다시 말하건대, 여자의 올가미이다. 그러나 부인들이 일단 그 두려움을 넘어서서 제대로 처신하기만 한다면, 자기 행복에 그토록 절대적으로 긴요한 일에서 자기 입지를 지킴으로써, 지금처럼 자기를 내던지는 것보다 더 확실하게 그것을 피할 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 비록 그들이 달리 할 수 있는 것만큼 빨리 결혼하지 못한다 해도, 더 안전하게 결혼함으로써 그 손해를 메우게 될 것이다. 나쁜 남편을 얻는 여자는 언제나 너무 일찍 결혼한 것이요, 좋은 남편을 얻는 여자는 결코 너무 늦게 결혼한 것이 아니다. 한마디로, 흉한 외모나 잃어버린 평판을 가진 경우를 제외하면, 처신만 잘한다면 언젠가는 안전하게 결혼할 수 없는 여자란 없으나, 만약 그녀가 서둘러 몸을 내던진다면, 십중팔구가 아니라 만 번 가운데 한 번 빼고는 그녀는 파멸하고 만다."]], [["But I come now to my own case, in which there was at this time no little nicety.", "그러나 나는 이제 내 자신의 경우로 오는데, 거기에는 이때 적잖은 미묘함이 있었다."], ["The circumstances I was in made the offer of a good husband the most necessary thing in the world to me, but I found soon that to be made cheap and easy was not the way.", "내가 처한 형편으로 보아 좋은 남편의 청혼은 내게 세상에서 가장 긴요한 일이었으나, 나는 곧 값싸고 만만하게 구는 것이 그 길이 아님을 알게 되었다."], ["It soon began to be found that the widow had no fortune, and to say this was to say all that was ill of me, for I began to be dropped in all the discourses of matrimony.", "머지않아 그 과부에게 재산이 없다는 것이 알려지기 시작했고, 이 말은 곧 나에 대한 온갖 나쁜 말을 다 하는 셈이었으니, 나는 혼인에 관한 모든 이야기에서 빠지기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["Being well-bred, handsome, witty, modest, and agreeable; all which I had allowed to my character—whether justly or no is not the purpose—I say, all these would not do without the dross, which way now become more valuable than virtue itself.", "교양 있고, 어여쁘고, 재치 있고, 정숙하고, 마음에 드는 것—이 모든 것이 내 인품으로 인정되었으나, 그것이 정당한지 아닌지는 여기서 따질 바가 아니거니와—다시 말하건대, 이 모든 것도 그 찌꺼기 없이는 소용이 없었으니, 이제 그것이 덕 그 자체보다도 더 값진 것이 되어 버린 것이다."], ["In short, the widow, they said, had no money.", "한마디로, 그들은 그 과부에게 돈이 없다고 말했다."]], [["I resolved, therefore, as to the state of my present circumstances, that it was absolutely necessary to change my station, and make a new appearance in some other place where I was not known, and even to pass by another name if I found occasion.", "그리하여 나는 내 현재 형편의 상태에 관해, 내 처지를 바꾸어, 나를 알지 못하는 어떤 다른 곳에서 새로이 모습을 드러내는 것이, 또 필요하다면 다른 이름으로 행세하는 것조차도 절대적으로 긴요하다고 결심했다."]], [["I communicated my thoughts to my intimate friend, the captain’s lady, whom I had so faithfully served in her case with the captain, and who was as ready to serve me in the same kind as I could desire.", "나는 내 생각을 가까운 벗인 그 선장 부인에게 털어놓았으니, 그녀가 선장과 겪은 일에서 내가 그토록 충실히 도와준 사람이요, 또 그녀도 내가 바랄 수 있는 만큼이나 똑같은 식으로 기꺼이 나를 도와줄 사람이었기 때문이다. 나는 거리낌 없이 그녀에게 내 형편을 털어놓았다."], ["I made no scruple to lay my circumstances open to her; my stock was but low, for I had made but about £540 at the close of my last affair, and I had wasted some of that; however, I had about £460 left, a great many very rich clothes, a gold watch, and some jewels, though of no extraordinary value, and about £30 or £40 left in linen not disposed of.", "내 밑천은 얼마 안 되었으니, 지난번 일이 끝났을 때 나는 약 540파운드밖에 마련하지 못했고, 그중 얼마는 써 버린 터였다. 그래도 내게는 약 460파운드가 남아 있었고, 매우 값비싼 옷이 많이 있었으며, 금시계 하나와 약간의 보석이 있었는데 그것들은 대단한 값어치는 아니었고, 처분하지 않은 아마포가 약 30파운드에서 40파운드어치 남아 있었다."]], [["My dear and faithful friend, the captain’s wife, was so sensible of the service I had done her in the affair above, that she was not only a steady friend to me, but, knowing my circumstances, she frequently made me presents as money came into her hands, such as fully amounted to a maintenance, so that I spent none of my own; and at last she made this unhappy proposal to me, viz.", "내 소중하고 충실한 벗, 그 선장의 아내는 내가 위의 그 일에서 자기에게 베푼 도움을 어찌나 절감했던지, 내게 한결같은 벗이 되어 주었을 뿐 아니라, 내 형편을 알기에 손에 돈이 들어올 때마다 자주 내게 선물을 주었으니, 그것이 충분히 한 사람의 생활비에 이르렀던지라, 나는 내 돈을 한 푼도 쓰지 않았다."], ["that as we had observed, as above, how the men made no scruple to set themselves out as persons meriting a woman of fortune, when they had really no fortune of their own, it was but just to deal with them in their own way and, if it was possible, to deceive the deceiver.", "그리고 마침내 그녀는 내게 이 불행한 제안을 했으니, 곧 앞서 말한 대로 우리가 지켜보았듯이, 남자들이 정작 자기 재산은 하나도 없으면서 거리낌 없이 재산 있는 여자를 얻을 자격이 있는 사람인 양 자기를 꾸미는 만큼, 그들의 수법 그대로 그들을 상대하여, 할 수만 있다면 그 속이는 자를 속이는 것이 마땅하다는 것이었다."]], [["The captain’s lady, in short, put this project into my head, and told me if I would be ruled by her I should certainly get a husband of fortune, without leaving him any room to reproach me with want of my own.", "한마디로 그 선장 부인이 이 계획을 내 머릿속에 심어 주며, 만약 내가 자기 지시를 따른다면 내가 분명 재산 있는 남편을 얻되, 그에게 나 자신의 재산이 없다고 나를 책망할 여지를 조금도 남기지 않을 것이라고 말했다."], ["I told her, as I had reason to do, that I would give up myself wholly to her directions, and that I would have neither tongue to speak nor feet to step in that affair but as she should direct me, depending that she would extricate me out of every difficulty she brought me into, which she said she would answer for.", "나는, 그럴 만한 이유가 있었던 터라, 그녀에게 나 자신을 온전히 그녀의 지시에 맡기겠노라고, 그리고 그 일에서 그녀가 시키는 대로가 아니고서는 말할 혀도 내디딜 발도 갖지 않겠노라고 말했으니, 그녀가 나를 몰아넣은 온갖 어려움에서 그녀가 나를 빠져나오게 해 주리라 믿었던 것이고, 그녀는 그것을 책임지겠노라고 했다."]], [["The first step she put me upon was to call her cousin, and go to a relation’s house of hers in the country, where she directed me, and where she brought her husband to visit me; and calling me cousin, she worked matters so about, that her husband and she together invited me most passionately to come to town and be with them, for they now live in a quite different place from where they were before.", "그녀가 내게 시킨 첫 단계는 그녀를 사촌이라 부르며 그녀가 일러 준 대로 시골에 있는 그녀의 친척 집으로 가는 것이었는데, 그곳으로 그녀가 자기 남편을 데려와 나를 방문하게 했고, 나를 사촌이라 부르며 일을 이리저리 잘 꾸며서, 그녀의 남편과 그녀가 함께 내게 시내로 와서 자기들과 함께 지내자고 더없이 열렬히 청했으니, 그들은 이제 전에 살던 곳과는 사뭇 다른 곳에 살고 있었기 때문이다."], ["In the next place, she tells her husband that I had at least £1500 fortune, and that after some of my relations I was like to have a great deal more.", "다음으로 그녀는 자기 남편에게, 내게 적어도 1500파운드의 재산이 있으며, 내 친척 몇몇이 죽고 나면 훨씬 더 많이 갖게 될 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["It was enough to tell her husband this; there needed nothing on my side. I was but to sit still and wait the event, for it presently went all over the neighbourhood that the young widow at Captain ——’s was a fortune, that she had at least £1500, and perhaps a great deal more, and that the captain said so; and if the captain was asked at any time about me, he made no scruple to affirm it, though he knew not one word of the matter, other than that his wife had told him so; and in this he thought no harm, for he really believed it to be so, because he had it from his wife: so slender a foundation will those fellows build upon, if they do but think there is a fortune in the game. With the reputation of this fortune, I presently found myself blessed with admirers enough, and that I had my choice of men, as scarce as they said they were, which, by the way, confirms what I was saying before. This being my case, I, who had a subtle game to play, had nothing now to do but to single out from them all the properest man that might be for my purpose; that is to say, the man who was most likely to depend upon the hearsay of a fortune, and not inquire too far into the particulars; and unless I did this I did nothing, for my case would not bear much inquiry.", "남편에게 이 말을 해 두는 것으로 충분했으니, 내 쪽에서는 아무것도 할 필요가 없었다. 나는 다만 가만히 앉아 일이 어떻게 되는지 기다리기만 하면 되었으니, 즉시 온 동네에, 아무개 선장 댁에 있는 그 젊은 과부가 재산가라는 것, 그녀에게 적어도 1500파운드, 어쩌면 훨씬 더 많은 재산이 있다는 것, 그리고 선장이 그렇게 말했다는 소문이 쫙 퍼졌기 때문이다. 그리고 누가 언제든 선장에게 나에 대해 물으면, 그는 그 일을 한마디도 알지 못하면서도, 자기 아내가 그렇게 말했다는 것 말고는 아무것도 모르면서도, 거리낌 없이 그것을 단언했다. 그리고 이 점에서 그는 아무런 해도 생각하지 않았으니, 그것을 아내에게서 들었기에 정말로 그렇다고 믿었기 때문이다. 노름판에 재산이 걸려 있다고 생각하기만 하면 그런 작자들은 그토록 빈약한 기초 위에 집을 짓는 법이다. 이 재산이라는 평판으로 나는 곧 흠모하는 자들이 넘쳐 나는 축복을 누리는 나 자신을 발견했고, 그들이 그토록 드물다고들 했건만 내가 남자를 골라잡을 수 있게 되었음을 알았으니, 그건 그렇고 이는 내가 앞서 한 말을 확증해 준다. 이것이 내 처지인지라, 교묘한 노름을 벌여야 했던 나로서는 이제 그들 모두 가운데서 내 목적에 가장 알맞을 남자를 골라내는 일밖에 할 것이 없었다. 즉 재산에 대한 풍문을 가장 곧이곧대로 믿고 그 자세한 내막을 너무 깊이 캐묻지 않을 법한 남자를 말이다. 그리고 내가 이를 해내지 못한다면 나는 아무것도 못 하는 것이었으니, 내 경우는 많은 캐물음을 견뎌 내지 못할 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["I picked out my man without much difficulty, by the judgment I made of his way of courting me.", "나는 그가 내게 구애하는 방식을 보고 내린 판단으로, 큰 어려움 없이 내 남자를 골라냈다."], ["I had let him run on with his protestations and oaths that he loved me above all the world; that if I would make him happy, that was enough; all which I knew was upon supposition, nay, it was upon a full satisfaction, that I was very rich, though I never told him a word of it myself.", "나는 그가 자기는 세상 그 무엇보다 나를 사랑한다고, 만약 내가 그를 행복하게 해 준다면 그것으로 족하다고 다짐하고 맹세하도록 내버려 두었으니, 그 모든 것이 내가 매우 부유하다는 가정 위에, 아니 충분한 확신 위에 놓인 것임을 나는 알고 있었다, 비록 나 자신은 그것에 대해 그에게 한마디도 한 적이 없었지만 말이다."]], [["This was my man; but I was to try him to the bottom, and indeed in that consisted my safety; for if he baulked, I knew I was undone, as surely as he was undone if he took me; and if I did not make some scruple about his fortune, it was the way to lead him to raise some about mine; and first, therefore, I pretended on all occasions to doubt his sincerity, and told him, perhaps he only courted me for my fortune. He stopped my mouth in that part with the thunder of his protestations, as above, but still I pretended to doubt.", "이 사람이 내 남자였다. 그러나 나는 그를 속속들이 시험해 보아야 했으니, 실로 내 안전은 거기에 달려 있었다. 만약 그가 주춤한다면, 그가 나를 잡았다가 망하는 것이 확실하듯, 나도 망하는 것이 확실하다는 것을 나는 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 그의 재산에 대해 얼마간 까다롭게 굴지 않는다면, 그것은 그로 하여금 내 재산에 대해 얼마간 까다롭게 굴도록 이끄는 길이었다. 그러므로 나는 우선 기회 있을 때마다 그의 진심을 의심하는 척하며, 어쩌면 그가 오직 내 재산 때문에 내게 구애하는 것이리라고 말했다. 그는 앞서 말한 대로 우레와 같은 다짐으로 그 점에 대해 내 입을 막았으나, 나는 여전히 의심하는 척했다."]], [["One morning he pulls off his diamond ring, and writes upon the glass of the sash in my chamber this line—", "어느 날 아침 그는 자기 다이아몬드 반지를 빼더니, 내 방 들창 유리에 이런 시구를 썼다—"]], [["“You I love, and you alone.”", "\"내가 사랑하는 이는 당신, 오직 당신뿐.\""]], [["I read it, and asked him to lend me his ring, with which I wrote under it, thus—", "나는 그것을 읽고, 그에게 반지를 빌려 달라 하여 그 아래에 이렇게 썼다—"]], [["“And so in love says every one.”", "\"그리고 누구나 사랑할 때 그렇게 말하지요.\""]], [["He takes his ring again, and writes another line thus—", "그는 다시 자기 반지를 받아 또 이런 한 줄을 썼다—"]], [["“Virtue alone is an estate.”", "\"덕만이 홀로 하나의 재산이라네.\""]], [["I borrowed it again, and I wrote under it—", "나는 다시 그것을 빌려 그 아래에 썼다—"]], [["“But money’s virtue, gold is fate.”", "\"하지만 돈이 곧 덕이요, 황금이 곧 운명이지요.\""]], [["He coloured as red as fire to see me turn so quick upon him, and in a kind of a rage told me he would conquer me, and writes again thus—", "그는 내가 그토록 잽싸게 자기에게 응수하는 것을 보고 불처럼 새빨개졌고, 일종의 격분에 차서 자기가 나를 정복하겠노라고 말하더니 다시 이렇게 썼다—"]], [["“I scorn your gold, and yet I love.”", "\"나는 당신의 황금을 경멸하오, 그래도 나는 사랑하오.\""]], [["I ventured all upon the last cast of poetry, as you’ll see, for I wrote boldly under his last—", "나는, 여러분도 보다시피, 시의 마지막 한 수에 모든 것을 걸었으니, 그의 마지막 구절 아래에 대담하게 이렇게 썼기 때문이다—"]], [["“I’m poor: let’s see how kind you’ll prove.”", "\"나는 가난해요. 당신이 얼마나 다정할지 어디 봅시다.\""]], [["This was a sad truth to me; whether he believed me or no, I could not tell; I supposed then that he did not.", "이것은 내게 서글픈 진실이었다. 그가 나를 믿었는지 아닌지 나로서는 알 수 없었으니, 그때 나는 그가 믿지 않았으리라 짐작했다."], ["However, he flew to me, took me in his arms, and, kissing me very eagerly, and with the greatest passion imaginable, he held me fast till he called for a pen and ink, and then told me he could not wait the tedious writing on the glass, but, pulling out a piece of paper, he began and wrote again—", "그러나 그는 내게로 날아와 나를 두 팔로 안고, 더없이 열렬하게, 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 열정으로 내게 입 맞추며, 펜과 잉크를 가져오라 할 때까지 나를 꼭 붙들었다. 그러고는 들창에 한 자 한 자 더디게 쓰기를 기다릴 수 없다고 하며, 종잇조각 하나를 꺼내 다시 쓰기 시작했다—"]], [["“Be mine, with all your poverty.”", "\"가난하든 어떻든 내 사람이 되어 주오.\""]], [["I took his pen, and followed him immediately, thus—", "나는 그의 펜을 받아 즉시 그를 따라 이렇게 썼다—"]], [["“Yet secretly you hope I lie.”", "\"그래도 당신은 속으로 내가 거짓말하기를 바라겠지요.\""]], [["He told me that was unkind, because it was not just, and that I put him upon contradicting me, which did not consist with good manners, any more than with his affection; and therefore, since I had insensibly drawn him into this poetical scribble, he begged I would not oblige him to break it off; so he writes again—", "그는 그것이 매정하다고 말했으니, 그것이 옳지 않은 데다, 자기로 하여금 나를 반박하게 만드는 것인데, 그것은 자기 애정에 못지않게 예절에도 맞지 않는 일이라는 것이었다. 그래서 내가 그를 자기도 모르게 이 시 짓는 끼적임 속으로 끌어들였으니, 부디 자기에게 그것을 중단하도록 강요하지 말아 달라고 청하며, 그는 다시 이렇게 썼다—"]], [["“Let love alone be our debate.”", "\"우리의 다툼은 오직 사랑에 관한 것만으로 합시다.\""]], [["I wrote again—", "나는 다시 썼다—"]], [["“She loves enough that does not hate.”", "\"미워하지 않는 여자라면 충분히 사랑하는 것이지요.\""]], [["This he took for a favour, and so laid down the cudgels, that is to say, the pen; I say, he took if for a favour, and a mighty one it was, if he had known all. However, he took it as I meant it, that is, to let him think I was inclined to go on with him, as indeed I had all the reason in the world to do, for he was the best-humoured, merry sort of a fellow that I ever met with, and I often reflected on myself how doubly criminal it was to deceive such a man; but that necessity, which pressed me to a settlement suitable to my condition, was my authority for it; and certainly his affection to me, and the goodness of his temper, however they might argue against using him ill, yet they strongly argued to me that he would better take the disappointment than some fiery-tempered wretch, who might have nothing to recommend him but those passions which would serve only to make a woman miserable all her days.", "그는 이것을 호의로 받아들이고는, 그리하여 몽둥이를, 즉 펜을 내려놓았다. 다시 말하건대, 그는 그것을 호의로 받아들였으니, 만약 그가 속사정을 다 알았더라면 그것은 대단한 호의였을 것이다. 그러나 그는 그것을 내가 의도한 대로 받아들였으니, 곧 내가 그와 계속 나아갈 마음이 있다고 그가 생각하도록 한 것이었고, 실로 나로서는 그렇게 할 온갖 이유가 다 있었으니, 그는 내가 일찍이 만나 본 가장 쾌활하고 명랑한 부류의 남자였기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 종종 나 자신을 돌이켜보며 어떻게\n그런 사람을 속이는 것은 갑절로 죄스러운 일이었다. 그러나 내 처지에 어울리는 안정된 자리를 마련하라고 나를 다그치던 그 절박함이 내게는 그렇게 할 권리가 되어 주었다. 또한 나에 대한 그의 애정과 그 너그러운 성품은, 그를 함부로 대하지 말아야 한다는 쪽으로 작용했을지언정, 나로서는 오히려 그가 그 어떤 불같은 성미의 못난 사내보다—여자를 평생 비참하게 만드는 것밖에는 내세울 것이 없는 그런 격정 말고는 내세울 것이 아무것도 없는 사내보다—이 실망을 더 잘 견뎌 내리라는 쪽으로 강하게 작용했다."]], [["Besides, though I jested with him (as he supposed it) so often about my poverty, yet, when he found it to be true, he had foreclosed all manner of objection, seeing, whether he was in jest or in earnest, he had declared he took me without any regard to my portion, and, whether I was in jest or in earnest, I had declared myself to be very poor; so that, in a word, I had him fast both ways; and though he might say afterwards he was cheated, yet he could never say that I had cheated him.", "게다가 비록 내가 그가 짐작한 대로 그토록 자주 내 가난을 두고 그에게 농을 던지긴 했으나, 그것이 사실로 밝혀졌을 때에 그는 온갖 종류의 이의를 미리 봉해 둔 셈이었다. 그가 농으로 말했든 진심으로 말했든, 그는 내 지참금을 전혀 따지지 않고 나를 맞겠다고 선언했고, 내가 농으로 말했든 진심으로 말했든, 나는 내가 무척 가난하다고 선언해 두었던 까닭이다. 그러니 한마디로 나는 그를 양쪽으로 단단히 옭아 둔 셈이었고, 비록 그가 나중에 속았다고 말할 수는 있을지언정, 내가 그를 속였다고는 결코 말할 수 없을 터였다."]], [["He pursued me close after this, and as I saw there was no need to fear losing him, I played the indifferent part with him longer than prudence might otherwise have dictated to me.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 그는 나를 바싹 따라다녔고, 나는 그를 잃을까 두려워할 까닭이 없음을 알았으므로, 분별이 아니었다면 권했을 것보다 더 오래 그에게 무심한 척하는 역할을 이어 갔다."], ["But I considered how much this caution and indifference would give me the advantage over him, when I should come to be under the necessity of owning my own circumstances to him; and I managed it the more warily, because I found he inferred from thence, as indeed he ought to do, that I either had the more money or the more judgment, and would not venture at all.", "그러나 나는 끝내 내 처지를 그에게 털어놓을 수밖에 없게 될 때, 이 신중함과 무심함이 내게 얼마나 큰 유리함을 줄 것인가를 헤아렸다. 그리고 더더욱 조심스럽게 일을 다루었으니, 그가 거기서, 마땅히 그래야 하듯이, 내가 돈이 더 많거나 분별이 더 깊어서 도무지 함부로 나서지 않는 것이라고 미루어 짐작한다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["I took the freedom one day, after we had talked pretty close to the subject, to tell him that it was true I had received the compliment of a lover from him, namely, that he would take me without inquiring into my fortune, and I would make him a suitable return in this, viz.", "어느 날 우리가 그 화제에 꽤 가까이 다가가 이야기를 나눈 뒤에, 나는 마음 놓고 그에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["that I would make as little inquiry into his as consisted with reason, but I hoped he would allow me to ask a few questions, which he would answer or not as he thought fit; and that I would not be offended if he did not answer me at all; one of these questions related to our manner of living, and the place where, because I had heard he had a great plantation in Virginia, and that he had talked of going to live there, and I told him I did not care to be transported.", "그가 연인으로서 내게 한 가지 호의, 곧 내 재산을 캐묻지 않고 나를 맞겠다는 호의를 베푼 것은 사실이며, 나도 이에 합당한 보답으로, 곧 이치에 닿는 한도 안에서 그의 재산에 대해서도 되도록 캐묻지 않겠으나, 다만 몇 가지 질문은 하도록 허락해 주기 바라며, 그것에 답하고 말고는 그가 알맞다고 여기는 대로 하면 되고, 설령 전혀 답하지 않더라도 나는 언짢아하지 않겠다고. 그 질문 가운데 하나는 우리가 어떻게, 또 어디서 살 것인가에 관한 것이었으니, 내가 듣기로 그가 버지니아에 큰 농장을 가지고 있고, 거기 가서 살겠다는 말을 했다기에, 나는 유배되고 싶지는 않다고 그에게 말했다."]], [["He began from this discourse to let me voluntarily into all his affairs, and to tell me in a frank, open way all his circumstances, by which I found he was very well to pass in the world; but that great part of his estate consisted of three plantations, which he had in Virginia, which brought him in a very good income, generally speaking, to the tune of £300, a year, but that if he was to live upon them, would bring him in four times as much.", "이 이야기를 시작으로 그는 자진하여 자기의 온갖 사정을 내게 털어놓기 시작했고, 솔직하고 거리낌 없는 태도로 자기의 모든 형편을 들려주었으니, 그로써 나는 그가 세상살이에 아주 넉넉한 처지라는 것을 알게 되었다. 다만 그의 재산 가운데 큰 몫은 버지니아에 둔 세 농장으로 이루어져 있었는데, 그것이 대체로 말해 연 삼백 파운드에 이르는 아주 좋은 수입을 가져다주었으나, 만약 그가 거기 살면서 직접 경영한다면 그 네 배는 벌어들이리라는 것이었다."], ["“Very well,”", "'아주 좋아.'"], ["thought I; “you shall carry me thither as soon as you please, though I won’t tell you so beforehand.”", "하고 나는 속으로 생각했다. '당신이 원하는 때에 곧바로 나를 거기로 데려가도 좋아, 비록 내가 미리 그렇게 말해 주지는 않겠지만.'"]], [["I jested with him extremely about the figure he would make in Virginia; but I found he would do anything I desired, though he did not seem glad to have me undervalue his plantations, so I turned my tale.", "나는 그가 버지니아에서 어떤 모습으로 행세할까를 두고 그를 몹시 놀려 댔으나, 그가 내 청이라면 무엇이든 다 들어주리라는 것을 알았다. 다만 그는 내가 자기 농장을 깎아내리는 것을 그리 달가워하지 않는 눈치였으므로, 나는 말머리를 돌렸다."], ["I told him I had good reason not to go there to live, because if his plantations were worth so much there, I had not a fortune suitable to a gentleman of £1200 a year, as he said his estate would be.", "나는 거기 가서 살지 않을 만한 그럴듯한 까닭이 있다고 그에게 말했으니, 그의 농장이 거기서 그만큼 값나가는 것이라면, 나는 그가 말한 대로 연 천이백 파운드짜리 재산을 가진 신사에게 어울릴 만한 재산을 갖추지 못했기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["He replied generously, he did not ask what my fortune was; he had told me from the beginning he would not, and he would be as good as his word; but whatever it was, he assured me he would never desire me to go to Virginia with him, or go thither himself without me, unless I was perfectly willing, and made it my choice.", "그는 너그럽게 대답하기를, 내 재산이 얼마인지 묻지 않겠으며, 처음부터 그러지 않겠다고 내게 말했으니 그 말을 그대로 지키겠다고 했다. 그러나 그것이 얼마이든 간에, 내가 더없이 기꺼이 원하여 스스로 택하는 것이 아니라면, 나더러 자기와 함께 버지니아로 가자고 결코 청하지 않을 것이며, 나 없이 자기 혼자 거기로 가지도 않겠노라고 다짐했다."]], [["All this, you may be sure, was as I wished, and indeed nothing could have happened more perfectly agreeable.", "이 모든 것이 내가 바라던 그대로였음은 여러분도 짐작할 수 있을 것이며, 사실 이보다 더 더할 나위 없이 흡족하게 일이 풀릴 수는 없었을 것이다. 나는 이 대목까지 일종의 무심함으로 끌고 나갔는데, 그는 처음보다도 그것을 더 자주 의아해했으나, 그 무심함이야말로 그의 구애를 떠받쳐 주는 유일한 버팀목이었다."], ["I carried it on as far as this with a sort of indifferency that he often wondered at, more than at first, but which was the only support of his courtship; and I mention it the rather to intimate again to the ladies that nothing but want of courage for such an indifferency makes our sex so cheap, and prepares them to be ill-used as they are; would they venture the loss of a pretending fop now and then, who carries it high upon the point of his own merit, they would certainly be less slighted, and courted more.", "내가 이 일을 굳이 언급하는 것은, 다시 한번 부인들에게 넌지시 일러 주고자 함이니, 바로 그런 무심함을 부릴 용기의 부족이야말로 우리 여자들을 그토록 값싸게 만들고, 지금처럼 함부로 대접받게끔 만드는 까닭이다. 만약 부인들이, 오로지 제 잘난 점만 내세우며 거들먹거리는 허세 부리는 멋쟁이 하나쯤 이따금 잃는 모험을 무릅쓸 수 있다면, 분명 덜 무시당하고 더 많이 구애받을 것이다."], ["Had I discovered really and truly what my great fortune was, and that in all I had not full £500 when he expected £1500, yet I had hooked him so fast, and played him so long, that I was satisfied he would have had me in my worst circumstances; and indeed it was less a surprise to him when he learned the truth than it would have been, because having not the least blame to lay on me, who had carried it with an air of indifference to the last, he would not say one word, except that indeed he thought it had been more, but that if it had been less he did not repent his bargain; only that he should not be able to maintain me so well as he intended.", "내가 정말로, 또 사실 그대로 내 대단한 재산이 얼마인지를, 그가 천오백 파운드를 기대하고 있을 때 사실 내가 가진 것은 통틀어 오백 파운드도 채 못 된다는 것을 드러냈더라면, 그래도 나는 그를 너무도 단단히 낚아 두었고 너무도 오래 어르고 다루었던 터라, 그가 나를 가장 나쁜 처지에 있다 해도 맞아 주었으리라고 확신했다. 게다가 그가 진실을 알게 되었을 때, 그것이 그에게는 다른 경우보다 덜 놀라운 일이었으니, 끝까지 무심한 태도로 일관한 나에게 그가 조금도 탓을 돌릴 데가 없었으므로, 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 다만 정말로 더 많을 줄 알았으나 설령 더 적다 한들 자기의 거래를 후회하지는 않으며, 다만 자기가 뜻했던 만큼 나를 잘 건사하지 못하게 될 뿐이라고만 했다."]], [["In short, we were married, and very happily married on my side, I assure you, as to the man; for he was the best-humoured man that every woman had, but his circumstances were not so good as I imagined, as, on the other hand, he had not bettered himself by marrying so much as he expected.", "요컨대 우리는 결혼했고, 적어도 그 남자에 관한 한, 내 쪽에서는 더없이 행복한 결혼이었다고 단언한다. 그는 일찍이 어떤 여자가 가져 본 적 없을 만큼 더없이 좋은 성품의 사내였기 때문이다. 그러나 그의 형편은 내가 짐작했던 것만큼 좋지는 않았고, 반대편으로는 그 또한 이 결혼으로 자기가 기대했던 만큼 처지를 끌어올리지는 못했다."]], [["When we were married, I was shrewdly put to it to bring him that little stock I had, and to let him see it was no more; but there was a necessity for it, so I took my opportunity one day when we were alone, to enter into a short dialogue with him about it.", "우리가 결혼했을 때, 나는 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 밑천을 그에게 가져다주고, 그것이 그게 전부임을 그가 알게 하느라 단단히 애를 먹었다. 그러나 그래야만 할 까닭이 있었으므로, 나는 어느 날 우리 둘만 있을 때를 틈타 그 일을 두고 그와 짤막한 이야기를 나누었다."], ["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["said I, “we have been married a fortnight; is it not time to let you know whether you have got a wife with something or with nothing?”", "\"우리가 결혼한 지 보름이 되었어요. 이제 당신이 무언가를 가진 아내를 얻은 건지, 아무것도 없는 아내를 얻은 건지 알려 드릴 때가 되지 않았나요?\""], ["“Your own time for that, my dear,”", "\"그건 당신 좋을 때 하구려, 여보.\""], ["says he; “I am satisfied that I have got the wife I love; I have not troubled you much,”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"나는 내가 사랑하는 아내를 얻은 것으로 흡족하오."], ["says he, “with my inquiry after it.”", "나는 그 일을 두고 당신을 그리 캐묻지 않았잖소.\" 하고 그가 말했다."]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"맞아요.\""], ["says I, “but I have a great difficulty upon me about it, which I scarce know how to manage.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"하지만 그 일로 제게 큰 걱정거리가 하나 있는데, 어떻게 다뤄야 할지 도무지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“What’s that, my dear?”", "\"그게 무엇이오, 여보?\""], ["says he.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"그게요,\""], ["says I, “’tis a little hard upon me, and ’tis harder upon you.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"제게는 좀 난처한 일이고, 당신에게는 더 난처한 일이에요."], ["I am told that Captain ——”", "제가 듣자니 ……대위가\""], ["(meaning my friend’s husband) “has told you I had a great deal more money than I ever pretended to have, and I am sure I never employed him to do so.”", "(내 친구의 남편을 두고 한 말이었다) \"당신에게, 제가 내세운 적도 없는 큰돈을 제가 가졌다고 말했다던데, 저는 분명 그에게 그렇게 하라고 시킨 적이 없거든요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"음,\""], ["says he, “Captain —— may have told me so, but what then?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"……대위가 내게 그렇게 말했을지도 모르지."], ["If you have not so much, that may lie at his door, but you never told me what you had, so I have no reason to blame you if you have nothing at all.”", "그런데 그게 어떻단 말이오? 당신이 그만큼 가지지 않았다면, 그건 그 사람 탓으로 돌리면 그만이고, 당신은 자기가 무엇을 가졌는지 내게 말한 적이 없으니, 설령 당신이 아무것도 가진 게 없다 한들 내가 당신을 탓할 까닭이 없소.\""]], [["“That’s is so just,”", "\"그게 너무도 옳고,\""], ["said I, “and so generous, that it makes my having but a little a double affliction to me.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"너무도 너그러운 말씀이라, 제가 가진 게 얼마 안 된다는 것이 제게는 갑절로 괴로운 일이 되네요.\""]], [["“The less you have, my dear,”", "\"가진 게 적을수록,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “the worse for us both; but I hope your affliction you speak of is not caused for fear I should be unkind to you, for want of a portion.", "\"우리 둘 다에게 더 나쁜 일이지. 하지만 당신이 말하는 그 괴로움이, 지참금이 없다고 내가 당신에게 매정하게 굴까 봐 두려워서 생긴 것은 아니길 바라오."], ["No, no, if you have nothing, tell me plainly, and at once; I may perhaps tell the captain he has cheated me, but I can never say you have cheated me, for did you not give it under your hand that you were poor?", "아니, 아니지, 가진 게 없다면 솔직하게, 그것도 단번에 내게 말해 주오. 어쩌면 내가 대위에게 나를 속였다고 말할 수는 있겠지만, 당신이 나를 속였다고는 결코 말할 수 없소."], ["and so I ought to expect you to be.”", "당신이 자기 손으로 직접 가난하다고 적어 주지 않았소? 그러니 나는 당신이 그러려니 하고 여겨야 마땅하지.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요,\""], ["said I, “my dear, I am glad I have not been concerned in deceiving you before marriage.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"여보, 결혼 전에 당신을 속이는 일에 제가 끼어들지 않았던 것이 다행이에요."], ["If I deceive you since, ’tis ne’er the worse; that I am poor is too true, but not so poor as to have nothing neither”; so I pulled out some bank bills, and gave him about £160.", "결혼한 뒤에 제가 당신을 속인다면, 그게 그나마 덜 나쁜 일일 테니까요. 제가 가난한 것은 너무도 사실이지만, 그렇다고 아무것도 없을 만큼 가난하지는 않답니다.\""], ["“There’s something, my dear,”", "그러고서 나는 은행권 몇 장을 꺼내어 그에게 백육십 파운드쯤을 건넸다. \"여기 좀 있어요, 여보.\""], ["said I, “and not quite all neither.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"그리고 이게 전부도 아니랍니다.\""]], [["I had brought him so near to expecting nothing, by what I had said before, that the money, though the sum was small in itself, was doubly welcome to him; he owned it was more than he looked for, and that he did not question by my discourse to him, but that my fine clothes, gold watch, and a diamond ring or two, had been all my fortune.", "내가 앞서 한 말로 그를 아무것도 기대하지 않을 지경까지 바싹 몰아 두었던 터라, 그 돈은 액수 자체로는 적었으나 그에게는 갑절로 반가웠다. 그는 그것이 자기가 기대했던 것보다 많다고 인정했고, 내가 그에게 한 이야기로 미루어 내 고운 옷가지며 금시계며 다이아몬드 반지 한두 개가 내 재산의 전부려니 했었노라고 했다."]], [["I let him please himself with that £160 two or three days, and then, having been abroad that day, and as if I had been to fetch it, I brought him £100 more home in gold, and told him there was a little more portion for him; and, in short, in about a week more I brought him £180 more, and about £60 in linen, which I made him believe I had been obliged to take with the £100 which I gave him in gold, as a composition for a debt of £600, being little more than five shillings in the pound, and overvalued too.", "나는 이삼 일 동안 그가 그 백육십 파운드로 흐뭇해하도록 내버려 두었다가, 그날 밖에 나갔다 들어와서는 마치 그것을 가지러 갔다 온 것처럼 금화로 백 파운드를 더 집에 들고 와서, 그에게 줄 지참금이 조금 더 있다고 말했다. 그러고서 한 일주일쯤 뒤에 나는 다시 백팔십 파운드를 더, 그리고 아마포로 육십 파운드어치쯤을 가져다주었는데, 그 아마포는 내가 그에게 금화로 준 백 파운드와 더불어, 육백 파운드짜리 빚에 대한 변제조로 어쩔 수 없이 받아 둔 것이라고 믿게 했으니, 그것은 일 파운드에 오 실링 남짓밖에 안 되는 데다 그나마 값도 후하게 쳐 준 것이었다."]], [["“And now, my dear,”", "\"그러니 이제, 여보,\""], ["says I to him, “I am very sorry to tell you, that there is all, and that I have given you my whole fortune.”", "하고 나는 그에게 말했다. \"참으로 안타깝게도 이게 전부이며, 제 재산을 몽땅 당신에게 드렸다는 걸 말씀드려야겠네요.\""], ["I added, that if the person who had my £600 had not abused me, I had been worth £1000 to him, but that as it was, I had been faithful to him, and reserved nothing to myself, but if it had been more he should have had it.", "나는 덧붙이기를, 내 육백 파운드를 가진 그 사람이 나를 등쳐먹지만 않았던들 나는 그에게 천 파운드의 값어치가 되었을 테지만, 형편이 이러하니 나는 그에게 충실하게 굴어 나 자신을 위해서는 한 푼도 남겨 두지 않았으며, 더 있었더라면 그것도 그가 가졌으리라고 했다."]], [["He was so obliged by the manner, and so pleased with the sum, for he had been in a terrible fright lest it had been nothing at all, that he accepted it very thankfully.", "그는 그 태도에 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 또 그 액수에 어찌나 흡족했던지—그는 그것이 아예 아무것도 아닐까 봐 끔찍한 공포에 휩싸여 있었던 까닭에—더없이 고마워하며 그것을 받아들였다."], ["And thus I got over the fraud of passing for a fortune without money, and cheating a man into marrying me on pretence of a fortune; which, by the way, I take to be one of the most dangerous steps a woman can take, and in which she runs the most hazard of being ill-used afterwards.", "이렇게 하여 나는, 돈도 없으면서 재산가로 행세하고 재산이 있는 척하여 한 남자를 속여 결혼하는 사기를 무사히 넘겼으니, 말이 나온 김에 덧붙이자면, 나는 이것이야말로 여자가 밟을 수 있는 가장 위험한 발걸음 가운데 하나이며, 그 일로 뒷날 함부로 대접받을 위험을 가장 크게 무릅쓰는 짓이라고 여긴다."]], [["My husband, to give him his due, was a man of infinite good nature, but he was no fool; and finding his income not suited to the manner of living which he had intended, if I had brought him what he expected, and being under a disappointment in his return of his plantations in Virginia, he discovered many times his inclination of going over to Virginia, to live upon his own; and often would be magnifying the way of living there, how cheap, how plentiful, how pleasant, and the like.", "내 남편은, 공정하게 말하자면, 한없이 좋은 천성을 지닌 사람이었으나 결코 바보는 아니었다. 그리고 만약 내가 그가 기대했던 것을 가져다주었더라면 누렸을 그런 생활 방식에 자기 수입이 걸맞지 않다는 것을 깨닫고, 또 버지니아에 있는 농장에서 들어오는 수입에 실망한 터라, 그는 여러 번 자기 소유지에서 살고자 버지니아로 건너가고 싶은 마음을 내비쳤다. 그리고 그곳의 생활 방식이 얼마나 비용이 적게 들고, 얼마나 풍족하며, 얼마나 즐거운지 따위를 종종 한껏 추켜세우곤 했다."]], [["I began presently to understand this meaning, and I took him up very plainly one morning, and told him that I did so; that I found his estate turned to no account at this distance, compared to what it would do if he lived upon the spot, and that I found he had a mind to go and live there; and I added, that I was sensible he had been disappointed in a wife, and that finding his expectations not answered that way, I could do no less, to make him amends, than tell him that I was very willing to go over to Virginia with him and live there.", "나는 곧바로 이 속뜻을 알아채기 시작했고, 어느 날 아침 그를 아주 솔직하게 붙들고 그렇다고 말했다. 곧 나는 그의 소유지가 이렇게 먼 거리에서는 그가 현지에 살면서 거두어들일 수익에 견주어 아무런 보탬이 못 된다는 것을 알았으며, 그가 거기 가서 살고 싶은 마음이 있음을 알았다고 했다. 그러고서 나는 덧붙이기를, 그가 아내를 얻는 일에 실망했음을 헤아리고 있으며, 그쪽으로 그의 기대가 채워지지 못한 것을 알았으니, 그 보상으로 내가 해 줄 수 있는 최소한의 일로서, 나는 더없이 기꺼이 그와 함께 버지니아로 건너가 거기서 살겠다고 그에게 말한다고 했다."]], [["He said a thousand kind things to me upon the subject of my making such a proposal to him.", "그는 내가 자기에게 그러한 제안을 하는 것을 두고 천 마디 다정한 말을 내게 늘어놓았다."], ["He told me, that however he was disappointed in his expectations of a fortune, he was not disappointed in a wife, and that I was all to him that a wife could be, and he was more than satisfied on the whole when the particulars were put together, but that this offer was so kind, that it was more than he could express.", "그는 비록 재산에 대한 기대에는 실망했을지언정 아내에 대해서는 실망하지 않았으며, 나는 아내가 될 수 있는 모든 것을 그에게 다 갖추어 준 사람이고, 이것저것 낱낱이 헤아려 합쳐 보면 전체로는 만족하고도 남는다고 했다. 다만 이 제안이 너무도 다정하여, 자기로서는 이루 다 말로 표현할 수 없을 지경이라고 했다."]], [["To bring the story short, we agreed to go.", "이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 우리는 가기로 합의했다. 그는 거기에 아주 좋은 집이 한 채 있고, 가구도 잘 갖추어져 있으며, 어머니가 살아 계셔서 그 집에 살고 계시고, 누이가 하나 있는데 그것이 자기가 가진 친척의 전부라고 했다."], ["He told me that he had a very good house there, that it was well furnished, that his mother was alive and lived in it, and one sister, which was all the relations he had; that as soon as he came there, his mother would remove to another house, which was her own for life, and his after her decease; so that I should have all the house to myself; and I found all this to be exactly as he had said.", "또 자기가 거기 도착하는 즉시 어머니는 다른 집으로 옮겨 가실 텐데, 그 집은 어머니가 살아 계시는 동안에는 어머니의 것이고 돌아가신 뒤에는 자기 것이 되며, 그리하여 나는 그 집 전체를 나 혼자 차지하게 되리라고 했다. 그리고 나는 이 모든 것이 그가 말한 그대로임을 알게 되었다."]], [["To make this part of the story short, we put on board the ship which we went in, a large quantity of good furniture for our house, with stores of linen and other necessaries, and a good cargo for sale, and away we went.", "이 대목의 이야기를 짧게 줄이자면, 우리는 우리가 타고 갈 배에 집에 쓸 좋은 가구를 잔뜩 실었고, 아마포와 그 밖의 필수품도 넉넉히 갖추었으며, 팔 만한 좋은 짐도 실었다. 그리고 우리는 떠났다."]], [["To give an account of the manner of our voyage, which was long and full of dangers, is out of my way; I kept no journal, neither did my husband.", "길고도 위험으로 가득했던 우리 항해의 형편을 일일이 늘어놓는 것은 내가 할 바가 아니니, 나도 항해 일지를 적지 않았고 내 남편도 마찬가지였다. 내가 말할 수 있는 것은 다만 끔찍한 항로를 지나며 무시무시한 폭풍에 두 번이나 혼이 났고, 한 번은 그보다 더 끔찍한 일을 겪었으니, 곧 한 해적이 배에 올라타 우리 식량을 거의 다 앗아 갔던 것이다."], ["All that I can say is, that after a terrible passage, frighted twice with dreadful storms, and once with what was still more terrible, I mean a pirate who came on board and took away almost all our provisions; and which would have been beyond all to me, they had once taken my husband to go along with them, but by entreaties were prevailed with to leave him;—I say, after all these terrible things, we arrived in York River in Virginia, and coming to our plantation, we were received with all the demonstrations of tenderness and affection, by my husband’s mother, that were possible to be expressed.", "그리고 나로서는 무엇보다 견딜 수 없었을 일은, 그들이 한때 내 남편을 데려가 자기들과 함께 가게 하려 했던 것이나, 간곡한 애원에 못 이겨 그를 두고 가도록 설득되었던 것이다. 다시 말해, 이 모든 끔찍한 일들을 겪은 끝에 우리는 버지니아의 요크강에 다다랐고, 우리 농장에 이르러서는 내 남편의 어머니로부터 표현할 수 있는 온갖 다정함과 애정의 표시를 받으며 맞아들여졌다."]], [["We lived here all together, my mother-in-law, at my entreaty, continuing in the house, for she was too kind a mother to be parted with; my husband likewise continued the same as at first, and I thought myself the happiest creature alive, when an odd and surprising event put an end to all that felicity in a moment, and rendered my condition the most uncomfortable, if not the most miserable, in the world.", "우리는 여기서 다 함께 살았으니, 내 시어머니는 내 간청에 따라 그 집에 계속 머무셨다. 그분은 헤어지기에는 너무도 다정한 어머니였기 때문이다. 내 남편 역시 처음과 다름없이 한결같았고, 나는 살아 있는 사람 가운데 가장 행복한 존재라고 여기던 차에, 기이하고도 놀라운 사건 하나가 그 모든 행복에 한순간에 종지부를 찍었으며, 내 처지를 세상에서 가장 비참하지는 않을지언정 가장 견디기 힘든 것으로 만들어 버렸다."]], [["My mother was a mighty cheerful, good-humoured old woman—I may call her old woman, for her son was above thirty; I say she was very pleasant, good company, and used to entertain me, in particular, with abundance of stories to divert me, as well of the country we were in as of the people.", "내 어머니는 무척이나 명랑하고 성품 좋은 노부인이었으니—그분을 노부인이라 불러도 좋을 것이, 그 아들이 서른 살이 넘었기 때문이다—다시 말해 그분은 매우 유쾌하고 좋은 말벗이었으며, 특히 나를 즐겁게 해 주려고 우리가 사는 그 고장과 그곳 사람들에 관한 온갖 이야기로 나를 곧잘 즐겁게 해 주곤 했다."]], [["Among the rest, she often told me how the greatest part of the inhabitants of the colony came thither in very indifferent circumstances from England; that, generally speaking, they were of two sorts; either, first, such as were brought over by masters of ships to be sold as servants.", "그중에서도 그분은 이 식민지 주민의 대부분이 잉글랜드에서 매우 보잘것없는 형편으로 이곳에 건너왔다는 것을 종종 내게 들려주었다. 곧, 대체로 말해 그들은 두 부류로 나뉘는데, 첫째는 하인으로 팔리려고 선장들에게 실려 온 자들이라고 했다."], ["“Such as we call them, my dear,”", "\"여보야, 우리는 그런 사람들을 그렇게 부르지만,\""], ["says she, “but they are more properly called slaves.”", "하고 그분은 말했다. \"더 정확히는 노예라고 불러야 마땅하단다.\""], ["Or, secondly, such as are transported from Newgate and other prisons, after having been found guilty of felony and other crimes punishable with death.", "둘째는, 중범죄나 그 밖에 사형으로 다스려지는 죄로 유죄판결을 받은 뒤에 뉴게이트와 그 밖의 감옥에서 유배된 자들이라고 했다."]], [["“When they come here,” says she, “we make no difference; the planters buy them, and they work together in the field till their time is out. When ’tis expired,” said she, “they have encouragement given them to plant for themselves; for they have a certain number of acres of land allotted them by the country, and they go to work to clear and cure the land, and then to plant it with tobacco and corn for their own use; and as the tradesmen and merchants will trust them with tools and clothes and other necessaries, upon the credit of their crop before it is grown, so they again plant every year a little more than the year before, and so buy whatever they want with the crop that is before them.", "\"그들이 이곳에 오면,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"우리는 가리지 않는단다. 농장주들이 그들을 사들이고, 그들은 기한이 다 찰 때까지 밭에서 함께 일하지. 기한이 끝나면,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"그들에게 스스로 농사를 짓도록 장려해 준단다. 나라에서 그들에게 일정한 면적의 땅을 떼어 주거든. 그러면 그들은 일에 나서서 땅을 개간하고 손질한 다음, 거기에 자기들이 쓸 담배와 옥수수를 심지. 그리고 장인들과 상인들이 작물이 자라기도 전에 그 작물을 담보 삼아 연장이며 옷이며 그 밖의 필수품을 외상으로 대 주니까, 그들은 또다시 해마다 전해보다 조금씩 더 심고, 그래서 눈앞에 있는 그 작물로 필요한 것은 무엇이든 사들이게 되는 거야."]], [["“Hence, child,”", "\"그렇게 해서, 얘야,\" 하고 그분이 말했다."], ["says she, “man a Newgate-bird becomes a great man, and we have,”", "\"뉴게이트의 새 같은 자도 큰 인물이 된단다. 그리고 우리한테는,\""], ["continued she, “several justices of the peace, officers of the trained bands, and magistrates of the towns they live in, that have been burnt in the hand.”", "하고 그분이 이어 말했다. \"손에 낙인이 찍힌 치안판사며, 민병대 장교며, 자기가 사는 고을의 행정관이 여럿 있단다.\""]], [["She was going on with that part of the story, when her own part in it interrupted her, and with a great deal of good-humoured confidence she told me she was one of the second sort of inhabitants herself; that she came away openly, having ventured too far in a particular case, so that she was become a criminal.", "그분은 그 이야기의 그 대목을 계속 이어 가다가, 거기서 자기 자신의 몫이 끼어드는 바람에 말을 멈추었고, 무던히 좋은 성품다운 거리낌 없는 태도로 자기 자신이 바로 그 둘째 부류의 주민 가운데 하나라고 내게 말했다. 곧, 어떤 특정한 사건에서 너무 멀리 모험을 감행하는 바람에 죄인이 되어, 떳떳이 드러난 채로 건너오게 되었다는 것이었다."], ["“And here’s the mark of it, child,”", "\"그리고 여기 그 표시가 있단다, 얘야.\""], ["says she; and, pulling off her glove, “look ye here,”", "하고 그분이 말했다. 그러고서 장갑을 벗으며 \"여기 좀 보렴.\""], ["says she, turning up the palm of her hand, and showed me a very fine white arm and hand, but branded in the inside of the hand, as in such cases it must be.", "하고 손바닥을 뒤집어, 무척이나 곱고 흰 팔과 손을 보여 주었으나, 그런 경우에 으레 그렇듯이 손 안쪽에 낙인이 찍혀 있었다."]], [["This story was very moving to me, but my mother, smiling, said, “You need not think a thing strange, daughter, for as I told you, some of the best men in this country are burnt in the hand, and they are not ashamed to own it. There’s Major ——,” says she, “he was an eminent pickpocket; there’s Justice Ba——r, was a shoplifter, and both of them were burnt in the hand; and I could name you several such as they are.”", "이 이야기는 내게 무척이나 가슴을 울렸으나, 내 어머니는 미소를 지으며 이렇게 말했다. \"딸아, 이상한 일로 여길 것 없단다. 내가 말했듯이, 이 고장에서 가장 훌륭한 사람들 가운데 더러도 손에 낙인이 찍혀 있고, 그들은 그것을 인정하기를 부끄러워하지 않으니까. 저기 ……소령은,\" 하고 그분이 말했다. \"이름난 소매치기였고, ……라는 판사는 들치기였는데, 둘 다 손에 낙인이 찍혔단다. 그들 같은 사람을 여럿 더 댈 수도 있지.\""]], [["We had frequent discourses of this kind, and abundance of instances she gave me of the like.", "우리는 이런 종류의 이야기를 자주 나누었고, 그분은 그와 비슷한 예를 숱하게 들려주었다. 얼마쯤 지나서, 그분이 불과 몇 주 전에 유배되어 온 어떤 사람의 이야기를 들려주고 있을 때, 나는 정겨운 태도로 그분 자신의 이야기를 좀 들려 달라고 청하기 시작했다."], ["After some time, as she was telling some stories of one that was transported but a few weeks ago, I began in an intimate kind of way to ask her to tell me something of her own story, which she did with the utmost plainness and sincerity; how she had fallen into very ill company in London in her young days, occasioned by her mother sending her frequently to carry victuals and other relief to a kinswoman of hers who was a prisoner in Newgate, and who lay in a miserable starving condition, was afterwards condemned to be hanged, but having got respite by pleading her belly, dies afterwards in the prison.", "그러자 그분은 더없이 꾸밈없고 진솔하게 그 이야기를 들려주었으니, 자기가 젊은 시절에 런던에서 매우 나쁜 무리에 빠지게 된 사정이었다. 그것은 그분의 어머니가 뉴게이트에 갇혀 비참하게 굶주리고 있던 어떤 친척 여자에게 먹을 것과 그 밖의 도움을 가져다주라고 그분을 자주 보냈던 데서 비롯된 것이었는데, 그 친척 여자는 나중에 교수형을 선고받았으나 임신을 사유로 형 집행이 유예되었다가 끝내 감옥에서 죽고 말았다."]], [["Here my mother-in-law ran out in a long account of the wicked practices in that dreadful place, and how it ruined more young people than all the town besides.", "여기서 내 시어머니는 그 끔찍한 곳에서 벌어지는 사악한 짓거리들에 대해, 또 그곳이 다른 온 도시를 합친 것보다 더 많은 젊은이를 어떻게 파멸시키는가에 대해 길게 늘어놓았다. \"그리고 얘야,\""], ["“And child,”", "하고 내 어머니가 말했다. \"너는 어쩌면 그것을 잘 모를 수도 있고, 어쩌면 그것에 대해 아무것도 들어 본 적이 없을 수도 있겠지."], ["says my mother, “perhaps you may know little of it, or, it may be, have heard nothing about it; but depend upon it,”", "하지만 이건 분명하단다,\" 하고 그분이 말했다."], ["says she, “we all know here that there are more thieves and rogues made by that one prison of Newgate than by all the clubs and societies of villains in the nation; ’tis that cursed place,”", "\"여기 사는 우리는 모두 안다, 뉴게이트라는 그 감옥 하나가 만들어 내는 도둑과 악당이 이 나라의 온갖 악당들의 패거리와 단체를 다 합친 것보다 더 많다는 것을. 바로 그 저주받은 곳이,\""], ["says my mother, “that half peopled this colony.”", "하고 내 어머니가 말했다. \"이 식민지의 절반을 사람으로 채운 게야.\""]], [["Here she went on with her own story so long, and in so particular a manner, that I began to be very uneasy; but coming to one particular that required telling her name, I thought I should have sunk down in the place.", "여기서 그분은 자기 이야기를 어찌나 길게, 또 어찌나 시시콜콜하게 이어 갔던지, 나는 매우 불안해지기 시작했다. 그러다가 그분의 이름을 말해야 하는 어떤 특정한 대목에 이르렀을 때, 나는 그 자리에서 푹 꺼져 들어가는 것만 같았다."], ["She perceived I was out of order, and asked me if I was not well, and what ailed me.", "그분은 내가 정신이 온전치 못한 것을 알아채고, 어디 편치 않으냐고, 무슨 일이냐고 물었다."], ["I told her I was so affected with the melancholy story she had told, and the terrible things she had gone through, that it had overcome me, and I begged of her to talk no more of it.", "나는 그분이 들려준 그 우울한 이야기와 그분이 겪은 그 끔찍한 일들에 너무도 마음이 흔들려 그만 압도되었다고 말하며, 그 이야기를 더는 하지 말아 달라고 간청했다. \"아니, 얘야,\""], ["“Why, my dear,”", "하고 그분이 무척 다정하게 말했다."], ["says she very kindly, “what need these things trouble you?", "\"무엇 때문에 이런 일들이 너를 괴롭혀야 한단 말이냐? 이 일들은 네가 태어나기 한참 전의 일이고, 지금은 내게 아무런 괴로움도 주지 않는단다."], ["These passages were long before your time, and they give me no trouble at all now; nay, I look back on them with a particular satisfaction, as they have been a means to bring me to this place.”", "아니, 오히려 나는 그 일들을 각별한 흡족함으로 돌아본다, 그것들이 나를 이곳에 데려다준 수단이 되었으니 말이다.\" 그러고서 그분은 어떻게 자기가 무척 운 좋게 좋은 집안에 들어가게 되었는지를 이어서 들려주었다."], ["Then she went on to tell me how she very luckily fell into a good family, where, behaving herself well, and her mistress dying, her master married her, by whom she had my husband and his sister, and that by her diligence and good management after her husband’s death, she had improved the plantations to such a degree as they then were, so that most of the estate was of her getting, not her husband’s, for she had been a widow upwards of sixteen years.", "거기서 처신을 잘하던 차에 여주인이 죽자 주인이 자기와 결혼했고, 그 사람과의 사이에서 내 남편과 그 누이를 낳았으며, 남편이 죽은 뒤로는 자기의 부지런함과 알뜰한 살림 솜씨로 농장을 그때만큼 일구어 냈으니, 재산의 대부분은 남편의 것이 아니라 자기가 모은 것이라고 했다. 그분은 십육 년 넘게 과부로 지내 온 터였다."]], [["I heard this part of the story with very little attention, because I wanted much to retire and give vent to my passions, which I did soon after; and let any one judge what must be the anguish of my mind, when I came to reflect that this was certainly no more or less than my own mother, and I had now had two children, and was big with another by my own brother, and lay with him still every night.", "나는 이 대목의 이야기를 거의 귀담아듣지 못했으니, 자리를 물러나 내 격정을 풀어 놓고 싶은 마음이 간절했기 때문이며, 곧이어 그렇게 했다. 그리고 누구든 한번 헤아려 보라, 이 사람이 다름 아닌 바로 내 친어머니이며, 나는 이제 아이를 둘이나 낳았고 또 하나를 내 친오라비의 씨로 배고 있으며, 여전히 밤마다 그와 한자리에 눕는다는 것을 곱씹게 되었을 때, 내 마음이 겪었을 그 고통이 어떠했겠는가를."]], [["I was now the most unhappy of all women in the world.", "나는 이제 세상의 모든 여자 가운데 가장 불행한 존재였다."], ["Oh!", "아아!"], ["had the story never been told me, all had been well; it had been no crime to have lain with my husband, since as to his being my relation I had known nothing of it.", "그 이야기를 내가 결코 듣지 않았더라면, 모든 것이 무사했을 것을. 내 남편과 한자리에 누운 것이 죄가 될 수 없었을 것이니, 그가 내 친척이라는 것에 관해서는 내가 아무것도 몰랐기 때문이다."]], [["I had now such a load on my mind that it kept me perpetually waking; to reveal it, which would have been some ease to me, I could not find would be to any purpose, and yet to conceal it would be next to impossible; nay, I did not doubt but I should talk of it in my sleep, and tell my husband of it whether I would or no. If I discovered it, the least thing I could expect was to lose my husband, for he was too nice and too honest a man to have continued my husband after he had known I had been his sister; so that I was perplexed to the last degree.", "나는 이제 마음에 너무도 무거운 짐을 지고 있어서 도무지 잠을 이룰 수가 없었다. 그것을 털어놓는다면 내게 얼마간 위안이 되겠으나, 그래 봐야 아무 소용이 없으리라는 것을 알았다. 그러면서도 그것을 감추기란 거의 불가능에 가까웠다. 아니, 나는 분명 잠결에 그것을 말해 버려, 내가 원하든 원치 않든 남편에게 그것을 털어놓고 말리라고 믿어 의심치 않았다. 만약 내가 그것을 드러낸다면, 내가 기대할 수 있는 최소한의 일은 남편을 잃는 것이었으니, 그는 자기가 내 오라비였음을 안 뒤에도 내 남편으로 머물기에는 너무도 까다롭고 너무도 정직한 사람이었다. 그리하여 나는 더할 나위 없는 지경으로 어찌할 바를 몰랐다."]], [["I leave it to any man to judge what difficulties presented to my view.", "내 눈앞에 어떤 난관들이 펼쳐졌을지는 누구든 헤아려 보도록 맡겨 두겠다."], ["I was away from my native country, at a distance prodigious, and the return to me unpassable.", "나는 내 고국에서 떨어져 어마어마하게 먼 거리에 있었고, 돌아갈 길은 내게 건너지 못할 것이었다."], ["I lived very well, but in a circumstance insufferable in itself.", "나는 아주 잘 살고 있었으나, 그 자체로 견딜 수 없는 처지에 놓여 있었다."], ["If I had discovered myself to my mother, it might be difficult to convince her of the particulars, and I had no way to prove them.", "만약 내가 어머니에게 내 정체를 드러낸다 해도, 그 세세한 정황을 그분께 납득시키기는 어려울 터였으며, 나로서는 그것을 증명할 길이 없었다."], ["On the other hand, if she had questioned or doubted me, I had been undone, for the bare suggestion would have immediately separated me from my husband, without gaining my mother or him, who would have been neither a husband nor a brother; so that between the surprise on one hand, and the uncertainty on the other, I had been sure to be undone.", "다른 한편으로, 만약 그분이 나를 의심하거나 미심쩍어한다면 나는 끝장이었으니, 그저 그런 암시만 흘려도 그것이 곧바로 나를 남편에게서 떼어 놓을 터인데, 그렇게 되면 어머니도 그도 얻지 못한 채, 그는 남편도 오라비도 아닌 처지가 되고 말 것이었다. 그리하여 한편의 놀라움과 다른 한편의 불확실함 사이에서, 나는 영락없이 끝장날 수밖에 없었다."]], [["In the meantime, as I was but too sure of the fact, I lived therefore in open avowed incest and whoredom, and all under the appearance of an honest wife; and though I was not much touched with the crime of it, yet the action had something in it shocking to nature, and made my husband, as he thought himself, even nauseous to me.", "그러는 동안, 나는 그 사실을 너무도 확실히 알고 있었던 까닭에, 정직한 아내의 외양을 한 채로 버젓이 드러난 근친상간과 매음 속에서 살고 있는 셈이었다. 그리고 비록 내가 그 죄에 대해 그리 마음이 찔리지는 않았다 해도, 그 행위 자체에는 본성을 거스르는 무언가 충격적인 것이 있어서, 그가 스스로 그렇게 여기던 대로의 내 남편을 내게 메스껍기까지 한 존재로 만들어 버렸다."]], [["However, upon the most sedate consideration, I resolved that it was absolutely necessary to conceal it all and not make the least discovery of it either to mother or husband; and thus I lived with the greatest pressure imaginable for three years more, but had no more children.", "그러나 더없이 차분하게 헤아린 끝에, 나는 그것을 모조리 감추고 어머니에게든 남편에게든 조금도 드러내지 않는 것이 절대로 필요하다고 결심했다. 그리하여 나는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 압박을 받으며 삼 년을 더 살았으나, 더는 아이를 낳지 않았다."]], [["During this time my mother used to be frequently telling me old stories of her former adventures, which, however, were no ways pleasant to me; for by it, though she did not tell it me in plain terms, yet I could easily understand, joined with what I had heard myself, of my first tutors, that in her younger days she had been both whore and thief; but I verily believed she had lived to repent sincerely of both, and that she was then a very pious, sober, and religious woman.", "이 동안 내 어머니는 자기의 지난 모험에 관한 옛이야기를 종종 들려주곤 했는데, 그러나 그것은 결코 내게 즐거운 일이 아니었다. 비록 그분이 분명한 말로 들려준 것은 아니었으나, 내가 직접 들은 것, 곧 내 첫 스승들에 관한 이야기와 합쳐 보면, 그분이 젊은 시절에 창녀이자 도둑이었음을 나는 쉽게 알아챌 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그분이 그 두 가지 모두를 진심으로 뉘우치며 살아왔으며, 이제는 매우 경건하고 절제 있고 신앙심 깊은 여인이 되었음을 정말로 믿었다."]], [["Well, let her life have been what it would then, it was certain that my life was very uneasy to me; for I lived, as I have said, but in the worst sort of whoredom, and as I could expect no good of it, so really no good issue came of it, and all my seeming prosperity wore off, and ended in misery and destruction.", "자, 그분의 지난 삶이 그때 어떠했든 간에, 분명한 것은 내 삶이 내게 매우 괴로웠다는 것이다. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 가장 고약한 종류의 매음 속에 살고 있었고, 그것에서 좋은 것을 기대할 수 없었던 만큼, 정말로 거기서 좋은 결말이 나오지도 않았으니, 나의 그 겉으로만 번지르르하던 번영은 모조리 닳아 없어져 비참과 파멸로 끝나고 말았다."], ["It was some time, indeed, before it came to this, for, but I know not by what ill fate guided, everything went wrong with us afterwards, and that which was worse, my husband grew strangely altered, forward, jealous, and unkind, and I was as impatient of bearing his carriage, as the carriage was unreasonable and unjust.", "사실 그 지경에 이르기까지는 얼마간 시간이 걸렸으니, 무슨 나쁜 운명에 이끌렸던 것인지는 모르겠으나, 그 뒤로 우리에게는 모든 일이 어긋나기 시작했고, 더 나쁜 것은, 내 남편이 이상하리만치 달라져 제멋대로 굴고, 시샘이 많아지고, 매정해졌으며, 나 또한 그의 처사가 부당하고 무례한 만큼이나 그것을 견디지 못해 안달이 났다는 것이다."], ["These things proceeded so far, that we came at last to be in such ill terms with one another, that I claimed a promise of him, which he entered willingly into with me when I consented to come from England with him, viz. that if I found the country not to agree with me, or that I did not like to live there, I should come away to England again when I pleased, giving him a year’s warning to settle his affairs.", "이런 일들이 너무 멀리 나아간 나머지, 우리는 마침내 서로 너무도 사이가 나빠졌고, 그래서 나는 그가 내게 했던 한 가지 약속을 요구하기에 이르렀으니, 그것은 내가 그와 함께 잉글랜드를 떠나오는 데 동의했을 때 그가 기꺼이 나와 맺었던 약속, 곧 만약 내가 그 고장이 내게 맞지 않는다거나 거기 사는 것이 싫다는 것을 알게 되면, 그가 자기 일을 정리하도록 일 년 전에 미리 알리고서 내가 원할 때 다시 잉글랜드로 떠나올 수 있게 한다는 것이었다."]], [["I say, I now claimed this promise of him, and I must confess I did it not in the most obliging terms that could be in the world neither; but I insisted that he treated me ill, that I was remote from my friends, and could do myself no justice, and that he was jealous without cause, my conversation having been unblamable, and he having no pretense for it, and that to remove to England would take away all occasion from him.", "다시 말해, 나는 이제 그에게 이 약속을 요구했는데, 솔직히 고백하건대 나는 그것을 세상에서 가장 정중한 말투로 하지도 않았다. 다만 나는 그가 나를 함부로 대했고, 내가 친구들에게서 멀리 떨어져 있어 나 자신에게 아무런 정당함도 베풀 수 없으며, 그가 까닭 없이 시샘을 부린다고 우겨 댔으니, 내 행실에는 나무랄 데가 없어 그가 그럴 구실이 전혀 없었으며, 잉글랜드로 옮겨 가면 그에게서 그럴 빌미를 모조리 없애 줄 것이라고 했다."]], [["I insisted so peremptorily upon it, that he could not avoid coming to a point, either to keep his word with me or to break it; and this, notwithstanding he used all the skill he was master of, and employed his mother and other agents to prevail with me to alter my resolutions; indeed, the bottom of the thing lay at my heart, and that made all his endeavours fruitless, for my heart was alienated from him as a husband.", "나는 너무도 단호하게 그것을 우겨 댔으므로, 그는 자기 말을 지키든 어기든 둘 중 하나로 결판을 내지 않을 수 없게 되었다. 그리고 이것은, 그가 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨를 다 부리고, 자기 어머니며 그 밖의 사람들을 동원하여 내 결심을 돌리도록 나를 설득하려 했음에도 그러했다."], ["I loathed the thoughts of bedding with him, and used a thousand pretenses of illness and humour to prevent his touching me, fearing nothing more than to be with child by him, which to be sure would have prevented, or at least delayed, my going over to England.", "사실 그 일의 근원은 내 마음속에 있었고, 바로 그것이 그의 모든 노력을 헛되게 만들었으니, 내 마음은 남편으로서의 그에게서 떠나 있었기 때문이다. 나는 그와 한자리에 눕는다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 났고, 그가 나를 건드리지 못하게 하려고 병이며 기분이며 갖가지 핑계를 천 가지나 둘러댔으니, 무엇보다 그의 씨를 또 배는 것이 두려웠던 까닭이며, 그렇게 되면 내가 잉글랜드로 건너가는 것을 분명 가로막거나 적어도 늦출 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["However, at last I put him so out of humour, that he took up a rash and fatal resolution; in short, I should not go to England; and though he had promised me, yet it was an unreasonable thing for me to desire it; that it would be ruinous to his affairs, would unhinge his whole family, and be next to an undoing him in the world; that therefore I ought not to desire it of him, and that no wife in the world that valued her family and her husband’s prosperity would insist upon such a thing.", "그러나 마침내 나는 그의 비위를 어찌나 거슬렀던지, 그는 무모하고도 운명적인 결심을 품게 되었다. 요컨대 나를 잉글랜드에 보내지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그리고 비록 그가 내게 약속하기는 했으나, 내가 그것을 바라는 것은 당치 않은 일이고, 그것은 자기 사업을 망치고 온 집안을 뒤흔들어, 거의 자기를 세상에서 파멸시키는 짓이 될 터이니, 따라서 나는 그것을 그에게 요구해서는 안 되며, 제 집안과 남편의 번영을 소중히 여기는 아내라면 세상에 그 누구도 그런 일을 우겨 대지 않으리라고 했다."]], [["This plunged me again, for when I considered the thing calmly, and took my husband as he really was, a diligent, careful man in the main work of laying up an estate for his children, and that he knew nothing of the dreadful circumstances that he was in, I could not but confess to myself that my proposal was very unreasonable, and what no wife that had the good of her family at heart would have desired.", "이 말이 나를 다시 곤경에 빠뜨렸으니, 내가 그 일을 차분히 헤아려 보고, 내 남편을 정말 있는 그대로—제 자식들을 위해 재산을 쌓는 본업에서 부지런하고 신중한 사람으로—여겨 보고, 또 그가 자기가 처한 그 끔찍한 정황에 대해 아무것도 모른다는 것을 생각하니, 나는 내 제안이 매우 당치 않은 것이며, 제 집안의 안녕을 진심으로 마음에 둔 아내라면 결코 바라지 않았을 일임을 스스로 인정하지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["But my discontents were of another nature; I looked upon him no longer as a husband, but as a near relation, the son of my own mother, and I resolved somehow or other to be clear of him, but which way I did not know, nor did it seem possible.", "그러나 내 불만은 다른 성질의 것이었다. 나는 더는 그를 남편으로 여기지 않았고, 가까운 친척으로, 내 친어머니의 아들로 여겼으며, 어떻게든 그에게서 벗어나기로 결심했으나, 어떤 방도로 그렇게 할지는 알 수 없었고, 그것이 가능할 것 같지도 않았다."]], [["It is said by the ill-natured world, of our sex, that if we are set on a thing, it is impossible to turn us from our resolutions; in short, I never ceased poring upon the means to bring to pass my voyage, and came that length with my husband at last, as to propose going without him.", "심술궂은 세상은 우리 여자들을 두고, 우리가 어떤 일에 마음을 정하면 그 결심에서 돌이키게 하기란 불가능하다고들 말한다. 요컨대 나는 내 항해를 성사시킬 방도를 곰곰이 궁리하기를 결코 멈추지 않았고, 마침내 남편에게 그 없이 혼자 가겠다는 제안을 하는 데까지 이르렀다."], ["This provoked him to the last degree, and he called me not only an unkind wife, but an unnatural mother, and asked me how I could entertain such a thought without horror, as that of leaving my two children (for one was dead) without a mother, and to be brought up by strangers, and never to see them more.", "이것이 그를 더없이 화나게 했고, 그는 나를 매정한 아내일 뿐 아니라 천륜을 저버린 어미라고 불렀으며, 어떻게 내가 두 아이를(하나는 죽었으므로) 어미 없이 남겨 두어 낯선 사람들 손에 길러지게 하고 두 번 다시 보지 않으려는 그런 생각을 끔찍해하지도 않고 품을 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["It was true, had things been right, I should not have done it, but now it was my real desire never to see them, or him either, any more; and as to the charge of unnatural, I could easily answer it to myself, while I knew that the whole relation was unnatural in the highest degree in the world.", "만약 사정이 제대로 된 것이었다면 나도 그렇게 하지 않았으리라는 것은 사실이지만, 이제 그도 그 아이들도 두 번 다시 보지 않는 것이 내 진정한 바람이었다. 그리고 천륜을 저버렸다는 비난에 관해서는, 나는 그 모든 관계가 세상에서 가장 극심하게 천륜을 거스르는 것임을 아는 한, 나 자신에게 손쉽게 대답할 수 있었다."]], [["However, it was plain there was no bringing my husband to anything; he would neither go with me nor let me go without him, and it was quite out of my power to stir without his consent, as any one that knows the constitution of the country I was in, knows very well.", "그러나 내 남편을 어떻게도 움직일 수 없다는 것만은 분명했다. 그는 나와 함께 가지도 않으려 했고, 나 혼자 가게 두지도 않으려 했으며, 내가 있던 그 고장의 사정을 아는 사람이라면 누구나 잘 알듯이, 그의 동의 없이 움직인다는 것은 전적으로 내 힘 밖의 일이었다."]], [["We had many family quarrels about it, and they began in time to grow up to a dangerous height; for as I was quite estranged from my husband (as he was called) in affection, so I took no heed to my words, but sometimes gave him language that was provoking; and, in short, strove all I could to bring him to a parting with me, which was what above all things in the world I desired most.", "우리는 그 일로 집안싸움을 숱하게 벌였고, 그 싸움은 차츰 위험한 지경에까지 자라나기 시작했다. 내가 (그렇게 불리던) 남편에게 애정으로는 완전히 멀어져 있었던 까닭에, 나는 내 말을 조심하지 않고 때때로 그에게 부아를 돋우는 말을 했으며, 요컨대 그가 나와 갈라서게 하려고 할 수 있는 온갖 짓을 다 했으니, 그것이야말로 세상의 그 무엇보다도 내가 가장 바라던 일이었다."]], [["He took my carriage very ill, and indeed he might well do so, for at last I refused to bed with him, and carrying on the breach upon all occasions to extremity, he told me once he thought I was mad, and if I did not alter my conduct, he would put me under cure; that is to say, into a madhouse.", "그는 내 처사를 무척 언짢게 여겼는데, 사실 그럴 만도 했으니, 끝내 나는 그와 한자리에 눕기를 거부했고, 사사건건 그 균열을 극단으로 몰고 갔던 까닭이다. 그는 한번은 내가 미친 것 같다고 하면서, 내가 행실을 고치지 않으면 나를 요양 보내겠다고 했으니, 다시 말해 정신병원에 넣겠다는 것이었다."], ["I told him he should find I was far enough from mad, and that it was not in his power, or any other villain’s, to murder me.", "나는 그에게, 내가 미친 것과는 거리가 멀다는 것을 알게 될 것이며, 나를 죽이는 것은 그의 힘으로도, 그 어떤 악당의 힘으로도 할 수 없는 일이라고 말했다."], ["I confess at the same time I was heartily frighted at his thoughts of putting me into a madhouse, which would at once have destroyed all the possibility of breaking the truth out, whatever the occasion might be; for that then no one would have given credit to a word of it.", "동시에 고백하건대, 나는 그가 나를 정신병원에 넣을 생각을 한다는 데에 진심으로 겁이 났으니, 그렇게 되면 어떤 계기가 생기든 간에 진실을 터뜨릴 모든 가능성이 단번에 무너질 터였기 때문이다. 그렇게 되면 그 누구도 그 말을 한마디도 곧이듣지 않을 것이었다."]], [["This therefore brought me to a resolution, whatever came of it, to lay open my whole case; but which way to do it, or to whom, was an inextricable difficulty, and took me many months to resolve.", "그리하여 이 일은, 무슨 결과가 닥치든 간에, 내 사정을 모조리 털어놓기로 결심하게 만들었다. 그러나 어떤 방도로 그것을 할지, 또 누구에게 할지는 풀 수 없는 난제였고, 결심하기까지 여러 달이 걸렸다."], ["In the meantime, another quarrel with my husband happened, which came up to such a mad extreme as almost pushed me on to tell it him all to his face; but though I kept it in so as not to come to the particulars, I spoke so much as put him into the utmost confusion, and in the end brought out the whole story.", "그러는 사이 남편과 또 한 차례 다툼이 일어났는데, 그것이 어찌나 미친 듯한 극단으로 치달았던지, 나는 거의 그 모든 것을 그의 면전에 대고 다 말해 버릴 뻔했다. 그러나 비록 세세한 데까지는 가지 않도록 억눌렀지만, 나는 그를 더없는 혼란에 빠뜨릴 만큼은 말해 버렸고, 끝내는 모든 이야기를 끄집어내고 말았다."]], [["He began with a calm expostulation upon my being so resolute to go to England; I defended it, and one hard word bringing on another, as is usual in all family strife, he told me I did not treat him as if he was my husband, or talk of my children as if I was a mother; and, in short, that I did not deserve to be used as a wife; that he had used all the fair means possible with me; that he had argued with all the kindness and calmness that a husband or a Christian ought to do, and that I made him such a vile return, that I treated him rather like a dog than a man, and rather like the most contemptible stranger than a husband; that he was very loth to use violence with me, but that, in short, he saw a necessity of it now, and that for the future he should be obliged to take such measures as should reduce me to my duty.", "그는 내가 잉글랜드로 가려고 그토록 단호한 데 대해 차분히 따지는 것으로 말문을 열었다. 나는 그것을 변호했고, 모든 집안 다툼에서 으레 그렇듯이 거친 말 한마디가 또 다른 거친 말을 불러왔는데, 그는 나더러 자기를 남편 대하듯 대하지 않고, 자기 자식들을 두고 어미인 양 말하지도 않는다고 했다. 요컨대 나는 아내로서 대접받을 자격이 없으며, 자기는 나에게 가능한 온갖 좋은 방도를 다 써 보았고, 남편으로서 또 기독교인으로서 마땅히 그래야 할 만큼 온갖 다정함과 차분함으로 나를 타일렀건만, 나는 그에게 그토록 비열한 보답을 하여, 그를 사람이라기보다 개처럼, 남편이라기보다 가장 멸시받을 낯선 자처럼 대했다고 했다. 그리고 자기는 나에게 폭력을 쓰기가 무척 꺼려지지만, 요컨대 이제는 그럴 필요를 느끼며, 앞으로는 나를 본분으로 되돌릴 만한 조치를 취하지 않을 수 없게 되었다고 했다."]], [["My blood was now fired to the utmost, though I knew what he had said was very true, and nothing could appear more provoked.", "이제 내 피는 극도로 끓어올랐으니, 비록 그가 한 말이 모두 옳다는 것을 알았어도, 그보다 더 격분한 모습은 없을 듯했다. 나는 그에게, 그의 좋은 방도든 고약한 방도든 나는 똑같이 경멸한다고 말했다."], ["I told him, for his fair means and his foul, they were equally contemned by me; that for my going to England, I was resolved on it, come what would; and that as to treating him not like a husband, and not showing myself a mother to my children, there might be something more in it than he understood at present; but, for his further consideration, I thought fit to tell him thus much, that he neither was my lawful husband, nor they lawful children, and that I had reason to regard neither of them more than I did.", "잉글랜드로 가는 일에 관해서는, 무슨 일이 닥치든 나는 그렇게 하기로 결심했고, 또 그를 남편 대하듯 하지 않고 자식들에게 어미 노릇을 보이지 않는 것에 관해서는, 거기에 그가 지금으로서는 헤아리지 못하는 무언가가 더 있을지도 모른다고 했다. 그러나 그가 더 깊이 생각해 보도록, 나는 이만큼은 말해 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여겼으니, 곧 그는 내 적법한 남편이 아니며 저들도 적법한 자식들이 아니고, 따라서 나는 그 둘 가운데 어느 쪽도 이제껏 여겨 온 것보다 더 위하지 않을 까닭이 있다고 했다."]], [["I confess I was moved to pity him when I spoke it, for he turned pale as death, and stood mute as one thunderstruck, and once or twice I thought he would have fainted; in short, it put him in a fit something like an apoplex; he trembled, a sweat or dew ran off his face, and yet he was cold as a clod, so that I was forced to run and fetch something for him to keep life in him.", "고백하건대 나는 그 말을 하면서 그가 가엾어 마음이 움직였으니, 그가 죽은 사람처럼 핼쑥해져서 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 말없이 서 있었기 때문이며, 한두 번은 그가 그대로 까무러칠 것만 같았다. 요컨대 그것은 그를 거의 졸중 같은 발작에 빠뜨렸으니, 그는 부들부들 떨었고, 얼굴에서는 땀인지 이슬인지가 흘러내렸으나, 그러면서도 그는 흙덩이처럼 차가웠다."], ["When he recovered of that, he grew sick and vomited, and in a little after was put to bed, and the next morning was, as he had been indeed all night, in a violent fever.", "그리하여 나는 달려가서 그의 목숨을 부지할 만한 무언가를 가져와야만 했다. 그가 거기서 회복되자 메스꺼워하며 토했고, 조금 뒤에 잠자리에 뉘어졌으며, 이튿날 아침에는, 실은 밤새 그러했듯이, 격렬한 열에 들떠 있었다."]], [["However, it went off again, and he recovered, though but slowly, and when he came to be a little better, he told me I had given him a mortal wound with my tongue, and he had only one thing to ask before he desired an explanation.", "그러나 그것은 다시 가라앉았고 그는 회복되었으나, 그저 더디게 나아갔을 뿐이다. 그리고 그가 조금 차도가 있게 되자, 그는 내가 내 혀로 자기에게 치명적인 상처를 입혔다고 말하며, 해명을 요구하기에 앞서 한 가지만 묻고 싶다고 했다."], ["I interrupted him, and told him I was sorry I had gone so far, since I saw what disorder it put him into, but I desired him not to talk to me of explanations, for that would but make things worse.", "나는 그의 말을 가로막고, 내 말이 그를 그토록 어지럽게 만든 것을 보니 내가 그렇게까지 멀리 나간 것이 유감이라고 말하면서, 다만 내게 해명 이야기는 하지 말아 달라고 했으니, 그래 봐야 일을 더 나쁘게 만들 뿐이었기 때문이다."]], [["This heightened his impatience, and, indeed, perplexed him beyond all bearing; for now he began to suspect that there was some mystery yet unfolded, but could not make the least guess at the real particulars of it; all that ran in his brain was, that I had another husband alive, which I could not say in fact might not be true, but I assured him, however, there was not the least of that in it; and indeed, as to my other husband, he was effectually dead in law to me, and had told me I should look on him as such, so I had not the least uneasiness on that score.", "이 말이 그의 조바심을 한층 돋우었고, 사실 그를 견딜 수 없을 만큼 어지럽혀 놓았다. 이제 그는 아직 밝혀지지 않은 어떤 비밀이 있다고 의심하기 시작했으나, 그 실제 정황에 대해서는 조금도 짐작할 수가 없었다. 그의 머릿속을 온통 맴도는 것은, 내게 다른 남편이 살아 있다는 것뿐이었는데, 사실 그것이 사실이 아닐 수는 없다고 나는 말할 수 없었으나, 어쨌든 나는 그에게 거기에는 그런 것이 조금도 없다고 단언했다. 그리고 사실 내 다른 남편으로 말하자면, 그는 법적으로 내게 사실상 죽은 사람이었고, 나더러 자기를 그런 사람으로 여기라고 그가 말해 두었던 터라, 나는 그 점에 대해서는 조금도 거리낌이 없었다."]], [["But now I found the thing too far gone to conceal it much longer, and my husband himself gave me an opportunity to ease myself of the secret, much to my satisfaction.", "그러나 이제 나는 그 일이 더 오래 감추기에는 너무 멀리 나갔다는 것을 알았고, 내 남편 스스로 그 비밀을 털어놓을 기회를 내게 마련해 주었으니, 나로서는 무척 흡족한 일이었다."], ["He had laboured with me three or four weeks, but to no purpose, only to tell him whether I had spoken these words only as the effect of my passion, to put him in a passion, or whether there was anything of truth in the bottom of them.", "그는 서너 주 동안 나를 다그쳤으나 아무 소용이 없었고, 다만 내가 그 말들을 그저 내 격정의 발로로, 그를 격분시키려고 한 것인지, 아니면 그 밑바닥에 무언가 진실이 있는지를 말해 달라고 했을 뿐이다. 그러나 나는 한결같이 완강했고, 그가 먼저 내가 잉글랜드로 가는 데 동의하지 않는 한 아무것도 해명하지 않으려 했는데, 그는 자기가 살아 있는 한 결코 그러지 않겠다고 했다."], ["But I continued inflexible, and would explain nothing, unless he would first consent to my going to England, which he would never do, he said, while he lived; on the other hand, I said it was in my power to make him willing when I pleased—nay, to make him entreat me to go; and this increased his curiosity, and made him importunate to the highest degree, but it was all to no purpose.", "다른 한편으로 나는, 내가 원할 때면 그를 기꺼이 응하게 만들—아니, 그가 나더러 가 달라고 애원하게 만들—힘이 내게 있다고 말했다. 그리고 이것이 그의 호기심을 키워, 그를 더없이 끈질기게 졸라 대게 만들었으나, 그것은 모두 아무 소용이 없었다."]], [["At length he tells all this story to his mother, and sets her upon me to get the main secret out of me, and she used her utmost skill with me indeed; but I put her to a full stop at once by telling her that the reason and mystery of the whole matter lay in herself, and that it was my respect to her that had made me conceal it; and that, in short, I could go no farther, and therefore conjured her not to insist upon it.", "마침내 그는 이 모든 이야기를 자기 어머니에게 털어놓고, 나에게서 그 핵심 비밀을 캐내라고 어머니를 부추겼으며, 그분은 정말로 내게 자기가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨를 다 썼다. 그러나 나는, 이 일 전체의 까닭과 비밀이 바로 그분 자신에게 있으며, 그분을 위하는 마음 때문에 내가 그것을 감추어 왔노라고 말함으로써 그분을 단번에 딱 멈춰 세웠다. 그러고서 요컨대 나는 더는 나아갈 수 없으니, 그러므로 그것을 우겨 대지 말아 달라고 그분께 간곡히 청했다."]], [["She was struck dumb at this suggestion, and could not tell what to say or to think; but, laying aside the supposition as a policy of mine, continued her importunity on account of her son, and, if possible, to make up the breach between us two.", "그분은 이 암시에 말문이 막혀, 무어라 말해야 할지 무어라 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 그러나 그분은 그 짐작을 내 술책쯤으로 제쳐 두고, 자기 아들을 위하여, 또 할 수만 있다면 우리 둘 사이의 균열을 메우려고 계속 졸라 댔다."], ["As to that, I told her that it was indeed a good design in her, but that it was impossible to be done; and that if I should reveal to her the truth of what she desired, she would grant it to be impossible, and cease to desire it.", "그것에 관해서, 나는 그것이 그분으로서는 참으로 좋은 뜻이지만 이루어지기란 불가능한 일이며, 만약 그분이 바라는 그 진실을 내가 드러낸다면, 그분도 그것이 불가능함을 인정하고 더는 바라지 않게 되리라고 말했다."], ["At last I seemed to be prevailed on by her importunity, and told her I dared trust her with a secret of the greatest importance, and she would soon see that this was so, and that I would consent to lodge it in her breast, if she would engage solemnly not to acquaint her son with it without my consent.", "마침내 나는 그분의 끈질긴 청에 못 이긴 척하며, 더없이 중대한 비밀 하나를 그분께 감히 맡겨 보겠고, 그분도 곧 그것이 그러함을 알게 될 것이며, 만약 그분이 내 동의 없이는 그것을 아들에게 알리지 않겠다고 엄숙하게 약속해 준다면, 그것을 그분의 가슴속에 묻어 두는 데 동의하겠다고 말했다."]], [["She was long in promising this part, but rather than not come at the main secret, she agreed to that too, and after a great many other preliminaries, I began, and told her the whole story.", "그분은 그 대목을 약속하기까지 오래 머뭇거렸으나, 그 핵심 비밀에 다가가지 못하느니 차라리 그것에도 응하기로 했다. 그리고 그 밖에 숱한 예비적인 말들을 늘어놓은 끝에, 나는 입을 열어 모든 이야기를 그분께 들려주었다."], ["First I told her how much she was concerned in all the unhappy breach which had happened between her son and me, by telling me her own story and her London name; and that the surprise she saw I was in was upon that occasion.", "먼저 나는, 그분이 자기 자신의 이야기와 런던에서 쓰던 이름을 내게 들려준 것으로 인해, 자기 아들과 나 사이에 일어난 그 모든 불행한 균열에 그분이 얼마나 깊이 얽혀 있는지를 말했으며, 그분이 본 그 내 놀라움이 바로 그 때문이었다고 했다."], ["Then I told her my own story, and my name, and assured her, by such other tokens as she could not deny, that I was no other, nor more or less, than her own child, her daughter, born of her body in Newgate; the same that had saved her from the gallows by being in her belly, and the same that she left in such-and-such hands when she was transported.", "그러고서 나는 내 자신의 이야기와 내 이름을 말했고, 그분이 부인할 수 없는 그 밖의 여러 표시로써, 내가 다름 아니라 바로 그분 자신의 아이, 그분의 딸, 뉴게이트에서 그분의 몸을 빌려 태어난 바로 그 아이이며, 뱃속에 있음으로써 그분을 교수대에서 구해 낸 바로 그 아이, 그분이 유배될 때 이러저러한 사람들 손에 남겨 두고 떠난 바로 그 아이임을 단언했다."]], [["It is impossible to express the astonishment she was in; she was not inclined to believe the story, or to remember the particulars, for she immediately foresaw the confusion that must follow in the family upon it.", "그분이 빠진 그 경악을 표현하기란 불가능하다. 그분은 그 이야기를 믿으려 들지도, 그 세세한 정황을 떠올리려 들지도 않았으니, 그로 인해 집안에 닥칠 혼란을 곧바로 내다보았기 때문이다."], ["But everything concurred so exactly with the stories she had told me of herself, and which, if she had not told me, she would perhaps have been content to have denied, that she had stopped her own mouth, and she had nothing to do but to take me about the neck and kiss me, and cry most vehemently over me, without speaking one word for a long time together.", "그러나 모든 것이 그분이 자기 자신에 관해 내게 들려준 이야기들과 어찌나 정확히 들어맞던지—만약 그분이 그것을 내게 들려주지 않았더라면 아마 기꺼이 부인했을 그 이야기들과 말이다—그분은 제 입으로 제 입을 막아 버린 셈이 되어, 이제 그분이 할 수 있는 일이라고는 내 목을 끌어안고 입을 맞추며, 한참 동안 한마디도 못 한 채 나를 끌어안고 더없이 격렬하게 우는 것뿐이었다. 마침내 그분이 터뜨렸다."], ["At last she broke out: “Unhappy child!”", "\"불행한 아이로구나!\" 하고 그분이 말했다."], ["says she, “what miserable chance could bring thee hither?", "\"무슨 비참한 운명이 너를 이리로 데려왔단 말이냐?"], ["and in the arms of my own son, too!", "그것도 내 친아들의 품으로! 끔찍한 계집아이야,\""], ["Dreadful girl,”", "하고 그분이 말했다."], ["says she, “why, we are all undone!", "\"아니, 우리는 모두 끝장이로구나!"], ["Married to thy own brother!", "네 친오라비와 결혼하다니!"], ["Three children, and two alive, all of the same flesh and blood!", "아이가 셋이고, 둘이 살아 있고, 모두 같은 살붙이라니!"], ["My son and my daughter lying together as husband and wife!", "내 아들과 내 딸이 부부로 한자리에 눕다니!"], ["All confusion and distraction for ever!", "영영 온통 혼란과 광란뿐이로구나!"], ["Miserable family!", "비참한 집안이로다!"], ["what will become of us?", "우리는 어찌 될 것인가?"], ["What is to be said?", "무슨 말을 해야 하나?"], ["What is to be done?”", "무엇을 해야 하나?\""], ["And thus she ran on for a great while; nor had I any power to speak, or if I had, did I know what to say, for every word wounded me to the soul.", "그러고서 그분은 한참 동안 이렇게 쏟아 냈다. 나로서는 입을 뗄 기력도 없었고, 설령 있었다 한들 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 한마디 한마디가 내 영혼을 후벼 팠기 때문이다."], ["With this kind of amazement on our thoughts we parted for the first time, though my mother was more surprised than I was, because it was more news to her than to me.", "이런 종류의 경악을 마음에 안은 채, 우리는 처음으로 헤어졌으나, 내 어머니가 나보다 더 놀랐으니, 그것이 나에게보다 그분에게 더 새로운 소식이었던 까닭이다."], ["However, she promised again to me at parting, that she would say nothing of it to her son, till we had talked of it again.", "그러나 헤어지면서 그분은 내게 다시 약속하기를, 우리가 그것을 다시 의논하기 전까지는 아들에게 그것에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않겠다고 했다."]], [["It was not long, you may be sure, before we had a second conference upon the same subject; when, as if she had been willing to forget the story she had told me of herself, or to suppose that I had forgot some of the particulars, she began to tell them with alterations and omissions; but I refreshed her memory and set her to rights in many things which I supposed she had forgot, and then came in so opportunely with the whole history, that it was impossible for her to go from it; and then she fell into her rhapsodies again, and exclamations at the severity of her misfortunes. When these things were a little over with her, we fell into a close debate about what should be first done before we gave an account of the matter to my husband. But to what purpose could be all our consultations? We could neither of us see our way through it, nor see how it could be safe to open such a scene to him. It was impossible to make any judgment, or give any guess at what temper he would receive it in, or what measures he would take upon it; and if he should have so little government of himself as to make it public, we easily foresaw that it would be the ruin of the whole family, and expose my mother and me to the last degree; and if at last he should take the advantage the law would give him, he might put me away with disdain and leave me to sue for the little portion that I had, and perhaps waste it all in the suit, and then be a beggar; the children would be ruined too, having no legal claim to any of his effects; and thus I should see him, perhaps, in the arms of another wife in a few months, and be myself the most miserable creature alive.", "여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 우리가 같은 화제로 두 번째 회담을 갖기까지는 오래 걸리지 않았다. 그때 그분은, 마치 자기가 내게 들려준 자기 자신의 이야기를 잊고 싶기라도 한 듯, 또는 내가 그 세세한 정황 가운데 일부를 잊었으리라 짐작하기라도 한 듯, 그것을 군데군데 바꾸고 빼면서 들려주기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 그분의 기억을 일깨우고, 그분이 잊었으리라 짐작되는 여러 가지를 바로잡아 주었으며, 그러고는 그 모든 이야기를 어찌나 때맞춰 들이밀었던지, 그분으로서는 도저히 거기서 벗어날 수가 없었다. 그러자 그분은 다시 격정에 휩싸여, 자기 불운의 가혹함을 두고 탄식을 쏟아 냈다. 이런 일들이 그분에게서 조금 가라앉았을 때, 우리는 그 일을 내 남편에게 알리기에 앞서 먼저 무엇을 해야 할지를 두고 빈틈없는 논의에 들어갔다. 그러나 우리의 모든 의논이 무슨 소용이 있었겠는가? 우리 둘 다 그 일을 헤쳐 나갈 길도 보이지 않았고, 그에게 그런 장면을 펼쳐 보이는 것이 어떻게 안전할 수 있을지도 알 수 없었다. 그가 그것을 어떤 마음가짐으로 받아들일지, 또 그 일로 어떤 조치를 취할지 판단하거나 짐작하기란 불가능했다. 그리고 만약 그가 자제력이 너무도 모자라 그것을 공공연히 드러낸다면, 그것이 온 집안의 파멸이 될 것이며, 어머니와 나를 더없는 지경으로 망신시키리라는 것을 우리는 쉽게 내다보았다. 또 만약 그가 끝내 법이 그에게 줄 이점을 취한다면, 그는 나를 멸시하며 내쫓고 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 재산을 받으려고 소송이나 하게 내버려 둘 수 있을 터인데, 어쩌면 그것을 소송에 몽땅 탕진하고는 거지 신세가 될지도 모를 일이었다. 아이들도 그의 재산에 대해 아무런 법적 권리가 없으니 마찬가지로 파멸할 것이었다. 그리하여 나는 어쩌면 몇 달 안에 그가 다른 아내의 품에 안긴 것을 보게 되고, 나 자신은 살아 있는 가장 비참한 존재가 될 것이었다."]], [["My mother was as sensible of this as I; and, upon the whole, we knew not what to do.", "내 어머니도 나만큼이나 이것을 절감하고 있었으며, 통틀어 우리는 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 얼마쯤 지나서 우리는 좀 더 차분한 결론에 이르렀으나, 그때는 또 이런 불행이 따랐으니, 곧 내 어머니의 의견과 내 의견이 서로 전혀 달랐고, 사실 서로 양립할 수 없었다는 것이다."], ["After some time we came to more sober resolutions, but then it was with this misfortune too, that my mother’s opinion and mine were quite different from one another, and indeed inconsistent with one another; for my mother’s opinion was, that I should bury the whole thing entirely, and continue to live with him as my husband till some other event should make the discovery of it more convenient; and that in the meantime she would endeavour to reconcile us together again, and restore our mutual comfort and family peace; that we might lie as we used to do together, and so let the whole matter remain a secret as close as death.", "내 어머니의 의견은, 내가 이 모든 일을 통째로 묻어 두고, 어떤 다른 사건이 그 폭로를 더 알맞게 만들어 줄 때까지 그를 내 남편으로 삼아 계속 함께 살아야 한다는 것이었다. 그리고 그러는 동안 그분이 우리 둘을 다시 화해시켜 우리의 서로에 대한 위안과 집안의 평화를 되찾도록 애써 보겠으며, 우리가 예전처럼 한자리에 누워 그 모든 일이 죽음처럼 단단히 비밀로 남도록 하자는 것이었다."], ["“For, child,”", "\"왜냐하면, 얘야,\""], ["says she, “we are both undone if it comes out.”", "하고 그분이 말했다. \"그것이 새어 나가면 우리는 둘 다 끝장이니까.\""]], [["To encourage me to this, she promised to make me easy in my circumstances, as far as she was able, and to leave me what she could at her death, secured for me separately from my husband; so that if it should come out afterwards, I should not be left destitute, but be able to stand on my own feet and procure justice from him.", "나를 이 쪽으로 부추기려고, 그분은 자기 힘이 닿는 한 내 형편을 편안하게 해 주고, 자기가 죽을 때 내 남편과는 따로 떼어 내게 확보해 둘 수 있는 것은 무엇이든 남겨 주겠다고 약속했다. 그리하여 나중에 그것이 새어 나오더라도 내가 빈털터리로 남겨지지 않고, 내 두 발로 설 수 있으며 그에게서 정당함을 얻어 낼 수 있게 하겠다는 것이었다."]], [["This proposal did not agree at all with my judgment of the thing, though it was very fair and kind in my mother; but my thoughts ran quite another way.", "이 제안은 비록 내 어머니로서는 무척 공정하고 다정한 것이었으나, 그 일에 대한 내 판단과는 도무지 맞지 않았다. 내 생각은 전혀 다른 쪽으로 흐르고 있었다."]], [["As to keeping the thing in our own breasts, and letting it all remain as it was, I told her it was impossible; and I asked her how she could think I could bear the thoughts of lying with my own brother.", "그 일을 우리 둘의 가슴속에만 간직하고 모든 것을 있는 그대로 내버려 두는 것에 관해서는, 나는 그것이 불가능하다고 그분께 말했다. 그리고 나는 그분께, 내 친오라비와 한자리에 눕는다는 생각을 내가 어찌 견딜 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["In the next place, I told her that her being alive was the only support of the discovery, and that while she owned me for her child, and saw reason to be satisfied that I was so, nobody else would doubt it; but that if she should die before the discovery, I should be taken for an impudent creature that had forged such a thing to go away from my husband, or should be counted crazed and distracted.", "다음으로 나는, 그분이 살아 계신 것만이 이 폭로의 유일한 버팀목이며, 그분이 나를 자기 자식으로 인정하고 내가 그러함을 흡족히 여길 만한 까닭을 보는 한 다른 누구도 그것을 의심하지 않겠지만, 만약 그분이 그 폭로 전에 돌아가신다면, 나는 남편에게서 벗어나려고 그런 일을 꾸며 낸 뻔뻔한 계집으로 여겨지거나, 정신이 나가고 실성한 사람으로 취급되리라고 그분께 말했다."], ["Then I told her how he had threatened already to put me into a madhouse, and what concern I had been in about it, and how that was the thing that drove me to the necessity of discovering it to her as I had done.", "그러고서 나는 그가 이미 나를 정신병원에 넣겠다고 위협했던 일, 또 그 일로 내가 얼마나 마음을 졸였던가, 그리고 바로 그것이 내가 한 대로 그분께 그것을 폭로할 수밖에 없도록 나를 내몬 까닭이었음을 그분께 말했다."]], [["From all which I told her, that I had, on the most serious reflections I was able to make in the case, come to this resolution, which I hoped she would like, as a medium between both, viz.", "이 모든 것으로부터 나는 그분께, 내가 이 일에 대해 할 수 있는 가장 진지한 숙고 끝에 이 결심에 이르렀으니, 그분이 양쪽의 절충안으로서 그것을 마음에 들어 하기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["that she should use her endeavours with her son to give me leave to go to England, as I had desired, and to furnish me with a sufficient sum of money, either in goods along with me, or in bills for my support there, all along suggesting that he might one time or other think it proper to come over to me.", "곧, 그분이 자기 아들에게 애써 권하여, 내가 바란 대로 잉글랜드로 가도록 나를 허락하게 하고, 함께 가져갈 물품으로든 거기서 나를 부양할 어음으로든 내게 넉넉한 돈을 마련해 주게 하되, 그가 언젠가 내게로 건너오는 것이 알맞다고 여길지도 모른다는 것을 줄곧 넌지시 비치게 한다는 것이었다."]], [["That when I was gone, she should then, in cold blood, and after first obliging him in the solemnest manner possible to secrecy, discover the case to him, doing it gradually, and as her own discretion should guide her, so that he might not be surprised with it, and fly out into any passions and excesses on my account, or on hers; and that she should concern herself to prevent his slighting the children, or marrying again, unless he had a certain account of my being dead.", "그리고 내가 떠난 뒤에, 그분이 그때 가서 냉정한 마음으로, 그리고 먼저 그에게 가능한 가장 엄숙한 방식으로 비밀을 지키겠다는 다짐을 받아 낸 다음, 그 일을 그에게 드러내되, 그가 그것에 놀라 나를 두고든 그분을 두고든 어떤 격정이나 무모한 짓에 빠져들지 않도록, 차근차근, 그리고 그분 자신의 분별이 이끄는 대로 하라고 했다. 또한 그분이 마음을 써서, 그가 내가 죽었다는 확실한 소식을 듣지 않는 한, 그가 아이들을 소홀히 하거나 다시 결혼하는 것을 막아 달라고 했다."]], [["This was my scheme, and my reasons were good; I was really alienated from him in the consequences of these things; indeed, I mortally hated him as a husband, and it was impossible to remove that riveted aversion I had to him. At the same time, it being an unlawful, incestuous living, added to that aversion, and though I had no great concern about it in point of conscience, yet everything added to make cohabiting with him the most nauseous thing to me in the world; and I think verily it was come to such a height, that I could almost as willingly have embraced a dog as have let him offer anything of that kind to me, for which reason I could not bear the thoughts of coming between the sheets with him. I cannot say that I was right in point of policy in carrying it such a length, while at the same time I did not resolve to discover the thing to him; but I am giving an account of what was, not of what ought or ought not to be.", "이것이 내 계책이었고, 내 이유들은 그럴듯했다. 나는 이 일들이 빚어낸 결과로 정말로 그에게서 마음이 떠나 있었으며, 사실 나는 남편으로서의 그를 죽도록 미워했고, 그에게 박힌 그 못 같은 혐오감을 떼어 내기란 불가능했다. 동시에 그것이 적법하지 못한 근친상간의 동거였다는 사실이 그 혐오감에 더해졌으니, 비록 내가 양심상으로는 그것에 대해 그리 크게 마음 쓰지 않았다 해도, 모든 것이 합쳐져 그와 함께 사는 것을 내게는 세상에서 가장 메스꺼운 일로 만들었다. 그리고 정말이지 나는, 그가 내게 그런 종류의 무언가를 청하게 두느니 차라리 거의 기꺼이 개를 끌어안는 편이 나을 지경에까지 이르렀다고 생각한다. 그런 까닭에 나는 그와 한 이불 속에 든다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다. 동시에 내가 그 일을 그에게 드러내기로 작정하지도 않은 채 그것을 그토록 멀리 끌고 간 것이, 처세상 옳았다고는 말할 수 없다. 그러나 나는 마땅히 어떠했어야 했는가, 어떠해서는 안 되었는가가 아니라, 무엇이 실제로 있었는가를 이야기하고 있는 것이다."]], [["In their directly opposite opinion to one another my mother and I continued a long time, and it was impossible to reconcile our judgments; many disputes we had about it, but we could never either of us yield our own, or bring over the other.", "서로 정반대인 의견 속에서 내 어머니와 나는 오랫동안 머물렀고, 우리의 판단을 일치시키기란 불가능했다. 우리는 그 일로 숱한 언쟁을 벌였으나, 우리 둘 중 어느 쪽도 자기 의견을 굽히지도, 상대를 자기 쪽으로 끌어오지도 결코 못했다."]], [["I insisted on my aversion to lying with my own brother, and she insisted upon its being impossible to bring him to consent to my going from him to England; and in this uncertainty we continued, not differing so as to quarrel, or anything like it, but so as not to be able to resolve what we should do to make up that terrible breach that was before us.", "나는 내 친오라비와 한자리에 눕는 것에 대한 혐오감을 우겨 댔고, 그분은 그가 나를 자기 곁에서 떠나 잉글랜드로 가도록 동의하게 만들기란 불가능하다는 것을 우겨 댔다. 그리고 이 불확실함 속에서 우리는, 다투거나 그 비슷한 무엇에 이를 만큼 갈라서지는 않았으나, 우리 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 균열을 메우려면 무엇을 해야 할지 결단을 내리지 못한 채로 머물러 있었다."]], [["At last I resolved on a desperate course, and told my mother my resolution, viz. that, in short, I would tell him of it myself.", "마침내 나는 한 가지 필사적인 방도를 정하고, 내 결심을 어머니에게 말했으니, 곧 요컨대 내가 그것을 그에게 직접 말하겠다는 것이었다."], ["My mother was frighted to the last degree at the very thoughts of it; but I bid her be easy, told her I would do it gradually and softly, and with all the art and good-humour I was mistress of, and time it also as well as I could, taking him in good-humour too.", "내 어머니는 그 생각만으로도 더없이 겁에 질렸다. 그러나 나는 그분을 안심시키며, 차근차근, 부드럽게, 또 내가 부릴 수 있는 온갖 솜씨와 좋은 기분으로 그렇게 할 것이며, 시기 또한 되도록 잘 골라 그를 좋은 기분일 때 붙들겠다고 말했다."], ["I told her I did not question but, if I could be hypocrite enough to feign more affection to him than I really had, I should succeed in all my design, and we might part by consent, and with a good agreement, for I might live him well enough for a brother, though I could not for a husband.", "나는, 만약 내가 실제 가진 것보다 더 많은 애정을 그에게 꾸며 보일 만큼 충분히 위선을 부릴 수만 있다면, 내 모든 계획에서 성공하여, 우리가 합의로, 또 원만한 약조로 갈라설 수 있으리라고 믿어 의심치 않는다고 했다. 비록 남편으로서는 못 하더라도, 오라비로서는 그를 그럭저럭 위할 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["All this while he lay at my mother to find out, if possible, what was the meaning of that dreadful expression of mine, as he called it, which I mentioned before: namely, that I was not his lawful wife, nor my children his legal children.", "이 내내 그는 자기 어머니에게 매달려, 앞서 내가 말한 그 끔찍한 표현—그가 그렇게 부른 대로—곧 내가 그의 적법한 아내가 아니며 내 아이들도 그의 적법한 자식들이 아니라는 그 말의 뜻이 가능하면 무엇인지 알아내려 했다."], ["My mother put him off, told him she could bring me to no explanations, but found there was something that disturbed me very much, and she hoped she should get it out of me in time, and in the meantime recommended to him earnestly to use me more tenderly, and win me with his usual good carriage; told him of his terrifying and affrighting me with his threats of sending me to a madhouse, and the like, and advised him not to make a woman desperate on any account whatever.", "내 어머니는 그를 따돌렸으니, 자기는 나에게서 어떤 해명도 끌어낼 수 없으나, 무언가 나를 몹시 괴롭히는 것이 있음을 알았으며, 머지않아 그것을 내게서 끌어내기를 바란다고 그에게 말했다. 그러면서 그러는 동안 그에게, 나를 더 다정하게 대하고 그 평소의 좋은 처신으로 나를 다독이라고 간곡히 권했으며, 그가 나를 정신병원에 보내겠다는 위협 따위로 나를 겁주고 떨게 한 일을 들어, 어떤 까닭으로든 여자를 절망에 빠뜨리지 말라고 충고했다."]], [["He promised her to soften his behaviour, and bid her assure me that he loved me as well as ever, and that he had no such design as that of sending me to a madhouse, whatever he might say in his passion; also he desired my mother to use the same persuasions to me too, that our affections might be renewed, and we might lie together in a good understanding as we used to do.", "그는 자기 행동을 누그러뜨리겠다고 그분께 약속했고, 자기가 여전히 나를 사랑하며, 격정에 못 이겨 무어라고 말했든 간에 나를 정신병원에 보낼 그런 생각은 없다는 것을 내게 확실히 전해 달라고 했다. 또 그는 내 어머니에게, 우리의 애정이 되살아나 우리가 예전처럼 좋은 양해 속에 한자리에 누울 수 있도록, 나에게도 똑같은 설득을 해 달라고 청했다."]], [["I found the effects of this treaty presently. My husband’s conduct was immediately altered, and he was quite another man to me; nothing could be kinder and more obliging than he was to me upon all occasions; and I could do no less than make some return to it, which I did as well as I could, but it was but in an awkward manner at best, for nothing was more frightful to me than his caresses, and the apprehensions of being with child again by him was ready to throw me into fits; and this made me see that there was an absolute necessity of breaking the case to him without any more delay, which, however, I did with all the caution and reserve imaginable.", "나는 곧바로 이 화해의 효과를 보았다. 내 남편의 처신은 즉시 달라졌고, 그는 내게 완전히 다른 사람이 되었다. 그가 사사건건 내게 보이는 태도보다 더 다정하고 더 정성스러울 수는 없었다. 나로서도 거기에 얼마간 보답하지 않을 수 없어 되도록 그렇게 했으나, 그것은 잘해야 어색한 모양새일 뿐이었으니, 그의 애무보다 더 끔찍한 것은 내게 없었고, 그의 씨를 또 밴다는 걱정이 나를 발작에 빠뜨릴 지경이었기 때문이다. 그리고 이것이 나로 하여금, 더는 미루지 말고 그 일을 그에게 터놓을 절대적인 필요가 있음을 알게 했으니, 그러나 나는 그것을 상상할 수 있는 온갖 신중함과 조심성으로 했다."]], [["He had continued his altered carriage to me near a month, and we began to live a new kind of life with one another; and could I have satisfied myself to have gone on with it, I believe it might have continued as long as we had continued alive together.", "그는 거의 한 달 가까이 나에게 달라진 처신을 이어 갔고, 우리는 서로 새로운 종류의 삶을 살기 시작했다. 만약 내가 그것을 계속해 나가도 좋다고 스스로 흡족해할 수만 있었다면, 그것은 우리가 함께 살아 있는 한 이어졌으리라고 나는 믿는다."], ["One evening, as we were sitting and talking very friendly together under a little awning, which served as an arbour at the entrance from our house into the garden, he was in a very pleasant, agreeable humour, and said abundance of kind things to me relating to the pleasure of our present good agreement, and the disorders of our past breach, and what a satisfaction it was to him that we had room to hope we should never have any more of it.", "어느 날 저녁, 우리 집에서 정원으로 나가는 입구에 정자 노릇을 하던 자그마한 차양 아래 함께 앉아 무척 다정하게 이야기를 나누고 있을 때, 그는 매우 즐겁고 흐뭇한 기분에 잠겨, 우리의 지금 이 좋은 화합의 기쁨과 지난 균열의 어지러움에 관해, 또 우리가 두 번 다시 그런 일을 겪지 않으리라고 바랄 여지가 있다는 것이 자기에게 얼마나 큰 흡족함인지에 관해, 다정한 말을 한껏 내게 늘어놓았다."]], [["I fetched a deep sigh, and told him there was nobody in the world could be more delighted than I was in the good agreement we had always kept up, or more afflicted with the breach of it, and should be so still; but I was sorry to tell him that there was an unhappy circumstance in our case, which lay too close to my heart, and which I knew not how to break to him, that rendered my part of it very miserable, and took from me all the comfort of the rest.", "나는 깊은 한숨을 내쉬고서, 우리가 늘 지켜 온 그 좋은 화합에서 나보다 더 기뻐할 사람도, 그 균열에 나보다 더 마음 아파할 사람도, 또 앞으로도 그러할 사람도 세상에 없다고 그에게 말했다. 다만 안타깝게도 우리 처지에는 한 가지 불행한 정황이 있어, 그것이 내 가슴 너무도 가까이 박혀 있고 그것을 어떻게 그에게 터놓아야 할지 알 수 없으며, 그것이 내 몫의 화합을 무척 비참하게 만들고 나머지 모든 위안마저 내게서 앗아 간다고 했다."]], [["He importuned me to tell him what it was.", "그는 그것이 무엇인지 말해 달라고 졸랐다."], ["I told him I could not tell how to do it; that while it was concealed from him I alone was unhappy, but if he knew it also, we should be both so; and that, therefore, to keep him in the dark about it was the kindest thing that I could do, and it was on that account alone that I kept a secret from him, the very keeping of which, I thought, would first or last be my destruction.", "나는 그것을 어떻게 말해야 할지 알 수 없다고 했으니, 그것이 그에게 감추어져 있는 동안에는 나 혼자만 불행하지만, 만약 그도 그것을 알게 되면 우리 둘 다 그렇게 될 터이며, 따라서 그것에 관해 그를 어둠 속에 두는 것이야말로 내가 할 수 있는 가장 다정한 일이라고 했다. 그리고 바로 그 까닭으로만 나는 그에게 비밀을 간직하는 것인데, 그 비밀을 간직하는 일 자체가 머지않아 내 파멸이 되리라고 나는 생각한다고 했다."]], [["It is impossible to express his surprise at this relation, and the double importunity which he used with me to discover it to him.", "이 이야기에 그가 빠진 놀라움과, 그것을 자기에게 털어놓으라며 그가 내게 부린 갑절의 채근을 표현하기란 불가능하다."], ["He told me I could not be called kind to him, nay, I could not be faithful to him if I concealed it from him.", "그는 만약 내가 그것을 자기에게 감춘다면, 나를 자기에게 다정하다고 할 수 없으며, 아니, 충실하다고도 할 수 없다고 했다."], ["I told him I thought so too, and yet I could not do it.", "나는 나도 그렇게 생각한다고, 그러면서도 그것을 할 수가 없다고 했다."], ["He went back to what I had said before to him, and told me he hoped it did not relate to what I had said in my passion, and that he had resolved to forget all that as the effect of a rash, provoked spirit.", "그는 앞서 내가 자기에게 한 말로 되돌아가, 그것이 내가 격정에 못 이겨 한 말과 관련된 것이 아니기를 바라며, 자기는 그 모든 것을 무모하고 격분한 마음의 소산으로 여겨 잊기로 작정했다고 했다."], ["I told him I wished I could forget it all too, but that it was not to be done, the impression was too deep, and I could not do it: it was impossible.", "나는 나도 그 모든 것을 잊을 수 있기를 바라지만, 그것은 될 일이 아니며, 그 흔적이 너무 깊이 박혀 도저히 그럴 수가 없으니, 불가능하다고 했다."]], [["He then told me he was resolved not to differ with me in anything, and that therefore he would importune me no more about it, resolving to acquiesce in whatever I did or said; only begged I should then agree, that whatever it was, it should no more interrupt our quiet and our mutual kindness.", "그러자 그는, 자기는 어떤 일로도 나와 다투지 않기로 작정했으며, 따라서 그 일로 더는 나를 채근하지 않고 내가 무엇을 하든 무슨 말을 하든 그대로 받아들이기로 했다고 하면서, 다만 그것이 무엇이든 간에 더는 우리의 평온과 우리 서로에 대한 다정함을 어지럽히지 않기로 그때 합의해 달라고만 청했다."]], [["This was the most provoking thing he could have said to me, for I really wanted his further importunities, that I might be prevailed with to bring out that which indeed it was like death to me to conceal; so I answered him plainly that I could not say I was glad not to be importuned, thought I could not tell how to comply.", "이것은 그가 내게 할 수 있는 말 가운데 가장 부아를 돋우는 것이었으니, 사실 나는 그의 더한 채근을 바라고 있었기 때문이다. 그래야 정말로 감추는 것이 내게 죽음과도 같던 그것을 끄집어내도록 못 이긴 척할 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["“But come, my dear,”", "그래서 나는 그에게 솔직하게, 채근받지 않게 되어 기쁘다고는 할 수 없으나, 그렇다고 어떻게 응해야 할지도 모르겠다고 대답했다. \"그런데 여보,\""], ["said I, “what conditions will you make with me upon the opening this affair to you?”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"이 일을 당신에게 터놓는다면, 당신은 나와 어떤 조건을 맺어 주겠어요?\""]], [["“Any conditions in the world,”", "\"세상의 그 어떤 조건이든,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that you can in reason desire of me.”", "\"당신이 이치에 맞게 내게 바랄 수 있는 것이라면 다 좋소.\" \"좋아요,\""], ["“Well,”", "하고 나는 말했다."], ["said I, “come, give it me under your hand, that if you do not find I am in any fault, or that I am willingly concerned in the causes of the misfortune that is to follow, you will not blame me, use me the worse, do me any injury, or make me be the sufferer for that which is not my fault.”", "\"그럼, 이렇게 당신 손으로 적어 주세요. 만약 당신이 내게 아무런 잘못이 없고, 내가 앞으로 닥칠 그 불행의 원인에 스스로 즐겨 가담한 것이 아니라는 것을 알게 되면, 나를 탓하지도, 나를 더 나쁘게 대하지도, 내게 어떤 해도 끼치지도, 내 잘못이 아닌 일로 나를 고통받게 하지도 않겠다고요.\""]], [["“That,”", "\"그것이야말로,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “is the most reasonable demand in the world: not to blame you for that which is not your fault.", "\"세상에서 가장 이치에 맞는 요구로구려. 당신 잘못이 아닌 일로 당신을 탓하지 않겠다니 말이오."], ["Give me a pen and ink,”", "펜과 잉크를 가져다주오.\""], ["says he; so I ran in and fetched a pen, ink, and paper, and he wrote the condition down in the very words I had proposed it, and signed it with his name.", "하고 그가 말했다. 그래서 나는 안으로 달려 들어가 펜과 잉크와 종이를 가져왔고, 그는 내가 제안한 바로 그 말 그대로 그 조건을 적은 다음, 자기 이름으로 서명했다."], ["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["says he, “what is next, my dear?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"다음은 무엇이오, 여보?\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"그게요,\""], ["says I, “the next is, that you will not blame me for not discovering the secret of it to you before I knew it.”", "하고 나는 말했다. \"다음은, 내가 그 비밀을 알기 전에 그것을 당신에게 드러내지 않았다고 해서 나를 탓하지 않겠다는 거예요.\""]], [["“Very just again,”", "\"그것도 지극히 옳소,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he; “with all my heart”; so he wrote down that also, and signed it.", "\"기꺼이 그러리다.\" 그러고서 그는 그것도 적고서 서명했다."]], [["“Well, my dear,” says I, “then I have but one condition more to make with you, and that is, that as there is nobody concerned in it but you and I, you shall not discover it to any person in the world, except your own mother; and that in all the measures you shall take upon the discovery, as I am equally concerned in it with you, though as innocent as yourself, you shall do nothing in a passion, nothing to my prejudice or to your mother’s prejudice, without my knowledge and consent.”", "\"좋아요, 여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"그럼 당신과 맺을 조건이 하나 더 있어요. 그건, 이 일에 관련된 사람은 당신과 나뿐이니, 당신 어머니 말고는 세상 어느 누구에게도 그것을 드러내지 않겠다는 거예요. 그리고 그 폭로로 인해 당신이 취할 모든 조치에서, 나도 당신과 똑같이 거기 얽혀 있되 당신만큼이나 결백한 만큼, 당신은 내가 알고 동의하지 않고서는 격정에 휩싸여 아무 일도 하지 않고, 나에게 해가 되는 일도, 당신 어머니에게 해가 되는 일도 아무것도 하지 않겠다는 거예요.\""]], [["This a little amazed him, and he wrote down the words distinctly, but read them over and over before he signed them, hesitating at them several times, and repeating them: “My mother’s prejudice!", "이 말이 그를 좀 놀라게 했고, 그는 그 말을 또박또박 적었으나, 서명하기 전에 그것을 몇 번이고 거듭 읽으며 여러 번 머뭇거렸고, 그것을 되뇌었다. \"내 어머니에게 해가 된다고!"], ["and your prejudice!", "또 당신에게 해가 된다고!"], ["What mysterious thing can this be?”", "이게 도대체 무슨 알 수 없는 일일까?\""], ["However, at last he signed it.", "그러나 마침내 그는 서명했다."]], [["“Well,”", "\"좋아요,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["says I, “my dear, I’ll ask you no more under your hand; but as you are to hear the most unexpected and surprising thing that perhaps ever befell any family in the world, I beg you to promise me you will receive it with composure and a presence of mind suitable to a man of sense.”", "\"여보, 당신 손으로 적어 달라고 더는 청하지 않을게요. 다만 당신은 아마 세상의 그 어떤 집안에 일찍이 닥쳤던 것 가운데 가장 뜻밖이고 가장 놀라운 일을 듣게 될 테니, 부디 그것을 침착하게, 또 분별 있는 사람에게 어울리는 마음가짐으로 받아들이겠다고 약속해 주세요.\""]], [["“I’ll do my utmost,”", "\"내 힘껏 그러리다.\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “upon condition you will keep me no longer in suspense, for you terrify me with all these preliminaries.”", "\"다만 더는 나를 애태우지 않는다는 조건으로 말이오. 이 모든 사전 절차로 당신은 나를 겁나게 하는구려.\""]], [["“Well, then,” says I, “it is this: as I told you before in a heat, that I was not your lawful wife, and that our children were not legal children, so I must let you know now in calmness and in kindness, but with affliction enough, that I am your own sister, and you my own brother, and that we are both the children of our mother now alive, and in the house, who is convinced of the truth of it, in a manner not to be denied or contradicted.”", "\"그럼, 좋아요,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"이거예요. 앞서 내가 격해서 당신에게, 내가 당신의 적법한 아내가 아니고 우리 아이들도 적법한 자식들이 아니라고 말했듯이, 이제 나는 당신에게 차분하게, 또 다정하게, 그러나 그만큼의 비통함을 안고 알려야겠어요. 나는 당신의 친누이이고, 당신은 내 친오라비이며, 우리는 둘 다 지금 살아 계셔 이 집에 있는 우리 어머니의 자식들이고, 그분은 부인하거나 반박할 수 없는 방식으로 그것이 진실임을 확신하고 계세요.\""]], [["I saw him turn pale and look wild; and I said, “Now remember your promise, and receive it with presence of mind; for who could have said more to prepare you for it than I have done?” However, I called a servant, and got him a little glass of rum (which is the usual dram of that country), for he was just fainting away. When he was a little recovered, I said to him, “This story, you may be sure, requires a long explanation, and therefore, have patience and compose your mind to hear it out, and I’ll make it as short as I can”; and with this, I told him what I thought was needful of the fact, and particularly how my mother came to discover it to me, as above. “And now, my dear,” says I, “you will see reason for my capitulations, and that I neither have been the cause of this matter, nor could be so, and that I could know nothing of it before now.”", "나는 그가 핼쑥해지고 얼이 빠진 표정을 짓는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 말했다. \"이제 당신의 약속을 기억하고, 그것을 침착한 마음으로 받아들여요. 내가 한 것보다 더, 당신을 이 일에 대비시키려고 무슨 말을 더 할 수 있었겠어요?\" 그러나 나는 하인을 불러 그에게 럼주를 작은 잔으로 한 잔 가져오게 했으니(그것이 그 고장에서 흔히 마시는 독한 술이다), 그가 막 까무러치려던 참이었기 때문이다. 그가 조금 정신을 차리자 나는 그에게 말했다. \"이 이야기는, 당신도 분명 알겠지만, 긴 해명이 필요한 일이에요. 그러니 참고서 마음을 가다듬어 끝까지 들어요. 나는 되도록 그것을 짧게 할게요.\" 그러고서 나는 그 사실 가운데 필요하다고 여긴 것을, 특히 내 어머니가 어떻게 그것을 내게 드러내게 되었는지를 앞서 말한 대로 그에게 들려주었다. \"그러니 이제, 여보,\" 하고 나는 말했다. \"당신은 내가 조건을 단 까닭을, 또 내가 이 일의 원인이 된 적도 없고 그럴 수도 없었으며, 지금 이전에는 그것에 대해 아무것도 알 수 없었다는 것을 알게 될 거예요.\""]], [["“I am fully satisfied of that,”", "\"나도 그것은 충분히 납득하오,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “but ’tis a dreadful surprise to me; however, I know a remedy for it all, and a remedy that shall put an end to your difficulties, without your going to England.”", "\"그러나 이건 내게 끔찍한 충격이오. 하지만 나는 이 모든 것에 대한 해결책을 알고 있고, 당신이 잉글랜드로 가지 않고도 당신의 어려움을 끝낼 해결책을 알고 있소.\""], ["“That would be strange,”", "\"그건 다른 모든 것과 마찬가지로,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["said I, “as all the rest.”", "\"참 이상한 일이겠네요.\" \"아니, 아니오,\""], ["“No, no,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “I’ll make it easy; there’s nobody in the way of it but myself.”", "\"내가 그것을 쉽게 만들어 주리다. 그것을 가로막는 사람은 나 말고는 아무도 없으니까.\""], ["He looked a little disordered when he said this, but I did not apprehend anything from it at that time, believing, as it used to be said, that they who do those things never talk of them, or that they who talk of such things never do them.", "그는 이 말을 하면서 좀 흐트러진 기색이었으나, 나는 그때는 그 말에서 아무것도 짐작하지 못했다. 흔히들 하는 말마따나, 그런 일을 하는 자들은 결코 그것을 입에 올리지 않으며, 그런 일을 입에 올리는 자들은 결코 그것을 하지 않는다고 믿었기 때문이다."]], [["But things were not come to their height with him, and I observed he became pensive and melancholy; and in a word, as I thought, a little distempered in his head. I endeavoured to talk him into temper, and to reason him into a kind of scheme for our government in the affair, and sometimes he would be well, and talk with some courage about it; but the weight of it lay too heavy upon his thoughts, and, in short, it went so far that he made attempts upon himself, and in one of them had actually strangled himself and had not his mother come into the room in the very moment, he had died; but with the help of a Negro servant she cut him down and recovered him.", "그러나 일은 아직 그에게서 극에 다다른 것이 아니었으니, 나는 그가 시름에 잠겨 우울해지는 것을 보았고, 한마디로 내가 보기에 머리가 조금 이상해진 것 같았다. 나는 그를 다독여 진정시키고, 이 일에서 우리가 처신할 일종의 방안에 대해 이치를 따져 가며 그를 설득해 보려 애썼다. 때때로 그는 멀쩡하여, 그 일을 두고 얼마간 용기를 내어 이야기하기도 했다. 그러나 그 무게가 그의 생각을 너무도 무겁게 짓눌렀고, 요컨대 일이 너무 멀리 나아가 그는 자기 자신에게 위해를 가하려 했으며, 그 가운데 한 번은 실제로 목을 매었으니, 만약 바로 그 순간에 그의 어머니가 방에 들어오지 않았더라면 죽고 말았을 것이다. 그러나 그분은 흑인 하인의 도움으로 그를 끌어 내려 살려 냈다."]], [["Things were now come to a lamentable height in the family.", "이제 집안은 통탄할 지경에 이르렀다. 그에 대한 내 연민이 이제 처음에 내가 정말로 그에게 품었던 그 애정을 되살리기 시작했고, 나는 진심으로, 내가 할 수 있는 온갖 다정한 처신으로 그 균열을 메우려 애썼다."], ["My pity for him now began to revive that affection which at first I really had for him, and I endeavoured sincerely, by all the kind carriage I could, to make up the breach; but, in short, it had gotten too great a head, it preyed upon his spirits, and it threw him into a long, lingering consumption, though it happened not to be mortal.", "그러나 요컨대 그것은 너무도 크게 부풀어 올라, 그의 기운을 갉아먹었고, 그를 길고 더디게 시들어 가는 폐병에 빠뜨렸으니, 다만 그것이 치명적인 데까지는 이르지 않았을 뿐이다. 이 곤경 속에서 나는 무엇을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으니, 그의 목숨이 눈에 띄게 기울어 가고 있었던 까닭이며, 어쩌면 나는 거기서 다시 결혼하여 내게 무척 이로울 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["In this distress I did not know what to do, as his life was apparently declining, and I might perhaps have married again there, very much to my advantage; it had been certainly my business to have stayed in the country, but my mind was restless too, and uneasy; I hankered after coming to England, and nothing would satisfy me without it.", "분명 그 고장에 머무는 것이 내가 할 일이었으나, 내 마음 역시 들떠 있고 편치 않았다. 나는 잉글랜드로 오기를 갈망했고, 그것 없이는 아무것도 나를 만족시키지 못했다."]], [["In short, by an unwearied importunity, my husband, who was apparently decaying, as I observed, was at last prevailed with; and so my own fate pushing me on, the way was made clear for me, and my mother concurring, I obtained a very good cargo for my coming to England.", "요컨대 지칠 줄 모르는 채근으로, 내가 본 대로 눈에 띄게 쇠해 가던 내 남편은 마침내 설득되었다. 그리하여 나 자신의 운명이 나를 떠밀어 길이 내게 환히 열렸고, 어머니도 거기에 동의하여, 나는 잉글랜드로 오는 데 쓸 아주 좋은 짐을 얻어 냈다."]], [["When I parted with my brother (for such I am now to call him), we agreed that after I arrived he should pretend to have an account that I was dead in England, and so might marry again when he would.", "내 오라비(이제 나는 그를 그렇게 불러야 한다)와 헤어질 때, 우리는 내가 도착한 뒤에 그가 내가 잉글랜드에서 죽었다는 소식을 받은 척하여, 자기가 원할 때 다시 결혼할 수 있게 하기로 합의했다. 그는 나와 누이로서 서신을 주고받고, 내가 사는 동안 나를 돕고 부양하기로 약속하고 다짐했다."], ["He promised, and engaged to me to correspond with me as a sister, and to assist and support me as long as I lived; and that if he died before me, he would leave sufficient to his mother to take care of me still, in the name of a sister, and he was in some respects careful of me, when he heard of me; but it was so oddly managed that I felt the disappointments very sensibly afterwards, as you shall hear in its time.", "또 만약 자기가 나보다 먼저 죽으면, 자기 어머니에게 넉넉한 것을 남겨, 여전히 누이라는 이름으로 나를 보살피게 하겠다고 했다. 그리고 그는 내 소식을 들으면 어떤 면으로는 나를 챙겨 주었으나, 그것이 어찌나 이상하게 처리되었던지, 나는 뒤에 그 실망을 무척 절절히 느꼈으니, 그것은 때가 되면 여러분이 듣게 될 것이다."]], [["I came away for England in the month of August, after I had been eight years in that country; and now a new scene of misfortunes attended me, which perhaps few women have gone through the life of.", "나는 그 고장에서 여덟 해를 지낸 뒤, 팔월에 잉글랜드를 향해 떠나왔다. 그리고 이제 새로운 불운의 장면이 나를 기다리고 있었으니, 그것은 아마 평생을 두고 그것을 겪어 본 여자가 거의 없을 만한 것이었다."]], [["We had an indifferent good voyage till we came just upon the coast of England, and where we arrived in two-and-thirty days, but were then ruffled with two or three storms, one of which drove us away to the coast of Ireland, and we put in at Kinsdale.", "우리는 잉글랜드 해안에 막 다다를 때까지 그럭저럭 괜찮은 항해를 했고, 서른두 날 만에 거기 이르렀으나, 그때 두세 차례의 폭풍에 시달렸다. 그 가운데 하나가 우리를 아일랜드 해안으로 떠밀어, 우리는 킨세일에 입항했다."], ["We remained there about thirteen days, got some refreshment on shore, and put to sea again, though we met with very bad weather again, in which the ship sprung her mainmast, as they called it, for I knew not what they meant.", "우리는 거기서 열사흘쯤 머물며 뭍에서 얼마간 원기를 회복하고 다시 바다로 나갔으나, 또다시 매우 험한 날씨를 만났고, 그 속에서 배가, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면, 큰돛대가 부러졌으니, 나는 그들이 무슨 말을 하는지 알지 못했다. 그러나 우리는 마침내 웨일스의 밀퍼드헤이븐으로 들어갔는데, 비록 그곳이 우리 항구에서 멀리 떨어져 있긴 했어도, 내 발을 내 고국, 브리튼섬의 굳은 땅 위에 무사히 디딘 터라, 나는 나에게 그토록 끔찍했던 그 물 위에 두 번 다시 그것을 내맡기지 않기로 결심했다."], ["But we got at last into Milford Haven, in Wales, where, though it was remote from our port, yet having my foot safe upon the firm ground of my native country, the isle of Britain, I resolved to venture it no more upon the waters, which had been so terrible to me; so getting my clothes and money on shore, with my bills of loading and other papers, I resolved to come for London, and leave the ship to get to her port as she could; the port whither she was bound was to Bristol, where my brother’s chief correspondent lived.", "그래서 나는 내 옷가지와 돈을, 또 내 선하증권과 그 밖의 서류를 뭍에 내려놓고서, 런던으로 가기로 결심했고, 배는 제 항구로 알아서 가도록 내버려 두었다. 배가 향하던 항구는 브리스틀이었는데, 거기에 내 오라비의 주된 거래처가 살고 있었다."]], [["I got to London in about three weeks, where I heard a little while after that the ship was arrived in Bristol, but at the same time had the misfortune to know that by the violent weather she had been in, and the breaking of her mainmast, she had great damage on board, and that a great part of her cargo was spoiled.", "나는 한 삼 주 만에 런던에 이르렀고, 얼마 뒤에 거기서 그 배가 브리스틀에 도착했다는 것을 들었으나, 동시에 그 배가 겪은 험한 날씨와 큰돛대가 부러진 일로 인해 선상에 큰 손상을 입었으며, 그 짐의 큰 몫이 못 쓰게 되었다는 불운한 소식을 알게 되었다."]], [["I had now a new scene of life upon my hands, and a dreadful appearance it had.", "이제 나는 내 손에 새로운 인생의 장면을 떠안았는데, 그것은 끔찍한 모습을 띠고 있었다. 나는 일종의 마지막 작별을 고하고 떠나온 터였다."], ["I was come away with a kind of final farewell.", "내가 가지고 온 것은, 만약 그것이 무사히 왔더라면 정말로 상당한 것이었고, 그 도움으로 나는 그런대로 잘 다시 결혼할 수도 있었을 것이다."], ["What I brought with me was indeed considerable, had it come safe, and by the help of it, I might have married again tolerably well; but as it was, I was reduced to between two or three hundred pounds in the whole, and this without any hope of recruit.", "그러나 사정이 이러하니, 나는\n전체 자산이 다 합쳐 봐야 이삼백 파운드 남짓으로 줄어들었고, 그나마도 다시 채울 가망이 전혀 없었다. 나는 완전히 벗 하나 없는 신세였으니, 아니, 아는 사람조차 하나 없었다."], ["I was entirely without friends, nay, even so much as without acquaintance, for I found it was absolutely necessary not to revive former acquaintances; and as for my subtle friend that set me up formerly for a fortune, she was dead, and her husband also; as I was informed, upon sending a person unknown to inquire.", "옛 친지들을 다시 살려 내지 않는 것이 절대적으로 필요하다는 것을 깨달았기 때문이다. 또 예전에 나를 재산가 행세 시켜 주었던 그 약삭빠른 벗으로 말하자면, 내가 사람을 시켜 은밀히 알아보게 한 바로는, 그녀는 죽었고 그 남편 또한 죽었다고 했다."]], [["The looking after my cargo of goods soon after obliged me to take a journey to Bristol, and during my attendance upon that affair I took the diversion of going to the Bath, for as I was still far from being old, so my humour, which was always gay, continued so to an extreme; and being now, as it were, a woman of fortune though I was a woman without a fortune, I expected something or other might happen in my way that might mend my circumstances, as had been my case before.", "얼마 지나지 않아 내 화물을 챙기러 가야 할 일이 생겨 브리스틀로 길을 떠나게 되었는데, 그 일을 처리하는 동안 나는 바스로 가는 기분 전환을 누렸다. 나는 아직 늙으려면 멀었고, 늘 명랑하던 내 기질도 여전히 극단적이라 할 만큼 명랑함을 잃지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 비록 재산 없는 여자였으나 마치 재산가 여자인 양 처신하고 있던 터라, 예전에 그랬듯이 내 처지를 나아지게 해 줄 무슨 일인가가 내 길에 일어날지도 모른다고 기대했다."]], [["The Bath is a place of gallantry enough; expensive, and full of snares.", "바스는 풍류가 넘치는 곳이었다. 돈이 많이 들고, 함정으로 가득한 곳이었다."], ["I went thither, indeed, in the view of taking anything that might offer, but I must do myself justice, as to protest I knew nothing amiss; I meant nothing but in an honest way, nor had I any thoughts about me at first that looked the way which afterwards I suffered them to be guided.", "나는 사실 무엇이든 기회가 닿는 대로 잡아 보겠다는 속셈으로 그곳에 갔으나, 나 자신을 위해 공정하게 밝혀 두자면, 나쁜 짓이라곤 전혀 염두에 두지 않았다고 단언해야겠다. 나는 오로지 정직한 방향만을 생각했고, 처음에는 훗날 내가 그렇게 끌려가도록 내버려 둔 그쪽으로 향하는 생각이라곤 조금도 품지 않았다."]], [["Here I stayed the whole latter season, as it is called there, and contracted some unhappy acquaintances, which rather prompted the follies I fell afterwards into than fortified me against them.", "이곳에서 나는 그들이 말하는 이른바 후반 사교철 내내 머물렀는데, 불운한 친지 몇을 사귀었으니, 그들은 훗날 내가 빠져든 어리석은 짓들에 맞서도록 나를 다잡아 주기는커녕 오히려 그 짓들을 부추겼다. 나는 제법 즐겁게 살았고, 좋은 무리와, 다시 말해 명랑하고 멋들어진 무리와 어울렸다."], ["I lived pleasantly enough, kept good company, that is to say, gay, fine company; but had the discouragement to find this way of living sunk me exceedingly, and that as I had no settled income, so spending upon the main stock was but a certain kind of bleeding to death; and this gave me many sad reflections in the interval of my other thoughts.", "그러나 이런 식의 생활이 나를 엄청나게 가라앉히고 있다는 것을, 또 정해진 수입이 없는 처지라 밑천을 헐어 쓰는 것은 일종의 확실한 출혈사에 다름없다는 것을 깨닫는 낙심이 따랐다. 이것은 다른 생각들 사이사이에 내게 많은 서글픈 성찰을 안겨 주었다."], ["However, I shook them off, and still flattered myself that something or other might offer for my advantage.", "그러나 나는 그 생각들을 떨쳐 내고, 그래도 무슨 일인가가 내게 이롭게 일어날지 모른다며 여전히 스스로를 다독였다."]], [["But I was in the wrong place for it.", "그러나 나는 그러기에는 잘못된 곳에 있었다. 이제 내가 있는 곳은 레드리프가 아니었으니, 거기서라면 내가 그럭저럭 살림을 차려 놓았을 경우 어떤 정직한 선장쯤이 명예로운 혼인의 조건을 걸고 내게 말을 걸어왔을 수도 있었다."], ["I was not now at Redriff, where, if I had set myself tolerably up, some honest sea captain or other might have talked with me upon the honourable terms of matrimony; but I was at the Bath, where men find a mistress sometimes, but very rarely look for a wife; and consequently all the particular acquaintances a woman can expect to make there must have some tendency that way.", "그러나 나는 바스에 있었고, 거기서 남자들은 더러 정부情婦를 구할지언정 아내를 찾는 일은 매우 드물었다. 따라서 여자가 그곳에서 사귈 수 있다고 기대할 만한 특별한 친지란 모두 그쪽으로 기우는 어떤 성향을 띠게 마련이었다."]], [["I had spent the first season well enough; for though I had contracted some acquaintance with a gentleman who came to the Bath for his diversion, yet I had entered into no felonious treaty, as it might be called.", "첫 사교철은 그런대로 잘 보냈다. 비록 기분 전환차 바스에 온 어느 신사와 친분을 트긴 했어도, 그렇게 부를 수 있다면 중범죄적 거래에는 발을 들이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["I had resisted some casual offers of gallantry, and had managed that way well enough.", "나는 우연히 던져진 몇몇 풍류의 제안을 물리쳤고, 그쪽으로는 그런대로 잘 처신했다."], ["I was not wicked enough to come into the crime for the mere vice of it, and I had no extraordinary offers made me that tempted me with the main thing which I wanted.", "나는 단지 악덕 그 자체를 위해 그 죄에 빠져들 만큼 사악하지는 않았고, 또 내가 원하던 그 본질적인 것으로 나를 유혹할 만한 특별한 제안을 받지도 않았다."]], [["However, I went this length the first season, viz.", "그러나 첫 사교철에 이만큼은 나아갔으니, 곧 내가 묵던 집의 안주인인 어떤 여자와 친분을 텄던 것이다."], ["I contracted an acquaintance with a woman in whose house I lodged, who, though she did not keep an ill house, as we call it, yet had none of the best principles in herself.", "그 여자는 우리가 말하는 이른바 못된 집을 운영하지는 않았으나, 그렇다고 자기 자신이 그리 훌륭한 원칙을 지닌 사람도 아니었다. 나는 어떤 경우에도 무슨 까닭으로든 내 평판에 조금의 흠집도 입지 않을 만큼 처신을 잘해 왔고, 내가 어울린 남자들은 모두 평판이 너무나 좋아서, 그들과 어울린다 하여 조금의 손가락질도 받지 않았다."], ["I had on all occasions behaved myself so well as not to get the least slur upon my reputation on any account whatever, and all the men that I had conversed with were of so good reputation that I had not given the least reflection by conversing with them; nor did any of them seem to think there was room for a wicked correspondence, if they had any of them offered it; yet there was one gentleman, as above, who always singled me out for the diversion of my company, as he called it, which, as he was pleased to say, was very agreeable to him, but at that time there was no more in it.", "또 그들 가운데 누구도, 설령 사악한 관계를 청했더라도 그럴 여지가 있다고 여기는 듯하지 않았다. 그러나 앞서 말한 한 신사가 있었으니, 그는 늘 자기 말마따나 내 동반의 즐거움을 위해 나를 콕 집어 따로 골라냈고, 그것이 자기에게 매우 흡족하다고 기꺼이 말하곤 했으나, 그때까지는 그 이상의 것은 없었다."]], [["I had many melancholy hours at the Bath after the company was gone; for though I went to Bristol sometime for the disposing my effects, and for recruits of money, yet I chose to come back to Bath for my residence, because being on good terms with the woman in whose house I lodged in the summer, I found that during the winter I lived rather cheaper there than I could do anywhere else. Here, I say, I passed the winter as heavily as I had passed the autumn cheerfully; but having contracted a nearer intimacy with the said woman in whose house I lodged, I could not avoid communicating to her something of what lay hardest upon my mind and particularly the narrowness of my circumstances, and the loss of my fortune by the damage of my goods at sea. I told her also, that I had a mother and a brother in Virginia in good circumstances; and as I had really written back to my mother in particular to represent my condition, and the great loss I had received, which indeed came to almost £500, so I did not fail to let my new friend know that I expected a supply from thence, and so indeed I did; and as the ships went from Bristol to York River, in Virginia, and back again generally in less time from London, and that my brother corresponded chiefly at Bristol, I thought it was much better for me to wait here for my returns than to go to London, where also I had not the least acquaintance.", "무리가 떠나고 나자 바스에서 나는 우울한 시간을 많이 보냈다. 비록 내 물품을 처분하고 돈을 다시 채우려고 이따금 브리스틀에 다녀오긴 했어도, 거처로는 바스로 돌아오기를 택했으니, 여름에 내가 묵던 집의 여자와 사이가 좋았던 덕에 겨우내 다른 어느 곳보다 오히려 더 싸게 지낼 수 있다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 여기서, 다시 말하건대, 나는 가을을 명랑하게 보냈던 만큼이나 겨울을 무겁게 보냈다. 그러나 내가 묵던 집의 그 여자와 더 가까운 친밀함을 맺은 터라, 내 마음을 가장 짓누르던 것들, 특히 내 형편의 옹색함과, 바다에서 물품이 상하는 바람에 재산을 잃은 일을 그녀에게 어느 정도 털어놓지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 또한 버지니아에 넉넉하게 사는 어머니와 오라비가 있다고 말했다. 그리고 실제로 어머니에게 따로 편지를 써 보내 내 처지와, 거의 오백 파운드에 달하던 그 막대한 손실을 알린 터였으므로, 거기서 도움을 받기를 기대하고 있다는 사실도 새 벗에게 빠짐없이 알렸고, 사실 그러기도 했다. 또 배들이 브리스틀에서 버지니아의 요크강으로 갔다가 다시 돌아오는 데에 대개 런던에서 가는 것보다 시간이 덜 걸렸고, 내 오라비도 주로 브리스틀에서 서신을 주고받았으므로, 나는 아는 사람이라곤 조금도 없는 런던으로 가느니 여기서 내 회신을 기다리는 편이 훨씬 낫다고 생각했다."]], [["My new friend appeared sensibly affected with my condition, and indeed was so very kind as to reduce the rate of my living with her to so low a price during the winter, that she convinced me she got nothing by me; and as for lodging, during the winter I paid nothing at all.", "새 벗은 내 처지에 마음으로 깊이 마음 쓰는 듯했고, 실로 너무도 친절하여 겨우내 그녀 집에서 지내는 비용을 너무나 낮은 값으로 깎아 주었으니, 그 정도면 그녀가 나로 인해 아무것도 벌지 못한다는 것을 내게 납득시켰다. 그리고 숙박료로 말하자면, 겨우내 나는 한 푼도 내지 않았다."]], [["When the spring season came on, she continued to be as kind to me as she could, and I lodged with her for a time, till it was found necessary to do otherwise.", "봄철이 다가오자 그녀는 할 수 있는 한 계속 내게 친절하게 굴었고, 나는 한동안 그녀 집에 묵었으니, 달리 해야 할 필요가 생길 때까지였다."], ["She had some persons of character that frequently lodged in her house, and in particular the gentleman who, as I said, singled me out for his companion the winter before; and he came down again with another gentleman in his company and two servants, and lodged in the same house.", "그녀 집에는 신분 있는 사람들이 자주 묵었는데, 특히 앞서 말했듯 지난겨울 나를 자기 동반으로 콕 집어 골라냈던 그 신사가 있었다. 그가 다시 내려왔는데, 다른 신사 한 명을 일행으로, 또 하인 둘을 데리고 와서 같은 집에 묵었다."], ["I suspected that my landlady had invited him thither, letting him know that I was still with her; but she denied it, and protested to me that she did not, and he said the same.", "나는 안주인이 내가 아직 자기 집에 있다는 것을 알려 그를 그리로 초대한 것이 아닌가 의심했으나, 그녀는 그것을 부인하며 자기는 그러지 않았다고 내게 맹세했고, 그도 같은 말을 했다."]], [["In a word, this gentleman came down and continued to single me out for his peculiar confidence as well as conversation.", "한마디로, 이 신사는 내려와서 대화뿐 아니라 자신의 각별한 신뢰의 대상으로도 나를 계속 콕 집어 골라냈다."], ["He was a complete gentleman, that must be confessed, and his company was very agreeable to me, as mine, if I might believe him, was to him.", "그가 완벽한 신사였다는 것은 인정해야겠고, 그의 동반은 내게 매우 흡족했으며, 그의 말을 믿어도 된다면 내 동반 또한 그에게 그러했다."], ["He made no professions to me but of an extraordinary respect, and he had such an opinion of my virtue, that, as he often professed, he believed if he should offer anything else, I should reject him with contempt.", "그는 내게 각별한 존경 외에는 아무런 마음도 고백하지 않았고, 내 덕성에 대해 어찌나 높이 여겼던지, 그가 종종 공언했듯이, 만약 자기가 그 외의 다른 무엇을 청한다면 내가 경멸하며 그를 물리치리라고 믿었다."], ["He soon understood from me that I was a widow; that I had arrived at Bristol from Virginia by the last ships; and that I waited at Bath till the next Virginia fleet should arrive, by which I expected considerable effects.", "그는 곧 내게서, 내가 과부라는 것, 지난번 배편으로 버지니아에서 브리스틀에 도착했다는 것, 그리고 상당한 자산이 도착하기를 기대하며 다음 버지니아 선단이 들어올 때까지 바스에서 기다리고 있다는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["I understood by him, and by others of him, that he had a wife, but that the lady was distempered in her head, and was under the conduct of her own relations, which he consented to, to avoid any reflections that might (as was not unusual in such cases) be cast on him for mismanaging her cure; and in the meantime he came to the Bath to divert his thoughts from the disturbance of such a melancholy circumstance as that was.", "나는 그에게서, 또 그를 아는 다른 이들에게서, 그에게 아내가 있으나 그 부인이 정신이 온전치 못하여 자기 친정 친척들의 보살핌 아래 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그가 그것에 동의한 것은, 그런 경우에 흔히 그렇듯, 아내의 치료를 잘못 관리했다고 자기에게 쏟아질 수 있는 비난을 피하기 위함이었고, 그러는 동안 그는 그런 우울한 사정의 심란함에서 마음을 돌리려고 바스에 온 것이었다."]], [["My landlady, who of her own accord encouraged the correspondence on all occasions, gave me an advantageous character of him, as a man of honour and of virtue, as well as of great estate.", "내 안주인은 제 발로 나서서 온갖 경우에 이 교제를 부추겼으니, 그를 명예와 덕을 갖춘 사람이자 또한 큰 재산가라고 내게 유리하게 평해 주었다. 그리고 실로 나도 그를 그렇게 말할 까닭이 충분했다."], ["And indeed I had a great deal of reason to say so of him too; for though we lodged both on a floor, and he had frequently come into my chamber, even when I was in bed, and I also into his when he was in bed, yet he never offered anything to me further than a kiss, or so much as solicited me to anything till long after, as you shall hear.", "우리 둘이 같은 층에 묵었고, 그가 자주 내 방에 들어왔으니 심지어 내가 잠자리에 들었을 때에도 그러했고, 나 또한 그가 잠자리에 들었을 때 그의 방에 들어갔으나, 그는 입맞춤 이상의 그 무엇도 내게 청한 적이 없었고, 앞으로 여러분이 듣게 되겠지만 그로부터 한참 뒤까지 무슨 일을 하자고 조르는 일조차 없었기 때문이다."]], [["I frequently took notice to my landlady of his exceeding modesty, and she again used to tell me, she believed it was so from the beginning; however, she used to tell me that she thought I ought to expect some gratification from him for my company, for indeed he did, as it were, engross me, and I was seldom from him.", "나는 그의 지나친 정숙함을 안주인에게 자주 언급했고, 그녀는 또 처음부터 그러했으리라 믿는다고 내게 말하곤 했다. 그러면서도 그녀는 내가 동반해 준 대가로 그에게서 어떤 사례를 기대해야 마땅하다고 말하곤 했으니, 실로 그가 말하자면 나를 독차지하다시피 했고 나는 좀처럼 그의 곁을 떠나지 않았기 때문이다."], ["I told her I had not given him the least occasion to think I wanted it, or that I would accept of it from him.", "나는 그가 내가 그것을 원한다거나 그에게서 그것을 받겠다고 생각할 만한 빌미를 조금도 준 적이 없다고 그녀에게 말했다. 그녀는 그 일을 자기가 맡겠다고 했고, 실제로 그렇게 했으며, 어찌나 능란하게 일을 처리했던지, 그녀가 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤 우리 둘만 처음 같이 있게 되었을 때, 그가 내 형편을 조금 캐묻기 시작했으니, 곧 뭍에 오른 뒤로 어떻게 지내 왔는지, 돈이 부족하지는 않은지 따위였다."], ["She told me she would take that part upon her, and she did so, and managed it so dexterously, that the first time we were together alone, after she had talked with him, he began to inquire a little into my circumstances, as how I had subsisted myself since I came on shore, and whether I did not want money.", "나는 매우 당당하게 버텼다. 내 담뱃잎 화물이 상하기는 했으나 아주 잃어버린 것은 아니라고, 내가 위탁받았던 상인이 나를 위해 너무도 정직하게 처리해 주어 부족함이 없었다고, 그리고 알뜰하게 꾸려 나가면 다음 선단 편에 기대하는 더 많은 물품이 올 때까지 버틸 수 있으리라 바란다고 말했다."], ["I stood off very boldly.", "또 그동안 지출을 줄여, 지난 사교철에는 하녀를 두었으나 이제는 없이 산다고, 또 그때는 그도 알다시피 일층에 침실과 식당을 두었으나 이제는 계단 두 층을 올라간 방 하나만 둔다는 둥의 이야기를 했다. \"그래도 나는,\""], ["I told him that though my cargo of tobacco was damaged, yet that it was not quite lost; that the merchant I had been consigned to had so honestly managed for me that I had not wanted, and that I hoped, with frugal management, I should make it hold out till more would come, which I expected by the next fleet; that in the meantime I had retrenched my expenses, and whereas I kept a maid last season, now I lived without; and whereas I had a chamber and a dining-room then on the first floor, as he knew, I now had but one room, two pair of stairs, and the like.", "하고 나는 말했다. \"지금도 그때만큼이나 흡족하게 살고 있어요.\""], ["“But I live,”", "그러면서 그의 동반이 안 그랬을 경우보다 훨씬 더 명랑하게 살게 해 준 방편이 되었으니 그 점은 그에게 무척 고맙다고 덧붙였다. 그렇게 하여 나는 당장은 무슨 제안이 들어올 여지를 모두 밀어내 버렸다."], ["said I, “as well satisfied now as I did then”; adding, that his company had been a means to make me live much more cheerfully than otherwise I should have done, for which I was much obliged to him; and so I put off all room for any offer for the present.", "그러나 머지않아 그는 다시 나를 공략하며, 내가 내 형편의 속사정을 그에게 믿고 털어놓기를 꺼리는 것을 알았는데 그것이 안타깝다고 말했다. 그러면서 자기가 그것을 캐물은 것은 결코 자기 호기심을 채우려는 의도가 아니라 오로지, 만약 필요한 일이 있다면, 나를 돕기 위함이었다고 단언했다."], ["However, it was not long before he attacked me again, and told me he found that I was backward to trust him with the secret of my circumstances, which he was sorry for; assuring me that he inquired into it with no design to satisfy his own curiosity, but merely to assist me, if there was any occasion; but since I would not own myself to stand in need of any assistance, he had but one thing more to desire of me, and that was, that I would promise him that when I was any way straitened, or like to be so, I would frankly tell him of it, and that I would make use of him with the same freedom that he made the offer; adding, that I should always find I had a true friend, though perhaps I was afraid to trust him.", "그러나 내가 어떤 도움도 필요하다고 인정하려 들지 않으니, 자기에게 더 바라는 것은 단 한 가지뿐이라며, 그것은 내가 어떤 식으로든 궁색해지거나 그리될 성싶을 때 솔직하게 그것을 자기에게 말해 주고, 또 자기가 제안한 그 거리낌 없는 마음 그대로 자기를 부려 달라고 약속해 달라는 것이라고 했다. 그러면서 덧붙이기를, 비록 내가 그를 믿기를 두려워했을지언정, 내게 진정한 벗이 있다는 것을 언제나 알게 될 것이라고 했다."]], [["I omitted nothing that was fit to be said by one infinitely obliged, to let him know that I had a due sense of his kindness; and indeed from that time I did not appear so much reserved to him as I had done before, though still within the bounds of the strictest virtue on both sides; but how free soever our conversation was, I could not arrive to that sort of freedom which he desired, viz.", "나는 한없이 큰 은혜를 입은 사람으로서 마땅히 해야 할 말이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않고, 그의 친절을 합당하게 헤아리고 있음을 그에게 알렸다. 그리고 실로 그때부터 나는 전처럼 그에게 그리 서먹하게 굴지 않았으나, 여전히 양쪽 다 가장 엄격한 덕의 테두리 안에 머물렀다."], ["to tell him I wanted money, though I was secretly very glad of his offer.", "그러나 우리 대화가 아무리 거리낌 없었어도, 나는 그가 바라던 그런 종류의 거리낌 없음, 곧 그에게 돈이 필요하다고 말하는 데까지는 다다를 수 없었으니, 속으로는 그의 제안이 매우 기쁘면서도 그러했다."]], [["Some weeks passed after this, and still I never asked him for money; when my landlady, a cunning creature, who had often pressed me to it, but found that I could not do it, makes a story of her own inventing, and comes in bluntly to me when we were together.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 몇 주가 지났으나, 나는 여전히 그에게 돈을 청한 적이 없었다. 그러던 차에 교활한 인물인 내 안주인이 종종 내게 그렇게 하라고 졸랐으나 내가 그러지 못하는 것을 알고는, 제가 지어낸 이야기 하나를 꾸며서, 우리가 함께 있을 때 불쑥 내게 들어왔다."], ["“Oh, widow!”", "\"어머, 과부댁!\""], ["says she, “I have bad news to tell you this morning.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"오늘 아침 안 좋은 소식을 전해 드릴 게 있어요.\""], ["“What is that?”", "\"무슨 일인데요?\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["said I; “are the Virginia ships taken by the French?”—for that was my fear.", "\"버지니아 배들이 프랑스인들에게 나포되기라도 했나요?\" 그것이 내 걱정거리였기 때문이다."], ["“No, no,”", "\"아뇨, 아뇨,\""], ["says she, “but the man you sent to Bristol yesterday for money is come back, and says he has brought none.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"어제 돈 가지러 브리스틀에 보내신 그 사람이 돌아왔는데, 한 푼도 못 가져왔다지 뭐예요.\""]], [["Now I could by no means like her project; I thought it looked too much like prompting him, which indeed he did not want, and I clearly saw that I should lose nothing by being backward to ask, so I took her up short.", "이제 나는 그녀의 꿍꿍이가 도무지 마음에 들지 않았다. 그것은 그를 너무 부추기는 것처럼 보였는데, 실로 그는 부추길 필요가 없는 사람이었고, 또 내가 청하기를 꺼린다 하여 잃을 것이 전혀 없다는 것을 나는 똑똑히 알고 있었으므로, 그녀의 말을 짧게 잘랐다."], ["“I can’t image why he should say so to you,”", "\"그 사람이 왜 당신한테 그런 소릴 했는지 도무지 모르겠네요,\" 하고 나는 말했다."], ["said I, “for I assure you he brought me all the money I sent him for, and here it is,”", "\"장담컨대 그 사람은 내가 가져오라 한 돈을 죄다 가져왔고, 여기 이렇게 있는걸요,\" 하고 나는 (기니 화폐 열두 닢쯤 든 지갑을 꺼내며) 말했다."], ["said I (pulling out my purse with about twelve guineas in it); and added, “I intend you shall have most of it by and by.”", "그러고는 \"이따 그중 대부분은 당신께 드릴 작정이에요\" 하고 덧붙였다."]], [["He seemed distasted a little at her talking as she did at first, as well as I, taking it, as I fancied he would, as something forward of her; but when he saw me give such an answer, he came immediately to himself again.", "그는 처음에 그녀가 그렇게 말하는 것을 나와 마찬가지로 조금 못마땅해하는 듯, 내가 짐작한 대로 그녀의 주제넘은 짓쯤으로 여기는 눈치였다. 그러나 내가 그런 대답을 하는 것을 보자, 그는 곧바로 다시 평소의 그로 돌아왔다."], ["The next morning we talked of it again, when I found he was fully satisfied, and, smiling, said he hoped I would not want money and not tell him of it, and that I had promised him otherwise.", "이튿날 아침 우리는 그 일을 다시 이야기했는데, 그때 나는 그가 완전히 만족했다는 것을 알았고, 그는 빙긋 웃으며 내가 돈이 부족해도 자기에게 말하지 않는 일이 없기를 바란다고, 또 내가 그러지 않겠노라 약속하지 않았느냐고 했다."], ["I told him I had been very much dissatisfied at my landlady’s talking so publicly the day before of what she had nothing to do with; but I supposed she wanted what I owed her, which was about eight guineas, which I had resolved to give her, and had accordingly given it her the same night she talked so foolishly.", "나는 어제 안주인이 자기와 아무 상관도 없는 일을 그렇게 드러내 놓고 떠벌린 것이 무척 못마땅했다고 말했다. 다만 짐작건대 그녀는 내가 그녀에게 진 빚, 곧 여덟 기니쯤 되던 그것을 받고 싶었던 모양이라고, 그래서 나는 그것을 줄 작정을 했고 그녀가 그토록 어리석게 떠들던 바로 그날 밤에 마침내 그것을 주었노라고 했다."]], [["He was in a might good humour when he heard me say I had paid her, and it went off into some other discourse at that time.", "내가 그녀에게 갚았다는 말을 듣자 그는 무척 기분이 좋아졌고, 그때는 그 일이 다른 이야기로 흘러가 버렸다."], ["But the next morning, he having heard me up about my room before him, he called to me, and I answering, he asked me to come into his chamber.", "그러나 이튿날 아침, 그가 자기보다 먼저 내가 방에서 일어나 부산을 떠는 소리를 듣고는 나를 불렀고, 내가 대답하자 자기 방으로 들어오라고 했다."], ["He was in bed when I came in, and he made me come and sit down on his bedside, for he said he had something to say to me which was of some moment.", "내가 들어갔을 때 그는 잠자리에 있었는데, 자기에게 좀 긴요한 할 말이 있다며 나를 침대 곁으로 와서 앉게 했다."], ["After some very kind expressions, he asked me if I would be very honest to him, and give a sincere answer to one thing he would desire of me.", "무척 다정한 말 몇 마디를 건넨 뒤, 그는 내게 자기에게 매우 정직해 줄 수 있겠는지, 자기가 청하는 한 가지에 진실한 대답을 해 줄 수 있겠는지 물었다."], ["After some little cavil at the word “sincere,” and asking him if I had ever given him any answers which were not sincere, I promised him I would.", "\"진실한\"이라는 말을 두고 내가 잠시 트집을 잡으며, 내가 그에게 진실하지 않은 대답을 한 적이 있느냐고 물은 뒤, 나는 그러겠노라 약속했다."], ["Why, then, his request was, he said, to let him see my purse.", "그러자 그의 청은 이러했으니, 내 지갑을 보여 달라는 것이었다."], ["I immediately put my hand into my pocket, and, laughing to him, pulled it out, and there was in it three guineas and a half.", "나는 곧바로 주머니에 손을 넣어, 그에게 웃어 보이며 지갑을 꺼냈는데, 그 안에는 삼 기니 반이 들어 있었다."], ["Then he asked me if there was all the money I had.", "그러자 그는 그것이 내가 가진 돈 전부냐고 물었다."], ["I told him No, laughing again, not by a great deal.", "나는 다시 웃으며, 아니라고, 어림도 없다고 말했다."]], [["Well, then, he said, he would have me promise to go and fetch him all the money I had, every farthing.", "그러자 그는 자기에게, 내가 가진 돈을 한 닢까지 죄다 가져오겠다고 약속하라고 했다."], ["I told him I would, and I went into my chamber and fetched him a little private drawer, where I had about six guineas more, and some silver, and threw it all down upon the bed, and told him there was all my wealth, honestly to a shilling.", "나는 그러겠다 하고 내 방으로 가서 작은 개인 서랍을 가져왔는데, 거기엔 육 기니쯤이 더 있었고 은화도 좀 있었다. 나는 그것을 모두 침대 위에 쏟아 놓고, 이것이 내 재산 전부라고, 정직하게 일 실링까지 다 합쳐 그렇다고 말했다."], ["He looked a little at it, but did not tell it, and huddled it all into the drawer again, and then reaching his pocket, pulled out a key, and bade me open a little walnut-tree box he had upon the table, and bring him such a drawer, which I did.", "그는 그것을 잠시 들여다보았으나 세어 보지는 않았고, 도로 모조리 서랍에 쓸어 담았다. 그러고는 자기 주머니에 손을 뻗어 열쇠 하나를 꺼내더니, 탁자 위에 있던 작은 호두나무 상자를 열어 어떤 서랍을 가져오라고 했고, 나는 그렇게 했다."], ["In which drawer there was a great deal of money in gold, I believe near two hundred guineas, but I knew not how much.", "그 서랍 안에는 금화로 된 돈이 많이 들어 있었으니, 아마 이백 기니 가까이 되었을 테지만 얼마인지는 알지 못했다."], ["He took the drawer, and taking my hand, made me put it in and take a whole handful.", "그는 그 서랍을 받아 들더니, 내 손을 잡아 그 안에 넣게 하고는 한 움큼 가득 집으라고 했다."], ["I was backward at that, but he held my hand hard in his hand, and put it into the drawer, and made me take out as many guineas almost as I could well take up at once.", "나는 그러기를 꺼렸으나, 그가 내 손을 자기 손으로 꽉 잡아 서랍 안에 넣고는, 한 번에 집어 올릴 수 있는 만큼의 기니를 거의 다 꺼내게 했다."]], [["When I had done so, he made me put them into my lap, and took my little drawer, and poured out all my money among his, and bade me get me gone, and carry it all home into my own chamber.", "내가 그렇게 하자, 그는 그것을 내 무릎 위에 놓게 하고, 내 작은 서랍을 가져다 내 돈을 모조리 자기 돈 속에 쏟아붓더니, 나더러 그만 가라고, 그것을 죄다 내 방으로 가져가라고 했다."]], [["I relate this story the more particularly because of the good-humour there was in it, and to show the temper with which we conversed.", "내가 이 이야기를 이토록 시시콜콜 늘어놓는 것은 거기에 깃든 화기애애함 때문이며, 또 우리가 어떤 기분으로 대화를 나누었는지 보여 주기 위함이다."], ["It was not long after this but he began every day to find fault with my clothes, with my laces and headdresses, and, in a word, pressed me to buy better; which, by the way, I was willing enough to do, though I did not seem to be so, for I loved nothing in the world better than fine clothes.", "이 일이 있은 지 오래지 않아 그는 날마다 내 옷을, 내 레이스와 머리 장식을 트집 잡기 시작했고, 한마디로 더 좋은 것을 사라고 졸랐다. 한데 말이 난 김에 말하자면, 나는 비록 그래 보이지 않으려 했어도 내심 기꺼이 그러고 싶었으니, 세상에서 고운 옷보다 더 좋아하는 것이 없었기 때문이다."], ["I told him I must housewife the money he had lent me, or else I should not be able to pay him again.", "나는 그가 빌려준 돈을 알뜰히 아껴 써야지, 안 그러면 그에게 다시 갚을 수가 없을 거라고 말했다."], ["He then told me, in a few words, that as he had a sincere respect for me, and knew my circumstances, he had not lent me that money, but given it me, and that he thought I had merited it from him by giving him my company so entirely as I had done.", "그러자 그는 몇 마디로, 자기는 내게 진심 어린 존경을 품고 있고 내 형편도 아는지라, 그 돈을 빌려준 것이 아니라 준 것이며, 또 내가 그토록 온전히 동반해 줌으로써 그에게서 그것을 받을 자격을 얻었다고 생각한다고 말했다."], ["After this he made me take a maid, and keep house, and his friend that come with him to Bath being gone, he obliged me to diet him, which I did very willingly, believing, as it appeared, that I should lose nothing by it, nor did the woman of the house fail to find her account in it too.", "이 일이 있은 뒤 그는 나로 하여금 하녀를 두고 살림을 꾸리게 했고, 바스에 함께 온 그의 벗이 떠나자 자기 식사를 내가 차려 주도록 하였다. 나는 그것으로 손해 볼 것이 없으리라 믿었고 실제로 그러했으므로 기꺼이 그렇게 했으며, 그 집 여자 또한 거기서 제 몫의 이득을 챙기는 데에 빠짐이 없었다."]], [["We had lived thus near three months, when the company beginning to wear away at the Bath, he talked of going away, and fain he would have me to go to London with him.", "우리가 이렇게 산 지 거의 석 달이 되었을 즈음, 바스에서 사람들이 하나둘 빠져나가기 시작하자 그는 떠날 이야기를 꺼냈고, 나더러 자기와 함께 런던에 가자고 간곡히 청했다. 나는 그 제안이 그리 편치 않았으니, 거기서 내가 어떤 처지로 살게 될지, 그가 나를 어떻게 대할지 알 수 없었기 때문이다."], ["I was not very easy in that proposal, not knowing what posture I was to live in there, or how he might use me.", "그러나 이 일을 두고 옥신각신하는 사이에 그가 몹시 앓아누웠다. 그는 무슨 볼일이 있어 서머싯셔의 셉턴이라는 곳에 나갔다가 거기서 매우 심하게 앓게 되었으니, 어찌나 심했던지 길을 떠날 수도 없었다."], ["But while this was in debate he fell very sick; he had gone out to a place in Somersetshire, called Shepton, where he had some business and was there taken very ill, and so ill that he could not travel; so he sent his man back to Bath, to beg me that I would hire a coach and come over to him.", "그래서 그는 자기 하인을 바스로 돌려보내, 내가 마차를 빌려 자기에게 와 주기를 청했다. 그는 떠나기 전에 자기 돈과 그 밖의 값진 것들을 모두 내게 맡겨 두었는데, 나는 그것들을 어찌해야 할지 몰랐으나 할 수 있는 한 잘 간수해 두고는, 숙소를 잠가 두고 그에게로 갔다."], ["Before he went, he had left all his money and other things of value with me, and what to do with them I did not know, but I secured them as well as I could, and locked up the lodgings and went to him, where I found him very ill indeed; however, I persuaded him to be carried in a litter to the Bath, where there was more help and better advice to be had.", "가 보니 그는 실로 매우 심하게 앓고 있었다. 그러나 나는 그를 설득하여 가마에 실어 바스로 옮기게 하였으니, 거기에는 손쓸 방도도 더 많고 더 나은 의술도 구할 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["He consented, and I brought him to the Bath, which was about fifteen miles, as I remember.", "그는 동의했고, 나는 그를 바스로 데려왔으니, 내 기억으로는 십오 마일쯤 되는 거리였다."], ["Here he continued very ill of a fever, and kept his bed five weeks, all which time I nursed him and tended him myself, as much and as carefully as if I had been his wife; indeed, if I had been his wife I could not have done more.", "여기서 그는 열병으로 계속 심하게 앓아 다섯 주일 동안 자리에 누워 있었는데, 그동안 내내 나는 마치 그의 아내라도 되는 양 손수, 그만큼이나 정성스럽게 그를 간호하고 돌보았다. 실로 내가 그의 아내였더라도 그 이상은 할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["I sat up with him so much and so often, that at last, indeed, he would not let me sit up any longer, and then I got a pallet-bed into his room, and lay in it just at his bed’s feet.", "나는 그토록 자주, 그토록 오래 그의 곁을 지켜 밤을 새웠으니, 마침내는 그가 더는 밤을 새우지 못하게 하기에 이르렀고, 그러자 나는 간이침대 하나를 그의 방에 들여놓고 바로 그의 침대 발치에서 잤다."]], [["I was indeed sensibly affected with his condition, and with the apprehension of losing such a friend as he was, and was like to be to me, and I used to sit and cry by him many hours together.", "나는 실로 그의 처지에, 또 그토록 좋은 벗이며 앞으로도 내게 그러할 그를 잃을지 모른다는 두려움에 마음이 사무치게 움직여, 그의 곁에 앉아 몇 시간씩 내리 울곤 했다."], ["However, at last he grew better, and gave hopes that he would recover, as indeed he did, though very slowly.", "그러나 마침내 그는 차도를 보였고 회복하리라는 희망을 주었으며, 실로 비록 아주 더디기는 했어도 회복했다."]], [["Were it otherwise than what I am going to say, I should not be backward to disclose it, as it is apparent I have done in other cases in this account; but I affirm, that through all this conversation, abating the freedom of coming into the chamber when I or he was in bed, and abating the necessary offices of attending him night and day when he was sick, there had not passed the least immodest word or action between us.", "지금 내가 하려는 말과 사정이 달랐다면, 이 기록에서 다른 경우들에 그래 왔음이 분명하듯이, 나는 그것을 밝히기를 망설이지 않았을 것이다. 그러나 단언하건대, 이 모든 교제를 통틀어, 나나 그가 잠자리에 있을 때 방에 드나든 거리낌 없음과, 그가 앓을 때 밤낮으로 시중든 불가피한 수발을 제하고는, 우리 사이에 조금이라도 정숙하지 못한 말이나 행동이 오간 적이 없었다."], ["Oh that it had been so to the last!", "아, 그것이 끝까지 그러했더라면!"]], [["After some time he gathered strength and grew well apace, and I would have removed my pallet-bed, but he would not let me, till he was able to venture himself without anybody to sit up with him, and then I removed to my own chamber.", "얼마 뒤 그는 기력을 차려 빠르게 나아 갔고, 나는 내 간이침대를 치우려 했으나, 그는 곁에서 밤새워 줄 사람 없이도 혼자 견딜 수 있게 될 때까지 그러지 못하게 했고, 그제야 나는 내 방으로 옮겼다."]], [["He took many occasions to express his sense of my tenderness and concern for him; and when he grew quite well, he made me a present of fifty guineas for my care and, as he called it, for hazarding my life to save his.", "그는 여러 기회에 나의 다정함과 자기를 향한 마음 씀에 대한 고마움을 표하였고, 완전히 나은 뒤에는 내게 오십 기니를 선물로 주었으니, 나를 돌봐 준 것과, 자기 말마따나 자기를 구하려 목숨을 건 것에 대한 것이었다."]], [["And now he made deep protestations of a sincere inviolable affection for me, but all along attested it to be with the utmost reserve for my virtue and his own.", "그리고 이제 그는 나를 향한 진실하고 깨뜨릴 수 없는 애정을 깊이 맹세했으나, 시종 그것이 내 덕성과 자기 자신의 덕성을 위해 더할 나위 없는 절제를 지킨 것임을 다짐했다."], ["I told him I was fully satisfied of it.", "나는 그것을 충분히 믿는다고 그에게 말했다."], ["He carried it that length that he protested to me, that if he was naked in bed with me, he would as sacredly preserve my virtue as he would defend it if I was assaulted by a ravisher.", "그는 그것을 어찌나 멀리까지 끌고 갔던지, 만약 자기가 나와 함께 벌거벗고 침대에 눕는다 해도, 마치 내가 능욕자에게 겁탈당할 때 막아 주기라도 하듯 신성하게 내 덕성을 지켜 주겠노라 맹세할 정도였다."], ["I believed him, and told him I did so; but this did not satisfy him, he would, he said, wait for some opportunity to give me an undoubted testimony of it.", "나는 그를 믿었고, 그렇다고 그에게 말했다. 그러나 이것으로도 그는 만족하지 못하여, 자기가 그것을 의심할 여지 없이 증명해 보일 어떤 기회를 기다리겠노라 했다."]], [["It was a great while after this that I had occasion, on my own business, to go to Bristol, upon which he hired me a coach, and would go with me, and did so; and now indeed our intimacy increased.", "이 일이 있은 지 한참 뒤, 내 볼일로 브리스틀에 갈 일이 생겼는데, 그러자 그는 나를 위해 마차를 빌려 나와 함께 가겠다고 했고 실제로 그렇게 했다. 그리하여 이제 우리의 친밀함은 실로 깊어졌다."], ["From Bristol he carried me to Gloucester, which was merely a journey of pleasure, to take the air; and here it was our hap to have no lodging in the inn but in one large chamber with two beds in it.", "브리스틀에서 그는 나를 글로스터로 데려갔는데, 그저 바람을 쐬려는 유흥의 여정이었다. 그리고 여기서 우리는 여관에 침대 둘이 놓인 큰 방 하나밖에 묵을 곳이 없는 처지가 되었다."], ["The master of the house going up with us to show his rooms, and coming into that room, said very frankly to him, “Sir, it is none of my business to inquire whether the lady be your spouse or no, but if not, you may lie as honestly in these two beds as if you were in two chambers,”", "집주인이 우리와 함께 올라가 방들을 보여 주다가 그 방에 들어와서는, 그에게 매우 거리낌 없이 말했다. \"나리, 이 부인이 나리의 부인이신지 아닌지 캐묻는 건 제 알 바 아닙니다만, 혹 아니시라면, 두 방에 따로 드신 거나 진배없이 이 두 침대에서 점잖게 주무실 수 있습죠.\""], ["and with that he pulls a great curtain which drew quite across the room and effectually divided the beds.", "그러면서 그는 방을 가로질러 완전히 쳐지는 큰 휘장을 끌어당겨, 침대들을 효과적으로 갈라놓았다. \"그렇군,\""], ["“Well,”", "하고 내 벗이 선뜻 말했다."], ["says my friend, very readily, “these beds will do, and as for the rest, we are too near akin to lie together, though we may lodge near one another”; and this put an honest face on the thing too.", "\"이 침대들이면 되겠소. 그리고 나머지로 말하자면, 우리는 함께 자기엔 너무 가까운 친척이라, 그저 서로 가까이 묵을 따름이오.\""], ["When we came to go to bed, he decently went out of the room till I was in bed, and then went to bed in the bed on his own side of the room, but lay there talking to me a great while.", "그리고 이 말은 그 일에도 정직한 낯을 씌워 주었다. 잠자리에 들 때가 되자 그는 내가 침대에 들 때까지 점잖게 방을 나가 있다가, 들어와서는 방의 자기 쪽 침대에 누웠는데, 거기 누운 채로 한참 동안 내게 말을 걸었다."]], [["At last, repeating his usual saying, that he could lie naked in the bed with me and not offer me the least injury, he starts out of his bed.", "마침내 그는 자기가 나와 함께 벌거벗고 침대에 누워도 조금의 해코지도 하지 않을 수 있다는, 늘 하던 그 말을 되뇌더니, 벌떡 자기 침대에서 일어났다."], ["“And now, my dear,”", "\"자, 이제, 여보,\""], ["says he, “you shall see how just I will be to you, and that I can keep my word,”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 당신께 얼마나 정직한지, 또 내가 약속을 지킬 수 있다는 걸 보여 주리다.\""], ["and away he comes to my bed.", "그러더니 그는 내 침대로 건너왔다."]], [["I resisted a little, but I must confess I should not have resisted him much if he had not made those promises at all; so after a little struggle, as I said, I lay still and let him come to bed.", "나는 조금 물리쳤으나, 고백하건대 그가 애당초 그런 약속들을 하지 않았더라면 나는 그를 그리 심하게 물리치지 않았을 것이다. 그래서 앞서 말했듯 잠깐의 실랑이 끝에, 나는 가만히 누워 그가 침대에 들도록 두었다."], ["When he was there he took me in his arms, and so I lay all night with him, but he had no more to do with me, or offered anything to me, other than embracing me, as I say, in his arms, no, not the whole night, but rose up and dressed him in the morning, and left me as innocent for him as I was the day I was born.", "그는 거기 와서 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았고, 그리하여 나는 밤새 그와 함께 누웠으나, 그는 내게 그 이상은 아무 짓도 하지 않았고, 말했다시피 두 팔로 끌어안는 것 외에 그 무엇도 청하지 않았으니, 밤새도록 그러했고, 다만 아침에 일어나 옷을 차려입고는, 나를 내가 태어난 날만큼이나 그에게 순결한 채로 남겨 두고 떠났다."]], [["This was a surprising thing to me, and perhaps may be so to others, who know how the laws of nature work; for he was a strong, vigorous, brisk person; nor did he act thus on a principle of religion at all, but of mere affection; insisting on it, that though I was to him the most agreeable woman in the world, yet, because he loved me, he could not injure me.", "이것은 내게 놀라운 일이었고, 자연의 법칙이 어떻게 작용하는지 아는 다른 이들에게도 아마 그러할 것이다. 그는 건장하고 정력이 넘치며 혈기 왕성한 사람이었으니 말이다. 또 그가 이렇게 처신한 것은 결코 신앙심의 원칙에서가 아니라 순전한 애정에서였으니, 비록 내가 세상에서 가장 마음에 드는 여자일지언정, 자기는 나를 사랑하기에 나를 해칠 수 없노라고 우겼다."]], [["I own it was a noble principle, but as it was what I never understood before, so it was to me perfectly amazing.", "나는 그것이 고귀한 원칙임을 인정하지만, 그것은 내가 전에 한 번도 이해해 본 적 없는 것이었던 만큼, 내게는 더할 나위 없이 놀라운 일이었다."], ["We traveled the rest of the journey as we did before, and came back to the Bath, where, as he had opportunity to come to me when he would, he often repeated the moderation, and I frequently lay with him, and he with me, and although all the familiarities between man and wife were common to us, yet he never once offered to go any farther, and he valued himself much upon it.", "우리는 나머지 여정을 전과 다름없이 다녔고 바스로 돌아왔다. 거기서 그는 마음 내킬 때면 언제든 내게 올 수 있었으므로 종종 그 절제를 되풀이했고, 나는 자주 그와 함께, 그는 나와 함께 누웠으며, 비록 부부 사이의 온갖 친밀함이 우리에게 예사로운 일이었으나, 그는 단 한 번도 그 이상으로 나아가려 하지 않았고, 그 점을 두고 스스로를 무척 대견해했다."], ["I do not say that I was so wholly pleased with it as he thought I was, for I own much wickeder than he, as you shall hear presently.", "그렇다고 내가 그가 생각한 만큼 그것에 온전히 흡족했다고는 말하지 않겠으니, 곧 여러분이 듣게 되겠지만 나는 그보다 훨씬 더 사악했음을 인정하기 때문이다."]], [["We lived thus near two years, only with this exception, that he went three times to London in that time, and once he continued there four months; but, to do him justice, he always supplied me with money to subsist me very handsomely.", "우리는 이렇게 거의 두 해를 살았는데, 다만 이런 예외가 있었으니, 그가 그동안 세 번 런던에 갔고, 한번은 거기서 넉 달을 머물렀다는 것이다. 그러나 그를 위해 공정히 말하자면, 그는 늘 나를 매우 넉넉하게 먹여 살릴 돈을 대 주었다."]], [["Had we continued thus, I confess we had had much to boast of; but as wise men say, it is ill venturing too near the brink of a command, so we found it; and here again I must do him the justice to own that the first breach was not on his part.", "우리가 계속 이렇게 살았더라면, 고백하건대 우리는 자랑할 거리가 많았을 것이다. 그러나 현명한 사람들이 말하듯, 계율의 벼랑 끝에 너무 가까이 다가가는 것은 위험한 짓이거니와, 우리가 바로 그러했다."], ["It was one night that we were in bed together warm and merry, and having drunk, I think, a little more wine that night, both of us, than usual, although not in the least to disorder either of us, when, after some other follies which I cannot name, and being clasped close in his arms, I told him (I repeat it with shame and horror of soul) that I could find in my heart to discharge him of his engagement for one night and no more.", "그리고 여기서 다시 나는 그를 위해 공정히 인정해야 하니, 첫 번째 허물은 그의 편에서 비롯된 것이 아니었다. 어느 날 밤 우리가 함께 침대에 누워 따뜻하고 흥겨워하다가, 둘 다 그날 밤 평소보다 술을 좀 더, 비록 조금도 둘 중 누구의 정신을 흩뜨릴 정도는 아니었으나, 마신 듯한데, 차마 입에 담을 수 없는 몇몇 객쩍은 짓 끝에, 그의 두 팔에 꼭 안긴 채로, 나는 그에게 (영혼의 수치와 전율 속에 거듭 말하거니와) 단 하룻밤만, 그 이상은 결코 아니되, 그의 맹세를 풀어 줄 마음이 든다고 말하고 말았다."]], [["He took me at my word immediately, and after that there was no resisting him; neither indeed had I any mind to resist him any more, let what would come of it.", "그는 곧바로 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들였고, 그 뒤로는 그를 물리칠 길이 없었다. 실로 나 또한 어떤 결과가 닥치든 더는 그를 물리칠 마음이 없었다."]], [["Thus the government of our virtue was broken, and I exchanged the place of friend for that unmusical, harsh-sounding title of whore.", "이리하여 우리 덕성의 다스림은 깨어졌고, 나는 벗이라는 자리를, 창녀라는 저 가락도 안 맞고 귀에 거슬리는 호칭과 맞바꾸었다."], ["In the morning we were both at our penitentials; I cried very heartily, he expressed himself very sorry; but that was all either of us could do at that time, and the way being thus cleared, and the bars of virtue and conscience thus removed, we had the less difficult afterwards to struggle with.", "아침에 우리는 둘 다 참회에 잠겼으니, 나는 매우 진심으로 울었고 그는 매우 미안한 마음을 드러냈다. 그러나 그것이 그때 우리 둘이 할 수 있는 전부였고, 이렇게 길이 트이고 덕성과 양심의 빗장이 이렇게 치워지자, 그 뒤로 우리가 씨름해야 할 어려움은 한결 줄어들었다."]], [["It was but a dull kind of conversation that we had together for all the rest of that week; I looked on him with blushes, and every now and then started that melancholy objection, “What if I should be with child now?", "그 주의 남은 날 내내 우리가 함께 나눈 대화는 그저 따분한 종류의 것이었다. 나는 얼굴을 붉히며 그를 바라보았고, 이따금씩 \"혹시 내가 지금 아이를 배면 어쩌지?"], ["What will become of me then?”", "그러면 나는 어찌 되는 거지?\""], ["He encouraged me by telling me, that as long as I was true to him, he would be so to me; and since it was gone such a length (which indeed he never intended), yet if I was with child, he would take care of that, and of me too.", "하는 그 우울한 걱정을 불쑥불쑥 꺼냈다. 그는 내가 자기에게 진실하기만 하다면 자기도 내게 그러할 것이라고, 또 일이 이렇게까지 멀리 갔으니(실로 그는 결코 그럴 작정은 아니었다) 만약 내가 아이를 배더라도 그것도 나도 잘 보살피겠노라 말하며 나를 다독였다."], ["This hardened us both.", "이것은 우리 둘을 다 무디게 만들었다."], ["I assured him if I was with child, I would die for want of a midwife rather than name him as the father of it; and he assured me I should never want if I should be with child.", "나는 만약 아이를 밴다면 산파가 없어 죽을지언정 그를 아비로 지목하지는 않겠노라 그에게 다짐했고, 그는 내가 아이를 배더라도 결코 궁하게 두지 않겠노라 내게 다짐했다."], ["These mutual assurances hardened us in the thing, and after this we repeated the crime as often as we pleased, till at length, as I had feared, so it came to pass, and I was indeed with child.", "이 서로의 다짐이 우리를 그 일에 무디게 만들었고, 그 뒤로 우리는 내키는 대로 그 죄를 거듭 저질렀으니, 마침내 내가 두려워하던 대로 그 일이 닥쳐와, 나는 실로 아이를 배게 되었다."]], [["After I was sure it was so, and I had satisfied him of it too, we began to think of taking measures for the managing it, and I proposed trusting the secret to my landlady, and asking her advice, which he agreed to.", "그것이 확실하다는 것을 알고, 또 그것을 그에게도 납득시킨 뒤, 우리는 그 일을 어떻게 처리할지 방도를 강구할 생각을 하기 시작했다. 나는 안주인에게 그 비밀을 털어놓고 그녀의 조언을 구하자고 제안했고, 그도 그에 동의했다."], ["My landlady, a woman (as I found) used to such things, made light of it; she said she knew it would come to that at last, and made us very merry about it.", "(내가 알게 된 바로는) 그런 일에 익숙한 여자인 내 안주인은 그것을 대수롭지 않게 여겼다. 그녀는 결국 그렇게 될 줄 알고 있었다고 말하며, 그 일을 두고 우리를 무척 즐겁게 해 주었다."], ["As I said above, we found her an experienced old lady at such work; she undertook everything, engaged to procure a midwife and a nurse, to satisfy all inquiries, and bring us off with reputation, and she did so very dexterously indeed.", "앞서 말했듯, 우리는 그녀가 그런 일에 노련한 노부인임을 알게 되었다. 그녀는 모든 일을 떠맡았으니, 산파와 유모를 구해 오고, 온갖 캐묻는 것을 무마하고, 우리를 평판에 흠 없이 빠져나오게 해 주겠노라 약속했고, 실로 매우 능란하게 그렇게 해냈다."]], [["When I grew near my time she desired my gentleman to go away to London, or make as if he did so.", "내 몸 풀 때가 가까워지자, 그녀는 내 신사더러 런던으로 떠나거나, 아니면 떠나는 척하라고 청했다."], ["When he was gone, she acquainted the parish officers that there was a lady ready to lie in at her house, but that she knew her husband very well, and gave them, as she pretended, an account of his name, which she called Sir Walter Cleve; telling them he was a very worthy gentleman, and that she would answer for all inquiries, and the like.", "그가 떠나자, 그녀는 자기 집에 곧 몸 풀 한 부인이 있는데, 자기는 그 남편을 매우 잘 안다며 교구 관리들에게 알렸고, 자기가 꾸며 댄 대로 그 이름을 일러 주었으니, 월터 클리브 경이라 했다. 그녀는 그가 매우 훌륭한 신사라고, 또 자기가 온갖 캐묻는 것에 책임지고 답하겠노라는 둥의 말을 했다."], ["This satisfied the parish officers presently, and I lay in with as much credit as I could have done if I had really been my Lady Cleve, and was assisted in my travail by three or four of the best citizens’ wives of Bath who lived in the neighbourhood, which, however, made me a little the more expensive to him.", "이것으로 교구 관리들은 곧바로 만족했고, 나는 실제로 내가 클리브 부인이라도 되었을 만큼이나 떳떳하게 몸을 풀었으며, 산고를 치를 때 인근에 사는 바스의 가장 점잖은 시민들의 아내 서넛의 도움을 받았으니, 다만 그 일로 그가 나 때문에 좀 더 비용을 들이게 되긴 했다."], ["I often expressed my concern to him about it, but he bid me not be concerned at it.", "나는 그것에 대해 종종 그에게 걱정을 내비쳤으나, 그는 그런 일로 걱정 말라고 일렀다."]], [["As he had furnished me very sufficiently with money for the extraordinary expenses of my lying in, I had everything very handsome about me, but did not affect to be gay or extravagant neither; besides, knowing my own circumstances, and knowing the world as I had done, and that such kind of things do not often last long, I took care to lay up as much money as I could for a wet day, as I called it; making him believe it was all spent upon the extraordinary appearance of things in my lying in.", "그가 내 몸 푸는 데에 드는 특별한 비용을 위해 돈을 매우 넉넉하게 대 준 덕에, 나는 주변의 모든 것을 매우 단정하게 갖추었으나, 그렇다고 화려하거나 사치스럽게 보이려 하지는 않았다. 게다가 내 형편을 알고, 또 내가 겪어 온 만큼 세상을 알며, 이런 종류의 일들이 오래 가지 않는 경우가 많다는 것을 아는지라, 나는 이른바 비 오는 날을 대비하여 할 수 있는 한 많은 돈을 챙겨 두려 애썼고, 그것이 다 내 몸 푸는 일의 그 특별한 겉치레에 쓰인 것처럼 그가 믿게 했다."]], [["By this means, and including what he had given me as above, I had at the end of my lying in about two hundred guineas by me, including also what was left of my own.", "이런 방편으로, 또 앞서 말한 대로 그가 내게 준 것까지 포함하여, 나는 몸 풀고 났을 즈음 내 손에 이백 기니쯤을 지니게 되었으니, 그중에는 본래 내 것에서 남은 것도 포함되어 있었다."]], [["I was brought to bed of a fine boy indeed, and a charming child it was; and when he heard of it he wrote me a very kind, obliging letter about it, and then told me, he thought it would look better for me to come away for London as soon as I was up and well; that he had provided apartments for me at Hammersmith, as if I came thither only from London; and that after a little while I should go back to the Bath, and he would go with me.", "나는 실로 잘생긴 사내아이를 낳았으니, 매력 넘치는 아이였다. 그가 그 소식을 듣고는 그것에 관해 매우 다정하고 자상한 편지를 내게 써 보냈고, 그러고는 내가 일어나 건강을 되찾는 대로 런던으로 떠나오는 편이 내게 더 좋아 보인다고 말했다. 자기가 해머스미스에 내가 묵을 거처를 마련해 두었으니, 마치 내가 런던에서 막 그리로 온 것처럼 하라고, 또 얼마 지나면 내가 다시 바스로 가야 할 텐데 그때는 자기가 나와 함께 가겠노라 했다."]], [["I liked this offer very well, and accordingly hired a coach on purpose, and taking my child, and a wet-nurse to tend and suckle it, and a maid-servant with me, away I went for London.", "나는 이 제안이 무척 마음에 들었고, 그리하여 일부러 마차 한 대를 빌려, 내 아이와, 그 아이를 돌보고 젖 먹일 유모와, 하녀 하나를 데리고 런던으로 떠났다."]], [["He met me at Reading in his own chariot, and taking me into that, left the servant and the child in the hired coach, and so he brought me to my new lodgings at Hammersmith; with which I had abundance of reason to be very well pleased, for they were very handsome rooms, and I was very well accommodated.", "그는 자기 마차를 타고 레딩에서 나를 맞았고, 나를 그 마차에 태우더니, 하인과 아이는 빌린 마차에 남겨 둔 채, 그렇게 나를 해머스미스의 새 거처로 데려갔다. 그곳에 대해 나는 매우 흡족해할 까닭이 충분했으니, 매우 단정한 방들이었고 나는 매우 잘 갖춰진 살림을 누렸기 때문이다."]], [["And now I was indeed in the height of what I might call my prosperity, and I wanted nothing but to be a wife, which, however, could not be in this case, there was no room for it; and therefore on all occasions I studied to save what I could, as I have said above, against a time of scarcity, knowing well enough that such things as these do not always continue; that men that keep mistresses often change them, grow weary of them, or jealous of them, or something or other happens to make them withdraw their bounty; and sometimes the ladies that are thus well used are not careful by a prudent conduct to preserve the esteem of their persons, or the nice article of their fidelity, and then they are justly cast off with contempt.", "그리고 이제 나는 실로 내가 번영이라 부를 만한 것의 절정에 있었고, 아내가 되는 것 말고는 부족한 것이 없었다. 그러나 이 경우에 그것은 될 수 없는 일이었고, 그럴 여지가 없었다. 그러므로 나는 앞서 말했듯, 모든 기회마다 궁핍의 때를 대비하여 모을 수 있는 것을 모으려 애썼으니, 이런 일들이 늘 이어지지는 않는다는 것을, 정부情婦를 두는 남자들은 흔히 그들을 갈아 치우고, 싫증을 내거나 질투하게 되며, 혹은 무슨 일인가가 생겨 그 너그러움을 거두게 마련이라는 것을, 또 더러 이렇게 좋은 대접을 받는 여자들이 신중한 처신으로 제 몸의 존중이나 정절이라는 미묘한 조목을 지키는 데에 소홀하여, 그러면 경멸 속에 마땅히 버림받는다는 것을 충분히 잘 알았기 때문이다."]], [["But I was secured in this point, for as I had no inclination to change, so I had no manner of acquaintance in the whole house, and so no temptation to look any farther.", "그러나 나는 이 점에서는 안전했으니, 나는 갈아 치울 마음이 없었던 데다, 온 집 안에 아는 사람이라곤 전혀 없어서 더 멀리 눈 돌릴 유혹도 없었기 때문이다. 나는 묵는 집의 식구들과, 옆집 목사 부인 말고는 어떤 무리와도 어울리지 않았다."], ["I kept no company but in the family when I lodged, and with the clergyman’s lady at next door; so that when he was absent I visited nobody, nor did he ever find me out of my chamber or parlour whenever he came down; if I went anywhere to take the air, it was always with him.", "그래서 그가 없을 때면 나는 누구도 찾아가지 않았고, 그가 내려올 때면 그는 언제나 나를 내 방이나 응접실에서 보았다. 어디든 바람을 쐬러 갈 때면 그것은 늘 그와 함께였다."]], [["The living in this manner with him, and his with me, was certainly the most undesigned thing in the world; he often protested to me, that when he became first acquainted with me, and even to the very night when we first broke in upon our rules, he never had the least design of lying with me; that he always had a sincere affection for me, but not the least real inclination to do what he had done. I assured him I never suspected him; that if I had I should not so easily have yielded to the freedom which brought it on, but that it was all a surprise, and was owing to the accident of our having yielded too far to our mutual inclinations that night; and indeed I have often observed since, and leave it as a caution to the readers of this story, that we ought to be cautious of gratifying our inclinations in loose and lewd freedoms, lest we find our resolutions of virtue fail us in the junction when their assistance should be most necessary.", "이렇게 그와 함께, 또 그가 나와 함께 산 것은 분명 세상에서 가장 의도치 않은 일이었다. 그는 종종 내게 맹세하기를, 처음 나와 알게 되었을 때부터, 심지어 우리가 처음으로 우리 규율을 깨뜨린 바로 그날 밤까지도, 자기는 나와 동침할 의도를 조금도 품은 적이 없었다고, 자기는 늘 나를 진실하게 사랑했으나 자기가 한 그 짓을 하려는 실제 의향은 조금도 없었다고 했다. 나는 그를 한 번도 의심한 적이 없다고, 만약 의심했더라면 그 일을 불러온 거리낌 없음에 그토록 쉽게 굴복하지 않았을 것이라고, 그러나 그것은 모두 불시의 일이었으며, 그날 밤 우리가 서로의 의향에 너무 멀리 굴복하고 만 우연에서 비롯된 것이라고 그에게 다짐했다. 그리고 실로 나는 그 뒤로 종종 깨달았으니, 이 이야기의 독자 여러분에게 경계로 남겨 두거니와, 우리는 헤프고 음란한 거리낌 없음 속에서 우리 의향을 채우는 것을 조심해야 마땅하다는 것이다. 자칫하면 덕성의 도움이 가장 절실히 필요한 그 길목에서, 덕을 지키려던 우리의 결심이 우리를 저버리는 것을 보게 될 테니 말이다."]], [["It is true, and I have confessed it before, that from the first hour I began to converse with him, I resolved to let him lie with me, if he offered it; but it was because I wanted his help and assistance, and I knew no other way of securing him than that.", "사실, 그리고 내가 앞서 고백했듯이, 그와 처음 대화를 나누기 시작한 그 시각부터, 나는 그가 청한다면 그가 나와 동침하도록 허락하기로 작정했다. 그러나 그것은 내가 그의 도움과 부조를 원했고, 그를 붙잡아 둘 다른 방도를 알지 못했기 때문이었다."], ["But when we were that night together, and, as I have said, had gone such a length, I found my weakness; the inclination was not to be resisted, but I was obliged to yield up all even before he asked it.", "그러나 그날 밤 우리가 함께 있다가, 앞서 말했듯 그토록 멀리까지 나아갔을 때, 나는 내 나약함을 깨달았다. 그 의향은 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 것이어서, 나는 그가 청하기도 전에 모든 것을 내어 줄 수밖에 없었다."]], [["However, he was so just to me that he never upbraided me with that; nor did he ever express the least dislike of my conduct on any other occasion, but always protested he was as much delighted with my company as he was the first hour we came together: I mean, came together as bedfellows.", "그러나 그는 내게 너무도 공정하여 그 일로 나를 한 번도 나무라지 않았고, 그 어떤 다른 경우에도 내 처신에 대해 조금의 못마땅함도 드러낸 적이 없었으며, 도리어 우리가 처음 함께한 그 시각만큼이나 늘 내 동반에 기뻐한다고 맹세했다. 함께했다는 것은, 곧 잠자리를 함께하는 사이로서 함께했다는 뜻이다."]], [["It is true that he had no wife, that is to say, she was as no wife to him, and so I was in no danger that way, but the just reflections of conscience oftentimes snatch a man, especially a man of sense, from the arms of a mistress, as it did him at last, though on another occasion.", "그에게 아내가 없었다는 것은, 곧 그녀가 그에게 아내나 다름없는 존재가 아니었다는 것은 사실이고, 그래서 나는 그쪽으로는 아무 위험이 없었다. 그러나 양심의 정당한 성찰은 흔히 한 남자를, 특히 분별 있는 남자를, 정부의 품에서 낚아채는 법이니, 비록 다른 계기에서이긴 했으나, 마침내 그에게도 그러했다."], ["On the other hand, though I was not without secret reproaches of my own conscience for the life I led, and that even in the greatest height of the satisfaction I ever took, yet I had the terrible prospect of poverty and starving, which lay on me as a frightful spectre, so that there was no looking behind me.", "다른 한편으로, 나 또한 내가 살아온 삶에 대해 양심의 은밀한 책망이 없지 않았고, 그것은 내가 누린 가장 큰 만족의 절정에서조차 그러했으나, 가난과 굶주림이라는 끔찍한 전망이 무서운 유령처럼 나를 짓눌렀으므로, 뒤돌아볼 수가 없었다."], ["But as poverty brought me into it, so fear of poverty kept me in it, and I frequently resolved to leave it quite off, if I could but come to lay up money enough to maintain me.", "그러나 가난이 나를 그 길로 끌어들였듯, 가난에 대한 두려움이 나를 그 안에 붙들어 두었고, 나는 종종 나를 부양할 만큼의 돈을 모을 수만 있다면 그것을 아주 끊어 버리리라 결심했다. 그러나 이런 것들은 무게 없는 생각들이어서, 그가 내게 올 때면 모조리 사라졌다."], ["But these were thoughts of no weight, and whenever he came to me they vanished; for his company was so delightful, that there was no being melancholy when he was there; the reflections were all the subject of those hours when I was alone.", "그의 동반이 어찌나 즐거웠던지 그가 곁에 있을 때면 우울해질 수가 없었다. 그런 성찰은 모두 내가 혼자 있던 그 시간들의 몫이었다."]], [["I lived six years in this happy but unhappy condition, in which time I brought him three children, but only the first of them lived; and though I removed twice in those six years, yet I came back the sixth year to my first lodgings at Hammersmith.", "나는 이 행복하면서도 불행한 처지로 여섯 해를 살았으니, 그동안 그에게 아이 셋을 낳아 주었으나 그중 첫째만 살았다. 그리고 비록 그 여섯 해 사이에 두 번 거처를 옮겼어도, 여섯 해째에 나는 해머스미스의 첫 거처로 돌아왔다."], ["Here it was that I was one morning surprised with a kind but melancholy letter from my gentleman, intimating that he was very ill, and was afraid he should have another fit of sickness, but that his wife’s relations being in the house with him, it would not be practicable to have me with him, which, however, he expressed his great dissatisfaction in, and that he wished I could be allowed to tend and nurse him as I did before.", "바로 여기서 나는 어느 날 아침, 내 신사가 보낸 다정하면서도 우울한 편지에 놀라게 되었다. 그가 매우 아프며 또 한 차례 병이 도질까 두렵다는 것, 그러나 그의 처가 친척들이 집에 그와 함께 있어 나를 그의 곁에 둘 수 없겠다는 것을 넌지시 알리는 내용이었는데, 그래도 그는 그 점에 대한 큰 불만을 드러내며, 전처럼 내가 그를 돌보고 간호하도록 허락받을 수 있으면 좋겠다고 했다."]], [["I was very much concerned at this account, and was very impatient to know how it was with him.", "나는 이 소식에 무척 마음이 쓰였고, 그가 어떤 상태인지 알고 싶어 몹시 안달이 났다."], ["I waited a fortnight or thereabouts, and heard nothing, which surprised me, and I began to be very uneasy indeed.", "나는 보름 남짓 기다렸으나 아무 소식도 듣지 못했고, 그것이 나를 놀라게 하여 실로 매우 불안해지기 시작했다. 다음 보름 동안 나는 거의 정신이 나갈 지경이었다고 말할 수 있을 것 같다."], ["I think, I may say, that for the next fortnight I was near to distracted.", "내게 각별히 힘들었던 것은 그가 정확히 어디에 있는지 알지 못한다는 것이었다."], ["It was my particular difficulty that I did not know directly where he was; for I understood at first he was in the lodgings of his wife’s mother; but having removed myself to London, I soon found, by the help of the direction I had for writing my letters to him, how to inquire after him, and there I found that he was at a house in Bloomsbury, whither he had, a little before he fell sick, removed his whole family; and that his wife and wife’s mother were in the same house, though the wife was not suffered to know that she was in the same house with her husband.", "처음에 나는 그가 처가 어머니의 거처에 있다고 알았으나, 내가 런던으로 거처를 옮긴 뒤로, 그에게 편지를 부치려고 받아 둔 주소의 도움으로, 곧 그를 수소문할 방도를 찾아냈고, 그리하여 그가 블룸즈베리의 어느 집에 있다는 것을 알아냈다. 그는 앓아눕기 조금 전에 온 식솔을 그리로 옮겼고, 그의 처와 처가 어머니도 같은 집에 있었으나, 다만 그 처는 자기가 남편과 같은 집에 있다는 것을 알지 못하도록 되어 있었다."]], [["Here I also soon understood that he was at the last extremity, which made me almost at the last extremity too, to have a true account.", "여기서 나는 또한 곧, 그가 마지막 고비에 이르렀음을 알게 되었고, 그것은 나 또한 그에 대한 참된 소식을 얻고자 거의 마지막 고비에 이르게 했다. 어느 날 밤 나는 호기심에 이끌려 동그란 모자에 밀짚모자를 쓴 하녀 차림으로 변장하여 그 집 문으로 갔다."], ["One night I had the curiosity to disguise myself like a servant-maid, in a round cap and straw hat, and went to the door, as sent by a lady of his neighbourhood, where he lived before, and giving master and mistress’s service, I said I was sent to know how Mr. —— did, and how he had rested that night.", "그가 전에 살던 동네의 어느 부인이 보내서 온 것처럼, 주인 내외께 안부를 여쭙는다며, 나는 ―― 씨께서 어떠하신지, 또 간밤에 어떻게 주무셨는지 알아보러 왔다고 말했다."], ["In delivering this message I got the opportunity I desired; for, speaking with one of the maids, I held a long gossip’s tale with her, and had all the particulars of his illness, which I found was a pleurisy, attended with a cough and a fever.", "이 전갈을 전하면서 나는 바라던 기회를 얻었으니, 하녀 가운데 하나와 이야기를 나누다가 그녀와 오래도록 수다를 떨어, 그의 병에 관한 온갖 자세한 사정을 죄다 알아냈다. 알고 보니 그것은 늑막염으로, 기침과 열을 동반한 것이었다."], ["She told me also who was in the house, and how his wife was, who, by her relation, they were in some hopes might recover her understanding; but as to the gentleman himself, in short she told me the doctors said there was very little hopes of him, that in the morning they thought he had been dying, and that he was but little better then, for they did not expect that he could live over the next night.", "그녀는 또한 누가 집에 있는지, 그의 처는 어떠한지도 일러 주었는데, 그녀의 말로는 처가 정신을 되찾을 가망이 좀 있다고들 한다고 했다. 그러나 신사 자신에 관해서는, 한마디로 의사들이 그에게 희망이 거의 없다고 했다는 것, 아침에는 그가 거의 죽어 가는 줄 알았다는 것, 그리고 그때도 좀 나아졌을 뿐이어서 다음 날 밤을 넘기지 못하리라 본다는 것이었다."]], [["This was heavy news for me, and I began now to see an end of my prosperity, and to see also that it was very well I had played to good housewife, and secured or saved something while he was alive, for that now I had no view of my own living before me.", "이것은 내게 무거운 소식이었고, 나는 이제 내 번영의 끝을 보기 시작했으며, 또한 그가 살아 있는 동안 내가 알뜰한 살림꾼 노릇을 하여 무언가를 챙기거나 모아 둔 것이 참으로 다행이었음을 보게 되었으니, 이제 내 앞에는 내 살길에 대한 어떤 전망도 없었기 때문이다."]], [["It lay very heavy upon my mind, too, that I had a son, a fine lovely boy, about five years old, and no provision made for it, at least that I knew of.", "또한 내게는 다섯 살쯤 된, 잘생기고 사랑스러운 사내아이 하나가 있는데, 적어도 내가 아는 한, 그 아이를 위해 마련된 것이 아무것도 없다는 사실이 내 마음을 몹시 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["With these considerations, and a sad heart, I went home that evening, and began to cast with myself how I should live, and in what manner to bestow myself, for the residue of my life.", "이런 생각들로 슬픈 마음을 안고 그날 저녁 나는 집으로 돌아와, 내 남은 평생을 어떻게 살아야 할지, 또 어떤 식으로 내 몸을 의탁해야 할지 혼자 궁리하기 시작했다."]], [["You may be sure I could not rest without inquiring again very quickly what was become of him; and not venturing to go myself, I sent several sham messengers, till after a fortnight’s waiting longer, I found that there was hopes of his life, though he was still very ill; then I abated my sending any more to the house, and in some time after I learned in the neighbourhood that he was about house, and then that he was abroad again.", "여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 나는 그가 어떻게 되었는지를 다시 한번 매우 서둘러 알아보지 않고는 가만히 있을 수가 없었다. 그리고 감히 내가 직접 가지는 못한 채, 나는 여러 차례 가짜 심부름꾼을 보냈고, 그리하여 다시 보름을 더 기다린 끝에, 비록 그가 여전히 매우 아프기는 해도 살아날 가망이 있다는 것을 알게 되었다. 그제야 나는 그 집에 더 사람을 보내는 일을 줄였고, 얼마 뒤 동네에서 그가 집 안을 돌아다닌다는 것을, 그리고 다시 바깥출입을 한다는 것을 알게 되었다."]], [["I made no doubt then but that I should soon hear of him, and began to comfort myself with my circumstances being, as I thought, recovered.", "그래서 나는 머지않아 그에게서 소식을 들으리라 의심치 않았고, 내가 생각하기에 내 형편이 회복되었다며 스스로 마음을 다독이기 시작했다."], ["I waited a week, and two weeks, and with much surprise and amazement I waited near two months and heard nothing, but that, being recovered, he was gone into the country for the air, and for the better recovery after his distemper.", "나는 한 주, 또 두 주를 기다렸고, 그러다 무척 놀랍고 어이없게도 거의 두 달을 기다렸으나, 그가 회복하여 바람을 쐬러, 또 병을 앓고 난 뒤 더 잘 추스르려 시골로 갔다는 것 말고는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다."], ["After this it was yet two months more, and then I understood he was come to his city house again, but still I heard nothing from him.", "그 뒤로 또 두 달이 더 지나서야, 나는 그가 다시 그의 시내 집으로 돌아왔다는 것을 알게 되었으나, 그래도 여전히 그에게서는 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다."]], [["I had written several letters for him, and directed them as usual, and found two or three of them had been called for, but not the rest.", "나는 그에게 편지를 여러 통 써서 늘 하던 대로 주소를 적어 보냈는데, 그중 두세 통은 그가 찾아갔으나 나머지는 그러지 않았음을 알게 되었다."], ["I wrote again in a more pressing manner than ever, and in one of them let him know, that I must be forced to wait on him myself, representing my circumstances, the rent of lodgings to pay, and the provision for the child wanting, and my own deplorable condition, destitute of subsistence for his most solemn engagement to take care of and provide for me.", "나는 어느 때보다 더 다급한 투로 다시 편지를 썼고, 그중 한 통에서는, 나로서는 내가 직접 그를 찾아갈 수밖에 없겠다고 알리며, 내 형편을, 내야 할 거처 임대료를, 아이에게 필요한 양육비가 부족하다는 것을, 또 그가 나를 보살피고 돌봐 주겠다던 그토록 엄숙한 맹세에도 불구하고 생계 수단마저 없는 내 비참한 처지를 호소했다."], ["I took a copy of this letter, and finding it lay at the house near a month and was not called for, I found means to have the copy of it put into his own hands at a coffee-house, where I had by inquiry found he used to go.", "나는 이 편지의 사본을 떠 두었고, 그 편지가 그 집에 거의 한 달이나 놓여 있으면서도 찾아가지 않는 것을 알자, 수소문 끝에 그가 자주 다닌다는 것을 알아낸 어느 커피점에서, 그 사본을 그의 손에 직접 쥐여 줄 방도를 찾아냈다."]], [["This letter forced an answer from him, by which, though I found I was to be abandoned, yet I found he had sent a letter to me some time before, desiring me to go down to the Bath again.", "이 편지는 그에게서 답장을 끌어냈는데, 그 답장으로 나는 비록 내가 버림받게 되리라는 것을 알았으나, 또한 그가 얼마 전에 내게 편지 한 통을 보내, 다시 바스로 내려가기를 청했다는 것도 알게 되었다."], ["Its contents I shall come to presently.", "그 내용은 곧이어 말하겠다."]], [["It is true that sick-beds are the time when such correspondences as this are looked on with different countenances, and seen with other eyes than we saw them with, or than they appeared with before.", "사실 병상은 이런 종류의 교제가 다른 낯빛으로 비치고, 우리가 전에 보던 것과는, 또는 전에 보이던 것과는 다른 눈으로 보이게 되는 때이다."], ["My lover had been at the gates of death, and at the very brink of eternity; and, it seems, had been struck with a due remorse, and with sad reflections upon his past life of gallantry and levity; and among the rest, criminal correspondence with me, which was neither more nor less than a long-continued life of adultery, and represented itself as it really was, not as it had been formerly thought by him to be, and he looked upon it now with a just and religious abhorrence.", "내 연인은 죽음의 문턱에, 영원의 벼랑 끝에 다다랐던 것이고, 보아하니 마땅한 회한에, 또 풍류와 경박함으로 지나온 자기 삶에 대한 슬픈 성찰에 사로잡혔던 것이다. 그 가운데서도 특히 나와의 그 죄스러운 교제가, 그것은 다름 아닌 오래 이어진 간음의 삶이었거니와, 그것이 본래 그러했던 모습 그대로, 곧 전에 그가 여기던 것과는 다른 모습으로 드러났고, 이제 그는 그것을 정당하고 신앙심 어린 혐오로 바라보았다."]], [["I cannot but observe also, and leave it for the direction of my sex in such cases of pleasure, that whenever sincere repentance succeeds such a crime as this, there never fails to attend a hatred of the object; and the more the affection might seem to be before, the hatred will be the more in proportion. It will always be so, indeed it can be no otherwise; for there cannot be a true and sincere abhorrence of the offence, and the love to the cause of it remain; there will, with an abhorrence of the sin, be found a detestation of the fellow-sinner; you can expect no other.", "나는 또한 다음을 언급하지 않을 수 없으니, 그런 쾌락의 경우에 처한 내 동성同性들에게 길잡이로 남겨 두거니와, 이와 같은 죄 뒤에 진실한 회개가 뒤따를 때면, 그 대상에 대한 미움이 반드시 함께 따라온다는 것이다. 그리고 전에 그 애정이 커 보였을수록, 그 미움도 그에 비례하여 더 커질 것이다. 언제나 그러할 것이며, 실로 달리 될 수가 없다. 그 죄에 대한 참되고 진실한 혐오가 있으면서, 동시에 그 원인에 대한 사랑이 남아 있을 수는 없기 때문이다. 죄에 대한 혐오와 더불어 함께 죄지은 자에 대한 증오가 따라오게 마련이니, 그 밖의 것은 기대할 수 없다."]], [["I found it so here, though good manners and justice in this gentleman kept him from carrying it on to any extreme but the short history of his part in this affair was thus: he perceived by my last letter, and by all the rest, which he went for after, that I was not gone to Bath, that his first letter had not come to my hand; upon which he write me this following:—", "여기서도 나는 그러함을 알았으니, 다만 이 신사의 경우 좋은 예의와 정의감이 그가 그것을 어떤 극단으로까지 밀고 가지 못하게 막았다. 이 일에서 그가 맡은 몫의 짧은 내력은 이러했다. 그는 내 마지막 편지로, 또 그가 나중에 찾아간 나머지 모든 편지로, 내가 바스로 가지 않았다는 것을, 자기 첫 편지가 내 손에 닿지 않았다는 것을 알아챘다. 그래서 그는 내게 다음과 같이 써 보냈다."]], [["“MADAM,—I am surprised that my letter, dated the 8th of last month, did not come to your hand; I give you my word it was delivered at your lodgings, and to the hands of your maid.", "\"부인, 지난달 8일 자로 보낸 내 편지가 당신 손에 닿지 않았다니 놀랍소. 내 장담하건대 그것은 당신 거처로, 또 당신 하녀의 손에 전해졌소."]], [["I need not acquaint you with what has been my condition for some time past; and how, having been at the edge of the grave, I am, by the unexpected and undeserved mercy of Heaven, restored again.", "지난 한동안 내가 어떤 처지에 있었는지, 또 어떻게 내가 무덤의 벼랑 끝에 이르렀다가 하늘의 뜻밖이며 분에 넘치는 자비로 다시 회복되었는지를 굳이 당신께 알릴 필요는 없겠소."], ["In the condition I have been in, it cannot be strange to you that our unhappy correspondence had not been the least of the burthens which lay upon my conscience.", "내가 처해 있던 그 처지에서, 우리의 불행한 교제가 내 양심을 짓누른 짐 가운데 결코 가장 작은 것이 아니었다는 사실이 당신께는 이상하게 들리지 않을 것이오."], ["I need say no more; those things that must be repented of, must be also reformed.", "더 말할 필요는 없겠소. 회개해야 마땅한 그런 일들은 또한 바로잡혀야 마땅한 법이오."]], [["I wish you would think of going back to the Bath.", "당신이 바스로 돌아가는 것을 생각해 주기 바라오."], ["I enclose you here a bill for £50 for clearing yourself at your lodgings, and carrying you down, and hope it will be no surprise to you to add, that on this account only, and not for any offence given me on your side, I can _see you no more_.", "여기 당신 거처의 셈을 치르고 당신을 그리로 내려보낼 비용으로 오십 파운드짜리 어음을 동봉하오. 그리고 다음을 덧붙이는 것이 당신께 놀라움이 되지 않기를 바라거니와, 오로지 이런 까닭에서이지 당신 편에서 내게 무슨 잘못을 저질러서가 아니라, 나는 더는 당신을 볼 수 없소."], ["I will take due care of the child; leave him where he is, or take him with you, as you please.", "아이는 내가 마땅히 보살피겠소. 당신 좋을 대로, 아이를 있는 곳에 두든 데려가든 하시오."], ["I wish you the like reflections, and that they may be to your advantage.—I am,”", "당신께도 그와 같은 성찰이 있기를, 또 그것이 당신께 이로움이 되기를 바라오. 그럼 이만,\""], ["etc.", "운운."]], [["I was struck with this letter as with a thousand wounds, such as I cannot describe; the reproaches of my own conscience were such as I cannot express, for I was not blind to my own crime; and I reflected that I might with less offence have continued with my brother, and lived with him as a wife, since there was no crime in our marriage on that score, neither of us knowing it.", "나는 이 편지에 천 군데를 찔린 듯한 상처를 입었으니, 차마 형언할 수 없는 그런 것이었다. 내 양심의 책망은 이루 말할 수 없는 것이었으니, 나는 내 죄에 눈멀어 있지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 나는, 차라리 내 오라비와 계속 머물러 아내로서 그와 사는 편이 죄가 덜했으리라 성찰했다. 우리 둘 다 그것을 알지 못했으니, 그 점에서는 우리의 혼인에 죄가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["But I never once reflected that I was all this while a married woman, a wife to Mr.", "그러나 나는 이 모든 동안 내가 유부녀라는 것을, ―― 씨, 곧 그 포목상의 아내라는 것을 단 한 번도 성찰하지 못했다."], ["—— the linen-draper, who, though he had left me by the necessity of his circumstances, had no power to discharge me from the marriage contract which was between us, or to give me a legal liberty to marry again; so that I had been no less than a whore and an adulteress all this while.", "그는 비록 자기 형편의 어쩔 수 없는 사정으로 나를 떠났으나, 우리 사이에 맺어진 혼인 계약에서 나를 풀어 줄 권한도, 내가 다시 혼인할 법적 자유를 줄 권한도 없었다. 그러니 나는 이 모든 동안 다름 아닌 한낱 창녀요 간음한 여자에 지나지 않았던 것이다."], ["I then reproached myself with the liberties I had taken, and how I had been a snare to this gentleman, and that indeed I was principal in the crime; that now he was mercifully snatched out of the gulf by a convincing work upon his mind, but that I was left as if I was forsaken of God’s grace, and abandoned by Heaven to a continuing in my wickedness.", "그리하여 나는 내가 누렸던 그 거리낌 없음을 두고, 또 내가 이 신사에게 어떻게 덫이 되었는지를 두고 나 자신을 책망했으며, 실로 내가 그 죄의 주범이었음을, 이제 그는 마음에 일어난 깨우침의 역사役事로 자비롭게 그 구렁에서 낚여 올려졌으나, 나는 마치 하느님의 은총에서 버림받은 듯, 또 내 사악함에 머물도록 하늘이 내버려 둔 듯 남겨졌음을 책망했다."]], [["Under these reflections I continued very pensive and sad for near month, and did not go down to the Bath, having no inclination to be with the woman whom I was with before; lest, as I thought, she should prompt me to some wicked course of life again, as she had done; and besides, I was very loth she should know I was cast off as above.", "이런 성찰 속에 나는 거의 한 달 동안 매우 시름에 잠겨 슬프게 지냈고, 바스로 내려가지 않았으니, 전에 함께 지내던 그 여자와 함께 있고 싶은 마음이 없었기 때문이다. 내 생각에, 그녀가 전에 그랬듯이 나를 다시 무슨 사악한 삶의 길로 부추길까 두려웠던 것이다. 게다가 나는 그녀가 앞서 말한 대로 내가 버림받았다는 것을 아는 것이 무척이나 싫었다."]], [["And now I was greatly perplexed about my little boy.", "그리고 이제 나는 내 어린 아들 일로 몹시 골치가 아팠다."], ["It was death to me to part with the child, and yet when I considered the danger of being one time or other left with him to keep without a maintenance to support him, I then resolved to leave him where he was; but then I concluded also to be near him myself too, that I then might have the satisfaction of seeing him, without the care of providing for him.", "그 아이와 헤어지는 것은 내게 죽음 같은 일이었으나, 언젠가 그 아이를 부양할 생계 수단도 없이 그 아이만 떠안게 될 위험을 생각하니, 그제야 나는 그 아이를 있는 곳에 두기로 결심했다. 그러나 그러면서도 나 또한 그 아이 가까이에 있어, 그 아이를 부양하는 짐은 지지 않으면서도 그 아이를 보는 만족은 누릴 수 있게 하리라 작정했다."]], [["I sent my gentleman a short letter, therefore, that I had obeyed his orders in all things but that of going back to the Bath, which I could not think of for many reasons; that however parting from him was a wound to me that I could never recover, yet that I was fully satisfied his reflections were just, and would be very far from desiring to obstruct his reformation or repentance.", "그리하여 나는 내 신사에게 짤막한 편지를 보내, 바스로 돌아가는 것 말고는 모든 일에서 그의 분부를 따랐다고, 그것만은 여러 까닭으로 도저히 생각할 수 없었다고 말했다. 또 그와 헤어지는 것은 내가 결코 회복할 수 없는 상처이긴 하나, 그래도 나는 그의 성찰이 정당하다는 것에 온전히 수긍하며, 그의 개심이나 회개를 가로막을 마음은 추호도 없다고 했다."]], [["Then I represented my own circumstances to him in the most moving terms that I was able.", "그러고 나서 나는 내가 할 수 있는 가장 가슴 저미는 말로 내 형편을 그에게 호소했다. 나는 처음에 그를 움직여 나를 향한 너그럽고 정직한 우정을 품게 한 그 불행한 곤경이, 비록 우리 교제의 죄스러운 부분은, 그것은 우리 둘 다 그때는 빠질 작정이 아니었다고 믿거니와, 끊어졌을지언정, 이제 나를 향한 작은 마음 씀을 그에게 일으켜 주기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["I told him that those unhappy distresses which first moved him to a generous and an honest friendship for me, would, I hope, move him to a little concern for me now, though the criminal part of our correspondence, which I believed neither of us intended to fall into at the time, was broken off; that I desired to repent as sincerely as he had done, but entreated him to put me in some condition that I might not be exposed to the temptations which the devil never fails to excite us to from the frightful prospect of poverty and distress; and if he had the least apprehensions of my being troublesome to him, I begged he would put me in a posture to go back to my mother in Virginia, from when he knew I came, and that would put an end to all his fears on that account.", "또 나도 그가 한 만큼 진실하게 회개하기를 바라나, 다만 가난과 곤경이라는 끔찍한 전망에서 악마가 결코 우리를 부추기기를 게을리하지 않는 그 유혹에 내가 내몰리지 않도록, 나를 어떤 처지에 놓아 달라고 그에게 간청했다. 그리고 만약 그가 내가 자기에게 성가신 존재가 될까 조금이라도 염려한다면, 그가 알다시피 내가 떠나온 곳, 버지니아의 어머니에게로 돌아갈 수 있게 나를 채비시켜 주기를 빌었으니, 그러면 그 점에 대한 그의 모든 두려움이 끝날 것이라고 했다."], ["I concluded, that if he would send me £50 more to facilitate my going away, I would send him back a general release, and would promise never to disturb him more with any importunities; unless it was to hear of the well-doing of the child, whom, if I found my mother living and my circumstances able, I would send for to come over to me, and take him also effectually off his hands.", "끝으로 나는, 만약 그가 내가 떠나는 일을 수월케 하도록 오십 파운드를 더 보내 준다면, 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서를 되돌려 보내겠노라고, 또 아이의 잘 지내는 소식을 듣고자 하는 것 말고는 다시는 어떤 성가신 졸라댐으로도 그를 어지럽히지 않겠노라 약속했다. 그 아이는, 만약 내 어머니가 살아 계시고 내 형편이 허락한다면, 내가 데려와 내게 오게 하여, 또한 그것을 그의 손에서 완전히 덜어 주겠노라 했다."]], [["This was indeed all a cheat thus far, viz.", "이것은 실로 여기까지는 모두 거짓이었으니, 곧 나는 버지니아로 갈 의도가 전혀 없었던 것이다."], ["that I had no intention to go to Virginia, as the account of my former affairs there may convince anybody of; but the business was to get this last £50 of him, if possible, knowing well enough it would be the last penny I was ever to expect.", "그곳에서의 내 지난 일에 대한 기록이 누구든 그 점을 납득시켜 줄 터이다. 그러나 핵심은 가능하다면 그에게서 이 마지막 오십 파운드를 받아 내는 것이었으니, 그것이 내가 그에게서 기대할 수 있는 마지막 한 푼이 되리라는 것을 충분히 잘 알고 있었기 때문이다."]], [["However, the argument I used, namely, of giving him a general release, and never troubling him any more, prevailed effectually with him, and he sent me a bill for the money by a person who brought with him a general release for me to sign, and which I frankly signed, and received the money; and thus, though full sore against my will, a final end was put to this affair.", "그러나 내가 쓴 논리, 곧 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서를 주고 다시는 그를 성가시게 하지 않겠다는 그 논리가 그에게 효과적으로 먹혀들었고, 그는 사람을 시켜 내게 그 돈의 어음을 보내왔는데, 그 사람은 내가 서명할 전면적인 면제 증서를 함께 가져왔다. 나는 그것에 선선히 서명하고 돈을 받았다. 그리하여, 비록 내 뜻과는 너무도 어긋났으나, 이 일에는 마침내 종지부가 찍혔다."]], [["And here I cannot but reflect upon the unhappy consequence of too great freedoms between persons stated as we were, upon the pretence of innocent intentions, love of friendship, and the like; for the flesh has generally so great a share in those friendships, that is great odds but inclination prevails at last over the most solemn resolutions; and that vice breaks in at the breaches of decency, which really innocent friendship ought to preserve with the greatest strictness. But I leave the readers of these things to their own just reflections, which they will be more able to make effectual than I, who so soon forgot myself, and am therefore but a very indifferent monitor.", "그리고 여기서 나는, 우리처럼 처지가 정해진 사람들 사이에서, 순수한 의도라느니, 우정의 사랑이라느니 하는 핑계로 너무 큰 거리낌 없음을 누리는 것의 불행한 결말을 성찰하지 않을 수 없다. 살이라는 것은 대개 그런 우정에서 차지하는 몫이 너무도 커서, 가장 엄숙한 결심조차 결국에는 의향이 이겨 버릴 공산이 매우 크기 때문이다. 또 악덕은 정숙함의 갈라진 틈으로 비집고 들어오는 법이니, 실로 순수한 우정이라면 그 정숙함을 더없이 엄격하게 지켜야 마땅한 것이다. 그러나 나는 이런 일들을 읽는 독자 여러분에게 그들 자신의 정당한 성찰을 맡기거니와, 그들은 나보다 그것을 더 잘 효력 있게 만들 수 있을 것이다. 나는 그토록 빨리 나 자신을 잊어버린 사람이니, 그러므로 그저 매우 시원찮은 훈계자일 따름이다."]], [["I was now a single person again, as I may call myself; I was loosed from all the obligations either of wedlock or mistress-ship in the world, except my husband the linen-draper, whom, I having not now heard from in almost fifteen years, nobody could blame me for thinking myself entirely freed from; seeing also he had at his going away told me, that if I did not hear frequently from him, I should conclude he was dead, and I might freely marry again to whom I pleased.", "이제 나는 다시, 나 자신을 그렇게 부를 수 있다면, 홀몸이 되었다. 나는 세상의 혼인이든 정부 노릇이든 그 모든 의무에서 풀려났으니, 다만 내 남편인 그 포목상만은 예외였다. 그러나 거의 십오 년 동안 그에게서 아무 소식도 듣지 못한 터라, 내가 그에게서 완전히 풀려났다고 여긴들 누구도 나를 탓할 수 없을 것이다. 게다가 그는 떠날 때 내게, 만약 그에게서 자주 소식을 듣지 못하거든 그가 죽은 줄로 알라고, 그러면 나는 누구든 마음에 드는 사람과 자유로이 다시 혼인해도 된다고 말했던 것이다."]], [["I now began to cast up my accounts.", "이제 나는 내 셈속을 따져 보기 시작했다."], ["I had by many letters and much importunity, and with the intercession of my mother too, had a second return of some goods from my brother (as I now call him) in Virginia, to make up the damage of the cargo I brought away with me, and this too was upon the condition of my sealing a general release to him, and to send it him by his correspondent at Bristol, which, though I thought hard of, yet I was obliged to promise to do.", "나는 여러 통의 편지와 많은 졸라댐으로, 또 어머니의 중재까지 곁들여, 버지니아에 있는 오라비에게서(나는 이제 그를 그렇게 부른다) 물품의 두 번째 회송을 받아, 내가 가지고 떠나온 화물의 손해를 메웠다. 그런데 이것 또한 내가 그에게 전면적인 면제 증서에 봉인하여 브리스틀에 있는 그의 거래처를 통해 보내 준다는 조건이었으니, 비록 나는 그것을 가혹하게 여겼으나, 그렇게 하겠노라 약속할 수밖에 없었다."], ["However, I managed so well in this case, that I got my goods away before the release was signed, and then I always found something or other to say to evade the thing, and to put off the signing it at all; till at length I pretended I must write to my brother, and have his answer, before I could do it.", "그러나 나는 이 경우에 너무도 잘 처신하여, 면제 증서에 서명하기 전에 내 물품을 먼저 빼냈고, 그런 다음 늘 무슨 핑계인가를 찾아내어 그 일을 피하고 서명 자체를 미루었다. 그러다 마침내 나는 그것을 할 수 있으려면 오라비에게 편지를 써서 그의 답을 받아야 한다고 둘러댔다."]], [["Including this recruit, and before I got the last £50, I found my strength to amount, put all together, to about £400, so that with that I had about £450.", "이 보충분을 합쳐, 또 마지막 오십 파운드를 받기 전에, 나는 내 자산이 죄다 합쳐 사백 파운드쯤에 이른다는 것을 알았고, 그래서 그것까지 더하면 사백오십 파운드쯤 되었다. 나는 그 위로 백 파운드를 더 모아 두었으나, 그것을 두고 한 가지 재난을 겪었으니, 이러했다."], ["I had saved above £100 more, but I met with a disaster with that, which was this—that a goldsmith in whose hands I had trusted it, broke, so I lost £70 of my money, the man’s composition not making above £30 out of his £100.", "그 돈을 맡겨 둔 어느 금세공인이 파산하는 바람에, 나는 내 돈 칠십 파운드를 잃었다. 그자의 화의和議로 그의 백 파운드에서 삼십 파운드밖에 돌아오지 않았기 때문이다."], ["I had a little plate, but not much, and was well enough stocked with clothes and linen.", "나는 약간의 은붙이가 있었으나 많지는 않았고, 옷가지와 아마포는 그런대로 넉넉히 갖추고 있었다."]], [["With this stock I had the world to begin again; but you are to consider that I was not now the same woman as when I lived at Redriff; for, first of all, I was near twenty years older, and did not look the better for my age, nor for my rambles to Virginia and back again; and though I omitted nothing that might set me out to advantage, except painting, for that I never stooped to, and had pride enough to think I did not want it, yet there would always be some difference seen between five-and-twenty and two-and-forty.", "이 밑천을 가지고 나는 다시 세상을 시작해야 했다. 그러나 여러분은, 내가 이제 레드리프에 살던 때의 그 여자와 같지 않았음을 헤아려야 한다. 우선 무엇보다, 나는 나이가 거의 스무 살은 더 들었고, 그 나이로도, 또 버지니아를 오간 떠돌이살이로도 더 나아 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 그리고 비록 나는 나를 돋보이게 할 수 있는 것이라면 무엇 하나 빠뜨리지 않았으나, 다만 화장만은 예외였으니, 나는 한 번도 거기까지 몸을 낮춘 적이 없었고, 그런 것은 필요 없다고 여길 만큼의 자존심이 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 스물다섯과 마흔둘 사이에는 언제나 어떤 차이가 보이게 마련이었다."]], [["I cast about innumerable ways for my future state of life, and began to consider very seriously what I should do, but nothing offered. I took care to make the world take me for something more than I was, and had it given out that I was a fortune, and that my estate was in my own hands; the last of which was very true, the first of it was as above. I had no acquaintance, which was one of my worst misfortunes, and the consequence of that was, I had no adviser, at least who could assist and advise together; and above all, I had nobody to whom I could in confidence commit the secret of my circumstances to, and could depend upon for their secrecy and fidelity; and I found by experience, that to be friendless is the worst condition, next to being in want that a woman can be reduced to: I say a woman, because ’tis evident men can be their own advisers, and their own directors, and know how to work themselves out of difficulties and into business better than women; but if a woman has no friend to communicate her affairs to, and to advise and assist her, ’tis ten to one but she is undone; nay, and the more money she has, the more danger she is in of being wronged and deceived; and this was my case in the affair of the £100 which I left in the hands of the goldsmith, as above, whose credit, it seems, was upon the ebb before, but I, that had no knowledge of things and nobody to consult with, knew nothing of it, and so lost my money.", "나는 앞으로의 삶의 처지를 두고 헤아릴 수 없이 많은 방도를 궁리했고, 무엇을 해야 할지 매우 진지하게 생각하기 시작했으나, 아무것도 손에 잡히지 않았다. 나는 세상이 나를 실제보다 무언가 더 대단한 사람으로 여기도록 신경 썼고, 내가 재산가이며 내 재산이 내 손안에 있다고 소문이 나게 했다. 뒤엣것은 매우 사실이었으나, 앞엣것은 앞서 말한 대로였다. 나는 아는 사람이 하나도 없었으니, 그것이 내 가장 큰 불운의 하나였고, 그 결과로 나는 조언자가, 적어도 함께 거들고 조언해 줄 수 있는 조언자가 하나도 없었다. 그리고 무엇보다, 내 형편의 비밀을 믿고 털어놓을 수 있고 그 비밀과 신의를 의지할 수 있는 사람이 하나도 없었다. 나는 경험으로 알았으니, 벗이 없다는 것은 여자가 내몰릴 수 있는 처지 가운데, 곤궁에 처한 것 다음으로 가장 나쁜 것이다. 내가 여자라고 말하는 것은, 분명 남자라면 제 스스로의 조언자가, 제 스스로의 인도자가 될 수 있고, 여자보다 더 잘 곤경에서 빠져나오고 일거리 속으로 들어가는 법을 알기 때문이다. 그러나 여자에게 제 일을 털어놓고 조언과 도움을 구할 벗이 하나도 없다면, 십중팔구 그녀는 파멸하고 만다. 아니, 게다가 돈이 많을수록 그녀는 더 부당한 일을 당하고 속아 넘어갈 위험에 처한다. 앞서 말한 대로 내가 그 금세공인의 손에 맡겨 둔 백 파운드의 일이 바로 내 경우였으니, 보아하니 그자의 신용은 그 전부터 기울고 있었으나, 사정에 어두운 데다 상의할 사람도 없던 나는 그것을 전혀 알지 못했고, 그래서 내 돈을 잃은 것이다."]], [["In the next place, when a woman is thus left desolate and void of counsel, she is just like a bag of money or a jewel dropped on the highway, which is a prey to the next comer; if a man of virtue and upright principles happens to find it, he will have it cried, and the owner may come to hear of it again; but how many times shall such a thing fall into hands that will make no scruple of seizing it for their own, to once that it shall come into good hands?", "다음으로, 여자가 이렇게 외롭게 버려지고 조언도 없이 남겨지면, 그녀는 큰길에 떨어진 돈주머니나 보석과 꼭 같아서, 다음에 오는 자에게 먹잇감이 된다. 만약 덕 있고 올곧은 원칙을 지닌 사람이 그것을 우연히 줍는다면 그는 그것을 알리게 하여 주인이 다시 그 소식을 듣게 할 것이다. 그러나 그런 것이 단 한 번 좋은 손에 들어갈 동안, 몇 번이나 거리낌 없이 그것을 제 것으로 채갈 손에 떨어지겠는가?"]], [["This was evidently my case, for I was now a loose, unguided creature, and had no help, no assistance, no guide for my conduct; I knew what I aimed at and what I wanted, but knew nothing how to pursue the end by direct means.", "이것이 분명 내 경우였으니, 나는 이제 풀려난, 이끌어 줄 이 없는 존재였고, 내 처신을 위한 도움도, 부조도, 인도자도 없었다. 나는 내가 무엇을 노리는지, 무엇을 원하는지는 알았으나, 곧바른 방도로 그 목적을 이루는 법은 전혀 알지 못했다."], ["I wanted to be placed in a settled state of living, and had I happened to meet with a sober, good husband, I should have been as faithful and true a wife to him as virtue itself could have formed.", "나는 안정된 살림의 처지에 놓이기를 원했고, 만약 내가 술 절제할 줄 알고 마음씨 좋은 남편을 만났더라면, 덕성 그 자체가 빚어낼 수 있는 만큼이나 충실하고 진실한 아내가 되었을 것이다. 만약 내가 그렇지 못했다면, 그 악덕은 언제나 의향의 문이 아니라 필요의 문으로 들어온 것이었다."], ["If I had been otherwise, the vice came in always at the door of necessity, not at the door of inclination; and I understood too well, by the want of it, what the value of a settled life was, to do anything to forfeit the felicity of it; nay, I should have made the better wife for all the difficulties I had passed through, by a great deal; nor did I in any of the time that I had been a wife give my husbands the least uneasiness on account of my behaviour.", "또 나는 그것의 결핍을 통해, 안정된 삶의 가치가 무엇인지를 너무도 잘 이해한 터라, 그 행복을 잃을 만한 일은 무엇도 하지 않았을 것이다. 아니, 나는 내가 겪어 온 그 모든 고난 덕에 도리어 훨씬 더 나은 아내가 되었을 것이며, 또 내가 아내였던 그 어느 때에도, 내 처신으로 남편들에게 조금의 불편도 끼친 적이 없었다."]], [["But all this was nothing; I found no encouraging prospect.", "그러나 이 모든 것이 부질없었으니, 나는 어떤 고무적인 전망도 찾지 못했다."], ["I waited; I lived regularly, and with as much frugality as became my circumstances, but nothing offered, nothing presented, and the main stock wasted apace.", "나는 기다렸고, 규칙적으로, 또 내 형편에 걸맞은 만큼 알뜰하게 살았으나, 아무것도 손에 잡히지 않았고, 아무것도 나타나지 않았으며, 밑천은 빠르게 줄어들었다."], ["What to do I knew not; the terror of approaching poverty lay hard upon my spirits.", "무엇을 해야 할지 나는 알지 못했고, 다가오는 가난의 두려움이 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["I had some money, but where to place it I knew not, nor would the interest of it maintain me, at least not in London.", "돈이 좀 있긴 했으나 어디에 둘지 알지 못했고, 그 이자로는 나를 부양할 수도 없었으니, 적어도 런던에서는 그러했다."]], [["At length a new scene opened.", "마침내 새로운 장면이 펼쳐졌다. 내가 묵던 집에 양갓집 부인 행세를 하는 북부 지방 여자가 하나 있었는데, 그녀의 이야기에서 자기 고장의 물가가 싸다는 것과 살림하기 수월하다는 것에 관한 자랑보다 더 자주 나오는 것이 없었으니, 모든 것이 얼마나 푸짐하고 얼마나 싼지, 얼마나 좋은 무리와 어울리는지 따위였다."], ["There was in the house where I lodged a north-country woman that went for a gentlewoman, and nothing was more frequent in her discourse than her account of the cheapness of provisions, and the easy way of living in her country; how plentiful and how cheap everything was, what good company they kept, and the like; till at last I told her she almost tempted me to go and live in her country; for I that was a widow, though I had sufficient to live on, yet had no way of increasing it; and that I found I could not live here under £100 a year, unless I kept no company, no servant, made no appearance, and buried myself in privacy, as if I was obliged to it by necessity.", "마침내 나는 그녀에게, 당신이 나를 거의 당신 고장에 가서 살고 싶게 만든다고 말했다. 나로 말하자면, 비록 살아갈 만큼은 충분히 있는 과부였으나, 그것을 불릴 길이 전혀 없었던 데다, 여기서는 어떤 무리와도 어울리지 않고, 하인도 두지 않고, 겉치레도 하지 않고, 마치 필요에 떠밀리기라도 한 듯 은둔에 파묻혀 살지 않는 한, 한 해에 백 파운드 아래로는 살 수 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["I should have observed, that she was always made to believe, as everybody else was, that I was a great fortune, or at least that I had three or four thousand pounds, if not more, and all in my own hands; and she was mighty sweet upon me when she thought me inclined in the least to go into her country.", "진작 말해 두었어야 할 것이, 그녀는 다른 모든 사람과 마찬가지로, 내가 큰 재산가라고, 적어도 삼사천 파운드는, 어쩌면 그 이상은 가지고 있고 그것이 다 내 손안에 있다고 늘 믿게 되어 있었다. 그래서 그녀는 내가 조금이라도 자기 고장에 갈 마음이 있다고 여기자 내게 무척 살갑게 굴었다."], ["She said she had a sister lived near Liverpool, that her brother was a considerable gentleman there, and had a great estate also in Ireland; that she would go down there in about two months, and if I would give her my company thither, I should be as welcome as herself for a month or more as I pleased, till I should see how I liked the country; and if I thought fit to live there, she would undertake they would take care, though they did not entertain lodgers themselves, they would recommend me to some agreeable family, where I should be placed to my content.", "그녀는 리버풀 근처에 사는 자매가 하나 있다고, 자기 오라비는 거기서 상당한 신사이며 아일랜드에도 큰 재산이 있다고, 자기는 두 달쯤 뒤에 그리로 내려갈 텐데, 만약 내가 거기까지 동행해 준다면, 내가 그 고장을 어떻게 여기는지 보게 될 때까지 한 달이고 그 이상이고 내 좋을 대로, 자기만큼이나 환영받을 것이라고 했다. 그리고 만약 내가 거기서 살 만하다고 여긴다면, 비록 자기네는 직접 하숙을 치지는 않으나, 어떤 마음 맞는 집안에 나를 추천하여 내 마음에 들게 자리 잡게 해 주도록 자기네가 책임지고 보살피겠노라고 장담했다."]], [["If this woman had known my real circumstances, she would never have laid so many snares, and taken so many weary steps to catch a poor desolate creature that was good for little when it was caught; and indeed I, whose case was almost desperate, and thought I could not be much worse, was not very anxious about what might befall me, provided they did me no personal injury; so I suffered myself, though not without a great deal of invitation and great professions of sincere friendship and real kindness—I say, I suffered myself to be prevailed upon to go with her, and accordingly I packed up my baggage, and put myself in a posture for a journey, though I did not absolutely know whither I was to go.", "만약 이 여자가 내 진짜 형편을 알았더라면, 잡고 보니 별 쓸모도 없는 가엾고 외로운 신세 하나를 잡으려고 그토록 많은 덫을 놓고 그토록 많은 고단한 발품을 팔지는 않았을 것이다. 실로 나는, 내 처지가 거의 가망 없는 데다 이보다 더 나빠질 수도 없으리라 여긴 터라, 그들이 내게 몸으로 해코지만 하지 않는다면 무슨 일이 닥치든 그리 마음 졸이지 않았다. 그래서 나는, 비록 많은 권유와 진실한 우정과 참된 친절에 대한 거창한 다짐이 없지는 않았으나, 다시 말하건대, 나는 그녀와 함께 가도록 설득당하기에 나를 내맡겼고, 그리하여 비록 어디로 가는지 확실히 알지도 못한 채로, 짐을 꾸리고 여정에 오를 채비를 갖추었다."]], [["And now I found myself in great distress; what little I had in the world was all in money, except as before, a little plate, some linen, and my clothes; as for my household stuff, I had little or none, for I had lived always in lodgings; but I had not one friend in the world with whom to trust that little I had, or to direct me how to dispose of it, and this perplexed me night and day. I thought of the bank, and of the other companies in London, but I had no friend to commit the management of it to, and keep and carry about with me bank bills, tallies, orders, and such things, I looked upon at as unsafe; that if they were lost, my money was lost, and then I was undone; and, on the other hand, I might be robbed and perhaps murdered in a strange place for them. This perplexed me strangely, and what to do I knew not.", "그리고 이제 나는 큰 곤경에 처한 나 자신을 발견했다. 세상에 내가 가진 보잘것없는 것은 앞서 말한 약간의 은붙이와 얼마간의 아마포와 내 옷가지를 빼고는 모두 돈이었다. 살림살이로 말하자면 거의 없거나 아예 없었으니, 나는 늘 셋방에서만 살아왔기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그 보잘것없는 것을 믿고 맡길 벗도, 그것을 어떻게 처분할지 일러 줄 벗도 세상에 하나 없었고, 이것이 밤낮으로 나를 괴롭혔다. 나는 은행과, 또 런던의 다른 회사들을 떠올렸으나, 그 운용을 맡길 벗이 없었다. 그리고 은행 어음이며 증서며 지급 명령서 따위를 내가 지니고 다니는 것은 안전치 못하게 여겨졌으니, 만약 그것들을 잃으면 내 돈도 잃는 것이고 그러면 나는 끝장이며, 다른 한편으로 낯선 곳에서 그것들 때문에 강도를 당하거나 어쩌면 살해당할 수도 있었기 때문이다. 이것이 나를 이상하게 괴롭혔고, 무엇을 해야 할지 나는 알지 못했다."]], [["It came in my thoughts one morning that I would go to the bank myself, where I had often been to receive the interest of some bills I had, which had interest payable on them, and where I had found a clerk, to whom I applied myself, very honest and just to me, and particularly so fair one time that when I had mistold my money, and taken less than my due, and was coming away, he set me to rights and gave me the rest, which he might have put into his own pocket.", "어느 날 아침 한 가지 생각이 떠올랐으니, 내가 직접 은행에 가 보자는 것이었다. 나는 이자가 붙는 어음 몇 장이 있어 그 이자를 받으러 거기에 자주 갔었고, 거기서 내가 찾아가 상대한 어떤 서기가 내게 매우 정직하고 공정했으니, 특히 한번은 어찌나 공정했던지, 내가 돈을 잘못 세어 받을 몫보다 적게 받고 나오려 하자, 그가 나를 바로잡아 나머지를 마저 주었던 것이다. 그것을 제 주머니에 넣을 수도 있었을 텐데 말이다."]], [["I went to him and represented my case very plainly, and asked if he would trouble himself to be my adviser, who was a poor friendless widow, and knew not what to do. He told me, if I desired his opinion of anything within the reach of his business, he would do his endeavour that I should not be wronged, but that he would also help me to a good sober person who was a grave man of his acquaintance, who was a clerk in such business too, though not in their house, whose judgment was good, and whose honesty I might depend upon. “For,” added he, “I will answer for him, and for every step he takes; if he wrongs you, madam, of one farthing, it shall lie at my door, I will make it good; and he delights to assist people in such cases—he does it as an act of charity.”", "나는 그를 찾아가 내 사정을 아주 솔직하게 털어놓고, 가엾게도 벗 없는 과부이며 무엇을 해야 할지 모르는 나의 조언자가 되어 주는 수고를 해 주겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 만약 자기 업무 범위 안에 드는 무엇이든 내가 그의 의견을 바란다면, 내가 부당한 일을 당하지 않도록 힘을 다하겠노라고, 그러나 또한 자기가 아는 점잖고 진중한 사람 하나를 소개해 주겠다고 했다. 그자는 비록 자기네 회사 소속은 아니나 그런 업무의 서기이며, 판단이 뛰어나고 그 정직함은 내가 의지해도 좋다고 했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 덧붙였다. \"제가 그 사람을, 또 그가 밟는 모든 절차를 보증하겠습니다. 부인, 만약 그가 부인께 단 한 푼이라도 부당한 짓을 한다면, 그것은 제 책임이 될 것이고, 제가 그것을 갚아 드리겠습니다. 그리고 그 사람은 그런 경우에 사람들을 돕기를 좋아합니다. 자선을 베푸는 셈 치고 그 일을 하는 거지요.\""]], [["I was a little at a stand in this discourse; but after some pause I told him I had rather have depended upon him, because I had found him honest, but if that could not be, I would take his recommendation sooner than any one’s else.", "나는 이 말에 잠시 머뭇거렸으나, 잠깐 멈칫한 뒤에 그에게, 나는 차라리 당신을 의지하고 싶다고, 당신이 정직하다는 것을 알았기 때문이라고, 그러나 만약 그럴 수 없다면 당신의 추천을 받아들이겠노라고 말했다. n의 누구의 것보다도 먼저 말이다."], ["“I dare say, madam,”", "\"감히 말씀드리건대, 부인,\""], ["says he, “that you will be as well satisfied with my friend as with me, and he is thoroughly able to assist you, which I am not.”", "그가 말했다, \"제 친구에게도 저에게만큼이나 만족하실 겁니다. 그 사람은 부인을 도울 능력이 충분하지만, 저는 그렇지 못하니까요.\""], ["It seems he had his hands full of the business of the bank, and had engaged to meddle with no other business than that of his office, which I heard afterwards, but did not understand then.", "보아하니 그는 은행 업무로 손이 가득 차서, 자기 직무 외에는 어떤 일에도 관여하지 않기로 약조했던 모양인데, 이는 내가 나중에야 들어 알게 된 일이지 그때는 이해하지 못했다."], ["He added, that his friend should take nothing of me for his advice or assistance, and this indeed encouraged me very much.", "그는 덧붙이기를, 자기 친구는 조언이나 도움에 대해 내게서 한 푼도 받지 않을 것이라고 했으니, 이는 참으로 나를 크게 안심시켰다."]], [["He appointed the same evening, after the bank was shut and business over, for me to meet him and his friend.", "그는 은행 문을 닫고 업무가 끝난 뒤인 바로 그날 저녁에, 나더러 자기와 자기 친구를 만나러 오라고 일러 주었다."], ["And indeed as soon as I saw his friend, and he began but to talk of the affair, I was fully satisfied that I had a very honest man to deal with; his countenance spoke it, and his character, as I heard afterwards, was everywhere so good, that I had no room for any more doubts upon me.", "그리고 정말이지 나는 그의 친구를 보자마자, 그가 그 일에 관해 말을 꺼내기 시작하자마자, 내가 매우 정직한 사람을 상대하게 되었다는 것을 충분히 알았으니, 그의 얼굴 생김새가 그것을 말해 주었고, 또 내가 나중에 들은 바로 그의 평판이 어디서나 너무도 좋았던지라, 나는 더 이상 의심을 품을 여지가 없었다."]], [["After the first meeting, in which I only said what I had said before, we parted, and he appointed me to come the next day to him, telling me I might in the meantime satisfy myself of him by inquiry, which, however, I knew not how well to do, having no acquaintance myself.", "첫 만남에서 나는 앞서 했던 말을 되풀이했을 뿐이었고, 우리는 헤어졌으며, 그는 나더러 이튿날 자기에게 오라고 약속을 정하면서, 그동안 내가 수소문하여 그에 관해 안심해도 좋다고 일러 주었으나, 나로서는 아는 사람도 없는 터라 그 일을 어떻게 잘 해낼지 알 수가 없었다."]], [["Accordingly I met him the next day, when I entered more freely with him into my case.", "그리하여 나는 이튿날 그를 만났고, 그제야 좀 더 거리낌 없이 내 사정 안으로 들어갔다."], ["I told him my circumstances at large: that I was a widow come over from America, perfectly desolate and friendless; that I had a little money, and but a little, and was almost distracted for fear of losing it, having no friend in the world to trust with the management of it; that I was going into the north of England to live cheap, that my stock might not waste; that I would willingly lodge my money in the bank, but that I durst not carry the bills about me, and the like, as above; and how to correspond about it, or with whom, I knew not.", "나는 내 처지를 죽 늘어놓았으니, 곧 나는 아메리카에서 건너온 과부로 완전히 의지가지없고 벗도 없는 신세이며, 약간의 돈이 있으나 그저 약간일 뿐인데, 그 관리를 믿고 맡길 벗이 세상에 하나도 없는 까닭에 그것을 잃을까 두려워 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이라고 말했다. 또 나는 밑천이 축나지 않도록 물가가 싼 잉글랜드 북부로 가서 살려 하며, 기꺼이 그 돈을 은행에 맡기고 싶으나 어음을 몸에 지니고 다닐 엄두는 나지 않는다는 둥, 앞서 말한 것과 같은 사정을 늘어놓았으며, 그 일을 어떻게, 또 누구와 더불어 연락해 처리할지 알 수 없다고 했다."]], [["He told me I might lodge the money in the bank as an account, and its being entered into the books would entitle me to the money at any time, and if I was in the north I might draw bills on the cashier and receive it when I would; but that then it would be esteemed as running cash, and the bank would give no interest for it; that I might buy stock with it, and so it would lie in store for me, but that then if I wanted to dispose if it, I must come up to town on purpose to transfer it, and even it would be with some difficulty I should receive the half-yearly dividend, unless I was here in person, or had some friend I could trust with having the stock in his name to do it for me, and that would have the same difficulty in it as before; and with that he looked hard at me and smiled a little. At last, says he, “Why do you not get a head steward, madam, that may take you and your money together into keeping, and then you would have the trouble taken off your hands?” “Ay, sir, and the money too, it may be,” said I; “for truly I find the hazard that way is as much as ’tis t’other way”; but I remember I said secretly to myself, “I wish you would ask me the question fairly, I would consider very seriously on it before I said No.”", "그는 내게 그 돈을 계좌로 은행에 맡기면 그것이 장부에 기재되어 언제든 그 돈에 대한 권리를 갖게 되고, 내가 북부에 있더라도 출납계 앞으로 어음을 발행하여 원할 때 받을 수 있다고 일러 주었으나, 다만 그리되면 그것은 유통 현금으로 간주되어 은행이 이자를 주지 않으리라고 했다. 또 그 돈으로 주식을 살 수도 있으니 그러면 그것이 나를 위해 비축되어 있겠으나, 다만 그것을 처분하고자 하면 일부러 상경하여 명의를 옮겨야 하고, 게다가 내가 직접 이곳에 있거나 그 주식을 자기 명의로 두고 나를 대신해 그 일을 해 줄 믿을 만한 벗이 없는 한 반년치 배당금을 받기조차 다소 어려울 터인데, 그 또한 앞서 말한 것과 같은 어려움이 따를 것이라고 했다. 그러고는 나를 빤히 쳐다보며 살짝 미소를 지었다. 마침내 그가 말했다. \"부인, 어찌하여 부인과 부인의 돈을 함께 거두어 맡아 줄 집사 우두머리 하나를 얻지 않으시오? 그러면 그 수고를 부인 손에서 덜어 낼 수 있을 텐데요.\" \"그럼요, 어르신, 그리고 어쩌면 돈까지도 덜어 가겠지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"정말이지 그쪽 방도의 위험이 이쪽 방도의 위험만큼이나 크다는 걸 알겠으니까요.\" 그러나 나는 속으로 가만히 이렇게 말한 것이 기억난다. \"당신이 그 질문을 점잖게 내게 던져 주면 좋으련만, 그러면 아니라고 말하기 전에 아주 진지하게 생각해 볼 텐데.\""]], [["He went on a good way with me, and I thought once or twice he was in earnest, but to my real affliction, I found at last he had a wife; but when he owned he had a wife he shook his head, and said with some concern, that indeed he had a wife, and no wife.", "그는 나와 더불어 꽤 멀리까지 이야기를 끌고 갔고, 나는 한두 번쯤 그가 진심이라고 생각했으나, 정말로 나를 비통하게 한 것은, 마침내 그에게 아내가 있다는 것을 알게 된 일이었다. 그러나 아내가 있음을 인정하면서 그는 고개를 가로젓고는 다소 근심스레, 정말이지 자기에게는 아내가 있으되 아내가 없다고 말했다."], ["I began to think he had been in the condition of my late lover, and that his wife had been distempered or lunatic, or some such thing.", "나는 그가 내 죽은 정부情夫와 같은 처지에 놓여 있어, 그의 아내가 병들었거나 미쳤거나 그 비슷한 무엇이리라 생각하기 시작했다."], ["However, we had not much more discourse at that time, but he told me he was in too much hurry of business then, but that if I would come home to his house after their business was over, he would by that time consider what might be done for me, to put my affairs in a posture of security.", "어쨌든 그때는 더 많은 이야기를 나누지 못했으나, 그는 내게 지금은 일이 너무 바쁘지만, 업무가 끝난 뒤에 자기 집으로 와 준다면 그때까지 내 일을 안전한 자세로 놓아 주기 위해 무엇을 할 수 있을지 궁리해 보겠다고 했다. 나는 가겠노라 말하고 그가 어디에 사는지 알고 싶다고 했다."], ["I told him I would come, and desired to know where he lived.", "그는 종이에 주소를 적어 주었고, 그것을 건네면서 내게 읽어 주고는 말했다."], ["He gave me a direction in writing, and when he gave it me he read it to me, and said, “There ’tis, madam, if you dare trust yourself with me.”", "\"여기 있습니다, 부인, 저를 믿고 부인 자신을 맡기실 엄두가 나신다면요.\" \"예, 어르신,\""], ["“Yes, sir,”", "내가 말했다. \"제 몸을 어르신께 맡겨도 좋을 듯합니다."], ["said I, “I believe I may venture to trust you with myself, for you have a wife, you say, and I don’t want a husband; besides, I dare trust you with my money, which is all I have in the world, and if that were gone, I may trust myself anywhere.”", "어르신께는 아내가 있다 하셨고 저는 남편을 원하지 않으니까요. 게다가 제 돈을, 제가 세상에 가진 전부를 어르신께 맡길 엄두가 나니, 그것이 사라진다 한들 제 몸이야 어디에든 맡길 수 있겠지요.\""]], [["He said some things in jest that were very handsome and mannerly, and would have pleased me very well if they had been in earnest; but that passed over, I took the directions, and appointed to attend him at his house at seven o’clock the same evening.", "그는 농담조로 몇 마디 했는데, 그것이 매우 멋지고 점잖아서, 진심이었다면 나를 흡족하게 했을 법한 말이었다. 그러나 그 일은 그냥 지나갔고, 나는 주소를 받아들고 같은 날 저녁 일곱 시에 그의 집으로 찾아가기로 약속을 정했다."]], [["When I came he made several proposals for my placing my money in the bank, in order to my having interest for it; but still some difficulty or other came in the way, which he objected as not safe; and I found such a sincere disinterested honesty in him, that I began to muse with myself, that I had certainly found the honest man I wanted, and that I could never put myself into better hands; so I told him with a great deal of frankness that I had never met with a man or woman yet that I could trust, or in whom I could think myself safe, but that I saw he was so disinterestedly concerned for my safety, that I said I would freely trust him with the management of that little I had, if he would accept to be steward for a poor widow that could give him no salary.", "내가 갔더니 그는 내 돈을 은행에 맡겨 이자를 받게 할 여러 방안을 내놓았으나, 여전히 이런저런 어려움이 끼어들었고, 그는 그것이 안전하지 않다며 반대했다. 그러고는 나는 그에게서 너무도 진실하고 사심 없는 정직함을 발견하여, 속으로 곰곰이 생각하기를, 내가 분명 내가 원하던 정직한 사람을 찾아냈으며 결코 이보다 더 나은 손에 나 자신을 맡길 수 없으리라고 여기게 되었다. 그리하여 나는 매우 솔직하게 그에게 말하기를, 나는 아직껏 믿을 만하거나 그와 더불어 내가 안전하다고 여길 만한 남자도 여자도 만나 본 적이 없으나, 그가 내 안전을 그토록 사심 없이 염려하는 것을 보았으니, 만약 그가 봉급 한 푼 못 주는 가엾은 과부의 집사가 되기를 받아들여 준다면 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것의 관리를 기꺼이 그에게 맡기겠다고 했다."]], [["He smiled and, standing up, with great respect saluted me.", "그는 미소를 짓고는 일어서서 매우 정중하게 내게 인사를 했다."], ["He told me he could not but take it very kindly that I had so good an opinion of him; that he would not deceive me, that he would do anything in his power to serve me, and expect no salary; but that he could not by any means accept of a trust, that it might bring him to be suspected of self-interest, and that if I should die he might have disputes with my executors, which he should be very loth to encumber himself with.", "그는 내가 자기를 그토록 좋게 여겨 주니 그저 매우 고맙게 받아들일 수밖에 없다고 했고, 나를 속이지 않을 것이며, 봉급은 바라지 않고 자기 힘닿는 데까지 무엇이든 해 나를 섬기겠노라 했다. 그러나 결코 그러한 신탁을 받아들일 수는 없으니, 그것이 자기를 사리사욕의 혐의로 끌고 갈 수 있고, 또 내가 죽기라도 하면 내 유언집행인들과 분쟁이 생길 수 있는데, 그런 짐을 스스로 떠안는 것은 몹시 꺼려진다고 했다."]], [["I told him if those were all his objections I would soon remove them, and convince him that there was not the least room for any difficulty; for that, first, as for suspecting him, if ever I should do it, now is the time to suspect him, and not put the trust into his hands, and whenever I did suspect him, he could but throw it up then and refuse to go any further.", "나는 그에게 그것이 그의 반대의 전부라면 곧 그것을 제거하고, 조금도 어려움의 여지가 없음을 납득시켜 주겠다고 했다. 왜냐하면 첫째, 그를 의심하는 일에 관해서라면, 내가 만약 언제고 그를 의심한다면 바로 지금이 그를 의심하여 신탁을 그의 손에 맡기지 않을 때이며, 또 내가 언제든 그를 의심하게 되면 그는 그때 그것을 던져 버리고 더 나아가기를 거절하면 그만이기 때문이었다."], ["Then, as to executors, I assured him I had no heirs, nor any relations in England, and I would have neither heirs nor executors but himself, unless I should alter my condition before I died, and then his trust and trouble should cease together, which, however, I had no prospect of yet; but I told him if I died as I was, it should be all his own, and he would deserve it by being so faithful to me as I was satisfied he would be.", "그다음, 유언집행인에 관해서라면, 나는 그에게 잉글랜드에 상속인도 친척도 없으며, 내가 죽기 전에 처지가 바뀌지 않는 한 그 자신 말고는 상속인도 유언집행인도 두지 않을 것이고, 그렇게 처지가 바뀌면 그의 신탁과 수고도 함께 끝날 것이라고 단언했으나, 다만 그런 전망은 아직 없다고 했다. 그러나 내가 지금 이대로 죽는다면 그것은 모두 그 자신의 것이 될 것이며, 내가 확신하는 만큼 그가 내게 충실히 해 줄 터이니 마땅히 그것을 받을 자격이 있으리라고 일러 주었다."]], [["He changed his countenance at this discourse, and asked me how I came to have so much good-will for him; and, looking very much pleased, said he might very lawfully wish he was a single man for my sake.", "그는 이 말에 낯빛이 바뀌더니, 어쩌다 내가 자기에게 그토록 큰 호의를 품게 되었느냐고 물었고, 매우 흡족한 표정으로, 나를 위해서라면 자기가 독신이기를 바라는 것도 지극히 떳떳한 일이겠다고 말했다."], ["I smiled, and told him as he was not, my offer could have no design upon him in it, and to wish, as he did, was not to be allowed, ’twas criminal to his wife.", "나는 미소 지으며, 그가 독신이 아닌 이상 내 제안에는 그를 향한 어떤 속셈도 담길 수 없으며, 그가 그러듯 바라는 것은 허락될 수 없으니, 그의 아내에게 죄가 되는 일이라고 말했다."]], [["He told me I was wrong.", "그는 내가 틀렸다고 했다."], ["“For,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["says he, “madam, as I said before, I have a wife and no wife, and ’twould be no sin to me to wish her hanged, if that were all.”", "그가 말했다, \"부인, 앞서 말씀드렸듯 제게는 아내가 있으되 아내가 없으니, 만약 그게 다라면 그 여자가 교수형당하기를 바란다 해도 제게 죄가 되지 않을 겁니다.\""], ["“I know nothing of your circumstances that way, sir,”", "\"그쪽 방면으로 어르신 사정을 전혀 모릅니다, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I; “but it cannot be innocent to wish your wife dead.”", "\"하지만 아내가 죽기를 바라는 것이 결백한 일일 수는 없지요.\""], ["“I tell you,”", "\"다시 말씀드리지만,\""], ["says he again, “she is a wife and no wife; you don’t know what I am, or what she is.”", "그가 거듭 말했다, \"그 여자는 아내이면서 아내가 아니랍니다. 부인은 제가 어떤 사람인지, 그 여자가 어떤 사람인지 모르세요.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그건 사실이지요,\""], ["said I; “sir, I do not know what you are, but I believe you to be an honest man, and that’s the cause of all my confidence in you.”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신, 어르신이 어떤 분인지는 모르나, 정직한 분이라 믿으며, 그것이 제가 어르신을 그토록 신뢰하는 까닭이랍니다.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"그래요, 그래요,\""], ["says he, “and so I am, I hope, too.", "그가 말했다. \"저 역시 그렇기를 바라지요."], ["But I am something else too, madam; for,”", "하지만 저는 또 다른 무엇이기도 하답니다, 부인."], ["says he, “to be plain with you, I am a cuckold, and she is a whore.”", "까놓고 말씀드리자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 오쟁이 진 사내요, 그 여자는 창녀랍니다.\""], ["He spoke it in a kind of jest, but it was with such an awkward smile, that I perceived it was what struck very close to him, and he looked dismally when he said it.", "그는 일종의 농담조로 그렇게 말했으나, 어찌나 어색한 미소를 띠었던지, 나는 그것이 그에게 매우 가깝게 와닿는 일임을 알아챘고, 그는 그 말을 할 때 음울한 표정이었다."]], [["“That alters the case indeed, sir,”", "\"그러면 정말 사정이 달라지는군요, 어르신,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “as to that part you were speaking of; but a cuckold, you know, may be an honest man; it does not alter that case at all.", "\"어르신이 말씀하시던 그 부분에 관해서는요. 하지만 오쟁이 진 사내라도, 아시다시피, 정직한 사람일 수 있지요."], ["Besides, I think,”", "그건 그 점을 조금도 바꾸지 않으니까요. 게다가 제 생각엔,\""], ["said I, “since your wife is so dishonest to you, you are too honest to her to own her for your wife; but that,”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신의 아내가 어르신에게 그토록 부정하다면, 어르신은 그 여자를 아내로 인정하기엔 너무 정직하신 분입니다만, 그건,\""], ["said I, “is what I have nothing to do with.”", "내가 말했다, \"제가 상관할 바가 아니지요.\""]], [["“Nay,” says he, “I do not think to clear my hands of her; for, to be plain with you, madam,” added he, “I am no contented cuckold neither: on the other hand, I assure you it provokes me the highest degree, but I can’t help myself; she that will be a whore, will be a whore.”", "\"아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 그 여자에게서 손을 털 생각이 없습니다. 까놓고 말씀드리자면, 부인,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"저는 또 만족하는 오쟁이 진 사내도 아니랍니다. 오히려 그 일은 저를 더없이 분노케 하지요. 그러나 어쩔 도리가 없으니, 창녀가 되려는 여자는 기어이 창녀가 되고 마는 법이지요.\""]], [["I waived the discourse and began to talk of my business; but I found he could not have done with it, so I let him alone, and he went on to tell me all the circumstances of his case, too long to relate here; particularly, that having been out of England some time before he came to the post he was in, she had had two children in the meantime by an officer of the army; and that when he came to England and, upon her submission, took her again, and maintained her very well, yet she ran away from him with a linen-draper’s apprentice, robbed him of what she could come at, and continued to live from him still.", "나는 그 이야기를 비껴가며 내 일을 꺼내려 했으나, 그가 그것을 끝낼 수 없으리라는 것을 알아채고는 그냥 내버려 두었고, 그는 자기 사정의 온갖 정황을 죽 늘어놓기 시작했는데, 여기 옮기기에는 너무 길다. 특히 그가 지금의 자리에 오기 얼마 전 한동안 잉글랜드를 떠나 있는 사이, 그 여자가 어떤 군 장교와의 사이에서 아이 둘을 낳았다는 것, 그리고 그가 잉글랜드에 돌아와 그 여자가 잘못을 빌자 다시 거두어들여 매우 잘 부양해 주었건만, 그 여자는 어떤 포목상 도제와 함께 그에게서 달아나며 손에 닿는 대로 그의 것을 훔쳐 갔고, 여전히 그를 떠나 살고 있다는 것이었다."], ["“So that, madam,”", "\"그러니, 부인,\""], ["says he, “she is a whore not by necessity, which is the common bait of your sex, but by inclination, and for the sake of the vice.”", "그가 말했다. \"그 여자는 흔히 여자들을 꾀는 미끼인 궁핍 때문이 아니라, 천성으로, 또 그 악덕 그 자체를 위하여 창녀랍니다.\""]], [["Well, I pitied him, and wished him well rid of her, and still would have talked of my business, but it would not do.", "나는 그가 가여워, 그가 그 여자를 잘 떼어 내기를 바랐고, 여전히 내 일을 이야기하려 했으나, 도무지 되지 않았다."], ["At last he looks steadily at me.", "마침내 그가 나를 빤히 쳐다보았다."], ["“Look you, madam,”", "\"보세요, 부인,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “you came to ask advice of me, and I will serve you as faithfully as if you were my own sister; but I must turn the tables, since you oblige me to do it, and are so friendly to me, and I think I must ask advice of you.", "\"부인은 제게 조언을 구하러 오셨고, 저는 부인이 제 친누이라도 되는 양 충실히 부인을 섬길 겁니다. 하지만 부인이 저더러 그러게 하시고, 또 제게 그토록 다정하시니, 이번엔 제가 처지를 뒤바꿔 부인께 조언을 구해야겠습니다."], ["Tell me, what must a poor abused fellow do with a whore?", "말씀해 주시오, 가엾게 능멸당한 사내가 창녀를 두고 어찌해야 하겠습니까?"], ["What can I do to do myself justice upon her?”", "그 여자에게 정당한 응징을 하려면 제가 무엇을 할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["sir,”", "어르신,\""], ["says I, “’tis a case too nice for me to advise in, but it seems she has run away from you, so you are rid of her fairly; what can you desire more?”", "내가 말했다. \"그건 제가 조언하기엔 너무 미묘한 경우입니다만, 보아하니 그 여자가 어르신에게서 달아났으니 어르신은 깨끗이 그 여자를 떼어 내신 셈이지요."], ["“Ay, she is gone indeed,”", "더 무엇을 바라시겠어요?\" \"그래요, 그 여자는 정말 가 버렸지요,\""], ["said he, “but I am not clear of her for all that.”", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 그래도 저는 그 여자에게서 자유롭지 못하답니다.\""]], [["“That’s true,”", "\"그건 사실이지요,\""], ["says I; “she may indeed run you into debt, but the law has furnished you with methods to prevent that also; you may cry her down, as they call it.”", "내가 말했다. \"그 여자가 정말 어르신을 빚더미에 앉힐 수는 있겠지만, 법이 그것 또한 막을 방도를 어르신께 마련해 주었으니, 이른바 공시 거부를 하시면 됩니다.\""]], [["“No, no,” says he, “that is not the case neither; I have taken care of all that; ’tis not that part that I speak of, but I would be rid of her so that I might marry again.”", "\"아니요, 아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"그것도 아니랍니다. 그 부분은 다 대비해 두었으니, 제가 말하는 건 그 부분이 아닙니다. 다만 저는 다시 결혼할 수 있도록 그 여자를 떼어 내고 싶은 겁니다.\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"그럼, 어르신,\""], ["says I, “then you must divorce her.", "내가 말했다. \"그렇다면 그 여자와 이혼하셔야지요."], ["If you can prove what you say, you may certainly get that done, and then, I suppose, you are free.”", "어르신이 하신 말씀을 입증할 수 있다면 분명 그 일을 해내실 수 있을 테고, 그러면, 짐작건대, 어르신은 자유의 몸이 되시겠지요.\""]], [["“That’s very tedious and expensive,”", "\"그건 아주 지루하고 돈이 많이 드는 일이지요,\""], ["says he.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Why,”", "\"왜요,\""], ["says I, “if you can get any woman you like to take your word, I suppose your wife would not dispute the liberty with you that she takes herself.”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신이 마음에 드는 여자를 구해 어르신 말을 믿게 할 수 있다면, 짐작건대 어르신의 아내도 자기가 누리는 그 자유를 어르신께서 누린다고 다투지는 못할 겁니다.\""]], [["“Ay,”", "\"그렇지요,\""], ["says he, “but ’twould be hard to bring an honest woman to do that; and for the other sort,”", "그가 말했다. \"하지만 정직한 여자를 끌어들여 그리하게 하는 건 어려운 일이지요."], ["says he, “I have had enough of her to meddle with any more whores.”", "그리고 그 다른 부류로 말하자면,\" 그가 말했다, \"저는 그 여자 하나로 창녀라면 더 상대하기에 진절머리가 나거든요.\""]], [["It occurred to me presently, “I would have taken your word with all my heart, if you had but asked me the question”; but that was to myself. To him I replied, “Why, you shut the door against any honest woman accepting you, for you condemn all that should venture upon you at once, and conclude, that really a woman that takes you now can’t be honest.”", "문득 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"당신이 그저 그 질문을 내게 던지기만 했다면, 나는 진심으로 당신의 말을 믿어 주었을 텐데.\" 그러나 그것은 속으로 한 말이었다. 그에게는 이렇게 답했다. \"왜요, 어르신은 어떤 정직한 여자가 어르신을 받아들이든 그 문을 닫아 버리시는군요. 어르신께 모험을 걸 모든 여자를 단번에 단죄하시고, 정말이지 지금 어르신을 받아들이는 여자는 정직할 수 없다고 결론지으시니까요.\""]], [["“Why,” says he, “I wish you would satisfy me that an honest woman would take me; I’d venture it”; and then turns short upon me, “Will you take me, madam?”", "\"아니요,\" 그가 말했다. \"정직한 여자가 저를 받아 줄 거라고 부인이 저를 납득시켜 주신다면 좋겠군요. 그러면 모험을 걸 텐데요.\" 그러고는 갑자기 내게로 돌아서며, \"부인, 저를 받아 주시겠습니까?\""]], [["“That’s not a fair question,” says I, “after what you have said; however, lest you should think I wait only for a recantation of it, I shall answer you plainly, No, not I; my business is of another kind with you, and I did not expect you would have turned my serious application to you, in my own distracted case, into a comedy.”", "\"그건 공정한 질문이 아니지요,\" 내가 말했다. \"어르신이 하신 말씀 뒤에 말이에요. 하지만 제가 그 말을 거두기만 기다린다고 여기실까 봐, 분명히 답해 드리지요. 아니요, 저는 아닙니다. 어르신과는 다른 종류의 볼일이 있으며, 저는 어르신이 제 절박한 처지에 관한 진지한 청을 희극으로 바꾸어 놓으시리라고는 예상하지 못했습니다.\""]], [["“Why, madam,”", "\"왜요, 부인,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “my case is as distracted as yours can be, and I stand in as much need of advice as you do, for I think if I have not relief somewhere, I shall be made myself, and I know not what course to take, I protest to you.”", "\"제 처지도 부인 처지만큼이나 절박하고, 부인 못지않게 조언이 절실합니다. 어딘가에서 구원을 얻지 못한다면 제가 미쳐 버릴 것 같으니, 단언컨대 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠거든요.\""]], [["“Why, sir,”", "\"왜요, 어르신,\""], ["says I, “’tis easy to give advice in your case, much easier than it is in mine.”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신 처지에선 조언을 드리기가 쉽지요, 제 처지에서보다 훨씬 쉽답니다.\""], ["“Speak then,”", "\"그럼 말씀해 주시오,\""], ["says he, “I beg of you, for now you encourage me.”", "그가 말했다. \"부탁드립니다, 이제 부인이 저를 북돋아 주시니까요.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"왜요,\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “if your case is so plain as you say it is, you may be legally divorced, and then you may find honest women enough to ask the question of fairly; the sex is not so scarce that you can want a wife.”", "\"어르신 처지가 말씀하시는 만큼 분명하다면, 어르신은 합법적으로 이혼하실 수 있고, 그러면 점잖게 청혼할 정직한 여자를 얼마든지 찾으실 수 있을 겁니다. 여자라는 성이 그리 귀하지 않으니 아내가 없어 곤란하실 일은 없지요.\""]], [["“Well, then,” said he, “I am in earnest; I’ll take your advice; but shall I ask you one question seriously beforehand?”", "\"좋습니다, 그럼,\" 그가 말했다. \"저는 진심입니다. 부인 조언을 따르지요. 하지만 그에 앞서 진지하게 한 가지만 여쭤도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“Any question,”", "\"무슨 질문이든요,\""], ["said I, “but that you did before.”", "내가 말했다. \"전에 하셨던 그것만 빼고요.\""]], [["“No, that answer will not do,”", "\"아니요, 그 답으론 안 됩니다,\""], ["said he, “for, in short, that is the question I shall ask.”", "그가 말했다. \"왜냐하면, 한마디로, 바로 그것이 제가 여쭐 질문이니까요.\""]], [["“You may ask what questions you please, but you have my answer to that already,”", "\"무슨 질문이든 마음껏 하셔도 좋지만, 그것에 대해선 이미 제 답을 들으셨지요,\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Besides, sir,”", "\"게다가, 어르신,\""], ["said I, “can you think so ill of me as that I would give any answer to such a question beforehand?", "내가 말했다. \"어르신은 제가 그런 질문에 미리 어떤 답이라도 내놓으리만치 저를 그토록 나쁘게 여기실 수 있나요?"], ["Can any woman alive believe you in earnest, or think you design anything but to banter her?”", "살아 있는 어떤 여자가 어르신이 진심이라고 믿거나, 어르신이 그 여자를 놀려먹는 것 말고 다른 무엇을 꾀한다고 여기겠어요?\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"그래요, 그래요,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “I do not banter you, I am in earnest; consider of it.”", "\"저는 부인을 놀리는 게 아니라 진심입니다. 한번 생각해 보세요.\""]], [["“But, sir,”", "\"하지만, 어르신,\" 내가 다소 진지하게 말했다."], ["says I, a little gravely, “I came to you about my own business; I beg of you to let me know, what you will advise me to do?”", "\"저는 제 볼일로 어르신을 찾아왔답니다. 부탁드리니, 제게 무엇을 하라고 조언하실지 알려 주세요.\""]], [["“I will be prepared,”", "\"준비해 두겠습니다,\""], ["says he, “against you come again.”", "그가 말했다. \"부인이 다시 오실 때까지요.\""]], [["“Nay,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["says I, “you have forbid my coming any more.”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신은 제가 더 오는 것을 금하셨잖아요.\""]], [["“Why so?”", "\"어찌하여요?\""], ["said he, and looked a little surprised.", "그가 말하며 다소 놀란 표정을 지었다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["said I, “you can’t expect I should visit you on the account you talk of.”", "내가 말했다. \"어르신이 말씀하시는 그런 명목으로 제가 어르신을 찾아오리라 기대하실 수는 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Well,” says he, “you shall promise me to come again, however, and I will not say any more of it till I have gotten the divorce, but I desire you will prepare to be better conditioned when that’s done, for you shall be the woman, or I will not be divorced at all; why, I owe it to your unlooked-for kindness, if it were to nothing else, but I have other reasons too.”", "\"좋습니다,\" 그가 말했다. \"그래도 부인은 제게 다시 오겠다고 약속하셔야 합니다. 그리고 이혼을 얻어 낼 때까지는 그 이야기를 더 꺼내지 않겠습니다. 다만 그 일이 끝나면 부인이 더 나은 처지가 되도록 준비해 주시기 바랍니다. 부인이 그 여자가 되어 주셔야 하니까요. 그러지 않으면 저는 아예 이혼하지 않겠습니다. 다른 까닭이 없다 해도, 부인의 뜻밖의 친절에 제가 빚을 진 것이거니와, 저는 다른 이유들도 있답니다.\""]], [["He could not have said anything in the world that pleased me better; however, I knew that the way to secure him was to stand off while the thing was so remote, as it appeared to be, and that it was time enough to accept of it when he was able to perform it; so I said very respectfully to him, it was time enough to consider of these things when he was in a condition to talk of them; in the meantime, I told him, I was going a great way from him, and he would find objects enough to please him better.", "그는 세상 그 무엇으로도 나를 그보다 더 기쁘게 할 수 없었다. 그러나 나는, 그를 붙드는 길은 일이 그렇게 멀어 보이는 동안 거리를 두는 것이며, 그가 그것을 이행할 능력이 되었을 때 받아들여도 늦지 않다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["We broke off here for the present, and he made me promise him to come again the next day, for his resolutions upon my own business, which after some pressing I did; though had he seen farther into me, I wanted no pressing on that account.", "그래서 나는 그에게 매우 정중하게, 그가 이런 일들을 이야기할 처지가 되었을 때 그것을 생각해도 늦지 않다고 했고, 그동안 나는 그에게서 멀리 떠나갈 것이며, 그는 나보다 더 마음에 드는 상대를 얼마든지 찾을 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 우리는 지금으로서는 여기서 이야기를 끊었고, 그는 내게 이튿날 다시 와서 내 일에 관한 그의 결정을 들으라고 약속하게 했으니, 나는 몇 차례 채근을 받은 끝에 그리했으나, 그가 내 속을 더 깊이 들여다보았더라면 그 일에 관해서라면 나는 채근이 전혀 필요 없었던 것이다."]], [["I came the next evening, accordingly, and brought my maid with me, to let him see that I kept a maid, but I sent her away as soon as I was gone in.", "그리하여 나는 이튿날 저녁에 갔고, 하녀를 두고 있음을 그에게 보이려고 하녀를 데려갔으나, 안으로 들어가자마자 곧 돌려보냈다."], ["He would have had me let the maid have stayed, but I would not, but ordered her aloud to come for me again about nine o’clock.", "그는 내게 하녀를 머물게 하라고 했으나 나는 그러지 않고, 큰 소리로 아홉 시쯤 다시 나를 데리러 오라고 하녀에게 일렀다."], ["But he forbade that, and told me he would see me safe home, which, by the way, I was not very well pleased with, supposing he might do that to know where I lived and inquire into my character and circumstances.", "그러나 그가 그것을 막으며, 나를 집까지 안전히 바래다주겠다고 했는데, 사실 나는 그것이 그리 달갑지 않았으니, 그가 내가 어디 사는지 알아내어 내 평판과 처지를 캐묻기 위해 그러는 것일지 모른다고 생각했기 때문이다."], ["However, I ventured that, for all that the people there or thereabout knew of me, was to my advantage; and all the character he had of me, after he had inquired, was that I was a woman of fortune, and that I was a very modest, sober body; which, whether true or not in the main, yet you may see how necessary it is for all women who expect anything in the world, to preserve the character of their virtue, even when perhaps they may have sacrificed the thing itself.", "그러나 나는 그것을 무릅썼으니, 그곳이나 그 인근 사람들이 나에 관해 아는 바라곤 죄다 내게 유리한 것이었기 때문이다. 그가 수소문한 끝에 나에 관해 얻은 평판이라곤, 내가 재산 있는 여자이며 매우 정숙하고 진중한 사람이라는 것뿐이었으니, 그것이 대체로 사실이든 아니든, 무릇 세상에서 무언가를 기대하는 모든 여자가, 어쩌면 그 정조라는 것 자체는 이미 희생했을지언정, 정조 있다는 평판만큼은 지키는 것이 얼마나 긴요한 일인지 여러분도 알 수 있을 것이다."]], [["I found, and was not a little pleased with it, that he had provided a supper for me.", "나는 그가 나를 위해 만찬을 마련해 둔 것을 알았고, 그것이 적잖이 기뻤다."], ["I found also he lived very handsomely, and had a house very handsomely furnished; all of which I was rejoiced at indeed, for I looked upon it as all my own.", "또 그가 매우 훌륭하게 살고 있으며, 매우 훌륭하게 꾸민 집을 가지고 있다는 것도 알았으니, 나는 그 모두가 참으로 기뻤다. 그 모두를 다 내 것으로 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["We had now a second conference upon the subject-matter of the last conference.", "우리는 이제 지난번 회담의 주제를 두고 두 번째 회담을 가졌다. 그는 정말이지 자기 속내를 아주 곧장 들이밀었다."], ["He laid his business very home indeed; he protested his affection to me, and indeed I had no room to doubt it; he declared that it began from the first moment I talked with him, and long before I had mentioned leaving my effects with him.", "그는 내게 자기 애정을 단언했고, 정말이지 나는 그것을 의심할 여지가 없었다. 그는 그것이 내가 그와 처음 이야기를 나눈 그 순간부터, 내가 그에게 내 재물을 맡기겠다는 이야기를 꺼내기 훨씬 전부터 비롯되었노라 단언했다."], ["“’Tis no matter when it began,”", "\"언제 비롯되었든 무슨 상관이랴,\""], ["thought I; “if it will but hold, ’twill be well enough.”", "나는 생각했다. \"그것이 변치만 않는다면 그것으로 충분하지.\""], ["He then told me how much the offer I had made of trusting him with my effects, and leaving them to him, had engaged him.", "그러고 나서 그는 내 재물을 자기에게 믿고 맡겨 그에게 남기겠다는 내 제안이 자기를 얼마나 사로잡았는지 말했다."], ["“So I intended it should,”", "\"그러라고 그런 거였지,\""], ["thought I, “but then I thought you had been a single man too.”", "나는 생각했다. \"하지만 그땐 당신도 독신인 줄 알았단 말이야.\""], ["After we had supped, I observed he pressed me very hard to drink two or three glasses of wine, which, however, I declined, but drank one glass or two.", "만찬을 마친 뒤 나는 그가 포도주를 두세 잔 마시라고 나를 몹시 채근하는 것을 알아챘으나, 사양하고 한두 잔만 마셨다."], ["He then told me he had a proposal to make to me, which I should promise him I would not take ill if I should not grant it.", "그러고 나서 그는 내게 한 가지 제안할 것이 있다며, 혹 그것을 들어주지 못하더라도 언짢게 여기지 않겠다고 약속해 달라고 했다."], ["I told him I hoped he would make no dishonourable proposal to me, especially in his own house, and that if it was such, I desired he would not propose it, that I might not be obliged to offer any resentment to him that did not become the respect I professed for him, and the trust I had placed in him in coming to his house; and begged of him he would give me leave to go away, and accordingly began to put on my gloves and prepare to be gone, though at the same time I no more intended it than he intended to let me.", "나는 그가 내게 불명예스러운 제안을 하지 않기를 바라며, 특히 자기 집에서는 더더욱 그렇기를 바란다고 했고, 만약 그런 것이라면 부디 그것을 제안하지 말아 달라고, 그래야 내가 그에게 표한 존경과 그의 집으로 찾아옴으로써 그에게 두었던 신뢰에 어울리지 않는 어떤 분노도 그에게 드러낼 필요가 없으리라고 했으며, 부디 떠나도 좋다고 허락해 달라 청하고는, 그러면서 장갑을 끼고 떠날 채비를 하기 시작했으나, 정작 그때 나는 그가 나를 떠나보낼 마음이 없는 것 못지않게 떠날 마음이 조금도 없었다."]], [["Well, he importuned me not to talk of going; he assured me he had no dishonourable thing in his thoughts about me, and was very far from offering anything to me that was dishonourable, and if I thought so, he would choose to say no more of it.", "자, 그는 떠난다는 말은 하지 말라고 나를 졸랐다. 그는 나에 관해 어떤 불명예스러운 것도 마음에 품지 않았으며, 불명예스러운 무언가를 내게 제안하는 것과는 거리가 멀다고 단언했고, 만약 내가 그렇게 여긴다면 그것에 관해서는 더 이상 말하지 않는 편을 택하겠노라 했다."]], [["That part I did not relish at all.", "그 부분이 나는 도무지 마뜩잖았다. 나는 그가 자기 자신에게 부끄러운 것이나 내가 듣기에 부적절한 것은 아무것도 말하지 않으리라 믿고서, 그가 하려는 말이라면 무엇이든 들을 준비가 되어 있다고 했다."], ["I told him I was ready to hear anything that he had to say, depending that he would say nothing unworthy of himself, or unfit for me to hear.", "그러자 그는 내게 자기 제안이 이러하다고 말했다. 곧 그가 아직 창녀인 자기 아내에게서 이혼을 얻어 내지 못했더라도 나와 결혼하자는 것이었다."], ["Upon this, he told me his proposal was this: that I would marry him, though he had not yet obtained the divorce from the whore his wife; and to satisfy me that he meant honourably, he would promise not to desire me to live with him, or go to bed with him till the divorce was obtained.", "그리고 자기가 떳떳한 뜻을 품고 있음을 내게 납득시키기 위해, 이혼이 성사될 때까지는 나더러 자기와 함께 살거나 잠자리를 같이하자고 청하지 않겠노라 약속하겠다고 했다. 내 마음은 그 제안에 첫마디부터 그러자고 답했으나, 그와 좀 더 위선을 떨 필요가 있었다."], ["My heart said yes to this offer at first word, but it was necessary to play the hypocrite a little more with him; so I seemed to decline the motion with some warmth, and besides a little condemning the thing as unfair, told him that such a proposal could be of no signification, but to entangle us both in great difficulties; for if he should not at last obtain the divorce, yet we could not dissolve the marriage, neither could we proceed in it; so that if he was disappointed in the divorce, I left him to consider what a condition we should both be in.", "그래서 나는 다소 발끈하며 그 제안을 사양하는 척했고, 게다가 그것을 온당치 못한 일이라며 약간 책망하면서, 그러한 제안은 우리 둘을 크나큰 곤경에 옭아매는 것 말고는 아무 의미도 없으리라고 했다. 왜냐하면 그가 끝내 이혼을 얻어 내지 못한다면 우리는 그 혼인을 무를 수도 없고, 그렇다고 그것을 밀고 나아갈 수도 없을 터이니, 만약 그가 이혼에 실패한다면 우리 둘이 어떤 처지에 놓일지 한번 생각해 보라고 했다."]], [["In short, I carried on the argument against this so far, that I convinced him it was not a proposal that had any sense in it.", "요컨대 나는 이에 반대하는 논변을 멀리까지 끌고 가서, 그것이 도무지 사리에 닿는 제안이 아님을 그에게 납득시켰다."], ["Well, then he went from it to another, and that was, that I would sign and seal a contract with him, conditioning to marry him as soon as the divorce was obtained, and to be void if he could not obtain it.", "그러자 그는 거기서 또 다른 제안으로 옮겨 갔으니, 곧 내가 그와 더불어 계약서에 서명하고 봉인하여, 이혼이 성사되는 즉시 그와 결혼하기로 조건을 정하되, 그가 이혼을 얻어 내지 못하면 그것을 무효로 한다는 것이었다."]], [["I told him such a thing was more rational than the other; but as this was the first time that ever I could imagine him weak enough to be in earnest in this affair, I did not use to say Yes at first asking; I would consider of it.", "나는 그런 것은 다른 제안보다 한결 합리적이라고 했으나, 다만 그가 이 일에 진심일 만큼 어수룩하리라고 내가 상상한 것은 이번이 처음인 데다, 나는 첫 청에 그러자고 말하는 버릇이 없으니 그것을 생각해 보겠노라 했다."]], [["I played with this lover as an angler does with a trout.", "나는 이 연인을 낚시꾼이 송어를 다루듯 가지고 놀았다."], ["I found I had him fast on the hook, so I jested with his new proposal, and put him off.", "나는 그를 단단히 낚싯바늘에 꿰어 두었음을 알았기에, 그의 새 제안을 농담으로 받아넘기며 그를 미루어 두었다."], ["I told him he knew little of me, and bade him inquire about me; I let him also go home with me to my lodging, though I would not ask him to go in, for I told him it was not decent.", "나는 그에게, 그가 나를 거의 모르니 나에 관해 수소문해 보라고 했다. 또 그가 내 숙소까지 나를 바래다 가게는 했으나 안으로 들어오라고 청하지는 않았으니, 그것이 점잖지 못하다고 했기 때문이다."]], [["In short, I ventured to avoid signing a contract of marriage, and the reason why I did it was because the lady that had invited me so earnestly to go with her into Lancashire insisted so positively upon it, and promised me such great fortunes, and such fine things there, that I was tempted to go and try.", "요컨대 나는 과감히 혼인 계약서 서명을 피했는데, 내가 그리한 까닭은, 나더러 함께 랭커셔로 가자고 그토록 간곡히 청했던 그 부인이 어찌나 단호하게 그 일을 우기며, 그곳에서 그토록 큰 재산이며 그토록 좋은 것들을 약속했던지, 가서 한번 시험해 보고 싶은 마음이 동했기 때문이다."], ["“Perhaps,”", "\"어쩌면,\""], ["said I, “I may mend myself very much”; and then I made no scruple in my thoughts of quitting my honest citizen, whom I was not so much in love with as not to leave him for a richer.", "나는 생각했다, \"내 처지를 크게 나아지게 할 수도 있겠지.\" 그러자 나는 마음속에서 내 정직한 시민과 헤어지는 데 아무 거리낌도 두지 않았으니, 나는 그를, 더 부유한 자를 위해 그를 떠나지 못할 만큼 깊이 사랑하지는 않았던 것이다."]], [["In a word, I avoided a contract; but told him I would go into the north, that he should know where to write to me by the consequence of the business I had entrusted with him; that I would give him a sufficient pledge of my respect for him, for I would leave almost all I had in the world in his hands; and I would thus far give him my word, that as soon as he had sued out a divorce from his first wife, he would send me an account of it, I would come up to London, and that then we would talk seriously of the matter.", "한마디로 나는 계약을 피했다. 다만 그에게, 나는 북부로 갈 것이며, 내가 그에게 맡긴 일의 진행을 통해 어디로 편지를 보내면 될지 알게 될 것이고, 내가 그를 존중한다는 충분한 담보를 그에게 줄 터이니, 곧 내가 세상에 가진 거의 전부를 그의 손에 맡겨 두겠노라고 했다. 또 이만큼은 그에게 약속하겠으니, 그가 자기 첫 아내에게서 이혼을 청구해 그 소식을 내게 보내는 즉시 내가 런던으로 올라올 것이고, 그러면 그때 우리가 그 일을 진지하게 의논하자고 했다."]], [["It was a base design I went with, that I must confess, though I was invited thither with a design much worse than mine was, as the sequel will discover.", "내가 품고 간 것은 비열한 속셈이었으니, 고백건대 그러했다. 다만 뒤에 드러나듯이, 나는 내 것보다 훨씬 더 고약한 속셈을 품은 자에게 그리로 초대받은 것이었다."], ["Well, I went with my friend, as I called her, into Lancashire.", "자, 나는 내가 벗이라 부르던 그 여자와 함께 랭커셔로 갔다."], ["All the way we went she caressed me with the utmost appearance of a sincere, undissembled affection; treated me, except my coach-hire, all the way; and her brother brought a gentleman’s coach to Warrington to receive us, and we were carried from thence to Liverpool with as much ceremony as I could desire.", "가는 내내 그 여자는 더없이 진실하고 꾸밈없는 애정을 풍기며 나를 어루만져 주었고, 가는 길 내내 마찻삯만 빼고 모든 것을 대접해 주었다. 그리고 그 여자의 오라비가 우리를 맞으러 워링턴까지 신사의 마차를 가지고 왔고, 우리는 거기서 내가 바랄 수 있는 만큼의 격식을 다 갖추어 리버풀까지 실려 갔다."], ["We were also entertained at a merchant’s house in Liverpool three or four days very handsomely; I forbear to tell his name, because of what followed.", "우리는 또한 리버풀의 어느 상인 집에서 사나흘간 매우 훌륭한 대접을 받았다. 뒤에 벌어진 일 때문에 그의 이름은 밝히지 않겠다."], ["Then she told me she would carry me to an uncle’s house of hers, where we should be nobly entertained.", "그러고 나서 그 여자는 자기 외삼촌 댁으로 나를 데려갈 텐데, 거기서 우리가 귀하게 대접받을 것이라고 했다."], ["She did so; her uncle, as she called him, sent a coach and four horses for us, and we were carried near forty miles I know not whither.", "그 여자는 그리했으니, 그 여자가 외삼촌이라 부르는 이가 우리를 위해 사두마차를 보내왔고, 우리는 어딘지도 모를 곳으로 사십 마일 가까이 실려 갔다."]], [["We came, however, to a gentleman’s seat, where was a numerous family, a large park, extraordinary company indeed, and where she was called cousin.", "그러나 우리는 어느 신사의 저택에 다다랐으니, 그곳에는 식솔이 많고, 큰 사냥터가 있으며, 정말이지 비범한 사람들이 모여 있었고, 거기서 그 여자는 사촌이라 불렸다."], ["I told her if she had resolved to bring me into such company as this, she should have let me have prepared myself, and have furnished myself with better clothes.", "나는 그 여자에게, 만약 이런 사람들 틈으로 나를 데려올 작정이었다면 내가 채비를 갖추고 더 나은 옷을 마련하게 해 주었어야 했다고 말했다."], ["The ladies took notice of that, and told me very genteelly they did not value people in their country so much by their clothes as they did in London; that their cousin had fully informed them of my quality, and that I did not want clothes to set me off; in short, they entertained me, not like what I was, but like what they thought I had been, namely, a widow lady of a great fortune.", "부인들이 그 점을 알아채고는 매우 점잖게 내게 말하기를, 자기네 고장에서는 런던에서처럼 사람을 옷으로 그리 따지지 않으며, 자기들 사촌이 내 신분에 관해 충분히 일러 주었으니 나를 돋보이게 할 옷 따위는 필요 없다고 했다. 요컨대 그들은 나를 실제의 나가 아니라 그들이 생각하는 나, 곧 큰 재산을 지닌 과부 부인으로 여겨 대접해 주었다."]], [["The first discovery I made here was, that the family were all Roman Catholics, and the cousin too, whom I called my friend; however, I must say that nobody in the world could behave better to me, and I had all the civility shown me that I could have had if I had been of their opinion.", "내가 여기서 처음으로 알아낸 사실은, 그 집안이 모두 로마 가톨릭교도이며, 내가 벗이라 부르던 그 사촌 또한 그러하다는 것이었다. 그러나 세상 누구도 내게 그보다 더 잘 대해 줄 수 없었다고 말해야겠으니, 내가 그들과 같은 견해를 지녔더라도 받았을 만한 온갖 정중한 대접을 다 받았던 것이다."], ["The truth is, I had not so much principle of any kind as to be nice in point of religion, and I presently learned to speak favourably of the Romish Church; particularly, I told them I saw little but the prejudice of education in all the difference that were among Christians about religion, and if it had so happened that my father had been a Roman Catholic, I doubted not but I should have been as well pleased with their religion as my own.", "사실 나는 신앙 문제에 까다로울 만큼 어떤 종류의 신조도 갖고 있지 않았던 터라, 곧 로마 교회를 호의적으로 말하는 법을 익혔다. 특히 나는 그들에게, 종교를 둘러싼 그리스도교도들 사이의 온갖 차이에서 교육의 편견 말고는 거의 아무것도 보이지 않으며, 만약 어쩌다 내 아버지가 로마 가톨릭교도였다면 나도 내 종교만큼이나 그들의 종교에 흡족해했으리라는 데 의심이 없다고 말했다."]], [["This obliged them in the highest degree, and as I was besieged day and night with good company and pleasant discourse, so I had two or three old ladies that lay at me upon the subject of religion too.", "이는 그들을 더없이 흡족하게 했고, 나는 밤낮으로 좋은 사람들과 유쾌한 담소에 둘러싸였을 뿐 아니라, 종교 문제로 나를 물고 늘어지는 나이 든 부인이 두셋 있었다."], ["I was so complaisant, that though I would not completely engage, yet I made no scruple to be present at their mass, and to conform to all their gestures as they showed me the pattern, but I would not come too cheap; so that I only in the main encouraged them to expect that I would turn Roman Catholic, if I was instructed in the Catholic doctrine as they called it, and so the matter rested.", "나는 어찌나 고분고분했던지, 완전히 발을 들이지는 않으면서도 그들의 미사에 참석하고, 그들이 본을 보여 주는 대로 그들의 온갖 몸짓을 따라 하는 데 거리낌이 없었으나, 너무 헐값에 넘어가려 하지는 않았다. 그리하여 대체로 나는, 그들이 가톨릭 교리라 부르는 것을 배운다면 로마 가톨릭교도가 되리라는 기대를 그들이 품도록 부추기는 데 그쳤고, 그렇게 그 일은 그대로 머물렀다."]], [["I stayed here about six weeks; and then my conductor led me back to a country village, about six miles from Liverpool, where her brother (as she called him) came to visit me in his own chariot, and in a very good figure, with two footmen in a good livery; and the next thing was to make love to me.", "나는 여기서 여섯 주쯤 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 나를 인도하던 그 여자가 나를 리버풀에서 여섯 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 시골 마을로 도로 데려갔는데, 거기서 그 여자의 (그 여자가 부르는 대로 하자면) 오라비가 좋은 제복을 입은 종복 둘을 거느리고 자기 사두마차를 타고 매우 훌륭한 풍채로 나를 찾아왔다. 그리고 그다음 일은 내게 구애하는 것이었다."], ["As it had happened to me, one would think I could not have been cheated, and indeed I thought so myself, having a safe card at home, which I resolved not to quit unless I could mend myself very much.", "내게 일어났던 일들로 보아, 사람들은 내가 속아 넘어갈 리 없으리라 여길 테고, 정말이지 나 자신도 그렇게 생각했으니, 집에 안전한 패를 쥐고 있어, 내 처지를 크게 나아지게 할 수 없는 한 그것을 버리지 않으리라 작정하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["However, in all appearance this brother was a match worth my listening to, and the least his estate was valued at was £1000 a year, but the sister said it was worth £1500 a year, and lay most of it in Ireland.", "그러나 모든 정황으로 보아 이 오라비는 내가 귀 기울일 만한 가치가 있는 혼처였으니, 그의 재산이 적게 잡아도 연 1000파운드로 평가되었으나, 그 누이는 연 1500파운드의 가치가 있으며 그 대부분이 아일랜드에 있다고 했다."]], [["I that was a great fortune, and passed for such, was above being asked how much my estate was; and my false friend taking it upon a foolish hearsay, had raised it from £500 to £5000, and by the time she came into the country she called it £15,000.", "큰 재산가이며 또 그런 사람으로 통하던 나는, 내 재산이 얼마인지 따위를 질문받을 처지를 넘어선 사람이었다. 그리고 내 거짓 벗은 어리석은 소문을 곧이듣고서 그것을 500파운드에서 5000파운드로 부풀렸으며, 그 고장에 들어올 무렵에는 1만 5000파운드라 일컫고 있었다."], ["The Irishman, for such I understood him to be, was stark mad at this bait; in short, he courted me, made me presents, and ran in debt like a madman for the expenses of his equipage and of his courtship.", "그 아일랜드인은—내가 알기로 그는 그러했다—이 미끼에 완전히 미쳐 버렸다. 요컨대 그는 내게 구애했고, 내게 선물을 했으며, 자기 행차와 구애에 드는 비용 때문에 미친 사람처럼 빚을 졌다."], ["He had, to give him his due, the appearance of an extraordinary fine gentleman; he was tall, well-shaped, and had an extraordinary address; talked as naturally of his park and his stables, of his horses, his gamekeepers, his woods, his tenants, and his servants, as if we had been in the mansion-house, and I had seen them all about me.", "그는, 공정히 말하자면, 비범하게 멋진 신사의 풍모를 지니고 있었으니, 키가 크고 몸매가 훤칠했으며 비범한 화술을 지녔다. 그는 자기 사냥터며 마구간이며 자기 말들, 사냥터지기들, 숲, 소작인들, 하인들에 관해, 마치 우리가 그 저택 안에 있어 내가 그것들을 죄다 내 둘레에서 본 것처럼 자연스럽게 이야기했다."]], [["He never so much as asked me about my fortune or estate, but assured me that when we came to Dublin he would jointure me in £600 a year good land; and that we could enter into a deed of settlement or contract here for the performance of it.", "그는 내 재산이나 자산에 관해서는 묻지도 않은 채, 다만 우리가 더블린에 가면 내게 연 600파운드의 좋은 땅을 과부 재산으로 떼어 주겠으며, 그것의 이행을 위해 여기서 양도 증서나 계약을 맺을 수 있다고 단언했다."]], [["This was such language indeed as I had not been used to, and I was here beaten out of all my measures; I had a she-devil in my bosom, every hour telling me how great her brother lived.", "이는 정말이지 내가 익숙지 않았던 종류의 말이었고, 나는 여기서 내 온갖 계산이 다 무너지고 말았다. 내 품속에는 암녀석 하나가 들어앉아, 시시각각 자기 오라비가 얼마나 호화롭게 사는지 내게 일러 주었다."], ["One time she would come for my orders, how I would have my coaches painted, and how lined; and another time what clothes my page should wear; in short, my eyes were dazzled.", "한번은 내 마차를 어떻게 칠하고 어떻게 안감을 댈지 내 분부를 받으러 왔고, 또 한번은 내 시동이 어떤 옷을 입을지 물었다. 요컨대 내 눈은 현혹되었다."], ["I had now lost my power of saying No, and, to cut the story short, I consented to be married; but to be the more private, we were carried farther into the country, and married by a Romish clergyman, who I was assured would marry us as effectually as a Church of England parson.", "나는 이제 아니라고 말하는 힘을 잃어버렸고, 이야기를 줄이자면, 결혼하기로 승낙하고 말았다. 다만 좀 더 은밀히 하려고 우리는 시골 더 깊숙이 실려 가, 어느 로마 가톨릭 성직자에게 결혼식을 올렸는데, 그가 잉글랜드 교회 목사 못지않게 우리를 확실히 맺어 주리라는 보장을 받았다."]], [["I cannot say but I had some reflections in this affair upon the dishonourable forsaking my faithful citizen, who loved me sincerely, and who was endeavouring to quit himself of a scandalous whore by whom he had been indeed barbarously used, and promised himself infinite happiness in his new choice; which choice was now giving up herself to another in a manner almost as scandalous as hers could be.", "이 일에서 나를 진심으로 사랑했던, 또 자기를 정말이지 야만스럽게 굴려먹은 어떤 수치스러운 창녀에게서 자기 몸을 떼어 내려 애쓰며 자신의 새로운 선택에서 무한한 행복을 기약했던 그 충실한 시민을 불명예스럽게 저버린 데 대해, 약간의 자책이 없었다고는 할 수 없다. 그런데 그 선택받은 여자는 이제, 그의 아내가 했을 법한 만큼이나 거의 수치스러운 방식으로 자기를 다른 자에게 내주고 있었던 것이다."]], [["But the glittering shoe of a great estate, and of fine things, which the deceived creature that was now my deceiver represented every hour to my imagination, hurried me away, and gave me no time to think of London, or of anything there, much less of the obligation I had to a person of infinitely more real merit than what was now before me.", "그러나 이제 나를 속이는 자가 된, 속아 넘어간 그 피조물이 시시각각 내 상상 앞에 그려 보인, 큰 재산이며 좋은 것들의 번쩍이는 신발이 나를 휩쓸어 가, 런던이나 그곳의 무엇을 생각할 겨를을 조금도 주지 않았으며, 지금 내 앞에 있는 것보다 무한히 더 참된 가치를 지닌 사람에게 내가 진 의무는 더더욱 생각할 겨를을 주지 않았다."]], [["But the thing was done; I was now in the arms of my new spouse, who appeared still the same as before; great even to magnificence, and nothing less than £1000 a year could support the ordinary equipage he appeared in.", "그러나 일은 이미 벌어졌다. 나는 이제 새 남편의 품에 안겼고, 그는 여전히 예전과 다름없어 보였으니, 호화롭다 할 만큼 화려했으며, 그가 차린 통상의 행차를 떠받치는 데는 적어도 연 1000파운드는 들 것 같았다."]], [["After we had been married about a month, he began to talk of my going to West Chester in order to embark for Ireland.", "우리가 결혼한 지 한 달쯤 되었을 때, 그는 아일랜드로 떠나는 배를 타기 위해 내가 웨스트체스터로 가는 일에 관해 이야기하기 시작했다."], ["However, he did not hurry me, for we stayed near three weeks longer, and then he sent to Chester for a coach to meet us at the Black Rock, as they call it, over against Liverpool.", "그러나 그는 나를 서두르게 하지 않았으니, 우리는 세 주가량을 더 머물렀고, 그러고 나서 그는 사람들이 블랙록이라 부르는, 리버풀 맞은편의 그곳으로 우리를 맞으러 마차를 보내 달라고 체스터로 사람을 보냈다."], ["Thither we went in a fine boat they call a pinnace, with six oars; his servants, and horses, and baggage going in the ferry-boat.", "우리는 거기로, 사람들이 피니스라 부르는, 노가 여섯 개 달린 멋진 배를 타고 갔고, 그의 하인들과 말들과 짐은 나룻배로 갔다."], ["He made his excuse to me that he had no acquaintance in Chester, but he would go before and get some handsome apartment for me at a private house.", "그는 내게 체스터에 아는 사람이 없으니, 먼저 가서 어느 개인 집에 나를 위해 훌륭한 거처를 구해 두겠다고 양해를 구했다."], ["I asked him how long we should stay at Chester.", "나는 그에게 체스터에 얼마나 머물 것이냐고 물었다."], ["He said, not at all, any longer than one night or two, but he would immediately hire a coach to go to Holyhead.", "그는 전혀 머물지 않을 것이며, 하룻밤이나 이틀 밤보다 더 길지는 않을 것이고, 곧장 홀리헤드로 갈 마차를 빌리겠다고 했다."], ["Then I told him he should by no means give himself the trouble to get private lodgings for one night or two, for that Chester being a great place, I made no doubt but there would be very good inns and accommodation enough; so we lodged at an inn in the West Street, not far from the Cathedral; I forget what sign it was at.", "그래서 나는 그에게, 하룻밤이나 이틀 밤을 위해 개인 숙소를 구하느라 수고할 일이 결코 아니니, 체스터가 큰 곳인 만큼 분명 매우 좋은 여관과 넉넉한 숙박 시설이 있을 것이라고 했다. 그리하여 우리는 대성당에서 멀지 않은 웨스트 거리의 어느 여관에 묵었는데, 어떤 간판을 단 곳이었는지는 잊어버렸다."]], [["Here my spouse, talking of my going to Ireland, asked me if I had no affairs to settle at London before we went off.", "여기서 내 남편은 내가 아일랜드로 가는 일을 이야기하다가, 떠나기 전에 런던에서 정리할 볼일이 없느냐고 물었다."], ["I told him No, not of any great consequence, but what might be done as well by letter from Dublin.", "나는 없다고, 대단한 것은 아무것도 없으며, 있다 해도 더블린에서 편지로 처리해도 될 만한 것뿐이라고 했다."], ["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["says he, very respectfully, “I suppose the greatest part of your estate, which my sister tells me is most of it in money in the Bank of England, lies secure enough, but in case it required transferring, or any way altering its property, it might be necessary to go up to London and settle those things before we went over.”", "그가 매우 정중하게 말했다. \"짐작건대 부인 재산의 대부분은, 내 누이 말로는 그 대부분이 잉글랜드 은행에 돈으로 있다는데, 충분히 안전하게 있겠지만, 혹 그것을 명의 이전하거나 어떤 식으로든 그 소유 형태를 바꿀 필요가 있다면, 우리가 건너가기 전에 런던으로 올라가 그런 일들을 정리하는 것이 좋을지도 모르겠소.\""]], [["I seemed to look strange at it, and told him I knew not what he meant; that I had no effects in the Bank of England that I knew of; and I hoped he could not say that I had ever told him I had.", "나는 그것을 의아하게 바라보는 척하며, 그에게 그게 무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 했다. 내가 아는 한 잉글랜드 은행에 아무런 재물도 없으며, 그에게 그런 게 있다고 말한 적이 없음을 그도 분명 말할 수 있으리라 바란다고 했다."], ["No, he said, I had not told him so, but his sister had said the greatest part of my estate lay there.", "그렇다고, 그는 내가 자기에게 그렇게 말하지는 않았으나, 자기 누이가 내 재산의 대부분이 거기 있다고 말했다고 했다."], ["“And I only mentioned it, me dear,”", "\"그래서 그저 그 이야기를 꺼낸 것뿐이오, 여보,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that if there was any occasion to settle it, or order anything about it, we might not be obliged to the hazard and trouble of another voyage back again”; for he added, that he did not care to venture me too much upon the sea.", "\"그것을 정리하거나 그에 관해 무언가 처리할 일이 있다면, 우리가 다시 건너왔다 가는 위험과 수고를 겪지 않아도 되게 말이오.\" 그러고는 자기는 나를 바다에 너무 많이 모험시키고 싶지 않다고 덧붙였다."]], [["I was surprised at this talk, and began to consider very seriously what the meaning of it must be; and it presently occurred to me that my friend, who called him brother, had represented me in colours which were not my due; and I thought, since it was come to that pitch, that I would know the bottom of it before I went out of England, and before I should put myself into I knew not whose hands in a strange country.", "나는 이 말에 깜짝 놀라, 그 뜻이 대체 무엇일까 매우 진지하게 헤아리기 시작했다. 그러자 곧 이런 생각이 떠올랐으니, 그를 오라비라 부르던 내 벗이 나를 내게 어울리지 않는 빛깔로 그려 보였구나 하는 것이었다. 그리고 나는, 일이 이 지경에 이르렀으니, 잉글랜드를 떠나기 전에, 또 낯선 나라에서 누구의 손인지도 모를 손에 나 자신을 맡기기 전에, 이 일의 밑바닥을 알아내야겠다고 생각했다."]], [["Upon this I called his sister into my chamber the next morning, and letting her know the discourse her brother and I had been upon the evening before, I conjured her to tell me what she had said to him, and upon what foot it was that she had made this marriage.", "그리하여 나는 이튿날 아침 그의 누이를 내 방으로 불러, 전날 저녁 그 오라비와 내가 나눈 이야기를 알려 주고는, 그 여자가 그에게 무슨 말을 했으며 무엇을 근거로 이 혼사를 성사시켰는지 말해 달라고 다그쳤다."], ["She owned that she had told him that I was a great fortune, and said that she was told so at London. “Told so!”", "그 여자는 자기가 그에게 내가 큰 재산가라고 말했다고 인정하면서, 런던에서 그렇게 들었노라고 했다."], ["says I warmly; “did I ever tell you so?”", "\"그렇게 들었다고요!\" 내가 발끈하며 말했다."], ["No, she said, it was true I did not tell her so, but I had said several times that what I had was in my own disposal.", "\"내가 당신에게 그렇게 말한 적이 있던가요?\" 아니요, 그 여자는 내가 자기에게 그렇게 말하지 않은 건 사실이나, 내 것이 내 마음대로 처분할 수 있는 것이라고 내가 여러 번 말했다고 했다."], ["“I did so,”", "\"그랬지요,\""], ["returned I very quickly and hastily, “but I never told you I had anything called a fortune; no, not that I had £100, or the value of £100, in the world.", "나는 매우 재빠르고 다급하게 받아쳤다. \"하지만 내가 당신에게 재산이라 할 만한 무엇이 있다고 말한 적은 없어요."], ["Any how did it consist with my being a fortune,”", "아니, 100파운드가, 아니 100파운드 값어치가 세상에 있다고 한 적조차 없지요. 게다가,\""], ["said I, “that I should come here into the north of England with you, only upon the account of living cheap?”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 그저 물가 싸게 살겠다는 까닭 하나로 당신과 함께 여기 잉글랜드 북부로 내려왔다는 것이, 내가 재산가라는 것과 어떻게 아귀가 맞는단 말인가요?\""], ["At these words, which I spoke warm and high, my husband, her brother (as she called him), came into the room, and I desired him to come and sit down, for I had something of moment to say before them both, which it was absolutely necessary he should hear.", "내가 발끈하여 높은 소리로 이 말들을 내뱉을 때, 내 남편, 곧 (그 여자가 부르는 대로 하자면) 그 오라비가 방으로 들어왔고, 나는 그에게 와서 앉으라고 청했으니, 둘 앞에서 할 중대한 말이 있고, 그도 그것을 꼭 들어야 했기 때문이다."]], [["He looked a little disturbed at the assurance with which I seemed to speak it, and came and sat down by me, having first shut the door; upon which I began, for I was very much provoked, and turning myself to him, “I am afraid,”", "그는 내가 그 말을 내뱉는 그 단호함에 다소 동요한 표정으로, 먼저 문을 닫고는 와서 내 곁에 앉았다. 그러자 나는—몹시 화가 나 있던 터라—그에게로 몸을 돌리며 입을 열었다."], ["says I, “my dear”", "\"여보,\" 내가 말했다(그의 편에서는 다정하게 말했으니), \"당신은 매우 큰 기만을 당하셨고, 나와 결혼함으로써 결코 돌이킬 수 없는 손해를 입으신 것 같아 두렵습니다."], ["(for I spoke with kindness on his side), “that you have a very great abuse put upon you, and an injury done you never to be repaired in your marrying me, which, however, as I have had no hand in it, I desire I may be fairly acquitted of it, and that the blame may lie where it ought to lie, and nowhere else, for I wash my hands of every part of it.”", "다만 나는 거기 손을 댄 바 없으니, 부디 그것에 대해 깨끗이 면책되고, 그 책임이 마땅히 있어야 할 곳에 놓이고 다른 어디에도 놓이지 않기를 바랍니다. 나는 그 모든 부분에서 손을 씻으니까요.\""]], [["“What injury can be done me, my dear,” says he, “in marrying you.", "\"여보, 내가 당신과 결혼하여 무슨 손해를 입을 수 있단 말이오,\""], ["I hope it is to my honour and advantage every way.”", "그가 말했다. \"그것은 모든 면에서 내 명예요 이득이라고 믿는데요.\""], ["“I will soon explain it to you,”", "\"곧 설명해 드리지요,\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “and I fear you will have no reason to think yourself well used; but I will convince you, my dear,”", "\"그리고 당신은 자신이 잘 대접받았다고 여길 만한 까닭이 없으리라 두렵습니다."], ["says I again, “that I have had no hand in it”; and there I stopped a while.", "하지만 나는 거기 손을 댄 바 없음을 당신께 납득시켜 드리겠어요, 여보,\" 내가 다시 말했고, 거기서 잠시 멈추었다."]], [["He looked now scared and wild, and began, I believe, to suspect what followed; however, looking towards me, and saying only, “Go on,” he sat silent, as if to hear what I had more to say; so I went on. “I asked you last night,” said I, speaking to him, “if ever I made any boast to you of my estate, or ever told you I had any estate in the Bank of England or anywhere else, and you owned I had not, as is most true; and I desire you will tell me here, before your sister, if ever I gave you any reason from me to think so, or that ever we had any discourse about it”; and he owned again I had not, but said I had appeared always as a woman of fortune, and he depended on it that I was so, and hoped he was not deceived. “I am not inquiring yet whether you have been deceived or not,” said I; “I fear you have, and I too; but I am clearing myself from the unjust charge of being concerned in deceiving you.", "이제 그는 겁먹고 혼이 나간 표정으로, 무슨 말이 뒤따를지 짐작하기 시작한 듯했다. 그러나 그는 나를 향해 그저 \"계속하시오\" 하고는, 내가 더 할 말을 들으려는 듯 말없이 앉아 있었다. 그래서 나는 계속했다. \"어젯밤 당신에게 물었지요,\" 내가 그에게 말했다. \"내가 당신에게 내 재산을 자랑한 적이 있는지, 혹은 잉글랜드 은행이나 다른 어디에 재산이 있다고 말한 적이 있는지 말이에요. 그리고 당신은 내가 그러지 않았다고 인정하셨고, 그건 더없는 사실이지요. 그러니 부디 여기 당신 누이 앞에서 말씀해 주세요, 내가 당신에게 그렇게 여길 만한 어떤 빌미라도 준 적이 있는지, 혹은 우리가 그에 관해 어떤 이야기라도 나눈 적이 있는지를요.\" 그러자 그는 다시 내가 그러지 않았음을 인정하면서도, 다만 내가 늘 재산 있는 여자로 보였고, 자기는 내가 그러하리라 믿고 의지했으며, 속지 않았기를 바란다고 했다. \"당신이 속았는지 아닌지를 지금 따지려는 게 아니에요,\" 내가 말했다. \"당신이 속았을까 두렵고, 나 또한 그렇지요. 다만 나는 당신을 속이는 데 가담했다는 부당한 혐의를 벗으려는 거랍니다."]], [["“I have been now asking your sister if ever I told her of any fortune or estate I had, or gave her any particulars of it; and she owns I never did.", "\"나는 방금 당신 누이에게, 내가 가진 재산이나 자산에 관해 자기에게 말한 적이 있는지, 혹은 그에 관한 어떤 세부라도 일러 준 적이 있는지 물었어요. 그리고 그 사람은 내가 결코 그러지 않았음을 인정했지요."], ["Any pray, madam,”", "그러니 부디, 부인,\""], ["said I, turning myself to her, “be so just to me, before your brother, to charge me, if you can, if ever I pretended to you that I had an estate; and why, if I had, should I come down into this country with you on purpose to spare that little I had, and live cheap?”", "내가 그 여자에게 몸을 돌리며 말했다. \"당신 오라비 앞에서 내게 공정하게, 할 수 있다면, 내가 당신에게 재산이 있는 체한 적이 있다고 책잡아 보세요."], ["She could not deny one word, but said she had been told in London that I had a very great fortune, and that it lay in the Bank of England.", "또 내게 재산이 있었다면, 어찌하여 내가 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 아끼고 물가 싸게 살겠다는 일부러의 까닭으로 당신과 함께 이 고장으로 내려왔겠어요?\" 그 여자는 한마디도 부인하지 못하면서, 다만 내게 매우 큰 재산이 있으며 그것이 잉글랜드 은행에 있다고 런던에서 들었노라고 했다."]], [["“And now, dear sir,”", "\"그러니 이제, 여보,\" 내가 다시 새 남편에게 몸을 돌리며 말했다."], ["said I, turning myself to my new spouse again, “be so just to me as to tell me who has abused both you and me so much as to make you believe I was a fortune, and prompt you to court me to this marriage?”", "\"부디 내게 공정하게 말해 주세요. 도대체 누가 당신과 나를 그토록 농락하여, 당신이 나를 재산가로 믿게 하고 이 혼사로 내게 구애하도록 부추겼나요?\""], ["He could not speak a word, but pointed to her; and, after some more pause, flew out in the most furious passion that ever I saw a man in my life, cursing her, and calling her all the whores and hard names he could think of; and that she had ruined him, declaring that she had told him I had £15,000, and that she was to have £500 of him for procuring this match for him.", "그는 한마디도 못 하고 그저 그 여자를 가리켰다. 그러고는 좀 더 멈칫하더니, 내 평생 본 적 없는 더없이 사나운 격분에 휩싸여, 그 여자를 저주하고 떠올릴 수 있는 온갖 창녀니 하는 모진 이름으로 불러대며, 그 여자가 자기를 망쳐 놓았다고 했고, 그 여자가 자기에게 내게 1만 5000파운드가 있다고 말했으며, 이 혼사를 맺어 준 대가로 자기에게서 500파운드를 받기로 되어 있었다고 폭로했다."], ["He then added, directing his speech to me, that she was none of his sister, but had been his whore for two years before, that she had had £100 of him in part of this bargain, and that he was utterly undone if things were as I said; and in his raving he swore he would let her heart’s blood out immediately, which frightened her and me too.", "그러고는 내게 말머리를 돌려, 그 여자는 자기 누이가 전혀 아니고 두 해 전부터 자기 창녀였으며, 그 여자가 이 거래의 일부로 자기에게서 100파운드를 받았고, 만약 일이 내가 말한 대로라면 자기는 완전히 끝장이라고 했다. 그리고 그는 길길이 날뛰며 당장 그 여자의 심장의 피를 뽑아내겠다고 맹세했으니, 그것이 그 여자도 나도 겁에 질리게 했다."], ["She cried, said she had been told so in the house where I lodged.", "그 여자는 울며, 내가 묵던 집에서 그렇게 들었다고 했다."], ["But this aggravated him more than before, that she should put so far upon him, and run things such a length upon no other authority than a hearsay; and then, turning to me again, said very honestly, he was afraid we were both undone.", "그러나 이는 그를 전보다 더 격앙시켰으니, 그 여자가 그토록 그를 속여먹고, 한낱 소문 말고는 다른 아무 근거도 없이 일을 그토록 멀리까지 끌고 갔다는 것이었다. 그러고는 다시 내게 몸을 돌려 매우 정직하게, 우리 둘 다 끝장난 것 같아 두렵다고 했다."], ["“For, to be plain, my dear, I have no estate,”", "\"왜냐하면, 까놓고 말해서, 여보, 나는 재산이 없소,\""], ["says he; “what little I had, this devil has made me run out in waiting on you and putting me into this equipage.”", "그가 말했다. \"내게 있던 그 얼마 안 되는 것을, 이 악마가 당신 시중을 들고 나를 이런 행차로 차려 입히느라 다 써 버리게 만들었단 말이오.\""], ["She took the opportunity of his being earnest in talking with me, and got out of the room, and I never saw her more.", "그 여자는 그가 나와 열심히 이야기하는 틈을 타 방에서 빠져나갔고, 나는 그 여자를 두 번 다시 보지 못했다."]], [["I was confounded now as much as he, and knew not what to say.", "이제 나는 그 못지않게 어쩔 줄 몰라, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 나는 여러모로 내 쪽이 더 나쁜 처지라고 생각했으나, 그가 자기는 끝장났고 게다가 재산도 없다는 말은 나를 그야말로 제정신이 아닌 지경으로 몰아넣었다."], ["I thought many ways that I had the worst of it, but his saying he was undone, and that he had no estate neither, put me into a mere distraction.", "\"이거 참,\" 내가 그에게 말했다."], ["“Why,”", "\"이건 지옥 같은 농간이었군요. 우리는 여기서 이중의 사기를 바탕으로 결혼한 셈이니까요."], ["says I to him, “this has been a hellish juggle, for we are married here upon the foot of a double fraud; you are undone by the disappointment, it seems; and if I had had a fortune I had been cheated too, for you say you have nothing.”", "보아하니 당신은 실망으로 끝장났고, 만약 내게 재산이 있었다면 나 또한 속았을 테지요. 당신은 가진 게 없다 하시니 말이에요.\""]], [["“You would indeed have been cheated, my dear,” says he, “but you would not have been undone, for £15,000 would have maintained us both very handsomely in this country; and I assure you,” added he, “I had resolved to have dedicated every groat of it to you; I would not have wronged you of a shilling, and the rest I would have made up in my affection to you, and tenderness of you, as long as I lived.”", "\"당신은 정말 속았을 거요, 여보,\" 그가 말했다. \"하지만 끝장나지는 않았을 거요. 1만 5000파운드면 이 고장에서 우리 둘이 아주 훌륭하게 살림을 꾸렸을 테니까. 그리고 단언컨대,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"나는 그 한 푼 한 푼을 다 당신에게 바치기로 작정했었소. 당신에게서 1실링도 빼앗지 않았을 것이고, 나머지는 내가 사는 동안 당신을 향한 애정과 당신을 향한 정성으로 채워 주었을 거요.\""]], [["This was very honest indeed, and I really believe he spoke as he intended, and that he was a man that was as well qualified to make me happy, as to his temper and behaviour, as any man ever was; but his having no estate, and being run into debt on this ridiculous account in the country, made all the prospect dismal and dreadful, and I knew not what to say, or what to think of myself.", "이것은 정말이지 매우 정직한 말이었고, 나는 그가 마음먹은 대로 말했으며, 또 그가 성품과 행실에 관한 한 일찍이 어떤 남자 못지않게 나를 행복하게 해 줄 자질을 갖춘 사람이라고 진정 믿는다. 그러나 그가 재산이 없고, 이 고장에서 이 우스꽝스러운 명목으로 빚을 진 탓에, 모든 전망이 암담하고 끔찍해졌으며, 나는 무슨 말을 해야 할지, 나 자신을 어떻게 생각해야 할지 알 수 없었다."]], [["I told him it was very unhappy that so much love, and so much good nature as I discovered in him, should be thus precipitated into misery; that I saw nothing before us but ruin; for as to me, it was my unhappiness that what little I had was not able to relieve us week, and with that I pulled out a bank bill of £20 and eleven guineas, which I told him I had saved out of my little income, and that by the account that creature had given me of the way of living in that country, I expected it would maintain me three or four years; that if it was taken from me, I was left destitute, and he knew what the condition of a woman among strangers must be, if she had no money in her pocket; however, I told him, if he would take it, there it was.", "나는 그에게, 그에게서 발견한 그토록 많은 사랑과 그토록 많은 선량함이 이렇듯 불행으로 곤두박질치다니 참으로 가슴 아픈 일이라고 했다. 또 우리 앞에 파멸 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않는다고 했으니, 내 처지로 말하자면, 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것이 우리를 한 주도 구해 내지 못한다는 것이 내 불행이라고 했다. 그러면서 나는 20파운드짜리 은행 어음 한 장과 11기니를 꺼내 보이며, 이것은 내 보잘것없는 수입에서 모은 것이고, 그 피조물이 그 고장의 생활 방식에 관해 내게 일러 준 바로는 이것으로 서너 해는 살 수 있으리라 예상했다고 했다. 또 이것을 내게서 빼앗아 가면 나는 빈털터리가 될 것이며, 낯선 사람들 틈에서 주머니에 돈 한 푼 없는 여자의 처지가 어떠해야 하는지 그도 알 것이라고 했다. 그러나 나는 그에게, 그가 그것을 가져가겠다면 여기 있다고 했다."]], [["He told me with a great concern, and I thought I saw tears stand in his eyes, that he would not touch it; that he abhorred the thoughts of stripping me and make me miserable; that, on the contrary, he had fifty guineas left, which was all he had in the world, and he pulled it out and threw it down on the table, bidding me take it, though he were to starve for want of it.", "그는 매우 근심스러운 빛으로, 그리고 그의 눈에 눈물이 고이는 것을 본 듯도 한데, 그것에 손대지 않겠다고 했다. 자기는 나를 발가벗겨 비참하게 만든다는 생각만으로도 진저리가 난다고 했고, 오히려 자기에게 50기니가 남았는데 그것이 세상에서 가진 전부라며, 그것을 꺼내 탁자 위에 던지고는, 자기가 그것이 없어 굶어 죽는 한이 있어도 내가 그것을 가지라고 했다."]], [["I returned, with the same concern for him, that I could not bear to hear him talk so; that, on the contrary, if he could propose any probable method of living, I would do anything that became me on my part, and that I would live as close and as narrow as he could desire.", "나는 그에 대한 같은 근심으로 받아쳐, 그가 그렇게 말하는 것을 차마 들을 수 없다고 했다. 오히려 만약 그가 살아갈 어떤 그럴듯한 방도라도 내놓을 수 있다면 나는 내 쪽에서 마땅한 무엇이든 하겠으며, 그가 바랄 수 있는 만큼 빠듯하고 옹색하게 살겠다고 했다."]], [["He begged of me to talk no more at that rate, for it would make him distracted; he said he was bred a gentleman, though he was reduced to a low fortune, and that there was but one way left which he could think of, and that would not do, unless I could answer him one question, which, however, he said he would not press me to.", "그는 내게 더 이상 그런 식으로 말하지 말아 달라고 빌었으니, 그것이 자기를 제정신이 아니게 만든다고 했다. 그는 자기가 비록 낮은 처지로 영락했으나 신사로 길러졌으며, 자기가 생각해 낼 수 있는 길은 단 하나밖에 남지 않았는데, 그것도 내가 한 가지 질문에 답해 주지 못하면 소용이 없다고 했다."], ["I told him I would answer it honestly; whether it would be to his satisfaction or not, that I could not tell.", "다만 그 질문을 내게 강요하지는 않겠다고 했다. 나는 정직하게 답하겠으나, 그것이 그를 만족시킬지 아닐지는 알 수 없다고 했다."]], [["“Why, then, my dear, tell me plainly,”", "\"그럼, 여보, 솔직히 말해 주시오,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “will the little you have keep us together in any figure, or in any station or place, or will it not?”", "\"당신이 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것으로 우리가 어떤 모양새로든, 어떤 처지나 어떤 곳에서든 함께 살아갈 수 있겠소, 없겠소?\""]], [["It was my happiness hitherto that I had not discovered myself or my circumstances at all—no, not so much as my name; and seeing these was nothing to be expected from him, however good-humoured and however honest he seemed to be, but to live on what I knew would soon be wasted, I resolved to conceal everything but the bank bill and the eleven guineas which I had owned; and I would have been very glad to have lost that and have been set down where he took me up.", "지금껏 내 다행이었던 것은, 나 자신이나 내 사정을 전혀 드러내지 않았다는 것, 아니, 내 이름조차 드러내지 않았다는 것이었다. 그리고 그가 아무리 쾌활하고 아무리 정직해 보인다 한들, 그에게서 기대할 수 있는 것이라곤 내가 곧 거덜 나리라 아는 것에 기대어 사는 일밖에 없음을 알았기에, 나는 이미 인정한 은행 어음과 11기니 말고는 모든 것을 숨기기로 작정했다."], ["I had indeed another bank bill about me of £30, which was the whole of what I brought with me, as well to subsist on in the country, as not knowing what might offer; because this creature, the go-between that had thus betrayed us both, had made me believe strange things of my marrying to my advantage in the country, and I was not willing to be without money, whatever might happen.", "그리고 나는 차라리 그것마저 잃고, 그가 나를 거두었던 그 자리에 도로 내려놓이기를 진정 바랐을 것이다. 사실 내게는 30파운드짜리 은행 어음이 한 장 더 있었는데, 그것이 내가 그 고장에서 살아갈 밑천으로, 또 무슨 일이 생길지 모른다는 생각에서 지니고 온 전부였다."], ["This bill I concealed, and that made me the freer of the rest, in consideration of his circumstances, for I really pitied him heartily.", "왜냐하면 이 피조물, 곧 우리 둘을 이렇게 배신한 그 중개인이, 내가 그 고장에서 유리하게 결혼한다는 둥 이상한 것들을 내게 믿게 만들었던 터라, 나는 무슨 일이 생기든 돈 없이 지내고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 이 어음을 나는 숨겼고, 그의 처지를 헤아려 나머지에 대해서는 더 후하게 굴 수 있었으니, 나는 정말로 그가 진심으로 가여웠기 때문이다."]], [["But to return to his question, I told him I never willingly deceived him, and I never would.", "그러나 그의 질문으로 돌아가, 나는 그에게 결코 일부러 그를 속인 적이 없으며 앞으로도 결코 그러지 않겠다고 했다."], ["I was very sorry to tell him that the little I had would not subsist us; that it was not sufficient to subsist me alone in the south country, and that this was the reason that made me put myself into the hands of that woman who called him brother, she having assured me that I might board very handsomely at a town called Manchester, where I had not yet been, for about £6 a year; and my whole income not being about £15 a year, I thought I might live easy upon it, and wait for better things.", "매우 유감스럽게도 내가 가진 그 얼마 안 되는 것으로는 우리가 살아갈 수 없으며, 그것은 남부 지방에서 나 혼자 살아가기에도 충분치 않다고 말했다. 그리고 바로 그것이 내가 그를 오라비라 부르던 그 여자의 손에 나 자신을 맡기게 된 까닭이라고 했으니, 그 여자가 내게, 아직 가 본 적은 없으나 맨체스터라는 고장에서 연 6파운드 정도로 매우 훌륭하게 하숙할 수 있다고 단언했기 때문이며, 내 전체 수입이 연 15파운드가 채 안 되는지라, 나는 그것으로 편안히 살며 더 나은 것을 기다릴 수 있으리라 생각했던 것이다."]], [["He shook his head and remained silent, and a very melancholy evening we had; however, we supped together, and lay together that night, and when we had almost supped he looked a little better and more cheerful, and called for a bottle of wine.", "그는 고개를 가로젓고 잠자코 있었으며, 우리는 매우 우울한 저녁을 보냈다. 그러나 우리는 함께 저녁을 먹고 그날 밤 함께 누웠는데, 저녁을 거의 다 먹었을 무렵 그는 낯빛이 조금 나아지고 한결 밝아져서 포도주 한 병을 가져오라고 했다."], ["“Come, my dear,”", "\"자, 여보,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “though the case is bad, it is to no purpose to be dejected.", "\"처지가 나쁘기는 해도 풀이 죽어 있어 봐야 아무 소용없소. 자, 되도록 마음 편히 가지시오."], ["Come, be as easy as you can; I will endeavour to find out some way or other to live; if you can but subsist yourself, that is better than nothing.", "나는 이러저러한 방도를 찾아 살아갈 길을 마련해 보겠소. 당신이 당신 한 몸이라도 건사할 수 있다면, 그게 아무것도 없는 것보다는 낫지."], ["I must try the world again; a man ought to think like a man; to be discouraged is to yield to the misfortune.”", "나는 세상에 다시 나가 부딪쳐 봐야 하오. 사내라면 사내답게 생각해야지."], ["With this he filled a glass and drank to me, holding my hand and pressing it hard in his hand all the while the wine went down, and protesting afterwards his main concern was for me.", "풀이 죽는다는 건 불운에 항복하는 것이오.\" 그러면서 그는 잔을 채워 내게 권했고, 포도주가 넘어가는 내내 내 손을 잡고 자기 손으로 그것을 꼭 쥐었으며, 그러고 나서 자기의 가장 큰 근심은 나라고 단언했다."]], [["It was really a true, gallant spirit he was of, and it was the more grievous to me. ’Tis something of relief even to be undone by a man of honour, rather than by a scoundrel; but here the greatest disappointment was on his side, for he had really spent a great deal of money, deluded by this madam the procuress; and it was very remarkable on what poor terms he proceeded. First the baseness of the creature herself is to be observed, who, for the getting £100 herself, could be content to let him spend three or four more, though perhaps it was all he had in the world, and more than all; when she had not the least ground, more than a little tea-table chat, to say that I had any estate, or was a fortune, or the like. It is true the design of deluding a woman of fortune, if I had been so, was base enough; the putting the face of great things upon poor circumstances was a fraud, and bad enough; but the case a little differed too, and that in his favour, for he was not a rake that made a trade to delude women, and, as some have done, get six or seven fortunes after one another, and then rifle and run away from them; but he was really a gentleman, unfortunate and low, but had lived well; and though, if I had had a fortune, I should have been enraged at the slut for betraying me, yet really for the man, a fortune would not have been ill bestowed on him, for he was a lovely person indeed, of generous principles, good sense, and of abundance of good-humour.", "그는 정말이지 참되고 의협심 있는 기질의 사람이었고, 그래서 나는 더욱 비통했다. 비열한 자에게 망하느니 명예로운 사람에게 망하는 것이 그래도 다소 위안이 되는 법이다. 그러나 여기서 가장 큰 실망은 그의 편에 있었으니, 그는 이 마담, 곧 그 뚜쟁이에게 속아 정말로 많은 돈을 써 버렸기 때문이다. 그리고 그가 얼마나 가련한 조건으로 일을 진행했는지가 매우 주목할 만했다. 첫째로 그 피조물 자신의 비열함을 눈여겨봐야 하니, 그 여자는 자기가 100파운드를 챙기려고, 그가 어쩌면 세상에 가진 전부, 아니 전부 이상을 서너 곱절 더 써 버리게 내버려 둘 수 있었던 것이다. 그러면서도 그 여자는, 한낱 차탁의 잡담 말고는, 내게 무슨 재산이 있다거나 내가 재산가라거나 하는 따위를 말할 손톱만 한 근거조차 없었던 것이다. 재산 있는 여자를—내가 만약 그랬더라면—농락하려는 속셈은 정말 충분히 비열한 일이었음이 사실이다. 가난한 처지에 큰 것들의 겉모습을 씌우는 것은 사기였고, 충분히 고약한 일이었다. 그러나 경우가 조금 다르기도 했으니, 그것도 그에게 유리한 쪽으로 그러했다. 왜냐하면 그는 여자를 농락하는 것을 업으로 삼고, 어떤 자들이 하듯 잇따라 예닐곱 명의 재산가를 손에 넣은 뒤 다 털어먹고 달아나는 그런 난봉꾼이 아니었기 때문이다. 그는 정말로 신사였고, 불운하고 처지가 낮으나 한때 넉넉히 살았던 사람이었다. 그리고 만약 내게 재산이 있었다면 나는 나를 배신한 그 잡년에게 격분했겠지만, 정말이지 그 남자로 말하자면, 재산이 그에게 잘못 주어진 셈은 아니었을 것이다. 그는 정말로 사랑스러운 사람으로, 너그러운 신조와 분별을 지녔으며 더없이 쾌활한 기질이었기 때문이다."]], [["We had a great deal of close conversation that night, for we neither of us slept much; he was as penitent for having put all those cheats upon me as if it had been felony, and that he was going to execution; he offered me again every shilling of the money he had about him, and said he would go into the army and seek the world for more.", "우리는 그날 밤 매우 허심탄회한 이야기를 많이 나누었으니, 둘 다 잠을 거의 자지 못했기 때문이다. 그는 마치 그것이 중죄여서 자기가 처형당하러 가기라도 하는 양, 내게 그 온갖 기만을 씌운 것을 뉘우쳤다. 그는 자기가 몸에 지닌 돈을 한 푼도 남김없이 다시 내게 내밀며, 자기는 군대에 들어가 더 많은 것을 찾아 세상으로 나가겠다고 했다."]], [["I asked him why he would be so unkind to carry me into Ireland, when I might suppose he could not have subsisted me there.", "나는 그에게, 거기서 나를 부양할 수 없었으리라 짐작되는데 어찌하여 그토록 매정하게 나를 아일랜드로 데려가려 했느냐고 물었다."], ["He took me in his arms.", "그는 나를 품에 안았다. \"여보,\""], ["“My dear,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “depend upon it, I never designed to go to Ireland at all, much less to have carried you thither, but came hither to be out of the observation of the people, who had heard what I pretended to, and withal, that nobody might ask me for money before I was furnished to supply them.”", "\"믿으시오, 나는 애당초 아일랜드에 갈 생각이 전혀 없었고, 당신을 거기로 데려갈 생각은 더더욱 없었소. 다만 내가 행세하던 바를 들은 사람들의 눈을 피하려고 여기로 온 것이고, 게다가 내가 그들에게 갚을 채비가 되기 전에 아무도 내게 돈을 요구하지 못하게 하려던 것이었소.\""]], [["“But where, then,”", "\"그럼 우리는,\""], ["said I, “were we to have gone next?”", "내가 말했다. \"그다음에 어디로 갔어야 하나요?\""]], [["“Why, my dear,” said he, “I’ll confess the whole scheme to you as I had laid it; I purposed here to ask you something about your estate, as you see I did, and when you, as I expected you would, had entered into some account with me of the particulars, I would have made an excuse to you to have put off our voyage to Ireland for some time, and to have gone first towards London.", "\"여보, 그게 말이오,\" 그가 말했다. \"내가 짜 놓았던 계략 전부를 당신에게 털어놓지요. 나는 여기서 당신에게 당신 재산에 관해 무언가 물을 작정이었소. 보다시피 그렇게 했고요. 그리고 당신이, 내가 예상한 대로, 그 세부에 관해 내게 무언가 설명을 늘어놓기 시작하면, 나는 핑계를 대어 우리의 아일랜드행을 한동안 미루고, 먼저 런던 쪽으로 갈 작정이었소."]], [["“Then, my dear,”", "\"그러고 나서, 여보,\""], ["said he, “I resolved to have confessed all the circumstances of my own affairs to you, and let you know I had indeed made use of these artifices to obtain your consent to marry me, but had now nothing to do but ask to your pardon, and to tell you how abundantly, as I have said above, I would endeavour to make you forget what was past, by the felicity of the days to come.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 내 처지의 온갖 정황을 당신에게 고백하고, 내가 정말로 당신의 결혼 승낙을 얻으려고 이런 술책을 썼음을 알리되, 이제는 당신의 용서를 구하는 것 말고는 할 일이 없으며, 앞서 말했듯이 다가올 나날의 행복으로 지난 일을 잊게 하려고 얼마나 넘치도록 애쓰겠는지를 당신에게 말할 작정이었소.\""]], [["“Truly,”", "\"정말이지,\" 내가 그에게 말했다."], ["said I to him, “I find you would soon have conquered me; and it is my affliction now, that I am not in a condition to let you see how easily I should have been reconciled to you, and have passed by all the tricks you had put upon me, in recompense of so much good-humour.", "\"당신은 곧 나를 정복했을 거예요. 그리고 내가 얼마나 쉽게 당신과 화해하고, 그 많은 쾌활함에 대한 보답으로 당신이 내게 씌운 온갖 술책을 다 눈감아 주었을지 당신에게 보여 줄 처지가 못 되는 것이 지금 내 비통함이랍니다."], ["But, my dear,”", "하지만, 여보,\""], ["said I, “what can we do now?", "내가 말했다."], ["We are both undone, and what better are we for our being reconciled together, seeing we have nothing to live on?”", "\"이제 우리가 무얼 할 수 있겠어요? 우리 둘 다 끝장났는데, 살아갈 게 아무것도 없는 마당에 서로 화해한들 무슨 보탬이 되겠어요?\""]], [["We proposed a great many things, but nothing could offer where there was nothing to begin with.", "우리는 많은 것을 궁리했으나, 시작할 밑천이 아무것도 없는 마당에 무엇 하나 떠오를 수가 없었다."], ["He begged me at last to talk no more of it, for, he said, I would break his heart; so we talked of other things a little, till at last he took a husband’s leave of me, and so we went to sleep.", "그는 마침내 더는 그 이야기를 하지 말아 달라고 빌었으니, 그것이 자기 가슴을 찢어 놓는다고 했다. 그래서 우리는 잠시 다른 이야기를 조금 하다가, 마침내 그가 남편으로서의 작별을 내게 고했고, 그렇게 우리는 잠이 들었다."]], [["He rose before me in the morning; and indeed, having lain awake almost all night, I was very sleepy, and lay till near eleven o’clock.", "그는 아침에 나보다 먼저 일어났다. 그리고 정말이지 나는 밤새 거의 뜬눈으로 누워 있던 터라 몹시 졸려서, 열한 시 가까이까지 누워 있었다."], ["In this time he took his horses and three servants, and all his linen and baggage, and away he went, leaving a short but moving letter for me on the table, as follows:—", "그사이 그는 자기 말들과 하인 셋, 그리고 자기 모든 내의와 짐을 챙겨 떠나 버렸으니, 탁자 위에 다음과 같은 짧지만 가슴 뭉클한 편지를 내게 남겨 두고서였다."]], [["“MY DEAR—I am a dog; I have abused you; but I have been drawn into do it by a base creature, contrary to my principle and the general practice of my life.", "\"내 사랑—나는 개 같은 놈이오. 나는 당신을 능멸했소."], ["Forgive me, my dear!", "하지만 나는 내 신조와 내 평생의 처신에 어긋나게, 어떤 비열한 피조물에게 꾐을 받아 그 일에 끌려든 것이오. 용서해 주오, 내 사랑!"], ["I ask your pardon with the greatest sincerity; I am the most miserable of men, in having deluded you.", "더없이 진심으로 당신의 용서를 비오. 나는 당신을 농락한, 사내들 가운데 가장 비참한 자요."], ["I have been so happy to possess you, and now am so wretched as to be forced to fly from you.", "나는 당신을 차지하는 더없는 복을 누렸으나, 이제 당신에게서 달아날 수밖에 없는 더없이 가련한 신세가 되었소. 용서해 주오, 내 사랑."], ["Forgive me, my dear; once more I say, forgive me!", "다시 한번 말하니, 용서해 주오! 나는 당신이 나로 인해 파멸하고, 나 자신은 당신을 부양할 수 없는 꼴을 차마 볼 수가 없소."], ["I am not able to see you ruined by me, and myself unable to support you.", "우리의 혼인은 아무것도 아니오. 나는 다시는 당신을 볼 수 없을 것이오."], ["Our marriage is nothing; I shall never be able to see you again; I here discharge you from it; if you can marry to your advantage, do not decline it on my account; I here swear to you on my faith, and on the word of a man of honour, I will never disturb your repose if I should know of it, which, however, is not likely.", "나는 여기서 당신을 그것에서 풀어 주오. 당신이 유리하게 결혼할 수 있다면, 나 때문에 사양하지 마오."], ["On the other hand, if you should not marry, and if good fortune should befall me, it shall be all yours, wherever you are.", "나는 여기서 내 신의를 걸고, 명예로운 사내의 말로써 당신에게 맹세하니, 설령 그 일을 알게 되더라도—그럴 법하지는 않으나—결코 당신의 안식을 흩뜨리지 않겠소. 한편 당신이 결혼하지 않고, 또 내게 행운이 닥친다면, 그것은 당신이 어디에 있든 모두 당신 것이 될 것이오."]], [["I have put some of the stock of money I have left into your pocket; take places for yourself and your maid in the stage-coach, and go for London; I hope it will bear your charges thither, without breaking into your own.", "나는 내게 남은 돈 밑천 가운데 얼마를 당신 주머니에 넣어 두었소. 당신과 당신 하녀의 역마차 자리를 잡아 런던으로 가시오."], ["Again I sincerely ask your pardon, and will do so as often as I shall ever think of you.", "당신 자신의 돈을 헐지 않고도 거기까지 가는 비용은 댈 수 있기를 바라오. 다시 한번 진심으로 당신의 용서를 비며, 당신을 생각할 때마다 거듭 그리하겠소."]], [["Adieu, my dear, for ever, I am, your most affectionately, J.E.”", "안녕히, 내 사랑, 영원히,\n당신을 더없이 다정히 사랑하는,"]], [["Nothing that ever befell me in my life sank so deep into my heart as this farewell.", "J.E.\" 내 평생 내게 닥친 그 무엇도 이 작별만큼 내 가슴 깊이 가라앉은 것은 없었다."], ["I reproached him a thousand times in my thoughts for leaving me, for I would have gone with him through the world, if I had begged my bread.", "나는 마음속으로 그가 나를 떠난 것을 천 번이고 책망했으니, 빌어먹는 한이 있어도 나는 세상 끝까지 그와 함께 갔을 것이기 때문이다."], ["I felt in my pocket, and there found ten guineas, his gold watch, and two little rings, one a small diamond ring worth only about £6, and the other a plain gold ring.", "나는 주머니를 더듬어 거기서 10기니와 그의 금시계, 그리고 작은 반지 둘을 찾아냈는데, 하나는 6파운드쯤 나가는 작은 다이아몬드 반지였고, 다른 하나는 무늬 없는 금반지였다."]], [["I sat me down and looked upon these things two hours together, and scarce spoke a word, till my maid interrupted me by telling me my dinner was ready.", "나는 앉아서 이 물건들을 두 시간이나 들여다보며 거의 한마디도 하지 않았는데, 마침내 하녀가 점심이 준비되었다고 일러 주어 나를 일깨웠다."], ["I ate but little, and after dinner I fell into a vehement fit of crying, every now and then calling him by his name, which was James.", "나는 거의 먹지 못했고, 점심 뒤에는 격렬하게 우는 발작에 빠져, 시시각각 그의 이름을 불러댔으니, 그 이름은 제임스였다. \"오, 제미!\""], ["“O Jemmy!”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I, “come back, come back.", "\"돌아와요, 돌아와요."], ["I’ll give you all I have; I’ll beg, I’ll starve with you.”", "내가 가진 걸 다 줄게요. 빌어먹고, 당신과 함께 굶을게요.\""], ["And thus I ran raving about the room several times, and then sat down between whiles, and then walking about again, called upon him to come back, and then cried again; and thus I passed the afternoon, till about seven o’clock, when it was near dusk, in the evening, being August, when, to my unspeakable surprise, he comes back into the inn, but without a servant, and comes directly up into my chamber.", "그러고는 이렇게 방 안을 몇 차례 미친 듯이 돌아다니다가, 사이사이 주저앉았다가, 다시 서성이며 그에게 돌아오라고 부르짖었다가, 그러고는 다시 울었다. 그렇게 나는 오후를 보냈으니, 일곱 시쯤, 때가 팔월이라 저녁 어스름이 가까웠을 무렵, 이루 말할 수 없이 놀랍게도 그가 여관으로 도로 돌아왔는데, 다만 하인 없이, 곧장 내 방으로 올라왔다."]], [["I was in the greatest confusion imaginable, and so was he too.", "나는 상상할 수 있는 더없는 혼란에 빠졌고, 그 또한 그러했다."], ["I could not imagine what should be the occasion of it, and began to be at odds with myself whether to be glad or sorry; but my affection biassed all the rest, and it was impossible to conceal my joy, which was too great for smiles, for it burst out into tears.", "나는 그것이 무슨 까닭일까 짐작할 수 없었고, 기뻐해야 할지 슬퍼해야 할지 나 자신과 다투기 시작했으나, 내 애정이 나머지 모든 것을 압도했고, 미소로는 담을 수 없을 만큼 너무도 큰 그 기쁨을 감추기란 불가능했으니, 그것이 눈물로 터져 나왔던 것이다."], ["He was no sooner entered the room but he ran to me and took me in his arms, holding me fast, and almost stopping my breath with his kisses, but spoke not a word.", "그는 방에 들어서기 무섭게 내게 달려와 나를 품에 안고는, 나를 꼭 붙들고 입맞춤으로 내 숨이 거의 막힐 지경이었으나, 한마디도 하지 못했다."], ["At length I began.", "마침내 내가 입을 열었다."], ["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\""], ["said I, “how could you go away from me?”", "내가 말했다. \"어떻게 나를 떠나갈 수 있었어요?\""], ["to which he gave no answer, for it was impossible for him to speak.", "그는 거기에 아무 대답도 하지 못했으니, 그로서는 도무지 말을 할 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["When our ecstasies were a little over, he told me he was gone about fifteen miles, but it was not in his power to go any farther without coming back to see me again, and to take his leave of me once more.", "우리의 황홀경이 좀 가라앉자, 그는 내게 열다섯 마일쯤 갔으나, 나를 다시 보고 한 번 더 작별을 고하러 돌아오지 않고는 도무지 더 갈 수가 없었다고 했다."]], [["I told him how I had passed my time, and how loud I had called him to come back again.", "나는 그에게 내가 어떻게 시간을 보냈는지, 또 얼마나 큰 소리로 그에게 돌아오라고 불렀는지 말했다."], ["He told me he heard me very plain upon Delamere Forest, at a place about twelve miles off.", "그는 열두 마일쯤 떨어진 어느 곳, 델러미어 숲에서 내 소리를 아주 또렷이 들었다고 했다. 나는 미소 지었다."], ["I smiled.", "\"아니요,\""], ["“Nay,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “do not think I am in jest, for if ever I heard your voice in my life, I heard you call me aloud, and sometimes I thought I saw you running after me.”", "\"내가 농담하는 줄 알지 마오. 내 평생 당신 목소리를 들은 적이 있다면, 나는 당신이 나를 큰 소리로 부르는 것을 들었고, 때로는 당신이 내 뒤를 쫓아 달려오는 것이 보이는 것 같았소.\""], ["“Why,”", "\"그럼,\""], ["said I, “what did I say?”—for I had not named the words to him.", "내가 말했다. \"내가 뭐라고 했나요?\"—나는 그에게 그 말을 입에 올린 적이 없었으니까."], ["“You called aloud,”", "\"당신은 큰 소리로,\""], ["says he, “and said, O Jemmy!", "그가 말했다. \"오, 제미!"], ["O Jemmy!", "오, 제미!"], ["come back, come back.”", "돌아와요, 돌아와요, 하고 말했소.\""]], [["I laughed at him.", "나는 그를 보고 웃었다. \"여보,\""], ["“My dear,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “do not laugh, for, depend upon it, I heard your voice as plain as you hear mine now; if you please, I’ll go before a magistrate and make oath of it.”", "\"웃지 마오. 분명히 말하건대, 나는 지금 당신이 내 목소리를 듣는 것만큼이나 또렷이 당신 목소리를 들었소."], ["I then began to be amazed and surprised, and indeed frightened, and told him what I had really done, and how I had called after him, as above.", "원한다면 치안판사 앞에 나가 그것을 두고 맹세라도 하겠소.\" 그러자 나는 어안이 벙벙하고 놀라며, 정말이지 겁이 나기 시작하여, 앞서와 같이 내가 실제로 무엇을 했는지, 또 어떻게 그의 뒤를 따라 불렀는지 그에게 말했다."]], [["When we had amused ourselves a while about this, I said to him: “Well, you shall go away from me no more; I’ll go all over the world with you rather.” He told me it would be a very difficult thing for him to leave me, but since it must be, he hoped I would make it as easy to me as I could; but as for him, it would be his destruction that he foresaw. However, he told me that he considered he had left me to travel to London alone, which was too long a journey; and that as he might as well go that way as any way else, he was resolved to see me safe thither, or near it; and if he did go away then without taking his leave, I should not take it ill of him; and this he made me promise.", "우리는 이 일을 두고 한동안 신기해하다가, 내가 그에게 말했다. \"자, 당신은 다시는 내게서 떠나가지 마세요. 차라리 내가 당신과 함께 온 세상을 돌아다닐게요.\" 그는 나를 떠나는 것이 자기로서는 매우 어려운 일일 것이나, 그래야만 한다면 부디 내가 되도록 마음 편히 받아들이기를 바란다고 했다. 다만 자기로 말하자면, 그것이 자기 파멸이 되리라는 것을 내다본다고 했다. 그러나 그는, 나를 혼자 런던까지 가도록 내버려 두었던 것을 헤아려 보았는데, 그것은 너무 긴 여정이며, 자기도 어차피 그 길로 가나 다른 길로 가나 마찬가지이니, 나를 거기까지, 혹은 그 근처까지 안전히 바래다주기로 작정했다고 했다. 그리고 그때 자기가 작별 인사 없이 떠나가더라도 내가 언짢게 여기지 말기를 바란다고 했고, 이것을 내게 약속하게 했다."]], [["He told me how he had dismissed his three servants, sold their horses, and sent the fellows away to seek their fortunes, and all in a little time, at a town on the road, I know not where.", "그는 어떻게 자기 하인 셋을 내보내고 그들의 말을 팔았으며, 그 친구들을 제 운수 찾아 떠나보냈는지, 그것도 길가의 어느 고을에서—어딘지는 모르겠다—삽시간에 그리했는지를 내게 말했다. \"그리고,\""], ["“And,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “it cost me some tears all alone by myself, to think how much happier they were than their master, for they could go to the next gentleman’s house to see for a service, whereas,”", "\"나 홀로 얼마간 눈물을 흘렸으니, 그들이 자기 주인보다 얼마나 더 행복한지 생각하니 말이오. 그들은 옆 신사의 집으로 가서 일자리를 알아볼 수 있지만,\""], ["said he, “I knew not wither to go, or what to do with myself.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 어디로 가야 할지, 나 자신을 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없었으니 말이오.\""]], [["I told him I was so completely miserable in parting with him, that I could not be worse; and that now he was come again, I would not go from him, if he would take me with him, let him go whither he would, or do what he would.", "나는 그에게, 그와 헤어지는 것이 너무도 완전히 비참하여 더 나빠질 수도 없으며, 이제 그가 다시 돌아왔으니, 그가 나를 데려가 준다면 어디를 가든, 무엇을 하든, 나는 그에게서 떠나지 않겠다고 했다."], ["And in the meantime I agreed that we would go together to London; but I could not be brought to consent he should go away at last and not take his leave of me, as he proposed to do; but told him, jesting, that if he did, I would call him back again as loud as I did before.", "그러는 한편 나는 우리가 함께 런던으로 가는 데 동의했다. 그러나 나는 그가 자기 말대로 끝내 떠나가며 내게 작별을 고하지 않는다는 것에는 도무지 응할 수 없어, 농담조로, 만약 그가 그러면 앞서처럼 큰 소리로 그를 도로 불러대겠다고 했다."], ["Then I pulled out his watch and gave it him back, and his two rings, and his ten guineas; but he would not take them, which made me very much suspect that he resolved to go off upon the road and leave me.", "그러고는 그의 시계를 꺼내 그에게 돌려주었고, 그의 반지 둘과 그의 10기니도 돌려주었으나, 그는 그것을 받으려 하지 않았으니, 그것이 나로 하여금 그가 길에서 떠나가 나를 버릴 작정이구나 하고 몹시 의심하게 만들었다."]], [["The truth is, the circumstances he was in, the passionate expressions of his letter, the kind, gentlemanly treatment I had from him in all the affair, with the concern he showed for me in it, his manner of parting with that large share which he gave me of his little stock left—all these had joined to make such impressions on me, that I really loved him most tenderly, and could not bear the thoughts of parting with him.", "사실 그가 처한 정황, 그의 편지에 담긴 절절한 표현, 이 모든 일에서 내가 그에게서 받은 친절하고 신사다운 대접, 거기서 그가 내게 보인 근심, 그리고 자기에게 남은 그 적은 밑천에서 그토록 큰 몫을 내게 나눠 주며 헤어지던 그 태도—이 모든 것이 한데 어우러져 내게 그런 깊은 인상을 남긴 터라, 나는 정말로 그를 더없이 애틋이 사랑하게 되었고, 그와 헤어진다는 생각을 견딜 수가 없었다."]], [["Two days after this we quitted Chester, I in the stage-coach, and he on horseback.", "이 일이 있고 이틀 뒤 우리는 체스터를 떠났으니, 나는 역마차로, 그는 말을 타고 갔다."], ["I dismissed my maid at Chester.", "나는 체스터에서 하녀를 내보냈다."], ["He was very much against my being without a maid, but she being a servant hired in the country, and I resolving to keep no servant at London, I told him it would have been barbarous to have taken the poor wench and have turned her away as soon as I came to town; and it would also have been a needless charge on the road, so I satisfied him, and he was easy enough on the score.", "그는 내가 하녀 없이 지내는 것을 몹시 반대했으나, 그 아이가 그 고장에서 고용한 하인인 데다 나는 런던에서 하인을 두지 않기로 작정했던 터라, 나는 그에게, 그 가엾은 계집아이를 데려갔다가 도시에 닿자마자 내쫓는 것은 야박한 일이 될 것이며, 또 그것은 길에서 쓸데없는 비용이 되리라고 했다. 그렇게 나는 그를 납득시켰고, 그도 그 점에 관해 충분히 마음을 놓았다."]], [["He came with me as far as Dunstable, within thirty miles of London, and then he told me fate and his own misfortunes obliged him to leave me, and that it was not convenient for him to go to London, for reasons which it was of no value to me to know, and I saw him preparing to go.", "그는 런던에서 삼십 마일 안쪽인 던스터블까지 나와 함께 왔고, 그러고는 운명과 자신의 불운 때문에 나를 떠날 수밖에 없으며, 자기가 런던으로 가는 것은 적절치 않다고, 내가 알아 봐야 아무 쓸모없는 이유들 때문에 그러하다고 했다. 그리고 나는 그가 떠날 채비를 하는 것을 보았다."], ["The stage-coach we were in did not usually stop at Dunstable, but I desiring it but for a quarter of an hour, they were content to stand at an inn-door a while, and we went into the house.", "우리가 탄 역마차는 보통 던스터블에 서지 않았으나, 내가 십오 분만 서 달라고 청하니, 그들은 잠시 여관 문 앞에 서 있는 데 응했고, 우리는 그 집 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["Being in the inn, I told him I had but one favour more to ask of him, and that was, that since he could not go any farther, he would give me leave to stay a week or two in the town with him, that we might in that time think of something to prevent such a ruinous thing to us both, as a final separation would be; and that I had something of moment to offer him, that I had never said yet, and which perhaps he might find practicable to our mutual advantage.", "여관에 들어, 나는 그에게 단 한 가지 청이 더 있다고 했으니, 곧 그가 더 멀리 갈 수 없다면, 그와 함께 이 고을에서 한두 주 머물도록 허락해 달라는 것이었다. 그동안 우리 둘 모두에게 최종 이별만큼 파멸적인 일을 막을 무언가를 궁리해 볼 수 있도록 말이다. 또 나는 그에게, 아직 한 번도 말한 적 없는 중대한 무언가를 내놓을 것이 있는데, 어쩌면 그가 우리 서로의 이익을 위해 실행할 만하다고 여길지도 모른다고 했다."]], [["This was too reasonable a proposal to be denied, so he called the landlady of the house, and told her his wife was taken ill, and so ill that she could not think of going any farther in the stage-coach, which had tired her almost to death, and asked if she could not get us a lodging for two or three days in a private house, where I might rest me a little, for the journey had been too much for me.", "이것은 거절하기에는 너무도 사리에 맞는 제안이었던지라, 그는 그 집 안주인을 불러, 자기 아내가 병이 났는데 너무 심해서 역마차로 더 가는 것은 생각도 할 수 없다고, 마차가 나를 거의 죽을 지경으로 지치게 했다고 말하고는, 내가 좀 쉴 수 있도록 어느 개인 집에 이삼 일 묵을 거처를 구해 줄 수 없겠느냐고, 여정이 내게 너무 벅찼다고 물었다."], ["The landlady, a good sort of woman, well-bred and very obliging, came immediately to see me; told me she had two or three very good rooms in a part of the house quite out of the noise, and if I saw them, she did not doubt but I would like them, and I should have one of her maids, that should do nothing else but be appointed to wait on me.", "그 안주인은 마음씨 좋은 여자로, 예의 바르고 매우 친절했는데, 곧장 나를 보러 와서는, 집 안의 한쪽, 소음에서 완전히 벗어난 곳에 매우 좋은 방이 두셋 있으니, 보면 분명 마음에 들 것이며, 자기 하녀 가운데 하나를 붙여, 오직 나를 시중드는 일만 맡게 하겠다고 했다. 이는 너무도 친절하여, 나는 그것을 받아들여 감사할 수밖에 없었다."], ["This was so very kind, that I could not but accept of it, and thank her; so I went to look on the rooms and liked them very well, and indeed they were extraordinarily furnished, and very pleasant lodgings; so we paid the stage-coach, took out our baggage, and resolved to stay here a while.", "그래서 나는 방들을 보러 갔고 매우 마음에 들었으니, 정말이지 그것들은 유난히 잘 꾸며져 있었고 매우 쾌적한 거처였다. 그리하여 우리는 역마차 삯을 치르고 짐을 내려, 여기서 한동안 머물기로 작정했다."]], [["Here I told him I would live with him now till all my money was spent, but would not let him spend a shilling of his own.", "여기서 나는 그에게, 이제 내 돈이 다 떨어질 때까지 그와 함께 살되, 그가 자기 돈은 1실링도 쓰지 못하게 하겠다고 했다."], ["We had some kind squabble about that, but I told him it was the last time I was like to enjoy his company, and I desired he would let me be master in that thing only, and he should govern in everything else; so he acquiesced.", "우리는 그 일을 두고 다정한 실랑이를 좀 벌였으나, 나는 그에게, 이것이 내가 그의 벗을 누릴 마지막 기회일 듯하니, 그 한 가지 일에서만큼은 부디 내가 주인이 되게 해 달라고, 다른 모든 일에서는 그가 다스리면 된다고 했다. 그래서 그는 응낙했다."]], [["Here one evening, taking a walk into the fields, I told him I would now make the proposal to him I had told him of; accordingly I related to him how I had lived in Virginia, that I had a mother I believed was alive there still, though my husband was dead some years.", "여기서 어느 날 저녁, 들판으로 산책을 나가, 나는 그에게 이제 내가 말했던 그 제안을 하겠노라고 했다. 그리하여 나는 그에게, 내가 버지니아에서 어떻게 살았는지, 내게 어머니가 있었는데 내 생각에는\n내 남편은 몇 해 전에 죽었으나 그녀는 거기서 아직 살아 있다고 했다."], ["I told him that had not my effects miscarried, which, by the way, I magnified pretty much, I might have been fortune good enough to him to have kept us from being parted in this manner.", "나는 그에게, 만약 내 재산이 잘못되지만 않았더라면—덧붙여 말하자면 나는 이것을 꽤나 부풀려 말했다—내가 이런 식으로 우리가 헤어지지 않을 만큼 그에게 넉넉한 재산이 되었을지도 모른다고 했다."], ["Then I entered into the manner of peoples going over to those countries to settle, how they had a quantity of land given them by the Constitution of the place; and if not, that it might be purchased at so easy a rate this it was not worth naming.", "그러고는 사람들이 그런 나라들로 정착하러 건너가는 방식, 그곳의 제도에 따라 어떻게 일정량의 땅을 받게 되는지, 그렇지 않다 하더라도 입에 올릴 가치도 없을 만큼 헐한 값으로 땅을 살 수 있다는 이야기를 풀어 놓았다."]], [["I then gave him a full and distinct account of the nature of planting; how with carrying over but two or three hundred pounds value in English goods, with some servants and tools, a man of application would presently lay a foundation for a family, and in a very few years be certain to raise an estate.", "그런 다음 나는 그에게 농장 경영의 본질에 관해 빠짐없이 또렷하게 설명해 주었다. 곧, 영국 상품으로 이삼백 파운드어치만 가지고 건너가서, 약간의 하인과 연장을 갖추면, 부지런한 사람은 곧 한 가문의 토대를 닦을 수 있고, 몇 해 안에 틀림없이 재산을 일굴 수 있다는 것이었다."]], [["I let him into the nature of the product of the earth; how the ground was cured and prepared, and what the usual increase of it was; and demonstrated to him, that in a very few years, with such a beginning, we should be as certain of being rich as we were now certain of being poor.", "나는 그에게 땅에서 나는 산물의 본질, 토지가 어떻게 손질되고 마련되는지, 그리고 그 통상적인 소출이 얼마나 되는지를 일러 주었고, 그러한 시작으로 몇 해 안에 우리가 지금 가난할 것이 확실한 만큼이나 부유해질 것이 확실하다는 것을 그에게 증명해 보였다."]], [["He was surprised at my discourse; for we made it the whole subject of our conversation for near a week together, in which time I laid it down in black and white, as we say, that it was morally impossible, with a supposition of any reasonable good conduct, but that we must thrive there and do very well.", "그는 내 이야기에 놀랐으니, 우리는 거의 일주일 내내 이것을 우리 대화의 온전한 주제로 삼았고, 그동안 나는 우리 말마따나 흑백으로 또렷이 적어 보였는데, 곧 어느 정도 분별 있는 처신을 전제한다면, 우리가 거기서 번창하여 아주 잘 살게 되지 않는 것은 도덕적으로 불가능하다는 것이었다."]], [["Then I told him what measures I would take to raise such a sum of £300 or thereabouts; and I argued with him how good a method it would be to put an end to our misfortunes and restore our circumstances in the world, to what we had both expected; and I added, that after seven years, if we lived, we might be in a posture to leave our plantations in good hands, and come over again and receive the income of it, and live here and enjoy it; and I gave him examples of some that had done so, and lived now in very good circumstances in London.", "그러고는 나는 그에게 그런 300파운드 안팎의 돈을 마련하기 위해 내가 어떤 방도를 취하겠는지 일러 주었고, 우리의 불운을 끝장내고 우리 둘 다 본디 기대했던 세상에서의 처지를 회복하는 데 그것이 얼마나 훌륭한 방책인지를 그와 따져 보았다. 그리고 덧붙이기를, 칠 년 뒤에 우리가 살아 있다면, 우리 농장을 좋은 사람들 손에 맡겨 두고 다시 건너와서 그 수입을 받아, 여기서 살며 그것을 누릴 형편이 될 수도 있다고 했다. 또 그렇게 하고서 지금 런던에서 아주 좋은 처지로 살아가는 몇몇 사람들의 예를 그에게 들어 주었다."]], [["In short, I pressed him so to it, that he almost agreed to it, but still something or other broke it off again; till at last he turned the tables, and he began to talk almost to the same purpose of Ireland.", "요컨대 나는 그를 어찌나 다그쳤던지 그가 거의 동의할 지경이었으나, 그래도 이런저런 것이 자꾸 그것을 깨뜨려 놓았으니, 마침내 그가 처지를 뒤바꾸어 거의 같은 취지로 아일랜드에 관해 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다."]], [["He told me that a man that could confine himself to country life, and that could find but stock to enter upon any land, should have farms there for £50 a year, as good as were here let for £200 a year; that the produce was such, and so rich the land, that if much was not laid up, we were sure to live as handsomely upon it as a gentleman of £3000 a year could do in England and that he had laid a scheme to leave me in London, and go over and try; and if he found he could lay a handsome foundation of living suitable to the respect he had for me, as he doubted not he should do, he would come over and fetch me.", "그는 시골 생활에 자신을 가둘 수 있고, 어떤 땅이든 들어가 부칠 밑천만 마련할 수 있는 사람이라면, 거기서는 연 50파운드면 여기서 연 200파운드에 세놓는 것만큼 좋은 농장을 얻을 수 있다고 했다. 소출이 그러하고 땅이 어찌나 기름진지, 많이 모아 두지 않더라도 우리가 잉글랜드에서 연 3000파운드를 가진 신사가 살 수 있는 만큼이나 번듯하게 거기서 살 수 있는 것이 확실하다고 했다. 그리고 자기는 나를 런던에 남겨 두고 건너가서 시험해 볼 계획을 세웠으며, 만약 나에 대한 자신의 존중에 걸맞은 살림의 그럴듯한 토대를 닦을 수 있겠다 싶으면—그렇게 되리라 그는 의심치 않았거니와—건너와서 나를 데려가겠다고 했다."]], [["I was dreadfully afraid that upon such a proposal he would have taken me at my word, viz.", "나는 이런 제안을 두고 그가 내 말을 곧이곧대로 받아들일까 봐, 다시 말해 내가 말하던 대로 그 보잘것없는 내 수입을 팔아 돈으로 바꾸어, 그가 그것을 아일랜드로 가지고 건너가 그것으로 자신의 시험을 해 보게 할까 봐 끔찍이도 두려웠다."], ["to sell my little income as I called it, and turn it into money, and let him carry it over into Ireland and try his experiment with it; but he was too just to desire it, or to have accepted it if I had offered it; and he anticipated me in that, for he added, that he would go and try his fortune that way, and if he found he could do anything at it to live, then, by adding mine to it when I went over, we should live like ourselves; but that he would not hazard a shilling of mine till he had made the experiment with a little, and he assured me that if he found nothing to be done in Ireland, he would then come to me and join in my project for Virginia.", "그러나 그는 너무도 올바른 사람이라 그것을 바라지도 않았고, 설령 내가 권했더라도 받아들이지 않았을 것이며, 오히려 그 점에서 나를 앞질렀으니, 그가 덧붙이기를, 자기가 가서 그 방면으로 운을 시험해 보고, 거기서 먹고살 만한 무언가를 할 수 있겠다 싶으면, 그때 내가 건너갈 때 내 것을 거기에 보태어 우리답게 살자고 했다. 다만 작은 것으로 시험을 마치기 전에는 내 돈에서 단 한 푼도 모험을 걸지 않겠다고 했고, 만약 아일랜드에서 할 수 있는 일이 아무것도 없으면 그때는 내게 와서 버지니아에 관한 내 계획에 합류하겠노라고 다짐했다."]], [["He was so earnest upon his project being to be tried first, that I could not withstand him; however, he promised to let me hear from him in a very little time after his arriving there, to let me know whether his prospect answered his design, that if there was not a possibility of success, I might take the occasion to prepare for our other voyage, and then, he assured me, he would go with me to America with all his heart.", "그는 자신의 계획을 먼저 시험해 보아야 한다는 데 어찌나 간곡했던지 나로서는 그를 막을 수가 없었다. 그래도 그는 거기 도착한 뒤 아주 짧은 시일 안에 자기 소식을 내게 알려, 자신의 전망이 자기 계획에 부합하는지 알게 해 주겠다고 약속했으니, 만약 성공의 가능성이 없다면 내가 그 기회를 틈타 우리의 다른 항해를 준비할 수 있도록 하기 위함이었다. 그러고는 그때는 진심으로 기꺼이 나와 함께 아메리카로 가겠노라고 다짐했다."]], [["I could bring him to nothing further than this.", "나는 그에게서 이 이상은 끌어낼 수 없었다."], ["However, those consultations entertained us near a month, during which I enjoyed his company, which indeed was the most entertaining that ever I met in my life before.", "그래도 이런 의논들은 거의 한 달 동안 우리를 즐겁게 해 주었으니, 그동안 나는 그의 벗 됨을 누렸는데, 그것은 참으로 내 평생 그전에 만나 본 가운데 가장 즐거운 것이었다."], ["In this time he let me into the whole story of his own life, which was indeed surprising, and full of an infinite variety sufficient to fill up a much brighter history, for its adventures and incidents, than any I ever saw in print; but I shall have occasion to say more of him hereafter.", "이 동안 그는 자기 자신의 삶의 이야기를 죄다 나에게 들려주었으니, 그것은 참으로 놀라웠고, 무한한 다채로움으로 가득 차서, 그 모험과 사건들로 말하자면 내가 일찍이 활자로 본 어떤 이야기보다도 훨씬 더 빛나는 역사를 채우고도 남을 만했다. 그러나 그에 관해서는 앞으로 더 이야기할 기회가 있을 것이다."]], [["We parted at last, though with the utmost reluctance on my side; and indeed he took his leave very unwillingly too, but necessity obliged him, for his reasons were very good why he would not come to London, as I understood more fully some time afterwards.", "우리는 마침내 헤어졌으니, 비록 내 쪽으로는 더할 나위 없이 마음이 내키지 않았으나 그러했고, 사실 그 역시 매우 마지못해 작별을 고했으나, 형편이 그를 어쩔 수 없게 했으니, 그가 런던에 오지 않으려 한 데에는 매우 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었던바, 이는 얼마 뒤에 내가 더 온전히 알게 되었다."]], [["I gave him a direction how to write to me, though still I reserved the grand secret, and never broke my resolution, which was not to let him ever know my true name, who I was, or where to be found; he likewise let me know how to write a letter to him, so that, he said, he would be sure to receive it.", "나는 그에게 내게 편지하는 법을 일러 주었으나, 그래도 그 큰 비밀만은 간직했고, 결코 내 결심을 깨뜨리지 않았으니, 곧 그에게 내 진짜 이름이며, 내가 누구인지, 어디서 찾을 수 있는지를 결코 알리지 않겠다는 것이었다. 그 역시 자기에게 편지 쓰는 법을 내게 알려 주었으니, 그렇게 하면 자기가 그것을 틀림없이 받게 될 거라고 했다."]], [["I came to London the next day after we parted, but did not go directly to my old lodgings; but for another nameless reason took a private lodging in St.", "우리가 헤어진 다음 날 나는 런던에 왔으나, 곧장 내 옛 거처로 가지는 않았다. 다만 또 다른, 이름 댈 수 없는 이유로, 클러큰웰 근처의 세인트존 거리, 혹은 속되게 일컫는 대로 하자면 세인트존스 거리에 사사로운 거처를 잡았다."], ["John’s Street, or, as it is vulgarly called, St. Jones’s, near Clerkenwell; and here, being perfectly alone, I had leisure to sit down and reflect seriously upon the last seven months’ ramble I had made, for I had been abroad no less.", "그리고 여기서, 완전히 혼자가 되어, 나는 차분히 앉아 지난 일곱 달 동안 내가 한 떠돌이 생활을 진지하게 되돌아볼 여유를 가졌으니, 그동안 나는 그에 못지않게 바깥으로 나돌아 다녔던 것이다."], ["The pleasant hours I had with my last husband I looked back on with an infinite deal of pleasure; but that pleasure was very much lessened when I found some time after that I was really with child.", "마지막 남편과 함께 보낸 즐거운 시간들을 나는 한없는 기쁨으로 돌이켜 보았다. 그러나 그 기쁨은 얼마 뒤에 내가 정말로 아이를 가졌다는 것을 알게 되었을 때 무척이나 줄어들었다."]], [["This was a perplexing thing, because of the difficulty which was before me where I should get leave to lie in; it being one of the nicest things in the world at that time of day for a woman that was a stranger, and had no friends, to be entertained in that circumstance without security, which, by the way, I had not, neither could I procure any.", "이는 골치 아픈 일이었으니, 어디서 해산할 허락을 얻을 것인가 하는 어려움이 내 앞에 놓여 있었기 때문이다. 그 시절에 낯선 데다 의지할 벗도 없는 여자가 보증 없이 그런 처지로 받아들여진다는 것은 세상에서 가장 까다로운 일 가운데 하나였으니, 덧붙여 말하자면 나는 보증이 없었고, 마련할 수도 없었다."]], [["I had taken care all this while to preserve a correspondence with my honest friend at the bank, or rather he took care to correspond with me, for he wrote to me once a week; and though I had not spent my money so fast as to want any from him, yet I often wrote also to let him know I was alive.", "그러는 동안 내내 나는 은행에 있는 정직한 내 벗과의 서신 왕래를 이어 가도록 마음을 써 왔는데, 아니 오히려 그가 나와 서신을 주고받으려고 마음을 썼다고 해야겠으니, 그는 내게 일주일에 한 번씩 편지를 썼던 것이다. 그리고 내가 그에게서 무언가를 받아야 할 만큼 내 돈을 빨리 써 버린 것은 아니었으나, 그래도 나는 종종 편지를 써서 내가 살아 있음을 그에게 알렸다."], ["I had left directions in Lancashire, so that I had these letters, which he sent, conveyed to me; and during my recess at St.", "나는 랭커셔에 일러 둔 바가 있어서, 그가 보내는 이 편지들이 내게 전달되도록 해 두었다."], ["Jones’s received a very obliging letter from him, assuring me that his process for a divorce from his wife went on with success, though he met with some difficulties in it that he did not expect.", "그리고 세인트존스에 은거하는 동안 나는 그에게서 매우 정중한 편지를 한 통 받았는데, 그는 아내와의 이혼 소송이 성공적으로 진행되고 있다고 확언하면서도, 자기가 예상치 못한 몇 가지 어려움에 부딪혔다고 했다."]], [["I was not displeased with the news that his process was more tedious than he expected; for though I was in no condition to have him yet, not being so foolish to marry him when I knew myself to be with child by another man, as some I know have ventured to do, yet I was not willing to lose him, and, in a word, resolved to have him if he continued in the same mind, as soon as I was up again; for I saw apparently I should hear no more from my husband; and as he had all along pressed to marry, and had assured me he would not be at all disgusted at it, or ever offer to claim me again, so I made no scruple to resolve to do it if I could, and if my other friend stood to his bargain; and I had a great deal of reason to be assured that he would stand to it, by the letters he wrote to me, which were the kindest and most obliging that could be.", "나는 그의 소송이 그가 예상한 것보다 더 더디다는 소식에 언짢지 않았으니, 비록 내가 아직 그를 맞아들일 처지가 못 되었음에도—내가 다른 남자의 아이를 가진 줄 뻔히 알면서, 내가 아는 몇몇이 감히 그러했던 것처럼 그와 결혼할 만큼 어리석지는 않았으므로—그래도 나는 그를 잃고 싶지 않았고, 한마디로 내가 다시 거동할 수 있게 되는 즉시, 그가 같은 마음을 이어 간다면 그를 맞아들이기로 작정했기 때문이다. 보아하니 내 남편에게서는 더 이상 소식이 없을 것이 분명했고, 그가 줄곧 결혼하라고 다그쳤고, 그 일을 두고 자기는 조금도 언짢아하지 않을 것이며, 결코 다시 나를 자기 사람이라 주장하지 않겠다고 다짐했던 터라, 나는 할 수만 있다면, 그리고 내 다른 벗이 자기 약속을 지킨다면 그렇게 하기로 작정하는 데 거리낌이 없었다. 그리고 그가 내게 쓴 편지들로 미루어, 그가 그 약속을 지키리라고 확신할 만한 충분한 이유가 있었으니, 그 편지들은 더없이 다정하고 정중한 것이었다."]], [["I now grew big, and the people where I lodged perceived it, and began to take notice of it to me, and, as far as civility would allow, intimated that I must think of removing.", "이제 나는 배가 불러 왔고, 내가 묵던 집 사람들이 그것을 알아채고 내게 그 일을 들먹이기 시작했으며, 예의가 허락하는 한도 안에서, 내가 거처를 옮길 생각을 해야 한다는 뜻을 넌지시 비쳤다."], ["This put me to extreme perplexity, and I grew very melancholy, for indeed I knew not what course to take.", "이 일은 나를 극도의 곤혹에 빠뜨렸고, 나는 몹시 우울해졌으니, 참으로 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["I had money, but no friends, and was like to have a child upon my hands to keep, which was a difficulty I had never had upon me yet, as the particulars of my story hitherto make appear.", "돈은 있었으나 벗이 없었고, 거두어 키워야 할 아이가 손에 들어올 판이었는데, 이는 지금까지 내 이야기의 세세한 대목들이 드러내듯이 내가 일찍이 겪어 본 적 없는 어려움이었다."]], [["In the course of this affair I fell very ill, and my melancholy really increased my distemper; my illness proved at length to be only an ague, but my apprehensions were really that I should miscarry.", "이 일을 겪는 와중에 나는 몹시 앓아눕게 되었고, 내 우울이 정말로 내 병을 키웠다. 내 병은 마침내 학질에 지나지 않는 것으로 드러났으나, 내가 정말로 걱정한 것은 유산하지나 않을까 하는 것이었다."], ["I should not say apprehensions, for indeed I would have been glad to miscarry, but I could never be brought to entertain so much as a thought of endeavouring to miscarry, or of taking any thing to make me miscarry; I abhorred, I say, so much as the thought of it.", "걱정이라고 말해서는 안 되겠으니, 사실 나는 유산했다면 기뻤을 것이기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 유산을 꾀하려 한다거나, 유산하게 하는 무언가를 먹으려 한다는 생각만은 결코 품을 수 없었으니, 다시 말하건대 나는 그런 생각조차 끔찍이 싫어했다."]], [["However, speaking of it in the house, the gentlewoman who kept the house proposed to me to send for a midwife.", "그러나 집에서 그 이야기를 하던 중에, 그 집을 꾸려 가던 점잖은 부인이 내게 산파를 부르자고 권했다."], ["I scrupled it at first, but after some time consented to it, but told her I had no particular acquaintance with any midwife, and so left it to her.", "처음엔 망설였으나 얼마 뒤 그러기로 했고, 다만 나는 어느 산파와도 특별히 아는 사이가 아니라고 일러 두고 그 일을 그녀에게 맡겼다."]], [["It seems the mistress of the house was not so great a stranger to such cases as mine was as I thought at first she had been, as will appear presently, and she sent for a midwife of the right sort—that is to say, the right sort for me.", "보아하니 그 집 안주인은 내 처지 같은 경우에, 처음 내가 짐작했던 것만큼 그리 낯선 사람이 아니었으니, 이는 곧 드러날 것이거니와, 그녀는 제대로 된 부류의 산파를—다시 말해 나에게 제대로 된 부류의 산파를—불러 주었다."]], [["The woman appeared to be an experienced woman in her business, I mean as a midwife; but she had another calling too, in which she was as expert as most women if not more.", "그 여자는 자기 일에, 곧 산파로서 노련한 여자로 보였다. 그러나 그녀에게는 또 다른 직업도 있었으니, 그 일에서도 그녀는 웬만한 여자들 못지않게, 아니 그 이상으로 능란했다."], ["My landlady had told her I was very melancholy, and that she believed that had done me harm; and once, before me, said to her, “Mrs.", "내 안주인은 그녀에게 내가 몹시 우울하다고, 그리고 그것이 내게 해를 끼친 것 같다고 일러 두었다. 그리고 한번은 내 앞에서 그녀에게 이렇게 말했다."], ["B——”", "\"B 부인\" (산파를 가리킨 것이었다), \"이 부인의 골칫거리는 아무래도 당신 영역에 꽤 들어맞는 종류인 것 같으니, 그러니 당신이 이분을 위해 무얼 해 줄 수 있거든 부디 그렇게 해 주세요."], ["(meaning the midwife), “I believe this lady’s trouble is of a kind that is pretty much in your way, and therefore if you can do anything for her, pray do, for she is a very civil gentlewoman”; and so she went out of the room.", "아주 점잖은 부인이거든요.\" 그러고는 그녀가 방에서 나갔다."]], [["I really did not understand her, but my Mother Midnight began very seriously to explain what she meant, as soon as she was gone.", "나는 정말로 그녀의 말을 알아듣지 못했으나, 나의 그 한밤의 산파 어멈은 그녀가 나가자마자 자기가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 아주 진지하게 설명하기 시작했다."], ["“Madam,”", "\"부인,\""], ["says she, “you seem not to understand what your landlady means; and when you do understand it, you need not let her know at all that you do so.", "그녀가 말했다, \"댁의 안주인이 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 못 알아들으신 것 같군요. 그리고 알아듣게 되시더라도, 댁이 알아들었다는 걸 그분에게 전혀 알릴 필요는 없어요."]], [["“She means that you are under some circumstances that may render your lying in difficult to you, and that you are not willing to be exposed.", "\"그분 말씀은, 댁이 해산을 어렵게 만들 만한 어떤 처지에 놓여 있고, 그것이 드러나기를 바라지 않으신다는 거예요. 더 말할 것도 없고, 다만 이 말씀만 드리죠."], ["I need say no more, but to tell you, that if you think fit to communicate so much of your case to me, if it be so, as is necessary, for I do not desire to pry into those things, I perhaps may be in a position to help you and to make you perfectly easy, and remove all your dull thoughts upon that subject.”", "만약 댁이 댁의 사정을, 그게 그렇다면, 필요한 만큼 저에게 털어놓는 게 좋겠다 싶으시면—저는 그런 일들을 캐고 들 마음은 없으니까요—제가 어쩌면 댁을 도와드리고, 댁을 완전히 마음 편하게 해 드리며, 그 일에 대한 댁의 온갖 울적한 생각을 없애 드릴 수 있는 처지에 있을지도 몰라요.\""]], [["Every word this creature said was a cordial to me, and put new life and new spirit into my heart; my blood began to circulate immediately, and I was quite another body; I ate my victuals again, and grew better presently after it.", "이 사람이 한 마디 한 마디가 내게는 강심제와 같아서, 내 가슴에 새 생명과 새 기운을 불어넣었다. 내 피가 곧장 돌기 시작했고, 나는 아주 딴사람이 되었다."], ["She said a great deal more to the same purpose, and then, having pressed me to be free with her, and promised in the solemnest manner to be secret, she stopped a little, as if waiting to see what impression it made on me, and what I would say.", "나는 다시 음식을 먹었고, 그러고 나서 곧 좋아졌다. 그녀는 같은 취지로 한참을 더 말했고, 그러고는 내게 자기에게 터놓으라고 다그치며 더없이 엄숙한 태도로 비밀을 지키겠노라 약속한 다음, 마치 그것이 내게 어떤 인상을 주는지, 내가 무슨 말을 할지 보려고 기다리기라도 하듯 잠시 멈추었다."]], [["I was too sensible to the want I was in of such a woman, not to accept her offer; I told her my case was partly as she guessed, and partly not, for I was really married, and had a husband, though he was in such fine circumstances and so remote at that time, as that he could not appear publicly.", "나는 그런 여자가 내게 얼마나 필요한지를 너무도 절감하던 터라 그녀의 제안을 받아들이지 않을 수 없었다. 나는 그녀에게, 내 사정은 일부는 그녀가 짐작한 대로이고 일부는 그렇지 않으니, 나는 실은 결혼한 몸이고 남편이 있으나, 다만 그가 그때 너무도 좋은 처지에 있고 너무도 멀리 떨어져 있어서 공공연히 나설 수가 없는 형편이라고 했다."]], [["She took me short, and told me that was none of her business; all the ladies that came under her care were married women to her.", "그녀는 내 말을 끊고는, 그것은 자기가 알 바 아니라고 했다. 자기 보살핌 아래 오는 부인들은 모두 자기에게는 결혼한 여자라는 것이었다."], ["“Every woman,” she says, “that is with child has a father for it,”", "\"아이를 가진 여자라면 누구든,\""], ["and whether that father was a husband or no husband, was no business of hers; her business was to assist me in my present circumstances, whether I had a husband or no.", "그녀가 말했다, \"그 아이의 아비가 있는 법이지요.\" 그리고 그 아비가 남편이든 남편이 아니든 그것은 자기가 알 바 아니며, 자기가 할 일은 내가 남편이 있든 없든 지금의 처지에서 나를 돕는 것이라고 했다."], ["“For, madam,”", "\"왜냐하면, 부인,\""], ["says she, “to have a husband that cannot appear, is to have no husband in the sense of the case; and, therefore, whether you are a wife or a mistress is all one to me.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"나설 수 없는 남편을 두었다는 것은, 이 경우의 의미로 보자면 남편이 없는 것과 마찬가지거든요. 그러니 댁이 아내든 정부情婦든 저에게는 다 매한가지예요.\""]], [["I found presently, that whether I was a whore or a wife, I was to pass for a whore here, so I let that go.", "나는 곧, 내가 창녀든 아내든 여기서는 창녀로 통해야 한다는 것을 알아차렸으니, 그래서 그 점은 그냥 넘겼다."], ["I told her it was true, as she said, but that, however, if I must tell her my case, I must tell it her as it was; so I related it to her as short as I could, and I concluded it to her thus.", "나는 그녀에게, 그녀가 말한 대로 그것은 사실이지만, 그래도 내가 내 사정을 그녀에게 말해야 한다면 있는 그대로 말해야 한다고 했다. 그래서 나는 되도록 짧게 그것을 그녀에게 들려주었고, 이렇게 매듭지었다."], ["“I trouble you with all this, madam,”", "\"이 모든 걸로 댁을 번거롭게 하는 건, 부인,\" 내가 말했다, \"댁이 전에 말씀하신 대로 그것이 댁의 일과 크게 상관있어서가 아니라, 바로 이 점이 상관있기 때문이에요."], ["said I, “not that, as you said before, it is much to the purpose in your affair, but this is to the purpose, namely, that I am not in any pain about being seen, or being public or concealed, for ’tis perfectly indifferent to me; but my difficulty is, that I have no acquaintance in this part of the nation.”", "곧, 저는 남에게 보이든, 공공연히 드러나든 감추어지든 그 일로는 조금도 괴롭지 않으니, 그건 제게 완전히 아무래도 좋은 일이거든요. 다만 제 어려움은, 이 고장에 제가 아는 사람이 하나도 없다는 거예요.\""]], [["“I understand you, madam”", "\"무슨 말씀인지 알겠어요, 부인.\""], ["says she; “you have no security to bring to prevent the parish impertinences usual in such cases, and perhaps,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그런 경우에 흔히 있는 교구의 성가신 참견을 막아 줄 보증을 마련하지 못하시는 거죠."], ["says she, “do not know very well how to dispose of the child when it comes.”", "그리고 어쩌면,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"아이가 태어났을 때 그 아이를 어찌 처리할지도 잘 모르시는 거고요.\""], ["“The last,”", "\"뒤엣것은,\""], ["says I, “is not so much my concern as the first.”", "내가 말했다, \"앞엣것만큼 제 걱정거리는 아니에요.\""], ["“Well, madam,”", "\"그럼, 부인,\""], ["answered the midwife, “dare you put yourself into my hands?", "산파가 대답했다, \"감히 댁을 제 손에 맡기시겠어요?"], ["I live in such a place; though I do not inquire after you, you may inquire after me.", "저는 어디어디에 살아요. 비록 저는 댁에 대해 캐묻지 않겠지만, 댁은 저에 대해 알아보셔도 돼요."], ["My name is B——; I live in such a street”—naming the street—“at the sign of the Cradle.", "제 이름은 B예요. 저는 어느 거리에 살죠\"—거리 이름을 댔다—\"요람 간판이 걸린 데예요."], ["My profession is a midwife, and I have many ladies that come to my house to lie in.", "제 직업은 산파이고, 우리 집에 와서 해산하는 부인들이 많답니다."], ["I have given security to the parish in general terms to secure them from any charge from whatsoever shall come into the world under my roof.", "저는 교구에 일반적인 조건으로 보증을 서서, 제 지붕 아래에서 세상에 나오는 누구든 그들에게 비용 부담을 지우지 않도록 해 놓았어요."], ["I have but one question to ask in the whole affair, madam,”", "이 모든 일에서 제가 여쭐 게 딱 한 가지 있어요, 부인,\""], ["says she, “and if that be answered you shall be entirely easy for all the rest.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그 한 가지에만 답이 되면 나머지는 모두 완전히 마음 놓으셔도 돼요.\""]], [["I presently understood what she meant, and told her, “Madam, I believe I understand you. I thank God, though I want friends in this part of the world, I do not want money, so far as may be necessary, though I do not abound in that neither”: this I added because I would not make her expect great things. “Well, madam,” says she, “that is the thing indeed, without which nothing can be done in these cases; and yet,” says she, “you shall see that I will not impose upon you, or offer anything that is unkind to you, and if you desire it, you shall know everything beforehand, that you may suit yourself to the occasion, and be neither costly or sparing as you see fit.”", "나는 곧 그녀가 무슨 뜻인지 알아차리고 이렇게 말했다. \"부인, 무슨 뜻인지 알 것 같아요. 고맙게도, 비록 이 세상 이 고장에는 제가 벗이 없으나, 돈은, 필요할 만큼은, 부족하지 않아요. 그렇다고 그것이 넉넉하다는 것도 아니지만요.\" 이렇게 덧붙인 것은 그녀가 큰 것을 기대하지 않게 하기 위함이었다. \"그럼, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그게 정말 핵심이지요. 그게 없으면 이런 경우에 아무것도 할 수가 없거든요. 그렇지만,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"제가 댁에게 바가지를 씌우거나, 댁에게 야박한 걸 들이밀지 않는다는 걸 보시게 될 거예요. 그리고 댁이 원하신다면, 모든 걸 미리 다 아시게 해서, 댁이 형편에 맞추어, 보시기에 좋을 대로 많이 쓰지도 적게 쓰지도 않게 하실 수 있어요.\" 나는 그녀에게, 당신은 내 처지를 너무도 완전히 잘 알아주는 것 같으니, 내가 당신에게 청할 것은 다만 이것뿐이라고 했다. 곧, 내가 당신에게 말한 대로, 나에게 돈은 충분히 있으나 많은 양은 아니니, 내가 되도록 군더더기 비용을 적게 들이도록 잘 마련해 달라는 것이었다."]], [["I told her she seemed to be so perfectly sensible of my condition, that I had nothing to ask of her but this, that as I had told her that I had money sufficient, but not a great quantity, she would order it so that I might be at as little superfluous charge as possible.", "그녀는, 그 비용 내역을 두세 가지 모양으로, 차림표처럼 가져올 테니, 내가 좋을 대로 고르라고 대답했다. 나는 그렇게 해 달라고 했다. 이튿날 그녀가 그것을 가져왔는데, 그녀의 세 가지 계산서 사본은 다음과 같았다."]], [["She replied that she would bring in an account of the expenses of it in two or three shapes, and like a bill of fare, I should choose as I pleased; and I desired her to do so.", "1. 식사 포함, 그녀의 집에서 석 달 묵는 값, 주당 10실링……6파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2. 한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 산욕용 침구 사용료……1파운드 10실링 0펜스"]], [["The next day she brought it, and the copy of her three bills was as follows:—", "3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사에게, 그리고 대부들과 서기에게……1파운드 10실링 0펜스\n4. 세례식에 벗 다섯을 청할 경우의 만찬 비용……1파운드 0실링 0펜스"]], [["1. For three months’ lodging in her house, including my diet, at 10s. a week . . . . . . . . . . . 6£, 0s., 0d. 2. For a nurse for the month, and use of childbed linen . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 10s., 0d. 3. For a minister to christen the child, and to the godfathers and clerk . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 10s., 0d. 4. For a supper at the christening if I had five friends at it . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 0s., 0d. For her fees as a midwife, and the taking off the trouble of the parish . . . . . . . . . . . . 3£, 3s., 0d. To her maid servant attending . . . . . . . . 0£, 10s., 0d. -------------- 13£, 13s., 0d.", "산파로서의 그녀의 수고비, 그리고 교구의 성가신 일을 떠맡아 주는 값……3파운드 3실링 0펜스\n시중드는 그녀의 하녀에게……0파운드 10실링 0펜스"]], [["This was the first bill; the second was the same terms:—", "―――――― 13파운드 13실링 0펜스\n이것이 첫째 계산서였고, 둘째 것은 같은 항목이었다."]], [["1. For three months’ lodging and diet, etc., at 20s. per week . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 13£, 0s., 0d. 2. For a nurse for the month, and the use of linen and lace . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 2£, 10s., 0d. 3. For the minister to christen the child, etc., as above . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 2£, 0s., 0d. 4. For supper and for sweetmeats . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 3£, 3s., 0d. For her fees as above . . . . . . . . . . . . 5£, 5s., 0d. For a servant-maid . . . . . . . . . . . . . 1£, 0s., 0d. -------------- 26£, 18s., 0d.", "1. 석 달간의 숙식 등, 주당 20실링……13파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2. 한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 침구와 레이스 사용료……2파운드 10실링 0펜스"]], [["This was the second-rate bill; the third, she said, was for a degree higher, and when the father or friends appeared:—", "3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사 등, 위와 같음……2파운드 0실링 0펜스\n4. 만찬과 과자류……3파운드 3실링 0펜스"]], [["1. For three months’ lodging and diet, having two rooms and a garret for a servant . . . . . . 30£, 0s., 0d., 2. For a nurse for the month, and the finest suit of childbed linen . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 4£, 4s., 0d. 3. For the minister to christen the child, etc. 2£, 10s., 0d. 4. For a supper, the gentlemen to send in the wine . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 6£, 0s., 0d. For my fees, etc. . . . . . . . . . . . . . 10£, 10s., 0d. The maid, besides their own maid, only . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 0£, 10s., 0d. -------------- 53£, 14s., 0d.", "위와 같은 그녀의 수고비……5파운드 5실링 0펜스\n하녀에게……1파운드 0실링 0펜스"]], [["I looked upon all three bills, and smiled, and told her I did not see but that she was very reasonable in her demands, all things considered, and for that I did not doubt but her accommodations were good.", "―――――― 26파운드 18실링 0펜스\n이것이 둘째 등급의 계산서였고, 셋째 것은, 그녀의 말로는, 한 단계 더 높은 것으로, 아비나 벗들이 나설 경우의 것이었다."]], [["She told me I should be judge of that when I saw them. I told her I was sorry to tell her that I feared I must be her lowest-rated customer.", "1."], ["“And perhaps, madam,” said I, “you will make me the less welcome upon that account.”", "석 달간의 숙식, 방 두 칸과 하인용 다락 한 칸……30파운드 0실링 0펜스\n2."], ["“No, not at all,” said she; “for where I have one of the third sort I have two of the second, and four to one of the first, and I get as much by them in proportion as by any; but if you doubt my care of you, I will allow any friend you have to overlook and see if you are well waited on or no.”", "한 달 동안의 유모, 그리고 가장 고운 산욕용 침구……4파운드 4실링 0펜스"]], [["Then she explained the particulars of her bill. “In the first place, madam,” said she, “I would have you observe that here is three months’ keeping; you are but ten shillings a week; I undertake to say you will not complain of my table. I suppose,” says she, “you do not live cheaper where you are now?” “No, indeed,” said I, “not so cheap, for I give six shillings per week for my chamber, and find my own diet as well as I can, which costs me a great deal more.”", "3. 아이에게 세례를 줄 목사 등……2파운드 10실링 0펜스\n4. 만찬, 포도주는 신사 측에서 보내올 것……6파운드 0실링 0펜스"]], [["“Then, madam,” says she, “if the child should not live, or should be dead-born, as you know sometimes happens, then there is the minister’s article saved; and if you have no friends to come to you, you may save the expense of a supper; so that take those articles out, madam,” says she, “your lying in will not cost you above £5, 3s. in all more than your ordinary charge of living.”", "나의 수고비 등……10파운드 10실링 0펜스\n저쪽 하녀 말고 따로 둘 하녀……0파운드 10실링 0펜스 ―――――― 53파운드 14실링 0펜스"]], [["This was the most reasonable thing that I ever heard of; so I smiled, and told her I would come and be her customer; but I told her also, that as I had two months and more to do, I might perhaps be obliged to stay longer with her than three months, and desired to know if she would not be obliged to remove me before it was proper. No, she said; her house was large, and besides, she never put anybody to remove, that had lain in, till they were willing to go; and if she had more ladies offered, she was not so ill-beloved among her neighbours but she could provide accommodations for twenty, if there was occasion.", "나는 세 계산서를 모두 들여다보고는 미소 지으며, 모든 것을 따져 볼 때 그녀의 요구가 매우 합당해 보이고, 그러니 그녀의 시설이 좋으리라는 것도 의심치 않는다고 말했다. 그녀는 그건 내가 직접 보면 판단할 수 있을 거라고 했다. 나는 그녀에게, 안타깝지만 아무래도 내가 그녀의 가장 낮은 등급의 손님이 되어야 할 것 같다고 했다. \"그리고 어쩌면, 부인,\" 내가 말했다, \"그 때문에 저를 덜 반기시게 될지도 모르겠네요.\" \"아니에요, 전혀 그렇지 않아요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"셋째 부류의 손님이 한 분 계실 때 둘째 부류는 두 분, 첫째 부류는 그 네 배가 계시거든요. 그리고 그분들에게서 비례해서 따지면 다른 어느 분에게서만큼이나 벌어요. 그렇지만 제가 댁을 잘 보살피는지 미덥지 않으시거든, 댁의 벗 누구든 와서 댁이 잘 시중받는지 아닌지 지켜보도록 허락해 드릴게요.\""]], [["I found she was an eminent lady in her way; and, in short, I agreed to put myself into her hands, and promised her. She then talked of other things, looked about into my accommodations where I was, found fault with my wanting attendance and conveniences, and that I should not be used so at her house. I told her I was shy of speaking, for the woman of the house looked stranger, or at least I thought so, since I had been ill, because I was with child; and I was afraid she would put some affront or other upon me, supposing that I had been able to give but a slight account of myself.", "그러고는 그녀가 계산서의 세부 항목을 설명했다. \"우선, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"여기 석 달치 비용이 있다는 걸 보셨으면 해요. 댁은 주당 10실링밖에 안 들어요. 단언컨대 댁은 제 밥상에 불평하지 않으실 거예요. 짐작건대,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"지금 계신 데서도 이보다 싸게 살지는 못하시죠?\" \"정말 그래요.\" 내가 말했다. \"이렇게 싸지 않아요. 방 하나에 주당 6실링을 내고, 게다가 제 끼니는 제가 할 수 있는 대로 알아서 마련해야 하는데, 그게 훨씬 더 많이 들거든요.\" \"그렇다면, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"만약 아이가 살지 못하거나, 댁도 아시다시피 가끔 그런 일이 있듯이 사산되거든, 그러면 목사 항목이 절약되지요. 그리고 댁을 찾아올 벗이 없으면 만찬 비용을 아끼실 수 있고요. 그러니 그 항목들을 빼면, 부인,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"댁의 해산 비용은 댁의 평소 생활비보다 다 합쳐 5파운드 3실링 넘게는 들지 않을 거예요.\""]], [["“Oh dear,” said she, “her ladyship is no stranger to these things; she has tried to entertain ladies in your condition several times, but she could not secure the parish; and besides, she is not such a nice lady as you take her to be; however, since you are a-going, you shall not meddle with her, but I’ll see you are a little better looked after while you are here than I think you are, and it shall not cost you the more neither.”", "이것은 내가 일찍이 들어 본 가운데 가장 합당한 것이었다. 그래서 나는 미소 지으며 그녀에게, 가서 그녀의 손님이 되겠다고 했다. 다만 나는 또한, 내게는 아직 두 달 남짓이 남았으니 아마도 석 달보다 더 오래 그녀에게 묵게 될지도 모른다고 하면서, 알맞은 때가 되기 전에 그녀가 나를 옮겨야 할 일은 없겠는지 알고 싶다고 했다. 아니라고, 그녀가 말했다. 자기 집은 넓고, 게다가 해산한 사람은 본인이 가고 싶어 할 때까지 누구도 옮기게 하는 법이 없으며, 더 많은 부인이 찾아온다 해도, 자기는 이웃들 사이에서 그리 밉보이지 않아서 필요하다면 스무 명도 거처를 마련해 줄 수 있다고 했다."]], [["I did not understand her at all; however, I thanked her, and so we parted.", "나는 그녀가 자기 분야에서 이름난 부인이라는 것을 알게 되었다. 그래서 한마디로, 나는 나 자신을 그녀의 손에 맡기기로 하고 그녀에게 약속했다."], ["The next morning she sent me a chicken roasted and hot, and a pint bottle of sherry, and ordered the maid to tell me that she was to wait on me every day as long as I stayed there.", "그러자 그녀는 다른 것들에 관해 이야기하다가, 내가 지금 묵는 곳의 시설을 둘러보고는, 내게 시중과 편의가 부족하다는 것을 흠잡으며, 자기 집에서는 이런 대접을 받지 않을 거라고 했다. 나는 말을 꺼내기가 조심스러웠다고 했으니, 그 집 여주인이 내가 아이를 가진 까닭에 내가 앓아누운 뒤로 더 서먹하게 굴거나, 적어도 내게는 그렇게 보였기 때문이며, 또 내가 나 자신에 관해 변변히 설명도 못 하는 처지라 여겨서, 그녀가 이런저런 모욕이라도 줄까 봐 두려웠던 것이다."]], [["This was surprisingly good and kind, and I accepted it very willingly. At night she sent to me again, to know if I wanted anything, and how I did, and to order the maid to come to her in the morning with my dinner. The maid had orders to make me some chocolate in the morning before she came away, and did so, and at noon she brought me the sweetbread of a breast of veal, whole, and a dish of soup for my dinner; and after this manner she nursed me up at a distance, so that I was mightily well pleased, and quickly well, for indeed my dejections before were the principal part of my illness.", "\"아이고,\" 그녀가 말했다, \"그 마님은 이런 일에 낯선 분이 아니에요. 댁 같은 처지의 부인들을 여러 번 받아 보려 했지만, 교구를 무마하질 못했던 거죠. 게다가 그분은 댁이 생각하시는 것만큼 까다로운 부인도 아니에요. 그래도, 댁이 떠나실 참이니, 그분 일에 끼어들지는 마시고, 댁이 여기 계시는 동안 제 생각보다 댁이 좀 더 잘 보살핌을 받도록 제가 살펴 드릴게요. 그렇다고 그 때문에 댁에게 비용이 더 드는 것도 아니고요.\" 나는 그녀의 말을 전혀 알아듣지 못했다. 그래도 나는 그녀에게 고맙다고 했고, 그렇게 우리는 헤어졌다. 이튿날 아침 그녀는 김이 오르는 구운 닭 한 마리와 셰리주 한 병을 보내고는, 하녀를 시켜 내게 전하기를, 내가 거기 머무는 동안 자기가 날마다 시중을 들겠다고 했다."]], [["I expected, as is usually the case among such people, that the servant she sent me would have been some imprudent brazen wench of Drury Lane breeding, and I was very uneasy at having her with me upon that account; so I would not let her lie in that house the first night by any means, but had my eyes about me as narrowly as if she had been a public thief.", "이것은 놀랄 만큼 좋고 친절한 일이라, 나는 매우 기꺼이 그것을 받아들였다. 밤에 그녀는 다시 사람을 보내어, 내게 필요한 것은 없는지, 몸은 어떤지 묻고는, 아침에 내 끼니와 함께 하녀를 자기에게 보내라고 일렀다. 하녀는 떠나오기 전 아침에 내게 초콜릿을 좀 타 주라는 지시를 받았고 그대로 했으며, 정오에는 송아지 가슴살 췌장 통째와 수프 한 접시를 내 끼니로 가져왔다. 이런 식으로 그녀는 멀찍이서 나를 보살펴 길러 주었으니, 나는 무척 흡족했고 곧 좋아졌다. 사실 그전의 내 의기소침함이 내 병의 주된 부분이었던 것이다."]], [["My gentlewoman guessed presently what was the matter, and sent her back with a short note, that I might depend upon the honesty of her maid; that she would be answerable for her upon all accounts; and that she took no servants into her house without very good security for their fidelity.", "나는 그런 사람들 사이에서 흔히 그렇듯이, 그녀가 내게 보낸 하인이 드루어리 레인 출신의 어떤 경솔하고 뻔뻔한 계집아이일 거라고 짐작했고, 그 때문에 그녀를 곁에 두는 것이 몹시 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["I was then perfectly easy; and indeed the maid’s behaviour spoke for itself, for a modester, quieter, soberer girl never came into anybody’s family, and I found her so afterwards.", "그래서 나는 첫날 밤에는 도무지 그녀를 그 집에서 재우려 하지 않았고, 마치 그녀가 공공연한 도둑이라도 되는 양 빈틈없이 눈을 부릅뜨고 있었다."]], [["As soon as I was well enough to go abroad, I went with the maid to see the house, and to see the apartment I was to have; and everything was so handsome and so clean and well, that, in short, I had nothing to say, but was wonderfully pleased and satisfied with what I had met with, which, considering the melancholy circumstances I was in, was far beyond what I looked for.", "나의 그 점잖은 부인은 곧 무슨 일인지 알아채고는, 짤막한 쪽지와 함께 그녀를 돌려보냈으니, 곧 자기 하녀의 정직함은 믿어도 된다는 것, 무슨 일이든 자기가 그녀를 책임지겠다는 것, 그리고 자기는 그 충실함에 대한 매우 좋은 보증 없이는 어떤 하인도 집에 들이지 않는다는 것이었다. 그제야 나는 완전히 마음이 놓였다. 그리고 사실 그 하녀의 행실이 스스로를 증명했으니, 그보다 더 얌전하고 조용하고 차분한 계집아이가 누구의 집에 들어온 적이 없었고, 나는 나중에도 그녀가 과연 그러함을 알게 되었다."]], [["It might be expected that I should give some account of the nature of the wicked practices of this woman, in whose hands I was now fallen; but it would be too much encouragement to the vice, to let the world see what easy measures were here taken to rid the women’s unwelcome burthen of a child clandestinely gotten. This grave matron had several sorts of practice, and this was one particular, that if a child was born, though not in her house (for she had occasion to be called to many private labours), she had people at hand, who for a piece of money would take the child off their hands, and off from the hands of the parish too; and those children, as she said, were honestly provided for and taken care of. What should become of them all, considering so many, as by her account she was concerned with, I cannot conceive.", "내가 바깥출입을 할 만큼 좋아지자마자, 나는 그 하녀와 함께 그 집을 보러 가서, 내가 쓰게 될 처소를 살펴보았다. 모든 것이 어찌나 보기 좋고 깨끗하고 훌륭한지, 한마디로 나는 흠잡을 데가 없었고, 내가 마주한 것에 놀랄 만큼 기쁘고 만족스러웠으니, 내가 처한 우울한 처지를 생각하면 그것은 내가 기대했던 것을 훨씬 넘어서는 것이었다. 이제 내가 그 손에 떨어진 이 여자의 사악한 행위의 본질에 관해 어느 정도 설명하리라 기대할 법도 하다. 그러나 은밀히 밴 아이라는 여자들의 달갑잖은 짐을 여기서 어떤 손쉬운 방도로 떨쳐 버리는지를 세상에 보여 주는 것은, 그 악덕을 너무 부추기는 일이 될 것이다. 이 점잖은 부인은 여러 가지 일을 했는데, 그중 하나가 이런 것이었다. 곧, 아이가 태어나면, 비록 자기 집에서가 아니더라도(그녀는 여러 사사로운 해산에 불려 갈 일이 있었으므로), 약간의 돈을 받고 그 아이를 그들의 손에서, 그리고 교구의 손에서도 떠맡아 줄 사람들을 대기시켜 두었던 것이다. 그리고 그런 아이들은, 그녀의 말로는, 정직하게 거두어져 보살핌을 받는다고 했다. 그녀의 말대로라면 그녀가 관여한 그 많은 아이가 다 어찌 되었을지, 나로서는 도무지 짐작이 가지 않는다."]], [["I had many times discourses upon that subject with her; but she was full of this argument, that she save the life of many an innocent lamb, as she called them, which would otherwise perhaps have been murdered; and of many women who, made desperate by the misfortune, would otherwise be tempted to destroy their children, and bring themselves to the gallows.", "나는 여러 번 그 일을 두고 그녀와 이야기를 나누었으나, 그녀는 늘 이 논리로 가득 차 있었다. 곧, 자기가 그녀의 말마따나 죄 없는 어린 양들의 목숨을 숱하게 구해 주었으니, 그러지 않았다면 그 양들은 어쩌면 죽임을 당했을 거라는 것, 또 불운에 절망한 나머지 그러지 않았다면 자기 아이를 없애고 스스로를 교수대로 끌고 갈 유혹에 빠졌을 숱한 여자들을 구해 주었다는 것이었다."], ["I granted her that this was true, and a very commendable thing, provided the poor children fell into good hands afterwards, and were not abused, starved, and neglected by the nurses that bred them up.", "나는 그녀에게, 그것이 사실이며 매우 칭찬할 만한 일이라는 것을 인정했으니, 다만 그 가엾은 아이들이 그 뒤에 좋은 손에 떨어져, 자기들을 길러 주는 유모들에게 학대받거나 굶주리거나 방치되지 않는다는 조건에서였다."], ["She answered, that she always took care of that, and had no nurses in her business but what were very good, honest people, and such as might be depended upon.", "그녀는, 자기는 늘 그 점에 마음을 쓰고, 자기 일에는 아주 선량하고 정직한, 믿을 만한 사람들 외에는 유모로 두지 않는다고 대답했다."]], [["I could say nothing to the contrary, and so was obliged to say, “Madam, I do not question you do your part honestly, but what those people do afterwards is the main question”; and she stopped my mouth again with saying that she took the utmost care about it.", "나는 그에 반대되는 말을 할 수 없었으니, 그래서 이렇게 말할 수밖에 없었다. \"부인, 댁이 댁의 몫을 정직하게 하시는 건 의심치 않아요. 하지만 그 사람들이 그 뒤에 무얼 하는지가 가장 큰 문제지요.\" 그러자 그녀는 자기가 그 점에 더없이 마음을 쓴다는 말로 다시 내 입을 막아 버렸다."]], [["The only thing I found in all her conversation on these subjects that gave me any distaste, was, that one time in discouraging about my being far gone with child, and the time I expected to come, she said something that looked as if she could help me off with my burthen sooner, if I was willing; or, in English, that she could give me something to make me miscarry, if I had a desire to put an end to my troubles that way; but I soon let her see that I abhorred the thoughts of it; and, to do her justice, she put it off so cleverly, that I could not say she really intended it, or whether she only mentioned the practice as a horrible thing; for she couched her words so well, and took my meaning so quickly, that she gave her negative before I could explain myself.", "이런 일들을 두고 나눈 그녀의 온갖 이야기 가운데 나에게 거슬리는 것이라곤 단 하나였으니, 그것은 한번 내가 만삭이 다 되어 가고 예정일이 다가온다는 이야기를 하던 중에, 그녀가 무언가 이런 식으로 들리는 말을 한 것이었다. 곧, 내가 원한다면 자기가 더 빨리 내 짐을 덜어 줄 수도 있다는 것, 쉬운 말로 하자면, 내가 그런 식으로 내 골칫거리를 끝장내고 싶다면 유산하게 하는 무언가를 줄 수 있다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 곧 그녀에게, 내가 그런 생각을 끔찍이 싫어한다는 것을 똑똑히 보여 주었다. 그리고 그녀를 위해 공정하게 말하자면, 그녀는 그것을 어찌나 능란하게 둘러댔던지, 나로서는 그녀가 정말로 그것을 의도한 것인지, 아니면 그저 그런 행위를 끔찍한 일로서 언급한 것뿐인지 단언할 수가 없었다. 그녀가 어찌나 말을 잘 감추고 내 뜻을 어찌나 빨리 알아챘던지, 내가 미처 내 뜻을 밝히기도 전에 그녀가 부정의 뜻을 내비쳤기 때문이다."]], [["To bring this part into as narrow a compass as possible, I quitted my lodging at St.", "이 대목을 되도록 짧게 줄이자면, 나는 세인트존스의 거처를 떠나 나의 새 후견인—집 안에서 그들이 그녀를 그렇게 불렀던 것이다—에게 갔다. 거기서 나는 참으로 어찌나 정중한 대접을 받고, 어찌나 세심한 보살핌을 받고, 어찌나 훌륭한 시중을 받고, 모든 것이 어찌나 좋았던지, 나는 그것에 놀랐고, 내 후견인이 거기서 무슨 이득을 얻는지 처음에는 알 수가 없었다."], ["Jones’s and went to my new governess, for so they called her in the house, and there I was indeed treated with so much courtesy, so carefully looked to, so handsomely provided, and everything so well, that I was surprised at it, and could not at first see what advantage my governess made of it; but I found afterwards that she professed to make no profit of lodgers’ diet, nor indeed could she get much by it, but that her profit lay in the other articles of her management, and she made enough that way, I assure you; for ’tis scarce credible what practice she had, as well abroad as at home, and yet all upon the private account, or, in plain English, the whoring account.", "그러나 나중에 알게 된바, 그녀는 묵는 이들의 식사로는 이윤을 남기지 않겠노라 공언했고, 사실 그것으로는 많이 벌 수도 없었으며, 그녀의 이윤은 그녀가 꾸리는 다른 항목들에 있었으니, 단언컨대 그녀는 그쪽으로 충분히 벌었다. 그녀가 집 안에서뿐 아니라 집 밖에서도 한 일이 어찌나 많은지 거의 믿기지 않을 정도였는데, 그 모든 것이 그 사사로운 쪽 일, 쉬운 말로 하자면 매춘 쪽 일이었던 것이다."]], [["While I was in her house, which was near four months, she had no less than twelve ladies of pleasure brought to bed within the doors, and I think she had two-and-thirty, or thereabouts, under her conduct without doors, whereof one, as nice as she was with me, was lodged with my old landlady at St. Jones’s.", "내가 그녀의 집에 있던 동안—거의 넉 달이었다—그녀는 적어도 열두 명의 환락의 여인들을 그 문 안에서 해산하게 했고, 문밖에서 그녀가 거느린 이가 서른두 명 안팎쯤 되었던 것 같은데, 그중 한 명은, 나에게 그러했듯이 까다로운 부인이었으나, 세인트존스의 내 옛 안주인 집에 묵고 있었다."]], [["This was a strange testimony of the growing vice of the age, and such a one, that as bad as I had been myself, it shocked my very senses.", "이것은 이 시대에 점점 자라나는 악덕에 대한 기이한 증거였으니, 나 자신이 그토록 못된 사람이었음에도 내 분별을 충격에 빠뜨릴 만한 것이었다."], ["I began to nauseate the place I was in and, about all, the wicked practice; and yet I must say that I never saw, or do I believe there was to be seen, the least indecency in the house the whole time I was there.", "나는 내가 있는 그곳이, 그리고 무엇보다도 그 사악한 행위가 역겨워지기 시작했다. 그러면서도 한마디 해 두어야겠으니, 내가 거기 있던 내내 그 집에서 조금이라도 점잖지 못한 일을 본 적이 없으며, 그런 일이 보일 만한 것이 있었으리라고도 생각지 않는다."]], [["Not a man was ever seen to come upstairs, except to visit the lying-in ladies within their month, nor then without the old lady with them, who made it a piece of honour of her management that no man should touch a woman, no, not his own wife, within the month; nor would she permit any man to lie in the house upon any pretence whatever, no, not though she was sure it was with his own wife; and her general saying for it was, that she cared not how many children were born in her house, but she would have none got there if she could help it.", "해산한 부인들이 산욕 한 달 안에 있을 때 문병하는 경우를 제외하고는, 어떤 남자도 이층으로 올라오는 것이 보인 적이 없었고, 그때에도 그 노부인이 함께 따라붙지 않은 적이 없었으니, 그녀는 어떤 남자도, 아니 자기 아내라 할지라도, 산욕 한 달 안에는 여자에게 손대게 하지 않는다는 것을 자기 운영의 명예로 삼았다. 또한 그녀는 어떤 핑계로도 남자가 그 집에서 자는 것을 허락하지 않았으니, 아니, 설령 그것이 그의 친아내인 것이 확실하다 해도 그러했다. 그리고 그에 대한 그녀의 입버릇은, 자기 집에서 얼마나 많은 아이가 태어나든 상관하지 않으나, 자기가 막을 수만 있다면 거기서 어떤 아이도 배지 않게 하겠다는 것이었다."]], [["It might perhaps be carried further than was needful, but it was an error of the right hand if it was an error, for by this she kept up the reputation, such as it was, of her business, and obtained this character, that though she did take care of the women when they were debauched, yet she was not instrumental to their being debauched at all; and yet it was a wicked trade she drove too.", "어쩌면 이것은 필요 이상으로 지나쳤을지도 모르나, 설령 그것이 오류라 하더라도 좋은 쪽으로 기운 오류였으니, 이로써 그녀는 자기 일의, 그것이 어떠하든, 평판을 지켜 냈고, 다음과 같은 평판을 얻었기 때문이다. 곧, 그녀가 여자들이 타락한 뒤에는 그들을 보살펴 주었으되, 그들이 타락하는 데에는 조금도 손을 보태지 않았다는 것이다. 그러면서도 그녀가 꾸려 가는 것 역시 사악한 장사였다."]], [["While I was there, and before I was brought to bed, I received a letter from my trustee at the bank, full of kind, obliging things, and earnestly pressing me to return to London.", "내가 거기 있는 동안, 그리고 내가 해산하기 전에, 나는 은행에 있는 나의 수탁인에게서 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 다정하고 정중한 말들로 가득했고, 런던으로 돌아오라고 간곡히 다그치는 것이었다."], ["It was near a fortnight old when it came to me, because it had been first sent into Lancashire, and then returned to me.", "그것이 내게 왔을 때는 부친 지 거의 보름이 다 된 것이었으니, 먼저 랭커셔로 보내졌다가 다시 내게로 돌려보내졌기 때문이다."], ["He concludes with telling me that he had obtained a decree, I think he called it, against his wife, and that he would be ready to make good his engagement to me, if I would accept of him, adding a great many protestations of kindness and affection, such as he would have been far from offering if he had known the circumstances I had been in, and which as it was I had been very far from deserving.", "그는 끝맺으며 내게 이르기를, 자기가, 그가 그렇게 불렀던 것 같은데, 아내에 대한 판결을 얻어 냈으며, 내가 자기를 받아 준다면 나에 대한 자기 약속을 지킬 준비가 되어 있다고 하면서, 다정함과 애정을 거듭 맹세하는 숱한 말들을 덧붙였는데, 만약 그가 내가 처해 온 사정을 알았더라면 결코 입에 올리지 않았을 그런 것들이었고, 또 현재의 나로서는 도무지 받을 자격이 없는 것들이었다."]], [["I returned an answer to his letter, and dated it at Liverpool, but sent it by messenger, alleging that it came in cover to a friend in town.", "나는 그의 편지에 답장을 보내며 그것을 리버풀에서 쓴 것으로 날짜를 적었으나, 인편으로 부치면서, 그것이 시내의 어떤 벗에게 봉투에 넣어 보내져 왔다고 둘러댔다."], ["I gave him joy of his deliverance, but raised some scruples at the lawfulness of his marrying again, and told him I supposed he would consider very seriously upon that point before he resolved on it, the consequence being too great for a man of his judgment to venture rashly upon a thing of that nature; so concluded, wishing him very well in whatever he resolved, without letting him into anything of my own mind, or giving any answer to his proposal of my coming to London to him, but mentioned at a distance my intention to return the latter end of the year, this being dated in April.", "나는 그가 풀려난 것을 축하해 주면서도, 그가 다시 결혼하는 것이 적법한지에 대해 몇 가지 거리낌을 내비쳤고, 그에게 이르기를, 그가 그것을 작정하기 전에 그 점을 아주 진지하게 생각해 보리라 짐작한다고, 그 결과가 너무도 중대하여 그만한 분별을 지닌 사람이 그런 성질의 일을 경솔하게 모험할 수는 없는 법이라고 했다. 그러고는, 그가 무엇을 작정하든 잘되기를 빈다는 말로 끝맺으면서도, 내 마음속 생각은 조금도 그에게 들이지 않았고, 그에게 가서 함께하라는 그의 제안에도 아무 답을 주지 않았으며, 다만 멀찍이서 그해 말에 돌아갈 생각이라는 것만 언급했으니, 이 편지의 날짜는 4월이었다."]], [["I was brought to bed about the middle of May and had another brave boy, and myself in as good condition as usual on such occasions.", "나는 5월 중순쯤 해산하여 또 하나의 늠름한 사내아이를 낳았고, 나 자신도 그런 경우에 늘 그렇듯이 좋은 상태였다."], ["My governess did her part as a midwife with the greatest art and dexterity imaginable, and far beyond all that ever I had had any experience of before.", "내 후견인은 산파로서 자기 몫을, 상상할 수 있는 가장 빼어난 솜씨와 능란함으로, 내가 일찍이 겪어 본 그 어떤 것도 훨씬 넘어서게 해냈다."]], [["Her care of me in my travail, and after in my lying in, was such, that if she had been my own mother it could not have been better.", "내 산고 중에, 그리고 산후 산욕 중에 그녀가 나를 보살핀 것은, 그녀가 내 친어머니였다 해도 그보다 더 나을 수 없었을 정도였다."], ["Let none be encouraged in their loose practices from this dexterous lady’s management, for she is gone to her place, and I dare say has left nothing behind her that can or will come up on it.", "이 능란한 부인의 솜씨를 보고 누구도 자기네 방종한 행위에 용기를 얻지 말지니, 그녀는 자기가 갈 데로 갔고, 단언컨대 그 뒤를 이을 만한 것이라곤 아무것도 남기지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["I think I had been brought to bed about twenty-two days when I received another letter from my friend at the bank, with the surprising news that he had obtained a final sentence of divorce against his wife, and had served her with it on such a day, and that he had such an answer to give to all my scruples about his marrying again, as I could not expect, and as he had no desire of; for that his wife, who had been under some remorse before for her usage of him, as soon as she had the account that he had gained his point, had very unhappily destroyed herself that same evening.", "내가 해산한 지 스무이틀쯤 되었을 때, 나는 은행에 있는 내 벗에게서 또 한 통의 편지를 받았는데, 거기에는 놀라운 소식이 담겨 있었다. 곧, 자기가 아내에 대한 이혼 최종 판결을 얻어 냈고, 그것을 어느 날 그녀에게 송달했으며, 또 그가 다시 결혼하는 것에 대한 내 온갖 거리낌에 대해, 내가 기대할 수도 없었고 그 자신도 바라지 않았던 그런 답을 줄 수 있게 되었다는 것이었다. 곧, 전부터 그를 대한 자기 처사에 어느 정도 회한을 품고 있던 그의 아내가, 그가 뜻을 이루었다는 소식을 듣자마자 바로 그날 저녁, 매우 불행하게도 스스로 목숨을 끊었다는 것이었다."]], [["He expressed himself very handsomely as to his being concerned at her disaster, but cleared himself of having any hand in it, and that he had only done himself justice in a case in which he was notoriously injured and abused.", "그는 그녀의 참변에 대해 자기가 마음 아파하고 있다는 것을 매우 점잖게 표현하면서도, 자기는 거기에 아무런 손도 대지 않았으며, 자기는 다만 누가 봐도 부당하게 해를 입고 학대받은 일에서 자기 자신에 대한 정당함을 도모했을 뿐이라고 변명했다."], ["However, he said that he was extremely afflicted at it, and had no view of any satisfaction left in his world, but only in the hope that I would come and relieve him by my company; and then he pressed me violently indeed to give him some hopes that I would at least come up to town and let him see me, when he would further enter into discourse about it.", "그러나 그는 그 일로 극도로 괴로워하고 있으며, 이 세상에서 자기에게 남은 어떤 위안의 전망도 없되, 다만 내가 와서 내 벗 됨으로 자기를 위로해 주리라는 희망만 있을 뿐이라고 했다. 그러고는 정말이지 격렬하게 나를 다그쳐, 적어도 내가 시내로 올라와 자기를 보게 해 주리라는 어떤 희망이라도 달라고 했으니, 그러면 그때 그 일에 관해 더 이야기를 나누겠다고 했다."]], [["I was exceedingly surprised at the news, and began now seriously to reflect on my present circumstances, and the inexpressible misfortune it was to me to have a child upon my hands, and what to do in it I knew not.", "나는 그 소식에 몹시 놀랐고, 이제 진지하게 내 현재의 처지를, 그리고 거두어 키워야 할 아이가 손에 있다는 것이 내게 얼마나 이루 말할 수 없는 불운인지를 곰곰이 되돌아보기 시작했으니, 그 일을 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["At last I opened my case at a distance to my governess.", "마침내 나는 내 후견인에게 멀찍이서 내 사정을 털어놓았다."], ["I appeared melancholy and uneasy for several days, and she lay at me continually to know what trouble me.", "나는 며칠 동안 우울하고 불안한 기색을 보였고, 그녀는 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는지 알아내려고 끊임없이 졸라 댔다."], ["I could not for my life tell her that I had an offer of marriage, after I had so often told her that I had a husband, so that I really knew not what to say to her.", "내가 그토록 여러 번 남편이 있다고 그녀에게 말해 놓은 마당에, 청혼을 받았다고는 죽어도 그녀에게 말할 수가 없었으니, 정말이지 그녀에게 무어라 해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["I owned I had something which very much troubled me, but at the same time told her I could not speak of it to any one alive.", "나는 나를 몹시 괴롭히는 무언가가 있다는 것은 인정했으나, 동시에 그녀에게, 그것을 살아 있는 누구에게도 말할 수 없다고 했다."]], [["She continued importuning me several days, but it was impossible, I told her, for me to commit the secret to anybody.", "그녀는 며칠을 두고 계속 졸라 댔으나, 나는 그녀에게, 그 비밀을 누구에게도 털어놓는 것은 불가능하다고 했다."], ["This, instead of being an answer to her, increased her importunities; she urged her having been trusted with the greatest secrets of this nature, that it was her business to conceal everything, and that to discover things of that nature would be her ruin.", "이것은 그녀에 대한 답이 되기는커녕 그녀의 졸라 댐을 더 키웠다. 그녀는, 자기가 이런 성질의 가장 큰 비밀들을 맡아 온 사람이라는 것, 모든 것을 감추는 것이 자기 일이라는 것, 그리고 그런 성질의 일들을 발설하는 것은 자기 파멸이 되리라는 것을 내세웠다."], ["She asked me if ever I had found her tattling to me of other people’s affairs, and how could I suspect her?", "그녀는 내게, 자기가 다른 사람들의 일을 두고 내게 수다 떠는 것을 본 적이 있느냐고, 그런데 어찌 자기를 의심할 수 있느냐고 물었다."], ["She told me, to unfold myself to her was telling it to nobody; that she was silent as death; that it must be a very strange case indeed that she could not help me out of; but to conceal it was to deprive myself of all possible help, or means of help, and to deprive her of the opportunity of serving me.", "그녀는 내게, 자기에게 털어놓는 것은 아무에게도 말하지 않는 것과 같다고, 자기는 죽음처럼 입이 무겁다고, 자기가 도와줄 수 없는 사정이라면 그것은 정말이지 아주 기이한 경우일 수밖에 없다고, 그러나 그것을 감추는 것은 나 자신에게서 온갖 가능한 도움이나 도움의 방도를 빼앗는 것이요, 그녀에게서는 나를 도울 기회를 빼앗는 것이라고 했다."], ["In short, she had such a bewitching eloquence, and so great a power of persuasion that there was no concealing anything from her.", "한마디로, 그녀에게는 사람을 호리는 그런 능변과 설득의 힘이 어찌나 대단했던지, 그녀에게 무엇 하나 감출 수가 없었다."]], [["So I resolved to unbosom myself to her.", "그래서 나는 그녀에게 속을 털어놓기로 작정했다."], ["I told her the history of my Lancashire marriage, and how both of us had been disappointed; how we came together, and how we parted; how he absolutely discharged me, as far as lay in him, free liberty to marry again, protesting that if he knew it he would never claim me, or disturb or expose me; that I thought I was free, but was dreadfully afraid to venture, for fear of the consequences that might follow in case of a discovery.", "나는 그녀에게 내 랭커셔 결혼의 내력을, 그리고 우리 둘 다 어떻게 낭패를 보았는지, 우리가 어떻게 함께 살게 되었고 어떻게 헤어졌는지, 그가 어떻게 자기 깜냥껏 나를 완전히 풀어 주어 다시 결혼할 자유를 주었는지, 자기가 그것을 알게 되더라도 결코 나를 자기 사람이라 주장하지도, 나를 성가시게 하거나 들추어내지도 않겠다고 다짐했는지를 들려주었다. 또 나는 내가 자유로운 몸이라고 생각했으나, 발각될 경우에 뒤따를지도 모를 결과가 두려워서 감히 모험을 걸기가 끔찍이도 겁이 났다고 했다."]], [["Then I told her what a good offer I had; showed her my friend’s two last letters, inviting me to come to London, and let her see with what affection and earnestness they were written, but blotted out the name, and also the story about the disaster of his wife, only that she was dead.", "그러고는 나는 그녀에게, 내가 얼마나 좋은 청혼을 받았는지를 말했다. 나는 그녀에게 내 벗의 마지막 두 편지를 보여 주어, 그것이 나를 런던으로 오라고 청하는 것임을, 그리고 그것이 얼마나 애정과 간곡함으로 쓰였는지를 보게 했으나, 이름은 지워 버렸고, 또 그의 아내의 참변에 관한 이야기도, 다만 그녀가 죽었다는 것 외에는 지워 버렸다."]], [["She fell a-laughing at my scruples about marrying, and told me the other was no marriage, but a cheat on both sides; and that, as we were parted by mutual consent, the nature of the contract was destroyed, and the obligation was mutually discharged.", "그녀는 결혼에 대한 내 거리낌을 두고 웃음을 터뜨리며, 그 다른 쪽 것은 결혼이 아니라 양쪽 모두에 대한 기만이었다고 했다. 그리고 우리가 상호 합의로 헤어졌으니, 그 계약의 본질은 깨어진 것이요, 그 의무는 서로 면제된 것이라고 했다."], ["She had arguments for this at the tip of her tongue; and, in short, reasoned me out of my reason; not but that it was too by the help of my own inclination.", "그녀에게는 이에 대한 논거들이 혀끝에 맺혀 있었으니, 한마디로 그녀는 따져서 나를 내 이성에서 벗어나게 했다. 물론 그것은 내 자신의 마음이 기우는 것이 거든 덕택이기도 했지만 말이다."]], [["But then came the great and main difficulty, and that was the child; this, she told me in so many words, must be removed, and that so as that it should never be possible for any one to discover it.", "그러나 그때 크고 주된 어려움이 닥쳤으니, 그것은 곧 아이였다. 이것은, 그녀가 노골적으로 내게 말하기를, 치워 버려야 하며, 그것도 누구도 결코 그것을 알아낼 수 없도록 해야 한다는 것이었다."], ["I knew there was no marrying without entirely concealing that I had had a child, for he would soon have discovered by the age of it that it was born, nay, and gotten too, since my parley with him, and that would have destroyed all the affair.", "나는 내게 아이가 있었다는 것을 완전히 감추지 않고는 결혼이 있을 수 없다는 것을 알고 있었으니, 그가 곧 그 나이로 미루어 그 아이가 내가 그와 이야기를 나눈 뒤에 태어났음을, 아니, 잉태되기까지 했음을 알아챌 것이고, 그러면 그 일이 다 깨어져 버릴 터였기 때문이다."]], [["But it touched my heart so forcibly to think of parting entirely with the child, and, for aught I knew, of having it murdered, or starved by neglect and ill-usage (which was much the same), that I could not think of it without horror.", "그러나 그 아이와 완전히 헤어진다는 것, 그리고 내가 알 수 없는 노릇이지만, 그 아이가 죽임을 당하거나, 방치와 학대로 굶어 죽는다는 것(이는 거의 같은 일이거니와)을 생각하면, 내 가슴이 어찌나 세차게 미어지던지, 나는 그것을 끔찍함 없이는 생각할 수가 없었다."], ["I wish all those women who consent to the disposing their children out of the way, as it is called, for decency sake, would consider that ’tis only a contrived method for murder; that is to say, a-killing their children with safety.", "나는, 점잖음을 위해서라며, 흔히 일컫는 대로 자기 아이를 어딘가로 치워 버리는 데 동의하는 모든 여자가, 그것이 그저 살인을 위해 꾸며진 방편일 뿐임을, 다시 말해 안전하게 자기 아이를 죽이는 일임을 헤아려 주기를 바란다."]], [["It is manifest to all that understand anything of children, that we are born into the world helpless, and incapable either to supply our own wants or so much as make them known; and that without help we must perish; and this help requires not only an assisting hand, whether of the mother or somebody else, but there are two things necessary in that assisting hand, that is, care and skill; without both which, half the children that are born would die, nay, though they were not to be denied food; and one half more of those that remained would be cripples or fools, lose their limbs, and perhaps their sense.", "아이에 관해 무엇이든 아는 모든 이에게 분명한 사실이거니와, 우리는 무력하게, 자기 자신의 필요를 채우기는커녕 그것을 알릴 능력조차 없이 세상에 태어나며, 도움 없이는 죽을 수밖에 없다. 그리고 이 도움에는 어머니든 다른 누구든 거들어 주는 손이 필요할 뿐 아니라, 그 거들어 주는 손에는 두 가지가 필요하니, 곧 보살핌과 솜씨다."], ["I question not but that these are partly the reasons why affection was placed by nature in the hearts of mothers to their children; without which they would never be able to give themselves up, as ’tis necessary they should, to the care and waking pains needful to the support of their children.", "이 둘이 다 없으면, 태어나는 아이의 절반은 죽을 것이요, 아니, 설령 먹을 것을 거부당하지 않는다 하더라도 그러할 것이며, 살아남은 자들 가운데서도 절반은 또 불구가 되거나 바보가 되어, 사지를 잃거나, 어쩌면 정신을 잃게 될 것이다. 나는 의심치 않거니와, 이런 것들이야말로 자연이 어머니의 가슴속에 자기 아이에 대한 애정을 심어 둔 이유의 일부이니, 그것 없이는 어머니들이 자기 아이를 지탱하는 데 필요한 보살핌과 뜬눈의 수고에, 마땅히 그래야 하듯이 자기 자신을 내던질 수 없을 것이다."]], [["Since this care is needful to the life of children, to neglect them is to murder them; again, to give them up to be managed by those people who have none of that needful affection placed by nature in them, is to neglect them in the highest degree; nay, in some it goes farther, and is a neglect in order to their being lost; so that ’tis even an intentional murder, whether the child lives or dies.", "이 보살핌이 아이의 생명에 필요한 것이므로, 그들을 방치하는 것은 곧 그들을 죽이는 것이다. 다시, 자연이 그 속에 심어 둔 그런 필요한 애정이라곤 조금도 지니지 못한 사람들에게 그들을 맡겨 다루게 하는 것은, 그들을 가장 높은 정도로 방치하는 것이다. 아니, 어떤 경우에는 그것이 더 나아가, 그들을 잃게 할 목적의 방치이니, 그리하여 그것은 아이가 살든 죽든 의도적인 살인이나 다름없다."]], [["All those things represented themselves to my view, and that is the blackest and most frightful form: and as I was very free with my governess, whom I had now learned to call mother, I represented to her all the dark thoughts which I had upon me about it, and told her what distress I was in.", "이 모든 것이 내 눈앞에 떠올랐으니, 그것도 가장 검고 가장 무시무시한 모양으로 떠올랐다. 그리고 나는 이제 어머니라고 부르는 법을 익힌 내 후견인에게 거리낌이 없던 터라, 그녀에게 내가 그 일을 두고 품고 있던 온갖 어두운 생각을 다 털어놓고, 내가 얼마나 큰 고통 속에 있는지를 말했다."], ["She seemed graver by much at this part than at the other; but as she was hardened in these things beyond all possibility of being touched with the religious part, and the scruples about the murder, so she was equally impenetrable in that part which related to affection.", "그녀는 이 대목에서는 다른 대목에서보다 훨씬 더 진지한 기색이었다. 그러나 그녀는 이런 일들에 도무지 마음을 움직일 가망이 없을 만큼 굳어 있어서, 신앙의 부분이며 살인에 대한 거리낌에 꿈쩍도 하지 않았듯이, 애정에 관한 그 부분에서도 똑같이 뚫고 들어갈 수가 없었다."], ["She asked me if she had not been careful and tender to me in my lying in, as if I had been her own child.", "그녀는 내게, 마치 내가 자기 친자식이라도 되는 듯이 산욕 중에 자기가 나를 세심하고 자상하게 보살피지 않았느냐고 물었다."], ["I told her I owned she had.", "나는 그녀가 그러했음을 인정한다고 했다."], ["“Well, my dear,”", "\"그래요, 내 사랑,\""], ["says she, “and when you are gone, what are you to me?", "그녀가 말했다, \"그런데 댁이 가 버리고 나면, 댁이 내게 뭐가 되죠?"], ["And what would it be to me if you were to be hanged?", "그리고 댁이 교수형을 당한다 한들 그게 내게 무슨 상관이겠어요?"], ["Do you think there are not women who, as it is their trade and they get their bread by it, value themselves upon their being as careful of children as their own mothers can be, and understand it rather better?", "그게 자기네 장사고 그걸로 밥벌이를 하느라, 친어머니가 할 수 있는 만큼이나 아이들을 알뜰히 보살피는 걸 자부하고, 어쩌면 더 잘 아는 여자들이 없다고 생각하세요? 아니에요, 아니고말고요, 아가,\""], ["Yes, yes, child,”", "그녀가 말했다, \"걱정 마요."], ["says she, “fear it not; how were we nursed ourselves?", "우리 자신은 어떻게 길러졌나요? 댁이 친어머니 손에 길러진 게 확실해요?"], ["Are you sure you was nursed up by your own mother?", "그런데도 댁은 토실토실하고 고운 걸요, 아가,\""], ["and yet you look fat and fair, child,”", "그 늙은 마귀할멈은 그렇게 말하며 내 얼굴을 쓰다듬었다. \"조금도 걱정 마요, 아가,\""], ["says the old beldam; and with that she stroked me over the face. “Never be concerned, child,”", "그녀는 자기 특유의 익살스러운 투로 이어 갔다, \"내 곁에는 살인자라곤 없어요."], ["says she, going on in her drolling way; “I have no murderers about me; I employ the best and the honestest nurses that can be had, and have as few children miscarry under their hands as there would if they were all nursed by mothers; we want neither care nor skill.”", "나는 구할 수 있는 가장 솜씨 좋고 가장 정직한 유모들만 쓰고, 그들 손에서 잘못되는 아이가, 죄다 친어머니가 젖을 먹였을 때만큼이나 적답니다. 우리에겐 보살핌도 솜씨도 모자라지 않아요.\""]], [["She touched me to the quick when she asked if I was sure that I was nursed by my own mother; on the contrary I was sure I was not; and I trembled, and looked pale at the very expression.", "내가 친어머니 손에 길러진 게 확실하냐고 그녀가 물었을 때, 그녀는 내 아픈 데를 찔렀다. 도리어 나는 그렇지 않았다는 것을 확실히 알고 있었으니, 나는 바로 그 말에 몸을 떨며 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["“Sure,”", "\"분명,\" 나는 속으로 중얼거렸다, \"이 사람이 마녀이거나 어떤 영(靈)과 통하는 게 아닐진대, 내가 나 자신을 알 수 있기도 전에 내게 무슨 일이 있었는지를 어찌 알 수 있겠어.\""], ["said I to myself, “this creature cannot be a witch, or have any conversation with a spirit, that can inform her what was done with me before I was able to know it myself”; and I looked at her as if I had been frightened; but reflecting that it could not be possible for her to know anything about me, that disorder went off, and I began to be easy, but it was not presently.", "그리고 나는 마치 겁에 질리기라도 한 듯이 그녀를 바라보았다. 그러나 그녀가 나에 관해 무엇 하나 알 수 있을 리 없다는 데 생각이 미치자 그 동요는 가셨고, 나는 마음이 놓이기 시작했으나, 그것이 곧바로는 아니었다."]], [["She perceived the disorder I was in, but did not know the meaning of it; so she ran on in her wild talk upon the weakness of my supposing that children were murdered because they were not all nursed by the mother, and to persuade me that the children she disposed of were as well used as if the mothers had the nursing of them themselves.", "그녀는 내가 빠진 동요를 알아챘으나 그 까닭은 알지 못했다. 그래서 그녀는 아이들이 친어머니 손에 다 길러지지 않는다 해서 그들이 죽임을 당한다고 짐작하는 내 어리석음을 두고 제멋대로 떠들어 댔으며, 자기가 처리하는 아이들은 그 어머니들이 직접 길렀더라도 그러했을 만큼이나 잘 대우받는다고 나를 설득하려 들었다."]], [["“It may be true, mother,”", "\"그게 사실일지도 모르죠, 어머니,\""], ["says I, “for aught I know, but my doubts are very strongly grounded indeed.”", "내가 말했다, \"제가 알 수 없는 노릇이지만요. 하지만 제 의심은 정말이지 매우 강하게 근거 지어진 거예요.\""], ["“Come, then,”", "\"그럼, 어디,\""], ["says she, “let’s hear some of them.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"그중 몇 개 들어 봅시다.\""], ["“Why, first,”", "\"왜냐하면, 우선,\""], ["says I, “you give a piece of money to these people to take the child off the parent’s hands, and to take care of it as long as it lives.", "내가 말했다, \"어머니는 그 사람들에게 약간의 돈을 주어, 그 아이를 부모의 손에서 떠맡아, 그 아이가 사는 동안 내내 보살피게 하잖아요."], ["Now we know, mother,”", "그런데 우리가 알다시피, 어머니,\""], ["said I, “that those are poor people, and their gain consists in being quit of the charge as soon as they can; how can I doubt but that, as it is best for them to have the child die, they are not over solicitous about life?”", "내가 말했다, \"그들은 가난한 사람들이고, 그들의 벌이는 되도록 빨리 그 부담에서 벗어나는 데 있어요. 그러니 그들로서는 그 아이가 죽는 게 가장 좋은 일이고 보면, 그들이 그 목숨에 그리 마음을 쓰지 않으리라는 걸 제가 어찌 의심하지 않을 수 있겠어요?\""]], [["“This is all vapours and fancy,”", "\"그건 다 헛것이고 공상이에요.\""], ["says the old woman; “I tell you their credit depends upon the child’s life, and they are as careful as any mother of you all.”", "그 노파가 말했다. \"내 말하건대, 그들의 신용은 그 아이의 목숨에 달려 있어서, 그들은 댁들 누구의 친어머니 못지않게 알뜰히 보살핀다니까요.\""]], [["“O mother,”", "\"오 어머니,\""], ["says I, “if I was but sure my little baby would be carefully looked to, and have justice done it, I should be happy indeed; but it is impossible I can be satisfied in that point unless I saw it, and to see it would be ruin and destruction to me, as now my case stands; so what to do I know not.”", "내가 말했다, \"제 어린것이 알뜰히 보살핌을 받고 마땅한 대우를 받는다는 것만 제가 확신할 수 있다면, 저는 정말이지 행복할 거예요. 하지만 그 점에서 제가 흡족해질 수 있으려면 제가 그 아이를 직접 보는 수밖에 없는데, 지금 제 처지에서 그 아이를 보는 것은 제게 파멸이요 멸망일 테니, 그러니 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""]], [["“A fine story!”", "\"별 이야기를 다!\""], ["says the governess.", "후견인이 말했다."], ["“You would see the child, and you would not see the child; you would be concealed and discovered both together.", "\"댁은 아이를 보고도 싶고, 아이를 보고 싶지 않기도 하고, 감추어지고도 싶고 드러나고도 싶고, 둘 다를 한꺼번에 바라는군요."], ["These are things impossible, my dear; so you must e’en do as other conscientious mothers have done before you, and be contented with things as they must be, though they are not as you wish them to be.”", "그건 불가능한 일이에요, 내 사랑. 그러니 댁은 댁보다 앞서 다른 양심적인 어머니들이 해 온 그대로 해야 하고, 일이 댁의 바람대로는 아니더라도 마땅히 그래야 하는 대로인 데에 만족해야 해요.\""]], [["I understood what she meant by conscientious mothers; she would have said conscientious whores, but she was not willing to disoblige me, for really in this case I was not a whore, because legally married, the force of former marriage excepted.", "나는 그녀가 양심적인 어머니들이라는 말로 무엇을 뜻하는지 알아들었다. 그녀는 양심적인 창녀들이라고 말하려 했던 것이나, 나를 언짢게 하고 싶지 않았던 것이다. 사실 이 경우에 나는 창녀가 아니었으니, 앞선 결혼의 효력을 제외하면 적법하게 결혼한 몸이었기 때문이다."]], [["However, let me be what I would, I was not come up to that pitch of hardness common to the profession; I mean, to be unnatural, and regardless of the safety of my child; and I preserved this honest affection so long, that I was upon the point of giving up my friend at the bank, who lay so hard at me to come to him and marry him, that, in short, there was hardly any room to deny him.", "그러나 내가 무엇이었든 간에, 나는 그 직업에 흔한 그런 모진 경지에까지는 이르지 못했으니, 다시 말해 천륜을 저버리고 내 아이의 안위를 아랑곳하지 않는 경지 말이다. 그리고 나는 이 정직한 애정을 어찌나 오래 간직했던지, 은행에 있는 내 벗을 거의 포기할 지경에까지 이르렀다. 그는 자기에게 와서 결혼하자고 어찌나 모질게 나를 다그쳤던지, 한마디로 그를 거절할 여지가 거의 없었는데도 말이다."]], [["At last my old governess came to me, with her usual assurance.", "마침내 나의 늙은 후견인이 여느 때의 그 자신만만함으로 내게 와서 말했다."], ["“Come, my dear,”", "\"자, 내 사랑,\""], ["says she, “I have found out a way how you shall be at a certainty that your child shall be used well, and yet the people that take care of it shall never know you, or who the mother of the child is.”", "그녀가 말했다, \"내가 한 가지 방도를 찾아냈어요. 댁의 아이가 잘 대우받으리라는 걸 댁이 확실히 알 수 있으면서도, 그 아이를 보살피는 사람들은 댁을 결코 알지 못하고, 그 아이의 어미가 누구인지도 결코 알지 못하게 할 방도 말이에요.\""]], [["“Oh mother,”", "\"오 어머니,\""], ["says I, “if you can do so, you will engage me to you for ever.”", "내가 말했다, \"그렇게 해 주실 수 있다면, 저는 영원히 어머니께 매인 몸이 될 거예요.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"자,\""], ["says she, “are you willing to be a some small annual expense, more than what we usually give to the people we contract with?”", "그녀가 말했다, \"우리가 보통 계약하는 사람들에게 주는 것보다, 댁이 매년 약간의 비용을 더 댈 용의가 있어요?\" \"그럼요,\""], ["“Ay,” says I, “with all my heart, provided I may be concealed.”", "내가 말했다, \"기꺼이요, 다만 제가 감추어질 수만 있다면요.\""], ["“As to that,”", "\"그 점은,\""], ["says the governess, “you shall be secure, for the nurse shall never so much as dare to inquire about you, and you shall once or twice a year go with me and see your child, and see how ’tis used, and be satisfied that it is in good hands, nobody knowing who you are.”", "후견인이 말했다, \"댁은 안전할 거예요. 유모는 감히 댁에 대해 캐물을 엄두조차 못 낼 테고, 댁은 일 년에 한두 번 나와 함께 가서 댁의 아이를 보고, 그 아이가 어떻게 대우받는지 보고, 그 아이가 좋은 손에 있다는 걸, 댁이 누구인지 아무도 모르는 채로, 확인할 수 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“Why,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said I, “do you think, mother, that when I come to see my child, I shall be able to conceal my being the mother of it?", "내가 말했다, \"어머니, 제가 제 아이를 보러 가면, 제가 그 아이의 어미라는 걸 감출 수 있을 거라고 생각하세요?"], ["Do you think that possible?”", "그게 가능하다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"걱정 마요, 걱정 마요,\""], ["says my governess, “if you discover it, the nurse shall be never the wiser; for she shall be forbid to ask any questions about you, or to take any notice.", "나의 후견인이 말했다, \"댁이 그걸 드러낸다 한들, 유모는 조금도 눈치채지 못할 거예요. 그 여자는 댁에 대해 어떤 것도 묻거나 어떤 것도 알은체하는 게 금지될 테니까요."], ["If she offers it, she shall lose the money which you are suppose to give her, and the child shall be taken from her too.”", "만약 그 여자가 그러려 들면, 댁이 줄 것으로 되어 있는 돈을 잃게 될 테고, 그 아이도 그 여자에게서 거두어들이게 될 거예요.\""]], [["I was very well pleased with this.", "나는 이것이 매우 흡족했다."], ["So the next week a countrywoman was brought from Hertford, or thereabouts, who was to take the child off our hands entirely for £10 in money.", "그래서 그다음 주에 하트퍼드 근처 어디에선가 어떤 시골 여자가 데려와졌는데, 그 여자는 돈 10파운드를 받고 그 아이를 우리 손에서 완전히 떠맡기로 되어 있었다."], ["But if I would allow £5 a year more of her, she would be obliged to bring the child to my governess’s house as often as we desired, or we should come down and look at it, and see how well she used it.", "그러나 만약 내가 그 여자에게 매년 5파운드를 더 주겠다면, 그 여자는 우리가 바랄 때마다 그 아이를 내 후견인의 집으로 데려오거나, 우리가 내려가서 그 아이를 보고 그 여자가 그 아이를 얼마나 잘 대우하는지 보게 할 의무를 지기로 했다."]], [["The woman was very wholesome-looking, a likely woman, a cottager’s wife, but she had very good clothes and linen, and everything well about her; and with a heavy heart and many a tear, I let her have my child.", "그 여자는 매우 건강해 보였고, 그럴듯한 여자였으며, 한 오두막 농부의 아내였으나, 옷가지며 속옷이 매우 좋았고, 자기 몸에 두른 모든 것이 단정했다. 나는 무거운 마음과 숱한 눈물로 그 여자에게 내 아이를 내주었다."], ["I had been down at Hertford, and looked at her and at her dwelling, which I liked well enough; and I promised her great things if she would be kind to the child, so she knew at first word that I was the child’s mother.", "나는 하트퍼드에 내려가 그 여자와 그 여자의 거처를 보았는데, 그것이 제법 마음에 들었다. 그리고 나는 그 여자에게, 그 아이에게 잘해 주기만 한다면 후하게 해 주겠노라 약속했으니, 그래서 그 여자는 첫마디부터 내가 그 아이의 어미인 것을 알았다."], ["But she seemed to be so much out of the way, and to have no room to inquire after me, that I thought I was safe enough.", "그러나 그 여자는 어찌나 외진 데에 있고, 나에 대해 알아볼 여지라곤 없어 보였던지, 나는 내가 충분히 안전하다고 생각했다. 그래서 한마디로, 나는 그 여자에게 그 아이를 내주는 데 동의했고, 그 여자에게 10파운드를 주었다."], ["So, in short, I consented to let her have the child, and I gave her £10; that is to say, I gave it to my governess, who gave it the poor woman before my face, she agreeing never to return the child back to me, or to claim anything more for its keeping or bringing up; only that I promised, if she took a great deal of care of it, I would give her something more as often as I came to see it; so that I was not bound to pay the £5, only that I promised my governess I would do it.", "다시 말해, 나는 그것을 내 후견인에게 주었고, 그녀가 내 면전에서 그 가난한 여자에게 주었으니, 그 여자는 결코 그 아이를 내게 돌려보내지도, 그 아이를 거두고 기르는 값으로 더 이상 무엇을 요구하지도 않기로 합의했다. 다만 나는, 그 여자가 그 아이를 무척 알뜰히 보살피거든, 내가 그 아이를 보러 갈 때마다 무언가 더 주겠노라 약속했을 뿐이다."], ["And thus my great care was over, after a manner, which though it did not at all satisfy my mind, yet was the most convenient for me, as my affairs then stood, of any that could be thought of at that time.", "그러니 나는 그 5파운드를 낼 의무가 있는 것은 아니었고, 다만 내 후견인에게 그렇게 하겠노라 약속했을 뿐이다. 이리하여 나의 큰 걱정은, 어떻게든 끝이 났으니, 비록 그것이 내 마음을 조금도 흡족케 하지는 못했으나, 그래도 그 당시 내 사정이 처한 형편으로는, 그때 떠올릴 수 있었던 어떤 방도보다도 내게 가장 편한 것이었다."]], [["I then began to write to my friend at the bank in a more kindly style, and particularly about the beginning of July I sent him a letter, that I proposed to be in town some time in August.", "그제야 나는 은행에 있는 내 벗에게 더 다정한 투로 편지를 쓰기 시작했고, 특히 7월 초쯤 그에게 편지 한 통을 보내어, 8월 어느 무렵에 시내에 가 있을 작정이라고 했다."], ["He returned me an answer in the most passionate terms imaginable, and desired me to let him have timely notice, and he would come and meet me, two day’s journey.", "그는 상상할 수 있는 가장 정열적인 말로 내게 답장을 보내어, 미리 알맞게 기별을 달라고, 그러면 자기가 이틀 길을 와서 나를 마중하겠노라고 했다."], ["This puzzled me scurvily, and I did not know what answer to make of it.", "이것은 나를 고약하게 난감하게 했으니, 나는 거기에 무어라 답해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["Once I resolved to take the stage-coach to West Chester, on purpose only to have the satisfaction of coming back, that he might see me really come in the same coach; for I had a jealous thought, though I had no ground for it at all, lest he should think I was not really in the country.", "한때 나는 일부러 웨스트체스터까지 역마차를 타고 갔다가 돌아오는 만족을 누리려고 작정했으니, 그가 내가 정말로 같은 마차로 들어오는 것을 보게 하려 함이었다. 나는, 비록 그럴 아무런 근거도 없었으나, 그가 내가 정말로 그 시골에 있던 것이 아니라고 여길까 봐 시샘 어린 생각이 들었던 것이다."], ["And it was no ill-grounded thought as you shall hear presently.", "그리고 그것은 곧 듣게 되겠지만, 근거 없는 생각도 아니었다."]], [["I endeavoured to reason myself out of it, but it was in vain; the impression lay so strong on my mind, that it was not to be resisted.", "나는 그런 생각에서 따져 벗어나려 애썼으나 헛일이었다. 그 인상이 내 마음에 어찌나 강하게 박혀 있던지 저항할 수가 없었다."], ["At last it came as an addition to my new design of going into the country, that it would be an excellent blind to my old governess, and would cover entirely all my other affairs, for she did not know in the least whether my new lover lived in London or in Lancashire; and when I told her my resolution, she was fully persuaded it was in Lancashire.", "마침내 그 생각이, 시골로 내려간다는 내 새 계획에 보태어, 나의 늙은 후견인에게 더없이 훌륭한 눈가림이 되어 내 다른 모든 일을 완전히 가려 주리라는 데에까지 미쳤으니, 그녀는 내 새 연인이 런던에 사는지 랭커셔에 사는지 도무지 알지 못했기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 그녀에게 내 결심을 말했을 때, 그녀는 그것이 랭커셔에 있는 일이라고 굳게 믿었다."]], [["Having taken my measure for this journey I let her know it, and sent the maid that tended me, from the beginning, to take a place for me in the coach.", "이 여행을 위해 채비를 마치고서 나는 그녀에게 그것을 알렸고, 처음부터 나를 시중들던 그 하녀를 보내어 마차에 내 자리를 잡아 두게 했다."], ["She would have had me let the maid have waited on me down to the last stage, and come up again in the waggon, but I convinced her it would not be convenient.", "그녀는 그 하녀가 마지막 역참까지 나를 시중들며 내려갔다가 짐마차로 다시 올라오게 하라고 하려 했으나, 나는 그것이 편치 않을 거라고 그녀를 설득했다."], ["When I went away, she told me she would enter into no measures for correspondence, for she saw evidently that my affection to my child would cause me to write to her, and to visit her too when I came to town again.", "내가 떠날 때 그녀는 내게, 서신 왕래를 위한 어떤 방도도 정하지 않겠다고 했으니, 내 아이에 대한 애정이 내가 다시 시내에 오면 그녀에게 편지하고 그녀를 찾게 만들리라는 것을 그녀가 빤히 알았기 때문이다."], ["I assured her it would, and so took my leave, well satisfied to have been freed from such a house, however good my accommodations there had been, as I have related above.", "나는 그녀에게 정말 그러리라고 확언하고는, 그렇게 작별을 고했으니, 앞에서 이야기한 대로 거기서의 내 시설이 아무리 좋았다 한들, 그런 집에서 벗어나게 된 것이 매우 흡족했다."]], [["I took the place in the coach not to its full extent, but to a place called Stone, in Cheshire, I think it is, where I not only had no manner of business, but not so much as the least acquaintance with any person in the town or near it.", "나는 마차 자리를 끝까지 가는 것으로 잡지 않고, 체셔에 있는, 내 생각엔 그곳인 것 같은데, 스톤이라는 곳까지만 잡았으니, 거기에는 내가 무슨 볼일이 있기는커녕, 그 마을이나 그 근처의 누구와도 조금도 아는 사이조차 아니었다."], ["But I knew that with money in the pocket one is at home anywhere; so I lodged there two or three days, till, watching my opportunity, I found room in another stage-coach, and took passage back again for London, sending a letter to my gentleman that I should be such a certain day at Stony-Stratford, where the coachman told me he was to lodge.", "그러나 나는, 주머니에 돈이 있으면 어디서든 제집 같다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 거기서 이삼 일을 묵으며 기회를 엿보다가, 다른 역마차에 자리가 난 것을 알고 런던으로 다시 돌아가는 차편을 잡고는, 내 신사에게 편지를 보내어, 내가 어느 날 스토니스트랫퍼드에 가 있겠다고 했으니, 마부가 내게 거기서 묵을 거라고 일러 주었던 것이다."]], [["It happened to be a chance coach that I had taken up, which, having been hired on purpose to carry some gentlemen to West Chester who were going for Ireland, was now returning, and did not tie itself to exact times or places as the stages did; so that, having been obliged to lie still on Sunday, he had time to get himself ready to come out, which otherwise he could not have done.", "내가 잡아탄 것은 마침 임시로 빌린 마차였는데, 아일랜드로 가려고 웨스트체스터로 가는 어떤 신사들을 모시려고 일부러 빌린 것이라, 이제 돌아가는 길이었고, 역마차들처럼 정확한 시각이나 장소에 매여 있지 않았다. 그래서 마부는 일요일에 가만히 묵을 수밖에 없었던 덕에, 나설 채비를 할 시간이 있었던바, 그렇지 않았다면 그러지 못했을 것이다."]], [["However, his warning was so short, that he could not reach to Stony-Stratford time enough to be with me at night, but he met me at a place called Brickhill the next morning, as we were just coming in to tow.", "그러나 그가 받은 기별이 너무 촉박했던 터라, 그는 밤에 나와 함께 있을 만큼 제때에 스토니스트랫퍼드에 닿을 수가 없었고, 다만 이튿날 아침, 우리가 막 마을로 들어서던 참에, 브릭힐이라는 곳에서 나를 만났다."]], [["I confess I was very glad to see him, for I had thought myself a little disappointed over-night, seeing I had gone so far to contrive my coming on purpose.", "고백하건대 나는 그를 보아 매우 기뻤으니, 일부러 그 먼 데까지 가서 내가 오는 것을 꾸며 놓은 마당에, 전날 밤 조금 낭패를 본 것 같았기 때문이다."], ["He pleased me doubly too by the figure he came in, for he brought a very handsome (gentleman’s) coach and four horses, with a servant to attend him.", "그는 또한 자기가 타고 온 차림새로 나를 갑절로 기쁘게 했으니, 그가 매우 멋진 (신사용) 마차와 말 네 필을 데리고, 자기를 시중드는 하인까지 거느리고 왔기 때문이다."]], [["He took me out of the stage-coach immediately, which stopped at an inn in Brickhill; and putting into the same inn, he set up his own coach, and bespoke his dinner.", "그는 곧 나를 역마차에서 내리게 했으니, 그 마차는 브릭힐의 한 여인숙에 멈추었다. 그러고는 같은 여인숙에 들어, 자기 마차를 세워 두고 저녁을 시켰다."], ["I asked him what he meant by that, for I was for going forward with the journey.", "나는 그것이 무슨 뜻이냐고 물었으니, 나는 여정을 계속 나아가고 싶었기 때문이다."], ["He said, No, I had need of a little rest upon the road, and that was a very good sort of a house, though it was but a little town; so we would go no farther that night, whatever came of it.", "그는 아니라고, 내가 길에서 약간 쉴 필요가 있다고, 그리고 이 집은 비록 작은 마을이긴 해도 매우 좋은 부류의 집이라고 했다. 그러니 무슨 일이 있어도 그날 밤은 더 이상 가지 말자는 것이었다."]], [["I did not press him much, for since he had come so to meet me, and put himself to so much expense, it was but reasonable I should oblige him a little too; so I was easy as to that point.", "나는 그를 그리 다그치지 않았으니, 그가 나를 마중하러 그렇게 와서 그토록 많은 비용을 들인 마당에, 나도 그를 좀 받들어 주는 것이 마땅했기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그 점에 대해 마음이 편했다."]], [["After dinner we walked to see the town, to see the church, and to view the fields, and the country, as is usual for strangers to do; and our landlord was our guide in going to see the church.", "저녁을 들고 나서 우리는 마을을 구경하러, 교회를 보러, 들판과 시골을 둘러보러 거닐었으니, 낯선 이들이 으레 하는 대로였고, 우리 여인숙 주인이 교회 구경을 가는 데 우리 길잡이가 되어 주었다. 나는 내 신사가 그 목사에 대해 꽤나 캐묻는 것을 눈여겨보았고, 곧 그가 분명 결혼하자고 제안하리라는 낌새를 챘다."], ["I observed my gentleman inquired pretty much about the parson, and I took the hint immediately that he certainly would propose to be married; and though it was a sudden thought, it followed presently, that, in short, I would not refuse him; for, to be plain, with my circumstances I was in no condition now to say No; I had no reason now to run any more such hazards.", "그리고 그것은 갑작스러운 생각이었으나, 곧이어 다음과 같은 생각이 따라왔으니, 한마디로 나는 그를 거절하지 않으리라는 것이었다. 솔직히 말해, 내 처지로는 나는 이제 안 된다고 말할 형편이 못 되었고, 더 이상 그런 모험을 무릅쓸 까닭이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["But while these thoughts ran round in my head, which was the work but of a few moments, I observed my landlord took him aside and whispered to him, though not very softly neither, for so much I overheard: “Sir, if you shall have occasion——”", "그러나 이런 생각들이 내 머릿속을 맴도는 동안—그것은 그저 몇 순간의 일이었지만—나는 우리 여인숙 주인이 그를 한쪽으로 데려가 귀엣말을 하는 것을 눈여겨보았는데, 그것도 그리 나직하지도 않아서, 나는 이만큼은 엿들었던 것이다. \"나리, 혹시 필요하시거든……\""], ["the rest I could not hear, but it seems it was to this purpose: “Sir, if you shall have occasion for a minister, I have a friend a little way off that will serve you, and be as private as you please.”", "그 나머지는 듣지 못했으나, 보아하니 이런 취지였던 것 같다. \"나리, 혹시 목사가 필요하시거든, 조금 떨어진 데에 나리를 도와줄 제 벗이 있는데, 나리께서 바라시는 만큼 은밀히 해 드릴 겁니다.\""], ["My gentleman answered loud enough for me to hear, “Very well, I believe I shall.”", "내 신사는 내가 들을 수 있을 만큼 큰 소리로 대답했다. \"아주 좋네, 아마 그래야 할 것 같으이.\""]], [["I was no sooner come back to the inn but he fell upon me with irresistible words, that since he had had the good fortune to meet me, and everything concurred, it would be hastening his felicity if I would put an end to the matter just there.", "내가 여인숙으로 돌아오자마자 그는 거역할 수 없는 말로 나를 몰아붙였으니, 자기가 다행히 나를 만났고 모든 것이 들어맞은 마당에, 만약 내가 바로 거기서 그 일을 매듭짓겠다면 그것이 자기 행복을 앞당기는 것이 되리라고 했다."], ["“What do you mean?”", "\"무슨 말씀이세요?\""], ["says I, colouring a little.", "내가 얼굴을 약간 붉히며 말했다."], ["“What, in an inn, and upon the road!", "\"아니, 여인숙에서, 그것도 길 위에서요!"], ["Bless us all,”", "맙소사,\""], ["said I, as if I had been surprised, “how can you talk so?”", "마치 내가 놀라기라도 한 듯이 내가 말했다, \"어떻게 그런 말씀을 하세요?\""], ["“Oh, I can talk so very well,”", "\"오, 나는 그런 말 아주 잘할 수 있다오,\""], ["says he, “I came a-purpose to talk so, and I’ll show you that I did”; and with that he pulls out a great bundle of papers.", "그가 말했다, \"일부러 그런 말을 하려고 온 거고, 내가 그랬다는 걸 보여 주리다.\" 그러면서 그는 서류 한 뭉치를 꺼냈다."], ["“You fright me,”", "\"겁나게 하시네요,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I; “what are all these?”", "\"이게 다 뭐예요?\" \"겁내지 마요, 내 사랑,\""], ["“Don’t be frighted, my dear,”", "그가 말하며 내게 입을 맞추었다. 그가 나를 \"내 사랑\"이라고 부를 만큼 거리낌 없었던 것은 이때가 처음이었다."], ["said he, and kissed me.", "그러고는 그가 거듭 말했다. \"겁내지 마요."], ["This was the first time that he had been so free to call me “my dear”; then he repeated it, “Don’t be frighted; you shall see what it is all”; then he laid them all abroad.", "이게 다 뭔지 보게 될 테니.\" 그러고는 그가 그것들을 죄다 펼쳐 놓았다."], ["There was first the deed or sentence of divorce from his wife, and the full evidence of her playing the whore; then there were the certificates of the minister and churchwardens of the parish where she lived, proving that she was buried, and intimating the manner of her death; the copy of the coroner’s warrant for a jury to sit upon her, and the verdict of the jury, who brought it in Non compos mentis.", "먼저 그의 아내로부터의 이혼 증서, 곧 판결문이 있었고, 그녀가 창녀짓을 했다는 충분한 증거가 있었다. 다음으로는 그녀가 살던 교구의 목사와 교구 위원들의 증명서들이 있었으니, 그녀가 매장되었음을 입증하고 그녀의 사인을 넌지시 알리는 것이었다."], ["All this was indeed to the purpose, and to give me satisfaction, though, by the way, I was not so scrupulous, had he known all, but that I might have taken him without it.", "그리고 그녀를 두고 배심을 열라는 검시관의 영장 사본, 그리고 그것을 심신상실이라 판정한 배심의 평결이 있었다. 이 모든 것은 정말이지 핵심에 닿는 것이었고, 나를 흡족케 하려는 것이었다."], ["However, I looked them all over as well as I could, and told him that this was all very clear indeed, but that he need not have given himself the trouble to have brought them out with him, for it was time enough.", "비록, 덧붙여 말하자면, 그가 모든 걸 알았더라면, 나는 그것 없이도 그를 받아들였을 만큼 그리 까다롭지 않았을 테지만 말이다. 그래도 나는 할 수 있는 대로 그것들을 다 훑어보고는, 그에게 이것이 정말이지 다 매우 명백하나, 그가 굳이 그것들을 가지고 오는 수고를 할 필요는 없었을 거라고, 그것은 천천히 해도 될 일이었다고 했다."], ["Well, he said, it might be time enough for me, but no time but the present time was time enough for him.", "그래, 그는 그것이 나에게는 천천히 해도 될 일일지 모르나, 자기에게는 지금 이 순간 말고는 어떤 때도 충분히 이른 때가 아니라고 했다."]], [["There were other papers rolled up, and I asked him what they were.", "다른 서류들이 둘둘 말려 있어서, 나는 그에게 그것들이 무엇이냐고 물었다."], ["“Why, ay,”", "\"아, 그래요,\""], ["says he, “that’s the question I wanted to have you ask me”; so he unrolls them and takes out a little shagreen case, and gives me out of it a very fine diamond ring.", "그가 말했다, \"그게 바로 내가 당신이 물어 주기를 바라던 거요.\" 그러고는 그가 그것들을 펼쳐, 작은 가죽 상자 하나를 꺼내더니, 그 안에서 매우 고운 다이아몬드 반지 하나를 내게 건넸다."], ["I could not refuse it, if I had a mind to do so, for he put it upon my finger; so I made him a curtsy and accepted it.", "설령 그러고 싶었다 한들 나는 그것을 거절할 수가 없었으니, 그가 그것을 내 손가락에 끼워 주었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그에게 살짝 무릎을 굽혀 절하고 그것을 받았다."], ["Then he takes out another ring: “And this,”", "그러고는 그가 또 다른 반지를 꺼냈다. \"그리고 이건,\""], ["says he, “is for another occasion,”", "그가 말했다, \"다른 경우를 위한 거요.\" 그러면서 그가 그것을 자기 주머니에 넣었다."], ["so he puts that in his pocket.", "\"어머, 그래도 좀 보여 주세요,\""], ["“Well, but let me see it, though,”", "내가 미소 지으며 말했다. \"그게 뭔지 짐작이 가요."], ["says I, and smiled; “I guess what it is; I think you are mad.”", "당신 미쳤군요.\" \"이보다 덜했다면 내가 미친 거였을 거요,\""], ["“I should have been mad if I had done less,”", "그가 말했으나, 여전히 내게 보여 주지 않았고, 나는 그것을 무척이나 보고 싶었다."], ["says he, and still he did not show me, and I had a great mind to see it; so I says, “Well, but let me see it.”", "그래서 내가 말했다. \"그래도 좀 보여 주세요.\""], ["“Hold,”", "\"가만,\""], ["says he, “first look here”; then he took up the roll again and read it, and behold!", "그가 말했다, \"먼저 이걸 봐요.\" 그러고는 그가 다시 그 두루마리를 집어 들어 읽었는데, 보라!"], ["it was a licence for us to be married.", "그것은 우리가 결혼해도 좋다는 허가서였다."], ["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says I, “are you distracted?", "내가 말했다, \"당신 정신 나갔어요?"], ["Why, you were fully satisfied that I would comply and yield at first word, or resolved to take no denial.”", "아니, 당신은 내가 첫마디에 응하고 따르리라고 완전히 확신했거나, 아니면 어떤 거절도 받지 않겠다고 작정했군요.\""], ["“The last is certainly the case,”", "\"뒤엣것이 분명 맞소,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he. “But you may be mistaken,”", "\"하지만 잘못 짚었을 수도 있잖아요,\""], ["said I.", "내가 말했다."], ["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니오,\""], ["says he, “how can you think so?", "그가 말했다, \"어찌 그렇게 생각할 수 있겠소?"], ["I must not be denied, I can’t be denied”; and with that he fell to kissing me so violently, I could not get rid of him.", "나는 거절당해서는 안 되고, 거절당할 수가 없소.\" 그러면서 그가 어찌나 격렬하게 내게 입맞추기 시작했던지, 나는 그에게서 벗어날 수가 없었다."]], [["There was a bed in the room, and we were walking to and again, eager in the discourse; at last he takes me by surprise in his arms, and threw me on the bed and himself with me, and holding me fast in his arms, but without the least offer of any indecency, courted me to consent with such repeated entreaties and arguments, protesting his affection, and vowing he would not let me go till I had promised him, that at last I said, “Why, you resolve not to be denied, indeed, I can’t be denied.”", "방 안에 침대 하나가 있었고, 우리는 이야기에 열을 올리며 이리저리 거닐고 있었다. 마침내 그가 불시에 나를 두 팔로 끌어안아 침대 위로 던지고는 자기도 함께 쓰러졌으니, 나를 두 팔로 꼭 붙든 채로, 그러나 조금의 무례한 짓도 없이, 거듭되는 간청과 논변으로 나에게 응하라고 졸랐으며, 자기 애정을 맹세하고, 내가 약속하기 전에는 결코 나를 놓아주지 않겠노라 다짐하니, 마침내 내가 말했다."], ["“Well, well,”", "\"원, 당신은 정말 거절당하지 않기로 작정했고, 나는 거절할 수가 없네요.\""], ["said I, and giving him a slight kiss, “then you shan’t be denied,”", "\"좋아요, 좋아,\" 내가 그에게 가볍게 입맞추며 말했다, \"그럼 당신은 거절당하지 않을 거예요,\""], ["said I; “let me get up.”", "내가 말했다, \"이제 좀 일으켜 주세요.\""]], [["He was so transported with my consent, and the kind manner of it, that I began to think once he took it for a marriage, and would not stay for the form; but I wronged him, for he gave over kissing me, and then giving me two or three kisses again, thanked me for my kind yielding to him; and was so overcome with the satisfaction and joy of it, that I saw tears stand in his eyes.", "그는 내 승낙과, 그 다정한 태도에 어찌나 도취했던지, 한순간 나는 그가 그것을 결혼으로 여겨 그 의식을 기다리지도 않으려는 게 아닌가 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 나는 그를 오해했으니, 그는 내게 입맞추기를 그만두었고, 그러고는 다시 두세 번 입맞추며, 자기에게 다정하게 응해 준 것에 고마워했으며, 그것의 만족과 기쁨에 어찌나 북받쳤던지, 나는 그의 눈에 눈물이 고이는 것을 보았다."]], [["I turned from him, for it filled my eyes with tears too, and I asked him leave to retire a little to my chamber.", "나는 그에게서 돌아섰으니, 그것이 내 눈에도 눈물을 가득 고이게 했기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 그에게 잠시 내 방으로 물러가게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["If ever I had a grain of true repentance for a vicious and abominable life for twenty-four years past, it was then.", "만약 내가 지난 스물네 해 동안의 악덕에 찌든 가증스러운 삶에 대해 한 톨이라도 참된 회개를 품은 적이 있다면, 그것은 바로 그때였다."], ["Oh, what a felicity is it to mankind, said I to myself, that they cannot see into the hearts of one another!", "오, 사람들이 서로의 마음속을 들여다볼 수 없다는 것이 인류에게 얼마나 다행한 일인가, 하고 나는 속으로 중얼거렸다."], ["How happy had it been for me if I had been wife to a man of so much honesty, and so much affection from the beginning!", "내가 처음부터 그토록 정직하고 그토록 애정 어린 남자의 아내였더라면 내게 얼마나 행복한 일이었을까!"]], [["Then it occurred to me, “What an abominable creature am I!", "그러자 이런 생각이 떠올랐다. \"나는 얼마나 가증스러운 인간인가!"], ["and how is this innocent gentleman going to be abused by me!", "그리고 이 순진한 신사가 나에게 얼마나 속아 넘어가려는가!"], ["How little does he think, that having divorced a whore, he is throwing himself into the arms of another!", "자기가 한 창녀와 이혼하고서, 또 다른 창녀의 품에 자기를 내던지고 있다는 걸 그가 얼마나 모르고 있는가!"], ["that he is going to marry one that has lain with two brothers, and has had three children by her own brother!", "형제 둘과 동침하고, 자기 친오라비에게서 세 아이를 낳은 여자와 결혼하려 한다는 걸! 뉴게이트에서 태어났고, 그 어미는 창녀였으며 지금은 유배된 도둑인 여자와!"], ["one that was born in Newgate, whose mother was a whore, and is now a transported thief!", "열세 명의 남자와 동침했고, 그를 본 뒤로 아이까지 낳은 여자와!"], ["one that has lain with thirteen men, and has had a child since he saw me!", "가엾은 신사여,\" 내가 말했다, \"그가 무슨 짓을 하려는 것인가?\""], ["Poor gentleman!” said I, “what is he going to do?”", "이렇게 나 자신을 책망하기를 마치자, 이런 생각이 뒤따랐다."], ["After this reproaching myself was over, it following thus: “Well, if I must be his wife, if it please God to give me grace, I’ll be a true wife to him, and love him suitably to the strange excess of his passion for me; I will make him amends if possible, by what he shall see, for the cheats and abuses I put upon him, which he does not see.”", "\"그래, 내가 그의 아내가 되어야 한다면, 신께서 내게 은총을 베푸시기를 바라거니와, 나는 그에게 참된 아내가 되어, 나를 향한 그의 그 기이하게 넘치는 정념에 걸맞게 그를 사랑하리라. 가능하다면, 그가 보게 될 것으로써, 그가 보지 못하는 내가 그에게 저지른 속임과 학대를 보상해 주리라.\""]], [["He was impatient for my coming out of my chamber, but finding me long, he went downstairs and talked with my landlord about the parson.", "그는 내가 방에서 나오기를 애타게 기다렸으나, 내가 더디 나오는 것을 보고는 아래층으로 내려가 우리 여인숙 주인과 그 목사에 대해 이야기를 나누었다."]], [["My landlord, an officious though well-meaning fellow, had sent away for the neighbouring clergyman; and when my gentleman began to speak of it to him, and talk of sending for him, “Sir,”", "주제넘긴 하나 선의는 있는 사람인 우리 여인숙 주인은 이웃 성직자를 부르러 사람을 보내 둔 터였다."], ["says he to him, “my friend is in the house”; so without any more words he brought them together.", "그래서 내 신사가 그에게 그 일을 이야기하며 그를 부르러 보내자고 말을 꺼내자, \"나리,\""], ["When he came to the minister, he asked him if he would venture to marry a couple of strangers that were both willing. The parson said that Mr.", "그가 그에게 말했다, \"제 벗이 집 안에 있습니다.\""], ["—— had said something to him of it; that he hoped it was no clandestine business; that he seemed to be a grave gentleman, and he supposed madam was not a girl, so that the consent of friends should be wanted.", "그러고는 더 말할 것도 없이 그가 두 사람을 만나게 해 주었다. 그 목사를 만나자, 그는 둘 다 원하는 한 쌍의 낯선 이들을 결혼시킬 엄두를 내겠느냐고 물었다."], ["“To put you out of doubt of that,” says my gentleman, “read this paper”; and out he pulls the license.", "그 목사는, 아무개 씨가 자기에게 그 일을 얼마간 말했다고, 자기는 이것이 은밀한 일이 아니기를 바란다고, 그가 점잖은 신사로 보이고, 부인도 처녀는 아닌 듯하니 벗들의 동의가 필요하지는 않을 거라 짐작한다고 했다."], ["“I am satisfied,” says the minister; “where is the lady?”", "\"그 점에 대해 의심을 거두시도록,\""], ["“You shall see her presently,” says my gentleman.", "내 신사가 말했다, \"이 서류를 읽어 보시오.\""]], [["When he had said thus he comes upstairs, and I was by that time come out of my room; so he tells me the minister was below, and that he had talked with him, and that upon showing him the license, he was free to marry us with all his heart, “but he asks to see you”; so he asked if I would let him come up.", "그가 면허장을 꺼냈다. \"되었습니다.\" 목사가 말했다. \"신부는 어디 계십니까?\" \"곧 보시게 될 겁니다.\" 내 신사가 말했다. 이렇게 말하고 그는 위층으로 올라왔는데, 그때쯤 나는 이미 내 방에서 나와 있었다. 그래서 그는 내게 목사가 아래층에 와 있으며, 자기가 그와 이야기를 나누었고, 면허장을 보여 주자 기꺼이 우리의 혼인을 주관하겠다고 했으나 \"당신을 한번 뵙기를 청한다\"고 전했다. 그러면서 그를 올라오게 해도 되겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["“’Tis time enough,”", "\"내일 아침이라도 늦지 않잖아요?\""], ["said I, “in the morning, is it not?”", "내가 말했다. \"그게 말이오, 여보.\""], ["“Why,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “my dear, he seemed to scruple whether it was not some young girl stolen from her parents, and I assured him we were both of age to command our own consent; and that made him ask to see you.”", "\"그자가 혹시 부모에게서 빼내 온 어린 처녀가 아닌가 미심쩍어하는 눈치라, 우리 둘 다 스스로 동의할 수 있는 나이라고 장담해 두었소. 그래서 당신을 보자고 한 거요.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["said I, “do as you please”; so up they brings the parson, and a merry, good sort of gentleman he was.", "내가 말했다. \"좋으실 대로 하세요.\""], ["He had been told, it seems, that we had met there by accident, that I came in the Chester coach, and my gentleman in his own coach to meet me; that we were to have met last night at Stony-Stratford, but that he could not reach so far.", "그리하여 목사를 올려보냈는데, 그는 쾌활하고 사람 좋은 신사였다. 듣자 하니 그는 우리가 이곳에서 우연히 만났으며, 내가 체스터 마차를 타고 왔고 내 신사는 나를 맞으러 자기 마차를 타고 왔으며, 어젯밤 스토니스트랫퍼드에서 만나기로 했으나 그가 거기까지 닿지 못했다는 이야기를 들었던 모양이다."], ["“Well, sir,”", "\"허허, 선생.\""], ["says the parson, “every ill turn has some good in it.", "목사가 말했다. \"모든 궂은일에는 좋은 구석이 있는 법이지요."], ["The disappointment, sir,”", "차질은 선생 몫이었으나,\""], ["says he to my gentleman, “was yours, and the good turn is mine, for if you had met at Stony-Stratford I had not had the honour to marry you.", "그가 내 신사를 향해 말했다. \"그 덕은 제 차지가 되었으니, 만약 두 분이 스토니스트랫퍼드에서 만나셨더라면 제가 혼례를 주관하는 영광을 누리지 못했을 테니 말입니다."], ["Landlord, have you a Common Prayer Book?”", "주인장, 공동기도서 한 권 있소?\""]], [["I started as if I had been frightened. “Lord, sir,” says I, “what do you mean? What, to marry in an inn, and at night too?” “Madam,” says the minister, “if you will have it be in the church, you shall; but I assure you your marriage will be as firm here as in the church; we are not tied by the canons to marry nowhere but in the church; and if you will have it in the church, it will be a public as a county fair; and as for the time of day, it does not at all weigh in this case; our princes are married in their chambers, and at eight or ten o’clock at night.”", "나는 깜짝 놀란 듯이 움찔했다. \"어머, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. \"무슨 말씀이세요? 여관에서, 그것도 밤에 혼례를 올리다니요?\" \"부인.\" 목사가 말했다. \"교회에서 올리시겠다면 그리하셔도 됩니다. 허나 여기서 올리는 혼인도 교회에서 올리는 것만큼 굳건하다는 것을 장담하지요. 교회법이 우리더러 교회 밖에서는 혼인을 주관하지 말라고 묶어 둔 게 아닙니다. 교회에서 올리시겠다면, 그것은 마치 고을 장날처럼 떠들썩한 공개 행사가 될 것입니다. 시각으로 말하자면, 이 경우에는 전혀 문제 될 게 없습니다. 우리 군주들도 밤 여덟 시나 열 시에 침소에서 혼례를 올리니까요.\""]], [["I was a great while before I could be persuaded, and pretended not to be willing at all to be married but in the church.", "나는 한참 동안 좀처럼 설득되지 않았고, 교회가 아니면 도무지 혼인할 마음이 없는 척했다."], ["But it was all grimace; so I seemed at last to be prevailed on, and my landlord and his wife and daughter were called up.", "그러나 그것은 죄다 시늉에 지나지 않았으니, 마침내 나는 못 이기는 척 넘어가는 듯했고, 주인 내외와 그 딸이 불려 올라왔다."], ["My landlord was father and clerk and all together, and we were married, and very merry we were; though I confess the self-reproaches which I had upon me before lay close to me, and extorted every now and then a deep sigh from me, which my bridegroom took notice of, and endeavoured to encourage me, thinking, poor man, that I had some little hesitations at the step I had taken so hastily.", "주인은 아버지 노릇이며 서기 노릇이며 죄다 한 몸으로 겸했고, 우리는 혼례를 올렸으며, 더없이 즐거웠다. 다만 고백하건대 전부터 나를 짓누르던 자책감이 바짝 들러붙어 이따금 깊은 한숨을 자아냈는데, 신랑은 그것을 알아채고 나를 다독이려 애쓰면서, 가엾게도 내가 그토록 성급하게 내디딘 걸음에 작은 망설임을 품고 있다고 여겼다."]], [["We enjoyed ourselves that evening completely, and yet all was kept so private in the inn that not a servant in the house knew of it, for my landlady and her daughter waited on me, and would not let any of the maids come upstairs, except while we were at supper.", "그날 저녁 우리는 더할 나위 없이 흡족하게 보냈고, 그러면서도 여관 안에서 모든 것이 어찌나 은밀하게 지켜졌던지 집 안의 하인 누구도 그 일을 알지 못했으니, 주인 아내와 그 딸이 나를 시중들면서 우리가 저녁을 드는 동안을 빼고는 하녀 누구도 위층에 올라오지 못하게 했기 때문이다."], ["My landlady’s daughter I called my bridesmaid; and sending for a shopkeeper the next morning, I gave the young woman a good suit of knots, as good as the town would afford, and finding it was a lace-making town, I gave her mother a piece of bone-lace for a head.", "나는 주인집 딸을 신부 들러리라 불렀고, 이튿날 아침 가게 주인을 불러 그 처녀에게 고을에서 구할 수 있는 가장 좋은 매듭 장식 한 벌을 사 주었으며, 이곳이 레이스 만드는 고을임을 알고서 그 어미에게는 머리에 두를 본레이스 한 조각을 주었다."]], [["One reason that my landlord was so close was, that he was unwilling the minister of the parish should hear of it; but for all that somebody heard of it, so at that we had the bells set a-ringing the next morning early, and the music, such as the town would afford, under our window; but my landlord brazened it out, that we were married before we came thither, only that, being his former guests, we would have our wedding-supper at his house.", "주인이 그토록 입을 다물었던 까닭 하나는, 교구 목사가 그 일을 듣지 않기를 바랐기 때문이다. 그러나 그런데도 누군가 그 소문을 들었던지, 이튿날 이른 아침 종을 울리게 했고, 고을에서 구할 수 있는 만큼의 악대가 우리 창 아래에서 연주를 했다. 그러나 주인은 뻔뻔스레 우리가 이곳에 오기 전에 이미 혼인한 사이이며, 다만 자기네 옛 손님인지라 혼례 만찬을 자기 집에서 든 것뿐이라고 둘러댔다."]], [["We could not find in our hearts to stir the next day; for, in short, having been disturbed by the bells in the morning, and having perhaps not slept overmuch before, we were so sleepy afterwards that we lay in bed till almost twelve o’clock.", "우리는 도저히 이튿날 떠날 마음이 나지 않았으니, 한마디로 아침에 종소리에 시달린 데다 아마 그 전에 잠을 그리 많이 못 잤던 터라, 그 뒤로 어찌나 졸렸던지 거의 정오까지 침대에 누워 있었다."]], [["I begged my landlady that we might not have any more music in the town, nor ringing of bells, and she managed it so well that we were very quiet; but an odd passage interrupted all my mirth for a good while.", "나는 주인 아내에게 고을에 더 이상 음악도 종소리도 없게 해 달라고 청했고, 그녀가 일을 어찌나 잘 처리했던지 우리는 매우 조용히 지낼 수 있었다. 그러나 묘한 사건 하나가 한동안 내 즐거움을 송두리째 망쳐 놓았다."], ["The great room of the house looked into the street, and my new spouse being belowstairs, I had walked to the end of the room; and it being a pleasant, warm day, I had opened the window, and was standing at it for some air, when I saw three gentlemen come by on horseback and go into an inn just against us.", "집의 큰 방은 거리를 내려다보았는데, 새 남편이 아래층에 있던 차에 나는 방 끝까지 걸어가 보았고, 따뜻하고 화창한 날이라 창문을 열고 바람을 쐬려고 거기 서 있다가, 신사 셋이 말을 타고 지나와 우리 맞은편 여관으로 들어가는 것을 보았다."]], [["It was not to be concealed, nor was it so doubtful as to leave me any room to question it, but the second of the three was my Lancashire husband.", "그것은 감출 수도 없었고, 의심하여 따져 볼 여지를 남길 만큼 모호하지도 않았으니, 그 셋 중 둘째가 바로 내 랭커셔 남편이었다. 나는 기절할 만큼 놀랐다."], ["I was frightened to death; I never was in such a consternation in my life; I though I should have sunk into the ground; my blood ran chill in my veins, and I trembled as if I had been in a cold fit of ague.", "평생 그런 경악에 빠져 본 적이 없었다. 땅속으로 꺼져 버리는 것만 같았고, 핏줄 속의 피가 싸늘하게 식었으며, 학질로 오한이 든 사람처럼 부들부들 떨렸다."], ["I say, there was no room to question the truth of it; I knew his clothes, I knew his horse, and I knew his face.", "거듭 말하거니와 그 사실을 의심할 여지가 없었으니, 나는 그의 옷을 알아보았고, 그의 말을 알아보았으며, 그의 얼굴을 알아보았다."]], [["The first sensible reflect I made was, that my husband was not by to see my disorder, and that I was very glad of it.", "내가 맨 처음 떠올린 분별 있는 생각은, 남편이 곁에 없어 내가 흐트러진 꼴을 보지 못한다는 것이었고, 나는 그것이 무척 다행스러웠다."], ["The gentlemen had not been long in the house but they came to the window of their room, as is usual; but my window was shut, you may be sure.", "신사들은 집에 든 지 얼마 되지 않아 으레 그러하듯 자기네 방 창가로 다가왔는데, 짐작하다시피 내 창문은 닫혀 있었다."], ["However, I could not keep from peeping at them, and there I saw him again, heard him call out to one of the servants of the house for something he wanted, and received all the terrifying confirmations of its being the same person that were possible to be had.", "그래도 나는 그들을 훔쳐보지 않을 수 없었고, 거기서 나는 다시 그를 보았으며, 그가 집 하인 하나를 불러 무언가 필요한 것을 청하는 소리를 들었고, 그가 바로 그 사람이라는 두려운 확증을 얻을 수 있는 한 죄다 얻고야 말았다."]], [["My next concern was to know, if possible, what was his business there; but that was impossible.", "그다음 내 관심사는 가능하다면 그가 그곳에 무슨 볼일로 왔는지를 아는 것이었으나, 그것은 불가능했다."], ["Sometimes my imagination formed an idea of one frightful thing, sometimes of another; sometimes I thought he had discovered me, and was come to upbraid me with ingratitude and breach of honour; and every moment I fancied he was coming up the stairs to insult me; and innumerable fancies came into my head of what was never in his head, nor ever could be, unless the devil had revealed it to him.", "때로 내 상상은 어떤 무시무시한 일을 그려 냈다가, 때로는 또 다른 일을 그려 냈다. 때로 나는 그가 나를 알아채고 배은망덕과 신의 저버림을 나무라러 온 것이라고 생각했고, 시시각각 그가 나를 모욕하러 계단을 올라오는 것만 같았으며, 그의 머릿속에는 도무지 들어 있지도 않았고 들어 있을 수도 없는—악마가 그에게 일러 주지 않은 다음에야—온갖 망상이 내 머릿속에 떠올랐다."]], [["I remained in this fright nearly two hours, and scarce ever kept my eye from the window or door of the inn where they were.", "나는 거의 두 시간 동안 이런 공포에 사로잡혀, 그들이 든 여관의 창문이나 문에서 좀처럼 눈을 떼지 못했다."], ["At last, hearing a great clatter in the passage of their inn, I ran to the window, and, to my great satisfaction, saw them all three go out again and travel on westward.", "마침내 그들 여관의 통로에서 요란한 소리가 들리기에 나는 창가로 달려갔고, 더없이 흡족하게도 셋이 모두 다시 나와 서쪽으로 길을 떠나는 것을 보았다."], ["Had they gone towards London, I should have been still in a fright, lest I should meet him on the road again, and that he should know me; but he went the contrary way, and so I was eased of that disorder.", "만약 그들이 런던 쪽으로 갔더라면, 나는 길에서 그를 다시 마주쳐 그가 나를 알아볼까 봐 여전히 공포에 떨었을 것이나, 그는 반대쪽으로 갔고, 그리하여 나는 그 흐트러짐에서 놓여났다."]], [["We resolved to be going the next day, but about six o’clock at night we were alarmed with a great uproar in the street, and people riding as if they had been out of their wits; and what was it but a hue-and-cry after three highwaymen that had robbed two coaches and some other travellers near Dunstable Hill, and notice had, it seems, been given that they had been seen at Brickhill at such a house, meaning the house where those gentlemen had been.", "우리는 이튿날 떠나기로 했으나, 밤 여섯 시쯤 거리에서 일어난 엄청난 소란에 화들짝 놀랐으니, 사람들이 마치 정신 나간 사람들처럼 말을 몰고 다녔다. 알고 보니 그것은 던스터블 언덕 근처에서 마차 두 대와 다른 길손 몇몇을 턴 노상강도 셋을 쫓는 추적의 외침이었고, 듣자 하니 그들이 브릭힐의 어느 집에서 목격되었다는 제보가 있었다는데, 바로 그 신사들이 들었던 집을 가리킨 것이었다."]], [["The house was immediately beset and searched, but there were witnesses enough that the gentlemen had been gone over three hours.", "그 집은 즉시 포위되어 수색당했으나, 그 신사들이 세 시간도 더 전에 떠났다는 증인이 충분했다."], ["The crowd having gathered about, we had the news presently; and I was heartily concerned now another way.", "군중이 몰려든 덕분에 우리는 곧 소식을 들었고, 나는 이제 또 다른 까닭으로 진심으로 마음이 쓰였다."], ["I presently told the people of the house, that I durst to say those were not the persons, for that I knew one of the gentlemen to be a very honest person, and of a good estate in Lancashire.", "나는 곧장 집 사람들에게, 그들이 범인이 아닐 거라고 감히 말할 수 있다고, 왜냐하면 그 신사들 중 한 사람이 매우 정직한 사람이며 랭커셔에 좋은 재산을 지닌 이임을 알기 때문이라고 일렀다."]], [["The constable who came with the hue-and-cry was immediately informed of this, and came over to me to be satisfied from my own mouth, and I assured him that I saw the three gentlemen as I was at the window; that I saw them afterwards at the windows of the room they dined in; that I saw them afterwards take horse, and I could assure him I knew one of them to be such a man, that he was a gentleman of a very good estate, and an undoubted character in Lancashire, from whence I was just now upon my journey.", "추적의 외침을 따라온 치안관은 즉시 이 말을 전해 듣고, 내 입으로 직접 확인하고자 나에게로 건너왔다. 나는 그에게, 창가에 있을 때 신사 셋을 보았고, 그 뒤에 그들이 식사한 방의 창가에서도 보았으며, 그 뒤에 그들이 말에 오르는 것까지 보았다고, 그리고 그들 중 한 사람이 바로 그런 사람—매우 좋은 재산을 지닌 신사이며 랭커셔에서 의심할 바 없는 평판을 지닌 이로, 내가 바로 지금 그곳에서 여행해 오는 길이라고—임을 분명히 안다고 장담했다."]], [["The assurance with which I delivered this gave the mob gentry a check, and gave the constable such satisfaction, that he immediately sounded a retreat, told his people these were not the men, but that he had an account they were very honest gentlemen; and so they went all back again.", "내가 이 말을 전한 그 단호함이 폭도 무리에게 제동을 걸었고, 치안관에게도 큰 확신을 주었던지, 그는 즉시 후퇴 신호를 울려 사람들에게 이들은 그 범인이 아니며 도리어 매우 정직한 신사들이라는 정보를 얻었노라 일렀고, 그리하여 그들은 모두 다시 돌아갔다."], ["What the truth of the matter was I knew not, but certain it was that the coaches were robbed at Dunstable Hill, and £560 in money taken; besides, some of the lace merchants that always travel that way had been visited too.", "이 일의 진상이 어떠했는지는 알 수 없으나, 던스터블 언덕에서 마차들이 털리고 현금 560파운드를 빼앗긴 것만은 분명했으며, 그 밖에 늘 그 길로 다니는 레이스 상인들도 봉변을 당했다."], ["As to the three gentlemen, that remains to be explained hereafter.", "그 신사 셋에 관해서는 뒤에 가서 밝혀질 일로 남겨 둔다."]], [["Well, this alarm stopped us another day, though my spouse was for travelling, and told me that it was always safest travelling after a robbery, for that the thieves were sure to be gone far enough off when they had alarmed the country; but I was afraid and uneasy, and indeed principally lest my old acquaintance should be upon the road still, and should chance to see me.", "그래, 이 소동 때문에 우리는 또 하루를 묶이고 말았으니, 내 남편은 떠나자는 쪽이었고, 강도 사건이 난 뒤에 여행하는 것이 늘 가장 안전하다고, 도둑들은 한 지방을 발칵 뒤집어 놓은 뒤에는 으레 멀리 달아나 버리기 마련이라고 내게 일렀다. 그러나 나는 겁이 나고 마음이 불안했으니, 사실 무엇보다 내 옛 정인이 아직 길에 있다가 어쩌다 나를 보게 될까 봐서였다."]], [["I never lived four pleasanter days together in my life.", "내 평생 그토록 즐거운 나흘을 연달아 보낸 적이 없었다."], ["I was a mere bride all this while, and my new spouse strove to make me entirely easy in everything.", "이 내내 나는 영락없는 신부였고, 새 남편은 모든 면에서 나를 더없이 편안하게 해 주려 애썼다."], ["Oh could this state of life have continued, how had all my past troubles been forgot, and my future sorrows avoided!", "아, 이 삶의 형편이 그대로 이어질 수만 있었다면, 지난날의 온갖 곤경이 죄다 잊히고 앞날의 슬픔도 면할 수 있었으련만!"], ["But I had a past life of a most wretched kind to account for, some of it in this world as well as in another.", "그러나 나에게는 가장 비참한 종류의 지난 삶이 있어 셈을 치러야 했으니, 그중 더러는 저세상에서뿐 아니라 이 세상에서도 마찬가지였다."]], [["We came away the fifth day; and my landlord, because he saw me uneasy, mounted himself, his son, and three honest country fellows with good firearms, and, without telling us of it, followed the coach, and would see us safe into Dunstable.", "우리는 닷새째 되는 날 떠났다. 주인은 내가 불안해하는 것을 보았던지 자기 자신과 아들, 그리고 좋은 총기를 갖춘 정직한 시골뜨기 셋을 말에 태우고, 우리에게는 알리지도 않은 채 마차를 뒤따라와 우리가 던스터블까지 무사히 가는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["We could do no less than treat them very handsomely at Dunstable, which cost my spouse about ten or twelve shillings, and something he gave the men for their time too, but my landlord would take nothing for himself.", "우리로서는 던스터블에서 그들을 후하게 대접하지 않을 수 없었으니, 그것이 남편에게 한 열에서 열두 실링쯤 들었고, 사람들에게는 시간을 내준 값으로 얼마간 더 주었으나, 주인은 자기 몫으로는 아무것도 받으려 하지 않았다."]], [["This was the most happy contrivance for me that could have fallen out; for had I come to London unmarried, I must either have come to him for the first night’s entertainment, or have discovered to him that I had not one acquaintance in the whole city of London that could receive a poor bride for the first night’s lodging with her spouse.", "이것은 나에게 일어날 수 있는 가장 다행스러운 방책이었으니, 만약 내가 혼인하지 않은 채 런던에 왔더라면, 첫날밤 묵을 곳을 그에게 청하든가, 아니면 런던 시내 전체에 신부가 남편과 함께 첫날밤 묵을 곳을 마련해 줄 만한 아는 사람이 단 하나도 없다는 것을 그에게 털어놓아야 했을 것이다."], ["But now, being an old married woman, I made no scruple of going directly home with him, and there I took possession at once of a house well furnished, and a husband in very good circumstances, so that I had a prospect of a very happy life, if I knew how to manage it; and I had leisure to consider of the real value of the life I was likely to live.", "그러나 이제 나는 결혼한 지 오래된 부인인지라 거리낌 없이 곧장 그와 함께 집으로 갔고, 거기서 단번에 잘 꾸며진 집과 매우 좋은 형편의 남편을 차지하게 되어, 꾸려 갈 줄만 안다면 매우 행복한 삶의 전망을 갖게 되었으며, 내가 살아가게 될 삶의 진정한 가치를 곰곰이 헤아릴 여유도 생겼다."], ["How different it was to be from the loose ungoverned part I had acted before, and how much happier a life of virtue and sobriety is, than that which we call a life of pleasure.", "그 삶이 내가 전에 연기했던 방종하고 절제 없는 부분과 얼마나 달랐는지, 그리고 덕스럽고 절제 있는 삶이 우리가 쾌락의 삶이라 부르는 것보다 얼마나 더 행복한지를."]], [["Oh had this particular scene of life lasted, or had I learned from that time I enjoyed it, to have tasted the true sweetness of it, and had I not fallen into that poverty which is the sure bane of virtue, how happy had I been, not only here, but perhaps for ever!", "아, 이 한 시절의 삶이 이어졌더라면, 혹은 내가 그것을 누리던 그때부터 그 참된 달콤함을 맛볼 줄 배웠더라면, 그리고 덕의 확실한 독약인 그 가난에 빠지지 않았더라면, 나는 여기서뿐 아니라 어쩌면 영원토록 얼마나 행복했을 것인가!"], ["for while I lived thus, I was really a penitent for all my life past.", "이렇게 사는 동안 나는 정말로 지난 삶 전체를 뉘우치는 참회자였으니까."], ["I looked back on it with abhorrence, and might truly be said to hate myself for it.", "나는 그 삶을 혐오스럽게 돌아보았고, 그로 인해 진정 나 자신을 미워했다고 해도 좋을 것이다."], ["I often reflected how my lover at the Bath, struck at the hand of God, repented and abandoned me, and refused to see me any more, though he loved me to an extreme; but I, prompted by that worst of devils, poverty, returned to the vile practice, and made the advantage of what they call a handsome face to be the relief to my necessities, and beauty be a pimp to vice.", "나는 바스에서의 내 정인이 신의 손에 일격을 맞고 뉘우쳐 나를 버렸으며, 나를 극진히 사랑했으면서도 더는 보려 하지 않았던 일을 자주 떠올렸다. 그러나 나는 그 악마들 가운데 가장 고약한 가난에 부추김을 받아 그 비열한 짓으로 되돌아갔고, 사람들이 곱다고 하는 얼굴을 밑천 삼아 내 궁핍을 메우고, 미모로 하여금 악덕의 포주 노릇을 하게 했던 것이다."]], [["Now I seemed landed in a safe harbour, after the stormy voyage of life past was at an end, and I began to be thankful for my deliverance.", "이제 나는 지난 삶의 험한 항해가 끝나고 안전한 항구에 닿은 듯했고, 내가 건짐받은 것에 감사하기 시작했다."], ["I sat many an hour by myself, and wept over the remembrance of past follies, and the dreadful extravagances of a wicked life, and sometimes I flattered myself that I had sincerely repented.", "나는 혼자서 여러 시간을 앉아, 지난날의 어리석음과 사악한 삶의 끔찍한 방탕을 떠올리며 울었고, 때로는 내가 진심으로 뉘우쳤다고 스스로를 다독였다."]], [["But there are temptations which it is not in the power of human nature to resist, and few know what would be their case if driven to the same exigencies.", "그러나 인간의 본성으로는 도저히 물리칠 수 없는 유혹이 있는 법이며, 똑같은 곤경에 내몰린다면 자기가 어찌 될지 아는 이는 드물다."], ["As covetousness is the root of all evil, so poverty is, I believe, the worst of all snares.", "탐욕이 모든 악의 뿌리이듯이, 가난은 내가 믿건대 모든 덫 가운데 가장 고약한 것이다."], ["But I waive that discourse till I come to an experiment.", "그러나 그 이야기는 한 실례를 들 데에 이를 때까지 미뤄 두겠다."]], [["I lived with this husband with the utmost tranquillity; he was a quiet, sensible, sober man; virtuous, modest, sincere, and in his business diligent and just.", "나는 이 남편과 더없는 평온 속에서 살았다. 그는 조용하고 분별 있고 절제 있는 사람이었으며, 덕스럽고 겸손하고 진실했고, 자기 일에 부지런하고 공정했다."], ["His business was in a narrow compass, and his income sufficient to a plentiful way of living in the ordinary way.", "그의 일은 규모가 크지 않았으나, 그 수입은 평범한 방식으로 넉넉하게 살아가기에 충분했다."], ["I do not say to keep an equipage, and make a figure, as the world calls it, nor did I expect it, or desire it; for as I abhorred the levity and extravagance of my former life, so I chose now to live retired, frugal, and within ourselves.", "세상에서 일컫는 대로 마차를 거느리고 위세를 부리며 산다는 말은 아니며, 나 또한 그것을 기대하지도 바라지도 않았으니, 지난 삶의 경박함과 방탕을 혐오한 만큼 이제는 은둔하여 검소하게, 우리끼리만 살기를 택했기 때문이다."], ["I kept no company, made no visits; minded my family, and obliged my husband; and this kind of life became a pleasure to me.", "나는 어울려 다니는 무리도 두지 않고 방문도 하지 않았으며, 집안을 살피고 남편을 받들었다. 이런 식의 삶이 나에게는 즐거움이 되었다."]], [["We lived in an uninterrupted course of ease and content for five years, when a sudden blow from an almost invisible hand blasted all my happiness, and turned me out into the world in a condition the reverse of all that had been before it.", "우리는 다섯 해 동안 끊김 없는 안락과 만족의 길을 걸었는데, 그때 거의 보이지도 않는 손이 가한 갑작스러운 일격이 내 모든 행복을 송두리째 날려 버리고, 나를 그 이전의 모든 것과는 정반대인 처지로 세상에 내던졌다."]], [["My husband having trusted one of his fellow-clerks with a sum of money, too much for our fortunes to bear the loss of, the clerk failed, and the loss fell very heavy on my husband, yet it was not so great neither but that, if he had had spirit and courage to have looked his misfortunes in the face, his credit was so good that, as I told him, he would easily recover it; for to sink under trouble is to double the weight, and he that will die in it, shall die in it.", "남편이 동료 서기 한 사람에게 우리 형편으로는 그 손실을 감당하기에 너무 큰 액수의 돈을 맡겼는데, 그 서기가 파산하는 바람에 그 손실이 남편에게 매우 무겁게 떨어졌다. 그래도 그것이 그토록 엄청난 것은 아니었으니, 만약 그가 자기 불운을 정면으로 마주할 기개와 용기가 있었더라면, 그의 신용은 매우 좋았으므로 내가 그에게 말했듯이 쉽게 회복할 수 있었을 것이다. 곤경에 짓눌려 가라앉는 것은 그 무게를 곱절로 만드는 일이며, 그 안에서 죽으려는 자는 그 안에서 죽고야 마는 법이니까."]], [["It was in vain to speak comfortably to him; the wound had sunk too deep; it was a stab that touched the vitals; he grew melancholy and disconsolate, and from thence lethargic, and died. I foresaw the blow, and was extremely oppressed in my mind, for I saw evidently that if he died I was undone.", "그에게 위로의 말을 건네는 것은 부질없는 일이었다. 상처가 너무 깊이 박혔던 것이다. 그것은 급소를 찌른 일격이었다. 그는 우울하고 의기소침해졌고, 거기서 무기력에 빠져 죽고 말았다. 나는 그 일격을 미리 내다보았고 마음이 극도로 짓눌렸으니, 그가 죽으면 내가 끝장이라는 것을 똑똑히 보았기 때문이다."]], [["I had had two children by him and no more, for, to tell the truth, it began to be time for me to leave bearing children, for I was now eight-and-forty, and I suppose if he had lived I should have had no more.", "나는 그에게서 자식 둘을 보았을 뿐 더는 두지 않았으니, 사실을 말하자면 내가 아이 낳기를 그만둘 때가 되었던 까닭이다. 나는 이제 마흔여덟이었으니, 설령 그가 살았더라도 더는 낳지 못했으리라 짐작한다."]], [["I was now left in a dismal and disconsolate case indeed, and in several things worse than ever.", "나는 이제 정말로 음울하고 의기소침한 처지에, 여러 면에서 그 어느 때보다 못한 형편에 남겨졌다."], ["First, it was past the flourishing time with me when I might expect to be courted for a mistress; that agreeable part had declined some time, and the ruins only appeared of what had been; and that which was worse than all this, that I was the most dejected, disconsolate creature alive.", "첫째로, 정부情婦로 구애받기를 기대할 만한 한창때가 나에게는 이미 지나가 버렸으니, 그 흡족한 부분은 얼마 전부터 기울었고 한때의 모습은 폐허만 남아 있었다. 그리고 이 모든 것보다 더 고약한 것은, 내가 살아 있는 가장 의기소침하고 낙담한 인간이라는 점이었다."], ["I that had encouraged my husband, and endeavoured to support his spirits under his trouble, could not support my own; I wanted that spirit in trouble which I told him was so necessary to him for bearing the burthen.", "남편을 격려하고 그 곤경 속에서 그의 기운을 떠받치려 애썼던 내가, 정작 내 기운은 떠받치지 못했다. 곤경 속에서 그 짐을 견디는 데 그토록 긴요하다고 그에게 일렀던 그 기개가 나에게는 없었던 것이다."]], [["But my case was indeed deplorable, for I was left perfectly friendless and helpless, and the loss my husband had sustained had reduced his circumstances so low, that though indeed I was not in debt, yet I could easily foresee that what was left would not support me long; that while it wasted daily for subsistence, I had not way to increase it one shilling, so that it would be soon all spent, and then I saw nothing before me but the utmost distress; and this represented itself so lively to my thoughts, that it seemed as if it was come, before it was really very near; also my very apprehensions doubled the misery, for I fancied every sixpence that I paid for a loaf of bread was the last that I had in the world, and that to-morrow I was to fast, and be starved to death.", "그러나 내 처지는 정말로 한심했으니, 나는 완전히 벗도 없고 도움도 없이 남겨졌고, 남편이 입은 손실로 그 형편이 어찌나 낮아졌던지, 비록 내가 빚을 진 것은 아니었으나, 남은 것으로는 나를 오래 부양하지 못하리라는 것을 쉽게 내다볼 수 있었다. 그것이 날마다 먹고사느라 줄어드는 동안, 나에게는 그것을 단 한 실링이라도 불릴 길이 없었으니, 머지않아 죄다 써 버릴 것이며, 그러면 내 앞에 보이는 것이라곤 더없이 극심한 곤궁뿐이었다. 그리고 이것이 내 생각 속에 어찌나 생생하게 떠올랐던지, 그것이 실제로는 그리 가깝지도 않은데 마치 벌써 닥쳐온 듯했다. 게다가 바로 그 불안이 비참함을 곱절로 만들었으니, 빵 한 덩이 값으로 치르는 6펜스마다 그것이 이 세상에서 내가 가진 마지막 돈이며, 내일이면 굶고 굶어 죽을 것만 같았다."]], [["In this distress I had no assistant, no friend to comfort or advise me; I sat and cried and tormented myself night and day, wringing my hands, and sometimes raving like a distracted woman; and indeed I have often wondered it had not affected my reason, for I had the vapours to such a degree, that my understanding was sometimes quite lost in fancies and imaginations.", "이 곤경 속에서 나에게는 거들어 줄 이도, 위로하거나 충고해 줄 벗도 없었다. 나는 앉아서 밤낮으로 울며 스스로를 괴롭혔고, 두 손을 쥐어짜며 때로는 실성한 여자처럼 헛소리를 했다. 사실 그것이 내 이성을 망가뜨리지 않은 것이 종종 의아할 정도였으니, 우울증이 어찌나 심했던지 내 분별이 때로는 망상과 상상 속에 아주 빠져 버리곤 했기 때문이다."]], [["I lived two years in this dismal condition, wasting that little I had, weeping continually over my dismal circumstances, and, as it were, only bleeding to death, without the least hope or prospect of help from God or man; and now I had cried too long, and so often, that tears were, as I might say, exhausted, and I began to be desperate, for I grew poor apace.", "나는 이 음울한 형편 속에서 두 해를 살았으니, 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 축내면서 음울한 처지를 두고 끊임없이 울었고, 말하자면 하느님께서든 사람에게서든 도움의 희망이나 전망이라곤 조금도 없이 그저 피 흘리며 죽어 가고 있을 뿐이었다. 이제 나는 너무 오래, 너무 자주 울었던지라 눈물이 말하자면 바닥나 버렸고, 빠르게 가난해지는 통에 절망에 빠지기 시작했다."]], [["For a little relief I had put off my house and took lodgings; and as I was reducing my living, so I sold off most of my goods, which put a little money in my pocket, and I lived near a year upon that, spending very sparingly, and eking things out to the utmost; but still when I looked before me, my very heart would sink within me at the inevitable approach of misery and want. Oh let none read this part without seriously reflecting on the circumstances of a desolate state, and how they would grapple with mere want of friends and want of bread; it will certainly make them think not of sparing what they have only, but of looking up to heaven for support, and of the wise man’s prayer, “Give me not poverty, lest I steal.”", "얼마간의 구제책으로 나는 집을 처분하고 셋방을 얻었다. 살림을 줄여 나가면서 살림살이도 대부분 팔아 치워 호주머니에 약간의 돈을 넣었고, 매우 아껴 쓰며 물건을 끝까지 늘려 가면서 거의 일 년을 그것으로 버텼다. 그러나 앞날을 내다볼 때면 비참함과 궁핍이 어쩔 수 없이 다가오는 통에 그저 가슴이 철렁 내려앉곤 했다. 아, 누구든 황량한 처지의 형편을, 그리고 벗도 빵도 없는 그 적나라한 궁핍과 어떻게 씨름하게 될지를 진지하게 헤아리지 않고서는 이 대목을 읽지 말기를. 그러면 그들은 분명 자기가 가진 것을 아낄 생각만 하는 것이 아니라, 떠받쳐 달라고 하늘을 우러러볼 것이며, 저 현자의 기도를 떠올릴 것이다. \"제게 가난을 주지 마옵소서. 도둑질을 할까 두렵나이다.\""]], [["Let them remember that a time of distress is a time of dreadful temptation, and all the strength to resist is taken away; poverty presses, the soul is made desperate by distress, and what can be done?", "곤경의 때는 무서운 유혹의 때이며, 저항할 모든 힘이 빼앗겨 버린다는 것을 그들이 기억하기를. 가난이 짓누르고, 영혼이 곤경에 절망하게 되니, 무엇을 할 수 있겠는가?"], ["It was one evening, when being brought, as I may say, to the last gasp, I think I may truly say I was distracted and raving, when prompted by I know not what spirit, and, as it were, doing I did not know what or why, I dressed me (for I had still pretty good clothes) and went out.", "어느 날 저녁이었으니, 말하자면 마지막 숨에 이르도록 내몰려, 진정 나는 실성하여 헛소리를 했다고 해도 좋을 그때, 나는 무슨 영靈에 부추김을 받았는지, 말하자면 내가 무엇을 왜 하는지도 모른 채, 옷을 차려입고(나에게는 아직 꽤 좋은 옷이 있었으니) 밖으로 나갔다. 나갈 때 내 머릿속에 무슨 꿍꿍이가 전혀 없었다는 것만은 분명하다."], ["I am very sure I had no manner of design in my head when I went out; I neither knew nor considered where to go, or on what business; but as the devil carried me out and laid his bait for me, so he brought me, to be sure, to the place, for I knew not whither I was going or what I did.", "어디로 갈지, 무슨 볼일로 갈지 알지도 따지지도 않았다. 다만 악마가 나를 끌어내어 미끼를 놓았던지라, 그가 분명 나를 그 자리로 데려갔으니, 내가 어디로 가는지 무엇을 하는지 알지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["Wandering thus about, I knew not whither, I passed by an apothecary’s shop in Leadenhall Street, when I saw lie on a stool just before the counter a little bundle wrapped in a white cloth; beyond it stood a maid-servant with her back to it, looking towards the top of the shop, where the apothecary’s apprentice, as I suppose, was standing upon the counter, with his back also to the door, and a candle in his hand, looking and reaching up to the upper shelf for something he wanted, so that both were engaged mighty earnestly, and nobody else in the shop.", "이렇게 어디로 가는지도 모른 채 떠돌다가 나는 레든홀 거리의 약종상 가게 앞을 지나게 되었는데, 계산대 바로 앞 걸상 위에 흰 천에 싸인 작은 보따리가 놓인 것이 보였다. 그 너머에는 하녀 하나가 등을 돌리고 서서 가게 위쪽을 바라보고 있었는데, 거기서는 짐작건대 약종상 견습공이 계산대 위에 올라서서 역시 문을 등진 채 손에 촛불을 들고, 필요한 무언가를 찾느라 맨 위 선반을 올려다보며 손을 뻗고 있었다. 그래서 두 사람 다 어찌나 골똘히 매여 있던지, 가게 안에 다른 사람은 아무도 없었다."]], [["This was the bait; and the devil, who I said laid the snare, as readily prompted me as if he had spoke, for I remember, and shall never forget it, ’twas like a voice spoken to me over my shoulder, “Take the bundle; be quick; do it this moment.” It was no sooner said but I stepped into the shop, and with my back to the wench, as if I had stood up for a cart that was going by, I put my hand behind me and took the bundle, and went off with it, the maid or the fellow not perceiving me, or any one else.", "이것이 미끼였다. 그리고 내가 덫을 놓았다고 말한 그 악마가, 마치 입으로 말하기라도 한 듯 선뜻 나를 부추겼으니, 내가 기억하기로—그리고 결코 잊지 못하리니—그것은 마치 내 어깨 너머로 들려오는 목소리 같았다. \"보따리를 집어라. 어서. 지금 당장 해치워라.\" 말이 떨어지기가 무섭게 나는 가게 안으로 들어섰고, 마치 지나가는 짐수레를 피해 비켜선 듯이 그 계집애에게 등을 돌린 채 손을 뒤로 돌려 보따리를 집어 들고는 그것을 들고 빠져나왔으니, 하녀도 그 녀석도, 다른 누구도 나를 알아채지 못했다."]], [["It is impossible to express the horror of my soul all the while I did it.", "그 일을 하는 내내 내 영혼의 공포가 어떠했는지는 이루 말할 수 없다."], ["When I went away I had no heart to run, or scarce to mend my pace.", "빠져나올 때 나는 뛸 엄두도, 걸음을 빨리할 엄두도 거의 나지 않았다."], ["I crossed the street indeed, and went down the first turning I came to, and I think it was a street that went through into Fenchurch Street.", "그래도 길을 건너 처음 나오는 길모퉁이로 접어들었는데, 그것은 펜처치 거리로 통하는 길이었던 것 같다. 거기서부터 나는 길을 건너고 어찌나 많은 길과 모퉁이를 돌았던지, 그것이 어느 길이었는지도, 내가 어디로 갔는지도 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["From thence I crossed and turned through so many ways and turnings, that I could never tell which way it was, not where I went; for I felt not the ground I stepped on, and the farther I was out of danger, the faster I went, till, tired and out of breath, I was forced to sit down on a little bench at a door, and then I began to recover, and found I was got into Thames Street, near Billingsgate.", "디딘 땅도 느껴지지 않았고, 위험에서 멀어질수록 나는 더 빨리 걸어, 마침내 지치고 숨이 차서 어느 집 문간의 작은 벤치에 주저앉을 수밖에 없었다. 그제야 나는 정신을 차리기 시작했고, 빌링스게이트 근처 템스 거리에 와 있다는 것을 알았다."], ["I rested me a little and went on; my blood was all in a fire; my heart beat as if I was in a sudden fright.", "나는 잠시 쉬었다가 다시 걸었다. 온몸의 피가 불타올랐고, 갑작스레 놀란 사람처럼 심장이 두근거렸다."], ["In short, I was under such a surprise that I still knew not wither I was going, or what to do.", "한마디로 나는 어찌나 경악에 빠져 있었던지, 여전히 어디로 가는지도, 무엇을 해야 할지도 알지 못했다."]], [["After I had tired myself thus with walking a long way about, and so eagerly, I began to consider and make home to my lodging, where I came about nine o’clock at night.", "이렇게 한참을 빙 돌아 그토록 다급하게 걸어 지친 뒤에야, 나는 정신을 차리고 셋방으로 발길을 돌렸고, 밤 아홉 시쯤 거기에 닿았다."]], [["When the bundle was made up for, or on what occasion laid where I found it, I knew not, but when I came to open it I found there was a suit of childbed-linen in it, very good and almost new, the lace very fine; there was a silver porringer of a pint, a small silver mug and six spoons, with some other linen, a good smock, and three silk handkerchiefs, and in the mug, wrapped up in a paper, 18s.", "그 보따리가 언제 꾸려진 것인지, 무슨 일로 내가 발견한 그 자리에 놓였던 것인지는 알 수 없었으나, 풀어 보니 그 안에는 매우 좋고 거의 새것인 산모용 아마포 한 벌이 들어 있었고, 레이스가 매우 고왔다."], ["6d. in money.", "1파인트들이 은 죽그릇 하나, 작은 은잔 하나와 숟가락 여섯 개, 그 밖에 아마포 약간, 좋은 속옷 한 벌과 비단 손수건 세 장이 있었으며, 그 잔 안에는 종이에 싼 현금 18실링 6펜스가 들어 있었다."]], [["All the while I was opening these things I was under such dreadful impressions of fear, and I such terror of mind, though I was perfectly safe, that I cannot express the manner of it.", "이 물건들을 풀어 보는 내내 나는 너무도 무서운 두려움에 사로잡혀, 비록 완전히 안전한데도 마음에 어찌나 큰 공포가 일었던지 그 모양을 이루 표현할 수가 없다."], ["I sat me down, and cried most vehemently.", "나는 주저앉아 더없이 격렬하게 울었다. \"맙소사.\""], ["“Lord,”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I, “what am I now?", "\"이제 나는 무엇인가?"], ["a thief!", "도둑이라니!"], ["Why, I shall be taken next time, and be carried to Newgate and be tried for my life!”", "아이고, 다음번에는 붙들려 뉴게이트로 끌려가 목숨을 걸고 재판을 받겠지!\""], ["And with that I cried again a long time, and I am sure, as poor as I was, if I had durst for fear, I would certainly have carried the things back again; but that went off after a while.", "그러고 나서 다시 오래 울었으니, 분명 나는 그토록 가난했음에도 두려움 때문에 엄두만 났더라면 틀림없이 그 물건들을 도로 가져갔을 것이다. 그러나 그것도 한참 뒤에는 사그라졌다."], ["Well, I went to bed for that night, but slept little; the horror of the fact was upon my mind, and I knew not what I said or did all night, and all the next day.", "그래, 그날 밤 나는 잠자리에 들었으나 거의 잠을 이루지 못했다. 그 짓의 공포가 내 마음을 짓눌렀고, 나는 밤새도록, 그리고 이튿날 온종일 내가 무슨 말을 하고 무엇을 했는지 알지 못했다."], ["Then I was impatient to hear some news of the loss; and would fain know how it was, whether they were a poor body’s goods, or a rich.", "그러고 나서 나는 그 분실에 관한 소식을 듣고 싶어 안달이 났고, 그것이 어떻게 된 일인지, 그것이 가난한 사람의 물건인지 부자의 물건인지 몹시 알고 싶었다. \"어쩌면,\""], ["“Perhaps,”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I, “it may be some poor widow like me, that had packed up these goods to go and sell them for a little bread for herself and a poor child, and are now starving and breaking their hearts for want of that little they would have fetched.”", "\"그건 나 같은 어느 가난한 과부의 것인지도 몰라. 자기와 가엾은 자식의 빵 한 조각을 위해 이 물건들을 꾸려 팔러 가던 길에, 이제 그 얼마 안 되는 것을 잃고 그것 때문에 굶주리며 애간장을 태우고 있는지도 모르지.\""], ["And this thought tormented me worse than all the rest, for three or four days’ time.", "그리고 이 생각이 서너 날 동안 나를 다른 무엇보다 더 괴롭혔다."]], [["But my own distresses silenced all these reflections, and the prospect of my own starving, which grew every day more frightful to me, hardened my heart by degrees.", "그러나 나 자신의 곤경이 이 모든 성찰을 잠재웠고, 날마다 더 무서워지는 내 굶주림의 전망이 차츰차츰 내 마음을 굳게 만들었다. 그때 특히 내 마음을 무겁게 짓누른 것은, 내가 한때 거듭났고 바라건대 지난날의 온갖 사악함을 뉘우쳤으며, 여러 해 동안 절제 있고 진중하고 은둔하는 삶을 살았는데, 이제는 내 처지의 무서운 궁핍에 떠밀려 영혼과 육체가 다 함께 파멸의 문으로 내몰리게 되었다는 것이었다."], ["It was then particularly heavy upon my mind, that I had been reformed, and had, as I hoped, repented of all my past wickedness; that I had lived a sober, grave, retired life for several years, but now I should be driven by the dreadful necessity of my circumstances to the gates of destruction, soul and body; and two or three times I fell upon my knees, praying to God, as well as I could, for deliverance; but I cannot but say, my prayers had no hope in them.", "두세 번 나는 무릎을 꿇고 힘닿는 대로 하느님께 건져 주시기를 빌었으나, 고백건대 내 기도에는 아무런 희망이 담겨 있지 않았다. 나는 어찌해야 할지 알지 못했다."], ["I knew not what to do; it was all fear without, and dark within; and I reflected on my past life as not sincerely repented of, that Heaven was now beginning to punish me on this side of the grave, and would make me as miserable as I had been wicked.", "밖은 온통 두려움이요, 안은 캄캄했다. 나는 지난 삶을 진심으로 뉘우치지 못한 것으로 돌아보았고, 이제 하늘이 무덤 이편에서 나를 벌하기 시작했으며, 내가 사악했던 만큼 나를 비참하게 만들려 하신다고 헤아렸다."]], [["Had I gone on here I had perhaps been a true penitent; but I had an evil counsellor within, and he was continually prompting me to relieve myself by the worst means; so one evening he tempted me again, by the same wicked impulse that had said “Take that bundle,”", "여기서 멈추었더라면 나는 어쩌면 참된 참회자가 되었을 것이다. 그러나 내 안에는 사악한 조언자가 있어, 끊임없이 가장 고약한 수단으로 스스로를 구제하라고 부추겼다."], ["to go out again and seek for what might happen.", "그리하여 어느 날 저녁 그는 \"저 보따리를 집어라\"고 했던 바로 그 사악한 충동으로 다시 나를 꾀어, 무슨 일이 닥치는지 다시 나가 찾아보게 했다."]], [["I went out now by daylight, and wandered about I knew not whither, and in search of I knew not what, when the devil put a snare in my way of a dreadful nature indeed, and such a one as I have never had before or since.", "이번에 나는 대낮에 나가, 어디로 가는지도 모른 채, 무엇을 찾는지도 모른 채 떠돌았는데, 그때 악마가 내 길에 정말로 무서운 종류의 덫을, 전에도 그 뒤로도 결코 겪어 보지 못한 그런 덫을 놓았다."], ["Going through Aldersgate Street, there was a pretty little child who had been at a dancing-school, and was going home, all alone; and my prompter, like a true devil, set me upon this innocent creature.", "올더스게이트 거리를 지나는데, 무용 학교에 갔다가 혼자서 집으로 가던 예쁜 어린아이 하나가 있었고, 내 부추기는 자가 영락없는 악마답게 나를 이 무고한 어린것에게 들이밀었다."], ["I talked to it, and it prattled to me again, and I took it by the hand and led it along till I came to a paved alley that goes into Bartholomew Close, and I led it in there.", "내가 말을 걸자 그것도 나에게 재잘거렸고, 나는 그 손을 잡고 바살러뮤클로스로 통하는 포장된 골목에 이를 때까지 데리고 가다가 그 안으로 이끌었다."], ["The child said that was not its way home.", "아이는 그 길이 집으로 가는 길이 아니라고 했다. 나는 말했다."], ["I said, “Yes, my dear, it is; I’ll show you the way home.”", "\"맞단다, 얘야. 내가 집으로 가는 길을 알려 주마.\""], ["The child had a little necklace on of gold beads, and I had my eye upon that, and in the dark of the alley I stooped, pretending to mend the child’s clog that was loose, and took off her necklace, and the child never felt it, and so led the child on again.", "아이는 금구슬로 된 작은 목걸이를 하고 있었는데, 나는 거기에 눈독을 들이고 있었다. 골목의 어둠 속에서 나는 헐거워진 아이의 나막신을 고쳐 주는 척하며 몸을 숙여 그 목걸이를 벗겨 냈고, 아이는 그것을 전혀 느끼지 못했다."], ["Here, I say, the devil put me upon killing the child in the dark alley, that it might not cry, but the very thought frighted me so that I was ready to drop down; but I turned the child about and bade it go back again, for that was not its way home.", "그리하여 다시 아이를 데리고 갔다. 여기서, 거듭 말하거니와, 악마는 아이가 울지 못하도록 그 어두운 골목에서 죽여 버리라고 나를 부추겼으나, 그 생각만으로도 어찌나 겁이 났던지 나는 그 자리에 쓰러질 뻔했다."], ["The child said, so she would, and I went through into Bartholomew Close, and then turned round to another passage that goes into St.", "그래서 나는 아이를 돌려세워 이쪽은 집으로 가는 길이 아니니 도로 돌아가라고 일렀다. 아이는 그러겠다고 했고, 나는 바살러뮤클로스로 빠져나와 세인트존 거리로 통하는 또 다른 길로 돌아 들어갔다."], ["John Street; then, crossing into Smithfield, went down Chick Lane and into Field Lane to Holborn Bridge, when, mixing with the crowd of people usually passing there, it was not possible to have been found out; and thus I enterprised my second sally into the world.", "그러고는 스미스필드로 건너가 칙 골목을 내려가 필드 골목을 거쳐 홀번 다리에 이르렀는데, 거기서 늘 그곳을 지나는 사람들의 무리에 섞이니 도무지 발각될 수가 없었다. 그리하여 나는 세상으로 나서는 두 번째 출격을 감행했다."]], [["The thoughts of this booty put out all the thoughts of the first, and the reflections I had made wore quickly off; poverty, as I have said, hardened my heart, and my own necessities made me regardless of anything.", "이 노획물 생각이 첫 번째 일에 대한 생각을 죄다 몰아냈고, 내가 했던 성찰도 금세 닳아 없어졌다. 앞서 말했듯이 가난이 내 마음을 굳게 만들었고, 나 자신의 궁핍이 무엇에도 아랑곳하지 않게 했다."], ["The last affair left no great concern upon me, for as I did the poor child no harm, I only said to myself, I had given the parents a just reproof for their negligence in leaving the poor little lamb to come home by itself, and it would teach them to take more care of it another time.", "이 마지막 일은 나에게 큰 거리낌을 남기지 않았으니, 그 가엾은 아이에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았던지라, 나는 그저 그 가엾은 어린양을 혼자 집에 가게 내버려 둔 부모의 부주의에 정당한 책망을 내린 것뿐이며, 그것이 다음번에는 아이를 더 잘 돌보도록 그들을 가르치리라고 혼자 되뇌었다."]], [["This string of beads was worth about twelve or fourteen pounds.", "이 구슬 줄은 한 열두 파운드에서 열네 파운드쯤 나가는 것이었다."], ["I suppose it might have been formerly the mother’s, for it was too big for the child’s wear, but that perhaps the vanity of the mother, to have her child look fine at the dancing-school, had made her let the child wear it; and no doubt the child had a maid sent to take care of it, but she, careless jade, was taken up perhaps with some fellow that had met her by the way, and so the poor baby wandered till it fell into my hands.", "짐작건대 그것은 본디 어미의 것이었을 텐데, 아이가 차기에는 너무 컸으니, 아마 무용 학교에서 제 아이를 곱게 보이려는 어미의 허영이 아이에게 그것을 차게 했을 것이다. 분명 아이에게는 돌보라고 딸려 보낸 하녀가 있었을 테지만, 그 칠칠치 못한 계집은 어쩌면 길에서 만난 어떤 사내에게 정신이 팔려 있었을 것이고, 그래서 그 가엾은 아기가 떠돌다가 내 손에 떨어진 것이다."]], [["However, I did the child no harm; I did not so much as fright it, for I had a great many tender thoughts about me yet, and did nothing but what, as I may say, mere necessity drove me to.", "그래도 나는 아이에게 아무 해도 끼치지 않았다. 겁조차 주지 않았으니, 나에게는 아직 다정한 마음이 많이 남아 있었고, 말하자면 순전한 궁핍이 나를 몰아붙인 것 외에는 아무것도 하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["I had a great many adventures after this, but I was young in the business, and did not know how to manage, otherwise than as the devil put things into my head; and indeed he was seldom backward to me.", "이 일이 있은 뒤로 나는 숱한 모험을 겪었으나, 이 일에 아직 풋내기였던지라 악마가 내 머릿속에 일을 집어넣어 주는 것 외에는 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알지 못했다. 사실 그는 좀처럼 나에게 게으르지 않았다."], ["One adventure I had which was very lucky to me.", "한번은 나에게 매우 운 좋은 모험이 있었다."], ["I was going through Lombard Street in the dusk of the evening, just by the end of Three King court, when on a sudden comes a fellow running by me as swift as lightning, and throws a bundle that was in his hand, just behind me, as I stood up against the corner of the house at the turning into the alley.", "어스름한 저녁녘에 롬바드 거리를 지나 스리킹스 마당 끄트머리 바로 옆을 지나는데, 별안간 한 사내가 번개처럼 잽싸게 내 곁을 달려 지나가며, 골목으로 꺾어 드는 모퉁이 집 벽에 기대어 선 내 바로 뒤에 손에 쥐고 있던 보따리 하나를 던졌다. 그것을 던져 넣으면서 그는 \"복 받으시오, 아주머니."], ["Just as he threw it in he said, “God bless you, mistress, let it lie there a little,” and away he runs swift as the wind.", "잠시만 거기 그대로 두시오\"라고 말하고는 바람처럼 잽싸게 달아났다."], ["After him comes two more, and immediately a young fellow without his hat, crying “Stop thief!”", "그 뒤로 둘이 더 쫓아왔고, 곧이어 모자도 안 쓴 젊은 사내가 \"도둑 잡아라!\""], ["and after him two or three more.", "외치며 따라왔으며, 그 뒤로 두셋이 더 따라왔다."], ["They pursued the two last fellows so close, that they were forced to drop what they had got, and one of them was taken into the bargain, and other got off free.", "그들은 뒤의 두 녀석을 어찌나 바짝 쫓았던지, 그 둘은 손에 넣었던 것을 떨굴 수밖에 없었고, 그중 하나는 덤으로 붙잡혔으며, 다른 하나는 무사히 달아났다."]], [["I stood stock-still all this while, till they came back, dragging the poor fellow they had taken, and lugging the things they had found, extremely well satisfied that they had recovered the booty and taken the thief; and thus they passed by me, for I looked only like one who stood up while the crowd was gone.", "나는 이 내내 그 자리에 못 박힌 듯 가만히 서 있었는데, 마침내 그들이 붙잡은 가엾은 녀석을 끌고, 찾아낸 물건들을 질질 끌면서, 노획물을 되찾고 도둑을 잡은 것에 더없이 흡족해하며 돌아왔다. 그리하여 그들은 내 곁을 지나갔으니, 나는 군중이 지나가는 동안 그저 비켜선 사람으로만 보였기 때문이다."]], [["Once or twice I asked what was the matter, but the people neglected answering me, and I was not very importunate; but after the crowd was wholly past, I took my opportunity to turn about and take up what was behind me and walk away.", "한두 번 나는 무슨 일이냐고 물었으나 사람들은 내 물음에 답하지 않았고, 나도 그리 끈질기게 묻지 않았다. 그러나 군중이 완전히 지나간 뒤에 나는 기회를 잡아 몸을 돌려 내 뒤에 있던 것을 집어 들고 걸어가 버렸다."], ["This, indeed, I did with less disturbance than I had done formerly, for these things I did not steal, but they were stolen to my hand.", "이번에는 정말로 전보다 덜 동요하며 했으니, 이 물건들은 내가 훔친 것이 아니라 내 손에 훔쳐다 바쳐진 것이었기 때문이다."], ["I got safe to my lodgings with this cargo, which was a piece of fine black lustring silk, and a piece of velvet; the latter was but part of a piece of about eleven yards; the former was a whole piece of near fifty yards.", "나는 이 짐을 들고 무사히 셋방에 닿았는데, 그것은 고운 검정 광택 명주 한 필과 우단 한 필이었다. 후자는 한 열한 야드쯤 되는 한 필의 일부였고, 전자는 쉰 야드 가까이 되는 온전한 한 필이었다."], ["It seems it was a mercer’s shop that they had rifled.", "듣자 하니 그들이 턴 곳은 어느 포목상 가게였던 모양이다."], ["I say rifled, because the goods were so considerable that they had lost; for the goods that they recovered were pretty many, and I believe came to about six or seven several pieces of silk.", "턴 곳이라 말하는 까닭은, 그들이 잃은 물건이 어찌나 상당했던지, 되찾은 물건만도 꽤 많아서 내 생각에 명주 여섯이나 일곱 필쯤 되었기 때문이다."], ["How they came to get so many I could not tell; but as I had only robbed the thief, I made no scruple at taking these goods, and being very glad of them too.", "그들이 어떻게 그토록 많이 손에 넣었는지는 알 수 없으나, 나는 도둑을 털었을 뿐이므로 이 물건들을 취하는 데 아무 거리낌이 없었고, 도리어 매우 기뻐했다."]], [["I had pretty good luck thus far, and I made several adventures more, though with but small purchase, yet with good success, but I went in daily dread that some mischief would befall me, and that I should certainly come to be hanged at last. The impression this made on me was too strong to be slighted, and it kept me from making attempts that, for ought I knew, might have been very safely performed; but one thing I cannot omit, which was a bait to me many a day. I walked frequently out into the villages round the town, to see if nothing would fall in my way there; and going by a house near Stepney, I saw on the window-board two rings, one a small diamond ring, and the other a gold ring, to be sure laid there by some thoughtless lady, that had more money than forecast, perhaps only till she washed her hands.", "여기까지 나는 꽤 운이 좋았고, 비록 얻는 것은 적었으나 좋은 성과를 거두며 몇 번 더 모험을 했다. 그러나 나는 무슨 화가 닥쳐 끝내는 틀림없이 교수형을 당하게 되리라는 두려움 속에 날마다 살았다. 이것이 내게 남긴 인상은 너무 강해서 가벼이 넘길 수가 없었으니, 그것이 어쩌면 매우 안전하게 해치울 수 있었을지도 모를 시도들을 못 하게 막았다. 그러나 한 가지는 빼놓을 수 없으니, 그것은 여러 날 동안 나에게 미끼가 되었던 일이다. 나는 마을 둘레의 촌락들로 자주 걸어 나가, 거기서 무언가 내 손에 떨어질 것이 없는지 살피곤 했다. 스테프니 근처의 어느 집을 지나는데, 창문 받침대 위에 반지 두 개가 놓인 것이 보였다. 하나는 작은 다이아몬드 반지였고, 다른 하나는 금반지였는데, 분명 신중함보다 돈이 더 많은 어느 분별없는 부인이, 아마 손을 씻을 동안만 거기에 놓아둔 것이었다."]], [["I walked several times by the window to observe if I could see whether there was anybody in the room or no, and I could see nobody, but still I was not sure.", "나는 그 방에 누가 있는지 없는지 알 수 있을까 하여 창문 옆을 여러 번 걸어 보았으나 아무도 보이지 않았고, 그래도 확신할 수가 없었다."], ["It came presently into my thoughts to rap at the glass, as if I wanted to speak with somebody, and if anybody was there they would be sure to come to the window, and then I would tell them to remove those rings, for that I had seen two suspicious fellows take notice of them.", "곧 유리를 두드려 누군가와 이야기하고 싶은 척하면 되겠다는 생각이 떠올랐으니, 만약 누가 있다면 분명 창가로 나올 테고, 그러면 내가 수상쩍은 사내 둘이 그 반지를 눈여겨보더라고 일러 주며 치우라고 하면 될 일이었다."], ["This was a ready thought.", "이것은 척척 떠오른 생각이었다."], ["I rapped once or twice and nobody came, when, seeing the coast clear, I thrust hard against the square of the glass, and broke it with very little noise, and took out the two rings, and walked away with them very safe.", "한두 번 두드렸으나 아무도 나오지 않았고, 주위에 인기척이 없는 것을 보고 나는 유리창을 향해 세게 밀어 아주 작은 소리만 내며 깨뜨려, 반지 두 개를 꺼내 들고 매우 무사히 걸어가 버렸다."], ["The diamond ring was worth about £3, and the other about 9s.", "다이아몬드 반지는 한 3파운드쯤 나갔고, 다른 하나는 한 9실링쯤 나갔다."]], [["I was now at a loss for a market for my goods, and especially for my two pieces of silk.", "이제 나는 내 물건들을 처분할 시장이, 특히 명주 두 필을 처분할 데가 막막했다."], ["I was very loth to dispose of them for a trifle, as the poor unhappy thieves in general do, who, after they have ventured their lives for perhaps a thing of value, are fain to sell it for a song when they have done; but I was resolved I would not do thus, whatever shift I made, unless I was driven to the last extremity.", "가엾고 불행한 도둑들이 흔히 그러하듯—목숨을 걸고 어쩌면 값나가는 것을 손에 넣고도 결국 헐값에 팔아넘기듯—나는 그것들을 푼돈에 처분하기가 몹시 싫었다. 나는 마지막 막다른 지경에 내몰리지 않는 한, 무슨 변통을 하든 그렇게는 하지 않으리라 작정했다."], ["However, I did not well know what course to take.", "그러나 어떤 길을 잡아야 할지 좀처럼 알 수 없었다."], ["At last I resolved to go to my old governess, and acquaint myself with her again.", "마침내 나는 옛 후견인에게 가서 다시 인사를 나누기로 했다."], ["I had punctually supplied the £5 a year to her for my little boy as long as I was able, but at last was obliged to put a stop to it.", "나는 형편이 닿는 한 어린 아들을 위해 그녀에게 해마다 5파운드를 어김없이 보냈으나, 끝내 그것을 멈출 수밖에 없었다."], ["However, I had written a letter to her, wherein I had told her that my circumstances were reduced very low; that I had lost my husband, and that I was not able to do it any longer, and so begged that the poor child might not suffer too much for its mother’s misfortunes.", "그래도 나는 그녀에게 편지를 써서, 내 형편이 매우 낮아졌으며, 남편을 잃었고, 더는 그것을 댈 수 없게 되었으니, 그 가엾은 아이가 제 어미의 불운으로 너무 큰 고생을 하지 않게 해 달라고 청해 두었다."]], [["I now made her a visit, and I found that she drove something of the old trade still, but that she was not in such flourishing circumstances as before; for she had been sued by a certain gentleman who had had his daughter stolen from him, and who, it seems, she had helped to convey away; and it was very narrowly that she escaped the gallows.", "이제 나는 그녀를 찾아갔고, 그녀가 여전히 옛 장사 비슷한 것을 하고 있으나 예전처럼 번창하는 형편은 아니라는 것을 알았다. 어느 신사가 딸을 도둑맞았는데, 듣자 하니 그녀가 그 아이를 빼돌리는 것을 거든 모양이라 그 신사에게 고소를 당했고, 가까스로 교수대를 면했던 것이다."], ["The expense also had ravaged her, and she was become very poor; her house was but meanly furnished, and she was not in such repute for her practice as before; however, she stood upon her legs, as they say, and as she was a stirring, bustling woman, and had some stock left, she was turned pawnbroker, and lived pretty well.", "그 비용 또한 그녀를 거덜 내어 그녀는 매우 가난해졌고, 집은 보잘것없이 꾸려져 있었으며, 그 솜씨로 예전 같은 평판도 얻지 못했다. 그래도 그녀는 사람들 말마따나 제 두 발로 버티고 서 있었으니, 부산스럽고 분주한 여자인 데다 밑천이 얼마간 남아 있어 전당포 주인으로 돌아서서 꽤 잘살고 있었다."]], [["She received me very civilly, and with her usual obliging manner told me she would not have the less respect for me for my being reduced; that she had taken care my boy was very well looked after, though I could not pay for him, and that the woman that had him was easy, so that I needed not to trouble myself about him till I might be better able to do it effectually.", "그녀는 나를 매우 정중하게 맞이했고, 늘 그렇듯 친절한 태도로, 내가 형편이 기울었다고 해서 나를 덜 존중하지는 않겠다고 말했다. 또 내가 비용을 댈 수 없는데도 내 아들이 매우 잘 보살핌받도록 보살펴 두었으며, 그 아이를 맡은 여자가 마음 편히 지내고 있으니, 내가 그것을 제대로 댈 수 있을 만큼 형편이 나아질 때까지는 그 아이 일로 마음 쓸 필요가 없다고 했다."]], [["I told her that I had not much money left, but that I had some things that were money’s worth, if she could tell me how I might turn them into money.", "나는 그녀에게 돈은 별로 남지 않았으나, 돈만큼 값나가는 물건이 얼마 있는데, 그것을 어떻게 돈으로 바꿀 수 있는지 일러 줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["She asked me what it was I had.", "그녀는 내가 무엇을 가졌느냐고 물었다."], ["I pulled out the string of gold beads, and told her it was one of my husband’s presents to me; then I showed her the two parcels of silk, which I told her I had from Ireland, and brought up to town with me; and the little diamond ring.", "나는 그 금구슬 줄을 꺼내 그것이 남편이 내게 준 선물 가운데 하나라고 말했다. 그러고 나서 명주 두 꾸러미를 보여 주며 그것은 아일랜드에서 얻어 마을로 가져온 것이라고 말했고, 작은 다이아몬드 반지도 보여 주었다."], ["As to the small parcel of plate and spoons, I had found means to dispose of them myself before; and as for the childbed-linen I had, she offered me to take it herself, believing it to have been my own.", "작은 은붙이와 숟가락 꾸러미는 전에 내가 직접 처분할 방도를 찾아냈고, 산모용 아마포는 그녀가 내 것인 줄 믿고 자기가 받아 가겠노라 제안했다."], ["She told me that she was turned pawnbroker, and that she would sell those things for me as pawn to her; and so she sent presently for proper agents that bought them, being in her hands, without any scruple, and gave good prices too.", "그녀는 자기가 전당포 주인으로 돌아섰으니 그 물건들을 자기에게 저당 잡힌 셈으로 해서 팔아 주겠노라 했다. 그리하여 그녀는 곧 적당한 중개인들을 불렀고, 그들은 그녀 손에 들어온 물건인지라 아무 거리낌 없이 그것들을 사 갔으며, 값도 후하게 쳐 주었다."]], [["I now began to think this necessary woman might help me a little in my low condition to some business, for I would gladly have turned my hand to any honest employment if I could have got it.", "이제 나는 이 요긴한 여자가 내 낮은 처지에 무슨 벌이라도 조금 거들어 줄 수 있지 않을까 생각하기 시작했으니, 얻을 수만 있다면 어떤 정직한 일에라도 기꺼이 손을 댈 마음이었기 때문이다."], ["But here she was deficient; honest business did not come within her reach.", "그러나 여기서 그녀는 부족했다. 정직한 일은 그녀의 손이 닿는 데 있지 않았다."], ["If I had been younger, perhaps she might have helped me to a spark, but my thoughts were off that kind of livelihood, as being quite out of the way after fifty, which was my case, and so I told her.", "내가 더 젊었더라면 어쩌면 그녀가 한량 하나를 붙여 주었을지도 모르나, 내 생각은 그런 식의 생계에서 떠나 있었으니, 내 경우인 쉰을 넘긴 뒤에는 도무지 어울리지 않는 길이었다. 나는 그녀에게 그렇게 말했다."]], [["She invited me at last to come, and be at her house till I could find something to do, and it should cost me very little, and this I gladly accepted of.", "마침내 그녀는 무슨 할 일을 찾을 때까지 자기 집에 와서 지내라고, 그래도 비용은 거의 들지 않을 거라고 청했고, 나는 이것을 기꺼이 받아들였다."], ["And now living a little easier, I entered into some measures to have my little son by my last husband taken off; and this she made easy too, reserving a payment only of £5 a year, if I could pay it.", "이제 형편이 조금 편해진 나는 마지막 남편에게서 본 어린 아들을 떠맡길 방도를 강구하기 시작했고, 이 일 또한 그녀가 수월하게 만들어 주었으니, 내가 댈 수 있다면 해마다 5파운드만 치르는 것으로 정해 주었다."], ["This was such a help to me, that for a good while I left off the wicked trade that I had so newly taken up; and gladly I would have got my bread by the help of my needle if I could have got work, but that was very hard to do for one that had no manner of acquaintance in the world.", "이것이 나에게 어찌나 큰 도움이 되었던지, 한동안 나는 그토록 막 손댔던 그 사악한 장사를 그만두었다. 일감을 구할 수만 있었다면 나는 기꺼이 바늘 솜씨로 빵을 벌었겠으나, 세상에 아무런 연줄도 없는 사람에게는 그것이 몹시 어려운 일이었다."]], [["However, at last I got some quilting work for ladies’ beds, petticoats, and the like; and this I liked very well, and worked very hard, and with this I began to live; but the diligent devil, who resolved I should continue in his service, continually prompted me to go out and take a walk, that is to say, to see if anything would offer in the old way.", "그래도 마침내 나는 부인들의 침대보며 페티코트며 그런 것들을 누비는 일감을 좀 얻었고, 이것이 나는 매우 마음에 들어 무척 열심히 일했으며, 이것으로 나는 살아가기 시작했다. 그러나 내가 자기 일에 계속 머물기를 작정한 그 부지런한 악마는 끊임없이 나를 부추겨 밖에 나가 한 바퀴 산책을 하라고, 다시 말해 옛 방식대로 무언가 손에 들어올 것이 없는지 살펴보라고 했다."]], [["One evening I blindly obeyed his summons, and fetched a long circuit through the streets, but met with no purchase, and came home very weary and empty; but not content with that, I went out the next evening too, when going by an alehouse I saw the door of a little room open, next the very street, and on the table a silver tankard, things much in use in public-houses at that time.", "어느 날 저녁 나는 분별없이 그 부름에 따라 길을 따라 멀리 한 바퀴 돌았으나 아무 소득이 없었고, 매우 지치고 빈손으로 집에 돌아왔다. 그래도 그것으로 성에 차지 않아 이튿날 저녁에도 나갔는데, 어느 맥줏집을 지나는데 거리 바로 옆 작은 방의 문이 열려 있고, 탁자 위에 은 큰 잔 하나가 놓인 것이 보였으니, 그 시절 술집에서 많이 쓰던 물건이었다."], ["It seems some company had been drinking there, and the careless boys had forgot to take it away.", "듣자 하니 어떤 패거리가 거기서 술을 마셨던 모양인데, 부주의한 사환 녀석들이 그것을 치우는 것을 잊었던 것이다."]], [["I went into the box frankly, and setting the silver tankard on the corner of the bench, I sat down before it, and knocked with my foot; a boy came presently, and I bade him fetch me a pint of warm ale, for it was cold weather; the boy ran, and I heard him go down the cellar to draw the ale. While the boy was gone, another boy came into the room, and cried, “D’ ye call?” I spoke with a melancholy air, and said, “No, child; the boy is gone for a pint of ale for me.”", "나는 거리낌 없이 그 칸막이 자리로 들어가 은 큰 잔을 의자 모퉁이에 올려놓고 그 앞에 앉아 발로 바닥을 굴렀다. 곧 사환 하나가 왔고, 나는 추운 날씨이니 따뜻한 맥주 한 파인트를 가져오라고 일렀다. 사환이 달려갔고, 나는 그가 맥주를 따르러 지하실로 내려가는 소리를 들었다. 그 녀석이 간 동안 다른 사환 하나가 방으로 들어와 \"부르셨어요?\" 하고 소리쳤다. 나는 침울한 기색으로 말했다. \"아니란다, 얘야. 그 애가 내게 맥주 한 파인트를 가지러 갔단다.\""]], [["While I sat here, I heard the woman in the bar say, “Are they all gone in the five?”", "이렇게 앉아 있는 동안, 나는 술청에 있던 여자가 \"5번 자리 손님들 다 갔니?\""], ["which was the box I sat in, and the boy said, “Yes.”", "하고 묻는 소리를 들었는데, 그것이 바로 내가 앉은 칸이었고, 사환은 \"예\""], ["“Who fetched the tankard away?”", "하고 답했다. \"그 큰 잔은 누가 치웠지?\""], ["says the woman.", "여자가 물었다."], ["“I did,”", "\"제가요.\""], ["says another boy; “that’s it,”", "다른 사환이 말했다. \"저거예요.\""], ["pointing, it seems, to another tankard, which he had fetched from another box by mistake; or else it must be, that the rogue forgot that he had not brought it in, which certainly he had not.", "짐작건대 그 녀석이 다른 칸에서 잘못 가져온 또 다른 큰 잔을 가리킨 것이었다. 아니면, 그 녀석이 그것을 들여오지 않았으면서도 들여온 줄로 잊어버린 것이 분명했으니, 사실 그는 분명 그러지 않았던 것이다."]], [["I heard all this, much to my satisfaction, for I found plainly that the tankard was not missed, and yet they concluded it was fetched away; so I drank my ale, called to pay, and as I went away I said, “Take care of your plate, child,”", "나는 이 모든 것을 매우 흡족하게 들었으니, 그 큰 잔이 없어졌다고 여겨지지 않으면서도 치워졌다고 결론지어진 것이 분명히 드러났기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 맥주를 마시고 셈을 청했으며, 나가면서 \"은붙이 잘 챙기렴, 얘야\""], ["meaning a silver pint mug, which he brought me drink in.", "하고 말했으니, 그가 내게 술을 가져다준 1파인트들이 은잔을 두고 한 말이었다."], ["The boy said, “Yes, madam, very welcome,”", "사환은 \"예, 부인. 또 오십시오\""], ["and away I came.", "하고 말했고, 나는 그곳을 빠져나왔다."]], [["I came home to my governess, and now I thought it was a time to try her, that if I might be put to the necessity of being exposed, she might offer me some assistance.", "나는 후견인에게 돌아왔고, 이제 그녀를 시험해 볼 때라고 생각했으니, 혹시 내가 드러나야 할 궁지에 몰릴 경우 그녀가 내게 무슨 도움을 줄 수 있을까 해서였다."], ["When I had been at home some time, and had an opportunity of talking to her, I told her I had a secret of the greatest consequence in the world to commit to her, if she had respect enough for me to keep it a secret.", "집에 돌아와 얼마간 지내다가 그녀와 이야기할 기회가 생겼을 때, 나는 그녀에게 세상에서 더없이 중대한 비밀 하나를 맡기고 싶은데, 만약 그녀가 그것을 비밀로 지켜 줄 만큼 나를 존중한다면 그러겠다고 말했다."], ["She told me she had kept one of my secrets faithfully; why should I doubt her keeping another?", "그녀는 자기가 내 비밀 하나를 충실히 지켜 주지 않았느냐고, 그런데 내가 어찌 또 하나를 지킬 것을 의심하느냐고 말했다."], ["I told her the strangest thing in the world had befallen me, and that it had made a thief of me, even without any design, and so told her the whole story of the tankard.", "나는 세상에서 가장 기이한 일이 나에게 닥쳤으며, 그것이 아무 꿍꿍이도 없이 나를 도둑으로 만들었다고 말하고는, 그 큰 잔에 관한 이야기를 죄다 들려주었다."], ["“And have you brought it away with you, my dear?”", "\"그래서 그걸 들고 왔단 말이냐, 얘야?\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“To be sure I have,”", "\"그럼요, 가져왔지요.\""], ["says I, and showed it her.", "내가 말하며 그것을 보여 주었다."], ["“But what shall I do now,”", "\"그런데 이제 어쩌죠?\""], ["says I; “must not carry it again?”", "내가 말했다. \"도로 가져다주면 안 될까요?\""]], [["“Carry it again!”", "\"도로 가져다줘!\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“Ay, if you are minded to be sent to Newgate for stealing it.”", "\"그래, 그걸 훔친 죄로 뉴게이트에 끌려가고 싶다면 그러려무나.\""], ["“Why,”", "\"아니,\""], ["says I, “they can’t be so base to stop me, when I carry it to them again?”", "내가 말했다. \"그걸 도로 가져다주는데 설마 나를 붙잡을 만큼 비열하지는 않겠지요?\""], ["“You don’t know those sort of people, child,”", "\"넌 그런 부류의 사람들을 모르는구나, 얘야.\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she; “they’ll not only carry you to Newgate, but hang you too, without any regard to the honesty of returning it; or bring in an account of all the other tankards they have lost, for you to pay for.”", "\"그자들은 너를 뉴게이트로 끌고 갈 뿐 아니라, 도로 돌려준 정직함 따위는 거들떠보지도 않고 교수형에 처할 거다. 아니면 자기네가 잃어버린 다른 잔들을 죄다 셈에 올려 너더러 물어내라고 할걸.\""], ["“What must I do, then?”", "\"그럼 어쩌면 좋아요?\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I.", "\"에이,\""], ["“Nay,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “as you have played the cunning part and stole it, you must e’en keep it; there’s no going back now.", "\"교활한 짓을 부려 훔쳤으니, 차라리 그냥 가지고 있으려무나. 이제 와서 물릴 수도 없는 노릇이다."], ["Besides, child,”", "게다가, 얘야,\""], ["says she, “don’t you want it more than they do?", "그녀가 말했다. \"네가 그자들보다 그게 더 필요하지 않으냐?"], ["I wish you could light of such a bargain once a week.”", "일주일에 한 번씩 그런 횡재를 만났으면 좋겠구나.\""]], [["This gave me a new notion of my governess, and that since she was turned pawnbroker, she had a sort of people about her that were none of the honest ones that I had met with there before.", "이 일로 나는 내 후견인에 대해 새로운 생각을 품게 되었으니, 그녀가 전당포 주인으로 돌아선 뒤로 내가 전에 거기서 만났던 정직한 부류와는 거리가 먼 그런 사람들을 둘레에 두고 있다는 것이었다."]], [["I had not been long there but I discovered it more plainly than before, for every now and then I saw hilts of swords, spoons, forks, tankards, and all such kind of ware brought in, not to be pawned, but to be sold downright; and she bought everything that came without asking any questions, but had very good bargains, as I found by her discourse.", "거기 머문 지 얼마 되지 않아 나는 그것을 전보다 더 또렷이 알게 되었으니, 이따금 칼자루며 숟가락이며 포크며 큰 잔이며 그런 온갖 물건이 저당 잡히려는 것이 아니라 아예 팔리려고 들어오는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그녀는 들어오는 것은 무엇이든 아무것도 묻지 않고 사들였으나, 그녀의 말로 미루어 짐작건대 매우 좋은 값에 사들였다."]], [["I found also that in following this trade she always melted down the plate she bought, that it might not be challenged; and she came to me and told me one morning that she was going to melt, and if I would, she would put my tankard in, that it might not be seen by anybody.", "또한 나는 그녀가 이 장사를 하면서 사들인 은붙이를 항상 녹여 버린다는 것을 알았으니, 임자가 나서서 따지지 못하게 하려는 것이었다. 어느 날 아침 그녀는 나에게 와서 녹이려는 참인데, 원한다면 아무에게도 눈에 띄지 않도록 내 큰 잔도 넣어 주겠노라 했다."], ["I told her, with all my heart; so she weighed it, and allowed me the full value in silver again; but I found she did not do the same to the rest of her customers.", "나는 진심으로 그러시라고 말했고, 그녀는 그것의 무게를 달아 그 값을 다시 은으로 온전히 쳐 주었다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 다른 손님들에게는 그렇게 해 주지 않는다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Some time after this, as I was at work, and very melancholy, she begins to ask me what the matter was, as she was used to do.", "이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 내가 일을 하며 매우 침울해하고 있는데 그녀가 늘 그러듯 무슨 일이냐고 묻기 시작했다."], ["I told her my heart was heavy; I had little work, and nothing to live on, and knew not what course to take.", "나는 마음이 무겁다고, 일감도 적고 먹고살 것도 없으며 어떤 길을 잡아야 할지 모르겠다고 말했다."], ["She laughed, and told me I must go out again and try my fortune; it might be that I might meet with another piece of plate.", "그녀는 웃으며, 다시 밖에 나가 운수를 시험해 보아야 한다고, 어쩌면 또 다른 은붙이를 만날지도 모르지 않느냐고 말했다. \"어머, 어머니!\""], ["“O mother!”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I, “that is a trade I have no skill in, and if I should be taken I am undone at once.”", "\"그건 제가 솜씨가 없는 장사인 데다, 붙잡히기라도 하면 단번에 끝장이에요.\""], ["Says she, “I could help you to a schoolmistress that shall make you as dexterous as herself.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"내가 너를 자기만큼 능숙하게 만들어 줄 여선생 하나를 붙여 줄 수 있단다.\""], ["I trembled at that proposal, for hitherto I had had no confederates, nor any acquaintance among that tribe.", "나는 그 제안에 몸서리를 쳤으니, 그때까지 나에게는 한패도 없었고 그 부류 가운데 아는 사람도 없었기 때문이다."], ["But she conquered all my modesty, and all my fears; and in a little time, by the help of this confederate, I grew as impudent a thief, and as dexterous as ever Moll Cutpurse was, though, if fame does not belie her, not half so handsome.", "그러나 그녀는 내 부끄러움도, 내 두려움도 죄다 정복해 버렸다. 그리하여 머지않아 이 한패의 도움으로 나는 몰 컷퍼스 못지않게 뻔뻔한 도둑이자 그 못지않게 능숙한 도둑이 되었으니, 다만 소문이 그녀를 헐뜯는 게 아니라면 그 절반만큼도 곱지는 못했다."]], [["The comrade she helped me to dealt in three sorts of craft, viz.", "* 몰 컷퍼스: 17세기 런던에서 악명 높았던 여성 소매치기이자 장물아비 메리 프리스의 별명."], ["shoplifting, stealing of shop-books and pocket-books, and taking off gold watches from the ladies’ sides; and this last she did so dexterously that no woman ever arrived to the performance of that art so as to do it like her.", "그녀가 붙여 준 동무는 세 가지 종류의 기술을 부렸으니, 곧 가게에서 물건 슬쩍하기, 가게 장부와 수첩 훔치기, 그리고 부인들의 옆구리에서 금시계 빼내기였다. 이 마지막 것을 그녀는 어찌나 능숙하게 해냈던지, 어떤 여자도 그 솜씨에 이르러 그녀처럼 해낸 적이 없었다."], ["I liked the first and the last of these things very well, and I attended her some time in the practice, just as a deputy attends a midwife, without any pay.", "나는 이 가운데 첫째와 마지막 것이 매우 마음에 들었고, 마치 부산파가 산파를 따라다니듯 보수도 없이 한동안 그녀를 따라다니며 실습을 했다."]], [["At length she put me to practice.", "마침내 그녀가 나를 실전에 내보냈다."], ["She had shown me her art, and I had several times unhooked a watch from her own side with great dexterity.", "그녀가 자기 기술을 보여 준 터라, 나는 여러 번 그녀 자신의 옆구리에서 시계를 매우 능숙하게 풀어낸 적이 있었다. 마침내 그녀가 나에게 한 표적을 보여 주었으니, 그것은 매혹적인 시계를 찬, 만삭의 젊은 부인이었다."], ["At last she showed me a prize, and this was a young lady big with child, who had a charming watch.", "그 일은 그녀가 교회에서 나올 때 해치워야 했다. 그녀는 그 부인의 한쪽 옆으로 다가가, 부인이 막 계단에 이르렀을 때 넘어지는 척하며 어찌나 세차게 부인에게 부딪쳤던지 부인을 크게 놀라게 했고, 둘 다 무섭게 비명을 질렀다."], ["The thing was to be done as she came out of church.", "그녀가 부인을 떠밀친 바로 그 순간, 나는 시계를 붙들었고, 그것을 올바른 방향으로 잡고 있던 터라 부인이 흠칫하는 통에 고리가 빠졌으며, 부인은 그것을 전혀 느끼지 못했다."], ["She goes on one side of the lady, and pretends, just as she came to the steps, to fall, and fell against the lady with so much violence as put her into a great fright, and both cried out terribly.", "나는 즉시 그 자리를 떴고, 내 여선생은 천천히 그 꾸며 낸 놀람에서 헤어나도록 내버려 두었으며, 부인도 마찬가지였다. 이윽고 시계가 없어진 것이 드러났다."], ["In the very moment that she jostled the lady, I had hold of the watch, and holding it the right way, the start she gave drew the hook out, and she never felt it.", "\"아이고,\" 내 동무가 말했다."], ["I made off immediately, and left my schoolmistress to come out of her pretended fright gradually, and the lady too; and presently the watch was missed.", "\"그렇다면 분명 나를 떠민 그 도둑놈들이었어요. 부인께서 진작 시계가 없어진 걸 알아채지 못하신 게 안타깝네요."], ["“Ay,” says my comrade, “then it was those rogues that thrust me down, I warrant ye; I wonder the gentlewoman did not miss her watch before, then we might have taken them.”", "그랬으면 그놈들을 잡을 수도 있었을 텐데요.\""]], [["She humoured the thing so well that nobody suspected her, and I was got home a full hour before her.", "그녀가 그 일을 어찌나 잘 둘러댔던지 아무도 그녀를 의심하지 않았고, 나는 그녀보다 꼬박 한 시간 먼저 집에 와 있었다."], ["This was my first adventure in company.", "이것이 패거리와 함께한 내 첫 모험이었다."], ["The watch was indeed a very fine one, and had a great many trinkets about it, and my governess allowed us £20 for it, of which I had half.", "그 시계는 정말로 매우 고운 것이었고, 거기에 자질구레한 장식이 많이 달려 있었는데, 내 후견인은 그것의 값으로 우리에게 20파운드를 쳐 주었고, 그중 절반을 내가 가졌다."], ["And thus I was entered a complete thief, hardened to the pitch above all the reflections of conscience or modesty, and to a degree which I must acknowledge I never thought possible in me.", "그리하여 나는 양심이나 부끄러움의 온갖 성찰을 죄다 넘어설 만큼 굳어진, 그것도 나 스스로 결코 나에게 가능하리라 생각해 본 적 없는 정도로 굳어진, 완벽한 도둑으로 입문했다."]], [["Thus the devil, who began, by the help of an irresistible poverty, to push me into this wickedness, brought me on to a height beyond the common rate, even when my necessities were not so great, or the prospect of my misery so terrifying; for I had now got into a little vein of work, and as I was not at a loss to handle my needle, it was very probable, as acquaintance came in, I might have got my bread honestly enough.", "이리하여, 거역할 수 없는 가난의 도움으로 나를 이 사악함에 밀어 넣기 시작했던 악마는, 정작 내 궁핍이 그리 심하지도 않고 비참함의 전망도 그리 무섭지 않게 되었을 때조차, 나를 보통의 도를 넘어선 높이로까지 끌고 갔다. 이제 나는 일감의 작은 길에 접어들었고, 바늘을 다루는 데 막힘이 없던 터라, 연줄이 들어옴에 따라 충분히 정직하게 빵을 벌 수 있었을 법한데도 말이다."]], [["I must say, that if such a prospect of work had presented itself at first, when I began to feel the approach of my miserable circumstances—I say, had such a prospect of getting my bread by working presented itself then, I had never fallen into this wicked trade, or into such a wicked gang as I was now embarked with; but practice had hardened me, and I grew audacious to the last degree; and the more so because I had carried it on so long, and had never been taken; for, in a word, my new partner in wickedness and I went on together so long, without being ever detected, that we not only grew bold, but we grew rich, and we had at one time one-and-twenty gold watches in our hands.", "말하지 않을 수 없는 것은, 만약 처음에, 내 비참한 처지가 다가오는 것을 느끼기 시작했을 때, 그런 일감의 전망이 나타났더라면—다시 말해 일해서 빵을 버는 그런 전망이 그때 나타났더라면—나는 결코 이 사악한 장사에, 혹은 지금 내가 발을 들인 그런 사악한 패거리에 빠지지 않았으리라는 것이다. 그러나 실습이 나를 굳게 만들었고, 나는 마지막 한도까지 대담해졌다. 더더욱 그러했던 까닭은, 내가 그것을 그토록 오래 해 오면서 한 번도 붙잡힌 적이 없었기 때문이다. 한마디로, 사악함의 새 동업자와 나는 한 번도 발각되지 않고 그토록 오래 함께해 나갔던지라, 우리는 대담해졌을 뿐 아니라 부유해졌으니, 한때 우리 손에 금시계 스물한 개를 쥐고 있기도 했다."]], [["I remember that one day being a little more serious than ordinary, and finding I had so good a stock beforehand as I had, for I had near £200 in money for my share, it came strongly into my mind, no doubt from some kind spirit, if such there be, that at first poverty excited me, and my distresses drove me to these dreadful shifts; so seeing those distresses were now relieved, and I could also get something towards a maintenance by working, and had so good a bank to support me, why should I now not leave off, as they say, while I was well?", "기억하건대 어느 날 나는 여느 때보다 조금 진지해져서, 내가 그토록 좋은 밑천을 미리 쟁여 두었음을 깨닫고—내 몫으로 현금이 거의 200파운드나 있었으니—이런 생각이 강하게 떠올랐다. 의심할 바 없이 어떤 선한 영靈에게서 왔을 것이니, 만약 그런 것이 있다면 말이다."], ["that I could not expect to go always free; and if I was once surprised, and miscarried, I was undone.", "곧 처음에는 가난이 나를 부추겼고, 내 곤경이 나를 이 무서운 변통으로 내몰았으나, 이제 그 곤경이 가셨고, 또 일해서 생계에 보탤 것도 얻을 수 있으며, 나를 떠받쳐 줄 그토록 좋은 밑천도 있으니, 어찌하여 사람들 말마따나 형편이 좋을 때 그만두지 않겠는가? 늘 자유로이 다닐 것을 기대할 수는 없으며, 한번 들켜 일을 그르치면 나는 끝장이라는 생각이었다."]], [["This was doubtless the happy minute, when, if I had hearkened to the blessed hint, from whatsoever had it came, I had still a cast for an easy life. But my fate was otherwise determined; the busy devil that so industriously drew me in had too fast hold of me to let me go back; but as poverty brought me into the mire, so avarice kept me in, till there was no going back. As to the arguments which my reason dictated for persuading me to lay down, avarice stepped in and said, “Go on, go on; you have had very good luck; go on till you have gotten four or five hundred pounds, and then you shall leave off, and then you may live easy without working at all.”", "이것은 의심할 바 없이 그 다행스러운 순간이었으니, 만약 내가 그 복된 암시에—그것이 어디서 왔든—귀를 기울였더라면, 나에게는 아직 편안한 삶을 향한 한 번의 기회가 있었다. 그러나 내 운명은 달리 정해져 있었다. 그토록 부지런히 나를 끌어들인 그 분주한 악마는 나를 너무 단단히 붙들고 있어 돌아가게 놓아주지 않았다. 가난이 나를 진창에 빠뜨렸듯이, 탐욕이 거기에 나를 붙들어, 끝내 돌아갈 길이 없게 되었다. 그만 손을 떼라고 내 이성이 일러 준 논거들에 대해서는, 탐욕이 끼어들어 이렇게 말했다. \"계속해, 계속해. 넌 운이 아주 좋았어. 사오백 파운드를 손에 넣을 때까지 계속하다가, 그러고 나서 그만두면, 그때는 아예 일하지 않고도 편안히 살 수 있어.\""]], [["Thus I, that was once in the devil’s clutches, was held fast there as with a charm, and had no power to go without the circle, till I was engulfed in labyrinths of trouble too great to get out at all.", "이리하여, 한때 악마의 손아귀에 든 나는 마치 주문에 걸린 듯 거기에 단단히 붙들려, 그 원 밖으로 나갈 힘이 없었고, 마침내 도무지 빠져나올 수 없을 만큼 거대한 곤경의 미궁에 휩싸이고 말았다."]], [["However, these thoughts left some impression upon me, and made me act with some more caution than before, and more than my directors used for themselves.", "그래도 이 생각들은 나에게 얼마간 인상을 남겨, 전보다 좀 더 조심스럽게, 또 내 길잡이들이 자기들끼리 부리는 것보다 더 조심스럽게 처신하게 만들었다."], ["My comrade, as I called her, but rather she should have been called my teacher, with another of her scholars, was the first in the misfortune; for, happening to be upon the hunt for purchase, they made an attempt upon a linen-draper in Cheapside, but were snapped by a hawk’s-eyed journeyman, and seized with two pieces of cambric, which were taken also upon them.", "내가 동무라고 불렀으나 차라리 내 선생이라 불렀어야 할 그 여자가, 그녀의 다른 제자 하나와 더불어 맨 먼저 불운을 맞았다. 노획물을 사냥하러 나섰다가, 그들이 칩사이드의 어느 포목상에게 시도를 했는데, 매의 눈을 가진 점원에게 덜미를 잡혀, 그들에게서 함께 압수된 케임브릭 천 두 필과 함께 붙들렸기 때문이다."]], [["This was enough to lodge them both in Newgate, where they had the misfortune to have some of their former sins brought to remembrance.", "이것만으로도 둘 다 뉴게이트에 갇히기에 충분했고, 거기서 그들은 지난날의 죄 가운데 몇 가지가 다시 들춰지는 불운을 겪었다."], ["Two other indictments being brought against them, and the facts being proved upon them, they were both condemned to die.", "그들에 대해 다른 두 건의 기소가 제기되었고, 그 사실이 입증되어 둘 다 사형을 선고받았다."], ["They both pleaded their bellies, and were both voted quick with child; though my tutoress was no more with child than I was.", "둘 다 임신을 사유로 호소했고, 둘 다 만삭으로 판정받았으나, 사실 내 여선생은 나만큼이나 임신한 상태가 아니었다."]], [["I went frequently to see them, and condole with them, expecting that it would be my turn next; but the place gave me so much horror, reflecting that it was the place of my unhappy birth, and of my mother’s misfortunes, and that I could not bear it, so I was forced to leave off going to see them.", "나는 그들을 보러, 그리고 그들을 위로하러 자주 갔으니, 다음번이 내 차례가 되리라 예상했기 때문이다. 그러나 그곳은 나에게 너무도 큰 공포를 주었으니, 그곳이 내 불운한 출생의 자리요 내 어머니의 불운의 자리임을 떠올리면 도무지 견딜 수가 없어, 나는 그들을 보러 가는 것을 그만둘 수밖에 없었다."]], [["And oh!", "아, 그들의 재난에서 경계를 받아들일 수만 있었더라면, 나는 여전히 행복했을 것이다."], ["could I have but taken warning by their disasters, I had been happy still, for I was yet free, and had nothing brought against me; but it could not be, my measure was not yet filled up.", "나는 아직 자유로웠고, 나에게 제기된 죄목도 없었으니까. 그러나 그럴 수가 없었으니, 내 분량이 아직 다 차지 않았던 것이다."]], [["My comrade, having the brand of an old offender, was executed; the young offender was spared, having obtained a reprieve, but lay starving a long while in prison, till at last she got her name into what they call a circuit pardon, and so came off.", "내 동무는 상습범의 낙인이 찍혀 처형되었고, 어린 범죄자는 사형 집행 유예를 얻어 목숨을 건졌으나, 오래도록 옥에서 굶주리며 누워 있다가 마침내 사람들이 순회사면이라 부르는 것에 이름을 올려 빠져나왔다."]], [["This terrible example of my comrade frighted me heartily, and for a good while I made no excursions; but one night, in the neighbourhood of my governess’s house, they cried “Fire.”", "내 동무의 이 끔찍한 본보기는 나를 진심으로 겁먹게 했고, 한동안 나는 출격을 하지 않았다. 그러나 어느 날 밤, 내 후견인 집 근처에서 사람들이 \"불이야\""], ["My governess looked out, for we were all up, and cried immediately that such a gentlewoman’s house was all of a light fire atop, and so indeed it was.", "하고 외쳤다. 우리는 모두 깨어 있던 터라 내 후견인이 내다보고는, 곧 어느 부인의 집 꼭대기가 온통 불길에 휩싸였다고 외쳤는데, 정말로 그러했다."], ["Here she gives me a job.", "여기서 그녀가 나에게 한 가지 일거리를 주었다. \"자, 얘야.\""], ["“Now, child,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “there is a rare opportunity, for the fire being so near that you may go to it before the street is blocked up with the crowd.”", "\"둘도 없는 기회다. 불이 어찌나 가까운지, 거리가 군중으로 막히기 전에 거기 갈 수 있겠어.\""], ["She presently gave me my cue.", "그녀는 곧 내게 할 말을 일러 주었다. \"가거라, 얘야.\""], ["“Go, child,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “to the house, and run in and tell the lady, or anybody you see, that you come to help them, and that you came from such a gentlewoman (that is, one of her acquaintance farther up the street).”", "\"그 집으로 가서, 뛰어 들어가 부인이나 보이는 누구에게든, 거들러 왔다고, 아무개 부인이 보내서 왔다고 말하렴.\" 곧 거리 위쪽에 사는 그녀의 아는 사람 하나를 가리킨 것이었다."], ["She gave me the like cue to the next house, naming another name that was also an acquaintance of the gentlewoman of the house.", "그녀는 옆집에도 똑같은 말을 일러 주며, 그 집 부인이 또한 아는 사람인 다른 이름 하나를 댔다."]], [["Away I went, and, coming to the house, I found them all in confusion, you may be sure.", "나는 떠났고, 그 집에 이르니 짐작하다시피 모두가 아수라장이었다."], ["I ran in, and finding one of the maids, “Lord!", "나는 뛰어 들어가 하녀 하나를 붙잡고 말했다."], ["sweetheart,”", "\"어머나!"], ["says I, “how came this dismal accident?", "얘야,\" 내가 말했다."], ["Where is your mistress?", "\"이 끔찍한 변고가 어쩌다 났니?"], ["Any how does she do?", "마님은 어디 계셔?"], ["Is she safe?", "어떠셔?"], ["And where are the children?", "무사하셔? 아이들은 어디 있고?"], ["I come from Madam —— to help you.”", "나는 아무개 마님이 보내서 너희를 도우러 왔단다.\""], ["Away runs the maid.", "하녀가 달려갔다."], ["“Madam, madam,”", "\"마님, 마님,\""], ["says she, screaming as loud as she could yell, “here is a gentlewoman come from Madam —— to help us.”", "그녀가 있는 힘껏 비명을 지르며 외쳤다. \"여기 아무개 마님이 보내서 우리를 도우러 온 부인이 계세요.\""], ["The poor woman, half out of her wits, with a bundle under her arm, an two little children, comes toward me.", "정신이 반쯤 나간 가엾은 여자가 팔에 보따리 하나를 끼고 어린아이 둘을 데리고 내게로 다가왔다. \"어머나!"], ["“Lord!", "부인,\""], ["madam,”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I, “let me carry the poor children to Madam ——,”", "\"이 가엾은 아이들을 제가 아무개 마님께 데려다드릴게요. 마님이 아이들을 보내 달라 하셨어요."], ["she desires you to send them; she’ll take care of the poor lambs;’ and immediately I takes one of them out of her hand, and she lifts the other up into my arms.", "가엾은 어린양들을 돌봐 주시겠대요.\" 그러고는 곧 내가 아이 하나를 그녀 손에서 받아 들자, 그녀는 다른 아이를 들어 내 품에 안겼다."], ["“Ay, do, for God’s sake,”", "\"그래요, 제발 그래 주세요.\""], ["says she, “carry them to her.", "그녀가 말했다. \"아이들을 마님께 데려다주세요."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["thank her for her kindness.”", "친절을 베풀어 주셔서 고맙다고 전해 주세요.\""], ["“Have you anything else to secure, madam?”", "\"또 챙겨야 할 게 있나요, 부인?\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I; “she will take care of it.”", "\"마님이 돌봐 주실 거예요.\" \"아이고!"], ["“Oh dear! ay,”", "그래요,\""], ["says she, “God bless her, and thank her.", "그녀가 말했다. \"마님께 복이 있기를, 고맙기도 하셔라."], ["Take this bundle of plate and carry it to her too.", "이 은붙이 보따리도 가져가서 마님께 전해 주세요."], ["Oh, she is a good woman.", "아, 정말 좋은 분이셔."], ["Oh Lord!", "아이고 맙소사!"], ["we are utterly ruined, utterly undone!”", "우리는 아주 망했어요, 아주 끝장이라고요!\""], ["And away she runs from me out of her wits, and the maids after her; and away comes I with the two children and the bundle.", "그러고는 정신이 나간 채 내게서 달아났고, 하녀들도 그 뒤를 따랐으며, 나는 두 아이와 보따리를 안고 그곳을 빠져나왔다."]], [["I was no sooner got into the street but I saw another woman come to me.", "거리로 나서자마자 또 다른 여자가 내게로 다가오는 것이 보였다."], ["“Oh!”", "\"어머!\""], ["says she, “mistress,”", "그녀가 가엾어하는 어조로 말했다. \"아주머니, 아이를 떨어뜨리시겠어요."], ["in a piteous tone, “you will let fall the child.", "자, 참 딱한 때로군요. 제가 거들어 드릴게요.\""], ["Come, this is a sad time; let me help you”; and immediately lays hold of my bundle to carry it for me.", "그러고는 곧 그것을 들어 주겠다며 내 보따리를 붙들었다. \"아니에요.\""], ["“No,”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I; “if you will help me, take the child by the hand, and lead it for me but to the upper end of the street; I’ll go with you and satisfy you for your pains.”", "\"거들고 싶거든, 아이 손을 잡고 이 거리 위쪽 끝까지만 끌어 주세요. 같이 가서 수고한 값을 치러 드릴게요.\""]], [["She could not avoid going, after what I said; but the creature, in short, was one of the same business with me, and wanted nothing but the bundle; however, she went with me to the door, for she could not help it. When we were come there I whispered her, “Go, child,” said I, “I understand your trade; you may meet with purchase enough.”", "내가 한 말을 듣고 그녀는 따라오지 않을 수 없었다. 그러나 한마디로 그 인간은 나와 같은 장사를 하는 자였고, 보따리 말고는 아무것도 바라는 게 없었다. 그래도 그녀는 어쩔 수 없이 나와 함께 그 문까지 갔다. 거기에 이르자 나는 그녀에게 속삭였다. \"가 보세요, 얘.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신 장사를 알겠어요. 노획물이야 얼마든지 만날 수 있을 테니.\""]], [["She understood me and walked off.", "그녀는 내 말을 알아듣고 가 버렸다."], ["I thundered at the door with the children, and as the people were raised before by the noise of the fire, I was soon let in, and I said, “Is madam awake?", "나는 아이들을 데리고 문을 쾅쾅 두드렸고, 사람들은 이미 불 소동으로 깨어 있던 터라 곧 안으로 들여보내 주었다. 나는 말했다."], ["Pray tell her Mrs.", "\"마님 깨어 계세요? 아무개 부인이 이 두 아이를 받아 주십사 청하신다고 전해 주세요."], ["—— desires the favour of her to take the two children in; poor lady, she will be undone, their house is all of a flame,”", "가엾은 부인, 큰일 났어요. 그 집이 온통 불길에 휩싸였답니다.\""], ["They took the children in very civilly, pitied the family in distress, and away came I with my bundle.", "그들은 매우 정중하게 아이들을 받아들이고 곤경에 빠진 그 가족을 가엾어했으며, 나는 보따리를 들고 빠져나왔다. 하녀 하나가 나에게 보따리도 두고 가는 거 아니냐고 물었다."], ["One of the maids asked me if I was not to leave the bundle too.", "나는 말했다. \"아니란다, 얘야."], ["I said, “No, sweetheart, ’tis to go to another place; it does not belong to them.”", "그건 다른 데로 갈 거야. 그 집 것이 아니거든.\""]], [["I was a great way out of the hurry now, and so I went on, clear of anybody’s inquiry, and brought the bundle of plate, which was very considerable, straight home, and gave it to my old governess.", "나는 이제 그 소동에서 멀찍이 벗어나 있던지라, 누구의 추궁도 받지 않고 계속 걸어가, 매우 상당한 은붙이 보따리를 곧장 집으로 가져와 내 옛 후견인에게 건넸다."], ["She told me she would not look into it, but bade me go out again to look for more.", "그녀는 그것을 들여다보지 않겠노라며, 더 찾으러 다시 나가 보라고 일렀다."]], [["She gave me the like cue to the gentlewoman of the next house to that which was on fire, and I did my endeavour to go, but by this time the alarm of fire was so great, and so many engines playing, and the street so thronged with people, that I could not get near the house whatever I would do; so I came back again to my governess’s, and taking the bundle up into my chamber, I began to examine it.", "그녀는 불이 난 집 옆집 부인에게도 똑같은 말을 일러 주었고, 나는 가 보려고 애썼으나 이때쯤에는 불 경보가 어찌나 커지고, 어찌나 많은 소방기가 물을 뿜어 대며, 거리가 어찌나 사람들로 붐볐던지, 무슨 수를 써도 그 집 근처에 갈 수가 없었다. 그래서 나는 다시 후견인 집으로 돌아와 보따리를 내 방으로 가지고 올라가 살펴보기 시작했다."], ["It is with horror that I tell what a treasure I found there; ’tis enough to say, that besides most of the family plate, which was considerable, I found a gold chain, an old-fashioned thing, the locket of which was broken, so that I suppose it had not been used some years, but the gold was not the worse for that; also a little box of burying-rings, the lady’s wedding-ring, and some broken bits of old lockets of gold, a gold watch, and a purse with about £24 value in old pieces of gold coin, and several other things of value.", "거기서 내가 어떤 보물을 찾아냈는지는 몸서리치며 이야기하거니와, 상당했던 그 집안 은붙이의 대부분 말고도, 금사슬 하나—구식 물건으로 거기 달린 로켓이 부서져 있어 몇 해 동안 쓰이지 않았던 듯하나, 그렇다고 금이 못해진 것은 아니었다—와 장례용 반지가 든 작은 상자 하나, 부인의 결혼반지, 그리고 부서진 옛 금 로켓 조각 몇 개, 금시계 하나, 그리고 옛 금화 조각으로 24파운드 값어치쯤 든 지갑 하나, 그 밖에 값나가는 여러 물건을 찾아냈다고 말하는 것으로 족하다."]], [["This was the greatest and the worst prize that ever I was concerned in; for indeed, though, as I have said above, I was hardened now beyond the power of all reflection in other cases, yet it really touched me to the very soul when I looked into this treasure, to think of the poor disconsolate gentlewoman who had lost so much by the fire besides; and who would think, to be sure, that she had saved her plate and best things; how she would be surprised and afflicted when she should find that she had been deceived, and should find that the person that took her children and her goods, had not come, as was pretended, from the gentlewoman in the next street, but that the children had been put upon her without her own knowledge.", "이것은 내가 손댄 가운데 가장 크고 가장 고약한 노획물이었다. 정말이지, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 다른 경우들에서는 이제 온갖 성찰의 힘을 넘어설 만큼 굳어져 있었으나, 이 보물을 들여다보았을 때만은 정말로 영혼 깊은 데까지 마음이 찔렸으니, 그 불로 그토록 많은 것을 잃은 데다, 분명 자기는 은붙이와 가장 좋은 물건들을 건졌으리라 믿을 그 가엾고 의기소침한 부인을 생각하면, 자기가 속았음을 알게 되고, 자기 아이들과 물건을 가져간 사람이 가장한 대로 옆 거리 부인에게서 온 것이 아니라, 아이들이 제 모르게 그 부인에게 떠맡겨졌음을 알게 될 때 그녀가 얼마나 놀라고 비통해할지를 생각하면 그러했다."]], [["I say, I confess the inhumanity of this action moved me very much, and made me relent exceedingly, and tears stood in my eyes upon that subject; but with all my sense of its being cruel and inhuman, I could never find in my heart to make any restitution.", "거듭 말하거니와, 고백건대 이 짓의 비인간성이 나를 무척 흔들어 놓았고 몹시 마음이 누그러지게 했으며, 그 일로 내 눈에 눈물이 고였다. 그러나 그것이 잔인하고 비인간적이라는 온갖 자각에도 불구하고, 나는 도무지 무언가를 돌려주려는 마음을 낼 수가 없었다."], ["The reflection wore off, and I began quickly to forget the circumstances that attended the taking them.", "그 성찰은 닳아 없어졌고, 나는 그것들을 가져올 때 따랐던 정황을 곧 잊어버리기 시작했다."]], [["Nor was this all; for though by this job I was become considerably richer than before, yet the resolution I had formerly taken, of leaving off this horrid trade when I had gotten a little more, did not return, but I must still get farther, and more; and the avarice joined so with the success, that I had no more thought of coming to a timely alteration of life, though without it I could expect no safety, no tranquillity in the possession of what I had so wickedly gained; but a little more, and a little more, was the case still.", "이것이 다가 아니었으니, 이 일거리로 내가 전보다 상당히 부유해졌는데도, 전에 품었던 결심, 곧 조금만 더 손에 넣으면 이 끔찍한 장사를 그만두겠다던 결심이 돌아오지 않았고, 도리어 나는 여전히 더 멀리, 더 많이 나아가야만 했다. 탐욕이 성공과 어찌나 한데 얽혔던지, 나는 제때에 삶을 바꿀 생각이 더는 들지 않았으니, 그것 없이는 그토록 사악하게 얻은 것을 지키는 데 아무런 안전도, 아무런 평온도 기대할 수 없는데도, 조금만 더, 조금만 더가 여전히 내 형편이었다."]], [["At length, yielding to the importunities of my crime, I cast off all remorse and repentance, and all the reflections on that head turned to no more than this, that I might perhaps come to have one booty more that might complete my desires; but though I certainly had that one booty, yet every hit looked towards another, and was so encouraging to me to go on with the trade, that I had no gust to the thought of laying it down.", "마침내 내 죄의 끈질긴 부추김에 굴복하여, 나는 온갖 회한과 참회를, 그리고 그 문제에 관한 온갖 성찰을 죄다 떨쳐 버렸으니, 그것들은 결국 이런 것 이상이 되지 못했다. 곧 어쩌면 내 욕망을 채워 줄 노획물 하나를 더 손에 넣게 될지도 모른다는 것. 그러나 분명히 그 하나를 손에 넣었어도, 한 번 한 번 손에 넣을 때마다 또 다른 것을 향하게 되었고, 그것이 나에게 어찌나 그 장사를 계속하도록 부추겼던지, 나는 그것을 그만둘 생각에는 도무지 구미가 당기지 않았다."]], [["In this condition, hardened by success, and resolving to go on, I fell into the snare in which I was appointed to meet with my last reward for this kind of life.", "이런 형편으로, 성공에 굳어져 계속하기로 작정한 채, 나는 이런 삶에 대한 마지막 보답을 만나도록 정해진 그 덫에 빠지고 말았다."], ["But even this was not yet, for I met with several successful adventures more in this way of being undone.", "그러나 그조차 아직은 아니었으니, 나는 이렇게 끝장으로 치닫는 길에서 성공적인 모험을 몇 차례 더 만났기 때문이다."]], [["I remained still with my governess, who was for a while really concerned for the misfortune of my comrade that had been hanged, and who, it seems, knew enough of my governess to have sent her the same way, and which made her very uneasy; indeed, she was in a very great fright.", "나는 여전히 내 후견인과 함께 머물렀는데, 그녀는 한동안 교수형을 당한 내 동무의 불운을 정말로 걱정했다. 듣자 하니 그 여자는 내 후견인을 같은 길로 보낼 만큼 그녀에 대해 충분히 알고 있었던지라, 그것이 그녀를 매우 불안하게 만들었으니, 그녀는 정말로 큰 공포에 휩싸여 있었다."]], [["It is true that when she was gone, and had not opened mouth to tell what she knew, my governess was easy as to that point, and perhaps glad she was hanged, for it was in her power to have obtained a pardon at the expense of her friends; but on the other hand, the loss of her, and the sense of her kindness in not making her market of what she knew, moved my governess to mourn very sincerely for her.", "사실 그 여자가 떠나면서도 자기가 아는 것을 발설하려고 입을 열지 않았던지라, 내 후견인은 그 점에 관해서는 마음이 놓였고, 어쩌면 그녀가 교수형을 당한 것이 다행이라 여겼을 것이다. 그녀로서는 제 동무들을 팔아넘기는 대가로 사면을 얻어 낼 수도 있었으니까."], ["I comforted her as well as I could, and she in return hardened me to merit more completely the same fate.", "그러나 다른 한편으로, 그녀를 잃은 일과, 자기가 아는 것을 미끼로 팔아넘기지 않은 그 친절함을 떠올리니, 내 후견인은 그녀를 위해 매우 진심으로 애도하게 되었다. 나는 힘닿는 대로 그녀를 위로했고, 그녀는 그 보답으로 나를 굳게 만들어 더더욱 온전히 똑같은 운명을 받을 만하게 했다."]], [["However, as I have said, it made me the more wary, and particularly I was very shy of shoplifting, especially among the mercers and drapers, who are a set of fellows that have their eyes very much about them.", "그래도 앞서 말했듯이 그것은 나를 더욱 조심스럽게 만들었으니, 특히 나는 가게 물건 슬쩍하기를 매우 꺼렸고, 특히 눈을 사방에 두고 있는 부류인 포목상과 옷감상 사이에서는 더욱 그러했다."], ["I made a venture or two among the lace folks and the milliners, and particularly at one shop where I got notice of two young women who were newly set up, and had not been bred to the trade.", "나는 레이스 장수들과 모자 장수들 사이에서 한두 번 모험을 했는데, 특히 새로 차린 데다 그 장사로 자란 사람들이 아닌 젊은 여자 둘이 있다는 것을 알게 된 어느 가게에서 그랬다."], ["There I think I carried off a piece of bone-lace, worth six or seven pounds, and a paper of thread.", "거기서 내 생각에 나는 예닐곱 파운드 값어치의 본레이스 한 조각과 실 한 꾸러미를 들고 나왔던 것 같다."], ["But this was but once; it was a trick that would not serve again.", "그러나 이것은 단 한 번뿐이었으니, 두 번은 통하지 않을 수법이었다."]], [["It was always reckoned a safe job when we heard of a new shop, and especially when the people were such as were not bred to shops.", "새 가게가 있다는 소문을 들으면, 특히 그 사람들이 가게 장사로 자란 사람들이 아닐 때는, 그것은 늘 안전한 일거리로 여겨졌다."], ["Such may depend upon it that they will be visited once or twice at their beginning, and they must be very sharp indeed if they can prevent it.", "그런 사람들은 시작할 무렵에 한두 번 봉변을 당하리라 믿어도 좋으며, 그것을 막을 수 있다면 그들은 정말로 매우 빈틈없는 사람임이 분명하다."]], [["I made another adventure or two, but they were but trifles too, though sufficient to live on.", "나는 한두 번 더 모험을 했으나, 그것들도 자질구레한 것이었고, 다만 먹고살기에는 충분했다."], ["After this nothing considerable offering for a good while, I began to think that I must give over the trade in earnest; but my governess, who was not willing to lose me, and expected great things of me, brought me one day into company with a young woman and a fellow that went for her husband, though as it appeared afterwards, she was not his wife, but they were partners, it seems, in the trade they carried on, and partners in something else.", "이 뒤로 한동안 이렇다 할 것이 나타나지 않자, 나는 정말로 이 장사를 그만두어야겠다고 생각하기 시작했다. 그러나 나를 잃고 싶어 하지 않고 나에게 큰 것을 기대하던 내 후견인이, 어느 날 나를 한 젊은 여자와 어울리게 했으니\n그 여자의 남편으로 통하던 사내가 하나 있었는데, 나중에 드러난 바로는 그녀는 그의 아내가 아니라 동업자였던 듯하니, 그들이 벌이는 장사의 동업자였고, 또 다른 일에서도 동업자였다."], ["In short, they robbed together, lay together, were taken together, and at last were hanged together.", "한마디로 그들은 함께 도둑질하고, 함께 자고, 함께 붙잡혔으며, 끝내 함께 교수형을 당했다."]], [["I came into a kind of league with these two by the help of my governess, and they carried me out into three or four adventures, where I rather saw them commit some coarse and unhandy robberies, in which nothing but a great stock of impudence on their side, and gross negligence on the people’s side who were robbed, could have made them successful.", "나는 후견인의 주선으로 이 두 사람과 일종의 동맹을 맺게 되었고, 그들은 나를 서너 번의 모험에 데리고 나갔는데, 거기서 나는 그들이 저지르는 거칠고 서툰 도둑질을 그저 지켜보았을 뿐이니, 그들 쪽의 어마어마한 뻔뻔함과 털리는 사람들 쪽의 지독한 부주의가 아니었더라면 결코 성공할 수 없었을 짓들이었다."], ["So I resolved from that time forward to be very cautious how I adventured upon anything with them; and indeed, when two or three unlucky projects were proposed by them, I declined the offer, and persuaded them against it.", "그래서 나는 그때부터 그들과 함께 무엇이든 도모하는 데 무척 신중하게 굴기로 마음먹었으며, 실제로 그들이 운수 사나운 계획을 두세 가지 내놓았을 때 나는 그 제안을 거절했고, 그들도 그만두도록 설득했다. 한번은 그들이 특별히, 낮 동안 눈여겨 보아두고 그것을 두는 자리까지 알아낸 어느 시계 장수에게서 금시계 세 개를 훔치자고 제안했다."], ["One time they particularly proposed robbing a watchmaker of three gold watches, which they had eyed in the daytime, and found the place where he laid them.", "그들 가운데 하나가 온갖 종류의 열쇠를 어찌나 많이 가졌던지, 그 시계 장수가 시계를 둔 곳을 여는 일쯤은 의심할 것도 없다고 했다."], ["One of them had so many keys of all kinds, that he made no question to open the place where the watchmaker had laid them; and so we made a kind of an appointment; but when I came to look narrowly into the thing, I found they proposed breaking open the house, and this, as a thing out of my way, I would not embark in, so they went without me.", "그리하여 우리는 일종의 약속을 잡았다. 그러나 내가 그 일을 자세히 들여다보니, 그들이 그 집을 부수고 들어가자는 것임을 알게 되었고, 이는 내 방식과는 어긋나는 일인지라 나는 거기에 끼어들려 하지 않았으므로, 그들은 나 없이 갔다."], ["They did get into the house by main force, and broke up the locked place where the watches were, but found but one of the gold watches, and a silver one, which they took, and got out of the house again very clear.", "그들은 힘으로 그 집에 들어가 시계가 든 잠긴 곳을 부수었으나, 금시계 하나와 은시계 하나밖에 찾지 못해 그것을 챙겼고, 다시 아주 깨끗이 집에서 빠져나왔다. 그러나 식구들이 놀라 \"도둑이야\""], ["But the family, being alarmed, cried out “Thieves,”", "하고 외치는 바람에, 사내는 쫓겨 붙잡혔다."], ["and the man was pursued and taken; the young woman had got off too, but unhappily was stopped at a distance, and the watches found upon her.", "젊은 여자도 빠져나오기는 했으나, 불행히도 멀리서 붙들렸고 그 몸에서 시계가 나왔다."], ["And thus I had a second escape, for they were convicted, and both hanged, being old offenders, though but young people.", "이리하여 나는 두 번째로 벗어났으니, 그들은 유죄판결을 받고 둘 다 교수형에 처해졌는데, 비록 나이는 젊었으나 상습범이었던 까닭이다."], ["As I said before that they robbed together and lay together, so now they hanged together, and there ended my new partnership.", "앞서 그들이 함께 도둑질하고 함께 잤다고 말한 대로, 이제 그들은 함께 교수형을 당했으며, 그렇게 나의 새로운 동업은 끝이 났다."]], [["I began now to be very wary, having so narrowly escaped a scouring, and having such an example before me; but I had a new tempter, who prompted me every day—I mean my governess; and now a prize presented, which as it came by her management, so she expected a good share of the booty.", "나는 그토록 아슬아슬하게 호된 일을 면한 데다 그런 본보기까지 눈앞에 둔 터라, 이제 무척 조심스러워지기 시작했다. 그러나 내게는 날마다 나를 부추기는 새로운 유혹자가 있었으니, 곧 내 후견인 말이다."], ["There was a good quantity of Flanders lace lodged in a private house, where she had gotten intelligence of it, and Flanders lace being prohibited, it was a good booty to any custom-house officer that could come at it.", "그리고 이제 한 건의 노획물이 나타났는데, 이것이 그녀의 주선으로 굴러들어 온 것인지라 그녀는 노획물의 좋은 몫을 기대하고 있었다. 어느 사사로운 집에 플랜더스 레이스가 상당량 보관되어 있다는 정보를 그녀가 얻었던 것인데, 플랜더스 레이스는 금지 품목이었으므로 그것에 손댈 수 있는 세관 관리에게는 좋은 노획물이었다."], ["I had a full account from my governess, as well of the quantity as of the very place where it was concealed, and I went to a custom-house officer, and told him I had such a discovery to make to him of such a quantity of lace, if he would assure me that I should have my due share of the reward.", "나는 후견인에게서 그 양과 더불어 그것이 숨겨진 바로 그 장소까지 소상히 들었고, 그래서 어느 세관 관리를 찾아가, 만일 내 정당한 상금 몫을 받게 되리라고 보장해 준다면 그만한 양의 레이스에 관해 이러이러한 정보를 알려 주겠다고 말했다."], ["This was so just an offer, that nothing could be fairer; so he agreed, and taking a constable and me with him, we beset the house.", "이는 더없이 정당한 제안이라 더 공정할 수 없었으므로, 그는 동의했고, 순경 한 명과 나를 데리고 가 그 집을 에워쌌다."], ["As I told him I could go directly to the place, he left it to me; and the hole being very dark, I squeezed myself into it, with a candle in my hand, and so reached the pieces out to him, taking care as I gave him some so to secure as much about myself as I could conveniently dispose of.", "내가 그 장소로 곧장 갈 수 있다고 말하자, 그는 그것을 내게 맡겼다. 구멍이 아주 어두웠으므로 나는 손에 촛불을 들고 그 안으로 비집고 들어가, 그에게 천 조각들을 꺼내 건넸는데, 몇 개를 건네면서도 내가 편히 처분할 수 있을 만큼은 내 몸에 챙겨 두도록 주의를 기울였다."], ["There was near £300 worth of lace in the hole, and I secured about £50 worth of it to myself.", "그 구멍에는 300파운드어치에 가까운 레이스가 있었고, 나는 그중 50파운드어치쯤을 내 것으로 챙겼다."], ["The people of the house were not owners of the lace, but a merchant who had entrusted them with it; so that they were not so surprised as I thought they would be.", "그 집 사람들은 레이스의 임자가 아니라, 그것을 그들에게 맡겨 둔 어느 상인이 임자였다. 그래서 그들은 내가 예상했던 만큼 놀라지는 않았다."]], [["I left the officer overjoyed with his prize, and fully satisfied with what he had got, and appointed to meet him at a house of his own directing, where I came after I had disposed of the cargo I had about me, of which he had not the least suspicion. When I came to him he began to capitulate with me, believing I did not understand the right I had to a share in the prize, and would fain have put me off with £20, but I let him know that I was not so ignorant as he supposed I was; and yet I was glad, too, that he offered to bring me to a certainty.", "나는 그 관리를 두고 나왔는데, 그는 노획물에 더없이 기뻐하며 자기가 챙긴 것에 흡족해했고, 자기가 일러 준 그 자신의 집에서 나와 만나기로 약속했다. 나는 내 몸에 지닌 짐을 처분한 뒤에 그리로 갔는데, 그는 그것에 대해 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 내가 그에게 갔을 때 그는, 내가 노획물의 몫에 대해 마땅히 누릴 권리를 모를 것이라 믿고서 나와 흥정을 시작하더니, 기어이 20파운드로 나를 떼어 버리려 했다. 그러나 나는 그가 짐작하는 만큼 무지하지 않다는 것을 그에게 알려 주었다. 그러면서도 그가 내게 확실한 금액을 제시해 주려 한 것이 다행스럽기도 했다."]], [["I asked £100, and he rose up to £30; I fell to £80, and he rose again to £40; in a word, he offered £50, and I consented, only demanding a piece of lace, which I thought came to about £8 or £9, as if it had been for my own wear, and he agreed to it. So I got £50 in money paid me that same night, and made an end of the bargain; nor did he ever know who I was, or where to inquire for me, so that if it had been discovered that part of the goods were embezzled, he could have made no challenge upon me for it.", "나는 100파운드를 요구했고, 그는 30파운드까지 올렸다. 나는 80파운드로 내려갔고, 그는 다시 40파운드로 올렸다. 한마디로, 그가 50파운드를 제시했고 나는 동의했는데, 다만 내가 쓰려는 것인 양 8, 9파운드쯤 되리라 짐작되는 레이스 한 조각을 요구했고, 그도 거기에 동의했다. 그리하여 나는 바로 그날 밤에 50파운드를 현금으로 받았고 그 거래를 끝냈다. 그는 내가 누구인지, 나를 어디서 수소문해야 하는지도 끝내 알지 못했으니, 설령 물품의 일부가 빼돌려진 것이 발각되었다 한들 그것을 두고 나를 추궁할 수 없었을 것이다."]], [["I very punctually divided this spoil with my governess, and I passed with her from this time for a very dexterous manager in the nicest cases.", "나는 이 노획물을 후견인과 아주 정확하게 나누었고, 이때부터 그녀에게 나는 가장 까다로운 일도 능숙하게 처리하는 솜씨꾼으로 통했다."], ["I found that this last was the best and easiest sort of work that was in my way, and I made it my business to inquire out prohibited goods, and after buying some, usually betrayed them, but none of these discoveries amounted to anything considerable, not like that I related just now; but I was willing to act safe, and was still cautious of running the great risks which I found others did, and in which they miscarried every day.", "나는 이 마지막 일이 내가 할 수 있는 일 가운데 가장 낫고 가장 손쉬운 종류임을 알게 되었고, 그래서 금지 품목을 알아내는 것을 내 일거리로 삼아, 더러 그것을 사들인 뒤 으레 밀고하곤 했으나, 이런 밀고들은 방금 이야기한 것처럼 대단한 액수에 이르지는 못했다. 다만 나는 안전하게 행동하기를 바랐고, 남들이 하다가 날마다 실패하는 것을 본 그 큰 위험을 무릅쓰는 일에는 여전히 조심스러웠다."]], [["The next thing of moment was an attempt at a gentlewoman’s good watch.", "다음으로 중요한 일은 어느 귀부인의 좋은 시계를 노린 시도였다."], ["It happened in a crowd, at a meeting-house, where I was in very great danger of being taken.", "그것은 어느 예배당에서 군중 속에서 일어났는데, 거기서 나는 붙잡힐 큰 위험에 처했다. 나는 그녀의 시계를 단단히 움켜쥐었으나, 누가 나를 그녀에게 떠밀기라도 한 듯 크게 부딪치며 그 순간에 시계를 한껏 당겼는데도, 그것이 빠지지 않는 것을 알고 그 즉시 손을 놓고는, 죽기라도 한 듯이 소리쳤다."], ["I had full hold of her watch, but giving a great jostle, as if somebody had thrust me against her, and in the juncture giving the watch a fair pull, I found it would not come, so I let it go that moment, and cried out as if I had been killed, that somebody had trod upon my foot, and that there were certainly pickpockets there, for somebody or other had given a pull at my watch; for you are to observe that on these adventures we always went very well dressed, and I had very good clothes on, and a gold watch by my side, as like a lady as other fold.", "누가 내 발을 밟았다고, 그리고 분명 여기에 소매치기가 있으니 누군가가 내 시계를 잡아당겼다고 외쳤던 것이다. 여러분이 알아 두어야 할 것은, 이런 모험에서 우리는 늘 옷을 아주 잘 차려입고 다녔다는 점이니, 나도 좋은 옷을 입고 옆구리에 금시계를 차서, 다른 사람들 못지않게 귀부인과 흡사했던 것이다."]], [["I had no sooner said so, but the other gentlewoman cried out “A pickpocket”", "내가 그렇게 말하기가 무섭게, 그 다른 귀부인도 \"소매치기야\""], ["too, for somebody, she said, had tried to pull her watch away.", "하고 외쳤으니, 누군가가 자기 시계를 잡아당기려 했다는 것이었다."]], [["When I touched her watch I was close to her, but when I cried out I stopped as it were short, and the crowd bearing her forward a little, she made a noise too, but it was at some distance from me, so that she did not in the least suspect me; but when she cried out “A pickpocket,”", "내가 그녀의 시계에 손댔을 때 나는 그녀에게 바싹 붙어 있었으나, 내가 소리쳤을 때는 말하자면 우뚝 멈추어 섰고, 군중이 그녀를 조금 앞으로 떠밀어 그녀도 소리를 냈는데, 그것은 나와는 다소 떨어진 곳이어서 그녀는 나를 조금도 의심하지 않았다. 그런데 그녀가 \"소매치기야\""], ["somebody cried, “Ay, and here has been another!", "하고 외치자, 누군가가 외치기를 \"그래, 여기도 또 한 사람 당했어!"], ["this gentlewoman has been attempted too.”", "이 귀부인도 당할 뻔했다고\" 했다."]], [["At that very instance, a little farther in the crowd, and very luckily too, they cried out “A pickpocket,”", "바로 그 순간에, 군중 속에서 조금 더 안쪽에서, 또한 아주 운 좋게도, 사람들이 다시 \"소매치기야\""], ["again, and really seized a young fellow in the very act.", "하고 외치며 정말로 한 젊은 사내를 현행범으로 붙들었다."], ["This, though unhappy for the wretch, was very opportunely for my case, though I had carried it off handsomely enough before; but now it was out of doubt, and all the loose part of the crowd ran that way, and the poor boy was delivered up to the rage of the street, which is a cruelty I need not describe, and which, however, they are always glad of, rather than to be sent to Newgate, where they lie often a long time, till they are almost perished, and sometimes they are hanged, and the best they can look for, if they are convicted, is to be transported.", "이는 그 가엾은 놈에게는 불운한 일이었으나 내 처지에는 더없이 때맞은 일이었으니, 비록 그 전에도 이미 내가 그 일을 제법 솜씨 있게 넘기긴 했어도, 이제는 의심의 여지가 없어졌고, 군중 가운데 떠도는 무리가 모두 그쪽으로 달려갔으며, 그 가엾은 소년은 길거리의 분노에 내맡겨졌으니, 그것은 내가 굳이 묘사할 필요 없는 잔혹함이거니와, 그런데도 그들은 뉴게이트로 보내지는 것보다는 차라리 그쪽을 늘 반긴다. 뉴게이트에서는 거의 다 죽어 갈 때까지 오래 갇혀 있기 일쑤이고, 때로는 교수형을 당하며, 유죄판결을 받을 경우 그들이 바랄 수 있는 가장 나은 것이라야 유배형이기 때문이다."]], [["This was a narrow escape to me, and I was so frighted that I ventured no more at gold watches a great while.", "이는 내게 아슬아슬한 모면이었고, 나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 한참 동안 다시는 금시계에 손대지 못했다."], ["There was indeed a great many concurring circumstances in this adventure which assisted to my escape; but the chief was, that the woman whose watch I had pulled at was a fool; that is to say, she was ignorant of the nature of the attempt, which one would have thought she should not have been, seeing she was wise enough to fasten her watch so that it could not be slipped up.", "사실 이 모험에는 내가 벗어나는 데 도움을 준 여러 정황이 겹쳐 있었으나, 가장 큰 것은 내가 시계를 잡아당겼던 그 여자가 어리석은 사람이었다는 점이다. 다시 말해, 그녀는 그런 시도의 본질을 몰랐는데, 시계를 슬쩍 빼낼 수 없도록 단단히 매어 둘 만큼 영리했던 것을 보면, 모르리라고는 생각하기 어려운 일이었다."], ["But she was in such a fright that she had no thought about her proper for the discovery; for she, when she felt the pull, screamed out, and pushed herself forward, and put all the people about her into disorder, but said not a word of her watch, or of a pickpocket, for at least two minutes’ time, which was time enough for me, and to spare.", "그러나 그녀는 어찌나 겁에 질렸던지, 범인을 가려내는 데 알맞은 생각이라곤 도무지 하지 못했다. 그녀는 당기는 것을 느끼자 비명을 지르며 앞으로 떠밀고 나가 주위 사람들을 온통 혼란에 빠뜨렸을 뿐, 적어도 두 사람 정도 셀 동안은 자기 시계에 대해서도, 소매치기에 대해서도 한마디 하지 않았으니, 그것은 내게 충분하고도 남는 시간이었다."], ["For as I had cried out behind her, as I have said, and bore myself back in the crowd as she bore forward, there were several people, at least seven or eight, the throng being still moving on, that were got between me and her in that time, and then I crying out “A pickpocket,”", "앞서 말했듯이 나는 그녀의 뒤에서 소리쳤고, 그녀가 앞으로 떠밀려 가는 동안 나는 군중 속에서 뒤로 물러났으므로, 그 시간 동안 사람들이, 적어도 일고여덟은, 여전히 움직이는 군중 속에서 나와 그녀 사이에 끼어들게 되었다. 그러고 나서 내가 그녀보다 오히려 먼저, 적어도 그만큼 빨리 \"소매치기야\""], ["rather sooner than she, or at least as soon, she might as well be the person suspected as I, and the people were confused in their inquiry; whereas, had she with a presence of mind needful on such an occasion, as soon as she felt the pull, not screamed out as she did, but turned immediately round and seized the next body that was behind her, she had infallibly taken me.", "하고 외쳤으니, 나만큼이나 그녀도 의심받는 사람이 될 수 있었고, 사람들은 누구를 찾아야 할지 갈팡질팡하게 되었다. 반면, 만일 그녀가 그런 경우에 필요한 침착함을 지녀, 당기는 것을 느끼자마자 그렇게 비명을 지르는 대신 곧장 돌아서서 바로 뒤에 있는 사람을 붙들었더라면, 그녀는 틀림없이 나를 잡았을 것이다."]], [["This is a direction not of the kindest sort to the fraternity, but ’tis certainly a key to the clue of a pickpocket’s motions, and whoever can follow it will as certainly catch the thief as he will be sure to miss if he does not.", "이는 그 패거리에게 그리 친절한 종류의 안내는 못 되지만, 소매치기의 동작을 푸는 단서의 열쇠임은 분명하니, 이를 따를 줄 아는 자라면 누구든 도둑을 틀림없이 잡을 것이요, 따르지 못하면 틀림없이 놓칠 것이다."]], [["I had another adventure, which puts this matter out of doubt, and which may be an instruction for posterity in the case of a pickpocket. My good old governess, to give a short touch at her history, though she had left off the trade, was, as I may say, born a pickpocket, and, as I understood afterwards, had run through all the several degrees of that art, and yet had never been taken but once, when she was so grossly detected, that she was convicted and ordered to be transported; but being a woman of a rare tongue, and withal having money in her pocket, she found means, the ship putting into Ireland for provisions, to get on shore there, where she lived and practised her old trade for some years; when falling into another sort of bad company, she turned midwife and procuress, and played a hundred pranks there, which she gave me a little history of in confidence between us as we grew more intimate; and it was to this wicked creature that I owed all the art and dexterity I arrived to, in which there were few that ever went beyond me, or that practised so long without any misfortune.", "내게는 또 다른 모험이 있었으니, 이것이 이 일을 의심의 여지 없게 만들어 주며, 소매치기의 경우에 대해 후세에 교훈이 될 만하다. 나의 착하고 늙은 후견인은 그 내력을 잠깐 짚어 두자면, 비록 그 장사에서는 손을 뗐어도, 말하자면 타고난 소매치기였고, 나중에 들어 알게 된 바로는 그 기술의 온갖 단계를 두루 거쳤으면서도 단 한 번밖에 붙잡힌 적이 없었는데, 그때 어찌나 호되게 발각되었던지 유죄판결을 받고 유배형에 처해질 처지였다. 그러나 보기 드물게 입담이 좋은 여자인 데다 주머니에 돈까지 지니고 있었던 터라, 배가 식량을 싣느라 아일랜드에 들렀을 때 그곳에 상륙할 방도를 찾아냈고, 거기서 몇 해 동안 살면서 옛 장사를 했다. 그러다가 또 다른 종류의 나쁜 무리에 끼어들어 산파 겸 포주가 되었고, 거기서 백 가지 농간을 부렸는데, 우리가 차츰 친해지면서 그녀는 우리 사이의 은밀한 이야기로 그 내력을 조금 들려주었다. 그리고 내가 다다른 그 모든 기술과 솜씨는 바로 이 사악한 여자 덕분이었으니, 그 기술에서 나를 넘어선 자도, 아무런 불운 없이 그토록 오래 그 짓을 한 자도 거의 없었다."]], [["It was after those adventures in Ireland, and when she was pretty well known in that country, that she left Dublin and came over to England, where, the time of her transportation being not expired, she left her former trade, for fear of falling into bad hands again, for then she was sure to have gone to wreck.", "그녀가 더블린을 떠나 잉글랜드로 건너온 것은 아일랜드에서 그런 모험을 겪고 그 나라에서 제법 이름이 알려진 뒤였으니, 잉글랜드에서는 유배 기간이 아직 끝나지 않은 터라 다시 나쁜 손에 떨어질까 두려워 이전의 장사를 그만두었다. 그랬다가는 영락없이 파멸할 것이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["Here she set up the same trade she had followed in Ireland, in which she soon, by her admirable management and good tongue, arrived to the height which I have already described, and indeed began to be rich, though her trade fell off again afterwards, as I have hinted before.", "여기서 그녀는 아일랜드에서 따르던 것과 똑같은 장사를 차렸는데, 그 일에서 곧 그 감탄할 만한 수완과 좋은 입담으로, 내가 이미 묘사한 그 정점에 다다라, 실로 부자가 되기 시작했으나, 앞서 넌지시 비친 대로 그 장사는 나중에 다시 기울었다."]], [["I mentioned thus much of the history of this woman here, the better to account for the concern she had in the wicked life I was now leading, into all the particulars of which she led me, as it were, by the hand, and gave me such directions, and I so well followed them, that I grew the greatest artist of my time and worked myself out of every danger with such dexterity, that when several more of my comrades ran themselves into Newgate presently, and by that time they had been half a year at the trade, I had now practised upwards of five years, and the people at Newgate did not so much as know me; they had heard much of me indeed, and often expected me there, but I always got off, though many times in the extremest danger.", "내가 여기서 이 여자의 내력을 이만큼 언급한 것은, 그녀가 내가 이제 이끌어 가던 그 사악한 삶에 관여한 바를 더 잘 설명하기 위해서이니, 그녀는 그 온갖 세세한 데까지 말하자면 내 손을 잡고 나를 이끌었고, 또 어찌나 잘 일러 주었던지, 나도 그것을 어찌나 잘 따랐던지, 나는 내 시대의 가장 위대한 솜씨꾼이 되어, 어떤 위험에서든 솜씨 좋게 빠져나왔다. 그리하여 내 동료들 가운데 여럿이 그 장사를 한 지 반년 만에 곧장 뉴게이트로 끌려들어 갈 때, 나는 이제 오 년 넘게 그 짓을 해 왔건만 뉴게이트의 사람들은 나를 알지조차 못했다. 사실 그들은 내 소문을 많이 들었고 종종 내가 거기 오리라 기다렸으나, 나는 여러 번 극도의 위험에 처하면서도 늘 빠져나갔다."]], [["One of the greatest dangers I was now in, was that I was too well known among the trade, and some of them, whose hatred was owing rather to envy than any injury I had done them, began to be angry that I should always escape when they were always catched and hurried to Newgate.", "내가 이제 처한 가장 큰 위험 가운데 하나는, 내가 그 장사판에서 너무 잘 알려졌다는 것이었으니, 그들 가운데 더러는, 내가 그들에게 끼친 어떤 해 때문이라기보다 오히려 시샘 탓에 나를 미워하던 자들이, 자기들은 늘 붙잡혀 뉴게이트로 끌려가는데 나는 늘 빠져나가는 것에 화를 내기 시작했다."], ["These were they that gave me the name of Moll Flanders; for it was no more of affinity with my real name or with any of the name I had ever gone by, than black is of kin to white, except that once, as before, I called myself Mrs.", "내게 몰 플랜더스라는 이름을 붙여 준 것이 바로 이들이었으니, 그 이름은 내 본명이나 내가 여태껏 써 온 어떤 이름과도, 검은빛이 흰빛과 닮지 않은 만큼이나 아무런 연고가 없었는데, 다만 앞서처럼 한 번, 곧 내가 민트에 몸을 숨겼을 때 나 자신을 플랜더스 부인이라 일컬은 적은 있었다."], ["Flanders; when I sheltered myself in the Mint; but that these rogues never knew, nor could I ever learn how they came to give me the name, or what the occasion of it was.", "그러나 그것을 이 악당들은 전혀 알지 못했고, 그들이 어떻게 그 이름을 붙이게 되었는지, 무슨 계기였는지 나도 끝내 알 수 없었다."]], [["I was soon informed that some of these who were gotten fast into Newgate had vowed to impeach me; and as I knew that two or three of them were but too able to do it, I was under a great concern about it, and kept within doors for a good while. But my governess—whom I always made partner in my success, and who now played a sure game with me, for that she had a share of the gain and no share in the hazard—I say, my governess was something impatient of my leading such a useless, unprofitable life, as she called it; and she laid a new contrivance for my going abroad, and this was to dress me up in men’s clothes, and so put me into a new kind of practice.", "뉴게이트에 단단히 갇힌 자들 가운데 더러가 나를 고발하겠노라 다짐했다는 것을 나는 곧 알게 되었다. 그들 가운데 두셋은 그럴 만한 능력이 너무도 충분하다는 것을 내가 알았던 터라, 나는 그 일로 크게 근심하여 한동안 집 안에 틀어박혀 있었다. 그러나 내 후견인은 내가 늘 그 성공의 동업자로 삼았던 사람이요, 이제 나와 더불어 안전한 노름을 하던 사람이었으니, 이득에는 몫이 있으되 위험에는 몫이 없었기 때문이다. 말하자면 내 후견인은 내가 그녀의 표현대로 그토록 쓸모없고 이문도 없는 삶을 사는 데 다소 안달이 났던 것이다. 그래서 그녀는 내가 바깥에 나갈 새로운 계책을 꾸몄으니, 그것은 나를 남자 옷으로 차려입혀 새로운 종류의 일에 투입하는 것이었다."]], [["I was tall and personable, but a little too smooth-faced for a man; however, I seldom went abroad but in the night, it did well enough; but it was a long time before I could behave in my new clothes—I mean, as to my craft. It was impossible to be so nimble, so ready, so dexterous at these things in a dress so contrary to nature; and I did everything clumsily, so I had neither the success nor the easiness of escape that I had before, and I resolved to leave it off; but that resolution was confirmed soon after by the following accident.", "나는 키가 크고 풍채가 좋았으나, 남자치고는 얼굴이 조금 너무 매끈했다. 그래도 나는 좀처럼 밤에밖에는 나가지 않았으므로 그런대로 잘 되었다. 그러나 새 옷차림으로 처신하는 데는, 내 말은 내 솜씨에 관한 한, 오랜 시간이 걸렸다. 본성에 그토록 어긋나는 옷차림으로는 이런 일에서 그토록 날래고, 그토록 재빠르고, 그토록 능숙하기가 불가능했고, 나는 무엇이든 서투르게 했으니, 예전에 누리던 성공도, 빠져나오는 수월함도 누리지 못했다. 그래서 나는 그것을 그만두기로 마음먹었는데, 그 결심은 곧이어 다음의 사건으로 굳어졌다."]], [["As my governess disguised me like a man, so she joined me with a man, a young fellow that was nimble enough at his business, and for about three weeks we did very well together.", "내 후견인은 나를 남자처럼 변장시켰을 뿐 아니라, 자기 일에 제법 날랜 어느 젊은 사내와 나를 짝지어 주었고, 약 삼 주 동안 우리는 함께 아주 잘 해 나갔다."], ["Our principal trade was watching shopkeepers’ counters, and slipping off any kind of goods we could see carelessly laid anywhere, and we made several good bargains, as we called them, at this work.", "우리의 주된 장사는 가게 주인들의 계산대를 살피다가, 아무 데나 부주의하게 놓인 온갖 물건을 슬쩍하는 것이었고, 우리는 이 일로, 우리가 부르는 대로 하자면, 여러 번 좋은 거래를 했다."], ["And as we kept always together, so we grew very intimate, yet he never knew that I was not a man, nay, though I several times went home with him to his lodgings, according as our business directed, and four or five times lay with him all night.", "그리고 우리는 늘 함께 다녔으므로 무척 친밀해졌으나, 그는 내가 남자가 아님을 끝내 알지 못했다. 아니, 내가 일의 사정에 따라 여러 번 그를 따라 그의 숙소로 가, 너덧 번은 그와 밤새 함께 잤는데도 그러했다."], ["But our design lay another way, and it was absolutely necessary to me to conceal my sex from him, as appeared afterwards.", "그러나 우리의 속셈은 다른 데 있었고, 나로서는 그에게 내 성별을 감추는 것이 절대적으로 필요했으니, 그것은 나중에 드러났다."], ["The circumstances of our living, coming in late, and having such and such business to do as required that nobody should be trusted with the coming into our lodgings, were such as made it impossible to me to refuse lying with him, unless I would have owned my sex; and as it was, I effectually concealed myself.", "우리의 생활 형편, 곧 늦게 돌아오는 것, 또 누구도 우리 숙소에 드나드는 것을 믿고 맡길 수 없게 하는 이런저런 일을 해야 했던 사정은, 내가 내 성별을 인정하지 않는 한 그와 함께 자기를 거절하기란 불가능하게 만들었다. 그리고 그런 형편에서 나는 나 자신을 감쪽같이 감추었다."], ["But his ill, and my good fortune, soon put an end to this life, which I must own I was sick of too, on several other accounts.", "그러나 그의 불운과 나의 행운이 곧 이 삶에 종지부를 찍었으니, 사실 나도 여러 다른 이유로 이 삶에 진저리가 났음을 인정해야겠다."], ["We had made several prizes in this new way of business, but the last would be extraordinary.", "우리는 이 새로운 방식의 장사로 여러 건의 노획물을 챙겼으나, 마지막 것은 유난한 것이 될 참이었다."], ["There was a shop in a certain street which had a warehouse behind it that looked into another street, the house making the corner of the turning.", "어느 거리에 가게가 하나 있었는데, 그 뒤로 다른 거리 쪽으로 면한 창고가 있어, 그 집이 모퉁이를 이루고 있었다."]], [["Through the window of the warehouse we saw, lying on the counter or showboard, which was just before it, five pieces of silks, besides other stuffs, and though it was almost dark, yet the people, being busy in the fore-shop with customers, had not had time to shut up those windows, or else had forgot it.", "창고의 창문을 통해, 그 바로 앞에 있는 계산대 혹은 진열대 위에 다른 천들과 더불어 비단 다섯 필이 놓여 있는 것이 보였는데, 거의 어두워졌는데도 사람들이 앞쪽 가게에서 손님을 보느라 분주해 그 창문들을 닫을 틈이 없었거나, 아니면 잊어버린 모양이었다."]], [["This the young fellow was so overjoyed with, that he could not restrain himself.", "이것에 그 젊은 사내는 어찌나 기뻐했던지 자신을 억누르지 못했다."], ["It lay all within his reach he said, and he swore violently to me that he would have it, if he broke down the house for it.", "그것이 죄다 손 닿는 데 있다고 그는 말했고, 집을 부수어서라도 그것을 손에 넣겠다고 내게 사납게 맹세했다."], ["I dissuaded him a little, but saw there was no remedy; so he ran rashly upon it, slipped out a square of the sash window dexterously enough, and without noise, and got out four pieces of the silks, and came with them towards me, but was immediately pursued with a terrible clutter and noise.", "나는 조금 말렸으나 어쩔 도리가 없음을 알았다. 그래서 그는 무모하게 달려들어, 미닫이창의 한 칸을 제법 솜씨 좋게, 소리도 없이 빼내고는 비단 네 필을 꺼내 그것을 들고 내 쪽으로 왔으나, 곧장 무시무시한 소동과 소음과 함께 추격당했다."], ["We were standing together indeed, but I had not taken any of the goods out of his hand, when I said to him hastily, “You are undone, fly, for God’s sake!”", "우리는 사실 함께 서 있었으나, 내가 그의 손에서 물건을 하나도 받지 않았을 때, 나는 그에게 다급히 말했다. \"넌 끝장났어, 제발 도망쳐!\""], ["He ran like lightning, and I too, but the pursuit was hotter after him because he had the goods, than after me.", "그는 번개처럼 달렸고, 나도 그러했으나, 그가 물건을 지녔던 탓에 추격은 나보다 그를 향해 더 거셌다."], ["He dropped two of the pieces, which stopped them a little, but the crowd increased and pursued us both.", "그는 비단 두 필을 떨어뜨려 그들을 잠시 멈칫하게 했으나, 군중은 불어나 우리 둘을 다 쫓았다."], ["They took him soon after with the other two pieces upon him, and then the rest followed me.", "그들은 곧 그를, 나머지 두 필을 지닌 채로 붙들었고, 그런 다음 나머지가 나를 쫓아왔다."], ["I ran for it and got into my governess’s house whither some quick-eyed people followed me so warmly as to fix me there.", "나는 죽어라 달려 후견인의 집으로 들어갔는데, 눈썰미 좋은 자들 몇이 어찌나 바싹 쫓아왔던지 나를 거기에 들어가는 것까지 봐 두었다."], ["They did not immediately knock, at the door, by which I got time to throw off my disguise and dress me in my own clothes; besides, when they came there, my governess, who had her tale ready, kept her door shut, and called out to them and told them there was no man come in there.", "그들이 곧장 문을 두드리지는 않은 덕에 나는 변장을 벗어던지고 내 옷으로 갈아입을 시간을 벌었다. 게다가 그들이 거기에 이르렀을 때, 미리 둘러댈 말을 준비해 둔 내 후견인이 문을 닫아 둔 채 그들에게 큰 소리로, 거기 들어온 남자는 없다고 말했다."], ["The people affirmed there did a man come in there, and swore they would break open the door.", "사람들은 분명히 남자가 거기 들어갔다고 우기며, 문을 부수고 들어가겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."]], [["My governess, not at all surprised, spoke calmly to them, told them they should very freely come and search her house, if they should bring a constable, and let in none but such as the constable would admit, for it was unreasonable to let in a whole crowd.", "내 후견인은 조금도 당황하지 않고 그들에게 차분히 말하기를, 순경을 데려온다면 자기 집을 아주 기꺼이 와서 뒤져도 좋으나, 순경이 들이는 자 외에는 아무도 들이지 않겠다고 했으니, 군중 전부를 들이는 것은 도리에 어긋나기 때문이라고 했다. 비록 그들이 군중이긴 했으나, 이를 그들도 거절할 수 없었다."], ["This they could not refuse, though they were a crowd.", "그리하여 곧장 순경이 불려 왔고, 그녀는 아주 기꺼이 문을 열어 주었다."], ["So a constable was fetched immediately, and she very freely opened the door; the constable kept the door, and the men he appointed searched the house, my governess going with them from room to room.", "순경이 문을 지키고, 그가 지정한 자들이 집을 뒤졌으며, 내 후견인은 방마다 그들을 따라다녔다. 내 방에 이르자 그녀는 나를 불러 큰 소리로 말했다."], ["When she came to my room she called to me, and said aloud, “Cousin, pray open the door; here’s some gentlemen that must come and look into your room.”", "\"사촌, 어서 문 좀 열어요. 여기 신사분들이 사촌 방을 들여다봐야 한다는군요.\""]], [["I had a little girl with me, which was my governess’s grandchild, as she called her; and I bade her open the door, and there sat I at work with a great litter of things about me, as if I had been at work all day, being myself quite undressed, with only night-clothes on my head, and a loose morning-gown wrapped about me.", "내게는 어린 계집아이가 하나 같이 있었는데, 후견인이 부르는 대로 하자면 그녀의 손녀였다. 나는 그 아이에게 문을 열라고 일렀고, 거기에 나는 온갖 물건을 잔뜩 늘어놓은 채 마치 하루 종일 일이라도 하고 있었던 양 앉아 있었으니, 나 자신은 옷도 제대로 갖춰 입지 않고 머리에 잠잘 때 쓰는 것만 두른 채, 헐렁한 아침 가운을 몸에 두르고 있었다."], ["My governess made a kind of excuse for their disturbing me, telling me partly the occasion of it, and that she had no remedy but to open the doors to them, and let them satisfy themselves, for all she could say to them would not satisfy them.", "내 후견인은 그들이 나를 방해한 데 대해 일종의 변명을 하며, 그 까닭을 내게 얼마간 일러 주고, 그들에게 문을 열어 주어 그들이 스스로 만족하게 하는 수밖에 없으니, 자기가 무슨 말을 해도 그들이 만족하지 않을 것이라고 했다."], ["I sat still, and bid them search the room if they pleased, for if there was anybody in the house, I was sure they were not in my room; and as for the rest of the house, I had nothing to say to that, I did not understand what they looked for.", "나는 가만히 앉아, 원한다면 방을 뒤지라고 일렀으니, 집 안에 누가 있다 한들 내 방에는 없는 것이 분명하기 때문이라 했다. 그리고 집의 나머지에 대해서는 나는 할 말이 없으며, 그들이 무엇을 찾는지 알지 못한다고 했다."]], [["Everything looked so innocent and so honest about me, that they treated me civiller than I expected, but it was not till they had searched the room to a nicety, even under the bed, in the bed, and everywhere else where it was possible anything could be hid.", "내 주위의 모든 것이 어찌나 결백하고 정직해 보였던지, 그들은 내가 예상했던 것보다 더 정중하게 나를 대했으나, 그것은 그들이 방을, 침대 밑이며 침대 속이며 무엇이든 숨길 수 있는 다른 모든 곳까지 샅샅이 뒤진 뒤의 일이었다."], ["When they had done this, and could find nothing, they asked my pardon for troubling me, and went down.", "그들은 이렇게 하고도 아무것도 찾아내지 못하자, 나를 성가시게 한 데 대해 용서를 구하고 아래층으로 내려갔다."]], [["When they had thus searched the house from bottom to top, and then top to bottom, and could find nothing, they appeased the mob pretty well; but they carried my governess before the justice.", "그들이 이렇게 집을 아래에서 위로, 다시 위에서 아래로 뒤지고도 아무것도 찾아내지 못하자, 군중을 제법 잘 달랬다. 그러나 그들은 내 후견인을 치안판사 앞으로 데려갔다."], ["Two men swore that they saw the man whom they pursued go into her house.", "두 사내가 자기들이 쫓던 그 남자가 그녀의 집으로 들어가는 것을 보았다고 맹세했다."], ["My governess rattled and made a great noise that her house should be insulted, and that she should be used thus for nothing; that if a man did come in, he might go out again presently for aught she knew, for she was ready to make oath that no man had been within her doors all that day as she knew of (and that was very true indeed); that it might be indeed that as she was abovestairs, any fellow in a fright might find the door open and run in for shelter when he was pursued, but that she knew nothing of it; and if it had been so, he certainly went out again, perhaps at the other door, for she had another door into an alley, and so had made his escape and cheated them all.", "내 후견인은 자기 집이 모욕당하고 자기가 아무 까닭 없이 이런 취급을 받는 데 대해 떠들썩하게 야단법석을 떨었으니, 설령 남자가 들어왔다 한들 자기가 알기로는 곧장 다시 나갔을 수도 있으며, 자기가 아는 한 그날 하루 종일 자기 집 문 안에 든 남자는 없었다고 맹세할 수 있다(그리고 그것은 실로 사실이었다)고 했다. 자기가 위층에 있는 동안 겁에 질린 어떤 사내가 쫓기다가 문이 열린 것을 보고 피신하러 뛰어들었을 수는 있겠으나 자기는 그것을 전혀 모르며, 설령 그러했다 한들 그는 분명 다시, 어쩌면 다른 문으로 나갔을 것이니, 자기 집에는 골목으로 통하는 다른 문이 또 있어, 그렇게 빠져나가 그들 모두를 따돌렸으리라고 했다."]], [["This was indeed probable enough, and the justice satisfied himself with giving her an oath that she had not received or admitted any man into her house to conceal him, or protect or hide him from justice.", "이것은 사실 제법 그럴듯했고, 치안판사는 그녀에게, 누군가를 숨겨 주거나, 보호하거나, 법망에서 가려 주기 위해 자기 집에 어떤 남자도 받아들이거나 들이지 않았다는 맹세를 받는 것으로 만족했다."], ["This oath she might justly take, and did so, and so she was dismissed.", "이 맹세는 그녀가 정당하게 할 수 있는 것이었고, 또 그렇게 했으므로, 그녀는 방면되었다."]], [["It is easy to judge what a fright I was in upon this occasion, and it was impossible for my governess ever to bring me to dress in that disguise again; for, as I told her, I should certainly betray myself.", "이 일로 내가 얼마나 겁에 질렸는지는 짐작하기 어렵지 않으니, 내 후견인은 결코 다시는 나를 그런 변장으로 차려입게 할 수 없었다. 내가 그녀에게 말했듯이, 나는 틀림없이 내 정체를 들킬 것이기 때문이었다."], ["My poor partner in this mischief was now in a bad case, for he was carried away before my Lord Mayor, and by his worship committed to Newgate, and the people that took him were so willing, as well as able, to prosecute him, that they offered themselves to enter into recognisances to appear at the sessions and pursue the charge against him.", "이 못된 짓의 가엾은 내 동업자는 이제 딱한 처지에 놓였으니, 그는 시장 각하 앞으로 끌려가 그 각하에 의해 뉴게이트에 수감되었고, 그를 붙든 사람들은 그를 기소할 의향도 능력도 어찌나 충분했던지, 자진하여 개정 기일에 출두해 그에 대한 고소를 밀고 나가겠노라 서약하겠다고 나섰다."]], [["However, he got his indictment deferred, upon promise to discover his accomplices, and particularly the man that was concerned with him in his robbery; and he failed not to do his endeavour, for he gave in my name, whom he called Gabriel Spencer, which was the name I went by to him; and here appeared the wisdom of my concealing my name and sex from him, which, if he had ever known I had been undone.", "그러나 그는 자기 공범들을, 특히 그 도둑질에 자기와 함께 가담한 사내를 밝히겠다는 약속으로 자기의 기소를 미루었고, 또 그 일에 애쓰기를 마다하지 않았으니, 그가 내 이름을 댔는데, 그는 나를 그에게 통하던 이름인 게이브리얼 스펜서라고 불렀다. 여기서 내가 그에게 내 이름과 성별을 감춘 지혜가 드러났으니, 만일 그가 그것을 알았더라면 나는 끝장났을 것이다."]], [["He did all he could to discover this Gabriel Spencer; he described me, he discovered the place where he said I lodged, and, in a word, all the particulars that he could of my dwelling; but having concealed the main circumstances of my sex from him, I had a vast advantage, and he never could hear of me.", "그는 이 게이브리얼 스펜서를 밝혀내려 할 수 있는 모든 짓을 다 했다. 그는 나를 묘사하고, 자기가 말하는 내 숙소의 장소를 밝혔으며, 한마디로 내가 사는 곳에 대해 알 수 있는 온갖 세세한 것을 밝혔다."], ["He brought two or three families into trouble by his endeavouring to find me out, but they knew nothing of me, any more than that I had a fellow with me that they had seen, but knew nothing of.", "그러나 내가 그에게 성별이라는 핵심 정황을 감추었던 터라, 나는 막대한 이점이 있었고, 그는 끝내 내 소식을 들을 수 없었다. 그는 나를 찾아내려는 노력으로 두세 가구를 곤경에 빠뜨렸으나, 그들은 나에 대해, 내게 자기들이 본 적 있는 사내 하나가 같이 있었다는 것 외에는 아무것도 몰랐고, 그조차도 알지 못했다."], ["And as for my governess, though she was the means of his coming to me, yet it was done at second-hand, and he knew nothing of her.", "그리고 내 후견인으로 말하자면, 비록 그녀가 그를 내게 데려온 매개이긴 했으나, 그것은 한 다리 건너 이루어진 일이라 그는 그녀에 대해 아무것도 몰랐다."]], [["This turned to his disadvantage; for having promised discoveries, but not being able to make it good, it was looked upon as trifling with the justice of the city, and he was the more fiercely pursued by the shopkeepers who took him.", "이는 그에게 불리하게 돌아갔으니, 밝혀내겠다고 약속해 놓고도 그것을 이루지 못하자, 그것이 이 도시의 사법을 우롱하는 것으로 여겨졌고, 그래서 그는 자기를 붙든 가게 주인들에게 더욱 사납게 추궁당했다."]], [["I was, however, terribly uneasy all this while, and that I might be quite out of the way, I went away from my governess’s for a while; but not knowing wither to wander, I took a maid-servant with me, and took the stage-coach to Dunstable, to my old landlord and landlady, where I had lived so handsomely with my Lancashire husband.", "그러나 나는 이 내내 끔찍하게 불안했으니, 아예 멀찍이 떨어져 있고자, 한동안 내 후견인의 집을 떠났다. 그러나 어디로 떠돌아야 할지 몰라, 하녀 하나를 데리고 역마차를 타고 던스터블의, 옛 집주인 내외에게로 갔으니, 거기서 나는 내 랭커셔 남편과 그토록 풍족하게 살았던 것이다."], ["Here I told her a formal story, that I expected my husband every day from Ireland, and that I had sent a letter to him that I would meet him at Dunstable at her house, and that he would certainly land, if the wind was fair, in a few days, so that I was come to spend a few days with them till he should come, for he was either come post, or in the West Chester coach, I knew not which; but whichsoever it was, he would be sure to come to that house to meet me.", "여기서 나는 그녀에게 그럴듯한 이야기를 늘어놓았으니, 내가 날마다 남편이 아일랜드에서 오기를 기다리고 있으며, 그에게 던스터블의 그녀 집에서 만나자고 편지를 보냈고, 바람만 순조롭다면 그가 며칠 안에 틀림없이 상륙할 것이므로, 그가 올 때까지 그들과 며칠 묵으러 왔노라고 했다. 그가 역마로 오는지, 아니면 웨스트 체스터 마차로 오는지는 모르겠으나, 어느 쪽이든 그는 틀림없이 나를 만나러 그 집으로 올 것이라고 했다."]], [["My landlady was mighty glad to see me, and my landlord made such a stir with me, that if I had been a princess I could not have been better used, and here I might have been welcome a month or two if I had thought fit.", "내 집주인 여자는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐했고, 집주인 남자는 나를 어찌나 떠받들었던지, 내가 공주였다 한들 그보다 더 나은 대접을 받지는 못했을 것이며, 내가 마음만 먹었다면 한두 달은 환대받을 수 있었을 것이다."]], [["But my business was of another nature.", "그러나 내 볼일은 다른 성질의 것이었다."], ["I was very uneasy (though so well disguised that it was scarce possible to detect me) lest this fellow should somehow or other find me out; and though he could not charge me with this robbery, having persuaded him not to venture, and having also done nothing in it myself but run away, yet he might have charged me with other things, and have bought his own life at the expense of mine.", "나는 무척 불안했으니(비록 들킬 가능성이 거의 없을 만큼 잘 변장하긴 했어도), 이 사내가 어떻게든 나를 찾아낼까 두려웠던 것이다. 그가 나더러 모험에 나서지 말라고 설득했고, 또 나 자신은 그 일에서 달아난 것 말고는 아무것도 하지 않았던 터라, 비록 그가 나를 이 도둑질로 고발할 수는 없었어도, 다른 일들로 나를 고발하여 내 목숨을 대가로 자기 목숨을 살 수는 있었을 것이다."]], [["This filled me with horrible apprehensions.", "이것이 나를 끔찍한 불안으로 채웠다."], ["I had no recourse, no friend, no confidante but my old governess, and I knew no remedy but to put my life in her hands, and so I did, for I let her know where to send to me, and had several letters from her while I stayed here.", "내게는 의지할 데도, 벗도, 늙은 후견인 외에는 속을 터놓을 사람도 없었으므로, 나는 내 목숨을 그녀의 손에 맡기는 수밖에 다른 방도를 알지 못했고, 그래서 그렇게 했으니, 그녀에게 내게 연락할 곳을 알려 두었고, 내가 여기 머무는 동안 그녀에게서 여러 통의 편지를 받았다."], ["Some of them almost scared me out my wits but at last she sent me the joyful news that he was hanged, which was the best news to me that I had heard a great while.", "그중 더러는 나를 거의 혼비백산하게 했으나, 마침내 그녀는 그가 교수형을 당했다는 기쁜 소식을 보내왔으니, 그것은 내가 오랫동안 들은 것 가운데 가장 반가운 소식이었다."]], [["I had stayed here five weeks, and lived very comfortably indeed (the secret anxiety of my mind excepted); but when I received this letter I looked pleasantly again, and told my landlady that I had received a letter from my spouse in Ireland, that I had the good news of his being very well, but had the bad news that his business would not permit him to come away so soon as he expected, and so I was like to go back again without him.", "나는 여기서 다섯 주를 머물렀고 실로 아주 편안하게 지냈으나(내 마음의 은밀한 불안만 빼면), 이 편지를 받자 나는 다시 즐거운 낯이 되어, 집주인 여자에게 아일랜드에 있는 남편에게서 편지를 받았는데, 그가 아주 잘 있다는 좋은 소식이 있었으나, 그의 일이 그가 기대했던 만큼 빨리 떠나오는 것을 허락지 않는다는 나쁜 소식이 있어, 나도 그를 두고 다시 돌아가야 할 것 같다고 말했다."]], [["My landlady complimented me upon the good news however, that I had heard he was well.", "내 집주인 여자는 그래도 그가 잘 있다는 소식을 들은 데 대해 내게 좋은 소식이라며 인사치레를 했다."], ["“For I have observed, madam,”", "\"제가 보기에 마님은,\""], ["says she, “you hadn’t been so pleasant as you used to be; you have been over head and ears in care for him, I dare say,”", "하고 그녀가 말하기를, \"여느 때만큼 즐거워 보이지 않으셨거든요. 그분 걱정에 머리끝까지 푹 빠져 계셨던 게 분명해요.\""], ["says the good woman; “’tis easy to be seen there’s an alteration in you for the better,”", "하고 그 착한 여자가 말했다. \"마님이 한결 나아지신 게 한눈에 보인다니까요.\""], ["says she.", "하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["“Well, I am sorry the esquire can’t come yet,”", "\"원, 그 나리께서 아직 못 오신다니 안됐습니다.\""], ["says my landlord; “I should have been heartily glad to have seen him.", "하고 내 집주인 남자가 말했다. \"저도 진심으로 그분을 뵙고 싶었는데 말이지요."], ["But I hope, when you have certain news of his coming, you’ll take a step hither again, madam,”", "하지만 그분이 오신다는 확실한 소식을 들으시거든, 마님, 다시 이리로 한 걸음 해 주시기를 바랍니다.\""], ["says he; “you shall be very welcome whenever you please to come.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"언제든 오고 싶으실 때 오시면 더없이 환영합니다.\""]], [["With all these fine compliments we parted, and I came merry enough to London, and found my governess as well pleased as I was.", "이 모든 멋진 인사치레와 함께 우리는 헤어졌고, 나는 제법 흥겹게 런던으로 돌아와, 내 후견인이 나만큼이나 기뻐하는 것을 보았다."], ["And now she told me she would never recommend any partner to me again, for she always found, she said, that I had the best luck when I ventured by myself.", "그리고 이제 그녀는 내게 다시는 어떤 동업자도 추천하지 않겠다고 말했으니, 자기가 보기에 내가 혼자 모험에 나설 때 늘 운이 가장 좋더라는 것이었다."], ["And so indeed I had, for I was seldom in any danger when I was by myself, or if I was, I got out of it with more dexterity than when I was entangled with the dull measures of other people, who had perhaps less forecast, and were more rash and impatient than I; for though I had as much courage to venture as any of them, yet I used more caution before I undertook a thing, and had more presence of mind when I was to bring myself off.", "그리고 실로 그러했으니, 나는 혼자 있을 때는 좀처럼 어떤 위험에도 처하지 않았고, 설령 처한다 해도, 어쩌면 나보다 앞을 덜 내다보고 더 무모하고 더 성마른 다른 사람들의 굼뜬 셈법에 얽혀 있을 때보다 더 솜씨 좋게 거기서 빠져나왔다. 모험에 나서는 용기로 말하자면 나도 그들 누구 못지않게 지녔으나, 어떤 일을 벌이기 전에 더 많은 신중을 기했고, 나 자신을 빼낼 때 더 많은 침착함을 지녔기 때문이다."]], [["I have often wondered even at my own hardiness another way, that when all my companions were surprised and fell so suddenly into the hand of justice, and that I so narrowly escaped, yet I could not all this while enter into one serious resolution to leave off this trade, and especially considering that I was now very far from being poor; that the temptation of necessity, which is generally the introduction of all such wickedness, was now removed; for I had near £500 by me in ready money, on which I might have lived very well, if I had thought fit to have retired; but I say, I had not so much as the least inclination to leave off; no, not so much as I had before when I had but £200 beforehand, and when I had no such frightful examples before my eyes as these were. From hence ’tis evident to me, that when once we are hardened in crime, no fear can affect us, no example give us any warning.", "나는 종종 또 다른 점에서 내 자신의 대담함에 놀라곤 했으니, 곧 내 동료들이 모두 불시에 당하여 그토록 갑작스레 법의 손에 떨어지고, 나는 그토록 아슬아슬하게 벗어났는데도, 이 내내 이 장사를 그만두겠다는 진지한 결심 하나 품지 못했다는 것이며, 더구나 내가 이제 결코 가난하지 않다는 점, 또 대개 그런 온갖 사악함의 입구가 되는 궁핍의 유혹이 이제 사라졌다는 점을 생각하면 더욱 그러했다. 나는 손에 500파운드에 가까운 현금을 지니고 있었으니, 은퇴하기로 마음먹었더라면 그것으로 아주 잘 살 수 있었을 것이다. 그러나 말하건대, 내게는 그만두려는 마음이 조금도 없었다. 아니, 손에 겨우 200파운드를 지녔던 예전, 그리고 이런 무시무시한 본보기들을 눈앞에 두지 않았던 예전만큼도 없었다. 이로 미루어 내게 분명해진 바는, 일단 우리가 죄에 굳어지고 나면 어떤 두려움도 우리를 움직이지 못하고, 어떤 본보기도 우리에게 아무런 경고가 되지 못한다는 것이다."]], [["I had indeed one comrade whose fate went very near me for a good while, though I wore it off too in time.", "사실 내게는 그 운명이 한동안 내 마음을 깊이 아프게 한 동료가 하나 있었으니, 그것도 차츰 닳아 없어지긴 했다."], ["That case was indeed very unhappy.", "그 경우는 실로 무척 불행했다."], ["I had made a prize of a piece of very good damask in a mercer’s shop, and went clear off myself, but had conveyed the piece to this companion of mine when we went out of the shop, and she went one way and I went another.", "나는 어느 포목상에서 아주 좋은 다마스크 천 한 필을 노획물로 챙겨, 나 자신은 무사히 빠져나왔으나, 가게에서 나올 때 그 천을 이 동료에게 건넸고, 그녀는 한쪽 길로, 나는 다른 길로 갔다."], ["We had not been long out of the shop but the mercer missed his piece of stuff, and sent his messengers, one one way and one another, and they presently seized her that had the piece, with the damask upon her.", "우리가 가게에서 나온 지 얼마 되지 않아 포목상이 자기 천이 없어진 것을 알아채고, 심부름꾼들을 보내, 한 사람은 이쪽으로 한 사람은 저쪽으로 보냈는데, 그들은 곧 천을 지닌 그녀를, 다마스크를 몸에 지닌 채 붙들었다."], ["As for me, I had very luckily stepped into a house where there was a lace chamber, up one pair of stairs, and had the satisfaction, or the terror indeed, of looking out of the window upon the noise they made, and seeing the poor creature dragged away in triumph to the justice, who immediately committed her to Newgate.", "나로 말하자면, 운 좋게도 위층 한 층에 레이스 방이 있는 어느 집으로 발을 들여놓아, 그들이 일으키는 소동을 창밖으로 내다보며, 그 가엾은 것이 의기양양하게 치안판사에게 끌려가는 것을, 그리고 그가 곧장 그녀를 뉴게이트에 수감하는 것을 지켜보는 만족, 아니 실로 공포를 누렸다."]], [["I was careful to attempt nothing in the lace chamber, but tumbled their goods pretty much to spend time; then bought a few yards of edging and paid for it, and came away very sad-hearted indeed for the poor woman, who was in tribulation for what I only had stolen.", "나는 레이스 방에서는 아무것도 시도하지 않도록 조심하고, 다만 시간을 끌려고 그들의 물건을 제법 뒤적거리기만 했다. 그런 다음 가장자리 장식 몇 야드를 사서 그 값을 치르고, 실로 무척 슬픈 마음으로 나왔으니, 그 가엾은 여자는 나 혼자 훔친 것 때문에 고난에 처한 것이었다."]], [["Here again my old caution stood me in good stead; namely, that though I often robbed with these people, yet I never let them know who I was, or where I lodged, nor could they ever find out my lodging, though they often endeavoured to watch me to it.", "여기서 또 나의 오랜 신중함이 큰 보탬이 되었으니, 곧 비록 내가 이 사람들과 자주 도둑질을 했어도, 결코 그들에게 내가 누구인지, 어디서 묵는지 알려 주지 않았다는 점이다. 그들은 종종 나를 미행해 내 숙소를 알아내려 했으나 끝내 찾아내지 못했다."], ["They all knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, though even some of them rather believed I was she than knew me to be so.", "그들은 모두 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알았으나, 그들 가운데 더러는 내가 그 여자임을 알았다기보다 오히려 그러리라 믿는 정도였다."], ["My name was public among them indeed, but how to find me out they knew not, nor so much as how to guess at my quarters, whether they were at the east end of the town or the west; and this wariness was my safety upon all these occasions.", "사실 내 이름은 그들 사이에 널리 알려졌으나, 어떻게 나를 찾아내야 할지 그들은 알지 못했고, 내가 묵는 곳이 도시의 동쪽 끝에 있는지 서쪽에 있는지조차 짐작하지 못했다. 그리고 이 조심성이 이 모든 경우에 나의 안전이었다."]], [["I kept close a great while upon the occasion of this woman’s disaster.", "나는 이 여자의 재난을 빌미로 한참 동안 몸을 사렸다."], ["I knew that if I should do anything that should miscarry, and should be carried to prison, she would be there and ready to witness against me, and perhaps save her life at my expense.", "만일 내가 무슨 일을 벌이다 실패해 감옥에 끌려간다면, 그녀가 거기 있다가 기꺼이 나에 대해 증언하고, 어쩌면 내 목숨을 대가로 자기 목숨을 구하리라는 것을 나는 알았다."], ["I considered that I began to be very well known by name at the Old Bailey, though they did not know my face, and that if I should fall into their hands, I should be treated as an old offender; and for this reason I was resolved to see what this poor creature’s fate should be before I stirred abroad, though several times in her distress I conveyed money to her for her relief.", "나는 내 이름이 올드베일리에서 제법 잘 알려지기 시작했음을, 비록 그들이 내 얼굴은 몰랐어도 그러함을, 그리고 만일 내가 그들의 손에 떨어진다면 상습범으로 취급되리라는 것을 생각했다. 이런 까닭에 나는 그 가엾은 것의 운명이 어찌 될지 보고 나서야 바깥에 나서기로 마음먹었으나, 그녀가 곤경에 처한 동안 여러 번 그녀를 구제하려고 돈을 전해 주었다."]], [["At length she came to her trial. She pleaded she did not steal the thing, but that one Mrs.", "마침내 그녀는 재판에 나섰다."], ["Flanders, as she heard her called (for she did not know her), gave the bundle to her after they came out of the shop, and bade her carry it home to her lodging. They asked her where this Mrs.", "그녀는 자기가 그 물건을 훔치지 않았으며, 다만 (자기가 알지는 못하나) 사람들이 플랜더스 부인이라 부르는 것을 들은 어떤 여자가 가게에서 나온 뒤에 자기에게 그 꾸러미를 건네며, 그것을 자기 숙소로 가져다 달라고 일렀노라 항변했다."], ["Flanders was, but she could not produce her, neither could she give the least account of me; and the mercer’s men swearing positively that she was in the shop when the goods were stolen, that they immediately missed them, and pursued her, and found them upon her, thereupon the jury brought her in guilty; but the Court, considering that she was really not the person that stole the goods, an inferior assistant, and that it was very possible she could not find out this Mrs.", "그들이 이 플랜더스 부인이 어디 있느냐고 묻자, 그녀는 나를 내놓지도 못했고, 나에 대해 조금도 설명하지 못했다. 그리고 포목상의 점원들이 물건이 도난당했을 때 그녀가 가게에 있었으며, 자기들이 곧장 그것이 없어진 것을 알아채고 그녀를 쫓아가 그 몸에서 찾아냈노라 단호히 맹세하자, 그에 따라 배심원단은 그녀에게 유죄 평결을 내렸다."], ["Flanders, meaning me, though it would save her life, which indeed was true—I say, considering all this, they allowed her to be transported, which was the utmost favour she could obtain, only that the Court told her that if she could in the meantime produce the said Mrs.", "그러나 법정은, 그녀가 사실 물건을 훔친 당사자가 아니라 종범에 불과하며, 비록 제 목숨을 구하는 일일지언정 이 플랜더스 부인, 곧 나를 찾아내는 것이 그녀로서는 도무지 불가능했으리라는 점을 고려하니, 실로 그것은 사실이었다. 말하건대, 이 모든 것을 고려하여 그들은 그녀에게 유배형을 허락했으니, 그것이 그녀가 얻을 수 있는 최대의 은전이었다."], ["Flanders, they would intercede for her pardon; that is to say, if she could find me out, and hand me, she should not be transported.", "다만 법정은 그녀에게, 그동안에 그 플랜더스 부인을 내놓을 수 있다면 그녀의 사면을 위해 주선해 주겠노라 일렀으니, 다시 말해 그녀가 나를 찾아내 넘긴다면 유배되지 않으리라는 것이었다."], ["This I took care to make impossible to her, and so she was shipped off in pursuance of her sentence a little while after.", "이것을 나는 그녀에게 불가능하도록 단단히 해 두었고, 그래서 그녀는 얼마 뒤에 그 판결에 따라 배에 실려 떠났다."]], [["I must repeat it again, that the fate of this poor woman troubled me exceedingly, and I began to be very pensive, knowing that I was really the instrument of her disaster; but the preservation of my own life, which was so evidently in danger, took off all my tenderness; and seeing that she was not put to death, I was very easy at her transportation, because she was then out of the way of doing me any mischief, whatever should happen.", "다시 한번 거듭 말하건대, 이 가엾은 여자의 운명은 나를 몹시 괴롭혔고, 나는 무척 수심에 잠기기 시작했으니, 내가 실로 그녀 재난의 도구였음을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 그토록 명백히 위태로운 내 자신의 목숨을 지키려는 마음이 내 온갖 측은함을 거두어 갔다. 그리고 그녀가 사형에 처해지지는 않은 것을 보고, 나는 그녀의 유배에 무척 마음이 놓였으니, 무슨 일이 생기든 그녀가 그때는 내게 해를 끼칠 길에서 벗어나 있었기 때문이다."]], [["The disaster of this woman was some months before that of the last-recited story, and was indeed partly occasion of my governess proposing to dress me up in men’s clothes, that I might go about unobserved, as indeed I did; but I was soon tired of that disguise, as I have said, for indeed it exposed me to too many difficulties.", "이 여자의 재난은 방금 이야기한 사건보다 몇 달 앞선 일이었고, 실로 내 후견인이 나를 남자 옷으로 차려입혀 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 돌아다니게 하자고 제안한 한 가지 빌미였으니, 실로 나는 그렇게 했다. 그러나 앞서 말했듯이 나는 곧 그 변장에 진력이 났으니, 실로 그것은 나를 너무 많은 어려움에 내몰았기 때문이다."]], [["I was now easy as to all fear of witnesses against me, for all those that had either been concerned with me, or that knew me by the name of Moll Flanders, were either hanged or transported; and if I should have had the misfortune to be taken, I might call myself anything else, as well as Moll Flanders, and no old sins could be placed into my account; so I began to run a-tick again with the more freedom, and several successful adventures I made, though not such as I had made before.", "이제 나는 나에 대한 증인에 대한 온갖 두려움에서 벗어나 마음이 편했으니, 나와 관련되었거나 나를 몰 플랜더스라는 이름으로 알던 자들은 모두 교수형을 당하거나 유배되었기 때문이다. 그리고 설령 불운하게 붙잡힌다 해도, 나는 몰 플랜더스 못지않게 다른 무엇으로든 나를 일컬을 수 있었고, 어떤 옛 죄도 내 앞으로 돌려질 수 없었다. 그래서 나는 더 거리낌 없이 다시 외상 장사를 시작했고, 여러 차례 성공적인 모험을 했으나, 예전에 했던 것만큼은 아니었다."]], [["We had at that time another fire happened not a great way off from the place where my governess lived, and I made an attempt there, as before, but as I was not soon enough before the crowd of people came in, and could not get to the house I aimed at, instead of a prize, I got a mischief, which had almost put a period to my life and all my wicked doings together; for the fire being very furious, and the people in a great fright in removing their goods, and throwing them out of window, a wench from out of a window threw a feather-bed just upon me.", "그 무렵 우리에게는 또 다른 화재가, 내 후견인이 사는 곳에서 그리 멀지 않은 곳에서 일어났는데, 나는 예전처럼 거기서 한 건을 시도했으나, 군중이 몰려들기 전에 미처 도착하지 못해 내가 노린 집에 다다를 수 없었던 탓에, 노획물 대신 봉변을 당했으니, 그것은 내 목숨과 내 온갖 사악한 짓에 거의 종지부를 찍을 뻔했다. 불길이 어찌나 사나웠던지, 사람들이 크게 겁에 질려 가재도구를 옮기고 창밖으로 내던지던 차에, 창밖에서 어느 계집아이가 깃털 침대를 바로 내 위로 던진 것이다."], ["It is true, the bed being soft, it broke no bones; but as the weight was great, and made greater by the fall, it beat me down, and laid me dead for a while.", "사실 침대가 푹신해 뼈가 부러지지는 않았으나, 그 무게가 큰 데다 떨어지면서 더 커진 탓에 나를 내리눌러, 한동안 죽은 듯이 쓰러뜨렸다."], ["Nor did the people concern themselves much to deliver me from it, or to recover me at all; but I lay like one dead and neglected a good while, till somebody going to remove the bed out of the way, helped me up.", "사람들은 그것에서 나를 빼내거나 나를 되살리는 데 그다지 마음 쓰지도 않았고, 나는 죽은 자처럼 한참 동안 버려진 채 누워 있다가, 누군가가 그 침대를 치우러 와서 나를 일으켜 주었다."], ["It was indeed a wonder the people in the house had not thrown other goods out after it, and which might have fallen upon it, and then I had been inevitably killed; but I was reserved for further afflictions.", "실로, 그 집 사람들이 그 위로 다른 가재도구를 더 던지지 않은 것이 천만다행이었으니, 그것이 그 위에 떨어졌더라면 나는 영락없이 죽었을 것이다. 그러나 나는 더 큰 고난을 위해 남겨진 몸이었다."]], [["This accident, however, spoiled my market for that time, and I came home to my governess very much hurt and bruised, and frighted to the last degree, and it was a good while before she could set me upon my feet again.", "그러나 이 사고는 그때의 내 장사를 망쳐 놓았고, 나는 몹시 다치고 멍이 든 채, 더없이 겁에 질려 내 후견인에게 돌아왔으며, 그녀가 나를 다시 일으켜 세우기까지는 한참이 걸렸다."]], [["It was now a merry time of the year, and Bartholomew Fair was begun.", "이제는 한 해 중 흥겨운 철이라 바살러뮤 시장이 시작되었다."], ["I had never made any walks that way, nor was the common part of the fair of much advantage to me; but I took a turn this year into the cloisters, and among the rest I fell into one of the raffling shops.", "나는 그쪽으로는 한 번도 발걸음을 한 적이 없었고, 시장의 흔한 부분은 내게 별 이득이 되지도 않았다. 그러나 나는 올해 회랑 쪽으로 발길을 돌렸고, 그러던 중 추첨 가게 중 하나에 들어가게 되었다."], ["It was a thing of no great consequence to me, nor did I expect to make much of it; but there came a gentleman extremely well dressed and very rich, and as ’tis frequent to talk to everybody in those shops, he singled me out, and was very particular with me.", "그것은 내게 그리 대수로운 일이 아니었고, 거기서 별 재미를 보리라 기대하지도 않았다. 그러나 더없이 잘 차려입고 무척 부유한 신사 하나가 들어왔는데, 그런 가게에서는 누구에게나 말을 거는 일이 흔하므로, 그는 나를 콕 집어 무척 각별하게 굴었다."], ["First he told me he would put in for me to raffle, and did so; and some small matter coming to his lot, he presented it to me (I think it was a feather muff); then he continued to keep talking to me with a more than common appearance of respect, but still very civil, and much like a gentleman.", "먼저 그는 내 몫으로 추첨에 돈을 걸어 주겠다며 그렇게 했고, 사소한 것이 그의 몫으로 떨어지자 그것을 내게 선사했는데(내 생각에 깃털 토시였던 것 같다), 그러고는 예사롭지 않게 공손한 태도로, 그러면서도 여전히 무척 정중하게, 신사답게 내게 계속 말을 걸었다."]], [["He held me in talk so long, till at last he drew me out of the raffling place to the shop-door, and then to a walk in the cloister, still talking of a thousand things cursorily without anything to the purpose.", "그는 어찌나 오래 나를 이야기에 붙들어 두었던지, 마침내 나를 추첨 가게에서 가게 문간으로, 그러고는 회랑의 산책로로 끌어내, 별 요점도 없이 천 가지 일을 두서없이 이야기했다."], ["At last he told me that, without compliment, he was charmed with my company, and asked me if I durst trust myself in a coach with him; he told me he was a man of honour, and would not offer anything to me unbecoming him as such.", "마침내 그는, 입에 발린 말이 아니라 내 동행에 매혹되었노라 말하며, 자기와 함께 마차에 몸을 맡길 용기가 있느냐고 물었다. 그는 자기가 명예를 아는 사람이며, 그런 사람으로서 어울리지 않는 어떤 짓도 내게 하지 않겠노라 말했다."], ["I seemed to decline it a while, but suffered myself to be importuned a little, and then yielded.", "나는 잠시 사양하는 척하다가, 조금 조르는 것을 못 이긴 척하고는 응했다."]], [["I was at a loss in my thoughts to conclude at first what this gentleman designed; but I found afterwards he had had some drink in his head, and that he was not very unwilling to have some more.", "나는 처음에 이 신사가 무엇을 노리는지 도무지 종잡을 수 없었으나, 나중에 알고 보니 그는 머릿속에 술기운이 좀 있었고, 더 마시고 싶은 마음도 그리 없지 않았다."], ["He carried me in the coach to the Spring Garden, at Knightsbridge, where we walked in the gardens, and he treated me very handsomely; but I found he drank very freely.", "그는 나이츠브리지의 스프링 가든으로 나를 마차에 태워 데려갔는데, 거기서 우리는 정원을 거닐었고, 그는 나를 무척 후하게 대접했다. 그러나 나는 그가 무척 거리낌 없이 술을 마시는 것을 알았다."], ["He pressed me also to drink, but I declined it.", "그는 내게도 마시기를 권했으나 나는 사양했다."]], [["Hitherto he kept his word with me, and offered me nothing amiss.", "여기까지 그는 내게 약속을 지켰고, 어떤 잘못된 것도 내게 제안하지 않았다."], ["We came away in the coach again, and he brought me into the streets, and by this time it was near ten o’clock at night, and he stopped the coach at a house where, it seems, he was acquainted, and where they made no scruple to show us upstairs into a room with a bed in it.", "우리는 다시 마차로 떠나왔고, 그는 나를 거리로 데리고 나왔는데, 이때쯤에는 밤 열 시 무렵이었다. 그가 마차를 어느 집 앞에 세웠는데, 그가 아는 듯한 집이었고, 거기서는 거리낌 없이 우리를 위층의, 침대가 있는 방으로 안내했다."], ["At first I seemed to be unwilling to go up, but after a few words I yielded to that too, being willing to see the end of it, and in hope to make something of it at last.", "처음에 나는 올라가기를 꺼리는 척했으나, 몇 마디 말이 오간 뒤 그것에도 응했으니, 그 끝을 보고 싶었고, 끝내 거기서 무언가를 챙기리라는 희망에서였다."], ["As for the bed, etc., I was not much concerned about that part.", "침대 따위에 관해서는, 나는 그 부분에 대해 별로 개의치 않았다."]], [["Here he began to be a little freer with me than he had promised; and I by little and little yielded to everything, so that, in a word, he did what he pleased with me; I need say no more.", "여기서 그는 약속했던 것보다 조금 더 거리낌 없이 굴기 시작했고, 나는 조금씩 모든 것에 응했으니, 한마디로 그는 나를 제멋대로 다루었다. 더 말할 필요는 없겠다."], ["All this while he drank freely too, and about one in the morning we went into the coach again.", "이 내내 그는 거리낌 없이 술도 마셨고, 새벽 한 시 무렵 우리는 다시 마차에 올랐다."], ["The air and the shaking of the coach made the drink he had get more up in his head than it was before, and he grew uneasy in the coach, and was for acting over again what he had been doing before; but as I thought my game now secure, I resisted him, and brought him to be a little still, which had not lasted five minutes but he fell fast asleep.", "바깥 공기와 마차의 흔들림이 그가 마신 술을 전보다 더 머리로 솟구치게 했고, 그는 마차 안에서 거북해하며 자기가 조금 전에 하던 짓을 다시 한번 벌이려 했다. 그러나 나는 이제 내 노름이 안전하다고 여겼던 터라 그를 뿌리쳤고, 그를 좀 잠잠하게 만들었는데, 그것은 오 분도 채 가지 않아 그가 깊이 잠들어 버렸다."]], [["I took this opportunity to search him to a nicety.", "나는 이 기회를 잡아 그를 샅샅이 뒤졌다."], ["I took a gold watch, with a silk purse of gold, his fine full-bottom periwig and silver-fringed gloves, his sword and fine snuff-box, and gently opening the coach door, stood ready to jump out while the coach was going on; but the coach stopped in the narrow street beyond Temple Bar to let another coach pass, I got softly out, fastened the door again, and gave my gentleman and the coach the slip both together, and never heard more of them.", "나는 금시계 하나와 금화가 든 비단 지갑 하나, 그의 멋진 풍성한 가발과 은실로 가장자리를 두른 장갑, 그의 칼과 멋진 코담배갑을 챙기고는, 마차 문을 살며시 열어 마차가 가는 동안 뛰어내릴 채비를 했다. 그러나 마차가 템플 바 너머 좁은 거리에서 다른 마차를 지나가게 하려고 멈춘 덕에, 나는 살그머니 내려 문을 다시 잠그고는, 내 신사와 마차를 한꺼번에 따돌렸으며, 그들에 관해서는 두 번 다시 아무것도 듣지 못했다."]], [["This was an adventure indeed unlooked for, and perfectly undesigned by me; though I was not so past the merry part of life, as to forget how to behave, when a fop so blinded by his appetite should not know an old woman from a young.", "이것은 실로 예기치 못한, 그리고 내가 전혀 계획하지 않은 모험이었다. 비록 내가 인생의 흥겨운 시절을 그토록 다 지나치지는 않아, 식욕에 눈이 멀어 늙은 여자를 젊은 여자와 구별하지 못하는 멋쟁이를 어떻게 대해야 할지는 잊지 않았어도 말이다."], ["I did not indeed look so old as I was by ten or twelve years; yet I was not a young wench of seventeen, and it was easy enough to be distinguished.", "사실 나는 내 나이보다 열에서 열두 살은 젊어 보였으나, 그래도 열일곱 살짜리 젊은 계집은 아니었으니, 충분히 구별될 만했다. 머릿속에 술이 오른 데다 마음속에 사악한 욕망이 함께 오른 남자만큼 터무니없고, 물리고, 우스꽝스러운 것은 없으니, 그는 한꺼번에 두 악마에게 사로잡혀, 물 없이 방아를 돌릴 수 없는 것 못지않게 자기 이성으로 자신을 다스리지 못한다."], ["There is nothing so absurd, so surfeiting, so ridiculous, as a man heated by wine in his head, and wicked gust in his inclination together; he is in the possession of two devils at once, and can no more govern himself by his reason than a mill can grind without water; his vice tramples upon all that was in him that had any good in it, if any such thing there was; nay, his very sense is blinded by its own rage, and he acts absurdities even in his views; such a drinking more, when he is drunk already; picking up a common woman, without regard to what she is or who she is, whether sound or rotten, clean or unclean, whether ugly or handsome, whether old or young, and so blinded as not really to distinguish.", "그의 악덕은 그 안에 무언가 좋은 것이 있었다면, 만일 그런 것이 있었다면, 그 좋은 것을 죄다 짓밟는다. 아니, 그의 분별마저 제 분노로 눈이 멀어, 그는 자기 견해에서조차 터무니없는 짓을 하니, 이미 취했는데도 더 마시는 짓, 흔해 빠진 여자를, 그 여자가 무엇이고 누구인지, 멀쩡한지 병들었는지, 깨끗한지 더러운지, 추한지 고운지, 늙었는지 젊었는지 아랑곳없이 집어 들고는, 눈이 멀어 실로 분간조차 못 하는 짓이 그것이다."], ["Such a man is worse than a lunatic; prompted by his vicious, corrupted head, he no more knows what he is doing than this wretch of mine knew when I picked his pocket of his watch and his purse of gold.", "그런 남자는 미치광이보다 더하니, 자기의 사악하고 타락한 머리에 부추김을 받아, 마치 이 가엾은 내 사내가 내가 그의 주머니에서 시계와 금화 지갑을 털어 갈 때 알지 못했던 것처럼, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 알지 못한다."]], [["These are the men of whom Solomon says, “They go like an ox to the slaughter, till a dart strikes through their liver”; an admirable description, by the way, of the foul disease, which is a poisonous deadly contagion mingling with the blood, whose centre or foundation is in the liver; from whence, by the swift circulation of the whole mass, that dreadful nauseous plague strikes immediately through his liver, and his spirits are infected, his vitals stabbed through as with a dart.", "이들이야말로 솔로몬이 이른 자들이니, \"소가 도살장으로 가듯, 화살이 그 간을 꿰뚫을 때까지 그들은 간다.\" 이는 말이 나온 김에 말하건대, 저 더러운 병에 대한 감탄할 만한 묘사이니, 그것은 핏속에 섞이는 독하고 치명적인 전염병으로, 그 중심 혹은 근원이 간에 있으며, 거기서 온몸의 빠른 순환을 통해 그 무시무시하고 메스꺼운 역병이 곧장 그의 간을 꿰뚫고, 그의 정기가 감염되며, 그의 급소가 화살에 찔리듯 꿰뚫린다."]], [["It is true this poor unguarded wretch was in no danger from me, though I was greatly apprehensive at first of what danger I might be in from him; but he was really to be pitied in one respect, that he seemed to be a good sort of man in himself; a gentleman that had no harm in his design; a man of sense, and of a fine behaviour, a comely handsome person, a sober solid countenance, a charming beautiful face, and everything that could be agreeable; only had unhappily had some drink the night before, had not been in bed, as he told me when we were together; was hot, and his blood fired with wine, and in that condition his reason, as it were asleep, had given him up.", "사실 이 가엾고 방심한 사내는 내게서 아무런 위험도 없었으나, 비록 처음에 나는 그에게서 내가 어떤 위험에 처할지 무척 걱정했어도 말이다. 그러나 한 가지 점에서 그는 실로 가엾이 여길 만했으니, 그 자신은 선량한 부류의 사람으로 보였던 것이다. 그 속셈에 아무런 악의도 없는 신사요, 분별 있는 사람이며, 처신이 훌륭하고, 풍채가 잘생기고 의젓하며, 침착하고 진중한 낯빛에, 매혹적이고 아름다운 얼굴에, 호감 가는 온갖 것을 다 갖춘 사람이었다. 다만 불행히도 그 전날 밤 술을 좀 마셨고, 우리가 함께 있을 때 그가 말한 대로 잠자리에 들지 못했으며, 몸이 달아오르고 피가 술로 끓어, 그런 상태에서 그의 이성이 말하자면 잠들어 그를 내맡겨 버린 것이었다."]], [["As for me, my business was his money, and what I could make of him; and after that, if I could have found out any way to have done it, I would have sent him safe home to his house and to his family, for ’twas ten to one but he had an honest, virtuous wife and innocent children, that were anxious for his safety, and would have been glad to have gotten him home, and have taken care of him till he was restored to himself.", "나로 말하자면, 내 볼일은 그의 돈이요, 그에게서 챙길 수 있는 것이었다. 그리고 그 뒤에, 만일 어떻게든 할 길을 찾을 수만 있었다면, 나는 그를 그의 집으로, 그의 가족에게로 무사히 돌려보냈을 것이니, 십중팔구 그에게는 정직하고 정숙한 아내와 죄 없는 자식들이 있어, 그의 안위를 걱정하며 그를 집으로 데려와 그가 제정신을 되찾을 때까지 돌보고 싶어 했을 것이기 때문이다."], ["And then with what shame and regret would he look back upon himself!", "그러면 그는 얼마나 부끄러움과 후회로 자신을 돌아보겠는가!"], ["how would he reproach himself with associating himself with a whore! picked up in the worst of all holes, the cloister, among the dirt and filth of all the town!", "창녀와, 그것도 온 도시의 진흙과 오물 속, 그 모든 소굴 가운데 가장 고약한 회랑에서 집어 든 창녀와 어울린 것을 두고 얼마나 자신을 책망하겠는가!"], ["how would he be trembling for fear he had got the pox, for fear a dart had struck through his liver, and hate himself every time he looked back upon the madness and brutality of his debauch!", "매독에 걸렸을까 봐, 화살이 자기 간을 꿰뚫었을까 봐 얼마나 벌벌 떨겠으며, 자기 방탕의 광기와 야만을 돌아볼 때마다 얼마나 자신을 미워하겠는가!"], ["how would he, if he had any principles of honour, as I verily believe he had—I say, how would he abhor the thought of giving any ill distemper, if he had it, as for aught he knew he might, to his modest and virtuous wife, and thereby sowing the contagion in the life-blood of his posterity.", "만일 그에게 명예의 원칙이 조금이라도 있었다면, 실로 나는 그에게 있었으리라 믿거니와, 말하건대 그가 어떤 몹쓸 병에 걸렸다면, 그로서는 알 수 없으나 걸렸을 수도 있으니, 그 병을 자기의 정숙하고 덕스러운 아내에게 옮겨, 그리하여 자기 후손의 생피에 그 전염병의 씨를 뿌린다는 생각을 그는 얼마나 혐오하겠는가."]], [["Would such gentlemen but consider the contemptible thoughts which the very women they are concerned with, in such cases as these, have of them, it would be a surfeit to them.", "그런 신사들이, 이런 경우에 자기들이 상대하는 바로 그 여자들이 그들에 대해 품는 경멸스러운 생각을 한번 헤아려 본다면, 그것은 그들에게 진저리 나는 일이 될 것이다."], ["As I said above, they value not the pleasure, they are raised by no inclination to the man, the passive jade thinks of no pleasure but the money; and when he is, as it were, drunk in the ecstasies of his wicked pleasure, her hands are in his pockets searching for what she can find there, and of which he can no more be sensible in the moment of his folly that he can forethink of it when he goes about it.", "앞서 말했듯이, 그들은 쾌락을 중히 여기지 않고, 남자에 대한 어떤 끌림으로 달뜨지도 않으며, 그 수동적인 계집은 돈 말고는 어떤 쾌락도 생각지 않으니, 그가 말하자면 자기의 사악한 쾌락의 황홀경에 취해 있을 때, 그녀의 손은 그의 주머니에 들어가 거기서 찾아낼 수 있는 것을 뒤지고 있는데, 그는 그 어리석음의 순간에 그것을 미리 헤아릴 수 없었던 것 못지않게 그 순간에도 그것을 조금도 느끼지 못한다."]], [["I knew a woman that was so dexterous with a fellow, who indeed deserved no better usage, that while he was busy with her another way, conveyed his purse with twenty guineas in it out of his fob-pocket, where he had put it for fear of her, and put another purse with gilded counters in it into the room of it.", "나는 어떤 사내에게, 실로 더 나은 대접을 받을 자격이 없는 사내에게 어찌나 능숙했던지, 그가 다른 식으로 그녀와 분주한 동안에 이십 기니가 든 그의 지갑을, 그가 그녀를 경계해 넣어 둔 시계 주머니에서 빼내고, 그 자리에 도금한 가짜 돈이 든 다른 지갑을 넣어 둔 여자를 하나 알았다."], ["After he had done, he says to her, “Now han’t you picked my pocket?”", "일을 다 마친 뒤 그가 그녀에게 말하기를, \"자, 너 내 주머니 털지 않았겠지?\" 했다."], ["She jested with him, and told him she supposed he had not much to lose; he put his hand to his fob, and with his fingers felt that his purse was there, which fully satisfied him, and so she brought off his money.", "그녀는 그와 농을 주고받으며, 그가 잃을 것이 많지는 않았으리라 짐작한다고 말했다. 그가 시계 주머니에 손을 넣어 손가락으로 자기 지갑이 거기 있는 것을 느끼고는 완전히 안심했으니, 그렇게 그녀는 그의 돈을 챙겼다."], ["And this was a trade with her; she kept a sham gold watch, that is, a watch of silver gilt, and a purse of counters in her pocket to be ready on all such occasions, and I doubt not practiced it with success.", "그리고 이것이 그녀에게는 장사였으니, 그녀는 가짜 금시계, 곧 은에 도금한 시계와 가짜 돈이 든 지갑을 주머니에 지녀 그런 온갖 경우에 대비해 두었고, 나는 그녀가 그것을 성공적으로 써먹었으리라 의심치 않는다."]], [["I came home with this last booty to my governess, and really when I told her the story, it so affected her that she was hardly able to forbear tears, to know how such a gentleman ran a daily risk of being undone every time a glass of wine got into his head.", "나는 이 마지막 노획물을 들고 내 후견인에게 돌아왔는데, 실로 내가 그 이야기를 들려주자 그녀는 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지 눈물을 참기 어려울 지경이었으니, 그런 신사가 술 한 잔이 머리에 오를 때마다 날마다 파멸할 위험을 무릅쓴다는 것을 알고서였다."]], [["But as to the purchase I got, and how entirely I stripped him, she told me it pleased her wonderfully.", "그러나 내가 챙긴 노획물에 관해, 그리고 내가 그를 얼마나 깡그리 털었는지에 관해서는, 그녀가 그것이 자기를 놀랍도록 기쁘게 한다고 말했다."], ["“Nay child,”", "\"아니, 얘야,\""], ["says she, “the usage may, for aught I know, do more to reform him than all the sermons that ever he will hear in his life.”", "하고 그녀가 말하기를, \"그렇게 당해 본 것이, 내가 알기로는, 그가 평생 듣게 될 그 모든 설교보다 그를 더 바로잡아 줄지도 모른단다.\""], ["And if the remainder of the story be true, so it did.", "그리고 이 이야기의 나머지가 사실이라면, 정말로 그러했다."]], [["I found the next day she was wonderful inquisitive about this gentleman; the description I had given her of him, his dress, his person, his face, everything concurred to make her think of a gentleman whose character she knew, and family too.", "이튿날 나는 그녀가 이 신사에 대해 놀랍도록 캐묻고 다니는 것을 알게 되었으니, 내가 그녀에게 들려준 그에 대한 묘사, 곧 그의 옷차림이며, 그의 풍채며, 그의 얼굴이며, 모든 것이 한데 들어맞아, 그녀는 자기가 그 인품을, 또 그 가문까지 아는 어떤 신사를 떠올리게 되었다."], ["She mused a while, and I going still on with the particulars, she starts up; says she, “I’ll lay £100 I know the gentleman.”", "그녀가 잠시 곰곰이 생각하는 사이, 내가 여전히 그 세세한 것을 이어 가자, 그녀가 벌떡 일어서며 말하기를, \"내가 100파운드를 걸고 말하건대, 나는 그 신사를 알아.\""]], [["“I am sorry you do,”", "\"그러지 않으셨으면 좋겠어요.\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “for I would not have him exposed on any account in the world; he has had injury enough already by me, and I would not be instrumental to do him any more.”", "\"세상 어떤 일이 있어도 그분이 망신당하는 건 바라지 않으니까요. 그분은 이미 저 때문에 충분히 봉변을 당하셨고, 저는 그분께 더는 어떤 짓도 하는 도구가 되고 싶지 않아요.\""], ["“No, no,”", "\"아니, 아니야,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “I will do him no injury, I assure you, but you may let me satisfy my curiosity a little, for if it is he, I warrant you I find it out.”", "\"장담컨대 나는 그에게 어떤 봉변도 끼치지 않을 거란다. 다만 내 호기심을 좀 풀게 해 다오."], ["I was a little startled at that, and told her, with an apparent concern in my face, that by the same rule he might find me out, and then I was undone.", "그가 그 사람이라면, 내가 틀림없이 알아낼 테니.\" 나는 그 말에 조금 흠칫 놀라, 얼굴에 역력한 근심을 띤 채 그녀에게 말하기를, 같은 이치로 그가 나를 찾아낼 수도 있고, 그러면 나는 끝장이라고 했다."], ["She returned warmly, “Why, do you think I will betray you, child?", "그녀가 발끈하여 대꾸하기를, \"원, 얘야, 내가 너를 팔아넘기리라 생각하느냐?"], ["No, no,”", "아니, 아니다,\""], ["says she, “not for all he is worth in the world.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그가 세상에서 가진 그 모든 것을 준다 해도 그러지 않아."], ["I have kept your counsel in worse things than these; sure you may trust me in this.”", "나는 이보다 더 고약한 일에서도 네 비밀을 지켜 주었는데, 이런 일에서야 분명 나를 믿을 수 있지 않겠느냐.\""], ["So I said no more at that time.", "그래서 나는 그때는 더 말하지 않았다."]], [["She laid her scheme another way, and without acquainting me of it, but she was resolved to find it out if possible.", "그녀는 다른 식으로 계책을 세웠고, 내게 알리지도 않은 채, 그러나 가능하다면 그것을 알아내기로 마음먹었다."], ["So she goes to a certain friend of hers who was acquainted in the family that she guessed at, and told her friend she had some extraordinary business with such a gentleman (who, by the way, was no less than a baronet, and of a very good family), and that she knew not how to come at him without somebody to introduce her.", "그래서 그녀는 자기가 짐작하는 그 가문에 정통한 어떤 친구에게 가, 그 친구에게 자기가 그런 신사와(그런데 그는 다름 아닌 준남작이요, 무척 좋은 가문 출신이었다) 특별한 볼일이 있는데, 자기를 소개해 줄 누군가 없이는 그에게 다가갈 방도를 모르겠다고 말했다."], ["Her friend promised her very readily to do it, and accordingly goes to the house to see if the gentleman was in town.", "그녀의 친구는 무척 선뜻 그렇게 해 주겠다고 약속했고, 그에 따라 그 신사가 시내에 있는지 알아보러 그 집으로 갔다."]], [["The next day she come to my governess and tells her that Sir —— was at home, but that he had met with a disaster and was very ill, and there was no speaking with him.", "이튿날 그녀는 내 후견인에게 와서, 아무개 경이 댁에 계시기는 한데, 봉변을 당해 무척 편찮으셔서 말을 나눌 수가 없다고 전했다."], ["“What disaster?”", "\"무슨 봉변요?\""], ["says my governess hastily, as if she was surprised at it.", "하고 내 후견인이, 마치 그것에 놀라기라도 한 듯 다급히 물었다."], ["“Why,”", "\"글쎄,\""], ["says her friend, “he had been at Hampstead to visit a gentleman of his acquaintance, and as he came back again he was set upon and robbed; and having got a little drink too, as they suppose, the rogues abused him, and he is very ill.”", "하고 그녀의 친구가 말하기를, \"그분이 햄스테드에 아는 신사를 찾아뵈러 가셨다가, 돌아오시는 길에 습격당해 강도를 만나셨다지 뭐예요. 게다가 그분도 술을 좀 드신 터라, 그놈들이 그분을 함부로 다뤄서, 무척 편찮으시대요.\""], ["“Robbed!”", "\"강도라니!\""], ["says my governess, “and what did they take from him?”", "하고 내 후견인이 말했다."], ["“Why,” says her friend, “they took his gold watch and his gold snuff-box, his fine periwig, and what money he had in his pocket, which was considerable, to be sure, for Sir —— never goes without a purse of guineas about him.”", "\"그래, 그놈들이 그분에게서 무얼 가져갔답니까?\""], ["“Pshaw!”", "\"그게,\""], ["says my old governess, jeering, “I warrant you he has got drunk now and got a whore, and she has picked his pocket, and so he comes home to his wife and tells her he has been robbed.", "하고 그녀의 친구가 말하기를, \"그분의 금시계와 금 코담배갑, 그분의 멋진 가발, 그리고 주머니에 지니셨던 돈을 가져갔다는데, 그게 분명 적지 않은 액수였을 거예요."], ["That’s an old sham; a thousand such tricks are put upon the poor women every day.”", "아무개 경께서는 늘 기니가 든 지갑을 지니고 다니시거든요.\""]], [["“Fie!” says her friend, “I find you don’t know Sir ——; why he is as civil a gentleman, there is not a finer man, nor a soberer, graver, modester person in the whole city; he abhors such things; there’s nobody that knows him will think such a thing of him.” “Well, well,” says my governess, “that’s none of my business; if it was, I warrant I should find there was something of that kind in it; your modest men in common opinion are sometimes no better than other people, only they keep a better character, or, if you please, are the better hypocrites.” “No, no,” says her friend, “I can assure you Sir —— is no hypocrite, he is really an honest, sober gentleman, and he has certainly been robbed.” “Nay,” says my governess, “it may be he has; it is no business of mine, I tell you; I only want to speak with him; my business is of another nature.” “But,” says her friend, “let your business be of what nature it will, you cannot see him yet, for he is not fit to be seen, for he is very ill, and bruised very much.” “Ay,” says my governess, “nay, then he has fallen into bad hands, to be sure.” And then she asked gravely, “Pray, where is he bruised?” “Why, in the head,” says her friend, “and one of his hands, and his face, for they used him barbarously.” “Poor gentleman,” says my governess, “I must wait, then, till he recovers”; and adds, “I hope it will not be long, for I want very much to speak with him.”", "\"피!\" 하고 내 늙은 후견인이 비웃으며 말했다. \"장담컨대 그분이 이제 술에 취해 창녀를 하나 집어 들었는데, 그 여자가 그분의 주머니를 털었고, 그래서 그분이 아내에게 돌아와 강도를 당했다고 한 게지요. 그건 케케묵은 속임수예요. 가엾은 여자들에게 그런 수작이 날마다 천 가지는 행해진다니까요.\" \"원, 별말씀을!\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"아무개 경을 모르시는군요. 그분으로 말하면 더없이 점잖은 신사라, 온 도시를 통틀어 그분보다 더 훌륭한 사람도, 더 침착하고 진중하고 정숙한 사람도 없어요. 그분은 그런 일을 질색하신답니다. 그분을 아는 사람이라면 누구도 그분을 두고 그런 생각은 안 할 거예요.\" \"그래요, 그래요,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그건 내가 알 바 아니지요. 내 일이라면야 장담컨대 거기에 그런 종류의 무언가가 있다는 걸 알아낼 테지만요. 흔히들 정숙하다는 남자들이 때로는 다른 사람들보다 나을 것도 없고, 다만 더 나은 평판을 지킬 뿐이거나, 굳이 말하자면 더 능란한 위선자일 뿐이지요.\" \"아니, 아니에요,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"장담하건대 아무개 경은 위선자가 아니에요. 그분은 정말로 정직하고 침착한 신사이고, 그분은 분명 강도를 당하신 거예요.\" \"원,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그럴 수도 있겠지요. 그건 내 일이 아니라니까요. 나는 그저 그분과 이야기를 나누고 싶을 뿐이에요. 내 볼일은 다른 성질의 것이거든요.\" \"하지만,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"볼일이 무슨 성질의 것이든 아직은 그분을 뵐 수 없어요. 그분은 뵐 만한 상태가 아니거든요. 무척 편찮으시고, 멍도 심하게 드셨어요.\" \"이런,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"아니, 그렇다면 그분이 분명 나쁜 손에 떨어지셨군요.\" 그러고는 진중하게 물었다. \"그래, 그분이 어디를 다치셨답니까?\" \"글쎄, 머리하고,\" 하고 그녀의 친구가 말했다. \"한쪽 손, 그리고 얼굴이에요. 그놈들이 그분을 무지막지하게 다뤘거든요.\" \"가엾은 신사로군요,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"그렇다면 나는 그분이 회복하실 때까지 기다려야겠네요.\" 그러고는 덧붙였다. \"오래 걸리지 않기를 바라요. 나는 그분과 무척 이야기를 나누고 싶거든요.\""]], [["Away she comes to me and tells me this story.", "그녀는 내게 와서 이 이야기를 들려주었다."], ["“I have found out your fine gentleman, and a fine gentleman he was,”", "\"내가 네 멋진 신사를 알아냈는데, 정말 멋진 신사더구나,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she; “but, mercy on him, he is in a sad pickle now.", "\"하지만 가엾기도 해라, 지금은 딱한 꼴이 되었지 뭐냐. 도대체 네가 그에게 무슨 짓을 했는지 모르겠구나."], ["I wonder what the d—l you have done to him; why, you have almost killed him.”", "원, 네가 그를 거의 죽여 놓았어.\" 나는 적잖이 당황하여 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["I looked at her with disorder enough.", "\"제가 그분을 죽였다고요!\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["“I killed him!”", "\"사람을 잘못 보신 게 틀림없어요."], ["says I; “you must mistake the person; I am sure I did nothing to him; he was very well when I left him,”", "분명 저는 그분께 아무 짓도 안 했는걸요. 제가 떠날 때 그분은 아주 멀쩡하셨어요.\""], ["said I, “only drunk and fast asleep.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"그저 취해서 곤히 잠드셨을 뿐이라고요.\""], ["“I know nothing of that,”", "\"그건 나도 모르겠다만,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “but he is in a sad pickle now”; and so she told me all that her friend had said to her.", "\"어쨌든 지금은 딱한 꼴이란다.\" 그러고는 그녀의 친구가 자기에게 한 말을 죄다 들려주었다."], ["“Well, then,”", "\"그렇다면,\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “he fell into bad hands after I left him, for I am sure I left him safe enough.”", "\"제가 떠난 뒤에 그분이 나쁜 손에 떨어지신 거예요. 제가 떠날 때 분명 그분을 멀쩡하게 두고 왔으니까요.\""]], [["About ten days after, or a little more, my governess goes again to her friend, to introduce her to this gentleman; she had inquired other ways in the meantime, and found that he was about again, if not abroad again, so she got leave to speak with him.", "열흘쯤 뒤에, 아니면 그보다 조금 더 지나, 내 후견인은 다시 그녀의 친구에게 가, 자기를 이 신사에게 소개해 달라고 했다. 그녀는 그동안 다른 경로로도 알아보아, 그가 다시 거동하게 되었고, 아직 바깥출입까지는 아니어도 그러함을 알아냈던 터라, 그와 이야기를 나눌 허락을 얻었다."]], [["She was a woman of a admirable address, and wanted nobody to introduce her; she told her tale much better than I shall be able to tell it for her, for she was a mistress of her tongue, as I have said already.", "그녀는 응대가 감탄할 만한 여자라 자기를 소개해 줄 사람이 따로 필요 없었으니, 그녀는 내가 그녀를 대신해 이야기할 수 있는 것보다 훨씬 더 잘 그 이야기를 풀어냈는데, 앞서 이미 말했듯이 그녀는 입담의 명수였기 때문이다."], ["She told him that she came, though a stranger, with a single design of doing him a service and he should find she had no other end in it; that as she came purely on so friendly an account, she begged promise from him, that if he did not accept what she should officiously propose he would not take it ill that she meddled with what was not her business.", "그녀는 그에게, 비록 낯선 사람이긴 하나 오로지 그에게 도움이 되고자 하는 단 하나의 뜻으로 왔으며, 그것 말고 다른 속셈이 없음을 알게 되리라고 말했다. 그리고 자기가 순전히 그토록 우정 어린 까닭으로 온 것이니, 만일 그가 자기가 주제넘게 제안하는 바를 받아들이지 않더라도, 자기가 자기 일도 아닌 데에 참견한 것을 언짢게 여기지 말아 달라고 그에게 약속을 청했다."], ["She assured him that as what she had to say was a secret that belonged to him only, so whether he accepted her offer or not, it should remain a secret to all the world, unless he exposed it himself; nor should his refusing her service in it make her so little show her respect as to do him the least injury, so that he should be entirely at liberty to act as he thought fit.", "그녀는 그에게, 자기가 할 말은 오직 그에게만 관계되는 비밀이니만큼, 그가 자기 제안을 받아들이든 받아들이지 않든, 그가 스스로 그것을 폭로하지 않는 한 온 세상에 비밀로 남으리라고 장담했다. 또 그가 그 일에서 자기의 도움을 거절한다 해도 그녀가 그토록 옹졸하게 자기 존중심을 드러내어 그에게 조금이라도 봉변을 끼치는 일은 없을 것이니, 그가 옳다고 여기는 대로 행할 완전한 자유를 누리게 되리라고 했다."]], [["He looked very shy at first, and said he knew nothing that related to him that required much secrecy; that he had never done any man any wrong, and cared not what anybody might say of him; that it was no part of his character to be unjust to anybody, nor could he imagine in what any man could render him any service; but that if it was so disinterested a service as she said, he could not take it ill from any one that they should endeavour to serve him; and so, as it were, left her a liberty either to tell him or not to tell, as she thought fit.", "그는 처음에 무척 경계하는 낯빛으로, 자기에게 그토록 비밀을 요하는 무슨 일이 있는지 모르겠다고 했다. 자기는 어느 누구에게도 잘못한 적이 없으며, 누가 자기에 대해 무슨 말을 하든 개의치 않는다고, 누구에게든 부당하게 구는 것은 자기 인품의 일부가 아니며, 어떤 사람이 무슨 일로 자기에게 도움을 줄 수 있을지 상상도 못 하겠다고 했다. 그러나 만일 그것이 그녀의 말마따나 그토록 사심 없는 도움이라면, 누가 자기에게 도움을 주려 애쓰는 것을 언짢게 여길 수는 없겠노라고 했다. 그러고는 말하자면 그녀에게, 옳다고 여기는 대로 자기에게 말하든 말든 하라는 자유를 남겼다."]], [["She found him so perfectly indifferent, that she was almost afraid to enter into the point with him; but, however, after some other circumlocutions she told him that by a strange and unaccountable accident she came to have a particular knowledge of the late unhappy adventure he had fallen into, and that in such a manner, that there was nobody in the world but herself and him that were acquainted with it, no, not the very person that was with him.", "그녀는 그가 어찌나 더없이 무심한지, 그와 본론으로 들어가기가 거의 두려웠으나, 그래도 몇 마디 다른 에두른 말을 한 뒤에 그에게 말하기를, 이상하고 설명하기 어려운 우연으로, 자기가 근래 그가 빠졌던 그 불행한 모험에 대해 각별히 알게 되었으며, 그것도 온 세상에 자기와 그 말고는, 아니 그와 함께 있던 바로 그 사람조차도 그것을 아는 이가 없을 만큼 그러하다고 했다."]], [["He looked a little angrily at first.", "그는 처음에 조금 화난 듯이 바라보았다. \"무슨 모험요?\""], ["“What adventure?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그게,\""], ["said he.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"나이츠브——에서 돌아오시다 강도를 만나신 일 말입니다."], ["“Why,”", "햄스테드라고 해야겠군요, 나리.\""], ["said she, “of your being robbed coming from Knightbr——; Hampstead, sir, I should say,”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"놀라지 마십시오, 나리,\""], ["says she.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"제가 그날 나리께서 스미스필드의 회랑에서 나이츠브리지의 스프링 가든으로, 거기서 다시 스트랜드의 아무개 집으로 옮기신 걸음걸음을, 그리고 그 뒤에 어떻게 마차 안에 잠든 채 남겨지셨는지를 죄다 말씀드릴 수 있다고 해서 말입니다."], ["“Be not surprised, sir,”", "말씀드리건대, 이것에 놀라지 마십시오. 나리, 저는 나리를 등쳐 먹으러 온 것이 아니라, 나리께 아무것도 청하지 않으며, 장담컨대 나리와 함께 있던 그 여자는 나리가 누구신지 전혀 모르며 앞으로도 결코 알지 못할 것입니다."], ["says she, “that I am able to tell you every step you took that day from the cloister in Smithfield to the Spring Garden at Knightsbridge, and thence to the —— in the Strand, and how you were left asleep in the coach afterwards.", "그러면서도 어쩌면 저는 나리께 더 큰 도움을 드릴 수 있을지도 모릅니다. 저는 단지 제가 이런 일들을 전해 들었다는 것을 알려 드리러, 마치 그것을 감춰 주는 대가로 뇌물이라도 바라는 양 온 것이 아니니까요."], ["I say, let not this surprise you, for, sir, I do not come to make a booty of you, I ask nothing of you, and I assure you the woman that was with you knows nothing who you are, and never shall; and yet perhaps I may serve you further still, for I did not come barely to let you know that I was informed of these things, as if I wanted a bribe to conceal them; assure yourself, sir,”", "나리,\" 하고 그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “that whatever you think fit to do or say to me, it shall be all a secret as it is, as much as if I were in my grave.”", "\"장담하건대, 나리께서 제게 무엇을 하시거나 말씀하시는 것이 옳다고 여기시든, 그것은 지금처럼 죄다 비밀로 남을 것입니다. 마치 제가 무덤 속에 있기라도 한 것처럼요.\""]], [["He was astonished at her discourse, and said gravely to her, “Madam, you are a stranger to me, but it is very unfortunate that you should be let into the secret of the worst action of my life, and a thing that I am so justly ashamed of, that the only satisfaction of it to me was, that I thought it was known only to God and my own conscience.”", "그는 그녀의 말에 깜짝 놀라, 진중하게 그녀에게 말했다. \"부인, 부인은 제게 낯선 분이지만, 제 평생 가장 못된 행실의, 그리고 제가 너무도 당연히 부끄러워하는 일의 비밀을 부인께서 알게 되셨다는 것은 무척 불운한 일입니다."], ["“Pray, sir,”", "그 일에서 제게 유일한 위안이라면, 그것이 오직 하느님과 제 양심만 아는 일이라 여겼다는 것이었으니까요.\" \"이런, 나리,\""], ["says she, “do not reckon the discovery of it to me to be any part of your misfortune.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"그것이 제게 알려진 것을 나리의 불행의 일부로 여기지 마십시오."], ["It was a thing, I believe, you were surprised into, and perhaps the woman used some art to prompt you to it; however, you will never find any just cause,”", "그것은, 제 생각에, 나리께서 얼떨결에 빠져드신 일이고, 어쩌면 그 여자가 무슨 술수를 써서 나리를 부추겼을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 나리께서는 제가 그것을 전해 듣게 된 것을 후회하실 어떤 정당한 까닭도 결코 찾지 못하실 것입니다.\""], ["said she, “to repent that I came to hear of it; nor can your own mouth be more silent in it that I have been, and ever shall be.”", "하고 그녀가 말했다. \"또한 나리 자신의 입도, 제가 그 일에서 입을 다물어 온 만큼, 그리고 앞으로도 언제나 다물 만큼보다 더 입이 무거울 수는 없을 것입니다.\""]], [["“Well,” says he, “but let me do some justice to the woman too; whoever she is, I do assure you she prompted me to nothing, she rather declined me. It was my own folly and madness that brought me into it all, ay, and brought her into it too; I must give her her due so far. As to what she took from me, I could expect no less from her in the condition I was in, and to this hour I know not whether she robbed me or the coachman; if she did it, I forgive her, and I think all gentlemen that do so should be used in the same manner; but I am more concerned for some other things that I am for all that she took from me.”", "\"그렇군요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"하지만 그 여자에게도 좀 공평하게 대해 줍시다. 그녀가 누구든, 장담하건대 그녀는 제게 아무것도 부추기지 않았고, 오히려 저를 마다했습니다. 그 모든 일에 저를 빠뜨린 것은, 그래요, 그녀까지 빠뜨린 것은 제 자신의 어리석음과 광기였습니다. 그 점만큼은 그녀에게 마땅한 몫을 돌려야겠지요. 그녀가 제게서 가져간 것으로 말하자면, 제가 처한 형편에서 그녀에게 그보다 못한 것을 기대할 수도 없었고, 지금 이 순간까지도 저는 그녀가 저를 턴 것인지 마부가 턴 것인지 모릅니다. 만일 그녀가 그랬다면 저는 그녀를 용서하며, 그렇게 구는 모든 신사도 마찬가지로 당해야 마땅하다고 생각합니다. 그러나 저는 그녀가 제게서 가져간 그 모든 것보다 다른 어떤 것들이 더 마음에 걸립니다.\""]], [["My governess now began to come into the whole matter, and he opened himself freely to her. First she said to him, in answer to what he had said about me, “I am glad, sir, you are so just to the person that you were with; I assure you she is a gentlewoman, and no woman of the town; and however you prevailed with her so far as you did, I am sure ’tis not her practice. You ran a great venture indeed, sir; but if that be any part of your care, I am persuaded you may be perfectly easy, for I dare assure you no man has touched her, before you, since her husband, and he has been dead now almost eight years.”", "이제 내 후견인은 그 모든 일의 본론으로 들어가기 시작했고, 그는 그녀에게 거리낌 없이 속을 털어놓았다. 먼저 그녀는 그가 나에 대해 한 말에 답하여 그에게 말했다. \"나리, 나리께서 함께 계셨던 그 사람에게 그토록 공평하게 대하시니 기쁩니다. 장담하건대 그녀는 양갓집 여자이지 거리의 여자가 아닙니다. 그리고 나리께서 그녀를 어떻게 그토록 넘어가게 하셨든, 그것이 그녀의 버릇이 아님은 분명합니다. 나리께서는 실로 큰 위험을 무릅쓰셨습니다, 나리. 그러나 그것이 나리 걱정의 일부라면, 장담컨대 나리께서는 완전히 마음 놓으셔도 됩니다. 감히 장담하건대, 나리 이전에는 그녀의 남편 이후로 어떤 남자도 그녀에게 손댄 적이 없으며, 그가 죽은 지도 이제 거의 여덟 해입니다.\""]], [["It appeared that this was his grievance, and that he was in a very great fright about it; however, when my governess said this to him, he appeared very well pleased, and said, “Well, madam, to be plain with you, if I was satisfied of that, I should not so much value what I lost; for, as to that, the temptation was great, and perhaps she was poor and wanted it.” “If she had not been poor, sir ——,” says my governess, “I assure you she would never have yielded to you; and as her poverty first prevailed with her to let you do as you did, so the same poverty prevailed with her to pay herself at last, when she saw you were in such a condition, that if she had not done it, perhaps the next coachman might have done it.”", "이것이 그의 근심이었고, 그가 그것에 대해 무척 큰 두려움에 떨고 있었던 것이 드러났다. 그러나 내 후견인이 그에게 이렇게 말하자, 그는 무척 흡족해하며 말했다. \"그렇군요, 부인, 솔직히 말씀드리자면, 제가 그 점에 대해 확신할 수만 있다면, 제가 잃은 것은 그리 대수롭지 않게 여길 것입니다. 그것으로 말하자면, 유혹이 컸고, 어쩌면 그녀는 가난해서 그것이 필요했을 테니까요.\" \"그녀가 가난하지 않았더라면, 아무개 경,\" 하고 내 후견인이 말했다. \"장담하건대 그녀는 결코 나리께 응하지 않았을 것입니다. 그리고 그녀의 가난이 처음에 그녀를 움직여 나리께서 하시는 대로 내버려 두게 한 것처럼, 바로 그 가난이 끝에 가서 그녀를 움직여, 나리께서 그런 형편에 계신 것을 보고 스스로 제 값을 챙기게 한 것입니다. 만일 그녀가 그러지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 다음 마부가 그랬을 테니까요.\""]], [["“Well,”", "\"그렇군요,\""], ["says he, “much good may it do her.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그녀에게 큰 복이 되기를."], ["I say again, all the gentlemen that do so ought to be used in the same manner, and then they would be cautious of themselves.", "다시 말하건대, 그렇게 구는 모든 신사는 마찬가지로 당해 마땅하며, 그러면 그들도 자신을 조심하게 되겠지요. 저는 그것에 대해서는 더 개의치 않습니다."], ["I have no more concern about it, but on the score which you hinted at before, madam.”", "다만 부인께서 앞서 넌지시 비치신 그 점에 관해서는 다릅니다.\" 여기서 그는 우리 사이에 오간 일을 두고 그녀와 좀 거리낌 없는 이야기를 나누었는데, 그것은 여자가 적기에는 그리 적절치 않은 것이며, 또 자기 아내와 관련하여 그의 마음에 얹힌 큰 두려움, 곧 자기가 나에게서 무슨 몹쓸 것을 옮았을까, 그래서 그것을 더 옮기게 될까 하는 두려움에 관한 것이었다."], ["Here he entered into some freedoms with her on the subject of what passed between us, which are not so proper for a woman to write, and the great terror that was upon his mind with relation to his wife, for fear he should have received any injury from me, and should communicate it farther; and asked her at last if she could not procure him an opportunity to speak with me.", "그러고는 마침내 그녀에게, 자기가 나와 이야기 나눌 기회를 주선해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 물었다. 내 후견인은 그에게, 내가 그런 일에서 깨끗한 여자이며, 그가 그 점에 관해서는 자기 아내와 함께 있는 것만큼이나 완전히 안전하다고 거듭 장담했다."], ["My governess gave him further assurances of my being a woman clear from any such thing, and that he was as entirely safe in that respect as he was with his own lady; but as for seeing me, she said it might be of dangerous consequence; but, however, that she would talk with me, and let him know my answer, using at the same time some arguments to persuade him not to desire it, and that it could be of no service to him, seeing she hoped he had no desire to renew a correspondence with me, and that on my account it was a kind of putting my life in his hands.", "그러나 나를 만나는 것으로 말하자면, 그녀는 그것이 위험한 결과를 부를 수 있다고 했다. 그러나 어쨌든 자기가 나와 이야기해 보고 그에게 내 대답을 알려 주겠다고 하면서, 동시에 그것을 바라지 말라고 그를 설득할 몇 가지 논거를 댔으니, 곧 그가 나와 다시 왕래를 트고 싶어 하지 않기를 바라는 만큼, 그것이 그에게 아무런 도움도 되지 않을 것이며, 또 내 처지에서는 그것이 일종의 내 목숨을 그의 손에 맡기는 일이라고 했다."]], [["He told her he had a great desire to see me, that he would give her any assurances that were in his power, not to take any advantages of me, and that in the first place he would give me a general release from all demands of any kind.", "그는 그녀에게, 자기가 무척 나를 만나고 싶으며, 나를 이용해 어떤 잇속도 챙기지 않겠다는, 자기 힘이 닿는 어떤 보장이든 그녀에게 주겠다고 했다. 또 무엇보다 먼저 자기가 내게 온갖 종류의 청구에 대한 전면적인 면책을 주겠노라 했다."], ["She insisted how it might tend to a further divulging the secret, and might in the end be injurious to him, entreating him not to press for it; so at length he desisted.", "그녀는 그것이 어떻게 그 비밀을 더 퍼뜨리는 쪽으로 이어질 수 있는지, 그리고 끝에 가서는 그에게 해가 될 수 있는지를 강조하며, 그것을 조르지 말아 달라고 간청했다. 그리하여 마침내 그는 단념했다."]], [["They had some discourse upon the subject of the things he had lost, and he seemed to be very desirous of his gold watch, and told her if she could procure that for him, he would willingly give as much for it as it was worth.", "그들은 그가 잃은 물건들을 두고 얼마간 이야기를 나누었고, 그는 그 금시계를 무척 되찾고 싶어 했고, 만약 그녀가 그것을 구해다 줄 수 있다면 값이 나가는 만큼 기꺼이 치르겠노라고 했다."], ["She told him she would endeavour to procure it for him, and leave the valuing it to himself.", "그녀는 그것을 구해 보겠노라 했고, 값은 그가 매기도록 그에게 맡기겠다고 했다."]], [["Accordingly the next day she carried the watch, and he gave her thirty guineas for it, which was more than I should have been able to make of it, though it seems it cost much more.", "이렇게 하여 이튿날 그녀가 시계를 가져갔고, 그는 그 값으로 삼십 기니를 주었는데, 그것은 내가 받아 낼 수 있었을 것보다 더 많은 액수였으니, 아무래도 처음 산 값은 훨씬 더 나갔던 모양이었다."], ["He spoke something of his periwig, which it seems cost him threescore guineas, and his snuff-box, and in a few days more she carried them too; which obliged him very much, and he gave her thirty more.", "그는 자기 가발에 대해서도 무어라 말했는데, 그것은 예순 기니나 들었던 듯했고, 또 코담뱃갑에 대해서도 말하기에, 며칠 더 지나 그녀가 그것들마저 가져다주었으니, 그는 무척 고마워하며 삼십 기니를 더 주었다."], ["The next day I sent him his fine sword and cane gratis, and demanded nothing of him, but I had no mind to see him, unless it had been so that he might be satisfied I knew who he was, which he was not willing to.", "이튿날 나는 그의 멋진 검과 지팡이를 거저 돌려보냈고 아무것도 요구하지 않았으나, 그를 보고 싶은 마음은 없었으니, 다만 그가 자기가 누구인지를 내가 안다는 사실을 확인하고 싶어 했더라면 모를 일이었지만, 그는 그것을 원치 않았다."]], [["Then he entered into a long talk with her of the manner how she came to know all this matter.", "그러자 그는 그녀가 어떻게 이 모든 일을 알게 되었는지를 두고 긴 이야기를 나누었다."], ["She formed a long tale of that part; how she had it from one that I had told the whole story to, and that was to help me dispose of the goods; and this confidante brought the things to her, she being by profession a pawnbroker; and she hearing of his worship’s disaster, guessed at the thing in general; that having gotten the things into her hands, she had resolved to come and try as she had done.", "그녀는 그 대목에 관해 긴 이야기를 지어냈으니, 내가 전말을 다 털어놓은 어떤 사람에게서 들었고, 그 사람이 내가 물건을 처분하는 것을 도와주기로 한 사람이며, 그 심복이 자기에게 물건을 가져왔는데, 자기는 직업이 전당포 주인인지라, 그분의 봉변을 전해 듣고 일을 대충 짐작했고, 물건이 자기 손에 들어온 터라 이렇게 한번 시도해 보기로 결심했노라고 했다."], ["She then gave him repeated assurances that it should never go out of her mouth, and though she knew the woman very well, yet she had not let her know, meaning me, anything of it; that is to say, who the person was, which, by the way, was false; but, however, it was not to his damage, for I never opened my mouth of it to anybody.", "그러고는 그것이 결코 자기 입 밖으로 새어 나가지 않으리라고 거듭 다짐하면서, 그 여자(나를 가리키는 것이었다)를 매우 잘 알지만 그녀에게는 그 일에 관해, 다시 말해 그 사람이 누구인지에 관해 아무것도 알리지 않았노라고 했으니, 그 말은 사실 거짓이었으나, 어찌 되었든 그에게 해가 될 것은 없었으니, 나는 그 일에 관해 누구에게도 입을 연 적이 없었기 때문이다."]], [["I had a great many thoughts in my head about my seeing him again, and was often sorry that I had refused it.", "그를 다시 만나는 문제를 두고 내 머릿속에는 갖가지 생각이 오갔고, 그것을 거절했던 일을 자주 후회했다."], ["I was persuaded that if I had seen him, and let him know that I knew him, I should have made some advantage of him, and perhaps have had some maintenance from him; and though it was a life wicked enough, yet it was not so full of danger as this I was engaged in.", "만약 내가 그를 만나, 내가 그를 안다는 것을 알게 했더라면, 그에게서 무언가 득을 보았을 테고, 어쩌면 그에게서 생계를 얻었을지도 모른다는 생각이 들었으니, 그것은 충분히 사악한 삶이긴 했어도, 내가 발을 들인 이 일만큼 위험으로 가득 차 있지는 않았던 것이다."], ["However, those thoughts wore off, and I declined seeing him again, for that time; but my governess saw him often, and he was very kind to her, giving her something almost every time he saw her.", "그러나 그런 생각들도 점차 사그라들어, 나는 이번만은 그를 다시 만나기를 마다했다. 그런데 내 후견인은 그를 자주 만났고, 그는 그녀에게 매우 친절하여, 만날 때마다 거의 빠짐없이 무언가를 주었다."], ["One time in particular she found him very merry, and as she thought he had some wine in his head, and he pressed her again very earnestly to let him see that woman that, as he said, had bewitched him so that night, my governess, who was from the beginning for my seeing him, told him he was so desirous of it that she could almost yield of it, if she could prevail upon me; adding that if he would please to come to her house in the evening, she would endeavour it, upon his repeated assurances of forgetting what was past.", "특히 한번은 그녀가 그를 보니 무척 흥겨워하고 있었는데, 그녀가 보기에 술기운이 좀 있는 듯했고, 그가 그날 밤 자기를 그토록 홀려 놓은 그 여자를 다시 보게 해 달라고 또 한 번 몹시 간곡하게 졸라 대자, 처음부터 내가 그를 만나는 쪽이었던 내 후견인은, 그가 그토록 간절히 바라니 만약 자기가 나를 설득할 수만 있다면 거의 응해 줄 수도 있겠노라 하고는, 지난 일을 잊겠다고 거듭 다짐만 한다면 저녁에 자기 집으로 와 주시면 한번 애써 보겠노라고 덧붙였다."]], [["Accordingly she came to me, and told me all the discourse; in short, she soon biassed me to consent, in a case which I had some regret in my mind for declining before; so I prepared to see him.", "이렇게 하여 그녀는 나를 찾아와 그 모든 이야기를 전했고, 한마디로, 전에 거절했던 일을 마음 한구석에 다소 후회하고 있던 터라, 그녀는 이내 나를 동의 쪽으로 기울게 만들었다. 그리하여 나는 그를 만날 채비를 했다."], ["I dressed me to all the advantage possible, I assure you, and for the first time used a little art; I say for the first time, for I had never yielded to the baseness of paint before, having always had vanity enough to believe I had no need of it.", "단언하건대 나는 할 수 있는 한 가장 돋보이게 차려입었고, 난생처음 약간의 기교를 부렸으니, 처음이라 함은 전에는 화장이라는 천한 짓에 한 번도 굴한 적이 없었기 때문이니, 늘 그런 것은 필요 없다고 믿을 만한 허영심이 충분히 있었던 것이다."]], [["At the hour appointed he came; and as she observed before, so it was plain still, that he had been drinking, though very far from what we call being in drink.", "약속한 시각에 그가 왔고, 그녀가 앞서 보았던 대로 여전히 분명했으니, 그가 술을 마셨다는 것이었는데, 다만 우리가 흔히 취했다고 하는 정도와는 거리가 멀었다."], ["He appeared exceeding pleased to see me, and entered into a long discourse with me upon the old affair.", "그는 나를 보고 무척 기뻐하는 듯했고, 나와 더불어 지난 일을 두고 긴 이야기에 들어갔다."], ["I begged his pardon very often for my share of it, protested I had not any such design when first I met him, that I had not gone out with him but that I took him for a very civil gentleman, and that he made me so many promises of offering no uncivility to me.", "나는 거기서 내가 맡은 몫에 대해 그에게 여러 번 용서를 빌었고, 처음 그를 만났을 때 그런 의도는 조금도 없었으며, 다만 그가 매우 점잖은 신사라고 여겼고 또 내게 어떤 무례도 범하지 않겠다고 그토록 여러 번 약속했기에 함께 나섰던 것이라고 항변했다."]], [["He alleged the wine he drank, and that he scarce knew what he did, and that if it had not been so, I should never have let him take the freedom with me that he had done.", "그는 자기가 마신 술을 핑계 삼으며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지조차 거의 몰랐고, 만약 그렇지만 않았더라면 내가 결코 그토록 마음대로 하게 두지 않았으리라고 했다."], ["He protested to me that he never touched any woman but me since he was married to his wife, and it was a surprise upon him; complimented me upon being so particularly agreeable to him, and the like; and talked so much of that kind, till I found he had talked himself almost into a temper to do the same thing over again.", "그는 아내와 결혼한 이래 나 말고는 어떤 여자도 손댄 적이 없으며, 그것은 자기에게도 뜻밖의 일이었노라고 항변했고, 내가 자기에게 그토록 각별히 마음에 든다고 추어올리며 그런 따위의 말을 늘어놓았으니, 마침내 나는 그가 같은 일을 다시 한번 벌일 만한 기분으로 스스로를 거의 몰아갔다는 것을 알아챘다."], ["But I took him up short.", "그러나 나는 그를 단번에 막아섰다."], ["I protested I had never suffered any man to touch me since my husband died, which was near eight years.", "나는 남편이 죽은 이래—그것이 근 여덟 해 전이었으니—어떤 남자도 나를 손대게 한 적이 없다고 항변했다."], ["He said he believed it to be so truly; and added that madam had intimated as much to him, and that it was his opinion of that part which made his desire to see me again; and that since he had once broke in upon his virtue with me, and found no ill consequences, he could be safe in venturing there again; and so, in short, it went on to what I expected, and to what will not bear relating.", "그는 정말 그러리라 믿는다고 했고, 부인(후견인)도 그에게 그쯤 넌지시 일러 주었으며, 바로 그 점에 대한 자기의 생각 때문에 나를 다시 보고 싶었던 것이라고 덧붙였다. 그러고는 이미 한 번 나와 더불어 자기의 덕을 깨뜨렸어도 아무 나쁜 결과가 없었으니, 다시 한번 거기에 모험을 걸어도 안전할 수 있겠다고 하더니, 한마디로, 그것은 내가 예상한 데로, 그리고 차마 옮길 수 없는 데로 흘러갔다."]], [["My old governess had foreseen it, as well as I, and therefore led him into a room which had not a bed in it, and yet had a chamber within it which had a bed, whither we withdrew for the rest of the night; and, in short, after some time being together, he went to bed, and lay there all night.", "내 늙은 후견인은 나만큼이나 그것을 미리 내다보았던 터라, 그를 침대가 없는 방으로 이끌었는데, 그 방 안쪽으로는 침대가 있는 작은 방이 또 있어서, 우리는 그날 밤의 나머지를 보내려고 그리로 물러갔다. 한마디로, 얼마 동안 함께 있다가 그는 잠자리에 들었고 밤새 거기 누워 있었다."], ["I withdrew, but came again undressed in the morning, before it was day, and lay with him the rest of the time.", "나는 물러났다가, 날이 새기 전 아침에 잠옷 차림으로 다시 와서 남은 시간을 그와 함께 누워 있었다."]], [["Thus, you see, having committed a crime once is a sad handle to the committing of it again; whereas all the regret and reflections wear off when the temptation renews itself.", "이렇듯, 여러분도 보다시피, 죄를 한 번 저지른다는 것은 그것을 다시 저지르는 데에 슬픈 빌미가 되니, 유혹이 새로이 찾아들면 온갖 후회와 반성은 다 닳아 없어지는 법이다."], ["Had I not yielded to see him again, the corrupt desire in him had worn off, and ’tis very probable he had never fallen into it with anybody else, as I really believe he had not done before.", "만약 내가 그를 다시 만나는 데 응하지 않았더라면, 그 안의 타락한 욕망은 사그라들었을 테고, 그가 다른 누구와도 그런 일에 빠지지 않았으리라는 것은 십중팔구 옳은 말이니, 내가 정말로 믿건대 그는 전에도 그런 적이 없었던 것이다."]], [["When he went away, I told him I hoped he was satisfied he had not been robbed again.", "그가 떠날 때, 나는 그가 다시 강탈당하지 않았다는 것을 확인했기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["He told me he was satisfied in that point, and could trust me again, and putting his hand in his pocket, gave me five guineas, which was the first money I had gained that way for many years.", "그는 그 점에 대해서는 만족하며 나를 다시 믿을 수 있노라 했고, 주머니에 손을 넣어 다섯 기니를 주었으니, 그것은 여러 해 만에 내가 그런 식으로 벌어들인 첫 돈이었다."]], [["I had several visits of the like kind from him, but he never came into a settled way of maintenance, which was what I would have best pleased with.", "나는 그에게서 비슷한 종류의 방문을 여러 번 받았으나, 그는 끝내 안정된 생계 지원의 길로는 들어서지 않았으니, 그것이야말로 내가 가장 흡족해했을 일이었다."], ["Once, indeed, he asked me how I did to live.", "사실 한번은 그가 내게 어떻게 살아가느냐고 물었다."], ["I answered him pretty quick, that I assured him I had never taken that course that I took with him, but that indeed I worked at my needle, and could just maintain myself; that sometime it was as much as I was able to do, and I shifted hard enough.", "나는 꽤 재빠르게 대답하기를, 단언하건대 그에게 한 그런 짓을 다른 누구에게도 한 적이 없으며, 다만 정말로 바느질로 일하여 겨우 제 한 몸을 건사할 수 있을 뿐이고, 때로는 그것이 내 힘으로 할 수 있는 전부라서 꽤나 빠듯하게 버텨 간다고 했다."]], [["He seemed to reflect upon himself that he should be the first person to lead me into that, which he assured me he never intended to do himself; and it touched him a little, he said, that he should be the cause of his own sin and mine too.", "그는 나를 그 길로 처음 끌어들인 사람이 자기였다는 사실을 두고 스스로를 책망하는 듯했는데, 자기로서는 결코 그럴 작정이 아니었노라고 내게 다짐했다. 그는 자기 죄와 내 죄의 원인이 자기여야 한다는 것이 마음에 좀 걸린다고 했다."], ["He would often make just reflections also upon the crime itself, and upon the particular circumstances of it with respect to himself; how wine introduced the inclinations, how the devil led him to the place, and found out an object to tempt him, and he made the moral always himself.", "그는 또한 그 죄 자체에 대해, 그리고 자기 자신과 관련된 그 죄의 특정한 정황들에 대해 자주 정당한 성찰을 하곤 했으니, 술이 어떻게 그 욕망을 불러들였는지, 악마가 어떻게 그를 그 자리로 이끌어 유혹할 대상을 찾아냈는지를 두고 늘 스스로 교훈을 끌어내곤 했다."]], [["When these thoughts were upon him he would go away, and perhaps not come again in a month’s time or longer; but then as the serious part wore off, the lewd part would wear in, and then he came prepared for the wicked part.", "이런 생각들이 그를 사로잡을 때면 그는 떠나가서, 어쩌면 한 달이 넘도록 다시 오지 않기도 했다. 그러나 그러다가 진지한 부분이 닳아 없어지면 음탕한 부분이 다시 자라나, 그때는 그 사악한 짓을 할 채비를 하고 오는 것이었다."], ["Thus we lived for some time; though he did not keep, as they call it, yet he never failed doing things that were handsome, and sufficient to maintain me without working, and, which was better, without following my old trade.", "우리는 한동안 이렇게 살았으니, 그가 이른바 첩살림을 차려 준 것은 아니었으나, 그래도 그는 점잖은 일들을, 내가 일하지 않고도 살아갈 만큼, 더 나아가 옛 장사를 다시 따르지 않고도 살아갈 만큼 충분히 베풀기를 한 번도 거른 적이 없었다."]], [["But this affair had its end too; for after about a year, I found that he did not come so often as usual, and at last he left if off altogether without any dislike to bidding adieu; and so there was an end of that short scene of life, which added no great store to me, only to make more work for repentance.", "그러나 이 관계 또한 끝이 있었으니, 한 해쯤 지나자 그가 예전만큼 자주 오지 않는다는 것을 알게 되었고, 마침내 그는 작별 인사도 마다하는 기색 없이 아예 발길을 끊어 버렸다. 그리하여 내 삶의 그 짧은 한 장면은 끝났으니, 그것은 내게 별다른 재산을 보태 주지 않았고, 다만 회개해야 할 거리만 더 늘려 놓았을 뿐이다."]], [["However, during this interval I confined myself pretty much at home; at least, being thus provided for, I made no adventures, no, not for a quarter of a year after he left me; but then finding the fund fail, and being loth to spend upon the main stock, I began to think of my old trade, and to look abroad into the street again; and my first step was lucky enough.", "그래도 이 동안에는 나는 꽤 집 안에 틀어박혀 지냈으니, 적어도 이렇게 살림이 마련되어 있는 터라 아무 모험도 벌이지 않았고, 그가 나를 떠난 뒤로 석 달이 넘도록 그러했다. 그러나 밑천이 바닥나는 것을 알고, 또 본전을 헐어 쓰기는 싫었던지라, 나는 옛 장사를 다시 생각하고, 또다시 거리로 나가 두리번거리기 시작했으니, 첫걸음은 꽤나 운이 좋았다."]], [["I had dressed myself up in a very mean habit, for as I had several shapes to appear in, I was now in an ordinary stuff-gown, a blue apron, and a straw hat and I placed myself at the door of the Three Cups Inn in St. John Street.", "나는 아주 초라한 옷차림으로 차려입었으니, 내게는 모습을 꾸밀 여러 형태가 있었던 터라, 이번에는 평범한 모직 가운에 파란 앞치마, 그리고 밀짚모자 차림으로, 세인트존 거리의 스리컵스 여관 문간에 자리를 잡았다."], ["There were several carriers used the inn, and the stage-coaches for Barnet, for Totteridge, and other towns that way stood always in the street in the evening, when they prepared to set out, so that I was ready for anything that offered, for either one or other.", "그 여관에는 여러 짐꾼이 드나들었고, 바닛이며 토터리지며 그쪽 방면의 다른 고을로 가는 역마차들이 저녁이면 출발 채비를 할 때 늘 거리에 서 있었으니, 그래서 나는 이쪽이든 저쪽이든 어느 것에든 손쓸 채비가 되어 있었다."], ["The meaning was this; people come frequently with bundles and small parcels to those inns, and call for such carriers or coaches as they want, to carry them into the country; and there generally attend women, porters’ wives or daughters, ready to take in such things for their respective people that employ them.", "그 까닭은 이러하니, 사람들이 더러 보따리며 작은 꾸러미를 들고 그런 여관들로 와서 자기가 원하는 짐꾼이나 마차를 불러 그것을 시골로 실어 보내곤 하는데, 거기에는 대개 여자들—짐꾼의 아내나 딸들—이 대기하고 있다가, 저마다 자기를 고용한 사람들을 위해 그런 물건들을 맡아 받곤 했던 것이다."]], [["It happened very oddly that I was standing at the inn gate, and a woman that had stood there before, and which was the porter’s wife belonging to the Barnet stage-coach, having observed me, asked if I waited for any of the coaches.", "참으로 묘하게도, 내가 여관 문간에 서 있을 때, 전부터 거기 서 있던 한 여자—바닛 역마차에 속한 짐꾼의 아내였다—가 나를 눈여겨보더니, 어느 마차를 기다리느냐고 물었다."], ["I told her Yes, I waited for my mistress, that was coming to go to Barnet.", "나는 그렇다고, 바닛으로 가시려고 오시는 내 마님을 기다린다고 했다."], ["She asked me who was my mistress, and I told her any madam’s name that came next me; but as it seemed, I happened upon a name, a family of which name lived at Hadley, just beyond Barnet.", "그녀가 내 마님이 누구냐고 묻기에, 나는 마침 떠오르는 아무 부인의 이름이나 댔는데, 공교롭게도 내가 댄 이름은, 바닛 바로 너머 해들리에 그 성을 쓰는 가문이 살고 있는 그런 이름이었던 모양이었다."]], [["I said no more to her, or she to me, a good while; but by and by, somebody calling her at a door a little way off, she desired me that if anybody called for the Barnet coach, I would step and call her at the house, which it seems was an alehouse.", "나는 한참 동안 그녀에게 더 말하지 않았고, 그녀도 내게 더 말하지 않았다. 그런데 이윽고 누군가가 조금 떨어진 어느 문간에서 그녀를 부르자, 그녀는 만약 누가 바닛 마차를 찾거든 그 집—아무래도 선술집인 듯했다—으로 가서 자기를 불러 달라고 내게 청했다."], ["I said Yes, very readily, and away she went.", "나는 매우 선뜻 그러겠다고 했고, 그녀는 가 버렸다."]], [["She was no sooner gone but comes a wench and a child, puffing and sweating, and asks for the Barnet coach.", "그녀가 가자마자 한 계집아이가 아이 하나를 데리고 씩씩거리며 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리면서 와서는 바닛 마차를 찾았다. 나는 즉시 \"여기요\""], ["I answered presently, “Here.”", "하고 대답했다. \"바닛 마차에 속한 사람이에요?\""], ["“Do you belong to the Barnet coach?”", "하고 그녀가 물었다. \"그렇단다, 얘야.\""], ["says she.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Yes, sweetheart,”", "\"무엇이 필요하니?\""], ["said I; “what do ye want?”", "\"승객 두 사람 자리가 필요해요.\""], ["“I want room for two passengers,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그분들이 어디 계시니, 얘야?\""], ["says she.", "내가 말했다."], ["“Where are they, sweetheart?” said I.", "\"여기 이 여자아이요, 부디 마차에 태워 주세요.\""], ["“Here’s this girl, pray let her go into the coach,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"저는 가서 마님을 모셔 올게요.\""], ["says she, “and I’ll go and fetch my mistress.”", "\"그럼 서두르렴, 얘야.\" 내가 말했다."], ["“Make haste, then, sweetheart,”", "\"안 그러면 자리가 다 찰지도 모르니까.\""], ["says I, “for we may be full else.”", "그 하녀는 겨드랑이에 큼직한 보따리를 끼고 있었다."], ["The maid had a great bundle under her arm; so she put the child into the coach, and I said, “You had best put your bundle into the coach too.”", "그래서 그녀가 아이를 마차에 태우자, 나는 \"네 보따리도 마차에 넣는 게 좋겠구나\" 하고 말했다."], ["“No,”", "\"아니에요.\""], ["says she, “I am afraid somebody should slip it away from the child.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"누가 아이한테서 슬쩍 채 갈까 봐 걱정돼요.\""], ["“Give to me, then,”", "\"그럼 내게 다오.\""], ["said I, “and I’ll take care of it.”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 잘 봐주마.\""], ["“Do, then,”", "\"그래 주세요.\""], ["says she, “and be sure you take of it.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그리고 꼭 잘 챙겨 주세요.\""], ["“I’ll answer for it,”", "\"내가 책임지마.\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “if it were for £20 value.”", "\"이게 이십 파운드짜리라 해도 말이다.\""], ["“There, take it, then,”", "\"그럼 자, 받으세요.\""], ["says she, and away she goes.", "그녀가 말하고는 가 버렸다."]], [["As soon as I had got the bundle, and the maid was out of sight, I goes on towards the alehouse, where the porter’s wife was, so that if I had met her, I had then only been going to give her the bundle, and to call her to her business, as if I was going away, and could stay no longer; but as I did not meet her, I walked away, and turning into Charterhouse Lane, then crossed into Bartholomew Close, so into Little Britain, and through the Bluecoat Hospital, into Newgate Street.", "보따리를 손에 넣자마자, 그리고 그 하녀가 보이지 않게 되자, 나는 짐꾼의 아내가 있는 선술집 쪽으로 걸어갔으니, 만약 그녀를 만났더라면 그저 보따리를 건네주고, 떠나야 해서 더는 있을 수 없는 양 그녀에게 일을 보라고 부르려던 것처럼 보였을 터였다. 그러나 그녀를 만나지 않았으므로, 나는 그대로 걸어가, 차터하우스 골목으로 꺾어 들어 바살러뮤 클로즈로 가로질러 갔고, 그리하여 리틀브리튼으로, 또 블루코트 자선원을 지나 뉴게이트 거리로 들어섰다."]], [["To prevent my being known, I pulled off my blue apron, and wrapped the bundle in it, which before was made up in a piece of painted calico, and very remarkable; I also wrapped up my straw hat in it, and so put the bundle upon my head; and it was very well that I did thus, for coming through the Bluecoat Hospital, who should I meet but the wench that had given me the bundle to hold.", "신원이 들통나지 않도록, 나는 파란 앞치마를 벗어, 전에는 무늬 입힌 옥양목 조각으로 싸여 있어 매우 눈에 띄던 그 보따리를 거기에 쌌다. 또 밀짚모자도 거기에 싸 넣고, 그리하여 그 보따리를 머리에 이었으니, 이렇게 한 것이 참으로 다행이었던 것이, 블루코트 자선원을 지나오다가 하필 누구를 만났겠는가, 다름 아닌 그 보따리를 내게 맡겼던 그 계집아이였다."], ["It seems she was going with her mistress, whom she had been gone to fetch, to the Barnet coaches.", "아무래도 그녀는 모셔 오러 갔던 마님과 함께 바닛 마차로 가는 길인 듯했다."]], [["I saw she was in haste, and I had no business to stop her; so away she went, and I brought my bundle safe home to my governess.", "그녀가 바쁘다는 것을 알았고, 나로서도 그녀를 붙들 까닭이 없었으니, 그녀는 그대로 가 버렸고, 나는 보따리를 무사히 내 후견인에게 가져왔다."], ["There was no money, nor plate, or jewels in the bundle, but a very good suit of Indian damask, a gown and a petticoat, a laced-head and ruffles of very good Flanders lace, and some linen and other things, such as I knew very well the value of.", "그 보따리에는 돈도, 은붙이도, 보석도 없었으나, 매우 좋은 인도산 다마스크 한 벌—가운과 페티코트—과 아주 좋은 플랜더스 레이스로 된 레이스 머리 장식과 소맷동, 그리고 약간의 아마포와 그 밖에 내가 그 값어치를 아주 잘 아는 물건들이 들어 있었다."]], [["This was not indeed my own invention, but was given me by one that had practised it with success, and my governess liked it extremely; and indeed I tried it again several times, though never twice near the same place; for the next time I tried it in White Chapel, just by the corner of Petticoat Lane, where the coaches stand that go out to Stratford and Bow, and that side of the country, and another time at the Flying Horse, without Bishopgate, where the Cheston coaches then lay; and I had always the good luck to come off with some booty.", "이것은 사실 내가 고안한 것이 아니라, 그것을 성공적으로 써먹어 본 어떤 사람이 내게 일러 준 것이었고, 내 후견인은 그것을 무척 마음에 들어 했다. 사실 나는 그 수법을 여러 번 다시 써먹었으나, 같은 장소 근처에서는 결코 두 번 하지 않았으니, 다음번에는 화이트채플에서, 바로 페티코트 골목 모퉁이 곁, 스트랫퍼드며 보며 그쪽 방면 시골로 나가는 마차들이 서 있는 데서 해 보았고, 또 한 번은 비숍게이트 바깥의 플라잉호스에서, 그 무렵 체스턴행 마차들이 서던 데서 해 보았다. 그리고 나는 늘 운이 좋아 무언가 노획물을 들고 빠져나왔다."]], [["Another time I placed myself at a warehouse by the waterside, where the coasting vessels from the north come, such as from Newcastle-upon-Tyne, Sunderland, and other places.", "또 한 번은 강가의 어느 창고 옆에 자리를 잡았으니, 그곳은 뉴캐슬어폰타인이며 선덜랜드며 그 밖의 데에서 오는 북쪽의 연안 배들이 닿는 데였다."], ["Here, the warehouses being shut, comes a young fellow with a letter; and he wanted a box and a hamper that was come from Newcastle-upon-Tyne.", "여기서, 창고가 닫혀 있을 때, 한 젊은이가 편지를 들고 와서는, 뉴캐슬어폰타인에서 온 상자 하나와 광주리 하나를 찾았다."], ["I asked him if he had the marks of it; so he shows me the letter, by virtue of which he was to ask for it, and which gave an account of the contents, the box being full of linen, and the hamper full of glass ware.", "나는 그에게 그것의 표지를 가지고 있느냐고 물었고, 그러자 그는 그것을 청하는 근거가 될 편지를 보여 주었는데, 거기에는 내용물에 대한 설명이 적혀 있었으니, 상자에는 아마포가 가득하고 광주리에는 유리 그릇이 가득하다는 것이었다."], ["I read the letter, and took care to see the name, and the marks, the name of the person that sent the goods, the name of the person that they were sent to; then I bade the messenger come in the morning, for that the warehouse-keeper would not be there any more that night.", "나는 그 편지를 읽고, 이름과 표지를, 곧 물건을 보낸 사람의 이름과 보내진 사람의 이름을 잘 봐 두었다. 그러고는 그 심부름꾼에게 아침에 다시 오라고 일렀으니, 창고지기가 그날 밤에는 더는 거기 있지 않을 것이기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["Away went I, and getting materials in a public house, I wrote a letter from Mr. John Richardson of Newcastle to his dear cousin Jemmy Cole, in London, with an account that he sent by such a vessel (for I remembered all the particulars to a title), so many pieces of huckaback linen, so many ells of Dutch holland and the like, in a box, and a hamper of flint glasses from Mr.", "나는 떠나가, 어느 선술집에서 필기구를 구해, 뉴캐슬의 존 리처드슨 씨가 런던에 있는 친애하는 사촌 제미 콜에게 보내는 편지를 한 통 썼으니, 그가 아무개 배편으로(나는 그 세세한 사항을 토씨 하나까지 다 기억하고 있었다) 허커백 아마포 몇 필, 네덜란드산 홀란드 천 몇 엘 따위를 상자 하나에 담아 보내고, 또 헨질 씨의 유리 공장에서 부싯돌 유리잔 한 광주리를 보낸다는 사연을 적었으며, 그 상자에는 I."], ["Henzill’s glasshouse; and that the box was marked I. C.", "C."], ["No. 1, and the hamper was directed by a label on the cording.", "1호라고 표시되어 있고 광주리는 묶음줄에 붙인 꼬리표로 표시되어 있다고 했다."]], [["About an hour after, I came to the warehouse, found the warehouse-keeper, and had the goods delivered me without any scruple; the value of the linen being about £22.", "한 시간쯤 뒤, 나는 창고로 가서 창고지기를 만나, 아무 의심도 받지 않고 물건을 넘겨받았으니, 아마포의 값어치는 약 이십이 파운드였다."]], [["I could fill up this whole discourse with the variety of such adventures, which daily invention directed to, and which I managed with the utmost dexterity, and always with success.", "나는 이 글 전체를, 날마다 떠오르는 착상이 이끄는 대로 따른, 또 내가 더할 나위 없는 솜씨로, 그리고 늘 성공적으로 해치운 그런 갖가지 모험들로 가득 채울 수도 있을 것이다."]], [["At length—as when does the pitcher come safe home that goes so very often to the well?—I fell into some small broils, which though they could not affect me fatally, yet made me known, which was the worst thing next to being found guilty that could befall me.", "마침내—그토록 자주 우물에 가는 항아리가 어찌 성하게 집으로 돌아오겠는가?—나는 몇 가지 자잘한 곤경에 빠지게 되었는데, 그것들이 나를 치명적으로 해칠 수는 없었으나, 그래도 나를 사람들에게 알려지게 만들었으니, 그것은 유죄판결을 받는 일 다음으로 내게 닥칠 수 있는 가장 고약한 일이었다."]], [["I had taken up the disguise of a widow’s dress; it was without any real design in view, but only waiting for anything that might offer, as I often did.", "나는 과부의 상복으로 변장을 했으니, 무슨 정해진 속셈이 있어서가 아니라, 그저 흔히 그러했듯이 무슨 일이 생길까 하여 기다리던 것이었다."], ["It happened that while I was going along the street in Covent Garden, there was a great cry of “Stop thief!", "마침 내가 코번트가든의 거리를 따라 걸어가고 있을 때 \"도둑이야!"], ["Stop thief!”", "도둑 잡아라!\""], ["some artists had, it seems, put a trick upon a shopkeeper, and being pursued, some of them fled one way, and some another; and one of them was, they said, dressed up in widow’s weeds, upon which the mob gathered about me, and some said I was the person, others said no.", "하는 큰 외침이 일었으니, 아무래도 어떤 솜씨꾼들이 어느 가게 주인에게 수작을 부린 듯했고, 쫓기게 되자 그들 가운데 더러는 이쪽으로, 더러는 저쪽으로 달아났는데, 그중 하나가 과부 상복 차림이었다는 것이었다. 그러자 군중이 내 주위로 몰려들어, 더러는 내가 그 사람이라 하고, 더러는 아니라고 했다."], ["Immediately came the mercer’s journeyman, and he swore aloud I was the person, and so seized on me.", "곧이어 그 포목상의 직공이 와서는, 내가 그 사람이라고 큰 소리로 맹세하더니 나를 붙들었다."], ["However, when I was brought back by the mob to the mercer’s shop, the master of the house said freely that I was not the woman that was in his shop, and would have let me go immediately; but another fellow said gravely, “Pray stay till Mr.", "그러나 군중에게 이끌려 그 포목상의 가게로 되돌려졌을 때, 그 집 주인은 내가 자기 가게에 있던 그 여자가 아니라고 선뜻 말하며 나를 즉시 놓아주려 했으나, 또 다른 자가 자못 진지하게 말하기를 \"아무개 씨가\"(그 직공을 가리키는 것이었다) \"돌아올 때까지 기다리시지요, 그가 저 여자를 압니다\" 하는 것이었다."], ["——” (meaning the journeyman) “comes back, for he knows her.”", "그리하여 그들은 나를 거의 반 시간이나 억지로 붙들어 두었다."], ["So they kept me by force near half an hour. They had called a constable, and he stood in the shop as my jailer; and in talking with the constable I inquired where he lived, and what trade he was; the man not apprehending in the least what happened afterwards, readily told me his name, and trade, and where he lived; and told me as a jest, that I might be sure to hear of his name when I came to the Old Bailey.", "그들이 치안관 한 사람을 불렀고, 그가 내 간수처럼 가게 안에 서 있었으니, 그 치안관과 이야기하면서 나는 그가 어디 사는지, 무슨 직업인지를 물었고, 그 사내는 뒤에 무슨 일이 일어날지 조금도 짐작하지 못한 채 선뜻 자기 이름과 직업, 사는 곳을 일러 주었고, 또 농담 삼아, 내가 올드베일리에 나오게 되면 틀림없이 자기 이름을 듣게 될 것이라고 했다."]], [["Some of the servants likewise used me saucily, and had much ado to keep their hands off me; the master indeed was civiller to me than they, but he would not yet let me go, though he owned he could not say I was in his shop before.", "하인들 가운데 더러도 나를 건방지게 다루며 손을 대지 않으려고 무던히도 애를 썼고, 주인은 사실 그들보다는 내게 점잖게 굴었으나, 그래도 나를 놓아주려 하지 않았으니, 비록 그는 내가 전에 자기 가게에 있었다고는 말할 수 없노라고 인정하면서도 그러했다."]], [["I began to be a little surly with him, and told him I hoped he would not take it ill if I made myself amends upon him in a more legal way another time; and desired I might send for friends to see me have right done me.", "나는 그에게 조금 퉁명스러워지기 시작하여, 훗날 더 합법적인 방식으로 그에게 앙갚음하더라도 언짢게 여기지 말기를 바란다고 하고, 내가 정당한 대접을 받는 것을 지켜봐 줄 벗들을 부르러 사람을 보내게 해 달라고 청했다."], ["No, he said, he could give no such liberty; I might ask it when I came before the justice of peace; and seeing I threatened him, he would take care of me in the meantime, and would lodge me safe in Newgate.", "안 된다고, 그는 그런 자유를 줄 수 없으니, 치안판사 앞에 나가서 청하라고 했다. 그러고는 내가 자기를 위협하는 것을 보고는, 그동안 자기가 나를 단속해 두겠으며, 뉴게이트에 안전하게 가둬 두겠노라고 했다."], ["I told him it was his time now, but it would be mine by and by, and governed my passion as well as I was able.", "나는 지금은 그의 때이지만, 머지않아 내 때가 올 것이라고 말하며, 할 수 있는 한 내 노여움을 다스렸다."], ["However, I spoke to the constable to call me a porter, which he did, and then I called for pen, ink, and paper, but they would let me have none.", "그래도 나는 치안관에게 짐꾼을 한 사람 불러 달라고 했고, 그는 그렇게 해 주었으며, 그러고서 나는 펜과 잉크와 종이를 달라고 했으나 그들은 아무것도 주려 하지 않았다."], ["I asked the porter his name, and where he lived, and the poor man told it me very willingly.", "나는 그 짐꾼에게 이름과 사는 곳을 물었고, 그 가엾은 사내는 매우 선뜻 일러 주었다."], ["I bade him observe and remember how I was treated there; that he saw I was detained there by force.", "나는 그에게, 내가 여기서 어떻게 대접받는지를 똑똑히 보고 기억해 두라고, 내가 여기 억지로 붙들려 있는 것을 그가 보았노라고 일렀다."], ["I told him I should want his evidence in another place, and it should not be the worse for him to speak.", "나는 다른 곳에서 그의 증언이 필요할 것이며, 입을 여는 것이 그에게 나쁠 것 없으리라고 말했다."], ["The porter said he would serve me with all his heart.", "그 짐꾼은 진심으로 나를 돕겠노라 했다. \"하지만 마님,\""], ["“But, madam,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “let me hear them refuse to let you go, then I may be able to speak the plainer.”", "\"저들이 마님을 못 가게 거절하는 걸 제가 듣게 해 주십쇼, 그래야 제가 더 분명하게 말할 수 있을 테니까요.\""]], [["With that I spoke aloud to the master of the shop, and said, “Sir, you know in your own conscience that I am not the person you look for, and that I was not in your shop before, therefore I demand that you detain me here no longer, or tell me the reason of your stopping me.”", "그 말에 나는 가게 주인에게 큰 소리로 말하기를 \"선생, 당신은 양심으로 알고 있겠지만 나는 당신이 찾는 그 사람이 아니고, 전에 당신 가게에 있지도 않았소. 그러니 나를 더는 여기 붙들어 두지 말든가, 아니면 나를 멈춰 세운 이유를 말하시오\""], ["The fellow grew surlier upon this than before, and said he would do neither till he thought fit.", "하였다. 그 사내는 이 말에 전보다 더 퉁명스러워져서, 자기 마음 내키기 전에는 둘 중 어느 것도 하지 않겠노라 했다."], ["“Very well,”", "\"아주 좋소.\""], ["said I to the constable and to the porter; “you will be pleased to remember this, gentlemen, another time.”", "나는 치안관과 짐꾼에게 말했다. \"여러분, 훗날 이것을 꼭 기억해 주시기 바라오.\""], ["The porter said, “Yes, madam”; and the constable began not to like it, and would have persuaded the mercer to dismiss him, and let me go, since, as he said, he owned I was not the person.", "짐꾼은 \"예, 마님\" 하였고, 치안관은 그것이 마음에 들지 않기 시작하여, 포목상에게 자기가 인정했듯이 내가 그 사람이 아닌 이상 나를 풀어 보내라고 설득하려 했다."], ["“Good, sir,”", "\"거참, 선생,\""], ["says the mercer to him tauntingly, “are you a justice of peace or a constable?", "포목상이 그에게 비꼬듯 말했다. \"당신이 치안판사요, 치안관이요?"], ["I charged you with her; pray do you do your duty.”", "내가 이 여자를 당신에게 맡겼으니, 부디 당신 할 일이나 하시오.\""], ["The constable told him, a little moved, but very handsomely, “I know my duty, and what I am, sir; I doubt you hardly know what you are doing.”", "치안관은 조금 마음이 상했으나 매우 점잖게 말하기를 \"나는 내 할 일과 내가 무엇인지를 압니다, 선생. 정작 당신이야말로 자기가 무얼 하고 있는지 모르는 것 같소\""], ["They had some other hard words, and in the meantime the journeyman, impudent and unmanly to the last degree, used me barbarously, and one of them, the same that first seized upon me, pretended he would search me, and began to lay hands on me.", "하였다. 그들은 또 몇 마디 거친 말을 주고받았고, 그러는 동안 그 직공은 끝까지 뻔뻔하고 사내답지 못하게 굴며 나를 야만스럽게 다루었으니, 그들 가운데 하나—처음에 나를 붙든 바로 그자였다—가 나를 수색하겠노라 하면서 내게 손을 대기 시작했다."], ["I spit in his face, called out to the constable, and bade him to take notice of my usage.", "나는 그의 얼굴에 침을 뱉고, 치안관을 불러, 내가 어떤 대접을 받는지 똑똑히 봐 두라고 일렀다."], ["“And pray, Mr. Constable,”", "\"그리고 치안관 선생,\""], ["said I, “ask that villain’s name,”", "나는 그 사내를 가리키며 말했다."], ["pointing to the man.", "\"저 악당의 이름을 좀 물어봐 주시오.\""], ["The constable reproved him decently, told him that he did not know what he did, for he knew that his master acknowledged I was not the person that was in his shop; “and,”", "치안관은 그를 점잖게 꾸짖으며, 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 모르고 있다고, 자기 주인조차 내가 가게에 있던 사람이 아니라고 인정한 것을 자기도 알고 있다고 말했다. \"그리고,\""], ["says the constable, “I am afraid your master is bringing himself, and me too, into trouble, if this gentlewoman comes to prove who she is, and where she was, and it appears that she is not the woman you pretend to.”", "치안관이 말했다. \"만약 이 부인이 자기가 누구이며 어디 있었는지를 증명하게 되고, 자네가 우기는 그 여자가 아니라는 것이 드러나면, 자네 주인이 자기 자신과 나까지도 곤경에 빠뜨리는 게 아닐까 걱정일세.\""], ["“Damn her,”", "\"빌어먹을 년,\""], ["says the fellow again, with a impudent, hardened face, “she is the lady, you may depend upon it; I’ll swear she is the same body that was in the shop, and that I gave the pieces of satin that is lost into her own hand.", "그 사내가 다시, 뻔뻔하고 모질어진 얼굴로 말했다. \"저 여자가 그 마님이오, 틀림없소이다."], ["You shall hear more of it when Mr. William and Mr.", "내 맹세컨대 가게에 있던 바로 그 몸뚱이요, 없어진 새틴 조각들을 바로 저 여자 손에 내가 건네줬단 말이오."], ["Anthony (those were other journeymen) come back; they will know her again as well as I.”", "윌리엄 씨와 앤서니 씨가(그들은 다른 직공들이었다) 돌아오면 더 들으시게 될 거요. 그들도 나만큼이나 저 여자를 다시 알아볼 테니.\""]], [["Just as the insolent rogue was talking thus to the constable, comes back Mr. William and Mr.", "그 무례한 악당이 치안관에게 이렇게 지껄이고 있을 바로 그때, 그가 부른 대로 윌리엄 씨와 앤서니 씨가 한 무리의 군중과 함께 돌아왔으니, 그들은 내가 행세한 것으로 되어 있던 그 진짜 과부를 데리고 왔다."], ["Anthony, as he called them, and a great rabble with them, bringing along with them the true widow that I was pretended to be; and they came sweating and blowing into the shop, and with a great deal of triumph, dragging the poor creature in the most butcherly manner up towards their master, who was in the back shop, and cried out aloud, “Here’s the widow, sir; we have catched her at last.”", "그들은 땀을 뻘뻘 흘리고 헐떡거리며 가게로 들어와, 자못 의기양양하게, 그 가엾은 것을 더없이 백정 같은 꼴로 끌고서 뒤쪽 가게에 있던 주인 앞으로 데려가더니, 큰 소리로 외쳤다. \"여기 과부가 있습니다, 선생."], ["“What do ye mean by that?”", "우리가 마침내 그 여자를 잡았습니다.\" \"그게 무슨 소리냐?\""], ["says the master.", "주인이 말했다."], ["“Why, we have her already; there she sits,”", "\"아니, 우리는 이미 그 여자를 잡았다, 저기 앉아 있지 않으냐.\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “and Mr. ——,”", "\"그리고 아무개 씨가\""], ["says he, “can swear this is she.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이 여자가 그 여자라고 맹세할 수 있소.\""], ["The other man, whom they called Mr. Anthony, replied, “Mr.", "그들이 앤서니 씨라 부른 다른 사내가 대꾸하기를 \"아무개 씨야 무어라 말하든, 무어라 맹세하든 마음대로 하시구려, 하지만 이 여자가 그 여자고, 저기 그녀가 훔친 새틴 자투리가 있소."], ["—— may say what he will, and swear what he will, but this is the woman, and there’s the remnant of satin she stole; I took it out of her clothes with my own hand.”", "내가 내 손으로 그녀 옷에서 꺼냈단 말이오\" 하였다."]], [["I sat still now, and began to take a better heart, but smiled and said nothing; the master looked pale; the constable turned about and looked at me.", "나는 이제 가만히 앉아, 한결 마음이 든든해지기 시작했으나, 미소만 지을 뿐 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 주인은 얼굴이 창백해졌고, 치안관은 돌아서서 나를 바라보았다."], ["“Let ’em alone, Mr.", "\"저들을 내버려 두시오, 치안관 선생.\""], ["Constable,”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I; “let “em go on.”", "\"저들이 계속하게 두시오.\""], ["The case was plain and could not be denied, so the constable was charged with the right thief, and the mercer told me very civilly he was sorry for the mistake, and hoped I would not take it ill; that they had so many things of this nature put upon them every day, that they could not be blamed for being very sharp in doing themselves justice.", "사정은 명백하여 부인할 수가 없었으니, 치안관은 진짜 도둑을 떠맡게 되었고, 포목상은 내게 매우 점잖게 말하기를, 그 잘못에 대해 미안하며, 내가 그것을 언짢게 여기지 않기를 바란다고, 자기들은 날마다 이런 종류의 일을 하도 많이 당하는 터라, 스스로 정당함을 지키느라 몹시 날카롭게 군다고 해서 탓할 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다. \"언짢게 여기지 말라니, 선생!\""], ["“Not take it ill, sir!”", "내가 말했다."], ["said I; “how can I take it well!", "\"내가 어찌 좋게 여길 수 있겠소!"], ["If you had dismissed me when your insolent fellow seized on me it the street, and brought me to you, and when you yourself acknowledged I was not the person, I would have put it by, and not taken it ill, because of the many ill things I believe you have put upon you daily; but your treatment of me since has been insufferable, and especially that of your servant; I must and will have reparation for that.”", "만약 당신의 무례한 자가 거리에서 나를 붙들어 당신에게 데려왔을 때, 또 당신 자신이 내가 그 사람이 아니라고 인정했을 때 나를 풀어 주었더라면, 나는 그것을 넘겨 버리고 언짢게 여기지 않았을 것이오, 당신들이 날마다 이런 고약한 일을 많이 당하리라 믿기에 말이오. 하지만 그 뒤로 당신이 나를 대접한 꼴은 참을 수 없는 것이었고, 특히 당신 하인이 그러했으니, 나는 그것에 대해 반드시 배상을 받아야겠고, 또 받을 것이오.\""]], [["Then he began to parley with me, said he would make me any reasonable satisfaction, and would fain have had me tell him what it was I expected.", "그러자 그는 나와 흥정을 시작하여, 무엇이든 합당한 보상을 해 주겠노라 하며, 내가 무엇을 기대하는지 부디 말해 달라고 했다."], ["I told him that I should not be my own judge, the law should decide it for me; and as I was to be carried before a magistrate, I should let him hear there what I had to say.", "나는 내가 내 일의 심판관이 될 수는 없으니, 법이 그것을 나 대신 판결할 것이며, 내가 치안판사 앞에 끌려가게 될 터이니, 내가 할 말은 거기서 그에게 들려주겠노라고 했다."], ["He told me there was no occasion to go before the justice now, I was at liberty to go where I pleased; and so, calling to the constable, told him he might let me go, for I was discharged.", "그는 이제 치안판사 앞에 갈 필요가 없으며, 내가 어디든 가고 싶은 데로 갈 자유가 있다고 하고는, 치안관을 불러, 나를 풀어 주어도 된다고, 나는 방면되었다고 일렀다."], ["The constable said calmly to him, “sir, you asked me just now if I knew whether I was a constable or justice, and bade me do my duty, and charged me with this gentlewoman as a prisoner.", "치안관은 그에게 차분히 말하기를 \"선생, 당신은 방금 내가 내 자신이 치안관인지 치안판사인지나 아느냐고 묻고, 내 할 일이나 하라고 하면서 이 부인을 죄수로 내게 맡겼소. 한데 선생, 보아하니 당신은 내 할 일이 무엇인지를 모르는 것 같소, 정작 나를 치안판사로 만들려 하다니 말이오."], ["Now, sir, I find you do not understand what is my duty, for you would make me a justice indeed; but I must tell you it is not in my power.", "하지만 내 말해 두건대 그건 내 권한 밖이오. 죄수를 떠맡았을 때 그를 붙들어 둘 수는 있어도, 그 죄수를 방면할 수 있는 것은 법과 치안판사뿐이오."], ["I may keep a prisoner when I am charged with him, but ’tis the law and the magistrate alone that can discharge that prisoner; therefore ’tis a mistake, sir; I must carry her before a justice now, whether you think well of it or not.”", "그러니 그건 잘못이오, 선생. 당신이 좋게 여기든 아니든 나는 이제 이 여자를 치안판사 앞으로 데려가야 하오.\""], ["The mercer was very high with the constable at first; but the constable happening to be not a hired officer, but a good, substantial kind of man (I think he was a corn-handler), and a man of good sense, stood to his business, would not discharge me without going to a justice of the peace; and I insisted upon it too.", "포목상은 처음에는 치안관에게 매우 거만하게 굴었으나, 그 치안관이 마침 삯을 받고 일하는 자가 아니라 착실하고 든실한 부류의 사람이었고(내 생각에 곡물 중개상이었던 듯하다) 분별 있는 사람이었던지라, 자기 할 일을 굽히지 않고, 치안판사에게 가지 않고는 나를 풀어 주려 하지 않았다. 그리고 나 또한 그것을 고집했다."], ["When the mercer saw that, “Well,”", "포목상은 그것을 보고 \"좋소,\" 하고 치안관에게 말했다."], ["says he to the constable, “you may carry her where you please; I have nothing to say to her.”", "\"당신 좋을 대로 이 여자를 데려가시오. 나는 이 여자한테 할 말이 없으니.\""], ["“But, sir,”", "\"하지만 선생,\" 치안관이 말했다."], ["says the constable, “you will go with us, I hope, for ’tis you that charged me with her.”", "\"당신도 우리와 함께 가셔야 하오, 이 여자를 내게 맡긴 게 바로 당신이니 말이오.\" \"아니, 나는 안 가오.\""], ["“No, not I,”", "포목상이 말했다."], ["says the mercer; “I tell you I have nothing to say to her.”", "\"내 말하지 않았소, 이 여자한테 할 말이 없다고.\" \"하지만 부디, 선생, 가셔야 하오.\""], ["“But pray, sir, do,”", "치안관이 말했다."], ["says the constable; “I desire it of you for your own sake, for the justice can do nothing without you.”", "\"당신 자신을 위해서라도 청하는 거요, 당신 없이는 치안판사가 아무것도 할 수 없으니 말이오.\" \"여보게, 이 사람아,\""], ["“Prithee, fellow,”", "포목상이 말했다."], ["says the mercer, “go about your business; I tell you I have nothing to say to the gentlewoman.", "\"자네 할 일이나 보러 가게. 내 말하지 않았나, 이 부인한테 할 말이 없다고."], ["I charge you in the king’s name to dismiss her.”", "국왕의 이름으로 자네에게 명하니, 이 여자를 풀어 주게.\" \"선생,\""], ["“Sir,”", "치안관이 말했다."], ["says the constable, “I find you don’t know what it is to be constable; I beg of you don’t oblige me to be rude to you.”", "\"보아하니 당신은 치안관이 된다는 게 무엇인지를 모르는구려. 부디 내가 당신에게 무례하게 굴도록 만들지 마시오.\""], ["“I think I need not; you are rude enough already,”", "\"그럴 필요 없을 것 같네, 자네는 이미 충분히 무례하니.\" 포목상이 말했다."], ["says the mercer.", "\"아니오, 선생,\" 치안관이 말했다."], ["“No, sir,”", "\"나는 무례하지 않소."], ["says the constable, “I am not rude; you have broken the peace in bringing an honest woman out of the street, when she was about her lawful occasion, confining her in your shop, and ill-using her here by your servants; and now can you say I am rude to you?", "당신이야말로 자기 떳떳한 일을 보던 정직한 여인을 거리에서 데려와 가게에 가두고, 여기서 당신 하인들을 시켜 학대하여 평온을 깨뜨려 놓고서, 이제 와 내가 당신에게 무례하다고 할 수 있소? 내 생각엔, 국왕의 이름으로 당신에게 나와 함께 가라 명하지도 않고, 당신 문 앞을 지나는 사람마다 붙들어 당신을 억지로 끌고 가는 일을 돕고 거들라고 명하지도 않으니, 나는 당신에게 점잖은 거요."], ["I think I am civil to you in not commanding or charging you in the king’s name to go with me, and charging every man I see that passes your door to aid and assist me in carrying you by force; this you cannot but know I have power to do, and yet I forbear it, and once more entreat you to go with me.”", "내게 그럴 권한이 있다는 것을 당신도 모르지 않을 텐데, 그래도 나는 그것을 삼가고, 다시 한번 당신에게 나와 함께 가 주기를 청하는 거요.\" 그래도 그는 이 모든 말에도 가려 하지 않았고, 치안관에게 험한 말을 퍼부었다."], ["Well, he would not for all this, and gave the constable ill language.", "그러나 치안관은 성미를 가라앉히고 도발에 넘어가려 하지 않았다. 그러자 내가 끼어들어 말했다."], ["However, the constable kept his temper, and would not be provoked; and then I put in and said, “Come, Mr.", "\"자, 치안관 선생, 저 사람은 내버려 두시오. 나는 저 사람을 치안판사 앞에 데려갈 방도를 얼마든지 찾아낼 테니, 그건 걱정하지 않소."], ["Constable, let him alone; I shall find ways enough to fetch him before a magistrate, I don’t fear that; but there’s the fellow,”", "한데 저기 저 사내,\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “he was the man that seized on me as I was innocently going along the street, and you are a witness of the violence with me since; give me leave to charge you with him, and carry him before the justice.”", "\"저자가 바로 내가 아무 죄 없이 거리를 걸어갈 때 나를 붙든 자이고, 그 뒤로 내게 가한 폭력은 당신이 증인이오. 부디 나로 하여금 저자를 당신에게 맡기게 하여, 치안판사 앞으로 데려가게 해 주시오.\""], ["“Yes, madam,”", "\"예, 마님.\""], ["says the constable; and turning to the fellow “Come, young gentleman,”", "치안관이 말하고는, 그 사내를 향해 돌아서서 그 직공에게 말했다."], ["says he to the journeyman, “you must go along with us; I hope you are not above the constable’s power, though your master is.”", "\"이리 오게, 젊은 양반, 자네는 우리와 함께 가야겠네. 자네 주인은 몰라도 자네는 치안관의 권한 위에 있지는 않을 테니 말이야.\""]], [["The fellow looked like a condemned thief, and hung back, then looked at his master, as if he could help him; and he, like a fool, encourage the fellow to be rude, and he truly resisted the constable, and pushed him back with a good force when he went to lay hold on him, at which the constable knocked him down, and called out for help; and immediately the shop was filled with people, and the constable seized the master and man, and all his servants.", "그 사내는 사형선고를 받은 도둑 같은 꼴로 뒷걸음질을 치더니, 마치 도와줄 수 있기라도 한 양 제 주인을 바라보았고, 그 주인은 어리석게도 그 사내가 무례하게 굴도록 부추겼으니, 그자는 정말로 치안관에게 맞서서, 치안관이 그를 붙들려고 다가서자 제법 거센 힘으로 그를 밀쳐 냈고, 그러자 치안관이 그를 때려눕히고 도와달라고 외쳤다. 그러자 즉시 가게가 사람들로 가득 찼고, 치안관은 주인과 그 사내와 그의 하인들을 모조리 붙잡았다."]], [["This first ill consequence of this fray was, that the woman they had taken, who was really the thief, made off, and got clear away in the crowd; and two other that they had stopped also; whether they were really guilty or not, that I can say nothing to.", "이 소동의 첫 번째 나쁜 결과는 이러했으니, 그들이 붙들었던, 정말로 도둑이었던 그 여자가 빠져나가 군중 속으로 감쪽같이 달아나 버린 것이었고, 그들이 멈춰 세웠던 다른 두 사람도 마찬가지였으니, 그들이 정말로 죄가 있었는지 없었는지는 내가 무어라 말할 수 없다."]], [["By this time some of his neighbours having come in, and, upon inquiry, seeing how things went, had endeavoured to bring the hot-brained mercer to his senses, and he began to be convinced that he was in the wrong; and so at length we went all very quietly before the justice, with a mob of about five hundred people at our heels; and all the way I went I could hear the people ask what was the matter, and other reply and say, a mercer had stopped a gentlewoman instead of a thief, and had afterwards taken the thief, and now the gentlewoman had taken the mercer, and was carrying him before the justice.", "이쯤 되자 그의 이웃 가운데 더러가 들어와, 사정을 알아보고는 일이 어떻게 돌아가는지를 보고서, 그 머리에 피가 솟구친 포목상을 정신 차리게 하려 애썼고, 그는 자기가 그르다는 것을 차츰 깨닫기 시작했다. 그리하여 마침내 우리는 다 함께 매우 조용히 치안판사 앞으로 갔으니, 약 오백 명쯤 되는 군중을 뒤에 줄줄이 거느린 채였다."], ["This pleased the people strangely, and made the crowd increase, and they cried out as they went, “Which is the rogue?", "그리고 가는 내내 나는 사람들이 무슨 일이냐고 묻는 소리며, 다른 이들이 대꾸하여, 어느 포목상이 도둑 대신 어느 부인을 붙들었다가 그 뒤에 진짜 도둑을 잡았는데, 이제 그 부인이 포목상을 잡아 치안판사 앞으로 끌고 가는 길이라고 말하는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 이것이 사람들을 묘하게 흡족하게 만들어 군중이 더 불어났고, 그들은 가면서 \"어느 놈이 악당이냐?"], ["which is the mercer?”", "어느 놈이 포목상이냐?\" 하고 외쳤으니, 특히 여자들이 그러했다."], ["and especially the women.", "그러다 그를 보면 \"저놈이다, 저놈이야\" 하고 외쳤고, 이따금 그에게 흙덩이가 제대로 한 방씩 날아들었다."], ["Then when they saw him they cried out, “That’s he, that’s he”; and every now and then came a good dab of dirt at him; and thus we marched a good while, till the mercer thought fit to desire the constable to call a coach to protect himself from the rabble; so we rode the rest of the way, the constable and I, and the mercer and his man.", "이렇게 우리는 한참을 행진했으니, 마침내 포목상이 군중에게서 제 몸을 지키려고 치안관에게 마차를 한 대 불러 달라고 청하는 편이 좋겠다고 여겼다. 그리하여 치안관과 나, 그리고 포목상과 그의 사내가 남은 길을 마차로 갔다."]], [["When we came to the justice, which was an ancient gentleman in Bloomsbury, the constable giving first a summary account of the matter, the justice bade me speak, and tell what I had to say.", "블룸즈베리에 있는 한 연로한 신사였던 치안판사 앞에 이르자, 치안관이 먼저 일의 개요를 보고하니, 치안판사는 내게 말해 보라고, 할 말이 있으면 하라고 했다. 그리고 먼저 그가 내 이름을 물었는데, 나는 그것을 대기가 무척 싫었으나 달리 도리가 없었으니, 내 이름은 메리 플랜더스이고, 나는 과부이며, 내 남편은 선장이었는데 버지니아로 가는 항해 중에 죽었노라고 했다."], ["And first he asked my name, which I was very loth to give, but there was no remedy, so I told him my name was Mary Flanders, that I was a widow, my husband being a sea captain, died on a voyage to Virginia; and some other circumstances I told which he could never contradict, and that I lodged at present in town with such a person, naming my governess; but that I was preparing to go over to America, where my husband’s effects lay, and that I was going that day to buy some clothes to put myself into second mourning, but had not yet been in any shop, when that fellow, pointing to the mercer’s journeyman, came rushing upon me with such fury as very much frighted me, and carried me back to his master’s shop, where, though his master acknowledged I was not the person, yet he would not dismiss me, but charged a constable with me.", "또 그가 결코 반박할 수 없을 그 밖의 몇 가지 정황도 말했고, 지금은 아무개라는 사람과 함께 시내에 묵고 있다고 하며 내 후견인의 이름을 댔다. 그러나 나는 내 남편의 재산이 놓여 있는 아메리카로 건너갈 채비를 하던 중이었으며, 그날은 약식 상복을 갖춰 입을 옷을 좀 사러 가던 길로, 아직 어느 가게에도 들어가지 않았는데, 그때 저 사내가—포목상의 직공을 가리키며—나를 몹시 놀라게 할 만큼 사납게 달려들어, 나를 제 주인의 가게로 도로 끌고 갔으며, 거기서 그의 주인이 내가 그 사람이 아니라고 인정했음에도 그는 나를 풀어 주려 하지 않고 치안관에게 나를 맡겼다고 했다."]], [["Then I proceeded to tell how the journeyman treated me; how they would not suffer me to send for any of my friends; how afterwards they found the real thief, and took the very goods they had lost upon her, and all the particulars as before.", "그러고서 나는 그 직공이 나를 어떻게 다루었는지, 그들이 어떻게 내가 벗들 가운데 누구에게도 사람을 보내지 못하게 했는지, 그 뒤에 그들이 어떻게 진짜 도둑을 찾아내어 자기들이 잃은 바로 그 물건을 그 여자에게서 발견했는지, 그리고 앞서 말한 대로 자초지종을 낱낱이 이야기해 나갔다."]], [["Then the constable related his case: his dialogue with the mercer about discharging me, and at last his servant’s refusing to go with him, when he had charged him with him, and his master encouraging him to do so, and at last his striking the constable, and the like, all as I have told it already.", "그러자 치안관이 자기 쪽 사정을 이야기했으니, 나를 풀어 주는 문제를 두고 포목상과 주고받은 대화며, 마침내 자기가 그 하인을 떠맡겼는데도 그 하인이 함께 가기를 거부한 일이며, 그 주인이 그렇게 하도록 부추긴 일이며, 끝내 그가 치안관을 때린 일 따위를, 모두 내가 이미 말한 대로 늘어놓았다."]], [["The justice then heard the mercer and his man.", "그러자 치안판사는 포목상과 그의 사내의 말을 들었다."], ["The mercer indeed made a long harangue of the great loss they have daily by lifters and thieves; that it was easy for them to mistake, and that when he found it he would have dismissed me, etc., as above.", "포목상은 사실 자기들이 날치기와 도둑들 때문에 날마다 입는 큰 손실에 대해 길게 일장연설을 늘어놓았으니, 자기들로서는 잘못 알기가 쉬운 일이며, 자기가 그것을 알아챘을 때 나를 풀어 주려 했다는 것 따위, 위에서 말한 대로였다."], ["As to the journeyman, he had very little to say, but that he pretended other of the servants told him that I was really the person.", "그 직공으로 말하자면, 다른 하인들이 나더러 정말 그 사람이라고 일러 주었다고 우긴 것 외에는, 할 말이 거의 없었다."]], [["Upon the whole, the justice first of all told me very courteously I was discharged; that he was very sorry that the mercer’s man should in his eager pursuit have so little discretion as to take up an innocent person for a guilty person; that if he had not been so unjust as to detain me afterward, he believed I would have forgiven the first affront; that, however, it was not in his power to award me any reparation for anything, other than by openly reproving them, which he should do; but he supposed I would apply to such methods as the law directed; in the meantime he would bind him over.", "대체로 보아, 치안판사는 무엇보다 먼저 내게 매우 정중하게, 나는 방면되었다고 했다. 그는 포목상의 사내가 그 열띤 추격 가운데 너무도 분별이 없어, 죄 있는 사람 대신 죄 없는 사람을 붙든 것이 매우 유감스럽다고 했고, 만약 그가 그 뒤에 나를 붙들어 두는 부당한 짓만 하지 않았더라면 내가 그 첫 모욕은 용서했으리라 믿는다고 했다. 그러나 어찌 되었든, 그들을 공개적으로 꾸짖는 것 외에는—그것은 자기가 하겠다고 했다—내게 어떤 배상도 내려 줄 권한이 자기에게는 없다고 했으나, 내가 법이 일러 주는 방도를 따르리라 짐작한다고 했다. 그동안 그는 그를 보석으로 묶어 두겠노라 했다."]], [["But as to the breach of the peace committed by the journeyman, he told me he should give me some satisfaction for that, for he should commit him to Newgate for assaulting the constable, and for assaulting me also.", "그러나 그 직공이 저지른 평온 침해에 대해서는, 그것에 대해 내게 어느 정도 만족을 주겠노라 하면서, 치안관을 폭행한 죄로, 또 나를 폭행한 죄로 그를 뉴게이트에 가두겠다고 했다."]], [["Accordingly he sent the fellow to Newgate for that assault, and his master gave bail, and so we came away; but I had the satisfaction of seeing the mob wait upon them both, as they came out, hallooing and throwing stones and dirt at the coaches they rode in; and so I came home to my governess.", "이렇게 하여 그는 그 폭행죄로 그 사내를 뉴게이트로 보냈고, 그의 주인은 보석금을 내어, 우리는 그곳을 떠났다. 그러나 나는 그 두 사람이 나올 때 군중이 그들을 떠받들어 모시는 꼴을 보는 만족을 누렸으니, 그들이 탄 마차에 함성을 지르고 돌과 흙을 던져 댔던 것이다. 그리하여 나는 내 후견인에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["After this hustle, coming home and telling my governess the story, she falls a-laughing at me.", "이 소동을 치르고 집에 돌아와 내 후견인에게 그 이야기를 들려주자, 그녀는 나를 보고 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"왜 그리 즐거워하세요?\""], ["“Why are you merry?”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I; “the story has not so much laughing room in it as you imagine; I am sure I have had a great deal of hurry and fright too, with a pack of ugly rogues.”", "\"이 이야기에는 당신이 생각하는 만큼 웃을 거리가 그리 많지 않아요. 정말이지 나는 한 무리의 고약한 악당들 때문에 무던히도 부산을 떨고 겁도 났단 말이에요.\""], ["“Laugh!”", "\"웃다니!\" 내 후견인이 말했다."], ["says my governess; “I laugh, child, to see what a lucky creature you are; why, this job will be the best bargain to you that ever you made in your life, if you manage it well.", "\"얘야, 나는 네가 얼마나 운 좋은 것인지가 보여서 웃는 거란다. 아니, 이 일은 네가 잘만 다루면 평생 해 본 거래 가운데 가장 수지맞는 거래가 될 거야."], ["I warrant you,”", "내 장담하건대,\""], ["says she, “you shall make the mercer pay you £500 for damages, besides what you shall get out of the journeyman.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"너는 그 포목상에게서 손해배상으로 오백 파운드를 받아 내게 될 거야, 그 직공한테서 받아 낼 것은 별도로 하고 말이지.\""]], [["I had other thoughts of the matter than she had; and especially, because I had given in my name to the justice of peace; and I knew that my name was so well known among the people at Hick’s Hall, the Old Bailey, and such places, that if this cause came to be tried openly, and my name came to be inquired into, no court would give much damages, for the reputation of a person of such a character.", "나는 이 일에 대해 그녀와는 다른 생각을 품고 있었으니, 특히 내가 치안판사에게 내 이름을 대 버린 까닭이었다. 그리고 나는 내 이름이 힉스홀이며 올드베일리며 그런 데의 사람들 사이에 너무도 잘 알려져 있어서, 만약 이 송사가 공개적으로 재판에 부쳐지고 내 이름이 캐어지게 되면, 어떤 법정도 그런 평판을 지닌 사람의 명예에 대해 큰 배상금을 매겨 주지는 않으리라는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["However, I was obliged to begin a prosecution in form, and accordingly my governess found me out a very creditable sort of a man to manage it, being an attorney of very good business, and of a good reputation, and she was certainly in the right of this; for had she employed a pettifogging hedge solicitor, or a man not known, and not in good reputation, I should have brought it to but little.", "그러나 어쩔 수 없이 정식으로 소송을 시작하게 되었고, 그리하여 내 후견인이 그것을 맡아 처리할 매우 믿을 만한 부류의 사람을 찾아내 주었으니, 일거리가 아주 많고 평판도 좋은 사무변호사였다. 그리고 그녀가 이 점에서 옳았던 것이 분명하니, 만약 그녀가 어떤 삼류 엉터리 변호사나, 알려지지도 않고 평판도 좋지 않은 사람을 썼더라면, 나는 이 일에서 별것을 얻어 내지 못했을 것이다."]], [["I met this attorney, and gave him all the particulars at large, as they are recited above; and he assured me it was a case, as he said, that would very well support itself, and that he did not question but that a jury would give very considerable damages on such an occasion; so taking his full instructions he began the prosecution, and the mercer being arrested, gave bail.", "나는 이 변호사를 만나, 위에 적힌 그대로 자초지종을 빠짐없이 일러 주었고, 그는 내게 단언하기를, 그의 말마따나 이것은 그 자체로 충분히 떠받쳐질 만한 사건이며, 이런 경우에 배심원단이 상당한 배상금을 매겨 주리라는 데 의심하지 않는다고 했다. 그리하여 그는 내 자세한 지시를 받아 소송을 시작했고, 포목상은 체포되어 보석금을 냈다."], ["A few days after his giving bail, he comes with his attorney to my attorney, to let him know that he desired to accommodate the matter; that it was all carried on in the heat of an unhappy passion; that his client, meaning me, had a sharp provoking tongue, that I used them ill, gibing at them, and jeering them, even while they believed me to be the very person, and that I had provoked them, and the like.", "그가 보석금을 낸 며칠 뒤, 그는 자기 변호사와 함께 내 변호사를 찾아와, 이 일을 원만히 매듭짓고 싶다는 뜻을 알리며, 모든 것이 불행한 격정의 열기 속에서 벌어진 일이고, 그의 의뢰인—곧 나를 가리키는 것이었다—이 날카롭고 도발적인 혀를 지녀, 그들이 나를 바로 그 사람이라 믿고 있던 동안에조차 내가 그들을 함부로 대하며 빈정대고 조롱하여 자기들을 도발했다는 둥 그런 식으로 말했다."]], [["My attorney managed as well on my side; made them believe I was a widow of fortune, that I was able to do myself justice, and had great friends to stand by me too, who had all made me promise to sue to the utmost, and that if it cost me a thousand pounds I would be sure to have satisfaction, for that the affronts I had received were insufferable.", "내 변호사도 내 쪽에서 못지않게 잘 다루었으니, 그들로 하여금 내가 재산 있는 과부이며, 스스로 정당함을 지킬 능력이 있고, 또 나를 후원해 줄 큰 벗들이 있어, 그들 모두가 끝까지 소송하라고 내게 다짐을 받았으며, 설령 천 파운드가 든다 해도 나는 반드시 배상을 받고야 말 것이라고 믿게 만들었으니, 내가 받은 모욕이 참을 수 없는 것이었기 때문이라고 했다. 그러나 그들은 내 변호사를 이 지경까지 끌고 갔으니, 곧 그가 불씨를 부채질하지 않겠노라고, 만약 내가 화해 쪽으로 기운다면 막지 않겠노라고, 그리고 차라리 나를 전쟁보다 평화 쪽으로 설득하겠노라고 약속하게 한 것이었고, 그들은 그 대가로 그가 손해 볼 일은 없으리라고 했다."], ["However, they brought my attorney to this, that he promised he would not blow the coals, that if I inclined to accommodation, he would not hinder me, and that he would rather persuade me to peace than to war; for which they told him he should be no loser; all which he told me very honestly, and told me that if they offered him any bribe, I should certainly know it; but upon the whole he told me very honestly that if I would take his opinion, he would advise me to make it up with them, for that as they were in a great fright, and were desirous above all things to make it up, and knew that, let it be what it would, they would be allotted to bear all the costs of the suit; he believed they would give me freely more than any jury or court of justice would give upon a trial.", "그는 이 모든 것을 내게 매우 정직하게 일러 주었고, 만약 그들이 자기에게 어떤 뇌물이라도 내민다면 내가 반드시 알게 될 것이라고 했다. 그러나 대체로 그는 내게 매우 정직하게 말하기를, 내가 자기 의견을 받아들이겠다면, 그들과 화해하라고 권하겠노라 했으니, 그들이 큰 겁에 질려 무엇보다도 이 일을 매듭짓기를 바라고, 또 일이 어찌 되든 자기들이 소송 비용을 모두 떠안게 되리라는 것을 알고 있는 터라, 어떤 배심원단이나 법정이 재판에서 매겨 줄 것보다 더 후하게 내게 선뜻 내어 주리라 믿는다고 했다."], ["I asked him what he thought they would be brought to.", "나는 그들이 어느 선까지 끌려올 것 같으냐고 물었다."], ["He told me he could not tell as to that, but he would tell me more when I saw him again.", "그는 그것까지는 알 수 없으나, 다시 나를 만날 때 더 일러 주겠노라고 했다."]], [["Some time after this, they came again to know if he had talked with me.", "이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 그들은 그가 나와 이야기를 나누었는지 알아보러 다시 왔다."], ["He told them he had; that he found me not so averse to an accommodation as some of my friends were, who resented the disgrace offered me, and set me on; that they blowed the coals in secret, prompting me to revenge, or do myself justice, as they called it; so that he could not tell what to say to it; he told them he would do his endeavour to persuade me, but he ought to be able to tell me what proposal they made.", "그는 그렇게 했노라고, 내가 일부 내 벗들만큼 화해에 그리 반대하지는 않더라고 했으니, 그 벗들은 내게 가해진 치욕에 분개하여 나를 부추기고, 은밀히 불씨를 부채질하며 내게 복수를, 곧 그들의 말마따나 스스로 정당함을 지키기를 다그치고 있어서, 그로서도 그것에 대해 무어라 말해야 할지 모르겠노라고 했다. 그는 그들에게 자기가 나를 설득해 보도록 애쓰겠으나, 그들이 어떤 제안을 하는지를 내게 일러 줄 수 있어야 한다고 했다."], ["They pretended they could not make any proposal, because it might be made use of against them; and he told them, that by the same rule he could not make any offers, for that might be pleaded in abatement of what damages a jury might be inclined to give.", "그들은 어떤 제안도 할 수 없다고 우겼으니, 그것이 자기들에게 불리하게 쓰일 수 있기 때문이라 했고, 그는 그들에게, 같은 이치로 자기도 어떤 제안을 내놓을 수 없다고 했으니, 그것이 배심원단이 매기려 할 배상금을 깎는 데에 변명거리로 내세워질 수 있기 때문이라 했다. 그러나 얼마간 이야기를 나누고, 그때든 그 밖의 다른 모임에서든 거기서 오간 것을 두고 어느 쪽도 이익을 취하지 않겠다고 서로 약속한 뒤, 그들은 일종의 협상에 이르렀다."], ["However, after some discourse and mutual promises that no advantage should be taken on either side, by what was transacted then or at any other of those meetings, they came to a kind of a treaty; but so remote, and so wide from one another, that nothing could be expected from it; for my attorney demanded £500 and charges, and they offered £50 without charges; so they broke off, and the mercer proposed to have a meeting with me myself; and my attorney agreed to that very readily.", "그러나 그것이 너무도 멀고, 서로 너무도 동떨어진 터라 거기서는 아무것도 기대할 수 없었으니, 내 변호사는 오백 파운드에 비용을 요구했고, 그들은 비용 없이 오십 파운드를 내놓았던 것이다. 그리하여 협상은 결렬되었고, 포목상은 나 자신과 직접 만남을 갖자고 제안했으며, 내 변호사는 그것에 매우 선뜻 동의했다."]], [["My attorney gave me notice to come to this meeting in good clothes, and with some state, that the mercer might see I was something more than I seemed to be that time they had me.", "내 변호사는 내게 이 만남에 좋은 옷을 입고, 어느 정도 위엄을 갖춰 오라고 일러 주었으니, 그들이 나를 붙들었던 그때 보이던 모습보다 내가 무언가 더 나은 사람임을 포목상이 알아보게 하려는 것이었다."], ["Accordingly I came in a new suit of second mourning, according to what I had said at the justice’s.", "그리하여 나는, 치안판사 앞에서 한 말대로, 약식 상복 새 옷 한 벌을 차려입고 갔다."], ["I set myself out, too, as well as a widow’s dress in second mourning would admit; my governess also furnished me with a good pearl necklace, that shut in behind with a locket of diamonds, which she had in pawn; and I had a very good figure; and as I stayed till I was sure they were come, I came in a coach to the door, with my maid with me.", "또 약식 상복의 과부 차림이 허락하는 한 최대한 나 자신을 돋보이게 꾸몄으니, 내 후견인도 자기가 전당으로 잡아 둔, 뒤에서 다이아몬드 로켓으로 잠그는 좋은 진주 목걸이를 내게 갖춰 주었다. 그리하여 나는 매우 좋은 자태를 갖추었고, 그들이 온 것이 확실해질 때까지 기다렸다가, 내 하녀를 데리고 마차를 타고 문 앞에 다다랐다."]], [["When I came into the room the mercer was surprised.", "내가 방으로 들어서자 포목상은 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["He stood up and made his bow, which I took a little notice of, and but a little, and went and sat down where my own attorney had pointed to me to sit, for it was his house.", "그는 일어서서 허리를 굽혀 인사했고, 나는 그것을 조금, 그저 조금만 알은체하고는, 가서 내 변호사가 내게 앉으라고 가리킨 자리에 앉았으니, 그 집은 그의 집이었기 때문이다."], ["After a little while the mercer said, he did not know me again, and began to make some compliments his way.", "잠시 뒤 포목상은 나를 다시 알아보지 못하겠노라 하면서, 자기 식으로 몇 마디 인사치레를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["I told him, I believed he did not know me at first, and that if he had, I believed he would not have treated me as he did.", "나는 그가 처음에는 나를 알아보지 못한 듯하며, 만약 알아보았더라면 나를 그런 식으로 대접하지는 않았으리라 믿는다고 말했다."]], [["He told me he was very sorry for what had happened, and that it was to testify the willingness he had to make all possible reparation that he had appointed this meeting; that he hoped I would not carry things to extremity, which might be not only too great a loss to him, but might be the ruin of his business and shop, in which case I might have the satisfaction of repaying an injury with an injury ten times greater; but that I would then get nothing, whereas he was willing to do me any justice that was in his power, without putting himself or me to the trouble or charge of a suit at law.", "그는 일어난 일에 대해 매우 미안하다고, 가능한 한 모든 배상을 하려는 자기 뜻을 증언하고자 이 만남을 마련한 것이라고 했다. 그는 내가 일을 극단으로 몰고 가지 않기를 바란다고 했으니, 그것은 자기에게 너무 큰 손실이 될 뿐 아니라 자기 사업과 가게의 파멸이 될 수도 있고, 그렇게 되면 나는 상해를 열 배나 더 큰 상해로 갚는 만족이야 누릴 수 있겠으나, 그때는 내가 아무것도 얻지 못할 것이라 했다. 반면에 자기는 소송의 수고나 비용을 자기 자신이나 내게 끼치지 않고도, 자기 힘이 닿는 한 어떤 정당함이든 내게 베풀 뜻이 있다고 했다."]], [["I told him I was glad to hear him talk so much more like a man of sense than he did before; that it was true, acknowledgment in most cases of affronts was counted reparation sufficient; but this had gone too far to be made up so; that I was not revengeful, nor did I seek his ruin, or any man’s else, but that all my friends were unanimous not to let me so far neglect my character as to adjust a thing of this kind without a sufficient reparation of honour; that to be taken up for a thief was such an indignity as could not be put up; that my character was above being treated so by any that knew me, but because in my condition of a widow I had been for some time careless of myself, and negligent of myself, I might be taken for such a creature, but that for the particular usage I had from him afterwards,—and then I repeated all as before; it was so provoking I had scarce patience to repeat it.", "나는 그가 전보다 훨씬 분별 있는 사람답게 말하는 것을 들으니 기쁘다고 했으며, 대개의 경우 모욕에 대한 사죄가 충분한 배상으로 쳐지는 것은 사실이나, 이번 일은 그렇게 매듭짓기에는 너무 멀리 갔다고 했다. 나는 복수심이 있는 것도 아니고, 그의 파멸이나 다른 누구의 파멸을 바라는 것도 아니지만, 내 벗들이 한결같이, 내가 충분한 명예 회복 없이 이런 종류의 일을 매듭지을 만큼 내 체면을 그토록 소홀히 하도록 내버려 두지 않으려 한다고 했다. 도둑으로 붙들린다는 것은 도무지 참아 넘길 수 없는 모욕이며, 내 체면은 나를 아는 어떤 이에게도 그런 식으로 대접받을 만한 것이 아니지만, 다만 과부의 처지에서 한동안 내 몸을 돌보지 않고 소홀히 한 까닭에 그런 부류로 오인될 수도 있었던 것이라고 했다. 그러나 그 뒤에 그에게서 받은 그 각별한 대접으로 말하자면—그러고서 나는 앞서와 똑같이 모든 것을 되풀이했으니, 그것은 너무도 부아가 치미는 일이라 되풀이하기에도 참을성이 거의 남아 있지 않았다."]], [["Well, he acknowledged all, and was might humble indeed; he made proposals very handsome; he came up to £100 and to pay all the law charges, and added that he would make me a present of a very good suit of clothes.", "그래, 그는 모든 것을 인정했고, 정말이지 무척 겸손해졌다. 그는 매우 점잖은 제안들을 내놓았으니, 백 파운드까지 올리고 모든 소송 비용을 치르겠다고 했으며, 거기에 매우 좋은 옷 한 벌을 선물하겠다고 덧붙였다."], ["I came down to £300, and I demanded that I should publish an advertisement of the particulars in the common newspapers.", "나는 삼백 파운드로 내려왔고, 또 그 자초지종을 일반 신문에 광고로 실을 것을 요구했다."]], [["This was a clause he never could comply with.", "이것은 그가 도저히 응할 수 없는 조항이었다."], ["However, at last he came up, by good management of my attorney, to £150 and a suit of black silk clothes; and there I agree, and as it were, at my attorney’s request, complied with it, he paying my attorney’s bill and charges, and gave us a good supper into the bargain.", "그러나 마침내 그는 내 변호사의 능숙한 처리로 백오십 파운드와 검은 비단옷 한 벌까지 올라왔고, 거기서 나는 동의했으니, 말하자면 내 변호사의 청에 따라 그것에 응한 셈이었다. 그는 내 변호사의 비용과 수임료를 치렀고, 덤으로 우리에게 좋은 저녁까지 대접했다."]], [["When I came to receive the money, I brought my governess with me, dressed like an old duchess, and a gentleman very well dressed, who we pretended courted me, but I called him cousin, and the lawyer was only to hint privately to him that his gentleman courted the widow.", "돈을 받으러 갈 때, 나는 내 후견인을 늙은 공작부인처럼 차려입혀 데려갔고, 또 매우 잘 차려입은 한 신사를 데려갔으니, 우리는 그가 내게 구애하는 사람인 양 꾸몄으나 나는 그를 사촌이라 불렀고, 변호사는 그저 그 신사가 그 과부에게 구애한다고 그에게 은밀히 귀띔만 하기로 했다."]], [["He treated us handsomely indeed, and paid the money cheerfully enough; so that it cost him £200 in all, or rather more.", "그는 정말이지 우리를 점잖게 대접했고, 돈도 꽤 흔쾌히 치렀으니, 그리하여 그에게는 통틀어 이백 파운드, 아니 그보다 좀 더 들었다."], ["At our last meeting, when all was agreed, the case of the journeyman came up, and the mercer begged very hard for him; told me he was a man that had kept a shop of his own, and been in good business, had a wife, and several children, and was very poor; that he had nothing to make satisfaction with, but he should come to beg my pardon on his knees, if I desired it, as openly as I pleased.", "마지막 만남에서, 모든 것이 합의되었을 때, 그 직공의 일이 거론되었고, 포목상은 그를 위해 무척 간곡히 빌었다. 그는 내게, 그자가 한때 자기 가게를 차려 제법 장사도 했던 사람이며, 아내와 여러 자식이 있고 몹시 가난하다고, 배상할 거리가 아무것도 없으나, 내가 원한다면 내가 바라는 만큼 공개적으로 무릎 꿇고 내게 용서를 빌 것이라고 했다."], ["I had no spleen at the saucy rogue, nor were his submissions anything to me, since there was nothing to be got by him, so I thought it was as good to throw that in generously as not; so I told him I did not desire the ruin of any man, and therefore at his request I would forgive the wretch; it was below me to seek any revenge.", "나는 그 건방진 악당에게 별다른 앙심도 없었고, 그에게서 얻어 낼 것이 아무것도 없는 터라 그의 굴복도 내게 아무것도 아니었으니, 인색하게 구느니 그것을 너그러이 던져 주는 편이 낫겠다고 여겼다. 그리하여 나는, 나는 어떤 사람의 파멸도 바라지 않으니, 그의 청에 따라 그 가엾은 자를 용서하겠노라고, 무슨 복수를 꾀하는 것은 내게 어울리지 않는 일이라고 했다."]], [["When we were at supper he brought the poor fellow in to make acknowledgment, which he would have done with as much mean humility as his offence was with insulting haughtiness and pride, in which he was an instance of a complete baseness of spirit, impious, cruel, and relentless when uppermost and in prosperity, abject and low-spirited when down in affliction.", "저녁을 들고 있을 때 그는 그 가엾은 사내를 데려와 사죄하게 했는데, 그자는 모욕적인 거만함과 오만으로 죄를 지었던 것만큼이나 비루한 비굴함으로 사죄하려 들었으니, 이 점에서 그는 영혼이 완전히 천박한 자의 본보기였다. 곧 우위에 서서 번성할 때는 불경하고 잔인하며 가차 없다가, 곤경에 빠져 처지면 비굴하고 풀이 죽는 그런 자였다."], ["However, I abated his cringes, told him I forgave him, and desired he might withdraw, as if I did not care for the sight of him, though I had forgiven him.", "그래도 나는 그의 굽실거림을 그치게 하고, 용서한다고 말한 뒤, 비록 용서는 했어도 그 꼴을 보고 싶지 않은 양 그가 물러가기를 청했다."]], [["I was now in good circumstances indeed, if I could have known my time for leaving off, and my governess often said I was the richest of the trade in England; and so I believe I was, for I had £700 by me in money, besides clothes, rings, some plate, and two gold watches, and all of them stolen, for I had innumerable jobs besides these I have mentioned.", "이제 나는 정말이지 형편이 좋았으니, 만약 손 떼야 할 때를 알 수만 있었더라면 말이다. 내 후견인은 자주 내가 잉글랜드에서 이 장사를 하는 자들 가운데 가장 부유하다고 했고, 나도 그렇게 믿었으니, 옷가지며 반지며 약간의 은붙이며 금시계 두 개 외에도 현금으로 칠백 파운드를 지니고 있었고, 그것들은 모두 훔친 것이었으니, 내가 언급한 것들 외에도 무수한 일거리가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Oh!", "아아!"], ["had I even now had the grace of repentance, I had still leisure to have looked back upon my follies, and have made some reparation; but the satisfaction I was to make for the public mischiefs I had done was yet left behind; and I could not forbear going abroad again, as I called it now, than any more I could when my extremity really drove me out for bread.", "만약 내가 그때라도 회개의 은총을 받았더라면, 나는 아직 내 어리석음을 돌아보고 얼마간 속죄할 겨를이 있었을 것이다. 그러나 내가 끼친 공공의 해악에 대해 치러야 할 대가는 아직 뒤에 남아 있었고, 나는 다시 밖으로 나가는 것을—이제 나는 그것을 그렇게 불렀거니와—참을 수가 없었으니, 정말로 궁핍에 내몰려 끼니를 위해 거리로 떠밀려 나가던 그 어느 때보다도 더 그러했다."]], [["It was not long after the affair with the mercer was made up, that I went out in an equipage quite different from any I had ever appeared in before.", "포목상과의 일이 매듭지어진 지 얼마 안 되어, 나는 전에 한 번도 보인 적 없는 전혀 다른 차림새로 나갔다."], ["I dressed myself like a beggar woman, in the coarsest and most despicable rags I could get, and I walked about peering and peeping into every door and window I came near; and indeed I was in such a plight now that I knew as ill how to behave in as ever I did in any.", "나는 구할 수 있는 가장 거칠고 가장 천한 누더기를 입고 거지 여인처럼 차려입었으며, 가까이 가는 문이며 창마다 들여다보고 엿보며 돌아다녔다. 사실 나는 이제 그런 꼴이라, 그 어느 때 못지않게 어떻게 처신해야 할지를 도무지 알 수 없었다."], ["I naturally abhorred dirt and rags; I had been bred up tight and cleanly, and could be no other, whatever condition I was in; so that this was the most uneasy disguise to me that ever I put on.", "나는 본래 더러움과 누더기를 끔찍이 싫어했으니, 단정하고 청결하게 자라난 터라 어떤 처지에 있든 달리될 수가 없었고, 그래서 이것은 내가 여태껏 걸쳐 본 변장 가운데 가장 거북한 것이었다."], ["I said presently to myself that this would not do, for this was a dress that everybody was shy and afraid of; and I thought everybody looked at me, as if they were afraid I should come near them, lest I should take something from them, or afraid to come near me, lest they should get something from me.", "나는 곧 혼잣말로, 이건 안 되겠다고 했으니, 이것은 누구나 꺼리고 두려워하는 차림이었고, 누구나 마치 내가 자기들에게서 무언가 빼앗아 갈까 봐 내가 가까이 올까 두려워하는 양, 아니면 자기들이 내게서 무언가 옮을까 두려워하여 내게 가까이 오기를 두려워하는 양 나를 쳐다보는 것 같았기 때문이다."], ["I wandered about all the evening the first time I went out, and made nothing of it, but came home again wet, draggled, and tired.", "처음 나간 날 나는 저녁 내내 돌아다녔으나 아무 소득도 얻지 못하고, 젖고 진창에 더럽혀지고 지친 채 다시 집으로 돌아왔다."], ["However, I went out again the next night, and then I met with a little adventure, which had like to have cost me dear.", "그래도 나는 이튿날 밤 다시 나갔는데, 그때 작은 모험 하나를 만났으니, 그것은 하마터면 내게 큰 대가를 치르게 할 뻔했다."], ["As I was standing near a tavern door, there comes a gentleman on horseback, and lights at the door, and wanting to go into the tavern, he calls one of the drawers to hold his horse.", "내가 어느 선술집 문간 가까이에 서 있을 때, 한 신사가 말을 타고 와서 문 앞에서 내렸고, 선술집에 들어가고 싶었던 그는 급사 하나를 불러 자기 말을 붙들고 있게 했다."], ["He stayed pretty long in the tavern, and the drawer heard his master call, and thought he would be angry with him.", "그는 선술집에 꽤 오래 머물렀고, 급사는 주인이 부르는 소리를 듣고는 주인이 자기에게 화를 낼까 봐 걱정했다. 그는 내가 자기 곁에 서 있는 것을 보고 내게 소리쳤다."], ["Seeing me stand by him, he called to me, “Here, woman,”", "\"여기요, 아주머니,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “hold this horse a while, till I go in; if the gentleman comes, he’ll give you something.”", "\"내가 들어갔다 올 동안 이 말 좀 잠깐 붙들고 계세요. 그 신사분이 나오면 무어라도 좀 주실 겁니다.\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그러지요,\""], ["says I, and takes the horse, and walks off with him very soberly, and carried him to my governess.", "내가 말하고는 말을 받아, 매우 점잖은 걸음으로 그 말을 끌고 가서, 내 후견인에게 데려갔다."]], [["This had been a booty to those that had understood it; but never was poor thief more at a loss to know what to do with anything that was stolen; for when I came home, my governess was quite confounded, and what to do with the creature, we neither of us knew.", "이것은 그것을 다룰 줄 아는 자들에게는 노획물이었겠으나, 훔친 것을 두고 무엇을 해야 할지 몰라 이만큼 쩔쩔맨 가엾은 도둑은 일찍이 없었을 것이다. 집에 돌아왔을 때 내 후견인은 완전히 당황했고, 우리 둘 다 그 짐승을 어찌해야 할지 알 수가 없었다."], ["To send him to a stable was doing nothing, for it was certain that public notice would be given in the _Gazette_, and the horse described, so that we durst not go to fetch it again.", "마구간에 보내는 것은 아무 소용이 없었으니, 틀림없이 가제트에 공고가 실리고 그 말의 생김새가 적혀 나올 터라, 우리는 감히 그것을 다시 가지러 갈 수도 없었다. * 가제트: 영국의 관보를 겸한 신문으로, 분실물이나 현상수배 공고가 실렸다."]], [["All the remedy we had for this unlucky adventure was to go and set up the horse at an inn, and send a note by a porter to the tavern, that the gentleman’s horse that was lost such a time was left at such an inn, and that he might be had there; that the poor woman that held him, having led him about the street, not being able to lead him back again, had left him there.", "이 운 나쁜 모험에 대해 우리가 쓸 수 있는 유일한 방책은, 가서 그 말을 어느 여관에 맡겨 두고, 짐꾼 편에 선술집으로 쪽지를 한 장 보내, 아무 때에 잃어버린 그 신사의 말이 아무 여관에 맡겨져 있으니 거기서 찾아갈 수 있다는 것, 그리고 그 말을 붙들고 있던 가엾은 여인이 그것을 거리로 끌고 다녔으나 도로 끌고 갈 수가 없어서 거기에 두고 갔다는 것을 알리는 일이었다."], ["We might have waited till the owner had published and offered a reward, but we did not care to venture the receiving the reward.", "우리는 주인이 공고를 내고 사례금을 내걸 때까지 기다릴 수도 있었으나, 그 사례금을 받는 모험을 무릅쓰고 싶지 않았다."]], [["So this was a robbery and no robbery, for little was lost by it, and nothing was got by it, and I was quite sick of going out in a beggar’s dress; it did not answer at all, and besides, I thought it was ominous and threatening.", "그리하여 이것은 강도질이면서 강도질이 아니었으니, 그것으로 잃은 것도 거의 없고 얻은 것도 없었다. 그리고 나는 거지 옷차림으로 나가는 것에 아주 진저리가 났으니, 그것은 도무지 들어맞지 않았고, 게다가 불길하고 위협적인 일로 여겨졌다."]], [["While I was in this disguise, I fell in with a parcel of folks of a worse kind than any I ever sorted with, and I saw a little into their ways too.", "이렇게 변장하고 있는 동안, 나는 여태껏 내가 어울려 본 어떤 무리보다도 더 고약한 부류의 패거리와 엮이게 되었고, 그들의 수법도 조금 들여다보게 되었다. 이들은 화폐 위조범들이었는데, 이익으로 치자면 내게 매우 좋은 제안을 했으나, 그들이 내게 발을 들이게 하려던 부분은 가장 위험한 부분이었다."], ["These were coiners of money, and they made some very good offers to me, as to profit; but the part they would have had me have embarked in was the most dangerous part.", "곧 그들의 말마따나 다이 그 자체를 다루는 일이었으니, 만약 내가 붙잡혔더라면 그것은 확실한 죽음이었고, 그것도 화형주에서—다시 말해, 화형주에 묶여 불에 타 죽는 것이었다. 그리하여 비록 내가 겉보기에는 거지에 지나지 않았고 그들이 끌어들이려고 내게 산더미 같은 금은을 약속했어도, 그것은 될 일이 아니었다."], ["I mean that of the very working the die, as they call it, which, had I been taken, had been certain death, and that at a stake—I say, to be burnt to death at a stake; so that though I was to appearance but a beggar, and they promised mountains of gold and silver to me to engage, yet it would not do.", "사실 만약 내가 정말로 거지였거나, 처음 시작하던 때처럼 절박했더라면, 어쩌면 그것에 응했을지도 모른다. 어찌 살아야 할지 모르는 자들이 죽는 것을 무어 그리 두려워하겠는가?"], ["It is true, if I had been really a beggar, or had been desperate as when I began, I might perhaps have closed with it; for what care they to die that can’t tell how to live?", "그러나 지금은 그것이 내 처지가 아니었으니, 적어도 나는 그런 끔찍한 위험은 무릅쓸 마음이 없었다. 게다가 화형주에서 불에 타 죽는다는 생각만으로도 내 영혼 깊숙이 공포가 사무쳐, 피가 얼어붙고, 몸서리치지 않고는 그것을 생각할 수 없을 만큼 신경이 곤두섰다."], ["But at present this was not my condition, at least I was for no such terrible risks as those; besides, the very thoughts of being burnt at a stake struck terror into my very soul, chilled my blood, and gave me the vapours to such a degree, as I could not think of it without trembling.", "* 다이: 화폐를 찍어 내는 데 쓰는 주형(鑄型). 당시 화폐 위조는 대역죄에 준하여 여성의 경우 화형에 처해졌다."]], [["This put an end to my disguise too, for as I did not like the proposal, so I did not tell them so, but seemed to relish it, and promised to meet again.", "이것은 또한 내 변장에도 종지부를 찍었으니, 나는 그 제안이 마음에 들지 않았으나 그들에게 그렇다고 말하지는 않고, 마음에 드는 척하며 다시 만나기로 약속했다."], ["But I durst see them no more; for if I had seen them, and not complied, though I had declined it with the greatest assurance of secrecy in the world, they would have gone near to have murdered me, to make sure work, and make themselves easy, as they call it.", "그러나 나는 감히 그들을 다시 만날 수 없었으니, 만약 내가 그들을 만나고도 응하지 않았다면, 비록 세상에서 가장 굳은 비밀 엄수의 다짐을 하며 거절했더라도, 그들은 확실히 일을 마무리 짓고 그들의 말마따나 마음을 편케 하려고 나를 죽이려 들었을 것이기 때문이다."], ["What kind of easiness that is, they may best judge that understand how easy men are that can murder people to prevent danger.", "그것이 어떤 종류의 마음 편함인지는, 위험을 막으려고 사람을 죽일 수 있는 자들이 얼마나 마음이 편한지를 아는 이들이 가장 잘 판단할 것이다."]], [["This and horse-stealing were things quite out of my way, and I might easily resolve I would have no more to say to them; my business seemed to lie another way, and though it had hazard enough in it too, yet it was more suitable to me, and what had more of art in it, and more room to escape, and more chances for a-coming off if a surprise should happen.", "이것도 말 도둑질도 도무지 내 길과는 동떨어진 일이라, 나는 그들과 더는 상관하지 않겠다고 쉽사리 결심할 수 있었다. 내 일은 다른 길에 놓여 있는 듯했으니, 비록 그것에도 위험이 충분히 들어 있긴 했어도, 그래도 그것이 내게 더 잘 맞았고, 그 안에 솜씨가 더 들어 있으며, 빠져나갈 여지도 더 많고, 불시의 봉변이 닥치더라도 모면할 가망이 더 많았던 것이다."]], [["I had several proposals made also to me about that time, to come into a gang of house-breakers; but that was a thing I had no mind to venture at neither, any more than I had at the coining trade.", "또한 그 무렵 나는 가택 침입강도단에 들어오라는 제안도 여러 번 받았으나, 그것도 화폐 위조 장사 못지않게 내가 무릅쓰고 싶지 않은 일이었다."], ["I offered to go along with two men and a woman, that made it their business to get into houses by stratagem, and with them I was willing enough to venture.", "나는 계략을 써서 남의 집에 들어가는 것을 업으로 삼던 두 사내와 한 여자를 따라가겠노라 제안했고, 그들과 함께라면 기꺼이 모험을 무릅쓸 만했다."], ["But there were three of them already, and they did not care to part, nor I to have too many in a gang, so I did not close with them, but declined them, and they paid dear for their next attempt.", "그러나 그들은 이미 셋인 데다, 그들도 갈라서기를 꺼렸고 나도 한 패거리에 사람이 너무 많은 것을 바라지 않았으니, 그리하여 나는 그들과 손잡지 않고 마다했는데, 그들은 다음번 시도에서 톡톡히 대가를 치렀다."]], [["But at length I met with a woman that had often told me what adventures she had made, and with success, at the waterside, and I closed with her, and we drove on our business pretty well.", "그러나 마침내 나는, 강가에서 어떤 모험을 했고 또 성공했는지를 내게 자주 들려주던 한 여자를 만나, 그녀와 손잡았고, 우리는 우리 일을 꽤 잘 꾸려 나갔다."], ["One day we came among some Dutch people at St. Catherine’s, where we went on pretence to buy goods that were privately got on shore.", "어느 날 우리는 세인트캐서린의 어떤 네덜란드인들 틈에 끼어들었으니, 우리는 몰래 뭍에 들여온 물건을 산다는 핑계로 갔던 것이다."], ["I was two or three times in a house where we saw a good quantity of prohibited goods, and my companion once brought away three pieces of Dutch black silk that turned to good account, and I had my share of it; but in all the journeys I made by myself, I could not get an opportunity to do anything, so I laid it aside, for I had been so often, that they began to suspect something, and were so shy, that I saw nothing was to be done.", "나는 두세 번 어느 집에 들어가, 거기서 상당한 양의 금제품(禁制品)을 보았고, 내 동료는 한번은 네덜란드산 검은 비단 세 필을 가지고 나와 톡톡히 재미를 보았으며, 나도 그것에서 내 몫을 챙겼다. 그러나 나 혼자 한 그 모든 나들이에서는 무언가를 할 기회를 도무지 잡지 못해서, 나는 그 일을 접어 두었으니, 내가 하도 자주 드나든 터라 그들이 무언가 의심하기 시작하여 어찌나 경계하는지, 더는 할 수 있는 일이 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["This baulked me a little, and I resolved to push at something or other, for I was not used to come back so often without purchase; so the next day I dressed myself up fine, and took a walk to the other end of the town.", "이것이 나를 좀 김새게 했고, 나는 무엇이든 한 건 해야겠다고 결심했으니, 그토록 자주 빈손으로 돌아오는 데에 익숙하지 않았던 것이다. 그리하여 이튿날 나는 곱게 차려입고, 시내의 다른 쪽 끝으로 산책을 나갔다."], ["I passed through the Exchange in the Strand, but had no notion of finding anything to do there, when on a sudden I saw a great cluttering in the place, and all the people, shopkeepers as well as others, standing up and staring; and what should it be but some great duchess come into the Exchange, and they said the queen was coming.", "나는 스트랜드의 익스체인지를 지나가고 있었으나, 거기서 무슨 할 일을 찾으리라는 생각은 없었는데, 그때 별안간 그곳이 크게 부산해지더니, 가게 주인들이며 다른 이들이며 사람들이 모두 일어서서 멀거니 바라보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그것이 무엇인고 하니, 어떤 대단한 공작부인이 익스체인지에 든 것이었고, 사람들은 여왕이 오신다고들 했다."], ["I set myself close up to a shop-side with my back to the counter, as if to let the crowd pass by, when keeping my eye upon a parcel of lace which the shopkeeper was showing to some ladies that stood by me, the shopkeeper and her maid were so taken up with looking to see who was coming, and what shop they would go to, that I found means to slip a paper of lace into my pocket and come clear off with it; so the lady-milliner paid dear enough for her gaping after the queen.", "나는 마치 군중을 지나가게 하려는 양 등을 계산대 쪽으로 돌리고 어느 가게 옆에 바짝 붙어 섰는데, 가게 주인이 내 곁에 서 있던 어떤 부인들에게 보여 주고 있던 한 묶음의 레이스에 눈을 둔 채였으니, 가게 주인과 그 하녀가 누가 오는지, 또 그들이 어느 가게로 갈지 보느라 어찌나 정신이 팔려 있던지, 나는 레이스 한 묶음을 내 주머니에 슬쩍 집어넣고 그것을 가지고 감쪽같이 빠져나올 방도를 찾았다. 그리하여 그 부인 모자상은 여왕을 향해 입을 헤벌리고 바라본 대가를 톡톡히 치른 셈이었다."]], [["I went off from the shop, as if driven along by the throng, and mingling myself with the crowd, went out at the other door of the Exchange, and so got away before they missed their lace; and because I would not be followed, I called a coach and shut myself up in it.", "나는 마치 무리에 떠밀려 가는 양 가게를 벗어나, 군중 속에 섞여들어 익스체인지의 다른 문으로 나갔고, 그리하여 그들이 레이스가 없어진 것을 알아채기 전에 빠져나왔다. 그리고 미행당하지 않으려고 마차를 한 대 불러 그 안에 들어가 몸을 숨겼다."], ["I had scarce shut the coach doors up, but I saw the milliner’s maid and five or six more come running out into the street, and crying out as if they were frightened.", "마차 문을 닫자마자, 나는 그 모자상의 하녀와 대여섯 사람이 거리로 뛰어나와 겁에 질린 듯 외쳐 대는 것을 보았다."], ["They did not cry “Stop thief!”", "아무도 달아나지 않았으니 \"도둑이야!\""], ["because nobody ran away, but I could hear the word “robbed,” and “lace,”", "하고 외치지는 않았으나, \"강도당했어\"라는 말과 \"레이스\"라는 말을 두세 번 들을 수 있었고, 그 계집아이가 두 손을 비틀며 겁에 질린 사람처럼 이리저리 멀거니 뛰어다니는 것을 보았다."], ["two or three times, and saw the wench wringing her hands, and run staring to and again, like one scared.", "나를 태운 마부가 마부석에 올라타려 했으나 아직 다 올라타지 못해서, 말이 움직이기 시작하지 않았다. 그래서 나는 몹시 불안했고, 그 레이스 묶음을 집어 들어, 마부 바로 뒤, 앞쪽으로 열리는 마차의 덮개 밖으로 떨어뜨릴 채비를 해 두었다."], ["The coachman that had taken me up was getting up into the box, but was not quite up, so that the horse had not begun to move; so that I was terrible uneasy, and I took the packet of lace and laid it ready to have dropped it out at the flap of the coach, which opens before, just behind the coachman; but to my great satisfaction, in less than a minute the coach began to move, that is to say, as soon as the coachman had got up and spoken to his horses; so he drove away without any interruption, and I brought off my purchase, which was worth near £20.", "그러나 무척 다행스럽게도, 채 일 분도 못 되어 마차가 움직이기 시작했으니, 다시 말해 마부가 올라타 자기 말들에게 말을 건네자마자였다. 그리하여 그는 아무 방해도 없이 마차를 몰아갔고, 나는 내 노획물을 가지고 빠져나왔으니, 그것은 이십 파운드 가까이 나가는 것이었다."]], [["The next day I dressed up again, but in quite different clothes, and walked the same way again, but nothing offered till I came into St.", "이튿날 나는 다시 차려입었으나, 전혀 다른 옷이었고, 같은 길로 다시 걸어 나갔다."], ["James’s Park, where I saw abundance of fine ladies in the Park, walking in the Mall, and among the rest there was a little miss, a young lady of about twelve or thirteen years old, and she had a sister, as I suppose it was, with her, that might be about nine years old.", "그러나 세인트제임스 공원에 이를 때까지는 아무 일도 생기지 않았는데, 거기서 나는 공원에서 몰 거리를 거닐고 있는 수많은 고운 부인을 보았고, 그 가운데 열두세 살쯤 된 어린 아가씨가 있었는데, 그녀에게는 아마 아홉 살쯤 된 여동생인 듯한 아이가 함께 있었다."], ["I observed the biggest had a fine gold watch on, and a good necklace of pearl, and they had a footman in livery with them; but as it is not usual for the footman to go behind the ladies in the Mall, so I observed the footman stopped at their going into the Mall, and the biggest of the sisters spoke to him, which I perceived was to bid him be just there when they came back.", "큰 아이가 고운 금시계를 차고 있고 좋은 진주 목걸이를 하고 있는 것을 눈여겨보았으며, 그들에게는 제복 차림의 하인 하나가 딸려 있었다. 그러나 하인이 몰에서 부인들의 뒤를 따르는 것은 흔한 일이 아니었던지라, 나는 그 하인이 그들이 몰에 들어설 때 멈춰 서는 것을 보았고, 자매 가운데 큰 아이가 그에게 무어라 말했는데, 내가 알아챈 바로는 그들이 돌아올 때 바로 거기 있으라고 이르는 것이었다."]], [["When I heard her dismiss the footman, I stepped up to him and asked him, what little lady that was?", "그녀가 하인을 물리는 것을 듣고, 나는 그에게 다가가, 저 어린 아가씨가 누구냐고 묻고는, 그녀와 함께 있는 아이가 얼마나 예쁜지, 또 그 손위 아가씨가 자라면 얼마나 기품 있고 몸가짐이 단정할지, 얼마나 부인다워질지, 얼마나 진중할지를 두고 그와 잠깐 한담을 나누었다."], ["and held a little chat with him about what a pretty child it was with her, and how genteel and well-carriaged the lady, the eldest, would be: how womanish, and how grave; and the fool of a fellow told me presently who she was; that she was Sir Thomas ——’s eldest daughter, of Essex, and that she was a great fortune; that her mother was not come to town yet; but she was with Sir William ——’s lady, of Suffolk, at her lodging in Suffolk Street, and a great deal more; that they had a maid and a woman to wait on them, besides Sir Thomas’s coach, the coachman, and himself; and that young lady was governess to the whole family, as well here as at home too; and, in short, told me abundance of things enough for my business.", "그러자 그 어리석은 사내는 대뜸 그녀가 누구인지를 내게 일러 주었으니, 그녀는 에식스의 토머스 ―경의 맏딸이며, 큰 재산을 물려받을 사람이라고, 그녀의 어머니는 아직 시내에 오지 않았으나, 그녀는 서퍽 거리의 숙소에서 서퍽의 윌리엄 ―경의 부인과 함께 있다고 했으며, 그 밖에도 많은 말을 했으니, 그들에게는 시중드는 하녀와 시중드는 여인이 있고, 그 밖에 토머스 경의 마차며, 마부며, 자기 자신이 있다는 것, 그리고 저 어린 아가씨가 여기서나 집에서나 온 집안의 살림을 맡고 있다는 것이었다. 한마디로, 그는 내 일거리에 충분할 만큼 많은 것을 내게 일러 주었다."]], [["I was very well dressed, and had my gold watch as well as she; so I left the footman, and I puts myself in a rank with this young lady, having stayed till she had taken one double turn in the Mall, and was going forward again; by and by I saluted her by her name, with the title of Lady Betty.", "나는 매우 잘 차려입고 있었고, 그녀 못지않게 금시계도 차고 있었다. 그래서 나는 그 하인을 떠나, 이 어린 아가씨가 몰에서 한 바퀴를 다 돌고 다시 앞으로 나아가려 할 때까지 기다렸다가, 그녀와 나란히 줄을 섰다."], ["I asked her when she heard from her father; when my lady her mother would be in town, and how she did.", "이윽고 나는 그녀의 이름을 부르며 베티 아가씨라는 호칭으로 인사를 건넸다. 나는 그녀에게 언제 아버지의 소식을 들었는지, 어머니 되시는 마님은 언제 시내에 오시는지, 또 그분은 안녕하신지를 물었다."]], [["I talked so familiarly to her of her whole family that she could not suspect but that I knew them all intimately.", "나는 그녀의 온 집안에 대해 어찌나 허물없이 이야기했던지, 그녀는 내가 그들 모두와 친밀하게 안다고밖에는 의심할 수 없었다."], ["I asked her why she would come abroad without Mrs. Chime with her (that was the name of her woman) to take of Mrs.", "나는 그녀에게 어찌하여 동생 주디스 아가씨를 돌볼 차임 부인(그것이 그녀의 시중드는 여인의 이름이었다)도 없이 밖에 나왔느냐고 물었다."], ["Judith, that was her sister. Then I entered into a long chat with her about her sister, what a fine little lady she was, and asked her if she had learned French, and a thousand such little things to entertain her, when on a sudden we saw the guards come, and the crowd ran to see the king go by to the Parliament House.", "그러고는 그녀의 동생에 대해 긴 한담에 들어가, 얼마나 고운 어린 아가씨인지를 말하고, 프랑스어를 배웠는지 등을 물으며 그녀의 흥을 돋울 자잘한 이야기를 수천 가지나 늘어놓았는데, 그때 별안간 근위병들이 오는 것을 보았고, 군중이 국왕이 의사당으로 가시는 것을 보려고 달려갔다."]], [["The ladies ran all to the side of the Mall, and I helped my lady to stand upon the edge of the boards on the side of the Mall, that she might be high enough to see; and took the little one and lifted her quite up; during which, I took care to convey the gold watch so clean away from the Lady Betty, that she never felt it, nor missed it, till all the crowd was gone, and she was gotten into the middle of the Mall among the other ladies.", "부인들이 모두 몰의 가장자리로 달려갔고, 나는 내 아가씨가 충분히 높이 서서 볼 수 있도록 몰 가의 판자 가장자리 위에 올라서게 도와주었으며, 어린 동생을 안아 번쩍 들어 올렸다. 그러는 동안 나는 베티 아가씨에게서 금시계를 어찌나 감쪽같이 빼냈던지, 그녀는 군중이 모두 흩어지고 그녀가 다른 부인들 틈에 끼어 몰 한복판으로 들어설 때까지, 그것을 느끼지도, 없어진 것을 알아채지도 못했다."]], [["I took my leave of her in the very crowd, and said to her, as if in haste, “Dear Lady Betty, take care of your little sister.”", "나는 바로 그 군중 속에서 그녀에게 작별을 고하며, 마치 바쁜 듯이 그녀에게 말했다. \"베티 아가씨, 어린 동생을 잘 보살피세요.\""], ["And so the crowd did as it were thrust me away from her, and that I was obliged unwillingly to take my leave.", "그리하여 군중이 말하자면 나를 그녀에게서 떠밀어 놓았고, 나는 마지못해 작별을 고할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["The hurry in such cases is immediately over, and the place clear as soon as the king is gone by; but as there is always a great running and clutter just as the king passes, so having dropped the two little ladies, and done my business with them without any miscarriage, I kept hurrying on among the crowd, as if I ran to see the king, and so I got before the crowd and kept so till I came to the end of the Mall, when the king going on towards the Horse Guards, I went forward to the passage, which went then through against the lower end of the Haymarket, and there I bestowed a coach upon myself, and made off, and I confess I have not yet been so good as my word, viz.", "이런 경우의 부산함은 곧 가라앉고, 국왕이 지나가시면 이내 그 자리가 휑해지는 법이다. 그러나 국왕이 지나가실 바로 그때는 늘 크게 뛰고 부산을 떠는 법이라, 그 두 어린 아가씨를 떨쳐 내고 아무 탈 없이 그들과의 일을 해치운 나는, 마치 국왕을 보려고 달려가는 양 군중 틈을 헤치며 계속 서둘러 나아갔고, 그리하여 군중보다 앞서 나가 몰 끝에 이를 때까지 그렇게 했다."], ["to go and visit my Lady Betty.", "국왕이 호스가즈 쪽으로 나아가시자, 나는 그 무렵 헤이마켓 아래쪽 끝을 가로질러 나 있던 통로 쪽으로 앞서 나아가, 거기서 나 자신에게 마차 한 대를 베풀고 빠져나왔다. 그리고 고백하건대 나는 아직 내 약속을, 곧 가서 베티 아가씨를 방문하겠다던 약속을 지키지 못했다."]], [["I was once of the mind to venture staying with Lady Betty till she missed the watch, and so have made a great outcry about it with her, and have got her into the coach, and put myself in the coach with her, and have gone home with her; for she appeared so fond of me, and so perfectly deceived by my so readily talking to her of all her relations and family, that I thought it was very easy to push the thing farther, and to have got at least the necklace of pearl; but when I considered that though the child would not perhaps have suspected me, other people might, and that if I was searched I should be discovered, I thought it was best to go off with what I had got, and be satisfied.", "나는 한때, 베티 아가씨가 시계가 없어진 것을 알아챌 때까지 그녀와 함께 머물러 있다가, 그녀와 더불어 그것을 두고 크게 야단법석을 떨고, 그녀를 마차에 태운 뒤 나도 그 마차에 함께 타서, 그녀와 함께 그녀의 집으로 가 볼 마음도 있었다. 그녀가 나를 어찌나 따르고, 또 내가 그녀의 모든 친척과 집안에 대해 그토록 거침없이 이야기한 데에 어찌나 감쪽같이 속아 넘어갔던지, 나는 그 일을 더 밀고 나가 적어도 그 진주 목걸이만은 손에 넣기가 매우 쉬우리라 여겼던 것이다. 그러나 비록 그 아이는 어쩌면 나를 의심하지 않았을지 몰라도 다른 사람들은 의심할 수 있고, 또 만약 내가 수색당하면 들통날 것임을 생각하니, 손에 넣은 것을 가지고 빠져나와 만족하는 편이 가장 낫겠다고 여겼다."]], [["I came accidentally afterwards to hear, that when the young lady missed her watch, she made a great outcry in the Park, and sent her footman up and down to see if he could find me out, she having described me so perfectly that he knew presently that it was the same person that had stood and talked so long with him, and asked him so many questions about them; but I gone far enough out of their reach before she could come at her footman to tell him the story.", "나는 그 뒤에 우연히 듣게 되었으니, 그 어린 아가씨가 시계가 없어진 것을 알았을 때 공원에서 크게 야단법석을 떨며, 자기 하인을 이리저리 보내 나를 찾아낼 수 있는지 보게 했고, 그녀가 나를 어찌나 완벽하게 묘사했던지 그가 대뜸 그것이 자기와 그토록 오래 서서 이야기를 나누고 그들에 대해 그토록 많은 것을 물었던 바로 그 사람임을 알아차렸다는 것이었다. 그러나 나는 그녀가 자기 하인에게 다가가 그 이야기를 할 수 있게 되기 전에 이미 그들의 손이 닿을 수 없는 곳까지 충분히 멀리 가 있었다."]], [["I made another adventure after this, of a nature different from all I had been concerned in yet, and this was at a gaming-house near Covent Garden.", "나는 이 일이 있은 뒤에 또 한 번 모험을 했는데, 그것은 여태껏 내가 관여한 어떤 것과도 성격이 다른 것이었으니, 이번에는 코번트가든 근처의 어느 도박장에서였다."]], [["I saw several people go in and out; and I stood in the passage a good while with another woman with me, and seeing a gentleman go up that seemed to be of more than ordinary fashion, I said to him, “Sir, pray don’t they give women leave to go up?” “Yes, madam,” says he, “and to play too, if they please.” “I mean so, sir,” said I. And with that he said he would introduce me if I had a mind; so I followed him to the door, and he looking in, “There, madam,” says he, “are the gamesters, if you have a mind to venture.” I looked in and said to my comrade aloud, “Here’s nothing but men; I won’t venture among them.” At which one of the gentlemen cried out, “You need not be afraid, madam, here’s none but fair gamesters; you are very welcome to come and set what you please.” So I went a little nearer and looked on, and some of them brought me a chair, and I sat down and saw the box and dice go round apace; then I said to my comrade, “The gentlemen play too high for us; come, let us go.”", "나는 여러 사람이 드나드는 것을 보았고, 통로에 꽤\n어떤 여자 한 사람과 함께 있던 중에, 어딘가 보통 이상으로 멋을 부린 듯한 신사 하나가 위층으로 올라가는 것을 보고 내가 그에게 말했다. \"선생님, 여쭙는데, 여자도 올라가게 해 주지 않나요?\" \"그럼요, 부인.\" 그가 대답했다. \"원하시면 노름도 하실 수 있고요.\" \"저도 그런 뜻이에요, 선생님.\" 내가 말했다. 그러자 그는 내가 마음이 있다면 안내해 주겠노라 했고, 나는 그를 따라 문간으로 갔다. 그가 안을 들여다보며 말했다. \"저기, 부인, 노름꾼들이 있습니다. 한번 걸어 보실 마음이 있다면요.\" 나는 안을 들여다보고는 일행에게 큰 소리로 말했다. \"여기는 남자들뿐이네요. 저 사람들 틈에 끼어들지는 않겠어요.\" 그러자 신사들 가운데 하나가 외쳤다. \"겁내실 것 없습니다, 부인. 여기는 점잖은 노름꾼들밖에 없으니, 들어오셔서 마음껏 거셔도 좋습니다.\" 그래서 나는 조금 더 가까이 다가가 구경했고, 그들 중 몇이 내게 의자를 가져다주어 자리에 앉아 주사위 통과 주사위가 빠르게 돌아가는 것을 지켜보았다. 그러고는 일행에게 말했다. \"이 신사들은 우리에게는 판돈이 너무 큰 노름을 하네요. 자, 가요.\""]], [["The people were all very civil, and one gentleman in particular encouraged me, and said, “Come, madam, if you please to venture, if you dare trust me, I’ll answer for it you shall have nothing put upon you here.”", "사람들은 모두 매우 정중했고, 한 신사가 특히 나를 부추기며 말했다. \"자, 부인, 한번 걸어 보실 마음이 있고, 또 저를 믿어 주신다면, 여기서 부인께 어떤 속임수도 쓰이지 않으리라는 것을 제가 보증하겠습니다.\""], ["“No, sir,”", "\"아니에요, 선생님.\""], ["said I, smiling, “I hope the gentlemen would not cheat a woman.”", "나는 미소를 지으며 말했다. \"신사분들이 설마 여자를 속이지는 않으시겠지요.\""], ["But still I declined venturing, though I pulled out a purse with money in it, that they might see I did not want money.", "그러면서도 나는 여전히 거는 것을 사양했는데, 다만 돈이 든 지갑을 꺼내 보여서 내가 돈이 없어서가 아님을 그들이 알게 했다."]], [["After I had sat a while, one gentleman said to me, jeering, “Come, madam, I see you are afraid to venture for yourself; I always had good luck with the ladies, you shall set for me, if you won’t set for yourself.” I told him, “Sir, I should be very loth to lose your money,” though I added, “I am pretty lucky too; but the gentlemen play so high, that I dare not indeed venture my own.”", "내가 한참 앉아 있자 한 신사가 빈정대듯 내게 말했다. \"자, 부인, 보아하니 부인 스스로를 위해 거는 것은 겁이 나시는 모양이군요. 저는 늘 숙녀분들과 함께하면 운이 좋았으니, 부인 스스로를 위해 걸지 않으시겠다면 저를 위해 걸어 주시지요.\" 나는 그에게 말했다. \"선생님, 저는 선생님 돈을 잃게 되는 것이 정말 싫습니다.\" 그러면서도 이렇게 덧붙였다. \"저도 운이 꽤 좋은 편이긴 하지만, 신사분들이 판돈을 너무 크게 거셔서 제 돈은 정말 걸 엄두가 안 나는 것이지요.\""]], [["“Well, well,”", "\"좋아요, 좋아요.\""], ["says he, “there’s ten guineas, madam; set them for me.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기 십 기니가 있으니, 부인, 저를 위해 걸어 주십시오.\""], ["So I took his money and set, himself looking on.", "그래서 나는 그의 돈을 받아 들고 걸었고, 그 자신은 지켜보았다."], ["I ran out nine of the guineas by one and two at a time, and then the box coming to the next man to me, my gentleman gave me ten guineas more, and made me set five of them at once, and the gentleman who had the box threw out, so there was five guineas of his money again.", "나는 한두 번에 한 번씩 걸어 아홉 기니를 날렸는데, 그러다 주사위 통이 내 옆자리 사람에게 돌아왔을 때 내 신사가 다시 십 기니를 더 주며 그중 다섯을 한꺼번에 걸게 했고, 주사위 통을 쥔 신사가 끗수가 나빠 그의 돈 다섯 기니가 다시 돌아왔다."], ["He was encouraged at this, and made me take the box, which was a bold venture.", "그는 이에 신이 나서 내게 주사위 통을 쥐게 했는데, 이는 대담한 모험이었다."], ["However, I held the box so long that I had gained him his whole money, and had a good handful of guineas in my lap, and which was the better luck, when I threw out, I threw but at one or two of those that had set me, and so went off easy.", "그러나 나는 주사위 통을 오래도록 쥐고 있어서 그의 돈을 전부 따 주었고, 내 무릎에는 한 움큼의 기니가 그득했으며, 게다가 더 운이 좋았던 것은, 내가 끗수를 내고 나설 때면 내게 돈을 걸어 준 사람들 가운데 한둘에게만 걸어, 그렇게 수월히 빠져나왔다는 점이다."]], [["When I was come this length, I offered the gentleman all the gold, for it was his own; and so would have had him play for himself, pretending I did not understand the game well enough.", "여기까지 이르자 나는 그 금화가 모두 그의 것이었으므로 그 신사에게 전부 내밀며, 노름을 잘 모른다는 핑계로 그더러 직접 걸라고 하려 했다."], ["He laughed, and said if I had but good luck, it was no matter whether I understood the game or no; but I should not leave off.", "그는 웃으며, 운만 좋다면 노름을 알든 모르든 상관없다고 했고, 그러니 나더러 그만두면 안 된다고 했다. 그래도 그는 처음에 넣었던 십오 기니를 빼내고는 나머지로 노름을 하라고 했다."], ["However, he took out the fifteen guineas that he had put in at first, and bade me play with the rest.", "나는 내가 얼마나 땄는지 보려고 그것을 헤아려 보려 했으나, 그가 말했다. \"아니, 아니요, 헤아리지 마세요."], ["I would have told them to see how much I had got, but he said, “No, no, don’t tell them, I believe you are very honest, and ’tis bad luck to tell them”; so I played on.", "부인은 매우 정직한 분이라 믿거니와, 헤아리는 것은 운에 좋지 않으니까요.\" 그래서 나는 노름을 계속했다."]], [["I understood the game well enough, though I pretended I did not, and played cautiously.", "나는 모르는 척했으나 사실 노름을 충분히 알고 있었고, 조심스럽게 했다."], ["It was to keep a good stock in my lap, out of which I every now and then conveyed some into my pocket, but in such a manner, and at such convenient times, as I was sure he could not see it.", "그것은 내 무릎에 두둑한 밑천을 쌓아 두고, 거기서 이따금 얼마씩을 내 호주머니로 슬쩍 옮기되, 그가 결코 보지 못하리라 확신할 만한 방식으로, 또 그만큼 알맞은 때에 하기 위함이었다."]], [["I played a great while, and had very good luck for him; but the last time I held the box, they set me high, and I threw boldly at all; I held the box till I gained near fourscore guineas, but lost above half of it back in the last throw; so I got up, for I was afraid I should lose it all back again, and said to him, “Pray come, sir, now, and take it and play for yourself; I think I have done pretty well for you.”", "나는 오랫동안 노름을 했고 그를 위해 운이 매우 좋았으나, 마지막으로 주사위 통을 쥐었을 때 그들이 내게 판돈을 크게 걸어 왔고, 나는 대담하게 전부를 걸었다. 나는 주사위 통을 쥐고 거의 팔십 기니에 가깝게 땄으나, 마지막 던지기에서 그 절반 넘게 도로 잃었다."], ["He would have had me play on, but it grew late, and I desired to be excused.", "그래서 나는 전부를 다시 잃을까 두려워 자리에서 일어나 그에게 말했다. \"자, 이제 선생님, 와서 이걸 받으시고 직접 걸어 보세요."], ["When I gave it up to him, I told him I hoped he would give me leave to tell it now, that I might see what I had gained, and how lucky I had been for him; when I told them, there were threescore and three guineas.", "제가 선생님을 위해 꽤 잘해 드린 것 같으니까요.\" 그는 내가 노름을 계속하기를 바랐으나, 시간이 늦어져 나는 양해를 구했다."], ["“Ay,”", "그것을 그에게 돌려주면서 나는, 이제는 내가 얼마나 땄는지, 또 그를 위해 얼마나 운이 좋았는지 알 수 있도록 헤아리게 해 주시기를 바란다고 말했다. 헤아려 보니 예순세 기니였다."], ["says I, “if it had not been for that unlucky throw, I had got you a hundred guineas.”", "\"아.\" 내가 말했다."], ["So I gave him all the money, but he would not take it till I had put my hand into it, and taken some for myself, and bid me please myself.", "\"그 운 나쁜 던지기만 아니었다면, 선생님께 백 기니는 따 드렸을 텐데요.\" 그래서 나는 그에게 돈을 전부 주었으나, 그는 내가 거기에 손을 넣어 얼마간 내 몫으로 가질 때까지 받으려 하지 않았고, 마음껏 가지라고 했다."], ["I refused it, and was positive I would not take it myself; if he had a mind to anything of that kind, it should be all his own doings.", "나는 거절하며, 절대로 내가 직접 가지지는 않겠다고 단호히 말했다. 그가 그런 식으로 무언가를 해 주고 싶다면, 그것은 전적으로 그 자신이 알아서 할 일이라고 했다."]], [["The rest of the gentlemen seeing us striving cried, “Give it her all”; but I absolutely refused that. Then one of them said, “D—n ye, Jack, halve it with her; don’t you know you should be always upon even terms with the ladies.” So, in short, he divided it with me, and I brought away thirty guineas, besides about forty-three which I had stole privately, which I was sorry for afterward, because he was so generous.", "나머지 신사들이 우리가 실랑이하는 것을 보고는 외쳤다. \"부인께 전부 드리시오.\" 그러나 나는 그것을 단호히 거절했다. 그러자 그들 가운데 하나가 말했다. \"이런, 잭, 부인하고 반씩 나누게. 숙녀분들과는 늘 공평한 조건이어야 한다는 걸 모르나.\" 그래서 한마디로, 그는 그것을 나와 반씩 나누었고, 나는 따로 몰래 훔친 마흔세 기니가량 외에 서른 기니를 가지고 나왔는데, 그가 그토록 너그러웠던 까닭에 나중에 그 일이 마음에 걸렸다."]], [["Thus I brought home seventy-three guineas, and let my old governess see what good luck I had at play.", "이렇게 나는 일흔세 기니를 집에 가져와, 늙은 후견인에게 노름에서 내가 얼마나 운이 좋았는지를 보여 주었다."], ["However, it was her advice that I should not venture again, and I took her counsel, for I never went there any more; for I knew as well as she, if the itch of play came in, I might soon lose that, and all the rest of what I had got.", "그러나 다시는 모험하지 말라는 것이 그녀의 충고였고, 나는 그 조언을 받아들여 다시는 그곳에 가지 않았다. 노름의 욕심이 한번 끼어들면, 나도 그녀만큼이나 잘 알았듯이, 그 돈과 내가 그동안 번 나머지 전부를 머지않아 잃을 수도 있기 때문이었다."]], [["Fortune had smiled upon me to that degree, and I had thriven so much, and my governess too, for she always had a share with me, that really the old gentlewoman began to talk of leaving off while we were well, and being satisfied with what we had got; but, I know not what fate guided me, I was as backward to it now as she was when I proposed it to her before, and so in an ill hour we gave over the thoughts of it for the present, and, in a word, I grew more hardened and audacious than ever, and the success I had made my name as famous as any thief of my sort ever had been at Newgate, and in the Old Bailey.", "운명이 내게 그토록 미소를 지어 주어, 나도 그렇고 내 후견인도 그렇고—그녀는 늘 나와 몫을 나누었으므로—참으로 많이 번 터라, 이 늙은 부인은 우리가 형편이 좋을 때 그만두고, 그동안 번 것에 만족하자는 이야기를 꺼내기 시작했다. 그러나 어떤 운명이 나를 이끌었는지 모르겠으나, 전에 내가 그녀에게 그것을 제안했을 때 그녀가 그러했던 것처럼, 이제는 내가 그 일에 미적거렸고, 그리하여 불운한 시각에 우리는 당분간 그 생각을 접어 두었으니, 한마디로 나는 그 어느 때보다 더 모질고 대담해졌으며, 그 성공으로 나는 뉴게이트와 올드베일리에서 내 부류의 어떤 도둑 못지않게 이름을 떨치게 되었다."]], [["I had sometime taken the liberty to play the same game over again, which is not according to practice, which however succeeded not amiss; but generally I took up new figures, and contrived to appear in new shapes every time I went abroad.", "나는 더러 같은 수법을 다시 써먹는 자유를 누리기도 했는데, 이는 통례에 어긋나는 일이었으나 그래도 그리 나쁘지 않게 먹혀들었다. 그러나 대개는 새로운 모습으로 꾸미고, 밖에 나갈 때마다 새로운 행색으로 나타나도록 궁리했다."]], [["It was not a rumbling time of the year, and the gentlemen being most of them gone out of town, Tunbridge, and Epsom, and such places were full of people.", "지금은 한 해 중 떠들썩한 철은 아니었고, 신사들 대부분이 시내를 떠난 터라 턴브리지며 엡섬이며 그런 곳들이 사람들로 가득했다. 그러나 시내는 한산했고, 나는 우리 장사도 다른 장사들과 마찬가지로 그 영향을 다소 느낀다고 생각했다."], ["But the city was thin, and I thought our trade felt it a little, as well as other; so that at the latter end of the year I joined myself with a gang who usually go every year to Stourbridge Fair, and from thence to Bury Fair, in Suffolk.", "그래서 그해 말미에 나는 해마다 스타브리지 장에 갔다가 거기서 서퍽주의 베리 장으로 가곤 하는 한 무리에 끼어들었다. 우리는 거기서 큰 수확을 기대했으나, 막상 가서 사정을 보니 나는 금세 싫증이 났다."], ["We promised ourselves great things there, but when I came to see how things were, I was weary of it presently; for except mere picking of pockets, there was little worth meddling with; neither, if a booty had been made, was it so easy carrying it off, nor was there such a variety of occasion for business in our way, as in London; all that I made of the whole journey was a gold watch at Bury Fair, and a small parcel of linen at Cambridge, which gave me an occasion to take leave of the place.", "단순한 소매치기 외에는 손댈 만한 가치 있는 것이 거의 없었고, 또 노획물이 생긴다 해도 그것을 가지고 빠져나오기가 그리 쉽지 않았으며, 런던에서처럼 우리 식으로 일할 거리가 그렇게 다양하지도 않았다. 이 여정 전체에서 내가 얻은 것이라곤 베리 장에서의 금시계 하나와 케임브리지에서의 자그마한 아마포 꾸러미 하나뿐이었는데, 그것이 내게 그곳을 떠날 구실을 주었다."], ["It was on old bite, and I thought might do with a country shopkeeper, though in London it would not.", "그것은 오래된 수법으로, 런던에서는 통하지 않겠지만 시골 가게 주인에게는 먹혀들 만하다고 생각했다."]], [["I bought at a linen-draper’s shop, not in the fair, but in the town of Cambridge, as much fine holland and other things as came to about seven pounds; when I had done, I bade them be sent to such an inn, where I had purposely taken up my being the same morning, as if I was to lodge there that night.", "나는 장터가 아니라 케임브리지 읍내에 있는 어느 포목상에서 고운 홀란드 천과 그 밖의 물건들을 약 칠 파운드어치쯤 사들였다. 다 사고 나서 나는, 마치 그날 밤 거기서 묵을 것처럼 바로 그날 아침에 일부러 잡아 둔 어느 여관으로 그것들을 보내 달라고 했다."]], [["I ordered the draper to send them home to me, about such an hour, to the inn where I lay, and I would pay him his money.", "나는 포목상에게 그 물건들을 일정한 시각에, 내가 묵는 여관으로 보내 달라고 하면서, 그러면 그의 돈을 치르겠다고 했다."], ["At the time appointed the draper sends the goods, and I placed one of our gang at the chamber door, and when the innkeeper’s maid brought the messenger to the door, who was a young fellow, an apprentice, almost a man, she tells him her mistress was asleep, but if he would leave the things and call in about an hour, I should be awake, and he might have the money.", "약속한 시각에 포목상이 물건을 보내왔는데, 나는 우리 무리 가운데 하나를 방문 앞에 세워 두었다. 여관 하녀가 심부름꾼을, 거의 다 자란 도제 청년을 문간으로 데려오자, 하녀는 그에게 자기 마님이 주무시고 계시니, 물건을 두고 갔다가 한 시간쯤 뒤에 다시 들르면 그때는 내가 깨어 있을 테고 돈을 받을 수 있으리라고 했다."], ["He left the parcel very readily, and goes his way, and in about half an hour my maid and I walked off, and that very evening I hired a horse, and a man to ride before me, and went to Newmarket, and from thence got my passage in a coach that was not quite full to St.", "그는 선뜻 꾸러미를 두고 제 갈 길로 갔고, 반시간쯤 뒤에 내 하녀와 나는 걸어 나갔다."], ["Edmund’s Bury, where, as I told you, I could make but little of my trade, only at a little country opera-house made a shift to carry off a gold watch from a lady’s side, who was not only intolerably merry, but, as I thought, a little fuddled, which made my work much easier.", "그리고 바로 그날 저녁 나는 말 한 필과 내 앞에서 말을 몰 사람을 하나 빌려 뉴마켓으로 갔고, 거기서 자리가 다 차지 않은 마차에 편승하여 세인트에드먼즈베리로 갔는데, 앞서 말했듯이 그곳에서는 내 장사로 거의 재미를 보지 못했고, 다만 어느 작은 시골 오페라 극장에서 한 부인의 옆구리에서 금시계를 슬쩍 빼내는 데 그쳤으니, 그 부인은 참을 수 없을 만큼 들떠 있었을 뿐 아니라 내가 보기에 조금 취해 있기까지 해서 내 일이 한결 수월했다."]], [["I made off with this little booty to Ipswich, and from thence to Harwich, where I went into an inn, as if I had newly arrived from Holland, not doubting but I should make some purchase among the foreigners that came on shore there; but I found them generally empty of things of value, except what was in their portmanteaux and Dutch hampers, which were generally guarded by footmen; however, I fairly got one of their portmanteaux one evening out of the chamber where the gentleman lay, the footman being fast asleep on the bed, and I suppose very drunk.", "나는 이 자그마한 노획물을 가지고 입스위치로 빠져나갔고, 거기서 다시 하리치로 갔는데, 거기서 나는 마치 막 홀란드에서 도착한 양 어느 여관에 들었으니, 그곳에 상륙하는 외국인들 틈에서 한몫 챙기리라는 것을 의심치 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 대개 여행 가방과 네덜란드식 바구니에 든 것 외에는 값나가는 물건이 없는 듯했고, 그것들은 대개 하인들이 지키고 있었다. 그러나 어느 날 저녁, 신사가 누워 있던 방에서 그들의 여행 가방 하나를 깨끗이 손에 넣었으니, 하인이 침대에서 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 짐작건대 몹시 취해 있었던 것이다."]], [["The room in which I lodged lay next to the Dutchman’s, and having dragged the heavy thing with much ado out of the chamber into mine, I went out into the street, to see if I could find any possibility of carrying it off.", "내가 묵던 방은 그 네덜란드인의 방 옆에 있었고, 그 무거운 물건을 무진 애를 써서 그 방에서 내 방으로 끌고 온 다음, 나는 그것을 가지고 빠져나갈 무슨 방도가 있을지 알아보러 거리로 나갔다."], ["I walked about a great while, but could see no probability either of getting out the thing, or of conveying away the goods that were in it if I had opened it, the town being so small, and I a perfect stranger in it; so I was returning with a resolution to carry it back again, and leave it where I found it.", "나는 한참을 돌아다녔으나, 그 물건을 가지고 나갈 가망도, 또 설령 그것을 열었다 한들 그 안의 물건들을 빼낼 가망도 보이지 않았으니, 고을이 워낙 작은 데다 나는 그곳에서 완전한 이방인이었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그것을 도로 가져다가 발견한 자리에 두기로 마음먹고 돌아오던 참이었다."], ["Just in that very moment I heard a man make a noise to some people to make haste, for the boat was going to put off, and the tide would be spent.", "바로 그 순간, 한 사내가 어떤 사람들에게 서두르라고, 배가 곧 떠나려 하고 물때가 다 되어 간다고 소리치는 것을 들었다."], ["I called to the fellow, “What boat is it, friend,”", "나는 그 사내를 불렀다. \"여보세요, 그게 무슨 배인가요?"], ["says I, “that you belong to?”", "댁이 타는 배가?\" 내가 물었다."], ["“The Ipswich wherry, madam,”", "\"입스위치행 거룻배입니다, 부인.\""], ["says he.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“When do you go off?”", "\"언제 떠나는데요?\""], ["says I.", "내가 물었다."], ["“This moment, madam,”", "\"지금 당장요, 부인.\""], ["says he; “do you want to go thither?”", "그가 말했다. \"그쪽으로 가시려고요?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그래요.\""], ["said I, “if you can stay till I fetch my things.”", "내가 말했다. \"내가 짐을 가져올 때까지 기다려 주신다면요.\""], ["“Where are your things, madam?” says he.", "\"짐이 어디 있는데요, 부인?\""], ["“At such an inn,” said I.", "그가 물었다."], ["“Well, I’ll go with you, madam,”", "\"어느 여관에요.\" 내가 말했다."], ["says he, very civilly, “and bring them for you.”", "\"그럼 제가 함께 가서, 부인 짐을 가져다드리지요.\" 그가 매우 정중하게 말했다."], ["“Come away, then,”", "\"그럼 어서 가요.\""], ["says I, and takes him with me.", "내가 말하며 그를 데리고 갔다."]], [["The people of the inn were in a great hurry, the packet-boat from Holland being just come in, and two coaches just come also with passengers from London, for another packet-boat that was going off for Holland, which coaches were to go back next day with the passengers that were just landed.", "여관 사람들은 무척 분주했으니, 홀란드에서 오는 우편선이 막 들어온 데다, 홀란드로 떠나려는 또 다른 우편선을 타려고 런던에서 온 승객들을 실은 마차 두 대도 막 도착한 참이었기 때문이다. 그 마차들은 막 상륙한 승객들을 태우고 이튿날 돌아갈 예정이었다."], ["In this hurry it was not much minded that I came to the bar and paid my reckoning, telling my landlady I had gotten my passage by sea in a wherry.", "이 부산함 속에서, 내가 계산대로 와 셈을 치르며 여주인에게 거룻배로 뱃길을 잡았노라 말한 것은 그리 주목받지 못했다."]], [["These wherries are large vessels, with good accommodation for carrying passengers from Harwich to London; and though they are called wherries, which is a word used in the Thames for a small boat rowed with one or two men, yet these are vessels able to carry twenty passengers, and ten or fifteen tons of goods, and fitted to bear the sea.", "이 거룻배들은 큰 배로, 하리치에서 런던까지 승객을 실어 나르기에 좋은 시설을 갖추고 있다. 그리고 비록 거룻배라 불리지만—이는 템스강에서 한두 사람이 노 저어 가는 작은 배를 가리키는 말이지만—이것들은 스무 명의 승객과 십 톤 내지 십오 톤의 물건을 실을 수 있고 바다를 견디도록 만들어진 배다."], ["All this I had found out by inquiring the night before into the several ways of going to London.", "이 모든 것을 나는 전날 밤 런던으로 가는 여러 길을 알아보던 중에 알아낸 것이었다."]], [["My landlady was very courteous, took my money for my reckoning, but was called away, all the house being in a hurry.", "내 여주인은 매우 친절하여 셈한 내 돈을 받았으나, 온 집이 부산한 통에 불려 가 버렸다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 두고, 그 사내를 내 방으로 데려가 그 트렁크, 아니 여행 가방을—그것은 트렁크 같았으므로—건넸고, 그것을 낡은 앞치마로 둘둘 싸자 그는 곧장 그것을 가지고 제 배로 갔으며, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐으니, 아무도 우리에게 그것에 대해 조금도 묻지 않았다."], ["So I left her, took the fellow up to my chamber, gave him the trunk, or portmanteau, for it was like a trunk, and wrapped it about with an old apron, and he went directly to his boat with it, and I after him, nobody asking us the least question about it; as for the drunken Dutch footman he was still asleep, and his master with other foreign gentlemen at supper, and very merry below, so I went clean off with it to Ipswich; and going in the night, the people of the house knew nothing but that I was gone to London by the Harwich wherry, as I had told my landlady.", "취한 네덜란드 하인으로 말하자면 그는 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 그의 주인은 다른 외국 신사들과 함께 아래층에서 저녁을 들며 매우 흥겨워하고 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 가지고 깨끗이 입스위치로 빠져나갔다. 그리고 밤에 떠난지라, 여관 사람들은 내가 여주인에게 말한 대로 하리치 거룻배를 타고 런던으로 갔다는 것밖에는 아무것도 몰랐다."]], [["I was plagued at Ipswich with the custom-house officers, who stopped my trunk, as I called it, and would open and search it.", "나는 입스위치에서 세관 관리들 때문에 골치를 앓았으니, 그들이 내가 트렁크라 부르던 것을 막아 세우고 열어 뒤지려 했던 것이다."], ["I was willing, I told them, they should search it, but husband had the key, and he was not yet come from Harwich; this I said, that if upon searching it they should find all the things be such as properly belonged to a man rather than a woman, it should not seem strange to them.", "나는 그들에게 뒤져도 좋다고, 다만 열쇠는 남편이 가지고 있는데 아직 하리치에서 오지 않았다고 말했다. 내가 이렇게 말한 것은, 그들이 뒤져서 안에 든 물건들이 모두 여자보다는 남자에게 어울리는 것들임을 발견하더라도 이상하게 여기지 않도록 하기 위함이었다."], ["However, they being positive to open the trunk I consented to have it be broken open, that is to say, to have the lock taken off, which was not difficult.", "그러나 그들이 트렁크를 열겠다고 단호히 나오기에, 나는 그것을 부수어 여는 것에, 다시 말해 자물쇠를 떼어 내는 것에 동의했으니, 그것은 어렵지 않은 일이었다."]], [["They found nothing for their turn, for the trunk had been searched before, but they discovered several things very much to my satisfaction, as particularly a parcel of money in French pistoles, and some Dutch ducatoons or rix-dollars, and the rest was chiefly two periwigs, wearing-linen, and razors, wash-balls, perfumes, and other useful things necessary for a gentleman, which all passed for my husband’s, and so I was quit to them.", "그들은 자기들 목적에 맞는 것은 아무것도 찾지 못했으니, 그 트렁크는 이미 한 번 뒤져진 터였기 때문이다. 그러나 그들은 나로서는 매우 흡족한 여러 물건을 찾아냈는데, 특히 프랑스 피스톨 금화 한 꾸러미와 네덜란드 두카톤화 혹은 릭스달러 약간이 있었고, 나머지는 주로 가발 두 개, 입을 아마포, 면도칼, 비누 덩이, 향수, 그리고 신사에게 필요한 그 밖의 쓸모 있는 물건들이었는데, 그 모두가 내 남편의 것으로 통했고, 그리하여 나는 그들에게서 풀려났다."]], [["It was now very early in the morning, and not light, and I knew not well what course to take; for I made no doubt but I should be pursued in the morning, and perhaps be taken with the things about me; so I resolved upon taking new measures. I went publicly to an inn in the town with my trunk, as I called it, and having taken the substance out, I did not think the lumber of it worth my concern; however, I gave it the landlady of the house with a charge to take great care of it, and lay it up safe till I should come again, and away I walked in to the street.", "이제는 아침 아주 이른 시각이었고 아직 밝지도 않았으며, 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 좋을지 알 수 없었다. 아침이 되면 틀림없이 추격당할 것이고, 어쩌면 물건을 지닌 채 붙잡힐지도 모른다는 생각에 의심의 여지가 없었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 새로운 조처를 취하기로 마음먹었다. 나는 내가 트렁크라 부르던 것을 가지고 보란 듯이 읍내의 어느 여관으로 갔는데, 알맹이는 이미 빼낸 터라 그 거추장스러운 껍데기는 내가 신경 쓸 가치가 없다고 여겼다. 그래도 나는 그것을 여관 여주인에게 맡기며, 매우 잘 간수하여 내가 다시 올 때까지 안전하게 보관해 두라고 단단히 당부하고는, 거리로 걸어 나갔다."]], [["When I was got into the town a great way from the inn, I met with an ancient woman who had just opened her door, and I fell into chat with her, and asked her a great many wild questions of things all remote to my purpose and design; but in my discourse I found by her how the town was situated, that I was in a street that went out towards Hadley, but that such a street went towards the water-side, such a street towards Colchester, and so the London road lay there.", "여관에서 한참 멀리 읍내로 들어갔을 때, 나는 막 문을 연 한 노파를 만나 그녀와 이야기를 나누게 되었고, 내 목적과 의도와는 전혀 동떨어진 여러 가지 엉뚱한 질문을 그녀에게 많이 했다. 그러나 그 이야기 속에서 나는 그녀를 통해 읍내가 어떻게 자리 잡혀 있는지를 알아냈으니, 내가 있는 거리는 해들리 쪽으로 나가는 길이고, 어느 거리는 물가 쪽으로, 어느 거리는 콜체스터 쪽으로 가며, 런던으로 가는 길은 저쪽에 있다는 것이었다."]], [["I had soon my ends of this old woman, for I only wanted to know which was the London road, and away I walked as fast as I could; not that I intended to go on foot, either to London or to Colchester, but I wanted to get quietly away from Ipswich.", "나는 곧 이 노파에게서 내 목적을 이루었으니, 나는 다만 어느 길이 런던으로 가는 길인지를 알고 싶었을 뿐이었고, 그리하여 나는 될 수 있는 한 빨리 걸어갔다. 런던으로든 콜체스터로든 걸어서 갈 작정이었던 것은 아니고, 다만 입스위치에서 조용히 빠져나가고 싶었던 것이다."]], [["I walked about two or three miles, and then I met a plain countryman, who was busy about some husbandry work, I did not know what, and I asked him a great many questions first, not much to the purpose, but at last told him I was going for London, and the coach was full, and I could not get a passage, and asked him if he could tell me where to hire a horse that would carry double, and an honest man to ride before me to Colchester, that so I might get a place there in the coaches.", "나는 이삼 마일쯤 걸어가다가, 무슨 농사일인지는 모르겠으나 어떤 농사일에 분주한 수수한 시골 사람 하나를 만났다. 나는 처음에는 별 요지 없는 질문을 많이 했으나, 마침내 그에게 런던으로 가는 길인데 마차가 다 차서 자리를 얻지 못했다고 말하고, 둘이 탈 만한 말과 콜체스터까지 내 앞에서 말을 몰아 줄 정직한 사람을 어디서 빌릴 수 있을지 일러 줄 수 있는지 물었으니, 그래야 거기서 마차에 자리를 잡을 수 있을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["The honest clown looked earnestly at me, and said nothing for above half a minute, when, scratching his poll, “A horse, say you and to Colchester, to carry double?", "그 정직한 시골뜨기는 나를 골똘히 바라보며 반 분이 넘도록 아무 말도 하지 않다가, 머리를 긁적이며 말했다. \"말이라, 그러니까, 콜체스터까지, 둘이 타고 가게요?"], ["why yes, mistress, alack-a-day, you may have horses enough for money.”", "아 그럼요, 아주머니, 저런, 돈만 있으면 말이야 얼마든지 구하시죠.\" \"그럼요, 여보세요.\""], ["“Well, friend,”", "내가 말했다. \"그건 당연하지요."], ["says I, “that I take for granted; I don’t expect it without money.”", "돈 없이야 바라지도 않아요.\" \"그런데요, 아주머니.\""], ["“Why, but, mistress,”", "그가 말했다. \"얼마나 내실 생각인데요?\""], ["says he, “how much are you willing to give?”", "\"아니.\" 내가 다시 말했다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"여보세요, 여기 시골 삯이 얼마나 되는지 제가 모르거든요."], ["says I again, “friend, I don’t know what your rates are in the country here, for I am a stranger; but if you can get one for me, get it as cheap as you can, and I’ll give you somewhat for your pains.”", "저는 타지 사람이라서요. 그렇지만 저를 위해 하나 구해 주실 수 있다면, 되도록 싸게 구해 주시면, 수고비로 얼마쯤 드릴게요.\""]], [["“Why, that’s honestly said too,”", "\"아, 그것 또한 정직한 말씀이군요.\" 시골 사람이 말했다."], ["says the countryman.", "\"그렇게 정직하지도 않지.\" 나는 속으로 말했다."], ["“Not so honest, neither,”", "\"네가 다 알게 된다면 말이야.\" \"그런데요, 아주머니.\""], ["said I to myself, “if thou knewest all.”", "그가 말했다. \"둘이 탈 만한 말이 한 마리 있는데, 제가 직접 아주머니와 함께 가도 별 상관없어요.\""], ["“Why, mistress,”", "그가 그렇게 말했다. \"그래 주시겠어요?\""], ["says he, “I have a horse that will carry double, and I don’t much care if I go myself with you,”", "내가 말했다. \"아무렴요, 정직한 분 같으니, 그래 주신다면 정말 기쁘겠어요."], ["and the like. “Will you?”", "사례는 후하게 해 드릴게요.\""], ["says I; “well, I believe you are an honest man; if you will, I shall be glad of it; I’ll pay you in reason.”", "\"그럼, 보세요, 아주머니.\" 그가 말했다."], ["“Why, look ye, mistress,”", "\"저도 아주머니께 무리한 셈을 하지는 않을게요."], ["says he, “I won’t be out of reason with you, then; if I carry you to Colchester, it will be worth five shillings for myself and my horse, for I shall hardly come back to-night.”", "콜체스터까지 모셔다드리면, 저랑 제 말 몫으로 오 실링은 받아야겠는데요. 오늘 밤에는 돌아오기 어려울 테니까요.\""]], [["In short, I hired the honest man and his horse; but when we came to a town upon the road (I do not remember the name of it, but it stands upon a river), I pretended myself very ill, and I could go no farther that night but if he would stay there with me, because I was a stranger, I would pay him for himself and his horse with all my heart.", "한마디로, 나는 그 정직한 사람과 그의 말을 빌렸다. 그러나 길에 있는 어느 읍에 이르렀을 때(이름은 기억나지 않으나 강가에 있는 곳이다), 나는 몹시 아픈 척하며 그날 밤은 더 갈 수 없으니, 내가 타지 사람이라 그러니 그가 거기서 나와 함께 머물러 준다면, 그와 그의 말 몫을 기꺼이 치르겠노라고 했다."], ["This I did because I knew the Dutch gentlemen and their servants would be upon the road that day, either in the stagecoaches or riding post, and I did not know but the drunken fellow, or somebody else that might have seen me at Harwich, might see me again, and so I thought that in one day’s stop they would be all gone by.", "내가 이렇게 한 것은, 그 네덜란드 신사들과 그 하인들이 그날 역마차로든 파발마로든 길에 나설 것을 알았기 때문이고, 또 그 취한 사내든, 아니면 하리치에서 나를 본 적이 있을 다른 누군가든 나를 다시 알아볼지 모른다 싶었기 때문이니, 그래서 나는 하루를 멈춰 있으면 그들이 모두 지나가 버리리라 생각한 것이다."]], [["We lay all that night there, and the next morning it was not very early when I set out, so that it was near ten o’clock by the time I got to Colchester.", "우리는 그날 밤을 그곳에서 묵었고, 이튿날 아침 그리 이르지 않게 길을 떠났으므로, 내가 콜체스터에 닿았을 때는 거의 열 시 무렵이었다."], ["It was no little pleasure that I saw the town where I had so many pleasant days, and I made many inquiries after the good old friends I had once had there, but could make little out; they were all dead or removed.", "그토록 즐거운 나날을 보냈던 그 읍을 다시 보게 된 것은 적잖은 기쁨이었고, 나는 한때 그곳에 있었던 좋은 옛 친구들을 여럿 수소문했으나 별 소득이 없었으니, 그들은 모두 죽었거나 떠나 버렸다."], ["The young ladies had been all married or gone to London; the old gentleman and the old lady that had been my early benefactress all dead; and which troubled me most, the young gentleman my first lover, and afterwards my brother-in-law, was dead; but two sons, men grown, were left of him, but they too were transplanted to London.", "젊은 아가씨들은 모두 시집을 갔거나 런던으로 가 버렸고, 일찍이 나의 은인이었던 노신사와 노부인은 다 세상을 떠났으며, 무엇보다 나를 가장 마음 아프게 한 것은, 내 첫 연인이자 훗날 내 시아주버니가 되었던 젊은 신사가 죽었다는 것이었다. 다만 그에게는 다 자란 두 아들이 남아 있었으나, 그들 역시 런던으로 옮겨 가 있었다."]], [["I dismissed my old man here, and stayed incognito for three or four days in Colchester, and then took a passage in a waggon, because I would not venture being seen in the Harwich coaches.", "나는 여기서 늙은이를 돌려보내고, 사나흘 동안 신분을 숨긴 채 콜체스터에 머물렀다가, 하리치 마차에 모습을 드러내는 모험을 하고 싶지 않아 짐마차에 편승했다."], ["But I needed not have used so much caution, for there was nobody in Harwich but the woman of the house could have known me; nor was it rational to think that she, considering the hurry she was in, and that she never saw me but once, and that by candlelight, should have ever discovered me.", "그러나 그렇게까지 조심할 필요는 없었으니, 하리치에서 나를 알아볼 수 있을 사람이라곤 여관집 여자뿐이었고, 또 그녀가 그토록 분주했던 데다 나를 단 한 번, 그것도 촛불 아래서밖에 보지 못한 것을 생각하면, 그녀가 나를 알아보았으리라고 여기는 것도 이치에 맞지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["I was now returned to London, and though by the accident of the last adventure I got something considerable, yet I was not fond of any more country rambles, nor should I have ventured abroad again if I had carried the trade on to the end of my days.", "이제 나는 런던으로 돌아왔고, 비록 지난번 모험의 우연으로 상당한 것을 얻기는 했으나, 더는 시골을 떠돌고 싶은 마음이 없었으며, 설령 죽는 날까지 이 장사를 계속한다 해도 다시는 멀리 나가지 않았을 것이다."], ["I gave my governess a history of my travels; she liked the Harwich journey well enough, and in discoursing of these things between ourselves she observed, that a thief being a creature that watches the advantages of other people’s mistakes, ’tis impossible but that to one that is vigilant and industrious many opportunities must happen, and therefore she thought that one so exquisitely keen in the trade as I was, would scarce fail of something extraordinary wherever I went.", "나는 후견인에게 내 여행담을 들려주었는데, 그녀는 하리치 여정을 꽤 마음에 들어 했고, 우리끼리 이런 일들을 이야기하던 중에 이렇게 말했다. 도둑이란 남의 실수에서 생기는 기회를 노리는 족속이니, 빈틈없고 부지런한 자에게는 숱한 기회가 생길 수밖에 없는 법이고, 따라서 나처럼 이 장사에 그토록 빼어나게 예리한 자라면 어디를 가든 무언가 비상한 것을 놓치는 일이 좀처럼 없으리라는 것이었다."]], [["On the other hand, every branch of my story, if duly considered, may be useful to honest people, and afford a due caution to people of some sort or other to guard against the like surprises, and to have their eyes about them when they have to do with strangers of any kind, for ’tis very seldom that some snare or other is not in their way.", "다른 한편으로, 내 이야기의 가지가지는, 마땅히 헤아려 본다면 정직한 사람들에게 유익할 수 있고, 이런저런 부류의 사람들에게 비슷한 봉변을 막도록, 또 어떤 종류든 낯선 이들을 상대할 때 정신을 차리고 있도록 마땅한 경계를 줄 수 있으니, 그들의 길목에 무슨 올가미가 놓여 있지 않은 경우란 매우 드물기 때문이다."], ["The moral, indeed, of all my history is left to be gathered by the senses and judgment of the reader; I am not qualified to preach to them.", "사실 내 이야기 전체의 교훈은 독자의 분별과 판단으로 거두어 내도록 남겨 두니, 나는 그들에게 설교할 자격이 없다."], ["Let the experience of one creature completely wicked, and completely miserable, be a storehouse of useful warning to those that read.", "완전히 사악하고 완전히 비참했던 한 인간의 경험이, 이 글을 읽는 이들에게 쓸모 있는 경고의 곳간이 되기를 바랄 뿐이다."]], [["I am drawing now towards a new variety of the scenes of life.", "이제 나는 새로운 삶의 장면들로 다가가고 있다."], ["Upon my return, being hardened by a long race of crime, and success unparalleled, at least in the reach of my own knowledge, I had, as I have said, no thoughts of laying down a trade which, if I was to judge by the example of other, must, however, end at last in misery and sorrow.", "돌아온 뒤, 길고 긴 범죄의 행로와 적어도 내가 아는 한 견줄 데 없는 성공으로 모질어진 나는, 앞서 말했듯이, 다른 이들의 본보기로 미루어 판단한다면 결국에는 비참과 슬픔으로 끝날 수밖에 없을 그 장사를 그만둘 생각이 조금도 없었다."]], [["It was on the Christmas day following, in the evening, that, to finish a long train of wickedness, I went abroad to see what might offer in my way; when going by a working silversmith’s in Foster Lane, I saw a tempting bait indeed, and not be resisted by one of my occupation, for the shop had nobody in it, as I could see, and a great deal of loose plate lay in the window, and at the seat of the man, who usually, as I suppose, worked at one side of the shop.", "그 일은 뒤이은 성탄절에, 저녁에 일어났다. 길고 긴 사악함의 행로를 마무리하려는 듯, 나는 내 길에 무엇이 걸려들지 보러 밖으로 나갔는데, 포스터 거리의 어느 은세공장이를 지나다가 참으로 솔깃한 미끼를, 나 같은 직업을 가진 자로서는 도저히 뿌리칠 수 없는 미끼를 보았으니, 내가 보기에 가게 안에는 아무도 없었고, 창가와, 짐작건대 그 사내가 으레 가게 한쪽에서 일하던 자리에 풀려나온 은그릇이 잔뜩 놓여 있었던 것이다."]], [["I went boldly in, and was just going to lay my hand upon a piece of plate, and might have done it, and carried it clear off, for any care that the men who belonged to the shop had taken of it; but an officious fellow in a house, not a shop, on the other side of the way, seeing me go in, and observing that there was nobody in the shop, comes running over the street, and into the shop, and without asking me what I was, or who, seizes upon me, an cries out for the people of the house.", "나는 대담하게 안으로 들어가 막 은그릇 하나에 손을 대려던 참이었고, 가게에 딸린 사내들이 그것을 간수하는 정도로 보아 그렇게 했더라면 그것을 깨끗이 가지고 나올 수도 있었을 것이다. 그러나 길 건너편의, 가게가 아니라 어느 집에 있던 참견 잘하는 사내 하나가 내가 들어가는 것을 보고, 또 가게에 아무도 없는 것을 알아채고는, 길을 가로질러 달려와 가게로 들어와서는, 내가 무엇인지, 누구인지도 묻지 않고 나를 붙들고 집 사람들을 부르며 소리쳤다."]], [["I had not, as I said above, touched anything in the shop, and seeing a glimpse of somebody running over to the shop, I had so much presence of mind as to knock very hard with my foot on the floor of the house, and was just calling out too, when the fellow laid hands on me.", "나는 앞서 말했듯이 가게의 무엇에도 손대지 않았는데, 누군가가 가게로 달려오는 것을 언뜻 보고 정신을 바짝 차려 발로 그 집 바닥을 아주 세게 굴렀고, 막 소리를 지르려던 참에 그 사내가 나를 붙잡은 것이었다."]], [["However, as I had always most courage when I was in most danger, so when the fellow laid hands on me, I stood very high upon it, that I came in to buy half a dozen of silver spoons; and to my good fortune, it was a silversmith’s that sold plate, as well as worked plate for other shops.", "그러나 나는 가장 위험할 때면 늘 가장 용기가 났던지라, 그 사내가 나를 붙잡았을 때 나는 아주 당당하게 버티며, 은수저 여섯 개를 사러 들어왔노라고 우겼다. 그리고 다행히도 그곳은 다른 가게들을 위해 은그릇을 가공할 뿐 아니라 은그릇을 팔기도 하는 은세공장이였다."], ["The fellow laughed at that part, and put such a value upon the service that he had done his neighbour, that he would have it be that I came not to buy, but to steal; and raising a great crowd.", "그 사내는 그 대목을 비웃으며, 자기가 이웃에게 해 준 봉사를 어찌나 대단하게 여기던지, 내가 사러 온 것이 아니라 훔치러 온 것이라고 우겨 대며 큰 군중을 모았다. 나는, 이때쯤 이웃 어딘가에서 불려 온 가게 주인에게, 여기서 소란을 피우고 이 일을 두고 입씨름하는 것은 부질없다고 말했다."], ["I said to the master of the shop, who by this time was fetched home from some neighbouring place, that it was in vain to make noise, and enter into talk there of the case; the fellow had insisted that I came to steal, and he must prove it, and I desired we might go before a magistrate without any more words; for I began to see I should be too hard for the man that had seized me.", "그 사내가 내가 훔치러 왔다고 우겨 대니 그것을 증명해야 할 것이라고, 그러니 더 말할 것 없이 치안판사 앞으로 가자고 했다. 나를 붙잡은 사내쯤은 충분히 감당해 낼 수 있으리라는 것이 보이기 시작했기 때문이다."]], [["The master and mistress of the shop were really not so violent as the man from t’other side of the way; and the man said, “Mistress, you might come into the shop with a good design for aught I know, but it seemed a dangerous thing for you to come into such a shop as mine is, when you see nobody there; and I cannot do justice to my neighbour, who was so kind to me, as not to acknowledge he had reason on his side; though, upon the whole, I do not find you attempted to take anything, and I really know not what to do in it.” I pressed him to go before a magistrate with me, and if anything could be proved on me that was like a design of robbery, I should willingly submit, but if not, I expected reparation.", "가게의 주인 내외는 길 건너편에서 온 사내만큼 사납지는 정말로 않았다. 그 사내가 말했다. \"아주머니, 댁이 무슨 좋은 뜻으로 가게에 들어왔을 수도 있겠지요, 내가 어찌 알겠소. 하지만 아무도 없는 게 빤히 보이는 우리 같은 가게에 들어온 것은 댁한테 위태로운 일로 보였단 말이오. 또 내게 그렇게 친절을 베푼 이웃에게, 그 사람 쪽에 일리가 있었다는 걸 인정하지 않는다면 도리가 아니지요. 하기야 전체로 보아 댁이 무얼 가져가려 한 건 아닌 듯하니, 나도 정말이지 이 일을 어찌해야 할지 모르겠소.\" 나는 그에게 나와 함께 치안판사 앞으로 가자고 다그쳤고, 만약 내게서 강도질의 의도 비슷한 무엇이라도 증명될 수 있다면 기꺼이 따르겠으나, 그렇지 않다면 배상을 받아야겠다고 했다."]], [["Just while we were in this debate, and a crowd of people gathered about the door, came by Sir T.", "우리가 이렇게 다투고 있고 군중이 문간에 모여들고 있는 바로 그때, 시市의 참사회원이자 치안판사인 T."], ["B., an alderman of the city, and justice of the peace, and the goldsmith hearing of it, goes out, and entreated his worship to come in and decide the case.", "B. 경이 지나갔는데, 금세공인이 그 소식을 듣고 나가서, 들어오셔서 이 일을 가려 주십사고 그 어른께 간청했다."]], [["Give the goldsmith his due, he told his story with a great deal of justice and moderation, and the fellow that had come over, and seized upon me, told his with as much heat and foolish passion, which did me good still, rather than harm.", "금세공인에게 공정을 기하자면, 그는 자기 이야기를 매우 공정하고 절도 있게 풀어냈고, 길을 건너와 나를 붙잡았던 사내는 자기 이야기를 그만큼 격하고 어리석은 격정으로 늘어놓았는데, 그것은 도리어 내게 해가 아니라 득이 되었다."], ["It came then to my turn to speak, and I told his worship that I was a stranger in London, being newly come out of the north; that I lodged in such a place, that I was passing this street, and went into the goldsmith’s shop to buy half a dozen of spoons.", "그러고는 내가 말할 차례가 되어, 나는 그 어른께, 나는 런던에서는 이방인으로 북쪽 지방에서 막 올라온 사람인데, 어느 곳에 묵고 있다고, 이 거리를 지나다가 은수저 여섯 개를 사려고 금세공인의 가게로 들어갔노라고 말했다."], ["By great luck I had an old silver spoon in my pocket, which I pulled out, and told him I had carried that spoon to match it with half a dozen of new ones, that it might match some I had in the country.", "천만다행으로 내게는 호주머니에 낡은 은수저가 하나 있었으니, 나는 그것을 꺼내며, 시골에 있는 내 수저들과 짝이 맞도록 새것 여섯 개와 맞춰 보려고 그 수저를 가지고 다녔노라고 말했다."], ["That seeing nobody I the shop, I knocked with my foot very hard to make the people hear, and had also called aloud with my voice; ’tis true, there was loose plate in the shop, but that nobody could say I had touched any of it, or gone near it; that a fellow came running into the shop out of the street, and laid hands on me in a furious manner, in the very moments while I was calling for the people of the house; that if he had really had a mind to have done his neighbour any service, he should have stood at a distance, and silently watched to see whether I had touched anything or no, and then have clapped in upon me, and taken me in the fact.", "가게에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 사람들이 듣도록 발을 아주 세게 굴렀고, 또 큰 소리로 부르기까지 했다고, 가게에 풀려나온 은그릇이 있었던 것은 사실이나 내가 그 어느 것에 손을 댔다거나 가까이 갔다고는 아무도 말할 수 없을 것이라고, 그런데 한 사내가 길에서 가게로 달려 들어와, 내가 바로 집 사람들을 부르고 있던 그 순간에 사납게 나를 붙들었다고 말했다. 만약 그가 정말로 이웃에게 무슨 도움을 줄 마음이었다면, 멀찍이 서서 내가 무엇에 손을 대는지 아닌지를 조용히 지켜보다가, 그런 다음에 나를 덮쳐 현장에서 붙잡았어야 했을 것이라고 했다."], ["“That is very true,” says Mr.", "\"그것은 매우 옳은 말이오.\""], ["Alderman, and turning to the fellow that stopped me, he asked him if it was true that I knocked with my foot?", "참사회원께서 말씀하시고는, 나를 막아 세운 사내를 돌아보며, 내가 발을 굴렀다는 것이 사실인지 물었다."], ["He said, yes, I had knocked, but that might be because of his coming.", "그는 그렇다고, 내가 발을 굴렀다고 했으나, 그것은 자기가 다가왔기 때문일 수도 있다고 했다."], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니.\""], ["says the alderman, taking him short, “now you contradict yourself, for just now you said she was in the shop with her back to you, and did not see you till you came upon her.”", "참사회원께서 그의 말을 가로채며 말씀하셨다. \"이제 자네가 스스로 모순되는 말을 하는군."], ["Now it was true that my back was partly to the street, but yet as my business was of a kind that required me to have my eyes every way, so I really had a glance of him running over, as I said before, though he did not perceive it.", "방금 자네는 저 부인이 자네에게 등을 돌린 채 가게 안에 있어서, 자네가 들이닥칠 때까지 자네를 보지 못했다고 하지 않았나.\" 그런데 내 등이 거리 쪽으로 어느 정도 향해 있었던 것은 사실이나, 내 일이 사방으로 눈을 두어야 하는 종류의 것인지라, 앞서 말했듯이 나는 정말로 그가 달려오는 것을 흘긋 보았으니, 다만 그가 그것을 알아채지 못했을 뿐이다."]], [["After a full hearing, the alderman gave it as his opinion that his neighbour was under a mistake, and that I was innocent, and the goldsmith acquiesced in it too, and his wife, and so I was dismissed; but as I was going to depart, Mr.", "충분한 심리 끝에 참사회원께서는, 그 이웃이 착오를 범한 것이며 나는 무죄라는 견해를 내놓으셨고, 금세공인도, 그의 아내도 그에 수긍하여, 그리하여 나는 풀려났다. 그러나 내가 막 떠나려 하는데 참사회원께서 말씀하셨다."], ["Alderman said, “But hold, madam, if you were designing to buy spoons, I hope you will not let my friend here lose his customer by the mistake.”", "\"그런데 잠깐, 부인, 만약 부인이 수저를 살 작정이셨다면, 이 착오 때문에 여기 내 친구가 손님을 놓치게 하지는 않으시리라 믿소.\" 나는 선뜻 대답했다."], ["I readily answered, “No, sir, I’ll buy the spoons still, if he can match my odd spoon, which I brought for a pattern”; and the goldsmith showed me some of the very same fashion.", "\"네, 그럼요, 본보기로 가져온 제 짝 안 맞는 수저에 맞춰 줄 수만 있다면 그래도 수저를 사겠어요.\" 그러자 금세공인이 바로 그 같은 모양의 수저 몇 개를 보여 주었다."], ["So he weighed the spoons, and they came to five-and-thirty shillings, so I pulls out my purse to pay him, in which I had near twenty guineas, for I never went without such a sum about me, whatever might happen, and I found it of use at other times as well as now.", "그래서 그가 수저의 무게를 다니 서른다섯 실링이 나왔고, 나는 값을 치르려고 지갑을 꺼냈는데, 거기에는 스무 기니 가까이 들어 있었으니, 나는 무슨 일이 있을지 몰라 늘 그만한 돈을 지니고 다녔고, 지금뿐 아니라 다른 때에도 그것이 쓸모 있음을 알았기 때문이다."]], [["When Mr.", "내 돈을 보고 참사회원께서 말씀하셨다."], ["Alderman saw my money, he said, “Well, madam, now I am satisfied you were wronged, and it was for this reason that I moved you should buy the spoons, and stayed till you had bought them, for if you had not had money to pay for them, I should have suspected that you did not come into the shop with an intent to buy, for indeed the sort of people who come upon these designs that you have been charged with, are seldom troubled with much gold in their pockets, as I see you are.”", "\"자, 부인, 이제 나는 부인이 부당한 일을 당했다고 확신하오. 내가 부인더러 수저를 사라고 권하고, 사실 때까지 머무른 것도 바로 이런 까닭이었소."], ["I smiled, and told his worship, that then I owed something of his favour to my money, but I hoped he saw reason also in the justice he had done me before.", "만약 부인에게 값을 치를 돈이 없었다면, 나는 부인이 살 마음으로 가게에 들어온 것이 아니라고 의심했을 것이오. 사실 부인이 뒤집어쓸 뻔한 그런 짓을 꾸미고 다니는 부류의 사람들은, 내가 보는 부인과는 달리 호주머니에 금화가 그리 많은 법이 좀처럼 없으니까요.\""], ["He said, yes, he had, but this had confirmed his opinion, and he was fully satisfied now of my having been injured.", "나는 미소를 지으며 그 어른께, 그렇다면 제가 받은 호의의 얼마쯤은 제 돈 덕분이로군요, 하지만 그 전에 제게 베풀어 주신 공정함에도 일리가 있음을 보셨기를 바란다고 말했다."], ["So I came off with flying colours, though from an affair in which I was at the very brink of destruction.", "그는 그렇다고, 그러나 이번 일이 자기 견해를 굳혀 주었고, 이제는 내가 부당한 일을 당했음을 충분히 확신한다고 했다. 그리하여 나는, 파멸의 벼랑 끝에 섰던 일에서 보란 듯이 빠져나왔다."]], [["It was but three days after this, that not at all made cautious by my former danger, as I used to be, and still pursuing the art which I had so long been employed in, I ventured into a house where I saw the doors open, and furnished myself, as I though verily without being perceived, with two pieces of flowered silks, such as they call brocaded silk, very rich.", "이 일이 있은 지 겨우 사흘 뒤, 나는 예전의 위험으로도 평소처럼 조금도 조심해지지 않은 채, 그토록 오래 종사해 온 그 기술을 여전히 좇아, 문이 열려 있는 것을 본 어느 집으로 들어갔고, 정말이지 들키지 않았다고 믿으며, 그들이 비단 능직이라 부르는 매우 값진 꽃무늬 비단 두 필을 챙겼다."], ["It was not a mercer’s shop, nor a warehouse of a mercer, but looked like a private dwelling-house, and was, it seems, inhabited by a man that sold goods for the weavers to the mercers, like a broker or factor.", "그곳은 포목상도 아니고 포목상의 창고도 아니었으며, 개인 살림집처럼 보였고, 또 그렇게 보아하니 직조공들의 물건을 포목상들에게 팔아 주는, 중개인이나 거간 같은 자가 사는 집이었다."]], [["That I may make short of this black part of this story, I was attacked by two wenches that came open-mouthed at me just as I was going out at the door, and one of them pulled me back into the room, while the other shut the door upon me.", "이 이야기의 이 어두운 부분을 짧게 줄이자면, 내가 막 문으로 나서려는데 두 계집이 입에 거품을 물고 내게 달려들어, 하나는 나를 방 안으로 끌어들이고 다른 하나는 내게 문을 닫아 버렸다. 나는 좋은 말로 달래려 했으나 그럴 틈이 없었으니, 불을 뿜는 두 마리 용도 그들보다 더 사납지는 못했을 것이다."], ["I would have given them good words, but there was no room for it, two fiery dragons could not have been more furious than they were; they tore my clothes, bullied and roared as if they would have murdered me; the mistress of the house came next, and then the master, and all outrageous, for a while especially.", "그들은 내 옷을 찢고, 마치 나를 죽이려는 듯이 윽박지르며 고래고래 소리를 질렀다. 다음으로 집 안주인이 왔고, 그다음에 주인이 왔으며, 특히 한동안은 모두가 길길이 날뛰었다."]], [["I gave the master very good words, told him the door was open, and things were a temptation to me, that I was poor and distressed, and poverty was when many could not resist, and begged him with tears to have pity on me.", "나는 주인에게 아주 좋은 말로, 문이 열려 있었고 물건들이 내게 유혹이 되었다고, 나는 가난하고 곤궁한데 가난이란 많은 이들이 떨치지 못하는 것이라고 말하며, 눈물을 흘리며 나를 가엾이 여겨 달라고 그에게 빌었다."], ["The mistress of the house was moved with compassion, and inclined to have let me go, and had almost persuaded her husband to it also, but the saucy wenches were run, even before they were sent, and had fetched a constable, and then the master said he could not go back, I must go before a justice, and answered his wife that he might come into trouble himself if he should let me go.", "집 안주인은 동정심이 일어 나를 보내 주려는 쪽으로 기울었고, 자기 남편도 거의 그렇게 설득할 뻔했으나, 그 건방진 계집들이 시키지도 않았는데 벌써 달려 나가 순경을 데려왔으니, 그러자 주인은, 이제 돌이킬 수 없게 되었다고, 나를 치안판사 앞으로 데려가야 한다고 말하며, 자기가 나를 보내 준다면 자기가 곤경에 빠질 수도 있노라고 아내에게 대답했다."]], [["The sight of the constable, indeed, struck me with terror, and I thought I should have sunk into the ground.", "순경을 본 것은 정말이지 나를 공포로 찔렀고, 나는 땅속으로 꺼져 들어갈 것만 같았다."], ["I fell into faintings, and indeed the people themselves thought I would have died, when the woman argued again for me, and entreated her husband, seeing they had lost nothing, to let me go.", "나는 까무러쳐 쓰러졌고, 정말이지 그 집 사람들마저 내가 죽으려나 보다고 여겼는데, 그러자 그 여자가 다시 나를 위해 변호하며, 잃은 것이 없으니 나를 보내 주자고 자기 남편에게 간청했다."], ["I offered him to pay for the two pieces, whatever the value was, though I had not got them, and argued that as he had his goods, and had really lost nothing, it would be cruel to pursue me to death, and have my blood for the bare attempt of taking them.", "나는, 비록 그 물건들을 가지지는 못했으나 그 값이 얼마든 그 두 필 값을 치르겠다고 그에게 제의하면서, 그는 물건을 도로 가졌고 정말로 잃은 것이 없으니, 그것들을 가져가려고 한 단순한 시도 때문에 나를 죽음으로 몰아 내 피를 보겠다는 것은 잔인한 일이라고 따졌다."], ["I put the constable in mind that I had broke no doors, nor carried anything away; and when I came to the justice, and pleaded there that I had neither broken anything to get in, nor carried anything out, the justice was inclined to have released me; but the first saucy jade that stopped me, affirming that I was going out with the goods, but that she stopped me and pulled me back as I was upon the threshold, the justice upon that point committed me, and I was carried to Newgate.", "나는 순경에게, 내가 문을 부수지도, 아무것도 가지고 나가지도 않았음을 일깨웠고, 치안판사 앞에 이르러 거기서 내가 들어오려고 무엇을 부수지도, 아무것도 가지고 나오지도 않았다고 항변하자, 치안판사는 나를 풀어 주려는 쪽으로 기울었다. 그러나 처음 나를 막아 세웠던 그 건방진 계집이, 내가 물건을 가지고 나가고 있었는데 자기가 나를 막아 세워 문지방을 막 넘으려는 나를 도로 끌어들였노라고 단언하자, 치안판사는 그 점을 두고 나를 구류하였고, 나는 뉴게이트로 끌려갔다."], ["That horrid place!", "그 끔찍한 곳! 그 이름을 입에 올리기만 해도 내 피가 다 얼어붙는다."], ["my very blood chills at the mention of its name; the place where so many of my comrades had been locked up, and from whence they went to the fatal tree; the place where my mother suffered so deeply, where I was brought into the world, and from whence I expected no redemption but by an infamous death: to conclude, the place that had so long expected me, and which with so much art and success I had so long avoided.", "내 동료들 가운데 그토록 많은 이가 갇혀 있었고, 거기서 운명의 나무로 끌려갔던 곳, 내 어머니가 그토록 깊은 고초를 겪었고, 내가 세상에 태어났으며, 거기서 나로서는 치욕스러운 죽음 외에는 아무 구원도 기대할 수 없었던 곳. 결론을 짓자면, 그토록 오랫동안 나를 기다려 왔고, 내가 그토록 많은 술수와 성공으로 그토록 오래 피해 왔던 그곳이었다."]], [["I was not fixed indeed; ’tis impossible to describe the terror of my mind, when I was first brought in, and when I looked around upon all the horrors of that dismal place.", "나는 정말이지 마음을 다잡지 못했다. 처음 그곳에 끌려들었을 때, 또 그 음산한 곳의 온갖 끔찍한 것을 둘러보았을 때 내 마음의 공포는 말로 다 할 수 없다."], ["I looked on myself as lost, and that I had nothing to think of but of going out of the world, and that with the utmost infamy: the hellish noise, the roaring, swearing, and clamour, the stench and nastiness, and all the dreadful crowd of afflicting things that I saw there, joined together to make the place seem an emblem of hell itself, and a kind of an entrance into it.", "나는 나 자신을 끝장난 존재로 여겼고, 이제는 세상을 떠나는 것밖에는, 그것도 더할 나위 없는 치욕 속에 떠나는 것밖에는 생각할 것이 없다고 여겼다. 지옥 같은 소음, 고함, 욕설과 아우성, 악취와 더러움, 그리고 거기서 내가 본 마음을 짓누르는 온갖 무시무시한 무리—이 모든 것이 한데 합쳐져 그곳을 지옥 그 자체의 표상이요, 일종의 지옥으로 들어가는 입구처럼 보이게 했다."]], [["Now I reproached myself with the many hints I had had, as I have mentioned above, from my own reason, from the sense of my good circumstances, and of the many dangers I had escaped, to leave off while I was well, and how I had withstood them all, and hardened my thoughts against all fear.", "이제 나는, 앞서 말했듯이 내 자신의 이성에서, 또 내 좋은 형편을 헤아리던 데서, 또 내가 빠져나온 숱한 위험을 떠올리던 데서 형편이 좋을 때 그만두라는 숱한 암시를 받았건만, 어떻게 그 모두를 물리치고 온갖 두려움에 맞서 내 마음을 굳혀 왔던가를 자책했다."], ["It seemed to me that I was hurried on by an inevitable and unseen fate to this day of misery, and that now I was to expiate all my offences at the gallows; that I was now to give satisfaction to justice with my blood, and that I was come to the last hour of my life and of my wickedness together.", "마치 내가 피할 수 없는 보이지 않는 운명에 떠밀려 이 비참의 날에 이른 것만 같았고, 이제는 교수대에서 내 모든 죄를 속죄해야 하며, 이제 내 피로 정의에 보상해야 하고, 내 삶과 내 사악함의 마지막 시각에 함께 다다른 것만 같았다."], ["These things poured themselves in upon my thoughts in a confused manner, and left me overwhelmed with melancholy and despair.", "이런 것들이 어지러이 내 생각 속으로 쏟아져 들어와, 나를 우울과 절망에 짓눌린 채로 남겨 두었다."]], [["Them I repented heartily of all my life past, but that repentance yielded me no satisfaction, no peace, no, not in the least, because, as I said to myself, it was repenting after the power of further sinning was taken away.", "그때 나는 지난 내 삶 전체를 진심으로 뉘우쳤으나, 그 뉘우침은 내게 아무런 흡족함도, 아무런 평안도 주지 못했으니, 조금도 그러지 못했다. 왜냐하면 내가 스스로에게 말했듯이, 그것은 더 죄지을 힘이 빼앗긴 뒤의 뉘우침이었기 때문이다."], ["I seemed not to mourn that I had committed such crimes, and for the fact as it was an offence against God and my neighbour, but I mourned that I was to be punished for it.", "나는 그런 죄를 저질렀다는 것을, 또 그 행위가 하느님과 내 이웃에 대한 죄였다는 점을 슬퍼하는 것이 아니라, 그 때문에 벌받게 되었다는 것을 슬퍼하는 듯했다."], ["I was a penitent, as I thought, not that I had sinned, but that I was to suffer, and this took away all the comfort, and even the hope of my repentance in my own thoughts.", "내 생각에 나는, 죄지은 것을 뉘우치는 참회자가 아니라, 벌받게 된 것을 뉘우치는 참회자였고, 이것이 내 마음속에서 뉘우침의 모든 위안을, 아니 그 희망마저 앗아 가 버렸다."]], [["I got no sleep for several nights or days after I came into that wretched place, and glad I would have been for some time to have died there, though I did not consider dying as it ought to be considered neither; indeed, nothing could be filled with more horror to my imagination than the very place, nothing was more odious to me than the company that was there.", "그 비참한 곳에 들어온 뒤로 여러 밤, 여러 날 동안 나는 잠을 이루지 못했고, 한동안은 차라리 거기서 죽었으면 좋겠다는 마음이었으나, 그러면서도 죽음을 마땅히 헤아려야 할 만큼 헤아린 것도 아니었다. 정말이지 내 상상에 그곳보다 더 공포로 가득한 것은 없었고, 거기 있던 무리보다 더 역겨운 것도 없었다."], ["Oh!", "아!"], ["if I had but been sent to any place in the world, and not to Newgate, I should have thought myself happy.", "세상 그 어디로든, 뉴게이트만 아닌 곳으로 보내졌더라면, 나는 나 자신을 행복하게 여겼으련만."]], [["In the next place, how did the hardened wretches that were there before me triumph over me!", "다음으로, 나보다 먼저 그곳에 있던 그 모진 인간들이 얼마나 나를 두고 의기양양해했던가!"], ["What!", "아니!"], ["Mrs. Flanders come to Newgate at last?", "플랜더스 부인이 마침내 뉴게이트에 들어왔다고?"], ["What! Mrs.", "아니!"], ["Mary, Mrs. Molly, and after that plain Moll Flanders?", "메리 부인이, 몰리 부인이, 그러고도 그저 몰 플랜더스가?"], ["They thought the devil had helped me, they said, that I had reigned so long; they expected me there many years ago, and was I come at last?", "그들은, 내가 그토록 오래 활개를 쳤으니 악마가 나를 도왔던 것이라고들 했다. 그들은 여러 해 전부터 나를 거기서 기다렸는데, 마침내 내가 왔느냐는 것이었다."], ["Then they flouted me with my dejections, welcomed me to the place, wished me joy, bid me have a good heart, not to be cast down, things might not be so bad as I feared, and the like; then called for brandy, and drank to me, but put it all up to my score, for they told me I was but just come to the college, as they called it, and sure I had money in my pocket, though they had none.", "그러고는 내 풀 죽은 모습을 두고 나를 놀려 대고, 이곳에 온 것을 환영하며, 잘 왔다고 인사하고, 기운을 내라고, 풀 죽지 말라고, 일이 내가 두려워하는 것만큼 나쁘지 않을 수도 있다는 둥 말했다. 그러고는 브랜디를 청해 내게 건배했으나, 그것을 모두 내 외상으로 달아 놓았으니, 그들 말로는 내가 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 이제 막 이 대학에 들어왔으니, 자기들에게는 없어도 내게는 분명 호주머니에 돈이 있으리라는 것이었다."]], [["I asked one of this crew how long she had been there.", "나는 이 패거리 가운데 하나에게 거기 온 지 얼마나 되었느냐고 물었다."], ["She said four months.", "그녀는 넉 달이라고 했다."], ["I asked her how the place looked to her when she first came into it.", "나는 그녀에게, 처음 들어왔을 때 이곳이 그녀에게 어떻게 보였느냐고 물었다. \"지금 댁한테 보이는 그대로였지.\""], ["“Just as it did now to you,”", "그녀가 말했다. \"무섭고 끔찍했어.\""], ["says she, “dreadful and frightful”; that she thought she was in hell; “and I believe so still,”", "자기가 지옥에 와 있다고 여겼다는 것이다. \"지금도 그렇게 믿고 있지.\""], ["adds she, “but it is natural to me now, I don’t disturb myself about it.”", "그녀가 덧붙였다. \"하지만 이젠 그게 자연스러워서, 나는 그걸로 마음 쓰지 않아.\""], ["“I suppose,”", "\"그럼 짐작건대.\""], ["says I, “you are in no danger of what is to follow?”", "내가 말했다. \"댁은 앞으로 닥칠 일에 대해서는 위험이 없는 거예요?\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니.\""], ["says she, “for you are mistaken there, I assure you, for I am under sentence, only I pleaded my belly, but I am no more with child than the judge that tried me, and I expect to be called down next sessions.”", "그녀가 말했다. \"그건 댁이 잘못 안 거야, 정말이지."], ["This “calling down”", "나는 이미 선고를 받았는데, 다만 임신을 사유로 호소했을 뿐이거든."], ["is calling down to their former judgment, when a woman has been respited for her belly, but proves not to be with child, or if she has been with child, and has been brought to bed.", "그렇지만 나는 나를 재판한 판사만큼이나 임신한 게 아니라서, 다음 개정기에 다시 불려 나가게 될 거야.\" 이 '불려 나간다'는 것은, 여자가 임신을 사유로 형 집행이 유예되었다가 임신한 것이 아닌 것으로 드러나거나, 혹은 임신했다 해도 이미 해산한 경우에, 앞서의 판결을 받으러 다시 불려 내려가는 것을 말한다."], ["“Well,”", "\"그래.\""], ["says I, “are you thus easy?”", "내가 말했다. \"그런데 그렇게 마음이 편해요?\""], ["“Ay,”", "\"그럼.\""], ["says she, “I can’t help myself; what signifies being sad?", "그녀가 말했다."], ["If I am hanged, there’s an end of me,”", "\"달리 어쩌겠어. 슬퍼해 봤자 무슨 소용이야?"], ["says she; and away she turns dancing, and sings as she goes the following piece of Newgate wit—", "교수형을 당하면, 그걸로 내 끝장인걸.\" 그녀가 말하고는, 춤추며 돌아서서 가면서 다음과 같은 뉴게이트 식 익살 한 토막을 노래했다."]], [["“If I swing by the string, I shall hear the bell ring, And then there’s an end of poor Jenny.”", "\"내가 밧줄에 매달려 흔들리면, 종소리가 들려오겠지, 그러면 가엾은 제니도 그걸로 끝장이라네.\""]], [["I mention this because it would be worth the observation of any prisoner, who shall hereafter fall into the same misfortune, and come to that dreadful place of Newgate, how time, necessity, and conversing with the wretches that are there familiarizes the place to them; how at last they become reconciled to that which at first was the greatest dread upon their spirits in the world, and are as impudently cheerful and merry in their misery as they were when out of it.", "내가 이것을 언급하는 까닭은, 훗날 이와 같은 불운에 빠져 뉴게이트라는 그 무시무시한 곳에 오게 될 어떤 죄수에게든, 시간과 필요와 거기 있는 인간들과의 어울림이 어떻게 그곳을 그들에게 익숙하게 만드는지, 처음에는 세상에서 그들의 정신을 가장 짓누르던 것에 어떻게 마침내 그들이 길들여지는지, 또 거기서 벗어나 있을 때 그러했던 만큼이나 그 비참 속에서도 어떻게 뻔뻔스럽게 명랑하고 흥겨워하는지를 눈여겨볼 만한 일이기 때문이다."]], [["I cannot say, as some do, this devil is not so black as he is painted; for indeed no colours can represent the place to the life, not any soul conceive aright of it but those who have been sufferers there.", "나는 더러 사람들이 그러듯 이 악마가 그려진 것만큼 그리 검지는 않다고는 말할 수 없으니, 정말이지 어떤 색채로도 그곳을 실제 그대로 그려 낼 수 없고, 거기서 고초를 겪어 본 이들 외에는 어떤 영혼도 그것을 올바로 헤아릴 수 없기 때문이다."], ["But how hell should become by degree so natural, and not only tolerable, but even agreeable, is a thing unintelligible but by those who have experienced it, as I have.", "그러나 지옥이 어떻게 차츰 그토록 자연스러워지고, 견딜 만할 뿐 아니라 심지어 흡족해지기까지 하는지는, 나처럼 그것을 겪어 본 이들 외에는 이해할 수 없는 일이다."]], [["The same night that I was sent to Newgate, I sent the news of it to my old governess, who was surprised at it, you may be sure, and spent the night almost as ill out of Newgate, as I did in it.", "내가 뉴게이트로 보내진 바로 그날 밤, 나는 그 소식을 내 늙은 후견인에게 알렸는데, 여러분도 짐작하다시피 그녀는 그 소식에 놀랐고, 내가 뉴게이트 안에서 그러했던 만큼이나 뉴게이트 밖에서 그날 밤을 거의 비참하게 보냈다."]], [["The next morning she came to see me; she did what she could to comfort me, but she saw that was to no purpose; however, as she said, to sink under the weight was but to increase the weight; she immediately applied herself to all the proper methods to prevent the effects of it, which we feared, and first she found out the two fiery jades that had surprised me. She tampered with them, offered them money, and, in a word, tried all imaginable ways to prevent a prosecution; she offered one of the wenches £100 to go away from her mistress, and not to appear against me, but she was so resolute, that though she was but a servant maid at £3 a year wages or thereabouts, she refused it, and would have refused it, as my governess said she believed, if she had offered her £500. Then she attacked the other maid; she was not so hard-hearted in appearance as the other, and sometimes seemed inclined to be merciful; but the first wench kept her up, and changed her mind, and would not so much as let my governess talk with her, but threatened to have her up for tampering with the evidence.", "이튿날 아침 그녀가 나를 보러 왔다. 그녀는 나를 위로하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다했으나, 그것이 부질없음을 알았다. 그러나 그녀의 말마따나 그 무게에 짓눌려 주저앉는 것은 그 무게를 더하는 것일 뿐이었다. 그녀는 곧장 우리가 두려워하던 그 결과를 막기 위한 온갖 적절한 방도에 매달렸는데, 먼저 나를 봉변에 빠뜨린 그 사나운 두 계집을 찾아냈다. 그녀는 그들을 구워삶고, 돈을 내밀며, 한마디로 기소를 막을 온갖 상상할 수 있는 수단을 다 써 보았다. 그녀는 그 계집들 가운데 하나에게 자기 여주인을 떠나 나를 거슬러 나서지 말라고 백 파운드를 제의했으나, 그녀는 어찌나 단호하던지, 연봉이 삼 파운드 남짓한 한낱 하녀에 불과한데도 그것을 거절했으니, 내 후견인이 믿기로는, 설령 오백 파운드를 제의했더라도 거절했으리라는 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 다른 하녀를 공략했다. 그녀는 겉보기에 다른 쪽만큼 모질지는 않았고, 때로는 자비를 베풀 마음이 있는 듯도 했다. 그러나 첫 번째 계집이 그녀를 부추겨 마음을 돌려놓았고, 내 후견인이 그녀와 이야기하는 것조차 못 하게 했을 뿐 아니라, 증거에 손을 댄다며 그녀를 고발하겠다고 으름장을 놓았다."]], [["Then she applied to the master, that is to say, the man whose goods had been stolen, and particularly to his wife, who, as I told you, was inclined at first to have some compassion for me; she found the woman the same still, but the man alleged he was bound by the justice that committed me, to prosecute, and that he should forfeit his recognisance.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 주인에게, 다시 말해 물건을 도둑맞은 사내에게, 특히 앞서 여러분에게 말했듯이 처음에 나를 다소 가엾이 여기는 쪽으로 기울었던 그의 아내에게 접근했다. 그녀는 그 여자가 여전히 한결같음을 알았으나, 남자는 자기를 구류한 치안판사에게 매여 기소를 해야 하며, 그렇지 않으면 자기 보석 보증금을 잃게 된다고 주장했다."]], [["My governess offered to find friends that should get his recognisances off of the file, as they call it, and that he should not suffer; but it was not possible to convince him that could be done, or that he could be safe any way in the world but by appearing against me; so I was to have three witnesses of fact against me, the master and his two maids; that is to say, I was as certain to be cast for my life as I was certain that I was alive, and I had nothing to do but to think of dying, and prepare for it.", "내 후견인은, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 그의 보석 보증서를 서류철에서 빼내, 그가 손해를 보지 않도록 해 줄 친구들을 찾아 주겠노라고 제의했다. 그러나 그가 그렇게 될 수 있다는 것을, 혹은 나를 거슬러 나서는 것 외에는 세상 어떤 방법으로도 자기가 안전할 수 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기란 불가능했다."], ["I had but a sad foundation to build upon, as I said before, for all my repentance appeared to me to be only the effect of my fear of death, not a sincere regret for the wicked life that I had lived, and which had brought this misery upon me, for the offending my Creator, who was now suddenly to be my judge.", "그리하여 나는 사실관계의 증인 셋을, 곧 주인과 그의 두 하녀를 거스르는 증인으로 두게 되었으니, 다시 말해 나는 내가 살아 있다는 것이 확실한 만큼이나 목숨을 빼앗기는 선고를 받으리라는 것이 확실했고, 죽음을 생각하고 그것을 준비하는 것 외에는 할 일이 없었다. 앞서 말했듯이 나는 의지할 슬픈 토대밖에 가지지 못했으니, 내 모든 뉘우침이 내게는 그저 죽음에 대한 두려움의 결과로만 보였을 뿐, 내가 살아온 사악한 삶에 대한, 그리고 이 비참을 내게 안긴 그 삶에 대한, 또 이제 갑자기 나의 심판자가 되실 나의 창조주를 거스른 데 대한 진정한 회한은 아니었기 때문이다."]], [["I lived many days here under the utmost horror of soul; I had death, as it were, in view, and thought of nothing night and day, but of gibbets and halters, evil spirits and devils; it is not to be expressed by words how I was harassed, between the dreadful apprehensions of death and the terror of my conscience reproaching me with my past horrible life.", "나는 여기서 여러 날을 영혼의 더할 나위 없는 공포 속에 살았다. 나는 말하자면 죽음을 눈앞에 두고 있었고, 밤낮으로 교수대와 올가미, 악령과 악마 외에는 아무것도 생각하지 않았다. 죽음에 대한 무시무시한 두려움과, 내 지난 끔찍한 삶을 책망하는 양심의 공포 사이에서 내가 얼마나 시달렸는지는 말로 표현할 수 없다."]], [["The ordinary of Newgate came to me, and talked a little in his way, but all his divinity ran upon confessing my crime, as he called it (though he knew not what I was in for), making a full discovery, and the like, without which he told me God would never forgive me; and he said so little to the purpose, that I had no manner of consolation from him; and then to observe the poor creature preaching confession and repentance to me in the morning, and find him drunk with brandy and spirits by noon, this had something in it so shocking, that I began to nauseate the man more than his work, and his work too by degrees, for the sake of the man; so that I desired him to trouble me no more.", "뉴게이트의 교무 사제가 내게 와서 그 나름의 방식으로 잠시 이야기했으나, 그의 모든 신학은 그가 부르는 대로 하자면 내 죄를(비록 그는 내가 무슨 죄로 들어왔는지도 몰랐지만) 고백하고, 낱낱이 털어놓는 따위에 매여 있었으니, 그렇게 하지 않으면 하느님께서 결코 나를 용서하지 않으시리라고 그가 내게 말했다. 그가 요지에 닿는 말을 하도 적게 하는 통에, 나는 그에게서 아무런 위안도 얻지 못했다. 게다가 이 가엾은 자가 아침에는 내게 고백과 회개를 설교하는 것을 보다가, 정오에는 브랜디며 독한 술에 취해 있는 꼴을 보니, 거기에는 어찌나 충격적인 데가 있던지, 나는 그자의 일보다 그자를 더 역겨워하기 시작했고, 그자 때문에 차츰 그자의 일까지도 역겨워졌다. 그래서 나는 그에게 더는 나를 성가시게 하지 말아 달라고 했다."]], [["I know not how it was, but by the indefatigable application of my diligent governess I had no bill preferred against me the first sessions, I mean to the grand jury, at Guildhall; so I had another month or five weeks before me, and without doubt this ought to have been accepted by me, as so much time given me for reflection upon what was past, and preparation for what was to come; or, in a word, I ought to have esteemed it as a space given me for repentance, and have employed it as such, but it was not in me.", "어찌 된 일인지 모르겠으나, 부지런한 내 후견인의 지칠 줄 모르는 진력 덕분에 나는 첫 개정기에는 기소되지 않았으니, 길드홀의 대배심에 회부되지 않았다는 말이다. 그래서 나는 한 달 내지 다섯 주의 시간을 더 갖게 되었고, 의심할 바 없이 나는 이것을 지나간 일을 성찰하고 다가올 일을 준비할 만큼의 시간을 받은 것으로, 혹은 한마디로 회개를 위해 주어진 말미로 여기고 그렇게 써야 마땅했으나, 내게는 그럴 마음이 없었다."], ["I was sorry (as before) for being in Newgate, but had very few signs of repentance about me.", "나는 (전과 같이) 뉴게이트에 있는 것을 슬퍼했을 뿐, 내게 회개의 기색은 거의 없었다."]], [["On the contrary, like the waters in the cavities and hollows of mountains, which petrify and turn into stone whatever they are suffered to drop on, so the continual conversing with such a crew of hell-hounds as I was, had the same common operation upon me as upon other people.", "도리어, 산의 동굴과 구덩이 속의 물이 떨어지도록 내버려 둔 무엇이든 돌처럼 굳히고 돌로 바꾸어 놓듯이, 나처럼 그런 지옥의 사냥개 같은 무리와 끊임없이 어울리는 것은 내게도 다른 사람들에게와 똑같은 작용을 했다."], ["I degenerated into stone; I turned first stupid and senseless, then brutish and thoughtless, and at last raving mad as any of them were; and, in short, I became as naturally pleased and easy with the place, as if indeed I had been born there.", "나는 돌로 굳어 갔으니, 처음에는 멍청하고 무감각해지더니, 그다음에는 짐승같이 분별없어지고, 마침내는 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 미쳐 날뛰게 되었다. 그리하여 한마디로, 나는 정말로 거기서 태어나기라도 한 것처럼 그곳에 자연스럽게 흡족하고 편안해졌다."]], [["It is scarce possible to imagine that our natures should be capable of so much degeneracy, as to make that pleasant and agreeable that in itself is the most complete misery.", "우리의 본성이 그토록 타락할 수 있어서, 그 자체로 더할 나위 없는 비참인 것을 즐겁고 흡족하게 만들 수 있다는 것은 좀처럼 상상하기 어려운 일이다."], ["Here was a circumstance that I think it is scarce possible to mention a worse: I was as exquisitely miserable as, speaking of common cases, it was possible for any one to be that had life and health, and money to help them, as I had.", "여기에 더 나쁜 것을 들기란 좀처럼 불가능하리라 여겨지는 처지가 있었으니, 곧 흔한 경우를 두고 말하자면, 생명과 건강과, 나처럼 그들을 도울 돈을 가진 그 누구라도 도달할 수 있는 만큼이나, 나는 더없이 지독하게 비참했던 것이다."]], [["I had weight of guilt upon me enough to sink any creature who had the least power of reflection left, and had any sense upon them of the happiness of this life, of the misery of another; then I had at first remorse indeed, but no repentance; I had now neither remorse nor repentance.", "내게는, 조금이라도 성찰의 힘이 남아 있고 이승의 행복과 저승의 비참에 대한 어떤 감각이라도 지닌 인간이라면 누구라도 가라앉히고 말 만큼의 죄의 무게가 얹혀 있었다. 그러고는 처음에는 정말이지 가책이 있었으나 회개는 없었고, 이제는 가책도 회개도 없었다."], ["I had a crime charged on me, the punishment of which was death by our law; the proof so evident, that there was no room for me so much as to plead not guilty.", "내게는 우리 법에 따라 그 형벌이 사형인 죄목이 씌워져 있었고, 그 증거가 어찌나 명백하던지 내가 무죄를 항변할 여지조차 없었다. 나는 상습범이라는 이름이 붙어 있었으므로, 몇 주 안에 죽음 외에는 기대할 것이 없었고, 나 자신도 빠져나갈 생각이 전혀 없었다."], ["I had the name of an old offender, so that I had nothing to expect but death in a few weeks’ time, neither had I myself any thoughts of escaping; and yet a certain strange lethargy of soul possessed me.", "그런데도 어떤 기이한 영혼의 무기력이 나를 사로잡았다. 내게는 아무 근심도, 아무 두려움도, 아무 슬픔도 없었으니, 처음의 놀라움은 가셨다."], ["I had no trouble, no apprehensions, no sorrow about me, the first surprise was gone; I was, I may well say, I know not how; my senses, my reason, nay, my conscience, were all asleep; my course of life for forty years had been a horrid complication of wickedness, whoredom, adultery, incest, lying, theft; and, in a word, everything but murder and treason had been my practice from the age of eighteen, or thereabouts, to three-score; and now I was engulfed in the misery of punishment, and had an infamous death just at the door, and yet I had no sense of my condition, no thought of heaven or hell at least, that went any farther than a bare flying touch, like the stitch or pain that gives a hint and goes off.", "나는, 어떻게라고 잘 말할 수 있으니, 나도 어떻게인지 모르는 채로, 내 감각도, 내 이성도, 아니 내 양심마저도 모두 잠들어 있었던 것이다. 사십 년에 걸친 내 삶의 행로는 사악함과 매음과 간통과 근친상간과 거짓말과 도둑질의 끔찍한 뒤엉킴이었고, 한마디로 살인과 반역을 제외한 모든 것이 열여덟 살 무렵부터 예순 살에 이르기까지 내 소행이었다."], ["I neither had a heart to ask God’s mercy, nor indeed to think of it.", "그리고 이제 나는 형벌의 비참 속에 잠겨 들었고, 치욕스러운 죽음이 바로 문간에 와 있었건만, 나는 내 처지에 대한 아무런 감각도, 적어도 잠깐 스쳐 가는 가벼운 접촉, 곧 힌트만 주고 사라지는 결림이나 통증 이상으로 천국이나 지옥에 대한 아무런 생각도 없었다."], ["And in this, I think, I have given a brief description of the completest misery on earth.", "나는 하느님의 자비를 구할 마음도, 정말이지 그것을 떠올릴 마음도 없었다. 그리고 이로써 나는 이 땅에서 가장 완전한 비참을 간략히 그려 낸 셈이라고 생각한다."]], [["All my terrifying thoughts were past, the horrors of the place were become familiar, and I felt no more uneasiness at the noise and clamours of the prison, than they did who made that noise; in a word, I was become a mere Newgate-bird, as wicked and as outrageous as any of them; nay, I scarce retained the habit and custom of good breeding and manners, which all along till now ran through my conversation; so thorough a degeneracy had possessed me, that I was no more the same thing that I had been, than if I had never been otherwise than what I was now.", "나를 두려움에 떨게 하던 온갖 생각은 지나갔고, 그곳의 끔찍함은 익숙해졌으며, 나는 그 소음을 내는 자들이 그러했던 것 못지않게 감옥의 소음과 아우성에 더는 불편함을 느끼지 않았다. 한마디로, 나는 한낱 뉴게이트의 새가 되어, 그들 가운데 누구 못지않게 사악하고 거칠어졌다. 아니, 지금까지 내내 내 대화에 배어 있던 좋은 가정교육과 예의범절의 습관마저 거의 간직하지 못했다. 어찌나 철저한 타락이 나를 사로잡았던지, 나는 마치 지금의 나 외에 다른 무엇이었던 적이 없기라도 한 것처럼, 더는 예전의 나와 같은 존재가 아니었다."]], [["In the middle of this hardened part of my life I had another sudden surprise, which called me back a little to that thing called sorrow, which indeed I began to be past the sense of before.", "내 삶의 이 모질어진 부분의 한가운데서, 나는 또 한 번 갑작스러운 놀라움을 겪었으니, 그것은 정말이지 그 전에 이미 그 감각을 넘어서 버린 슬픔이라는 것으로 나를 잠시 도로 불러 세웠다."], ["They told me one night that there was brought into the prison late the night before three highwaymen, who had committed robbery somewhere on the road to Windsor, Hounslow Heath, I think it was, and were pursued to Uxbridge by the country, and were taken there after a gallant resistance, in which I know not how many of the country people were wounded, and some killed.", "어느 날 밤 사람들이 내게 말하기를, 그 전날 밤늦게 감옥으로 노상강도 세 명이 끌려 들어왔는데, 그들은 윈저로 가는 길 어딘가에서—내 생각에 그것은 하운슬로 황야였던 듯하다—강도질을 저질렀고, 시골 사람들에게 억스브리지까지 추격당했으며, 거기서 용감하게 저항한 끝에 붙잡혔는데, 그 와중에 시골 사람들 가운데 몇이나 부상당하고 더러는 죽었는지 나는 모른다고 했다."]], [["It is not to be wondered that we prisoners were all desirous enough to see these brave, topping gentlemen, that were talked up to be such as their fellows had not been known, and especially because it was said they would in the morning be removed into the press-yard, having given money to the head master of the prison, to be allowed the liberty of that better part of the prison.", "우리 죄수들이 모두 이 용맹하고 한가락 하는 신사들을, 그 패거리가 일찍이 알려진 바 없는 그런 자들이라고 떠받들어지는 이들을 너무도 보고 싶어 한 것은 놀랄 일이 아니다. 더욱이 그들이 감옥의 우두머리 간수에게 돈을 주어 감옥의 그 더 나은 구역을 쓸 자유를 얻었으므로, 아침에 압류 마당으로 옮겨지리라는 말이 있었기에 더욱 그러했다."], ["So we that were women placed ourselves in the way, that we would be sure to see them; but nothing could express the amazement and surprise I was in, when the very first man that came out I knew to be my Lancashire husband, the same who lived so well at Dunstable, and the same who I afterwards saw at Brickhill, when I was married to my last husband, as has been related.", "그래서 우리 여자들은 그들을 꼭 보려고 길목에 자리를 잡았다. 그러나 맨 처음 나온 사내가 다름 아닌 나의 랭커셔 남편임을, 던스터블에서 그토록 잘 살았던 바로 그 사람이며, 앞서 이야기한 대로 내가 마지막 남편과 결혼했을 때 내가 훗날 브릭힐에서 보았던 바로 그 사람임을 알아보았을 때 내가 빠진 그 경악과 놀라움은 무엇으로도 표현할 수 없다."]], [["I was struck dumb at the sight, and knew neither what to say nor what to do; he did not know me, and that was all the present relief I had.", "나는 그 광경에 말문이 막혔고, 무슨 말을 해야 할지도 무엇을 해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그가 나를 알아보지 못한 것, 그것만이 그 순간 내가 받은 위안의 전부였다."], ["I quitted my company, and retired as much as that dreadful place suffers anybody to retire, and I cried vehemently for a great while.", "나는 일행에게서 물러나, 그 끔찍한 곳이 누구에게든 허락하는 만큼 멀찍이 물러나 한참을 격하게 울었다. \"끔찍한 인간이로구나, 나라는 것은.\""], ["“Dreadful creature that I am,”", "나는 말했다. \"내가 얼마나 많은 가엾은 사람들을 비참하게 만들었던가?"], ["said I, “how many poor people have I made miserable?", "얼마나 많은 절망에 빠진 인간들을 악마에게 보냈던가?\""], ["How many desperate wretches have I sent to the devil?”", "그는 체스터에서 내게, 자기가 그 혼인으로 파멸했으며 내 탓으로 자기 처지가 절망에 빠졌노라고 했었으니, 내가 재산가인 줄로 여기고 갚을 수 있는 것보다 더 많은 빚을 졌으며, 어떤 길을 택해야 할지 모르겠다고 했었다."], ["He had told me at Chester he was ruined by that match, and that his fortunes were made desperate on my account; for that thinking I had been a fortune, he was run into debt more than he was able to pay, and that he knew not what course to take; that he would go into the army and carry a musket, or buy a horse and take a tour, as he called it; and though I never told him that I was a fortune, and so did not actually deceive him myself, yet I did encourage the having it thought that I was so, and by that means I was the occasion originally of his mischief.", "군에 들어가 총을 들든지, 아니면 말을 사서 그가 부르는 대로 하자면 한 바퀴 떠돌든지 하겠다고 했었다. 그리고 비록 내가 그에게 내가 재산가라고 말한 적은 결코 없어서 나 자신이 실제로 그를 속인 것은 아니었으나, 나는 내가 그러하다고 여겨지는 것을 부추기기는 했으니, 그런 방식으로 나는 애초에 그의 불행의 빌미가 되었던 것이다."]], [["The surprise of the thing only struck deeper into my thoughts, and gave me stronger reflections than all that had befallen me before.", "그 일의 놀라움은 다만 내 생각 속으로 더 깊이 파고들어, 그 전에 내게 닥쳤던 그 모든 것보다 더 강한 성찰을 안겨 주었다."], ["I grieved day and night for him, and the more for that they told me he was the captain of the gang, and that he had committed so many robberies, that Hind, or Whitney, or the Golden Farmer were fools to him; that he would surely be hanged if there were no more men left in the country he was born in; and that there would abundance of people come in against him.", "나는 밤낮으로 그를 위해 슬퍼했고, 그가 그 패거리의 두목이며, 어찌나 많은 강도질을 저질렀던지 하인드나 휘트니나 골든 파머도 그에 비하면 어수룩하다는 말을, 또 그가 태어난 지방에 사람이 더는 남아 있지 않다 해도 그는 틀림없이 교수형을 당하리라는 말을, 또 그를 거슬러 무수히 많은 사람이 나설 것이라는 말을 그들에게 듣고는 더욱 슬퍼했다."], ["I was overwhelmed with grief for him; my own case gave me no disturbance compared to this, and I loaded myself with reproaches on his account.", "나는 그를 위한 슬픔에 압도되었고, 내 자신의 처지는 이에 비하면 아무런 동요도 주지 못했으며, 나는 그에 대한 자책으로 나 자신을 짓눌렀다."], ["I bewailed his misfortunes, and the ruin he was now come to, at such a rate, that I relished nothing now as I did before, and the first reflections I made upon the horrid, detestable life I had lived began to return upon me, and as these things returned, my abhorrence of the place I was in, and of the way of living in it, returned also; in a word, I was perfectly changed, and become another body.", "나는 그의 불행을, 또 그가 이제 다다른 파멸을 어찌나 슬퍼했던지, 이제는 그 전처럼 아무것에도 흥미를 느끼지 못했고, 내가 살아온 끔찍하고 가증스러운 삶에 대한 최초의 성찰이 내게 되돌아오기 시작했으며, 이런 것들이 되돌아옴에 따라 내가 있는 그곳에 대한, 또 거기서 살아가는 방식에 대한 혐오도 되돌아왔다. 한마디로, 나는 완전히 바뀌어 다른 사람이 되었다."]], [["While I was under these influences of sorrow for him, came notice to me that the next sessions approaching there would be a bill preferred to the grand jury against me, and that I should be certainly tried for my life at the Old Bailey.", "그를 위한 이런 슬픔의 영향 아래 있던 동안, 다음 개정기가 다가오니 대배심에 나를 거스르는 기소장이 제출될 것이며, 올드베일리에서 틀림없이 목숨을 걸고 재판받게 되리라는 통지가 내게 왔다."], ["My temper was touched before, the hardened, wretched boldness of spirit which I had acquired abated, and conscious in the prison, guilt began to flow in upon my mind.", "내 마음은 이미 누그러진 터였고, 내가 익혀 온 그 모질고 사악한 마음의 대담함이 가라앉았으며, 감옥에서 죄를 의식하면서 죄책감이 내 마음속으로 흘러들기 시작했다."], ["In short, I began to think, and to think is one real advance from hell to heaven.", "한마디로 나는 생각하기 시작했고, 생각한다는 것은 지옥에서 천국으로 향하는 하나의 진정한 전진이다."], ["All that hellish, hardened state and temper of soul, which I have said so much of before, is but a deprivation of thought; he that is restored to his power of thinking, is restored to himself.", "내가 앞서 그토록 많이 이야기한 그 모든 지옥 같고 모질어진 영혼의 상태와 기질은 다름 아닌 생각의 박탈일 뿐이니, 생각하는 힘을 되찾은 자는 자기 자신을 되찾는 것이다."]], [["As soon as I began, I say, to think, the first think that occurred to me broke out thus: “Lord!", "이를테면 내가 생각하기 시작하자마자, 맨 처음 떠오른 생각이 이렇게 터져 나왔다. \"주여!"], ["what will become of me?", "나는 어찌 될까요?"], ["I shall certainly die!", "나는 틀림없이 죽겠지요!"], ["I shall be cast, to be sure, and there is nothing beyond that but death!", "나는 틀림없이 선고를 받을 테고, 그 너머에는 죽음밖에 없으니!"], ["I have no friends; what shall I do?", "내게는 친구도 없는데, 어찌해야 하나요?"], ["I shall be certainly cast!", "나는 틀림없이 선고를 받을 거예요!"], ["Lord, have mercy upon me!", "주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!"], ["What will become of me?”", "나는 어찌 될까요?\""], ["This was a sad thought, you will say, to be the first, after so long a time, that had started into my soul of that kind, and yet even this was nothing but fright at what was to come; there was not a word of sincere repentance in it all.", "여러분은 이것이 그토록 오랜 시간이 흐른 뒤에 내 영혼에 그런 종류로 떠오른 첫 생각치고는 슬픈 생각이라고 말할 테고, 그런데도 이것조차 다가올 일에 대한 두려움일 뿐이어서, 그 안에는 진심 어린 회개의 말이라곤 한마디도 없었다."], ["However, I was indeed dreadfully dejected, and disconsolate to the last degree; and as I had no friend in the world to communicate my distressed thoughts to, it lay so heavy upon me, that it threw me into fits and swoonings several times a day.", "그러나 나는 정말이지 더없이 끔찍하게 풀이 죽고 더할 나위 없이 비탄에 잠겼다. 그리고 내 비참한 생각을 나눌 친구가 세상에 하나도 없었던지라, 그것이 너무도 무겁게 나를 짓눌러, 하루에도 몇 번씩 발작과 졸도에 빠뜨렸다."], ["I sent for my old governess, and she, give her her due, acted the part of a true friend.", "나는 내 늙은 후견인을 부르러 사람을 보냈는데, 공정하게 말하건대 그녀는 진정한 친구의 몫을 다했다."], ["She left no stone unturned to prevent the grand jury finding the bill.", "그녀는 대배심이 기소장을 인정하지 못하도록 온갖 수단을 다 동원했다."], ["She sought out one or two of the jurymen, talked with them, and endeavoured to possess them with favourable dispositions, on account that nothing was taken away, and no house broken, etc.; but all would not do, they were over-ruled by the rest; the two wenches swore home to the fact, and the jury found the bill against me for robbery and house-breaking, that is, for felony and burglary.", "그녀는 배심원 한둘을 찾아내어 그들과 이야기하고, 아무것도 가져간 것이 없고 집을 부수지도 않았다는 등의 이유로 그들이 호의적인 마음을 품도록 애썼다. 그러나 모두 헛일이었으니, 그들은 나머지 사람들에게 눌렸고, 두 계집이 그 사실관계를 단단히 못 박아 증언하여, 배심은 강도질과 가택 침입, 곧 중죄와 주거 침입 절도로 나를 거스르는 기소장을 인정했다."]], [["I sunk down when they brought me news of it, and after I came to myself again, I thought I should have died with the weight of it.", "그들이 내게 그 소식을 가져왔을 때 나는 주저앉았고, 다시 정신을 차린 뒤에는 그 무게에 짓눌려 죽을 것만 같았다. 내 후견인은 내게 진정한 어머니의 몫을 다했으니, 나를 가엾이 여기고, 나와 함께, 또 나를 위해 울었으나, 나를 도울 수는 없었다."], ["My governess acted a true mother to me; she pitied me, she cried with me, and for me, but she could not help me; and to add to the terror of it, ’twas the discourse all over the house that I should die for it.", "게다가 그 공포를 더하느라, 온 옥사에 내가 그 일로 죽으리라는 말이 떠돌았다. 나는 그들이 저희끼리 그것을 이야기하는 것을 자주 들을 수 있었고, 그들이 고개를 절레절레 흔들며 가엾다는 둥, 그곳에서 으레 그러듯 그런 말을 하는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["I could hear them talk it among themselves very often, and see them shake their heads and say they were sorry for it, and the like, as is usual in the place.", "그러나 여전히 아무도 내게 와서 자기 생각을 말해 주지는 않았는데, 마침내 간수 가운데 하나가 내게 은밀히 와서 한숨을 쉬며 말했다. \"이런, 플랜더스 부인, 금요일에 재판을 받게 됐어요.\""], ["But still nobody came to tell me their thoughts, till at last one of the keepers came to me privately, and said with a sigh, “Well, Mrs.", "(.) \"어쩌실 작정이오?\" 나는 행주처럼 하얗게 질려 말했다."], ["Flanders, you will be tried on Friday”", "\"어찌해야 할지는 하느님께서나 아시겠지요. 나로서는 어찌해야 할지 모르겠어요.\""], ["(.); “what do you intend to do?”", "\"이봐요.\" 그가 말했다."], ["I turned as white as a clout, and said, “God knows what I shall do; for my part, I know not what to do.”", "\"내 빈말은 안 하리다, 죽음을 준비하시는 게 좋겠소. 선고를 받을 게 걱정이거든요."], ["“Why,” says he, “I won’t flatter you, I would have you prepare for death, for I doubt you will be cast; and as they say you are an old offender, I doubt you will find but little mercy.", "게다가 사람들 말로 댁이 상습범이라니, 자비는 거의 못 받지 싶소."], ["They say,”", "사람들 말이,\""], ["added he, “your case is very plain, and that the witnesses swear so home against you, there will be no standing it.”", "그가 덧붙였다. \"댁 사건은 아주 명백한 데다, 증인들이 어찌나 댁을 단단히 못 박아 증언하는지, 버틸 재간이 없을 거라더군요.\""]], [["This was a stab into the very vitals of one under such a burthen as I was oppressed with before, and I could not speak to him a word, good or bad, for a great while; but at last I burst out into tears, and said to him, “Lord!", "이것은 나처럼 그 전에 이미 짓눌려 있던 자의 급소를 찌르는 일격이었고, 나는 한참 동안 그에게 좋은 말이든 나쁜 말이든 한마디도 할 수 없었다. 그러다 마침내 나는 울음을 터뜨리며 그에게 말했다."], ["Mr.", "\"주여!"], ["——, what must I do?”", "○○ 씨, 나는 어찌해야 하나요?\" \"어찌하긴!\""], ["“Do!”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “send for the ordinary; send for a minister and talk with him; for, indeed, Mrs.", "\"교무 사제를 부르러 보내요. 목사를 불러 그와 이야기를 나누시오."], ["Flanders, unless you have very good friends, you are no woman for this world.”", "정말이지, 플랜더스 부인, 매우 좋은 친구들이 있지 않은 한, 댁은 이 세상 사람이 아니거든요.\""]], [["This was plain dealing indeed, but it was very harsh to me, at least I thought it so.", "이것은 정말이지 솔직한 처사였으나, 내게는, 적어도 내 생각에는 매우 가혹했다."], ["He left me in the greatest confusion imaginable, and all that night I lay awake.", "그는 나를 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 혼란 속에 남겨 두었고, 그날 밤 내내 나는 깨어 누워 있었다."], ["And now I began to say my prayers, which I had scarce done before since my last husband’s death, or from a little while after.", "그리고 이제 나는 기도를 드리기 시작했는데, 마지막 남편이 죽은 뒤로, 혹은 그 얼마 뒤로는 거의 드린 적이 없는 기도였다."], ["And truly I may well call it saying my prayers, for I was in such a confusion, and had such horror upon my mind, that though I cried, and repeated several times the ordinary expression of “Lord, have mercy upon me!” I never brought myself to any sense of my being a miserable sinner, as indeed I was, and of confessing my sins to God, and begging pardon for the sake of Jesus Christ.", "그리고 정말이지 나는 그것을 기도를 드린다고밖에 부를 수 없으니, 나는 어찌나 혼란에 빠지고 마음에 어찌나 공포가 서려 있던지, 비록 울며 \"주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\"라는 그 흔한 말을 여러 번 되풀이했으나, 정말이지 그러했듯이 내가 비참한 죄인임을, 또 내 죄를 하느님께 고백하고 예수 그리스도를 위하여 용서를 구한다는 그 어떤 감각에도 나 자신을 이르게 하지 못했기 때문이다."], ["I was overwhelmed with the sense of my condition, being tried for my life, and being sure to be condemned, and then I was as sure to be executed, and on this account I cried out all night, “Lord, what will become of me?", "나는 목숨을 걸고 재판받게 되었고, 틀림없이 단죄받으리라는, 그러면 틀림없이 처형되리라는 내 처지에 대한 감각에 압도되어, 이런 까닭에 밤새도록 부르짖었다. \"주여, 나는 어찌 될까요?"], ["Lord!", "주여!"], ["what shall I do?", "나는 어찌해야 하나요?"], ["Lord!", "주여!"], ["I shall be hanged!", "나는 교수형을 당하겠지요!"], ["Lord, have mercy upon me!”", "주여, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!\""], ["and the like.", "그런 말을 되풀이했다."]], [["My poor afflicted governess was now as much concerned as I, and a great deal more truly penitent, though she had no prospect of being brought to trial and sentence. Not but that she deserved it as much as I, and so she said herself; but she had not done anything herself for many years, other than receiving what I and others stole, and encouraging us to steal it. But she cried, and took on like a distracted body, wringing her hands, and crying out that she was undone, that she believed there was a curse from heaven upon her, that she should be damned, that she had been the destruction of all her friends, that she had brought such a one, and such a one, and such a one to the gallows; and there she reckoned up ten or eleven people, some of which I have given account of, that came to untimely ends; and that now she was the occasion of my ruin, for she had persuaded me to go on, when I would have left off. I interrupted her there. “No, mother, no,” said I, “don’t speak of that, for you would have had me left off when I got the mercer’s money again, and when I came home from Harwich, and I would not hearken to you; therefore you have not been to blame; it is I only have ruined myself, I have brought myself to this misery”; and thus we spent many hours together.", "내 가엾고 비탄에 잠긴 후견인은 이제 나만큼이나 마음을 졸였고, 비록 재판과 선고에 회부될 가망은 없었으나 훨씬 더 진심으로 뉘우치고 있었다. 그렇다고 그녀가 나만큼 그럴 만하지 않은 것은 아니었고, 그녀 자신도 그렇게 말했다. 다만 그녀는 여러 해 동안, 나와 다른 이들이 훔쳐 온 것을 받고 우리가 그것을 훔치도록 부추기는 것 외에는 직접 한 일이 없을 뿐이었다. 그러나 그녀는 미친 사람처럼 울며 안달했으니, 두 손을 비틀고, 자기는 끝장났다고, 하늘로부터 자기에게 저주가 내린 것이 분명하다고, 자기는 지옥에 떨어질 것이라고, 자기는 자기 친구들을 모두 파멸시켰다고, 이러이러한 자를, 또 이러이러한 자를, 또 이러이러한 자를 교수대로 보냈다고 부르짖었다. 그러고는 거기서 열 명인가 열한 명을 꼽았는데, 그중 더러는 내가 그 비명횡사를 이야기한 바 있고, 또 이제는 자기가 내 파멸의 빌미가 되었으니, 내가 그만두려 했을 때 자기가 나더러 계속하라고 설득했기 때문이라고 했다. 나는 거기서 그녀의 말을 가로막았다. \"아니에요, 어머니, 아니에요.\" 내가 말했다. \"그런 말씀 마세요. 어머니는 제가 포목상의 돈을 도로 손에 넣었을 때, 또 제가 하리치에서 돌아왔을 때 제가 그만두기를 바라셨지만, 제가 어머니 말씀을 들으려 하지 않았던걸요. 그러니 어머니는 잘못한 게 없어요. 나 자신을 파멸시킨 건 오직 저예요, 나 자신을 이 비참에 빠뜨린 건 저라고요.\" 이렇게 우리는 여러 시간을 함께 보냈다."]], [["Well, there was no remedy; the prosecution went on, and on the Thursday I was carried down to the sessions-house, where I was arraigned, as they called it, and the next day I was appointed to be tried.", "자, 어쩔 도리가 없었다."], ["At the arraignment I pleaded “Not guilty,”", "기소는 진행되었고, 목요일에 나는 사법회관으로 끌려 내려가 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 기소 인정 신문을 받았으며, 이튿날 재판받도록 정해졌다."], ["and well I might, for I was indicted for felony and burglary; that is, for feloniously stealing two pieces of brocaded silk, value £46, the goods of Anthony Johnson, and for breaking open his doors; whereas I knew very well they could not pretend to prove I had broken up the doors, or so much as lifted up a latch.", "기소 인정 신문에서 나는 \"무죄\"를 항변했는데, 그럴 만도 했으니, 나는 중죄와 주거 침입 절도로, 곧 앤서니 존슨의 소유물인 능직 비단 두 필을, 값으로 사십육 파운드어치를 중죄로 훔치고 그의 문을 부수어 연 죄목으로 기소되었기 때문이다. 그런데 나는 그들이 내가 문을 부수었다거나, 빗장 하나라도 들어 올렸다는 것을 증명하는 척조차 할 수 없음을 너무도 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["On the Friday I was brought to my trial.", "금요일에 나는 재판정에 끌려 나갔다."], ["I had exhausted my spirits with crying for two or three days before, so that I slept better the Thursday night than I expected, and had more courage for my trial than indeed I thought possible for me to have.", "나는 그 전 이삼 일 동안 울며 기운을 다 소진한 터라, 목요일 밤에는 예상보다 잘 잤고, 정말이지 내가 가질 수 있으리라 여겼던 것보다 더 큰 용기를 재판에 임해 지니고 있었다."]], [["When the trial began, the indictment was read, I would have spoke, but they told me the witnesses must be heard first, and then I should have time to be heard.", "재판이 시작되어 기소장이 낭독되자 나는 말을 하려 했으나, 그들은 먼저 증인들의 말을 들어야 하며, 그런 다음에 내가 진술할 시간을 가지게 되리라고 했다."], ["The witnesses were the two wenches, a couple of hard-mouthed jades indeed, for though the thing was truth in the main, yet they aggravated it to the utmost extremity, and swore I had the goods wholly in my possession, that I had hid them among my clothes, that I was going off with them, that I had one foot over the threshold when they discovered themselves, and then I put t’ other over, so that I was quite out of the house in the street with the goods before they took hold of me, and then they seized me, and brought me back again, and they took the goods upon me.", "증인들은 그 두 계집으로, 정말이지 입버릇 사나운 한 쌍의 계집이었으니, 그 일이 대체로는 사실이었음에도, 그들은 그것을 극단으로 부풀려, 내가 물건을 온전히 내 손에 쥐고 있었다고, 내가 그것을 옷 속에 숨겼다고, 내가 그것을 가지고 빠져나가고 있었다고, 자기들이 모습을 드러냈을 때 내가 한쪽 발을 문지방 너머에 두고 있었고 그러고 나서 다른 발도 마저 넘겨, 그들이 나를 붙들기 전에 내가 물건을 가지고 집을 완전히 벗어나 거리에 나와 있었다고, 그래서 자기들이 나를 붙잡아 도로 데려왔으며 내게서 물건을 빼앗았다고 맹세했다."], ["The fact in general was all true, but I believe, and insisted upon it, that they stopped me before I had set my foot clear of the threshold of the house.", "대체로 그 사실은 모두 진실이었으나, 나는 그들이 내가 집 문지방을 채 벗어나기도 전에 나를 막아 세웠다고 믿었고, 또 그렇게 우겼다."], ["But that did not argue much, for certain it was that I had taken the goods, and I was bringing them away, if I had not been taken.", "그러나 그것은 별 의미가 없었으니, 내가 물건을 가져갔고, 붙잡히지만 않았다면 그것을 가지고 빠져나가고 있었으리라는 것은 분명했기 때문이다."]], [["But I pleaded that I had stole nothing, they had lost nothing, that the door was open, and I went in, seeing the goods lie there, and with design to buy.", "그러나 나는, 내가 아무것도 훔치지 않았다고, 그들은 아무것도 잃지 않았다고, 문이 열려 있었고 나는 거기 놓인 물건을 보고 사려는 마음으로 들어갔노라고 항변했다."], ["If, seeing nobody in the house, I had taken any of them up in my hand it could not be concluded that I intended to steal them, for that I never carried them farther than the door to look on them with the better light.", "집에 아무도 없는 것을 보고 그것들 가운데 무엇을 손에 들었다 한들, 내가 그것을 훔치려 했다고 단정할 수는 없으니, 나는 그것을 더 밝은 빛으로 보려고 문 너머로는 결코 가져가지 않았기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["The Court would not allow that by any means, and made a kind of a jest of my intending to buy the goods, that being no shop for the selling of anything, and as to carrying them to the door to look at them, the maids made their impudent mocks upon that, and spent their wit upon it very much; told the Court I had looked at them sufficiently, and approved them very well, for I had packed them up under my clothes, and was a-going with them.", "법정은 그것을 결코 받아들이지 않았고, 내가 물건을 사려 했다는 것을 일종의 농담거리로 삼았으니, 그곳은 무엇을 파는 가게가 아니었기 때문이다. 또 물건을 보려고 문간으로 가져갔다는 것에 대해서는, 하녀들이 거기에 뻔뻔스러운 조롱을 퍼붓고 그 일에 한껏 재치를 부려 댔으니, 내가 그것들을 충분히 보았고 매우 만족스러워했다고, 왜냐하면 내가 그것들을 옷 밑에 챙겨 넣고 그것들을 가지고 가고 있었기 때문이라고 법정에 말했다."]], [["In short, I was found guilty of felony, but acquitted of the burglary, which was but small comfort to me, the first bringing me to a sentence of death, and the last would have done no more.", "한마디로, 나는 중죄로는 유죄가 인정되었으나 주거 침입 절도로는 무죄가 선고되었는데, 그것은 내게 거의 위안이 되지 못했으니, 앞엣것이 나를 사형 선고로 이끌었고 뒤엣것도 그 이상은 하지 못했을 것이기 때문이다."], ["The next day I was carried down to receive the dreadful sentence, and when they came to ask me what I had to say why sentence should not pass, I stood mute a while, but somebody that stood behind me prompted me aloud to speak to the judges, for that they could represent things favourably for me.", "이튿날 나는 그 무시무시한 선고를 받으러 끌려 내려갔고, 그들이 ……에 이르렀을 때\n내가 어찌하여 선고가 내려져서는 안 되는지 할 말이 있느냐고 묻기에, 나는 한동안 입을 다물고 서 있었으나, 내 뒤에 서 있던 누군가가 큰 소리로 재판관들에게 말씀을 올리라고, 그분들이 나를 위해 사정을 호의적으로 헤아려 줄 수 있을 것이라고 일러 주었다."], ["This encouraged me to speak, and I told them I had nothing to say to stop the sentence, but that I had much to say to bespeak the mercy of the Court; that I hoped they would allow something in such a case for the circumstances of it; that I had broken no doors, had carried nothing off; that nobody had lost anything; that the person whose goods they were was pleased to say he desired mercy might be shown (which indeed he very honestly did); that, at the worst, it was the first offence, and that I had never been before any court of justice before; and, in a word, I spoke with more courage that I thought I could have done, and in such a moving tone, and though with tears, yet not so many tears as to obstruct my speech, that I could see it moved others to tears that heard me.", "이에 용기를 얻어 나는 입을 열어, 선고를 막을 만한 할 말은 없으나 법정의 자비를 청할 말은 많다고 말했고, 이러한 경우에는 그 정황을 어느 정도 참작해 주시기를 바란다고, 나는 문을 부수지도 않았고 아무것도 가지고 나오지 않았으며, 아무도 무엇 하나 잃지 않았고, 그 물건의 주인이 자비를 베풀어 주기를 바란다고 말해 주었으며(이는 실로 그가 매우 정직하게 그렇게 해 준 것이었다), 아무리 나쁘게 보아도 그것은 초범이며 나는 그 전에 한 번도 어떤 사법 법정에도 선 적이 없다고 말했다. 한마디로, 나는 내가 할 수 있으리라 여겼던 것보다 더 큰 용기를 내어, 그토록 사람을 움직이는 어조로, 비록 눈물을 흘리기는 했으나 말을 가로막을 만큼 많은 눈물은 아니게끔 말했고, 내 말을 듣던 다른 이들마저 눈물짓게 한 것을 볼 수 있었다."]], [["The judges sat grave and mute, gave me an easy hearing, and time to say all that I would, but, saying neither Yes nor No to it, pronounced the sentence of death upon me, a sentence that was to me like death itself, which, after it was read, confounded me.", "재판관들은 엄숙하고 말없이 앉아, 내 말을 너그럽게 들어 주고 내가 하고 싶은 말을 다 할 시간을 주었으나, 그에 대해 가타부타 한마디도 하지 않은 채 내게 사형 선고를 내렸으니, 그것은 내게 죽음 그 자체와도 같은 선고였고, 그것이 낭독된 뒤에 나는 정신이 아득해졌다."], ["I had no more spirit left in me, I had no tongue to speak, or eyes to look up either to God or man.", "내게는 더 이상 기력이 남아 있지 않았고, 말할 혀도, 하느님이든 사람이든 우러러볼 눈도 없었다."]], [["My poor governess was utterly disconsolate, and she that was my comforter before, wanted comfort now herself; and sometimes mourning, sometimes raging, was as much out of herself, as to all outward appearance, as any mad woman in Bedlam.", "가엾은 내 후견인은 더없이 비탄에 잠겼으니, 전에는 나를 위로해 주던 그녀가 이제는 스스로 위로를 필요로 했고, 때로는 슬퍼하고 때로는 광란하며, 겉으로 보기에는 베들럼의 어떤 미친 여자 못지않게 제정신이 아니었다."], ["Nor was she only disconsolate as to me, but she was struck with horror at the sense of her own wicked life, and began to look back upon it with a taste quite different from mine, for she was penitent to the highest degree for her sins, as well as sorrowful for the misfortune.", "그녀는 나 때문에만 비탄에 잠긴 것이 아니라, 자기 자신의 사악한 삶을 자각하는 데서 오는 공포에 사로잡혀, 나와는 사뭇 다른 마음으로 그것을 되돌아보기 시작했으니, 그녀는 그 불운을 슬퍼하는 것에 더하여 자기 죄에 대해 더할 나위 없이 깊이 참회했기 때문이다."], ["She sent for a minister, too, a serious, pious, good man, and applied herself with such earnestness, by his assistance, to the work of a sincere repentance, that I believe, and so did the minister too, that she was a true penitent; and, which is still more, she was not only so for the occasion, and at that juncture, but she continued so, as I was informed, to the day of her death.", "그녀는 또한 목사 한 사람을 불렀으니, 진지하고 경건한 선량한 사람으로, 그의 도움을 받아 진실한 참회의 일에 어찌나 간곡히 매달렸던지, 나는 그녀가 진정한 참회자라고 믿게 되었고 그 목사 역시 그러했다. 더욱이 그녀는 그때 그 순간만 그러했던 것이 아니라, 내가 들은 바로는 죽는 날까지 한결같이 그러했다."]], [["It is rather to be thought of than expressed what was now my condition.", "지금 내 처지가 어떠했는지는 말로 표현하기보다 마음으로 헤아릴 일이다."], ["I had nothing before me but present death; and as I had no friends to assist me, or to stir for me, I expected nothing but to find my name in the dead warrant, which was to come down for the execution, the Friday afterwards, of five more and myself.", "내 앞에는 닥쳐온 죽음 외에는 아무것도 없었고, 나를 도와주거나 나를 위해 애써 줄 벗 하나 없었으니, 나는 그저 그 이튿주 금요일에 나 말고 다섯 사람을 더 처형하기 위해 내려올 사형집행영장에서 내 이름을 발견하게 되리라는 것밖에는 아무것도 기대할 수 없었다."]], [["In the meantime my poor distressed governess sent me a minister, who at her request first, and at my own afterwards, came to visit me.", "그러는 사이, 비탄에 빠진 가엾은 내 후견인이 목사 한 사람을 내게 보냈는데, 그는 처음에는 그녀의 청으로, 나중에는 나 자신의 청으로 나를 찾아왔다."], ["He exhorted me seriously to repent of all my sins, and to dally no longer with my soul; not flattering myself with hopes of life, which, he said, he was informed there was no room to expect, but unfeignedly to look up to God with my whole soul, and to cry for pardon in the name of Jesus Christ.", "그는 내게 모든 죄를 진지하게 참회하고 더는 내 영혼을 가지고 미적거리지 말라고, 그가 듣기로는 기대할 여지가 전혀 없다는 목숨에 헛된 희망을 품지 말고, 온 영혼을 다해 거짓 없이 하느님을 우러러보며 예수 그리스도의 이름으로 용서를 빌라고 간곡히 권면했다."], ["He backed his discourses with proper quotations of Scripture, encouraging the greatest sinner to repent, and turn from their evil way, and when he had done, he kneeled down and prayed with me.", "그는 자기 말을 적절한 성경 구절로 뒷받침하여, 아무리 큰 죄인이라도 참회하고 그 악한 길에서 돌아서도록 북돋웠으며, 말을 마치고는 무릎을 꿇고 나와 함께 기도했다."]], [["It was now that, for the first time, I felt any real signs of repentance.", "내가 참회의 어떤 진정한 기미라도 처음 느낀 것은 바로 이때였다."], ["I now began to look back upon my past life with abhorrence, and having a kind of view into the other side of time, and things of life, as I believe they do with everybody at such a time, began to look with a different aspect, and quite another shape, than they did before.", "나는 이제 내 지난 삶을 혐오하며 되돌아보기 시작했고, 시간의 저편과 삶의 일들을 어느 정도 들여다보게 되면서—이런 때에는 누구나 그러하리라 믿거니와—전과는 다른 면모로, 전혀 다른 모습으로 그것들을 바라보기 시작했다."], ["The greatest and best things, the views of felicity, the joy, the griefs of life, were quite other things; and I had nothing in my thoughts but what was so infinitely superior to what I had known in life, that it appeared to me to be the greatest stupidity in nature to lay any weight upon anything, though the most valuable in this world.", "가장 위대하고 가장 좋은 것들, 행복의 전망, 삶의 기쁨과 슬픔이 전혀 다른 것이 되어 있었고, 내 머릿속에는 내가 살면서 알던 것보다 한없이 우월한 것들밖에 없어서, 이 세상에서 가장 값진 것일지라도 무엇이든 거기에 무게를 두는 것이야말로 천하에 가장 큰 어리석음으로 여겨졌다."], ["The word eternity represented itself with all its incomprehensible additions, and I had such extended notions of it, that I know not how to express them.", "영원이라는 말이 그 헤아릴 수 없는 온갖 의미를 더한 채 스스로를 드러냈고, 나는 그것에 대해 어찌나 광대한 관념을 품게 되었던지 그것을 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모를 지경이었다."], ["Among the rest, how vile, how gross, how absurd did every pleasant thing look!—I mean, that we had counted pleasant before—especially when I reflected that these sordid trifles were the things for which we forfeited eternal felicity.", "무엇보다, 온갖 즐거운 것들—내 말은, 우리가 전에 즐겁다고 여겼던 것들 말이다—이 얼마나 비루하고 얼마나 천박하고 얼마나 터무니없어 보였던가! 특히 이런 더러운 하찮은 것들이 바로 우리가 영원한 행복을 내던지고 맞바꾼 것들임을 곱씹어 보았을 때 더욱 그러했다."]], [["With these reflections came, of mere course, severe reproaches of my own mind for my wretched behaviour in my past life; that I had forfeited all hope of any happiness in the eternity that I was just going to enter into, and on the contrary was entitled to all that was miserable, or had been conceived of misery; and all this with the frightful addition of its being also eternal.", "이런 성찰과 더불어, 당연한 수순으로, 내 지난 삶의 비참한 행실에 대한 내 마음의 준엄한 자책이 찾아왔으니, 내가 막 들어서려 하는 그 영원 속에서 누릴 어떠한 행복의 희망도 다 내던져 버렸으며, 도리어 비참한 모든 것, 일찍이 비참함으로 상상되어 온 모든 것을 받아 마땅하게 되었고, 게다가 이 모든 것이 또한 영원하다는 무서운 사실까지 더해진다는 것이었다."]], [["I am not capable of reading lectures of instruction to anybody, but I relate this in the very manner in which things then appeared to me, as far as I am able, but infinitely short of the lively impressions which they made on my soul at that time; indeed, those impressions are not to be explained by words, or if they are, I am not mistress of words enough to express them.", "나는 누구에게 가르침의 강론을 펼칠 만한 사람이 못 되거니와, 다만 그때 내게 사물이 비쳐 보였던 그 모습 그대로를 내가 할 수 있는 한 이야기할 뿐이지만, 그것은 그때 내 영혼에 새겨졌던 생생한 인상에는 한없이 못 미치는 것이니, 실로 그 인상들은 말로 설명할 수 없는 것이거나, 설령 설명할 수 있다 해도 내가 그것을 표현할 만큼 말을 부릴 줄 아는 사람이 못 되는 것이다."], ["It must be the work of every sober reader to make just reflections on them, as their own circumstances may direct; and, without question, this is what every one at some time or other may feel something of; I mean, a clearer sight into things to come than they had here, and a dark view of their own concern in them.", "그것들에 대해 저마다 자기 처지가 일러 주는 대로 올바른 성찰을 하는 것은 분별 있는 모든 독자 각자의 몫일 터이며, 의심할 바 없이 이는 누구나 언젠가 한 번쯤은 그 무언가를 느끼게 될 일이니, 내 말은, 여기서 지녔던 것보다 더 또렷하게 다가올 일들을 들여다보고, 그 일들에 얽힌 자기 자신의 처지를 어둡게 내다보는 것 말이다."]], [["But I go back to my own case.", "그러나 나는 내 이야기로 되돌아간다."], ["The minister pressed me to tell him, as far as I thought convenient, in what state I found myself as to the sight I had of things beyond life.", "그 목사는 내가 적절하다고 여기는 한도 안에서, 삶 너머의 일들을 내다본 그 시야에 관하여 내가 나 자신을 어떤 상태에 있다고 느끼는지 말해 달라고 다그쳤다."], ["He told me he did not come as ordinary of the place, whose business it is to extort confessions from prisoners, for private ends, or for the further detecting of other offenders; that his business was to move me to such freedom of discourse as might serve to disburthen my own mind, and furnish him to administer comfort to me as far as was in his power; and assured me, that whatever I said to him should remain with him, and be as much a secret as if it was known only to God and myself; and that he desired to know nothing of me, but as above to qualify him to apply proper advice and assistance to me, and to pray to God for me.", "그는 자신이 사사로운 목적을 위해서나 다른 범죄자들을 더 적발하기 위해서 죄수들에게서 자백을 짜내는 것이 본분인 그곳의 전속 교목으로서 온 것이 아니라고 말했으며, 자기가 할 일은 내가 마음을 털어놓도록 자유롭게 이야기하게 이끌어, 내 마음의 짐을 덜어 주고, 자기가 할 수 있는 한 내게 위안을 베풀 수 있게 하는 것이라고 했다. 그러면서 내가 그에게 하는 어떤 말이든 그에게만 머무를 것이며, 오직 하느님과 나 자신만이 아는 것처럼 비밀로 지켜질 것이라고, 또 자기는 위에서 말한 대로 내게 알맞은 충고와 도움을 베풀고 나를 위해 하느님께 기도하기에 합당하게 하려는 것 말고는 나에 대해 아무것도 알고자 하지 않는다고 다짐해 주었다."]], [["This honest, friendly way of treating me unlocked all the sluices of my passions.", "나를 대하는 이 정직하고 다정한 태도가 내 온갖 격정의 수문을 모조리 열어젖혔다."], ["He broke into my very soul by it; and I unravelled all the wickedness of my life to him.", "그는 그것으로 내 영혼 깊은 곳까지 파고들었고, 나는 내 삶의 온갖 사악함을 그에게 낱낱이 풀어놓았다."], ["In a word, I gave him an abridgment of this whole history; I gave him a picture of my conduct for fifty years in miniature.", "한마디로, 나는 그에게 이 모든 이력을 간추려 들려주었고, 오십 년에 걸친 내 행실을 축소판으로 그려 보였다."]], [["I hid nothing from him, and he in return exhorted me to sincere repentance, explained to me what he meant by repentance, and then drew out such a scheme of infinite mercy, proclaimed from heaven to sinners of the greatest magnitude, that he left me nothing to say, that looked like despair, or doubting of being accepted; and in this condition he left me the first night.", "나는 그에게 아무것도 숨기지 않았고, 그는 그 답례로 내게 진실한 참회를 권하며 자기가 참회로써 무엇을 뜻하는지 설명해 주었으며, 그런 다음 하늘로부터 가장 극악한 죄인들에게까지 선포된 무한한 자비의 도리를 어찌나 잘 펼쳐 보였던지, 절망이나 받아들여지지 못하리라는 의심 같은 빛을 띤 말은 내게 한마디도 남기지 않았다. 이런 상태로 그는 첫날 밤 나를 두고 떠났다."]], [["He visited me again the next morning, and went on with his method of explaining the terms of divine mercy, which according to him consisted of nothing more, or more difficult, than that of being sincerely desirous of it, and willing to accept it; only a sincere regret for, and hatred of, those things I had done, which rendered me so just an object of divine vengeance.", "그는 이튿날 아침 다시 나를 찾아와, 하느님의 자비의 조건을 설명하는 자기 방식을 이어 갔는데, 그에 따르면 그것은 그것을 진실로 갈망하고 기꺼이 받아들이려는 것, 다만 나를 하느님의 응징에 그토록 마땅한 대상으로 만든 그 행한 일들에 대한 진실한 뉘우침과 미움 외에는 더 많지도, 더 어렵지도 않은 것이었다."], ["I am not able to repeat the excellent discourses of this extraordinary man; ’tis all that I am able to do, to say that he revived my heart, and brought me into such a condition that I never knew anything of in my life before.", "나는 이 비범한 사람의 훌륭한 강론을 되풀이해 옮길 능력이 없으니, 그가 내 마음을 되살려 내, 내가 평생 한 번도 알지 못했던 그런 상태로 나를 이끌었다고 말하는 것이 내가 할 수 있는 전부다."], ["I was covered with shame and tears for things past, and yet had at the same time a secret surprising joy at the prospect of being a true penitent, and obtaining the comfort of a penitent—I mean, the hope of being forgiven; and so swift did thoughts circulate, and so high did the impressions they had made upon me run, that I thought I could freely have gone out that minute to execution, without any uneasiness at all, casting my soul entirely into the arms of infinite mercy as a penitent.", "나는 지난 일들에 대한 부끄러움과 눈물에 뒤덮여 있으면서도, 동시에 진정한 참회자가 되어 참회자의 위안—내 말은, 용서받으리라는 희망 말이다—을 얻게 되리라는 전망에 어떤 은밀하고도 놀라운 기쁨을 느꼈다. 또한 생각이 어찌나 빠르게 돌고, 그것들이 내게 남긴 인상이 어찌나 드높이 차올랐던지, 나는 한 점 불안도 없이 그 순간 당장이라도 형장에 나아가, 참회자로서 내 영혼을 무한한 자비의 품에 온전히 내던질 수 있으리라 여겨졌다."]], [["The good gentleman was so moved also in my behalf with a view of the influence which he saw these things had on me, that he blessed God he had come to visit me, and resolved not to leave me till the last moment; that is, not to leave visiting me.", "이 선량한 신사 또한 이런 것들이 내게 미치는 영향을 보고 나를 위하여 어찌나 마음이 움직였던지, 자기가 나를 찾아오게 된 것을 두고 하느님께 감사하며 마지막 순간까지 나를 떠나지 않기로—다시 말해 나를 찾아오기를 그만두지 않기로—결심했다."]], [["It was no less than twelve days after our receiving sentence before any were ordered for execution, and then upon a Wednesday the dead warrant, as they call it, came down, and I found my name was among them.", "선고를 받고 나서 누군가의 처형이 명해지기까지는 자그마치 열이틀이나 걸렸고, 그러고는 어느 수요일에 그들이 이르는 대로 사형집행영장이 내려왔는데, 그 안에 내 이름이 들어 있는 것을 알게 되었다."], ["A terrible blow this was to my new resolutions; indeed my heart sank within me, and I swooned away twice, one after another, but spoke not a word.", "이것은 내 새로운 결심에 무서운 타격이었다. 실로 내 가슴은 무너져 내렸고, 나는 잇따라 두 번이나 까무러쳤으나 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["The good minister was sorely afflicted for me, and did what he could to comfort me with the same arguments, and the same moving eloquence that he did before, and left me not that evening so long as the prisonkeepers would suffer him to stay in the prison, unless he would be locked up with me all night, which he was not willing to be.", "선량한 목사는 나 때문에 몹시 괴로워하며, 전과 똑같은 논리와 똑같이 사람을 움직이는 언변으로 나를 위로하려고 할 수 있는 일을 다 했고, 밤새도록 나와 함께 갇혀 있으려 하지는 않았으므로—그것은 그가 내키지 않아 한 일이었다—그날 저녁 간수들이 그를 옥 안에 머물게 허락하는 한 오래도록 나를 떠나지 않았다."]], [["I wondered much that I did not see him all the next day, it being the day before the time appointed for execution; and I was greatly discouraged, and dejected in my mind, and indeed almost sank for want of the comfort which he had so often, and with such success, yielded me on his former visits.", "이튿날 하루 종일 그를 보지 못한 것이 나는 몹시 의아했으니, 그날이 바로 처형으로 정해진 날의 전날이었기 때문이다. 나는 크게 낙담하여 마음이 풀이 죽었고, 실로 그가 앞서 여러 차례 그토록 큰 효험으로 베풀어 주던 그 위안이 없어 거의 주저앉을 지경이었다."], ["I waited with great impatience, and under the greatest oppressions of spirits imaginable, till about four o’clock he came to my apartment; for I had obtained the favour, by the help of money, nothing being to be done in that place without it, not to be kept in the condemned hole, as they call it, among the rest of the prisoners who were to die, but to have a little dirty chamber to myself.", "나는 상상할 수 있는 가장 큰 정신의 압박 아래, 크나큰 초조함 속에서 네 시쯤까지 기다렸고, 그제야 그가 내 방으로 찾아왔으니, 나는 돈의 힘을 빌려—그곳에서는 돈 없이는 아무것도 되지 않았다—그들이 사형수 굴이라 부르는 곳에서 죽음을 앞둔 다른 죄수들 틈에 갇혀 있지 않고, 더럽고 작으나마 나 혼자 쓰는 방 하나를 가지는 호의를 얻어 두었던 것이다."]], [["My heart leaped within me for joy when I heard his voice at the door, even before I saw him; but let any one judge what kind of motion I found in my soul, when after having made a short excuse for his not coming, he showed me that his time had been employed on my account; that he had obtained a favourable report from the Recorder to the Secretary of State in my particular case, and, in short, that he had brought me a reprieve.", "그를 보기도 전에 문간에서 그의 목소리를 들었을 때, 내 가슴은 기쁨으로 펄쩍 뛰었다. 그러나 그가 오지 못한 데 대해 짧게 변명을 하고 나서, 자기가 그동안 나를 위해 시간을 써 왔다는 것을, 나의 그 특정한 사건에 대하여 형사법원 기록관으로부터 국무대신에게 가는 호의적인 보고를 얻어 냈다는 것을, 한마디로 나에게 형 집행 유예를 받아 왔다는 것을 보여 주었을 때 내 영혼에 어떤 떨림이 일었는지는 누구든 헤아려 보시기를."]], [["He used all the caution that he was able in letting me know a thing which it would have been a double cruelty to have concealed; and yet it was too much for me; for as grief had overset me before, so did joy overset me now, and I fell into a much more dangerous swooning than I did at first, and it was not without a great difficulty that I was recovered at all.", "그는 감추었더라면 이중의 잔혹함이 되었을 일을 내게 알리면서도 할 수 있는 온갖 신중을 다 기울였다. 그런데도 그것은 내게 너무 벅찼으니, 전에는 슬픔이 나를 뒤엎었던 것처럼 이제는 기쁨이 나를 뒤엎어, 나는 처음보다 훨씬 더 위태로운 혼절에 빠졌고, 여간한 어려움 끝에야 겨우 정신을 차렸다."]], [["The good man having made a very Christian exhortation to me, not to let the joy of my reprieve put the remembrance of my past sorrow out of my mind, and having told me that he must leave me, to go and enter the reprieve in the books, and show it to the sheriffs, stood up just before his going away, and in a very earnest manner prayed to God for me, that my repentance might be made unfeigned and sincere; and that my coming back, as it were, into life again, might not be a returning to the follies of life which I had made such solemn resolutions to forsake, and to repent of them.", "그 선량한 사람은 내게 더없이 그리스도교다운 권면을 하여, 형 집행 유예의 기쁨이 내 지난 슬픔의 기억을 마음에서 몰아내게 하지 말라고 일렀으며, 그 유예를 장부에 등재하고 주(州) 장관들에게 보여 주러 가야 하니 나를 떠나야 한다고 말하고는, 떠나기 직전에 일어서서 더없이 간곡한 태도로 나를 위해 하느님께 기도하여, 내 참회가 거짓 없고 진실하게 되기를, 그리고 이를테면 다시 삶으로 돌아오는 것이 내가 그토록 엄숙하게 버리고 참회하기로 결심한 삶의 어리석음으로 되돌아가는 것이 되지 않기를 빌었다."], ["I joined heartily in the petition, and must needs say I had deeper impressions upon my mind all that night, of the mercy of God in sparing my life, and a greater detestation of my past sins, from a sense of the goodness which I had tasted in this case, than I had in all my sorrow before.", "나는 그 기원에 진심으로 동참했고, 그날 밤 내내 내 목숨을 살려 주신 하느님의 자비에 대해, 또 이번 일에서 내가 맛본 그 선하심을 자각함으로 인해 내 지난 죄에 대한 더 큰 혐오에 대해, 전에 겪은 그 모든 슬픔 속에서보다 더 깊은 인상을 마음에 새겼다고 말하지 않을 수 없다."]], [["This may be thought inconsistent in itself, and wide from the business of this book; particularly, I reflect that many of those who may be pleased and diverted with the relation of the wild and wicked part of my story may not relish this, which is really the best part of my life, the most advantageous to myself, and the most instructive to others.", "이것은 그 자체로 모순되어 보일 수도 있고, 이 책의 본분에서 동떨어진 것으로 여겨질 수도 있다. 특히 나는, 내 이야기의 거칠고 사악한 부분을 들으며 즐거워하고 기분 전환을 할 만한 많은 이들이 실은 내 삶의 가장 좋은 부분이요, 나 자신에게 가장 이로우며 다른 이들에게 가장 교훈이 되는 이 부분은 달가워하지 않을지도 모른다는 것을 곱씹어 본다."], ["Such, however, will, I hope, allow me the liberty to make my story complete.", "그러나 그런 이들도 내 이야기를 온전하게 마무리할 자유만은 부디 내게 허락해 주기를 바란다."], ["It would be a severe satire on such to say they do not relish the repentance as much as they do the crime; and that they had rather the history were a complete tragedy, as it was very likely to have been.", "그들이 죄악만큼 참회를 달가워하지 않는다고, 또 이 이력이 충분히 그리될 법했던 대로 완전한 비극이 되었더라면 차라리 더 좋아했으리라고 말한다면, 그것은 그들에 대한 가혹한 풍자가 될 것이다."]], [["But I go on with my relation.", "그러나 나는 내 이야기를 이어 간다."], ["The next morning there was a sad scene indeed in the prison.", "이튿날 아침 옥 안에는 실로 처참한 광경이 펼쳐졌다."], ["The first thing I was saluted with in the morning was the tolling of the great bell at St. Sepulchre’s, as they call it, which ushered in the day.", "아침에 나를 가장 먼저 맞이한 것은 그들이 이르는 대로 세인트세펄커 교회의 큰 종이 울리는 소리였으니, 그것이 그날의 시작을 알렸다."], ["As soon as it began to toll, a dismal groaning and crying was heard from the condemned hole, where there lay six poor souls who were to be executed that day, some from one crime, some for another, and two of them for murder.", "종이 울리기 시작하자마자, 그날 처형될 가엾은 여섯 영혼이 누워 있는 사형수 굴에서 음산한 신음과 울부짖음이 들려왔는데, 더러는 이런 죄로, 더러는 저런 죄로, 그중 둘은 살인죄로 처형될 자들이었다."]], [["This was followed by a confused clamour in the house, among the several sorts of prisoners, expressing their awkward sorrows for the poor creatures that were to die, but in a manner extremely differing one from another.", "이에 뒤이어 옥사 안의 여러 부류의 죄수들 사이에서 어수선한 떠들썩함이 일어, 죽음을 앞둔 그 가엾은 자들에 대한 어설픈 슬픔을 저마다 드러냈으나, 그 방식은 서로 극도로 달랐다."], ["Some cried for them; some huzzaed, and wished them a good journey; some damned and cursed those that had brought them to it—that is, meaning the evidence, or prosecutors—many pitying them, and some few, but very few, praying for them.", "더러는 그들을 위해 울었고, 더러는 환호하며 잘 가라고 빌어 주었으며, 더러는 그들을 그 지경에 이르게 한 자들—다시 말해 증인이나 고발인을 두고 하는 말이다—을 저주하고 욕했으며, 많은 이가 그들을 가엾이 여겼고, 몇몇은, 그러나 아주 적은 몇몇은 그들을 위해 기도했다."]], [["There was hardly room for so much composure of mind as was required for me to bless the merciful Providence that had, as it were, snatched me out of the jaws of this destruction.", "이를테면 이 파멸의 아가리에서 나를 낚아채 주신 자비로운 섭리에 감사할 만큼의 마음의 평정을 차릴 여유조차 거의 없었다."], ["I remained, as it were, dumb and silent, overcome with the sense of it, and not able to express what I had in my heart; for the passions on such occasions as these are certainly so agitated as not to be able presently to regulate their own motions.", "나는 이를테면 그것을 자각하는 데 압도되어 벙어리처럼 말없이 있었으며, 내 마음속에 있는 것을 표현할 수가 없었으니, 이런 경우의 격정이란 분명 어찌나 요동치는지 당장은 제 움직임을 다스릴 수 없는 법이기 때문이다."]], [["All the while the poor condemned creatures were preparing to their death, and the ordinary, as they call him, was busy with them, disposing them to submit to their sentence—I say, all this while I was seized with a fit of trembling, as much as I could have been if I had been in the same condition, as to be sure the day before I expected to be; I was so violently agitated by this surprising fit, that I shook as if it had been in the cold fit of an ague, so that I could not speak or look but like one distracted.", "그러는 내내 그 가엾은 사형수들이 죽음을 준비하고 있었고, 그들이 이르는 대로 하자면 전속 교목이 그들에게 선고에 순순히 따르도록 권하며 분주히 오가고 있었으니—내 말은, 그러는 내내 나는 떨림의 발작에 사로잡혀, 마치 내가 똑같은 처지에 있기라도 한 것처럼, 분명 그 전날 내가 그러하리라 예상했던 그만큼이나 떨었다. 나는 이 놀라운 발작에 어찌나 격렬하게 흔들렸던지, 학질의 오한이 든 것처럼 몸을 떨어, 실성한 사람처럼이 아니고서는 말도 못 하고 쳐다보지도 못했다."], ["As soon as they were all put into carts and gone, which, however, I had not courage enough to see—I say, as soon as they were gone, I fell into a fit of crying involuntarily, and without design, but as a mere distemper, and yet so violent, and it held me so long, that I knew not what course to take, nor could I stop, or put a check to it, no, not with all the strength and courage I had.", "그들이 모두 수레에 실려 떠나가자—그것을 차마 지켜볼 용기는 내게 없었지만—내 말은, 그들이 떠나가자마자, 나는 나도 모르게 아무런 작정도 없이, 그저 하나의 발작인 양 울음의 발작에 빠져들었으나 어찌나 격렬한지, 또 어찌나 오래도록 나를 붙들었던지, 나는 어찌해야 할지를 알 수 없었고, 내가 지닌 온 힘과 용기를 다해도 그것을 멈추거나 억누를 수가 없었다."], ["This fit of crying held me near two hours, and, as I believe, held me till they were all out of the world, and then a most humble, penitent, serious kind of joy succeeded; a real transport it was, or passion of joy and thankfulness, but still unable to give vent to it by words, and in this I continued most part of the day.", "이 울음의 발작은 두 시간 가까이 나를 붙들었으니, 내가 믿기로는 그들이 모두 이 세상을 떠날 때까지 나를 붙들었고, 그런 다음 더없이 겸허하고 참회하는 진지한 종류의 기쁨이 그 뒤를 이었다. 그것은 진정한 황홀경, 곧 기쁨과 감사의 격정이었으나, 여전히 말로는 그것을 터뜨릴 수가 없었고, 나는 그날 대부분을 이런 상태로 보냈다."]], [["In the evening the good minister visited me again, and then fell to his usual good discourses.", "저녁이 되어 그 선량한 목사가 다시 나를 찾아와, 늘 하던 그 선한 강론을 시작했다. 그는 나에게는 아직 참회를 위한 여유가 주어졌으나, 그 가엾은 여섯 사람의 처지는 이미 결정되어 이제 구원의 제의가 미치지 못하는 곳에 있게 되었음을 두고 다행이라고 했다."], ["He congratulated my having a space yet allowed me for repentance, whereas the state of those six poor creatures was determined, and they were now past the offers of salvation; he earnestly pressed me to retain the same sentiments of the things of life that I had when I had a view of eternity; and at the end of all told me I should not conclude that all was over, that a reprieve was not a pardon, that he could not yet answer for the effects of it; however, I had this mercy, that I had more time given me, and that it was my business to improve that time.", "그는 내가 영원을 내다보았을 때 삶의 일들에 대해 품었던 그 똑같은 마음가짐을 간직하라고 간곡히 권했으며, 그 모든 말 끝에는 내가 모든 것이 끝났다고 단정해서는 안 된다고, 형 집행 유예가 사면은 아니라고, 자기가 아직 그 결과를 장담할 수는 없다고 일렀다. 그러나 나는 더 많은 시간이 주어진 이 자비를 입었으니, 그 시간을 잘 쓰는 것이 내 할 일이라고 했다."]], [["This discourse, though very seasonable, left a kind of sadness on my heart, as if I might expect the affair would have a tragical issue still, which, however, he had no certainty of; and I did not indeed, at that time, question him about it, he having said that he would do his utmost to bring it to a good end, and that he hoped he might, but he would not have me be secure; and the consequence proved that he had reason for what he said.", "이 강론은 매우 시의적절하기는 했으나, 마치 그 일이 여전히 비극적인 결말을 맞이할 수도 있을 것처럼 내 마음에 일종의 슬픔을 남겼는데, 다만 그것에 대해 그는 아무런 확신이 없었고, 나 역시 그때는 정말로 그것에 대해 그에게 캐묻지 않았으니, 그가 좋은 결말로 이끌기 위해 최선을 다하겠다고, 또 그러기를 바란다고는 했으나 내가 안심하기를 바라지는 않는다고 말했기 때문이며, 그 결과는 그가 한 말에 그럴 만한 까닭이 있었음을 증명해 주었다."]], [["It was about a fortnight after this that I had some just apprehensions that I should be included in the next dead warrant at the ensuing sessions; and it was not without great difficulty, and at last a humble petition for transportation, that I avoided it, so ill was I beholding to fame, and so prevailing was the fatal report of being an old offender; though in that they did not do me strict justice, for I was not in the sense of the law an old offender, whatever I was in the eye of the judge, for I had never been before them in a judicial way before; so the judges could not charge me with being an old offender, but the Recorder was pleased to represent my case as he thought fit.", "그러고 나서 약 두 주 뒤에, 나는 다가오는 개정기(開廷期)의 다음 사형집행영장에 내가 포함되리라는 어느 정도 근거 있는 염려를 품게 되었고, 여간한 어려움이 아니고서는, 그리고 마침내 유배형을 청하는 겸허한 탄원이 아니고서는 그것을 면할 수 없었으니, 나는 명성에 그토록 못된 빚을 졌고, 상습범이라는 그 치명적인 소문이 그토록 위세를 떨쳤던 것이다. 다만 그 점에서 그들이 내게 엄정한 정의를 베푼 것은 아니었으니, 재판관의 눈에 내가 무엇으로 비쳤든 간에 법의 의미로 보자면 나는 상습범이 아니었고, 전에 사법적인 방식으로 그들 앞에 선 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이다. 그러므로 재판관들이 나를 상습범이라고 몰아붙일 수는 없었으나, 형사법원 기록관이 제멋대로 내 사건을 그렇게 진술했던 것이다."]], [["I had now a certainty of life indeed, but with the hard conditions of being ordered for transportation, which indeed was hard condition in itself, but not when comparatively considered; and therefore I shall make no comments upon the sentence, nor upon the choice I was put to.", "이제 나는 실로 목숨을 부지하게 되었으나, 유배형에 처해진다는 가혹한 조건이 따랐으니, 그것은 그 자체로 보면 실로 가혹한 조건이었으나 견주어 헤아려 보면 그렇지 않았다. 그러므로 나는 그 선고에 대해서도, 내게 주어진 선택에 대해서도 아무 논평을 하지 않으련다."], ["We shall all choose anything rather than death, especially when ’tis attended with an uncomfortable prospect beyond it, which was my case.", "우리는 누구나 죽음보다는 무엇이든 가리지 않고 택하게 마련이니, 더구나 그 너머에 음울한 전망이 따르는 죽음이라면 더욱 그러한데, 그것이 바로 내 경우였다."]], [["The good minister, whose interest, though a stranger to me, had obtained me the reprieve, mourned sincerely for this part.", "내게는 낯선 사람이면서도 그 영향력으로 형 집행 유예를 얻어 준 그 선량한 목사는 이 대목을 두고 진심으로 슬퍼했다."], ["He was in hopes, he said, that I should have ended my days under the influence of good instruction, that I should not have been turned loose again among such a wretched crew as they generally are, who are thus sent abroad, where, as he said, I must have more than ordinary secret assistance from the grace of God, if I did not turn as wicked again as ever.", "그는 내가 좋은 가르침의 영향 아래 생을 마치게 되기를 바랐다고, 이렇게 외국으로 보내지는 자들이 으레 그러한 그 비참한 무리 가운데로 다시 풀려나지 않기를 바랐다고 했으며, 거기서는 하느님의 은총으로부터 예사롭지 않은 은밀한 도움을 받지 않는 한, 내가 다시 전처럼 사악해지고 말 것이라고 했다."]], [["I have not for a good while mentioned my governess, who had during most, if not all, of this part been dangerously sick, and being in as near a view of death by her disease as I was by my sentence, was a great penitent—I say, I have not mentioned her, nor indeed did I see her in all this time; but being now recovering, and just able to come abroad, she came to see me.", "내가 한동안 내 후견인에 대해 언급하지 않았는데, 그녀는 이 부분의 거의 내내, 아니 어쩌면 줄곧 위중하게 앓아, 내가 선고로 인해 죽음에 다가선 만큼이나 자기 병으로 인해 죽음에 가까이 다가서 있었던 터라, 크나큰 참회자가 되어 있었다. 내 말은, 나는 그녀에 대해 언급하지 않았고, 실로 이 모든 동안 그녀를 보지도 못했다는 것이다. 그러나 이제 회복되어 겨우 바깥출입을 할 수 있게 되자 그녀가 나를 보러 왔다."]], [["I told her my condition, and what a different flux and reflux of tears and hopes I had been agitated with; I told her what I had escaped, and upon what terms; and she was present when the minister expressed his fears of my relapsing into wickedness upon my falling into the wretched companies that are generally transported.", "나는 그녀에게 내 처지를, 그리고 내가 눈물과 희망의 어떤 밀물과 썰물에 시달려 왔는지를 이야기했고, 무엇을 면했으며 어떤 조건으로 그러했는지를 들려주었다. 목사가 내가 으레 유배되는 그 비참한 무리에 휩쓸려 다시 사악함으로 빠져들까 염려하는 말을 했을 때 그녀가 그 자리에 함께 있었다."], ["Indeed I had a melancholy reflection upon it in my own mind, for I knew what a dreadful gang was always sent away together, and I said to my governess that the good minister’s fears were not without cause.", "실로 나도 내 마음속으로 그것을 두고 우울하게 곱씹었으니, 늘 얼마나 끔찍한 패거리가 한꺼번에 보내지는지를 알고 있었기 때문이며, 나는 후견인에게 그 선량한 목사의 염려가 까닭 없는 것이 아니라고 말했다. \"아니에요, 아니에요.\""], ["“Well, well,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “but I hope you will not be tempted with such a horrid example as that.”", "\"하지만 그런 끔찍한 본보기 따위에 당신이 넘어가지는 않으리라 믿어요.\""], ["And as soon as the minister was gone, she told me she would not have me discouraged, for perhaps ways and means might be found out to dispose of me in a particular way, by myself, of which she would talk further to me afterward.", "그리고 목사가 가자마자, 그녀는 내가 낙심하지 말기를 바란다고, 어쩌면 나를 특별한 방식으로, 나 혼자 따로 처분할 길과 방도가 찾아질지도 모른다고, 그것에 대해서는 나중에 더 이야기하겠다고 했다."]], [["I looked earnestly at her, and I thought she looked more cheerful than she usually had done, and I entertained immediately a thousand notions of being delivered, but could not for my life image the methods, or think of one that was in the least feasible; but I was too much concerned in it to let her go from me without explaining herself, which, though she was very loth to do, yet my importunity prevailed, and, while I was still pressing, she answered me in a few words, thus: “Why, you have money, have you not?", "나는 그녀를 진지하게 바라보았고, 그녀가 여느 때보다 더 명랑해 보인다고 생각했으며, 곧장 풀려나리라는 천 가지 생각을 품었으나, 도무지 그 방법을 그려 볼 수도, 조금이라도 실현 가능한 길 하나를 떠올릴 수도 없었다. 그러나 나는 그 일에 너무도 마음이 쏠려 있던 터라 그녀가 자기 뜻을 밝히지 않은 채 내게서 떠나가게 둘 수가 없었으니, 그녀는 몹시 내키지 않아 했으나 내 간청이 이겨, 내가 여전히 다그치는 동안 그녀는 몇 마디 말로 이렇게 대답했다."], ["Did you ever know one in your life that was transported and had a hundred pounds in his pocket, I’ll warrant you, child?”", "\"아니, 당신, 돈이 있잖아요, 안 그래요? 살면서 유배되는 사람이 주머니에 백 파운드를 지니고 있는 걸 본 적이 있어요, 장담컨대, 응?\""], ["says she.", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["I understood her presently, but told her I would leave all that to her, but I saw no room to hope for anything but a strict execution of the order, and as it was a severity that was esteemed a mercy, there was no doubt but it would be strictly observed.", "나는 곧 그녀의 뜻을 알아차렸으나, 그 일은 모두 그녀에게 맡기겠다고, 다만 명령이 엄정하게 집행되는 것 외에는 무엇을 바랄 여지도 보이지 않는다고, 또 그것이 자비로 여겨지는 가혹한 처분인 만큼 분명 엄정하게 지켜지리라는 데 의심의 여지가 없다고 말했다."], ["She said no more but this: “We will try what can be done,”", "그녀는 이 말밖에는 더 하지 않았다. \"할 수 있는 일을 한번 해 봅시다.\""], ["and so we parted for that night.", "그렇게 우리는 그날 밤 헤어졌다."]], [["I lay in the prison near fifteen weeks after this order for transportation was signed.", "나는 이 유배형 명령에 서명이 떨어진 뒤로 거의 열다섯 주를 더 옥에 있었다."], ["What the reason of it was, I know not, but at the end of this time I was put on board of a ship in the Thames, and with me a gang of thirteen as hardened vile creatures as ever Newgate produced in my time; and it would really well take up a history longer than mine to describe the degrees of impudence and audacious villainy that those thirteen were arrived to, and the manner of their behaviour in the voyage; of which I have a very diverting account by me, which the captain of the ship who carried them over gave me the minutes of, and which he caused his mate to write down at large.", "그 까닭이 무엇이었는지는 모르겠으나, 이 기간이 끝날 무렵 나는 템스강에 떠 있는 한 배에 태워졌고, 나와 함께 내 시절 뉴게이트가 배출한 가장 모질고 비루한 무리 열셋이 함께 실렸다. 그 열셋이 다다른 뻔뻔함과 대담무쌍한 악행의 정도와, 항해 중에 그들이 보인 행실의 양상을 묘사하자면 실로 내 이야기보다 더 긴 이력이 필요할 것인데, 나는 그것에 대한 매우 흥미로운 기록을 지니고 있으니, 그들을 실어 나른 그 배의 선장이 내게 그 요점을 일러 주고, 자기 항해사를 시켜 그것을 소상히 받아 적게 한 것이다."]], [["It may perhaps be thought trifling to enter here into a relation of all the little incidents which attended me in this interval of my circumstances; I mean, between the final order of my transportation and the time of my going on board the ship; and I am too near the end of my story to allow room for it; but something relating to me and my Lancashire husband I must not omit.", "내 처지가 이렇게 바뀌어 가는 이 막간에 내게 일어난 온갖 자질구레한 사건들을 여기서 죄다 늘어놓는 것은 어쩌면 부질없는 일로 여겨질지도 모른다. 내 말은, 내 유배에 대한 최종 명령과 내가 배에 오른 때 사이의 일들 말이다. 게다가 나는 내 이야기의 끝에 너무 가까이 와 있어 그럴 여유가 없다. 그러나 나와 내 랭커셔 남편에 관련된 무언가만은 빠뜨릴 수 없다."]], [["He had, as I have observed already, been carried from the master’s side of the ordinary prison into the press-yard, with three of his comrades, for they found another to add to them after some time; here, for what reason I knew not, they were kept in custody without being brought to trial almost three months.", "그는, 내가 이미 말했듯이, 일반 감옥의 미결수 쪽에서 그의 동료 셋과 함께 프레스야드로 옮겨졌는데, 얼마 뒤 거기에 한 사람이 더 보태졌다. 무슨 까닭에서인지는 모르겠으나, 그들은 거의 석 달 동안 재판에 회부되지도 않은 채 구금되어 있었다."], ["It seems they found means to bribe or buy off some of those who were expected to come in against them, and they wanted evidence for some time to convict them.", "보아하니 그들은 자기들에게 불리하게 나설 것으로 예상되던 자들 가운데 몇몇을 매수하거나 돈으로 입막음할 방도를 찾아낸 듯했고, 그래서 한동안 그들을 유죄로 만들 증거가 모자랐던 것이다."], ["After some puzzle on this account, at first they made a shift to get proof enough against two of them to carry them off; but the other two, of which my Lancashire husband was one, lay still in suspense.", "이 때문에 처음에는 한참을 헤맨 끝에, 그들 중 둘에 대해서는 처형할 만한 증거를 그럭저럭 마련해 냈으나, 나머지 둘은—내 랭커셔 남편이 그중 하나였다—여전히 미결 상태로 남아 있었다."], ["They had, I think, one positive evidence against each of them, but the law strictly obliging them to have two witnesses, they could make nothing of it.", "내 생각에 그들 각각에 대해 확실한 증인이 하나씩은 있었으나, 법이 증인 둘을 두도록 엄격히 규정하고 있어 아무것도 어찌하지 못했다."], ["Yet it seems they were resolved not to part with the men neither, not doubting but a further evidence would at last come in; and in order to this, I think publication was made, that such prisoners being taken, any one that had been robbed by them might come to the prison and see them.", "그러나 보아하니 그들은 그 사람들을 놓아줄 마음도 없어, 끝내는 추가 증거가 들어오리라 의심치 않았던 것이며, 이를 위해 내 생각에 그러한 죄수들이 잡혀 있으니 그들에게 강도를 당한 자는 누구든 옥으로 와서 그들을 볼 수 있다는 공고가 났던 것이다."]], [["I took this opportunity to satisfy my curiosity, pretending that I had been robbed in the Dunstable coach, and that I would go to see the two highwaymen.", "나는 이 기회를 틈타 내 호기심을 채우기로 하여, 던스터블 마차에서 강도를 당했으니 그 두 노상강도를 보러 가겠다고 꾸며 댔다."], ["But when I came into the press-yard, I so disguised myself, and muffled my face up so, that he could see little of me, and consequently knew nothing of who I was; and when I came back, I said publicly that I knew them very well.", "그러나 프레스야드로 들어갈 때 나는 어찌나 변장을 하고 얼굴을 꽁꽁 싸맸던지, 그가 나를 거의 볼 수 없었고, 따라서 내가 누구인지 전혀 알아채지 못했다. 그리고 돌아와서는, 나는 그들을 아주 잘 안다고 공공연히 말했다."]], [["Immediately it was rumoured all over the prison that Moll Flanders would turn evidence against one of the highwaymen, and that I was to come off by it from the sentence of transportation.", "이내 옥 전체에 몰 플랜더스가 그 노상강도 가운데 하나에게 불리한 증언을 할 것이며, 그로써 유배형 선고를 면하게 되리라는 소문이 퍼졌다."]], [["They heard of it, and immediately my husband desired to see this Mrs. Flanders that knew him so well, and was to be an evidence against him; and accordingly I had leave given to go to him.", "그들이 그 소문을 들었고, 곧장 내 남편이 자기를 그토록 잘 알고 자기에게 불리한 증인이 되려 한다는 그 플랜더스 부인을 보고 싶다고 청했으며, 그리하여 나는 그에게 가도 좋다는 허락을 받았다."], ["I dressed myself up as well as the best clothes that I suffered myself ever to appear in there would allow me, and went to the press-yard, but had for some time a hood over my face.", "나는 거기서 내가 입고 나선 적 있는 가장 좋은 옷이 허락하는 한 잘 차려입고 프레스야드로 갔으나, 한동안은 얼굴에 두건을 두르고 있었다."], ["He said little to me at first, but asked me if I knew him.", "그는 처음에는 내게 별말을 하지 않다가, 자기를 아느냐고 물었다."], ["I told him, Yes, very well; but as I concealed my face, so I counterfeited my voice, that he had not the least guess at who I was.", "나는 그렇다, 아주 잘 안다고 말했으나, 얼굴을 가린 것처럼 목소리도 꾸며 내, 그는 내가 누구인지 조금도 짐작하지 못했다."], ["He asked me where I had seen him.", "그는 어디서 자기를 보았느냐고 물었다."], ["I told him between Dunstable and Brickhill; but turning to the keeper that stood by, I asked if I might not be admitted to talk with him alone.", "나는 던스터블과 브릭힐 사이에서라고 말했고, 곁에 서 있던 간수에게 돌아서서, 그와 단둘이 이야기하도록 해 줄 수 없겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He said Yes, yes, as much as I pleased, and so very civilly withdrew.", "그는 그러라고, 그러라고, 원하는 만큼 그러라고 하고는 아주 정중하게 물러갔다."]], [["As soon as he was gone, I had shut the door, I threw off my hood, and bursting out into tears, “My dear,”", "그가 가자마자 나는 문을 닫고 두건을 벗어 던지고는, 울음을 터뜨리며 말했다. \"여보, 나를 모르겠어요?\""], ["says I, “do you not know me?”", "그는 얼굴이 창백해지더니, 벼락이라도 맞은 사람처럼 말없이 서 있다가, 그 놀라움을 도무지 가라앉히지 못한 채 이 말밖에는 더 하지 못했다."], ["He turned pale, and stood speechless, like one thunderstruck, and, not able to conquer the surprise, said no more but this, “Let me sit down”; and sitting down by a table, he laid his elbow upon the table, and leaning his head on his hand, fixed his eyes on the ground as one stupid.", "\"좀 앉읍시다.\" 그러고는 탁자 옆에 앉아 탁자 위에 팔꿈치를 괴고, 손에 머리를 기댄 채 멍한 사람처럼 두 눈을 바닥에 박았다."], ["I cried so vehemently, on the other hand, that it was a good while ere I could speak any more; but after I had given some vent to my passion by tears, I repeated the same words, “My dear, do you not know me?”", "한편 나는 어찌나 격렬하게 울었던지 한참이 지나서야 다시 말을 꺼낼 수 있었다. 그러나 눈물로 격정을 어느 정도 쏟아 낸 뒤에 나는 똑같은 말을 되풀이했다."], ["At which he answered, Yes, and said no more a good while.", "\"여보, 나를 모르겠어요?\" 그러자 그는 그렇다고 대답하고는 한참 동안 더 말이 없었다."]], [["After some time continuing in the surprise, as above, he cast up his eyes towards me and said, “How could you be so cruel?”", "앞서 말한 그 놀라움 속에 얼마간 잠겨 있다가, 그는 나를 향해 눈을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"어떻게 그리도 잔인할 수 있소?\""], ["I did not readily understand what he meant; and I answered, “How can you call me cruel?", "나는 그가 무슨 뜻으로 한 말인지 얼른 알아듣지 못해 이렇게 대답했다. \"어떻게 나를 잔인하다고 하시는 거예요?"], ["What have I been cruel to you in?”", "내가 당신에게 무슨 잔인한 짓을 했다고요?\""], ["“To come to me,”", "\"이런 곳으로 나를 찾아오는 게,\""], ["says he, “in such a place as this, is it not to insult me?", "그가 말했다. \"나를 모욕하는 게 아니고 무엇이오?"], ["I have not robbed you, at least not on the highway.”", "나는 당신에게서 빼앗은 게 없소, 적어도 노상에서는 말이오.\""]], [["I perceived by this that he knew nothing of the miserable circumstances I was in, and thought that, having got some intelligence of his being there, I had come to upbraid him with his leaving me.", "나는 이로써 그가 내가 처한 비참한 사정을 전혀 모르고 있으며, 자기가 거기 있다는 어떤 소식을 듣고서 내가 그를 버리고 떠난 일을 두고 그를 책망하러 온 줄로 여기고 있음을 알아차렸다."], ["But I had too much to say to him to be affronted, and told him in few words, that I was far from coming to insult him, but at best I came to condole mutually; that he would be easily satisfied that I had no such view, when I should tell him that my condition was worse than his, and that many ways.", "그러나 나는 그에게 할 말이 너무 많았던 터라 화를 낼 수도 없어, 몇 마디로 그에게 말하기를, 나는 결코 그를 모욕하러 온 것이 아니라, 잘해야 서로의 처지를 함께 슬퍼하러 온 것이며, 내 처지가 그의 처지보다, 그것도 여러모로 더 나쁘다고 말해 주면 내게 그런 속셈이 없다는 것을 쉬이 납득하리라고 했다."], ["He looked a little concerned at the general expression of my condition being worse than his, but, with a kind smile, looked a little wildly, and said, “How can that be?", "그는 내 처지가 자기보다 더 나쁘다는 그 막연한 표현에 다소 걱정스러운 빛을 띠었으나, 다정한 미소를 지으며 다소 얼떨떨한 얼굴로 말했다. \"그게 어찌 그럴 수 있소?"], ["When you see me fettered, and in Newgate, and two of my companions executed already, how can your your condition be worse than mine?”", "내가 족쇄를 차고 뉴게이트에 갇혀 있고, 내 동료 둘은 이미 처형되었는데, 당신 처지가 어떻게 내 처지보다 더 나쁠 수 있단 말이오?\""]], [["“Come, my dear,”", "\"이봐요, 여보.\" 내가 말했다."], ["says I, “we have a long piece of work to do, if I should be to relate, or you to hear, my unfortunate history; but if you are disposed to hear it, you will soon conclude with me that my condition is worse than yours.”", "\"내가 내 불운한 이력을 들려주거나 당신이 그걸 듣자면 한참이나 긴 이야기가 될 거예요. 하지만 당신이 들을 마음이 있다면, 내 처지가 당신보다 더 나쁘다는 데 곧 나와 같은 결론에 이르게 될 거예요.\""], ["“How is that possible,”", "\"그게 어떻게 가능하단 말이오,\" 그가 다시 말했다."], ["says he again, “when I expect to be cast for my life the very next sessions?”", "\"나는 바로 다음 개정기에 사형을 선고받을 처지인데?\" \"그래요,\""], ["“Yes,”", "내가 말했다."], ["says I, “’tis very possible, when I shall tell you that I have been cast for my life three sessions ago, and am under sentence of death; is not my case worse than yours?”", "\"그게 아주 가능한 일이에요, 내가 세 번의 개정기 전에 이미 사형을 선고받았고 지금 사형 선고 아래 있다고 당신에게 말하면 말이에요. 내 처지가 당신보다 더 나쁘지 않은가요?\""]], [["Then indeed, he stood silent again, like one struck dumb, and after a while he starts up.", "그제야 그는 실로 다시 벙어리가 된 사람처럼 말없이 서 있다가, 잠시 뒤 벌떡 일어섰다. \"불행한 부부로구려!\""], ["“Unhappy couple!”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he. “How can this be possible?”", "\"어떻게 이런 일이 있을 수 있단 말이오?\""], ["I took him by the hand.", "나는 그의 손을 잡았다."], ["“Come, my dear,”", "\"이봐요, 여보.\""], ["said I, “sit down, and let us compare our sorrows.", "내가 말했다. \"앉아서 우리의 슬픔을 견주어 봅시다."], ["I am a prisoner in this very house, and in much worse circumstances than you, and you will be satisfied I do not come to insult you, when I tell you the particulars.”", "나는 바로 이 옥의 죄수이고, 당신보다 훨씬 더 나쁜 처지에 있어요. 내가 그 자초지종을 말해 주면, 당신은 내가 당신을 모욕하러 온 게 아니라는 걸 납득하게 될 거예요.\""], ["And with this we sat down together, and I told him so much of my story as I thought was convenient, bringing it at last to my being reduced to great poverty, and representing myself as fallen into some company that led me to relieve my distresses by way that I had been utterly unacquainted with, and that they making an attempt at a tradesman’s house, I was seized upon for having been but just at the door, the maid-servant pulling me in; that I neither had broke any lock nor taken anything away, and that notwithstanding that, I was brought in guilty and sentenced to die; but that the judges, having been made sensible of the hardship of my circumstances, had obtained leave to remit the sentence upon my consenting to be transported.", "그리고 이렇게 우리는 함께 앉았고, 나는 내 이야기 가운데 적절하다 여겨지는 만큼을 그에게 들려주어, 마침내 내가 극심한 가난에 내몰린 데까지 이르게 했고, 내가 어떤 무리에 휩쓸려 들어가 도무지 익숙지 않은 방식으로 내 곤궁을 면하려 했다고, 그들이 어떤 상인의 집을 털려 했을 때 나는 막 그 문 앞에 있었다는 이유로—하녀가 나를 안으로 끌어들였던 것이다—붙들렸다고, 나는 자물쇠 하나 부수지 않고 아무것도 가져가지 않았는데도 유죄로 끌려가 사형을 선고받았으나, 재판관들이 내 처지의 가혹함을 알게 되어 내가 유배되는 데 동의하는 조건으로 그 선고를 감면받을 허가를 얻어 냈다고 나 자신을 그려 보였다."]], [["I told him I fared the worse for being taken in the prison for one Moll Flanders, who was a famous successful thief, that all of them had heard of, but none of them had ever seen; but that, as he knew well, was none of my name.", "나는 그에게, 내가 옥에서 몰 플랜더스라는 자로 오인된 탓에 더 나쁜 처지에 놓였다고 말했으니, 그자는 그들 모두가 소문은 들었으되 누구도 본 적은 없는, 이름난 데다 성공한 도둑이었으나, 당신도 잘 알다시피 그건 내 이름이 아니었던 것이다."], ["But I placed all to the account of my ill fortune, and that under this name I was dealt with as an old offender, though this was the first thing they had ever known of me.", "그러나 나는 그 모든 것을 내 불운의 탓으로 돌렸으니, 이 이름 아래 나는 상습범으로 다루어졌으되 실은 이것이 그들이 나에 대해 알게 된 첫 번째 일이었다."], ["I gave him a long particular of things that had befallen me since I saw him, but I told him if I had seen him since he might think I had, and then gave him an account how I had seen him at Brickhill; how furiously he was pursued, and how, by giving an account that I knew him, and that he was a very honest gentleman, one Mr. ——, the hue-and-cry was stopped, and the high constable went back again.", "나는 그를 본 뒤로 내게 닥쳤던 일들을 길게 낱낱이 들려주었는데, 다만 그 뒤로 그를 본 적이 있었다면 그가 내가 그랬으리라 여길 수도 있겠다고 말한 다음, 내가 브릭힐에서 그를 본 일을 들려주었으니, 그가 얼마나 맹렬히 쫓기고 있었는지, 그리고 내가 그를 알며 그가 아무개 씨라는 매우 정직한 신사라고 진술함으로써 어떻게 추격이 멈추고 치안 책임자가 발길을 돌렸는지를 들려주었다."]], [["He listened most attentively to all my story, and smiled at most of the particulars, being all of them petty matters, and infinitely below what he had been at the head of; but when I came to the story of Brickhill, he was surprised.", "그는 내 이야기를 죄다 더없이 귀 기울여 들으며 그 자질구레한 대목 대부분에 미소를 지었으니, 그것들이 모두 사소한 일들이요, 그가 우두머리 노릇을 하던 일들에 견주면 한없이 하찮은 것들이었기 때문이다. 그러나 내가 브릭힐 이야기에 이르자 그는 놀라워했다."], ["“And was it you, my dear,”", "\"아니, 그게 당신이었소, 여보,\""], ["said he, “that gave the check to the mob that was at our heels there, at Brickhill?”", "그가 말했다. \"거기 브릭힐에서 우리 뒤를 바짝 쫓던 그 무리를 막아 준 게?\""], ["“Yes,”", "\"그래요,\""], ["said I, “it was I indeed.”", "내가 말했다. \"그게 정말 나였어요.\""], ["And then I told him the particulars which I had observed him there.", "그러고 나서 나는 거기서 그를 두고 눈여겨본 자초지종을 들려주었다. \"그렇다면,\""], ["“Why, then,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he, “it was you that saved my life at that time, and I am glad I owe my life to you, for I will pay the debt to you now, and I’ll deliver you from the present condition you are in, or I will die in the attempt.”", "\"그때 내 목숨을 구해 준 게 당신이었구려, 그리고 내가 당신에게 목숨을 빚진 것이 기쁘오, 이제 내가 당신에게 그 빚을 갚을 테니 말이오. 당신을 지금 처한 이 처지에서 구해 내든지, 아니면 그러다 죽든지 하겠소.\""]], [["I told him, by no means; it was a risk too great, not worth his running the hazard of, and for a life not worth his saving.", "나는 그에게 결코 그러지 말라고, 그것은 너무 큰 위험이라 그가 무릅쓸 만한 것이 못 되며, 구할 가치도 없는 목숨을 위해 그럴 일이 아니라고 말했다."], ["’Twas no matter for that, he said, it was a life worth all the world to him; a life that had given him a new life; “for,”", "그런 건 아무래도 좋다고, 그것은 그에게 온 세상보다 값진 목숨이라고, 자기에게 새 삶을 준 목숨이라고 그는 말했다. \"왜냐하면,\""], ["says he, “I was never in real danger of being taken, but that time, till the last minute when I was taken.”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 잡히던 그 마지막 순간 말고는, 그때 그 한 번 말고는 실제로 잡힐 위험에 처한 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이오.\""], ["Indeed, he told me his danger then lay in his believing he had not been pursued that way; for they had gone off from Hockey quite another way, and had come over the enclosed country into Brickhill, not by the road, and were sure they had not been seen by anybody.", "실로 그는 그때 자기 위험은 그쪽 길로 추격당하지 않았으리라 믿었던 데 있었다고 말했으니, 그들이 호키에서 전혀 다른 길로 빠져나가, 큰길이 아니라 울타리 친 들판을 가로질러 브릭힐로 들어왔던 터라, 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않았으리라 확신했던 것이다."]], [["Here he gave me a long history of his life, which indeed would make a very strange history, and be infinitely diverting.", "여기서 그는 내게 자기 삶의 긴 내력을 들려주었는데, 실로 그것은 매우 기이한 이력이 될 만했고 한없이 흥미로울 것이었다. 그는 나와 결혼하기 약 열두 해 전부터 노상강도 일에 들어섰다고, 그를 오라비라 부르던 그 여자는 사실 그의 누이도, 그와 아무런 혈연도 아니라 그들 패거리에 속한 자였다고, 그와 연락을 주고받으며 늘 시내에 살았고 아는 사람이 두루 많았다고 말했다."], ["He told me he took to the road about twelve years before he married me; that the woman which called him brother was not really his sister, or any kin to him, but one that belonged to their gang, and who, keeping correspondence with him, lived always in town, having good store of acquaintance; that she gave them a perfect intelligence of persons going out of town, and that they had made several good booties by her correspondence; that she thought she had fixed a fortune for him when she brought me to him, but happened to be disappointed, which he really could not blame her for; that if it had been his good luck that I had had the estate, which she was informed I had, he had resolved to leave off the road and live a retired, sober life but never to appear in public till some general pardon had been passed, or till he could, for money, have got his name into some particular pardon, that so he might have been perfectly easy; but that, as it had proved otherwise, he was obliged to put off his equipage and take up the old trade again.", "그녀가 그들에게 시내를 떠나는 사람들에 대한 정확한 정보를 주어, 그녀의 연통 덕분에 그들이 여러 차례 짭짤한 노획물을 거두었다고, 그녀는 나를 그에게 데려오면서 그를 위해 한밑천 잡아 주었다고 여겼으나 그만 어긋나고 말았는데, 그것을 그가 정말로 그녀 탓으로 돌릴 수는 없는 노릇이라고 했다. 만약 그녀가 들은 대로 내가 재산을 지니고 있었더라면 그것이 그의 행운이 되어, 그는 노상 일을 그만두고 은둔하여 점잖게 살기로 마음먹었으되, 일반사면령이 내리거나 돈으로 자기 이름을 어떤 특별사면에 올려 넣어 완전히 마음 편해질 수 있을 때까지는 결코 공공연히 모습을 드러내지 않으려 했다고, 그러나 사정이 달리 풀린 터라 마차며 거마(車馬)를 처분하고 다시 옛 직업으로 돌아설 수밖에 없었다고 했다."]], [["He gave me a long account of some of his adventures, and particularly one when he robbed the West Chester coaches near Lichfield, when he got a very great booty; and after that, how he robbed five graziers, in the west, going to Burford Fair in Wiltshire to buy sheep.", "그는 자기 모험담 가운데 몇 가지를 길게 들려주었는데, 특히 한번은 리치필드 근처에서 웨스트체스터 마차들을 털어 매우 큰 노획물을 거둔 일과, 그 뒤에 윌트셔의 버퍼드 장으로 양을 사러 가던 서부의 목축업자 다섯을 턴 일이 있었다."], ["He told me he got so much money on those two occasions, that if he had known where to have found me, he would certainly have embraced my proposal of going with me to Virginia, or to have settled in a plantation on some other parts of the English colonies in America.", "그는 이 두 차례에 어찌나 많은 돈을 거두었던지, 만약 나를 어디서 찾을 수 있을지만 알았더라면, 나와 함께 버지니아로 가거나 아메리카에 있는 다른 어느 영국 식민지의 농장에 자리 잡자던 내 제안을 분명 기꺼이 받아들였으리라고 말했다."]], [["He told me he wrote two or three letters to me, directed according to my order, but heard nothing from me.", "그는 내가 일러 준 주소대로 부쳐 내게 두세 통의 편지를 썼으나 나로부터 아무 소식도 듣지 못했다고 말했다."], ["This I indeed knew to be true, but the letters coming to my hand in the time of my latter husband, I could do nothing in it, and therefore chose to give no answer, that so he might rather believe they had miscarried.", "이것은 실로 사실임을 내가 알고 있었으나, 그 편지들이 내 나중 남편과 살던 때에 내 손에 들어온 터라 나로서는 어찌할 도리가 없어, 차라리 아무 답도 하지 않기로 했던 것이니, 그래야 그가 편지들이 전해지지 못한 것이려니 여기게 될 것이었기 때문이다."]], [["Being thus disappointed, he said, he carried on the old trade ever since, though when he had gotten so much money, he said, he did not run such desperate risks as he did before.", "이렇게 어긋나 버린 터라, 그는 그 뒤로 줄곧 옛 일을 이어 왔다고 말했으나, 그만큼 많은 돈을 거둔 뒤로는 전처럼 그토록 무모한 위험을 무릅쓰지는 않았다고 했다. 그러고 나서 그는 돈을 너무 모질게 내놓지 않으려는 신사들과 노상에서 벌인 여러 차례의 거칠고 필사적인 격투에 대해 어느 정도 들려주었고, 자기가 입은 상처 몇 군데를 보여 주었다."], ["Then he gave me some account of several hard and desperate encounters which he had with gentlemen on the road, who parted too hardly with their money, and showed me some wounds he had received; and he had one or two very terrible wounds indeed, as particularly one by a pistol bullet, which broke his arm, and another with a sword, which ran him quite through the body, but that missing his vitals, he was cured again; one of his comrades having kept with him so faithfully, and so friendly, as that he assisted him in riding near eighty miles before his arm was set, and then got a surgeon in a considerable city, remote from that place where it was done, pretending they were gentlemen travelling towards Carlisle and that they had been attacked on the road by highwaymen, and that one of them had shot him into the arm and broke the bone.", "그에게는 실로 매우 끔찍한 상처가 한두 군데 있었으니, 특히 권총 탄환에 팔이 부러진 상처와, 칼에 몸을 완전히 꿰뚫린 상처가 있었으나, 그것이 급소를 비껴간 덕에 그는 다시 나았다. 그의 동료 하나가 어찌나 충실하고 다정하게 그의 곁을 지켰던지, 그가 팔을 접합하기 전에 팔십 마일 가까이 말을 타고 가도록 도왔으며, 그런 다음 그 일이 벌어진 곳에서 멀리 떨어진 상당한 규모의 도시에서 외과의를 불러, 자기들은 칼라일 쪽으로 여행하는 신사들인데 노상에서 노상강도들에게 습격당해, 그중 하나가 자기 팔을 쏘아 뼈를 부러뜨렸다고 둘러댔다는 것이다."]], [["This, he said, his friend managed so well, that they were not suspected at all, but lay still till he was perfectly cured.", "이것을 그의 벗이 어찌나 능란하게 처리했던지, 그들은 전혀 의심받지 않은 채, 그가 완전히 나을 때까지 가만히 숨어 지냈다고 그는 말했다."], ["He gave me so many distinct accounts of his adventures, that it is with great reluctance that I decline the relating them; but I consider that this is my own story, not his.", "그가 내게 자기 모험담을 어찌나 많은 개별 이야기로 들려주었던지, 나는 그것들을 옮기기를 단념하는 것이 몹시 아쉽다. 그러나 나는 이것이 그의 이야기가 아니라 내 이야기임을 헤아린다."]], [["I then inquired into the circumstances of his present case at that time, and what it was he expected when he came to be tried.", "그러고 나서 나는 그때 그가 처한 사건의 정황과, 재판에 회부될 때 그가 무엇을 예상하는지를 캐물었다. 그는 자기에게 불리한 증거가 전혀 없거나 있어도 아주 미미하다고 말했으니, 그들 모두가 함께 기소된 세 건의 강도 가운데, 자기는 단 한 건에만 가담했던 것이 다행이었고, 게다가 그 사건에 대해 댈 수 있는 증인이 단 하나뿐이라 그것으로는 부족했기 때문이다."], ["He told me that they had no evidence against him, or but very little; for that of three robberies, which they were all charged with, it was his good fortune that he was but in one of them, and that there was but one witness to be had for that fact, which was not sufficient, but that it was expected some others would come in against him; that he thought indeed, when he first saw me, that I had been one that came of that errand; but that if somebody came in against him, he hoped he should be cleared; that he had had some intimation, that if he would submit to transport himself, he might be admitted to it without a trial, but that he could not think of it with any temper, and thought he could much easier submit to be hanged.", "다만 다른 몇몇이 자기에게 불리하게 나서리라 예상된다고, 처음 나를 보았을 때는 실로 내가 그런 볼일로 온 자인 줄 여겼다고, 그러나 누군가 자기에게 불리하게 나선다 해도 자기는 무죄로 풀려나기를 바란다고 했다. 만약 자청하여 스스로 유배를 떠나는 데 응하면 재판 없이 그것이 허락될 수도 있다는 언질을 받았으나, 도무지 마음 편히 그것을 생각할 수 없었고, 차라리 교수형을 당하는 편이 훨씬 받아들이기 쉬울 것 같다고 했다."]], [["I blamed him for that, and told him I blamed him on two accounts; first, because if he was transported, there might be a hundred ways for him that was a gentleman, and a bold enterprising man, to find his way back again, and perhaps some ways and means to come back before he went.", "나는 그 점을 두고 그를 나무랐고, 두 가지 까닭에서 그를 나무란다고 말했다. 첫째는, 그가 유배된다면 신사이자 대담하고 진취적인 사람인 그에게는 다시 돌아올 길이 백 가지는 있을 것이며, 어쩌면 떠나기 전에 돌아올 길과 방도가 생길지도 모른다는 것이었다."], ["He smiled at that part, and said he should like the last the best of the two, for he had a kind of horror upon his mind at his being sent over to the plantations, as Romans sent condemned slaves to work in the mines; that he thought the passage into another state, let it be what it would, much more tolerable at the gallows, and that this was the general notion of all the gentlemen who were driven by the exigence of their fortunes to take the road; that at the place of execution there was at least an end of all the miseries of the present state, and as for what was to follow, a man was, in his opinion, as likely to repent sincerely in the last fortnight of his life, under the pressures and agonies of a jail and the condemned hole, as he would ever be in the woods and wilderness of America; that servitude and hard labour were things gentlemen could never stoop to; that it was but the way to force them to be their own executioners afterwards, which was much worse; and that therefore he could not have any patience when he did but think of being transported.", "그는 그 대목에 미소 지으며, 둘 중에서는 후자가 가장 마음에 든다고 말했으니, 마치 로마인들이 사형을 선고받은 노예들을 광산에서 일하라고 보냈듯이 자기가 농장으로 보내진다는 데 일종의 공포가 마음에 서려 있었기 때문이며, 다른 세상으로 건너가는 길은, 그것이 무엇이든 간에, 교수대에서가 훨씬 더 견딜 만하다고 여겼으며, 이것이야말로 형편의 절박함에 떠밀려 노상에 나선 모든 신사들의 일반적인 생각이라고 했다. 처형장에서는 적어도 현세의 온갖 비참함에 끝이 있고, 그 뒤에 따라올 것으로 말하자면, 자기 생각에 사람이란 옥과 사형수 굴의 압박과 고뇌 아래 삶의 마지막 두 주 동안에도, 아메리카의 숲과 황야에서 평생 그러할 만큼이나 진실하게 참회할 수 있다는 것이며, 종살이와 고된 노역은 신사로서는 결코 몸을 굽힐 수 없는 일이요, 그것은 결국 그들을 나중에 스스로 제 목숨을 끊는 자로 내모는 길에 지나지 않으니 그것이 훨씬 더 나쁘다고, 그러므로 자기는 유배된다는 것을 생각만 해도 도무지 참을 수가 없다고 했다."]], [["I used the utmost of my endeavour to persuade him, and joined that known woman’s rhetoric to it—I mean, that of tears.", "나는 그를 설득하려고 온갖 노력을 다 기울였고, 거기에 저 알려진 여인의 수사학—내 말은, 눈물 말이다—을 보탰다."], ["I told him the infamy of a public execution was certainly a greater pressure upon the spirits of a gentleman than any of the mortifications that he could meet with abroad could be; that he had at least in the other a chance for his life, whereas here he had none at all; that it was the easiest thing in the world for him to manage the captain of a ship, who were, generally speaking, men of good-humour and some gallantry; and a small matter of conduct, especially if there was any money to be had, would make way for him to buy himself off when he came to Virginia.", "나는 그에게, 공개 처형의 오명이야말로 신사의 심령에 외국에서 마주칠 수 있는 그 어떤 굴욕보다도 분명 더 큰 압박이 되리라고, 후자에는 적어도 목숨을 부지할 가망이 있으나 여기서는 그런 것이 전혀 없다고, 대체로 사람 좋고 어느 정도 호기로운 부류인 배의 선장을 구슬리는 일은 그에게 세상에서 가장 쉬운 일이며, 약간의 처신, 특히 손에 쥘 돈이 좀 있다면 그것으로 그가 버지니아에 이르렀을 때 돈을 치르고 풀려날 길이 열릴 것이라고 말했다."]], [["He looked wistfully at me, and I thought I guessed at what he meant, that is to say, that he had no money; but I was mistaken, his meaning was another way.", "그는 나를 애틋하게 바라보았고, 나는 그의 뜻을 짐작했다고 여겼으니, 다시 말해 그에게 돈이 없다는 뜻인 줄 알았다. 그러나 나는 잘못 짚었으니, 그의 뜻은 다른 데 있었다."], ["“You hinted just now, my dear,”", "\"여보, 당신이 방금 넌지시 말했잖소,\" 그가 말했다."], ["said he, “that there might be a way of coming back before I went, by which I understood you that it might be possible to buy it off here.", "\"내가 떠나기 전에 돌아올 길이 있을지도 모른다고 말이오. 그 말을 나는 여기서 돈을 치르고 그것을 면할 수도 있다는 뜻으로 알아들었소."], ["I had rather give £200 to prevent going, than £100 to be set at liberty when I came there.”", "나는 거기 이르러 풀려나는 데 백 파운드를 치르느니, 차라리 떠나는 것을 막는 데 이백 파운드를 치르겠소.\""], ["“That is, my dear,”", "\"그건, 여보,\" 내가 말했다."], ["said I, “because you do not know the place so well as I do.”", "\"당신이 그곳을 나만큼 잘 알지 못하기 때문이에요.\" \"그럴지도 모르지,\""], ["“That may be,”", "그가 말했다."], ["said he; “and yet I believe, as well as you know it, you would do the same, unless it is because, as you told me, you have a mother there.”", "\"그래도 나는, 당신이 그곳을 아무리 잘 안다 해도, 당신 역시 똑같이 할 거라고 믿소. 당신이 내게 말했듯이 거기 어머니가 계시다는 것 때문이 아니라면 말이오.\""]], [["I told him, as to my mother, it was next to impossible but that she must be dead many years before; and as for any other relations that I might have there, I knew them not now; that since the misfortunes I had been under had reduced me to the condition I had been in for some years, I had not kept up any correspondence with them; and that he would easily believe, I should find but a cold reception from them if I should be put to make my first visit in the condition of a transported felon; that therefore, if I went thither, I resolved not to see them; but that I had many views in going there, if it should be my fate, which took off all the uneasy part of it; and if he found himself obliged to go also, I should easily instruct him how to manage himself, so as never to go a servant at all, especially since I found he was not destitute of money, which was the only friend in such a condition.", "나는 그에게, 내 어머니로 말하자면, 그분이 여러 해 전에 이미 돌아가셨을 것이 거의 틀림없다고 말했고, 거기 있을지도 모를 다른 친척들로 말하자면 이제 나는 그들을 알지 못한다고, 내가 겪은 불운들이 나를 여러 해 동안 처해 있던 그 처지로 몰아넣은 뒤로 나는 그들과 아무런 연락도 이어 오지 않았다고, 또 내가 유배된 중범죄자의 처지로 처음 찾아가야 한다면 그들에게서 냉랭한 대접밖에 받지 못하리라는 것을 그가 쉬이 믿으리라고 말했다. 그러므로 내가 만약 그리로 간다 해도 그들을 보지 않기로 마음먹었다고, 다만 만약 그것이 내 운명이 된다면 거기 가는 데에는 그 일의 괴로운 부분을 죄다 덜어 줄 여러 가지 속셈이 있다고, 그리고 그 역시 가야 할 처지에 놓인다면, 내가 그에게 처신하는 법을 쉬이 일러 주어, 결코 종으로는 가지 않게 하겠다고, 특히 내가 보기에 그에게는 돈이 없지 않으니 더욱 그러하다고, 그런 처지에서 돈이야말로 유일한 벗이라고 말했다."]], [["He smiled, and said he did not tell me he had money.", "그는 미소 지으며, 자기에게 돈이 있다고는 말하지 않았다고 했다."], ["I took him up short, and told him I hoped he did not understand by my speaking, that I should expect any supply from him if he had money; that, on the other hand, though I had not a great deal, yet I did not want, and while I had any I would rather add to him than weaken him in that article, seeing, whatever he had, I knew in the case of transportation he would have occasion of it all.", "나는 그의 말을 얼른 가로막으며, 내가 그렇게 말한 것을 두고, 그에게 돈이 있다 한들 내가 무슨 도움을 바라리라고 여기지는 말기를 바란다고 말했다. 도리어, 내게 돈이 많지는 않으나 그래도 부족하지는 않으며, 내게 무엇이 있는 한 그것을 두고 그를 축내기보다는 차라리 그에게 보태 주겠다고, 그가 무엇을 지녔든 유배의 경우에는 그것이 죄다 필요할 것임을 내가 알기 때문이라고 했다."]], [["He expressed himself in a most tender manner upon that head.", "그는 그 점에 대해 더없이 다정한 태도로 자기 마음을 드러냈다."], ["He told me what money he had was not a great deal, but that he would never hide any of it from me if I wanted it, and that he assured me he did not speak with any such apprehensions; that he was only intent upon what I had hinted to him before he went; that here he knew what to do with himself, but that there he should be the most ignorant, helpless wretch alive.", "그는 자기가 지닌 돈이 많지는 않으나, 만약 내가 필요로 한다면 그중 어느 것도 결코 내게 감추지 않겠다고, 또 자기는 결코 그런 우려에서 말한 것이 아니라고 다짐했다. 자기는 다만 떠나기 전에 내가 넌지시 비친 그것에만 마음을 쏟고 있다고, 여기서는 자기 한 몸 어찌해야 할지 알지만, 거기서는 살아 있는 가장 무지하고 무력한 가련한 자가 되리라고 했다."]], [["I told him he frighted and terrified himself with that which had no terror in it; that if he had money, as I was glad to hear he had, he might not only avoid the servitude supposed to be the consequence of transportation, but begin the world upon a new foundation, and that such a one as he could not fail of success in, with the common application usual in such cases; that he could not but call to mind that it was what I had recommended to him many years before and had proposed it for our mutual subsistence and restoring our fortunes in the world; and I would tell him now, that to convince him both of the certainty of it and of my being fully acquainted with the method, and also fully satisfied in the probability of success, he should first see me deliver myself from the necessity of going over at all, and then that I would go with him freely, and of my own choice, and perhaps carry enough with me to satisfy him that I did not offer it for want of being able to live without assistance from him, but that I thought our mutual misfortunes had been such as were sufficient to reconcile us both to quitting this part of the world, and living where nobody could upbraid us with what was past, or we be in any dread of a prison, and without agonies of a condemned hole to drive us to it; this where we should look back on all our past disasters with infinite satisfaction, when we should consider that our enemies should entirely forget us, and that we should live as new people in a new world, nobody having anything to say to us, or we to them.", "나는 그에게, 아무런 두려울 것도 없는 일을 두고 그가 스스로 겁먹고 떨고 있다고 말했다. 그에게 돈이 있다면—그렇다는 것을 들으니 기쁘거니와—유배의 결과로 여겨지는 그 종살이를 피할 수 있을 뿐 아니라, 새로운 토대 위에서 세상을 다시 시작할 수 있으며, 그런 경우에 으레 들이는 평범한 노력만으로도 실패할 리 없는 그런 시작을 할 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 그것은 내가 여러 해 전에 그에게 권했던 것이요, 우리 둘이 함께 먹고살며 세상에서 우리의 형편을 다시 일으키자고 제안했던 것임을 그가 떠올리지 않을 수 없으리라고 했다. 그리고 이제 그에게 말하건대, 그것의 확실함과 내가 그 방법을 속속들이 알고 있다는 것, 또 내가 성공의 가망을 온전히 확신하고 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기 위하여, 먼저 내가 아예 건너갈 필요에서 나 자신을 벗어나게 하는 것을 그가 보게 될 것이며, 그런 다음 내가 기꺼이, 내 스스로의 선택으로 그와 함께 가겠다고, 그리고 어쩌면 내가 그의 도움 없이는 살아갈 수 없어서 그것을 제안하는 것이 아님을 그가 납득할 만큼 충분한 것을 지니고 가겠다고 했다. 다만 나는 우리가 함께 겪은 불운이 우리 둘로 하여금 이 세상의 이 부분을 떠나, 아무도 우리의 지난 일을 두고 우리를 책망할 수 없고, 우리도 옥을 두려워할 일이 없으며, 사형수 굴의 고뇌에 떠밀릴 일도 없는 곳에서 살아가는 데 우리를 화해시키기에 충분했다고 여긴다고 했다. 그곳에서 우리는 우리의 지난 온갖 재난을 한없는 만족 속에서 되돌아보게 될 것이니, 우리의 원수들이 우리를 까맣게 잊으리라는 것을, 그리고 우리가 새로운 세상에서 새사람으로 살아가, 아무도 우리에게 할 말이 없고 우리도 그들에게 할 말이 없으리라는 것을 헤아릴 때 그러하리라고 했다."]], [["I pressed this home to him with so many arguments, and answered all his own passionate objections so effectually that he embraced me, and told me I treated him with such sincerity and affection as overcame him; that he would take my advice, and would strive to submit to his fate in hope of having the comfort of my assistance, and of so faithful a counsellor and such a companion in his misery.", "나는 어찌나 많은 논거로 이것을 그에게 절실히 다그쳤던지, 또 그 자신의 온갖 격정 어린 반박에 어찌나 효과적으로 답했던지, 그는 나를 끌어안으며, 내가 자기를 그토록 진실하고 애정 어린 마음으로 대해 자기를 굴복시켰다고 말했다. 자기는 내 충고를 받아들이겠다고, 내 도움의 위안을, 그리고 자기 불행 속에서 그토록 충실한 조언자요 그런 동반자를 얻으리라는 희망을 품고 자기 운명에 순순히 따르고자 애쓰겠다고 했다."], ["But still he put me in mind of what I had mentioned before, namely, that there might be some way to get off before he went, and that it might be possible to avoid going at all, which he said would be much better.", "그러나 그는 여전히, 내가 앞서 언급한 것, 곧 떠나기 전에 빠져나갈 어떤 길이 있을지도 모른다는 것, 아예 가지 않는 것이 가능할지도 모른다는 것을 내게 일깨웠고, 그렇게 되면 훨씬 더 나을 것이라고 했다."], ["I told him he should see, and be fully satisfied, that I would do my utmost in that part too, and if it did not succeed, yet that I would make good the rest.", "나는 그에게, 그 부분에서도 내가 최선을 다하는 것을 보고 온전히 납득하게 될 것이며, 만약 그것이 성공하지 못한다 해도 나머지만은 반드시 이루어 내겠다고 말했다."]], [["We parted after this long conference with such testimonies of kindness and affection as I thought were equal, if not superior, to that at our parting at Dunstable; and now I saw more plainly than before, the reason why he declined coming at that time any farther with me toward London than Dunstable, and why, when we parted there, he told me it was not convenient for him to come part of the way to London to bring me going, as he would otherwise have done.", "이 긴 대화 끝에 우리는 던스터블에서 헤어질 때의 그것과 맞먹는, 아니 어쩌면 그보다 더한 다정함과 애정의 표시를 나누며 헤어졌다. 그리고 이제 나는 전보다 더 분명하게, 그가 그때 런던을 향해 나와 함께 던스터블보다 더 멀리 가기를 마다한 까닭을, 또 거기서 헤어질 때 그가 어찌하여 내게 런던으로 가는 길 일부를 바래다주는 것이 자기에게 편치 않다고—그렇지 않았다면 그리해 주었을 것을—말했는지를 알게 되었다."], ["I have observed that the account of his life would have made a much more pleasing history than this of mine; and, indeed, nothing in it was more strange than this part, viz. that he carried on that desperate trade full five-and-twenty years and had never been taken, the success he had met with had been so very uncommon, and such that sometimes he had lived handsomely, and retired in place for a year or two at a time, keeping himself and a man-servant to wait on him, and had often sat in the coffee-houses and heard the very people whom he had robbed give accounts of their being robbed, and of the place and circumstances, so that he could easily remember that it was the same.", "나는 그의 삶의 내력이 내 것보다 훨씬 더 재미있는 이력이 되었으리라고 말한 바 있거니와, 실로 그 가운데서도 이 부분, 곧 그가 그 무모한 일을 꼬박 스물다섯 해 동안이나 이어 오면서 한 번도 잡힌 적이 없었다는 것보다 더 기이한 것은 없었으니, 그가 거둔 성공이 어찌나 보기 드문 것이었던지, 때로는 한 번에 한두 해씩 어느 곳에 은거하여 풍족하게 지내며, 자기 시중을 들 하인 하나를 두고 자기를 건사했으며, 종종 커피하우스에 앉아, 자기가 턴 바로 그 사람들이 강도를 당한 일과 그 장소와 정황을 늘어놓는 것을 들으면서 그것이 같은 사건임을 쉬이 떠올리곤 했던 것이다."]], [["In this manner, it seems, he lived near Liverpool at the time he unluckily married me for a fortune.", "이런 식으로, 보아하니 그는 한밑천을 노리고 불운하게 나와 결혼했을 무렵 리버풀 근처에 살고 있었다."], ["Had I been the fortune he expected, I verily believe, as he said, that he would have taken up and lived honestly all his days.", "만약 내가 그가 기대했던 그 한밑천이었더라면, 그가 말했듯이, 나는 그가 정말로 손을 씻고 평생 정직하게 살았으리라 믿는다."]], [["He had with the rest of his misfortunes the good luck not to be actually upon the spot when the robbery was done which he was committed for, and so none of the persons robbed could swear to him, or had anything to charge upon him.", "그는 그 온갖 불운 가운데서도, 자기가 갇히게 된 그 강도 사건이 벌어졌을 때 실제로 그 자리에 있지 않았던 행운을 누렸으니, 그래서 강도를 당한 사람들 가운데 누구도 그를 두고 맹세할 수 없었고, 그에게 씌울 만한 것도 없었다."], ["But it seems as he was taken with the gang, one hard-mouthed countryman swore home to him, and they were like to have others come in according to the publication they had made; so that they expected more evidence against him, and for that reason he was kept in hold.", "그러나 보아하니 그가 패거리와 함께 잡혔던 터라, 입이 험한 어떤 시골 사람 하나가 그를 두고 단단히 맹세했고, 그들이 낸 공고에 따라 다른 자들도 나설 듯하여, 그래서 그에게 불리한 증거가 더 들어오리라 예상되었고, 그런 까닭에 그는 구금된 채 있었던 것이다."]], [["However, the offer which was made to him of admitting him to transportation was made, as I understood, upon the intercession of some great person who pressed him hard to accept of it before a trial; and indeed, as he knew there were several that might come in against him, I thought his friend was in the right, and I lay at him night and day to delay it no longer.", "그러나 그를 유배에 부치도록 허락하는 그 제의는, 내가 알기로 어떤 지체 높은 인물의 알선으로 이루어진 것이었으니, 그 사람이 재판에 앞서 그것을 받아들이라고 그를 모질게 다그쳤던 것이다. 실로 그에게 불리하게 나설 자가 여럿 있을 수 있음을 그가 아는 터라, 나는 그의 벗이 옳았다고 여겨, 더는 미루지 말라고 밤낮으로 그를 졸랐다."]], [["At last, with much difficulty, he gave his consent; and as he was not therefore admitted to transportation in court, and on his petition, as I was, so he found himself under a difficulty to avoid embarking himself as I had said he might have done; his great friend, who was his intercessor for the favour of that grant, having given security for him that he should transport himself, and not return within the term.", "마침내 여간한 어려움 끝에 그는 동의했다. 그런데 그는 나처럼 법정에서, 또 자기 탄원에 따라 유배에 부쳐진 것이 아니었으므로, 내가 그가 그리할 수 있었으리라 말한 대로 스스로 승선하는 것을 피하기가 어려운 처지에 놓이게 되었으니, 그에게 그 허가의 은전을 알선해 준 그 지체 높은 벗이, 그가 스스로 유배를 떠나며 기한 안에 돌아오지 않으리라는 보증을 서 주었기 때문이다."]], [["This hardship broke all my measures, for the steps I took afterwards for my own deliverance were hereby rendered wholly ineffectual, unless I would abandon him, and leave him to go to America by himself; than which he protested he would much rather venture, although he were certain to go directly to the gallows.", "이 난관이 내 모든 계획을 어그러뜨렸으니, 그 뒤로 나 자신의 구원을 위해 내가 밟은 절차들이 이로 인해 죄다 헛것이 되고 말았기 때문이며, 그를 저버리고 그가 홀로 아메리카로 가도록 내버려 두지 않는 한 그러했는데, 그는 설령 곧장 교수대로 가는 것이 확실하다 할지라도 차라리 그쪽을 무릅쓰겠다고 단언했다."]], [["I must now return to my case.", "이제 나는 내 이야기로 돌아가야겠다."], ["The time of my being transported according to my sentence was near at hand; my governess, who continued my fast friend, had tried to obtain a pardon, but it could not be done unless with an expense too heavy for my purse, considering that to be left naked and empty, unless I had resolved to return to my old trade again, had been worse than my transportation, because there I knew I could live, here I could not.", "내 선고에 따라 유배될 시기가 가까이 다가와 있었다. 내 변함없는 벗으로 남아 있던 후견인이 사면을 얻어 보려 애썼으나, 내 주머니에 비추어 너무 버거운 비용을 들이지 않고서는 그리할 수 없었으니, 내가 옛 직업으로 다시 돌아갈 결심을 하지 않은 한, 빈털터리에 알몸으로 남겨지는 것은 내 유배보다 더 나쁜 일이었던 까닭이다."], ["The good minister stood very hard on another account to prevent my being transported also; but he was answered, that indeed my life had been given me at his first solicitations, and therefore he ought to ask no more.", "거기서는 내가 살아갈 수 있음을 알았으나 여기서는 그럴 수 없었기 때문이다. 그 선량한 목사도 내가 유배되는 것을 막으려고 다른 명목으로 매우 애썼으나, 실로 내 목숨이 그의 첫 간청으로 이미 주어진 것이니 더는 청해서는 안 된다는 답을 들었다."], ["He was sensibly grieved at my going, because, as he said, he feared I should lose the good impressions which a prospect of death had at first made on me, and which were since increased by his instructions; and the pious gentleman was exceedingly concerned about me on that account.", "그는 내가 떠나는 것을 두고 절실히 슬퍼했으니, 그의 말처럼, 죽음의 전망이 처음에 내게 새긴, 그리고 그 뒤로 그의 가르침으로 더해진 그 좋은 인상들을 내가 잃을까 두려워했기 때문이며, 그 경건한 신사는 그 때문에 나를 두고 몹시 걱정했다."]], [["On the other hand, I really was not so solicitous about it as I was before, but I industriously concealed my reasons for it from the minister, and to the last he did not know but that I went with the utmost reluctance and affliction.", "다른 한편으로, 나는 실로 전만큼 그것을 두고 노심초사하지는 않았으나, 그 까닭만은 목사에게 애써 감추었으니, 끝까지 그는 내가 더없는 마뜩잖음과 비통함 속에 떠나는 줄로만 알고 있었다."]], [["It was in the month of February that I was, with seven other convicts, as they called us, delivered to a merchant that traded to Virginia, on board a ship, riding, as they called it, in Deptford Reach.", "내가 그들이 우리를 일컫는 대로 일곱 명의 다른 죄수와 함께 버지니아로 무역하는 한 상인에게 넘겨져, 그들이 이르는 대로 뎁트퍼드리치에 정박해 있는 한 배에 태워진 것은 이월의 일이었다."], ["The officer of the prison delivered us on board, and the master of the vessel gave a discharge for us.", "옥의 관리가 우리를 배에 인도했고, 그 배의 선장이 우리에 대한 인수증을 써 주었다."]], [["We were for that night clapped under hatches, and kept so close that I thought I should have been suffocated for want of air; and the next morning the ship weighed, and fell down the river to a place they call Bugby’s Hole, which was done, as they told us, by the agreement of the merchant, that all opportunity of escape should be taken from us.", "그날 밤 우리는 승강구 아래 갇혔는데, 어찌나 빽빽하게 가두어 두었던지 나는 공기가 모자라 질식하는 줄로만 알았다. 이튿날 아침 배가 닻을 올려 강을 따라 내려가, 그들이 버그비스홀이라 부르는 곳에 이르렀으니, 우리에게 일러 준 바로는 우리에게서 모든 도망의 기회를 앗아 두려는 그 상인의 합의에 따른 것이었다."], ["However, when the ship came thither and cast anchor, we were allowed more liberty, and particularly were permitted to come up on the deck, but not up on the quarter-deck, that being kept particularly for the captain and for passengers.", "그러나 배가 거기 이르러 닻을 내리자, 우리는 더 많은 자유를 허락받았고, 특히 갑판 위로 올라오는 것은 허락되었으되, 그것은 특별히 선장과 승객들을 위해 마련된 곳이었던 까닭에 후갑판 위로 올라오는 것은 허락되지 않았다."]], [["When by the noise of the men over my head, and the motion of the ship, I perceived that they were under sail, I was at first greatly surprised, fearing we should go away directly, and that our friends would not be admitted to see us any more; but I was easy soon after, when I found they had come to an anchor again, and soon after that we had notice given by some of the men where we were, that the next morning we should have the liberty to come up on deck, and to have our friends come and see us if we had any.", "머리 위 사람들의 소란과 배의 움직임으로 그들이 돛을 올렸음을 알아차렸을 때, 나는 처음에 우리가 곧장 떠나가 우리 벗들이 더는 우리를 보러 오도록 허락되지 않을까 두려워 크게 놀랐다. 그러나 곧 그들이 다시 닻을 내린 것을 알고는 이내 마음이 놓였고, 그 직후에 우리가 어디에 있는지를 몇몇 선원이 우리에게 일러 주며, 이튿날 아침 우리가 갑판 위로 올라오고, 벗이 있다면 그들이 우리를 보러 오게 할 자유를 누리게 되리라고 알려 주었다."]], [["All that night I lay upon the hard boards of the deck, as the passengers did, but we had afterwards the liberty of little cabins for such of us as had any bedding to lay in them, and room to stow any box or trunk for clothes and linen, if we had it (which might well be put in), for some of them had neither shirt nor shift or a rag of linen or woollen, but what was on their backs, or a farthing of money to help themselves; and yet I did not find but they fared well enough in the ship, especially the women, who got money from the seamen for washing their clothes, sufficient to purchase any common things that they wanted.", "그날 밤 내내 나는 승객들이 그러하듯 갑판의 딱딱한 널빤지 위에 누워 있었으나, 그 뒤로 우리 가운데 거기에 깔 침구를 가진 자들에게는 작은 선실을 쓸 자유와, 가진 것이 있다면 옷이며 속옷을 넣을 상자나 궤짝 하나를 쟁여 둘 자리가 허락되었으니(그것은 충분히 들여놓을 만했다), 그들 가운데 더러는 등에 걸친 것 말고는 셔츠도 슈미즈도, 아마포든 모직이든 헝겊 한 조각도, 제 한 몸 건사할 한 푼의 돈도 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 그들이 배에서 그런대로 잘 지내지 못하는 것 같지는 않았으니, 특히 여자들은 선원들의 옷을 빨아 주고 돈을 벌어, 자기들에게 필요한 흔한 물건들을 사기에 넉넉했다."]], [["When the next morning we had the liberty to come up on the deck, I asked one of the officers of the ship, whether I might not have the liberty to send a letter on shore, to let my friends know where the ship lay, and to get some necessary things sent to me.", "이튿날 아침 우리가 갑판 위로 올라올 자유를 누리게 되었을 때, 나는 배의 한 관리에게, 내가 뭍으로 편지를 한 통 보내 내 벗들에게 배가 어디에 정박해 있는지 알리고, 내게 필요한 물건 몇 가지를 보내 받을 자유가 없겠느냐고 물었다."], ["This was, it seems, the boatswain, a very civil, courteous sort of man, who told me I should have that, or any other liberty that I desired, that he could allow me with safety.", "보아하니 이 사람은 갑판장으로, 매우 점잖고 정중한 부류의 사람이었는데, 그는 내게 그것이든, 그가 무사히 허락해 줄 수 있는 다른 어떤 자유든 누리게 해 주겠다고 말했다."], ["I told him I desired no other; and he answered that the ship’s boat would go up to London the next tide, and he would order my letter to be carried.", "나는 다른 것은 바라지 않는다고 말했고, 그는 이튿날 밀물에 배의 보트가 런던으로 올라갈 것이니 내 편지를 실어 보내도록 분부하겠다고 대답했다."]], [["Accordingly, when the boat went off, the boatswain came to me and told me the boat was going off, and that he went in it himself, and asked me if my letter was ready he would take care of it.", "그리하여 보트가 떠날 때, 갑판장이 내게 와서 보트가 떠난다고, 자기도 그 안에 타고 간다고 일러 주며, 내 편지가 준비되었다면 자기가 그것을 맡아 주겠다고 물었다."], ["I had prepared myself, you may be sure, pen, ink, and paper beforehand, and I had gotten a letter ready directed to my governess, and enclosed another for my fellow-prisoner, which, however, I did not let her know was my husband, not to the last.", "여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 나는 미리 펜과 잉크와 종이를 마련해 두었던 터라, 내 후견인에게 보내는 편지 한 통을 준비해 두었고, 그 안에 내 동료 죄수에게 보내는 또 한 통을 동봉했는데, 다만 끝까지 그가 내 남편이라는 것은 그녀에게 알리지 않았다."], ["In that to my governess, I let her know where the ship lay, and pressed her earnestly to send me what things I knew she had got ready for me for my voyage.", "후견인에게 보내는 편지에는 배가 어디에 정박해 있는지를 알리고, 내 항해를 위해 그녀가 마련해 두었음을 내가 아는 그 물건들을 보내 달라고 간곡히 졸랐다."]], [["When I gave the boatswain the letter, I gave him a shilling with it, which I told him was for the charge of a messenger or porter, which I entreated him to send with the letter as soon as he came on shore, that if possible I might have an answer brought back by the same hand, that I might know what was become of my things; “for sir,”", "내가 갑판장에게 편지를 건넬 때, 나는 그것과 함께 그에게 일 실링을 주며, 그것은 심부름꾼이나 짐꾼의 삯에 쓰라는 것이라고 말하고, 그가 뭍에 닿는 대로 그 편지를 들려 보내 주기를 간청했으니, 될 수 있으면 같은 사람 손에 답장을 받아 와, 내 물건들이 어찌 되었는지 알 수 있게 하기 위함이었다. \"선생님, 만약에,\""], ["says I, “if the ship should go away before I have them on board, I am undone.”", "내가 말했다. \"그 물건들을 배에 들이기 전에 배가 떠나 버리면, 나는 끝장이거든요.\""]], [["I took care, when I gave him the shilling, to let him see that I had a little better furniture about me than the ordinary prisoners, for he saw that I had a purse, and in it a pretty deal of money; and I found that the very sight of it immediately furnished me with very different treatment from what I should otherwise have met with in the ship; for though he was very courteous indeed before, in a kind of natural compassion to me, as a woman in distress, yet he was more than ordinarily so afterwards, and procured me to be better treated in the ship than, I say, I might otherwise have been; as shall appear in its place.", "내가 그에게 일 실링을 줄 때, 나는 그가 내게 여느 죄수들보다 조금 나은 살림을 갖추고 있음을 보게끔 신경을 썼으니, 그는 내게 지갑이 있고 그 안에 꽤 많은 돈이 들어 있는 것을 보았던 것이다. 그리고 나는 바로 그것을 본 것만으로도 그가 내게, 그러지 않았더라면 배에서 받았을 것과는 사뭇 다른 대접을 즉시 해 주었음을 알게 되었다. 그는 실로 전에도, 곤경에 처한 여인인 내게 일종의 자연스러운 연민으로 매우 정중했으나, 그 뒤로는 예사롭지 않게 더 그러했고, 내가 그러지 않았더라면 받았을 것보다 배에서 더 나은 대접을 받도록 주선해 주었으니, 이는 마땅한 자리에서 드러날 것이다."]], [["He very honestly had my letter delivered to my governess’s own hands, and brought me back an answer from her in writing; and when he gave me the answer, gave me the shilling again.", "그는 매우 정직하게 내 편지를 후견인의 손에 직접 전해 주고, 그녀에게서 글로 쓴 답장을 받아다 주었으며, 그 답장을 내게 건넬 때 일 실링도 도로 주었다. \"여기,\""], ["“There,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “there’s your shilling again too, for I delivered the letter myself.”", "\"당신 일 실링도 도로 받으시오, 편지는 내가 직접 전했으니 말이오.\" 나는 그 일에 어찌나 놀랐던지 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었으나, 잠시 머뭇거린 뒤에 말했다."], ["I could not tell what to say, I was so surprised at the thing; but after some pause, I said, “Sir, you are too kind; it had been but reasonable that you had paid yourself coach-hire, then.”", "\"선생님, 너무 친절하시네요. 그렇다면 그 마차 삯이라도 받으시는 게 도리였을 텐데요.\""]], [["“No, no,”", "\"아니요, 아니요,\""], ["says he, “I am overpaid.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 넘치게 받았소."], ["What is the gentlewoman?", "그 부인은 무엇이오?"], ["Your sister.”", "당신 언니요?\""]], [["“No, sir,”", "\"아니에요, 선생님,\""], ["says I, “she is no relation to me, but she is a dear friend, and all the friends I have in the world.”", "내가 말했다. \"그분은 제 친척이 아니라 소중한 벗이고, 이 세상에서 제가 가진 벗의 전부예요.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"허,\""], ["says he, “there are few such friends in the world.", "그가 말했다. \"세상에 그런 벗은 드물지요."], ["Why, she cried after you like a child.”", "아니, 그 부인은 당신을 두고 어린아이처럼 울던걸요.\""], ["“Ay,”", "\"그럼요,\""], ["says I again, “she would give a hundred pounds, I believe, to deliver me from this dreadful condition I am in.”", "내가 다시 말했다. \"내가 처한 이 무서운 처지에서 나를 구해 낼 수 있다면 그분은 백 파운드라도 내놓을 거예요.\""]], [["“Would she so?”", "\"그렇단 말이오?\""], ["says he.", "그가 말했다."], ["“For half the money I believe I could put you in a way how to deliver yourself.”", "\"그 절반이면 내가 당신 스스로 빠져나갈 길을 일러 줄 수 있을 것 같소만.\""], ["But this he spoke softly, that nobody could hear.", "그러나 이 말을 그는 아무도 들을 수 없게 나직이 했다."]], [["“Alas!", "\"아아!"], ["sir,”", "선생님,\""], ["said I, “but then that must be such a deliverance as, if I should be taken again, would cost me my life.”", "내가 말했다. \"하지만 그건 만약 다시 잡히기라도 하면 제 목숨을 앗아 갈 그런 종류의 탈출일 거예요.\""], ["“Nay,”", "\"아니지,\""], ["said he, “if you were once out of the ship, you must look to yourself afterwards; that I can say nothing to.”", "그가 말했다. \"당신이 일단 배에서 나가면, 그 뒤로는 당신 스스로 알아서 해야지요."], ["So we dropped the discourse for that time.", "그건 내가 뭐라 할 수 없는 일이오.\" 그래서 우리는 그때는 그 이야기를 그만두었다."]], [["In the meantime, my governess, faithful to the last moment, conveyed my letter to the prison to my husband, and got an answer to it, and the next day came down herself to the ship, bringing me, in the first place, a sea-bed as they call it, and all its furniture, such as was convenient, but not to let the people think it was extraordinary.", "그러는 사이, 마지막 순간까지 충실했던 내 후견인은 내 편지를 옥에 있는 내 남편에게 전해 그에 대한 답장을 받아다 주었고, 이튿날에는 몸소 배로 내려와, 무엇보다 먼저 그들이 이르는 대로 선상 침대 하나와, 요긴하되 사람들이 유별나다고 여기지 않을 만한 그 온갖 부속품을 내게 가져다주었다. 그녀는 선상 궤짝 하나도 가져왔는데, 그것은 선원들을 위해 만들어진 그런 궤짝으로, 온갖 편의 용품이 갖추어져 있고, 내게 필요할 만한 거의 모든 것이 채워져 있었다."], ["She brought with her a sea-chest—that is, a chest, such as are made for seamen, with all the conveniences in it, and filled with everything almost that I could want; and in one of the corners of the chest, where there was a private drawer, was my bank of money—this is to say, so much of it as I had resolved to carry with me; for I ordered a part of my stock to be left behind me, to be sent afterwards in such goods as I should want when I came to settle; for money in that country is not of much use where all things are brought for tobacco, much more is it a great loss to carry it from hence.", "그리고 그 궤짝의 한 귀퉁이, 비밀 서랍이 있는 곳에는 내 은행, 곧 내 돈이 들어 있었으니, 다시 말해 내가 가지고 가기로 마음먹은 만큼의 돈이었다. 나는 내 밑천의 일부를 뒤에 남겨 두어, 거기 이르러 자리를 잡을 때 내게 필요할 물품의 형태로 나중에 부쳐 오도록 분부해 두었으니, 그 나라에서는 모든 것이 담배로 거래되는 터라 돈이 그리 쓸모가 없을뿐더러, 여기서 그것을 가지고 가는 것은 더더욱 큰 손해이기 때문이다."]], [["But my case was particular; it was by no means proper to me to go thither without money or goods, and for a poor convict, that was to be sold as soon as I came on shore, to carry with me a cargo of goods would be to have notice taken of it, and perhaps to have them seized by the public; so I took part of my stock with me thus, and left the other part with my governess.", "그러나 내 경우는 사정이 남달랐으니, 내가 돈도 물품도 없이 그리로 가는 것은 결코 마땅치 않았고, 뭍에 닿는 대로 팔려 나갈 가련한 죄수가 한 무더기의 물품을 가지고 간다면 사람들의 눈에 띄어, 어쩌면 관헌에게 압수당할지도 모르는 일이었다. 그래서 나는 내 밑천의 일부를 이렇게 가지고 가고, 나머지 일부는 후견인에게 맡겨 두었다."]], [["My governess brought me a great many other things, but it was not proper for me to look too well provided in the ship, at least till I knew what kind of a captain we should have.", "후견인은 그 밖에도 많은 물건을 내게 가져다주었으나, 적어도 우리가 어떤 부류의 선장을 만나게 될지 알기 전까지는 내가 배에서 너무 잘 갖춘 티를 내는 것이 마땅치 않았다."], ["When she came into the ship, I thought she would have died indeed; her heart sank at the sight of me, and at the thoughts of parting with me in that condition, and she cried so intolerably, I could not for a long time have any talk with her.", "그녀가 배에 들어섰을 때, 나는 그녀가 정말로 죽고 마는 줄 알았다. 그런 처지의 나를 보고, 또 그런 형편의 나와 헤어진다는 생각에 그녀는 가슴이 무너져, 어찌나 견딜 수 없이 울던지, 나는 한참 동안 그녀와 아무런 이야기도 나눌 수 없었다."]], [["I took that time to read my fellow-prisoner’s letter, which, however, greatly perplexed me.", "나는 그 틈을 타 내 동료 죄수의 편지를 읽었는데, 그것은 나를 몹시 난감하게 했다."], ["He told me he was determined to go, but found it would be impossible for him to be discharged time enough for going in the same ship, and which was more than all, he began to question whether they would give him leave to go in what ship he pleased, though he did voluntarily transport himself; but that they would see him put on board such a ship as they should direct, and that he would be charged upon the captain as other convict prisoners were; so that he began to be in despair of seeing me till he came to Virginia, which made him almost desperate; seeing that, on the other hand, if I should not be there, if any accident of the sea or of mortality should take me away, he should be the most undone creature there in the world.", "그는 떠나기로 마음을 굳혔으나, 같은 배로 떠날 만큼 제때에 방면되기는 불가능할 것 같다고, 게다가 무엇보다도, 비록 자청하여 스스로 유배를 떠나는 처지이긴 하나, 자기가 원하는 배로 가도록 허락해 줄지가 의심스러워지기 시작했다고, 도리어 그들이 자기를 지정하는 배에 태우는 것을 지켜볼 것이며, 다른 죄수들과 마찬가지로 자기가 그 선장에게 인계되리라고 적어 왔다. 그래서 그는 자기가 버지니아에 이를 때까지 나를 보지 못하게 될까 절망하기 시작했고, 그 때문에 거의 자포자기에 빠졌으니, 다른 한편으로, 만약 내가 거기 있지 않게 된다면, 만약 바다의 어떤 사고나 죽음이 나를 앗아 간다면, 자기는 거기서 이 세상에서 가장 신세 망친 가련한 자가 되고 말리라고 여겼기 때문이다."]], [["This was very perplexing, and I knew not what course to take.", "이것은 매우 난감한 일이라, 나는 어떤 방도를 취해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 나는 후견인에게 갑판장의 이야기를 들려주었고, 그녀는 내가 그와 흥정해 보기를 몹시 다그쳤다."], ["I told my governess the story of the boatswain, and she was mighty eager with me treat with him; but I had no mind to it, till I heard whether my husband, or fellow-prisoner, so she called him, could be at liberty to go with me or no.", "그러나 나는 내 남편, 그러니까 그녀가 일컫는 대로 하면 내 동료 죄수가 나와 함께 갈 자유를 얻을 수 있을지 없을지를 듣기 전까지는 그럴 마음이 없었다. 마침내 나는 그를 내 남편이라는 것만 빼고는 그 일 전부를 그녀에게 털어놓을 수밖에 없었다."], ["At last I was forced to let her into the whole matter, except only that of his being my husband.", "나는 그녀에게, 만약 그가 같은 배로 갈 자유를 얻을 수 있다면 함께 가기로 그와 확실한 거래, 곧 약조를 맺었으며, 그에게 돈이 있음을 알게 되었다고 말했다."], ["I told her I had made a positive bargain or agreement with him to go, if he could get the liberty of going in the same ship, and that I found he had money.", "그러고 나서 나는 그녀에게, 우리가 거기 이르면 내가 무엇을 하려는지, 우리가 어떻게 농사를 짓고 자리를 잡으며, 한마디로 더는 어떤 모험도 없이 어떻게 부유해질 것인지를 길게 늘어놓았고, 큰 비밀로서, 그가 배에 오르는 대로 우리가 혼인하기로 되어 있다고 그녀에게 말했다."]], [["Then I read a long lecture to her of what I proposed to do when we came there, how we could plant, settle, and, in short, grow rich without any more adventures; and, as a great secret, I told her that we were to marry as soon as he came on board.", "그녀는 이 말을 듣자 곧 기꺼이 내가 가는 데 동의했고, 그때부터 그를 제때에 옥에서 빼내어 나와 같은 배로 가게 하는 것을 자기 일로 삼았으니, 마침내 그것이 이루어지긴 했으나, 여간한 어려움이 아니었고, 또 유배된 죄수의 온갖 절차를 다 거치지 않고서는 안 되었는데, 그는 아직 재판을 받지 않았으니 실은 그런 죄수가 아니었던지라, 그것은 그에게 크나큰 굴욕이었다."]], [["She soon agreed cheerfully to my going when she heard this, and she made it her business from that time to get him out of the prison in time, so that he might go in the same ship with me, which at last was brought to pass, though with great difficulty, and not without all the forms of a transported prisoner-convict, which he really was not yet, for he had not been tried, and which was a great mortification to him.", "이제 우리의 운명이 정해져, 우리 둘 다 배에 올라, 노예로 팔려 갈 운명인 유배된 죄수라는 그 비천한 신분으로, 나는 다섯 해 동안, 그는 보증 아래 실제로 버지니아행에 묶이게 되었으니\n잉글랜드로 다시는 돌아오지 않겠다는 보증을 살아 있는 한 지켜야 한다는 조건이 붙자, 그는 몹시 풀이 죽고 의기소침해졌다."], ["As our fate was now determined, and we were both on board, actually bound to Virginia, in the despicable quality of transported convicts destined to be sold for slaves, I for five years, and he under bonds and security not to return to England any more, as long as he lived, he was very much dejected and cast down; the mortification of being brought on board, as he was, like a prisoner, piqued him very much, since it was first told him he should transport himself, and so that he might go as a gentleman at liberty.", "그가 그렇게 죄수처럼 배에 끌려와 실린 굴욕이 그를 크게 자극했으니, 처음에는 그가 자원하여 유배를 떠나도 되며, 따라서 자유로운 신사로서 갈 수 있다고 들었기 때문이다. 사실 그는 거기에 도착했을 때 우리처럼 팔리도록 명령받지는 않았고, 그런 까닭에 선장에게 뱃삯을 치러야 했으니, 우리는 그러지 않았다."], ["It is true he was not ordered to be sold when he came there, as we were, and for that reason he was obliged to pay for his passage to the captain, which we were not; as to the rest, he was as much at a loss as a child what to do with himself, or with what he had, but by directions.", "나머지에 관해서는, 그는 어린아이처럼, 자기 자신을 어찌해야 할지, 가진 것을 어찌해야 할지 갈피를 잡지 못하고 그저 지시를 따를 뿐이었다."]], [["Our first business was to compare our stock.", "우리가 맨 먼저 할 일은 우리의 밑천을 견주어 보는 것이었다."], ["He was very honest to me, and told me his stock was pretty good when he came into the prison, but the living there as he did in a figure like a gentleman, and, which was ten times as much, the making of friends, and soliciting his case, had been very expensive; and, in a word, all his stock that he had left was £108, which he had about him all in gold.", "그는 내게 아주 솔직하게, 자기가 감옥에 들어왔을 때만 해도 밑천이 꽤 괜찮았으나, 거기서 신사 행세하며 산 생활과, 그것의 열 배에 달하는, 친구를 사귀고 자기 사정을 청원하는 일이 무척 비용이 많이 들었다고 말했다. 한마디로, 그에게 남은 밑천 전부는 백팔 파운드였고, 그것을 모두 금화로 몸에 지니고 있었다."]], [["I gave him an account of my stock as faithfully, that is to say, of what I had taken to carry with me, for I was resolved, whatever should happen, to keep what I had left with my governess in reserve; that in case I should die, what I had with me was enough to give him, and that which was left in my governess’s hands would be her own, which she had well deserved of me indeed.", "나도 마찬가지로 충실하게 내 밑천을 그에게 알려 주었으니, 다시 말해 내가 지니고 가려고 챙긴 것에 관해서 말이다. 무슨 일이 일어나든 후견인에게 맡겨 둔 것은 따로 비축해 두기로 결심한 까닭이었다. 혹여 내가 죽더라도 내가 지닌 것만으로도 그에게 주기에 충분할 터이고, 후견인의 손에 남겨 둔 것은 그녀 자신의 몫이 될 터인데, 실로 그녀는 내게서 그것을 받을 만한 자격이 충분했다."]], [["My stock which I had with me was £246 some odd shillings; so that we had £354 between us, but a worse gotten estate was scarce ever put together to begin the world with.", "내가 지니고 있던 밑천은 이백사십육 파운드 남짓의 몇 실링이었으니, 그래서 우리 둘 사이에는 삼백오십사 파운드가 있었던 셈이나, 세상을 새로 시작하는 밑천으로 이보다 더 나쁘게 얻은 재산을 한데 그러모은 일은 좀처럼 없었으리라."]], [["Our greatest misfortune as to our stock was that it was all in money, which every one knows is an unprofitable cargo to be carried to the plantations.", "우리 밑천에 관한 가장 큰 불운은 그것이 모두 돈이라는 점이었으니, 누구나 알다시피 식민지로 가져가기에 돈은 이득이 없는 짐이다. 그의 것은, 그가 내게 말한 대로, 정말로 세상에 그가 가진 전부였으리라 믿는다."], ["I believe his was really all he had left in the world, as he told me it was; but I, who had between £700 and £800 in bank when this disaster befell me, and who had one of the faithfullest friends in the world to manage it for me, considering she was a woman of manner of religious principles, had still £300 left in her hand, which I reserved as above; besides, some very valuable things, as particularly two gold watches, some small pieces of plate, and some rings—all stolen goods.", "그러나 이 재난이 내게 닥쳤을 때 나는 은행에 칠백에서 팔백 파운드 사이의 돈을 두고 있었고, 또 그것을 관리해 줄, 세상에서 가장 충실한 벗 가운데 하나를 두었으니, 그녀가 어느 정도 신앙적 원칙을 지닌 여자임을 생각하면, 앞서 말한 대로 따로 비축해 둔 삼백 파운드가 여전히 그녀의 손에 남아 있었다. 게다가 매우 값나가는 물건 몇 가지도 있었으니, 특히 금시계 두 개, 작은 은그릇 몇 점, 반지 몇 개였는데, 모두 훔친 물건이었다."], ["The plate, rings, and watches were put in my chest with the money, and with this fortune, and in the sixty-first year of my age, I launched out into a new world, as I may call it, in the condition (as to what appeared) only of a poor, naked convict, ordered to be transported in respite from the gallows.", "은그릇과 반지와 시계는 돈과 함께 내 궤짝에 넣어 두었고, 이 재산을 지니고, 또 내 나이 예순한 살에, 나는 (겉보기에는) 교수대에서 형 집행이 유예되어 유배되라는 명을 받은 가엾고 헐벗은 중범죄자에 지나지 않는 처지로, 새로운 세계라 부를 만한 곳으로 나아갔다."], ["My clothes were poor and mean, but not ragged or dirty, and none knew in the whole ship that I had anything of value about me.", "내 옷은 초라하고 보잘것없었으나 누덕누덕하거나 더럽지는 않았고, 배 안의 그 누구도 내가 무언가 값진 것을 몸에 지니고 있음을 알지 못했다."]], [["However, as I had a great many very good clothes and linen in abundance, which I had ordered to be packed up in two great boxes, I had them shipped on board, not as my goods, but as consigned to my real name in Virginia; and had the bills of loading signed by a captain in my pocket; and in these boxes was my plate and watches, and everything of value except my money, which I kept by itself in a private drawer in my chest, which could not be found, or opened, if found, without splitting the chest to pieces.", "그렇지만 나는 아주 좋은 옷가지가 많고 아마포가 넉넉했던지라, 그것들을 큰 상자 두 개에 꾸려 두라고 일러 두고는, 내 물건으로서가 아니라 버지니아에 있는 내 본명 앞으로 보내는 화물로 배에 실었다. 그리고 어느 선장이 서명한 선하증권을 내 주머니에 지니고 있었다. 또 이 상자들 안에는 내 은그릇과 시계와, 돈을 뺀 온갖 값진 것이 들어 있었으니, 돈만은 따로 내 궤짝의 비밀 서랍에 넣어 두었는데, 그 서랍은 찾아낼 수도 없거니와, 설령 찾아낸다 해도 궤짝을 산산조각 내지 않고서는 열 수 없는 것이었다."]], [["In this condition I lay for three weeks in the ship, not knowing whether I should have my husband with me or no, and therefore not resolving how or in what manner to receive the honest boatswain’s proposal, which indeed he thought a little strange at first.", "이런 처지로 나는 배 안에서 세 주를 지냈는데, 남편이 나와 함께 갈지 어떨지 알 수 없었던 까닭에, 그 정직한 갑판장의 제안을 어떻게, 무슨 방식으로 받아들일지 결심을 내리지 못하고 있었으니, 사실 그도 처음에는 그것을 조금 이상하게 여겼다."]], [["At the end of this time, behold my husband came on board.", "이 기간이 끝날 무렵, 보라, 내 남편이 배에 올랐다."], ["He looked with a dejected, angry countenance, his great heart was swelled with rage and disdain; to be dragged along with three keepers of Newgate, and put on board like a convict, when he had not so much as been brought to a trial.", "그는 풀이 죽고 성난 얼굴빛이었으며, 그 큰 마음은 분노와 멸시로 부풀어 있었다. 재판조차 받지 않았는데도 뉴게이트의 간수 셋에게 끌려와 죄수처럼 배에 실린 것이었다."], ["He made loud complaints of it by his friends, for it seems he had some interest; but his friends got some check in their application, and were told he had had favour enough, and that they had received such an account of him, since the last grant of his transportation, that he ought to think himself very well treated that he was not prosecuted anew.", "그는 친구들을 통해 그 일을 크게 항의했으니, 그에게는 어느 정도 연줄이 있었던 모양이다. 그러나 그의 친구들은 청원하다가 제동이 걸렸고, 그가 충분히 은전을 입었다는 것과, 그의 유배가 마지막으로 허가된 이후로 그에 관해 그런 보고를 받았으니 새로이 기소되지 않은 것만으로도 매우 후한 대접을 받았다고 여겨야 마땅하다는 말을 들었다."], ["This answer quieted him at once, for he knew too much what might have happened, and what he had room to expect; and now he saw the goodness of the advice to him, which prevailed with him to accept of the offer of a voluntary transportation.", "이 대답이 그를 단숨에 잠잠하게 했으니, 그는 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있었는지, 또 그가 무엇을 예상할 만했는지를 너무도 잘 알았기 때문이다. 그리고 이제 그는 자발적인 유배의 제안을 받아들이도록 그를 설득했던, 그 충고의 훌륭함을 깨달았다."], ["And after this his chagrin at these hell-hounds, as he called them, was a little over, he looked a little composed, began to be cheerful, and as I was telling him how glad I was to have him once more out of their hands, he took me in his arms, and acknowledged with great tenderness that I had given him the best advice possible.", "이렇게 그가 지옥의 사냥개들이라 부르던 자들에 대한 그의 울분이 조금 가라앉은 뒤, 그는 다소 차분해 보였고 명랑해지기 시작했다. 내가 그를 다시 한번 그들의 손아귀에서 벗어나게 해서 얼마나 기쁜지 이야기하자, 그는 나를 두 팔로 끌어안고는, 더없는 다정함으로, 내가 그에게 더없이 좋은 충고를 해 주었음을 인정했다."], ["“My dear,”", "\"여보,\" 그가 말했다."], ["says he, “thou has twice saved my life; from henceforward it shall be all employed for you, and I’ll always take your advice.”", "\"당신은 두 번이나 내 목숨을 구했소. 이제부터 그 목숨은 모두 당신을 위해 쓰일 것이고, 나는 언제나 당신의 충고를 따르겠소.\""]], [["The ship began now to fill; several passengers came on board, who were embarked on no criminal account, and these had accommodations assigned them in the great cabin, and other parts of the ship, whereas we, as convicts, were thrust down below, I know not where.", "이제 배가 차기 시작했다. 범죄와는 무관하게 승선한 여러 승객이 배에 올랐는데, 이들에게는 큰 선실이나 배의 다른 부분에 숙소가 배정되었으나, 우리는 중범죄자라 하여 어딘지도 모를 아래쪽으로 밀쳐 넣어졌다."], ["But when my husband came on board, I spoke to the boatswain, who had so early given me hints of his friendship in carrying my letter.", "그러나 내 남편이 배에 올랐을 때, 나는 일찍이 내 편지를 전해 주며 호의를 넌지시 보였던 그 갑판장에게 말을 걸었다."], ["I told him he had befriended me in many things, and I had not made any suitable return to him, and with that I put a guinea into his hand.", "나는 그에게, 그가 여러 가지로 나를 도와주었는데도 내가 그에 합당한 보답을 하지 못했다고 말하며, 그러면서 그의 손에 기니 한 닢을 쥐여 주었다."], ["I told him that my husband was now come on board; that though we were both under the present misfortune, yet we had been persons of a different character from the wretched crew that we came with, and desired to know of him, whether the captain might not be moved to admit us to some conveniences in the ship, for which we would make him what satisfaction he pleased, and that we would gratify him for his pains in procuring this for us.", "나는 그에게, 이제 내 남편이 배에 올랐으며, 우리 둘 다 지금의 불운에 처해 있긴 하나, 우리는 함께 끌려온 그 비참한 무리와는 다른 부류의 사람들이었다고 말하고, 우리가 그가 원하는 만큼 사례를 할 터이니, 그리고 그가 우리를 위해 이 일을 주선해 주는 수고에 대해 후하게 보답할 터이니, 선장이 마음을 움직여 우리에게 배 안에서 얼마간 편의를 베풀어 주지 않겠는지 그에게 물어보고 싶다고 했다."], ["He took the guinea, as I could see, with great satisfaction, and assured me of his assistance.", "그는 내가 보기에 매우 흡족해하며 기니를 받았고, 자기가 돕겠노라고 내게 장담했다."]], [["Then he told us he did not doubt but that the captain, who was one of the best-humoured gentlemen in the world, would be easily brought to accommodate us as well as we could desire, and, to make me easy, told me he would go up the next tide on purpose to speak to the captain about it.", "그러더니 그는 우리에게, 세상에서 가장 너그러운 신사 가운데 하나인 선장이 우리가 바라는 만큼 기꺼이 우리를 편의 봐주리라는 것을 의심치 않는다고 말했고, 나를 안심시키려고, 그 일을 일부러 선장에게 말하러 다음 물때에 올라가겠다고 했다. 다음 날 아침, 마침 평소보다 조금 늦잠을 자고는, 일어나서 밖을 내다보기 시작했을 때, 나는 갑판장이 사내들 틈에서 평소의 일을 하고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["The next morning, happening to sleep a little longer than ordinary, when I got up, and began to look abroad, I saw the boatswain among the men in his ordinary business.", "그가 거기 있는 것을 보고 나는 조금 우울해져서, 그에게 말을 걸려고 다가가니, 그가 나를 보고 내 쪽으로 왔는데, 그가 먼저 말할 틈을 주지 않고 나는 미소 지으며 말했다."], ["I was a little melancholy at seeing him there, and going forward to speak to him, he saw me, and came towards me, but not giving him time to speak first, I said, smiling, “I doubt, sir, you have forgot us, for I see you are very busy.”", "\"선장님, 우리를 잊으신 것 같네요. 보아하니 매우 바쁘신 듯하니 말이에요.\""], ["He returned presently, “Come along with me, and you shall see.”", "그가 곧 대답했다. \"저와 함께 가십시다."], ["So he took me into the great cabin, and there sat a good sort of a gentlemanly man for a seaman, writing, and with a great many papers before him.", "그러면 보실 겁니다.\" 그렇게 그는 나를 큰 선실로 데려갔는데, 거기에는 뱃사람치고는 점잖은 신사다운 사람이 앉아서, 앞에 수많은 서류를 늘어놓고 글을 쓰고 있었다."]], [["“Here,” says the boatswain to him that was a-writing, “is the gentlewoman that the captain spoke to you of”; and turning to me, he said, “I have been so far from forgetting your business, that I have been up at the captain’s house, and have represented faithfully to the captain what you said, relating to you being furnished with better conveniences for yourself and your husband; and the captain has sent this gentleman, who is mate of the ship, down with me, on purpose to show you everything, and to accommodate you fully to your content, and bid me assure you that you shall not be treated like what you were at first expected to be, but with the same respect as other passengers are treated.”", "\"여기,\" 갑판장이 글을 쓰고 있던 그에게 말했다. \"선장님께서 당신께 말씀하신 그 부인입니다.\" 그러고는 내게 돌아서서 말했다. \"저는 당신의 일을 잊기는커녕, 선장님 댁까지 올라가서, 당신과 당신 남편을 위해 더 나은 편의를 갖춰 주는 일에 관해 당신이 하신 말씀을 선장님께 충실히 전했습니다. 그리고 선장님께서는 이 신사분을, 곧 이 배의 항해사를 일부러 저와 함께 내려보내, 당신께 모든 것을 보여드리고, 당신이 만족하시도록 충분히 편의를 봐드리게 하셨으며, 당신이 처음에 예상되던 그런 대접이 아니라 다른 승객들과 똑같은 존중을 받게 되리라는 것을 당신께 장담하라고 제게 분부하셨습니다.\""]], [["The mate then spoke to me, and, not giving me time to thank the boatswain for his kindness, confirmed what the boatswain had said, and added that it was the captain’s delight to show himself kind and charitable, especially to those that were under any misfortunes, and with that he showed me several cabins built up, some in the great cabin, and some partitioned off, out of the steerage, but opening into the great cabin on purpose for the accommodation of passengers, and gave me leave to choose where I would.", "그러자 항해사가 내게 말을 걸었는데, 내가 갑판장의 친절에 감사할 틈도 주지 않고, 갑판장이 한 말을 확인해 주며, 선장은 친절하고 자비로운 모습을 보이기를, 특히 어떤 불운에 처한 이들에게 그러기를 좋아한다고 덧붙였다. 그러면서 그는 내게, 어떤 것은 큰 선실에 만들어지고, 어떤 것은 조타실에서 칸막이로 막되 일부러 승객 편의를 위해 큰 선실 쪽으로 트인, 여러 선실을 보여주며 내가 원하는 곳을 고르게 했다."], ["However, I chose a cabin which opened into the steerage, in which was very good conveniences to set our chest and boxes, and a table to eat on.", "그렇지만 나는 조타실 쪽으로 트인 선실을 골랐으니, 거기에는 우리 궤짝과 상자들을 둘 매우 좋은 자리와, 식사할 탁자가 있었다."]], [["The mate then told me that the boatswain had given so good a character of me and my husband, as to our civil behaviour, that he had orders to tell me we should eat with him, if we thought fit, during the whole voyage, on the common terms of passengers; that we might lay in some fresh provisions, if we pleased; or if not, he should lay in his usual store, and we should have share with him.", "그러더니 항해사는 내게, 우리의 점잖은 처신에 관해 갑판장이 나와 내 남편에 대해 너무도 좋게 말해 두었으므로, 우리가 원한다면 항해 내내 일반 승객의 통상적인 조건으로 선장과 함께 식사하게 될 것이라고 전하라는 분부를 받았다고 말했다. 우리가 원한다면 신선한 식량을 좀 사 두어도 좋고, 그러지 않겠다면 선장이 평소의 저장 식량을 마련할 터이니 우리가 그와 나누면 된다고 했다."], ["This was very reviving news to me, after so many hardships and afflictions as I had gone through of late.", "이는 근래 내가 겪어 온 그토록 많은 고난과 고초 끝에, 내게 무척이나 생기를 되살려 주는 소식이었다."], ["I thanked him, and told him the captain should make his own terms with us, and asked him leave to go and tell my husband of it, who was not very well, and was not yet out of his cabin.", "나는 그에게 감사를 표하고, 선장이 우리와 자기 마음대로 조건을 정하면 된다고 말했으며, 가서 남편에게 이를 알려도 되겠는지 허락을 청했으니, 남편은 몸이 그리 좋지 않아 아직 선실에서 나오지 않은 터였다."], ["Accordingly I went, and my husband, whose spirits were still so much sunk with the indignity (as he understood it) offered him, that he was scarce yet himself, was so revived with the account that I gave him of the reception we were like to have in the ship, that he was quite another man, and new vigour and courage appeared in his very countenance.", "그래서 나는 갔고, 남편은 (그가 받아들이기로는) 자기에게 가해진 모욕으로 여전히 기운이 너무도 가라앉아서 아직 제정신이 아닐 지경이었는데, 우리가 배에서 받게 될 대접에 관해 내가 전해 준 이야기에 어찌나 생기를 되찾았는지, 완전히 다른 사람이 되어, 새로운 활기와 용기가 바로 그 얼굴빛에 떠올랐다."], ["So true is it, that the greatest of spirits, when overwhelmed by their afflictions, are subject to the greatest dejections, and are the most apt to despair and give themselves up.", "가장 큰 기개를 지닌 자도 그 고난에 짓눌리면 가장 큰 의기소침에 빠지기 쉽고, 가장 절망하여 스스로를 내던지기 쉽다는 것은 이토록 참된 일이다."]], [["After some little pause to recover himself, my husband came up with me, and gave the mate thanks for the kindness, which he had expressed to us, and sent suitable acknowledgment by him to the captain, offering to pay him by advance, whatever he demanded for our passage, and for the conveniences he had helped us to.", "마음을 추스르려 잠시 멈춘 뒤, 내 남편이 나와 함께 올라가서 우리에게 베풀어 준 친절에 대해 항해사에게 감사를 표하고, 그를 통해 선장에게 합당한 사례의 뜻을 전했으니, 우리 뱃삯과, 그가 우리에게 마련해 준 편의에 대해 무엇이든 요구하는 대로 미리 치르겠노라고 했다."], ["The mate told him that the captain would be on board in the afternoon, and that he would leave all that till he came.", "항해사는 그에게, 선장이 오후에 배에 오를 터이니 그 모든 일은 선장이 올 때까지 두겠다고 말했다."], ["Accordingly, in the afternoon the captain came, and we found him the same courteous, obliging man that the boatswain had represented him to be; and he was so well pleased with my husband’s conversation, that, in short, he would not let us keep the cabin we had chosen, but gave us one that, as I said before, opened into the great cabin.", "그리하여 오후에 선장이 왔는데, 우리는 그가 갑판장이 묘사한 그대로 예의 바르고 친절한 사람임을 알았다. 그는 내 남편의 대화에 어찌나 흡족해했는지, 한마디로 우리가 고른 선실을 쓰지 못하게 하고, 앞서 말한 대로 큰 선실 쪽으로 트인 선실을 우리에게 내주었다."]], [["Nor were his conditions exorbitant, or the man craving and eager to make a prey of us, but for fifteen guineas we had our whole passage and provisions and cabin, ate at the captain’s table, and were very handsomely entertained.", "그의 조건도 터무니없지 않았고, 그 사람은 욕심을 부려 우리를 먹잇감으로 삼으려 안달하지도 않았으니, 다만 십오 기니로 우리는 항해 전체와 식량과 선실을 얻었고, 선장의 식탁에서 식사하며 매우 융숭한 대접을 받았다."]], [["The captain lay himself in the other part of the great cabin, having let his round house, as they call it, to a rich planter who went over with his wife and three children, who ate by themselves.", "선장은 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 자기 후갑판실을, 아내와 세 아이를 데리고 건너가는 어느 부유한 농장주에게 빌려준 터라, 큰 선실의 다른 부분에 자리를 잡았는데, 그 가족은 따로 식사를 했다."], ["He had some other ordinary passengers, who quartered in the steerage, and as for our old fraternity, they were kept under the hatches while the ship lay there, and came very little on the deck.", "그에게는 다른 평범한 승객도 몇몇 있어 조타실에 묵었고, 우리의 옛 동무들로 말하자면, 배가 거기 정박해 있는 동안 승강구 아래에 갇혀 있어 갑판에는 거의 올라오지 못했다."]], [["I could not refrain acquainting my governess with what had happened; it was but just that she, who was so really concerned for me, should have part in my good fortune.", "나는 일어난 일을 후견인에게 알리지 않을 수 없었으니, 나를 그토록 진심으로 염려해 준 그녀가 내 행운에 한몫을 하는 것은 마땅한 일이었다. 게다가 나는 그녀의 도움을 받아 여러 필수품을 마련해야 했는데, 전에는 그것이 남들 눈에 띄지 않게 하려고, 누군가 내가 그런 것을 지닌 것을 보게 하기를 꺼렸지만, 이제 나는 물건을 둘 선실과 자리가 생겼으므로, 항해 중에 우리가 편하도록 좋은 물건을 잔뜩 주문했으니, 펀치를 만들고 우리의 은인인 선장을 대접할 브랜디, 설탕, 레몬 따위와, 항해 중에 먹고 마실 온갖 것이었다."], ["Besides, I wanted her assistance to supply me with several necessaries, which before I was shy of letting anybody see me have, that it might not be public; but now I had a cabin and room to set things in, I ordered abundance of good things for our comfort in the voyage, as brandy, sugar, lemons, etc., to make punch, and treat our benefactor, the captain; and abundance of things for eating and drinking in the voyage; also a larger bed, and bedding proportioned to it; so that, in a word, we resolved to want for nothing in the voyage.", "또한 더 큰 침대와, 거기에 어울리는 침구도 주문했다. 한마디로, 우리는 항해 중에 아무것도 부족하지 않기로 작정한 것이다."]], [["All this while I had provided nothing for our assistance when we should come to the place and begin to call ourselves planters; and I was far from being ignorant of what was needful on that occasion; particularly all sorts of tools for the planter’s work, and for building; and all kinds of furniture for our dwelling, which, if to be bought in the country, must necessarily cost double the price.", "이 모든 동안에도 나는, 우리가 그곳에 도착해 우리 자신을 농장주라 일컫기 시작할 때 우리에게 도움이 될 것을 아무것도 마련하지 못한 터였다. 그리고 나는 그런 경우에 무엇이 필요한지를 결코 모르지 않았으니, 특히 농장 일과 건축에 쓸 온갖 연장과, 우리 거처에 쓸 온갖 가구가 그것인데, 이는 그 나라에서 사려면 반드시 두 배의 값이 들 터였다."]], [["So I discoursed that point with my governess, and she went and waited upon the captain, and told him that she hoped ways might be found out for her two unfortunate cousins, as she called us, to obtain our freedom when we came into the country, and so entered into a discourse with him about the means and terms also, of which I shall say more in its place; and after thus sounding the captain, she let him know, though we were unhappy in the circumstances that occasioned our going, yet that we were not unfurnished to set ourselves to work in the country, and we resolved to settle and live there as planters, if we might be put in a way how to do it.", "그래서 나는 그 점을 후견인과 의논했고, 그녀는 가서 선장을 찾아뵙고는, 자기가 사촌이라 부르던 우리 두 불운한 자가 그 나라에 도착했을 때 자유를 얻을 방도가 찾아질 수 있기를 바란다고 말하면서, 그 방법과 조건에 관해서도 그와 의논에 들어갔는데, 그에 관해서는 적당한 자리에서 더 말하겠다. 이렇게 선장의 속을 떠본 뒤, 그녀는 그에게, 우리가 비록 우리를 떠나게 만든 그 정황으로 인해 불행하긴 하나, 그 나라에서 일을 시작할 채비가 없지 않으며, 그곳에 정착해 농장주로 살아갈 길만 마련된다면 그리하기로 작정했다는 것을 알렸다."], ["The captain readily offered his assistance, told her the method of entering upon such business, and how easy, nay, how certain it was for industrious people to recover their fortunes in such a manner.", "선장은 선뜻 도움을 베풀겠다고 하며, 그녀에게 그런 사업에 착수하는 방법과, 부지런한 사람들이 그런 식으로 재산을 회복하기가 얼마나 쉬운지, 아니, 얼마나 확실한지를 일러 주었다. \"부인,\""], ["“Madam,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “’tis no reproach to any many in that country to have been sent over in worse circumstances than I perceive your cousins are in, provided they do but apply with diligence and good judgment to the business of that place when they come there.”", "\"그 나라에서는 제가 보기에 당신 사촌들이 처한 것보다 더 나쁜 처지로 보내져 온 것도 누구에게든 결코 수치가 아니랍니다. 그저 그들이 그곳에 도착해서 그 고장의 일에 부지런함과 좋은 분별로 임하기만 한다면 말이지요.\""]], [["She then inquired of him what things it was necessary we should carry over with us, and he, like a very honest as well as knowing man, told her thus: “Madam, your cousins in the first place must procure somebody to buy them as servants, in conformity to the conditions of their transportation, and then, in the name of that person, they may go about what they will; they may either purchase some plantations already begun, or they may purchase land of the Government of the country, and begin where they please, and both will be done reasonably.” She bespoke his favour in the first article, which he promised to her to take upon himself, and indeed faithfully performed it, and as to the rest, he promised to recommend us to such as should give us the best advice, and not to impose upon us, which was as much as could be desired.", "그러자 그녀는 그에게, 우리가 가지고 건너가야 할 것이 무엇인지 물었고, 그는 매우 정직할 뿐 아니라 식견 있는 사람으로서 그녀에게 이렇게 일러 주었다. \"부인, 당신 사촌들은 우선 그들의 유배 조건에 따라 자기들을 종으로 사 줄 누군가를 구해야 합니다. 그런 다음 그 사람의 이름으로 그들은 무엇이든 마음대로 할 수 있지요. 이미 시작된 농장을 사들이든, 아니면 그 나라 정부로부터 땅을 사서 원하는 곳에서 시작하든, 둘 다 합당한 값에 할 수 있습니다.\" 그녀는 첫 번째 항목에 관해 그의 호의를 청했고, 그는 그 일을 자기가 떠맡겠노라고 그녀에게 약속했으며, 실로 충실히 그것을 해냈다. 나머지에 관해서는, 우리에게 가장 좋은 조언을 해 줄 만한 이들에게 우리를 추천하고, 우리를 속이지 않게 하겠노라고 약속했으니, 그것은 바랄 수 있는 전부였다."]], [["She then asked him if it would not be necessary to furnish us with a stock of tools and materials for the business of planting, and he said, “Yes, by all means.”", "그러더니 그녀는 그에게, 우리에게 농사일에 쓸 연장과 자재를 한 밑천 갖춰 줄 필요는 없겠는지 물었고, 그는 \"그렇습니다, 무슨 수를 써서라도요\"라고 말했다."], ["And then she begged his assistance in it.", "그러자 그녀는 그 일에 그의 도움을 청했다."], ["She told him she would furnish us with everything that was convenient whatever it cost her.", "그녀는 그에게, 비용이 얼마가 들든 우리에게 편리한 것은 무엇이든 다 갖춰 주겠노라고 말했다."], ["He accordingly gave her a long particular of things necessary for a planter, which, by his account, came to about fourscore or a hundred pounds.", "그래서 그는 그녀에게 농장주에게 필요한 물건의 긴 목록을 일러 주었는데, 그의 셈으로는 그것이 팔십 내지 백 파운드쯤에 이르렀다."], ["And, in short, she went about as dexterously to buy them, as if she had been an old Virginia merchant; only that she bought, by my direction, above twice as much of everything as he had given her a list of.", "한마디로, 그녀는 마치 노련한 버지니아 상인이라도 되는 양 능란하게 그것들을 사러 다녔으니, 다만 내 지시에 따라 그가 일러 준 목록의 모든 것을 두 배 넘게 사들였다는 점만 달랐다."]], [["These she put on board in her own name, took his bills of loading for them, and endorsed those bills of loading to my husband, insuring the cargo afterwards in her own name, by our order; so that we were provided for all events, and for all disasters.", "이것들을 그녀는 자기 이름으로 배에 실었고, 그것들의 선하증권을 받아 내 남편 앞으로 배서했으며, 그 후 우리 지시에 따라 자기 이름으로 화물에 보험을 들었으니, 그리하여 우리는 온갖 사태와 온갖 재난에 대비가 되었다."]], [["I should have told you that my husband gave her all his whole stock of £108, which, as I have said, he had about him in gold, to lay out thus, and I gave her a good sum besides; so that I did not break into the stock which I had left in her hands at all, but after we had sorted out our whole cargo, we had yet near £200 in money, which was more than enough for our purpose.", "내 남편이 그녀에게 자기 밑천 백팔 파운드 전부를, 앞서 말했듯이 그가 금화로 몸에 지니고 있던 것을, 이렇게 쓰라고 내주었다는 것을 말해 두었어야 했다. 그리고 나도 그 밖에 상당한 액수를 그녀에게 주었으니, 그래서 나는 그녀의 손에 맡겨 둔 밑천에는 전혀 손을 대지 않았으며, 우리 화물을 전부 갖춘 뒤에도 우리에게는 여전히 이백 파운드 가까운 돈이 남아 있었는데, 그것은 우리 목적에 차고도 남았다."]], [["In this condition, very cheerful, and indeed joyful at being so happily accommodated as we were, we set sail from Bugby’s Hole to Gravesend, where the ship lay about ten more days, and where the captain came on board for good and all.", "이런 처지로, 매우 명랑하게, 실로 그토록 만족스럽게 편의를 봐주신 것에 기뻐하며, 우리는 버그비스홀에서 그레이브젠드로 출항했고, 배는 그곳에 열흘 남짓 더 정박했으며, 거기서 선장이 아주 눌러앉으려 배에 올랐다."], ["Here the captain offered us a civility, which indeed we had no reason to expect, namely, to let us go on shore and refresh ourselves, upon giving our words in a solemn manner that we would not go from him, and that we would return peaceably on board again.", "여기서 선장은 우리가 실로 기대할 까닭이 전혀 없던 호의를 베풀었으니, 곧 우리가 그에게서 달아나지 않고 다시 얌전히 배로 돌아오겠다고 엄숙하게 약속하면, 뭍에 올라 기분을 풀고 오게 해 주겠다는 것이었다."], ["This was such an evidence of his confidence in us, that it overcame my husband, who, in a mere principle of gratitude, told him, as he could not be in any capacity to make a suitable return for such a favour, so he could not think of accepting of it, nor could he be easy that the captain should run such a risk.", "이것은 우리에 대한 그의 신뢰의 그런 증거였던지라, 내 남편을 감복시켰으니, 그는 순전한 감사의 마음에서, 자기로서는 그런 호의에 합당한 보답을 할 처지가 도무지 못 되므로 그것을 받아들일 생각조차 할 수 없으며, 선장이 그런 위험을 무릅쓰게 하는 것도 마음 편치 않다고 그에게 말했다."], ["After some mutual civilities, I gave my husband a purse, in which was eighty guineas, and he put in into the captain’s hand.", "서로 몇 마디 예의를 주고받은 뒤, 나는 남편에게 팔십 기니가 든 지갑 하나를 건넸고, 그는 그것을 선장의 손에 쥐여 주었다. \"자, 선장님,\""], ["“There, captain,”", "그가 말했다."], ["says he, “there’s part of a pledge for our fidelity; if we deal dishonestly with you on any account, ’tis your own.”", "\"여기 우리 신의의 담보 일부가 있습니다. 우리가 어떤 일로든 당신을 부정하게 대한다면, 그것은 당신 것입니다.\""], ["And on this we went on shore.", "그러고 나서 우리는 뭍에 올랐다."]], [["Indeed, the captain had assurance enough of our resolutions to go, for that having made such provision to settle there, it did not seem rational that we would choose to remain here at the expense and peril of life, for such it must have been if we had been taken again.", "실로 선장은 우리가 떠나리라는 것에 대한 확신이 충분했으니, 우리가 그곳에 정착하려고 그런 채비를 갖춰 둔 마당에, 목숨을 건 비용과 위험을 무릅쓰면서까지 여기 남기를 택하리라는 것은 이치에 닿지 않아 보였기 때문이다. 만약 우리가 다시 붙잡힌다면 그것은 분명 그런 꼴이 되었을 터이다."], ["In a word, we went all on shore with the captain, and supped together in Gravesend, where we were very merry, stayed all night, lay at the house where we supped, and came all very honestly on board again with him in the morning.", "한마디로, 우리는 선장과 함께 모두 뭍에 올라 그레이브젠드에서 함께 저녁을 먹었고, 거기서 매우 흥겹게 보냈으며, 밤새 머물러 우리가 저녁을 먹은 그 집에서 잤고, 아침에 모두 그와 함께 아주 떳떳이 다시 배에 올랐다."], ["Here we bought ten dozen bottles of good beer, some wine, some fowls, and such things as we thought might be acceptable on board.", "여기서 우리는 좋은 맥주 열두 다스와 포도주 약간, 닭 몇 마리, 그리고 배 안에서 환영받을 만하다 싶은 것들을 샀다."]], [["My governess was with us all this while, and went with us round into the Downs, as did also the captain’s wife, with whom she went back.", "내 후견인은 이 모든 동안 우리와 함께 있었고, 우리와 더불어 다운스까지 돌아 나왔으며, 선장의 아내도 그랬으니, 후견인은 그 아내와 함께 돌아갔다."], ["I was never so sorrowful at parting with my own mother as I was at parting with her, and I never saw her more.", "나는 내 친어머니와 헤어질 때도 그녀와 헤어질 때만큼 슬프지는 않았고, 그 뒤로 다시는 그녀를 보지 못했다."], ["We had a fair easterly wind sprung up the third day after we came to the Downs, and we sailed from thence the 10th of April.", "우리가 다운스에 다다른 지 사흘째 되는 날 순한 동풍이 일었고, 우리는 사월 십 일에 그곳에서 출항했다."], ["Nor did we touch any more at any place, till, being driven on the coast of Ireland by a very hard gale of wind, the ship came to an anchor in a little bay, near the mouth of a river, whose name I remember not, but they said the river came down from Limerick, and that it was the largest river in Ireland.", "그 뒤로는 어디에도 들르지 않았으나, 매우 거센 강풍에 아일랜드 해안으로 밀려가, 배가 이름은 기억나지 않는 어느 강 어귀 근처의 작은 만에 닻을 내렸다. 사람들 말로는 그 강이 리머릭에서 내려온다 했고, 아일랜드에서 가장 큰 강이라고 했다."]], [["Here, being detained by bad weather for some time, the captain, who continued the same kind, good-humoured man as at first, took us two on shore with him again.", "여기서 나쁜 날씨에 한동안 발이 묶이게 되자, 처음과 똑같이 친절하고 너그러운 사람이던 선장이 다시 우리 둘을 데리고 뭍에 올랐다."], ["He did it now in kindness to my husband indeed, who bore the sea very ill, and was very sick, especially when it blew so hard.", "그가 이제 이렇게 한 것은 실로 내 남편에 대한 친절에서였으니, 남편은 바다를 몹시 견디지 못해 무척이나 멀미를 했고, 특히 그렇게 바람이 거세게 불 때는 더했다."], ["Here we bought in again a store of fresh provisions, especially beef, pork, mutton, and fowls, and the captain stayed to pickle up five or six barrels of beef to lengthen out the ship’s store.", "여기서 우리는 다시 신선한 식량을, 특히 소고기, 돼지고기, 양고기, 닭을 한 밑천 사들였고, 선장은 배의 저장 식량을 늘리려고 소고기 대여섯 통을 절여 두느라 머물렀다."], ["We were here not above five days, when the weather turning mild, and a fair wind, we set sail again, and in two-and-forty days came safe to the coast of Virginia.", "우리는 여기서 닷새도 채 있지 않았는데, 날씨가 온화해지고 순풍이 불자 다시 출항했고, 마흔이틀 만에 무사히 버지니아 해안에 다다랐다."]], [["When we drew near to the shore, the captain called me to him, and told me that he found by my discourse I had some relations in the place, and that I had been there before, and so he supposed I understood the custom in their disposing the convict prisoners when they arrived.", "우리가 해안에 가까워졌을 때, 선장이 나를 불러서, 내 말투로 보아 내게 그곳에 친척이 좀 있고 또 전에도 거기 와 본 적이 있는 듯하니, 중범죄자가 도착했을 때 그들을 처분하는 관습을 내가 알고 있으리라 짐작한다고 말했다."], ["I told him I did not, and that as to what relations I had in the place, he might be sure I would make myself known to none of them while I was in the circumstances of a prisoner, and that as to the rest, we left ourselves entirely to him to assist us, as he was pleased to promise us he would do.", "나는 모른다고 했고, 그곳에 내게 어떤 친척이 있든, 내가 죄수의 처지에 있는 동안에는 그 누구에게도 나를 알리지 않으리라는 것만은 그가 분명히 믿어도 좋으며, 나머지에 관해서는 그가 우리에게 약속해 준 대로 우리를 도와주는 것을 그에게 전적으로 맡기겠다고 했다."], ["He told me I must get somebody in the place to come and buy us as servants, and who must answer for us to the governor of the country, if he demanded us.", "그는 내게, 우리를 종으로 사러 와서, 만약 그 나라 총독이 우리를 요구하면 우리에 대해 책임져 줄 누군가를 그곳에서 구해야 한다고 말했다."], ["I told him we should do as he should direct; so he brought a planter to treat with him, as it were, for the purchase of these two servants, my husband and me, and there we were formally sold to him, and went ashore with him.", "나는 그가 지시하는 대로 하겠다고 했다. 그래서 그는 어느 농장주를 데려와, 이를테면 이 두 종, 곧 내 남편과 나를 사들이는 일을 그와 흥정하게 했고, 거기서 우리는 격식대로 그에게 팔려 그와 함께 뭍에 올랐다."], ["The captain went with us, and carried us to a certain house, whether it was to be called a tavern or not I know not, but we had a bowl of punch there made of rum, etc., and were very merry.", "선장이 우리와 함께 가서, 우리를 어떤 집으로 데려갔는데, 그곳을 술집이라 불러야 할지 어떨지는 모르겠으나, 우리는 거기서 럼주 따위로 만든 펀치 한 사발을 마시고 매우 흥겹게 지냈다."], ["After some time the planter gave us a certificate of discharge, and an acknowledgment of having served him faithfully, and we were free from him the next morning, to go wither we would.", "얼마쯤 지난 뒤 그 농장주가 우리에게 방면 증서와, 우리가 자기를 충실히 섬겼다는 확인서를 주었고, 우리는 이튿날 아침 그에게서 풀려나 어디든 가고 싶은 곳으로 갈 수 있었다."]], [["For this piece of service the captain demanded of us six thousand weight of tabacco, which he said he was accountable for to his freighter, and which we immediately bought for him, and made him a present of twenty guineas besides, with which he was abundantly satisfied.", "이 일을 봐준 대가로 선장은 우리에게 담배 육천 무게를 요구했는데, 그가 자기 화주에게 그것을 책임져야 한다고 했고, 우리는 그것을 곧바로 사서 그에게 주었으며, 그 밖에 이십 기니를 선물로 안겼으니, 그는 그것으로 더없이 만족스러워했다."]], [["It is not proper to enter here into the particulars of what part of the colony of Virginia we settled in, for divers reasons; it may suffice to mention that we went into the great river Potomac, the ship being bound thither; and there we intended to have settled first, though afterwards we altered our minds.", "여러 까닭으로, 우리가 버지니아 식민지의 어느 지역에 정착했는지 그 세세한 사정을 여기 적어 넣는 것은 적절치 않다. 다만, 배가 그리로 향하던 까닭에 우리가 큰 강 포토맥강으로 들어갔다는 것만 언급하면 충분하겠다. 그리고 거기서 우리는 처음에 정착하려 했으나, 나중에 마음을 바꾸었다."]], [["The first thing I did of moment after having gotten all our goods on shore, and placed them in a storehouse, or warehouse, which, with a lodging, we hired at the small place or village where we landed—I say, the first thing was to inquire after my mother, and after my brother (that fatal person whom I married as a husband, as I have related at large). A little inquiry furnished me with information that Mrs.", "우리의 모든 물건을 뭍에 내려, 우리가 상륙한 작은 고장이랄까 마을에서 거처와 함께 빌린 창고, 곧 물품 보관소에 넣어 둔 뒤에 내가 한 첫 번째 중요한 일은, 내 어머니와, 그리고 (앞서 길게 이야기했듯이 내가 남편으로 결혼했던 그 운명의 사람인) 내 오라비의 행방을 수소문하는 것이었다."], ["——, that is, my mother, was dead; that my brother (or husband) was alive, which I confess I was not very glad to hear; but which was worse, I found he was removed from the plantation where he lived formerly, and where I lived with him, and lived with one of his sons in a plantation just by the place where we landed, and where we had hired a warehouse.", "조금 알아보니, 이런저런 부인, 곧 내 어머니가 돌아가셨고, 내 오라비(혹은 남편)는 살아 있다는 정보를 얻었는데, 솔직히 그 소식을 듣고 나는 그리 기쁘지 않았다. 그러나 더 나쁜 것은, 그가 예전에 살던, 그리고 내가 그와 함께 살던 농장에서 옮겨, 우리가 상륙해 창고를 빌린 바로 그 자리 근처의 농장에서 자기 아들 중 하나와 살고 있다는 것을 알게 된 일이었다."]], [["I was a little surprised at first, but as I ventured to satisfy myself that he could not know me, I was not only perfectly easy, but had a great mind to see him, if it was possible to so do without his seeing me.", "나는 처음에는 조금 놀랐으나, 그가 나를 알아볼 수 없으리라고 스스로 마음을 다잡고 나니, 완전히 마음이 놓였을 뿐 아니라, 그가 나를 보지 않고서 그럴 수만 있다면 그를 보고 싶은 마음이 간절해졌다."], ["In order to that I found out by inquiry the plantation where he lived, and with a woman of that place whom I got to help me, like what we call a chairwoman, I rambled about towards the place as if I had only a mind to see the country and look about me.", "그러기 위해 나는 수소문하여 그가 사는 농장을 알아냈고, 우리가 가정부라 부르는 따위로 나를 도와줄 사람으로 구한, 그 고장의 어느 여자와 함께, 마치 그저 그 시골을 구경하며 둘러보고 싶은 양 그 자리 쪽으로 어슬렁어슬렁 다녔다."], ["At last I came so near that I saw the dwellinghouse.", "마침내 나는 그 살림집이 보일 만큼 가까이 다가갔다."], ["I asked the woman whose plantation that was; she said it belonged to such a man, and looking out a little to our right hands, “there,”", "나는 그 여자에게 저것이 누구의 농장이냐고 물었고, 그녀는 어떤 사람의 것이라고 하더니, 우리 오른쪽을 조금 내다보며 \"저기,\" 하고 말했다."], ["says she, “is the gentleman that owns the plantation, and his father with him.”", "\"저분이 저 농장의 주인이고, 함께 있는 분은 그의 아버지예요.\" \"그분들의 세례명은 무엇인가요?\""], ["“What are their Christian names?”", "내가 말했다. \"그 노신사분의 이름은 모르겠어요,\""], ["said I. “I know not,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “what the old gentleman’s name is, but the son’s name is Humphrey; and I believe,”", "\"하지만 아들의 이름은 험프리예요. 그리고 아버지도 그럴 거예요,\""], ["says she, “the father’s is so too.”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["You may guess, if you can, what a confused mixture of joy and fight possessed my thoughts upon this occasion, for I immediately knew that this was nobody else but my own son, by that father she showed me, who was my own brother.", "이 일로 인해 얼마나 어지러운 기쁨과 두려움이 뒤섞여 내 생각을 사로잡았는지는, 여러분이 짐작할 수 있다면 짐작해 보시라. 나는 그녀가 가리킨 그 아버지로 보아, 이가 다름 아닌 내 친아들임을 곧바로 알아차렸으니, 그 아버지가 내 친오라비였기 때문이다."], ["I had no mask, but I ruffled my hood so about my face, that I depended upon it that after above twenty years’ absence, and withal not expecting anything of me in that part of the world, he would not be able to know anything of me.", "나는 가면이 없었으나, 두건을 얼굴 둘레로 잔뜩 헝클어 두르고는, 이십 년 넘게 떨어져 있었던 데다 세상의 그 지역에서 나에 관한 어떤 것도 예상하지 못할 그가 나를 전혀 알아보지 못하리라 믿었다."], ["But I need not have used all that caution, for the old gentleman was grown dim-sighted by some distemper which had fallen upon his eyes, and could but just see well enough to walk about, and not run against a tree or into a ditch.", "그러나 나는 그 모든 조심을 다 할 필요가 없었으니, 그 노신사는 눈에 어떤 병이 생겨 침침해진 터라, 나무에 부딪히거나 도랑에 빠지지 않고 돌아다닐 수 있을 만큼만 겨우 보일 뿐이었다. 함께 있던 여자가 그저 우연히, 그것이 내게 얼마나 중요한 일인지는 전혀 모르고 그렇게 말해 준 것이었다."], ["The woman that was with me had told me that by a mere accident, knowing nothing of what importance it was to me.", "그들이 우리에게 가까이 다가오자, 나는 \"그분이 당신을 알아보나요, 오언 부인?\""], ["As they drew near to us, I said, “Does he know you, Mrs.", "하고 물었다(사람들이 그 여자를 그렇게 불렀다)."], ["Owen?”", "\"네,\""], ["(so they called the woman). “Yes,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["said she, “if he hears me speak, he will know me; but he can’t see well enough to know me or anybody else”; and so she told me the story of his sight, as I have related.", "\"제가 말하는 걸 들으면 저를 알아보세요. 하지만 저나 다른 누구를 알아볼 만큼 잘 보지는 못하시죠.\""], ["This made me secure, and so I threw open my hoods again, and let them pass by me.", "그러고는 내가 이야기한 대로 그의 시력에 관한 사연을 들려주었다."], ["It was a wretched thing for a mother thus to see her own son, a handsome, comely young gentleman in flourishing circumstances, and durst not make herself known to him, and durst not take any notice of him.", "이것이 나를 안심시켰고, 그래서 나는 다시 두건을 젖히고는 그들이 내 곁을 지나가게 두었다. 한 어머니가 이렇게 자기 친아들을, 번창하는 형편의 잘생기고 훤칠한 젊은 신사를 보면서도, 감히 자신을 그에게 알리지 못하고, 감히 그를 아는 체하지 못하는 것은 비참한 일이었다."], ["Let any mother of children that reads this consider it, and but think with what anguish of mind I restrained myself; what yearnings of soul I had in me to embrace him, and weep over him; and how I thought all my entrails turned within me, that my very bowels moved, and I knew not what to do, as I now know not how to express those agonies!", "이 글을 읽는 자식 둔 어머니라면 누구든 이를 헤아려, 내가 어떤 마음의 고뇌로 나 자신을 억눌렀는지, 그를 끌어안고 그 위에 눈물 흘리고 싶은 어떤 영혼의 갈망이 내 안에 있었는지, 또 어떻게 내 온 오장이 안에서 뒤집히고, 바로 그 창자가 움직이는 듯했으며, 어쩔 줄을 몰랐는지를 그저 생각해 보시라. 지금도 나는 그 고통을 어떻게 표현해야 할지 모르겠다!"], ["When he went from me I stood gazing and trembling, and looking after him as long as I could see him; then sitting down to rest me, but turned from her, and lying on my face, wept, and kissed the ground that he had set his foot on.", "그가 내게서 멀어져 갈 때 나는 응시하며 떨면서 서서, 보일 수 있는 한 멀리까지 그를 바라보았고, 그러고는 쉬려고 주저앉되, 그녀에게서 돌아앉아 얼굴을 땅에 대고 엎드려 울며, 그가 발을 디뎠던 땅에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["I could not conceal my disorder so much from the woman but that she perceived it, and thought I was not well, which I was obliged to pretend was true; upon which she pressed me to rise, the ground being damp and dangerous, which I did accordingly, and walked away.", "나는 내 흐트러진 모습을 그 여자에게 충분히 감출 수 없어서 그녀가 알아차렸고, 내가 몸이 좋지 않은 줄로 여겼는데, 나는 그것이 사실인 양 꾸며 댈 수밖에 없었다. 그러자 그녀는 땅이 축축하고 위험하다며 일어나라고 재촉했고, 나는 그에 따라 일어나 걸어갔다."]], [["As I was going back again, and still talking of this gentleman and his son, a new occasion of melancholy offered itself thus.", "다시 돌아가면서 여전히 이 신사와 그 아들에 관해 이야기하던 중에, 새로운 우울거리가 이렇게 떠올랐다. 그 여자가, 마치 나를 기분 전환시켜 주려고 이야기 하나를 들려주려는 양 입을 열었다."], ["The woman began, as if she would tell me a story to divert me: “There goes,”", "\"저기,\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["says she, “a very odd tale among the neighbours where this gentleman formerly live.”", "\"이 신사분이 예전에 살던 곳의 이웃들 사이에 아주 묘한 이야기가 돌고 있어요.\" \"그게 무엇인가요?\""], ["“What was that?”", "내가 말했다. \"글쎄요,\""], ["said I.", "그녀가 말했다. \"저 노신사분이 젊었을 때 잉글랜드에 갔다가, 거기서 어느 젊은 부인과 사랑에 빠졌대요."], ["“Why,”", "일찍이 본 적 없는 가장 아리따운 여자 중 하나였다지요."], ["says she, “that old gentleman going to England, when he was a young man, fell in love with a young lady there, one of the finest women that ever was seen, and married her, and brought her over hither to his mother who was then living.", "그래서 그 여자와 결혼해서, 그때 살아 계시던 자기 어머니에게로 이리 데려왔어요. 그는 그 여자와 여기서 여러 해를 살았고,\""], ["He lived here several years with her,”", "그녀가 이어 말했다. \"그 여자에게서 여러 아이를 얻었는데, 지금 그와 함께 있던 그 젊은 신사도 그중 하나예요."], ["continued she, “and had several children by her, of which the young gentleman that was with him now was one; but after some time, the old gentlewoman, his mother, talking to her of something relating to herself when she was in England, and of her circumstances in England, which were bad enough, the daughter-in-law began to be very much surprised and uneasy; and, in short, examining further into things, it appeared past all contradiction that the old gentlewoman was her own mother, and that consequently that son was his wife’s own brother, which struck the whole family with horror, and put them into such confusion that it had almost ruined them all.", "그런데 얼마 뒤, 그의 어머니인 그 노부인이 며느리에게, 그녀가 잉글랜드에 있었을 때의 자신에 관한 일과, 꽤나 나빴던 잉글랜드에서의 자기 처지에 관한 무언가를 이야기하다가, 며느리가 무척이나 놀라고 불안해하기 시작했대요. 한마디로, 일을 더 캐물어 보니, 그 노부인이 바로 자기 친어머니이고, 따라서 그 아들이 자기 아내의 친오라비라는 것이 의심의 여지 없이 드러났던 거예요."], ["The young woman would not live with him; the son, her brother and husband, for a time went distracted; and at last the young woman went away for England, and has never been heard of since.”", "그것이 온 집안을 공포에 빠뜨리고, 그들을 그런 혼란에 몰아넣어, 하마터면 그들 모두를 파멸시킬 뻔했지요. 그 젊은 여자는 그와 함께 살려 하지 않았고, 그녀의 오라비이자 남편인 그 아들은 한동안 미쳐 버렸으며, 끝내 그 젊은 여자는 잉글랜드로 떠나서, 그 뒤로 소식이 끊겼답니다.\""]], [["It is easy to believe that I was strangely affected with this story, but ’tis impossible to describe the nature of my disturbance.", "내가 이 이야기에 이상하리만치 동요했으리라는 것은 쉽게 믿어질 터이나, 내 동요의 본질을 묘사하기란 불가능하다."], ["I seemed astonished at the story, and asked her a thousand questions about the particulars, which I found she was thoroughly acquainted with.", "나는 그 이야기에 놀란 척하며, 그녀에게 세세한 정황에 관해 수천 가지 질문을 던졌는데, 그녀가 그것을 속속들이 알고 있음을 알게 되었다."], ["At last I began to inquire into the circumstances of the family, how the old gentlewoman, I mean my mother, died, and how she left what she had; for my mother had promised me very solemnly, that when she died she would do something for me, and leave it so, as that, if I was living, I should one way or other come at it, without its being in the power of her son, my brother and husband, to prevent it.", "마침내 나는 그 집안의 정황을 캐묻기 시작했으니, 그 노부인, 곧 내 어머니가 어떻게 돌아가셨는지, 가진 것을 어떻게 남기셨는지를 물었다. 내 어머니는 자신이 죽을 때 나를 위해 무언가를 해 두겠노라고, 그래서 내가 살아 있다면 어떤 식으로든 그것을 손에 넣을 수 있도록, 그녀의 아들, 곧 내 오라비이자 남편이 그것을 막을 힘이 없도록 그렇게 남겨 두겠노라고 내게 매우 엄숙하게 약속한 터였기 때문이다."], ["She told me she did not know exactly how it was ordered, but she had been told that my mother had left a sum of money, and had tied her plantation for the payment of it, to be made good to the daughter, if ever she could be heard of, either in England or elsewhere; and that the trust was left with this son, who was the person that we saw with his father.", "그녀는 그것이 정확히 어떻게 처리되었는지는 모르겠으나, 내 어머니가 돈을 한몫 남기고, 그 지급을 위해 자기 농장을 담보로 묶어 두어, 잉글랜드에서든 다른 어디에서든 그 딸의 소식을 듣게 되면 딸에게 틀림없이 건네지도록 했다고 들었으며, 그 신탁이 이 아들에게 맡겨졌는데, 그가 바로 우리가 그 아버지와 함께 본 그 사람이라고 했다."]], [["This was news too good for me to make light of, and, you may be sure, filled my heart with a thousand thoughts, what course I should take, how, and when, and in what manner I should make myself known, or whether I should ever make myself know or no.", "이는 가벼이 여기기에는 내게 너무도 좋은 소식이어서, 여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 내가 어떤 길을 택해야 할지, 어떻게, 언제, 무슨 방식으로 나 자신을 알릴지, 아니 도대체 나 자신을 알리기는 할지 어떨지, 수천 가지 생각으로 내 마음을 가득 채웠다."]], [["Here was a perplexity that I had not indeed skill to manage myself in, neither knew I what course to take.", "여기 실로 나 자신이 헤쳐 나갈 재간이 없는 난처함이 있었으니, 나는 어떤 길을 택해야 할지도 알지 못했다."], ["It lay heavy upon my mind night and day.", "그것은 밤낮으로 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀다."], ["I could neither sleep nor converse, so that my husband perceived it, and wondered what ailed me, strove to divert me, but it was all to no purpose.", "나는 잠을 잘 수도, 이야기를 나눌 수도 없었으니, 그래서 남편이 그것을 알아차리고 내게 무슨 일인지 의아해하며 나를 기분 전환시키려 애썼으나, 모두 헛수고였다."], ["He pressed me to tell him what it was troubled me, but I put it off, till at last, importuning me continually, I was forced to form a story, which yet had a plain truth to lay it upon too.", "그는 내게 무엇이 나를 괴롭히는지 말하라고 다그쳤지만, 나는 그것을 미루다가, 마침내 그가 끊임없이 졸라 대는 통에, 그래도 분명한 진실을 바탕에 깔 수 있는 이야기 하나를 지어낼 수밖에 없었다."], ["I told him I was troubled because I found we must shift our quarters and alter our scheme of settling, for that I found I should be known if I stayed in that part of the country; for that my mother being dead, several of my relations were come into that part where we then was, and that I must either discover myself to them, which in our present circumstances was not proper on many accounts, or remove; and which to do I knew not, and that this it was that made me so melancholy and so thoughtful.", "나는 그에게, 우리가 거처를 옮기고 정착 계획을 바꿔야 한다는 것을 알게 되어 괴롭다고 말했으니, 내가 그 지역에 머무른다면 알려지게 되리라는 것을 알았기 때문이라고 했다. 내 어머니가 돌아가신 까닭에 내 친척 여럿이 우리가 그때 있던 지역으로 들어왔으므로, 나는 그들에게 나 자신을 드러내든지—이는 여러 까닭으로 우리의 지금 처지에서 적절치 않았다—아니면 옮겨 가야 하는데, 어느 쪽을 해야 할지 모르겠으며, 바로 이것이 나를 그토록 우울하고 그토록 수심에 잠기게 한다고 했다."]], [["He joined with me in this, that it was by no means proper for me to make myself known to anybody in the circumstances in which we then were; and therefore he told me he would be willing to remove to any other part of the country, or even to any other country if I thought fit.", "그는, 우리가 그때 처한 그 정황에서 내가 누구에게든 나 자신을 알리는 것은 결코 적절치 않다는 점에서 나와 뜻을 같이했다. 그러므로 그는 내게, 내가 좋다고 여긴다면 그 나라의 다른 어느 지역으로든, 심지어 다른 어느 나라로든 기꺼이 옮겨 가겠다고 말했다."], ["But now I had another difficulty, which was, that if I removed to any other colony, I put myself out of the way of ever making a due search after those effects which my mother had left.", "그러나 이제 나는 또 다른 어려움에 부딪혔으니, 곧 내가 다른 어느 식민지로든 옮겨 가면, 내 어머니가 남긴 그 재산을 마땅히 찾아볼 길에서 영영 벗어나게 된다는 것이었다."], ["Again I could never so much as think of breaking the secret of my former marriage to my new husband; it was not a story, as I thought, that would bear telling, nor could I tell what might be the consequences of it; and it was impossible to search into the bottom of the thing without making it public all over the country, as well who I was, as what I now was also.", "또한 나는 내 옛 결혼의 비밀을 새 남편에게 털어놓을 생각은 도무지 할 수조차 없었으니, 그것은 내 생각에 입에 올릴 만한 이야기가 아니었고, 그 결과가 어찌 될지도 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 그리고 내가 지금 누구이며 지난날 누구였는지를 온 나라에 공공연히 드러내지 않고서는 그 일의 밑바닥을 캐는 것이 불가능했다."]], [["In this perplexity I continued a great while, and this made my spouse very uneasy; for he found me perplexed, and yet thought I was not open with him, and did not let him into every part of my grievance; and he would often say, he wondered what he had done that I would not trust him with whatever it was, especially if it was grievous and afflicting. The truth is, he ought to have been trusted with everything, for no man in the world could deserve better of a wife; but this was a thing I knew not how to open to him, and yet having nobody to disclose any part of it to, the burthen was too heavy for my mind; for let them say what they please of our sex not being able to keep a secret, my life is a plain conviction to me of the contrary; but be it our sex, or the man’s sex, a secret of moment should always have a confidant, a bosom friend, to whom we may communicate the joy of it, or the grief of it, be it which it will, or it will be a double weight upon the spirits, and perhaps become even insupportable in itself; and this I appeal to all human testimony for the truth of.", "이런 난처함 속에서 나는 오랫동안 지냈고, 이것이 내 남편을 무척 불안하게 했으니, 그는 내가 난처해하는 것을 알면서도, 내가 자기에게 솔직하지 않고 내 고민의 구석구석을 다 알려 주지 않는다고 여겼기 때문이다. 그래서 그는 종종, 무엇이 되었든, 특히 그것이 괴롭고 고통스러운 일이라면, 내가 자기를 믿고 털어놓지 않는데 자기가 무슨 잘못을 했는지 의아하다고 말하곤 했다. 사실 그는 모든 것을 믿고 털어놓을 만한 사람이었으니, 세상의 어떤 남자도 아내에게서 그보다 더 나은 대접을 받을 자격이 없었을 터이기 때문이다. 그러나 이는 내가 그에게 어떻게 털어놓아야 할지 모르는 일이었고, 그러면서도 그 일부나마 털어놓을 사람이 아무도 없으니, 그 짐은 내 마음에 너무도 무거웠다. 우리 여자들이 비밀을 지키지 못한다고 사람들이 무어라 떠들든, 내 삶은 그 반대의 분명한 증거이지만, 우리 여자의 일이든 남자의 일이든, 중대한 비밀에는 언제나 그것의 기쁨이든 슬픔이든, 어느 쪽이든 그것을 나눌 수 있는 속내 털어놓을 이가, 가슴을 터놓을 벗이 있어야 하니, 그러지 않으면 그것은 정신에 이중의 무게가 되고, 어쩌면 그 자체로 견딜 수 없는 것이 되어 버린다. 이것의 진실에 관해서는 모든 인간의 증언에 호소하는 바이다."]], [["And this is the cause why many times men as well as women, and men of the greatest and best qualities other ways, yet have found themselves weak in this part, and have not been able to bear the weight of a secret joy or of a secret sorrow, but have been obliged to disclose it, even for the mere giving vent to themselves, and to unbend the mind oppressed with the load and weights which attended it.", "그리고 이것이 바로, 여자뿐 아니라 남자도, 다른 면에서는 가장 위대하고 훌륭한 자질을 지닌 남자조차도, 여러 번 이 점에서 자신이 약함을 깨닫고, 비밀스러운 기쁨이나 비밀스러운 슬픔의 무게를 견디지 못해, 그저 자기 속을 토해 내고, 그에 따르는 짐과 무게에 짓눌린 마음을 풀어 놓기 위해서라도 그것을 털어놓을 수밖에 없었던 까닭이다."], ["Nor was this any token of folly or thoughtlessness at all, but a natural consequence of the thing; and such people, had they struggled longer with the oppression, would certainly have told it in their sleep, and disclosed the secret, let it have been of what fatal nature soever, without regard to the person to whom it might be exposed.", "그리고 이것은 결코 어리석음이나 경솔함의 표시가 아니라, 그 일의 자연스러운 귀결이었다. 그런 사람들은, 그 짓눌림과 더 오래 싸웠더라면, 필시 잠결에 그것을 말해 버려, 그것이 아무리 치명적인 성질의 것이라 해도, 또 그것이 드러날 상대가 누구인지도 아랑곳없이, 그 비밀을 털어놓고 말았을 것이다."], ["This necessity of nature is a thing which works sometimes with such vehemence in the minds of those who are guilty of any atrocious villainy, such as secret murder in particular, that they have been obliged to discover it, though the consequence would necessarily be their own destruction.", "이 본성의 필연은, 어떤 극악무도한 악행, 특히 은밀한 살인을 저지른 자들의 마음속에서 때로 그토록 격렬하게 작용하는 것이어서, 그 결과가 필연적으로 자신의 파멸이 되리라는 것을 알면서도 그것을 드러낼 수밖에 없게 만든다."], ["Now, though it may be true that the divine justice ought to have the glory of all those discoveries and confessions, yet ’tis as certain that Providence, which ordinarily works by the hands of nature, makes use here of the same natural causes to produce those extraordinary effects.", "그런데, 그 모든 발각과 고백의 영광이 신의 정의에 돌아가야 한다는 것이 참일지라도, 통상 본성의 손을 빌려 일하는 섭리가 여기서 바로 그 자연적 원인을 이용해 그런 비범한 결과를 빚어낸다는 것 또한 그만큼 확실하다."]], [["I could give several remarkable instances of this in my long conversation with crime and with criminals.", "나는 범죄와, 또 범죄자들과 오래 어울리며 이에 관한 주목할 만한 사례를 여럿 들 수 있다."], ["I knew one fellow that, while I was in prison in Newgate, was one of those they called then night-fliers.", "내가 뉴게이트 감옥에 있을 때, 그때 사람들이 야간 비행자라 부르던 자들 가운데 하나인 어느 사내를 알았다."], ["I know not what other word they may have understood it by since, but he was one who by connivance was admitted to go abroad every evening, when he played his pranks, and furnished those honest people they call thief-catchers with business to find out the next day, and restore for a reward what they had stolen the evening before.", "그 뒤로 그것을 무슨 다른 말로 이해해 왔는지는 모르겠으나, 그는 묵인 아래 매일 저녁 밖으로 나가도록 허락받은 자로, 그때 자기 못된 짓을 저지르고는, 사람들이 도둑잡이라 부르는 그 정직한 자들에게, 이튿날 알아내어 보상을 받고 전날 저녁에 도둑맞은 것을 되찾아 줄 일거리를 마련해 주었다. 이 사내는, 마치 깨어 있을 때 그것을 말하기로 약속이라도 한 듯이, 또 거기에 아무 해도 위험도 없는 듯이, 잠결에 자기가 한 모든 일과 자기가 밟은 모든 단계, 무엇을 어디서 훔쳤는지를 어김없이 말해 버렸다."], ["This fellow was as sure to tell in his sleep all that he had done, and every step he had taken, what he had stolen, and where, as sure as if he had engaged to tell it waking, and that there was no harm or danger in it, and therefore he was obliged, after he had been out, to lock himself up, or be locked up by some of the keepers that had him in fee, that nobody should hear him; but, on the other hand, if he had told all the particulars, and given a full account of his rambles and success, to any comrade, any brother thief, or to his employers, as I may justly call them, then all was well with him, and he slept as quietly as other people.", "그러므로 그는 밖에 다녀온 뒤에는, 아무도 그가 하는 말을 듣지 못하도록, 스스로를 가두거나, 그를 삯 받고 맡은 간수 중 누군가에게 가두어지게 할 수밖에 없었다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 만약 그가 자기 떠돌이질과 성과의 전말을 어느 동무에게든, 어느 도둑 형제에게든, 혹은 정당히 그렇게 부를 만한 그의 고용주들에게든 낱낱이 털어놓고 죄다 이야기하고 나면, 그에게는 모든 것이 무사했고, 그는 다른 사람들처럼 조용히 잤다."]], [["As the publishing this account of my life is for the sake of the just moral of very part of it, and for instruction, caution, warning, and improvement to every reader, so this will not pass, I hope, for an unnecessary digression concerning some people being obliged to disclose the greatest secrets either of their own or other people’s affairs.", "내 삶에 관한 이 기록을 출간하는 것은 그 모든 부분의 올바른 교훈을 위해서이고, 모든 독자에게 가르침과 경계와 경고와 향상을 주기 위해서이므로, 어떤 사람들이 자기 자신의 일이든 남의 일이든 가장 큰 비밀을 털어놓을 수밖에 없게 되는 것에 관한 이 이야기도, 바라건대 불필요한 여담으로 치부되지는 않을 것이다."]], [["Under the certain oppression of this weight upon my mind, I laboured in the case I have been naming; and the only relief I found for it was to let my husband into so much of it as I thought would convince him of the necessity there was for us to think of settling in some other part of the world; and the next consideration before us was, which part of the English settlements we should go to. My husband was a perfect stranger to the country, and had not yet so much as a geographical knowledge of the situation of the several places; and I, that, till I wrote this, did not know what the word geographical signified, had only a general knowledge from long conversation with people that came from or went to several places; but this I knew, that Maryland, Pennsylvania, East and West Jersey, New York, and New England lay all north of Virginia, and that they were consequently all colder climates, to which for that very reason, I had an aversion. For that as I naturally loved warm weather, so now I grew into years I had a stronger inclination to shun a cold climate. I therefore considered of going to Carolina, which is the only southern colony of the English on the continent of America, and hither I proposed to go; and the rather because I might with great ease come from thence at any time, when it might be proper to inquire after my mother’s effects, and to make myself known enough to demand them.", "내 마음을 짓누르는 이 무게의 확실한 짓눌림 아래, 나는 앞서 말한 그 일로 괴로워했다. 그리고 그에 대해 내가 찾은 유일한 위안은, 우리가 세상의 다른 어느 지역에 정착할 것을 생각해야 할 필요가 있다는 것을 그에게 납득시키기에 충분하리라 여겨지는 만큼만 그것을 남편에게 털어놓는 것이었다. 그래서 우리 앞에 놓인 다음 고려 사항은, 잉글랜드 정착지 가운데 어느 지역으로 갈 것인가였다. 내 남편은 그 나라에 완전히 낯선 자라, 여러 고장의 위치에 관해 지리적 지식조차 아직 없었다. 그리고 나로 말하자면, 이 글을 쓰기 전까지 지리적이라는 말이 무슨 뜻인지도 몰랐던 터라, 여러 곳에서 오거나 여러 곳으로 가는 사람들과 오래 이야기를 나눈 데서 얻은 일반적인 지식밖에 없었다. 그러나 이것만은 알았으니, 메릴랜드, 펜실베이니아, 동서 저지, 뉴욕, 뉴잉글랜드가 모두 버지니아 북쪽에 있고, 따라서 모두 더 추운 기후라는 것이었는데, 바로 그 까닭으로 나는 그곳들이 싫었다. 나는 본디 따뜻한 날씨를 좋아한 데다, 이제 나이가 들수록 추운 기후를 피하고 싶은 마음이 더 강해진 까닭이었다. 그래서 나는 캐롤라이나로 가는 것을 고려했으니, 그곳은 아메리카 대륙에 있는 잉글랜드인의 유일한 남쪽 식민지였고, 나는 이리로 가기를 제안했다. 더욱이 그곳에서라면 내 어머니의 재산을 알아보고, 그것을 요구할 만큼 충분히 나 자신을 알리는 것이 적절할 때, 언제든 매우 손쉽게 거기서 올 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["With this resolution I proposed to my husband our going away from where we was, and carrying all our effects with us to Carolina, where we resolved to settle; for my husband readily agreed to the first part, viz.", "이 결심으로 나는 남편에게, 우리가 있던 곳을 떠나, 우리의 모든 재산을 가지고 캐롤라이나로 가서 거기에 정착하자고 제안했다."], ["that was not at all proper to stay where we was, since I had assured him we should be known there, and the rest I effectually concealed from him.", "남편은 첫 번째 부분, 곧 우리가 있던 곳에 머무르는 것이 전혀 적절치 않다는 것에 선뜻 동의했으니, 내가 그에게 우리가 거기서 알려지게 될 것이라고 장담한 까닭이었고, 나머지는 그에게 완전히 숨겼다."]], [["But now I found a new difficulty upon me.", "그러나 이제 나는 새로운 어려움에 부딪힌 것을 깨달았다. 그 주된 일이 여전히 내 마음을 무겁게 짓눌렀으니, 내 어머니가 나를 위해 해 둔 것이라는 그 큰일을 어떤 식으로든 알아보지 않고서는 그 나라를 떠날 생각을 할 수 없었다."], ["The main affair grew heavy upon my mind still, and I could not think of going out of the country without somehow or other making inquiry into the grand affair of what my mother had done for me; nor could I with any patience bear the thought of going away, and not make myself known to my old husband (brother), or to my child, his son; only I would fain have had this done without my new husband having any knowledge of it, or they having any knowledge of him, or that I had such a thing as a husband.", "또 나는 내 옛 남편(오라비)이나, 내 자식인 그의 아들에게 나 자신을 알리지 않은 채 떠난다는 생각을 도무지 견딜 수 없었다. 다만 나는 내 새 남편이 그것을 전혀 알지 못하게, 또 그들이 그를 전혀 알지 못하게, 그리고 내게 남편이라는 것이 있다는 것조차 알지 못하게 이 일을 하고 싶었다."]], [["I cast about innumerable ways in my thoughts how this might be done.", "나는 이것이 어떻게 이루어질 수 있을지 머릿속으로 수없이 많은 길을 궁리했다."], ["I would gladly have sent my husband away to Carolina with all our goods, and have come after myself, but this was impracticable; he would never stir without me, being himself perfectly unacquainted with the country, and with the methods of settling there or anywhere else.", "나는 기꺼이 남편을 우리 모든 물건과 함께 캐롤라이나로 먼저 보내고, 나 자신은 뒤따라가고 싶었으나, 이것은 실행 불가능한 일이었다. 그는 그 나라에도, 거기서든 다른 어디서든 정착하는 방법에도 완전히 낯선 자라, 나 없이는 결코 움직이려 하지 않을 터였다."], ["Then I thought we would both go first with part of our goods, and that when we were settled I should come back to Virginia and fetch the remainder; but even then I knew he would never part with me, and be left there to go on alone.", "그다음에 나는, 우리 둘이 먼저 물건의 일부를 가지고 함께 가고, 정착하고 나면 내가 버지니아로 돌아와 나머지를 가져오면 되겠다고 생각했으나, 그때도 그가 결코 나와 떨어져, 혼자 일을 꾸려 나가도록 거기 남겨지지 않으리라는 것을 알았다. 사정은 분명했다."], ["The case was plain; he was bred a gentleman, and by consequence was not only unacquainted, but indolent, and when we did settle, would much rather go out into the woods with his gun, which they call there hunting, and which is the ordinary work of the Indians, and which they do as servants; I say, he would rather do that than attend the natural business of his plantation.", "그는 신사로 자란지라, 따라서 일에 낯설 뿐 아니라 게을렀고, 우리가 정착한다 해도, 그곳에서 사냥이라 부르는 것, 곧 본디 인디언들의 일이며 그들이 종으로서 하는 그 일을 하려고 총을 들고 숲으로 나가기를 훨씬 더 좋아할 터였다. 다시 말해, 그는 자기 농장의 마땅한 일을 돌보기보다 그러기를 더 좋아할 것이었다."]], [["These were therefore difficulties insurmountable, and such as I knew not what to do in. I had such strong impressions on my mind about discovering myself to my brother, formerly my husband, that I could not withstand them; and the rather, because it ran constantly in my thoughts, that if I did not do it while he lived, I might in vain endeavour to convince my son afterward that I was really the same person, and that I was his mother, and so might both lose the assistance and comfort of the relation, and the benefit of whatever it was my mother had left me; and yet, on the other hand, I could never think it proper to discover myself to them in the circumstances I was in, as well relating to the having a husband with me as to my being brought over by a legal transportation as a criminal; on both which accounts it was absolutely necessary to me to remove from the place where I was, and come again to him, as from another place and in another figure.", "그러므로 이것들은 극복할 수 없는 어려움이었고, 나는 어찌해야 할지 알 수 없는 그런 종류의 것이었다. 나는 한때 내 남편이었던 내 오라비에게 나 자신을 드러내는 것에 관해 마음에 그토록 강한 인상을 받아, 그것을 떨쳐 낼 수가 없었다. 더욱이, 그가 살아 있는 동안 그것을 하지 않으면, 나중에 내가 정말로 바로 그 사람이며 그의 어머니라는 것을 내 아들에게 납득시키려 해 봐야 헛수고일지 모르고, 그리하여 그 혈연의 도움과 위안도, 내 어머니가 내게 남긴 것이 무엇이든 그 혜택도 둘 다 잃을지 모른다는 생각이 끊임없이 내 머릿속을 맴돌았기 때문이다. 그러나 다른 한편으로, 나는 내가 처한 정황에서, 곧 내게 남편이 있다는 것에 관해서나, 내가 범죄자로서 합법적 유배에 의해 끌려왔다는 것에 관해서나, 그들에게 나 자신을 드러내는 것이 적절하다고는 결코 생각할 수 없었다. 이 두 가지 까닭으로, 내가 있던 곳에서 옮겨 가서, 다른 곳에서 다른 모습으로 다시 그에게 오는 것이 내게는 절대적으로 필요했다."]], [["Upon those considerations, I went on with telling my husband the absolute necessity there was of our not settling in Potomac River, at least that we should be presently made public there; whereas if we went to any other place in the world, we should come in with as much reputation as any family that came to plant; that, as it was always agreeable to the inhabitants to have families come among them to plant, who brought substance with them, either to purchase plantations or begin new ones, so we should be sure of a kind, agreeable reception, and that without any possibility of a discovery of our circumstances.", "이러한 고려에서, 나는 남편에게, 우리가 포토맥강에 정착해서는 안 될 절대적인 필요가 있다는 것, 적어도 거기서는 우리가 곧 공공연히 알려지게 되리라는 것을 계속 이야기했다. 반면에 우리가 세상의 다른 어느 곳으로 가면, 우리는 농사지으러 온 어느 집안 못지않은 평판을 가지고 들어가게 될 것이라고 했다. 살림을 가지고 와서 농장을 사들이든 새로 시작하든 농사지으러 자기들 틈에 들어오는 집안을 갖는 것은 늘 주민들에게 반가운 일이므로, 우리는 우리 정황이 발각될 가능성 없이, 친절하고 반가운 영접을 분명히 받게 되리라고 했다."]], [["I told him in general, too, that as I had several relations in the place where we were, and that I durst not now let myself be known to them, because they would soon come into a knowledge of the occasion and reason of my coming over, which would be to expose myself to the last degree, so I had reason to believe that my mother, who died here, had left me something, and perhaps considerable, which it might be very well worth my while to inquire after; but that this too could not be done without exposing us publicly, unless we went from hence; and then, wherever we settled, I might come, as it were, to visit and to see my brother and nephews, make myself known to them, claim and inquire after what was my due, be received with respect, and at the same time have justice done me with cheerfulness and good will; whereas, if I did it now, I could expect nothing but with trouble, such as exacting it by force, receiving it with curses and reluctance, and with all kinds of affronts, which he would not perhaps bear to see; that in case of being obliged to legal proofs of being really her daughter, I might be at loss, be obliged to have recourse to England, and it may be to fail at last, and so lose it, whatever it might be. With these arguments, and having thus acquainted my husband with the whole secret so far as was needful of him, we resolved to go and seek a settlement in some other colony, and at first thoughts, Carolina was the place we pitched upon.", "나는 또 그에게 대략, 우리가 있던 곳에 내 친척이 여럿 있는데, 그들이 곧 내가 건너온 계기와 까닭을 알게 되어 나를 더없이 욕보이게 될 터이므로 이제 그들에게 나 자신을 알릴 엄두를 낼 수 없으며, 또 여기서 돌아가신 내 어머니가 내게 무언가를, 어쩌면 상당한 것을 남기셨으리라 믿을 만한 까닭이 있으니, 그것을 알아볼 만한 가치가 충분할지 모른다고 말했다. 그러나 이것 또한 우리가 여기를 떠나지 않고서는 우리를 공공연히 드러내지 않고서는 할 수 없는 일이며, 그런 뒤에 우리가 어디에 정착하든, 나는 이를테면 내 오라비와 조카들을 방문하고 만나러 와서, 그들에게 나 자신을 알리고, 내 몫인 것을 청구하고 알아보며, 존중받으면서 동시에 흔쾌히, 선의로 정의를 받을 수 있을 것이라고 했다. 반면에 만약 지금 그것을 한다면, 나는 힘으로 그것을 강요하고, 저주와 마지못함과 온갖 종류의 모욕 속에서 그것을 받는 따위의 곤경 외에는 아무것도 기대할 수 없으며, 그는 어쩌면 그 꼴을 차마 보지 못할 것이라고 했다. 또 내가 정말로 그녀의 딸이라는 것을 법적으로 입증해야 하는 경우, 나는 갈피를 잡지 못하고 잉글랜드에 의지할 수밖에 없게 되며, 어쩌면 끝내 실패하여, 그것이 무엇이든 그것을 잃게 될지 모른다고 했다. 이러한 논거로, 그리고 이렇게 그에게 필요한 만큼 그 비밀의 전모를 알린 뒤, 우리는 다른 어느 식민지에서 정착지를 찾으러 가기로 결심했고, 첫 생각에 캐롤라이나가 우리가 정한 곳이었다."]], [["In order to this we began to make inquiry for vessels going to Carolina, and in a very little while got information, that on the other side the bay, as they call it, namely, in Maryland, there was a ship which came from Carolina, laden with rice and other goods, and was going back again thither, and from thence to Jamaica, with provisions.", "이를 위해 우리는 캐롤라이나로 가는 배를 수소문하기 시작했고, 머지않아, 그들이 부르는 대로 하자면 만 건너편, 곧 메릴랜드에, 쌀과 그 밖의 물건을 싣고 캐롤라이나에서 온 배가 한 척 있는데, 다시 그리로 돌아갔다가 거기서 식량을 싣고 자메이카로 가려 한다는 정보를 얻었다."], ["On this news we hired a sloop to take in our goods, and taking, as it were, a final farewell of Potomac River, we went with all our cargo over to Maryland.", "이 소식을 듣고 우리는 우리 물건을 실을 외돛배 한 척을 빌려, 이를테면 포토맥강에 마지막 작별을 고하고는, 우리의 모든 화물과 함께 메릴랜드로 건너갔다."]], [["This was a long and unpleasant voyage, and my spouse said it was worse to him than all the voyage from England, because the weather was but indifferent, the water rough, and the vessel small and inconvenient.", "이것은 길고 불쾌한 항해였고, 내 남편은 잉글랜드에서 온 항해 전체보다 자기에게는 더 고약했다고 말했으니, 날씨가 시원찮고, 물결이 거칠고, 배가 작고 불편했기 때문이다. 다음으로, 우리는 포토맥강을 백 마일이나 거슬러 올라간, 웨스트모얼랜드 카운티라 부르는 지역에 있었는데, 그 강은 버지니아에서 단연 가장 큰 강이고, 내가 듣기로는 곧장 바다로 흘러드는 것이 아니라 다른 강으로 흘러드는 강 중에서 세계에서 가장 큰 강이라 하니, 그래서 우리는 그 강에서 험한 날씨를 만나 자주 큰 위험에 처했다."], ["In the next place, we were full a hundred miles up Potomac River, in a part which they call Westmoreland County, and as that river is by far the greatest in Virginia, and I have heard say it is the greatest river in the world that falls into another river, and not directly into the sea, so we had base weather in it, and were frequently in great danger; for though we were in the middle, we could not see land on either side for many leagues together.", "우리가 한가운데 있으면서도 몇 리그씩이나 양쪽 어느 편의 뭍도 볼 수 없었기 때문이다. 그다음에는 큰 강 혹은 만인 체서피크를 건너야 했는데, 그곳은 포토맥강이 흘러드는 데로, 폭이 거의 삼십 마일이었고, 우리는 이름도 모르는 더 큰 망망대해로 들어섰으니, 그리하여 우리의 항해는 보잘것없는 형편없는 외돛배에 우리의 온 보물을 싣고 꼬박 이백 마일에 이르렀던 것이다."], ["Then we had the great river or bay of Chesapeake to cross, which is where the river Potomac falls into it, near thirty miles broad, and we entered more great vast waters whose names I know not, so that our voyage was full two hundred miles, in a poor, sorry sloop, with all our treasure, and if any accident had happened to us, we might at last have been very miserable; supposing we had lost our goods and saved our lives only, and had then been left naked and destitute, and in a wild, strange place not having one friend or acquaintance in all that part of the world.", "우리에게 어떤 사고라도 일어났더라면, 끝내 우리는 매우 비참해졌을지 모른다. 우리가 물건을 잃고 목숨만 건졌다고 치면, 그러고 나서 헐벗고 빈털터리로, 세상의 그 지역에 벗이나 아는 이 하나 없이, 거칠고 낯선 곳에 버려졌을 터이기 때문이다."], ["The very thought of it gives me some horror, even since the danger is past.", "위험이 지나간 지금조차, 그 생각만 해도 나는 얼마간 끔찍하다."]], [["Well, we came to the place in five days’ sailing; I think they call it Philip’s Point; and behold, when we came thither, the ship bound to Carolina was loaded and gone away but three days before.", "자, 우리는 닷새 항해 끝에 그곳에 다다랐으니, 그곳을 필립스포인트라 부르는 듯하다. 그런데 보라, 우리가 거기 도착했을 때, 캐롤라이나행 배는 짐을 싣고 떠나 버린 지 사흘밖에 안 된 터였다."], ["This was a disappointment; but, however, I, that was to be discouraged with nothing, told my husband that since we could not get passage to Carolina, and that the country we was in was very fertile and good, we would, if he liked of it, see if we could find out anything for our tune where we was, and that if he liked things we would settle here.", "이것은 실망스러운 일이었다. 그러나 그래도 무엇에도 좌절하지 않을 나는 남편에게, 우리가 캐롤라이나행 배편을 얻을 수 없는 데다, 우리가 있는 그 고장이 매우 비옥하고 좋으니, 그가 좋다면 우리가 있는 데서 우리 형편에 맞는 무언가를 찾아낼 수 있을지 알아보고, 그가 마음에 든다면 여기에 정착하자고 말했다."]], [["We immediately went on shore, but found no conveniences just at that place, either for our being on shore or preserving our goods on shore, but was directed by a very honest Quaker, whom we found there, to go to a place about sixty miles east; that is to say, nearer the mouth of the bay, where he said he lived, and where we should be accommodated, either to plant, or to wait for any other place to plant in that might be more convenient; and he invited us with so much kindness and simple honesty, that we agreed to go, and the Quaker himself went with us.", "우리는 곧바로 뭍에 올랐으나, 바로 그 자리에서는 우리가 뭍에 있을 편의도, 우리 물건을 뭍에 보존할 편의도 찾지 못했고, 다만 거기서 만난 매우 정직한 퀘이커 한 사람의 안내로, 동쪽으로 육십 마일쯤 떨어진, 다시 말해 만 어귀에 더 가까운 어느 곳으로 가라는 말을 들었다. 그가 거기 산다며, 거기서라면 우리가 농사짓든, 아니면 더 편리할지 모를 다른 농사 자리를 기다리든, 편의를 얻으리라고 했다. 그가 우리를 그토록 친절하고 꾸밈없는 정직함으로 초대한지라, 우리는 가기로 했고, 그 퀘이커가 직접 우리와 함께 갔다."]], [["Here we bought us two servants, viz.", "여기서 우리는 종 둘을 샀으니, 곧 리버풀 배에서 막 뭍에 내린 잉글랜드인 하녀 하나와 흑인 사내종 하나였는데, 그 나라에 정착하려는 모든 사람에게 절대적으로 필요한 것이었다. 이 정직한 퀘이커는 우리에게 매우 도움이 되었으니, 우리가 그가 제안한 곳에 다다르자, 우리 물건을 둘 편리한 창고와, 우리 자신과 우리 종들을 위한 숙소를 찾아 주었다."], ["an English woman-servant just come on shore from a ship of Liverpool, and a Negro man-servant, things absolutely necessary for all people that pretended to settle in that country.", "그리고 그 후 두 달쯤 지나, 그의 안내에 따라, 우리는 우리 농장을 일굴 요량으로 그 나라 총독에게서 큰 땅 한 뙈기를 받았으며, 그리하여 우리는 캐롤라이나로 갈 생각을 완전히 접었다. 여기서 우리는 매우 잘 영접받았고, 우리가 일을 채비하고, 땅을 충분히 개간하고, 집 지을 목재와 자재를 마련할 때까지 편리한 숙소를 얻었으니, 이 모든 것을 우리는 퀘이커의 안내에 따라 처리했다."], ["This honest Quaker was very helpful to us, and when we came to the place that he proposed to us, found us out a convenient storehouse for our goods, and lodging for ourselves and our servants; and about two months or thereabouts afterwards, by his direction, we took up a large piece of land from the governor of that country, in order to form our plantation, and so we laid the thoughts of going to Carolina wholly aside, having been very well received here, and accommodated with a convenient lodging till we could prepare things, and have land enough cleared, and timber and materials provided for building us a house, all which we managed by the direction of the Quaker; so that in one year’s time we had nearly fifty acres of land cleared, part of it enclosed, and some of it planted with tabacco, though not much; besides, we had garden ground and corn sufficient to help supply our servants with roots and herbs and bread.", "그리하여 일 년 사이에 우리는 거의 오십 에이커의 땅을 개간했고, 그 일부에 울타리를 두르고, 일부에는 담배를 심었으니, 많지는 않았다. 게다가 우리는 종들에게 뿌리채소와 푸성귀와 빵을 대주는 데 보탬이 될 만큼의 채마밭과 곡식도 두었다."]], [["And now I persuaded my husband to let me go over the bay again, and inquire after my friends.", "그리고 이제 나는 남편을 설득해 다시 만을 건너가 내 친구들의 행방을 알아보러 가게 해 달라고 했다. 그는 이제 그것에 더 기꺼이 동의했으니, 그를 흥겹게 해 줄 그의 총—거기서 사냥이라 부르며 그가 무척 즐기던 것—말고도, 그를 충분히 바쁘게 할 일거리가 그의 손에 있었기 때문이다."], ["He was the willinger to consent to it now, because he had business upon his hands sufficient to employ him, besides his gun to divert him, which they call hunting there, and which he greatly delighted in; and indeed we used to look at one another, sometimes with a great deal of pleasure, reflecting how much better that was, not than Newgate only, but than the most prosperous of our circumstances in the wicked trade that we had been both carrying on.", "그리고 실로 우리는 때때로 무척이나 흐뭇하게 서로를 바라보며, 그것이 뉴게이트보다 나을 뿐 아니라, 우리 둘 다 벌이던 그 못된 장사에서 가장 번창했던 우리 형편보다도 얼마나 더 나은지를 곱씹곤 했다. 우리 일은 매우 좋은 상태에 있었다."], ["Our affair was in a very good posture; we purchased of the proprietors of the colony as much land for £35, paid in ready money, as would make a sufficient plantation to employ between fifty and sixty servants, and which, being well improved, would be sufficient to us as long as we could either of us live; and as for children, I was past the prospect of anything of that kind.", "우리는 식민지의 소유주들에게서, 현금으로 치른 삼십오 파운드로, 종 오십에서 육십 명을 부릴 만한 충분한 농장이 될 만큼의 땅을 사들였는데, 그 땅을 잘 일구면 우리 둘 중 누구든 살아 있는 한 우리에게 충분할 터였다. 그리고 자식으로 말하자면, 나는 그런 것에 대한 가망은 이미 지난 나이였다."]], [["But out good fortune did not end here.", "그러나 우리의 행운은 여기서 끝나지 않았다."], ["I went, as I have said, over the bay, to the place where my brother, once a husband, lived; but I did not go to the same village where I was before, but went up another great river, on the east side of the river Potomac, called Rappahannock River, and by this means came on the back of his plantation, which was large, and by the help of a navigable creek, or little river, that ran into the Rappahannock, I came very near it.", "나는 앞서 말한 대로 만을 건너, 한때 남편이었던 내 오라비가 사는 곳으로 갔다. 그러나 전에 갔던 그 마을로 가지 않고, 포토맥강 동쪽에 있는, 래퍼해녹강이라 부르는 또 다른 큰 강을 거슬러 올라갔으니, 이렇게 해서 그의 농장 뒤편으로 나오게 되었는데, 그 농장은 컸고, 래퍼해녹으로 흘러드는 항행 가능한 샛강, 곧 작은 강의 도움으로, 나는 그것에 매우 가까이 다가갔다."]], [["I was now fully resolved to go up point-blank to my brother (husband), and to tell him who I was; but not knowing what temper I might find him in, or how much out of temper rather, I might make him by such a rash visit, I resolved to write a letter to him first, to let him know who I was, and that I was come not to give him any trouble upon the old relation, which I hoped was entirely forgot, but that I applied to him as a sister to a brother, desiring his assistance in the case of that provision which our mother, at her decease, had left for my support, and which I did not doubt but he would do me justice in, especially considering that I was come thus far to look after it.", "나는 이제 내 오라비(남편)에게 곧장 정면으로 올라가서, 내가 누구인지 그에게 말하기로 완전히 결심했다. 그러나 내가 그를 어떤 기분으로 마주하게 될지, 아니 그런 무모한 방문으로 내가 그를 얼마나 언짢게 만들지 알 수 없었으므로, 나는 먼저 그에게 편지를 써서, 내가 누구인지, 그리고 내가 옛 혈연을 들어—그것은 완전히 잊혔기를 바란다고—그에게 어떤 폐를 끼치러 온 것이 아니라, 우리 어머니가 돌아가실 때 내 부양을 위해 남겨 둔 그 재산의 일로 그의 도움을 청하며 누이가 오라비에게 하듯 호소하는 것이라고 알리기로 결심했으니, 더욱이 내가 그것을 알아보러 이토록 멀리까지 온 것을 생각하면, 그가 그 일에서 내게 마땅히 정당하게 대해 주리라는 것을 의심치 않았다."]], [["I said some very tender, kind things in the letter about his son, which I told him he knew to be my own child, and that as I was guilty of nothing in marrying him, any more than he was in marrying me, neither of us having then known our being at all related to one another, so I hoped he would allow me the most passionate desire of once seeing my one and only child, and of showing something of the infirmities of a mother in preserving a violent affect for him, who had never been able to retain any thought of me one way or other.", "나는 편지에 그의 아들에 관해 매우 다정하고 친절한 말을 좀 적었으니, 그에게, 그 아이가 내 친자식임을 그가 안다는 것과, 또 내가 그를 남편으로 맞은 데 죄가 없는 것이 그가 나를 아내로 맞은 데 죄가 없는 것과 마찬가지이며, 그때 우리 둘 다 서로가 도무지 혈연 사이임을 알지 못했으므로, 그러니 단 하나뿐인 내 자식을 한 번이라도 보고 싶은 더없이 애틋한 소망과, 나에 관해 이렇다 저렇다 어떤 생각도 결코 품을 수 없었던 그에게 격렬한 애정을 간직하는, 어미로서의 어떤 연약함을 보이는 것을 그가 허락해 주기를 바란다고 말했다."]], [["I did believe that, having received this letter, he would immediately give it to his son to read, I having understood his eyes being so dim, that he could not see to read it; but it fell out better than so, for as his sight was dim, so he had allowed his son to open all letters that came to his hand for him, and the old gentleman being from home, or out of the way when my messenger came, my letter came directly to my son’s hand, and he opened and read it.", "나는 그가 이 편지를 받으면, 그것을 곧바로 아들에게 읽으라고 주리라 믿었으니, 그의 눈이 너무 침침해 그것을 읽을 수 없다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 일은 그보다 더 잘 풀렸으니, 그의 시력이 침침했던 까닭에 그는 자기 앞으로 온 모든 편지를 아들이 열어 보도록 허락해 둔 터였고, 내 심부름꾼이 왔을 때 그 노신사가 집에 없거나 자리를 비운 까닭에, 내 편지가 곧장 내 아들의 손에 들어가, 그가 그것을 열어 읽었다."]], [["He called the messenger in, after some little stay, and asked him where the person was who gave him the letter. The messenger told him the place, which was about seven miles off, so he bid him stay, and ordering a horse to be got ready, and two servants, away he came to me with the messenger. Let any one judge the consternation I was in when my messenger came back, and told me the old gentleman was not at home, but his son was come along with him, and was just coming up to me. I was perfectly confounded, for I knew not whether it was peace or war, nor could I tell how to behave; however, I had but a very few moments to think, for my son was at the heels of the messenger, and coming up into my lodgings, asked the fellow at the door something. I suppose it was, for I did not hear it so as to understand it, which was the gentlewoman that sent him; for the messenger said, “There she is, sir”; at which he comes directly up to me, kisses me, took me in his arms, and embraced me with so much passion that he could not speak, but I could feel his breast heave and throb like a child, that cries, but sobs, and cannot cry it out.", "그는 잠시 머문 뒤 심부름꾼을 불러들여, 그에게 그 편지를 준 사람이 어디 있는지 물었다. 심부름꾼이 그 장소를 일러 주었는데, 칠 마일쯤 떨어진 곳이었다. 그래서 그는 그에게 기다리라 하고, 말 한 필과 종 둘을 채비시키더니, 심부름꾼과 함께 내게로 왔다. 내 심부름꾼이 돌아와, 노신사는 집에 없으나 그 아들이 함께 와서 막 내게로 올라오는 중이라고 말했을 때, 내가 어떤 경악에 빠졌을지 누구든 헤아려 보시라. 나는 그것이 화평인지 전쟁인지 알 수 없어 완전히 어쩔 줄을 몰랐고, 어떻게 처신해야 할지도 알 수 없었다. 그러나 나는 생각할 겨를이 아주 잠깐밖에 없었으니, 내 아들이 심부름꾼의 바로 뒤를 따라와, 내 숙소로 올라오면서 문간에 있던 자에게 무언가를 물었기 때문이다. 짐작건대, 그것은 누가 그를 보냈는가 하는 그 부인이 누구냐는 것이었으리라. 내가 알아들을 만큼 똑똑히 듣지는 못했으나 말이다. 심부름꾼이 \"저기 계십니다, 나리\" 하고 말했고, 그러자 그는 곧장 내게로 올라와, 내게 입을 맞추고, 나를 두 팔로 끌어안았으며, 어찌나 격정에 차서 나를 부둥켜안았는지 말을 잇지 못했다. 다만 나는 그의 가슴이 마치, 울다가 흐느끼되 끝내 울음을 터뜨리지 못하는 아이처럼 들썩이며 두근거리는 것을 느낄 수 있었다."]], [["I can neither express nor describe the joy that touched my very soul when I found, for it was easy to discover that part, that he came not as a stranger, but as a son to a mother, and indeed as a son who had never before known what a mother of his own was; in short, we cried over one another a considerable while, when at last he broke out first.", "내가 알아차렸을 때 내 영혼을 사무치게 건드린 그 기쁨을 나는 표현할 수도 묘사할 수도 없으니, 그 점만은 쉽게 알아차릴 수 있었거니와, 그가 낯선 자로서가 아니라 어머니에게 오는 아들로서, 실로 자기 친어머니가 어떤 분인지 전에는 결코 알지 못했던 아들로서 왔다는 것을 알았기 때문이다. 한마디로, 우리는 한참 동안 서로 부둥켜안고 울었는데, 마침내 그가 먼저 입을 열었다."], ["“My dear mother,”", "\"사랑하는 어머니,\""], ["says he, “are you still alive?", "그가 말했다. \"아직 살아 계셨군요?"], ["I never expected to have seen your face.”", "저는 어머니 얼굴을 다시 뵈리라고는 한 번도 기대하지 못했어요.\""], ["As for me, I could say nothing a great while.", "나로 말하자면, 한참 동안 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["After we had both recovered ourselves a little, and were able to talk, he told me how things stood. As to what I had written to his father, he told me he had not showed my letter to his father, or told him anything about it; that what his grandmother left me was in his hands, and that he would do me justice to my full satisfaction; that as to his father, he was old and infirm both in body and mind; that he was very fretful and passionate, almost blind, and capable of nothing; and he questioned whether he would know how to act in an affair which was of so nice a nature as this; and that therefore he had come himself, as well to satisfy himself in seeing me, which he could not restrain himself from, as also to put it into my power to make a judgment, after I had seen how things were, whether I would discover myself to his father or no.", "우리 둘이 조금 마음을 추스르고 이야기할 수 있게 된 뒤, 그는 내게 일이 어떻게 되어 있는지 말해 주었다. 내가 그의 아버지에게 써 보낸 것에 관해서는, 자기가 내 편지를 아버지에게 보이지도, 그에 관해 아버지에게 아무 말도 하지 않았다고 했다. 그의 할머니가 내게 남긴 것은 자기 손에 있으며, 내가 완전히 만족하도록 내게 정당하게 해 주겠다고 했다. 그의 아버지로 말하자면, 늙고 몸과 마음이 다 쇠약하며, 매우 짜증을 잘 내고 성을 잘 내며, 거의 눈이 멀어, 아무것도 할 수 없는 형편이라 했다. 그리고 그는 아버지가 이토록 미묘한 성질의 일에서 어떻게 처신해야 할지 알기나 할지 의심스럽다고 했다. 그러므로 그는, 도무지 참을 수 없어 나를 직접 만나 흡족해하려는 까닭에서도, 또 일이 어떻게 되어 있는지 내가 본 뒤에 아버지에게 나 자신을 드러낼지 말지를 내가 판단할 수 있게 해 주려는 까닭에서도, 자기가 직접 왔다고 했다."]], [["This was really so prudently and wisely managed, that I found my son was a man of sense, and needed no direction from me. I told him I did not wonder that his father was as he had described him, for that his head was a little touched before I went away; and principally his disturbance was because I could not be persuaded to conceal our relation and to live with him as my husband, after I knew that he was my brother; that as he knew better than I what his father’s present condition was, I should readily join with him in such measure as he would direct; that I was indifferent as to seeing his father, since I had seen him first, and he could not have told me better news than to tell me that what his grandmother had left me was entrusted in his hands, who, I doubted not, now he knew who I was, would, as he said, do me justice. I inquired then how long my mother had been dead, and where she died, and told so many particulars of the family, that I left him no room to doubt the truth of my being really and truly his mother.", "이것은 실로 너무도 신중하고 슬기롭게 처리되었던지라, 나는 내 아들이 분별 있는 사람이며 내게서 어떤 지시도 받을 필요가 없음을 알았다. 나는 그에게, 그의 아버지가 그가 묘사한 그대로인 것이 놀랍지 않으니, 내가 떠나기 전에도 그의 머리가 조금 이상했었다고 말했다. 그리고 그의 동요의 주된 까닭은, 내가 그를 내 오라비임을 안 뒤에도, 우리 혈연을 감추고 그를 내 남편으로 삼아 함께 살자는 설득에 내가 응할 수 없었기 때문이라고 했다. 그가 그의 아버지의 지금 형편을 나보다 더 잘 아니, 나는 그가 지시하는 대로의 조치에 선뜻 따르겠다고 했다. 또 내가 그를 먼저 보았으니 그의 아버지를 보는 것에는 무관심하며, 그의 할머니가 내게 남긴 것이 그의 손에 맡겨져 있다는 말보다 그가 내게 더 좋은 소식을 들려줄 수는 없었을 것이라고 했다. 이제 그가 내가 누구인지 알았으니, 그가 말한 대로 내게 정당하게 해 주리라는 것을 나는 의심치 않았다. 그러고 나서 나는 내 어머니가 돌아가신 지 얼마나 되었는지, 어디서 돌아가셨는지를 물었고, 그 집안에 관해 너무도 많은 세세한 사정을 이야기한지라, 그에게 내가 정말로 참되이 그의 어머니라는 진실을 의심할 여지를 조금도 남기지 않았다."]], [["My son then inquired where I was, and how I had disposed myself.", "그러자 내 아들은 내가 어디 있는지, 어떻게 지내 왔는지 물었다."], ["I told him I was on the Maryland side of the bay, at the plantation of a particular friend who came from England in the same ship with me; that as for that side of the bay where he was, I had no habitation.", "나는 그에게, 나는 만의 메릴랜드 쪽에, 나와 같은 배로 잉글랜드에서 온 어느 각별한 친구의 농장에 있으며, 그가 있는 만의 그쪽으로 말하자면 내게는 거처가 없다고 말했다."], ["He told me I should go home with him, and live with him, if I pleased, as long as I lived; that as to his father, he knew nobody, and would never so much as guess at me.", "그는 내게, 내가 원한다면 자기와 함께 집으로 가서 평생 함께 살자고, 그의 아버지로 말하자면 아무도 알아보지 못하니 나를 짐작조차 하지 못할 것이라고 했다."], ["I considered of that a little, and told him, that though it was really no concern to me to live at a distance from him, yet I could not say it would be the most comfortable thing in the world to me to live in the house with him, and to have that unhappy object always before me, which had been such a blow to my peace before; that though I should be glad to have his company (my son), or to be as near him as possible while I stayed, yet I could not think of being in the house where I should be also under constant restraint for fear of betraying myself in my discourse, nor should I be able to refrain some expressions in my conversing with him as my son, that might discover the whole affair, which would by no means be convenient.", "나는 그것을 조금 헤아려 보고 그에게 말하기를, 그와 떨어져 사는 것이 내게 실로 아무 문제가 아니긴 하나, 그와 한집에 살면서, 전에 내 평안에 그토록 큰 타격이었던 그 불행한 존재를 늘 눈앞에 두는 것이 내게 세상에서 가장 마음 편한 일이라고는 할 수 없겠다고 했다. 비록 그(내 아들)와 함께 있거나, 내가 머무는 동안 가능한 한 그와 가까이 있는 것은 기쁘겠으나, 내가 말로 나 자신을 들킬까 두려워 끊임없이 조심해야 하는 그 집에 있을 생각은 할 수 없으며, 또 그를 내 아들로 여기고 이야기하다가 그 일의 전모를 드러낼 만한 어떤 표현을 삼가지 못할 터인데, 그것은 결코 적절치 않은 일이라고 했다."]], [["He acknowledged that I was right in all this.", "그는 이 모든 점에서 내가 옳다고 인정했다."], ["“But then, dear mother,”", "\"하지만 그렇다면, 사랑하는 어머니,\""], ["says he, “you shall be as near me as you can.”", "그가 말했다. \"되도록 제 곁에 가까이 계세요.\""], ["So he took me with him on horseback to a plantation next to his own, and where I was as well entertained as I could have been in his own.", "그래서 그는 나를 말에 태워 자기 농장 바로 옆 농장으로 데려갔는데, 거기서 나는 그의 집에서 받았을 만큼 잘 대접받았다."], ["Having left me there he went away home, telling me we would talk of the main business the next day; and having first called me his aunt, and given a charge to the people, who it seems were his tenants, to treat me with all possible respect.", "나를 거기 남겨 두고 그는 집으로 돌아가면서, 우리가 그 주된 일은 이튿날 이야기하자고 했고, 먼저 나를 자기 고모라 부르고, 그곳 사람들—이들은 그의 소작인인 듯했다—에게 나를 더없이 정중하게 대접하라고 분부했다."], ["About two hours after he was gone, he sent me a maid-servant and a Negro boy to wait on me, and provisions ready dressed for my supper; and thus I was as if I had been in a new world, and began secretly now to wish that I had not brought my Lancashire husband from England at all.", "그가 떠난 지 두 시간쯤 뒤에, 그는 내 시중을 들 하녀 하나와 흑인 사내아이 하나, 그리고 내 저녁거리로 이미 요리해 둔 음식을 보내 주었다. 그리하여 나는 마치 새로운 세계에라도 있는 듯했고, 이제 내심 내가 잉글랜드에서 내 랭커셔 남편을 데려오지 않았더라면 하고 바라기 시작했다."]], [["However, that wish was not hearty neither, for I loved my Lancashire husband entirely, as indeed I had ever done from the beginning; and he merited from me as much as it was possible for a man to do; but that by the way.", "그렇지만 그 바람도 진심은 아니었으니, 나는 내 랭커셔 남편을 온전히 사랑했고, 실로 처음부터 줄곧 그래 왔으며, 그는 한 남자가 할 수 있는 만큼 내게서 사랑받을 자격이 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 이 이야기는 그저 곁들여 말해 둔다."]], [["The next morning my son came to visit me again almost as soon as I was up.", "이튿날 아침 내 아들은 내가 일어나자마자 거의 곧바로 다시 나를 찾아왔다."], ["After a little discourse, he first of all pulled out a deerskin bag, and gave it me, with five-and-fifty Spanish pistoles in it, and told me that was to supply my expenses from England, for though it was not his business to inquire, yet he ought to think I did not bring a great deal of money out with me, it not being usual to bring much money into that country.", "조금 이야기를 나눈 뒤, 그는 무엇보다 먼저 사슴 가죽 주머니 하나를 꺼내 그 안에 스페인 피스톨 쉰다섯 닢을 넣어 내게 주면서, 그것은 내가 잉글랜드에서 오는 데 든 비용을 대 주려는 것이라고 했다. 그것을 캐묻는 것이 자기 할 일은 아니지만, 그래도 내가 돈을 많이 가지고 나오지는 않았으리라 여겨야 마땅하니, 그 나라로 돈을 많이 가지고 들어오는 일이 흔치 않기 때문이라고 했다."], ["Then he pulled out his grandmother’s will, and read it over to me, whereby it appeared that she had left a small plantation, as he called it, on York River, that is, where my mother lived, to me, with the stock of servants and cattle upon it, and given it in trust to this son of mine for my use, whenever he should hear of my being alive, and to my heirs, if I had any children, and in default of heirs, to whomsoever I should by will dispose of it; but gave the income of it, till I should be heard of, or found, to my said son; and if I should not be living, then it was to him, and his heirs.", "그런 다음 그는 자기 할머니의 유언장을 꺼내 내게 죽 읽어 주었는데, 그에 따르면 그녀는 요크강에, 곧 내 어머니가 살던 곳에 있는, 그가 부르는 대로 작은 농장 하나를, 그 위의 종들과 가축들의 밑천과 함께 내게 남겼고, 그것을 내 아들인 이 사람에게 신탁으로 맡겨, 그가 내가 살아 있다는 것을 듣게 될 때마다 내가 쓰도록 하고, 내게 자식이 있다면 내 상속인에게, 상속인이 없으면 내가 유언으로 처분하는 누구에게든 주도록 했음이 드러났다. 다만 내 소식을 듣거나 나를 찾아낼 때까지의 그 수입은 앞서 말한 내 아들에게 주고, 만약 내가 살아 있지 않다면 그것은 그와 그의 상속인에게 가도록 했다."]], [["This plantation, though remote from him, he said he did not let out, but managed it by a head-clerk (steward), as he did another that was his father’s, that lay hard by it, and went over himself three or four times a year to look after it.", "이 농장은 그에게서 멀리 떨어져 있긴 하나, 그는 그것을 세놓지 않고 수석 사무원(집사)을 두어 관리하는데, 그 바로 곁에 있는 그의 아버지 소유의 또 다른 농장도 그렇게 관리한다고 했으며, 자기가 직접 일 년에 서너 번 건너가 그것을 돌본다고 했다."], ["I asked him what he thought the plantation might be worth.", "나는 그에게 그 농장이 얼마나 가치가 있겠는지 물었다."], ["He said, if I would let it out, he would give me about £60 a year for it; but if I would live on it, then it would be worth much more, and, he believed, would bring me in about £150 a year.", "그는, 내가 그것을 세놓는다면 자기가 그것에 대해 일 년에 육십 파운드쯤 주겠으나, 내가 거기서 산다면 훨씬 더 가치가 있어, 자기 생각에는 내게 일 년에 백오십 파운드쯤을 안겨 줄 것이라고 했다."], ["But seeing I was likely either to settle on the other side of the bay, or might perhaps have a mind to go back to England again, if I would let him be my steward he would manage it for me, as he had done for himself, and that he believed he should be able to send me as much tobacco to England from it as would yield me about £100 a year, sometimes more.", "그러나 내가 만의 다른 쪽에 정착할 듯하거나, 어쩌면 다시 잉글랜드로 돌아갈 마음이 있을지 모르니, 내가 자기를 내 집사로 삼아 준다면, 자기 것을 관리하듯 그것을 나를 위해 관리하겠으며, 내게 보내 줄 수 있으리라 믿는다고 했으니\n그 농장에서 잉글랜드로 담배를 실어 보내면 내게 한 해 100파운드쯤, 때로는 그보다 더 많은 수입을 안겨 주리라는 것이었다."]], [["This was all strange news to me, and things I had not been used to; and really my heart began to look up more seriously than I think it ever did before, and to look with great thankfulness to the hand of Providence, which had done such wonders for me, who had been myself the greatest wonder of wickedness perhaps that had been suffered to live in the world.", "이것은 내게 죄다 낯선 소식이요, 내가 익숙하지 못한 일들이었다. 그리하여 실로 내 마음은, 내 평생 어느 때보다도 진지하게 위를 우러르기 시작하였고, 나 같은 자에게—어쩌면 세상에 살도록 용납된 자들 가운데 가장 큰 사악함의 경이였을 나에게—그토록 놀라운 일들을 베풀어 주신 섭리의 손길을 깊은 감사로 우러러보기 시작하였다."], ["And I must again observe, that not on this occasion only, but even on all other occasions of thankfulness, my past wicked and abominable life never looked so monstrous to me, and I never so completely abhorred it, and reproached myself with it, as when I had a sense upon me of Providence doing good to me, while I had been making those vile returns on my part.", "그리고 다시 한번 짚어 두지 않을 수 없으니, 이 경우만이 아니라 감사할 만한 다른 모든 경우에도, 지난날의 사악하고 가증스러운 내 삶이 그토록 흉측하게 보인 적이 없었으며, 내가 그것을 그토록 온전히 혐오하고 그 일로 스스로를 책망한 적이 없었으니, 그것은 바로 내가 섭리께서 내게 선을 베풀고 계신다는 것을 절감하면서도, 내 쪽에서는 그토록 비열하게 갚아 온 것을 깨달았을 때였다."]], [["But I leave the reader to improve these thoughts, as no doubt they will see cause, and I go on to the fact.", "그러나 이런 생각들은 독자 여러분이 알아서 좋은 쓸모로 살려 주시리라 믿고 그 일은 맡겨 두며, 나는 본 이야기로 넘어가겠다."], ["My son’s tender carriage and kind offers fetched tears from me, almost all the while he talked with me.", "아들의 다정한 태도와 친절한 제안에, 그가 나와 이야기를 나누는 거의 내내 내게서 눈물이 솟았다."], ["Indeed, I could scarce discourse with him but in the intervals of my passion; however, at length I began, and expressing myself with wonder at my being so happy to have the trust of what I had left, put into the hands of my own child, I told him, that as to the inheritance of it, I had no child but him in the world, and was now past having any if I should marry, and therefore would desire him to get a writing drawn, which I was ready to execute, by which I would, after me, give it wholly to him and to his heirs.", "사실 나는 북받치는 감정이 잦아드는 사이사이가 아니고서는 그와 이야기를 나눌 수조차 없었으나, 그래도 마침내 입을 떼었으니, 내가 남겨 두고 간 것의 관리가 다름 아닌 내 친자식의 손에 맡겨지는 이런 복을 누리게 된 데 대해 놀라움을 표하며, 그 상속에 관해서는, 세상에 그 말고는 자식이 없거니와 이제 설령 내가 결혼한다 해도 더 자식을 둘 나이가 지났으니, 내가 죽은 뒤 그것을 온전히 그와 그의 상속인들에게 넘긴다는 증서를 한 장 작성해 주기를 바라며, 그러면 내가 기꺼이 서명하겠노라고 일렀다."], ["And in the meantime, smiling, I asked him what made him continue a bachelor so long.", "그리고 그러는 사이에, 나는 미소를 지으며 무엇 때문에 이토록 오래 홀아비로 지냈느냐고 물었다."], ["His answer was kind and ready, that Virginia did not yield any great plenty of wives, and that since I talked of going back to England, I should send him a wife from London.", "그의 대답은 다정하고 거침이 없었으니, 버지니아에서는 아내가 그리 흔하게 나지 않거니와, 내가 잉글랜드로 돌아간다는 말을 하니 런던에서 아내 하나를 보내 달라는 것이었다."]], [["This was the substance of our first day’s conversation, the pleasantest day that ever passed over my head in my life, and which gave me the truest satisfaction.", "이것이 우리가 나눈 첫날 대화의 요지였으니, 내 평생 머리 위로 지나간 가장 즐거운 날이요, 내게 가장 참된 만족을 안겨 준 날이었다."], ["He came every day after this, and spent a great part of his time with me, and carried me about to several of his friends’ houses, where I was entertained with great respect.", "그는 이후로 날마다 찾아와 많은 시간을 나와 함께 보냈고, 나를 여러 벗의 집으로 데리고 다녔는데, 그곳에서 나는 큰 존경을 받으며 대접받았다."], ["Also I dined several times at his own house, when he took care always to see his half-dead father so out of the way that I never saw him, or he me.", "또한 나는 여러 차례 그의 집에서 식사를 하였는데, 그럴 때면 그는 반쯤 죽은 자기 아버지를 늘 멀찍이 치워 두어, 나는 한 번도 그를 보지 못하였고 그 또한 나를 보지 못하였다."], ["I made him one present, and it was all I had of value, and that was one of the gold watches, of which I mentioned above, that I had two in my chest, and this I happened to have with me, and I gave it him at his third visit.", "나는 그에게 선물 하나를 주었으니, 그것이 내게 있는 값나가는 전부였다. 그것은 앞서 내 궤짝에 두 개가 있다고 언급한 그 금시계 가운데 하나로, 마침 몸에 지니고 있던 터라 세 번째 방문 때 그에게 주었다."], ["I told him I had nothing of any value to bestow but that, and I desired he would now and then kiss it for my sake.", "나는 그것 말고는 줄 만한 값진 것이 아무것도 없으니, 나를 생각하여 이따금 거기에 입맞춤이나 해 달라고 일렀다."], ["I did not indeed tell him that I had stole it from a gentlewoman’s side, at a meeting-house in London.", "다만 그것을 런던의 어느 예배당에서 어느 귀부인의 옆구리에서 훔쳤다는 말은 정말이지 그에게 하지 않았다."], ["That’s by the way.", "그건 그렇고."]], [["He stood a little while hesitating, as if doubtful whether to take it or no; but I pressed it on him, and made him accept it, and it was not much less worth than his leather pouch full of Spanish gold; no, though it were to be reckoned as if at London, whereas it was worth twice as much there, where I gave it him.", "그는 받아야 할지 말아야 할지 망설이듯 잠시 머뭇거리며 서 있었으나, 나는 그것을 그의 손에 떠안기듯 권하여 기어이 받게 하였으니, 그것은 스페인 금화가 가득 든 그의 가죽 주머니보다 결코 그리 값이 못하지 않았다. 아니, 설령 런던 기준으로 따진다 해도 그러하였거니와, 내가 그것을 그에게 준 이곳에서는 그 곱절의 값어치가 있었다."], ["At length he took it, kissed it, told me the watch should be a debt upon him that he would be paying as long as I lived.", "마침내 그는 그것을 받아 입을 맞추고는, 이 시계가 내가 살아 있는 한 갚아 나갈 빚이 될 것이라고 말했다."]], [["A few days after he brought the writings of gift, and the scrivener with them, and I signed them very freely, and delivered them to him with a hundred kisses; for sure nothing ever passed between a mother and a tender, dutiful child with more affection.", "며칠 뒤 그는 증여 증서를 가져왔고, 대서인도 함께 데려왔으므로, 나는 아주 흔쾌히 서명하여 백 번의 입맞춤과 더불어 그에게 건넸으니, 어머니와 다정하고 효심 깊은 자식 사이에 이보다 더한 애정으로 오간 일은 일찍이 없었으리라."], ["The next day he brings me an obligation under his hand and seal, whereby he engaged himself to manage and improve the plantation for my account, and with his utmost skill, and to remit the produce to my order wherever I should be; and withal, to be obliged himself to make up the produce £100 a year to me.", "이튿날 그는 자기 손으로 쓰고 인장을 찍은 증서 한 장을 가져왔는데, 그로써 그는 내 몫으로 농장을 자신의 온 솜씨를 다하여 경영하고 가꾸며, 내가 어디에 있든 내 지시에 따라 그 수익을 송금하고, 아울러 그 수익을 한 해 100파운드로 맞춰 주기로 스스로 의무를 졌다."], ["When he had done so, he told me that as I came to demand it before the crop was off, I had a right to produce of the current year, and so he paid me £100 in Spanish pieces of eight, and desired me to give him a receipt for it as in full for that year, ending at Christmas following; this being about the latter end of August.", "그렇게 해 놓고는, 내가 수확이 끝나기 전에 청구하러 왔으니 올해 수익에 대한 권리가 내게 있다고 말하며, 스페인 8레알 은화로 100파운드를 치러 주고는, 그해 분, 곧 다가오는 크리스마스로 끝나는 한 해 분을 완납받았다는 영수증을 자기에게 써 달라고 하였다. 때는 8월 하순 무렵이었다."]], [["I stayed here about five weeks, and indeed had much ado to get away then.", "나는 이곳에서 다섯 주가량 머물렀는데, 실은 떠나기가 여간 힘들지 않았다."], ["Nay, he would have come over the bay with me, but I would by no means allow him to it.", "아니, 그는 나와 함께 만을 건너오려고까지 하였으나, 나는 결코 그것을 허락하지 않았다."], ["However, he would send me over in a sloop of his own, which was built like a yacht, and served him as well for pleasure as business.", "그래도 그는 자기 소유의 외돛배 한 척에 나를 태워 보내 주었으니, 그것은 요트처럼 지어져 그에게 사업에도 유람에도 두루 쓸모가 있었다."], ["This I accepted of, and so, after the utmost expressions both of duty and affection, he let me come away, and I arrived safe in two days at my friend’s the Quaker’s.", "이것은 내가 받아들였고, 그리하여 더없는 효심과 애정의 표현을 다 나눈 뒤, 그는 나를 떠나보냈으며, 나는 이틀 만에 무사히 내 벗인 퀘이커 여인의 집에 다다랐다."]], [["I brought over with me for the use of our plantation, three horses, with harness and saddles, some hogs, two cows, and a thousand other things, the gift of the kindest and tenderest child that ever woman had.", "나는 우리 농장에 쓰려고 마구와 안장을 갖춘 말 세 필, 돼지 몇 마리, 암소 두 마리, 그 밖에도 천 가지나 되는 물건들을 가져왔으니, 모두가 일찍이 어떤 여인도 두지 못했을 만큼 더없이 친절하고 다정한 자식이 준 선물이었다. 나는 남편에게 이번 항해의 모든 세세한 일을 들려주었으나, 다만 아들을 사촌이라 일컬었다."], ["I related to my husband all the particulars of this voyage, except that I called my son my cousin; and first I told him that I had lost my watch, which he seemed to take as a misfortune; but then I told him how kind my cousin had been, that my mother had left me such a plantation, and that he had preserved it for me, in hopes some time or other he should hear from me; then I told him that I had left it to his management, that he would render me a faithful account of its produce; and then I pulled him out the £100 in silver, as the first year’s produce; and then pulling out the deerskin purse with the pistoles, “And here, my dear,”", "먼저 시계를 잃어버렸다고 말하니 그는 그것을 불운으로 여기는 듯하였으나, 이어 내 사촌이 얼마나 친절했는지, 어머니가 내게 그런 농장을 남겨 주었고 그가 언젠가 내 소식을 듣게 되기를 바라며 그것을 나를 위해 간수해 왔다는 것을 말해 주었다. 그러고 나서 나는 그것을 그의 경영에 맡겨 두었으며, 그가 그 수익에 대해 충실히 보고해 줄 것이라고 말한 뒤, 첫해 수익으로 100파운드 은화를 꺼내 보였다."], ["says I, “is the gold watch.”", "이어 피스톨 금화가 든 사슴가죽 지갑을 꺼내며 \"그리고 여보, 여기 그 금시계가 있어요.\""], ["My husband—so is Heaven’s goodness sure to work the same effects in all sensible minds where mercies touch the heart—lifted up both hands, and with an ecstacy of joy, “What is God a-doing,”", "하고 말했다. 내 남편은—자비가 마음을 울리는 곳에서는 분별 있는 모든 마음에 한결같은 결과를 빚어내는 것이 하늘의 선하심이 틀림없으니—두 손을 들어 올리며 환희에 겨워 \"나처럼 이렇게 은혜를 모르는 개 같은 놈에게 하느님께서 무엇을 해 주고 계신단 말인가!\""], ["says he, “for such an ungrateful dog as I am!”", "하고 외쳤다."], ["Then I let him know what I had brought over in the sloop, besides all this; I mean the horses, hogs, and cows, and other stores for our plantation; all which added to his surprise, and filled his heart with thankfulness; and from this time forward I believe he was as sincere a penitent, and as thoroughly a reformed man, as ever God’s goodness brought back from a profligate, a highwayman, and a robber.", "그러고 나서 나는 이 모든 것 말고도 그 외돛배에 실어 온 것, 곧 말과 돼지와 암소, 그리고 우리 농장에 쓸 다른 물품들을 알려 주었으니, 이 모두가 그의 놀라움을 한층 더하고 그의 마음을 감사로 가득 채웠다. 그리고 이때부터 줄곧 그는, 방탕아요 노상강도요 강도였던 자를 하느님의 선하심이 돌이켜 데려온 그 누구 못지않게 진실한 참회자요, 더없이 철저하게 거듭난 사람이 되었다고 나는 믿는다."], ["I could fill a larger history than this with the evidence of this truth, and but that I doubt that part of the story will not be equally diverting as the wicked part, I have had thoughts of making a volume of it by itself.", "나는 이 진실의 증거만으로도 이보다 더 큰 이야기책을 채울 수 있으련만, 그 부분의 이야기가 사악한 부분만큼 흥미롭지는 못하리라는 의구심이 들어서 그렇지, 그것만 따로 한 권의 책으로 엮을까 하는 생각을 해 본 적도 있다."]], [["As for myself, as this is to be my own story, not my husband’s, I return to that part which related to myself.", "나 자신으로 말하자면, 이것은 내 남편의 이야기가 아니라 내 자신의 이야기여야 하므로, 나는 나 자신과 관련된 부분으로 돌아가겠다."], ["We went on with our plantation, and managed it with the help and diversion of such friends as we got there by our obliging behaviour, and especially the honest Quaker, who proved a faithful, generous, and steady friend to us; and we had very good success, for having a flourishing stock to begin with, as I have said, and this being now increased by the addition of £150 sterling in money, we enlarged our number of servants, built us a very good house, and cured every year a great deal of land.", "우리는 우리 농장을 계속 꾸려 나갔으니, 우리의 싹싹한 처신으로 그곳에서 사귄 벗들의 도움과 어울림으로, 특히 우리에게 충실하고 너그럽고 한결같은 벗임을 입증한 그 정직한 퀘이커 여인의 도움으로 그것을 경영하였다. 그리고 우리는 매우 큰 성공을 거두었으니, 앞서 말했듯 번창하는 밑천으로 시작한 데다, 이제 거기에 은화 150파운드가 더해져 불어났으므로, 우리는 하인의 수를 늘리고, 매우 좋은 집을 한 채 지었으며, 해마다 많은 땅을 일구어 놓았다."], ["The second year I wrote to my old governess, giving her part with us of the joy of our success, and order her how to lay out the money I had left with her, which was £250 as above, and to send it to us in goods, which she performed with her usual kindness and fidelity, and this arrived safe to us.", "둘째 해에 나는 옛 후견인에게 편지를 써, 우리의 성공이 주는 기쁨을 그녀와 나누고, 위에서 말한 250파운드, 곧 내가 그녀에게 맡겨 둔 돈을 어떻게 쓸지 일러 주며, 그것을 물품으로 사서 우리에게 보내 달라고 하였더니, 그녀는 늘 그렇듯 친절하고 충실하게 그 일을 해냈고, 그것은 무사히 우리에게 도착하였다."]], [["Here we had a supply of all sorts of clothes, as well for my husband as for myself; and I took especial care to buy for him all those things that I knew he delighted to have; as two good long wigs, two silver-hilted swords, three or four fine fowling-pieces, a fine saddle with holsters and pistols very handsome, with a scarlet cloak; and, in a word, everything I could think of to oblige him, and to make him appear, as he really was, a very fine gentleman.", "이로써 우리는 남편과 나 둘 다를 위한 온갖 종류의 옷을 갖추게 되었다. 그리고 나는 그가 갖기를 좋아한다는 것을 내가 아는 온갖 물건을 그를 위해 사는 데 각별히 마음을 썼으니, 곧 좋은 긴 가발 두 개, 은 손잡이가 달린 칼 두 자루, 멋진 새 사냥총 서너 자루, 권총집과 권총이 딸린 아주 잘빠진 안장 하나에 진홍색 망토 하나, 한마디로 그를 흡족하게 하고, 또 그가 실제로 그러하듯 아주 훌륭한 신사로 보이게 할 만한 것이라면 내가 생각해 낼 수 있는 온갖 것이었다."], ["I ordered a good quantity of such household stuff as we yet wanted, with linen of all sorts for us both.", "나는 우리에게 아직 부족한 살림살이도 넉넉히 주문하였고, 둘이 함께 쓸 온갖 종류의 아마포도 들였다. 나 자신으로 말하자면, 이미 매우 잘 갖추고 있던 터라 옷이나 아마포가 거의 필요치 않았다."], ["As for myself, I wanted very little of clothes or linen, being very well furnished before.", "내 나머지 짐은 온갖 종류의 철물, 말에 쓸 마구, 연장, 하인들 옷, 그리고 모직물, 직물, 서지, 양말, 신발, 모자 따위, 곧 하인들이 입는 그런 것들로 이루어졌으며, 하인들 옷을 지을 옷감도 통째로 들였으니, 모두가 그 퀘이커 여인의 지시에 따른 것이었다. 그리고 이 모든 짐이 무사히, 온전한 상태로 도착하였는데, 옛 후견인이 나를 위해 골라 준 건장한 처녀 하인 셋도 함께 왔으니, 그곳에도, 또 우리가 그들에게 시킬 일에도 어지간히 알맞았다."], ["The rest of my cargo consisted in iron-work of all sorts, harness for horses, tools, clothes for servants, and woollen cloth, stuffs, serges, stockings, shoes, hats, and the like, such as servants wear; and whole pieces also to make up for servants, all by direction of the Quaker; and all this cargo arrived safe, and in good condition, with three woman-servants, lusty wenches, which my old governess had picked for me, suitable enough to the place, and to the work we had for them to do; one of which happened to come double, having been got with child by one of the seamen in the ship, as she owned afterwards, before the ship got so far as Gravesend; so she brought us a stout boy, about seven months after her landing.", "그중 하나는 마침 배가 부른 채로 왔으니, 그녀가 나중에 실토한 바로는 배가 그레이브젠드에 채 닿기도 전에 배의 선원 하나에게 임신을 당한 것이었다. 그리하여 그녀는 상륙한 지 일곱 달쯤 뒤에 우리에게 튼튼한 사내아이 하나를 낳아 주었다."]], [["My husband, you may suppose, was a little surprised at the arriving of all this cargo from England; and talking with me after he saw the account of this particular, “My dear,”", "여러분도 짐작하겠지만, 내 남편은 잉글랜드에서 온 이 모든 짐이 도착하자 조금 놀랐다. 그리고 이 짐의 명세서를 본 뒤 나와 이야기를 나누며 \"여보, 이게 다 어찌 된 영문이오?"], ["says he, “what is the meaning of all this?", "우리가 너무 깊이 빚을 지게 되는 건 아닌지 걱정이오."], ["I fear you will run us too deep in debt: when shall we be able to make return for it all?”", "우리가 언제 이걸 다 갚을 수 있겠소?\" 하고 물었다."], ["I smiled, and told him that it was all paid for; and then I told him, that what our circumstances might expose us to, I had not taken my whole stock with me, that I had reserved so much in my friend’s hands, which now we were come over safe, and was settled in a way to live, I had sent for, as he might see.", "나는 미소를 지으며 그것이 죄다 값이 치러진 것이라고 말한 뒤, 우리 처지가 어떤 일을 겪게 할지 몰라 내 밑천을 전부 가지고 오지 않고 벗의 손에 그만큼을 남겨 두었던 것인데, 이제 우리가 무사히 건너와 살아갈 길이 잡혔으니 그것을 가져오라고 사람을 보냈던 것이며, 그가 보다시피 이렇게 도착한 것이라고 일러 주었다."]], [["He was amazed, and stood a while telling upon his fingers, but said nothing.", "그는 어안이 벙벙하여 한참 동안 손가락을 꼽아 가며 셈을 하면서도 아무 말이 없었다."], ["At last he began thus: “Hold, let’s see,”", "마침내 그가 이렇게 입을 떼었다. \"가만, 어디 봅시다.\""], ["says he, telling upon his fingers still, and first on his thumb; “there’s £246 in money at first, then two gold watches, diamond rings, and plate,”", "하면서 그는 여전히 손가락을 꼽되, 우선 엄지부터 꼽으며 \"처음에 돈으로 246파운드, 그다음 금시계 두 개에 다이아몬드 반지들, 그리고 은붙이.\""], ["says he, upon the forefinger.", "하고는 검지를 꼽았다."], ["Then upon the next finger, “Here’s a plantation on York River, £100 a year, then £150 in money, then a sloop load of horses, cows, hogs, and stores”; and so on to the thumb again.", "그러고는 그다음 손가락을 꼽으며 \"여기 요크강에 있는 한 해 100파운드짜리 농장 하나, 그다음 돈 150파운드, 그다음 말과 암소와 돼지와 물품을 가득 실은 외돛배 한 척.\" 하고 셈하다가 다시 엄지로 돌아왔다."], ["“And now,”", "\"그리고 이제,\""], ["says he, “a cargo cost £250 in England, and worth here twice the money.”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"잉글랜드에서 250파운드 들인, 여기서는 그 곱절 값이 나가는 짐 한 무더기.\""], ["“Well,”", "\"그래서,\""], ["says I, “what do you make of all that?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"그걸 다 합쳐 어떻게 보세요?\""], ["“Make of it?”", "\"어떻게 보냐고?\""], ["says he; “why, who says I was deceived when I married a wife in Lancashire?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"아, 내가 랭커셔에서 아내를 얻을 때 속았다고 누가 그러던가?"], ["I think I have married a fortune, and a very good fortune too,”", "나는 한 재산, 그것도 아주 좋은 재산하고 결혼한 셈이지.\""], ["says he.", "하고 그가 말했다."]], [["In a word, we were now in very considerable circumstances, and every year increasing; for our new plantation grew upon our hands insensibly, and in eight years which we lived upon it, we brought it to such pitch, that the produce was at least £300 sterling a year; I mean, worth so much in England.", "한마디로, 우리는 이제 상당히 넉넉한 형편이 되었고, 해마다 형편이 나아졌다. 우리의 새 농장은 어느새 부쩍부쩍 자라났으니, 우리가 그곳에서 산 여덟 해 동안 그것을 어찌나 일구어 놓았던지, 수익이 적어도 한 해 300파운드, 곧 잉글랜드에서 그만한 값어치에 이를 정도였다."]], [["After I had been a year at home again, I went over the bay to see my son, and to receive another year’s income of my plantation; and I was surprised to hear, just at my landing there, that my old husband was dead, and had not been buried above a fortnight. This, I confess, was not disagreeable news, because now I could appear as I was, in a married condition; so I told my son before I came from him, that I believed I should marry a gentleman who had a plantation near mine; and though I was legally free to marry, as to any obligation that was on me before, yet that I was shy of it, lest the blot should some time or other be revived, and it might make a husband uneasy. My son, the same kind, dutiful, and obliging creature as ever, treated me now at his own house, paid me my hundred pounds, and sent me home again loaded with presents.", "다시 집에 돌아온 지 한 해가 지난 뒤, 나는 아들을 보고 또 농장의 또 한 해 수익을 받으러 만을 건너갔다. 그런데 그곳에 막 내리자마자, 내 늙은 남편이 죽어 묻힌 지 보름이 채 안 되었다는 소식을 듣고 놀랐다. 솔직히 말해 이것은 거슬리는 소식이 아니었으니, 이제 나는 있는 그대로의 모습, 곧 기혼의 처지로 나설 수 있게 되었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 아들에게서 떠나오기 전에, 내 농장 가까이에 농장을 가진 어떤 신사와 결혼하게 될 것 같다고 말하면서, 비록 이전에 내게 지워졌던 어떤 의무에 관해서는 법적으로 결혼할 자유가 있긴 하나, 그 오점이 언젠가 다시 들춰져 남편을 불편하게 할까 봐 나는 그것을 꺼린다고 일러 두었다. 여전히 한결같이 친절하고 효심 깊고 싹싹한 내 아들은, 이번에는 자기 집에서 나를 대접하고, 100파운드를 치러 주었으며, 선물을 잔뜩 실어 나를 다시 집으로 돌려보냈다. 이 일이 있고 얼마 뒤, 나는 아들에게 내가 결혼했다는 것을 알리고 그를 우리에게로 초대하였으며, 남편도 그에게 매우 정중한 편지를 써서 와서 만나 보자고 청하였다. 그리하여 그는 몇 달 뒤 과연 찾아왔는데, 마침 잉글랜드에서 온 내 짐이 들어온 바로 그때였다. 나는 그 짐이 죄다 내 것이 아니라 남편의 재산에 속한 것이라고 그가 믿게 해 두었다."]], [["Some time after this, I let my son know I was married, and invited him over to see us, and my husband wrote a very obliging letter to him also, inviting him to come and see him; and he came accordingly some months after, and happened to be there just when my cargo from England came in, which I let him believe belonged all to my husband’s estate, not to me.", "짚어 두어야 할 것은, 그 늙은 비참한 자, 곧 내 오라비(남편)가 죽었을 때, 나는 그제야 그 모든 일에 관해, 또 앞서 사촌이라 일컬었던 이 사람이 그 잘못된 불행한 결합으로 낳은 내 친아들이라는 것에 관해, 남편에게 거리낌 없이 다 털어놓았다는 점이다. 그는 그 이야기에 조금도 마음이 흔들리지 않았고, 설령 우리가 노인이라 부르던 그 사람이 살아 있었다 해도 자기는 마찬가지로 편안했을 것이라고 말했다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그건 당신 잘못도, 그 사람 잘못도 아니었으니까. 그건 막을 도리가 없는 착오였소.\" 그는 다만, 내가 그 사람이 내 오라비인 줄 안 뒤에도 그것을 감추고 아내로 함께 살자고 한 그 사람만은 나무랐으니, 그것이야말로 비열한 짓이었다고 말했다. 이리하여 이 모든 어려움은 다 풀어졌고, 우리는 더없이 친절하고 더없이 안락하게 함께 살았다."]], [["It must be observed that when the old wretch my brother (husband) was dead, I then freely gave my husband an account of all that affair, and of this cousin, as I had called him before, being my own son by that mistaken unhappy match.", "우리는 늙었다. 나는 거의 일흔 살이 되어 잉글랜드로 돌아왔고, 남편은 예순여덟이니, 우리는 정해진 유배 기간을 훨씬 넘겨 마쳤다."], ["He was perfectly easy in the account, and told me he should have been as easy if the old man, as we called him, had been alive. “For,”", "그리고 이제, 우리 둘이 함께 겪어 온 그 모든 고생과 그 모든 비참함에도 불구하고, 우리 둘 다 마음도 몸도 건강하다."], ["said he, “it was no fault of yours, nor of his; it was a mistake impossible to be prevented.” He only reproached him with desiring me to conceal it, and to live with him as a wife, after I knew that he was my brother; that, he said, was a vile part.", "남편은 우리 일을 정리하느라 나보다 얼마간 더 그곳에 남아 있었고, 처음에 나는 그에게로 돌아갈 작정이었으나, 그의 청에 따라 그 결심을 바꾸었으니, 그도 잉글랜드로 건너왔으며, 여기서 우리는 우리가 살아온 사악한 삶에 대한 진실한 참회로 남은 여생을 보내기로 결심하였다."], ["Thus all these difficulties were made easy, and we lived together with the greatest kindness and comfort imaginable.", "1683년에 쓰다"]]];
